	{
		title = "Eared seal",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of marine mammals",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Pinnipeds" },
		aliases = { "Otariid", "Otary", "Otariidae" },
		summary = "An **eared seal**, **otariid**, or **otary**, is any member of the [[Marine mammal|marine mammal]] family **Otariidae**, one of three groupings of pinnipeds. They comprise 15 [[Neontology|extant]] [[Species|species]] in seven [[Genus|genera]] (another species became extinct in the 1950s) and are commonly known either as sea lions or fur seals, distinct from true seals (phocids) and the [[Walrus|walrus]] (odobenids). Otariids are adapted to a semiaquatic lifestyle, feeding and migrating in the water, but breeding and resting on land or ice. They reside in subpolar, temperate, and equatorial waters throughout the [[Pacific Ocean|Pacific]] and Southern Oceans, the southern [[Indian Ocean|Indian]], and Atlantic Oceans. They are conspicuously absent in the north Atlantic.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The words "otariid" and "otary" come from the [[Ancient Greek]] ὠ (ōtárion), meaning "little ear", referring to the small but visible external ear flaps (pinnae), which distinguishes them from the [[Earless seal|phocids]].',
			},
		},
		related = { "Pinniped" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Otariidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Earless seal",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of mammals",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Pinnipeds" },
		aliases = { "Earless seals", "Phocids", "True seals" },
		summary = "The **earless seals**, also known as **phocids** or **true seals**, are one of the three main groups of [[Mammal|mammals]] within the seal lineage, [[Pinniped|Pinnipedia]]. All true seals are members of the family **Phocidae**. They are sometimes called **crawling seals** to distinguish them from the fur seals and sea lions of the family [[Eared seal|Otariidae]]. Seals live in the [[Ocean|oceans]] of both hemispheres and, with the exception of the more [[Tropics|tropical]] monk seals, are mostly confined to [[Polar regions|polar]], subpolar, and [[Temperate climate|temperate]] climates. The Baikal seal is the only species of exclusively freshwater seal.",
		related = { "Walrus" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Phocidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Earthworm",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Terrestrial invertebrate, order Opisthopora",
		tags = { "Invertebrates, others", "Lophotrochozoa" },
		summary = 'An **earthworm** is a [[Soil|soil]]-dwelling [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial]] [[Invertebrate|invertebrate]] that belongs to the [[Phylum|phylum]] Annelida. The term is the [[Common name|common name]] for the largest members of the [[Class (taxonomy)|class]] (or subclass, depending on the author) Oligochaeta. In classical systems, they were in the order of Opisthopora since the male pores opened posterior to the female pores, although the internal male segments are anterior to the female. Theoretical [[Cladistics|cladistic]] studies have placed them in the suborder Lumbricina of the order Haplotaxida, but this may change. Other [[Slang|slang]] names for earthworms include "dew-worm", "rainworm", "nightcrawler", and "angleworm" (from its use as [[Angling|angling]] hookbait).',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Larger terrestrial earthworms are also called megadriles (which translates to "big worms") as opposed to the microdriles ("small worms") in the families Tubificidae ([[Semiaquatic|semiaquatic]]), Lumbricidae and Enchytraeidae ([[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial]]). The megadriles are characterized by a distinct clitellum (more extensive than that of microdriles) and a vascular system with true capillaries. Earthworms are commonly found in moist, compost-rich soil, eating a wide variety of [[Organic matter|organic matters]], which include [[Detritus|detritus]], living [[Protozoa|protozoa]], nematodes, [[Bacteria|bacteria]], [[Mushroom|fungi]] and [[Algae|algae]] (including [[Diatom|diatoms]] and [[Cyanobacteria|cyanobacteria]]). An earthworm\'s digestive system runs the length of its body. They are one of nature\'s most important [[Detritivore|detritivores]] and coprophages, and also serve directly as [[Food|food]] for many low-level consumers ([[Predation|predation]]) within the [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]] (e.g. [[Wild boar|wild boars]], [[Mole (animal)|moles]], blackbirds, [[Centipede|centipedes]]), ground beetles, [[Flatworm|flatworms]].',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Lumbricina" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Earwig",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐞",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of insects",
		tags = { "Arthropods", "Insects and other hexapods" },
		aliases = { "Earwigs", "Dermaptera" },
		summary = '**Earwigs** make up the [[Insect|insect]] [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] **Dermaptera**. With about 2,000 [[Species|species]] in 12 [[Family (taxonomy)|families]], they are one of the smaller insect orders. Earwigs have characteristic cerci, a pair of forceps-like pincers on their abdomen, and membranous wings folded underneath short, rarely used forewings, hence the scientific order name, "skin wings". Earwigs are found on all continents except [[Antarctica]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Earwigs are mostly [[Nocturnal animal|nocturnal]] and often hide in small, moist crevices during the day, and are active at night, feeding on a wide variety of insects and plants. Damage to foliage, flowers, and various crops is commonly blamed on earwigs, especially the common earwig Forficula auricularia. Earwigs have five molts in the year before they become adults. Many earwig species display maternal care, which is uncommon among insects. Female earwigs may care for their eggs; the ones that do will continue to watch over [[Nymph (biology)|nymphs]] until their second molt. Extant Dermaptera belong to the suborder Neodermaptera, which first appeared during the [[Cretaceous]]. Some earwig specimen fossils are placed with extinct suborders Archidermaptera or Eodermaptera, the former dating to the [[Late Triassic]] and the latter to the Middle Jurassic.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Termite", "Cockroach" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Dermaptera" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Eastern copperhead",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of reptile",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Reptiles" },
		summary = "The eastern copperhead has distinctive, dark brown, hourglass-shaped markings, overlaid on a light reddish brown or brown/gray background. The body type is heavy, rather than slender. Neonates are born with green or yellow tail tips, which progress to a darker brown or black within one year. Adults grow to a typical length (including tail) of 50–95 cm.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In most of the United States of America, the eastern copperhead favors [[Deciduous|deciduous forest]] and mixed woodlands. It may occupy rock outcroppings and ledges, but is also found in low-lying, swampy regions. During the winter, it hibernates in dens or limestone crevices, often together with timber rattlesnakes and black rat snakes. The eastern copperhead is known to feed on a wide variety of prey, including invertebrates (primarily [[Arthropod|arthropods]]) and [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]]. Like most pit vipers, the eastern copperhead is generally an ambush predator; it takes up a promising position and waits for suitable prey to arrive. As a common species within its range, it may be encountered by humans. Unlike other viperids, it often "freezes" instead of slithering away and fleeing, due to its habit of relying on excellent camouflage. Bites occur due to people unknowingly stepping on or near them.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Lizard", "Turtle" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Agkistrodon contortrix" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Echidna",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of mammals",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Monotremes" },
		aliases = { "Echidnas", "Spiny anteaters", "Tachyglossidae" },
		summary = "**Echidnas**, sometimes known as **spiny anteaters**, are quill-covered [[Monotreme|monotremes]] (egg-laying [[Mammal|mammals]]) belonging to the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Tachyglossidae**, living in [[Australia]] and [[New Guinea]]. The four [[Neontology|extant]] species of echidnas and the [[Platypus|platypus]] are the only living mammals that lay eggs and the only surviving members of the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] Monotremata. The diet of some species consists of ants and termites, but they are not closely related to the American [[Anteater|true anteaters]] or to [[Hedgehog|hedgehogs]]. Their young are called puggles.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Echidnas evolved between 20 and 50 million years ago and descend from an amphibious, [[Platypus|platypus]]-like monotreme.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Tachyglossidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Echinoderm",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Marine phylum of animals often with radial symmetries",
		tags = { "Animals", "Echinoderms" },
		aliases = { "Echinodermata" },
		summary = 'An **echinoderm** is any [[Animal|animal]] of the [[Phylum|phylum]] **Echinodermata**, which includes [[Starfish|starfish]], brittle stars, [[Sea urchin|sea urchins]], sand dollars and [[Sea cucumber|sea cucumbers]], as well as the sessile sea lilies or "stone lilies". While bilaterally symmetrical as [[Larva|larvae]], as adults echinoderms are recognisable by their usually five-pointed radial symmetry (pentamerous symmetry), and are found on the sea bed at every ocean depth from the [[Intertidal zone|intertidal zone]] to the abyssal zone. The phylum contains about 7,600 living [[Species|species]], making it the second-largest group of deuterostomes after the [[Chordate|chordates]], as well as the largest marine-only phylum. The first definitive echinoderms appeared near the start of the [[Cambrian]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Echinoderms are important both ecologically and geologically. Ecologically, there are few other groupings so abundant in the [[Deep sea|deep sea]], as well as [[Continental shelf|shallower oceans]]. Most echinoderms are able to [[Asexual reproduction|reproduce asexually]] and [[Regeneration (biology)|regenerate]] tissue, organs and limbs; in some cases, they can undergo complete regeneration from a single limb. Geologically, the value of echinoderms is in their ossified dermal [[Endoskeleton|endoskeletons]], which are major contributors to many [[Limestone|limestone]] formations and can provide valuable clues as to the geological environment. They were the most used species in regenerative research in the 19th and 20th centuries. Further, some scientists hold that the radiation of echinoderms was responsible for the Mesozoic Marine Revolution.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Echinodermata" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Eel",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of fishes",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Fishes" },
		aliases = { "Eels", "Anguilliformes" },
		summary = "**Eels** are [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]] belonging to the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] **Anguilliformes**, which consists of eight suborders, 20 [[Family (taxonomy)|families]], 164 [[Genus|genera]], and about 1000 [[Species|species]]. Eels undergo considerable development from the early larval stage to the eventual adult stage and are usually [[Predation|predators]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The term "eel" is also used for some other eel-shaped fish, such as [[Electric eel|electric eels]] (genus Electrophorus), swamp eels (order Synbranchiformes), slime eels (class Myxini), and deep-sea spiny eels (family Notacanthidae). However, these other [[Clade|clades]], with the exception of deep-sea spiny eels, whose order Notacanthiformes is the sister clade to true eels, [[Convergent evolution|evolved their eel-like shapes independently]] from the true eels. As a general rule, most eels are marine. Exceptions are the catadromous genus Anguilla and the freshwater moray, which spend most of their life in freshwater, the [[Fish migration|anadromous]] rice-paddy eel, which spawns in freshwater, and the freshwater snake eel Stictorhinus.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Milkfish" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Anguilliformes" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electric eel",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of fishes in South America",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Fishes" },
		aliases = { "Electric eels", "Electrophorus", "Electrophorinae" },
		summary = "The **electric eels** are a [[Genus|genus]], **Electrophorus**, of neotropical [[Freshwater fish|freshwater fish]] from South America in the family Gymnotidae, of which they are the only members of the subfamily **Electrophorinae**. They are known for their ability to stun their prey by generating electricity, delivering shocks at up to 860 [[Volt|volts]]. Their electrical capabilities were first studied in 1775, contributing to the invention of the [[Electric battery|electric battery]] in 1800.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Despite their name, electric eels are not closely related to the true eels (Anguilliformes) but are members of the electroreceptive knifefish order Gymnotiformes. In 2019, electric eels were split into three species: for more than two centuries before that, the genus was believed to be monotypic, containing only Electrophorus electricus. They are nocturnal, obligate air-breathing animals, with poor vision complemented by electrolocation; they mainly eat fish. Electric eels grow for as long as they live, adding more vertebrae to their spinal column. Males are larger than females. Some captive specimens have lived for over 20 years.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Ophidiiformes" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Electrophorus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electric ray",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of cartilaginous fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Batoidea" },
		aliases = { "Electric rays", "Torpediniformes" },
		summary = "The **electric rays** are a group of rays, flattened cartilaginous fish with enlarged pectoral fins, composing the order **Torpediniformes**. They are known for being capable of producing an electric discharge, ranging from 8 to 220 [[Volt|volts]], depending on species, used to stun prey and for defense. There are 69 species in four families.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Perhaps the best known members are those of the [[Genus|genus]] Torpedo. The name comes from the [[Latin]], 'to be stiffened or paralyzed', from the effect on someone who touches the fish.",
