	{
		title = "Elk",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of deer",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		summary = 'The word "elk" originally referred to the European variety of the [[Moose|moose]], Alces alces, but was transferred to Cervus canadensis by North American colonists.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The name "wapiti" is derived from a Shawnee and Cree word meaning "white rump", after the distinctive light fur around the tail region which the animals may fluff-up or raise to signal their agitation or distress to one another, when fleeing perceived threats, or among males courting females and sparring for dominance. A similar trait is seen in other artiodactyl species, like the bighorn sheep, [[Pronghorn|pronghorn]] and the white-tailed deer, to varying degrees. Elk dwell in open forest and forest-edge habitats, grazing on grasses and sedges and browsing higher-growing plants, leaves, twigs and bark. Males also engage in ritualized mating behaviors during the mating season, including posturing to attract females, antler-wrestling (sparring), and bugling, a loud series of throaty whistles, bellows, screams, and other vocalizations that establish dominance over other males and aim to attract females.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Cervus canadensis" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Emu",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Large flightless bird endemic to Australia",
		tags = { "Birds", "Ratites" },
		summary = "The **emu** is a [[Species|species]] of [[Flightless bird|flightless bird]] [[Endemism|endemic]] to [[Australia]], where it is the tallest native bird. It is the only [[Neontology|extant]] member of the [[Genus|genus]] Dromaius and the third-tallest living bird after its African [[Ratite|ratite]] relatives, the [[Common ostrich|common ostrich]] and Somali ostrich. The emu's native ranges cover most of the Australian mainland. The Tasmanian, Kangaroo Island and King Island subspecies became [[Extinction|extinct]] after the European settlement of Australia in 1788.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The emu has soft, brown feathers, a long neck, and long legs. It can grow up to 1.9 m in height. It is a robust [[Bipedalism|bipedal]] runner that can travel great distances, and when necessary can sprint at 48 km/h. It is [[Omnivore|omnivorous]] and forages on a variety of plants and insects, and can go for weeks without eating. It drinks infrequently, but takes in copious amounts of fresh water when the opportunity arises. Breeding takes place in May and June, and fighting among females for a mate is common. Females can mate several times and lay several clutches of eggs in one season. The male incubates the eggs; during this process he hardly eats or drinks and loses a significant amount of weight. The eggs hatch after around eight weeks, and the young are nurtured by their fathers. They reach full size after around six months, but can remain as a family unit until the next breeding season.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Tyto" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Dromaius novaehollandiae" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Endoskeleton",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Internal support structure of an animal",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'An **endoskeleton** (from [[Ancient Greek]] ἔ (éndon), meaning "inside", and (skeletós), meaning "skeleton") is a structural frame ([[Skeleton|skeleton]]) — usually composed of mineralized tissue — on the inside of an [[Animal|animal]], overlaid by soft tissues. Endoskeletons serve as structural support against [[Gravity|gravity]] and mechanical loads, and provide anchoring attachment sites for skeletal muscles to transmit force and allow movements and [[Animal locomotion|locomotion]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'[[Vertebrate|Vertebrates]] and the closely related cephalochordates are the predominant animal [[Clade|clade]] with endoskeletons (made of mostly [[Bone|bone]] and sometimes cartilage, as well as notochordal glycoprotein and collagen fibers), although [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]] such as [[Sponge|sponges]] also have evolved a form of "rebar" endoskeletons made of diffuse meshworks of [[Calcite|calcite]]/[[Silicon dioxide|silica]] structural elements called spicules, and [[Echinoderm|echinoderms]] have a dermal calcite endoskeleton known as ossicles. Some coleoid [[Cephalopod|cephalopods]] ([[Squid|squids]] and cuttlefish) have an internalized vestigial [[Aragonite|aragonite]]/calcite-[[Chitin|chitin]] shell known as gladius or cuttlebone; these can serve as muscle attachments, but their main function is often to maintain [[Buoyancy|buoyancy]] rather than to give structural support, and their body shape is largely maintained by their hydroskeleton.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Equidae",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of hoofed mammals",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Perissodactyla" },
		aliases = { "Horse family" },
		summary = "**Equidae** (commonly known as the **horse family**) is the [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomic]] [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of horses and related animals, including asses, [[Zebra|zebras]], and many extinct [[Species|species]] known only from [[Fossil|fossils]]. The family evolved more than 50 million years ago, in the [[Eocene]] epoch, from a small, multi-toed [[Ungulate|ungulate]] into larger, single-toed animals. All [[Neontology|extant]] species are in the [[Genus|genus]] Equus, which originated in North America. Equidae belongs to the order [[Perissodactyla]], which includes the extant [[Tapir|tapirs]] and [[Rhinoceros|rhinoceros]], and several extinct families. It is more specifically grouped within the superfamily Equoidea, the only other family being the extinct Palaeotheriidae.",
		sections = { { "Overview", "The term **equid** refers to any member of this family, including any equine." } },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Equidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Eurasian eagle-owl",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦉",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of owl",
		tags = { "Birds", "Strigiformes" },
		aliases = { "Bubo bubo" },
		summary = "The **Eurasian eagle-owl** (**Bubo bubo**) is a species of eagle-owl, a type of bird that resides much over [[Eurasia]]. It is often just called the **eagle-owl** in Europe and Asia.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is one of the largest species of [[Owl|owl]]. Females can grow to a total length of 75 cm, with a wingspan of 188 cm. Males are slightly smaller. This bird has distinctive ear tufts, with upper parts that are mottled with darker blackish colouring and tawny. The wings and tail are barred. The underparts are a variably hued buff, streaked with darker colouring. The facial disc is not very defined. The orange eyes are distinctive. At least 12 [[Subspecies|subspecies]] of the Eurasian eagle-owl are described. Eurasian eagle-owls are found in many habitats; mostly mountainous and [[Rock (geology)|rocky]] areas, often near varied woodland edge and near [[Shrub|shrubby]] areas with openings or [[Wetland|wetlands]]. They also inhabit coniferous forests, [[Steppe|steppes]], and remote areas. Occasionally, they are found in farmland and in [[Park|park]]-like settings in European and Asian cities and, very rarely, in busier urban areas. The eagle-owl is mostly a [[Nocturnal animal|nocturnal predator]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Galliformes", "Bird of prey" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Bubo bubo" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Eurasian magpie",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of bird",
		tags = { "Birds", "Corvoidea" },
		aliases = { "Common magpie", "Pica pica" },
		summary = 'The **Eurasian magpie** or **common magpie** (**Pica pica**) is a resident breeding bird throughout the northern part of the Eurasian continent. It is one of several birds in the [[Corvidae|crow family]] (corvids) that are designated as magpies, and it belongs to the Holarctic radiation of "monochrome" magpies. In Europe, the term "magpie" is used by [[English language|English]] speakers as a synonym for the Eurasian magpie; the only other species of magpie in Europe is the Iberian magpie (Cyanopica cooki), which is restricted to the [[Iberian Peninsula]]. Despite sharing a name and similar colouration, they are not closely related to the Australian magpie, which is an artamid, only sharing the same clade (Corvides).',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Eurasian magpie is one of the most intelligent birds. The expansion of its nidopallium is approximately the same in its relative size as the brains of [[Chimpanzee|chimpanzees]], [[Gorilla|gorillas]], [[Orangutan|orangutans]] and [[Human|humans]]. It is one of the few bird species known to pass the mirror test.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Honeyeater" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Pica pica" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "European badger",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of mustelid",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Carnivora" },
		aliases = { "Meles meles", "Eurasian badger" },
		summary = 'The **European badger** (**Meles meles**), also known as the **Eurasian badger**, is a species of badger in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Mustelidae]] native to [[Europe]] and [[West Asia]] and parts of [[Central Asia]]. It is classified as [[Least-concern species|least concern]] on the [[IUCN Red List]] due to its wide range and large, stable population size which is thought to be increasing in some regions. Several subspecies are recognised, with the nominate subspecies (M. m. meles) predominating in most of Europe. In the UK it is the only extant species, and is simply known as the "**badger**".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The European badger is a powerfully built animal with a small head, stocky body, small black eyes and short tail, and a coat of black, white, brown, and grey fur. Its weight varies, ranging from 7–13 kg in spring to 15–17 kg in autumn before the winter sleep period. It is [[Nocturnal animal|nocturnal]] and [[Sociality|social]], living in burrows and sleeping during the day in one of several setts within its territory. These burrows have multiple chambers and entrances and comprise extensive systems of underground passages measuring 35–81 m in length. Several badger families use these setts for decades. Badgers are particular about the cleanliness of their burrows, carrying in fresh bedding and removing soiled material. They defecate in latrines situated strategically outside their setts or en route to others.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cougar", "Weasel" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Meles meles" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "European bison",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Eurasian species of mammal",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		summary = "It is one of two extant species of bison, alongside the [[American bison]]. The European bison is the heaviest wild [[Terrestrial animal|land animal]] in [[Europe]], and individuals in the past may have been even larger than their modern-day descendants. During [[Late antiquity|late antiquity]] and the [[Middle Ages]], bison became extinct in most of Europe and [[Asia]], surviving into the 20th century only in northern-central Europe and the northern [[Caucasus Mountains]]. During the early years of the 20th century, the remaining bison were hunted to extinction in the wild, with domestic bison later reintroduced into the wild.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"By the late 2010s, the species numbered several thousand and had been returned to the wild by captive breeding programmes. It is no longer in immediate danger of extinction, but remains absent from most of its historical range. It is not to be confused with the [[Aurochs|aurochs]] (Bos primigenius), the extinct ancestor of domestic [[Cattle|cattle]], with which it once co-existed. Besides humans, bison have few predators. In the 19th century, there were scattered reports of wolves, lions, tigers, and bears hunting bison. European bison were [[Overexploitation|hunted to extinction]] in the wild in the early 20th century, with the last wild animals of the B. b. bonasus subspecies being shot in the Białowieża Forest (on today's Belarus–Poland border) in 1921. The last of the Caucasian wisent subspecies (B. b. caucasicus) was shot in the northwestern Caucasus in 1927. The Carpathian wisent (B. b.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Bison bonasus" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Near Threatened" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "European robin",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of bird",
		tags = { "Birds", "Passerida" },
		aliases = { "Erithacus rubecula", "Robin", "Robin redbreast" },
		summary = "The **European robin** (**Erithacus rubecula**), known simply as the **robin** or **robin redbreast** in the British Isles, is a small insectivorous [[Passerine|passerine]] bird belonging to the [[Old World flycatcher]] family Muscicapidae. It is found across Europe, as far east as Western [[Siberia]], and as far south as North Africa. It is [[Sedentism|sedentary]] in the west and south of its range, and [[Bird migration|migratory]] in the north and east of its range where winters are harsher.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is 12.5–14.0 cm in length. First-winter immatures resemble the adults, except they have more obvious yellow-brown tips to the wing covert feathers, which are inconspicuous or absent in adults.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Sunbird", "Finch" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Erithacus rubecula" },
