	},
	{
		title = "Flatfish",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Suborder of fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		aliases = { "Pleuronectoidei" },
		summary = '**Flatfish** are a group of [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]] belonging to the [[Order (taxonomy)|suborder]] **Pleuronectoidei** of the order Carangiformes. Their collective [[Common name|common name]] is due to their habit of lying on one side of their laterally-compressed body (flattened side-to-side) upon the seafloor; in this position, both eyes lie on the side of the head facing upwards, while the other side of the head and body (the "blind side") lies on the substrate. This loss of symmetry, a unique adaptation in [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]], stems from one eye "migrating" towards the other during the juvenile\'s [[Metamorphosis|metamorphosis]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Due to interspecific variation, some species tend to face their left side upward, some their right side, and others face either side upward; members of Pleuronectidae lie on their left side, with eyes on the right, Paralichthyidae lie on their right side, with eyes on the left, while the "primitive" genus Psettodes may develop into "right-facing" or "left-facing" individuals. They are one of the most [[Species diversity|diverse]] groups of demersal fish. Their cryptic coloration and habits, a form of [[Camouflage|camouflage]], conceals them from potential predators. Many species are of interest for fisheries.',
			},
		},
		related = { "European seabass", "Perch" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Pleuronectoidei" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Flatworm",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Phylum of soft-bodied invertebrates",
		tags = { "Invertebrates, others", "Platyzoa" },
		aliases = { "Platyhelminthes", "Flatworms", "Flat worms" },
		summary = "**Platyhelminthes** is a phylum of relatively simple bilaterian, [[Segmentation (biology)|unsegmented]], soft-bodied [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]] commonly called **flatworms** or **flat worms**. Being acoelomates (having no body cavity), and having no specialised circulatory and respiratory [[Organ (biology)|organs]], they are restricted to having flattened shapes that allow [[Oxygen|oxygen]] and nutrients to pass through their bodies by [[Diffusion|diffusion]]. The digestive cavity has only one opening for both ingestion (intake of nutrients) and egestion (removal of undigested wastes); as a result, the [[Food|food]] can not be processed continuously.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"However, since the turbellarians have since been proven not to be [[Monophyly|monophyletic]], this classification is now deprecated. Free-living flatworms are mostly predators, and live in [[Water|water]] or in shaded, humid terrestrial environments, such as leaf litter. The eggs of trematodes are excreted from their main hosts, whereas adult cestodes generate vast numbers of hermaphroditic, segment-like proglottids that detach when mature, are excreted, and then release eggs. Because they do not have internal body cavities, Platyhelminthes have historically been regarded as a primitive stage in the [[Evolution|evolution]] of bilaterians (animals with bilateral symmetry and hence distinct front and rear ends). However, analyses since the mid-1980s have separated out one subgroup, the Acoelomorpha, as [[Basal (phylogenetics)|basal]] bilaterians – closer to the original [[Bilateria|bilaterians]] than to any other modern groups.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Platyhelminthes" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Flightless bird",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Birds that cannot fly",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Flightless birds" },
		summary = "**Flightless birds** are [[Bird|birds]] that cannot fly, as they have, through [[Evolution|evolution]], lost the ability. There are over 60 extant species, including the well-known ratites (ostriches (Struthio), [[Emu|emus]] (Dromaius), [[Cassowary|cassowaries]] (Casuarius), [[Rhea (bird)|rheas]], [[Kiwi (bird)|kiwis]] (Apteryx)), and [[Penguin|penguins]] (Sphenisciformes). The smallest flightless bird is the Inaccessible Island rail (length 12.5 cm, weight 34.7 g). The largest (both heaviest and tallest) flightless bird, which is also the largest living bird in general, is the [[Common ostrich|common ostrich]] (2.7 m, 156 kg).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some domesticated birds, such as the domestic chicken, have lost the ability to fly for extended periods, although their ancestral species, the red junglefowl and others, respectively, are capable of extended flight. There were families of flightless birds, such as the now-extinct Phorusrhacidae, that evolved to be powerful terrestrial predators. Furthermore, they also share traits with some of the ratites of being giant, flightless birds with vestigial wings, long legs, and long necks, although they are unrelated.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Fly",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🪰",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of insects",
		tags = { "Insects and other hexapods", "Flies (Diptera)" },
		aliases = { "Diptera" },
		summary = "A **fly** is an [[Insect|insect]] of the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] **Diptera**, named for the single pair of wings. The hindwings have evolved into halteres, organs that sense rotation, giving flies precise control of their flight. Diptera is a large order containing more than 150,000 [[Species|species]], including horse-flies, crane flies, hoverflies, mosquitoes and others.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Flies have a mobile head, with a pair of large compound eyes, and mouthparts designed either for piercing and sucking (in mosquitoes, black flies, and robber flies) or for lapping and sucking (in the other groups). Their wing arrangement gives them great manoeuvrability in flight, and claws and pads on their feet enable them to cling to smooth surfaces. Flies undergo complete [[Metamorphosis|metamorphosis]]; the eggs are often laid on the larval food-source and the larvae, which lack true limbs, develop in a protected environment, often inside their food source. The pupa is a tough capsule from which the adult emerges when ready to do so; flies mostly have short lives as adults. Diptera is one of the major insect orders and of considerable ecological and human importance. Flies are major pollinators, second only to bees and their [[Hymenoptera|Hymenopteran]] relatives.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Black fly", "Tabanidae" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Diptera" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Flying fish",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of marine fish that can make powerful, self-propelled leaps out of water",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Fishes" },
		aliases = { "Exocoetidae", "Flying cod" },
		summary = 'The **Exocoetidae** are a [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of saltwater [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]] in the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] Beloniformes, known colloquially as **flying fish** or **flying cod**, with about 64 [[Species|species]] in seven [[Genus|genera]]. While they do not "fly" in the same way a bird does, flying fish can make powerful leaps out of the water where their long, [[Wing|wing]]-like paired fins act as aerofoils to generate [[Lift (force)|lift]] and enable prolonged [[Gliding flight|gliding]] for considerable distances above the water surface. The main reason for this behavior is thought to be to escape from underwater predators, which include [[Swordfish|swordfish]], [[Mackerel|mackerel]], [[Tuna|tuna]], and [[Marlin|marlin]], among others, though their periods of flight expose them to attack by aerial predators such as [[Frigatebird|frigatebirds]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'[[Barbados]] is known as "the land of the flying fish" and the fish is one of the national symbols of the country. The French Exocet anti-ship missile is also named after them, as the missile can be launched from underwater, and take a low, sea-skimming trajectory before striking the targets.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Osteichthyes", "Coelacanth" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Exocoetidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Forage fish",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Small prey fish",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Prey fish", "Bait fish" },
		summary = "**Forage fish**, also called **prey fish** or **bait fish**, are small pelagic fish that feed on [[Plankton|planktons]] (i.e. planktivores) and other small aquatic organisms (e.g. [[Krill|krill]]). They are in turn preyed upon by various [[Predation|predators]] including larger fish, [[Seabird|seabirds]] and [[Marine mammal|marine mammals]], this making them [[Keystone species|keystone species]] in their [[Aquatic ecosystem|aquatic ecosystems]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The typical ocean forage fish feed at the lower trophic level of the food chain, often by filter feeding. They include particularly fishes of the order Clupeiformes ([[Herring|herrings]], [[Sardine|sardines]], shad, hilsa, menhaden, anchovies, and [[Sprat|sprats]]), but also other small fish, including halfbeaks, silversides, [[Smelt (fish)|smelt]] such as capelin and goldband fusiliers. Forage fish compensate for their small size by forming [[Shoaling and schooling|schools]]. Some swim in synchronised grids with their mouths open so they can efficiently filter plankton. These schools can become immense [[Shoaling and schooling|shoals]] which move along coastlines and [[Fish migration|migrate]] across open oceans. The shoals are concentrated energy resources for great marine predators. The predators keenly focus on the shoals, acutely aware of their numbers and whereabouts, and make migrations themselves that can span thousands of miles to pursue them.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Fox",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Group of carnivorous mammals",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Carnivora" },
		aliases = { "Foxes" },
		summary = '**Foxes** are small-to-medium-sized [[Omnivore|omnivorous]] [[Mammal|mammals]] belonging to several [[Genus|genera]] of the family [[Canidae]]. They have a flattened skull; upright, triangular ears; a pointed, slightly upturned snout; and a long, bushy [[Tail|tail]] ("brush").',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Twelve [[Species|species]] belong to the [[Monophyly|monophyletic]] "true fox" group of genus Vulpes. Another 25 current or [[Extinction|extinct]] species are sometimes called foxes but are either part of the [[Paraphyly|paraphyletic]] group of the South American foxes or belong to an outlying group, the latter consisting of the bat-eared fox, the gray fox, and the island fox. Foxes live on every continent except Antarctica. The most common and widespread species of fox is the red fox (Vulpes vulpes) with about 47 recognized [[Subspecies|subspecies]]. The global distribution of foxes, together with their widespread reputation for cunning, has contributed to their prominence in popular culture and folklore in many societies around the world. The hunting of foxes with packs of hounds, long an established pursuit in Europe, especially in the [[British Isles]], was exported by European settlers to various parts of the [[New World]].',
			},
		},
		related = { "Snow leopard" },
	},
	{
		title = "Freshwater fish",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fish that mostly live in freshwater",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Freshwater fish** are [[Fish|fish]] species that spend some or all of their lives in [[Body of water|bodies]] of [[Fresh water|fresh water]] such as [[River|rivers]], [[Lake|lakes]], [[Pond|ponds]] and inland [[Wetland|wetlands]], where the [[Salinity|salinity]] is less than 1.05%. These environments differ from marine habitats in many ways, especially the difference in levels of osmolarity. To survive in fresh water, fish need a range of [[Physiology|physiological]] [[Adaptation|adaptations]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"41.24% of all known species of [[Fish|fish]] are found in fresh water. This is primarily due to the rapid [[Speciation|speciation]] that the scattered habitats make possible. When dealing with [[Pond|ponds]] and lakes, one might use the same basic models of speciation as when studying island biogeography.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Friesian horse",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Breed of horse",
		tags = { "Cats", "Horses" },
		aliases = { "Friesian" },
		summary = "The **Friesian** ( in Dutch; in West Frisian) is a horse breed originating in the province of Friesland in the northern Netherlands. The breed nearly became extinct on more than one occasion. It is classified as a light draught horse, and the modern-day Friesian horse is used for riding and driving. This breed is most known for its all-black coat colour, long, flowing mane and tail, feathering on its legs, high head carriage, and high-stepping action.",
