	{
		title = "Galaxiidae",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of mostly small freshwater fish",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Osmeriformes" },
		summary = "The **Galaxiidae** are a family of mostly small freshwater [[Fish|fish]] in the [[Southern Hemisphere]]. The majority live in Southern Australia or [[New Zealand]], but some are found in [[South Africa]], southern [[South America]], Lord Howe Island, [[New Caledonia]], and the [[Falkland Islands]]. One galaxiid species, the common galaxias (Galaxias maculatus), is probably the most widely naturally distributed freshwater fish in the Southern Hemisphere. They are coolwater species, found in [[Temperate climate|temperate]] [[Latitude|latitudes]], with only one species known from [[Subtropics|subtropical]] habitats. Many specialise in living in cold, high-altitude upland rivers, streams, and lakes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some galaxiids live in fresh water all their lives, but many have a partially marine lifecycle. In these cases, [[Larva|larvae]] are hatched in a river, but are washed downstream to the ocean, later returning to rivers as juveniles to complete their development to full adulthood. This pattern differs from that of [[Salmon|salmon]], which only return to fresh water to breed, and is described as [[Fish migration|amphidromous]]. Freshwater galaxiid species are threatened, some gravely, by exotic salmonid species, particularly [[Trout|trout]] species, which prey upon galaxiids and compete with them for food. Exotic salmonids have been introduced by humans to many different land masses (e.g. Australia, New Zealand), with little apparent thought as to ecological impacts on native fish.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Salmonidae" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Galaxiidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Galliformes",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of heavy-bodied ground-feeding birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Galliformes" },
		aliases = { "Gallinaceous birds", "Landfowl" },
		summary = "**Galliformes**, also known as **gallinaceous birds** or **landfowl**, is an [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] of heavy-bodied [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial]] [[Bird|birds]] that contains about 290 [[Species|species]]. The order is divided into five [[Family (taxonomy)|families]]: Phasianidae ([[Chicken|chicken]] and junglefowls, Old World quails, partridges, pheasants, [[Turkey (bird)|turkeys]], [[Peafowl|peafowl]] and [[Grouse|grouse]]), Odontophoridae (New World quails), Numididae ([[Guinea fowl|guinea fowl]]), Cracidae (including chachalacas and curassows), and Megapodiidae (incubator birds like malleefowl and brushturkeys). Galliformes and the semi-aquatic order Anseriformes (waterfowl) are collectively called fowl.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Galliform birds have adapted to most environments except innermost [[Desert|deserts]] and perpetually icy regions, inhabiting every [[Continent|continent]] except [[Antarctica]]. They are important in their ecosystems as seed dispersers and [[Insectivore|insectivores]]/vermivores, and many are skilled ground-dwellers that escape [[Predation|predators]] by running rather than flying. They are mainly non-migratory. The name galliformes derives from the [[Type genus|type genus]] Gallus, [[Latin]] for "[[Chicken|rooster]]". Several galliform species (most notably the red junglefowl, which have been [[Domestication|domesticated]] into the [[Chicken|chicken]]) have been farmed by [[Human|humans]] as [[Poultry|poultry]] for their [[Meat|meat]] and [[Egg|eggs]], and the [[Wildlife|wild]] species are [[Hunting|hunted]] as upland game birds, where they are known as **gamefowl**.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Common pheasant" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Galliformes" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Galápagos tortoise",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of reptile",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Reptiles" },
		aliases = { "Chelonoidis niger", "Galápagos giant tortoise" },
		summary = "The **Galápagos tortoise** (**Chelonoidis niger**), also called the **Galápagos giant tortoise**, is a very large species of [[Tortoise|tortoise]] in the [[Genus|genus]] Chelonoidis (which also contains three smaller species from mainland South America). The species comprises 15 [[Subspecies|subspecies]] (12 [[Neontology|extant]] and 3 [[Extinction|extinct]]). It is the largest living species of tortoise, and can weigh up to 417 kg. They are also the largest extant terrestrial cold-blooded animals (ectotherms).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'With lifespans in the wild of over 100 years, it is one of the longest-lived vertebrates. Captive Galapagos tortoises can live up to 177 years. For example, a captive individual, Harriet, lived for at least 175 years. Spanish explorers, who discovered the islands in the 16th century, named them after the Spanish galápago, meaning "tortoise". Galápagos tortoises are native to seven of the [[Galápagos Islands]]. Shell size and shape vary between subspecies and populations. On islands with humid highlands and abundant low vegetation, the tortoises are larger, with domed shells and short necks; on islands with dry lowlands and less ground-level vegetation, the tortoises are smaller, with "saddleback" shells and long necks. [[Charles Darwin]]\'s observations of these differences on the second voyage of the Beagle in 1835 contributed to the [[Evolution|development of his theory of evolution]].',
			},
		},
		related = { "Alligator snapping turtle", "Coral snake", "Green iguana", "Painted turtle" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Chelonoidis niger" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Game fish",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Popular fish targeted in recreational fishing",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Sport fish" },
		summary = "**Game fish**, **sport fish** or **quarry** refer to popular [[Fish|fish]] species pursued by [[Recreational fishing|recreational fishers]] (typically [[Angling|anglers]]), and can be [[Freshwater fish|freshwater]] or saltwater fish. Game fish can be [[Fish as food|eaten after being caught]], preserved as [[Taxidermy|taxidermy]] (though rare), or released after capture. Some game fish are also targeted [[Commercial fishing|commercially]], particularly less bony species such as [[Salmon|salmon]] and [[Tuna|tuna]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Specimens of game fish whose measurements (body length and weight) significantly exceed the species' average are sometimes known as **trophy fish**, as such captures are often presented as bragging rights among fishers.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Garter snake",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Common name for snakes of the genus Thamnophis",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Reptiles" },
		aliases = { "Thamnophis" },
		summary = "**Garter snake** is the [[Common name|common name]] for small to medium-sized [[Snake|snakes]] belonging to the [[Genus|genus]] **Thamnophis** in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Colubridae. They are native to [[North America|North]] and [[Central America]], ranging from central [[Canada]] in the north to [[Costa Rica]] in the south.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"With about 37 recognized species and 52 subspecies, garter snakes are highly variable in appearance; generally, they have large round eyes with rounded pupils, a slender build, keeled scales (appearing 'raised'), and a pattern of longitudinal stripes that may or may not include spots (although some have no stripes at all). Certain subspecies have stripes of blue, yellow, or red, mixed with black tops and beige-tan underbelly markings. They also vary significantly in total length, from 18 to 51 in. With no real consensus on the classification of the species of Thamnophis, disagreements between [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomists]] and disputed sources (such as field guides) are common. One area of debate, for example, is whether two specific types of snake are separate species, or [[Subspecies|subspecies]] of the same.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Lizard", "Turtle" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Thamnophis" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gastropod",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Class of molluscs",
		tags = { "Animals", "Mollusks" },
		aliases = { "Gastropods" },
		summary = "**Gastropods** are a vast and diverse group of [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]] within the phylum [[Mollusca]], comprising the animals commonly known as snails and slugs. With an estimated 65,000 to 80,000 living species, they form the second-largest animal [[Class (taxonomy)|class]] after the [[Insect|insects]]. The [[Fossil|fossil record]] of gastropods extends back to the Late Cambrian. As of 2017, 721 [[Family (taxonomy)|families]] are recognized—476 extant (some with fossil representatives) and 245 extinct known only from fossils.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Gastropods inhabit an extraordinary range of environments, including marine, [[Fresh water|freshwater]], and [[Terrestrial ecosystem|terrestrial]] ecosystems. The term snail generally refers to gastropods with a large external shell into which the body can fully retract, while slugs have no shell or a small internal one, and semislugs can only partially withdraw into their reduced shell. Marine gastropods include familiar forms such as abalones, conches, periwinkles, whelks, and cowries, whose shells are usually coiled in adulthood. In contrast, limpets and related groups coil their shells only in the larval stage, retaining a simple conical form as adults. Because of their vast diversity, gastropods exhibit remarkable variation in [[Anatomy|anatomy]], [[Behavior|behavior]], feeding, and [[Reproduction|reproduction]], making broad generalizations difficult. Gastropods remain one of evolution's most successful and adaptable animal lineages.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Gastropoda" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gaur",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Largest species of the bovid family",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		aliases = { "Indian bison" },
		summary = "The **gaur**, also known as the **Indian bison**, is a large bovine native to the [[Indian subcontinent]] and [[Southeast Asia]], and has been listed as [[Vulnerable species|Vulnerable]] on the [[IUCN Red List]] since 1986. The global population was estimated at a maximum of 21,000 mature individuals in 2016, with the majority of those existing in India. It is the largest species among the wild cattle and the [[Bovidae]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The domesticated gayal or mithun originated partly from the wild gaur and is most common in the border regions of [[Northeast India]] ([[Assam]], [[Manipur]], Nagaland) and [[Bangladesh]] with [[Myanmar]] and [[Yunnan]], [[China]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "African buffalo" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Bos gaurus" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Vulnerable" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gazelle",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of mammals",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		aliases = { "Gazella" },
		summary = "A **gazelle** is one of many [[Antelope|antelope]] species in the [[Genus|genus]] **Gazella**. There are also seven species included in two further genera; Eudorcas and Nanger, which were formerly considered subgenera of Gazella. A third former subgenus, Procapra, includes three living species of Asian gazelles.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Gazelles are known as swift animals. Some can run at bursts as high as 60 mph or run at a sustained speed of 30 mph. Gazelles are found mostly in the deserts, grasslands, and [[Savanna|savannas]] of [[Africa]], but they are also found in southwest and [[Central Asia|central Asia]] and the [[Indian subcontinent]]. They tend to live in herds, and eat fine, easily digestible plants and leaves. Gazelles are relatively small antelopes, most standing 2–3.5 ft high at the shoulder, and are generally fawn-colored. The gazelle genera are Gazella, Eudorcas, and Nanger. The [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomy]] of these genera is confused, and the classification of [[Species|species]] and [[Subspecies|subspecies]] has been an unsettled issue. Currently, the genus Gazella is widely considered to contain about 10 species. One species is extinct: the Queen of Sheba's gazelle. Most surviving gazelle species are considered threatened to varying degrees.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Gazella" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gecko",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Lizards belonging to the suborder Gekkota",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Reptiles" },
		aliases = { "Geckos" },
		summary = "**Geckos** are small, mostly carnivorous [[Lizard|lizards]] that have a wide distribution, found on every continent except [[Antarctica]]. Belonging to the [[Order (taxonomy)|suborder]] **Gekkota**, geckos are found in warm [[Climate|climates]]. They range from 1.6 to 67 cm in length. They are the most species-rich group of lizards, with nearly 2,000 different species of geckos existing.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Geckos are unique among lizards for their [[Animal communication|vocalisations]], which differ from species to species. Most geckos in the family Gekkonidae use chirping or clicking sounds in their social interactions. Tokay geckos (Gekko gecko) are known for their loud mating calls, and some other species are capable of making hissing noises when alarmed or threatened. All geckos, except species in the family Eublepharidae, lack eyelids; instead, the outer surface of the eyeball has a [[Transparency and translucency|transparent]] membrane, the brille. They have a fixed lens within each iris that enlarges in darkness to let in more light. Since they cannot blink, species without eyelids generally lick their own brilles when they need to clear them of dust and dirt, in order to keep them clean and moist.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Turtle" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Gekkota" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Genus",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Taxonomic rank above species and below family",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Genera", "Genus (biology)" },
		summary = "**Genus** is a [[Taxonomic rank|taxonomic rank]] above [[Species|species]] and below [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] as used in the biological classification of [[Neontology|living]] and [[Fossil|fossil]] organisms as well as viruses. In [[Binomial nomenclature|binomial nomenclature]], the genus name forms the first part of the binomial species name for each species within the genus.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Panthera leo (lion) and Panthera onca (jaguar) are two species within the genus Panthera. Panthera is a genus within the family [[Felidae]]. The composition of a genus is determined by [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomists]]. The standards for genus classification are not strictly codified, so different authorities often produce different classifications for genera. Reasonable Compactness – a genus should not be expanded needlessly. Distinctness – with respect to evolutionarily relevant criteria, i.e. [[Ecology|ecology]], [[Morphology (biology)|morphology]], or [[Biogeography|biogeography]]; DNA sequences are a consequence rather than a condition of diverging evolutionary lineages except in cases where they directly inhibit gene flow (e.g. postzygotic barriers). Moreover, genera should be composed of [[Phylogenetics|phylogenetic]] units of the same kind as other (analogous) genera.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Geometer moth",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of insects",
		tags = { "Insects and other hexapods", "Lepidoptera" },
		aliases = { "Geometer moths", "Geometridae" },
		summary = 'The **geometer moths** are [[Moth|moths]] belonging to the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Geometridae** of the insect [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] [[Lepidoptera]], the moths and butterflies. Their scientific name derives from the Ancient Greek (derivative form of or "the earth"), and "measure" in reference to the way their larvae, or **inchworms**, appear to measure the earth as they move along in a looping fashion. Geometridae is a very large family, containing around 23,000 described species; over 1400 species from six subfamilies are indigenous to North America alone. A well-known member is the peppered moth, Biston betularia, which has been the subject of numerous studies in [[Population genetics|population genetics]]. Several other geometer moths are notorious [[Pest (organism)|pests]].',
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Geometridae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gibbon",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of apes",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Primates" },
		aliases = { "Gibbons", "Hylobatidae" },
		summary = "**Gibbons** are [[Ape|apes]] in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Hylobatidae**. The family historically contained one [[Genus|genus]], but now is split into four extant genera and 20 [[Species|species]]. Gibbons live in subtropical and tropical forests from eastern [[Bangladesh]] and [[Northeast India]] to [[Mainland Southeast Asia|Southeast Asia]] and [[Indonesia]] (including the islands of [[Sumatra]], [[Borneo]] and Java).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Like all of the apes, gibbons are [[Tail|tailless]]. Unlike most of the great apes, gibbons frequently form long-term pair bonds. Their primary mode of locomotion, brachiation, involves swinging from branch to branch for distances up to 15 m, at speeds as fast as 55 km/h. They can also make leaps up to 8 m, and walk bipedally with their arms raised for balance. They are the fastest of all tree-dwelling, nonflying mammals.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Slow loris" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Hylobatidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gnathostomata",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Infraphylum of vertebrates",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Vertebrates, general classification" },
		summary = "**Gnathostomata** (; from [[Ancient Greek]]: (gnathos) 'jaw' + (stoma) 'mouth') are jawed [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]]. Gnathostome diversity comprises roughly 60,000 species, which accounts for 99% of all [[Neontology|extant]] vertebrates, including all living bony fishes (both [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned]] and lobe-finned, including the [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial]] [[Tetrapod|tetrapods]]) and cartilaginous fishes, as well as [[Extinction|extinct]] prehistoric fish such as [[Placoderm|placoderms]] and acanthodians. Most gnathostomes have retained ancestral traits like true teeth, a stomach, and paired appendages (pectoral and pelvic fins, [[Limb (anatomy)|limbs]], [[Wing|wings]], etc.).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Other traits are elastin, horizontal semicircular canal of the inner ear, myelinated [[Neuron|neurons]], and an adaptive immune system which has discrete lymphoid organs (spleen and thymus) and uses V(D)J recombination to create antigen recognition sites, rather than using genetic recombination in the variable lymphocyte receptor gene. It is now assumed that Gnathostomata evolved from ancestors that already possessed two pairs of paired fins. Until recently these ancestors, known as antiarchs, were thought to have lacked pectoral or pelvic fins. In addition to this, some [[Placoderm|placoderms]] were shown to have a third pair of paired appendages, that had been modified to claspers in males and pelvic basal plates in females—a pattern not seen in any other vertebrate group. The jawless Osteostraci are generally considered the closest [[Sister group|sister taxon]] of Gnathostomata.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Gnathostomata" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Goat",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of domesticated mammal",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		aliases = { "Domestic goat", "Capra hircus" },
		summary = "The **goat** or **domestic goat** (**Capra hircus**) is a species of goat-antelope that is mostly kept as [[Livestock|livestock]]. It was [[Domestication|domesticated]] from the wild goat (C. aegagrus) of Southwest Asia and [[Eastern Europe]]. The goat is a member of the family [[Bovidae]] and is closely related to the [[Sheep|sheep]]. It was one of the first animals to be domesticated, in Iran around 10,000 years ago.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Goats have been used for [[Milk|milk]], meat, wool, and skins across much of the world. Milk from goats is often turned into cheese. In 2022, there were more than 1.1 billion goats living in the world, of which 150 million were in India.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Capra hircus" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Domesticated" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gobioidei",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		summary = "The **Gobioidei** are a [[Order (taxonomy)|suborder]] of percomorph fish. Many of these fishes are called **gobies**. It is by far the largest and most diverse order within the order Gobiiformes, and one of the most diverse groups of [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]] in general.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The suborder, which was previously considered a suborder of [[Perciformes]], is made up of about 2,211 species that are divided between seven families. Phylogenetic relationships of the Gobioidei have been elucidated using molecular data. Gobies are primarily small species, often with large heads and tapered bodies, that live in marine water, but roughly 10% of these species inhabit fresh water. This order is composed chiefly of benthic or burrowing species; like many other benthic fishes, most gobioids do not have a gas bladder or any other means of controlling their buoyancy in water, so they must spend most of their time on or near the bottom. Traditionally most of the species called gobies have been classified in the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] [[Perciformes]] as the [[Order (taxonomy)|suborder]] Gobioidei but in the 5th Edition of Fishes of the World this suborder is elevated to an order Gobiiformes within the [[Clade|clade]] Percomorpha.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cichlid", "Ocean sunfish" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Gobioidei" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Golden eagle",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦅",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of eagle",
		tags = { "Birds", "Accipitriformes and Falconiformes" },
		aliases = { "Aquila chrysaetos" },
		summary = "The **golden eagle** (**Aquila chrysaetos**) is a [[Bird of prey|bird of prey]] living in the [[Northern Hemisphere]]. It is the most widely distributed species of [[Eagle|eagle]]. Like all eagles, it belongs to the family Accipitridae. They are one of the best-known [[Bird of prey|birds of prey]] in the Northern Hemisphere. These birds are dark brown, with lighter golden-brown plumage on their napes. Immature eagles of this species typically have white on the tail and often have white markings on the wings. Golden eagles use their agility and speed combined with powerful feet and large, sharp talons to hunt a variety of prey, mainly [[Hare|hares]], [[Rabbit|rabbits]], and [[Marmot|marmots]] and other ground squirrels.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Golden eagles maintain home ranges or territories that may be as large as 200 km². They build large nests in cliffs and other high places to which they may return for several breeding years. Most breeding activities take place in the spring; they are monogamous and may remain together for several years or possibly for life. Females lay up to four [[Bird egg|eggs]], and then incubate them for six weeks. Typically, one or two young survive to fledge in about three months. These juvenile golden eagles usually attain full independence in the fall, after which they wander widely until establishing a territory for themselves in four to five years. Once widespread across the Holarctic, it has disappeared from many areas that are heavily populated by humans.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Peregrine falcon", "Falcon" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Aquila chrysaetos" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Goldfish",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Freshwater fish common in aquariums",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Cypriniformes" },
		aliases = { "Carassius auratus" },
		summary = "The **goldfish** (**Carassius auratus**) is a [[Freshwater fish|freshwater fish]] in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Cyprinidae of the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] [[Cypriniformes]]. It is commonly kept as a pet in indoor [[Aquarium|aquariums]], and is one of the most popular aquarium fish. Goldfish released into the wild have become an [[Invasive species|invasive pest]] in parts of North America and Australia.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Native to [[China]], the goldfish is a relatively small member of the genus Carassius (which also includes the Prussian carp and the crucian carp). It was first [[Selective breeding|selectively bred]] for color in imperial China more than 1,000 years ago, where several distinct [[Breed|breeds]] were developed. Goldfish breeds vary greatly in size, body shape, fin configuration, and coloration. Various combinations of white, yellow, orange, red, brown, and black are known.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Salmon", "Salmonidae" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Carassius auratus" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Goose",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Common name for a group of waterfowl",
		tags = { "Birds", "Anseriformes" },
		summary = 'This group comprises the [[Genus|genera]] Anser (grey geese and white geese) and Branta (black geese). Some members of the Tadorninae subfamily (e.g., Egyptian goose, Orinoco goose) are commonly called geese, but are not considered "true geese" taxonomically. More distantly related members of the family Anatidae are [[Swan|swans]], most of which are larger than true geese, and [[Duck|ducks]], which are smaller.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Young birds before fledging are called **goslings**. The collective noun for a group of geese on the ground is a **gaggle**; when in flight, they are called a **skein**, a **team**, or a **wedge**; when flying close together, they are called a **plump**.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Mallard" },
	},
	{
		title = "Grasshopper",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐞",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Common name for a group of insects",
		tags = { "Insects and other hexapods", "Orthoptera" },
		aliases = { "Grasshoppers" },
		summary = "**Grasshoppers** are a group of [[Insect|insects]] belonging to the [[Order (taxonomy)|suborder]] Caelifera. They are amongst what are possibly the most ancient living groups of chewing herbivorous insects, dating back to the early Triassic, around 250 million years ago.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Grasshoppers are typically ground-dwelling insects with powerful hind legs which allow them to escape from threats by leaping vigorously. Their front legs are shorter and used for grasping food. As hemimetabolous insects, they do not undergo complete metamorphosis; they hatch from an egg into a [[Nymph (biology)|nymph]] or "hopper" which undergoes five moults, becoming more similar to the adult insect at each developmental stage. The grasshopper hears through the tympanal organ which can be found in the first segment of the abdomen attached to the thorax; while its sense of vision is in the compound eyes, a change in light intensity is perceived in the simple eyes (ocelli). At high population densities and under certain environmental conditions, some grasshopper species can change colour and behavior and form swarms. Under these circumstances, they are known as [[Locust|locusts]].',
			},
		},
		related = { "Moth", "Scale insect" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Acridomorpha" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Green iguana",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of reptile",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Reptiles" },
		aliases = { "Iguana iguana", "American iguana", "Common green iguana" },
		summary = "The **green iguana** (**Iguana iguana**), also known as the **American iguana** or the **common green iguana**, is a large, [[Arboreal locomotion|arboreal]], mostly herbivorous [[Species|species]] of [[Lizard|lizard]] of the [[Genus|genus]] Iguana. Usually, this animal is simply called the **iguana**. The green iguana ranges over a large geographic area; it is native from southern [[Brazil]] and [[Paraguay]] as far north as [[Mexico]]. It is highly invasive in many subtropical and tropical environments throughout the world.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A [[Herbivore|herbivore]], it has adapted significantly with regard to locomotion and osmoregulation as a result of its diet. It grows to 1.7 m in length from head to tail, although a few specimens have grown more than 2 m with bodyweights upward of 20 lb. Although this species is commonly kept as a [[Pet|pet]] because of its disposition and coloration, it can be challenging to care for properly. Its requirements for adequate space, lighting, and heat can be difficult for hobbyists to meet.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Turtle" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Iguana iguana" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Grouper",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		aliases = { "Groupers", "Epinephelidae" },
		summary = "**Groupers** are a diverse group of marine [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]] in the family **Epinephelidae**, in the order [[Perciformes]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Groupers were long considered a [[Subfamily|subfamily]] of the **seabasses** in [[Serranidae]], but are now treated as distinct. Not all members of this family are called "**groupers**". The common name "grouper" is usually given to fish in one of two large [[Genus|genera]]: Epinephelus and Mycteroperca. In addition, the species classified in the small genera Anyperidon, Chromileptes, Dermatolepis, Graciela, Saloptia, and Triso are also called "groupers". Fish in the genus Plectropomus are referred to as "coral groupers". These genera are all classified in the subfamily Epiphelinae. However, some of the hamlets (genus Alphestes), the hinds (genus Cephalopholis), the lyretails (genus Variola), and some other small genera (Gonioplectrus, Niphon, Paranthias) are also in this subfamily, and occasional species in other serranid genera have common names involving the word "grouper".',
			},
		},
		related = { "Cichlid", "Ocean sunfish" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Epinephelidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Grouse",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tribe of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Galliformes" },
		summary = "**Grouse** are a group of birds from the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] [[Galliformes]], in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Phasianidae. Grouse are presently assigned to the [[Tribe (taxonomy)|tribe]] **Tetraonini** (formerly the subfamily **Tetraoninae** or the family **Tetraonidae**), a classification supported by [[Mitochondrial DNA|mitochondrial DNA]] sequence studies, and applied by the AviList, International Ornithological Congress, the American Ornithologists' Union, and others.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The [[Turkey (bird)|turkeys]] are closely allied with grouse, but they have traditionally been excluded from Tetraonini, often placed in their own tribe, subfamily, or family; certain more modern treatments also exclude them. This is reflected in some more recent circumscriptions. Grouse inhabit [[Temperate climate|temperate]] and [[Subarctic|subarctic]] regions of the [[Northern Hemisphere]], from arctic [[Tundra|tundra]] to [[Pine|pine forests]] to moorland and [[Mountain|mountainsides]], from 83°N (the rock ptarmigan in northern [[Greenland]]) to 28°N (Attwater's prairie-chicken in [[Texas]]), or to 16°30'N when the turkeys (ocellated turkey in [[Belize]]) are included.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Common pheasant" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Tetraonini" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gruiformes",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Gruiformes" },
		summary = "The **Gruiformes** are an [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] containing a considerable number of living and extinct [[Bird|bird]] [[Family (taxonomy)|families]], with a widespread geographical diversity. Gruiform means 'crane form'.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Traditionally, a number of wading and terrestrial bird families that did not seem to belong to any other order were classified together as Gruiformes. These include 15 species of large [[Crane (bird)|cranes]], about 145 species of smaller crakes and rails, as well as a variety of families comprising one to three extant species, such as the Heliornithidae, the limpkin (Aramidae), or the Psophiidae. Other birds have been placed in this order more out of necessity to place them somewhere; this has caused the expanded Gruiformes to lack distinctive apomorphies. Recent studies indicate that these "odd Gruiformes" are if at all only loosely related to the cranes, rails, and relatives ("core Gruiformes").',
			},
		},
		related = { "Rail (bird)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Gruiformes" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Guanaco",
		category = "Animal",
