	{
		title = "Herring",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Forage fish, mostly belonging to the family Clupeidae",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Clupeiformes" },
		summary = "**Herring** are various species of [[Forage fish|forage fish]], belonging to the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] Clupeiformes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Herring often move in large [[Shoaling and schooling|schools]] around fishing banks and near the coast, found particularly in shallow, temperate waters of the North Pacific and North Atlantic Oceans, including the [[Baltic Sea]], as well as off the west coast of South America. Three species of Clupea (the [[Type genus|type genus]] of the herring family Clupeidae) are recognised, and comprise about 90% of all herrings captured in fisheries. The most abundant of these species is the Atlantic herring, which comprises over half of all herring capture. Fish called herring are also found in the [[Arabian Sea]], [[Indian Ocean]], and [[Bay of Bengal]]. Herring played an important role in the history of marine fisheries in Europe, and early in the 20th century, their study was fundamental to the development of fisheries science. These oily fish also have a long history as an important food fish, and are often salted, smoked, or pickled.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cypriniformes", "Piranha" },
	},
	{
		title = "Hexapoda",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Subphylum of arthropods",
		tags = { "Arthropods", "Insects and other hexapods" },
		aliases = { "Hexapods" },
		summary = "The [[Subphylum|subphylum]] **Hexapoda** or **hexapods** comprises the largest [[Clade|clade]] of [[Arthropod|arthropods]] and includes most of the extant arthropod species. It includes the [[Crown group|crown group]] class Insecta (true insects), as well as the much smaller class Entognatha, which includes three classes of wingless arthropods that were once considered insects: Collembola (springtails), Protura (coneheads) and Diplura (two-pronged bristletails). The insects and springtails are very abundant and are some of the most important [[Pollinator|pollinators]], basal consumers, [[Scavenger|scavengers]]/[[Detritivore|detritivores]] and micropredators in [[Terrestrial ecosystem|terrestrial]] environments.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Hexapods are named for their most distinctive feature: a three-part [[Body plan|body plan]] with a consolidated thorax and three pairs of legs. Most other arthropods have more than three pairs of legs. Most recent studies have recovered Hexapoda as a subgroup of Pancrustacea.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Earwig", "Mantis" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Hexapoda" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hide (skin)",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🖐️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Animal skin treated for human use",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Hide" },
		summary = 'A **hide** or **skin** is an animal [[Skin|skin]] treated for human use. The word "hide" is related to the Dutch word and the German word, which mean [[Skin|skin]]. The industry defines hides as "skins" of large animals e.g. cow, buffalo; while skins refer to "skins" of smaller animals: [[Goat|goat]], [[Sheep|sheep]], [[Deer|deer]], [[Pig|pig]], [[Fish|fish]], [[Crocodile|alligator]], [[Snake|snake]], etc. Common commercial hides include [[Leather|leather]] from [[Cattle|cattle]] and other [[Livestock|livestock]] animals, buckskin, [[Crocodile|alligator skin]] and snake skin. All are used for shoes, clothes, leather bags, belts, or other [[Fashion|fashion]] accessories. Leather is also used in [[Car|cars]], upholstery, interior decorating, horse tack and harnesses.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Skins are sometimes still gathered from [[Hunting|hunting]] and processed at a domestic or artisanal level but most leather making is now [[Industrialisation|industrialized]] and large-scale. Various [[Tannin|tannins]] are used for this purpose. Hides are also used as processed chews for dogs or other pets. The term "skin" is sometimes expanded to include furs, which are harvested from various species, including [[Felidae|cats]], [[Mustelidae|mustelids]], and [[Bear|bears]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hippopotamus",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦛",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of large semi-aquatic mammal",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		summary = 'It is one of only two [[Neontology|extant]] species in the family Hippopotamidae, the other being the pygmy hippopotamus (Choeropsis liberiensis or Hexaprotodon liberiensis). Its name comes from the [[Ancient Greek]] for "river horse".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"After [[Elephant|elephants]] and [[Rhinoceros|rhinoceroses]], the hippopotamus is the next largest land mammal. It is also the largest extant land artiodactyl. Despite their physical resemblance to [[Pig|pigs]] and other terrestrial even-toed ungulates, the closest living relatives of the hippopotamids are [[Cetacean|cetaceans]] ([[Blue whale|whales]], [[Dolphin|dolphins]], [[Porpoise|porpoises]], etc.), from which they diverged about 55 million years ago. Hippos are recognisable for their barrel-shaped torsos, wide-opening mouths with large canine tusks, nearly hairless bodies, short legs, and large size: adults average 1.5 tonnes for bulls (males) and 1.3 tonnes for cows (females). Hippos inhabit rivers, lakes, and [[Mangrove|mangrove]] swamps. Territorial bulls each preside over a stretch of water and a group of five to thirty cows and calves. [[Mating]] and birth both occur in the water. During the day, hippos remain cool by staying in water or mud, emerging at dusk to graze on grasses.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Hippopotamus amphibius" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Vulnerable" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hoatzin",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of bird in South America",
		tags = { "Birds", "Cuculiformes" },
		aliases = { "Opisthocomus hoazin" },
		summary = "The **hoatzin** (**Opisthocomus hoazin**) is a species of tropical [[Bird|bird]] found in swamps, riparian forests, and [[Mangrove|mangroves]] of the [[Amazon basin|Amazon]] and Orinoco Basins in South America. It is the only [[Neontology|extant]] species in the genus **Opisthocomus** which is the only extant genus in the family Opisthocomidae under the order Opisthocomiformes. Despite being the subject of intense debate by specialists, the [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomic]] position of this family is still far from clear.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The hoatzin is notable for its chicks having primitive claws on two of their wing digits. It is unique among birds in possessing a digestive system that significantly supports the [[Fermentation|fermentation]] and the effective breakdown of plant matter, a trait more commonly known from [[Herbivore|herbivorous]] [[Ungulate|ungulate]]-[[Ruminant|ruminant]] [[Mammal|mammals]] and some [[Primate|primates]]. This bird is also the national bird of [[Guyana]], where the local name for this bird is **Canje pheasant**.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Swift (bird)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Opisthocomus hoazin" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Holstein Friesian",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐄",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Breed of dairy cattle",
		tags = { "Cats", "Cattle" },
		summary = "The **Holstein Friesian** (HF) is an international breed or group of breeds of dairy cattle. It originated in Fryslân, stretching from the Dutch province of North Holland to the German state of [[Schleswig-Holstein]]. It is the dominant breed in industrial dairy farming worldwide, and is found in more than 160 countries. It is known by many names, among them **Holstein**, **Friesian** and **Black and White**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"With the growth of the [[New World]], a demand for milk developed in North America and South America, and dairy breeders in those regions at first imported their [[Livestock|livestock]] from the Netherlands. Today, the breed is used for milk in the north of Europe, and for meat in the south of Europe. After 1945, European cattle breeding and dairy products became increasingly confined to certain regions due to the development of national infrastructure. This change led to the need to designate some animals for dairy production and others for beef production; previously, milk and beef had been produced from dual-purpose animals. Today, more than 80% of dairy production takes place north of the line between [[Bordeaux]] and [[Venice]], and more than 60% of the cattle in Europe are found there as well.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Arabian horse", "Mule" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Country", "Netherlands, Germany" },
			{ "Weight", "680–770 kg" },
			{ "Height", "145–165 cm" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hominidae",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of primates",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Great apes" },
		summary = "The **Hominidae**, whose members are known as the **great apes**, are a taxonomic [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of [[Primate|primates]] that includes eight [[Neontology|extant]] species in four [[Genus|genera]]: [[Orangutan|Pongo]] (the Bornean, Sumatran and Tapanuli orangutan); [[Gorilla]] (the eastern and western gorilla); [[Pan (genus)|Pan]] (the [[Chimpanzee|chimpanzee]] and the [[Bonobo|bonobo]]); and [[Homo]], of which only [[Human|modern humans (Homo sapiens)]] remain.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Numerous revisions in classifying the great apes have caused the use of the term hominid to change over time. The original meaning of "hominid" referred only to humans (Homo) and their closest extinct relatives. However, by the 1990s humans and other apes were considered to be "hominids". The earlier restrictive meaning has now been largely assumed by the term [[Hominini|hominin]], which however additionally includes Pan. Hominins more closely related to humans than to Pan are called Hominina, while the current meaning of "hominid" includes all the great apes including humans. Usage still varies, however, and some scientists and laypersons still use "hominid" in the original restrictive sense; the scholarly literature generally shows the traditional usage until the turn of the 21st century.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Hominidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Homo",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of hominins",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Primates" },
		aliases = { "Modern humans" },
		summary = "**Homo** is a [[Genus|genus]] of great ape (family [[Hominidae]]) that emerged from the genus [[Australopithecus]] (subtribe Hominina), encompassing a single [[Neontology|extant]] [[Species|species]], [[Human|Homo sapiens]] (**modern humans**), along with a number of [[Extinction|extinct]] species (e.g. [[Homo erectus]] and Homo neanderthalensis) classified as either ancestral or [[Sister group|closely related]] to [[Human|modern humans]], collectively called **archaic humans**. Homo, together with the genus [[Paranthropus]], is probably most closely related to the species Australopithecus africanus within [[Australopithecus]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The closest living relatives of Homo are of the [[Hominini|hominin]] genus [[Pan (genus)|Pan]] (chimpanzees and bonobos), with the ancestors of Pan and Homo estimated to have diverged around 5.7–11 million years ago during the Late Miocene. The oldest member of the genus is [[Homo habilis]], with [[Fossil|fossil]] records of just over 2 million years ago. [[Homo erectus|H. erectus]] appeared about 2 million years ago and spread throughout [[Africa]] (debatably as another species called Homo ergaster) and [[Eurasia]] in several migrations. The species was adaptive and successful, and persisted for more than a million years before gradually diverging into new species around 500,000 years ago. [[Early modern human|Anatomically modern humans]] (early H. sapiens) emerged close to 300,000 to 200,000 years ago in [[Africa]], and H. neanderthalensis emerged around the same time in [[Europe]] and [[West Asia|Western Asia]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Homo" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Homo erectus",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Extinct species of archaic human",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Primates" },
		summary = "**Homo erectus** is an [[Extinction|extinct]] [[Species|species]] of [[Homo|archaic human]] from the [[Pleistocene]], spanning nearly 2 million years. It is the first human species to evolve a humanlike [[Body plan|body plan]] and gait, to leave Africa and colonize Asia and Europe, and to [[Control of fire by early humans|wield fire]]. Some populations of H. erectus were ancestors of later human species, including H. heidelbergensis—the last common ancestor of [[Human|modern humans]], [[Neanderthal|Neanderthals]], and [[Denisovan|Denisovans]]. As such a widely distributed species both geographically and temporally, H. erectus anatomy varies considerably. Subspecies are sometimes recognized: H. e. erectus, H. e. pekinensis, H. e. soloensis, H. e. ergaster, H. e. georgicus, and H. e. tautavelensis.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The species was first described by Eugène Dubois in 1893 as "Pithecanthropus erectus" using a skullcap, molar, and femur from [[Java]], Indonesia. Further discoveries around East Asia were used to contend that humanity evolved out of Asia. Based on historical race concepts, it was argued that local H. erectus populations evolved directly into local modern human populations (polycentrism) rather than all humanity sharing a single [[Early modern human|anatomically modern]] ancestor (monogenism). As the fossil record improved over the mid-to-late 20th century, the "Out of Africa" theory and monogenism became the consensus. The typical skull has a pronounced brow ridge, a protruding jaw, and large teeth. The bones are much thicker than in modern humans. East Asian populations normally have an even more robust skeleton and larger brain volume—averaging 1000 cc.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Homo erectus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Homo habilis",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Archaic human species from 2.4 to 1.65 mya",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Primates" },
		summary = "**Homo habilis** is an extinct [[Species|species]] of archaic human from the Early Pleistocene of East and South Africa about 2.4 million years ago to 1.65 million years ago ([[Million years ago|mya]]). It is among the oldest species of archaic humans. Suggestions for pushing back the age to 2.8 Mya were made in 2015 based on the discovery of a jawbone. Upon species description in 1964, H. habilis was highly contested, with many researchers recommending it be [[Synonym (taxonomy)|synonymised]] with Australopithecus africanus, the only other early [[Hominini|hominin]] known at the time, but H. habilis received more recognition as time went on and more relevant discoveries were made.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'By the 1980s, H. habilis was proposed to have been a human ancestor, directly evolving into [[Homo erectus]], which directly led to modern humans. This viewpoint is now debated. Several specimens with insecure species identification were assigned to H. habilis, leading to arguments for splitting, namely into "H. rudolfensis" and "H. gautengensis" of which only the former has received wide support. H. habilis brain size generally varied from 500 to 900 cm3. The body proportions of H. habilis are only known from two highly fragmentary skeletons, and is based largely on assuming a similar anatomy to the earlier australopithecines. Because of this, it has also been proposed H. habilis be moved to the [[Genus|genus]] [[Australopithecus]] as **Australopithecus habilis**. However, the interpretation of H. habilis as a small-statured human with inefficient long-distance travel capabilities has been challenged.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Rhesus macaque" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Homo habilis" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Honey badger",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of mammal",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Carnivora" },
		aliases = { "Mellivora capensis", "Ratel" },
		summary = "The **honey badger** (**Mellivora capensis**), also known as the **ratel** ( or ), is a [[Mammal|mammal]] widely distributed across [[Africa]], Southwest Asia, and the [[Indian subcontinent]]. It is the sole living representative of the Mellivorinae subfamily of the [[Mustelidae]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It has a fairly long body, with a distinctly thick-set and broad back, and remarkably loose skin, allowing the badger to turn and twist freely within it. The largest terrestrial mustelid in Africa, the honey badger measures 55 to 77 cm long and weighs up to 16 kg. It has two pairs of mammae, and an eversible anal pouch. The honey badger is a solitary animal that can be active at any time of day, depending on the location. It is primarily a [[Carnivore|carnivorous]] species and has few natural [[Predation|predators]] because of its thick skin, strength and ferocious defensive abilities. Adults maintain large home ranges, and display scent-marking behavior. The species has no fixed breeding period. After a [[Gestation|gestation]] of 50–70 days, a female will give birth to an average of one to two cubs that will remain under her care for 1–1 1/4 years.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Mellivora capensis" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Honeyeater",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Corvoidea" },
		aliases = { "Honeyeaters", "Meliphagidae" },
		summary = "The **honeyeaters** are a large and diverse [[Family (taxonomy)|family]], **Meliphagidae**, of small to medium-sized birds. The family includes the Australian chats, myzomelas, friarbirds, wattlebirds, miners and melidectes. They are most common in [[Australia]] and [[New Guinea]], but can also be found in [[New Zealand]], the Pacific islands as far east as [[Samoa]] and [[Tonga]], and the Indonesian islands to the north and west of New Guinea known as Wallacea. [[Bali]], on the other side of the Wallace Line, has a single species.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In total, there are 186 species in 55 [[Genus|genera]], roughly half of them native to Australia, many of the remainder occupying New Guinea. With their closest relatives, the Maluridae (Australian fairy-wrens), Pardalotidae (pardalotes), and Acanthizidae (thornbills, Australian warblers, scrubwrens, etc.), they comprise the superfamily Meliphagoidea and originated early in the evolutionary history of the oscine [[Passerine|passerine]] radiation. Although honeyeaters look and behave very much like other nectar-feeding passerines around the world (such as the [[Sunbird|sunbirds]] and flowerpeckers), they are unrelated, and the similarities are the consequence of [[Convergent evolution|convergent evolution]]. The extent of the evolutionary partnership between honeyeaters and Australasian flowering plants is unknown, but probably substantial.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Lyrebird" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Meliphagidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Honeyguide",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of near passerine birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Piciformes" },
		aliases = { "Honeyguides", "Indicatoridae" },
		summary = "**Honeyguides** ([[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Indicatoridae**) are a family of 16 species of birds in the order Piciformes. They are also known as **indicator birds**, or **honey birds**, although the latter term is also used more narrowly to refer to species of the genus Prodotiscus. They have an [[Old World]] tropical distribution, with the greatest number of species in [[Africa]] and two in [[Asia]]. These birds are best known for their interaction with humans. Honeyguides are noted and named from one or two species that will deliberately lead humans directly to bee colonies, so that they can feast on the grubs and beeswax that are left behind. Interaction between honeyguides and [[Honey badger|honey badgers]] has also been reported.",
		related = { "Galliformes", "Bird of prey" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Indicatoridae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hoopoe",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Monotypic family of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Coraciiformes" },
		aliases = { "Hoopoes" },
		summary = '**Hoopoes** are colourful [[Bird|birds]] found across [[Africa]], [[Asia]], and [[Europe]], notable for their distinctive "crown" of feathers which can be raised or lowered at will. Two living and one extinct species are recognized, though for many years both extant species were lumped as a single species—Upupa epops. In fact, some taxonomists still consider the species to be conspecific. On the other hand, some authorities recognize three living species, with the African hoopoe (U. africana) treated as separate from the Eurasian hoopoe (U. epops). The Eurasian hoopoe is common in its range and has a large population, so it is evaluated as [[Least-concern species|Least Concern]] on the [[IUCN Red List]] of Threatened Species.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"However, their numbers are declining in Western Europe. Conversely, the hoopoe has been increasing in numbers at the tip of the South Sinai, Sharm el-Sheikh. There are dozens of nesting pairs that remain resident all year round.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Galliformes", "Bird of prey" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Upupa" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hornbill",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Coraciiformes" },
		aliases = { "Hornbills", "Bucerotidae" },
		summary = "**Hornbills** are [[Bird|birds]] found in tropical and [[Subtropics|subtropical]] Africa, Asia and [[Melanesia]] of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Bucerotidae**. They are characterized by a long, down-curved bill which is frequently brightly coloured and sometimes has a horny casque on the upper mandible. Hornbills have a two-lobed [[Kidney|kidney]]. They are the only birds in which the first and second neck vertebrae (the atlas and axis respectively) are fused together; this probably provides a more stable platform for carrying the bill. The family is omnivorous, feeding on fruit and small animals. They are monogamous breeders nesting in natural cavities in trees and sometimes cliffs.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A number of mainly [[Island|insular]] species of hornbill with small ranges are [[Threatened species|threatened]] with [[Extinction|extinction]], mainly in Southeast Asia. In the [[Neotropical realm]], [[Toucan|toucans]] occupy the hornbills' [[Ecological niche|ecological niche]], an example of [[Convergent evolution|convergent evolution]]. Despite their close appearances, the two groups are not very closely related, with toucans being allied with the [[Woodpecker|woodpeckers]], [[Honeyguide|honeyguides]] and several families of barbet, while hornbills (and their close relatives, the ground hornbills) are allied with the [[Hoopoe|hoopoes]] and wood-hoopoes.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Galliformes", "Bird of prey" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Bucerotidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Horned lizard",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of reptiles",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Reptiles" },
		aliases = { "Phrynosoma", "Horned lizards", "Horny toads" },
		summary = "**Phrynosoma**, whose members are known as the **horned lizards**, **horny toads**, or **horntoads**, is a [[Genus|genus]] of North American [[Lizard|lizards]] and the [[Type genus|type genus]] of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Phrynosomatidae. Their [[Common name|common names]] refer directly to their horns or to their flattened, rounded bodies (squat bodied), and blunt snouts.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The [[Genus|generic name]] Phrynosoma means "toad-bodied". In common with true toads ([[Amphibian|amphibians]] of the family Bufonidae), horned lizards tend to move sluggishly, often remain motionless, and rely on their remarkable camouflage to avoid detection by predators. They are adapted to arid or semiarid areas. The spines on the lizard\'s back and sides are modified reptile scales, which prevent water loss through the skin, whereas the horns on the head are true horns (i.e., they have a bony core). A urinary bladder is absent. Of the 21 species of horned lizards, 15 are native to the US. The largest-bodied and most widely distributed of the American species is the Texas horned lizard.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Turtle" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Phrynosoma" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hornet",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of eusocial wasp",
		tags = { "Insects and other hexapods", "Hymenoptera" },
		aliases = { "Hornets", "Vespa" },
		summary = "**Hornets** are [[Wasp|wasps]] of the genus **Vespa** in the subfamily Vespinae (the vespine wasps). They are the largest of the [[Eusociality|eusocial]] wasps. Species like the European hornet can grow to 35 mm in length, while other species like the Asian giant hornet (Vespa mandarinia) can exceed 50 mm. They are similar in appearance to their close relatives, the yellowjackets, but are distinguished from other vespine wasps by the relatively large top margin of the head. Worldwide, 22 species of Vespa are recognized. Most species only occur in the tropics of Asia, though the European hornet (V. crabro) is widely distributed throughout Europe, Russia, North America, and north-eastern Asia.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Wasps of the genus Dolichovespula native to North America are commonly called hornets (e.g., bald-faced hornet), but are actually yellowjackets. Like other social wasps, hornets build communal nests by chewing wood to make a papery pulp. Each nest has one queen, which lays eggs and is attended by workers that, while genetically female, cannot lay fertile eggs. Most species make exposed nests in trees and shrubs, but some (such as Vespa orientalis) build their nests underground or in other cavities. In the tropics, these nests may last year-round, but in temperate areas, the nest dies over the winter, with lone queens hibernating in leaf litter or other insulative material until the spring. Male hornets are docile and do not have stingers.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Carpenter ant", "Western honey bee" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Vespa" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hound",
