	{
		title = "Lepidoptera",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐞",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of insects including moths and butterflies",
		tags = { "Insects and other hexapods", "Lepidoptera" },
		aliases = { "Lepidopterans" },
		summary = "**Lepidoptera** or **lepidopterans** are an [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] of winged insects which include [[Butterfly|butterflies]] and [[Moth|moths]]. About 180,000 [[Species|species]] of the Lepidoptera have been described, representing 8.6% of the total described species of living organisms, making it the second largest insect order (behind Coleoptera) with 126 [[Family (taxonomy)|families]] and 46 [[Taxonomic rank|superfamilies]], and one of the most widespread and widely recognizable insect orders in the world.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Lepidopterans have several distinctive features, especially coloured scales that cover their bodies, large triangular wings, and a [[Proboscis|proboscis]] for drinking [[Nectar|nectar]]. Almost all species have membranous wings. [[Mating]] and egg-laying are normally near or on host plants for the [[Larva|larvae]]. Butterflies and moths undergo complete metamorphosis. The larva is called a [[Caterpillar|caterpillar]]; it has a cylindrical body with a well-developed head, chewing mouthparts, three pairs of legs and up to five pairs of prolegs. As it grows, it changes in appearance, going through a series of stages called instars. Once mature, it develops into a [[Pupa|pupa]]. A few butterflies and many moth species spin a [[Silk|silk]] casing or cocoon for protection prior to pupating, while others just go underground. A butterfly pupa, called a chrysalis, has a hard skin, usually with no cocoon.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cricket (insect)", "Orthoptera", "Swallowtail butterfly" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Lepidoptera" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Leptodactylidae",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of amphibians",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Amphibians" },
		aliases = { "Southern frogs" },
		summary = "The **southern frogs** form the **Leptodactylidae**, a name that comes from Greek meaning a bird or other animal having slender toes. They are a diverse [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of [[Frog|frogs]] that most likely diverged from other hyloids during the [[Cretaceous]]. The family has undergone major taxonomic revisions in recent years, including the reclassification of the former subfamily Eleutherodactylinae into its own family the Eleutherodactylidae; the Leptodactylidae now number 206 species in 13 genera distributed throughout [[Mexico]], the [[Caribbean]], and [[Central America|Central]] and [[South America]]. The family includes terrestrial, burrowing, aquatic, and arboreal members, inhabiting a wide range of habitats.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Several of the genera within the Leptodactylidae lay their eggs in foam nests. These can be in crevices, on the surface of water, or on forest floors. These foam nests are some of the most varied among frogs. When eggs hatch in nests on the forest floor, the [[Tadpole|tadpoles]] remain within the nest, without eating, until [[Metamorphosis|metamorphosis]]. Fossils of Leptodactylidae have been found in Maastrichtian India.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Amphibian" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Leptodactylidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lizard",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🦎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Informal group of reptiles",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Reptiles" },
		summary = '**Lizard** is the [[Common name|common name]] used for all squamate [[Reptile|reptiles]] that are not [[Snake|snakes]] (and to a lesser extent amphisbaenians or "worm lizards"), encompassing over 7,000 [[Species|species]]. They have a [[Cosmopolitan distribution|cosmopolitan distribution]], ranging across all continents except [[Antarctica]], as well as most oceanic island chains. [[Neontology|Extant]] lizard species vary in size from the 13.5 mm nano-chameleon and the 14–18 mm Jaragua dwarf gecko to the 3-meter-long [[Komodo dragon]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Most lizards are quadrupedal with a side-to-side swaying gait, although some lizards are capable of running on hindlimbs. Some lineages (known as "legless lizards") have secondarily lost their [[Limb (anatomy)|limbs]] and developed a snake-like elongated body due to [[Convergent evolution|convergent evolution]]. Some lizards, such as the [[Arboreal locomotion|arboreal]] Draco, are capable of [[Gliding flight|gliding flight]] using fanned-out gular membranes and costal patagia as [[Wing|wings]]. They are often [[Territory (animal)|territorial]], the males fighting off other males and signalling, often with bright colours, to attract mates and to intimidate rivals. Lizards are mainly carnivorous, often being ambush predators; smaller lizard species eat [[Insect|insects]] and other [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]], while the larger monitor lizards can prey upon [[Tetrapod|tetrapods]] as big as [[Water buffalo|water buffalo]].',
			},
		},
		related = { "Boidae", "Eastern copperhead", "Horned lizard", "Red-eared slider" },
	},
	{
		title = "Lobster",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🦞",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of clawed marine crustaceans",
		tags = { "Arthropods", "Crustaceans" },
		aliases = { "Lobsters", "Nephropidae", "Homaridae" },
		summary = "**Lobsters** are [[Malacostraca|malacostracan]] decapod crustaceans of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Nephropidae** or its [[Synonym (taxonomy)|synonym]] **Homaridae**. They have long bodies with muscular tails and live in crevices or burrows on the sea floor. Three of their five pairs of legs have claws, including the first pair, which are usually much larger than the others. Highly prized as [[Seafood|seafood]], lobsters are economically important and are often one of the most profitable commodities in the coastal areas they populate.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Commercially important species include two species of Homarus from the northern Atlantic Ocean; the smaller Nephrops norvegicus, known as true scampi, Norway lobster, or langoustine; and multiple small species in the genus Metanephrops, commonly called Pacific scampi.",
			},
