	{
		title = "Mackerel",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Pelagic fish",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		summary = '**Mackerel** is a common name applied to a number of different species of pelagic fish, mostly from the family Scombridae. They are found in both temperate and tropical seas, typically in the open ocean but occasionally along the coast. Mackerel species typically have deeply forked tails and vertical "[[Tiger|tiger]]-like" stripes on their backs with an [[Iridescence|iridescent]] green-blue quality. Many are restricted in their distribution ranges and live in separate populations or fish stocks based on geography. Some stocks [[Fish migration|migrate]] in large [[Shoaling and schooling|schools]] along the coast to suitable spawning grounds, where they spawn in fairly shallow waters.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"After spawning they return the way they came in smaller schools to suitable feeding grounds, often near an area of upwelling. From there they may move offshore into deeper waters and spend the winter in relative inactivity. Other stocks migrate across oceans. Smaller mackerel are [[Forage fish|forage fish]] for larger predators, including larger mackerel and Atlantic cod. Flocks of seabirds, [[Blue whale|whales]], dolphins, sharks, and schools of larger fish such as [[Tuna|tuna]] and [[Marlin|marlin]] follow mackerel schools and attack them in sophisticated and cooperative ways. Mackerel flesh is high in omega-3 oils and is intensively harvested by humans. In 2009, over 5 million tons were landed by commercial fishermen. Sport fishermen value the fighting abilities of the king mackerel.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cichlid", "Ocean sunfish" },
	},
	{
		title = "Mahi-mahi",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of fish",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		aliases = { "Common dolphinfish", "Dolphinfish", "Dorado" },
		summary = "The **mahi-mahi**, **common dolphinfish**, **dolphinfish**, **dolphin** or ** dorado** (**Coryphaena hippurus**) is a surface-dwelling [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]] found in off-shore [[Temperate climate|temperate]], [[Tropics|tropical]], and [[Subtropics|subtropical]] waters worldwide. It is also widely called dorado (not to be confused with Salminus brasiliensis, a freshwater fish) and dolphin (not to be confused with the aquatic mammal [[Dolphin|dolphin]]). It is one of two members of the family Coryphaenidae, the other being the pompano dolphinfish. These fish are most commonly found in the waters around the [[Gulf of Mexico]], [[Costa Rica]], [[Hawaii]], and the [[Indian Ocean]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In Italy it is called corifena, lampuga or pesce capone, and has even given its name to the caponata though eggplant has now taken the place of the fish.",
