	{
		title = "Product (chemistry)",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species formed from chemical reactions",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Products" },
		summary = "**Products** are the chemical species formed from [[Chemical reaction|chemical reactions]]. During a chemical reaction, [[Reagent|reactants]] are transformed into products after passing through a high energy transition state. This process results in the consumption of the reactants. It can be a spontaneous reaction or mediated by [[Catalysis|catalysts]] which lower the energy of the transition state, and by [[Solvent|solvents]] which provide the chemical environment necessary for the reaction to take place. When represented in [[Chemical equation|chemical equations]], products are by convention drawn on the right-hand side, even in the case of reversible reactions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The properties of products such as their energies help determine several characteristics of a chemical reaction, such as whether the reaction is exergonic or endergonic. Additionally, the properties of a product can make it easier to extract and purify following a chemical reaction, especially if the product has a different [[State of matter|state of matter]] than the reactants. **Spontaneous reaction** Where R is reactant and P is product. **Catalysed reaction** Where R is reactant, P is product and C is catalyst. Much of chemistry research is focused on the [[Chemical synthesis|synthesis]] and characterization of beneficial products, as well as the detection and removal of undesirable products.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pronghorn",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of North American hoofed mammal",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		aliases = { "Antilocapra americana" },
		summary = "The **pronghorn** (**Antilocapra americana**) is a species of artiodactyl (even-toed, hoofed) [[Mammal|mammal]] indigenous to interior western and central North America. Though not an [[Antelope|antelope]], it is known colloquially in North America as the **American antelope**, **prong buck**, **pronghorn antelope**, and **prairie antelope**, because it closely resembles the antelopes of the [[Old World]] and fills a similar [[Ecological niche|ecological niche]] due to parallel evolution. It is the only surviving member of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Antilocapridae.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"During the [[Pleistocene]] epoch, about 11 other antilocaprid species existed in North America, many with long or spectacularly twisted horns. Three other genera (Capromeryx, Stockoceros and Tetrameryx) existed when humans entered North America, but are now [[Extinction|extinct]]. The pronghorn's closest living relatives are the [[Giraffe|giraffe]] and okapi. The antilocaprids are part of the infraorder Pecora, making them distant relatives of [[Deer|deer]], [[Bovidae|bovids]], and moschids. The pronghorn is the fastest land mammal in the Americas, with running speeds up to 55 mph. It is the symbol of the American Society of Mammalogists.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Antilocapra americana" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pseudoscorpion",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of arachnids",
		tags = { "Arthropods", "Arachnids" },
		aliases = { "Pseudoscorpions", "False scorpions", "Book scorpions" },
		summary = "**Pseudoscorpions**, also known as **false scorpions** or **book scorpions**, are small, scorpion-like [[Arachnid|arachnids]] belonging to the order **Pseudoscorpiones**, also known as **Pseudoscorpionida** or **Chelonethida**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Pseudoscorpions are generally beneficial to humans because they prey on clothes moth larvae, carpet beetle larvae, booklice, [[Ant|ants]], mites, and small flies. They are common in many environments, but they are rarely noticed due to their small size. When people see pseudoscorpions, especially indoors, they often mistake them for ticks or small [[Spider|spiders]]. Pseudoscorpions often carry out phoresis, a form of [[Commensalism|commensalism]] in which one organism uses another for the purpose of transport.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Jumping spider" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Pseudoscorpiones" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pteropus",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦇",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of large bats",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Bats" },
		summary = "**Pteropus** (suborder Yinpterochiroptera) is a [[Genus|genus]] of [[Megabat|megabats]] which are among the largest bats in the world. They are commonly known as **fruit bats** or **flying foxes**, among other colloquial names. They live in [[South Asia]], [[Southeast Asia]], [[Australia]], [[East Africa]], and some oceanic islands in the Indian and Pacific Oceans. There are at least 60 [[Neontology|extant]] species in the genus.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Flying foxes eat fruit and other plant matter, and occasionally consume insects as well. They locate resources with their keen sense of smell. Most, but not all, are [[Nocturnal animal|nocturnal]]. They navigate with keen eyesight, as they cannot [[Animal echolocation|echolocate]]. They have long life spans and low reproductive outputs, with females of most species producing only one offspring per year. Their slow life history makes their populations vulnerable to threats such as [[Overexploitation|overhunting]], culling, and natural disasters. Six flying fox species have been made [[Extinction|extinct]] in modern times by overhunting. Flying foxes are often persecuted for their real or perceived role in [[Pest (organism)|damaging crops]]. They are ecologically beneficial by assisting in the regeneration of forests via seed dispersal. They benefit ecosystems and human interests by [[Pollinator|pollinating]] plants.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Pteropus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pterosaur",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🦎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Extinct order of flying reptiles",
		tags = { "Reptiles", "Dinosaurs and prehistoric reptiles" },
		aliases = { "Pterosaurs", "Pterosauria" },
		summary = "**Pterosaurs** are an extinct [[Clade|clade]] of warm-blooded flying reptiles in the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] **Pterosauria**. They existed during most of the [[Mesozoic]]: from the [[Late Triassic]] to the end of the [[Cretaceous]] (228 million to 66 million years ago). Pterosaurs are the earliest [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]] known to have evolved powered flight. Their wings were formed by a membrane of skin, muscle, and other [[Tissue (biology)|tissues]] stretching from the ankles to a dramatically lengthened fourth finger.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Traditionally, pterosaurs were divided into two major types. Basal pterosaurs (also called non-pterodactyloid pterosaurs or 'rhamphorhynchoids') were smaller animals, with up to a 2 m wingspan, fully toothed jaws and, typically, long tails. Their wide wing membranes probably included and connected the hindlimbs. On the ground, they would have had an awkward sprawling posture due to short metacarpals, but the anatomy of their joints and strong claws would have made them effective climbers, and some may have lived in trees. Basal pterosaurs were [[Insectivore|insectivores]], piscivores or [[Predation|predators]] of small land vertebrates. Later pterosaurs (pterodactyloids) evolved many sizes, shapes, and lifestyles. Pterodactyloids had narrower wings with free hindlimbs, highly reduced tails, and long necks with large heads.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Sauropoda" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Pterosauria" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pterygota",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐞",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Subclass of insects",
		tags = { "Arthropods", "Insects and other hexapods" },
		summary = "**Pterygota** is a [[Class (taxonomy)|subclass]] of [[Insect|insects]] that includes all winged insects and groups which lost them secondarily.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Pterygota group comprises 99.9% of all insects. The orders not included are the Archaeognatha (jumping bristletails) and the Zygentoma (silverfishes and firebrats), two primitively wingless insect orders. Unlike Archaeognatha and Zygentoma, the pterygotes do not have styli or vesicles on their abdomen (also absent in some zygentomans), and with the exception of the majority of mayflies, are also missing the median terminal filament which is present in the ancestrally wingless insects. The oldest known representatives of the group appeared during the mid-Carboniferous, around 328–324 million years ago, and the group subsequently underwent rapid diversification. Claims that they originated substantially earlier during the [[Silurian]] or [[Devonian]] based on [[Molecular clock|molecular clock]] estimates are unlikely based on the fossil record, and are likely analytical artefacts.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Louse", "Phasmatodea" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Pterygota" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Rabbit",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐇",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mammals of the family Leporidae",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Lagomorphs" },
		aliases = { "Rabbits", "Bunnies" },
		summary = "**Rabbits**, or **bunnies**, are small [[Mammal|mammals]] in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Leporidae (which also includes the [[Hare|hares]]), which is in the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] [[Lagomorpha]] (which also includes pikas). They are familiar throughout the world as a small herbivore, a prey animal, a domesticated form of livestock, and a pet, having a widespread effect on [[Ecology|ecologies]] and cultures. The most widespread rabbit [[Genus|genera]] are Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus. The former, Oryctolagus, includes the European rabbit, Oryctolagus cuniculus, which is the ancestor of the hundreds of breeds of domestic rabbit and has been introduced on every continent except [[Antarctica]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The latter, Sylvilagus, includes over 13 wild rabbit species, among them the cottontails and tapetis. Wild rabbits not included in Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus include several species of limited [[Species distribution|distribution]], including the pygmy rabbit, volcano rabbit, and Sumatran striped rabbit. Rabbits are a [[Paraphyly|paraphyletic]] grouping, and do not constitute a [[Clade|clade]], as hares (belonging to the genus Lepus) are nested within the Leporidae clade and are not described as rabbits. Although once considered [[Rodent|rodents]], lagomorphs diverged earlier and have a number of traits rodents lack, including two extra incisors. Similarities between rabbits and rodents were once attributed to [[Convergent evolution|convergent evolution]], but studies in [[Molecular biology|molecular biology]] have found a [[Common descent|common ancestor]] between lagomorphs and rodents and place them in the clade Glires.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Rail (bird)",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Gruiformes" },
		aliases = { "Rails", "Rallidae" },
		summary = "**Rails** (avian family **Rallidae**) are a large, [[Cosmopolitan distribution|cosmopolitan]] [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of small- to medium-sized terrestrial or semi-amphibious [[Bird|birds]]. The family exhibits considerable diversity in its forms, and includes such ubiquitous species as the crakes, coots, and gallinules; other rail species are extremely rare or endangered. Many are associated with [[Wetland|wetland]] habitats, some being semi-aquatic like waterfowl (such as the coot), but many more are wading birds or shorebirds. The ideal rail habitats are [[Marsh|marsh]] areas, including rice paddies, and flooded fields or open forest. They are especially fond of dense vegetation for nesting.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The rail family is found in every [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial]] [[Habitat|habitat]] with the exception of dry [[Desert|desert]], polar or freezing regions, and [[Alpine climate|alpine]] areas (above the snow line). Members of Rallidae occur on every continent except [[Antarctica]]. Numerous unique island species are known. The rails have suffered disproportionally from human changes to the environment, and an estimated several hundred species of island rails have become extinct because of this. Several island species of rails remain [[Endangered species|endangered]], and conservation organisations and governments continue to work to prevent their extinction.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Crane (bird)", "Bustard" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Rallidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ranidae",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of frogs",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Amphibians" },
		aliases = { "True frogs" },
		summary = '**Ranidae** is a [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of [[Frog|frogs]], sometimes called "**true frogs**". They have the widest distribution of any frog family. They are abundant throughout most of the world, occurring on all [[Continent|continents]] except Antarctica. The true frogs are present in North America, northern South America, Europe, Africa (including [[Madagascar]]), and Asia. The Asian range extends across the [[East Indies]] to [[New Guinea]] and a single species, the Australian wood frog (Papurana daemelii), has spread into the far north of Australia.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Typically, true frogs are smooth and moist-skinned, with large, powerful legs and extensively webbed feet. The true frogs vary greatly in size, ranging from small—such as the wood frog (Lithobates sylvatica)—to large. Many of the true frogs are aquatic or live close to water. Most species lay their eggs in the water and go through a tadpole stage. However, as in most families of frogs, there is large variation of [[Habitat|habitat]] within the family. There are also arboreal species of true frogs, and the family includes some of the very few [[Amphibian|amphibians]] that can live in [[Brackish water|brackish water]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Ranidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Rat",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐀",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Several genera of rodents",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Rodents" },
		aliases = { "Rats" },
		summary = '**Rats** are various medium-sized, long-tailed [[Rodent|rodents]]. [[Species]] of rats are found throughout the order Rodentia, with the genus Rattus containing the most familiar rats. Other rat genera include Neotoma (pack rats), Bandicota (bandicoot rats) and Dipodomys (kangaroo rats). Among the most well-known rats are the black rat and [[Brown rat|brown rat]], both of which are native to [[Asia]]. Rats belong to the same subfamily as [[Mouse|mice]] and are often distinguished based on their size. Usually the [[Common name|common name]] of a large muroid rodent will include the word "rat", while a smaller muroid\'s name will include "mouse". The common terms rat and mouse are not [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomically]] specific.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Like mice, they are known to invade properties for food, water, warmth, and shelter. There are 56 known species of rats in the world. Rats are found in every region inhabited by humans, excluding Antarctica. The most common species [[Commensalism|live alongside]] humans, but many diverse rat species are unique to island locations and are threatened by [[Habitat destruction|habitat loss]] or [[Competition (biology)|competition]] with other rodent species.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Muridae" },
	},
	{
		title = "Rattlesnake",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Reptiles" },
		summary = "Rattlesnakes are [[Predation|predators]] that live in a wide array of habitats, hunting small animals such as birds and rodents.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Rattlesnakes receive their name from the rattle located at the end of their tails, which makes a loud rattling noise when vibrated that deters predators. The 36 known species of rattlesnakes have between 65 and 70 subspecies, all native to the [[Americas]], ranging from central Argentina to southern Canada. The largest rattlesnake, the eastern diamondback, can measure up to 2.4 m in length. Rattlesnakes are preyed upon by hawks, [[Weasel|weasels]], kingsnakes, and a variety of other species. Rattlesnakes are heavily preyed upon as neonates, while they are still weak and immature. Rattlesnake populations in many areas are severely [[Threatened species|threatened]] by [[Habitat destruction|habitat destruction]], [[Poaching|poaching]], and extermination campaigns.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Lizard", "Turtle" },
	},
	{
		title = "Red deer",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of hoofed mammal",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		aliases = { "Cervus elaphus" },
		summary = "The **red deer** (**Cervus elaphus**) is a species of [[Deer|deer]] in the genus Cervus. It is one of the largest deer species. A male red deer is called a **stag** or **hart**, and a female is called a **hind**. The red deer inhabits most of [[Europe]], the [[Caucasus Mountains]] region, [[Anatolia]], [[Iran]], and parts of [[West Asia|Western Asia]]. It also inhabits the [[Atlas Mountains]] of Northern Africa, being the only living species of deer to inhabit Africa. Red deer have been introduced to other areas, including [[Australia]], [[New Zealand]], the [[United States]], [[Canada]], [[Peru]], [[Uruguay]], [[Chile]] and [[Argentina]]. In many parts of the world, the meat (venison) from red deer is used as a food source.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The red deer is a [[Ruminant|ruminant]], characterized by a four-chambered stomach. [[Genetics|Genetic]] evidence indicates that the red deer, as traditionally defined, is a species group, rather than a single species, though exactly how many species the group includes remains disputed. The ancestor of the red deer probably originated in central Asia. Although at one time red deer were rare in parts of Europe, they were never close to extinction. Reintroduction and conservation efforts, such as in the United Kingdom and Portugal, have resulted in an increase of red deer populations, while other areas, such as North Africa, have continued to show a population decline.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Cervus elaphus" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Red-eared slider",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Subspecies of turtle",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Reptiles" },
		aliases = { "Red-eared terrapin", "Trachemys scripta elegans" },
		summary = "The **red-eared slider** or **red-eared terrapin** (**Trachemys scripta elegans**) is a subspecies of the pond slider (Trachemys scripta), a terrapin belonging to the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Emydidae. Native to the southern United States and extreme northern Mexico, it is popular as a pet across the world, and is the most [[Invasive species|invasive]] chelonian. It is the most commonly traded chelonian in the world.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The red-eared slider is native to the central and southern United States and northern [[Mexico]], but has become established in other places because of pet releases, and has become invasive in many areas where it outcompetes native species. The red-eared slider is included in the list of the world's 100 most invasive species.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Lizard", "Turtle" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Trachemys scripta" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Red-winged blackbird",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of bird in North and Central America",
		tags = { "Birds", "Passerida" },
		aliases = { "Agelaius phoeniceus" },
		summary = "The **red-winged blackbird** (**Agelaius phoeniceus**) is a [[Passerine|passerine]] bird of the family [[Icterid|Icteridae]] found in most of [[North America]] and much of [[Central America]]. It breeds from [[Alaska]] and Newfoundland south to Florida, the [[Gulf of Mexico]], [[Mexico]], and [[Guatemala]], with isolated populations in western [[El Salvador]], northwestern [[Honduras]], and northwestern [[Costa Rica]]. It may winter as far north as [[Pennsylvania]] and [[British Columbia]], but northern populations are generally [[Bird migration|migratory]], moving south to Mexico and the [[Southern United States]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Claims have been made that it is the most abundant living land bird in North America, as bird-counting censuses of wintering red-winged blackbirds sometimes show that loose flocks can number in excess of a million birds per flock and the full number of breeding pairs across North and Central America may exceed 250 million in peak years. It also ranks among the best-studied wild bird species in the world. Seeds and insects make up the bulk of the red-winged blackbird's diet.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Sunbird", "Finch" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Agelaius phoeniceus" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Reindeer",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of deer",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		aliases = { "Caribou", "Rangifer tarandus" },
		summary = 'The **reindeer** or **caribou** (**Rangifer tarandus**) is a species of [[Deer|deer]] with circumpolar distribution, native to [[Arctic]], [[Subarctic|subarctic]], [[Tundra|tundra]], [[Taiga|boreal]], and mountainous regions of Northern Europe, Siberia, and North America. It is the only representative of the genus **Rangifer**. More recent studies suggest the splitting of reindeer and caribou (North American terminology). "All caribou and reindeer throughout the world are considered to be the same species, but there are 7 subspecies."',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Reindeer occur in both [[Animal migration|migratory]] and sedentary populations, and their herd sizes vary greatly in different regions. The tundra subspecies are adapted for extreme cold, and some are adapted for long-distance migration. Reindeer vary greatly in size and color from the smallest, the Svalbard reindeer (R. (t.) platyrhynchus), to the largest, Osborn's caribou (R. t. osborni). Although reindeer are quite numerous, some species and subspecies are in decline and considered [[Vulnerable species|vulnerable]]. They are unique among deer (Cervidae) in that females may have antlers, although the prevalence of antlered females varies by subspecies. Reindeer are the only successfully semi-domesticated deer on a large scale in the world. Both wild and domestic reindeer have been an important source of food, clothing, and shelter for [[Circumpolar peoples|Arctic people]] from prehistorical times. They are still herded and hunted today.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Rangifer tarandus" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Vulnerable" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Remora",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family (Echeneidae) of ray-finned fish",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		aliases = { "Suckerfish", "Sharksucker", "Echeneidae" },
		summary = "The **remora**, sometimes called **suckerfish** or **sharksucker**, is any of a family (**Echeneidae**) of [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]] in the order Carangiformes. Depending on species, they grow to 30–110 cm long. Their distinctive first dorsal fins take the form of a modified oval, sucker-like organ with slat-like structures that open and close to create suction and take a firm hold against the skin of larger marine animals. The disk is made up of stout, flexible membranes that can be raised and lowered to generate suction. By sliding backward, the remora can increase the suction, or it can release itself by swimming forward. Remoras sometimes attach to small boats, and have been observed attaching to divers as well.",
		sections = { { "Overview", "They swim well on their own, with a sinuous, or curved, motion." } },
		related = { "European seabass", "Marlin" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Echeneidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Resplendent quetzal",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of bird",
		tags = { "Birds", "Trogoniformes" },
		aliases = { "Pharomachrus mocinno" },
		summary = "The **resplendent quetzal** (**Pharomachrus mocinno**) is a small bird found in [[Central America]] and southern [[Mexico]] that lives in [[Tropical forest|tropical forests]], particularly montane cloud forests. They are part of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Trogonidae and have two recognized [[Subspecies|subspecies]], P. m. mocinno and P. m. costaricensis. Like other quetzals, the resplendent is mostly [[Omnivore|omnivorous]]; its diet mainly consists of fruits of plants in the laurel family, Lauraceae, but it occasionally also preys on [[Insect|insects]], lizards, frogs and snails.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The species is well known for its colorful and complex plumage that differs substantially between sexes. These birds hollow holes in decaying trees or use ones already made by [[Woodpecker|woodpeckers]] as a nest site. They are known to take turns while incubating, males throughout the day and females at night. The female usually lays one to three eggs, which hatch in 17 to 19 days. The quetzal is an altitudinal migrant, migrating from the slopes to the canopy of the forest. This occurs during the breeding season, which varies depending on the location, but usually commences in March and extends as far as August. The resplendent quetzal is considered near threatened on the [[IUCN Red List]], with [[Habitat destruction|habitat destruction]] being the main threat. It has an important role in [[Mesoamerica|Mesoamerican mythology]], and is closely associated with [[Quetzalcōātl|Quetzalcoatl]], a deity.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Galliformes", "Sunbird" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Pharomachrus mocinno" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Near Threatened" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Rhea (bird)",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Ratites" },
		aliases = { "Rhea", "Ñandú" },
		summary = "The **rhea**, also known as the **ñandú** is a [[South America|South American]] [[Ratite|ratite]] ([[Flightless bird|flightless bird]] without a keel on the sternum bone) of the order Rheiformes. Rheas are distantly related to the two African ostriches and Australia's [[Emu|emu]] (the largest, second-largest and third-largest living ratites, respectively), with rheas placing just behind the emu in height and overall size.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most taxonomic authorities recognize two [[Neontology|extant]] species: the greater, or American, rhea (Rhea americana); and the lesser, or Darwin's, rhea (Rhea pennata). The [[International Union for Conservation of Nature]] (IUCN) classifies the puna rhea as another species instead of a subspecies of the lesser rhea. The IUCN currently rates the greater and puna rheas as near-threatened in their native ranges, while Darwin's rhea is of least concern, having recovered from past threats to its survival. In addition, the feral population of the greater rhea in Germany appears to be growing. However, control efforts are underway and seem to succeed in controlling the birds' population growth. Similarly to ostriches and emus, rheas are fairly popular livestock and pets, regularly kept and bred on farms, ranches, private parks, and by aviculturists, mainly in North and South America and Europe.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Swift (bird)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Rhea" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Rhesus macaque",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of Old World monkey",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Primates" },
		aliases = { "Macaca mulatta", "Rhesus monkey" },
		summary = "The **rhesus macaque** (**Macaca mulatta**), colloquially **rhesus monkey**, is a species of [[Old World monkey]] in the Macaca genus. There are between six and nine recognised subspecies split between two groups, the Chinese-derived and the Indian-derived. Generally brown or grey in colour, it is 47–53 cm in length with a 20.7–22.9 cm tail and weighs 5.3–7.7 kg. It is native to [[South Asia|South]], [[Central Asia|Central]], and [[Southeast Asia]] and has the widest geographic range of all non-human [[Primate|primates]], occupying a great diversity of altitudes and habitats.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The rhesus macaque is [[Diurnality|diurnal]], arboreal, and terrestrial. It is mostly [[Herbivore|herbivorous]], feeding mainly on [[Fruit|fruit]], but also eating [[Seed|seeds]], [[Root|roots]], buds, [[Bark (botany)|bark]], and [[Cereal|cereals]]. Rhesus macaques living in cities also eat human food and trash. They are gregarious, with troops comprising 20–200 individuals. The social groups are matrilineal. Individuals communicate with a variety of facial expressions, vocalisations, body postures, and gestures. As a result of the rhesus macaque's relatively easy upkeep, wide availability, and closeness to humans anatomically and physiologically, it has been used extensively in medical and biological research. It has facilitated many scientific breakthroughs including vaccines for rabies, smallpox, polio, and antiretroviral medication to treat HIV/AIDS. A rhesus macaque became the first primate astronaut in 1948. The rhesus is listed as [[Least-concern species|Least Concern]] in the [[IUCN Red List]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Homo", "Ape" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Macaca mulatta" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Rhinoceros",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🦏",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of mammals",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Perissodactyla" },
		aliases = { "Rhino", "Rhinocerotidae" },
		summary = "A **rhinoceros**, commonly abbreviated to **rhino**, is a member of any of the five [[Neontology|extant]] species (or numerous extinct species) of odd-toed ungulates (perissodactyls) in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Rhinocerotidae**. It is the only living family in the superfamily Rhinocerotoidea (whose members are also sometimes called rhinoceroses; the term **true rhinoceroses** is sometimes used for Rhinocerotidae).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Two of the extant species, the black rhinoceros (Diceros bicornis) and the white rhinoceros (Ceratotherium simum) are native to [[Africa]], while the other three, the Javan rhinoceros (Rhinoceros sondaicus), Indian rhinoceros (Rhinoceros unicornis) and Sumatran rhinoceros (Dicerorhinus sumatrensis) are native to [[South Asia|South]] and [[Southeast Asia]]. Rhinoceroses are some of the largest remaining [[Megafauna|megafauna]]: all weigh over half a [[Tonne|tonne]] in adulthood. They have one or two horns, a thick 1.5–5 cm, protective skin formed from layers of collagen positioned in a [[Crystal structure|lattice]] structure, and relatively small brains 400–600 g for mammals of their size. They have a [[Herbivore|herbivorous]] diet, generally eating leafy material, although their ability to ferment food in their hindgut allows them to subsist on more fibrous plant matter when necessary.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Rhinocerotidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Rock dove",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🕊️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ancestor of domestic pigeon",
		tags = { "Birds", "Columbiformes" },
		aliases = { "Columba livia", "Common pigeon", "Rock pigeon" },
		summary = 'The **rock dove** (**Columba livia**), also known as the **common pigeon** or **rock pigeon** (but see also Petrophassa), is a member of the bird family [[Columbidae]] (doves and pigeons). In common usage, it is often simply referred to as the "pigeon", although the rock dove is the [[Wildlife|wild]] form of the bird; the pigeons familiar to most people are the [[Domestication|domesticated]] forms of the wild rock dove.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The domestic pigeon, often called "Columba livia domestica", which includes about 1,000 different breeds, is descended from this species. Escaped domestic pigeons are the origin of feral pigeons around the world. Both forms can vary widely in the colour and pattern of their [[Plumage|plumage]] unlike their wild ancestor, being red, brown, checkered, uniformly coloured, or pied. [[Habitat|Habitats]] include various open and semi-open environments where they are able to [[Forage|forage]] on the ground. [[Cliff|Cliffs]] and rock ledges are used for roosting and [[Reproduction|breeding]] in the wild. Originating in [[Southern Europe]], [[North Africa]], and [[West Asia|Western Asia]], when including their domestic and feral descendants, they are an example of a [[Least-concern species|least-concern species]] per [[International Union for Conservation of Nature|IUCN]] among birds, being abundant with an estimated [[Population (biology)|population]] of 17 to 28 million wild and feral birds in Europe alone and up to 120 million worldwide.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Hoopoe" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Columba livia" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Rodent",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of mammals",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Rodents" },
		aliases = { "Rodents", "Rodentia" },
		summary = "**Rodents** are a group of [[Mammal|mammals]] belonging to the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] **Rodentia** ( or ) characterized by a single pair of continuously growing incisors in each of the upper and lower jaws. Rodents make up about 40% of all mammal species. Rodents are native to all major landmasses except Antarctica and a handful of isolated oceanic islands — though humans have since [[Introduced species|introduced]] them to nearly all of these places too. Most rodents are small animals with robust bodies, short limbs, and long tails. They use their sharp incisors to gnaw food, excavate burrows, and defend themselves. Most [[Herbivore|eat plant material]], but some have more varied diets.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Rodents are extremely diverse in their ecology and lifestyles and can be found in almost every terrestrial habitat, including human-made environments. Species can be [[Arboreal locomotion|arboreal]], fossorial (burrowing), saltatorial/ricochetal (leaping on their hind legs), or [[Semiaquatic|semiaquatic]]. They tend to be social animals, and many species live in societies with complex ways of communicating with each other. Mating among rodents can vary from monogamy, to polygyny, to promiscuity. Many have litters of underdeveloped, altricial young, while others are precocial (relatively well-developed) at birth. The rodent [[Fossil|fossil]] record dates back to the [[Paleocene]] of Asia. Rodents greatly diversified in the [[Eocene]], as they spread across continents, sometimes even crossing oceans.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Rodentia" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ruminant",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Hoofed herbivorous grazing or browsing mammals",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		aliases = { "Ruminants", "Ruminantia" },
		summary = '**Ruminants** are herbivorous grazing or browsing artiodactyls belonging to the [[Order (taxonomy)|suborder]] **Ruminantia** that are able to acquire nutrients from plant-based food by fermenting it in a specialized stomach prior to digestion, principally through microbial actions. The process, which takes place in the front part of the digestive system and therefore is called foregut fermentation, typically requires the fermented ingesta (known as cud) to be regurgitated and chewed again. The process of rechewing the ingesta to further break down plant matter and stimulate digestion is called **rumination** or **chewing the cud**. The word ruminant comes from the Latin rūminārī, "to ruminate", from rūmen, the first stomach.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The roughly 200 species of ruminants include both domestic and wild species. Ruminating mammals include [[Cattle|cattle]], all domesticated and wild bovines, [[Goat|goats]], [[Sheep|sheep]], [[Giraffe|giraffes]], [[Deer|deer]], [[Gazelle|gazelles]], and [[Antelope|antelopes]]. It has also been suggested that notoungulates also relied on rumination, as opposed to other atlantogenatans that rely on the more typical hindgut fermentation, though this is not entirely certain. Ruminants represent the most diverse group of living [[Ungulate|ungulates]]. The suborder Ruminantia includes six different families: Tragulidae, Giraffidae, Antilocapridae, Cervidae, Moschidae, and [[Bovidae]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Ruminantia" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Salamander",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐸",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of amphibians",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Amphibians" },
		aliases = { "Salamanders" },
		summary = '**Salamanders** are a group of [[Amphibian|amphibians]] typically characterized by their [[Lizard|lizard]]-like appearance, with slender bodies, blunt snouts, short limbs projecting at right angles to the body, and the presence of a tail in both larvae and adults. All ten extant salamander families are grouped together under the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] **Urodela**, the sole surviving order from the group Caudata. Urodela is a scientific Latin term based on the Ancient Greek: ourà dēlē "conspicuous tail". Caudata is the Latin for "tailed ones", from: "tail".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Salamander diversity is highest in eastern [[North America]], especially in the [[Appalachian Mountains]]; most [[Species|species]] are found in the Holarctic realm, with some species present in the [[Neotropical realm]]. Salamanders never have more than four toes on their front legs and five on their rear legs, but some species have fewer digits and others lack hind limbs. Their permeable skin usually makes them reliant on habitats in or near water or other cool, damp places. Some salamander species are fully aquatic throughout their lives, some take to the water intermittently, and others are entirely terrestrial as adults. This group of amphibians is capable of [[Regeneration (biology)|regenerating]] lost limbs as well as other damaged parts of their bodies.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Hylidae", "True toad" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Urodela" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Salmon",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Commercially important migratory fish",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Salmoniformes" },
		summary = "**Salmon** are any of several commercially important [[Species|species]] of euryhaline [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]] from the [[Genus|genera]] Salmo and Oncorhynchus of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Salmonidae]], native to [[Tributary|tributaries]] of the North Atlantic (Salmo) and North Pacific (Oncorhynchus) basins. Salmon is a colloquial or [[Common name|common name]] used for fish in this group, but is not a scientific name. Other closely related fish in the same family include [[Trout|trout]], char, grayling, whitefish, lenok and taimen, all coldwater fish of the [[Subarctic|subarctic]] and cooler [[Temperate climate|temperate]] regions with some sporadic [[Endorheic basin|endorheic]] populations in [[Central Asia]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Salmon are typically [[Fish migration|anadromous]]: they hatch in the shallow [[Gravel|gravel]] beds of [[Fresh water|freshwater]] headstreams and spend their juvenile years in [[River|rivers]], [[Lake|lakes]] and freshwater [[Wetland|wetlands]], migrate to the [[Ocean|ocean]] as adults and live like sea fish, then return to their freshwater birthplace to reproduce. However, populations of several species are restricted to fresh waters (i.e. landlocked) throughout their lives. [[Folklore]] has it that the fish return to the exact [[Stream|stream]] where they themselves hatched to spawn, and tracking studies have shown this to be mostly true. A portion of a returning salmon run may stray and spawn in different freshwater systems; the percent of straying depends on the species of salmon. Homing behavior has been shown to depend on olfactory memory.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Bichir", "Loricariidae" },
	},
	{
		title = "Salmonidae",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of ray-finned fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Salmoniformes" },
		aliases = { "Salmonoidei", "Salmonids", "Salmonoids" },
		summary = '**Salmonidae** is a [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]], the only extant member of the [[Order (taxonomy)|suborder]] **Salmonoidei**, consisting of 11 extant [[Genus|genera]] and over 200 [[Species|species]] collectively known as "**salmonids**" or "**salmonoids**". The family includes [[Salmon|salmon]] (both Atlantic and Pacific species), [[Trout|trout]] (both ocean-going and landlocked), char, graylings, freshwater whitefishes, taimens and lenoks, all coldwater mid-level predatory fish that inhabit the [[Subarctic|subarctic]] and cool [[Temperate climate|temperate]] waters of the [[Northern Hemisphere]]. The Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar), whose [[Latin]] name became that of its [[Genus|genus]] Salmo, is also the eponym of the family and order names.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Salmonids have a relatively primitive appearance among [[Teleost|teleost]] fish, with the pelvic fins being placed far back, and an adipose fin towards the rear of the back. They have slender bodies with rounded scales and forked tail fins, and their mouths contain a single row of sharp teeth. Although the smallest salmonid species is just 13 cm long for adults, most salmonids are much larger, with the largest reaching 2 m. All salmonids are migratory fish that spawn in the shallow [[Gravel|gravel]] beds of [[Fresh water|freshwater]] headstreams, spend the growing juvenile years in [[River|rivers]], [[Stream|creeks]], small [[Lake|lakes]] and [[Wetland|wetlands]], but migrate downstream upon maturity and spend most of their adult lives at much larger waterbodies. Many salmonid species are euryhaline and migrate to the [[Ocean|sea]] or [[Brackish water|brackish]] estuaries as soon as they approach adulthood, returning to the upper streams only to reproduce.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Goldfish" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Salmonidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sandpiper",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Charadriiformes" },
		aliases = { "Scolopacidae", "Sandpipers" },
		summary = "**Scolopacidae** is a large family of shorebirds, or waders, which mainly includes many species known as **sandpipers**, but also others such as woodcocks, curlews, and snipes. Most of these species eat small [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]] picked out of the mud or soil. There is no evidence that different lengths of [[Beak|bills]] enable multiple species to feed in the same [[Habitat|habitat]], particularly on the [[Coast|coast]], without direct competition for food. On sandy beaches, species with different bill lengths feed on the only abundant invertebrate, such as mole crabs Emerita.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Sandpipers have long bodies and legs, and narrow wings. Most species have a narrow bill, but the form and length are variable. They are small to medium-sized birds, measuring 12 to 66 cm in length. The bills are sensitive, allowing the birds to feel the mud and [[Sand|sand]] as they probe for food. They generally have dull [[Plumage|plumage]], with cryptic brown, grey, or streaked patterns, although some display brighter colours during the breeding season. Most species nest in open areas and defend their territories with aerial displays. The nest itself is a simple scrape in the ground, in which the bird typically lays three or four [[Bird egg|eggs]]. The young of most species are precocial.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Tern", "Gull" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Scolopacidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sarcopterygii",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Clade of lobe-finned fish",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Fishes" },
		summary = "**Sarcopterygii** is a [[Clade|clade]] (traditionally a [[Class (taxonomy)|class]] or subclass) of [[Vertebrate|vertebrate]] [[Animal|animals]] which includes a group of bony fishes and all four legged land animals, including humans. The common name for this clade is **lobe-finned fish**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Lobe-finned fish have muscular limb buds (lobes) within their [[Fish fin|fins]], which are supported by appendicular skeletons. This is in contrast to the other clade of bony fishes, the [[Actinopterygii]], which have only [[Skin|skin]]-covered bony spines supporting the fins. Sarcopterygii emerged in the [[Silurian]] and early [[Devonian]] periods consisting entirely of aquatic species, but today the vast majority of its species are land animals called [[Tetrapod|tetrapods]]. This mostly [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial]] clade of vertebrates are now recognized as having evolved from sarcopterygian ancestors and are most closely related to [[Lungfish|lungfishes]]. The lobed fins evolved into [[Limb (anatomy)|limbs]], and foregut diverticulum eventually evolved into air-breathing [[Lung|lungs]]. These changes made it possible for tetrapods to explore and eventually permeate terrestrial ecosystems.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Coral reef fish" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Sarcopterygii" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sardine",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Common name for various small, oily forage fish",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Clupeiformes" },
		aliases = { "Pilchard" },
		summary = '**Sardine** and **pilchard** are [[Common name|common names]] for various species of small, oily [[Forage fish|forage fish]] in the [[Herring|herring]] suborder Clupeoidei. The term "sardine" was first used in [[English language|English]] during the early 15th century, possibly referring to [[Sardinia]], around which sardines were once supposedly abundant.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The terms "sardine" and "pilchard" are not precise, and what is meant depends on the region. The [[United Kingdom]]\'s Sea Fish Industry Authority, for example, classifies sardines as young pilchards. One criterion suggests fish shorter in length than 15 cm are sardines, and larger fish are pilchards. The [[Food and Agriculture Organization|FAO]]/[[World Health Organization|WHO]] Codex standard for canned sardines cites 21 species that may be classed as sardines. FishBase, a database of information about fish, calls at least six species pilchards, over a dozen just sardines, and many more with the two basic names qualified by various adjectives.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Characiformes", "Nurse shark" },
	},
	{
		title = "Sauropoda",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦕",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Extinct clade of saurischian dinosaurs",
		tags = { "Reptiles", "Dinosaurs and prehistoric reptiles" },
		aliases = { "Sauropods" },
		summary = "**Sauropoda**, whose members are called **sauropods**, is a [[Clade|clade]] of saurischian ('lizard-hipped') [[Dinosaur|dinosaurs]]. Sauropods had very long necks, long tails, small heads (relative to the rest of their body), and four thick, pillar-like legs. They are notable for the enormous sizes attained by some species, and the group includes the largest animals to have ever lived on land. Well-known [[Genus|genera]] include Alamosaurus, Apatosaurus, Argentinosaurus, Brachiosaurus, Brontosaurus, Camarasaurus, [[Diplodocus]], Dreadnoughtus, and Mamenchisaurus.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The oldest known unequivocal sauropod dinosaurs are known from the [[Early Jurassic]]. Isanosaurus and Antetonitrus were originally described as [[Triassic]] sauropods, but their age, and in the case of Antetonitrus also its sauropod status, were subsequently questioned. Sauropod-like sauropodomorph tracks from the Fleming Fjord Formation ([[Greenland]]) might, however, indicate the occurrence of the group in the [[Late Triassic]]. By the [[Late Jurassic]] (150 million years ago), sauropods had become widespread (especially the diplodocids and brachiosaurids). By the [[Late Cretaceous]], one group of sauropods, the titanosaurs, had replaced all others and had a near-global distribution. However, as with all other non-avian dinosaurs alive at the time, the titanosaurs died out in the [[Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Triceratops" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Sauropoda" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sawfish",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Batoidea" },
		summary = "**Sawfish** are a [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of very large [[Batomorphi|rays]] characterized by a long, narrow, flattened rostrum, or nose extension, lined with sharp transverse teeth, arranged in a way that resembles a [[Saw|saw]]. They are among the largest fish, with some species reaching lengths around 7-7.6 m. They are found worldwide in tropical and [[Subtropics|subtropical]] regions in coastal [[Seawater|marine]] and [[Brackish water|brackish]] estuarine waters, as well as freshwater rivers and lakes. All species are critically endangered.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"They should not be confused with sawsharks (order Pristiophoriformes) or the extinct sclerorhynchoids (order Rajiformes), which have a similar appearance, or [[Swordfish|swordfish]] (family Xiphiidae), which have a similar name but a very different appearance. Sawfishes are relatively slow breeders and the females give birth to live young. They feed on fish and invertebrates that are detected and captured with the use of their saw. Sawfish have been known and hunted for thousands of years, and play an important mythological and spiritual role in many societies around the world. Once common, sawfish have experienced a drastic decline in recent decades, and the only remaining strongholds are in Northern Australia and [[Florida]], United States. All five species are rated as [[Critically endangered|critically endangered]] by the [[International Union for Conservation of Nature]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Electric ray" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Pristidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Scale insect",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐞",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Superfamily of insects",
		tags = { "Insects and other hexapods", "Hemiptera (true bugs)" },
		aliases = { "Scale insects" },
		summary = "**Scale insects** are small [[Insect|insects]] of the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] [[Hemiptera]], suborder Sternorrhyncha. Adult females typically have soft bodies and no limbs, and are concealed underneath domed scales, extruding quantities of wax for protection. Some species are hermaphroditic, with a combined ovotestis instead of separate ovaries and testes. Males, in the species where they occur, have legs and sometimes wings, and resemble small flies. Scale insects are [[Herbivore|herbivores]], piercing plant tissues with their mouthparts and remaining in one place, feeding on sap. The excess fluid they imbibe is secreted as honeydew on which sooty mold tends to grow.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The insects often have a [[Mutualism (biology)|mutualistic relationship]] with [[Ant|ants]], which feed on the honeydew and protect them from [[Predation|predators]]. There are about 8,000 described species. The oldest fossils of the group date to the [[Late Jurassic]], preserved in [[Amber|amber]]. They were already substantially diversified by the [[Early Cretaceous]] suggesting an earlier origin during the [[Triassic]] or [[Jurassic]]. Their closest relatives are the jumping plant lice, [[Whitefly|whiteflies]], phylloxera bugs and aphids. The majority of female scale insects remain in one place as adults, with newly hatched nymphs, known as "crawlers", being the only mobile life stage, apart from the short-lived males. The reproductive strategies of many species include at least some amount of [[Asexual reproduction|asexual reproduction]] by parthenogenesis.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Locust" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Coccoidea" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Scallop",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of shellfish, many edible",
		tags = { "Animals", "Mollusks" },
		aliases = { "Pectinidae" },
		summary = "**Scallop** is a [[Common name|common name]] that encompasses various species of marine [[Bivalvia|bivalve]] [[Mollusca|molluscs]] in the [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomic]] [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Pectinidae**, the scallops. However, this common name is also sometimes applied to species in other closely related families within the superfamily Pectinoidea, which also includes the thorny oysters.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Scallops are a [[Cosmopolitan distribution|cosmopolitan]] family of bivalves found in all of the world\'s oceans, although never in fresh water. They are one of very few groups of bivalves to be primarily "free-living", with many species capable of rapidly swimming short distances and even migrating some distance across the ocean floor. A small minority of scallop species live cemented to rocky substrates as adults, while others attach themselves to stationary or rooted objects such as seagrass at some point in their lives by means of a filament they secrete called a byssal thread. The majority of scallop species, however, live recumbent on sandy substrates, though when they sense the presence of a predator such as a [[Starfish|starfish]], they may attempt to escape by swimming swiftly but erratically through the water using jet propulsion created by repeatedly clapping their shells together.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Pectinidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Scorpaeniformes",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Obsolete order of ray-finned fishes",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Fishes" },
		aliases = { "Scleroparei" },
		summary = "The **Scorpaeniformes** are a diverse [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] of [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]], encompassing species such as the stonefish, lionfish, and sculpins, but have also been called the **Scleroparei**. It is one of the five largest orders of bony fishes by number of species, with over 1,320.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'They are known as "mail-cheeked" fishes due to their distinguishing characteristic, the suborbital stay: a backwards extension of the third circumorbital bone (part of the lateral head/cheek skeleton, below the eye socket) across the cheek to the preoperculum, to which it is connected in most species. Scorpaeniform fishes are [[Carnivore|carnivorous]], mostly feeding on crustaceans and on smaller fish. Most species live on the sea bottom in relatively shallow waters, although species are known from deep water, from the midwater, and even from fresh water. They typically have spiny heads, and rounded pectoral and caudal fins. Most species are less than 30 cm in length, but the full size range of the order varies from the velvetfishes belonging to the family Aploactinidae, which can be just 2 cm long as adults, to the skilfish (Erilepis zonifer), which can reach 183 cm in total length.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Osteichthyes", "Coelacanth" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Scorpaeniformes" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Scorpion",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🦂",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Predatory order of arachnids",
		tags = { "Arthropods", "Arachnids" },
		aliases = { "Scorpions", "Scorpiones" },
		summary = "**Scorpions** ([[Order (taxonomy)|order]] **Scorpiones**) are predatory [[Arachnid|arachnids]] with eight legs, a pair of grasping pincers (or chelae) and a narrow, segmented tail, often carried in a characteristic forward curve over the back and always ending with a stinger. The evolutionary history of scorpions goes back to the Silurian Period, approximately 430 million years ago. They mainly live in [[Desert|deserts]] but have adapted to a wide range of environmental conditions, and can be found on all continents except [[Antarctica]]. There are over 2,500 described [[Species|species]], with 22 extant (living) families recognized to date. Their [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomy]] is being revised to account for 21st-century genomic studies.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Scorpions primarily prey on [[Insect|insects]] and other [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]], but some species hunt [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]]. During courtship, the male and female grasp each other's pincers and dance while the male tries to move the female onto his sperm packet. All known species give live birth and the female cares for the young as their [[Exoskeleton|exoskeletons]] harden, transporting them on her back. The exoskeleton contains fluorescent chemicals and glows under [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] light. The vast majority of species do not seriously threaten humans, and healthy adults usually do not need medical treatment after a sting. Scorpions appear in art, folklore, mythology, and commercial brands. Scorpion motifs are woven into kilim carpets for protection from their sting. Scorpius is the name of a constellation; the corresponding astrological sign is Scorpio.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Jumping spider" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Scorpiones" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sea cucumber",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🥒",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Class of echinoderms",
		tags = { "Animals", "Echinoderms" },
		aliases = { "Sea cucumbers", "Holothuroidea" },
		summary = "**Sea cucumbers** are [[Echinoderm|echinoderms]] from the [[Class (taxonomy)|class]] **Holothuroidea**. They are benthic marine animals found on the sea floor worldwide, and the number of known **holothuroid** species worldwide is about 1,786, with the greatest number being in the Asia–Pacific region. Sea cucumbers serve a useful role in the [[Marine ecosystem|marine ecosystem]] as [[Detritivore|detritivores]] who help recycle nutrients, breaking down [[Detritus|detritus]] and other [[Organic matter|organic matter]], after which microbes can continue the [[Decomposition|decomposition]] process.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Sea cucumbers have a leathery [[Skin|skin]] and an elongated body containing a single, branched gonad; they are named for their overall resemblance to the fruit of the [[Cucumber|cucumber]] plant. Like all [[Echinoderm|echinoderms]], sea cucumbers have a calcified dermal [[Endoskeleton|endoskeleton]], which is usually reduced to isolated microscopic ossicles (or sclerites) joined by connective tissue. In some species these can sometimes be enlarged to flattened plates, forming an armoured [[Cuticle|cuticle]]. In some abyssal or [[Pelagic zone|pelagic]] species such as Pelagothuria natatrix (order Elasipodida, family Pelagothuriidae), the skeleton is absent and there is no calcareous ring. Many species of sea cucumbers are foraged as food by [[Human|humans]], and some species are cultivated in [[Aquaculture|aquaculture]] systems. They are considered a delicacy [[Seafood|seafood]], especially in Asian cuisines, and the harvested product is variously referred to as trepang, namako, bêche-de-mer, or balate.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Boletus edulis" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Holothuroidea" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sea otter",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of marine mammal",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Carnivora" },
		aliases = { "Enhydra lutris" },
		summary = "The **sea otter** (**Enhydra lutris**) is a [[Marine mammal|marine mammal]] native to the coasts of the northern and eastern [[Pacific Ocean|North Pacific Ocean]]. Adult sea [[Otter|otters]] typically weigh between 14 and 45 kg, making them the heaviest members of the [[Mustelidae|weasel family]], but among the smallest marine mammals. Unlike most marine mammals, the sea otter's primary form of insulation is an exceptionally thick coat of [[Fur|fur]], the densest in the animal kingdom. Although it can walk on land, the sea otter is capable of living exclusively in the ocean.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The sea otter inhabits nearshore environments, where it dives to the sea floor to [[Foraging|forage]]. It preys mostly on marine [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]] such as [[Sea urchin|sea urchins]], various mollusks and [[Crustacean|crustaceans]], and some species of [[Fish|fish]]. Its foraging and eating habits are noteworthy in several respects. Its use of rocks to dislodge prey and to open shells makes it one of the few mammal species to use tools. In most of its range, it is a [[Keystone species|keystone species]], controlling [[Sea urchin|sea urchin]] populations which would otherwise inflict extensive damage to kelp forest [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]]. Its diet includes prey species that are also valued by humans as food, leading to conflicts between sea otters and fisheries. Sea otters, whose numbers were once estimated at 150,000–300,000, were hunted extensively for their fur between 1741 and 1911, and the world population fell to 1,000–2,000 individuals living in a fraction of their historic range.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Enhydra lutris" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Endangered" },
		},
	},
	{
