	{
		title = "Sea turtle",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Reptiles of the superfamily Chelonioidea",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Reptiles" },
		aliases = { "Sea turtles", "Chelonioidea", "Marine turtles" },
		summary = "**Sea turtles** (superfamily **Chelonioidea**), sometimes called **marine turtles**, are reptiles of the order Testudines and of the suborder Cryptodira. The seven existing species of sea turtles are the flatback, green, hawksbill, leatherback, loggerhead, Kemp's ridley, and olive ridley. Five of the seven species are listed as threatened with extinction globally on the [[IUCN Red List]] of Threatened Species. The remaining two are not considered to be threatened with extinction, one of which, the flatback turtle, is found only in the waters of Australia, Papua New Guinea, and Indonesia.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Sea turtles can be categorized as hard-shelled (cheloniid) or leathery-shelled (dermochelyid). The only dermochelyid species of sea turtle is the leatherback.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Adder", "Common snapping turtle", "Gecko", "Mamba", "Sea snake" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Chelonioidea" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sea urchin",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Class of marine invertebrates",
		tags = { "Animals", "Echinoderms" },
		aliases = { "Sea urchins", "Urchins", "Echinoidea" },
		summary = "**Sea urchins** or **urchins** are the [[Class (taxonomy)|class]] **Echinoidea** within the [[Echinoderm|echinoderms]]. Approximately 950 species live on the seabed, inhabiting all oceans and depths from the [[Intertidal zone|intertidal zone]] to the [[Deep sea|deep sea]]. They typically have a globular body covered by spiny protective tests (hard shells), typically from 3 to 10 cm across. Sea urchins move slowly, crawling with their tube feet, and sometimes pushing themselves with their spines. They feed primarily on [[Algae|algae]] but also eat slow-moving or sessile animals such as crinoids and [[Sponge|sponges]]. Their predators include [[Shark|sharks]], [[Sea otter|sea otters]], [[Starfish|starfish]], wolf eels, triggerfish, and [[Human|humans]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When unchecked by predators, urchins can create urchin barrens, damaged environments devoid of large algae and the animals associated with them. Like all echinoderms, adult sea urchins have pentagonal symmetry with their [[Echinoderm|pluteus larvae]] featuring bilateral (mirror) symmetry; The latter indicates that they belong to the [[Bilateria]], along with [[Chordate|chordates]], [[Arthropod|arthropods]], [[Annelid|annelids]] and [[Mollusca|molluscs]]. Sea urchins are found in every ocean and in every climate, from the [[Tropics|tropics]] to the [[Polar regions|polar regions]], and inhabit marine benthic (sea bed) habitats, from rocky shores to hadal zone depths. The fossil record of the echinoids dates from the [[Ordovician]] period, some 450 million years ago. The closest echinoderm relatives of the sea urchin are the [[Sea cucumber|sea cucumbers]] (Holothuroidea), which like them are deuterostomes, a clade that includes the [[Chordate|chordates]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Echinoidea" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Seabird",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Birds living in the maritime environment",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Birds" },
		aliases = { "Seabirds", "Marine birds" },
		summary = "**Seabirds** (also known as **marine birds**) are [[Bird|birds]] that are [[Adaptation|adapted]] to life within the [[Marine ecosystem|marine]] environment. While seabirds vary greatly in lifestyle, behaviour and physiology, they often exhibit striking [[Convergent evolution|convergent evolution]], as the same environmental problems and feeding [[Ecological niche|niches]] have resulted in similar adaptations. The first seabirds evolved in the [[Cretaceous]] period, while modern seabird families emerged in the [[Paleogene]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Seabirds generally live longer, [[Reproduction|breed]] later and have fewer young than many other birds, but they invest a great deal of time in their young. Most [[Species|species]] nest in colonies, varying in size from a few dozen birds to millions. Many species are famous for undertaking long annual [[Bird migration|migrations]], crossing the [[Equator|equator]] or circumnavigating the Earth in some cases. They feed both at the ocean's surface and below it, and even on each other. Seabirds can be highly [[Pelagic zone|pelagic]], coastal, or in some cases spend a part of the year away from the sea entirely. Seabirds and humans have a long history together: They have provided food to [[Hunting|hunters]], guided [[Fishing|fishers]] to fishing stocks, and led sailors to land. Many species are currently [[Threatened species|threatened]] by human activities such as oil spills, fishing activity, offshore development, [[Climate change|climate change]] and severe weather.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Archaeopteryx" },
	},
	{
		title = "Seahorse",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of bony fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		aliases = { "Hippocampus" },
		summary = "A **seahorse** (also written sea-horse and sea horse) is any of 47 species of small marine [[Osteichthyes|bony fish]] in the [[Genus|genus]] **Hippocampus**. Having a head and neck suggestive of a [[Horse|horse]], seahorses also feature segmented bony armour, an upright posture and a curled prehensile tail. Along with the pipefishes and seadragons (Phycodurus and Phyllopteryx) they form the family Syngnathidae. They can be found in seas, brackish [[Estuary|estuaries]] and [[Lagoon|lagoons]].",
		related = { "Grouper", "Serranidae" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Hippocampus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Secretarybird",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Bird of prey",
		tags = { "Birds", "Accipitriformes and Falconiformes" },
		aliases = { "Secretary bird", "Sagittarius serpentarius" },
		summary = "The **secretarybird** or **secretary bird** (**Sagittarius serpentarius**) is a large [[Bird of prey|bird of prey]] that is [[Endemism|endemic]] to Africa. It is mostly terrestrial, spending most of its time on the ground, and is usually found in the open grasslands and [[Savanna|savanna]] of the [[Sub-Saharan Africa|sub-Saharan region]]. John Frederick Miller described the species in 1779. A member of the order Accipitriformes, which also includes many other diurnal birds of prey such as [[Eagle|eagles]], hawks, kites, vultures, and harriers, it is placed in its own family, the Sagittariidae.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The secretarybird is instantly recognizable as a very large bird with an eagle-like body on [[Crane (bird)|crane-like]] legs that give the bird a height of as much as 1.3 m. The sexes are similar in appearance. Adults have a featherless, red-orange face and predominantly grey plumage, with a flattened dark crest and black flight feathers and thighs. Breeding can take place at any time of year, but tends to be late in the dry season. The nest is built at the top of a thorny tree, and a clutch of one to three [[Egg|eggs]] is laid. In years with plentiful food, all three young can survive to fledging. Insects and small vertebrates make up its diet. Although the secretarybird resides over a large range, the results of localized surveys suggest that the total population is experiencing a rapid decline, probably as a result of [[Habitat destruction|habitat destruction]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Peregrine falcon", "Falcon" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Sagittarius serpentarius" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Endangered" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Segmentation (biology)",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Division of some animal and plant body plans into a series of segments",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Segmentation" },
		summary = '**Segmentation** in biology is the division of some [[Animal|animal]] and [[Plant|plant]] [[Body plan|body plans]] into a linear series of repetitive segments that may or may not be interconnected to each other. This article focuses on the segmentation of [[Animal|animal]] body plans, specifically using the examples of the taxa Arthropoda, Chordata, and Annelida. These three groups form segments by using a "growth zone" to direct and define the segments. While all three have a generally segmented body plan and use a growth zone, they use different mechanisms for generating this patterning. Even within these groups, different organisms have different mechanisms for segmenting the body.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Segmentation of the body plan is important for allowing free movement and development of certain body parts. It also allows for regeneration in specific individuals.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Serranidae",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		summary = "**Serranidae** is a [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of marine fish belonging to the order [[Perciformes]]. The family used to contain about 450 species in 65 genera, including some of the sea basses and [[Grouper|groupers]], but taxonomic revisions split out Epinephelinae (groupers), Grammistini (soapfish), and Anthiadinae (anthias); as a consequence, this family is now much less speciose. Representatives of this group live in [[Tropics|tropical]] and subtropical seas worldwide.",
