	{
		title = "Sheep",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of domesticated mammal",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Artiodactyla" },
		summary = "Although the term sheep can apply to other species in the genus Ovis, in everyday usage it almost always refers to domesticated sheep. Like all ruminants, sheep are members of the order [[Artiodactyla]], the even-toed ungulates. There are around 1.2 billion domestic sheep as of 2019, making them easily the most common species of sheep. An adult female is referred to as a ewe; an intact male as a ram, occasionally a tup; a castrated male as a wether; and a young sheep as a lamb.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Sheep are most likely descended from the wild mouflon of Europe and Asia, with Iran being a geographic envelope of the domestication center. One of the earliest animals to be domesticated for agricultural purposes, sheep are raised for fleeces, meat ([[Lamb and mutton|lamb, hogget, or mutton]]), and sheep milk. A sheep's [[Wool|wool]] is the most widely used animal fiber, and is usually harvested by shearing. Ovine meat is called [[Lamb and mutton|lamb and mutton]], with lamb describing meat from younger animals and mutton meat from older ones. Sheep continue to be important for wool and meat today, and are also occasionally raised for pelts, as dairy animals, or as model organisms for science. Sheep husbandry is done throughout the majority of the inhabited world. It was central to agriculture and textiles throughout Eurasia for millennia.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Ovis aries" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Domesticated" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Shoaling and schooling",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "In biology, any group of fish that stay together for social reasons",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Shoaling", "Schooling" },
		summary = "In biology, any group of [[Fish|fish]] that stay together for social reasons are **shoaling**, and if the group is swimming in the same direction in a coordinated manner, they are **schooling**. In common usage, the terms are sometimes used rather loosely. About one quarter of fish species shoal all their lives, and about one half shoal for part of their lives.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Fish derive many benefits from shoaling behaviour including defence against predators (through better predator detection and by diluting the chance of individual capture), enhanced [[Foraging|foraging]] success, and higher success in finding a mate. Fish benefit from schooling through increased hydrodynamic efficiency, meaning they move faster and expend less energy. Fish use many traits to choose shoalmates. Generally they prefer larger shoals, shoalmates of their own species, shoalmates similar in size and appearance to themselves, healthy fish, and kin (when recognized). The oddity effect posits that any shoal member that stands out in appearance will be preferentially targeted by predators, thus homogenising shoals.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Shoebill",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of bird",
		tags = { "Birds", "Pelecanimorphae" },
		aliases = { "Balaeniceps rex", "Whale-headed stork", "Whalebill" },
		summary = "The **shoebill** (**Balaeniceps rex**), also known as the **whale-headed stork**, **whalebill**, and **shoe-billed stork**, is a large long-legged wading bird. It is named for its enormous, shoe-shaped [[Beak|bill]]. It has a somewhat [[Stork|stork]]-like overall form and was previously classified as a stork in the order Ciconiiformes, but genetic evidence has now placed it along with [[Pelican|pelicans]], [[Heron|herons]], and its closest relative, the hamerkop, all in the order Pelecaniformes. Adults are mainly grey, while juveniles are browner. It lives in tropical [[East Africa]] in large swamps from [[South Sudan]] down to [[Zambia]].",
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Balaeniceps rex" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Vulnerable" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Shrew",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of mammals",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Soricomorpha" },
		aliases = { "Shrews", "Soricidae" },
		summary = "**Shrews** ([[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Soricidae**) are small [[Mole (animal)|mole]]-like [[Mammal|mammals]] classified in the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] Eulipotyphla. True shrews are not to be confused with treeshrews, otter shrews, elephant shrews, West Indies shrews, or marsupial shrews and shrew opossums, which belong to different families or orders.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although its external appearance is generally same as a long-nosed [[Mouse|mouse]], a shrew is not a [[Rodent|rodent]], as mice are. It is, in fact, a much closer relative of hedgehogs and moles; shrews are related to rodents only in that both belong to the Boreoeutheria magnorder. Shrews have sharp, spike-like teeth, whereas rodents have gnawing front incisor teeth. Shrews are [[Cosmopolitan distribution|distributed almost worldwide]]. Among the major tropical and temperate land masses, only [[New Guinea]], [[Australia]], [[New Zealand]], and [[South America]] have no native shrews. However, as a result of the Great American Interchange, [[South America]] does have a relatively recently naturalised population, present only in the northern [[Andes]]. The shrew family has 385 known species, making it the fourth-most species-diverse mammal family.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Soricidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Shrike",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Corvoidea" },
		aliases = { "Shrikes", "Laniidae" },
		summary = "**Shrikes** are [[Passerine|passerine]] birds of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Laniidae**. The family is composed of 34 species in four [[Genus|genera]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The family name, and that of the larger genus, Lanius, is derived from the [[Latin]] word for "butcher", and some shrikes are also known as **butcherbirds** because of the habit, particularly of males, of impaling prey onto plant spines within their territories. These larders have multiple functions, attracting females and serving as food stores. The common English name shrike is from [[Old English]], alluding to the shrike\'s shriek-like call.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Bird-of-paradise", "Lyrebird" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Laniidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Shrimp",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🦐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Decapod crustaceans",
		tags = { "Arthropods", "Crustaceans" },
		summary = 'The name usually refers to [[Decapod|decapods]] belonging to the Caridea or Dendrobranchiata, although some crustaceans outside this order are also referred to as "shrimp".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'More narrow definitions may be restricted to Caridea, to smaller species of either of the aforementioned groups, or only the marine species. Under a broader definition, shrimp may be synonymous with prawn, covering stalk-eyed swimming [[Crustacean|crustaceans]] with long, narrow muscular tails (abdomens), long whiskers ([[Antenna (zoology)|antennae]]), and slender, biramous legs. They swim forward by paddling the swimmerets on the underside of their abdomens, although their escape response is typically repeated flicks with the tail, driving them backwards very quickly ("lobstering"). [[Crab|Crabs]] and [[Lobster|lobsters]] have strong walking legs, whereas shrimp typically have thin, fragile legs which they use primarily for perching. Shrimp are widespread and abundant.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Ostracod" },
	},
	{
		title = "Siamese cat",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐈",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Breed of domestic cat",
		tags = { "Animal breeds and hybrids", "Cats" },
		summary = "The **Siamese cat** is one of the first distinctly recognised breeds of domestic cat. It is [[Selective breeding|selectively bred]] since the end of the 19th-century from the Wichianmat landrace, one of several varieties of cats native to [[Thailand]] (known as Siam before 1939), and is pedigreed in all major cat fancier and breeder organisations. Siamese cats have a distinctive colourpoint coat, resulting from a temperature-sensitive type of albinism. Distinct features like blue almond-shaped eyes, a triangular wedged head shape, large wide-set ears, an elongated, slender, and muscular body, and various colours of point colouration characterise the breed.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The breed was one of the most popular cat breeds in [[Europe]] and [[North America]] in the 19th century. Around the 1950s, the modern-style body with slender and angular features became the preferred type by Siamese enthusiasts. From the 1980s onwards, the traditional "old-style" (rounder-built) cats started to officially split off from the (modern-style) Siamese breed and were re-established by multiple registries as the separate Thai cat breed. The terms "Siamese" or "Thai" are used for cats from these specific breeds, which are by definition all purebred cats with a known and formally registered [[Genealogy|ancestry]], also known as the cat\'s pedigree or "paperwork". The Siamese is used as foundation stock in several new cat breeds; by [[Crossbreed|crossbreeding]] them with other cats. The Oriental and Colourpoint Shorthair were developed to expand the range of Siamese coat patterns.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Amphibian", "Earthworm" },
	},
	{
		title = "Siamese fighting fish",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of fish native to Southeast Asia",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		aliases = { "Betta splendens", "Siamese fighter", "Betta" },
		summary = 'The **Siamese fighting fish** (**Betta splendens**), commonly known as the **Siamese fighter**, or locally as the **betta**, is a [[Freshwater fish|freshwater fish]] native to [[Southeast Asia|Mainland Southeast Asia]], namely [[Cambodia]], [[Laos]], [[Myanmar]], [[Malaysia]], [[Thailand]], and [[Vietnam]]. It is one of more than 70 [[Species|species]] of the [[Genus|genus]] Betta, but the only one eponymously called "betta", owing to its global popularity as a pet; Betta splendens are among the most popular aquarium fish in the world, due to their diverse and colorful [[Morphology (biology)|morphology]] and relatively low maintenance.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Betta fish are endemic to the central plain of Thailand, where they were first domesticated more than 1,000 years ago, among the earliest of any fish. Bettas became known outside Thailand through King Rama III (1788–1851), who is said to have given some to Theodore Cantor, a Danish physician, [[Zoology|zoologist]], and botanist. They first appeared in the West in the late 19th century, and within decades became popular as ornamental fish. B. splendens\'s long history of selective breeding has produced a wide variety of coloration and finnage, earning it the moniker "designer fish of the aquatic world". Bettas are well known for being highly territorial, with males frequently engaging in aggressive interactions when kept together; without a means of escape, this usually results in the death of one or both fish. Female bettas can also become territorial toward one another in confined spaces.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Cichlid", "Ocean sunfish" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Betta splendens" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Vulnerable" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sirenia",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Order of aquatic herbivorous mammals",
		tags = { "Vertebrates", "Mammals" },
		aliases = { "Sea cows", "Sirenians" },
		summary = "The **Sirenia**, commonly referred to as **sea cows** or **sirenians**, are an [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] of fully aquatic, [[Herbivore|herbivorous]] [[Mammal|mammals]] that inhabit swamps, rivers, estuaries, marine wetlands, and coastal marine waters. The extant Sirenia comprise two distinct [[Family (taxonomy)|families]]: Dugongidae (the dugong and the now extinct Steller's sea cow) and Trichechidae (manatees, namely the Amazonian manatee, West Indian manatee, and West African manatee) with a total of three species.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Protosirenidae (Eocene sirenians) and Prorastomidae (terrestrial sirenians) families are extinct. Sirenians are classified in the [[Clade|clade]] Paenungulata, alongside the [[Elephant|elephants]] and the [[Hyrax|hyraxes]], and evolved in the [[Eocene]] 50 million years ago (mya). The Dugongidae diverged from the Trichechidae in the late Eocene or [[Rupelian|early Oligocene]] (30–35 mya). Sirenians grow to between 2.5 and 4 m in length and 1500 kg in weight. The recently extinct Steller's sea cow was the largest known sirenian to have lived, reaching lengths of 10 m and weights of 5 to 10 tonnes. Sirenians have a large, fusiform body which reduces drag through the water and heavy bones that act as ballast to counteract the buoyancy of their [[Blubber|blubber]]. They have a thin layer of blubber and consequently are sensitive to temperature fluctuations, which cause large-scale migrations when water temperatures dip too low.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Armadillo" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Sirenia" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Skate (fish)",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Batoidea" },
		aliases = { "Skates", "Rajidae" },
		summary = '**Skates** are [[Chondrichthyes|cartilaginous fish]] belonging to the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Rajidae** in the superorder [[Batomorphi|Batoidea]] of rays. More than 150 species have been described, in 17 genera. Softnose skates and pygmy skates were previously treated as subfamilies of Rajidae (Arhynchobatinae and Gurgesiellinae), but are now considered as distinct families. Alternatively, the name "skate" is used to refer to the entire order of Rajiformes (families Anacanthobatidae, Arhynchobatidae, Gurgesiellidae and Rajidae).',
		sections = {
			{ "Overview", "Members of Rajidae are distinguished by a stiff snout and a rostrum that is not reduced." },
		},
		related = { "Koi" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Rajidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Skink",
		category = "Animal",
