	{
		title = "Slow loris",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of primates from Southeast Asia",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Primates" },
		aliases = { "Slow lorises", "Nycticebus" },
		summary = "**Slow lorises** are a group of several [[Species|species]] of [[Nocturnal animal|nocturnal]] [[Strepsirrhini|strepsirrhine]] [[Primate|primates]] that make up the [[Genus|genus]] **Nycticebus**. Found in [[Southeast Asia]] and nearby areas, they range from [[Bangladesh]] and [[Northeast India]] in the west to the Sulu Archipelago in the [[Philippines]] in the east, and from [[Yunnan]] province in China in the north to the island of [[Java]] in the south.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although many previous classifications recognized as few as a single all-inclusive species, there are now at least eight that are considered valid: the Sunda slow loris (N. coucang), Bengal slow loris (N. bengalensis), Javan slow loris (N. javanicus), Philippine slow loris (N. menagensis), Bangka slow loris (N. bancanus), Bornean slow loris (N. borneanus), Kayan River slow loris (N. kayan) and Sumatran slow loris (N. hilleri). A ninth species, the pygmy slow loris (X. pygmaeus), was recently moved to the new genus Xanthonycticebus. After the pygmy slow loris, the group's closest relatives are the slender lorises of southern India and Sri Lanka. Their next closest relatives are the African lorisids, the pottos, false pottos, and angwantibos. They are less closely related to the remaining lorisoids (the various types of [[Galago|galago]]), and more distantly to the [[Lemur|lemurs]] of [[Madagascar]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Homo", "Ape" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Nycticebus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Smelt (fish)",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Osmeriformes" },
		aliases = { "Smelts", "Osmeridae" },
		summary = "**Smelts** are a family of small [[Fish|fish]], the **Osmeridae**, found in the North Atlantic and North Pacific oceans, as well as rivers, streams and lakes in Europe, North America and Northeast Asia. They are also known as **freshwater smelts** or **typical smelts** to distinguish them from the related Argentinidae (herring smelts or argentines), Bathylagidae (deep-sea smelts), and Retropinnidae (Australian and New Zealand smelts).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some smelt species are common in the North American Great Lakes, and in the lakes and seas of the northern part of Europe, where they run in large [[Shoaling and schooling|schools]] along the saltwater coastline during spring migration to their spawning streams. In some western parts of the United States, smelt populations have greatly declined in recent decades, leading to their protection under the Endangered Species Act. The Delta smelt (Hypomesus transpacificus) found in the Sacramento Delta of California, and the eulachon (Thaleichthys pacificus) found in the Northeast Pacific and adjacent rivers, are both protected from harvest. Some species of smelts are among the few fish that sportsmen have been allowed to net, using hand-held dip nets, either along the coastline or in streams. Some sportsmen also ice fish for smelt. They are often fried and eaten whole.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Hammerhead shark" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Osmeridae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Snow leopard",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐆",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of large felid",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Carnivora" },
		aliases = { "Panthera uncia" },
		summary = "The **snow leopard** (**Panthera uncia**) is a [[Felidae|felid species]] in the genus Panthera. It has a thick whitish to grey fur with black spots on the head and neck, with larger rosettes on the back, flanks and bushy tail. Males are larger than the females, and both sexes have long canine teeth. Its small rounded ears, broad paws, and thick tails are adaptations for living in cold, mountainous environments.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The snow leopard is native to the mountain ranges of [[Asia]], ranging from eastern [[Hindu Kush]], the [[Himalayas]] and the [[Tibetan Plateau]] to southern [[Siberia]], [[Mongolia]] and [[Western China|western China]]. It inhabits [[Alpine climate|alpine]] and subalpine zones at elevations of 3000–4500 m featuring cliffs and ridges, but also lives at lower elevations in the northern part of its range. It is a [[Sociality|solitary animal]] and is mostly active at dawn and twilight. It is a [[Carnivore|carnivore]] and actively hunts its prey, which include various ungulates and domestic livestock. The snow leopard was long classified in the monotypic genus Uncia. Since [[Phylogenetics|phylogenetic]] studies revealed the relationships among Panthera species, it has been considered a member of that [[Genus|genus]]. Two subspecies were described based on [[Morphology (biology)|morphological]] differences, but [[Genetics|genetic]] differences between the two have not been confirmed.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cougar", "Weasel" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Panthera uncia" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Vulnerable" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Songbird",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Suborder of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Passerines" },
		aliases = { "Passeri" },
		summary = "A **songbird** is a [[Bird|bird]] belonging to the [[Order (taxonomy)|suborder]] **Passeri** of the [[Passerine|perching birds]]. Another name that is sometimes seen as the scientific or vernacular name is **Oscines**, from [[Latin]] oscen, 'songbird'.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Songbirds form one of the two major lineages of extant perching birds (~4,000 species), the other being the Tyranni (~1,000 species), which are most diverse in the Neotropics and absent from many parts of the world. The Tyranni have a simpler syrinx musculature, and while their vocalizations are often just as complex and striking as those of songbirds, they are altogether more mechanical sounding. There is a third perching bird lineage, the Acanthisitti of [[New Zealand]], of which only two species remain alive today. Recent estimates indicate that songbirds originated 50 million years ago. The distribution of their basal lineages suggests that their origin and initial diversification occurred exclusively in the Australian continent; and only about 40 million years ago, oscines started to colonize [[Eurasia]], [[Africa]], and eventually the [[Americas]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Trogon" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Passeri" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Species distribution",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Geographical area in which a species can be found",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Species distribution**, or **species** **dispersion**, is the manner in which a biological [[Taxon|taxon]] is spatially arranged. The geographic limits of a particular taxon's distribution is its **range**, often represented as shaded areas on a map. Patterns of distribution change depending on the scale at which they are viewed, from the arrangement of individuals within a small family unit, to patterns within a population, or the distribution of the entire [[Species|species]] as a whole (range). Species distribution is not to be confused with dispersal, which is the movement of individuals away from their region of origin or from a population center of high [[Population density|density]].",
		related = { "Time in Turkey" },
	},
	{
		title = "Species diversity",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Number of distinct species in a biological community",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Species diversity** is the number of different [[Species|species]] that are represented in a given community (a dataset). The effective number of species refers to the number of equally abundant species needed to obtain the same mean proportional species abundance as that observed in the dataset of interest (where all species may not be equally abundant). Meanings of species diversity may include species richness, taxonomic or phylogenetic diversity, and/or species evenness. Species richness is a simple count of species. Taxonomic or phylogenetic diversity is the genetic relationship between different groups of species. Species evenness quantifies how equal the abundances of the species are.",
		related = { "Telephone numbers in the United Kingdom" },
	},
	{
		title = "Spectral line",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Distinctive narrow spectral feature of chemical species",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'A **spectral line** is a weaker or stronger region in an otherwise uniform and continuous spectrum. It may result from emission or [[Absorption (electromagnetic radiation)|absorption]] of [[Light|light]] in a narrow [[Frequency|frequency]] range, compared with the nearby frequencies. Spectral lines are often used to identify [[Atom|atoms]] and [[Molecule|molecules]]. These "fingerprints" can be compared to the previously collected ones of atoms and molecules, and are thus used to identify the atomic and molecular components of [[Star|stars]] and [[Planet|planets]], which would otherwise be impossible.',
	},
	{
		title = "Sperm whale",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐋",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Largest species of toothed whale",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Cetaceans" },
		aliases = { "Cachalot", "Physeter macrocephalus" },
		summary = "The **sperm whale** or **cachalot** (**Physeter macrocephalus**) is the largest of the toothed whales and the largest toothed [[Predation|predator]]. It is the only living member of the [[Genus|genus]] Physeter and one of three extant [[Species|species]] in the sperm whale superfamily Physeteroidea, along with the pygmy sperm whale and dwarf sperm whale of the genus Kogia.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The sperm whale is a [[Pelagic zone|pelagic]] [[Mammal|mammal]] with a worldwide range, and will migrate seasonally for feeding and breeding. Females and young males live together in groups, while mature males (bulls) live solitary lives outside of the mating season. The females cooperate to protect and nurse their young. Females give birth every four to twenty years and care for the calves for more than a decade. Mature males average 16 m in length, with the head representing up to one-third of the animal's length. Plunging to 2250 m, it is the third deepest diving mammal, exceeded only by the southern elephant seal and Cuvier's beaked whale. The sperm whale uses [[Animal echolocation|echolocation]] and vocalization with source level as loud as 236 [[Decibel|decibels]] underwater, the loudest of any animal. It has the largest brain on [[Earth]], more than five times heavier than a human's. Sperm whales can live 70 years or more.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Physeter macrocephalus" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Vulnerable" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spider monkey",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐒",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of mammals belonging to the New World monkeys",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Primates" },
		aliases = { "Spider monkeys", "Ateles" },
		summary = "**Spider monkeys** are [[New World monkey|New World monkeys]] belonging to the [[Genus|genus]] **Ateles**, part of the subfamily Atelinae, family Atelidae. Like other atelines, they are found in [[Tropical forest|tropical forests]] of Central and South America, from southern [[Mexico]] to [[Brazil]]. The genus consists of seven species, all of which are under threat; the brown spider monkey is critically endangered. They are also notable for their ability to be easily bred in captivity.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"According to the Saint Louis Zoo, spider monkeys, which have arms longer than their legs, but lack thumbs, are so-called because they hang from branches by their prehensile tail, with their long limbs suspended in the air, for which they resemble spiders dangling from a web. They are one of the largest New World monkeys. Spider monkeys live in the upper layers of the [[Rainforest|rainforest]] and [[Forage|forage]] in the high canopy, from 25 to 30 m. Due to their large size, spider monkeys require large tracts of moist evergreen forests, and prefer undisturbed primary rainforest. They primarily eat fruits, but will also occasionally consume leaves, flowers, and insects. They are social animals and live in bands of up to 35 individuals, but will split up to forage during the day. Meta-analyses on primate cognition studies conducted in the 2000s indicated spider monkeys are the most intelligent New World monkeys.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Ateles" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spitz",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐕",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Dog type",
		tags = { "Cats", "Dogs" },
		summary = 'The **spitz** is a type of [[Dog|domestic dog]] including between 50 and 70 breeds depending on classification. There is no precise definition of the type, but most spitz breeds have pricked ears, almond-shaped eyes, a pointed muzzle, a double coat, and a tail that curves over the back. The name derives from the German, meaning "pointed (dog)", in reference to the shape of the muzzle.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most of the spitzes seen today originate from the [[Arctic]] region or from [[Siberia]]. Johann Friedrich Gmelin described the type as Canis pomeranus in his 1788 revision of [[Systema Naturae]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Arabian horse", "Mule" },
