	{
		title = "Stingray",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Suborder of fishes",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Batoidea" },
		aliases = { "Stingrays" },
		summary = "**Stingrays** are a group of [[Batomorphi|rays]], a type of cartilaginous fish. They are classified in the suborder **Myliobatoidei** of the order Myliobatiformes and consist of eleven families: Hexatrygonidae (sixgill stingrays), Gymnuridae (butterfly rays), Plesiobatidae (deepwater stingrays), Urolophidae (round stingrays), Myliobatidae (eagle rays), Aetobatidae (pelagic eagle rays), Mobulidae (manta and devil rays), Rhinopteridae (cownose rays), Dasyatidae (whiptail stingrays), Potamotrygonidae (river stingrays), and Urotrygonidae (American round stingrays). There are about 220 known stingray species organized into 29 genera.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Stingrays are common in coastal [[Tropics|tropical]] and [[Subtropics|subtropical]] marine waters throughout the world. Some species, such as the thorntail stingray (Dasyatis thetidis), are found in warmer [[Temperate climate|temperate]] oceans and others, such as the deepwater stingray (Plesiobatis daviesi), are found in the deep ocean. The river stingrays and a number of whiptail stingrays (such as the Niger stingray (Fontitrygon garouaensis)) are restricted to [[Fresh water|fresh water]]. Most myliobatoids are demersal (inhabiting the next-to-lowest zone in the water column), but some, such as the pelagic stingray and the eagle rays, are [[Pelagic zone|pelagic]]. Stingray species are progressively becoming threatened or vulnerable to [[Extinction|extinction]], particularly as a consequence of unregulated fishing. As of 2013, 45 species have been listed as [[Vulnerable species|vulnerable]] or [[Endangered species|endangered]] by the [[International Union for Conservation of Nature|IUCN]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Hammerhead shark" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Myliobatoidei" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Stoat",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of mammal",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Carnivora" },
		aliases = { "Mustela erminea", "Eurasian ermine", "Ermine" },
		summary = "The **stoat** (**Mustela erminea**), also known as the **Eurasian ermine** or **ermine**, is a species of mustelid native to [[Eurasia]] and the northern regions of [[North America]]. Because of its wide circumpolar distribution, it is listed as [[Least-concern species|Least Concern]] on the [[IUCN Red List]]. The name ermine is used especially in its pure white winter coat of the stoat or its fur. Ermine fur was used in the 15th century by Catholic monarchs, who sometimes used it as the mozzetta cape. It has long been used on the ceremonial robes of members of the United Kingdom [[House of Lords]]. It was also used in capes on images such as the Infant Jesus of Prague.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The stoat was introduced into New Zealand in the late 19th century to control rabbits. However, they have had a devastating effect on native bird populations; as such, the species was nominated as one of the world\'s top 100 "worst invaders".',
			},
		},
		related = { "Snow leopard" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Mustela erminea" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Stork",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Wading Stork",
		tags = { "Birds", "Pelecanimorphae" },
		aliases = { "Storks" },
		summary = "**Storks** are large, long-legged, long-necked wading birds with long, stout [[Beak|bills]]. They belong to the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Ciconiidae**, and make up the order **Ciconiiformes**. Ciconiiformes previously included a number of other families, such as [[Heron|herons]] and [[Ibis|ibises]], but those families have been moved to the order Pelecaniformes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Storks dwell in many regions and tend to live in drier habitats than the closely related herons, spoonbills and ibises; they also lack the powder down that those groups use to clean off fish slime. Bill-clattering is an important mode of [[Animal communication|communication]] at the nest. Many species are [[Bird migration|migratory]]. Most storks eat [[Frog|frogs]], [[Fish|fish]], [[Insect|insects]], [[Earthworm|earthworms]], small [[Bird|birds]] and small [[Mammal|mammals]]. There are 20 living [[Species|species]] of storks in six [[Genus|genera]]. Various terms are used to refer to groups of storks, two frequently used ones being a muster of storks and a phalanx of storks. Storks tend to use soaring, gliding flight, which conserves energy. Soaring requires thermal air currents. Ottomar Anschütz's famous 1884 album of photographs of storks inspired the design of Otto Lilienthal's experimental [[Glider (aircraft)|gliders]] of the late nineteenth century.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Antbird", "Frigatebird", "Shoebill" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Ciconiidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Strepsirrhini",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Suborder of primates",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Primates" },
		aliases = { "Strepsirhini" },
		summary = '**Strepsirrhini** or **Strepsirhini** is a [[Order (taxonomy)|suborder]] of [[Primate|primates]] that includes the lemuriform primates, which consist of the [[Lemur|lemurs]] of Madagascar, [[Galago|galagos]] ("bushbabies") and pottos from Africa, and the lorises from India and [[Southeast Asia]]. Collectively they are referred to as strepsirrhines.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Also belonging to the suborder are the extinct adapiform primates, which thrived during the [[Eocene]] in Europe, North America, and Asia, but disappeared from most of the Northern Hemisphere as the climate cooled. Adapiforms are sometimes referred to as being "lemur-like", although the diversity of both lemurs and adapiforms does not support this comparison. Strepsirrhines are defined by their "wet" (moist) rhinarium (the tip of the snout) – hence the colloquial but inaccurate term "wet-nosed" – similar to the rhinaria of canines and felines. They also have a smaller brain than comparably sized simians, large olfactory lobes for smell, a vomeronasal organ to detect pheromones, and a bicornuate uterus with an epitheliochorial placenta.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Slow loris" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Strepsirrhini" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sturgeon",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ray-finned fish",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Chondrosteans" },
		aliases = { "Acipenseridae" },
		summary = "**Sturgeon** are the 27 species of fish belonging to the family **Acipenseridae**. The earliest sturgeon fossils date to the [[Late Cretaceous]], and are descended from other, earlier acipenseriform fish, which date back to the [[Early Jurassic]] period, some 174 to 201 million years ago. They are one of two living families of the Acipenseriformes alongside paddlefish (Polyodontidae). Sturgeons are native to subtropical, temperate and sub-Arctic rivers, lakes and coastlines of [[Eurasia]] and [[North America]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Sturgeons are long-lived, late-maturing fishes with distinctive characteristics, such as a [[Fish fin|heterocercal caudal fin]] similar to those of [[Shark|sharks]], and an elongated, spindle-like body that is smooth-skinned, scaleless, and armored with five lateral rows of bony plates called scutes. Several species can grow quite large, typically ranging 7–12 ft in length. The largest sturgeon on record was a beluga female captured in the Volga Delta in 1827, measuring 7.2 m long and weighing 1,571 kg. The family is grouped into five genera: Acipenser, Huso, Scaphirhynchus, Sinosturio, and Pseudoscaphirhynchus. One species (S. dabryanus) may be extinct in the wild, and another (P. fedtschenkoi) may be entirely extinct. Beginning in 2011 H. naccarii was presumed extinct in the wild, but in 2022 was downgraded to [[Critically endangered|critically endangered]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Goldfish" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Acipenseridae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Subspecies",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Taxonomic rank subordinate to species",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'A **subspecies** is a [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomic]] rank below [[Species|species]], used for populations that live in different areas and vary in size, shape, or other physical characteristics ([[Morphology (biology)|morphology]]), but that can successfully interbreed. Not all species have subspecies, but for those that do there must be at least two. Subspecies is abbreviated as **subsp.** or **ssp.** and the singular and plural forms are the same ("the subspecies is" or "the subspecies are").',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In [[Zoology|zoology]], under the International Code of Zoological Nomenclature, the subspecies is the only taxonomic rank below that of species that can receive a name. In [[Botany|botany]] and [[Mycology|mycology]], under the International Code of Nomenclature for algae, fungi, and plants, other infraspecific ranks, such as [[Variety (botany)|variety]], may be named. In [[Bacteriology|bacteriology]] and virology, under standard bacterial nomenclature and virus nomenclature, there are recommendations but not strict requirements for recognizing other important infraspecific ranks. A taxonomist decides whether to recognize a subspecies. A common criterion for recognizing two distinct populations as subspecies rather than full species is their ability to interbreed even if some male offspring may be sterile. The differences between subspecies are usually less distinct than the differences between species.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sulidae",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Pelecanimorphae" },
		summary = "The [[Bird|bird]] [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Sulidae** comprises the gannets and boobies. Collectively called **sulids**, they are medium-large coastal [[Seabird|seabirds]] that plunge-dive for [[Fish|fish]] and similar [[Predation|prey]]. The 10 [[Species|species]] in this family are often considered congeneric in older sources, placing all in the [[Genus|genus]] Sula. However, Sula (true boobies) and Morus (gannets) can be distinguished via [[Morphology (biology)|morphological]], [[Ethology|behavioral]], and DNA sequence characters. Abbott's booby (Papasula) is given its own genus, as it stands apart from both in these respects. It appears to be a distinct and ancient lineage, maybe closer to the gannets than to the true boobies.",
		related = { "Stork", "Heron" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Sulidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sunbird",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Passerida" },
		aliases = { "Sunbirds", "Spiderhunters", "Nectariniidae" },
		summary = "**Sunbirds** and **spiderhunters** make up the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Nectariniidae** of [[Passerine|passerine]] birds. They are small, slender passerines from the [[Old World]], usually with downward-curved bills. Many are brightly coloured, often with iridescent feathers, particularly in the males. Many species also have especially long tail feathers. Their range extends through most of Africa to the Middle East, South Asia, South-east Asia and southern China, to Indonesia, New Guinea and northern Australia. Species diversity is highest in equatorial regions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are 152 species in 16 [[Genus|genera]]. Their family name is from most sunbirds feeding largely on [[Nectar|nectar]], but they will also catch [[Insect|insects]] and [[Spider|spiders]], especially when feeding their young. Flowers that prevent access to their nectar because of their shape (for example, very long and narrow flowers) are simply punctured at the base near the nectaries, from which the birds sip the nectar. [[Fruit]] is also part of the diet of some species. Their flight is fast and direct, thanks to their short wings. The sunbirds have counterparts in two very distantly related groups: the [[Hummingbird|hummingbirds]] of the Americas and the [[Honeyeater|honeyeaters]] of Australia. The resemblances are due to [[Convergent evolution|convergent evolution]] brought about by a similar nectar-feeding lifestyle. Some sunbird species can take nectar by hovering like a hummingbird, but they usually perch to feed.",
			},
		},
