	{
		title = "Swallowtail butterfly",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦋",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Butterflies of family Papilionidae",
		tags = { "Insects and other hexapods", "Lepidoptera" },
		aliases = { "Swallowtail butterflies", "Papilionidae" },
		summary = "**Swallowtail butterflies** are large, colorful [[Butterfly|butterflies]] in the family **Papilionidae**, and include over 550 [[Species|species]]. Though the majority are [[Tropics|tropical]], members of the family inhabit every [[Continent|continent]] except [[Antarctica]]. The family includes the largest butterflies in the world, the birdwing butterflies of the genus Ornithoptera.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Swallowtails have a number of distinctive features; for example, the papilionid [[Caterpillar|caterpillar]] bears a repugnatorial organ called the osmeterium on its prothorax. The osmeterium normally remains hidden, but when threatened, the larva turns it outward through a transverse dorsal groove by inflating it with fluid. The forked appearance in some of the swallowtails\' hindwings, which can be seen when the butterfly is resting with its wings spread, gave rise to the common name swallowtail. As for its formal name, [[Carl Linnaeus|Linnaeus]] chose Papilio for the [[Type genus|type genus]], as papilio is Latin for "butterfly". For the specific epithets of the genus, Linnaeus applied the names of Greek figures to the swallowtails. The [[Type species|type species]]: Papilio machaon honored Machaon, one of the sons of Asclepius, mentioned in the [[Iliad]]. Further, the species Papilio homerus is named after the Greek poet, [[Homer]].',
			},
		},
		related = { "Moth", "Lepidoptera" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Papilionidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Swan",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🦢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tribe of large water birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Anseriformes" },
		aliases = { "Swans", "Cygnus" },
		summary = "**Swans** are birds of the genus **Cygnus** within the family Anatidae. The swans' closest relatives include [[Goose|geese]] and [[Duck|ducks]]. Swans are grouped with the closely related geese in the subfamily Anserinae, forming the [[Tribe (taxonomy)|tribe]] **Cygnini**. Sometimes, they are considered a distinct subfamily, **Cygninae**. They are the largest waterfowl and are often among the largest flighted birds in their range.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are six living and many [[Extinction|extinct]] species of swan; in addition, there is a species known as the coscoroba swan, which is no longer considered one of the **true swans**. Swans usually mate for life, although separation sometimes occurs, particularly following nesting failure, and if a mate dies, the remaining swan will take up with another. The number of [[Bird egg|eggs]] in each clutch ranges from three to eight.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cockatoo" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Cygnus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Swift (bird)",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of birds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Apodiformes" },
		aliases = { "Apodidae", "Swifts" },
		summary = "The **Apodidae**, or **swifts**, form a family of highly aerial [[Bird|birds]]. They are superficially similar to [[Swallow|swallows]] but are not closely related to any [[Passerine|passerine]] species. Swifts are placed in the order Apodiformes along with [[Hummingbird|hummingbirds]]. The treeswifts are closely related to the true swifts but form a separate family, the Hemiprocnidae.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Resemblances between swifts and swallows are due to [[Convergent evolution|convergent evolution]], reflecting similar life styles based on catching [[Insect|insects]] in flight. The family name, Apodidae, is derived from the [[Greek language|Greek]] ἄ (ápous), meaning "footless", a reference to the small, weak legs of these most aerial of birds. The tradition of depicting swifts without feet continued into the Middle Ages, as seen in the heraldic martlet.',
			},
