	{
		title = "Swordfish",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fish which has a long, pointed bill",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		aliases = { "Xiphias gladius", "Broadbill" },
		summary = "The **swordfish** (**Xiphias gladius**), also known as the **broadbill** in some countries, are large, highly migratory predatory fish characterized by a long, flat, sword-like, pointed bill. They are the sole member of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Xiphiidae**. They are a popular sport fish of the billfish category. Swordfish are elongated, round-bodied, and lose all teeth and scales by adulthood. These fish are found widely in tropical and temperate parts of the Atlantic, [[Pacific Ocean|Pacific]], and [[Indian Ocean|Indian Oceans]], and can typically be found from near the surface to a depth of 550 m, and exceptionally up to depths of 2,878 m. They commonly reach 3 m in length, and the maximum reported is 4.55 m in length and 650 kg in weight.",
		related = { "Gobioidei", "Mahi-mahi", "Wrasse" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Xiphias gladius" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Near Threatened" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Synapsida",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Clade of tetrapods",
		tags = { "Reptiles", "Dinosaurs and prehistoric reptiles" },
		aliases = { "Synapsid" },
		summary = "**Synapsida** is a diverse group of [[Tetrapod|tetrapod]] [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]] that includes all [[Mammal|mammals]] and their extinct relatives. It is one of the two major [[Clade|clades]] of the group Amniota, the other being the more diverse group Sauropsida (which includes all extant [[Reptile|reptiles]] and [[Bird|birds]]). **Synapsids** have a single lower temporal fenestra, an opening low in the skull roof behind each eye socket. These openings create a bony arch that allows for the attachment of larger jaw muscles and a more efficient bite. While previously argued to be distinctive to synapsids, single temporal fenestrae are also found in many primitive sauropsids.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'It has recently been argued that the last common ancestor of synapsids and sauropsids shared a single lower temporal fenestra. The [[Basal (phylogenetics)|basal]] amniotes (reptiliomorphs) from which synapsids evolved were historically simply called "reptiles". Therefore, non-mammalian synapsids were then described as **mammal-like reptiles** in classical systematics, and non-therapsid synapsids were also referred to as **pelycosaurs** or **pelycosaur-grade synapsids**. These [[Paraphyly|paraphyletic]] terms have fallen out of favor and are only used informally (if at all) in modern literature, as it is now known that all extant reptiles are more closely related to each other and birds than to synapsids, so the word "reptile" has been re-defined to mean only members of Sauropsida or even just an under-clade thereof.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Iguanodon" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Synapsida" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tabanidae",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of insects",
		tags = { "Insects and other hexapods", "Flies (Diptera)" },
		aliases = { "Horse flies", "Deer flies" },
		summary = "**Horse flies** and **deer flies** are true flies in the family **Tabanidae** in the [[Insect|insect]] order Diptera. The adults are often large and agile in flight. They prefer to fly in sunlight, avoiding dark and shady areas, and are inactive at night. They are found all over the world except for some islands and the polar regions (Hawaii, Greenland, Iceland). Both horse flies and botflies (Oestridae) are sometimes referred to as **gadflies**. Contrary to popular belief, horse flies cannot see [[Infrared|infrared light]] or otherwise detect heat at a distance.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Adult horse flies feed on nectar and plant exudates; males have weak mouthparts, but females have mouthparts strong enough to puncture the skin of large animals. The larvae are predaceous and grow in semiaquatic habitats. They can reduce growth rates in cattle and lower the milk output of cows if suitable shelters are not provided. Horse flies have long appeared in literature. The father of [[Greek tragedy]], [[Aeschylus]], depicts a gadfly driving Io to madness in Prometheus Bound through its relentless pursuit. The philosopher [[Socrates]] later adopted the gadfly as an ironic mascot for his persistent questioning of those who spoke with him.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Drosophila melanogaster", "Housefly" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Tabanidae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tail",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Distinct, flexible appendage to the torso of an animal",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **tail** is the elongated section at the rear end of a bilaterian [[Animal|animal]]'s body; in general, the term refers to a distinct, flexible appendage extending backwards from the midline of the torso. In [[Vertebrate|vertebrate]] animals that [[Evolution|evolved]] to lose their tails (e.g. [[Frog|frogs]] and hominid [[Primate|primates]]), the coccyx is the homologous vestigial of the tail. While tails are primarily considered a feature of vertebrates, some [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]] such as [[Scorpion|scorpions]] and [[Springtail|springtails]], as well as snails and slugs, have tail-like appendages that are also referred to as tails.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Tail-shaped objects are sometimes referred to as "caudate" (e.g. caudate lobe, caudate nucleus), and the body part associated with or proximal to the tail are given the adjective "caudal" (which is considered a more precise anatomical terminology).',
			},
		},
		related = { "Epoch (astronomy)" },
	},
	{
		title = "Tapir",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Herbivorous mammal",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Perissodactyla" },
		aliases = { "Tapirs", "Tapiridae" },
		summary = "**Tapirs** are large [[Herbivore|herbivorous]] [[Mammal|mammals]] belonging to the family **Tapiridae**. They are similar in shape to a pig, with a short, prehensile nose trunk ([[Proboscis|proboscis]]). Tapirs inhabit jungle and forest regions of [[South America|South]] and [[Central America]] and [[Southeast Asia]]. They are one of three extant branches of [[Perissodactyla]] (odd-toed ungulates), alongside [[Equidae|equines]] and rhinoceroses. Only a single [[Genus|genus]], Tapirus, is extant. Tapirs migrated into South America from North America during the [[Pleistocene]] epoch after the formation of the Isthmus of Panama as part of the Great American Interchange.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Tapirs were present across North America, but became extinct in the region at the end of the [[Late Pleistocene]], around 12,000 years ago.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Tapiridae" },
		},
