	{
		title = "Tasmanian devil",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Australian carnivorous marsupial",
		tags = { "Mammals", "Marsupials" },
		aliases = { "Sarcophilus harrisii" },
		summary = "The **Tasmanian devil** (**Sarcophilus harrisii**; palawa kani: purinina) is a carnivorous [[Marsupial|marsupial]] of the family Dasyuridae. It was formerly present across mainland Australia, but became extinct there around 3,500 years ago; it is now confined to the island of [[Tasmania]]. The size of a small dog, the Tasmanian devil became the largest carnivorous marsupial in the world following the [[Extinction|extinction]] of the thylacine in 1936. It is related to quolls, and distantly related to the thylacine. It is characterised by its stocky and muscular build, black fur, pungent odour, extremely loud screech, keen sense of smell, and ferocity when feeding.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Tasmanian devil's large head and neck allow it to generate among the strongest bites per unit body mass of any extant predatory land mammal. It hunts prey and scavenges on [[Carrion|carrion]]. Although devils are usually solitary, they sometimes eat and defecate together in a communal location. Unlike most other dasyurids, the devil [[Thermoregulation|thermoregulates]] effectively, and is active during the middle of the day without overheating. Despite its rotund appearance, it is capable of surprising speed and endurance, and can climb trees and swim across rivers. Devils are not monogamous. Males fight one another for females, and guard their partners to prevent female infidelity. Females can ovulate three times in as many weeks during the mating season, and 80% of two-year-old females are seen to be pregnant during the annual mating season.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Dasyuromorphia", "Peramelemorphia" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Sarcophilus harrisii" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Endangered" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Termite",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐞",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Social insects related to cockroaches",
		tags = { "Arthropods", "Insects and other hexapods" },
		aliases = { "Termites" },
		summary = '**Termites** are a group of [[Eusociality|eusocial]] insects which [[Detritivore|consume a variety of decaying plant material]], generally in the form of [[Wood|wood]], leaf litter, and soil humus. They are distinguished by their beaded (moniliform) [[Antenna (zoology)|antennae]] and the soft-bodied, unpigmented worker caste for which they have been commonly termed "**white ants**"; however, they are not [[Ant|ants]] but highly derived [[Cockroach|cockroaches]]; they are genetically closer to some cockroach groups than these are to other cockroaches. About 2,997 extant [[Species|species]] are currently described, 2,125 of which are members of the family Termitidae.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Termites comprise the [[Order (taxonomy)|infraorder]] **Isoptera**, or alternatively the [[Taxonomic rank|epifamily]] **Termitoidae**, within the order Blattodea (the cockroaches). Termites were once classified in a separate [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] from cockroaches, but [[Phylogenetics|phylogenetic]] studies in the 21st century indicate that they evolved from cockroaches, as they are deeply nested within the group ([[Clade|clade]]), and the [[Sister group|sister group]] to wood-eating cockroaches of the genus Cryptocercus. Previous estimates suggested the divergence took place during the [[Jurassic]] or [[Triassic]]. More recent estimates suggest that they have an origin during the [[Late Jurassic]], with later evidence revealing fossils from this time period. Termite colonies are commonly described as superorganisms due to the collective behaviors of the individuals which form a self-governing entity: the colony itself.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Earwig", "Phasmatodea" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Isoptera" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tern",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Subfamily of seabirds",
		tags = { "Birds", "Charadriiformes" },
		aliases = { "Terns", "Sterninae" },
		summary = "**Terns** are [[Seabird|seabirds]] in the subfamily **Sterninae** of the wider [[Gull|gull]] and tern [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Laridae. They have a worldwide distribution and are normally found near the [[Ocean|sea]], [[River|rivers]], or [[Wetland|wetlands]]. Terns are treated in eleven [[Genus|genera]] in a subgroup of the family Laridae, which also includes several genera of [[Gull|gulls]] and the skimmers (Rynchops). They are slender, lightly built birds with long, forked tails, narrow wings, long bills, and relatively short legs. Most species are pale grey above and white below with a contrasting black cap to the head, but the marsh terns, the black-bellied tern, the Inca tern, and some noddies have dark body [[Plumage|plumage]] for at least part of the year.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The sexes are identical in appearance, but young birds are readily distinguishable from adults. Terns have a non-breeding plumage, which usually involves a white forehead and much-reduced black cap. The Chinese crested tern is [[Critically endangered|critically endangered]] and three other species are classed as [[Endangered species|endangered]]. International agreements provide a measure of protection, but adults and eggs of some species are still used for food in the tropics.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Ara (bird)", "Sandpiper" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Sterninae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Terrestrial animal",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Animals living on land",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Terrestrial animals" },
		summary = "**Terrestrial animals** are [[Animal|animals]] that live predominantly or entirely on [[Land|land]] (e.g., [[Cat|cats]], [[Chicken|chickens]], [[Ant|ants]], most [[Spider|spiders]]), as compared with [[Aquatic animal|aquatic animals]] (e.g., [[Fish|fish]], [[Blue whale|whales]], [[Octopus|octopuses]], [[Lobster|lobsters]], etc.), who live predominantly or entirely in bodies of water; and [[Semiaquatic|semiaquatic]] animals (e.g., crocodilians, [[Pinniped|seals]], [[Platypus|platypus]] and most [[Amphibian|amphibians]]), who inhabit coastal, riparian or [[Wetland|wetland]] areas and rely on both aquatic and terrestrial [[Habitat|habitats]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While most [[Insect|insects]] (who constitute over half of all known [[Species|species]] in the animal [[Kingdom (taxonomy)|kingdom]]) are terrestrial, some groups, such as mosquitoes and dragonflies, spend their [[Egg|egg]] and [[Larva|larval]] [[Biological life cycle|stages]] in water but emerge as fully terrestrial adults ([[Imago|imagos]]) after completing [[Metamorphosis|metamorphosis]]. Terrestrial animals conduct [[Respiration (physiology)|respiratory]] gas exchange directly with the [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]], typically via specialized respiratory [[Organ (biology)|organs]] known as [[Lung|lungs]], or via cutaneous respiration across the [[Skin|skin]]. Without the [[Buoyancy|buoyancy]] of an aqueous environment to support their [[Weight|weight]], they have [[Evolution|evolved]] robust [[Skeleton|skeletons]] that can hold up their body shape, as well as powerful appendages known as legs or [[Limb (anatomy)|limbs]] to facilitate terrestrial locomotion, although some perform limbless locomotion using body surface projections such as scales and setae.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Terrier",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐕",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Dog type",
		tags = { "Cats", "Dogs" },
		summary = "**Terrier** is a type of dog originally bred to hunt vermin. A terrier is a [[Dog|dog]] of any one of many breeds or landraces of the terrier type, which are typically small, wiry, game, and fearless. There are five different groups of terrier, with each group having different shapes and sizes.",
		related = { "Arabian horse", "Mule" },
	},
	{
		title = "Territory (animal)",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Area a wild animal consistently defends",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Ethology|ethology]], **territory** is the sociographical area that an [[Animal|animal]] consistently defends against conspecific [[Competition (biology)|competition]] (or, occasionally, against animals of other [[Species|species]]) using agonistic behaviors or (less commonly) real physical [[Aggression|aggression]]. Animals that actively defend territories in this way are referred to as being **territorial** or displaying **territorialism**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Territoriality is only shown by a minority of species. More commonly, an individual or a group of animals occupies an area that it habitually uses but does not necessarily defend; this is called its home range. The home ranges of different groups of animals often overlap, and in these overlap areas the groups tend to avoid each other rather than seeking to confront and expel each other. Within the home range there may be a core area that no other individual group uses, but, again, this is as a result of avoidance. Animals defend their territories to protect access to food, mates and nesting sites. Some species defend small sites while others control vast territories. Territorial dynamics may also change seasonally, often intensifying during breeding periods.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tetraodontidae",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of pufferfish",
		tags = { "Fishes", "Acanthomorpha" },
		summary = "**Tetraodontidae** is a [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of marine and freshwater fish in the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] Tetraodontiformes. The family includes many familiar species variously called **pufferfishes**, **puffers**, **balloonfishes**, **blowfishes**, **blowers**, **blowies**, **bubblefishes**, **globefishes**, **swellfishes**, **toadfishes**, **toadies**, **botetes**, **toadle**, **honey toads**, **sugar toads**, and **sea squabs**. They are [[Morphology (biology)|morphologically]] similar to the closely related porcupinefish, which have large external spines (unlike the thinner, hidden spines of the Tetraodontidae, which are only visible when the fish have puffed up).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The family name comes from [[Ancient Greek]] - (tetra-), meaning "four", and ὀ (odoús), meaning "tooth", referring to the four teeth of the type genus Tetraodon. Other pufferfish species with nontoxic flesh, such as the northern puffer, Sphoeroides maculatus, of the Chesapeake Bay, are considered a delicacy elsewhere.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Guppy", "Serranidae" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Tetraodontidae" },
		},
