	{
		title = "Theropoda",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🦕",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Extant clade of dinosaurs",
		tags = { "Reptiles", "Dinosaurs and prehistoric reptiles" },
		summary = "Theropods, both extant and extinct, are characterized by hollow bones and three toes and claws on each limb. They are generally classed as saurischian dinosaurs, placing them closer to sauropodomorphs than to ornithischians. They were ancestrally [[Carnivore|carnivorous]], although a number of theropod groups evolved to become [[Herbivore|herbivores]] and [[Omnivore|omnivores]]. Many members of the subgroup Coelurosauria were covered with feathers, and it is possible that they were also present in other theropods. In the [[Jurassic]], [[Bird|birds]] evolved from small specialized coelurosaurian theropods, to about 11,000 living species, making theropods the only group of dinosaurs that remain.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Theropods first appeared during the Carnian age of the [[Late Triassic]] [[Geologic time scale|period]] 231.4 million years ago ([[Year|Ma]]) and included the majority of large terrestrial carnivores from the [[Early Jurassic]] until the end of the [[Cretaceous]], about 66 Ma, including the largest terrestrial carnivorous animals ever, such as [[Tyrannosaurus]] and Giganotosaurus, though non-avian theropods exhibited considerable size diversity, with some non-avian theropods like scansoriopterygids being no bigger than small birds.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Velociraptor" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Theropoda" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Thoroughbred",
		category = "Animal",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Horse breed developed for racing",
		tags = { "Cats", "Horses" },
		summary = 'The **Thoroughbred** is a horse breed developed for horse racing. Although the word thoroughbred is sometimes used to refer to any breed of purebred horse, it technically refers only to the Thoroughbred breed. Thoroughbreds are considered "hot-blooded" horses that are known for their agility, speed, and spirit.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Thoroughbred was developed in 17th- and 18th-century England, when native mares were [[Crossbreed|crossbred]] with imported stallions of [[Arabian horse|Arabian]], Barb, and Turkoman breeding. All modern Thoroughbreds can trace their pedigrees to three stallions originally imported into England in the 17th and 18th centuries, and to a larger number of foundation mares of mostly English breeding. During the 18th and 19th centuries, the Thoroughbred breed spread throughout the world; they were imported into North America starting in 1730 and into Australia, Europe, Japan and South America during the 19th century. Millions of Thoroughbreds exist today, and around 100,000 foals are registered each year worldwide. Thoroughbreds are used mainly for racing, but are also bred for other riding disciplines such as show jumping, combined training, dressage, [[Polo|polo]], and fox hunting.",
			},
		},
		related = { "American Brahman", "Friesian horse", "Hound", "Spitz" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Country", "England" },
		},
	},
	{
