	{
		title = "Mesoamerican architecture",
		category = "Art",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Building traditions of pre-Columbian Mesoamerica",
		tags = { "Architecture", "Styles" },
		summary = "**Mesoamerican architecture** is the set of [[Architecture|architectural]] traditions produced by [[Pre-Columbian era|pre-Columbian]] cultures and civilizations of [[Mesoamerica]], traditions which are best known in the form of public, ceremonial and urban monumental buildings and structures. The distinctive features of Mesoamerican architecture encompass a number of different regional and historical styles, which however are significantly interrelated. These styles developed throughout the different phases of Mesoamerican history as a result of the intensive cultural exchange between the different cultures of the Mesoamerican culture area through thousands of years.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Mesoamerican architecture is mostly noted for its pyramids, which are the largest such structures outside of [[Ancient Egypt]]. One interesting and widely researched topic is the relation between [[Cosmology|cosmovision]], religion, [[Geography|geography]], and [[Architecture|architecture]] in Mesoamerica. Much seems to suggest that many traits of Mesoamerican architecture were governed by religious and mythological ideas. For example, the layout of most Mesoamerican cities seem to be influenced by the cardinal directions and their [[Mythology|mythological]] and [[Symbol|symbolic]] meanings in Mesoamerican culture. Another part of Mesoamerican architecture is its iconography. The monumental architecture of Mesoamerica was decorated with images of religious and cultural significance, and also in many cases with writing in some of the Mesoamerican writing systems.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Chinese architecture", "Gothic architecture" },
