	{
		title = "Aeneid",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Latin epic poem by Virgil",
		tags = { "Specific works of poetry", "Poetic works of antiquity" },
		summary = "The **Aeneid** is a Latin [[Epic poetry|epic poem]] that tells the [[Mythology|legendary]] story of Aeneas, a [[Troy|Trojan]] who fled the [[Trojan War|fall of Troy]] and travelled to Italy, where he became the ancestor of the [[Roman Empire|Romans]]. Written by the Roman poet [[Virgil]] between 29 and 19 BC, the Aeneid comprises 9,896 lines in [[Dactylic hexameter|dactylic hexameter]]. The first six of its twelve books tell the story of Aeneas' wanderings from Troy to Italy, and the latter six tell of the Trojans' ultimately victorious war upon the Latins, under whose name Aeneas and his Trojan followers are destined to be subsumed.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The hero Aeneas was already known to Graeco-Roman legend and myth, having been a character in the [[Iliad]]. Virgil took the disconnected tales of Aeneas' wanderings, his vague association with the foundation of [[Roman Empire|Rome]], and his description as a personage of no fixed characteristics other than a scrupulous pietas, and fashioned the Aeneid into a compelling founding myth or national epic that tied Rome to the legends of Troy, explained the Punic Wars, glorified traditional Roman virtues, and legitimised the [[Julio-Claudian dynasty]] as descendants of the founders, heroes, and gods of Rome and Troy. The Aeneid is widely regarded as Virgil's masterpiece and one of the greatest works of [[Latin literature]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Masnavi" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Author", "[[Virgil]]" },
			{ "First held", "19 BC" },
			{ "Country", "[[Roman Republic]], [[Roman Empire]]" },
			{ "Language", "[[Classical Latin]]" },
			{ "Genre", "[[Epic poetry|Epic poem]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Aesop's Fables",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📚",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Collection of fables credited to Aesop",
		tags = { "Specific works of fiction", "Fiction of antiquity" },
		summary = "Of varied and unclear origins, the stories associated with his name have descended to modern times through several sources and continue to be reinterpreted in different verbal [[Register (sociolinguistics)|registers]] and in popular as well as artistic media.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The fables were part of [[Oral tradition|oral tradition]] and were not collected until about three centuries after Aesop's death. By that time, a variety of other stories, jokes and proverbs were being ascribed to him, although some of that material was from sources earlier than him or came from beyond the Greek cultural sphere. The process of inclusion has continued until the present, with some of the fables unrecorded before the [[Late Middle Ages]] and others arriving from outside Europe. Manuscripts in [[Latin]] and [[Greek language|Greek]] were important avenues of transmission, although poetical treatments in European vernaculars eventually formed another. On the arrival of [[Printing|printing]], collections of Aesop's fables were among the earliest books in a variety of languages. Through the means of later collections, and translations or adaptations of them, Aesop's reputation as a fabulist was transmitted throughout the world.",
			},
		},
		related = { "The Hunchback of Notre-Dame", "One Hundred Years of Solitude" },
	},
	{
		title = "African literature",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📚",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Literature originating from Africa",
		tags = { "Literature", "Literatures by language and area" },
		aliases = { "Orature" },
		summary = '**African literature ** is [[Literature|literature]] from [[Africa]], either [[Oral literature|oral]] ("**orature**") or written in African and Afro-Asiatic languages. Examples of pre-colonial African literature can be traced back to at least the fourth century AD. The best-known is the Kebra Negast, or "Book of Kings", from the 14th century AD. Another well-known book is the Garima Gospels, one of the oldest known surviving bibles in the world, written in Ge\'ez around 500 AD.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Among the first pieces of African literature to receive significant worldwide critical acclaim was [[Things Fall Apart]], by [[Chinua Achebe]], published in 1958. African literature in the late colonial period increasingly features themes of [[Liberty|liberation]] and independence. Post-colonial literature has become increasingly diverse, with some writers returning to their native languages. Common themes include the clash between past and present, tradition and modernity, self and community, and politics and development. Overall, female writers are today far better represented in African literature than they were before independence. The internet has also changed the landscape of African literature, leading to the rise of digital reading and publishing platforms such as OkadaBooks.",
			},
		},
		related = { "English literature", "Ancient Greek literature" },
	},
}
]]></string>
