	{
		title = "Latin American literature",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📚",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Oral and written tradition",
		tags = { "Literature", "Literatures by language and area" },
		summary = "**Latin American literature** consists of the [[Oral literature|oral]] and written [[Literature|literature]] of [[Latin America]] in several languages, particularly in Spanish, Portuguese, and the [[Indigenous languages of the Americas|indigenous languages of Latin America]]. Latin American literature rose to particular prominence globally during the second half of the 20th century, largely due to the international success of the style known as [[Magical realism|magical realism]]. As such, the region's literature is often associated solely with this style, with the 20th century literary movement known as [[Latin American Boom]], and with its most famous exponent, [[Gabriel García Márquez]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Latin American literature has a rich and complex tradition of literary production that dates back many centuries.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Donald Duck" },
	},
	{
		title = "Latin literature",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📚",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "Literature", "Literatures by language and area" },
		summary = "**Latin literature** includes the essays, histories, poems, plays, and other writings written in the [[Latin]] language. The beginning of formal Latin literature dates to 240 BC, when the first stage play in Latin was performed in Rome. Latin literature flourished for the next six centuries. The classical era of Latin literature can be roughly divided into several periods: early Latin literature, the golden age, the imperial period and Late Antiquity.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Latin was the language of the ancient Romans as well as being the [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] of Western and Central Europe throughout the [[Middle Ages]]. Latin literature features the work of Roman authors, such as [[Cicero]], [[Virgil]], [[Ovid]] and [[Horace]], but also includes the work of European writers after the fall of the Empire, from religious writers like [[Thomas Aquinas]] (1225–1274) to secular writers like [[Francis Bacon]] (1561–1626), [[Baruch Spinoza]] (1632–1677), and [[Isaac Newton]] (1642–1727).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Leaves of Grass",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Expansive Walt Whitman poetry collection",
		tags = { "Specific works of poetry", "Poetic works of modern era" },
		summary = "**Leaves of Grass** is a poetry collection by American poet [[Walt Whitman]]. After self-publishing it in 1855, he spent most of his professional life writing, revising, and expanding the collection until his death in 1892. Either six or nine separate editions of the book were produced, depending on how one defines a new edition. The continual modifications to Leaves of Grass resulted in vastly different copies of it circulating in Whitman's lifetime. The first edition was a slim tract of twelve poems, and the last was a compilation of over 400 poems.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The book represents a celebration of Whitman's philosophy of life and humanity in which he praises nature and the individual's role in it. He catalogues the expansiveness of American democracy. Rather than dwell on religious or spiritual themes, he focuses primarily on the body and the material world. With very few exceptions, Whitman's poems do not rhyme or follow conventional rules for meter and line length. Leaves of Grass was notable for its discussion of delight in sensual pleasures at a time when such candid displays were considered immoral. Over the decades, however, the collection has infiltrated popular culture and become recognized as one of the central works of American poetry.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Paradise Lost", "The Raven" },
		infobox = {
