	{
		title = "Midnight's Children",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧒",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "1981 novel by Salman Rushdie",
		tags = { "Specific works of fiction", "Fiction of modern era" },
		summary = "**Midnight's Children** is the second novel by Indian-British writer Salman Rushdie, published in 1981 by Jonathan Cape with cover design by Bill Botten, about India's transition from [[British Raj|British colonial rule]] to [[Indian independence movement|independence]] and partition. It is a postcolonial, [[Postmodern literature|postmodern]] and magical realist story told by its chief protagonist, Saleem Sinai, set in the context of historical events. The style of preserving history with fictional accounts is self-reflexive. In the way it interweaves a personal with a national biography it has similarities to Günter Grass's The Tin Drum.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Midnight's Children sold over one million copies in the UK alone and won the [[Booker Prize]] and James Tait Black Memorial Prize in 1981. It was also awarded the special Booker of Bookers prize in 1993, and the Best of the Booker in 2008, to celebrate the Booker Prize's 25th and 40th anniversaries. In 2003 the novel appeared at number 100 on the [[BBC]]'s [[The Big Read]] poll which determined the UK's \"best-loved novels\" of all time.",
