	{
		title = "Narration",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📚",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Written or spoken commentary",
		tags = { "Literature", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Narration** is the use of a written or spoken commentary to [[Storytelling|convey]] a [[Narrative|story]] to an [[Audience|audience]]. Narration is conveyed by a **narrator**: a specific person, or unspecified literary voice, developed by the creator of the story to deliver information to the audience, particularly about the plot: the series of events. Narration is a required element of all written stories ([[Book|novels]], [[Short story|short stories]], poems, memoirs, etc.), presenting the story in its entirety. It is optional in most other storytelling formats, such as films, plays, television shows and video games, in which the story can be conveyed through other means, like dialogue between characters or visual action.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The **narrative mode**, which is sometimes also used as a synonym for narrative technique, encompasses the set of choices through which the creator of the story develops their narrator and narration: Narrative point of view, perspective, or voice: the choice of grammatical person used by the narrator to establish whether or not the narrator and the audience are participants in the story; also, this includes the scope of the information or knowledge that the narrator presents Narrative tense: the choice of either the past or present [[Grammatical tense|grammatical tense]] to establish either the prior completion or current immediacy of the plot Narrative technique: any of the various other methods chosen to help narrate a story, such as establishing the story's setting (location in time and space), developing characters, exploring themes (main ideas or topics), structuring the plot, intentionally expressing certain details but not others, following or subverting genre norms, employing certain linguistic styles and using various other storytelling devices.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Poetry" },
	},
	{
		title = "Narrative",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "📚",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Account that presents connected events",
		tags = { "Literature", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Story", "Tale" },
		summary = "A **narrative**, **story**, or **tale** is any account of a series of related events or experiences, whether [[Non-fiction|non-fictional]] (memoir, [[Biography|biography]], news report, documentary, [[Travel literature|travelogue]], etc.) or [[Fiction|fictional]] ([[Fairy tale|fairy tale]], [[Fable|fable]], [[Mythology|legend]], [[Thriller (genre)|thriller]], [[Book|novel]], etc.). Narratives can be presented through a sequence of written or spoken words, through still or moving images, or through any combination of these.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Narrative is expressed in all mediums of human creativity, art, and entertainment, including [[Public speaking|speech]] ([[Oral literature|oral literature]]), [[Literature|literature]], [[Theatre|theatre]], [[Dance|dance]], [[Music|music]] and [[Song|song]], [[Comics|comics]], [[Journalism|journalism]], [[Animation|animation]], [[Video|video]] (including [[Film|film]] and [[Television|television]]), [[Video game|video games]], radio, [[Game|structured]] and [[Play (activity)|unstructured recreation]], and potentially even purely [[Visual arts|visual arts]] like [[Painting|painting]], [[Sculpture|sculpture]], [[Drawing|drawing]], and [[Photography|photography]], as long as a sequence of events is presented. The social and cultural activity of humans sharing narratives is called [[Storytelling|storytelling]], the vast majority of which has taken the form of oral storytelling. Since the rise of literate societies however, many narratives have been additionally recorded, created, or otherwise passed down in [[Literature|written form]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Nibelungenlied",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Middle High German epic poem from around 1200",
		tags = { "Specific works of poetry", "Poetic works of antiquity" },
		aliases = { "The Song of the Nibelungs" },
		summary = "The, translated as **The Song of the Nibelungs**, is an [[Epic poetry|epic poem]] written around 1200 in Middle High German. Its anonymous poet was likely from the region of Passau. The is based on an oral tradition of Germanic heroic legend that has some of its origin in historic events and individuals of the 5th and 6th centuries and that spread throughout almost all of [[Germanic languages|Germanic-speaking]] Europe. Scandinavian parallels to the German poem are found especially in the heroic lays of the [[Poetic Edda]] and in the Völsunga saga.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The poem is split into two parts. In the first part, the prince Siegfried comes to Worms to acquire the hand of the [[Burgundians|Burgundian]] princess Kriemhild from her brother King Gunther. Gunther agrees to let Siegfried marry Kriemhild if Siegfried helps Gunther acquire the warrior-queen Brünhild as his wife. In the second part, the widow Kriemhild is married to Etzel, king of the [[Huns]]. Her revenge results in the death of all the Burgundians who came to Etzel's court as well as the destruction of Etzel's kingdom and the death of Kriemhild herself. The was the first heroic epic put into writing in the German vernacular, helping to found a larger genre of written heroic poetry there. The poem's tragedy appears to have bothered its medieval audience, and very early on a sequel was written, the, which made the tragedy less final. The poem was forgotten after around 1500 but was rediscovered in 1755.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Epic of Gilgamesh" },
	},
	{
		title = "Nicomachean Ethics",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📚",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Work on ethics by Aristotle",
		tags = { "Specific works of nonfiction", "Nonfiction of antiquity" },
		summary = "The **Nicomachean Ethics** is [[Aristotle]]'s best-known work on [[Ethics|ethics]]: the science of the good for human life, that which is [[Teleology|the goal or end]] at which all our actions aim. It consists of ten sections, referred to as books, and is closely related to Aristotle's Eudemian Ethics. The work is essential for the interpretation of Aristotelian ethics.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The text centers upon the question of how to best live, a theme previously explored in the works of [[Plato]], Aristotle's friend and teacher. In Aristotle's [[Metaphysics (Aristotle)|Metaphysics]], he describes how [[Socrates]], the friend and teacher of Plato, turned philosophy to human questions, whereas [[Pre-Socratic philosophy|pre-Socratic philosophy]] had only been theoretical and concerned with [[Natural science|natural science]]. Ethics, Aristotle claimed, is practical rather than [[Theory|theoretical]], in the Aristotelian senses of these terms. It is not merely an investigation into what good consists of, but it aims to be of practical help in achieving the good. It is connected to another of Aristotle's practical works, Politics, which reflects a similar goal: for people to become good, through the creation and maintenance of social institutions.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Republic (Plato)", "The Book of Healing" },
	},
	{
		title = "Nineteen Eighty-Four",
		category = "Book",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📖",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "1949 dystopian novel by George Orwell",
		tags = { "Specific works of fiction", "Fiction of modern era" },
		aliases = { "1984" },
		summary = "**Nineteen Eighty-Four** (also published as **1984**) is a dystopian and [[Speculative fiction|speculative fiction]] novel by the English writer [[George Orwell]]. It was published on 8 June 1949 by Secker & Warburg as Orwell's ninth and final completed book. Thematically, it centres on [[Totalitarianism|totalitarianism]], mass surveillance and repressive regimentation of people and behaviours. Nineteen Eighty-Four has often been regarded as a classic and has been acknowledged for its impact on twentieth-century literature.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The story takes place in a fictional future. The year is believed to be 1984, but this is uncertain. Much of the world is in perpetual war. Great Britain, now known as Airstrip One, has become a province of the totalitarian superstate Oceania, which is led by Big Brother, a dictatorial leader supported by an intense cult of personality manufactured by the Party\'s Thought Police. The Party engages in omnipresent government surveillance and, through the Ministry of Truth, historical negationism and constant propaganda to persecute individuality and independent thinking. Orwell described his book as a "[[Satire|satire]]", and a display of the "perversions to which a [[Planned economy|centralised economy]] is liable", while also stating he believed "that something resembling it could arrive". The novel examines the role of truth and facts within societies and the ways in which they can be manipulated.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Investiture of the Gods", "On the Road", "The Little Prince" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Author", "[[George Orwell]]" },
			{ "Country", "United Kingdom" },
