		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Capital and largest city in Northern Ireland",
		tags = { "Western Europe", "United Kingdom" },
		summary = "**Belfast** is the capital, largest city and main port of [[Northern Ireland]]. It stands on the banks of the river Lagan, and is connected to the open sea through Belfast Lough and the North Channel. The city is the most populous in Northern Ireland and the second-largest, after [[Dublin]], on the island of [[Ireland]], with a recorded population in 2021 of 345,418 and of 704,406 for the greater metropolitan area.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While chartered as an English settlement in 1613, the town's early growth was driven by an influx of Scottish Presbyterians. Their descendants' disaffection with [[Kingdom of Ireland|Ireland]]'s Anglican establishment contributed to the rebellion of 1798 and to the 1801 union with [[Kingdom of Great Britain|Great Britain]] that followed. Access to British capital and markets were keys to industrial growth. When granted [[City status in the United Kingdom|city status]] in 1888, Belfast was the world's largest centre of [[Linen|linen]] manufacture, and by the 1900s its shipyards were building up to a quarter of all United Kingdom tonnage. New industry, which drew large numbers of Catholics from rural districts, was accompanied by growing sectarian tensions in what had been a largely Protestant town. A legacy of conflict is the barrier-reinforced separation of Protestant and Catholic [[Working class|working-class]] districts.",
			},
		},
