	{
		title = "Daegu",
		category = "City",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "City in South Korea",
		tags = { "Asia", "Eastern Asia" },
		aliases = { "Taegu", "Daegu Metropolitan City" },
		summary = "**Daegu**, formerly spelled **Taegu** and officially **Daegu Metropolitan City**, is a city in southeastern [[South Korea]]. It is the third-largest urban agglomeration in South Korea after [[Seoul]] and [[Busan]]; the fourth-largest metropolitan city in the nation with over 2.3 million residents; and the second-largest city after Busan in the Yeongnam region in southeastern South Korea. Daegu and the surrounding North Gyeongsang Province are often referred to as Daegu-Gyeongbuk, with a total population of over 5 million.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Daegu is located in south-eastern Korea about 80 km from the coast, near the Geumho River and its mainstream, Nakdong River in Gyeongsang Province. The Daegu basin is the central plain of the Yeongnam region. In ancient times, the Daegu area was part of the proto-kingdom Jinhan. Subsequently, Daegu came under the control of the Silla Kingdom, which unified the Korean Peninsula. During the [[Joseon]] period, the city was the capital of Gyeongsang Province, one of the traditional eight provinces of the country. Daegu was an economic motor of Korea during the 1960s–1980s period. The humid subtropical climate of Daegu is ideal for producing high-quality apples, thus the nickname, "Apple City". Daegu is also known as "Textile City", as textiles used to be the main industry of the city.',
			},
		},
