		description = "Capital and largest city of Angola",
		tags = { "Africa", "Central Africa" },
		summary = "**Luanda** is the [[Capital city|capital]] and largest city of [[Angola]]. It is Angola's primary [[Port|port]], and its major industrial, cultural and urban centre. Located on Angola's northern Atlantic coast, Luanda is Angola's [[Administrative centre|administrative centre]], its chief seaport, and also the capital of the Luanda Province. Luanda and its [[Metropolitan area|metropolitan area]] is the most populous Portuguese-speaking national capital in the world and the most populous Lusophone city outside [[Brazil]]. In 2024 the population reached more than 8.8 million inhabitants (a third of Angola's population).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Among the oldest colonial cities of Africa, Luanda was founded in January 1576 as São Paulo da Assunção de Loanda by Portuguese explorer Paulo Dias de Novais, being occasionally called "Leonda" or "St Paul de Leonda" by non-Portuguese sources. At the start of the Angolan Civil War in 1975, most of the white Portuguese left as refugees, principally migrating to Portugal. Luanda\'s population increased greatly from internal refugees fleeing the war, but its infrastructure was inadequate to handle the increase. This also caused the exacerbation of slums, or musseques, around Luanda. In the 21st century, the city has been undergoing a major reconstruction. Many new large developments are taking place that will alter its cityscape significantly. The city is also notable as an economic centre for oil, and a refinery is located in the city.',
