	{
		title = "Medellín",
		category = "City",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "City in Colombia",
		tags = { "South America", "Colombia" },
		summary = "**Medellín**, officially the **Special District of Science, Technology and Innovation of Medellín**, is the capital of the department of Antioquia, and the second-largest city in [[Colombia]] after [[Bogotá]]. It is located in the Aburrá Valley, a central region of the [[Andes|Andes Mountains]], in northwestern South America. The city's population was 2,427,129 at the 2018 census. The metro area of Medellín is the second-largest urban agglomeration in Colombia in terms of population and economy, with more than 4 million people.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In 1616, the Spaniard Francisco de Herrera Campuzano erected a small indigenous village (poblado) known as "Saint Lawrence of Aburrá" (San Lorenzo de Aburrá), located in the present-day El Poblado commune. On 2 November 1675, the queen consort Mariana of Austria founded the "Town of Our Lady of Candelaria of Medellín" (Villa de Nuestra Señora de la Candelaria de Medellín) in the Aná region, which today corresponds to the center of the city (east-central zone) and first describes the region as "Medellín". In 1826, the city was named the capital of the Department of Antioquia by the National Congress of the nascent Republic of Gran Colombia, comprising present-day Colombia, Venezuela, Ecuador, and Panama. After Colombia won its independence from Spain, Medellín became the capital of the Federal State of Antioquia until 1888, with the proclamation of the Colombian Constitution of 1886.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
