			{ "Built", "c. 2500 BC" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mombasa",
		category = "City",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "City in Mombasa County, Kenya",
		tags = { "Africa", "Eastern Africa" },
		summary = "**Mombasa** is a coastal city in southeastern [[Kenya]] along the [[Indian Ocean]]. It was the first capital of British East Africa, before [[Nairobi]] was elevated to capital status in 1907. It now serves as the capital of Mombasa County. Buildings in the Central Business District are blue and white, representing the Indian Ocean. It is the country's oldest (c. 900 A.D.) and second-largest city after Nairobi, with a population of about 1,208,333 people according to the 2019 census.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Mombasa's location on the Indian Ocean made it a historic trading centre, and it has been controlled by many countries because of its strategic location. Kenyan school history books place the founding of Mombasa as 900 AD. It must have already been a prosperous trading town in the 12th century, as the Arab geographer al-Idrisi mentions it in 1151. It was a part of the Kilwa Sultanate from approximately the early 14th century until the dissolution of the sultanate in 1513. The oldest stone mosque in Mombasa, Mnara, was built c. 1300. The Mandhry Mosque, built in 1570, has a minaret that contains a regionally specific ogee arch. The city later came under the occupation and control of the Omani Empire in the late 17th century. Throughout the early modern period, Mombasa was a key node in the complex and far-reaching Indian Ocean trading networks.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Harare", "Maputo" },
		infobox = {
