		title = "Varanasi",
		category = "City",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Metropolis in Uttar Pradesh, India",
		tags = { "Southern Asia", "India" },
		aliases = { "Kashi" },
		summary = "**Varanasi**, or **Kashi**, is a city on the [[Ganges|Ganges river]] in [[North India|northern India]] that has a central place in the traditions of pilgrimage, death, and mourning in the Hindu world. The city also has a syncretic tradition of Islamic artisanship that underpins its religious tourism. Located in the middle-Ganges valley in the southeastern part of the state of [[Uttar Pradesh]], Varanasi lies on the left bank of the river. It is 692 km to the southeast of India's capital [[New Delhi]] and 320 km to the southeast of the state capital, [[Lucknow]]. It lies 121 km downstream of [[Prayagraj]], where the confluence with the Yamuna river is another major Hindu pilgrimage site.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Varanasi is one of the world's oldest continually inhabited cities. Kashi, its ancient name, was associated with a kingdom of the same name in the first millennium BCE. The Lion capital of Ashoka at nearby Sarnath has been interpreted to be a commemoration of the Buddha's first sermon there in the fifth century BCE. In the 8th century, Adi Shankara established the worship of Shiva as an official sect of Varanasi. In the mid-second millennium several figures of the Bhakti movement were born here. They included [[Kabir]], Ravidas [[Tulsidas|and Tulsidas]]. Kabir came to influence traditions in Hinduism, Sikhism, and Sufism. Tulsidas wrote his [[Awadhi language]] epic, the Ramcharitmanas, which is a popular vernacular reworking of the Sanskrit [[Ramayana]]. In the 16th century, Rajput nobles in the service of the [[Mughal Empire|Mughal emperor]] [[Akbar]], sponsored work on Hindu temples in the city.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Government", "[[Municipal corporation|Municipal Corporation]]" },
