		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Abiotic components", "Abiotic factors" },
		summary = "In [[Ecology|ecology]], **abiotic components** or **abiotic factors** are non-living [[Chemical property|chemical]] and [[Physical property|physical]] parts of the [[Natural environment|environment]] that affect living [[Organism|organisms]] and the functioning of [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]]. Abiotic factors and the phenomena associated with them underpin biology as a whole. They affect a wide range of species, across all forms of environmental conditions, such as [[Marine life|marine]] or [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial animals]]. [[Human|Humans]] can [[Human impact on the environment|make or change]] abiotic factors in a species' environment. For instance, [[Fertilizer|fertilizers]] can affect a snail's [[Habitat|habitat]], or the [[Greenhouse gas|greenhouse gases]] which humans utilize can change marine [[PH|pH]] levels.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Abiotic components include physical conditions and non-living resources that affect living organisms in terms of [[Developmental biology|growth]], maintenance, and [[Reproduction|reproduction]]. Resources are distinguished as substances or objects in the environment required by one organism and consumed or otherwise made unavailable for use by other organisms. Component degradation of a substance occurs by chemical or physical processes, e.g. hydrolysis. All non-living components of an ecosystem, such as [[Atmosphere|atmospheric conditions]] and [[Water resources|water resources]], are called abiotic components.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Metaphor" },
