		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Distinctive way of pronouncing a language",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Accent" },
		summary = "In [[Sociolinguistics|sociolinguistics]], an **accent** is a way of pronouncing a [[Language|language]] that is distinctive to a [[Country|country]], [[Region|area]], [[Social class|social class]], or [[Individual|individual]]. An accent may be identified with the locality in which its speakers reside (a regional or geographical accent), the socioeconomic status of its speakers, their [[Ethnicity|ethnicity]] (an ethnolect), their [[Caste|caste]] or social class (a social accent), or influence from their [[First language|first language]] (a foreign accent).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Accents typically differ in quality of voice, pronunciation and distinction of vowels and consonants, stress, and [[Prosody (linguistics)|prosody]]. Although grammar, semantics, vocabulary, and other language characteristics often vary concurrently with accent, the word "accent" may refer specifically to the differences in pronunciation, whereas the word "[[Dialect|dialect]]" encompasses the broader set of linguistic differences. "Accent" is often a subset of "dialect".',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Accompaniment",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Part of a musical composition",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '**Accompaniment** is the musical part which provides the [[Rhythm|rhythmic]] and/or harmonic support for the [[Melody|melody]] or main themes of a [[Song|song]] or instrumental piece. There are many different styles and types of accompaniment in different genres and styles of music. In homophonic music, the main accompaniment approach used in [[Popular music|popular music]], a clear vocal melody is supported by subordinate [[Chord (music)|chords]]. In [[Popular music|popular music]] and traditional music, the accompaniment parts typically provide the "beat" for the music and outline the chord progression of the song or instrumental piece.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The accompaniment for a vocal melody or instrumental solo can be played by a single musician playing an instrument such as [[Piano|piano]], [[Pipe organ|pipe organ]], or [[Guitar|guitar]]. While any instrument can in theory be used as an accompaniment instrument, keyboard and guitar-family instruments tend to be used if there is only a single instrument, as these instruments can play chords and basslines simultaneously (chords and a bassline are easier to play simultaneously on keyboard instruments, but a fingerpicking guitarist can play chords and a bassline simultaneously on guitar). A solo singer can accompany themself by playing guitar or piano while they sing, and in some rare cases, a solo singer can even accompany themself just using their voice and body (e.g., Bobby McFerrin).",
			},
		},
		related = { "Apabhraṃśa" },
	},
