		description = "Legal and anthropological concept",
		aliases = { "Affinity" },
		summary = "In law and in [[Cultural anthropology|cultural anthropology]], **affinity** is the [[Kinship|kinship]] relationship created or that exists between two people as a result of someone's [[Marriage|marriage]]. It is the relationship each party in the marriage has to the family of the other party in the marriage. It does not cover the marital relationship itself.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Laws, traditions and customs relating to affinity vary considerably, sometimes ceasing with the death of one of the marriage partners through whom affinity is traced, and sometimes with the divorce of the marriage partners. In addition to kinship by marriage, "affinity" can sometimes also include kinship by [[Adoption|adoption]] or a [[Stepfamily|step relationship]]. Which relationships are prohibited vary from jurisdiction to jurisdiction, and have varied over time. In some countries, especially in the past, the prohibited relationships were based on religious laws. The degree of affinity is considered the same as the consanguineal level of the couple that was joined, so that, for example, the degree of affinity of a husband to his sister-in-law is two, the same as the wife would be to her sister on the basis of consanguinity.',
			},
