	{
		title = "Ancient Greek philosophy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🤔",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Philosophical origins and foundation of Western civilization",
		tags = { "Philosophy", "Philosophy by region and period" },
		aliases = { "Greek philosophy" },
		summary = "**Ancient Greek philosophy** arose in the 6th century BCE. It dealt with a wide variety of subjects, including [[Astronomy|astronomy]], [[Epistemology|epistemology]], [[Mathematics|mathematics]], [[Political philosophy|political philosophy]], [[Ethics|ethics]], [[Metaphysics|metaphysics]], [[Ontology|ontology]], [[Logic|logic]], [[Biology|biology]], [[Rhetoric|rhetoric]] and [[Aesthetics|aesthetics]]. Greek philosophy continued throughout the [[Hellenistic period]] and later evolved into Roman philosophy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Greek philosophy has influenced much of [[Western culture]] since its inception, and can be found in many aspects of public education. [[Alfred North Whitehead]] once claimed: "The safest general characterization of the [[Western philosophy|European philosophical tradition]] is that it consists of a series of footnotes to [[Plato]]". Clear, unbroken lines of influence lead from [[Ancient Greece|ancient Greek]] and Hellenistic philosophers to Roman philosophy, early Islamic philosophy, [[Middle Ages|medieval]] [[Scholasticism|scholasticism]], the European [[Renaissance]] and the [[Age of Enlightenment]]. Greek philosophy was influenced to some extent by the older wisdom literature and mythological [[Cosmogony|cosmogonies]] of the [[Ancient Near East|ancient Near East]], though the extent of this influence is widely debated.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ancient Macedonians",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ancient Greek ethnic group",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Macedonians" },
		summary = "The **Macedonians** were an ancient tribe that lived on the alluvial plain around the rivers Haliacmon and lower Axios in the northeastern part of mainland Greece. Essentially an [[Ancient Greece|ancient Greek people]], they established the kingdom of [[Macedonia (ancient kingdom)|Macedon]] ruled from Aigai by the Argeads, and gradually expanded from their homeland along the Haliacmon valley on the northern edge of the Greek world, absorbing or driving out neighbouring non-Greek tribes, primarily Thracian and Illyrian. They spoke Ancient Macedonian, which is usually classified as a dialect of Northwest Greek, and occasionally as a dialect of Aeolic Greek or as a distinct Hellenic language (i.e. a sister language of [[Greek language|Greek]]).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"However, the [[Lingua franca|prestige language]] of Macedon during the [[Classical Greece|Classical era]] was [[Attic Greek]], replaced by [[Koine Greek]] during the Hellenistic era. Their religious beliefs mirrored those of other Greeks, following the main deities of the Greek pantheon, although the Macedonians continued [[Archaic Greece|Archaic]] burial practices that had ceased in other parts of [[Greece]] after the 6th century BC. Aside from the monarchy, the core of Macedonian society was its nobility. Similar to the aristocracy of neighboring Thessaly, their wealth was largely built on herding horses and [[Cattle|cattle]]. Although composed of various clans, the kingdom of Macedon, established around the 7th century BC, is mostly associated with the Argead dynasty and the tribe named after it. The dynasty, also known as the Temenid dynasty, was allegedly founded by Perdiccas I, descendant of the legendary Temenus of Argos.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ancient Near East",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏠",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Home of many cradles of civilization",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **ancient Near East** was home to many [[Cradle of civilization|cradles of civilization]], spanning [[Mesopotamia|Ancient Mesopotamia]], the [[Levant|ancient Levant]], [[Ancient Egypt|ancient Egypt]], [[Elam|ancient Elam]], [[Anatolia|ancient Anatolia or Asia Minor]], the [[South Caucasus|ancient South Caucasus]] and [[Armenian highlands]], and [[Arabian Peninsula|ancient Northern Arabia]] (including Dilmun). It is generally not considered to include Pre-Islamic Arabia as a whole, the [[Berbers|ancient Berbers]], or [[Nubia|ancient Nubia]], though it is sometimes classified as encompassing [[History of Iran|ancient Iran]], ancient Cyprus, [[Ancient Carthage]], and the Aegean civilization.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Given the nature and scope of their subject matter, the fields of ancient Near East studies and Near Eastern archaeology, including the subfields of Assyriology and Egyptology, are among the most prominent with regard to research in the realm of [[Ancient history|ancient history]], alongside such disciplines as [[Classics]], Classical archaeology, biblical studies, biblical archaeology, Jewish studies, Christian studies, Iranology, Sinology, and Indology, some of which overlap with them. Historically, the [[Near East]] denoted an area roughly encompassing [[West Asia|central West Asia]] and [[Egypt]], having been focused on the lands between [[Greece]] in the west, [[Iran]] in the east, the [[Caucasus]] in the north, and [[Saudi Arabia|Arabia]] in the south. It therefore largely corresponds with the central core area of the modern-day geopolitical concept of the [[Middle East]].",
			},
