		description = "Intense hostile emotional state of mind",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Emotions and traits" },
		aliases = { "Wrath", "Rage" },
		summary = "**Anger**, also known as **wrath** or **rage**, is an intense [[Emotion|emotional]] state involving a strong, uncomfortable and non-cooperative response to a perceived provocation, hurt, or threat.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Some view anger as an emotion that triggers part of the fight or flight response. Anger becomes the predominant feeling [[Behavior|behaviorally]], [[Cognition|cognitively]], and [[Physiology|physiologically]] when a person makes the conscious choice to take action to immediately stop the threatening behavior of another outside force. Anger can have many physical and mental consequences. The external expression of anger can be found in facial expressions, [[Body language|body language]], physiological responses, and at times public acts of [[Aggression|aggression]]. Facial expressions can range from inward angling of the eyebrows to a full frown. While most of those who experience anger explain its arousal as a result of "what has happened to them", psychologists point out that an angry person can very well be mistaken because anger causes a loss in self-monitoring capacity and objective observability.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Jealousy" },
	},
