	{
		title = "Apparent magnitude",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Brightness of a celestial object observed from the Earth",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Apparent magnitude** (**m**) is a measure of the brightness of a [[Star|star]], [[Astronomical object|astronomical object]] or other celestial objects like [[Satellite|artificial satellites]]. Its value depends on its intrinsic [[Luminosity|luminosity]], its distance, and any [[Extinction (astronomy)|extinction]] of the object's light caused by interstellar dust or atmosphere along the line of sight to the observer.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Unless stated otherwise, the word magnitude in astronomy usually refers to a celestial object's apparent magnitude. The magnitude scale likely dates to before the ancient Roman astronomer [[Ptolemy|Claudius Ptolemy]], whose star catalog popularized the system by listing stars from 1st magnitude (brightest) to 6th magnitude (dimmest). The modern scale was mathematically defined to closely match this historical system by Norman Pogson in 1856. The scale is reverse logarithmic: the brighter an object is, the lower its [[Magnitude (astronomy)|magnitude]] number. For example, a magnitude 2.0 star is 2.512 times as bright as a magnitude 3.0 star, 6.31 times as bright as a magnitude 4.0 star, and precisely 100 times as bright as a magnitude 7.0 star. The brightest astronomical objects have negative apparent magnitudes, such as [[Venus]] at magnitude −4.2 or [[Sirius]] at magnitude −1.46.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Appeal",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Legal request to have a case reviewed by a higher authority",
		tags = { "Law", "Basics" },
		summary = "In [[Law|law]], an **appeal** is the process in which cases or decisions are reviewed by a higher authority, where parties request a formal change to an official decision. Appeals function both as a process for error correction as well as a process of clarifying and interpreting law. Although appellate courts have existed for thousands of years, [[Common law|common law]] countries did not incorporate an affirmative right to appeal into their jurisprudence until the 19th century.",
		related = { "Jurisprudence" },
	},
	{
		title = "Applied arts",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch in the production of consumer goods",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **applied arts** are all the arts that apply [[Design|design]] and [[Decorative arts|decoration]] to everyday and essentially practical objects in order to make them [[Aesthetics|aesthetically]] pleasing. The term is used in distinction to the fine arts, which are those that produce objects with no practical use, whose only purpose is to be beautiful or stimulate the intellect in some way. In practice, the two often overlap. Applied arts largely overlap with [[Decorative arts|decorative arts]], and the modern making of applied art is usually called [[Design|design]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Examples of applied arts are: [[Industrial design]] – mass-produced objects. [[Sculpture]] – also counted as a fine art. [[Architecture]] – also counted as a fine art. Crafts – also counted as a fine art. [[Culinary arts]] [[Ceramic art]] Automotive design [[Fashion design]] [[Calligraphy]] [[Interior design]] [[Graphic design]] Cartographic (map) design",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Aquarium",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Transparent tank of water for fish and water-dwelling species",
		tags = { "Entertainment", "Entertainment and leisure" },
		summary = 'Fishkeepers use aquaria to keep [[Fish|fish]], [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]], [[Amphibian|amphibians]], aquatic [[Reptile|reptiles]], such as [[Turtle|turtles]], and [[Aquatic plant|aquatic plants]]. The term aquarium, coined by English naturalist Philip Henry Gosse, combines the [[Latin]] root aqua, meaning "water," with the suffix -arium, meaning "a place for relating to or connected with."',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The aquarium principle was fully developed in 1850 by the chemist Robert Warington, who explained that aquatic plants placed in a container of water could produce enough [[Oxygen|oxygen]] to support animals, so long as the numbers of animals did not grow too large. The aquarium craze was launched in early [[Victorian era|Victorian England]] by Gosse, who created and stocked the first public aquarium at the London Zoo in 1853, and published the first manual, The Aquarium: An Unveiling of the Wonders of the Deep Sea in 1854. Small aquariums are kept in the home by [[Hobby|hobbyists]]. There are large public aquariums in many cities. Public aquariums keep fish and other [[Aquatic animal|aquatic animals]] in large tanks. A large aquarium may have [[Otter|otters]], [[Dolphin|dolphins]], [[Shark|sharks]], [[Penguin|penguins]], [[Pinniped|seals]], and [[Blue whale|whales]]. Many aquarium tanks also have plants. An aquarist owns fish or maintains an aquarium, typically constructed of glass or high-strength acrylic.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Professional wrestling" },
	},
	{
		title = "Aquatic ecosystem",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ecosystem in a body of water",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **aquatic ecosystem** is an [[Ecosystem|ecosystem]] found in and around a [[Body of water|body of water]], in contrast to land-based [[Terrestrial ecosystem|terrestrial ecosystems]]. Aquatic ecosystems contain communities of [[Organism|organisms]]—**aquatic life**—that are dependent on each other and on their environment. The two main types of aquatic ecosystems are [[Marine ecosystem|marine ecosystems]] and [[Freshwater ecosystem|freshwater ecosystems]]. Freshwater ecosystems may be lentic (slow moving water, including [[Pond|pools]], [[Pond|ponds]], and [[Lake|lakes]]); lotic (faster moving water, for example [[Stream|streams]] and [[River|rivers]]); and [[Wetland|wetlands]] (areas where the soil is saturated or inundated for at least part of the time).",
	},
	{
		title = "Aqueous solution",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Solution in which the solvent is water",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **aqueous solution** is a [[Solution (chemistry)|solution]] in which the [[Solvent|solvent]] is [[Water|water]]. It is mostly shown in [[Chemical equation|chemical equations]] by appending (aq) to the relevant [[Chemical formula|chemical formula]]. For example, a solution of [[Salt|table salt]], also known as [[Sodium chloride|sodium chloride]] (NaCl), in water would be represented as Na+(aq) + Cl−(aq). The word aqueous (which comes from aqua) means pertaining to, related to, similar to, or dissolved in, water. As water is an excellent solvent and is also naturally abundant, it is a ubiquitous solvent in [[Chemistry|chemistry]]. Since water is frequently used as the solvent in experiments, the word solution refers to an aqueous solution, unless the solvent is specified.",
		sections = {
			{ "Overview", "A non-aqueous solution is a solution in which the solvent is a liquid, but is not water." },
		},
