	{
		title = "Assamese language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Indo-Aryan language of India",
		tags = { "Indo-European languages", "Indo-Aryan languages" },
		aliases = { "Assamese", "Asamiya", "Oxomiya" },
		summary = "**Assamese**, also known by its [[Endonym and exonym|endonyms]] **Asamiya** or **Oxomiya** ( Ôxômiya ), is an Indo-Aryan language spoken mainly in the north-eastern Indian state of [[Assam]], where it is an official language. It has long served as a [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] in parts of [[Northeast India]]. It has over 15 million native speakers and 8.3 million [[Second language|second language speakers]] according to [[Ethnologue]], although this claim is widely disputed by academics, based on census figures. Assamese was designated as a [[Classical languages of India|classical Indian language]] by the [[Government of India]] on 3 October 2024 on account of its antiquity and literary traditions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Nefamese, an Assamese-based pidgin in Arunachal Pradesh, was used as a lingua franca before being replaced by [[Hindi]]; and Nagamese, an Assamese-based [[Creole language]], continues to be widely used in Nagaland. The Kamtapuri language of Rangpur Division of Bangladesh and the Cooch Behar and Jalpaiguri districts of India is linguistically closest to Assamese and they both are descended from the Kamarupi Prakrit. In the past, it was the court language of the Ahom kingdom from the 17th century. After the differentiation of [[Maithili language|Maithili]] and [[Odia language|Odia]], Assamese was in its formative stages in the 12th century CE from the middle Indo-Aryan Magadhi Prakrit. Its sister languages include Angika, [[Bengali language|Bengali]], Bishnupriya Manipuri, Chakma, Chittagonian, Hajong, Rajbangsi, Surjapuri, [[Maithili language|Maithili]], Rohingya and Sylheti.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Spoken in", "[[Languages with official recognition in India|India]]" },
			{ "Region", "[[Northeast India]] [[Assam]], Arunachal Pradesh, Meghalaya, Nagaland" },
