		title = "Beta decay",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of radioactive decay",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Nuclear physics|nuclear physics]], **beta decay** is a type of [[Radioactivity|radioactive decay]] in which an [[Atomic nucleus|atomic nucleus]] emits a [[Beta particle|beta particle]] (fast energetic [[Electron|electron]] or [[Positron|positron]]), transforming into an isobar of that [[Nuclide|nuclide]]. For example, beta decay of a [[Neutron|neutron]] transforms it into a [[Proton|proton]] by the emission of an electron accompanied by an antineutrino; or, conversely a proton is converted into a neutron by the emission of a positron with a [[Neutrino|neutrino]] in what is called positron emission. Neither the beta particle nor its associated (anti-)neutrino exist within the nucleus prior to beta decay, but are created in the decay process. By this process, unstable atoms obtain a more stable ratio of protons to neutrons.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The probability of a nuclide decaying due to beta and other forms of decay is determined by its [[Nuclear binding energy|nuclear binding energy]]. The binding energies of all existing nuclides form what is called the nuclear band or valley of stability. For either electron or positron emission to be energetically possible, the energy release or Q value must be positive. Beta decay is a consequence of the [[Weak interaction|weak force]], which is characterized by relatively long decay times. Nucleons are composed of [[Up quark|up quarks]] and [[Down quark|down quarks]], and the weak force allows a [[Quark|quark]] to change its flavour by means of a virtual W boson leading to creation of an electron/antineutrino or positron/neutrino pair. For example, a neutron, composed of two down quarks and an up quark, decays to a proton composed of a down quark and two up quarks.",
			},
		},
	},
