		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔣",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Set of morphological features common to members of a phylum of animals",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Ground plan" },
		summary = "A **body plan**, or **ground plan** is a set of [[Morphology (biology)|morphological]] [[Phenotypic trait|features]] common to many members of a [[Phylum|phylum]] of [[Animal|animals]]. The [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]] share one body plan, while [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]] have many.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'This term, usually applied to animals, envisages a "blueprint" encompassing aspects such as symmetry, layers, [[Segmentation (biology)|segmentation]], nerve, [[Limb (anatomy)|limb]], and gut disposition. Evolutionary developmental biology seeks to explain the origins of diverse body plans. Body plans have historically been considered to have evolved in a flash in the Ediacaran biota; filling the [[Cambrian explosion]] with the results, and a more nuanced understanding of animal evolution suggests gradual development of body plans throughout the early Palaeozoic. Recent studies in [[Animal|animals]] and [[Plant|plants]] started to investigate whether evolutionary constraints on body plan structures can explain the presence of developmental constraints during embryogenesis such as the phenomenon referred to as phylotypic stage.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Pacific Community" },
