		summary = "**Bosnian** is the standard variety of the [[Serbo-Croatian]] language mainly used by Bosniaks. It is one of the three [[Official language|official languages]] of [[Bosnia and Herzegovina]], a co-official language in [[Montenegro]], and an officially recognized minority language in [[Croatia]], [[Serbia]], [[North Macedonia]] and [[Kosovo]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Bosnian uses both the Latin and Cyrillic alphabets, with Latin in everyday use. It is notable among the [[Variety (linguistics)|varieties]] of Serbo-Croatian for a number of [[Arabic]], [[Persian language|Persian]] and Ottoman Turkish loanwords, largely due to the language's interaction with those cultures through Islamic ties. Bosnian is based on the most widespread dialect of Serbo-Croatian, Shtokavian, more specifically on Eastern Herzegovinian, which is also the basis of standard Croatian, Serbian and Montenegrin varieties. Therefore, the Declaration on the Common Language of Croats, Serbs, Bosniaks and Montenegrins was issued in 2017 in Sarajevo. Although the common name for the common language remains 'Serbo-Croatian', newer alternatives such as 'Bosnian-Croatian-Serbian' and 'Bosnian-Croatian-Montenegrin-Serbian' (commonly abbreviated 'BCMS') have been increasingly utilised since the 1990s, especially within diplomatic circles.",
