	{
		title = "Bourgeoisie",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Social class",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'The **bourgeoisie** are a [[Social class|social class]] of business owners, [[Merchant|merchants]] and [[Wealth|wealthy]] people, in general, which emerged in the [[Late Middle Ages]], originally as a "[[Middle class|middle class]]" between the [[Peasant|peasantry]] and aristocracy. They are traditionally contrasted with the proletariat by their wealth, political power, and education, as well as their access to and control of cultural, social, and financial capital.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The bourgeoisie in its original sense is intimately linked to the political ideology of [[Liberalism|liberalism]] and its existence within cities, recognised as such by their urban [[Charter|charters]] (e.g., municipal charters, town privileges, German town law), so there was no bourgeoisie apart from the citizenry of the cities. Rural [[Peasant|peasants]] came under a different legal system. In [[Marxism|Marxist theory]], the bourgeoisie and the proletariat form an antagonistic relationship based on the former's exploitation of the latter's labour within the system known as [[Capitalism|capitalism]]. The main activity of the bourgeoisie within this framework is the accumulation of capital through the ownership and control of the means of production such as [[Factory|factories]], [[Land|land]], and [[Technology|technology]]. By [[Employment|employing]] the proletariat, they extract surplus value from the workers' labour, a process fundamental to the functioning and expansion of the system.",
			},
		},
	},
