	{
		title = "Causality",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "How one process influences another",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Causality** is an influence by which one event, process, state, or subject (i.e., a cause) contributes to the production of another event, process, state, or object (i.e., an effect) where the cause is at least partly responsible for the effect, and the effect is at least partly dependent on the cause. The **cause** of something may also be described as the **reason** behind the event or process.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In general, a process can have multiple causes, which are also said to be causal factors for it, and all lie in its [[Past|past]]. An effect can in turn be a cause of, or causal factor for, many other effects, which all lie in its [[Future|future]]. Thus, the distinction between cause and effect either follows from or else provides the distinction between past and future. While the former viewpoint is more prevalent in physics, some writers have held that causality is [[Metaphysics|metaphysically]] prior to notions of time and space. Causality is an [[Abstraction|abstraction]] that indicates how the world progresses. As such, it is a basic concept, and one might expect it to be more apt as an explanation of other concepts of progression than as something to be explained by yet more fundamental ideas. The concept is like those of agency and efficacy. For this reason, a leap of [[Intuition|intuition]] may be needed to grasp it.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Causality (physics)",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physics of the cause–effect relation",
		tags = { "Metaphysics", "Concepts" },
		summary = "In physics, **causality** requires the cause of an event to be in the past light cone of the result and to be ultimately [[Reductionism|reducible]] to [[Fundamental interaction|fundamental interactions]]. Similarly, a cause cannot have an effect outside its future light cone.",
		related = { "Meaning of life", "Ontology" },
	},
	{
		title = "Cebuano language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Austronesian language of the Philippines",
		tags = { "Specific languages", "Austronesian languages" },
		aliases = { "Cebuano" },
		summary = "**Cebuano** is an [[Austronesian languages|Austronesian language]] spoken in the southern [[Philippines]] by the Bisaya people and other ethnic groups as a secondary language. It is natively, though informally, called by the generic name **Bisayâ**, or **Binisayâ** (both terms are translated into English as Visayan, though this should not be confused with other Bisayan languages) and sometimes referred to in English sources as **Cebuan**. It is spoken by the Visayan ethnolinguistic groups native to the islands of Cebu, Bohol, Siquijor, the eastern half of Negros, the western half of Leyte, the northern coastal areas of Northern Mindanao and the eastern part of Zamboanga del Norte due to Spanish settlements during the 18th century.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In modern times, it has also spread to the Davao Region, Cotabato, Camiguin, parts of the Dinagat Islands, and the lowland regions of Caraga, often displacing native languages in those areas (most of which are closely related to it). While [[Tagalog language|Tagalog]] has the largest number of native speakers among the languages of the Philippines today, Cebuano had the largest native-language-speaking population from the 1950s until about the 1980s. It is by far the most widely spoken of the Bisayan languages. Cebuano is the [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] of Central Visayas, the western parts of Eastern Visayas, some western parts of Palawan, and most parts of [[Mindanao]]. The name Cebuano is derived from the island of Cebu, which is the source of Standard Cebuano. Cebuano is also the primary language in Western Leyte—noticeably in Ormoc.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Spoken in", "Philippines" },
			{ "Speakers", "20 million" },
			{ "Date", "2023 estimate" },
			{ "Writing system", "[[Latin script|Latin]] (Filipino alphabet) Philippine Braille Historically Badlit" },
		},
