	{
		title = "Census",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Compilation of information about a given population",
		tags = { "Sociology" },
		summary = 'A **census** is the procedure of systematically acquiring, recording, and calculating information about the members of a given [[Statistical population|population]], which is then usually presented through statistics. This term is used mostly in connection with national population and housing censuses. Other common censuses include censuses of agriculture, traditional culture, businesses, supplies, and traffic censuses. The [[United Nations]] (UN) defines the essential features of population and housing censuses as "individual enumeration, universality within a defined territory, simultaneity, and defined periodicity", and recommends that population censuses be conducted at least every ten years.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'UN recommendations also cover census topics to be collected, official definitions, classifications, and other information to coordinate international practices. The [[United Nations|UN]]\'s [[Food and Agriculture Organization]] (FAO), in turn, defines the census of agriculture as "a statistical operation for collecting, processing and disseminating data on the structure of agriculture, covering the whole or a significant part of a country." "In a census of agriculture, data are collected at the holding level." The word is of [[Latin]] origin: during the [[Roman Republic]], the census was a list of all adult males fit for military service. The modern census is essential for international comparisons of all types of statistics, and censuses collect data on many attributes of a population, not just the number of individuals.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Center of mass",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Unique point where the weighted relative position of the distributed mass sums to zero",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Balance point" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], the **center of mass** of a distribution of [[Mass|mass]] in [[Space|space]] (sometimes referred to as the **barycenter** or **balance point**) is the unique point at any given time where the weighted [[Position (geometry)|relative position]] of the distributed mass sums to zero. For a rigid body containing its center of mass, this is the point to which a force may be applied to cause a linear acceleration without an angular acceleration. Calculations in [[Mechanics|mechanics]] are often simplified when formulated with respect to the center of mass. It is a hypothetical point where the entire mass of an object may be assumed to be concentrated to visualise its motion.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In other words, the center of mass is the particle equivalent of a given object for the application of [[Newton's laws of motion]]. In the case of a single rigid body, the center of mass is fixed in relation to the body, and if the body has uniform [[Density|density]], it will be located at the centroid. The center of mass may be located outside the [[Physical object|physical body]], as is sometimes the case for hollow or open-shaped objects, such as a horseshoe. In the case of a distribution of separate bodies, such as the planets of the [[Solar System]], the center of mass may not correspond to the position of any individual member of the system. The center of mass is a useful reference point for calculations in mechanics that involve masses distributed in space, such as the [[Momentum|linear]] and [[Angular momentum|angular momentum]] of planetary bodies and rigid body dynamics.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Centimetre–gram–second system of units",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Variant of the metric system",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "CGS" },
		summary = "The **centimetre–gram–second system of units** (**CGS** or **cgs**) is a variant of the [[Metric system|metric system]] based on the centimetre as the unit of [[Length|length]], the gram as the unit of [[Mass|mass]], and the [[Second|second]] as the unit of [[Time|time]]. All CGS [[Mechanics|mechanical]] units are unambiguously derived from these three base units, but there are several different ways in which the CGS system was extended to cover [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The CGS system has mainly been supplanted by the MKS system based on the [[Metre|metre]], [[Kilogram|kilogram]], and second, which was in turn extended and replaced by the [[International System of Units]] (SI). In many fields of science and engineering, SI is the only system of units in use, but CGS is still prevalent in certain subfields. In measurements of purely mechanical systems (involving units of length, mass, [[Force|force]], [[Energy|energy]], [[Pressure|pressure]], and so on), the differences between CGS and SI are straightforward: the unit-conversion factors are all [[Exponentiation|powers of 10]] as and. For example, the CGS unit of force is the dyne, which is defined as 1 g⋅cm/s2, so the SI unit of force, the [[Newton (unit)|newton]] (1 kg⋅m/s2), is equal to 100000 dynes. In contrast, converting measurements of electromagnetic quantities—such as [[Electric charge|electric charge]], electric and magnetic fields, and voltage—between CGS and SI systems is considerably more complex.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Central Africa Time",
