	{
		title = "Central nervous system",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧠",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Brain and spinal cord",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "CNS" },
		summary = "The **central nervous system** (**CNS**) is the part of the [[Nervous system|nervous system]] consisting primarily of the [[Brain|brain]], and the spinal cord. The retina is also technically part of the CNS. The CNS is named so because the brain integrates the received information and coordinates and influences the activity of all parts of the bodies of [[Bilateria|bilaterally symmetric and triploblastic animals]]—that is, all multicellular animals except [[Sponge|sponges]] and diploblasts. It is a structure composed of nervous tissue positioned along the rostral (nose end) to caudal (tail end) axis of the body and may have an enlarged section at the rostral end which is a brain.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Only arthropods, cephalopods and vertebrates have a true brain, though precursor structures exist in onychophorans, gastropods and lancelets. The rest of this article exclusively discusses the vertebrate central nervous system, which is radically distinct from all other animals.",
			},
