	{
		title = "Channel (geography)",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Narrow body of water",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Channel" },
		summary = "In [[Physical geography|physical geography]] and [[Hydrology|hydrology]], a **channel** is a [[Landform|landform]] on which a relatively narrow [[Body of water|body of water]] is situated, such as a [[River|river]], [[River delta|river delta]] or [[Strait|strait]]. While channel typically refers to a natural formation, the cognate term [[Canal|canal]] denotes a similar artificial structure.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Channels are important for the functionality of [[Port|ports]] and other bodies of water used for navigability for shipping. Naturally, channels will change their depth and capacity due to [[Erosion|erosion]] and [[Deposition (geology)|deposition]] processes. Humans maintain navigable channels by [[Dredging|dredging]] and other engineering processes. By extension, the term also applies to fluids other than water, e.g., lava channels. The term is also traditionally used to describe the waterless surface features on [[Venus]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Charge carrier",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Free-moving particle which carries an electric charge",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Solid-state physics|solid state physics]], a **charge carrier** is a [[Particle|particle]] or quasiparticle that is free to move, carrying an [[Electric charge|electric charge]], especially the particles that carry electric charges in [[Electrical conductor|electrical conductors]]. Examples are [[Electron|electrons]], [[Ion|ions]] and holes. In a conducting medium, an [[Electric field|electric field]] can exert force on these free particles, causing a net motion of the particles through the medium; this is what constitutes an [[Electric current|electric current]]. The [[Electron|electron]] and the [[Proton|proton]] are the **elementary charge carriers**, each carrying one [[Elementary charge|elementary charge]] (e), of the same magnitude and opposite sign.",
	},
