		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], a **charged particle** is a [[Particle|particle]] with an [[Electric charge|electric charge]]. For example, some [[Elementary particle|elementary particles]], like the [[Electron|electron]] or [[Quark|quarks]] are charged. Some composite particles like protons are charged particles. An [[Ion|ion]], such as a [[Molecule|molecule]] or [[Atom|atom]] with a surplus or deficit of [[Electron|electrons]] relative to [[Proton|protons]] are also charged particles.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A [[Plasma (physics)|plasma]] is a collection of charged particles, atomic nuclei and separated electrons, but can also be a gas containing a significant proportion of charged particles. Charged particles are labeled as either positive or negative. The designations are arbitrary. Nothing is inherent to a positively charged particle that makes it "positive", and the same goes for negatively charged particles.',
			},
