		tags = { "Specific languages", "Sino-Tibetan languages" },
		aliases = { "Chinese" },
		summary = "**Chinese** ([[Spoken language|spoken]]:, [[Writing|written]]:) is the umbrella term for almost all [[Sinitic languages]] except Macro-Bai languages, widely recognized as a collection of [[Variety (linguistics)|language varieties]], natively spoken by the [[Han Chinese]] and some ethnic minorities in Greater China, as well as by communities of the [[Overseas Chinese|overseas Chinese]]. Approximately 1.39 billion people, or 17% of the global population, speak one of the [[Varieties of Chinese|varieties of Chinese]] as their [[First language|first language]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The different Chinese language varieties together form the largest branch of the [[Sino-Tibetan languages]]. While the Chinese government defines all spoken Chinese varieties as merely diverse [[Dialect|dialects]] of a single language, the frequent lack of [[Mutual intelligibility|mutual intelligibility]], especially among those outside of the dominant [[Mandarin Chinese|northern varieties]], has led [[Linguistics|linguists]] to consider them as separate languages within a [[Language family|language family]]. Investigation of the [[Historical linguistics|historical relationships]] among the varieties of Chinese is ongoing. Currently, most classifications posit 7 to 13 main regional groups based on phonetic developments from [[Middle Chinese]], of which the most spoken by far is [[Mandarin Chinese|Mandarin]] with 66%, or around 800 million speakers, followed by [[Min Chinese|Min]] (75 million, e.g., [[Hokkien]] and Teochew), [[Wu Chinese|Wu]] (74 million, e.g., [[Shanghainese]]) and [[Yue Chinese|Yue]] (68 million, e.g., [[Cantonese]]).",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Date", "2017–2024" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Chinese painting",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🖼️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Artistic tradition",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Chinese painting** is one of the oldest continuous artistic traditions in the world. It is also called danqing. Traditional painting involves essentially the same techniques as calligraphy and is done with a brush dipped in black ink or coloured pigments; oils are not used. As with calligraphy, the most popular materials on which paintings are made are paper and silk. The finished work can be mounted on scrolls, such as hanging scrolls or handscrolls. Traditional painting can also be done on album sheets, walls, lacquerware, folding screens, and other media.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The two main techniques in Chinese painting are: Gongbi, meaning "meticulous", uses highly detailed brushstrokes that delimit details very precisely. It is often highly colored and usually depicts figural or narrative subjects. It is often practiced by artists working for the royal court or in independent workshops. Ink and wash painting, in Chinese shuǐ-mò also loosely termed watercolor or brush painting, and also known as "literati painting", as it was one of the "four arts" of the Chinese Scholar-official class. In theory this was an art practiced by gentlemen, a distinction that begins to be made in writings on art from the [[Song dynasty]], though in fact the careers of leading exponents could benefit considerably. This style is also referred to as "xieyi" or freehand style. [[Landscape painting]] was regarded as the highest form of Chinese painting, and generally still is.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Chinese people",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👥",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Chinese people**, or simply **Chinese**, are people or [[Ethnicity|ethnic groups]] identified with China. Understandings of who is considered Chinese have shifted across time through social construction.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"People of Chinese ethnicity or ancestry who live outside Greater China may consider themselves [[Overseas Chinese|overseas Chinese]]. In some areas throughout the world, ethnic enclaves known as Chinatowns are home to populations of overseas Chinese.",
			},
		},
	},
}
]]></string>
          </Properties>
