	},
	{
		title = "Classical liberalism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ideology supporting both civil and economic liberties",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "English liberalism", "Whiggism" },
		summary = "**Classical liberalism** (sometimes called **English liberalism**, and historically called **Whiggism**) is a political tradition and a branch of [[Liberalism|liberalism]] that advocates [[Free market|free market]] and laissez-faire economics and [[Civil liberties|civil liberties]] under the [[Rule of law|rule of law]], with special emphasis on individual autonomy, limited government, economic freedom, [[Political freedom|political freedom]] and [[Freedom of speech|freedom of speech]]. Classical liberalism, contrary to liberal branches like social liberalism or modern liberalism in the United States, looks more negatively on social policies, [[Tax|taxation]] and state involvement in the lives of individuals, and it advocates deregulation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Until the [[Great Depression]] and the rise of social liberalism, classical liberalism was called economic liberalism. Later, the term was applied as a retronym, to distinguish earlier 19th-century liberalism from social liberalism. By modern standards, in the United States, the bare term liberalism often means social or progressive liberalism and is located on the center-left, but in Europe and Australia, the bare term liberalism often means classical liberalism. Classical liberalism developed in the early 18th century, building on ideas dating back to the 16th century, and was foundational to the [[American Revolution]] and the "American Project" more broadly.',
			},
		},
	},
