		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Speech sound articulated by closing the vocal tract fully or partially",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		summary = "In articulatory phonetics, a **consonant** is a speech sound that is articulated with complete or partial closure of the vocal tract. Examples are and [b], pronounced with the lips; and [d], pronounced with the front of the tongue; and [g], pronounced with the back of the tongue;, pronounced with the glottis;, [v], and [z] pronounced by forcing air through a narrow channel (fricatives); and and, which have air flowing through the nose (nasals). Most consonants are pulmonic, using air pressure from the lungs to generate a sound. Very few natural languages are non-pulmonic, making use of ejectives, implosives, and clicks. Contrasting with consonants are [[Vowel|vowels]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Since the number of speech sounds in the world's languages is much greater than the number of letters in any one [[Alphabet|alphabet]], [[Linguistics|linguists]] have devised systems such as the [[International Phonetic Alphabet]] to assign a unique and unambiguous [[Symbol|symbol]] to each attested consonant.",
