	{
		title = "Conspiracy theory",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Attributing events to an improbable conspiracy",
		tags = { "Sociology" },
		summary = "A **conspiracy theory** is an explanation for an event or situation that asserts the existence of a conspiracy (generally by powerful sinister groups, often political in motivation), when other explanations are more probable. The term generally has a negative connotation, as it can often be based in [[Prejudice|prejudice]], emotional conviction, insufficient evidence or paranoia. A conspiracy theory is distinct from a conspiracy; it refers to a hypothesized conspiracy with specific characteristics, including but not limited to opposition to the mainstream consensus among those who are qualified to evaluate its accuracy, such as [[Scientist|scientists]] or [[Historian|historians]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'As such, conspiracy theories are identified as lay theories. Conspiracy theories are usually resistant to [[Falsifiability|falsification]] either by evidence against them or a lack of evidence for them. They are reinforced by circular reasoning: both evidence against the conspiracy and absence of evidence for it are misinterpreted as evidence of its truth. Psychologist Stephan Lewandowsky observes "the stronger the evidence against a conspiracy, the more the conspirators must want people to believe their version of events." As a consequence, the conspiracy becomes a matter of faith rather than something that can be proven or disproven. Studies have linked belief in conspiracy theories to distrust of authority and political cynicism. Some researchers suggest that **conspiracist ideation**—belief in conspiracy theories—may be psychologically harmful or pathological.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Constantine Samuel Rafinesque",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "French polymath and naturalist (1783–1840)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Constantine Samuel Rafinesque-Schmaltz" },
		summary = "**Constantine Samuel Rafinesque-Schmaltz** (22 October 1783 – 18 September 1840) was a French [[Polymath|polymath]] of the early 19th century. Born near [[Constantinople]] in the [[Ottoman Empire]], he was self-educated in France.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As a young man, Rafinesque-Schmaltz traveled to the United States, eventually settling in [[Ohio]] in 1815. There, he made significant contributions to botany, zoology, and the study of prehistoric earthworks in North America. Beyond his previous extensive work in Europe, he also contributed to the study of ancient Mesoamerican linguistics. In 1836, Rafinesque was the first person to use the term [[Taíno]] as a way of referring to the [[Indigenous peoples of the Caribbean|Indigenous peoples of the Greater Antilles]]. Rafinesque had a reputation as an eccentric and erratic genius. He was an autodidact who excelled in various fields of knowledge, as a zoologist, botanist, writer and polyglot. He wrote prolifically on such diverse topics as anthropology, biology, geology, and linguistics, but was honored in none of these fields during his lifetime.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "22 October 1783" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Galata, [[Constantinople]], [[Ottoman Empire]]" },
			{ "Died", "18 September 1840" },
			{ "Field", "Anthropology, botany, zoology, geology, linguistics" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Constitution",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fundamental principles that govern a state",
		tags = { "Law", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Supreme law" },
		summary = "A **constitution**, or **supreme law**, is the aggregate of fundamental [[Principle|principles]] or established [[Precedent|precedents]] that constitute the legal basis of a [[Polity|polity]], [[Organization|organization]] or other type of entity, and commonly determines how that entity is to be governed.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When these principles are written down into a single document or set of legal documents, those documents may be said to embody a written constitution; if they are encompassed in a single comprehensive document, it is said to embody a codified constitution. The constitution of the United Kingdom is a notable example of an uncodified constitution; it is instead written in numerous fundamental acts of a legislature, court cases, and treaties. Constitutions concern different levels of organizations, from [[Sovereign state|sovereign countries]] to [[Company|companies]] and unincorporated [[Club (organization)|associations]]. A [[Treaty|treaty]] that establishes an [[International organization|international organization]] is also its constitution, in that it would define how that organization is constituted. Within [[State (polity)|states]], a constitution defines the principles upon which the state is based, the procedure in which laws are made, and by whom.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Injunction" },
	},
	{
		title = "Constitution of the United States",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Supreme law of the United States",
		tags = { "Law", "Specific documents" },
		summary = "The **Constitution of the United States** is the supreme law of the [[United States|United States of America]]. It superseded the Articles of Confederation, the nation's first [[Constitution|constitution]], on March 4, 1789. Originally including seven articles, the Constitution defined the foundational structure of the [[Federal government of the United States|federal government]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The drafting of the Constitution by many of the nation's [[Founding Fathers of the United States|Founding Fathers]], often referred to as its framing, was completed at the Constitutional Convention, which assembled at Independence Hall in [[Philadelphia]] between May 25 and September 17, 1787. Influenced by [[Common law|English common law]] and the Enlightenment [[Liberalism|liberalism]] of philosophers like [[John Locke]] and [[Montesquieu]], the Constitution's first three articles embody the doctrine of the [[Separation of powers|separation of powers]], in which the federal government is divided into the [[United States Congress|legislative]], [[Bicameralism|bicameral]] Congress; the [[Federal government of the United States|executive]], led by the [[President of the United States|president]]; and the judiciary, within which the [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]] has apex jurisdiction. Articles IV, V, and VI embody concepts of [[Federalism|federalism]], describing the rights and responsibilities of state governments, the [[U.S. state|states]] in relationship to the [[Federal government of the United States|federal government]], and the process of constitutional amendment.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Constitutional monarchy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Form of government",
		tags = { "Politics and government", "Forms of government" },
		aliases = {
			"Limited monarchy",
			"Parliamentary monarchy",
			"Democratic monarchy",
			"Semi-constitutional monarchy",
		},
		summary = "**Constitutional monarchy**, also known as **limited monarchy**, **parliamentary monarchy** or **democratic monarchy**, is a form of [[Monarchy|monarchy]] in which the monarch exercises their authority in accordance with a constitution and is not alone in making decisions. Constitutional monarchies differ from [[Absolute monarchy|absolute monarchies]] (in which a monarch is the only decision-maker) in that they are bound to exercise powers and authorities within limits prescribed by an established legal framework. The monarch is frequently perceived as a visible symbol of [[Nationalism|national unity]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The powers of constitutional monarchs vary. In some countries, the monarch has virtually no executive or policy-making power and is primarily a hereditary symbolic head of state (who may be an emperor, king or queen, prince or grand duke), while in other countries, the monarch has meaningful formal powers (such as veto power, appointment power, and power to dissolve parliament). In countries such as [[Liechtenstein]], [[Monaco]], [[Morocco]], [[Jordan]], [[Kuwait]], [[Bahrain]], [[Thailand]] and [[Bhutan]], the constitution grants substantial discretionary powers to the sovereign, while in [[United Kingdom]] and other [[Commonwealth realm|Commonwealth realms]], the [[Kingdom of the Netherlands|Netherlands]], [[Spain]], [[Luxembourg]], [[Belgium]], [[Denmark]], [[Norway]], [[Sweden]], [[Lesotho]], [[Malaysia]], [[Cambodia]], and [[Japan]], the monarch retains significantly less, if any, personal discretion in the exercise of their authority.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
