	{
		title = "Conurbation",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Group of settlements linked by continuous urban area",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **conurbation** is a region consisting of a number of [[Metropolis|metropolises]], cities, large towns, and other [[Urban area|urban areas]] which, through [[Population growth|population growth]] and physical expansion, have merged to form one continuous urban or industrially developed area. In most cases, a conurbation is a polycentric urbanised area in which transportation has developed to link areas. They create a single urban [[Labour economics|labour market]] or travel to work area.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Conurbations often emerged in coal-mining regions during the period of the [[Industrial Revolution]]. Patrick Geddes coined the term in his book Cities in Evolution (1915). He drew attention to the ability of the new technology at the time of electric power and motorised transport to allow cities to spread and agglomerate together, and gave as examples "Midlandton" in England, the Ruhr in Germany, Randstad in the Netherlands, and the Northeastern Seaboard in the United States. For census purposes, the term as described is used in Britain, whereas in the United States a related concept of [[Metropolitan area|metropolitan area]] is used instead. Each polycentric "metropolitan area" may have its own common designation such as San Francisco Bay Area or the Dallas–Fort Worth metroplex. Internationally the term "urban agglomeration" is often used to convey a similar meaning to "conurbation".',
