	{
		title = "Cultural relativism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Anthropological view",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Cultural relativism** is the view that the [[Value (ethics)|values]] (such as moral values) of a [[Culture|culture]] must be understood in their own cultural context and not judged according to the standards of a different culture. It asserts the equal validity of all points of view and the [[Relativism|relative]] nature of truth, which is determined by an individual or their culture.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The concept was established by [[Anthropologist|anthropologist]] [[Franz Boas]], who first articulated the idea in 1887: "civilization is not something absolute, but...is relative, and... our ideas and conceptions are true only so far as our civilization goes". However, Boas did not use the phrase "cultural relativism". The concept was spread by Boas\'s students, such as Robert Lowie. The first use of the term recorded in the [[Oxford English Dictionary]] was by philosopher and social theorist Alain Locke in 1924 to describe Lowie\'s "extreme cultural relativism", found in the latter\'s 1917 book Culture and Ethnology. The term became common among anthropologists after Boas\' death in 1942, to express their synthesis of a number of ideas he had developed.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cultural studies",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Academic field",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Cultural theory" },
		summary = "**Cultural studies** or **cultural theory** is an academic field that explores the dynamics of contemporary [[Culture|culture]] (including the politics of [[Popular culture|popular culture]]) and its social and historical foundations. Cultural studies researchers investigate how cultural practices relate to wider systems of [[Power (political science)|power]] associated with, or operating through, social phenomena. Employing cultural analysis, cultural studies views cultures not as fixed, bounded, stable, and discrete entities, but rather as constantly interacting and changing sets of practices and processes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"British Marxist academics initially developed cultural studies in the late 1950s, 1960s, and 1970s, and it has since been taken up and transformed by scholars from many different disciplines around the world. Cultural studies is avowedly, and even radically, interdisciplinary and can sometimes be seen as anti-disciplinary. A key concern for cultural studies practitioners is the examination of the forces within and through which socially organized people conduct and participate in the construction of their everyday lives.",
			},
		},
		related = { "The Big Read" },
	},
	{
		title = "Culture",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Social behavior and norms of a society",
		tags = { "Culture", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Culture** ( or ) is a concept that encompasses the social behavior, [[Institution|institutions]], and [[Social norm|norms]] found in human societies, as well as the [[Knowledge|knowledge]], [[Belief|beliefs]], arts, [[Law|laws]], customs, capabilities, [[Attitude (psychology)|attitudes]], and habits of the individuals in these groups. Culture often originates from or is attributed to a specific region or location.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Humans acquire culture through the [[Learning|learning]] processes of enculturation and [[Socialization|socialization]], which is shown by the diversity of cultures across societies. A cultural norm codifies acceptable conduct in society; it serves as a guideline for behavior, dress, language, and demeanor in a situation, which serves as a template for expectations in a social group. Accepting only a monoculture in a social group can bear risks, just as a single species can wither in the face of environmental change, for lack of functional responses to such change. Thus in military culture, [[Courage|valor]] is counted as a typical behavior for an individual, and duty, honor, and loyalty to the social group are regarded as virtues or functional responses in the continuum of conflict. In religion, analogous attributes can be identified in a social group.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cuneiform",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "✍️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Writing system of the ancient Near East",
		tags = { "Language", "Writing systems" },
		summary = "**Cuneiform** is a logo-syllabic [[Writing system|writing system]] that was used to write several languages of the [[Ancient Near East|ancient Near East]]. The script was in active use from the early [[Bronze Age]] until the 1st century BC. Cuneiform scripts are marked by and named for the characteristic wedge-shaped impressions which form their [[Grapheme|signs]]. Cuneiform is the earliest known writing system and was originally developed to write the [[Sumerian language]] of southern [[Mesopotamia]] (modern [[Iraq]]).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Over the course of its history, cuneiform was adapted to write a number of languages in addition to Sumerian. [[Akkadian language|Akkadian]] texts are attested from the 24th century BC onward and make up the bulk of the cuneiform record. Akkadian cuneiform was itself adapted to write the Hittite language in the early 2nd millennium BC. The other languages with significant cuneiform corpora are Eblaite, Elamite, Hurrian, Luwian, and Urartian. The Old Persian alphabet features cuneiform-style signs. Most recently, links between Safaitic abecedaries, the [[Amorite language]], the Ugaritic alphabet and Akkadian syllabic cuneiform have been found. The extended use of the Safaitic script also coincided with the period in which the latest known cuneiform tablet to have been written, an astronomical almanac from Uruk, was dated (AD 79/80).",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Logographic" },
			{ "Region", "[[Sumer]]" },
			{ "Family", "Proto-cuneiform (Proto-writing)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Curiosity",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Quality related to inquisitive thinking",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Emotions and traits" },
		summary = "**Curiosity** can be a quality related to inquisitive thinking, such as exploration, investigation, and [[Learning|learning]], evident in humans and other animals. It can also refer to something (an event, object, person, etc.) which can cause curiosity. When it is for an object it may be called a curio, and a collection of curios or curiosities may be stored together as a collection called curiosities.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term curiosity can also denote the behavior, characteristic, or [[Emotion|emotion]] of being curious, in regard to the desire to gain knowledge or [[Information|information]]. Curiosity helps [[Developmental psychology|human development]], from which derives the process of [[Learning|learning]] and desire to acquire [[Knowledge|knowledge]] and [[Skill|skill]]. Curiosity as a behavior and emotion is the driving force behind human development, such as progress in [[Science|science]], [[Language|language]], and industry. Curiosity can be considered to be an [[Evolution|evolutionary]] [[Adaptation|adaptation]] based on an organism's ability to learn. Certain curious animals (namely, [[Corvidae|corvids]], [[Octopus|octopuses]], [[Dolphin|dolphins]], [[Elephant|elephants]], [[Rat|rats]], etc.) will pursue information in order to adapt to their surrounding and learn how things function. This behavior is termed neophilia, the love of new things. For animals, a fear of the unknown or the new, neophobia, is much more common, especially later in life.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Guilt (emotion)" },
	},
	{
		title = "Curriculum",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Educational plan",
		tags = { "Education" },
		summary = "In [[Education|education]], a **curriculum** is the totality of student experiences that occur in an educational process. The term often refers specifically to a planned sequence of instruction, or to a view of the student's experiences in terms of the educator's or school's instructional goals. A curriculum may incorporate the planned interaction of pupils with instructional content, materials, resources, and processes for evaluating the attainment of educational objectives. Curricula are split into several categories: the explicit, the implicit (including the hidden), the excluded, and the extracurricular.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Curricula may be tightly standardized or may include a high level of instructor or learner autonomy. Many countries have national curricula in [[Primary education|primary]] and [[Secondary education|secondary education]], such as the United Kingdom's National Curriculum. [[UNESCO]]'s International Bureau of Education has the primary mission of studying curricula and their implementation worldwide.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Lesson" },
	},
