	{
		title = "Cyclotron",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of particle accelerator",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **cyclotron** is a type of [[Particle accelerator|particle accelerator]] invented by [[Ernest Lawrence]] in 1929–1930 at the [[University of California, Berkeley]], and patented in 1932. A cyclotron accelerates [[Charged particle|charged particles]] outwards from the center of a flat cylindrical vacuum chamber along a spiral path. The particles are held to a spiral trajectory by a static [[Magnetic field|magnetic field]] and accelerated by a rapidly varying [[Electric field|electric field]]. Lawrence was awarded the 1939 [[Nobel Prize in Physics]] for this invention.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The cyclotron was the first "cyclical" accelerator. The primary accelerators before the development of the cyclotron were electrostatic accelerators, such as the Cockcroft–Walton generator and the Van de Graaff generator. In these accelerators, particles would cross an accelerating [[Electric field|electric field]] only once. Thus, the energy gained by the particles was limited by the maximum electrical potential that could be achieved across the accelerating region. This potential was in turn limited by electrostatic breakdown to a few million volts. In a cyclotron, by contrast, the particles encounter the accelerating region many times by following a spiral path, so the output energy can be many times the energy gained in a single accelerating step. Cyclotrons were the most powerful particle accelerator technology until the 1950s, when they were surpassed by the synchrotron.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cylinder",
