	},
	{
		title = "Diadochi",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Political rivals in the aftermath of Alexander the Great's death",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Diadochi** were the rival generals, families, and friends of [[Alexander the Great]] who fought for control over his empire after his death in 323 BC. The Wars of the Diadochi mark the beginning of the [[Hellenistic period]] from the [[Mediterranean Sea]] to the Indus River Valley.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The most notable Diadochi include Perdicas, Eumenes, Lysimachus, Cassander, [[Ptolemy I Soter|Ptolemy]], [[Seleucus I Nicator|Seleucus]], [[Antigonus I Monophthalmus|Antigonus]] and his son Demetrius Poliorcetes, with the last ones founding Ptolemaic, Seleucid and Antigonid dynasties respectively, that lasted for several centuries after the end of the Wars of the Successors, ruling in [[Egypt]], [[Levant]], [[Anatolia|Asia-Minor]], [[Mesopotamia]], [[Achaemenid Empire|Persia]] and [[Macedonia (ancient kingdom)|Macedon]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dialect",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Variant of a language",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		summary = "A **dialect** is a [[Variety (linguistics)|variety]] of language spoken by a particular group of people. This may include dominant and [[Standard language|standardized]] varieties as well as [[Vernacular|vernacular]], unwritten, or non-standardized varieties, such as those used in developing countries or isolated areas. The dialects of the same language are mainly distinguished from each other by differences in linguistic features such as [[Phonology|phonology]], [[Morphology (linguistics)|morphology]], [[Syntax|syntax]] and [[Vocabulary|vocabulary]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The term "dialect" is most often used to refer to regional dialects—i.e., linguistic varieties that are spoken in a particular geographical region, distinguished from those of other regions. Varieties associated with groups defined in other ways may be referred to as "dialects", or with more specialized terms such as sociolect (the dialect of a [[Social class|social class]]) and ethnolect (the dialect of an [[Ethnicity|ethnic group]]). An idiolect is the unique variety of language used by a particular individual. The non-standard dialects of a language with a [[Writing system|writing system]] will operate at different degrees of distance from the standardized written form. An oral dialect or idiolect may be portrayed in written form with eye dialect.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dialect continuum",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Geographic range of dialects that vary more strongly at the distant ends",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Dialect chain" },
		summary = "A **dialect continuum** or **dialect chain** is a series of [[Variety (linguistics)|language varieties]] spoken across some geographical area such that neighboring varieties are [[Mutual intelligibility|mutually intelligible]], but the differences accumulate over distance so that widely separated varieties may not be. This is a typical occurrence with widely spread languages and language families around the world, when these languages did not spread recently. Some prominent examples include the [[Indo-Aryan languages]] across large parts of [[India]], [[Varieties of Arabic|varieties of Arabic]] across north Africa and southwest Asia, the [[Turkic languages]], the [[Varieties of Chinese|varieties of Chinese]], and parts of the [[Romance languages|Romance]], [[Germanic languages|Germanic]] and [[Slavic languages|Slavic]] families in Europe.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Terms used in older literature include **dialect area** (Leonard Bloomfield) and **L-complex** (Charles F. Hockett). Dialect continua typically occur in long-settled agrarian populations, as innovations spread from their various points of origin as waves. In this situation, hierarchical classifications of varieties are impractical. Instead, dialectologists map variation of various language features across a dialect continuum, drawing lines called isoglosses between areas that differ with respect to some feature. A variety within a dialect continuum may be developed and codified as a [[Standard language|standard language]], and then serve as an authority for part of the continuum, e.g. within a particular political unit or geographical area.",
			},
		},
		related = { "National Medal of Science" },
	},
	{
		title = "Dialectic",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Method of reasoning and philosophical argument",
		tags = { "Philosophy", "Logic" },
		aliases = { "Dialectical method" },
		summary = "**Dialectic**, also known as the **dialectical method,** is a method of [[Reason|reasoning]] and [[Philosophy|philosophical]] argument that emphasizes the progression of thought through a process of resolving [[Contradiction|contradictions]]. The term, rooted in the [[Ancient Greek]] dialegesthai ('to [[Conversation|converse]]'), was popularized by [[Plato]]'s [[Socratic dialogue|Socratic dialogues]], which presented dialectic as a discourse between two or more people holding different points of view about a subject but wishing to establish the [[Truth|truth]] through reasoned argumentation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Throughout its history, the concept of dialectic has undergone significant transformations. In antiquity, it was developed as a form of "objective dialectic" by [[Pre-Socratic philosophy|pre-Socratic philosophers]] like [[Heraclitus]], who focused on the cosmic principles of change and the unity of opposites. This was followed by a "subjective dialectic" pioneered by the [[Sophist|Sophists]] and [[Socrates]], which shifted the focus to human [[Consciousness|consciousness]] and the art of argumentation. [[Plato]] elevated dialectic to the supreme method of philosophical inquiry, viewing it as the path to understanding the transcendent [[Theory of forms|Forms]]. His student, [[Aristotle]], redefined it as a method for critically examining reputable opinions (endoxa). In the modern era, particularly within [[German idealism]], dialectic was revived and systematized.',
			},
		},
	},
}
]]></string>
          </Properties>
