		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Historical grouping of flowering plants",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Dicotyledons", "Dicots", "Dicotyls" },
		summary = "The **dicotyledons**, also known as **dicots** (or, more rarely, **dicotyls**), are one of the two groups into which all the [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] (angiosperms) were formerly divided. The name refers to one of the typical characteristics of the group: namely, that the [[Seed|seed]] has two embryonic leaves or cotyledons. There are around 200,000 [[Species|species]] within this group. The other group of flowering plants were called [[Monocotyledon|monocotyledons]] (or monocots), typically each having one cotyledon. Historically, these two groups formed the two divisions of the flowering plants.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Largely from the 1990s onwards, molecular phylogenetic research confirmed what had already been suspected: that dicotyledons are not a group made up of all the descendants of a common ancestor (i.e., they are not a [[Monophyly|monophyletic]] group). Rather, a number of lineages, such as the [[Magnoliids|magnoliids]] and groups now collectively known as the basal angiosperms, diverged earlier than the monocots did; in other words, monocots evolved from within the dicots, as traditionally defined. The traditional dicots are thus a [[Paraphyly|paraphyletic]] group. The [[Eudicots|eudicots]] are the largest monophyletic group within the dicotyledons. They are distinguished from all other flowering plants by the structure of their [[Pollen|pollen]]. Other dicotyledons and the monocotyledons have monosulcate pollen (or derived forms): grains with a single sulcus.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dictatorship",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Autocratic form of government",
		tags = { "Politics and government", "Forms of government" },
		summary = "A **dictatorship** is a form of government which is characterized by a leader who holds [[Autocracy|absolute or near-absolute political power]]. Politics in a dictatorship are controlled by a dictator, and they are facilitated through an inner circle of elites that includes advisers, generals, and other high-ranking officials. The dictator maintains control by influencing and appeasing the inner circle and repressing any opposition, which may include rival political parties, armed resistance, or disloyal members of the dictator's inner circle.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Dictatorships can be formed by a military coup that overthrows the previous government through force or they can be formed by a self-coup in which elected leaders make their rule permanent. Dictatorships are [[Authoritarianism|authoritarian]] or [[Totalitarianism|totalitarian]], and they can be classified as [[Military dictatorship|military dictatorships]], [[One-party state|one-party dictatorships]], and personalist dictatorships. The Latin word dictator originated in the early [[Roman Republic]] to refer to a constitutional office with "a temporary grant of absolute power to a leader to handle some emergency." Modern dictatorships first developed in the 19th century, which included Bonapartism in Europe and caudillos in Latin America.',
			},
		},
	},
