	{
		title = "Diglossia",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Community restriction of languages or dialects to specific settings",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		summary = 'In [[Linguistics|linguistics]], **diglossia** is where two [[Dialect|dialects]] or [[Language|languages]] are used (in fairly strict compartmentalization) by a single language community. In addition to the community\'s everyday or [[Vernacular|vernacular]] language variety (labeled "L" or "low" variety), a second, highly codified [[Variety (linguistics)|lect]] (labeled "H" or "high") is used in certain situations such as [[Literature|literature]], [[Education|formal education]], or other specific settings, but not used normally for ordinary conversation. The H variety may have no [[First language|native speakers]] within the community. In cases of three dialects, the term **triglossia** is used. When referring to two writing systems coexisting for a single language, the term digraphia is used.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The high variety may be an older stage of the same language (as in [[Middle Ages|medieval Europe]], where [[Latin]] (H) remained in formal use even as [[Romance languages|colloquial speech]] (L) diverged), an unrelated language, or a distinct yet closely related present-day dialect.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dignity",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Person's right to be valued, respected and treated ethically",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Emotions and traits" },
		summary = '**Dignity** is the [[Rights|right]] of a person to be valued and respected for their own sake, and to be treated ethically. In this context, it is of significance in [[Morality|morality]], [[Ethics|ethics]], [[Law|law]] and [[Politics|politics]] as an extension of the [[Age of Enlightenment|Enlightenment]]-era concepts of inherent, inalienable rights. The term may also be used to describe personal conduct, as in "behaving with dignity".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The content of contemporary dignity is derived from the [[Universal Declaration of Human Rights]] of 1948, summarized in the principle that every human being has the right to human dignity. In Article 1, it is stipulated that 'All human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights. They are endowed with reason and conscience and should act towards one another in a spirit of brotherhood\".",
