	{
		title = "Domestic worker",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Person who works within the employer's household",
		tags = { "Business and economics", "Employment" },
		aliases = { "Domestic servant" },
		summary = 'A **domestic worker** or **domestic servant** is a person who works within a residence and performs a variety of household services for an individual, from providing cleaning and household maintenance, or [[Cooking|cooking]], [[Laundry|laundry]] and [[Ironing|ironing]], or care for children and elderly dependents, and other household errands. The term "domestic service" applies to the equivalent occupational category. In traditional English contexts, such a person was said to be "in service".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Some domestic workers live within their employer\'s household. In some cases, the contribution and skill of servants whose work encompassed complex management tasks in large households have been highly valued. However, for the most part, domestic work tends to be demanding and is commonly considered to be undervalued, despite often being necessary. Although legislation protecting domestic workers is in place in many countries, it is often not extensively enforced. Servant is an older English word for "domestic worker", though not all servants worked inside the home. Domestic service, or the employment of people for wages in their employer\'s residence, was sometimes simply called "service" and has often been part of a hierarchical system.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dominance hierarchy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of socially subordinate ranking",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Pecking order" },
		summary = "In the zoological field of [[Ethology|ethology]], a **dominance hierarchy** (formerly and colloquially called a **pecking order**) is a type of social [[Hierarchy|hierarchy]] that arises when members of animal social groups interact, creating a ranking system.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In social living groups, members are likely to compete for access to limited resources and [[Mating|mating]] opportunities. Based on repetitive interactions, a social order is created that is subject to change each time a dominant animal is challenged by a subordinate one. In [[Eusociality|eusocial]] animals, whether mammals or insects, aggressive interactions often lead to the suppression of reproduction in non-dominant individuals. Such interactions may be ritualised, and an individual's resulting rank in the dominance hierarchy may be advertised to other individuals by visual or chemical cues. Suppression operates in some species on the reproductive hormones of non-dominant individuals. Dominance hierarchies exist in many bird species, first observed in the domestic chicken, where the hierarchy is maintained by pecking with the beak.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dominion of India",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "British dominion in southern Asia from 1947 to 1950",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Union of India" },
		summary = "The **Dominion of India**, officially the **Union of India**, was an independent [[Dominion|dominion]] in the British [[Commonwealth of Nations]] existing between 15 August 1947 and 26 January 1950. Until its [[Indian independence movement|independence]], India had been ruled as an informal empire by the United Kingdom. The empire, also called the [[British Raj]] and sometimes the British Indian Empire, consisted of regions, collectively called [[Presidencies and provinces of British India|British India]], that were directly administered by the British government, and regions, called the princely states, that were ruled by Indian rulers under a system of paramountcy, in favour of the British. The Dominion of India was formalised by the passage of the Indian Independence Act 1947.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'This same Act also formalised an independent [[Dominion of Pakistan]], which was composed of the two regions of British India that are today [[Pakistan]] and [[Bangladesh]]. The Dominion of India was also referred to as "India" in common parlance, but it differed from British India in that it was now independent and geographically smaller: large swathes of the former territory now formed the also newly created but now distinct Dominion of Pakistan. Under the Act, the King remained the monarch in right of new dominions, but the British government relinquished all responsibility for the governing of its former territories. The British Government also revoked its treaty rights and obligations with the rulers of the more than 500 princely states within the region, and instead advised them to join in a political union with either India or Pakistan.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Capital", "[[New Delhi]]" },
			{ "People are called", "Indian" },
			{ "Government", "Federal [[Constitutional monarchy|parliamentary constitutional monarchy]]" },
			{ "Currency", "Indian rupee" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dominion of Pakistan",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "British dominion in South Asia from 1947 to 1956",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Dominion of Pakistan**, officially **Pakistan**, was the [[Pakistan|Pakistani state]] between 1947 and 1956, when it existed as an independent federal [[Dominion|dominion]] in the British [[Commonwealth of Nations]]. It was established on 14 August 1947 when it was created from [[Presidencies and provinces of British India|British India]] under the Indian Independence Act 1947 of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|British parliament]], which also established an independent [[Dominion of India]] on 15 August. The dominion ceased to exist on 23 March 1956, when the Constituent Assembly of Pakistan adopted a constitution which established Pakistan as a [[Republic|republic]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The new dominion consisted of those [[Presidencies and provinces of British India|presidencies and provinces of British India]] which were allocated to it in the Partition of India. Until 1947, these regions had been ruled by the United Kingdom as a part of the [[British Empire]]. Its status as a federal dominion ended in 1956 with the completion of the constitution of Pakistan, which established the country as a republic. The constitution also administratively split the nation into West Pakistan and East Pakistan. Until then, these provinces had been governed as a singular entity, despite being separate geographic [[Enclave and exclave|exclaves]]. Eventually, the East became Bangladesh and the West became Pakistan. During the year that followed its independence, the new country was joined by the princely states of Pakistan, which were ruled by princes who had previously been in subsidiary alliances with the British.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Government", "Federal [[Constitutional monarchy|constitutional monarchy]]" },
			{ "Capital", "[[Karachi]]" },
			{ "Largest city", "[[Dhaka|Dacca]]" },
			{ "Official languages", "[[English language|English]]" },
			{ "National languages", "[[Urdu]], [[Bengali language|Bengali]]" },
			{ "Currency", "Indian rupee (1947–1948) Pakistani rupee (1948–1956)" },
			{ "Area", "1,030,373 km²" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dominoes",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of tile-based games",
		tags = { "Entertainment", "Games" },
		summary = "**Dominoes** are a family of tile-based games played with pieces. Each domino is a rectangular tile, usually with a line dividing its face into two square ends. Each end is marked with a number of spots (also called pips or dots) or is blank. The backs of the tiles in a set are indistinguishable, either blank or having some common design. The gaming pieces make up a domino set, sometimes called a deck or pack. The traditional European domino set consists of 28 tiles, also known as pieces, bones, rocks, stones, men, cards or just dominoes, featuring all combinations of spot counts between zero and six.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A domino set is a generic gaming device, similar to playing cards or [[Dice|dice]], in that a variety of games can be played with a set. Another form of entertainment using domino pieces is the practice of domino toppling. The earliest mention of dominoes is from [[Song dynasty]] China found in the text Former Events in Wulin by Zhou Mi (1232–1298). Modern dominoes first appeared in France during the mid-18th century, but they differ from Chinese dominoes in a number of respects, and there is no confirmed link between the two. European dominoes may have developed independently, or Italian missionaries in China may have brought the game to Europe, although there is no evidence of this. The name "domino" is probably derived from the resemblance to a kind of carnival costume worn during the Venetian Carnival, often consisting of a black-hooded robe and a white mask.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Game", "Mahjong" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Genre", "Tile-based game" },
			{ "Players", "Varies depending on game" },
			{ "Playing time", "Varies depending on game" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Donald Trump",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "President of the United States (2017–2021; since 2025)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Donald John Trump" },
		summary = "**Donald John Trump** is an American politician, media personality, and businessman who is the 47th [[President of the United States|president of the United States]]. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he served as the 45th president from 2017 to 2021.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Born into a wealthy New York City family, Trump graduated from the [[University of Pennsylvania]] in 1968 with a bachelor's degree in economics. He launched side ventures, many licensing the Trump name, and filed for six business bankruptcies in the 1990s and 2000s. From 2004 to 2015, he hosted the reality television show The Apprentice, bolstering his reputation as a wealthy tycoon. Presenting himself as a political outsider, Trump won the 2016 presidential election against [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]] nominee Hillary Clinton. During his first presidency, Trump imposed a travel ban on seven Muslim-majority countries, expanded the Mexico–United States border wall, and enforced a family separation policy on the border. He rolled back environmental and business regulations, signed the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, and appointed three Supreme Court justices.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "14 June 1946" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Queens, New York City, U.S." },
			{ "Education", "[[University of Pennsylvania]] ([[Bachelor of Science|BS]])" },
			{ "Occupation", "Politician, businessman, media personality" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dopant",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Substance added to material to alter its properties",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Doping agent" },
		summary = "A **dopant** (also called a **doping agent**) is a small amount of a substance added to a material to alter its physical properties, such as electrical or [[Optics|optical]] properties. The amount of dopant is typically very low compared to the material being doped.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When doped into [[Crystal|crystalline]] substances, the dopant's atoms get incorporated into the crystal lattice of the substance. The crystalline materials are frequently either crystals of a [[Semiconductor|semiconductor]] such as [[Silicon|silicon]] and [[Germanium|germanium]] for use in solid-state electronics, or [[Transparency and translucency|transparent]] crystals for use in the production of various [[Laser|laser]] types; however, in some cases of the latter, noncrystalline substances such as [[Glass|glass]] can also be doped with impurities. In solid-state electronics using the proper types and amounts of dopants in semiconductors is what produces the p-type semiconductors and n-type semiconductors that are essential for making [[Transistor|transistors]] and [[Diode|diodes]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Double First-Class Construction",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎓",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ongoing Chinese higher education development scheme",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Together known as" },
		summary = 'The **World First-Class Universities and First-Class Academic Disciplines Construction**, together known as **Double First-Class Initiative**, is a higher education development and sponsorship scheme of the Chinese central government, initiated in 2015. The universities included in the Double First Class Initiative are recognized as "**Double First-Class Universities**". There are 147 universities and colleges selected to be part of the program.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The program was announced under the Xi Jinping administration in 2015 to replace the original higher education development programs Project 211 and Project 985.",
