	{
		title = "Doubt",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Status between belief and disbelief",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Emotions and traits" },
		summary = "**Doubt** is a [[Mental state|mental state]] in which the [[Mind|mind]] remains suspended between two or more contradictory [[Proposition|propositions]], and is uncertain about them. Doubt on an [[Emotion|emotional]] level is indecision between [[Belief|belief]] and disbelief. It may involve uncertainty, distrust or lack of conviction on certain [[Fact|facts]], actions, motives, or [[Decision-making|decisions]]. Doubt can result in delaying or rejecting relevant action out of concern for mistakes or missed opportunities.",
	},
	{
		title = "Down quark",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of quark",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **down quark** (symbol: d) is a type of [[Elementary particle|elementary particle]], and a major constituent of [[Matter|matter]]. The down quark is the second-lightest of all [[Quark|quarks]], and combines with other quarks to form composite particles called hadrons. Down quarks are most commonly found in [[Atomic nucleus|atomic nuclei]], where they combine with [[Up quark|up quarks]] to form [[Proton|protons]] and [[Neutron|neutrons]]. The proton is made of one down quark with two up quarks, and the neutron is made up of two down quarks with one up quark. Because they are found in every single known atom, down quarks are present in all everyday matter that we interact with.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The down quark is part of the first generation of matter, has an [[Electric charge|electric charge]] of −1/3 [[Elementary charge|e]] and a [[Quark|bare mass]] of 4.7 MeV/c2. Like all quarks, the down quark is an elementary [[Fermion|fermion]] with [[Spin (physics)|spin]] 1/2, and experiences all four [[Fundamental interaction|fundamental interactions]]: [[Gravity|gravitation]], [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]], [[Weak interaction|weak interactions]], and [[Strong interaction|strong interactions]]. The [[Antiparticle|antiparticle]] of the down quark is the **down antiquark** (sometimes called antidown quark or simply antidown), which differs from it only in that some of its properties have equal magnitude but opposite sign. Its existence (along with that of the up and strange quarks) was postulated in 1964 by [[Murray Gell-Mann]] and George Zweig to explain the Eightfold Way classification scheme of [[Hadron|hadrons]]. The down quark was first observed by experiments at the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center in 1968.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Discovered", "SLAC (1968)" },
			{ "Mass", "4.7 MeV/c2" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Drainage divide",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Elevated terrain that separates neighbouring drainage basins",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Water divide", "Ridgeline", "Watershed" },
		summary = "A **drainage divide**, **water divide**, **ridgeline**, **watershed**, **water parting** or **height of land** is elevated [[Terrain|terrain]] that separates neighboring [[Drainage basin|drainage basins]]. On rugged land, the divide lies along topographical ridges, and may be in the form of a single range of [[Hill|hills]] or [[Mountain|mountains]], known as a **dividing range**. On flat terrain, especially where the ground is [[Marsh|marshy]], the divide may be difficult to discern.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A triple divide is a point, often a summit, where three drainage basins meet. A valley floor divide is a low drainage divide that runs across a [[Valley|valley]], sometimes created by [[Deposition (geology)|deposition]] or stream capture. Major divides separating rivers that drain to different seas or oceans are continental divides. The term height of land is used in Canada and the United States to refer to a drainage divide. It is frequently used in border descriptions, which are set according to the "doctrine of natural boundaries". In [[Glacier|glaciated]] areas it often refers to a low point on a divide where it is possible to portage a canoe from one river system to another.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dravidian languages",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Language family",
		tags = { "Language", "Language families" },
		summary = "The **Dravidian languages** are a [[Language family|family of languages]] spoken by 250 million people, primarily in [[South India]], but also in parts of [[North India]], [[Bangladesh]] and [[Nepal]], north-east [[Sri Lanka]], and south-west [[Pakistan]], with pockets in [[Afghanistan]] and [[Iran]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The most commonly spoken Dravidian languages are (in descending order) [[Telugu language|Telugu]], [[Tamil language|Tamil]], [[Kannada]], and [[Malayalam]], all of which [[Classical languages of India|have long literary traditions]]. Smaller literary languages are Tulu and Kodava. Together with several smaller languages such as Gondi, these languages cover the southern part of India and the northeast of [[Sri Lanka]], and account for the overwhelming majority of speakers of Dravidian languages. Malto and Kurukh are spoken in isolated pockets in eastern India. Kurukh is also spoken in parts of [[Nepal]], [[Bhutan]] and [[Bangladesh]]. Brahui is mostly spoken in the Balochistan region of [[Pakistan]], Iranian Balochistan, [[Afghanistan]] and around the Marw oasis in [[Turkmenistan]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Region", "[[South India]], north-east and central [[Sri Lanka]] and south-west [[Pakistan]]" },
			{ "Family", "One of the world's primary language families" },
			{ "Speakers", "250 million" },
			{ "Date", "2020" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dredging",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Excavation of sediment, usually under water",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Dredging** is the excavation of material from a water environment. Possible reasons for dredging include improving existing water features; reshaping land and water features to alter [[Drainage|drainage]], navigability, and commercial use; constructing [[Dam|dams]], [[Levee|dikes]], and other controls for streams and shorelines; and recovering valuable mineral deposits or marine life having commercial value. In all but a few situations the excavation is undertaken by a specialist floating plant, known as a dredger.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Usually the main objectives of dredging is to recover material of value, or to create a greater depth of water. Dredging systems can either be shore-based, brought to a location based on [[Barge|barges]], or built into purpose-built vessels. Dredging can have environmental impacts: it can disturb marine sediments, creating dredge plumes which can lead to both short- and long-term [[Water pollution|water pollution]], damage or destroy [[Marine ecosystem|seabed ecosystems]], and release legacy human-sourced toxins captured in the sediment. These environmental impacts can reduce marine wildlife populations, contaminate sources of drinking water, and interrupt economic activities such as fishing.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
