	{
		title = "Ductility",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Degree to which a material under stress irreversibly deforms before failure",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Malleable", "Ductile" },
		summary = "**Ductility** is the ability of a material to sustain significant plastic [[Deformation (engineering)|deformation]] before fracture when undergoing tension, i.e. when the relevant [[Elastic modulus|elastic modulus]] is Young's; the equivalent for deforming under bulk compression, i.e. when using the bulk modulus, is **malleability**. Historically, materials were considered malleable if they were amenable to forming by hammering or rolling. [[Lead]] is an example of a material which is substantially more malleable than ductile. Plastic deformation is the permanent distortion of a material under applied stress, as opposed to elastic deformation, which is reversible upon removing the stress.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ductility is a critical mechanical performance indicator, particularly in applications that require materials to bend, stretch, or deform in other ways without breaking. The extent of ductility can be quantitatively assessed using the percent elongation at break, given by the equation: This formula helps in quantifying how much a material can stretch under tensile stress before failure, providing key insights into its ductile behavior. Ductility is an important consideration in engineering and manufacturing. It defines a material's suitability for certain manufacturing operations (such as cold working) and its capacity to absorb mechanical overload like in an engine. Some metals that are generally described as ductile include [[Gold|gold]] Plutonium, Uranium and [[Copper|copper]], while [[Platinum|platinum]] is the most ductile of all metals in pure form.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Duke of Burgundy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Title used by the rulers of the Duchy of Burgundy",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Duke of Burgundy** was a title used by the rulers of the [[Duchy of Burgundy]], from its establishment in 843 to its annexation by the French crown in 1477, and later by members of the [[House of Habsburg]], including [[Holy Roman Emperor|Holy Roman Emperors]] and kings of Spain, who claimed Burgundy proper and ruled the Burgundian Netherlands.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Duchy of Burgundy was a small portion of the traditional lands of the [[Burgundians]] west of the river Saône which, in 843, was allotted to Charles the Bald's kingdom of the West Franks. Under the [[Ancien régime|Ancien Régime]], the duke of Burgundy was the premier lay peer of the [[Kingdom of France]]. Beginning with Robert II of France, the title was held by the Capetians, the French royal family. In 1032 King Henry I of France granted the duchy to his younger brother, Robert, who founded the House of Burgundy. John granted the duchy to his younger son, Philip the Bold, in 1363. The Valois dukes gradually came to rule over a vast complex of territories known as the Burgundian State, and became dangerous rivals to the senior French royal line of the [[House of Valois]]. When the male line of the Valois dukes of Burgundy became extinct in 1477, the Duchy of Burgundy was confiscated by Louis XI of France.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Conservation status", "Extinct" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Duke of Normandy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Medieval ruler of the Duchy of Normandy",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In the [[Middle Ages]], the **duke of Normandy** was the ruler of the [[Duchy of Normandy]]. The duchy arose out of a grant of land to the [[Vikings|Viking]] leader Rollo by the French king Charles the Simple in 911. In 924 and again in 933, Normandy was expanded by royal grant. Rollo's male-line descendants continued to rule it until 1135, and cognatic descendants ruled it until 1204. In 1202 King [[Philip II of France]] declared Normandy a forfeited fief and by 1204 his army had conquered it (with the exception of the Channel Islands). It remained a French royal province thereafter, still called the Duchy of Normandy, but only occasionally granted to a duke of the royal house as an appanage.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Despite both the 13th century loss of mainland Normandy, the renunciation of the title by King Henry III of England in the Treaty of Paris (1259), and the extinction of the duchy itself in modern-day France, the monarch of the United Kingdom is regardless still often informally referred to by the title "duke of Normandy". This is the title used whether the monarch is a king or a queen.',
