	{
		title = "Effects of climate change",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "Effects of [[Climate change|climate change]] are well documented and growing for Earth's [[Natural environment|natural environment]] and human societies. Changes to the [[Climate system|climate system]] include an overall warming trend, changes to precipitation patterns, and more [[Extreme weather|extreme weather]]. As the climate changes it impacts the natural environment with effects such as more intense forest fires, thawing [[Permafrost|permafrost]], and [[Desertification|desertification]]. These changes impact ecosystems and societies, and can become irreversible once tipping points are crossed. Climate activists are engaged in a range of activities around the world that seek to ameliorate these issues or prevent them from happening.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The **effects of climate change** vary in timing and location. Up until now the Arctic has warmed faster than most other regions due to climate change feedbacks. Surface air temperatures over land have also increased at about twice the rate they do over the ocean, causing intense heat waves. These temperatures would stabilize if [[Greenhouse gas emissions|greenhouse gas emissions]] were [[Climate change mitigation|brought under control]]. Ice sheets and oceans absorb the vast majority of excess heat in the atmosphere, delaying effects there but causing them to accelerate and then continue after surface temperatures stabilize. [[Sea level rise]] is a particular long term concern as a result. The effects of ocean warming also include marine heatwaves, ocean stratification, deoxygenation, and changes to ocean currents. The [[Ocean acidification|ocean is also acidifying]] as it absorbs carbon dioxide from the atmosphere.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Egg",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🥚",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Organic vessel in which an embryo first begins to develop",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'An **egg** is an organic vessel grown by an animal to carry a possibly [[Fertilisation|fertilized]] egg cell – a [[Zygote|zygote]]. Within the vessel, an [[Embryo|embryo]] is incubated until it has become an animal fetus that can survive on its own, at which point the animal hatches. Reproductive structures similar to the egg in other [[Kingdom (taxonomy)|kingdoms]] are termed "[[Spore|spores]]", or in spermatophytes "[[Seed|seeds]]", or in [[Gametophyte|gametophytes]] "egg cells".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most [[Arthropod|arthropods]], vertebrates (excluding live-bearing mammals), and [[Mollusca|mollusks]] lay eggs, although some, such as [[Scorpion|scorpions]], do not. [[Reptile]] eggs, [[Bird egg|bird eggs]], and [[Monotreme|monotreme]] eggs are laid out of water and are surrounded by a protective shell, either flexible or inflexible. Eggs laid on land or in nests are usually kept within a warm and favorable temperature range while the embryo grows. When the embryo is adequately developed it hatches; i.e., breaks out of the egg's shell. Some embryos have a temporary egg tooth they use to crack, pip, or break the eggshell or covering. For people, [[Eggs as food|eggs are a popular food]] item, and they appear on menus worldwide. Eggs remain an important symbol in folklore and mythology, symbolizing life, healing, and rebirth. They are frequently the subject of decoration. Egg collecting has been popular in some cultures, although the practice is now banned in many jurisdictions.",
			},
