		description = "Process of changing something to use electricity",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Electrification** is the process of powering by [[Electricity|electricity]] and, in many contexts, the introduction of such power by changing over from an earlier power source. In the context of [[History of technology|history of technology]] and [[Economic development|economic development]], electrification refers to the build-out of the [[Electricity generation|electricity generation]] and [[Electric power distribution|electric power distribution]] systems. In the context of sustainable energy, electrification refers to the build-out of super grids and smart grids with distributed energy resources (such as energy storage) to accommodate the energy transition to [[Renewable energy|renewable energy]] and the switch of end-uses to electricity.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The electrification of particular sectors of the economy, particularly out of context, is called by modified terms such as [[Mass production|factory electrification]], household electrification, rural electrification and railway electrification. In the context of sustainable energy, terms such as [[Electric vehicle|transport electrification]] (referring to [[Electric vehicle|electric vehicles]]) or heating electrification (referring to heat pumps powered with solar photovoltaics) are used. It may also apply to changing industrial processes, such as smelting, melting, separating or refining from coal or coke heating, or from chemical processes to some type of electric process such as electric arc furnace, electric induction or resistance heating, or [[Electrolysis|electrolysis]] or electrolytic separating.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Geometric albedo" },
	},
	{
		title = "Electrode",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Electrical conductor used to make contact with nonmetallic parts of a circuit",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **electrode** is an [[Electrical conductor|electrical conductor]] used to make contact with a nonmetallic part of a [[Electronic circuit|circuit]] (e.g. a [[Semiconductor|semiconductor]], an [[Electrolyte|electrolyte]], a [[Vacuum|vacuum]] or a gas). In electrochemical cells, electrodes are essential parts that can consist of a variety of materials (chemicals) depending on the type of cell. An electrode may be called either a [[Cathode|cathode]] or [[Anode|anode]] according to the direction of the electric current, unrelated to the potential difference between electrodes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'[[Michael Faraday]] coined the term "electrode" in 1833; the word recalls the Greek ἤ and ὁ. The electrophore, invented by Johan Wilcke in 1762, was an early version of an electrode used to study static electricity.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electrolyte",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Substance whose dissolved ions conduct electricity",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **electrolyte** is a substance that conducts [[Electricity|electricity]] through the movement of [[Ion|ions]], but not through the movement of [[Electron|electrons]]. This includes most soluble [[Salt (chemistry)|salts]], [[Acid|acids]], and [[Base (chemistry)|bases]], dissolved in a polar solvent like water. Upon dissolving, the substance separates into [[Ion|cations]] and [[Ion|anions]], which disperse uniformly throughout the solvent. Solid-state electrolytes also exist. In medicine and sometimes in chemistry, the term electrolyte refers to the substance that is dissolved.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Electrically, such a solution is neutral. If an [[Electric potential|electric potential]] is applied to such a solution, the cations of the solution are drawn to the [[Electrode|electrode]] that has an abundance of [[Electron|electrons]], while the anions are drawn to the electrode that has a deficit of electrons. The movement of anions and cations in opposite directions within the solution amounts to a current. Some gases, such as [[Hydrogen chloride|hydrogen chloride]] (HCl), under conditions of high temperature or low pressure can also function as electrolytes. Electrolyte solutions can also result from the dissolution of some biological (e.g., [[DNA]], polypeptides) or synthetic polymers (e.g., polystyrene sulfonate), termed "polyelectrolytes", which contain charged [[Functional group|functional groups]]. A substance that dissociates into ions in solution or when molten acquires the capacity to conduct electricity.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electronegativity",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tendency of an atom to attract a shared pair of electrons",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Electronegativity**, symbolized as , is the tendency for an [[Atom|atom]] of a given [[Chemical element|chemical element]] to attract shared [[Electron|electrons]] (or electron density) when forming a [[Chemical bond|chemical bond]]. An atom's electronegativity is affected by both its [[Atomic number|atomic number]] and the distance at which its [[Valence electron|valence electrons]] reside from the charged nucleus. The higher the associated electronegativity, the more an atom or a substituent group attracts electrons. Electronegativity serves as a simple way to quantitatively estimate the bond energy, and the sign and magnitude of a bond's [[Chemical polarity|chemical polarity]], which characterizes a bond along the continuous scale from covalent to [[Ionic bonding|ionic bonding]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The loosely defined term **electropositivity** is the opposite of electronegativity: it characterizes an element\'s tendency to donate valence electrons. On the most basic level, electronegativity is determined by factors like the nuclear charge (the more protons an atom has, the more "pull" it will have on electrons) and the number and location of other electrons in the [[Electron shell|electron shells]] (the more electrons an atom has, the farther from the [[Atomic nucleus|nucleus]] the valence electrons will be, and as a result, the less positive charge they will experience—both because of their increased distance from the nucleus and because the other electrons in the lower energy core [[Atomic orbital|orbitals]] will act to shield the valence electrons from the positively charged nucleus).',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electronic circuit",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Electrical circuit with active components",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **electronic circuit** is composed of individual [[Electronic component|electronic components]], such as [[Resistor|resistors]], [[Transistor|transistors]], [[Capacitor|capacitors]], [[Inductor|inductors]] and [[Diode|diodes]], connected by conductive [[Wire|wires]] or traces through which [[Electric current|electric current]] can flow. It is a type of electrical circuit. For a circuit to be referred to as electronic, rather than electrical, generally at least one [[Electronic component|active component]] must be present. The combination of components and wires allows various simple and complex operations to be performed: signals can be amplified, computations can be performed, and data can be moved from one place to another.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Circuits can be constructed of discrete components connected by individual pieces of wire, but today it is much more common to create interconnections by photolithographic techniques on a laminated substrate (a [[Printed circuit board|printed circuit board]] or PCB) and solder the components to these interconnections to create a finished circuit. In an [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuit]] or IC, the components and interconnections are formed on the same substrate, typically a [[Semiconductor|semiconductor]] such as doped [[Silicon|silicon]] or (less commonly) gallium arsenide. An electronic circuit can usually be categorized as an analog circuit, a digital circuit, or a mixed-signal circuit (a combination of analog circuits and digital circuits). The most widely used [[Semiconductor device|semiconductor device]] in electronic circuits is the MOSFET (metal–oxide–semiconductor field-effect transistor).",
			},
		},
		related = { "Group of 77" },
	},
	{
		title = "Electronic component",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Discrete device in an electronic system",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **electronic component** is any basic discrete electronic device or physical entity part of an [[Electronics|electronic system]] used to affect [[Electron|electrons]] or their associated [[Electromagnetic field|fields]]. Electronic components are mostly industrial products, available in a singular form and are not to be confused with electrical elements, which are conceptual abstractions representing idealized electronic components and elements. A datasheet for an electronic component is a technical document that provides detailed information about the component's specifications, characteristics, and performance.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Discrete circuits are made of individual electronic components that only perform one function each as packaged, which are known as discrete components, although strictly the term discrete component refers to such a component with [[Semiconductor|semiconductor]] material such as individual [[Transistor|transistors]]. Electronic components have a number of [[Electric terminal|electrical terminals]] or leads. These leads connect to other electrical components, often over wire, to create an [[Electrical network|electronic circuit]] with a particular function (for example an [[Amplifier|amplifier]], radio receiver, or oscillator). Basic electronic components may be packaged discretely, as arrays or networks of like components, or integrated inside of packages such as [[Semiconductor|semiconductor]] [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuits]], hybrid integrated circuits, or thick film devices.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electronics industry",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Industry that focuses in the electronics production",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **electronics industry** is the [[Industry (economics)|industry]] that produces electronic devices. It emerged in the 20th century and is today one of the largest global industries. Contemporary society uses a vast array of electronic devices, including the [[Consumer electronics|consumer electronics]] and home appliances most people are familiar with. The electronics industry builds electronics in factories [[Automation|automated]] in turn by many different kinds of electronic devices.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Electronic products today are primarily assembled from metal–oxide–semiconductor (MOS) [[Transistor|transistors]] and [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuits]], the latter principally by photolithography and often on [[Printed circuit board|printed circuit boards]]. Circuit boards are assembled largely using surface-mount technology, which typically involves the automated placement of electronic parts on circuit boards using pick-and-place machines. Surface-mount technology and pick-and-place [[Robot|robots]] make it possible to assemble large numbers of circuit boards at high speed. International regulation and environmental legislation have been developed to address the issues. The electronics industry consists of various branches. The central driving force behind the entire electronics industry is the semiconductor industry, which has annual sales of over US$481 billion as of 2018.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Double First-Class Construction" },
