		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Fundamental particle" },
		summary = "In the [[Standard Model]] of [[Particle physics|particle physics]], an **elementary particle** or **fundamental particle** is a [[Subatomic particle|subatomic particle]] that is not composed of other particles. The [[Standard Model]] recognizes seventeen distinct particles—twelve [[Fermion|fermions]] and five [[Boson|bosons]]. As a consequence of flavor and [[Quantum chromodynamics|color]] combinations and [[Antimatter|antimatter]], the fermions and bosons are known to have 48 and 13 variations, respectively. These 61 elementary particles include [[Electron|electrons]] and other [[Lepton|leptons]], [[Quark|quarks]], and the fundamental [[Boson|bosons]]. [[Subatomic particle|Subatomic particles]] such as [[Proton|protons]] or [[Neutron|neutrons]], which [[Quark|contain]] two or more elementary particles, are known as composite particles.",
