	{
		title = "Ellipsoid",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Quadric surface that looks like a deformed sphere",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **ellipsoid** is a surface that can be obtained from a [[Sphere|sphere]] by deforming it by means of directional scalings, or more generally, of an affine transformation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"An ellipsoid is a quadric surface; that is, a [[Surface (mathematics)|surface]] that may be defined as the zero set of a [[Polynomial|polynomial]] of degree two in three variables. Among quadric surfaces, an ellipsoid is characterized by either of the two following properties. Every planar [[Cross section (geometry)|cross section]] is either an [[Ellipse|ellipse]], or is empty, or is reduced to a single point. It is bounded, which means that it may be enclosed in a sufficiently large sphere. An ellipsoid has three pairwise [[Perpendicular|perpendicular]] axes of symmetry which intersect at a center of symmetry, called the center of the ellipsoid. The [[Line segment|line segments]] that are delimited on the axes of symmetry by the ellipsoid are called the principal axes, or simply axes of the ellipsoid. If the three axes have different lengths, the figure is a **triaxial ellipsoid** (rarely **scalene ellipsoid**), and the axes are uniquely defined.",
			},
		},
	},
