	{
		title = "Embryophyte",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Subclade of green plants, also known as land plants",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Embryophytes", "Embryophyta", "Plantae sensu strictissimo" },
		summary = 'The **embryophytes** are [[Plant|plants]] of the [[Clade|clade]] **Embryophyta**, also known as **Plantae sensu strictissimo** or **land plants**. They are complex multicellular [[Eukaryote|eukaryotes]] with [[Biological life cycle|haplodiplontic life cycles]]. The name "embryophyte" derives from their innovative characteristic of nurturing the [[Embryo|embryonic]] [[Sporophyte|sporophytes]] within the tissues of the parent [[Gametophyte|gametophytes]] during the early stages of multicellular development. They are the [[Dominance (ecology)|dominant]] group of autotrophs that make up the [[Vegetation|vegetation]] on [[Earth]]\'s [[Terrestrial ecosystem|terrestrial]] and [[Wetland|wetland]] [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]], and represent the quintessential concept of "plants" most familiar to the common knowledge. The study of embryophytes is called phytology.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Embryophytes consist of the relatively species-poor non-vascular plants and the much more species-rich [[Vascular plant|vascular plants]] (tracheophytes). The former are soft-bodied groundcovers that include three divisions of [[Bryophyte|bryophytes]], namely [[Moss|Bryophyta]] (mosses), Marchantiophyta (liverworts) and Anthocerotophyta (hornworts); while the latter are vertical-growing "higher plants" with lignified [[Plant stem|stem]], absorptive [[Root|roots]] and [[Stoma|stomatous]] [[Leaf|leaves]], which include all [[Neontology|extant]] polysporangiates such as [[Lycophyte|lycophytes]] (clubmosses, firmosses, spikemosses and quillworts), polypodiophytes (ferns), [[Gymnosperm|gymnosperms]] ([[Conifer|conifers]], [[Cycad|cycads]], gnetophytes and gingko) and [[Flowering plant|angiosperms]] (flowering plants). Since their boom during the mid/late [[Cretaceous]], angiosperms have been the dominant group of embryophytes.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Embryophytes" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Emergency management",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Dealing with all humanitarian aspects of emergencies",
		tags = { "Society", "Services and institutions" },
		aliases = { "Disaster management", "Emergency preparedness" },
		summary = "**Disaster management** (also called **Emergency management** or **emergency preparedness**) is the systematic reduction of a community's vulnerability to natural and man-made hazards as well as the coordinated response to disasters after they occur. Professional emergency management activities include search and rescue, providing for the basic needs of affected individuals, and issuing evacuation alerts. The management of disasters requires collaboration between individuals, households, non-governmental organizations, and local, provincial, and federal governments.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although many different terminologies exist globally, the activities of emergency management can be generally categorized into preparedness, response, mitigation, and recovery, although other terms such as disaster risk reduction and prevention are also common. The outcome of emergency management is to prevent disasters and where this is not possible, to reduce their harmful impacts.",
