	{
		title = "Environmental degradation",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Any change or disturbance to the environment perceived to be deleterious or undesirable",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Environmental degradation** is the deterioration of the environment through depletion of resources such as quality of [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]], [[Water|water]] and [[Soil|soil]]; the destruction of ecosystems; [[Habitat destruction|habitat destruction]]; the [[Extinction|extinction]] of [[Wildlife|wildlife]]; and [[Pollution|pollution]]. It is defined as any change or disturbance to the environment perceived to be deleterious or undesirable. The environmental degradation process amplifies the impact of environmental issues which leave lasting impacts on the environment.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Environmental degradation is one of the ten threats officially cautioned by the High-level Panel on Threats, Challenges and Change of the [[United Nations]]. The United Nations International Strategy for Disaster Reduction defines environmental degradation as "the reduction of the capacity of the environment to meet social and ecological objectives, and needs". Environmental degradation comes in many types. When [[Habitat destruction|natural habitats are destroyed]] or [[Natural resource|natural resources]] are depleted, the environment is degraded; direct environmental degradation, such as deforestation, which is readily visible; this can be caused by more indirect process, such as the build up of plastic pollution over time or the buildup of greenhouse gases that causes tipping points in the climate system. Efforts to counteract this problem include environmental protection and environmental resources management.',
			},
		},
		related = { "The Independent" },
	},
	{
		title = "Environmentalism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🤔",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Philosophy about Earth protection",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Environmentalism** is a broad philosophy, [[Ideology|ideology]], and social movement about supporting [[Life|life]], [[Habitat|habitats]], and surroundings. While environmentalism focuses on the environmental and nature-related aspects of green ideology and politics, **ecologism** combines the ideology of social ecology and environmentalism. Ecologism is a term more commonly used in continental European languages, while environmentalism is more commonly used in English, but the words have slightly different connotations.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Environmentalism advocates the preservation, restoration and improvement of the [[Natural environment|natural environment]] and critical earth system elements or processes such as the [[Climate|climate]], and may be referred to as a movement to control [[Pollution|pollution]] or protect plant and animal [[Biodiversity|diversity]]. For this reason, concepts such as a land ethics, environmental ethics, [[Biodiversity|biodiversity]], [[Ecology|ecology]], and the biophilia hypothesis figure predominantly. The environmentalist movement encompasses various approaches to addressing environmental issues, including free market environmentalism, evangelical environmentalism, and the environmental conservation movement. At its crux, environmentalism is an attempt to balance relations between humans and the various natural systems on which they depend in such a way that all the components are accorded a proper degree of [[Sustainability|sustainability]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Epic Cycle",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "History of the Trojan War told in poems",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Epic Cycle** was a collection of [[Ancient Greek]] [[Epic poetry|epic poems]], composed in [[Dactylic hexameter|dactylic hexameter]] and related to the story of the [[Trojan War]], including the Cypria, the Aethiopis, the so-called Little Iliad, the Iliupersis, the Nostoi, and the Telegony. Scholars sometimes include the two Homeric epics, the [[Iliad]] and the [[Odyssey]], among the poems of the Epic Cycle, but the term is more often used to specify the non-Homeric poems as distinct from the Homeric ones.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Unlike the Iliad and the Odyssey, the cyclic epics survive only in fragments and summaries from [[Late antiquity|Late Antiquity]] and the [[Byzantine Empire|Byzantine period]]. The Epic Cycle was the distillation, in literary form, of an [[Oral tradition|oral tradition]] that had developed during the Greek Dark Age, which was based in part on localised hero cults. The traditional material from which the literary epics were drawn combines Bronze Age Mycenaean culture with the perspective and experience of Iron Age Greece. In modern scholarship, the study of the historical and literary relationship between the Homeric epics and the rest of the cycle is called Neoanalysis.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Epistemology",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Philosophical study of knowledge",
		tags = { "Philosophy", "Epistemology" },
		summary = "**Epistemology** is the branch of [[Philosophy|philosophy]] that examines the nature, origin, and limits of [[Knowledge|knowledge]]. Also called the **theory of knowledge**, it explores different types of knowledge, such as propositional knowledge about facts, practical knowledge in the form of skills, and knowledge by acquaintance as a familiarity through experience. Epistemologists study the concepts of [[Belief|belief]], [[Truth|truth]], and [[Justification (epistemology)|justification]] to understand the nature of knowledge. To discover how knowledge arises, they investigate sources of justification, such as [[Perception|perception]], [[Introspection|introspection]], [[Memory|memory]], [[Reason|reason]], and [[Testimony|testimony]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The school of [[Philosophical skepticism|skepticism]] questions the human ability to attain knowledge, while fallibilism says that knowledge is never certain. [[Empiricism|Empiricists]] hold that all knowledge comes from sense experience, whereas [[Rationalism|rationalists]] believe that some knowledge does not depend on it. Coherentists argue that a belief is justified if it is consistent with other beliefs. [[Foundationalism|Foundationalists]], by contrast, maintain that the justification of basic beliefs does not depend on other beliefs. Internalism and externalism debate whether justification is determined solely by mental states or also by external circumstances. Separate branches of epistemology focus on knowledge in specific fields, like scientific, mathematical, moral, and religious knowledge. Naturalized epistemology relies on empirical methods and discoveries, whereas formal epistemology uses formal tools from [[Logic|logic]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Epistle",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "✉️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Letter written for a didactic purpose",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'An **epistle** is a writing directed or sent to a person or group of people, usually an elegant and formal didactic [[Letter (message)|letter]]. The epistle genre of letter-writing was common in [[Ancient Egypt|ancient Egypt]] as part of the scribal-school writing curriculum. The letters in the New Testament written by apostles to the early Christians are usually referred to as epistles; those traditionally attributed to Paul are known as Pauline epistles and the others as catholic (i.e., "general") epistles.',
