	{
		title = "Eutrophication",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Accumulation of nutrients in water",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Eutrophication** is a general term describing a process in which [[Nutrient|nutrients]] accumulate in a body of water, resulting in an increased growth of organisms that may deplete the [[Oxygen|oxygen]] in the water; i.e. the process of too many plants growing on the surface of a river, lake, etc., often because chemicals that are used to help crops grow have been carried there by rain. Eutrophication may occur naturally or as a result of human actions. Manmade, or cultural, eutrophication occurs when [[Sewage|sewage]], industrial wastewater, fertilizer runoff, and other nutrient sources are released into the environment.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Such nutrient pollution usually causes algal blooms and bacterial growth, resulting in the depletion of dissolved oxygen in water and causing substantial [[Environmental degradation|environmental degradation]]. Many policies have been introduced to combat eutrophication, including the United Nations Development Program (UNDP)'s sustainability development goals. Approaches for prevention and reversal of eutrophication include minimizing point source pollution from sewage and agriculture as well as other nonpoint pollution sources. Additionally, the introduction of bacteria and algae-inhibiting organisms such as [[Shellfish|shellfish]] and seaweed can also help reduce nitrogen pollution, which in turn controls the growth of [[Cyanobacteria|cyanobacteria]], the main source of harmful algae blooms.",
			},
		},
		related = { "National Recording Registry" },
	},
	{
		title = "Evapotranspiration",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Natural processes of water movement within the water cycle",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Evapotranspiration** (**ET**) refers to the combined processes that move water from the Earth's surface (open water and ice surfaces, bare soil and [[Vegetation|vegetation]]) into the [[Atmosphere of Earth|atmosphere]], including both [[Evaporation|evaporation]] and [[Transpiration|transpiration]]. Evapotranspiration is an important part of the local [[Water cycle|water cycle]] and [[Climate|climate]], and measurement of it plays a key role in water resource management and [[Irrigation|agricultural irrigation]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Evidence",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Material supporting an assertion",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Evidence** for a [[Proposition|proposition]] is what supports the proposition. It is usually understood as an indication that the proposition is [[Truth|true]]. The exact definition and role of evidence vary across different fields.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In [[Epistemology|epistemology]], evidence is what [[Justification (epistemology)|justifies]] beliefs or what makes it rational to hold a certain doxastic attitude. For example, a perceptual experience of a tree may serve as evidence to justify the belief that there is a tree. In this role, evidence is usually understood as a private [[Mental state|mental state]]. In [[Phenomenology (philosophy)|phenomenology]], evidence is limited to intuitive knowledge, often associated with the controversial assumption that it provides indubitable access to truth. In [[Science|science]], scientific evidence is information gained through the [[Scientific method|scientific method]] that confirms or disconfirms [[Hypothesis|scientific hypotheses]], acting as a neutral arbiter between competing [[Scientific theory|theories]]. Measurements of [[Mercury (planet)|Mercury]]'s \"anomalous\" [[Orbit|orbit]], for example, are seen as evidence that confirms [[Albert Einstein|Einstein]]'s theory of general relativity.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Evidence (law)",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Body of facts in a legal proceeding",
		tags = { "Law", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Rules of evidence", "Proof" },
		summary = "The [[Law|law]] of **evidence**, also known as the **rules of evidence**, encompasses the rules and legal principles that govern the **proof** of [[Fact|facts]] in a legal proceeding. These rules determine what evidence must or must not be considered by the trier of fact in reaching its decision. The trier of fact is a judge in bench trials, or the jury in any cases involving a jury. The law of evidence is also concerned with the quantum (amount), quality, and type of proof needed to prevail in litigation. The rules vary depending upon whether the venue is a criminal court, civil court, or family court, and they vary by jurisdiction.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The **quantum of evidence** is the amount of evidence needed; the quality of proof is how reliable such evidence should be considered. Important rules that govern admissibility concern hearsay, authentication, relevance, privilege, [[Witness|witnesses]], [[Opinion|opinions]], expert testimony, identification and rules of physical evidence. There are various standards of evidence, standards showing how strong the evidence must be to meet the legal burden of proof in a given situation, ranging from reasonable suspicion to preponderance of the evidence, clear and convincing evidence, or beyond a reasonable doubt. There are several types of evidence, which vary from one another in form or source. When a dispute, whether relating to a civil or criminal matter, reaches the court there will always be a number of issues which one party will have to prove in order to persuade the court to find in their favour.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Lawsuit" },
	},
	{
		title = "Evolutionary psychology",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of psychology",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Psychology schools" },
		summary = "**Evolutionary psychology** is a theoretical approach in [[Psychology|psychology]] that examines cognition and behavior from a modern [[Evolution|evolutionary]] perspective. It seeks to identify [[Human|human]] psychological [[Adaptation|adaptations]] with regard to the ancestral problems they evolved to solve.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Adaptationist thinking about [[Physiology|physiological]] mechanisms, such as the [[Heart|heart]], [[Lung|lungs]], and the [[Liver|liver]], is common in [[Evolutionary biology|evolutionary biology]]. The evolutionary psychologists argue that much of human behavior is the output of psychological adaptations that evolved to solve recurrent problems in human ancestral environments. Some evolutionary psychologists argue that evolutionary theory can provide a foundational, metatheoretical framework that integrates the entire field of psychology, much as evolutionary biology has done for biology. Evolutionary psychologists hold that behaviors or traits that occur universally in all cultures are good candidates for evolutionary adaptations, including the abilities to infer others' emotions, discern kin from non-kin, identify and prefer healthier mates, and cooperate with others.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Gestalt psychology" },
	},
	{
		title = "Exam",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Educational assessment",
		tags = { "Education" },
		aliases = { "Examination", "Evaluation", "Test" },
		summary = "An **examination** (**exam** or **evaluation**), or **test**, is an educational assessment intended to measure a test-taker's [[Knowledge|knowledge]], [[Skill|skill]], aptitude, [[Physical fitness|physical fitness]], or classification in many other topics (e.g., [[Belief|beliefs]]).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Tests vary in style, rigor and requirements. There is no general consensus or invariable standard for test formats and difficulty. Often, the format and difficulty of the test is dependent upon the educational philosophy of the instructor, subject matter, class size, policy of the educational institution, and requirements of accreditation or governing bodies. A test may be administered formally or informally. An example of an informal test is a reading test administered by a parent to a child. A formal test might be a final examination administered by a teacher in a classroom or an IQ test administered by a psychologist in a clinic. Formal testing often results in a grade or a test score. A test score may be interpreted with regard to a norm or criterion, or occasionally both. The norm may be established independently, or by [[Statistics|statistical]] analysis of a large number of participants.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Education", "Learning" },
	},
	{
		title = "Exchange rate",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Rate at which one currency will be exchanged for another",
		tags = { "Banking and finance", "Money" },
		summary = "In finance, an **exchange rate** is the rate at which one [[Money|currency]] will be exchanged for another currency. Currencies are most commonly national currencies, but may be sub-national as in the case of [[Hong Kong]] or supra-national as in the case of the [[Euro|euro]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The exchange rate is also regarded as the value of one country's currency in relation to another currency. For example, an interbank exchange rate of 141 [[Japanese yen]] to the [[United States dollar]] means that ¥141 will be exchanged for US$1 or that US$1 will be exchanged for ¥141. In this case it is said that the price of a dollar in relation to yen is ¥141, or equivalently that the price of a yen in relation to dollars is $1/141. The exchange rate may be quoted as a ratio, for instance, USD/EUR might be equal to 0.8625. Each country determines the exchange rate regime that will apply to its currency. For example, a currency may be floating, pegged (fixed), or a hybrid. Governments can impose certain limits and controls on exchange rates. Countries can also have a strong or weak currency.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Executive (government)",
