		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🦴",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "External skeleton of an organism",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **exoskeleton** is a [[Skeleton|skeleton]] that is on the exterior of an [[Animal|animal]] in the form of hardened integument, which both supports the body's shape and protects the internal organs, in contrast to an internal [[Endoskeleton|endoskeleton]] (e.g. that of a human) which is enclosed underneath other soft tissues. Some large, hard and non-flexible protective exoskeletons are known as **shell** or **armour**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Examples of exoskeletons in animals include the cuticle skeletons shared by [[Arthropod|arthropods]] ([[Insect|insects]], chelicerates, myriapods and [[Crustacean|crustaceans]]) and [[Tardigrade|tardigrades]], as well as the skeletal cups formed by hardened secretion of stony corals, the test/tunic of sea squirts and [[Sea urchin|sea urchins]], and the prominent mollusc shell shared by snails, [[Bivalvia|clams]], tusk shells, chitons and nautilus. Some [[Vertebrate|vertebrate]] animals, such as the [[Turtle|turtle]], have both an endoskeleton and a protective exoskeleton.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Expansion of the universe",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Increase in distance between parts of the universe",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **expansion of the universe** is the increase in distance between gravitationally unbound parts of the [[Observable universe|observable universe]] with time. It is an intrinsic expansion, so it does not mean that the universe expands into anything or that space exists outside it. To any observer in the universe, it appears that all but the nearest galaxies (which are bound to each other by gravity) move away at [[Hubble's law|speeds that are proportional to their distance from the observer]], on average. While objects cannot move faster than light, this limitation applies only with respect to local [[Frame of reference|reference frames]] and does not limit the recession rates of cosmologically distant objects.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The expansion of the universe was discovered by separate theoretical and observational work in the 1920s. Since then, the expansion has become a core aspect of the [[Astrophysics|astrophysical]] field of [[Cosmology|cosmology]]. Many major scientific projects have sought to characterize the expansion and understand its effects. Cosmic expansion is a key feature of [[Big Bang]] cosmology. Within the theory of [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]], it is modeled mathematically with the Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker (FLRW) metric. The consensus or "standard" model of cosmology, the Lambda-CDM model, hypothesizes different expansion rates during different times, depending on the physical properties of the contents of spacetime. The very earliest expansion, called inflation, saw the universe suddenly expand by a factor of at least 1026 in every direction about 10−32 of a second after the Big Bang.',
			},
		},
	},
