		emoji = "👁️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **eye** is a sensory organ that allows an [[Organism|organism]] to perceive [[Visual perception|visual]] information. It detects [[Light|light]] and converts it into electro-chemical impulses in neurons (neurones). It is part of an organism's visual system.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In higher organisms, the eye is a complex [[Optics|optical]] system that collects light from the surrounding environment, regulates its intensity through a diaphragm, [[Focus (optics)|focuses]] it through an adjustable assembly of lenses to form an [[Image|image]], converts this image into a set of electrical signals, and transmits these signals to the [[Brain|brain]] through neural pathways that connect the eye via the optic nerve to the visual cortex and other areas of the brain. Eyes with resolving power have come in ten fundamentally different forms, classified into compound eyes and non-compound eyes. Compound eyes are made up of multiple small visual units, and are common on [[Insect|insects]] and [[Crustacean|crustaceans]]. Non-compound eyes have a single lens and focus light onto the retina to form a single image. This type of eye is common in mammals, including humans. The simplest eyes are pit eyes.",
