		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Basic law of electromagnetism",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]], **Faraday's law of induction** describes how a changing [[Magnetic field|magnetic field]] can induce an [[Electric current|electric current]] in a circuit. This phenomenon, known as [[Electromagnetic induction|electromagnetic induction]], is the fundamental operating principle of [[Transformer|transformers]], [[Inductor|inductors]], and many types of [[Electric motor|electric motors]], generators and solenoids.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In the literature, however, Faraday's law is used to refer to two closely related but technically distinct statements, either of which can be used to explain the phenomenon of induced current described above. One is the **Maxwell–Faraday equation**, one of [[Maxwell's equations]], which states that a time-varying magnetic field is always accompanied by a circulating [[Electric field|electric field]]. This law applies to the fields themselves and does not require the presence of a physical circuit. The other is **Faraday's flux rule**, or the **Faraday–Lenz law**, which relates the [[Electromotive force|electromotive force]] (emf) around a closed conducting loop to the time rate of change of magnetic flux through the loop. This rule can be derived from the first in specific context of a closed circuit. The flux rule accounts for two mechanisms by which an emf can be generated.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Farmer",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Person engaged in agriculture, raising living organisms for food or raw materials",
		tags = { "Anthropology", "Societal adaptations" },
		summary = 'A **farmer** is a person engaged in [[Agriculture|agriculture]], raising living organisms for food or raw materials. The term usually applies to people who do some combination of raising field crops, [[Orchard|orchards]], vineyards, [[Poultry|poultry]], or other [[Livestock|livestock]]. A farmer might own the farmland or might work as a laborer on land owned by others. In most developed economies, a "farmer" is usually a [[Farm|farm]] owner (landowner), while employees of the farm are known as farm workers (or farmhands). However, in other older definitions a farmer was a person who promotes or improves the growth of plants, land, or crops or raises animals (as livestock or fish) by labor and attention.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Over half a billion farmers are smallholders, most of whom are in developing countries and who economically support almost two billion people. Globally, women constitute more than 40% of agricultural employees.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "[[Employment]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Father",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Male parent",
		tags = { "Family members" },
		aliases = { "Dad", "Daddy" },
		summary = "A **father**, **dad**, or **daddy**, is the male [[Parent|parent]] of a [[Child|child]]. Besides the paternal bonds of a father to his child or children, fathers may have a parental, legal, and social relationship with their child or children that carries with it certain rights and obligations.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A biological father may have legal obligations to a child not raised by him, such as an obligation of monetary support. A **putative father** is a man whose biological relationship to a child is alleged but has not been established. A **stepfather** is a non-biological male parent married to a child\'s parent and forms a family unit but generally does not have the legal rights and responsibilities of a parent in relation to the child. An **adoptive father** is a man who has become the child\'s parent through the legal process of [[Adoption|adoption]]. The [[Adjective|adjective]] "paternal" refers to a father and comparatively to "maternal" for a [[Mother|mother]]. The [[Verb|verb]] "to father" means to procreate or to sire a child from which also derives the noun "fathering". Related terms of endearment are dad (dada, daddy), baba, papa, pappa, papasita, (pa, pap) and pop.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Sibling", "Wife" },
	},
	{
		title = "Fauvism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Early 20th-century artistic style",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Fauvism** is a style of painting and an [[Art movement|art movement]] that emerged in France at the beginning of the 20th century. It was the style of, a group of [[Modern art|modern artists]] whose works emphasized painterly qualities and strong color over the [[Representation (arts)|representational]] or realistic values retained by [[Impressionism]]. While Fauvism as a style began around 1904 and continued beyond 1910, the movement as such lasted only a few years, 1905–1908, and had three exhibitions. The leaders of the movement were André Derain and [[Henri Matisse]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Fear",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "😊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Basic emotion induced by a perceived threat",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Emotions and traits" },
		summary = "**Fear** is an [[Emotion|emotional]] state which is aroused by threatening situations. The feeling of fear can be the direct result of certain kinds of sensory stimulation, such as sounds which resemble screaming. It can also result from the anticipation that undesirable events will possibly or certainly occur, which can motivate efforts to avoid or mitigate those events, or from [[Imagination|imagining]] threats which are not actually present. While fear is typically felt to be a negative, unpleasant emotion, humans sometimes take pleasure in intentionally making themselves afraid.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Fear is often a component of thrilling or adventurous experiences, and certain forms of popular media are based around evoking fear, such as horror films. Maladaptive levels of fear are closely associated with specific phobias and with internalizing psychopathologies like depression, anxiety, and suicidality. The experience of fear involves a number of physiological changes, which in [[Human|humans]] include more rapid [[Breathing|breathing]] and heart rate, dilation of the pupils, spikes in cortisol levels, and elevated electrodermal activity. These qualities overlap considerably with the physiology of other negative, arousing emotions, such as [[Anger|anger]], [[Sadness|sadness]], [[Disgust|disgust]], and [[Anxiety|anxiety]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Self-esteem" },
	},
	{
		title = "Feather",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Body-covering structure of birds",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Feathers" },
		summary = "**Feathers** are epidermal growths that form a distinctive outer covering, or [[Plumage|plumage]], on both [[Bird|avian]] (bird) and some non-avian [[Dinosaur|dinosaurs]] and other [[Archosaur|archosaurs]]. They are the most complex integumentary structures found in [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]] and an example of a complex evolutionary novelty. They are among the characteristics that distinguish the extant birds from other living groups.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although feathers cover most of the bird's body, they arise only from certain well-defined tracts on the skin. They aid in flight, thermal insulation, and waterproofing. In addition, coloration helps in communication and protection. The study of feathers is called **plumology** (or **plumage science**). People use feathers in many ways that are practical, cultural, and religious. Feathers are both soft and excellent at trapping [[Heat|heat]]; thus, they are sometimes used in high-class bedding, especially [[Pillow|pillows]], blankets, and mattresses. They are also used as filling for winter [[Clothing|clothing]] and outdoor bedding, such as quilted coats and sleeping bags. [[Goose]] and eider down have great loft, the ability to expand from a compressed, stored state to trap large amounts of compartmentalized, insulating air. Feathers of large birds (most often [[Goose|geese]]) have been and are used to make quill pens.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Federal Information Processing Standards",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Technical standard",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "FIPS" },
		summary = "The **Federal Information Processing Standards** (**FIPS**) of the United States are a set of publicly announced [[Standardization|standards]] that the [[National Institute of Standards and Technology]] (NIST) has developed for use in computer systems of non-military United States government agencies and contractors. FIPS standards establish requirements for ensuring computer security and interoperability, and are intended for cases in which suitable industry standards do not already exist.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
