	{
		title = "French Fifth Republic",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Government of France since 1958",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **French Fifth Republic** is [[France]]'s current [[Republic|republican system]] of [[Government|government]]. It was established on 4 October 1958 by [[Charles de Gaulle]] under the Constitution of the Fifth Republic.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The Fifth Republic emerged from the collapse of the Fourth Republic, replacing the former [[Parliamentary republic|parliamentary republic]] with a [[Semi-presidential system|semi-presidential]] (or dual-executive) system that split powers between a president as [[Head of state|head of state]] and a prime minister as [[Head of government|head of government]]. [[Charles de Gaulle]], who was the first French president elected under the Fifth Republic in December 1958, believed in a strong head of state, which he described as embodying ("the spirit of the nation"). Under the Fifth Republic, the president has the right to dissolve the national assembly and hold new parliamentary elections. If the president has a majority in the national assembly, the president sets domestic policy and the prime minister puts it into practice. During a presidential mandate, the president can also change prime ministers and reshuffle the government.',
