	{
		title = "Fresh water",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Naturally occurring water with low amounts of dissolved salts",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Freshwater" },
		summary = "**Fresh water** or **freshwater** is any naturally occurring liquid or frozen [[Water|water]] containing low [[Concentration|concentrations]] of dissolved [[Salt (chemistry)|salts]] and other total dissolved solids. The term excludes [[Seawater|seawater]] and [[Brackish water|brackish water]], but it does include non-salty mineral-rich waters, such as chalybeate springs. Fresh water may encompass frozen and meltwater in [[Ice sheet|ice sheets]], [[Ice cap|ice caps]], [[Glacier|glaciers]], snowfields and [[Iceberg|icebergs]], natural [[Precipitation|precipitations]] such as [[Rain|rainfall]], snowfall, [[Hail|hail]]/sleet and graupel, and [[Surface runoff|surface runoffs]] that form inland bodies of water such as [[Wetland|wetlands]], [[Pond|ponds]], [[Lake|lakes]], [[River|rivers]], [[Stream|streams]], as well as [[Groundwater|groundwater]] contained in [[Aquifer|aquifers]], subterranean rivers and lakes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Water is critical to the survival of all living [[Organism|organisms]]. Many organisms can thrive on salt water, but the great majority of [[Vascular plant|vascular plants]] and most [[Insect|insects]], [[Amphibian|amphibians]], [[Reptile|reptiles]], [[Mammal|mammals]] and [[Bird|birds]] need fresh water to survive. Fresh water is the [[Water resources|water resource]] that is of the most and immediate use to humans. Fresh water is not always [[Drinking water|potable water]], that is, water safe to drink by [[Human|humans]]. Much of the [[Earth|earth]]'s fresh water (on the surface and groundwater) is to a substantial degree unsuitable for human consumption without treatment. Fresh water can easily become [[Water pollution|polluted by human activities]] or due to naturally occurring processes, such as erosion. Fresh water makes up less than 3% of the world's water resources, and just 1% of that is readily available. About 70% of the world's freshwater reserves are frozen in [[Antarctica]]. Just 3% of it is extracted for human consumption.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Freshwater ecosystem",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Part of Earth's aquatic ecosystems",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Freshwater ecosystems" },
		summary = "**Freshwater ecosystems** are a subset of Earth's [[Aquatic ecosystem|aquatic ecosystems]] that include the biological communities inhabiting [[Fresh water|freshwater]] waterbodies such as lakes, [[Pond|ponds]], rivers, [[Stream|streams]], springs, bogs, and [[Wetland|wetlands]]. They can be contrasted with [[Marine ecosystem|marine ecosystems]], which have a much higher [[Salinity|salinity]]. Freshwater habitats can be classified by different factors, including temperature, light penetration, nutrients, and vegetation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are three basic types of freshwater ecosystems: lentic (slow moving water, including pools, [[Pond|ponds]], and lakes), lotic (faster moving [[Stream|streams]], for example [[Stream|creeks]] and [[River|rivers]]) and [[Wetland|wetlands]] (semi-aquatic areas where the soil is saturated or inundated for at least part of the time). Freshwater ecosystems contain 41% of the world's known [[Fish|fish]] species. Freshwater ecosystems have undergone substantial transformations over time, which has impacted various characteristics of the ecosystems. Original attempts to understand and monitor freshwater ecosystems were spurred on by threats to human health (for example [[Cholera|cholera]] outbreaks due to [[Sewage|sewage]] contamination). Early monitoring focused on chemical indicators, then bacteria, and finally algae, fungi and protozoa.",
			},
		},
	},
