		title = "Geocentric orbit",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Orbit around Earth",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Earth-centered orbit", "Earth orbit" },
		summary = "A **geocentric orbit**, **Earth-centered orbit**, or **Earth orbit** involves any object [[Orbit|orbiting]] [[Earth]], such as the [[Moon]] or [[Satellite|artificial satellites]]. In 1997, [[NASA]] estimated there were approximately 2,465 artificial satellite payloads orbiting Earth and 6,216 pieces of space debris as tracked by the Goddard Space Flight Center. More than 16,291 objects previously launched have undergone orbital decay and entered [[Atmosphere of Earth|Earth's atmosphere]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A spacecraft enters orbit when its [[Centripetal force|centripetal]] [[Acceleration|acceleration]] due to [[Gravity|gravity]] is less than or equal to the [[Centrifugal force|centrifugal]] acceleration due to the horizontal component of its velocity. For a [[Low Earth orbit|low Earth orbit]], this velocity is about 7.8 km/s; by contrast, the fastest crewed airplane speed ever achieved (excluding speeds achieved by deorbiting spacecraft) was 2.2 km/s in 1967 by the North American X-15. The energy required to reach Earth orbital velocity at an [[Altitude|altitude]] of 600 km is about 36 MJ/kg, which is six times the energy needed merely to climb to the corresponding altitude. Spacecraft with a perigee below about 2000 km are subject to drag from the Earth's atmosphere, which decreases the orbital altitude. The rate of orbital decay depends on the satellite's cross-sectional area and mass, as well as variations in the air density of the upper atmosphere.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
