		summary = "It also provides a necessary condition for processes such as [[Chemical reaction|chemical reactions]] that may occur under these conditions. The Gibbs free energy is expressed as",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'This maximum can be attained only in a completely reversible process. When a system transforms reversibly from an initial state to a final state under these conditions, the decrease in Gibbs free energy equals the work done by the system to its surroundings, minus the work of the [[Pressure|pressure]] forces. The Gibbs energy is the thermodynamic potential that is minimized when a system reaches [[Chemical equilibrium|chemical equilibrium]] at constant pressure and temperature when not driven by an applied electrolytic voltage. Its derivative with respect to the reaction coordinate of the system then vanishes at the equilibrium point. The concept of Gibbs free energy, originally called available energy, was developed in the 1870s by the American scientist [[Josiah Willard Gibbs]]. In 1873, Gibbs described this "available energy" as',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gill",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Respiratory organ in aquatic animals",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **gill** is a specialized respiratory organ that many [[Aquatic animal|aquatic animals]] use for aquatic gas exchange, i.e. to extract dissolved [[Oxygen|oxygen]] from [[Water|water]] and to excrete carbon dioxide. **Branchia** is the zoologists' academic name for gills.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The gills of some semi-aquatic [[Clade|clades]] (e.g. [[Crab|crabs]], terrestrial hermit crabs and amphibious fishes), have also adapted to allow [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]] [[Respiration (physiology)|respiration]] ([[Breathing|breathing]]) on land provided the gills are kept moist. In some [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial]] chelicerates species (tetrapulmonates such as [[Spider|spiders]] and [[Scorpion|scorpions]]), their ancestral gills have [[Evolution|evolved]] into fully air-breathing book lungs. In some planktivorous bony fish species (e.g. silver and bighead carps), the gills are also used as a filter feeding organ via comb-like projections called gill rakers. The microscopic structure of a gill presents a large [[Surface area|surface area]] in contact with the external environment, which allows optimal [[Diffusion|diffusion]].",
			},
		},
	},
