	{
		title = "Good and evil",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Philosophical dichotomy",
		tags = { "Ethics", "Concepts" },
		summary = 'In [[Philosophy|philosophy]], religion, and [[Psychology|psychology]], "**good and evil**" is a common dichotomy. In religions with Manichaean and Abrahamic influence, evil is perceived as the dualistic antagonistic opposite of good, in which good should prevail and evil should be defeated.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Evil is often used to denote profound immorality. Evil has also been described as a [[Supernatural|supernatural]] force. Definitions of evil vary, as does the analysis of its motives. However, elements that are commonly associated with evil involve unbalanced behavior involving expediency, selfishness, ignorance, or [[Negligence|negligence]]. The principal study of good and evil (or morality) is [[Ethics|ethics]], of which there are three major branches: normative ethics concerning how we ought to behave, applied ethics concerning particular moral issues, and metaethics concerning the nature of morality itself.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Is–ought problem" },
