		tags = { "Politics and government", "Forms of government" },
		summary = "A **government** is the system or group of people which lead a [[State (polity)|state]]. As such, it is responsible for directing the only entity whose use of force is socially acceptable. Governments have come in many forms, whether dominated by one person ([[Autocracy|autocracy]]) a small ruling class ([[Oligarchy|oligarchy]]), or the masses ([[Democracy|democracy]]).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The main types of modern political systems recognized are [[Democracy|democracies]], totalitarian regimes, and, sitting between these two, [[Authoritarianism|authoritarian regimes]] with a variety of hybrid regimes. Modern classification systems also include [[Monarchy|monarchies]] as a standalone entity or as a hybrid system of the main three.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Government of India",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'The **Government of India** is the national authority of the [[India|Republic of India]], established in accordance with the Constitution of India. As outlined in Part I, India is a "Union of States", a term often used interchangeably to refer to the "Union Government" or the Government of India, representing the central authority. India is a [[Parliamentary republic|parliamentary republic]] led by the president of India, who, as the ceremonial Head of state, holds formal executive power. Dictated by parliamentary elections, the president appoints the prime minister as the head of government, along with other ministers.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"India is divided into [[States and union territories of India|28 states and 8 union territories]], making a total of 36 subnational entities. The governance of these entities is described in the Constitution as a quasi-federal system, blending elements of both [[Federalism|federal]] and [[Unitary state|unitary]] governments. While the states are self-governing administrative divisions with their own state governments, the [[Union territory|union territories]] are directly governed by the Union Government, under the administration of a lieutenant governor or an appointed administrator, who is chosen by the president of India. India operates under a dual federalist system, where the Constitution outlines the powers and limitations of both the states and union territories. The government's formal seat is at Parliament House in [[New Delhi]]. It has three primary branches: the legislature, the executive, and the judiciary.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Grain",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Edible dry seed",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **grain** is a small, hard, dry [[Fruit|fruit]] (caryopsis)–with or without an attached hull layer–harvested for [[Human|human]] or [[Animal|animal]] consumption. A **grain crop** is a grain-producing plant. The two main types of commercial grain crops are [[Cereal|cereals]] and legumes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"After being harvested, dry grains are more durable than other [[Staple food|staple foods]], such as starchy fruits (plantains, [[Breadfruit|breadfruit]], etc.) and [[Tuber|tubers]] ([[Sweet potato|sweet potatoes]], [[Cassava|cassava]], and more).This durability has made grains well suited to industrial agriculture, since they can be mechanically [[Harvest|harvested]], transported by rail or ship, stored for long periods in silos, and milled for [[Flour|flour]] or pressed for oil. The grain market is a major global [[Agriculture|agricultural market]] that includes crops such as [[Maize|maize]], [[Rice|rice]], soybeans, [[Wheat|wheat]], and other grains.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Grammar",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Structural rules of a language",
		tags = { "Basics", "Grammar" },
		summary = "In [[Linguistics|linguistics]], **grammar** is the system of rules that governs how a [[Natural language|natural language]] is structured and used, as evidenced by its speakers or [[Writer|writers]]. Grammar rules may concern the use of [[Clause|clauses]], [[Phrase|phrases]], and [[Word|words]]. The term may also refer to the study of such rules, a subject that includes [[Phonology|phonology]], [[Morphology (linguistics)|morphology]], and [[Syntax|syntax]], together with [[Phonetics|phonetics]], [[Semantics|semantics]], and [[Pragmatics|pragmatics]]. There are in effect two different ways to study grammar: traditional grammar and theoretical grammar.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Fluency in a particular language variety involves a speaker internalizing these rules, many or most of which are [[Language acquisition|acquired]] by observing other speakers, as opposed to intentional study or instruction. Much of this internalization occurs during early childhood; learning a language later in life usually involves more direct instruction. The term grammar can also describe the linguistic behaviour of groups of speakers and writers rather than individuals. Differences in scale can impact this definition: for example, English grammar could describe the rules followed by every one of the language's speakers. At a smaller scale, it may refer to rules shared by smaller groups of speakers. A description, study, or analysis of such rules may also be known as a grammar, or as a grammar book. A reference work describing the grammar of a language is called a reference grammar or simply a grammar.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Conjunction (grammar)" },
	},
	{
		title = "Grammatical case",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Categorization of nouns and modifiers by function",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **grammatical case** is a category of [[Noun|nouns]] and noun modifiers (determiners, [[Adjective|adjectives]], participles, and numerals) that corresponds to one or more potential grammatical functions for a nominal group in a wording. In various languages, nominal groups consisting of a noun and its modifiers belong to one of a few such categories. For instance, in [[English language|English]], one says I see them and they see me: the nominative pronouns I / they represent the perceiver, and the accusative pronouns them/me represent the phenomenon perceived. Here, nominative and accusative are cases, that is, categories of pronouns corresponding to the functions they have in representation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'English has largely lost its inflected case system but personal pronouns still have three cases, which are simplified forms of the nominative, accusative (including functions formerly handled by the dative), and genitive cases. They are used with personal pronouns: subjective case (I, you, he, she, it, we, they, who, whoever), objective case (me, you, him, her, it, us, them, whom, whomever), and possessive case (my, mine; your, yours; his; her, hers; its; our, ours; their, theirs; whose; whosever). Forms such as I, he, and we are used for the subject ("**I** kicked John"), and forms such as me, him, and us are used for the object ("John kicked **me**"). As a language evolves, cases can merge (for instance, in [[Ancient Greek]], the locative case merged with the dative), a phenomenon known as syncretism.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Bycatch" },
	},
	{
		title = "Grammatical gender",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Linguistic system of noun classification",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Linguistics|linguistics]], a **grammatical gender** system is a specific form of a noun class system, where [[Noun|nouns]] are assigned to gender categories that are often not related to the real-world qualities of the entities denoted by those nouns. [[English language|English]] does not have this system. In languages with grammatical gender, most or all nouns inherently carry one value of the grammatical category called gender. The values present in a given language, of which there are usually two or three, are called the genders of that language. Determiners, [[Adjective|adjectives]], and [[Pronoun|pronouns]] also change their form depending on the noun to which they refer.",
	},
	{
		title = "Grammatical tense",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⏳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Expression of time reference in grammar",
		tags = { "Basics", "Grammar" },
		aliases = { "Tense" },
		summary = "In [[Grammar|grammar]], **tense** is a category that expresses time reference. Tenses are usually manifested by the use of specific forms of [[Verb|verbs]], particularly in their conjugation patterns.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The main tenses found in many languages include the past, present, and future. Some languages have only two distinct tenses, such as past and nonpast, or future and nonfuture, while some languages make finer tense distinctions, such as remote vs recent past, or near vs remote future. There are also tenseless languages, like most of the [[Varieties of Chinese|Chinese languages]]; however, these languages do refer to time in different ways. Tenses generally express time relative to the moment of speaking. In some contexts, however, their meaning may be relativized to a point in the past or future which is established in the discourse (the moment being spoken about). This is called relative (as opposed to absolute) tense. Some languages have different verb forms or constructions which manifest relative tense, such as pluperfect ("past-in-the-past") and "future-in-the-past".',
			},
		},
	},
