	},
	{
		title = "Graphical user interface",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "GUI" },
		summary = "A **graphical user interface**, or **GUI**, is a form of [[User interface|user interface]] that allows users to interact with electronic devices through [[Graphics|graphical]] icons and visual indicators such as secondary notation. In many applications, GUIs are used instead of text-based UIs, which are based on typed command labels or text navigation. GUIs were introduced in reaction to the perceived steep learning curve of command-line interfaces (CLIs), which require commands to be typed on a [[Keyboard|computer keyboard]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The actions in a GUI are usually performed through direct manipulation of the graphical elements. Beyond computers, GUIs are used in many handheld [[Mobile device|mobile devices]] such as MP3 players, portable media players, gaming devices, [[Smartphone|smartphones]] and smaller household, office and industrial controls. The term GUI tends not to be applied to other lower-display resolution [[User interface|types of interfaces]], such as [[Video game|video games]] (where heads-up displays (HUDs) are preferred), or not including flat screens like volumetric displays because the term is restricted to the scope of 2D display screens able to describe generic information, in the tradition of the [[Computer science|computer science]] research at the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Graphics",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Visual presentation on some surface",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Graphics** are [[Visual perception|visual]] [[Image|images]] or [[Design|designs]] on some surface, such as a wall, [[Canvas|canvas]], screen, [[Paper|paper]], or stone, to inform, [[Illustration|illustrate]], or entertain. In contemporary usage, it includes a pictorial representation of data, as in design and manufacture, in typesetting and the graphic arts, and in educational and recreational software. Images that are generated by a computer are called computer graphics.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Examples are [[Photography|photographs]], drawings, line art, [[Graph of a function|mathematical graphs]], line graphs, charts, diagrams, [[Typography|typography]], [[Number|numbers]], [[Symbol|symbols]], geometric designs, [[Map|maps]], engineering drawings, or other [[Image|images]]. Graphics often combine text, [[Illustration|illustration]], and [[Color|color]]. [[Graphic design]] may consist of the deliberate selection, creation, or arrangement of [[Typography|typography]] alone, as in a brochure, flyer, poster, web site, or book without any other element. The objective can be clarity or effective communication, association with other cultural elements, or merely the creation of a distinctive style. Graphics can be functional or artistic. The latter can be a recorded version, such as a [[Photography|photograph]], or an interpretation by a scientist to highlight essential features, or an artist, in which case the distinction with imaginary graphics may become blurred. It can also be used for architecture.",
			},
		},
		related = { "BBC News" },
	},
	{
		title = "Grass",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Collective name for three families of plants",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Grass** refers to various [[Family (taxonomy)|families]] of [[Plant|plants]]. The three major families of **grasslike plants** are true grasses ([[Poaceae]]), sedges ([[Cyperaceae]]), and rushes (Juncaceae). Lawns and pasturelands are typically composed of true grasses, five of which cover 46% of the world's [[Arable land|arable land]]: [[Rice|rice]], [[Wheat|wheat]], [[Maize|maize]], [[Barley|barley]], and [[Sugarcane|sugar cane]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'"Grass" as a name has been applied to a wide group of unrelated plants including herbaceous plants whose leaves and stems are eaten by both domesticated and wild animals. The word may have its origin in the Proto-Indo-European root, meaning to grow. Grass can refer to a green area, such as a lawn, park, or a field, and is often used for recreation or for sports such as lawn tennis or bowls. Beginning in the 1970s, some sports venues have installed artificial grass to reduce maintenance costs. In the early 2020s, the slang term "touching grass" emerged, usually used as a remark towards individuals perceived as having poor social acumen due to excessive [[Internet|internet]] use and therefore lacking adequate exposure to the outside world.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gravitational collapse",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Contraction of an astronomical object due to the influence of its gravity",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Gravitational collapse** is the contraction of an [[Astronomical object|astronomical object]] due to the influence of its own [[Gravity|gravity]], which tends to draw matter inward toward the center of gravity. Gravitational collapse is a fundamental mechanism for structure formation in the universe. Over time an initial, relatively smooth distribution of [[Matter|matter]], after sufficient [[Accretion (astrophysics)|accretion]], may collapse to form pockets of higher density, such as [[Star|stars]] or [[Black hole|black holes]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Star formation]] involves a gradual gravitational collapse of [[Interstellar medium|interstellar medium]] into clumps of [[Molecular cloud|molecular clouds]] and potential [[Protostar|protostars]]. The compression caused by the collapse raises the temperature until thermonuclear fusion occurs at the center of the star, at which point the collapse gradually comes to a halt as the outward thermal pressure balances the gravitational forces. The star then exists in a state of [[Thermodynamic equilibrium|thermodynamic equilibrium]]. During the star's evolution a star might collapse again and reach several new states of equilibrium.",
			},
