		related = { "New wave music" },
	},
	{
		title = "Greco-Roman world",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Areas of influence by ancient Greece and Rome",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Greco-Roman civilization", "Greco-Roman culture", "Greco-Latin culture" },
		summary = 'The **Greco-Roman world** – also **Greco-Roman civilization**, **Greco-Roman culture**, or **Greco-Latin culture** (spelled **Græco-** or **Graeco-** in [[British English]]) – is the modern term for the regions that historically and culturally fell under the direct influence of the language, culture, government, and religion of the [[Ancient Greece|ancient Greeks]] and [[Roman Empire|Romans]]. The two cultures first blended significantly under the military authority of the [[Roman Republic]], and then the [[Roman Empire]]. The term "[[Classical antiquity|classical antiquity]]" describes the accompanying time period when Greco-Roman culture exerted its greatest influence and dominance on the European continent.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In these regions, the cultural perceptions, ideas, and sensitivities of these peoples became dominant in classical antiquity. That process was aided by the universal adoption of [[Greek language|Greek]] as the language of intellectual culture and commerce in the [[Eastern Mediterranean]] and of [[Latin]] as the language of [[Public administration|public administration]] and of [[Law|law]], especially in the Western Mediterranean. Greek and Latin were never the native languages of many or most of the rural peasants, who formed the great majority of the [[Roman Empire]]'s population. They became the languages of the [[Urban area|urban]] and cosmopolitan elites and the Empire's [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] for those who lived within the large territories and populations outside the [[Macedonia (ancient kingdom)|Macedonian settlements]] and the Roman colonies. All Roman citizens of note and accomplishment, regardless of their ethnic extractions, spoke and wrote in Greek or Latin.",
			},
		},
	},
