		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Greek Dark Ages** (c. 1180–800 BC) was a period in Ancient Greece that emerged from the wider [[Late Bronze Age collapse]], which affected much of the [[Eastern Mediterranean]]. This age was characterized by the collapse of Mycenaean civilization, where the palaces and cities of the Mycenaeans were either destroyed, abandoned, or both.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"At around the same time, the [[Hittites|Hittite]] civilization in [[Anatolia|Asia Minor]] (modern-day [[Turkey]]) also suffered serious disruption and collapse, with cities such as [[Troy]] being destroyed. Moreover, in Egypt, the [[New Kingdom of Egypt|New Kingdom]] fell into disarray, leading to the Third Intermediate Period of Egypt. Following this mass destruction, there were fewer, smaller settlements, which suggests widespread famine and depopulation. On the Greek mainland, the [[Linear B]] script, used by Mycenaean bureaucrats to write the [[Greek language]], ceased to be used. The later [[Greek alphabet]] did not develop until hundreds of years later, in the beginning of the Protohistoric Iron Age, c. 800 BC.",
