		tags = { "Politics and government", "State structure and administration" },
		summary = "A **head of state** is the public persona of a [[Sovereign state|sovereign state]]. The name given to the office of head of state depends on the country's form of [[Government|government]] and any [[Separation of powers|separation of powers]]; the powers of the office in each country range from being also the [[Head of government|head of government]] to being little more than a ceremonial figurehead.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In a [[Parliamentary system|parliamentary system]], such as India or the United Kingdom, the head of state usually has mostly ceremonial powers, with a separate head of government. However, in some parliamentary systems, like South Africa, there is an executive president that is both head of state and head of government. Likewise, in some parliamentary systems the head of state is not the head of government, but still has significant powers, for example Morocco. In contrast, in other parliamentary systems such as Sweden and Japan, the head of state is purely ceremonial without any meaningful power. Meanwhile, in presidential systems, the head of state is also the head of government. In a [[Semi-presidential system|semi-presidential system]], such as France, elements of the presidential and parliamentary systems are combined, with separate heads of state and government who officially exercise executive power alongside each other.",
