		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sinitic language spoken in East Asia",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Hokkien** is a variety of Chinese from the Southern Min branch of the [[Sinitic languages]], native to the Minnan region in the southern part of Fujian province in southeastern [[China]], as well as the island of [[Taiwan]]. It is also referred to as **Quanzhang**, named after the prefecture-level cities of [[Quanzhou]] and Zhangzhou.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Taiwanese Hokkien is one of the national languages of Taiwan, and is also widely spoken within the overseas Chinese diaspora in [[Singapore]], [[Malaysia]], the [[Philippines]], [[Indonesia]], [[Cambodia]], [[Myanmar]], [[Hong Kong]], [[Thailand]], [[Brunei]], [[Vietnam]], and elsewhere across the world. [[Mutual intelligibility]] between Hokkien dialects varies, but they are still held together by ethnolinguistic identity. In [[Maritime Southeast Asia|maritime Southeast Asia]], Hokkien historically served as the [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] amongst overseas Chinese communities of all dialectal subgroups due to the prominence of Hokkien sailors in the Nanyang maritime trade networks since the [[Ming dynasty]], and it still remains as the most spoken variety of Chinese in the region today, including in Singapore, Malaysia, Philippines, Indonesia, and [[Brunei]]. This applied to a lesser extent to [[Mainland Southeast Asia|mainland Southeast Asia]].",
			},
