	{
		title = "Hominini",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tribe of mammals",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Hominin" },
		summary = "The **Hominini** (hominins) form a [[Tribe (taxonomy)|taxonomic tribe]] of the [[Subfamily|subfamily]] Homininae (hominines). They comprise two extant [[Genus|genera]]: [[Homo]] ([[Human|humans]]) and [[Pan (genus)|Pan]] ([[Chimpanzee|chimpanzees]] and [[Bonobo|bonobos]]), and in standard usage exclude the genus Gorilla ([[Gorilla|gorillas]]), which is grouped separately within the subfamily Homininae.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term Hominini was originally introduced by Camille Arambourg (1948), who combined the categories of Hominina and Simiina pursuant to Gray's classifications (1825). Traditionally, [[Chimpanzee|chimpanzees]], [[Gorilla|gorillas]] and [[Orangutan|orangutans]] were grouped together, excluding humans, as pongids. Since Gray's classifications, evidence accumulating from genetic [[Phylogenetic tree|phylogeny]] confirmed that humans, chimpanzees, and gorillas are more closely related to each other than to the orangutan. The orangutans were reassigned to the family [[Hominidae]] (great apes), which already included humans; and the gorillas were grouped as a separate tribe (Gorillini) of the subfamily Homininae. Still, details of this reassignment remain contested, and of publishing since (on tribe Hominini), not every source excludes gorillas and not every source includes chimpanzees.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Hominini" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Honour",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Concept of worthiness and respectability",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Interpersonal relations" },
		aliases = { "Honor" },
		summary = "**Honour** ([[English in the Commonwealth of Nations|Commonwealth English]]) or **honor** ([[American English]]; [[American and British English spelling differences|see spelling differences]]) is a quality of a person that is of both social teaching and personal ethos, that manifests itself as a code of conduct, and has various elements such as [[Courage|valour]], [[Chivalry|chivalry]], honesty, and compassion. It is an abstract concept entailing a perceived quality of worthiness and respectability that affects both the social standing and the self-evaluation of an individual or of institutions such as a family, school, regiment, or nation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Accordingly, individuals (or institutions) are assigned worth and stature based on the harmony of their actions with a specific code of honour, and with the moral code of the society at large. [[Samuel Johnson]], in his A Dictionary of the English Language (1755), defined honour as having several senses, the first of which was "[[Nobility|nobility]] of soul, magnanimity, and a scorn of meanness". This sort of honour derives from the perceived virtuous conduct and personal integrity of the person endowed with it. Johnson also defined honour in relationship to "reputation" and "fame"; to "privileges of rank or birth", and as "respect" of the kind which "places an individual socially and determines his right to precedence". This sort of honour is often not so much a function of moral or ethical excellence, as it is a consequence of power.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Blame", "Trust (social science)" },
	},
	{
		title = "Hope",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Optimistic state of mind",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Emotions and traits" },
		summary = '**Hope** is an optimistic [[Mental state|state of mind]] that is based on an expectation of desirable outcomes with respect to events and circumstances in one\'s own life, or the world at large. As a verb, Merriam-Webster defines hope as "to expect with confidence" or "to cherish a desire with anticipation".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
