	{
		title = "Humanities",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Academic disciplines that study society and culture",
		summary = '**Humanities** are [[Academic discipline|academic disciplines]] that study aspects of [[Human|human]] [[Society|society]] and [[Culture|culture]], including [[Philosophy|certain fundamental questions asked by humans]]. During the [[Renaissance]], the term "humanities" referred to the study of classical literature and language, as opposed to the study of religion or the concept of "divinity". The study of the humanities was a key part of the secular curriculum in universities at the time. Today, the humanities are more frequently defined as any fields of study outside of [[Natural science|natural sciences]], [[Social science|social sciences]], formal sciences (like [[Mathematics|mathematics]]), and [[Applied science|applied sciences]] (or professional training).',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"They use methods that are primarily [[Critical theory|critical]], speculative, or interpretative and have a significant historical element—as distinguished from the mainly empirical approaches of [[Science|science]]. The humanities include the academic study of [[Philosophy|philosophy]], religion, [[History|history]], language arts ([[Literature|literature]], [[Writing|writing]], [[Public speaking|oratory]], [[Rhetoric|rhetoric]], [[Poetry|poetry]], etc.), the [[Performing arts|performing arts]] (theater, [[Music|music]], [[Dance|dance]], etc.), and the [[Visual arts|visual arts]] ([[Painting|painting]], [[Sculpture|sculpture]], [[Photography|photography]], [[Filmmaking|filmmaking]], etc.). The word humanities comes from the Renaissance Latin phrase, which translates to study of humanity. The was a course of study comprising grammar, literature, rhetoric, history, and moral philosophy, primarily drawn from the study of Latin and Greek classics.",
