	{
		title = "Hunter-gatherer",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👥",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Peoples who forage or hunt for most or all of their food",
		tags = { "Anthropology", "Societal adaptations" },
		aliases = { "Forager" },
		summary = "A **hunter-gatherer** or **forager** is a [[Human|human]] living in a [[Community|community]] (or according to an ancestrally derived lifestyle) in which most or all [[Food|food]] is obtained by procuring naturally occurring edible items from the local [[Wildlife|wildlife]] and [[Vegetation|vegetation]] via [[Hunting|hunting]], [[Fishing|fishing]] and/or gathering fruits, seeds and other [[Plant|plant]] parts (collectively known as [[Vegetable|vegetables]]) as well as edible fungi such as [[Mushroom|mushrooms]]. This is the human equivalent of the [[Predation|predation]] and [[Foraging|foraging]] behaviors seen in all [[Omnivore|omnivorous]] [[Animal|animals]], and was a common practice among most [[Prehistory|prehistoric]] human societies, particularly before the rise of [[Agriculture|agriculture]] during the Neolithic period.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Hunter-gatherer societies, usually a [[Tribe|tribe]] of only a few dozen people, were and are typically fully or semi-[[Nomad|nomadic]], in contrast with the [[Sedentism|sedentary]] and more populous [[Agrarian society|agricultural societies]] whose [[Food security|food security]] rely mainly on cultivating [[Crop|crops]]/produces and [[Animal husbandry|raising]] domesticated animals. Hunting and gathering emerged with [[Homo erectus]] about 1.8 million years ago and was humanity's original and most enduringly successful [[Competition (biology)|competitive]] [[Adaptation|adaptation]] in the natural world, occupying at least 90 percent of the [[Human history|human history]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hunting",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏹",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Business and economics" },
		summary = "Recreationally hunted species are generally referred to as the game, and are usually [[Mammal|mammals]] and [[Bird|birds]]. A person participating in a hunt is a **hunter** or **huntress**; a natural area used for hunting is called a game reserve; and an experienced hunter who helps organise a hunt and/or manage the game reserve is also known as a gamekeeper.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Hunting activities by humans arose in [[Homo erectus]] or earlier, in the order of millions of years ago. Hunting has become deeply embedded in various human cultures and was once an important part of rural economies—classified by economists as part of primary production alongside [[Forestry|forestry]], [[Agriculture|agriculture]], and [[Fishery|fishery]]. Apart from [[Food|food]] provision, hunting can be a means of population control. Hunting advocates state that regulated hunting can be a necessary component of modern wildlife management, for example to help maintain a healthy proportion of animal populations within an environment's ecological carrying capacity when natural checks such as natural predators are absent or insufficient, or to provide funding for breeding programs and maintenance of natural reserves and conservation parks.",
			},
