		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Hydrogen sulphide" },
		summary = "**Hydrogen sulfide** (preferred IUPAC name and [[American English]]) or **hydrogen sulphide** ([[English in the Commonwealth of Nations|Commonwealth English]]) is a [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]] with the [[Chemical formula|formula]] H2S. Trace amounts in the ambient atmosphere have a characteristic foul odor of rotten eggs. Swedish chemist Carl Wilhelm Scheele is credited with having discovered the chemical composition of purified hydrogen sulfide in 1777.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When it is inhaled or its salts are ingested in high amounts, damage to organs occurs rapidly with symptoms ranging from breathing difficulties to convulsions and death. Despite this, the [[Human body|human body]] produces small amounts of this sulfide and its mineral salts, and uses it as a signalling molecule. Hydrogen sulfide is often produced from the microbial breakdown of [[Organic matter|organic matter]] in the absence of [[Oxygen|oxygen]], such as in [[Swamp|swamps]] and sewers; this process is commonly known as anaerobic digestion, carried out by sulfate-reducing microorganisms. It also occurs in volcanic gases, natural gas deposits, and sometimes in well-drawn water.",
