		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **insectivore** is a [[Carnivore|carnivorous]] animal or plant which eats [[Insect|insects]]. An alternative term is **entomophage**, which can also refer to the human practice of eating insects.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The first vertebrate insectivores were [[Amphibian|amphibians]]. When they evolved 400 million years ago, the first amphibians were piscivores, with numerous sharp conical teeth, much like a modern [[Crocodile|crocodile]]. The same tooth arrangement is however also suited for eating animals with [[Exoskeleton|exoskeletons]], thus the ability to eat insects can stem from piscivory. At one time, insectivorous [[Mammal|mammals]] were scientifically classified in an [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] called Insectivora. This order is now abandoned, as not all insectivorous mammals are closely related. Most of the Insectivora taxa have been reclassified; those that have not yet been reclassified and found to be truly related to each other remain in the order Eulipotyphla. Although individually small, insects exist in enormous numbers. Insects make up a very large part of the animal biomass in almost all non-marine, non-polar environments.",
