	{
		title = "Insulator (electricity)",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Material that does not conduct an electric current",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Electrical insulator" },
		summary = "An **electrical insulator** is a material in which [[Electric current|electric current]] does not flow freely. The atoms of the insulator have tightly bound electrons which cannot readily move. Other materials—[[Semiconductor|semiconductors]] and [[Electrical conductor|conductors]]—conduct electric current more easily. The property that distinguishes an insulator is its resistivity; insulators have higher resistivity than semiconductors or conductors. The most common examples are non-metals.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A perfect insulator does not exist because even the materials used as insulators contain small numbers of mobile charges ([[Charge carrier|charge carriers]]) which can carry current. In addition, all insulators become electrically conductive when a sufficiently large voltage is applied that the electric field tears [[Electron|electrons]] away from the atoms. This is known as electrical breakdown, and the voltage at which it occurs is called the breakdown voltage of an insulator. Some materials such as [[Glass|glass]], paper and PTFE, which have high resistivity, are very good electrical insulators. A much larger class of materials, even though they may have lower bulk resistivity, are still good enough to prevent significant current from flowing at normally used voltages, and thus are employed as insulation for electrical wiring and [[Electrical cable|cables]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Insurance",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Protection from financial loss",
		tags = { "Business and economics" },
		summary = "It is a form of risk management, primarily used to protect against the risk of a contingent or uncertain loss.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"An entity which provides insurance is known as an **insurer**, **insurance company**, **insurance carrier**, or underwriter. A person or entity who buys insurance is known as a **policyholder**, while a person or entity covered under the policy is called an **insured**. The insurance transaction involves the policyholder assuming a guaranteed, known, and relatively small loss in the form of a payment to the insurer (a premium) in exchange for the insurer's promise to compensate the insured in the event of a covered loss. The loss may or may not be financial, but it must always be reducible to financial terms. Furthermore, it usually involves something in which the insured has an insurable interest established by ownership, possession, or pre-existing relationship.",
