	{
		title = "International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "NGO enabling communication about chemistry",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "IUPAC" },
		summary = 'The **International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry** (**IUPAC** ) is an international federation of National Adhering Organizations working for the advancement of the chemical sciences, especially by developing nomenclature and terminology. It is a member of the International Science Council (ISC). IUPAC is registered in [[Zurich|Zürich]], Switzerland, and its administrative office, known as the "IUPAC Secretariat", is in Research Triangle Park, [[North Carolina]], United States.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"IUPAC was established in 1919 as the successor of the International Congress of Applied Chemistry for the advancement of [[Chemistry|chemistry]]. Its members, the National Adhering Organizations, can be national chemistry societies, national academies of sciences, or just other bodies representing chemists. There are a total of fifty-four National Adhering Organizations and three Associate National Adhering Organizations. IUPAC's Inter-divisional Committee on Nomenclature and Symbols (IUPAC nomenclature) is the recognized world authority in developing standards for naming chemical elements and compounds. Since its creation, IUPAC has been run by many different committees with different responsibilities. These committees run different projects; of those which include [[Standardization|standardizing]] nomenclature, finding ways to bring chemistry to the world, and publishing works.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "International non-governmental organization, standards organization" },
			{ "Headquarters", "Research Triangle Park, North Carolina, United States" },
			{ "Language", "English" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "International airport",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "✈️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Airport with facilities for international travel (border control and customs)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **international airport** may be a marketing term and can be an [[Airport|airport]] with customs and border control facilities, enabling passengers to travel between [[Country|countries]] around the world. International airports are usually larger than domestic airports, and feature longer runways and have facilities to accommodate heavier aircraft such as the Boeing 747, Boeing 777, and the Airbus A380 commonly used for international and intercontinental travel. International airports often host domestic flights, which helps feed both passengers and cargo into international ones (and vice versa).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Buildings, operations, and management have become increasingly sophisticated since the mid-20th century, when international airports began to provide infrastructure for international civilian flights. Detailed technical standards have been developed to ensure safety and common coding systems have been implemented to provide global consistency. The physical structures that serve millions of individual passengers and flights are among the most complex and interconnected in the world. By the second decade of the 21st century, over 1,200 international airports existed with around 3.8 billion international passengers as of January 2023 along with 50 million metric tonnes of cargo passing through them annually.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
	},
	{
		title = "International law",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Norms in international relations",
		tags = { "Law", "Subjects" },
		aliases = { "Public international law", "The law of nations" },
		summary = "**International law**, also known as **public international law** and **the law of nations**, is the set of [[Rule of law|rules]], norms, [[Customary law|legal customs]] and standards that [[State (polity)|states]] and other actors feel an obligation to, and generally do, obey in their mutual relations. In international relations, actors are simply the individuals and collective entities, such as states, [[International organization|international organizations]], and non-state groups, which can make behavioral choices, whether lawful or unlawful. Rules are formal, typically written expectations that outline required behavior, while norms are informal, often unwritten guidelines about appropriate behavior that are shaped by custom and social practice.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It establishes norms for states across a broad range of domains, including war and [[Diplomacy|diplomacy]], [[Trade|economic relations]], and [[Human rights|human rights]]. International law differs from state-based domestic legal systems in that it operates largely through consent, since there is no universally accepted authority to enforce it upon [[Sovereign state|sovereign states]]. States and non-state actors may choose to not abide by international law, and even to breach a [[Treaty|treaty]], but such violations, particularly of peremptory norms, can be met with disapproval by others and in some cases coercive action including diplomacy, economic sanctions, and war. The lack of a final authority in international law can also cause far-reaching differences. This is partly the effect of states being able to interpret international law in a manner which they see fit, which can lead to problematic stances that have large local effects.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "International relations",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of relationships between states",
		tags = { "Politics and government", "Politics" },
		aliases = { "IR", "International studies", "International politics" },
		summary = "**International relations** (**IR**, and also referred to as **international studies**, **international politics**, or **international affairs**) is an academic discipline. In a broader sense, the study of IR, in addition to multilateral relations, concerns all activities among states—such as war, [[Diplomacy|diplomacy]], [[Trade|trade]], and foreign policy—as well as relations with and among other international actors, such as [[International organization|intergovernmental organizations]] (IGOs), international nongovernmental organizations (INGOs), international legal bodies, and [[Multinational corporation|multinational corporations]] (MNCs).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"International relations is generally classified as a major multidiscipline of [[Political science|political science]], along with comparative politics, political methodology, [[Political philosophy|political theory]], and [[Public administration|public administration]]. It often draws heavily from other fields, including [[Anthropology|anthropology]], economics, [[Geography|geography]], history, [[International law|law]], [[Philosophy|philosophy]], and [[Sociology|sociology]]. There are several schools of thought within IR, of which the most prominent are realism, liberalism, and constructivism. While international politics has been analyzed since [[Ancient history|antiquity]], it did not become a discrete field of study until 1919, when it was first offered as an undergraduate major by Aberystwyth University in the [[United Kingdom]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "International trade",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🤝",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Exchange across international borders",
		tags = { "Business and economics" },
		summary = "**International trade** is the exchange of [[Capital (economics)|capital]], [[Goods|goods]], and [[Service (economics)|services]] across international borders or [[Territory|territories]] because there is a need or demand for goods or services.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In most countries, such [[Trade|trade]] represents a significant share of [[Gross domestic product|gross domestic product]] (GDP). While international trade has existed throughout history (for example Uttarapatha, [[Silk Road]], Amber Road, salt roads), its economic, social, and political importance has been on the rise in recent centuries. Carrying out trade at an international level is a complex process when compared to domestic trade. When trade takes place between two or more [[State (polity)|states]], factors like [[Money|currency]], government policies, economy, [[Judiciary|judicial system]], laws, and markets influence trade. To ease and justify the process of trade between countries of different economic standing in the modern era, some international economic organizations were formed, such as the [[World Trade Organization]]. These organizations work towards the facilitation and growth of international trade.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Internet Archive",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "American non-profit digital archive",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'The **Internet Archive** is an American non-profit library founded in 1996 by Brewster Kahle that runs a digital library website, archive.org. It provides free access to collections of digitized media including [[Website|websites]], software applications, [[Music|music]], audiovisual, and print materials. The Archive also advocates a free and open [[Internet]]. Its mission is committing to provide "universal access to all knowledge".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Internet Archive allows the public to upload and download digital material to its data cluster, but the bulk of its data is collected automatically by its web crawlers, which work to preserve as much of the public web as possible. The Wayback Machine, its web archive, contains more than 1 trillion web captures. The Archive also oversees numerous book digitization projects, collectively one of the world's largest book digitization efforts. The archive is used frequently by journalists and Wikipedia editors.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Language", "English" },
			{ "Location", "300 Funston Avenue Richmond District [[San Francisco]], California, U.S." },
			{ "Founder", "Brewster Kahle" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Interpretatio graeca",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Methodology for cultural comparison",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '**Interpretatio graeca**, or "interpretation by means of Greek [models]", refers to the tendency of the ancient Greeks to identify foreign deities with their own gods. It is a discourse used to interpret or attempt to understand the mythology and religion of other cultures using familiar terms; a comparative methodology using ancient Greek religious concepts and practices, deities, and [[Greek mythology|myths]], [[Comparative mythology|equivalencies, and shared characteristics]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The phrase may describe Greek efforts to explain others' beliefs and myths, as when [[Herodotus]] describes Egyptian religion in terms of perceived Greek analogues, or when Dionysius of Halicarnassus and [[Plutarch]] document Roman cults, temples, and practices under the names of equivalent Greek deities. may also describe non-Greeks' interpretation of their own belief systems by comparison or assimilation with Greek models, as when [[Roman people|Romans]] adapt Greek myths and iconography under the names of their own gods. is comparative discourse in reference to ancient Roman religion and [[Roman mythology|myth]], as in the formation of a distinctive Gallo-Roman religion. Both the Romans and the Gauls reinterpreted Gallic religious traditions in relation to Roman models, particularly Imperial cult. Jan Assmann considers the polytheistic approach to internationalizing gods as a form of \"intercultural translation\":",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Intertidal zone",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Area of coast exposed only at low tide",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Foreshore" },
		summary = "The **intertidal zone**, or **foreshore**, is the area above water level at low [[Tide|tide]] and underwater at high tide; in other words, it is the part of the littoral zone within the tidal range. This area can include several types of habitats with various species of [[Marine life|life]], such as sea stars, [[Sea urchin|sea urchins]], and many species of [[Coral reef|coral]] with regional differences in biodiversity. Sometimes it is referred to as the littoral zone or [[Coast|seashore]], although those can be defined as a wider region.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The intertidal zone also includes steep rocky [[Cliff|cliffs]], sandy [[Beach|beaches]], [[Bog|bogs]] or wetlands (e.g., vast mudflats). This area can be a narrow strip, such as in Pacific islands that have only a narrow tidal range, or can include many meters of shoreline where shallow beach slopes interact with high tidal excursion. The **peritidal zone** is similar but somewhat wider, extending from above the highest tide level to below the lowest. Organisms in the intertidal zone are well-adapted to their environment, facing high levels of interspecific competition and the rapidly changing conditions that come with the tides. The intertidal zone is also home to several species from many different [[Phylum|phyla]] (Porifera, Annelida, Coelenterata, [[Mollusca]], Arthropoda, etc.). The water that comes with the tides can vary from [[Brackish water|brackish waters]], fresh with [[Rain|rain]], to highly [[Seawater|saline]] and dry [[Salt|salt]], with drying between tidal inundations.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Biographical Memoirs of Fellows of the Royal Society" },
	},
	{
		title = "Introspection",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Examining one's own thoughts and feelings",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Concepts" },
		summary = "**Introspection** is the examination of one's own [[Consciousness|conscious]] [[Thought|thoughts]] and [[Feeling|feelings]]. In [[Psychology|psychology]], the process of introspection relies on the observation of one's [[Mental representation|mental state]], while in a [[Spirituality|spiritual]] context it may refer to the examination of one's soul. Introspection is closely related to human self-reflection and self-discovery and is contrasted with [[Observation|external observation]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'It generally provides a privileged access to one\'s own [[Mental state|mental states]], not mediated by other sources of knowledge, so that individual experience of the mind is unique. Introspection can determine any number of mental states including: sensory, bodily, cognitive, emotional and so forth. Introspection has been a subject of philosophical discussion for thousands of years. The [[Philosophy|philosopher]] [[Plato]] asked, "...why should we not calmly and patiently review our own thoughts, and thoroughly examine and see what these appearances in us really are?" While introspection is applicable to many facets of philosophical thought it is perhaps best known for its role in [[Epistemology|epistemology]]; in this context introspection is often compared with [[Perception|perception]], [[Reason|reason]], [[Memory|memory]], and [[Testimony|testimony]] as a source of [[Knowledge|knowledge]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Intuition",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ability to acquire knowledge without conscious reasoning",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '**Intuition** is the ability to acquire [[Knowledge|knowledge]] without recourse to conscious [[Reason|reasoning]] or needing an explanation. Different fields use the word "intuition" in very different ways, including but not limited to: direct access to unconscious knowledge; unconscious [[Cognition|cognition]]; gut feelings; inner sensing; inner insight to unconscious pattern-recognition; and the ability to understand something instinctively, without any need for conscious reasoning. Intuitive knowledge tends to be approximate or heuristic.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The word intuition comes from the [[Latin]] verb translated as 'consider' or from the [[Middle English|Late Middle English]] word, 'to contemplate'. Use of intuition is sometimes referred to as responding to a \"gut feeling\" or \"trusting your gut\".",
			},
