		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ancient Greek dialect",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Ionian" },
		summary = "**Ionian** or **Ionic Greek** was a subdialect of the Eastern or [[Attic Greek|Attic]]–Ionic dialect group of [[Ancient Greek]]. On the basis of inscriptions, three subdialects of Ionic may be discerned. The Ionic group traditionally comprises three dialectal varieties that were spoken in Euboea (West Ionic), the northern Cyclades (Central Ionic), and from c. 1000BC onward in [[Ionia|Asiatic Ionia]] (East Ionic), where Ionian colonists from [[Athens]] founded their cities. Ionic was the base of several literary language forms of the [[Archaic Greece|Archaic]] and [[Classical Greece|Classical]] periods, both in poetry and prose.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The works of [[Homer]] and [[Hesiod]] are among the most popular [[Ancient Greek literature|poetic works]] that were written in a literary form of the Ionic dialect, known as Epic or [[Homeric Greek]]. The oldest [[Ancient Greek literature|Greek prose]], including that of [[Heraclitus]], [[Herodotus]], [[Democritus]], and [[Hippocrates]], was also written in Ionic. By the end of the 5th century BC, Ionic was supplanted by Attic, which had become the dominant dialect of the Greek world.",
			},
