		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ethnic group native to Italy",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Italians** are an [[Ethnicity|ethnic group]] and [[Nation|nation]] native to the Italian geographical region. Italians share a common culture, [[History of Italy|history]], [[Cultural heritage|ancestry]] and [[Italian language|language]]. Their ancestors, differing regionally, include the various Italic peoples, notably among them the [[Roman people|ancient Romans]], who helped create and evolve the Italian identity. The Latin equivalent of the term Italian had been in use for natives of the geographical region since [[Classical antiquity|antiquity]]. Ethnic Italians (a group which includes people of Italian descent without Italian citizenship) can be distinguished from Italian nationals, who are citizens of [[Italy]] regardless of ancestry or nation of residence.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The majority of Italian nationals are native speakers of the country\'s official language, Standard Italian, a [[Romance languages|Romance language]] of the [[Indo-European languages|Indo-European language family]] that evolved from the [[Vulgar Latin]]. However, many of them also speak a regional or minority language native to Italy, the existence of which predates the national language of Standard Italian. (According to [[UNESCO]], there are approximately 30 languages native to Italy, although many are often incorrectly referred to as "Italian dialects".) In addition to the approximately 55 million Italians living in Italy (91% of the Italian national population), Italian-speaking groups are found in neighboring nations, including Switzerland, France, the regions of Istria and Dalmatia, and the entire population of [[San Marino]].',
