	{
		title = "Kuomintang",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "KMT" },
		summary = "The **Kuomintang** (**KMT**) is a major [[Political party|political party]] in the Republic of China (Taiwan). It was the [[One-party state|sole ruling party]] of [[Mainland China]] from 1928 to 1949 until [[Retreat of the government of the Republic of China to Taiwan|its relocation to Taiwan]], and in Taiwan ruled under martial law until 1987. The KMT is a centre-right to right-wing party and the largest in the Pan-Blue Coalition, one of Taiwan's two main political groups. Its primary rival is the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP), the largest party in the Pan-Green Coalition. As of 2025, the KMT is the largest single party in the Legislative Yuan and is chaired by Cheng Li-wun.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The party was founded by Sun Yat-sen in 1894 in Honolulu, Republic of Hawaii, as the Revive China Society. He reformed the party in 1919 in the Shanghai French Concession under its current name. From 1926 to 1928, the KMT under Chiang Kai-shek unified China in the Northern Expedition against regional warlords, leading to the fall of the Beiyang government. After initially allying with the [[Chinese Communist Party]] (CCP) in the First United Front, the party under Chiang purged communist members. It was the [[One-party state|sole ruling party]] of China from 1928 to 1949 but gradually lost control while fighting the [[Empire of Japan]] in the Second Sino-Japanese War and the CCP in the Chinese Civil War. In December 1949, the [[Retreat of the government of the Republic of China to Taiwan|Kuomintang retreated to Taiwan]] following its defeat by the communists. From 1949 to 1987, the KMT ruled Taiwan as a [[One-party state|one-party state]] after the February 28 incident.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Founder", "Sun Yat-sen" },
			{ "Headquarters", "No. 232–234, Sec. 2, Bade Rd., Zhongshan District, Taipei City 104, [[Taiwan]]" },
			{ "Country", "the Republic of China" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Kurdish language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Northwestern Iranian dialect continuum",
		tags = { "Indo-European languages", "Iranian languages" },
		aliases = { "Kurdish" },
		summary = "**Kurdish** is a Northwestern Iranian language or [[Dialect continuum|group of languages]] spoken by [[Kurds]] in the region of [[Kurdistan]], namely in southeast Turkey, northern Iraq, northwest Iran, and northern Syria. It is also spoken in northeast Iran, as well as in certain areas of [[Armenia]] and [[Azerbaijan]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Kurdish [[Variety (linguistics)|varieties]] constitute a [[Dialect continuum|dialect continuum]] except for Zaza, with some [[Mutual intelligibility|mutually unintelligible]] varieties, and collectively have 29 million native speakers. The main varieties of Kurdish are Kurmanji, Sorani, and Southern Kurdish. The majority of the Kurds speak Kurmanji, and most Kurdish texts are written in Kurmanji and Sorani. Kurmanji is written in the Hawar alphabet, a derivation of the [[Latin script]], and Sorani is written in the Sorani alphabet, a derivation of the [[Arabic script]]. A separate group of non-Kurdish Northwestern Iranian languages, the Zaza–Gorani languages, are also spoken by several million ethnic Kurds. The literary output in Kurdish was mostly confined to poetry until the early 20th century, when more general literature became developed. Today, the two principal written Kurdish dialects are Kurmanji and Sorani.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Spoken in", "[[Armenia]], [[Azerbaijan]], [[Iran]], [[Iraq]], [[Syria]], [[Turkey]]" },
			{ "Region", "[[Kurdistan]]; Also [[Anatolia]], [[Caucasus]], Khorasan, Kurdish diaspora" },
			{ "Date", "2017–2024" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Kurds",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Iranic ethnic group",
		tags = { "Ethnology", "Ethnic groups" },
		aliases = { "Kurdish people" },
		summary = "**Kurds**, or the **Kurdish people**, are an [[Iranian peoples|Iranic ethnic group]] from [[West Asia]]. They are indigenous to [[Kurdistan]], which is a geographic region spanning southeastern [[Turkey]], northwestern [[Iran]], northern [[Iraq]], and northeastern [[Syria]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The [[Kurdish language|Kurdish languages]] and the Zaza–Gorani languages, both of which belong to the Western Iranic branch of the [[Iranian languages|Iranic language family]], are the native languages of the Kurdish people. Other widely spoken languages among the community are those of their host countries or neighbouring regions, such as [[Turkish language|Turkish]], [[Persian language|Persian]], or [[Arabic]]. The most prevalent religion among Kurds is Sunni Islam, with Shia Islam and Alevism being significant Islamic minorities. Yazidism, which is the ethnic religion of the Kurdish-speaking Yazidi people, is the largest non-Islamic minority religion among the broader Kurdish community, followed by Yarsanism, Zoroastrianism, and Christianity. Although they exercise autonomy in Iraq, the Kurds are a stateless nation.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Quechua people" },
	},
	{
		title = "La Marseillaise",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "National anthem of France",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '"" is the [[National anthem|national anthem]] of [[France]]. It was written in 1792 by Claude Joseph Rouget de Lisle in [[Strasbourg]] after the First French Republic declared war against [[Archduchy of Austria|Austria]], and was originally titled "" ("War Song for the Army of the Rhine").',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The French National Convention adopted it as the [[French First Republic|First Republic]]\'s anthem in 1795. The song acquired its nickname after being sung in [[Paris]] by Fédérés (volunteers) from [[Marseille]] marching to the capital. Its evocative melody and lyrics have led to its widespread use as a song of revolution and its incorporation into many pieces of classical and popular music. In 2013, the Italian violinist Guido Rimonda pointed out that the incipit of Giovanni Battista Viotti\'s "Tema e variazioni in Do maggiore" strongly resembles the anthem. This incipit was first thought to have been published before "La Marseillaise", but that appears to be a misconception, as Viotti published several variations on "La Marseillaise" in 1795 and wrote as a note "I have never composed the quartets below".',
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Country", "France" },
			{ "Author", "Claude Joseph Rouget de Lisle" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Labour Party (UK)",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🎉",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Political party",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Labour Party" },
		summary = "The **Labour Party**, commonly **Labour**, is a political party in the [[United Kingdom]]. It sits on the centre-left of the left–right political spectrum, and has historically been an alliance of democratic socialists, social democrats and [[Trade union|trade unionists]]. It has been the governing party since the 2024 general election. Andy Burnham became Leader of the Labour Party and Prime Minister of the United Kingdom in 2026, succeeding Keir Starmer, who had been elected party leader in 2020 and led Labour into government. There have been twelve Labour governments and eight Labour prime ministers.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The party meets annually during Autumn for the Labour Party Conference, during which delegates from local parties and trade unions vote on party policy, and senior figures address the audience from the Conference platform. The Labour Party was founded in 1900, having emerged from the trade union movement and socialist parties of the 19th century. In 1918 it became guided by socialism and a commitment to "common ownership" via nationalisation. It was electorally weak before the [[World War I|First World War]], but in the early 1920s overtook the Liberal Party to become the main opposition to the Conservative Party, and briefly formed a minority government under Ramsay MacDonald in 1924. In 1929 Labour became the largest party in the House of Commons for the first time, with 287 seats, but fell short of a majority, forming another minority government.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Founded", "27 February 1900 (as the Labour Representation Committee)" },
			{ "Country", "the United Kingdom" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Labour economics",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📈",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of the markets for wage labour",
		tags = { "Business and economics" },
		summary = "**Labour economics** is the subfield of [[Economics|economics]] concerned with the study of [[Work (human activity)|labour]] as an input to [[Production (economics)|economic production]]. Broadly, it surveys labor [[Market (economics)|markets]] and the economic decisions of agents (i.e., workers and employers) participating in such markets.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Topics of study include the labour supply of workers and how it is affected by variables such as age, [[Education|education]], [[Gender|gender]] and childbearing, as well as the labour demand by firms searching for different forms of labour as an input in the production of goods and services. Other topics of study in labour economics include [[School|schooling]] and human capital, [[Economic inequality|inequality]] and [[Discrimination|discrimination]], collective bargaining and [[Trade union|trade unions]], technological change and [[Unemployment|unemployment]], [[Ownership|ownership]] and monopsony, and [[Public policy|public policies]] such as unemployment benefits, [[Pension|pensions]], [[Health care|health care]] and minimum wages.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ladakh",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Union territory administered by India",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Ladakh** is a region administered by [[India]] as a [[Union territory|union territory]] and constitutes an eastern portion of the larger [[Kashmir]] region that has been the subject of a [[Kashmir|dispute]] between India and [[Pakistan]] since 1947 and India and [[China]] since 1959. Ladakh is bordered by the [[Tibet Autonomous Region]] of China to the east, the Indian state of [[Himachal Pradesh]] to the south, both the Indian-administered union territory of [[Jammu and Kashmir (union territory)|Jammu and Kashmir]] and the Pakistan-administered [[Gilgit-Baltistan]] to the west, and the southwest corner of [[Xinjiang]] across the Karakoram Pass in the far north.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It extends from the Siachen Glacier in the [[Karakoram]] range to the north to the main Great [[Himalayas]] to the south. The eastern end, consisting of the uninhabited Aksai Chin plains, is claimed by the Indian Government as part of Ladakh, but has been under Chinese control since 1962. In the past, Ladakh gained importance from its strategic location at the crossroads of important trade routes, but as Chinese authorities closed the borders between Tibet Autonomous Region and Ladakh in the 1960s, international trade dwindled. Since 1974, the [[Government of India]] has encouraged tourism in Ladakh. Ladakh was established as a union territory of India on 31 October 2019, following the passage of the Jammu and Kashmir Reorganisation Act. Prior to that, it was part of the Jammu and Kashmir state. The largest city in Ladakh is Leh, followed by Kargil, each of which headquarters a district.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Region administered by India as a [[Union territory|union territory]]" },
			{ "Area", "59,146 km²" },
			{ "Population", "274,289" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Landmass",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Large area of land",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Land mass" },
		summary = "A **landmass**, or **land mass**, is a large [[Region|region]] or area of [[Land|land]] that is in one piece and not noticeably broken up by [[Ocean|oceans]]. The term is often used to refer to lands surrounded by an ocean or [[Ocean|sea]], such as a [[Continent|continent]] or a large [[Island|island]]. In the field of [[Geology|geology]], a landmass is a defined section of [[Continental crust|continental crust]] extending above [[Sea level|sea level]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Continents are often thought of as distinct landmasses and may include any islands that are part of the associated [[Continental shelf|continental shelf]]. When multiple continents form a single contiguous land connection, the connected continents may be viewed as a single landmass. [[Earth]]'s largest landmasses are (starting with largest):",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Landscape",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Visible features of a land area",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **landscape** is the visible features of an area of land, its [[Landform|landforms]], and how they integrate with [[Nature|natural]] or human-made features, often considered in terms of their aesthetic appeal. A landscape includes the physical elements of geophysically defined [[Landform|landforms]] such as mountains, hills, water bodies such as [[River|rivers]], [[Lake|lakes]], [[Pond|ponds]] and the [[Ocean|sea]], living elements of land cover including indigenous [[Vegetation|vegetation]], human elements including different forms of land use, buildings, and [[Structure|structures]], and transitory elements such as [[Lighting|lighting]] and [[Weather|weather]] conditions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Combining both their physical origins and the [[Culture|cultural]] overlay of human presence, often created over millennia, landscapes reflect a living synthesis of people and place that is vital to local and national identity. The character of a landscape helps define the self-image of the people who inhabit it and a sense of place that differentiates one region from other regions. It is the dynamic backdrop to people's lives. Landscape can be as varied as farmland, a landscape park or wilderness. The [[Earth]] has a vast range of landscapes including the icy landscapes of [[Polar regions|polar regions]], [[Mountain|mountainous]] landscapes, vast arid [[Desert|desert]] landscapes, [[Island|islands]], and [[Coast|coastal]] landscapes, densely [[Forest|forested]] or wooded landscapes including past boreal forests and tropical rainforests and [[Agriculture|agricultural]] landscapes of [[Temperate climate|temperate]] and [[Tropics|tropical]] regions.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Language acquisition",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Process in which a first language is being acquired",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Language acquisition** is the process by which humans acquire the capacity to perceive and comprehend [[Language|language]]. In other words, it is how human beings gain the ability to be aware of language, to understand it, and to produce and use [[Word|words]] and [[Sentence (linguistics)|sentences]] to communicate.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Language acquisition involves structures, rules, and representation. The capacity to successfully use language requires human beings to acquire a range of tools, including [[Phonology|phonology]], [[Morphology (linguistics)|morphology]], [[Syntax|syntax]], [[Semantics|semantics]], and an extensive [[Vocabulary|vocabulary]]. Language can be vocalized as in speech, or manual as in [[Sign language|sign]]. Human language capacity is represented in the brain. Even though human language capacity is finite, one can say and understand an effectively infinite number of sentences, which is based on a syntactic principle called recursion. Evidence suggests that every individual has three recursive mechanisms that allow sentences to go indeterminately. These three mechanisms are: relativization, complementation and coordination.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Language family",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Group of languages related through a common ancestor",
		tags = { "Language", "Language families" },
		summary = "A **language family** is a group of [[Language|languages]] related through descent from a common ancestor, called the [[Proto-language|proto-language]] of that family. The term family is a metaphor borrowed from biology, with the tree model used in [[Historical linguistics|historical linguistics]] analogous to a family tree, or to [[Phylogenetic tree|phylogenetic trees]] of taxa used in evolutionary [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomy]]. Linguists thus describe the daughter languages within a language family as being genetically related. The divergence of a proto-language into daughter languages typically occurs through geographical separation, with different regional dialects of the proto-language undergoing different language changes and thus becoming distinct languages over time.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"One well-known example of a language family is the [[Romance languages]], including [[Spanish language|Spanish]], [[French language|French]], [[Italian language|Italian]], [[Portuguese language|Portuguese]], [[Romanian language|Romanian]], [[Catalan language|Catalan]], Romansh, and many others, all of which are descended from [[Vulgar Latin]]. The Romance family itself is part of the larger Indo-European family, which includes many other languages native to [[Europe]] and [[South Asia]], all believed to be descended from a common ancestor known as Proto-Indo-European. The Indo-European family is the largest language family in terms of number of living speakers, and the best-studied. A language family is usually said to contain at least two languages, although [[Language isolate|language isolates]] — languages that are not related to any other language — are occasionally referred to as families that contain one language. Conversely, there is no upper bound to the number of languages a family can contain.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Language isolate",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "That is, an isolate is a [[Language family|language family]] that only contains one member. [[Basque language|Basque]] in Europe, Ainu (if counted as a single language) and Burushaski in Asia, Sandawe and Hadza in Africa, Haida and Zuni in North America, Kanoê and Trumai in South America, Tiwi and possibly Porome in Oceania are examples of language isolates, as too are well attested extinct languages such as [[Sumerian language|Sumerian]] and Elamite. The exact number of language isolates is unknown due to insufficient data on several languages; that is, there is a fuzzy boundary between language isolates and unclassified languages.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Researchers may have differing criteria on how much comparative work needs to be done before concluding that a language is an isolate. "Isolate" does not mean that a language has no relatives, only that any relationships are too distant to be detectable. Most established families of oral languages – including isolates – are assumed to be related to each other at a time depth too great for us to reconstruct. (See linguistic monogenesis.) Another possibility is that the language arose independently and does not share a common linguistic genesis with any other language. This possibility is often posited for sign languages that are thought to have developed independently of other oral or sign languages. In some classifications, a language may be counted as an isolate once all known relatives are extinct.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Languages of Africa",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The number of languages natively spoken in [[Africa]] is variously estimated (depending on the delineation of [[Language|language]] vs. [[Dialect|dialect]]) at between 1,250 and 2,100, and by some counts at over 3,000. [[Nigeria]] alone has over 500 languages (according to SIL Ethnologue), one of the greatest concentrations of linguistic diversity in the world. The **languages of Africa** belong to many distinct [[Language family|language families]], among which the largest are:",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Niger–Congo languages|Niger–Congo]], which include the large Atlantic-Congo and [[Bantu languages|Bantu branches]] in [[West Africa|West]], [[Central Africa|Central]], [[Southeast Africa|Southeast]] and [[Southern Africa]]. [[Afroasiatic languages]] are spread throughout [[West Asia|Western Asia]], [[North Africa]], the [[Horn of Africa]] and parts of the [[Sahel]]. Saharan, Nilotic and Central Sudanic languages (grouped under the hypothetical [[Nilo-Saharan languages|Nilo-Saharan]] macro-family), are present in East Africa and [[Sahel]]. [[Austronesian languages]] are spoken in [[Madagascar]] and parts of the [[Comoros]]. Khoe–Kwadi languages are spoken mostly in [[Namibia]] and [[Botswana]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Houghton Mifflin Harcourt" },
	},
	{
		title = "Languages of India",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Languages of India** belong to several language families, the major ones being the [[Indo-Aryan languages]] spoken by 78.05% of Indians and the [[Dravidian languages]] spoken by 19.64% of Indians; both families together are sometimes known as Indic languages. Languages spoken by the remaining 2.31% of the population belong to the [[Austroasiatic languages|Austroasiatic]], [[Sino-Tibetan languages|Sino–Tibetan]], [[Kra–Dai languages|Tai–Kadai]], Andamanese, and a few other minor language families and [[Language isolate|isolates]]. According to the People's Linguistic Survey of India, India has the second highest number of languages (780), after [[Papua New Guinea]] (841). [[Ethnologue]] lists a lower number of 459.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Article 343 of the Constitution of India stated that the [[Official language|official language]] of the Union is [[Hindi]] in [[Devanagari]] script, with official use of [[English language|English]] to continue for 15 years. Opposition to increased Hindi usage in South India led to the passage of the Official Languages Act in 1963, amending the constitution to allow for the continuation of English alongside Hindi in the Indian government indefinitely. The form of numerals to be used for the official purposes of the Union are "the international form of Indian numerals", which are referred to as [[Arabic numerals]] in most English-speaking countries. Despite some misconceptions, Hindi is not the [[National language|national language]] of India; the Constitution of India does not give any language the status of national language.',
			},
		},
		related = { "UN/LOCODE" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Country", "India" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Languages of Indonesia",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "[[Indonesia]] is home to over 700 living [[Language|languages]] spoken across its extensive [[Archipelago|archipelago]]. This significant linguistic variety constitutes approximately 10% of the world’s total languages, positioning Indonesia as the second most linguistically diverse nation globally, following [[Papua New Guinea]]. The majority of these languages belong to the [[Austronesian languages|Austronesian language]] family, prevalent in the western and central regions of Indonesia, including languages such as Acehnese, [[Sundanese language|Sundanese]], and Buginese.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In contrast, the eastern regions, particularly [[Western New Guinea|Papua]] and the [[Maluku Islands]], are home to more than 150 Papuan languages, which are distinct from the Austronesian family and represent a unique linguistic heritage. The language most widely spoken as a native language is [[Javanese language|Javanese]], primarily by the [[Javanese people]] in the central and eastern parts of [[Java]] Island, as well as across many other islands due to migration. Languages in Indonesia are classified into nine categories: [[National language|national language]], locally used indigenous languages, regional lingua francas, foreign and [[Second language|additional languages]], heritage languages, languages in the religious domain, English as a lingua franca, and [[Sign language|sign languages]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Johann Georg Wagler" },
	},
	{
		title = "Languages of the European Union",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'The [[European Union]] (EU) has 24 [[Official language|official languages]], of which the three most natively spoken ones are [[German language|German]], [[French language|French]] and [[Italian language|Italian]]. Previously, [[English language|English]], French and German were considered "procedural" languages, but this notion was abandoned by the [[European Commission]], whereas the [[European Parliament]] accepts all official languages as [[Working language|working languages]]. Today, English and French are used in the day-to-day workings of the institutions of the EU. Institutions have the right to define the linguistic regime of their working, but the Commission and a number of other institutions have not done so, as indicated by several judicial rulings.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The EU asserts that it is in favour of linguistic diversity. This principle is enshrined in Article 22 of the Charter of Fundamental Rights (art. 22) and in the Treaty on European Union (art. 3(3) TEU). In the EU, language policy is the responsibility of member states, and the EU does not have a common language policy; EU institutions play a supporting role in this field, based on the principle of "subsidiarity"; they promote a European dimension in the member states\' language policies. The EU encourages all its citizens to be [[Multilingualism|multilingual]]; specifically, it encourages them to be able to speak two languages in addition to their [[First language|native language]]. Though the EU has very limited influence in this area, as the content of educational systems is the responsibility of individual member states, a number of EU funding programmes actively promote language learning and linguistic diversity.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Languages with official recognition in India",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Languages designated official status by the Constitution of India",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Scheduled languages", "Official languages of India" },
		summary = "As of 2025, 22 languages have been classified as **scheduled languages** under the Eighth Schedule to the Constitution of India. There is no [[National language|national language]] of [[India]], per its constitution. The union government uses [[Hindi]] and [[English language|English]] for schedule/official works, while state governments and union territories use their respective languages for official works.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When the constitution was adopted in 1950, article 343 declared that Hindi would be the official language and English would serve as an additional official language for a period not exceeding 15 years. Article 344(1) defined a set of 14 regional languages which were represented in the Official Languages Commission. The commission was to suggest steps to be taken to progressively promote the use of Hindi as the official language of the country. The Official Languages Act, 1963, which came into effect on 26 January 1965, made provision for the continuation of English as an official language alongside Hindi.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lao language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Kra–Dai language",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Lao", "Laotian" },
		summary = "**Lao**, sometimes referred to as **Laotian**, is a Southwestern Tai language and the official language of [[Laos]]. It is also spoken in the Isan region of northeastern [[Thailand]], where it is usually called the Isan language. The political split of the Lao-speaking population along the Mekong River resulted from the Franco-Siamese crisis of 1893. Lao is written in the Lao script, an [[Abugida|abugida]] that evolved from early Tai scripts.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Lao is a [[Tone (linguistics)|tonal]] and analytic language, characteristics shared with other [[Kra–Dai languages|Kra-Dai]] languages as well as nearby [[Sino-Tibetan languages|Sino-Tibetan]] and [[Austroasiatic languages|Austroasiatic]] languages. It features high [[Mutual intelligibility|mutual intelligibility]] with [[Thai language|Thai]] and Isan, enabling direct communication between speakers despite differences in their respective writing systems. The [[Vientiane]] dialect serves as the de facto standard. In addition to being the sole official language of Laos, Lao serves as a [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] among the country's ethnically and linguistically diverse population. It is also spoken by diaspora communities, primarily in the United States, France, and Australia.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Market capitalization" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Date", "2015" },
