	{
		title = "Lexicon",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Vocabulary of a language or branch of knowledge",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Linguistics|linguistics]], a lexicon is a language's inventory of lexemes. The word lexicon derives from Greek word, neuter of meaning 'of or for words'.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Linguistic theories generally regard human languages as consisting of two parts: a lexicon, essentially a catalogue of a language's words (its wordstock); and a [[Grammar|grammar]], a system of rules which allow for the combination of those words into meaningful sentences. The lexicon is also thought to include bound morphemes, which cannot stand alone as words (such as most affixes). In some analyses, compound words and certain classes of idiomatic expressions, collocations and other phrasemes are also considered to be part of the lexicon. [[Dictionary|Dictionaries]] are lists of the lexicon, in alphabetical order, of a given language; usually, however, bound morphemes are not included.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Dictionary.com" },
	},
	{
		title = "Liberal democracy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗳️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Form of government",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Western-style democracy" },
		summary = "**Liberal democracy**, also called **Western-style democracy**, is a form of government that combines the organization of a [[Democracy|democracy]] with ideas of [[Liberalism|liberal]] political philosophy. Common elements within a liberal democracy are: a [[Representative democracy|representative democracy]] with multiple viable political parties and universal suffrage; a [[Separation of powers|separation of powers]] into different branches of government, including an independent judiciary; the [[Rule of law|rule of law]] in everyday life as part of an open society; a [[Market economy|market]]-based [[Capitalism|capitalist]] economy with [[Private property|private property]]; and [[State (polity)|state]]-guaranteed protections for the [[Human rights|human rights]], [[Civil and political rights|civil rights]], [[Civil liberties|civil liberties]], and [[Political freedom|political freedoms]] of legally equal citizens.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Under liberal democracy, government authority is legitimately exercised only in accordance with written, publicly disclosed laws adopted and enforced in accordance with established procedure. In practice, such systems often draw upon a [[Constitution|constitution]], either codified or uncodified, to delineate the powers of government and enshrine the [[Social contract|social contract]]. Liberal democracies may take various and mixed forms, such as a [[Constitutional monarchy|constitutional monarchic]] or [[Republic|republican]] constitutional system; a [[Parliamentary system|parliamentary]], [[Presidential system|presidential]], or [[Semi-presidential system|semi-presidential]] governmental system; and a [[Common law|common]], [[Civil law (legal system)|civil]], or [[Customary law|customary]] legal system. Liberal-democratic regimes are contrasted with illiberal democracies, hybrid regimes, and [[Dictatorship|dictatorships]]; as well as [[Direct democracy|direct democracies]] and socialist democracies.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Lonely Planet" },
	},
	{
		title = "Liberalism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🤔",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Philosophy of individual rights and liberty",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Liberalism** is a [[Political philosophy|political]] and moral philosophy based on the rights of the individual, [[Liberty|liberty]], consent of the governed, political equality, right to private property, and equality before the law. Different liberals espouse various and sometimes conflicting views depending on their understanding of these principles but generally support [[Liberal democracy|liberal democracy]], [[Private property|private property]], market economies, individual rights (including [[Civil and political rights|civil rights]] and [[Human rights|human rights]]), secularism, [[Rule of law|rule of law]], economic and [[Political freedom|political freedom]], [[Freedom of speech|freedom of speech]], [[Freedom of the press|freedom of the press]], freedom of assembly, and [[Freedom of religion|freedom of religion]]. Liberalism is frequently cited as the dominant [[Ideology|ideology]] of [[Modern era|modern history]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Liberalism became a distinct movement in the [[Age of Enlightenment]], gaining popularity among [[Western world|Western]] philosophers and economists. Liberalism sought to replace the norms of [[Nobility|hereditary privilege]], state religion, [[Absolute monarchy|absolute monarchy]], the divine right of kings, and traditional conservatism with [[Representative democracy|representative democracy]], rule of law, and equality under the law. Liberals also ended mercantilist policies, royal monopolies, and other trade barriers, instead promoting [[Free trade|free trade]] and marketization. The philosopher [[John Locke]] is often credited with founding liberalism as a distinct tradition based on the [[Social contract|social contract]], arguing that each man has a natural right to life, liberty and property, and governments must not violate these [[Rights|rights]]. While the British liberal tradition emphasized expanding democracy, French liberalism emphasized rejecting [[Authoritarianism|authoritarianism]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Pierre André Latreille" },
	},
	{
		title = "Liberty",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Philosophical and political concept",
		tags = { "Society", "Social status" },
		summary = "**Liberty** is the state of being [[Freedom|free]] within society from restrictions imposed by [[Authority|authority]] on one's way of life, behavior, or political views. The concept of liberty can vary depending on perspective and context. In the constitutional law of the United States, ordered liberty means creating a balanced society where individuals have the freedom to act without unnecessary interference (negative liberty) and access to opportunities and resources to pursue their goals (positive liberty), all within a fair legal system.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Sometimes liberty is differentiated from freedom by using the word "freedom" primarily, if not exclusively, to mean the ability to do as one wills and what one has the power to do; and using the word "liberty" to mean the absence of arbitrary restraints, taking into account the rights of all involved. In this sense, the exercise of liberty is subject to capability and limited by the rights of others. Thus liberty entails the responsible use of freedom under the [[Rule of law|rule of law]] without depriving anyone else of their freedom. Liberty can be taken away as a form of punishment. In many countries, people can be deprived of their liberty if they are convicted of criminal acts. Liberty\'s [[Etymology|etymology]] is from the [[Latin]] word liber, from Proto-Italic, from [[Proto-Indo-European language|Proto-Indo-European]], from (\'people\').',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Library of Alexandria",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📚",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Library in ancient Alexandria, Egypt",
		tags = { "Libraries", "Africa" },
		summary = "The **Library of Alexandria** in [[Alexandria]], Egypt, was one of the largest and most significant libraries of the ancient world. The library was part of a larger research institution called the Mouseion, which was dedicated to the Muses, the nine goddesses of the arts. The idea of a universal library in Alexandria may have been proposed by Demetrius of Phalerum, an exiled Athenian statesman living in Alexandria, to [[Ptolemy I Soter]], who may have established plans for the library, but the library itself was probably not built until the reign of his son Ptolemy II Philadelphus.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The library quickly acquired many [[Papyrus|papyrus]] [[Scroll|scrolls]], owing largely to the Ptolemaic kings' aggressive and well-funded policies for procuring texts. It is unknown how many scrolls were housed at any given time, but estimates range from 40,000 to 700,000 at its height. Alexandria came to be regarded as the capital of knowledge and learning, in part because of the Great Library.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Country", "[[Ptolemaic Kingdom]]" },
			{ "Type", "National library" },
			{ "Established", "Possibly during the reign of Ptolemy II Philadelphus (285–246 BC)" },
			{ "Location", "[[Alexandria]], Egypt" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Library of Congress",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📚",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "US Congress research library",
		tags = { "Libraries", "Americas" },
		aliases = { "LOC" },
		summary = "The **Library of Congress** (**LOC**) is a research library in Washington, D.C., serving as the library and research service for the [[United States Congress]] and the de facto national library of the United States. It also administers copyright law through the United States Copyright Office, and it houses the Congressional Research Service.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Founded in 1800, the Library of Congress is the oldest federal cultural institution in the United States. It is housed in three buildings on Capitol Hill, adjacent to the United States Capitol, along with the National Audio-Visual Conservation Center in Culpeper, Virginia, and additional storage facilities at Fort George G. Meade and Cabin Branch in Hyattsville, Maryland. The library\'s functions are overseen by the librarian of Congress, and its buildings are maintained by the architect of the Capitol. The library is one of the largest libraries in the world, containing approximately 173 million items and employing over 3,000 staff. Its collections are "universal, not limited by subject, format, or national boundary, and include research materials from all parts of the world and in more than 470 languages".',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Location", "101 Independence Ave., S.E., Washington, D.C., U.S." },
			{ "Established", "April 24, 1800" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Libretto",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Text used in an extended musical work such as an opera or musical",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **libretto** is the text used in, or intended for, an extended musical work such as an [[Opera|opera]], operetta, masque, [[Oratorio|oratorio]], cantata or [[Musical theatre|musical]]. The term libretto is also sometimes used to refer to the story line of a [[Ballet|ballet]] or the texts of major liturgical works, such as the Mass, requiem, or sacred cantata.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The [[Italian language|Italian]] word is the diminutive of the word libro ("book"). Sometimes other-language equivalents are used for libretti in that language, livret for French works, Textbuch for German and libreto for Spanish. A libretto is distinct from a synopsis or scenario of the plot, in that the libretto contains all the words and stage directions, while a synopsis summarizes the plot. Some [[Ballet|ballet]] historians also use the word libretto to refer to the 15- to 40-page books which were on sale to 19th century ballet audiences in Paris and contained a very detailed description of the ballet\'s story, scene by scene. The relationship of the **librettist** (that is, the writer of a libretto) to the [[Composer|composer]] in the creation of a musical work has varied over the centuries, as have the sources and the writing techniques employed.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lie",
		category = "Concept",
