	{
		title = "Mainland China",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Geopolitical term for China without Hong Kong, Macau, Taiwan",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '"**Mainland China**" is a [[Geopolitics|geopolitical]] term defined as the territory under direct administration of the [[China|People\'s Republic of China]] (PRC) in the aftermath of the Chinese Civil War. In addition to the geographical mainland, the geopolitical sense of the term includes islands such as [[Hainan]], Chongming, and Zhoushan.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'By convention, the following territories are excluded from the term: [[Special administrative regions of China]], which are regarded as subdivisions of the country, but retain distinct administrative, judicial and economic systems from those on the mainland: [[Hong Kong]], formerly a [[British Hong Kong|British colony]] [[Macau]], formerly a Portuguese colony Taiwan, along with Penghu, Kinmen, Matsu and other minor islands, are collectively known as the Taiwan Area, where has been the major territorial base of the government of Taiwan (ROC) since 1950. The ROC still claims the "Mainland Area" as its constitutionally defined territory. An alteration of the constitution in this regard would be interpreted by PRC as a formal declaration of independence, triggering military action according to PRC\'s Anti-Secession Law. In Taiwan, the term is often used to refer to all territories administered by the PRC.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "People are called", "[[Chinese people|Chinese]]" },
			{ "Languages", "[[Standard Chinese]] (de facto)" },
			{ "Area", "9,596,961 km²" },
			{ "Population", "1,400,050,000" },
			{ "Currency", "[[Renminbi]] (RMB)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Maithili language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Indo-Aryan language spoken in India and Nepal",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Maithili" },
		summary = "**Maithili** (Tirhuta:, ) is an Indo-Aryan language spoken in parts of [[India]] and [[Nepal]]. It is native to the Mithila region, which encompasses parts of the eastern Indian states of [[Bihar]] and [[Jharkhand]] as well as Nepal's Koshi and Madhesh Provinces. It is one of the 22 scheduled [[Languages of India|languages of India]]. It is the second most commonly spoken native Nepalese language constitutionally registered as one of the fourteen provincial official languages of Nepal.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is spoken by over 75 million people. The language is predominantly written in [[Devanagari]] this time, but Tirhuta script which is the historical and original script of Maithili, remains widely used today. The Indian government has stepped up efforts to bring the Tirhuta, the original script of Maithili language onto major digital platforms, including Google Keyboard and Android and iOS operating systems, in a move welcomed by linguists and language activists. The Maithili language has been included in the CBSE curriculum without the Tirhuta script which is the original script of Maithili.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Spoken in", "[[India]] and [[Nepal]]" },
			{ "Region", "Mithila" },
			{ "Speakers", "75 million" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Major non-NATO ally",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Special designation of the United States",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "MNNA" },
		summary = "A **major non-NATO ally** (**MNNA**) is a designation given by the [[Federal government of the United States|United States government]] to countries that have strategic working relationships with the United States Armed Forces while not being members of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization ([[NATO]]).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While MNNA status does not automatically constitute a mutual defense pact with the [[United States]]—as would be the case through NATO membership—it does confer a variety of military and financial advantages that are otherwise unobtainable by non-NATO countries. The designation also denotes strong diplomatic and economic ties and is considered a symbol of mutual friendship. There are currently 22 major non-NATO allies across four continents: 12 in [[Asia]], four in [[Africa]], four in [[South America]], and two in [[Oceania]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Founder", "Sam Nunn" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Major professional sports leagues in the United States and Canada",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏆",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "Major professional sports leagues in the United States and Canada, which compete in the sports of [[American football|football]], [[Baseball|baseball]], [[Basketball|basketball]], [[Ice hockey|ice hockey]], and [[Association football|soccer]], include the [[National Football League]] (NFL), [[Major League Baseball]] (MLB), the [[NBA|National Basketball Association]] (NBA), the [[National Hockey League]] (NHL), and Major League Soccer (MLS). The NFL only competes in the United States, with the prominent [[American football|gridiron football]] league in Canada being the Canadian Football League (CFL).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'MLB, the NBA, the NFL, and the NHL are commonly referred to as the **"Big Four"**. Each of these is the wealthiest professional club competition in its sport worldwide and, along with the [[Premier League]], make up the top five sports leagues by revenue in the world. Each of the Big Four leagues, as well as MLS and the CFL, averages at least 15,000 fans in attendance per game as of 2024. The NFL has the largest stadiums on average in the world, ranging in capacity from just over 60,000 to almost 100,000 spectators, while MLB ballparks generally hold between 30,000 and 50,000 fans. Venues used primarily by MLS and CFL vary more widely in capacity, from about 20,000 to about 60,000. The two indoor leagues, the NHL and NBA, play mostly in arenas that hold 18,000 to 20,000 seats.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Malagasy language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Austronesian language of Madagascar",
		tags = { "Specific languages", "Austronesian languages" },
		summary = "The standard variety, called Official Malagasy, is one of the [[Official language|official languages]] of Madagascar, alongside [[French language|French]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Malagasy is the westernmost Austronesian language, brought to Madagascar with the settlement of [[Austronesian peoples|Austronesian speakers]] from the Sunda Islands (about 7,300 kilometres or 4,500 miles away) around the 5th century AD or perhaps between the 7th and 13th centuries. The Malagasy language is one of the Barito languages and is most closely related to the Maʼanyan language, still spoken on [[Borneo]]. Malagasy also includes numerous [[Malay language|Malay]] loanwords from the time of the early Austronesian settlement and trading between Madagascar and the Sunda Islands. After c. 1000 AD, Malagasy incorporated numerous [[Bantu languages|Bantu]] and [[Arabic]] loanwords brought over by traders and new settlers. Malagasy is spoken by around 25 million people in Madagascar and the [[Comoros]]. Most people in Madagascar speak it as a first language, as do some people of Malagasy descent elsewhere.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Spoken in", "[[Madagascar]], [[Mayotte]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Malay language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Austronesian language",
		tags = { "Specific languages", "Austronesian languages" },
		aliases = { "Malay" },
		summary = "**Malay** is an [[Austronesian languages|Austronesian language]] native to several islands of Maritime Southeast Asia and the [[Malay Peninsula]] on mainland Asia. The language is an [[Official language|official language]] of [[Brunei]], [[Malaysia]], and [[Singapore]], where the standardised forms are known as Standard Malay. Within the Malay language family, another standardised form which is known as [[Indonesian language|Indonesian]] is the official language in Indonesia and also one of the working languages of [[Timor-Leste]]. Malay is the ethnic language of [[Malays (ethnic group)|Malays]] in Sumatra, Borneo and surrounding islands in Indonesia, the [[Malay Peninsula]], southeast [[Philippines]], southern Thailand, and the Southern Province of [[Sri Lanka]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Altogether, it is spoken as a first language by about 80 million people and as a first or second language by close to 300 million. The language is pluricentric and a macrolanguage, i.e., a group of [[Mutual intelligibility|mutually intelligible]] speech varieties, or [[Dialect continuum|dialect continuum]], that have no traditional name in common, and which may be considered distinct languages by their speakers.",
			},
