	{
		title = "March equinox",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "☀️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "When sun appears directly over equator",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Northward equinox" },
		summary = "The **March equinox** or **northward equinox** is the [[Equinox|equinox]] on the [[Earth]] when the subsolar point appears to leave the [[Southern Hemisphere]] and cross the celestial equator, heading northward as seen from Earth. The March equinox is known as the vernal equinox (or spring equinox) in the [[Northern Hemisphere]] and as the autumnal equinox in the Southern Hemisphere.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"On the [[Gregorian calendar]] at 0° longitude, the northward equinox usually occurs on March 20. However, it can occur as early as March 19 (which happened most recently in 1796, and will happen next in 2044), and it can occur as late as March 21 (which happened most recently in 2007, and will happen next in 2102). For a common year the [[Gregorian calendar|computed time slippage]] is about 5 hours 49 minutes later than the previous year, and for a leap year about 18 hours 11 minutes earlier than the previous year. Balancing the increases of the common years against the losses of the leap years keeps the calendar date of the March equinox from drifting more than one day from March 20 each year.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mariana Islands",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏝️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Archipelago in the north-western Pacific Ocean",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "The Marianas" },
		summary = "The **Mariana Islands**, also simply **the Marianas**, are a crescent-shaped [[Archipelago|archipelago]] comprising the summits of fifteen longitudinally oriented, mostly [[Volcano|dormant volcanic]] mountains in the north-western [[Pacific Ocean]], between the 12th and 21st parallels north and along the 145th meridian east. They lie south-southeast of Japan, west-southwest of [[Hawaii]], north of [[New Guinea]], and east of the [[Philippines]], demarcating the [[Philippine Sea]]'s eastern limit.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"They are found in the northern part of the western [[Oceania|Oceanic]] sub-region of [[Micronesia]], and are politically divided into two jurisdictions of the United States: the Commonwealth of the [[Northern Mariana Islands]] and, at the southern end of the chain, the territory of [[Guam]]. The islands were named after the influential Spanish queen Mariana of Austria following their colonization in the 17th century. The indigenous inhabitants are the Chamorro people. Archaeologists in 2013 reported findings which indicated that the people who first settled the Marianas arrived there after making what may have been at the time the longest uninterrupted ocean voyage in human history. They further reported findings which suggested that Tinian is likely to have been the first island in Oceania to have been settled by humans.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Bond albedo" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Location", "Pacific Ocean" },
			{ "Country", "United States [[Guam]] (U.S.) [[Northern Mariana Islands]] (U.S.)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Marine ecosystem",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ecosystem in saltwater environment",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Marine ecosystems" },
		summary = "**Marine ecosystems** are the largest of [[Earth]]'s [[Aquatic ecosystem|aquatic ecosystems]] and exist in [[Saline water|waters that have a high salt]] content. These systems contrast with [[Freshwater ecosystem|freshwater ecosystems]], which have a lower [[Salt|salt]] content. Marine waters cover more than 70% of the surface of the Earth and account for more than 97% of Earth's water supply and 90% of habitable space on Earth. Seawater has an average salinity of 35 [[Parts-per notation|parts per thousand]] of water. Actual salinity varies among different marine ecosystems. Marine ecosystems can be divided into many zones depending upon water depth and shoreline features. The [[Ocean|oceanic]] zone is the vast open part of the ocean where animals such as whales, sharks, and tuna live.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The benthic zone consists of substrates below water where many invertebrates live. The [[Intertidal zone|intertidal zone]] is the area between high and low tides. Other near-shore (neritic) zones can include mudflats, seagrass meadows, [[Mangrove|mangroves]], rocky [[Intertidal zone|intertidal systems]], salt marshes, [[Coral reef|coral reefs]], kelp forests and [[Lagoon|lagoons]]. In the deep water, [[Hydrothermal vent|hydrothermal vents]] may occur where chemosynthetic [[Sulfur|sulfur]] [[Bacteria|bacteria]] form the base of the food web. Marine ecosystems are characterized by the biological [[Marine life|community of organisms]] that they are associated with and their physical environment. [[Class (taxonomy)|Classes]] of organisms found in marine ecosystems include [[Brown algae|brown algae]], [[Dinoflagellate|dinoflagellates]], [[Coral reef|corals]], [[Cephalopod|cephalopods]], [[Echinoderm|echinoderms]], and [[Shark|sharks]]. Marine ecosystems are important sources of [[Ecosystem service|ecosystem services]] and [[Fishing industry|food and jobs for significant portions of the global population]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Marine life",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧂",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Organisms that live in salt water",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Sea life", "Ocean life" },
		summary = "**Marine life**, which is also known as **sea life** or **ocean life**, refers to all the marine organisms that live in salt water habitats, or ecological communities that encompass all [[Aquatic animal|aquatic animals]], [[Aquatic plant|plants]], [[Algae|algae]], fungi, protists, single-celled microorganisms and associated viruses living in the [[Saline water|saline water]] of marine habitats, either the sea water of marginal seas and [[Ocean|oceans]], or the [[Brackish water|brackish water]] of [[Coast|coastal]] [[Wetland|wetlands]], [[Lagoon|lagoons]], [[Estuary|estuaries]] and inland seas. As of 2023, more than 242,000 marine [[Species|species]] have been documented, and perhaps two million marine species are yet to be documented. On average, researches describe about 2,300 new marine species each year.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The study of marine life spans into multiple fields, which is primarily marine biology, as well as biological oceanography. By volume, oceans provide about 90% of the living space on [[Earth]], and served as the cradle of life and vital biotic sanctuaries throughout Earth's geological history. The earliest known life forms evolved as anaerobic [[Prokaryote|prokaryotes]] ([[Archaea|archaea]] and [[Bacteria|bacteria]]) in the [[Archean]] oceans around the [[Deep sea|deep sea]] [[Hydrothermal vent|hydrothermal vents]], before photoautotrophs appeared and allowed the microbial mats to expand into shallow water marine environments. The Great Oxygenation Event of the early Proterozoic significantly altered the marine chemistry, which likely caused a widespread anaerobe [[Extinction event|extinction event]] but also led to the [[Evolution|evolution]] of [[Eukaryote|eukaryotes]] through [[Symbiogenesis|symbiogenesis]] between surviving anaerobes and aerobes.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Marine pollution",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Pollution of oceans from substances discarded by humans",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Marine pollution** occurs when substances used or spread by humans, such as industrial, agricultural, and residential [[Waste|waste]]; particles; [[Noise|noise]] or light; excess carbon dioxide; or invasive organisms enter the [[Ocean|ocean]] and cause harmful effects there. The majority of this waste (80%) comes from land-based activity, although marine transportation significantly contributes as well. It is a combination of chemicals and trash, most of which comes from land sources and is washed or blown into the ocean. This pollution results in damage to the [[Natural environment|environment]], to the health of all organisms, and to economic structures worldwide.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Since most inputs come from land, via rivers, [[Sewage|sewage]], or the [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]], it means that [[Continental shelf|continental shelves]] are more vulnerable to pollution. [[Air pollution]] is also a contributing factor, as it carries iron, carbonic acid, [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]], silicon, sulfur, pesticides, and dust particles into the ocean. The pollution often comes from nonpoint sources such as agricultural [[Surface runoff|runoff]], wind-blown debris, and dust. These nonpoint sources are largely due to runoff that enters the ocean through rivers, but wind-blown debris and dust can also play a role, as these pollutants can settle into waterways and oceans. Pathways of pollution include direct discharge, land runoff, ship pollution, bilge pollution, [[Dredging|dredging]] (which can create dredge plumes), atmospheric pollution and, potentially, deep sea mining.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Diabetes" },
	},
	{
		title = "Maritime Southeast Asia",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Cultural and economic area within Southeast Asia",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Maritime Southeast Asia** comprises the [[Southeast Asia|Southeast Asian]] countries of [[Brunei]], [[Indonesia]], [[Malaysia]], the [[Philippines]], [[Singapore]], and [[Timor-Leste]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The terms **Island Southeast Asia** and **Insular Southeast Asia** are sometimes given the same meaning as Maritime Southeast Asia. Other definitions restrict Island Southeast Asia to just the islands between [[Mainland Southeast Asia]] and the continental shelf of [[Australia]] and [[New Guinea]]. There is some variability as to whether [[Taiwan]] is included in this. Peter Bellwood includes Taiwan in his definition, as did Robert Blust, whilst there are examples that do not. The 16th-century term "[[East Indies]]" and the later 19th-century term "[[Malay Archipelago]]" are also used to refer to Maritime Southeast Asia. Arab merchants and cartographers referred to Maritime Southeast Asia as Al-Jaza\'ir Al-Jawi or Java archipelago. People originating from this region were commonly identified by the name \'Al-Jawi\'.',
