	{
		title = "Material",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Substance or mixture of substances that constitutes an object",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **material** is a [[Matter|substance]] or [[Mixture|mixture of substances]] that constitutes an [[Physical object|object]]. Materials can be pure or impure, living or non-living matter. Materials can be classified on the basis of their [[Physical property|physical]] and [[Chemical property|chemical properties]], or on their geological origin or biological function. [[Materials science]] is the study of materials, their properties, and their applications.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'[[Raw material|Raw materials]] can be processed in different ways to influence their properties, by purification, shaping or the introduction of other materials. New materials can be produced from raw materials by [[Chemical synthesis|synthesis]]. In industry, materials are inputs to manufacturing processes to produce productS or more complex materials, and the nature and quantity of materials used may form part of the calculation for the cost of a product or delivery under contract, Such as where contract costS are calculated on a "time and materials" basis.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Material culture",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physical aspects of culture",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Material culture** is [[Culture|culture]] manifested by the [[Artifact (archaeology)|physical objects]] and [[Architecture|architecture]] of a society. The term is primarily used in [[Archaeology|archaeology]] and [[Anthropology|anthropology]], but is also of interest to [[Sociology|sociology]], [[Geography|geography]] and [[History|history]]. The field considers artifacts in relation to their specific cultural and historic contexts, communities and belief systems. It includes the usage, consumption, creation and trade of objects as well as the behaviors, norms and [[Ritual|rituals]] that the objects create or take part in.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Material culture is contrasted with symbolic culture or non-material culture, which include non-material symbols, beliefs and social constructs. However, some scholars include in material culture other intangible phenomena like sound, smell and events, while some even consider it to include language and media. Material culture can be described as any object that humans use to survive, define social relationships, represent facets of identity, or benefit peoples' state of mind, social, or economic standing. The scholarly analysis of material culture, which can include both human made and natural or altered objects, is called **material culture studies**. It is an interdisciplinary field and methodology that tells of the relationships between people and their things: the making, history, preservation and interpretation of objects.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Materialism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Philosophical view that matter is the fundamental substance of nature",
		tags = { "Metaphysics", "Approaches" },
		summary = "In [[Philosophy|philosophy]] and [[Metaphysics|metaphysics]], **materialism** is a form of [[Monism|monism]] holding that [[Matter|matter]] is the fundamental [[Substance theory|substance]] of [[Nature|nature]], so that all things, including [[Mind|mind]] and [[Consciousness|consciousness]], arise from material interactions and depend on physical processes, including those of the [[Human brain|human brain]] and [[Nervous system|nervous system]]. It contrasts with monistic [[Idealism|idealism]], which treats consciousness as fundamental, and is related to [[Naturalism (philosophy)|naturalism]], the view that only [[Scientific law|natural laws]] and forces operate in the [[Universe|universe]], and to [[Physicalism|physicalism]], the view that all that exists is ultimately physical.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Physicalism extends materialism by including forms of physicality beyond ordinary matter (e.g. [[Spacetime|spacetime]], energy, forces, exotic matter), and some use the terms interchangeably. Alternative or opposing views to materialism or physicalism include idealism, pluralism, [[Mind–body dualism|dualism]], solipsism, panpsychism, and other forms of [[Monism|monism]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mathematical model",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Description of a system using mathematical concepts and language",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **mathematical model** is an abstract description of a concrete [[System|system]] using mathematical concepts and language. The process of developing a mathematical [[Model|model]] is termed **mathematical modeling**. Mathematical models are used in many fields, including [[Applied mathematics|applied mathematics]], [[Natural science|natural sciences]], [[Social science|social sciences]] and [[Engineering|engineering]]. In particular, the field of [[Operations research|operations research]] studies the use of mathematical modelling and related tools to solve problems in business or military operations. A model may help to characterize a system by studying the effects of different components, which may be used to make predictions about behavior or solve specific problems.",
	},
