	{
		title = "Meaning (philosophy)",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🤔",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sense or significance of an entity",
		tags = { "Philosophy", "Other branches of philosophy" },
		aliases = { "Meaning" },
		summary = "**Meaning** is the sense or significance of something, such as an expression, representation, or event. It concerns what an entity conveys, stands for, refers to, or makes intelligible. Meaning is often understood as a relational concept that connects an entity to something else, such as the connection between the word dog and a four-legged mammal.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Philosophy|Philosophical]] inquiry usually centers on linguistic meaning, studying how words, [[Sentence (linguistics)|sentences]], and utterances acquire content and convey [[Information|information]]. It examines how expressions describe the world and evoke emotions while distinguishing between speaker meaning and conventional meaning, and between literal and figurative meaning. Central debates address the contrast between sense and reference; the roles of vagueness, [[Ambiguity|ambiguity]], and context-dependence; and how the meaning of a complex expression depends on the meanings of its parts. Philosophers also discuss non-linguistic meaning associated with gestures, pictures, [[Action (philosophy)|actions]], and natural signs, such as footprints. Another topic concerns the [[Meaning of life|meaning of life]], asking whether human life or existence as a whole has a higher purpose. Theories of meaning seek to identify its nature, sources, and functions, typically focusing on linguistic meaning.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Meaning of life",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Philosophical and spiritual question",
		tags = { "Metaphysics", "Concepts" },
		summary = 'The **meaning of life** is the concept of an individual\'s [[Life|life]], human life, or [[Existence|existence]] in general having an inherent significance or a [[Meaning (philosophy)|philosophical point]]. There is no consensus on the specifics of such a concept, or whether the concept itself even exists in any [[Subjectivity and objectivity (philosophy)|objective]] sense. Thinking and discourse on the topic is sought in the English language through [[Question|questions]] such as—but not limited to—"What is the meaning of life?", "What is the purpose of existence?", and "Why are we here?". There have been many proposed answers to these questions from many different cultural and ideological backgrounds.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The search for life's meaning has produced much [[Philosophy|philosophical]], scientific, theological, and [[Metaphysics|metaphysical]] speculation throughout history. Different people and cultures believe in different answers to this question. Opinions vary on the usefulness of using [[Time|time]] and resources in the pursuit of an answer. Excessive pondering can be indicative of, or lead to, an existential crisis. The meaning of life can be derived from philosophical and religious contemplation of, and scientific inquiries about, [[Existence|existence]], social ties, [[Consciousness|consciousness]], and [[Happiness|happiness]]. Many other issues are also involved, such as symbolic meaning, [[Ontology|ontology]], value, [[Teleology|purpose]], [[Ethics|ethics]], [[Good and evil|good and evil]], [[Free will|free will]], the existence of one or multiple gods, conceptions of God, the soul, and the afterlife.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Causality (physics)" },
	},
