		summary = "**Means of communication** or **media** are ways used by people to [[Communication|communicate]] and exchange [[Information|information]] with each other as an information sender and a receiver. Diverse arrays of media that reach a large audience via mass communication are called [[Mass media|mass media]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Mechanical advantage",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏋️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Measure of the force amplification achieved by using a tool",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Mechanical advantage** is a measure of the [[Force|force]] amplification achieved by using a tool, mechanical device or machine system. The device trades off input forces against movement to obtain a desired amplification in the output force. The model for this is the law of the [[Lever|lever]]. Machine components designed to manage forces and movement in this way are called mechanisms. An ideal mechanism transmits power without adding to or subtracting from it. This means the ideal machine does not include a power source, is frictionless, and is constructed from rigid bodies that do not deflect or wear.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The performance of a real system relative to this ideal is expressed in terms of efficiency factors that take into account departures from the ideal.",
			},
		},
		related = { "CRC Press" },
	},
	{
		title = "Mechanical energy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sum of potential and kinetic energy",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In physical science, **mechanical energy** is the sum of macroscopic [[Potential energy|potential]] and [[Kinetic energy|kinetic]] energies. The principle of conservation of mechanical energy states that if an isolated [[Physical system|system]] or a closed system is subject only to conservative forces, then the mechanical energy is constant. If an object moves in the opposite direction of a conservative net force, the potential energy will increase; and if the [[Speed|speed]] (not the [[Velocity|velocity]]) of the object changes, the kinetic energy of the object also changes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In all real systems, however, nonconservative forces, such as frictional forces, will be present, but if they are of negligible [[Magnitude (mathematics)|magnitude]], the mechanical energy changes little and its conservation is a useful approximation. In elastic collisions, the kinetic energy is conserved, but in inelastic collisions some mechanical energy may be converted into [[Thermal energy|thermal energy]]. The equivalence between lost mechanical energy and an increase in [[Temperature|temperature]] was discovered by [[James Prescott Joule]]. Many devices are used to convert mechanical energy to or from other forms of [[Energy|energy]], e.g. an [[Electric motor|electric motor]] converts [[Electrical energy|electrical energy]] to mechanical energy, an [[Electric generator|electric generator]] converts mechanical energy into [[Electrical energy|electrical energy]] and a [[Heat engine|heat engine]] converts [[Heat|heat]] to mechanical energy.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mechanical equilibrium",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏋️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "When the net force on a particle is zero",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Classical mechanics|classical mechanics]], a [[Particle|particle]] is in **mechanical equilibrium** if the net force on that particle is zero. By extension, a [[Physical system|physical system]] made up of many parts is in mechanical equilibrium if the net force on each of its individual parts is zero.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In addition to defining mechanical equilibrium in terms of force, there are many alternative definitions for mechanical equilibrium which are all mathematically equivalent. In terms of momentum, a system is in equilibrium if the momentum of its parts is all constant. In terms of velocity, the system is in equilibrium if velocity is constant. In a rotational mechanical equilibrium the angular momentum of the object is conserved and the net [[Torque|torque]] is zero. More generally in conservative systems, equilibrium is established at a point in configuration space where the gradient of the [[Potential energy|potential energy]] with respect to the generalized coordinates is zero. If a particle in equilibrium has zero velocity, that particle is in **static equilibrium**.",
			},
		},
	},
