	{
		title = "Minority group",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sociological/demographic category",
		tags = { "Society", "Social status" },
		summary = 'The term "**minority group**" has different meanings, depending on the context. According to common usage, it can be defined simply as a group in society with the lowest number of individuals, or less than half of a population. Usually a minority group is disempowered relative to the majority, and that characteristic lends itself to different applications of the term minority.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In terms of sociology, economics, and politics, a demographic that takes up the smallest fraction of the population is not necessarily labelled the "minority" if it wields dominant power. In the academic context, the terms "minority" and "majority" are used in terms of hierarchical power structures. For example, in South Africa, during Apartheid, white Europeans held virtually all social, economic, and political power over black Africans. For this reason, black Africans are the "minority group", despite the fact that they outnumber white Europeans in South Africa. This is why academics more frequently use the term "minority group" to refer to a category of people who experience relative disadvantage, as compared to members of a dominant social group.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Social stratification", "Feminism" },
	},
	{
		title = "Miscibility",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ability of two substances to form a homogeneous solution when mixed",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '**Miscibility** is the property of two [[Substance (chemistry)|substances]] to mix in all proportions (that is, to fully dissolve in each other at any [[Concentration|concentration]]), forming a homogeneous [[Mixture|mixture]] (a [[Solution (chemistry)|solution]]). Such substances are said to be **miscible** (etymologically equivalent to the common term "mixable"). The term is most often applied to [[Liquid|liquids]], but also applies to [[Solid|solids]] and [[Gas|gases]]. An example in liquids is the miscibility of [[Water|water]] and ethanol as they mix in all proportions.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"By contrast, substances are said to be **immiscible** if the mixture does not form a solution for certain proportions. For one example, [[Oil|oil]] is not soluble in water, so these two solvents are immiscible. As another example, butanone (methyl ethyl ketone) is immiscible in water: it is soluble in water up to about 275 grams per liter, but will separate into two [[Phase (matter)|phases]] beyond that.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mitochondrial DNA",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "DNA located in mitochondria",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "MDNA", "MtDNA" },
		summary = "**Mitochondrial DNA** (**mDNA** or **mtDNA**) is the [[DNA]] located in the [[Mitochondrion|mitochondria]] [[Organelle|organelles]] in a [[Eukaryote|eukaryotic]] cell that converts chemical energy from organic compounds into [[Adenosine triphosphate|adenosine triphosphate]] (ATP). Mitochondrial DNA is a small portion of the DNA contained in a eukaryotic cell; most of the DNA is in the [[Cell nucleus|cell nucleus]], and, in plants and algae, the DNA also is found in [[Plastid|plastids]], such as [[Chloroplast|chloroplasts]]. Mitochondrial DNA is responsible for coding of 13 essential subunits of the complex oxidative phosphorylation (OXPHOS) system which has a role in cellular energy conversion.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Human mitochondrial DNA was the first significant part of the [[Human genome|human genome]] to be sequenced. This sequencing revealed that human mtDNA has 16,569 base pairs and encodes 13 [[Protein|proteins]]. As in other vertebrates, the human mitochondrial [[Genetic code|genetic code]] differs slightly from nuclear DNA. Since animal mtDNA evolves faster than nuclear genetic markers, it represents a mainstay of [[Phylogenetics|phylogenetics]] and [[Evolutionary biology|evolutionary biology]]. It also permits tracing the relationships of populations, and so has become important in [[Anthropology|anthropology]] and [[Biogeography|biogeography]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Model",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Informative representation of an entity",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **model** is an informative representation of an object, person, or system. The term originally denoted the plans of a building in 16th-century English, and derived via [[French language|French]] and [[Italian language|Italian]] ultimately from [[Latin]] modulus, a measure.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Models can be divided into physical models (e.g. a ship model) and abstract models (e.g. a set of mathematical equations describing the workings of the atmosphere for the purpose of weather forecasting). Abstract or conceptual models are central to [[Philosophy of science|philosophy of science]]. In scholarly research and [[Applied science|applied science]], a model should not be confused with a [[Theory|theory]]: while a model seeks only to represent reality with the purpose of better understanding or predicting the world, a theory is more ambitious in that it claims to be an explanation of reality.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Modern English",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Stage of the English language from the contemporary period",
		tags = { "Indo-European languages", "Germanic languages" },
		aliases = { "New English", "NE", "Present-day English" },
		summary = "**Modern English**, sometimes called **New English** (**NE**) or **present-day English** (**PDE**) as opposed to [[Middle English|Middle]] and [[Old English]], is the form of the [[English language]] that has been spoken since the Great Vowel Shift in [[England]], which began in the late 14th century and was completed by the 17th century.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"With some differences in vocabulary, texts that date from the early 17th century, such as the works of [[William Shakespeare]] and the King James Bible, are considered Modern English, or more specifically, [[Early Modern English]] or Elizabethan English. Through colonisation, the [[British Empire]] spread English to many regions of the world, such as Anglo-America, the [[Indian subcontinent]], [[Africa]], [[Australia]] and [[New Zealand]]. Modern English has many dialects spoken in many countries throughout the world, sometimes collectively referred to as the [[English-speaking world]]. These dialects include [[American English|American]], [[Australian English|Australian]], [[British English|British]] (containing Anglo-English, Scottish English and Welsh English), [[Canadian English|Canadian]], [[New Zealand English|New Zealand]], Caribbean, Hiberno-English (including Ulster English), [[Indian English|Indian]], Sri Lankan, Pakistani, Nigerian, Philippine, Singaporean, and [[South African English]].",
