	{
		title = "Modern Standard Arabic",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Standardized literary variety of Arabic",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "MSA", "Modern Written Arabic", "MWA" },
		summary = "**Modern Standard Arabic** (**MSA**) or **Modern Written Arabic** (**MWA**) is the variety of [[Standard language|standardized]], [[Literary language|literary]] [[Arabic]] that developed in the [[Arab world]] in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, and in some usages also the variety of spoken Arabic that approximates this written standard. MSA is the language used in [[Literature|literature]], academia, print, [[Mass media|mass media]], and [[Law|law]] and [[Legislation|legislation]], though it is generally not spoken as a [[First language|first language]], similar to Contemporary Latin.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'It is a pluricentric standard language taught throughout the Arab world in formal education, differing significantly from many [[Vernacular|vernacular]] [[Varieties of Arabic|varieties of Arabic]] that are commonly spoken as mother tongues in the area; these are only partially mutually intelligible with both MSA and with each other depending on their proximity in the [[Dialect continuum|Arabic dialect continuum]]. Native speakers of Arabic generally do not distinguish between "Modern Standard Arabic" and "Classical Arabic" as separate languages; they refer to both as **Fuṣḥā Arabic** or **al-ʻArabīyah al-Fuṣḥā**, meaning "the most eloquent Arabic". They consider the two forms to be two historical periods of one language. When the distinction is made, they do refer to MSA as **Fuṣḥā al-ʻAṣr**, meaning "Contemporary Fuṣḥā" or "Modern Fuṣḥā", and to CA as **Fuṣḥā at-Turāth**, meaning "Hereditary Fuṣḥā" or "Historical Fuṣḥā".',
			},
