	{
		title = "Nation",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Community based on common ethnic, cultural or political identity",
		tags = { "Politics and government", "Politics" },
		summary = "A **nation** is a type of [[Social organization|social organization]] where a collective [[Identity (social science)|identity]]—a national identity—has emerged from a combination of shared features across a given population, such as [[Language|language]], [[History|history]], [[Ethnicity|ethnicity]], [[Culture|culture]], [[Territory|territory]], or [[Society|society]]. Some nations are constructed around ethnicity (see ethnic nationalism) while others are bound by political [[Constitution|constitutions]] (see civic nationalism).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A nation is generally more overtly political than an [[Ethnicity|ethnic group]]. Benedict Anderson defined a nation as "an imagined political community […] imagined because the members of even the smallest nation will never know most of their fellow-members, meet them, or even hear of them, yet in the minds of each lives the image of their communion", while Anthony D. Smith defined nations as cultural-political communities that have become conscious of their [[Autonomy|autonomy]], unity and particular interests. Black\'s Law Dictionary also defines nation as a community of people inhabiting a defined territory and organized under an independent government. Thus, nation can be synonymous with [[State (polity)|state]] or [[Country|country]]. Indeed, according to Thomas Hylland Eriksen, what distinguishes nations from other forms of collective identity, like ethnicity, is this very relationship with the state.',
			},
		},
	},
