	{
		title = "News",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📰",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Information about current events",
		tags = { "Mass media" },
		summary = '**News** is [[Information|information]] about current events. This may be provided through many different [[Means of communication|media]]: word of mouth, [[Printing|printing]], [[Mail|postal systems]], [[Broadcasting|broadcasting]], [[Telecommunications|electronic communication]], or through the [[Testimony|testimony]] of [[Witness|observers and witnesses]] to events. News is sometimes called "**hard news**" to differentiate it from soft media.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Subject matters for news reports include war, [[Government|government]], [[Politics|politics]], [[Education|education]], [[Health|health]], [[Economy|economy]], [[Business|business]], [[Fashion|fashion]], [[Sport|sport]], [[Entertainment|entertainment]], and the [[Climate change|environment]], as well as quirky or unusual events. Government proclamations, concerning [[Monarchy|royal ceremonies]], [[Law|laws]], [[Tax|taxes]], [[Public health|public health]], and criminals, have been dubbed news since ancient times. [[Technology|Technological]] and [[Social change|social developments]], often driven by government communication and espionage networks, have increased the speed with which news can spread, as well as influenced its content. Throughout history, people have transported new information through oral means. Having developed in China over centuries, [[Newspaper|newspapers]] became established in Europe during the [[Early modern period|early modern period]]. In the 20th century, [[Radio|radio]] and [[Television|television]] became important means of transmitting news. While in the 21st century, the [[Internet|internet]] has also begun to play a similar role.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "News media",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎆",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Elements of mass media that focus on delivering news",
		tags = { "Mass media" },
		aliases = { "News industry" },
		summary = "The **news media** or **news industry** are forms of [[Mass media|mass media]] that focus on delivering [[News|news]] to the general public. These sources include news agencies, [[Newspaper|newspapers]], news magazines, news channels, etc.",
		related = { "The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
	},
	{
		title = "Newspaper",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "📰",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Scheduled publication of information about current events",
		tags = { "Mass media", "Newspapers" },
		summary = "A **newspaper** is a periodical publication containing written [[News|information about current events]] and is often typed in black ink with a white or gray background. Newspapers can cover a wide variety of fields such as [[Politics|politics]], [[Business|business]], [[Sport|sports]], [[Art|art]], [[Science|science]], and religions. They often include materials such as opinion columns, weather forecasts, reviews of local services, obituaries, birth notices, [[Crossword|crosswords]], [[Sudoku|sudoku puzzles]], editorial cartoons, [[Comic strip|comic strips]], and advice columns.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most newspapers are businesses, and they pay their expenses with a mixture of subscription revenue, newsstand sales, and [[Advertising|advertising]] revenue. The [[Journalism|journalism]] organizations that publish newspapers are themselves often [[Metonymy|metonymically]] called newspapers. Newspapers have traditionally been published [[Printing|in print]] (usually on cheap, low-grade [[Paper|paper]] called newsprint). However, today most newspapers are also published on [[Website|websites]] as online newspapers, and some have even abandoned their print versions entirely. Newspapers developed in the 17th century as information sheets for merchants. By the early 19th century, many cities in Europe, as well as North and South America, published newspapers. Some newspapers with high editorial independence, high journalism quality, and large circulation are viewed as newspapers of record.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Le Monde", "The Wall Street Journal" },
	},
	{
		title = "Niger–Congo languages",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Large language family of Sub-Saharan Africa",
		tags = { "Language", "Language families" },
		aliases = { "Niger–Congo" },
		summary = "**Niger–Congo** is a proposed [[Language family|family]] of [[Languages of Africa|African languages]] spoken over the majority of [[Sub-Saharan Africa|sub-Saharan Africa]]. It unites the Mande languages, the Atlantic–Congo languages (which share a characteristic noun class system), and possibly several smaller groups of languages that are difficult to classify. If valid, Niger–Congo would be the world's largest language family in terms of member languages, the third-largest in terms of speakers, and [[Languages of Africa|Africa's largest]] in terms of geographical area. The number of named Niger–Congo languages listed by [[Ethnologue]] is 1,540.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The proposed family would be the third-largest in the world by number of native speakers, with around 600 million people as of 2025. Within Niger–Congo, the [[Bantu languages]] alone account for 350 million people (2015), or half the total Niger–Congo speaking population. The most widely spoken Niger–Congo languages by number of native speakers include [[Yoruba language|Yoruba]], [[Fula language|Fula]], [[Igbo language|Igbo]], Lingala, Mooré, Ewe, Fon, [[Zulu language|Zulu]], Shona, [[Xhosa language|Xhosa]], Twi and Fante, Sesotho, [[Swahili]], Edo, Gã and Dangme; the most widely spoken by the total number of speakers is Swahili, which is used as a [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] in parts of eastern and [[Southeast Africa|southeastern Africa]]. While the ultimate genetic unity of the core of Niger–Congo (called Atlantic–Congo) is widely accepted, the internal cladistic structure is not well established. Other primary branches may include Mande, Dogon, Ijaw, Katla and Rashad.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Algic languages" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Region", "[[Africa]]" },
			{ "Family", "Proposed language family" },
			{ "Speakers", "600 million" },
			{ "Date", "2025" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Night sky",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌙",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Appearance of the sky in a clear night",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **night sky** is the [[Night|nighttime]] appearance of celestial objects like [[Star|stars]], [[Planet|planets]], and the [[Moon]], which are visible in a clear [[Sky|sky]] between sunset and sunrise, when the [[Sun]] is below the [[Horizon|horizon]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Natural light sources in a night sky include moonlight, starlight, and airglow, depending on location and timing. [[Aurora|Aurorae]] light up the skies above the polar circles. Occasionally, a large coronal mass ejection from the Sun or simply high levels of [[Solar wind|solar wind]] may extend the phenomenon toward the [[Equator]]. The night sky and studies of it have a historical place in both ancient and modern cultures. In the past, for instance, farmers have used the status of the night sky as a [[Calendar|calendar]] to determine when to plant crops. Many cultures have drawn [[Constellation|constellations]] between stars in the sky, using them in association with [[Mythology|legends]] and [[Mythology|mythology]] about their deities. The history of astrology has generally been based on the belief that relationships between heavenly bodies influence or explain events on Earth.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Lomonosov Gold Medal" },
	},
	{
		title = "Nightclub",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Club for drinking, dancing and other entertainment during the night",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Dance club" },
		summary = "A **nightclub** or **dance club** is a club that is open at [[Night|night]], usually for [[Drinking|drinking]], dancing and other [[Entertainment|entertainment]]. Nightclubs often have a [[Bar (establishment)|bar]] and **discotheque** (usually simply known as **[[Disco|disco]]**) with a dance floor, laser lighting displays, and a stage for live music or a disc jockey (DJ) who mixes recorded music. Nightclubs tend to be smaller than live music venues like [[Theatre|theatres]] and [[Stadium|stadiums]], with few or no seats for customers.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Nightclubs generally restrict access to people in terms of age, attire, and behaviors. Nightclubs typically have dress codes to prohibit people wearing informal, indecent, offensive, gym, or gang-related attire from entering. Unlike other entertainment venues, nightclubs are more likely to use bouncers to screen prospective patrons for entry. The busiest nights for a nightclub are Friday and Saturday nights. Most nightclubs cater to a particular music genre or sound for branding effects.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Nihilism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Rejection of certain ideas about reality",
		tags = { "Ethics", "Approaches" },
		summary = "**Nihilism** is a family of [[Philosophy|philosophical]] views that reject the existence of any objectively meaningful [[Meaning of life|purpose]], [[Morality|moral value]], [[Truth|truth]], [[Knowledge|knowledge]], or related concepts. Nihilistic views span several branches of philosophy, including [[Ethics|ethics]], value theory, [[Epistemology|epistemology]], and [[Metaphysics|metaphysics]]. Nihilism is also described as a broad cultural phenomenon or historical movement that pervades [[Modernity|modernity]] in the [[Western world]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Existential nihilism asserts that [[Life|life]] has no objective meaning or purpose. The idea that all individual and societal values are ultimately pointless has been associated with various responses. They range from general indifference and existential crises to transformative reinterpretations of established ideals and a creative embrace of personal meaning-making. Moral nihilism, a related view, denies the [[Subjectivity and objectivity (philosophy)|objective]] existence of morality, arguing that moral evaluations and practices rest on misguided assumptions without any foundation in external [[Reality|reality]]. In epistemology or the theory of knowledge, nihilism challenges knowledge and truth. According to [[Relativism|relativism]], knowledge, truth, or [[Meaning (philosophy)|meaning]] are relative to the perspectives of specific individuals or cultural contexts. This implies there is no independent framework to assess which opinion is ultimately correct.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Nikita Khrushchev",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Leader of the Soviet Union from 1953 to 1964",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "Born in a village near Kursk, Khrushchev was employed as a metal worker during his youth and was a political commissar in the Russian Civil War. Under the sponsorship of Lazar Kaganovich, he rose through the ranks of the Soviet hierarchy. He was also present at the Battle of Stalingrad, a fact in which he took great pride.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"His domestic policies, aimed at bettering the lives of ordinary citizens, were often ineffective, especially in agriculture. Hoping eventually to rely on missiles for national defense, Khrushchev ordered major cuts in conventional forces. Despite such cuts, Khrushchev's time in office saw the tensest years of the Cold War, culminating in the 1962 Cuban Missile Crisis. As leader of the Soviet Union, Khrushchev enjoyed considerable popularity during the late 1950s due to the successful launching of [[Sputnik 1|Sputnik]] in 1957 as well as favorable outcomes in the 1956 Suez Crisis, 1957 Syrian Crisis, and the 1960 U-2 incident. However, by the early 1960s, Khrushchev's hold on power had been significantly weakened by his domestic policy failures and handling of the Cuban Missile Crisis.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Birthplace", "Kalinovka, Dmitriyevsky Uyezd, Russia" },
			{ "Died", "11 September 1971" },
			{ "Education", "Industrial Academy" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Nilo-Saharan languages",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Proposed family of Native African languages",
		tags = { "Language", "Language families" },
		summary = "The **Nilo-Saharan languages** are a proposed linguistic family consisting of around 210 Native African languages spoken by somewhere around 70 million speakers, mainly in the upper parts of the Chari and [[Nile]] rivers, including historic [[Nubia]], north of where the two tributaries of the Nile meet. The languages extend through 17 nations in the northern half of Africa: from [[Algeria]] to [[Benin]] in the west; from [[Libya]] to the [[Democratic Republic of the Congo]] in the centre; and from [[Egypt]] to [[Tanzania]] in the east.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As indicated by its hyphenated name, Nilo-Saharan is a family of the African interior, including the greater Nile Basin and the Central [[Sahara|Sahara Desert]]. Most of its proposed constituent divisions are found in the modern countries of [[Sudan]] and [[South Sudan]], through which the [[Nile|Nile River]] flows. In his book The Languages of Africa (1963), Joseph Greenberg named the group and argued it was a genetic family. It contained all the languages that were not included in the [[Niger–Congo languages|Niger–Congo]], [[Afroasiatic languages|Afroasiatic]] or [[Khoisan languages|Khoisan]] families. Although some linguists have referred to the phylum as \"Greenberg's wastebasket\", into which he placed all the otherwise unaffiliated non-click languages of Africa, other specialists in the field have accepted it as a working hypothesis since Greenberg's classification. The language speakers mentioned in the classifications may have migrated into different regions of Africa.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Speakers", "c. 70 million for all branches listed below." },
			{ "Region", "[[Central Africa]], north-central Africa and [[East Africa]]" },
			{ "Family", "Proposed language family" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Nitrogen dioxide",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌬️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Compound containing nitrogen and oxygen",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Nitrogen dioxide** is a [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]] with the formula NO2. One of several nitrogen oxides, nitrogen dioxide is a reddish-brown gas. It is a paramagnetic, bent molecule with C2v point group symmetry. Industrially, NO2 is an intermediate in the synthesis of [[Nitric acid|nitric acid]], millions of tons of which are produced each year, primarily for the production of [[Fertilizer|fertilizers]]. It is a free radical.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Cooking with a gas stove produces nitrogen dioxide which causes poorer indoor air quality. It is also included in the NOx family of [[Air pollution|atmospheric pollutants]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Nitrogen fixation",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌬️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Conversion of dinitrogen into ammonia",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Nitrogen fixation** is a chemical process by which the abundant but relatively inert molecular dinitrogen (N2) is converted into bioavailable nitrogen compounds such as [[Ammonia|ammonia]] (NH3) and [[Nitrate|nitrates]] (NO3). It occurs both biologically and abiologically, the latter either naturally through weather phenomena (e.g. [[Lightning|lightning]]) or artificially by [[Chemical industry|chemical industries]]. Biological nitrogen fixation or diazotrophy is catalyzed by [[Enzyme|enzymes]] called nitrogenases, produced by [[Microorganism|microorganisms]] such as [[Cyanobacteria|cyanobacteria]] and rhizobia. These enzyme complexes are encoded by the Nif genes (or Nif homologs) and contain [[Iron|iron]], often with a second metal (usually [[Molybdenum|molybdenum]], but sometimes [[Vanadium|vanadium]]).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some nitrogen-fixing bacteria have symbiotic relationships with [[Plant|plants]], especially [[Legume|legumes]], [[Moss|mosses]], and aquatic ferns such as Azolla. Looser non-symbiotic relationships between diazotrophs and plants are often referred to as associative, as seen in nitrogen fixation on [[Rice|rice]] roots. Nitrogen fixation occurs between some [[Termite|termites]] and [[Mushroom|fungi]]. It occurs naturally in the air by means of NOx production by [[Lightning|lightning]]. Nitrogen fixation is a key component of the nitrogen cycle and is essential to [[Life|life]] on [[Earth]], as all vital [[Organic compound|organic compounds]] such as [[DNA]] and [[Protein|proteins]] contain nitrogen. Industrial nitrogen fixation, most notably through the [[Haber process]], underpins the manufacture of all nitrogenous industrial products, which include [[Fertilizer|fertilizers]], pharmaceuticals, [[Textile|textiles]], [[Dye|dyes]] and explosives.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Nobel Foundation",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
