	{
		title = "Noun",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Part of speech that names an object or set of objects",
		tags = { "Basics", "Grammar" },
		summary = "In [[Grammar|grammar]], a **noun** is a word that denotes a concrete or abstract entity, like living creatures, places, actions, events, qualities, states of existence, and ideas. To summarize this, nouns signify an object or idea. A noun may serve as an object or subject within a [[Phrase|phrase]], [[Clause|clause]], or [[Sentence (linguistics)|sentence]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In [[Linguistics|linguistics]], nouns constitute a lexical category (part of speech) defined according to how its members combine with members of other lexical categories. The [[Syntax|syntactic]] occurrence of nouns differs among languages. In English, prototypical nouns are common nouns or proper nouns that can occur with determiners, articles and [[Adjective|attributive adjectives]], and can function as the head of a noun phrase. According to traditional and popular classification, [[Pronoun|pronouns]] are distinct from nouns, but in much modern theory they are considered a subclass of nouns. Every language has various linguistic and grammatical distinctions between nouns and [[Verb|verbs]].",
			},
		},
