	{
		title = "Nutrient",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Substance that an organism uses to live",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **nutrient** is a [[Substance (chemistry)|substance]] used by an organism to survive, grow and reproduce. The requirement for dietary nutrient intake applies to [[Animal|animals]], [[Plant|plants]], [[Mushroom|fungi]] and [[Protist|protists]]. Nutrients can be incorporated into cells for [[Metabolism|metabolic purposes]] or excreted by cells to create non-cellular structures such as [[Hair|hair]], scales, [[Feather|feathers]], or [[Exoskeleton|exoskeletons]]. Some nutrients can be metabolically converted into smaller molecules in the process of releasing energy such as for [[Carbohydrate|carbohydrates]], [[Lipid|lipids]], [[Protein (nutrient)|proteins]] and [[Fermentation|fermentation]] products (ethanol or [[Vinegar|vinegar]]) leading to end-products of water and carbon dioxide. All organisms require water.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Essential nutrients for animals are the energy sources, some of the [[Amino acid|amino acids]] that are combined to create [[Protein|proteins]], a subset of [[Fatty acid|fatty acids]], vitamins and certain [[Mineral (nutrient)|minerals]]. Plants require more diverse minerals absorbed through roots, plus carbon dioxide and oxygen absorbed through leaves. [[Mushroom|Fungi]] live on dead or living organic matter and meet nutrient needs from their host. Different types of organisms have different essential nutrients. Ascorbic acid (vitamin C) is essential to humans and some animal species but most other animals and many plants are able to synthesize it. Nutrients may be [[Organic compound|organic]] or inorganic: organic compounds include most compounds containing carbon, while all other chemicals are inorganic. Inorganic nutrients include [[Iron|iron]], [[Selenium|selenium]], and [[Zinc|zinc]], while organic nutrients include, protein, fats, sugars and vitamins.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Nutrition",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🦠",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Provision to cells and organisms to support life",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Nutrition** is the [[Biochemistry|biochemical]] and [[Physiology|physiological]] process by which an [[Organism|organism]] uses [[Food|food]] and [[Water|water]] to support its [[Life|life]]. The intake of these substances provides organisms with [[Nutrient|nutrients]] (divided into macro- and micro-) which can be [[Metabolism|metabolized]] to create energy and chemical structures; too much or too little of an essential nutrient can cause malnutrition. Nutritional science, the study of nutrition as a hard science, typically emphasizes human nutrition.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The type of organism determines what nutrients it needs and how it obtains them. Organisms obtain nutrients by consuming [[Organic matter|organic matter]], consuming inorganic matter, absorbing light, or some combination of these. Some can produce nutrients internally by consuming basic elements, while others must consume other organisms to obtain pre-existing nutrients. All forms of life require [[Carbon|carbon]], energy, and [[Water|water]] as well as various other [[Molecule|molecules]]. Animals require complex nutrients such as [[Carbohydrate|carbohydrates]], [[Lipid|lipids]], and [[Protein|proteins]], obtaining them by consuming other organisms. Humans have developed [[Agriculture|agriculture]] and [[Cooking|cooking]] to replace [[Foraging|foraging]] and advance human nutrition. Plants acquire nutrients through the [[Soil|soil]] and the atmosphere. Fungi absorb nutrients around them by breaking them down and absorbing them through the [[Mycelium|mycelium]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "OLED",
