		description = "Diode that emits light from an organic compound",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Organic light-emitting diode", "Organic electroluminescent", "Organic EL" },
		summary = "An **organic light-emitting diode** (**OLED**), also known as **organic electroluminescent** (**organic EL**) **diode**, is a type of [[Light-emitting diode|light-emitting diode]] (LED) in which the emissive electroluminescent layer is an [[Organic compound|organic compound]] film that emits light in response to an electric current. This organic layer is situated between two [[Electrode|electrodes]]; typically, at least one of these electrodes is transparent.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"OLEDs are used to create digital displays in devices such as [[Television set|television]] screens, [[Computer monitor|computer monitors]], and portable systems such as [[Smartphone|smartphones]] and [[Handheld game console|handheld game consoles]]; its significant advantage over [[Liquid-crystal display|liquid-crystal displays]] (LCD) is that the individual pixels emit their own light, requiring no backlight like LED-lit LCDs. OLEDs are fundamentally different from LEDs and do not employ a crystalline structure. There are two main families of OLED: those based on small molecules and those employing [[Polymer|polymers]]. Adding mobile [[Ion|ions]] to an OLED creates a light-emitting electrochemical cell (LEC) which has a slightly different mode of operation. An OLED display matrix can have various control schemes:",
			},
