		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Great October Socialist Revolution", "October coup", "Bolshevik coup" },
		summary = "The **October Revolution**, also known as the **Great October Socialist Revolution** (in Soviet historiography), **October coup**, **Bolshevik coup**, **Bolshevik Revolution**, and occasionally the **November Revolution**, was the second of [[Russian Revolution|two revolutions]] in Russia in 1917. It was led by Vladimir Lenin's [[Bolsheviks]] as part of the broader Russian Revolution of 1917–1923. It was the precipitating event of the Russian Civil War.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The October Revolution followed and capitalised on the February Revolution earlier that year, which had led to the abdication of Nicholas II and the creation of the Russian Provisional Government. The provisional government, led by Alexander Kerensky, had taken power after Grand Duke Michael, the younger brother of Nicholas II, declined to take power. During this time, urban workers began to organize into councils (soviets) wherein revolutionaries criticized the provisional government and its actions. It declared the [[Russian Republic]] on 1 [N.S. 14] September 1917. The situation grew critical in late 1917 as the Directorate, led by the left-wing Party of Socialist-Revolutionaries (SRs), controlled the government. The far-left Bolsheviks were deeply unhappy with the government, and began spreading calls for a military uprising.",
			},
