		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Amount by which an orbit deviates from a perfect circle",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In astrodynamics, the **orbital eccentricity** of an [[Astronomical object|astronomical object]] is a dimensionless parameter that determines the amount by which its [[Orbit|orbit]] around another body deviates from a perfect [[Circle|circle]]. A value of 0 is a circular orbit, values between 0 and 1 form an elliptic orbit, 1 is a [[Parabola|parabolic]] (escape orbit or capture orbit), and greater than 1 is a [[Hyperbola|hyperbola]]. The term derives its name from the parameters of [[Conic section|conic sections]], as every Kepler orbit is a conic section. It is normally used for the isolated two-body problem, but extensions exist for objects following a rosette orbit through the Galaxy.",
		related = { "Pearson Education" },
	},
	{
		title = "Orbital resonance",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Regular and periodic mutual gravitational influence of orbiting bodies",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'In [[Celestial mechanics|celestial mechanics]], **orbital resonance** occurs when [[Orbit|orbiting]] bodies exert regular, [[Periodic function|periodic]] gravitational influence on each other, usually because their [[Orbital period|orbital periods]] are related by a ratio of small [[Integer|integers]]. Most commonly, this relationship is found between a pair of objects (binary resonance). The physical principle behind orbital resonance is similar in concept to pushing a child on a swing, whereby the orbit and the swing both have a natural frequency, and the body doing the "pushing" will act in periodic repetition to have a cumulative effect on the motion.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Orbital resonances greatly enhance the mutual gravitational influence of the bodies (i.e., their ability to alter or constrain each other's orbits). In most cases, this results in an unstable interaction, in which the bodies exchange [[Momentum|momentum]] and shift orbits until the resonance no longer exists. Under some circumstances, a resonant system can be self-correcting and thus stable. Examples are the near 28:14:7:3 resonance of [[Jupiter]]'s moons [[Io (moon)|Io]], [[Europa (moon)|Europa]], [[Ganymede (moon)|Ganymede]] and [[Callisto (moon)|Callisto]], and the 3:2 resonance between [[Neptune]] and [[Pluto]]. Unstable resonances with [[Saturn]]'s inner moons give rise to gaps in the [[Rings of Saturn|rings of Saturn]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Order (taxonomy)",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Taxonomic rank between class and family",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Order", "Order (biology)", "Suborder", "Infraorder" },
		summary = "**Order** is one of the eight major hierarchical [[Taxonomic rank|taxonomic ranks]] in Linnaean taxonomy. It is classified between [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] and [[Class (taxonomy)|class]]. Like the other ranks, orders reflect shared ancestry; for example, all [[Owl|owls]] belong to the order Strigiformes. In biological classification systems, orders and their usage are defined by nomenclature codes. An immediately higher rank, **superorder**, is sometimes added directly above order, with **suborder** directly beneath order.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"What does and does not belong to each order is determined by a taxonomist, as is whether a particular order should be recognized at all. Often there is no exact agreement, with different taxonomists each taking a different position. There are no hard rules that a taxonomist needs to follow in describing or recognizing an order. Some taxa are accepted almost universally, while others are recognized only rarely. The name of an order is usually written with a capital letter. For some groups of organisms, their orders may follow consistent naming schemes. Orders of [[Plant|plants]], [[Mushroom|fungi]], and algae use the suffix (e.g. Dictyotales).",
			},
		},
	},
