	{
		title = "Orientalism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Imitation or depiction of Eastern cultures",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'In [[Art history|art history]], [[Literature|literature]], and [[Cultural studies|cultural studies]], **Orientalism** is the imitation or depiction of aspects of the [[Eastern world]] (or "Orient") by writers, designers, and artists from the [[Western world]]. Orientalist painting, particularly of [[West Asia]] and [[North Africa]], was one of the many specialties of 19th-century [[Academic art|academic art]], and Western literature was influenced by a similar interest in Oriental themes.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"This reflected a number of factors: the increasing ease of access to Asia and [[North Africa]] by artists, the still-colourful clothing worn in Asia, in contrast to relatively drab Western clothing (for men at least), the curiousity of Western art buyers about Asian life, especially in its traditional forms, and increasing economic connections between West and East, with trade and [[Colonialism|colonialism]]. Since the publication of [[Edward Said]]'s Orientalism in 1978, much academic discourse has begun to use the term 'Orientalism' to refer to a general patronizing Western attitude towards [[Asia|Asian]] and North African societies. In Said's analysis, 'the West' essentializes these societies as static and undeveloped. This fabricates a view of Oriental culture that can be studied, depicted, and reproduced in the service of [[Imperialism|imperial power]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Origin of language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Relationship between language and human evolution",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		summary = "The **origin of language**, its relationship with [[Human evolution|human evolution]], and its consequences have been subjects of study for centuries. Scholars wishing to study the origins of language draw inferences from evidence such as the [[Human evolution|fossil record]], archaeological evidence, and contemporary language diversity. They may also study [[Language acquisition|language acquisition]] as well as comparisons between human [[Language|language]] and systems of [[Animal communication|animal communication]] (particularly those of other primates). Many argue for the close relation between the origins of language and the origins of [[Behavioral modernity|modern human behavior]], but there is little agreement about the facts and implications of this connection.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The shortage of direct, [[Empirical evidence|empirical evidence]] has caused many scholars to regard the entire topic as unsuitable for serious study; in 1866, the Linguistic Society of Paris banned any existing or future debates on the subject, a prohibition which remained influential across much of the [[Western world]] until the late twentieth century. Various hypotheses have been developed on the emergence of language. While [[Charles Darwin]]'s theory of evolution by [[Evolution|natural selection]] had provoked a surge of speculation on the origin of language over a century and a half ago, the speculations had not resulted in a scientific consensus by 1996. Despite this, academic interest had returned to the topic by the early 1990s. [[Linguistics|Linguists]], archaeologists, [[Psychologist|psychologists]], and [[Anthropologist|anthropologists]] have renewed the investigation into the origin of language with modern methods.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Oromo language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Cushitic language",
		tags = { "Specific languages", "Afroasiatic languages" },
		aliases = { "Oromo" },
		summary = "**Oromo** is an Afroasiatic language belonging to the Cushitic branch, primarily spoken by the Oromo people, native to the [[Ethiopia|Ethiopian]] state of Oromia and northern Kenya. It is used as a [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] in Oromia and northeastern Kenya. It is officially written in the [[Latin script]], although traditional scripts are also informally used.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"With more than 41.7 million speakers making up 33.8% of the total Ethiopian population, Oromo has the largest number of native speakers in Ethiopia and ranks as the second most widely spoken language in Ethiopia by total number of speakers (including second-language speakers), following [[Amharic]]. Forms of Oromo are spoken as a first language by an additional half-million people in parts of northern and eastern [[Kenya]]. It is also spoken by smaller numbers of emigrants in other African countries such as [[South Africa]], [[Libya]], [[Egypt]] and [[Sudan]]. Oromo is the most widely spoken Cushitic language and among the five [[Languages of Africa|languages of Africa]] with the largest mother-tongue populations.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Spoken in", "[[Ethiopia]], [[Kenya]], [[Somalia]]" },
			{ "Region", "Oromia" },
			{ "Date", "2019–2024" },
