	{
		title = "Pastoral",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genre relating to shepherds and the countryside",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **pastoral** genre of literature, art, or music depicts an idealised form of the shepherd's lifestyle – [[Herd|herding]] livestock around open areas of [[Landscape|land]] according to the [[Season|seasons]] and the changing availability of water and pasture. The target audience is typically an urban one. A pastoral is a work of this [[Genre|genre]]. A piece of music in the genre is usually referred to as a pastorale.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The genre is also known as **bucolic**, from the [[Greek language|Greek]], from, meaning a cowherd. Arcadia is the traditional setting for the pastoral. Originating in [[Classical antiquity|classical antiquity]], and less prominent in the [[Middle Ages]], the genre revived in the Renaissance, and continued to be very important until at least the eighteenth century. From [[Romanticism]] onwards, living in a rural and natural setting continued to be a major concern in the arts, but usually without protagonists imagined as farmworkers, the essential mark of the pastoral.",
