	{
		title = "Perception",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Interpretation of sensory information",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Concepts" },
		summary = "**Perception** is the identification, interpretation and organization of [[Sense|sensory]] information, in order to represent and understand the presented information or environment. All perception involves signals that go through the [[Nervous system|nervous system]], which in turn result from physical or chemical stimulation of the sensory system. Vision involves [[Light|light]] striking the retina of the [[Eye|eye]]; smell is mediated by odor molecules; and [[Hearing|hearing]] involves [[Sound|pressure waves]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Perception is not only the passive receipt of these [[Signal processing|signals]], but it is also shaped by the recipient's learning, [[Memory|memory]], expectation, and [[Attention|attention]]. Sensory input is a process that transforms this low-level information to higher-level information (e.g., extracts shapes for object recognition). The following process connects a person's concepts and expectations (or [[Knowledge|knowledge]]) with restorative and selective mechanisms, such as [[Attention|attention]], that influence perception. Perception depends on complex functions of the [[Nervous system|nervous system]], but subjectively seems mostly effortless because this processing happens outside [[Consciousness|conscious]] awareness. Since the rise of [[Experimental psychology|experimental psychology]] in the 19th century, psychology's understanding of perception has progressed by combining a variety of techniques.",
			},
		},
	},
