	{
		title = "Personal name",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔣",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Set of names by which an individual is known",
		tags = { "Basics", "Grammar" },
		aliases = { "Full name", "Prosoponym" },
		summary = 'A **personal name**, **full name** or **prosoponym** is the set of [[Name|names]] by which an individual person or animal is known. When taken together as a [[Phrase|phrase]], they all relate to that one individual. In many cultures, the term is synonymous with the birth name or legal name of the individual. Historically and colloquially, the term "personal name" has also been synonymous with "[[Given name|given name]]" or "first name", in exclusion of other types of names that may make up an individual\'s full name. In linguistic classification, personal names of people are studied within a specific onomastic discipline, called anthroponymy.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In [[Western culture]], nearly all individuals possess at least one [[Given name|given name]] (also known as a first name, forename, or Christian name), together with a [[Surname|surname]] (also known as a last name or family name). In the name "James Smith", for example, James is the first name and Smith is the surname. Surnames in the West generally indicate that the individual belongs to a family, a tribe, or a clan, although the exact relationships vary: they may be given at birth, taken upon adoption, changed upon marriage, and so on. Where there are two or more given names, typically only one (in English-speaking cultures usually the first) is used in normal speech.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Personal union",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Situation of two states sharing a monarch without merging",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **personal union** is a combination of two or more [[Monarchy|monarchical]] [[State (polity)|states]] that have the same [[Monarch|monarch]] while their boundaries, laws, and interests remain distinct. A real union, by contrast, involves the constituent states being to some extent interlinked, such as by sharing some limited governmental institutions. It differs from a [[Federation|federation]] in that each constituent state has an independent government, whereas a federal state is united by a central government. The ruler in a personal union does not need to be a hereditary monarch.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term was coined by German jurist Johann Stephan Pütter, introducing it into Elementa iuris publici germanici (Elements of German Public Law) of 1760. Personal unions can arise for several reasons, such as: inheritance through a dynastic union, e.g., Louis X of France inherited [[Kingdom of France|France]] from [[Philip IV of France|his father]] and [[Kingdom of Navarre|Navarre]] from his mother [[Decolonization|decolonization]], where ex-colonies install the monarch of the former colonizing power as their own upon becoming independent, e.g., several former members of the [[British Empire]] (then becoming [[Commonwealth realm|Commonwealth realms]]) [[Autonomy|autonomization]], e.g., instead of annexing Finland into the [[Russian Empire]], Alexander I of Russia organized Finland as an autonomous grand duchy and acted as its [[Head of state|head of state]]",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Personality",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Psychological characteristics of an individual",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Concepts" },
		summary = "**Personality** describes the behavioral, cognitive, and [[Emotion|emotional]] patterns that make up a person's unique time-dependent adjustment to life. Personality is relatively stable, but can change over time due to experiences and developmental processes. Although there is no consensus on the definition of personality, most theories in personality focus on traits, [[Motivation|motivation]], skills, and [[Identity (social science)|identity]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Research in [[Personality psychology|personality psychology]] generally attempts to explain the characteristics of a person that underlie differences in behavior. Personality characteristics are related to many life outcomes, such as work and relationship success, mental health, well-being, and longevity. For example, an individual can develop anger during their life, or they could fall into a state of depression. Over time, psychologists have taken many different approaches to study personality, which can be organized across dispositional, biological, intrapsychic (psychodynamic), cognitive-experiential, social and cultural, and adjustment domains. The various approaches used to study personality today reflect the influence of the first theorists in the field, a group that includes [[Sigmund Freud]], Alfred Adler, [[Gordon Allport]], Hans Eysenck, [[Abraham Maslow]], and [[Carl Rogers]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Personality psychology",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of psychology focused on personality",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Personality psychology** is a branch of [[Psychology|psychology]] that examines [[Personality|personality]] and its variation among [[Individual|individuals]]. It aims to show how people are individually different due to psychological forces. Its areas of focus include: Describing what personality is. Documenting how personalities develop. Explaining the mental processes of personality and how they affect functioning. Providing a framework for understanding individuals.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'"Personality" is a dynamic and organized set of characteristics possessed by an individual that uniquely influences their environment, [[Cognition|cognition]], [[Emotion|emotions]], [[Motivation|motivations]], and [[Behavior|behaviors]] in various situations. The word personality originates from the [[Latin]], which means "[[Mask|mask]]". Personality also pertains to the pattern of [[Thought|thoughts]], [[Feeling|feelings]], social adjustments, and behaviors persistently exhibited over time that strongly influence one\'s expectations, self-conceptions, [[Value (ethics)|values]], and [[Attitude (psychology)|attitudes]]. Environmental and situational effects on behaviour are influenced by psychological mechanisms within a person. Personality also predicts human reactions to other people and their behavior, problems, and stress.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Clinical psychology" },
	},
	{
		title = "Pest (organism)",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Organism harmful to human concerns",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Pest" },
		summary = "A **pest** is any organism harmful to humans or human concerns. The term is particularly used for creatures that damage crops, livestock, and forestry or cause a nuisance to people, especially in their homes. Humans have modified the environment for their own purposes and are intolerant of other creatures occupying the same space when their activities impact adversely on human objectives. Thus, an elephant is unobjectionable in its natural habitat but a pest when it tramples crops.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some animals are disliked because they bite or sting; [[Wolf|wolves]], [[Snake|snakes]], [[Wasp|wasps]], [[Ant|ants]], [[Bee|bees]], [[Bed bug|bed bugs]], mosquitos, fleas and ticks belong in this category. Others enter the home; these include [[Housefly|houseflies]], which land on and contaminate food; [[Beetle|beetles]], which tunnel into the woodwork; and other animals that scuttle about on the floor at night, like [[Rat|rats]], [[Mouse|mice]], and [[Cockroach|cockroaches]], which are often associated with unsanitary conditions. Agricultural and horticultural crops are attacked by a wide variety of pests, the most important being [[Rodent|rodents]], [[Insect|insects]], mites, [[Nematode|nematodes]] and gastropod molluscs. Plants have their own defences against these attacks but these may be overwhelmed, especially in habitats where the plants are already stressed, or where the pests have been accidentally introduced and may have no natural enemies.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pet",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Animal kept for companionship rather than utility",
		tags = { "Family members" },
		aliases = { "Companion animal" },
		summary = "A **pet**, or **companion animal**, is an [[Animal|animal]] kept primarily for a person's company or entertainment rather than as a [[Working animal|working animal]], [[Livestock|livestock]], or a laboratory animal. Popular pets are often considered to have attractive/cute appearances, [[Animal cognition|intelligence]], and relatable personalities, but some pets may be taken in on an altruistic basis (such as a stray animal) and accepted by the owner regardless of these characteristics.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Two of the most popular pets are [[Dog|dogs]] and [[Cat|cats]]. Other animals commonly kept include rabbits; ferrets; pigs; [[Rodent|rodents]] such as gerbils, [[Hamster|hamsters]], chinchillas, rats, mice, and [[Guinea pig|guinea pigs]]; [[Bird|birds]] such as [[Parrot|parrots]], [[Passerine|passerines]], and fowls; [[Reptile|reptiles]] such as [[Turtle|turtles]], [[Lizard|lizards]], [[Snake|snakes]], and iguanas; [[Aquarium|aquatic pets]] such as [[Fish|fish]], freshwater snails, and saltwater snails; amphibians such as [[Frog|frogs]] and salamanders; and [[Arthropod|arthropod]] pets such as [[Tarantula|tarantulas]] and hermit crabs. Smaller pets include rodents, while the equine and bovine group include the largest companion animals. Pets provide their owners, or guardians, both physical and emotional benefits. Walking a dog can provide both the human and the dog with exercise, fresh air, and social interaction. Pets can give companionship to people who are living alone or elderly adults who do not have adequate social interaction with other people.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Brother", "Sister" },
	},
