	{
		title = "Philosophical realism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Philosophical concept",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Philosophical realism**—usually not treated as a position of its own but as a stance towards other subject matters—is the view that a certain kind of thing (ranging widely from abstract objects like numbers to moral statements to the physical world itself) has mind-independent existence, i.e. that it exists even in the absence of any [[Mind|mind]] perceiving it or that its [[Existence|existence]] is not just a mere appearance in the eye of the beholder. This includes a number of positions within [[Epistemology|epistemology]] and [[Metaphysics|metaphysics]] which express that a given thing instead exists independently of [[Knowledge|knowledge]], [[Thought|thought]], or understanding.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"This can apply to items such as the physical world, the [[Past|past]] and [[Future|future]], other minds, and the self, though may also apply less directly to things such as universals, mathematical truths, moral truths, and [[Thought|thought]] itself. However, realism may also include various positions which instead reject metaphysical treatments of reality altogether. Realism can also be a view about the properties of reality in general, holding that reality exists independent of the [[Mind|mind]], as opposed to non-realist views (like some forms of [[Philosophical skepticism|skepticism]] and solipsism) which question the certainty of anything beyond one's own mind. Philosophers who profess realism often claim that [[Truth|truth]] consists in a [[Correspondence theory of truth|correspondence]] between cognitive representations and reality. Realists tend to believe that whatever we believe now is only an approximation of reality but that the accuracy and fullness of understanding can be improved.",
			},
		},
		related = { "National Academy of Sciences" },
	},
	{
		title = "Philosophical skepticism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Philosophical views that question the possibility of knowledge or certainty",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Philosophical skepticism** is a family of [[Philosophy|philosophical]] views that question the possibility of [[Knowledge|knowledge]]. It differs from other forms of [[Skepticism|skepticism]] in that it even rejects very plausible knowledge claims that belong to basic [[Common sense|common sense]]. Philosophical skeptics are often classified into two general categories: Those who deny all possibility of knowledge, and those who advocate for the suspension of judgment due to the inadequacy of evidence. This distinction is modeled after the differences between the Academic skeptics and the Pyrrhonian skeptics in [[Ancient Greek philosophy|ancient Greek philosophy]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Pyrrhonian skepticism is a practice of suspending judgment, and skepticism in this sense is understood as a way of life that helps the practitioner achieve inner peace. Some types of philosophical skepticism reject all forms of knowledge while others limit this rejection to certain fields, for example, knowledge about [[Morality|moral]] doctrines or about the external world. Some theorists criticize philosophical skepticism based on the claim that it is a self-refuting idea since its proponents seem to claim to know that there is no knowledge. Other objections focus on its implausibility and distance from regular life.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Philosophy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🤔",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of general and fundamental questions",
		tags = { "Philosophy" },
		aliases = { "Philosopher", "Philosophical" },
		summary = "**Philosophy** is a systematic study of general and fundamental questions concerning topics like [[Existence|existence]], [[Knowledge|knowledge]], [[Mind|mind]], [[Reason|reason]], [[Language|language]], and value. It is a rational and critical inquiry that reflects on its methods and assumptions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Historically, many of the individual [[Science|sciences]], such as [[Physics|physics]] and [[Psychology|psychology]], formed part of philosophy. However, they are considered separate academic disciplines in the modern sense of the term. Influential traditions in the [[History of philosophy|history of philosophy]] include [[Western philosophy|Western]], [[Islamic philosophy|Arabic–Persian]], [[Indian philosophy|Indian]], and [[Chinese philosophy]]. Western philosophy originated in [[Ancient Greece]] and covers a wide area of philosophical subfields. A central topic in Arabic–Persian philosophy is the relation between reason and revelation. Indian philosophy combines the [[Spirituality|spiritual]] problem of how to reach enlightenment with the exploration of the nature of reality and the ways of arriving at knowledge. Chinese philosophy focuses principally on practical issues about right social conduct, government, and self-cultivation. Major branches of philosophy are [[Epistemology|epistemology]], [[Ethics|ethics]], [[Logic|logic]], and [[Metaphysics|metaphysics]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
