		description = "Branch of philosophy",
		tags = { "Philosophy", "Other branches of philosophy" },
		summary = "**Philosophy of mind** is a branch of [[Philosophy|philosophy]] that deals with the nature of the [[Mind|mind]] and its relation to the body and the [[Reality|external world]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The mind–body problem is a paradigmatic issue in philosophy of mind, although a number of other issues are addressed; these include the hard problem of consciousness and the nature of particular mental states. Aspects of the mind that are studied include mental events, mental functions, mental properties, [[Consciousness|consciousness]] and its neural correlates, the ontology of the mind, the nature of [[Cognition|cognition]] and of [[Thought|thought]], and the relationship of the mind to the body. Dualism and [[Monism|monism]] are the two central schools of thought on the mind–body problem, although nuanced views have arisen that do not fit either category neatly. Dualism finds its entry into [[Western philosophy]] thanks to [[René Descartes]] in the [[17th century]].",
