		title = "Phoneme",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Basic unit of phonology",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		summary = "A **phoneme** is a set of similar speech sounds that are perceptually regarded by the speakers of a language as a single basic sound—the smallest possible [[Phonetics|phonetic]] unit—that helps distinguish one [[Word|word]] from another. All languages contain phonemes (or the spatial–gestural equivalent in [[Sign language|sign languages]]), and all spoken languages include both [[Consonant|consonant]] and [[Vowel|vowel]] phonemes. Phonemes are studied under [[Phonology|phonology]], a branch of [[Linguistics|linguistics]], a discipline encompassing the scientific study of language, writing, speech, and related matters.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"For example, indicates the phoneme, or set of sounds, used in the middle of the English word pot. The exact phonetic reality of this phoneme varies by accent, with the pronunciation in many British and Australian accents, in many US and Irish accents, in Great Lakes accents of the US, etc. Still, counts as one phoneme because it contrasts with, say, the of put or of pit.",
