	{
		title = "Phosphor",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Luminescent substance",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **phosphor** is a substance that exhibits the phenomenon of luminescence; it emits light when exposed to some type of [[Radiant energy|radiant energy]]. The term is used both for [[Fluorescence|fluorescent]] or phosphorescent substances which glow on exposure to [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] or visible light, and cathodoluminescent substances which glow when struck by an electron beam (cathode rays) in a [[Cathode ray tube|cathode-ray tube]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When a phosphor is exposed to radiation, the orbital [[Electron|electrons]] in its [[Molecule|molecules]] are excited to a higher [[Energy level|energy level]]; when they return to their former level they emit the energy as light of a certain color. Phosphors can be classified into two categories: fluorescent substances which emit the energy immediately and stop glowing when the exciting radiation is turned off, and phosphorescent substances which emit the energy after a delay, so they keep glowing after the radiation is turned off, decaying in brightness over a period of milliseconds to days. Fluorescent materials are used in applications in which the phosphor is excited continuously: [[Cathode ray tube|cathode-ray tubes]] (CRT) and plasma video display screens, fluoroscope screens, fluorescent lights, scintillation sensors, most white [[Light-emitting diode|LEDs]], and luminous paints for black light art.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Penguin Books" },
	},
	{
		title = "Photon energy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Energy carried by a photon",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Photon energy** is the [[Energy|energy]] carried by a single [[Photon|photon]]. The amount of energy is directly proportional to the photon's [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic]] [[Frequency|frequency]] and thus, equivalently, is inversely proportional to the [[Wavelength|wavelength]]. The higher the photon's frequency, the higher its energy. Equivalently, the longer the photon's wavelength, the lower its energy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Photon energy can be expressed using any energy unit. Among the units commonly used to denote photon energy are the [[Electronvolt|electronvolt]] (eV) and the [[Joule|joule]] (as well as its multiples, such as the microjoule). As one joule equals 6.24×10^18 eV, the larger units may be more useful in denoting the energy of photons with higher frequency and higher energy, such as [[Gamma ray|gamma rays]], as opposed to lower energy photons as in the optical and [[Radio frequency|radio frequency]] regions of the [[Electromagnetic spectrum|electromagnetic spectrum]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Phrase",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Group of one or more words",
		tags = { "Basics", "Grammar" },
		aliases = { "Expression" },
		summary = 'In [[Grammar|grammar]], a **phrase** — called an **expression** in some contexts — is a group of one or more words acting as a grammatical unit. It can be used within a [[Sentence (linguistics)|sentence]], so this means that a **phrase** can be treated as a unit within a larger structure. For instance, the English sentence "the squirrel is very happy" is a clause phrase which contains the noun phrase "the squirrel" and the verb phrase "is very happy". Additionally, "very happy" is an adjective phrase. Phrases can consist of a single word or a complete sentence. In theoretical linguistics, phrases are often analyzed as units of syntactic structure such as a constituent.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'There is a difference between the common use of the term phrase and its technical use in linguistics. In common usage, a phrase is usually a group of words with some special idiomatic meaning or other significance, such as "all rights reserved", "economical with the truth", or "kick the bucket". It may be a [[Euphemism|euphemism]], a saying or [[Proverb|proverb]], a fixed expression, a figure of speech, etc. In [[Linguistics|linguistics]], these are known as phrasemes. In theories of [[Syntax|syntax]], a phrase is any group of words, or sometimes a single word, which plays a particular role within the syntactic structure of a [[Sentence (linguistics)|sentence]]. It does not have to have any special meaning or significance, or even exist anywhere outside of the sentence being analyzed, but it must function there as a complete grammatical unit.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Phylum",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Taxonomic rank",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Biology|biology]], a **phylum** is a level of classification, or [[Taxonomic rank|taxonomic rank]], that is below [[Kingdom (taxonomy)|kingdom]] and above [[Class (taxonomy)|class]]. Traditionally, the term division has been used in [[Botany|botany]] instead of phylum, although the International Code of Nomenclature for algae, fungi, and plants accepts the terms as equivalent. Depending on definitions, the animal kingdom Animalia contains about 32 phyla, the plant kingdom Plantae contains about 14 phyla, and the fungus kingdom [[Mushroom|Fungi]] contains about eight phyla. Current research in [[Phylogenetics|phylogenetics]] is uncovering the relationships among phyla within larger clades like Ecdysozoa and Embryophyta.",
		related = { "Metaphor" },
	},
	{
		title = "Physical constant",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Universal and unchanging physical quantity",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **physical constant** is a non-varying [[Physical quantity|quantity]] that appears in a theory or model of some physical phenomena. Some physical constants appear in models of fundamental phenomena and impact many parts of physics. These constants include the [[Speed of light|speed of light]] in vacuum c, the gravitational constant G, the [[Planck constant]] h, the electric constant 0, and the [[Elementary charge|elementary charge]] e for examples. Tables of the numerical value of these fundamental physical constants or **universal constants** are carefully maintained and widely consulted. Some physical constants are [[Coefficient|coefficients]] or parameters assumed to be constant in specialized models without being universal constants.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Examples include the characteristic time, characteristic length, or characteristic number (dimensionless) of a given system, or material constants (e.g., Madelung constant, [[Electrical resistivity and conductivity|electrical resistivity]], and [[Heat capacity|heat capacity]]) of a particular material or substance. Physical constants cannot be explained by the theory that incorporates them but their values may be determined by other, more fundamental theories. Dimensioned constants include the values set by the [[International System of Units]] convention in the [[International System of Units|defining constants]] and the derived units built upon those constants, as well as many nonstandard constants still in widespread use as units. Dimensionless constants are characteristics of natural phenomena and their values must be measured experimentally.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Physical object",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Identifiable collection of matter",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Material object", "Object", "Body" },
		summary = "In [[Natural language|natural language]] and physical science, a **physical object** or **material object** (or simply an **object** or **body**) is a collection of [[Matter|matter]], usually contiguous, with a defined boundary, that exists in [[Space|space]] and [[Time|time]]. It is usually contrasted with abstract objects and [[Mental representation|mental objects]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Also in common usage, an object is not constrained to consist of the same collection of [[Matter|matter]]. Atoms or parts of an object may change over time. An object is usually meant to be defined by the simplest representation of the boundary consistent with the observations. However the laws of physics only apply directly to objects that consist of the same collection of matter. In [[Physics|physics]], an object is an identifiable collection of [[Matter|matter]], which may be constrained by an identifiable boundary, and may move as a unit by translation or rotation, in [[Three-dimensional space|3-dimensional space]]. Each object has a unique identity, independent of any other properties. Two objects may be identical, in all properties except position, but still remain distinguishable. In most cases the boundaries of two objects may not overlap at any point in time. The property of identity allows objects to be counted.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Physical quantity",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Measurable  property of a material or system",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **physical quantity** (or simply **quantity**) is a property of a material or system that can be quantified by [[Measurement|measurement]]. A physical quantity can be expressed as a value, which is a pair of a numerical value and a [[Unit of measurement|unit of measurement]]. [[Vector quantity|Vector quantities]] have, besides numerical value and unit, direction or orientation in space.",
	},
	{
		title = "Physical system",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Portion of the universe chosen for analysis",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **physical system** is a collection of [[Physical object|physical objects]] under study. The collection differs from a [[Set (mathematics)|set]]; the objects must coexist and have some physical relationship. In other words, it is a portion of the physical universe chosen for analysis. Everything outside the system is known as the environment, which is ignored except for its effects on the system.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The split between system and environment is the analyst\'s choice, generally made to simplify the analysis. For example, the water in a lake, the water in half of a lake, or an individual molecule of water in the lake can each be considered a physical system. An isolated system is one that has negligible interaction with its environment. Often a system in this sense is chosen to correspond to the more usual meaning of [[System|system]], such as a particular machine. In the study of quantum coherence, the "system" may refer to the microscopic properties of an object (e.g. the mean of a [[Pendulum|pendulum]] bob), while the relevant "environment" may be the internal degrees of freedom, described classically by the pendulum\'s thermal vibrations.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Physicalism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Metaphysical thesis",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'In [[Metaphysics|metaphysics]], **physicalism** is the view that everything is physical, that there is nothing over and above the physical, and that everything supervenes on the physical. It stands in direct opposition to [[Idealism|idealism]], which asserts that reality arises from the [[Mind|mind]]. Physicalism is a form of ontological [[Monism|monism]]—a single-[[Substance theory|substance]] account of the nature of our universe\'s [[Reality|reality]] (physicalism is open to the possibility of different universes with different physics, and is open to the possibility of causal openness via a foundational logical mechanism or mechanisms), in contrast to "two-substance" ([[Mind–body dualism|mind–body dualist]]) or "many-substance" (pluralist) views.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Physicalism is closely related to [[Naturalism (philosophy)|naturalism]], though important distinctions exist between them. Physicalism is also closely related to [[Materialism|materialism]], and has evolved from materialism with advancements in the physical sciences in explaining observed phenomena. The terms "physicalism" and "materialism" are often used interchangeably, but can be distinguished on the basis that [[Physics|physics]] describes more than just matter. Physicalism encompasses [[Matter|matter]], but also [[Energy|energy]], physical laws, [[Space|space]], [[Time|time]], [[Spacetime|spacetime]], exotic matter, [[Structure|structure]], physical processes, [[Information|information]], state, and [[Force|forces]], among other things, as described by physics and other sciences. According to the PhilPapers Survey 2020, physicalism is the majority view among philosophers, at 51.9%, but there is also significant opposition to it.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Phytoplankton",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Autotrophic members of the plankton ecosystem",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '**Phytoplankton** are the autotrophic (self-feeding) components of the [[Plankton|plankton]] community and a key part of ocean and freshwater [[Aquatic ecosystem|ecosystems]]. The name comes from [[Ancient Greek]] (phutón), meaning "[[Plant|plant]]", and (planktós), meaning "drifter, wanderer, roamer", and thus, "plant drifter".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Phytoplankton obtain their energy through [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]], as trees and other plants do on land. This means phytoplankton must receive sunlight, so they live in the well-lit surface layers (euphotic zone) of oceans and lakes. Compared with terrestrial plants, phytoplankton are distributed over a larger surface area, experience less seasonal variation, and have markedly faster turnover rates (days versus decades). As a result, phytoplankton respond rapidly on a global scale to climate variations. Phytoplankton are very diverse, comprising photosynthesizing bacteria ([[Cyanobacteria|cyanobacteria]]) and various unicellular [[Protist|protist]] groups (notably the [[Diatom|diatoms]]). Most phytoplankton are too small to be individually seen with the unaided eye.",
			},
		},
	},
