		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **plectrum** is a small flat tool used for plucking or strumming of a [[String instrument|stringed instrument]]. For hand-held instruments such as [[Guitar|guitars]] and [[Mandolin|mandolins]], the plectrum is often called a pick and is held as a separate tool in the player's hand. Players of lap steel guitar and [[Bluegrass music|bluegrass]] style banjo music often wear a **fingerpick**, a style of plectrum that clips onto or wraps around the end of the fingers and thumb. In [[Harpsichord|harpsichords]], the plectra (traditionally made of crow quill) are attached to the [[Harpsichord|jack mechanism]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Pliny the Younger",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Roman lawyer, author and magistrate (61 – c. 113)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Gaius Plinius Caecilius Secundus" },
		summary = "**Gaius Plinius Caecilius Secundus**, better known in English as **Pliny the Younger**, was a lawyer, author, and magistrate of [[Roman Empire|Ancient Rome]]. Pliny's uncle, [[Pliny the Elder]], helped raise and educate him.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Pliny the Younger wrote 369 letters, of which 247 survived, and which are of some historical value. These include 121 official letters addressed to [[Trajan|Emperor Trajan]] (reigned 98–117). Some are addressed to reigning emperors or to notables such as the historian [[Tacitus]]. Pliny served as an imperial magistrate under Trajan, and his letters to Trajan provide one of the few surviving records of the relationship between the imperial office and provincial governors. Pliny rose through a series of civil and military offices, the cursus honorum. He was a friend of the historian Tacitus and might have employed the biographer Suetonius on his staff. Pliny also came into contact with other well-known men of the period, including the philosophers Artemidorus and Euphrates the Stoic, during his time in [[Roman Syria|Syria]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "AD 61" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Novum Comum, [[Roman Italy|Italia]], [[Roman Empire]]" },
			{ "Died", "c. AD 113 (aged c. 52)" },
			{ "Occupation", "Politician, judge, author" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Poaching",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏹",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Illegal hunting or fishing of wildlife",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Poaching** is the illegal hunting or capturing of wild animals, usually associated with land use rights. Poaching was once performed by impoverished peasants for subsistence purposes and to supplement meager diets. It was set against the hunting privileges of nobility and territorial rulers.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Since the 1980s, the term \"poaching\" has also been used to refer to the illegal harvesting of wild plants. In agricultural terms, the term 'poaching' is also applied to the loss of soils or grass by the damaging action of feet of livestock, which can affect availability of productive land, water pollution through increased runoff and welfare issues for cattle. Stealing livestock, as in cattle raiding, classifies as theft rather than poaching. The United Nations' Sustainable Development Goal 15 enshrines the sustainable use of all wildlife. It targets the taking of action on dealing with poaching and trafficking of protected species of flora and fauna to ensure their availability for present and future generations.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
	},
	{
		title = "Poet",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Person who writes and publishes poetry",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **poet** is a person who studies and creates [[Poetry|poetry]]. Poets may describe themselves as such or be described as such by others. A poet might simply be the creator ([[Thought|thinker]], songwriter, [[Writer|writer]], or author) who creates (composes) poems ([[Oral tradition|oral]] or [[Literature|written]]), or someone who also [[Performance art|performs]] their art to an [[Audience|audience]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The work of a poet is essentially one of communication, expressing ideas either in a literal sense (such as describing a specific event or place) or [[Metaphor|metaphorically]]. Poets have existed since [[Prehistory|prehistory]], in nearly all languages, and have produced works that vary greatly in different cultures and periods. Throughout each [[Civilization|civilization]] and language, poets have used various styles that have changed over time, resulting in countless poets as diverse as the [[Literature|literature]] that (since the advent of [[Writing|writing]] systems) they have produced.",
