		description = "Regions around Earth's geographical poles",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Frigid zones", "Polar zones", "Polar regions of Earth", "Polar region" },
		summary = "The **polar regions**, also called the **frigid zones** or **polar zones**, of [[Earth]] are Earth's polar ice caps, the [[Region|regions]] of the planet that surround its [[Geographical pole|geographical poles]] (the [[North Pole]] and the [[South Pole]]), lying within the polar circles. These **high latitudes** are dominated by floating sea ice covering much of the [[Arctic Ocean]] in the north, and by the [[Antarctic ice sheet]] on the continent of [[Antarctica]] and the [[Southern Ocean]] in the south.",
	},
	{
		title = "Polemic",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Contentious rhetoric",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Polemic** is contentious [[Rhetoric|rhetoric]] intended to support a specific position by forthright claims and to undermine the opposing position. The practice of such argumentation is called **polemics**, which are seen in arguments on controversial topics. A person who writes polemics, or speaks polemically, is called a **polemicist**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Polemics often concern questions in religion or politics. A polemical style of writing was common in [[Ancient Greece]], as in the writings of the historian Polybius. Polemics again became common in [[Middle Ages|medieval]] and early modern times. Since then, famous polemicists have included satirist [[Jonathan Swift]], Italian physicist and mathematician [[Galileo Galilei|Galileo]], French theologian Jean Calvin, French Enlightenment writer, historian, and philosopher [[Voltaire]], Russian author [[Leo Tolstoy]], socialist philosophers [[Karl Marx]] and [[Friedrich Engels]], novelist [[George Orwell]], playwright [[George Bernard Shaw]], communist revolutionary Vladimir Lenin, [[Linguistics|linguist]] [[Noam Chomsky]], social critics H. L. Mencken, Christopher Hitchens and Peter Hitchens, feminists such as Andrea Dworkin and existential philosophers [[Søren Kierkegaard]] and [[Friedrich Nietzsche]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Police",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Law-enforcement body",
		tags = { "Law", "Institutions and professions" },
		summary = "The **police** are a constituted body of people empowered by a [[State (polity)|state]] with the aim of [[Law enforcement|enforcing the law]] and protecting the public order as well as the [[Public|public]] itself. This commonly includes ensuring the [[Safety|safety]], [[Health|health]], and possessions of citizens, and to prevent crime and civil disorder. The term is most commonly associated with the police forces of a [[Sovereign state|sovereign state]] that are authorized to exercise the police power of that state within a defined legal or territorial area of responsibility.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Police forces are often defined as being separate from the military and other organizations involved in the defense of the state against foreign aggressors; however, gendarmerie are military units charged with civil policing. Police forces are usually public sector services, funded through taxes. Law enforcement is only part of policing activity. Policing has included an array of activities in different situations, but the predominant ones are concerned with the preservation of order. In some societies, in the late 18th and early 19th centuries, these developed within the context of maintaining the class system and the protection of [[Private property|private property]]. Police forces have become ubiquitous in complex modern [[Society|societies]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Polish language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "West Slavic language",
		tags = { "Indo-European languages", "Slavic languages" },
		aliases = { "Polish" },
		summary = "**Polish** is a West Slavic language of the Lechitic subgroup, within the [[Indo-European languages|Indo-European language family]], and is written in the [[Latin script]]. It is primarily spoken in [[Poland]] and serves as the [[Official language|official language]] of the country, as well as the language of the Polish diaspora around the world. In 2024, there were over 39.7 million Polish native speakers. It ranks as the sixth-most-spoken among [[Languages of the European Union|languages of the European Union]]. Polish is subdivided into regional dialects. It maintains strict T–V distinction, obligatory honorifics, and other formalities when addressing individuals.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The traditional 32-letter Polish alphabet has nine additions to the letters of the basic 26-letter [[Latin alphabet]], while removing three (x, q, v). Those three letters are at times included in an extended 35-letter alphabet. The traditional set comprises 23 consonants and nine written vowels, including two nasal vowels denoted by a reversed diacritic hook called an. Polish is a [[Fusional language|synthetic and fusional]] language which has seven [[Grammatical case|grammatical cases]]. It has fixed [[Stress (linguistics)|penultimate stress]] and an abundance of palatal consonants. Contemporary Polish developed in the 1700s as the successor to the medieval Old Polish (10th–16th centuries) and Middle Polish (16th–18th centuries). Among the major languages, it is most closely related to [[Slovak language|Slovak]] and [[Czech language|Czech]] but differs in terms of pronunciation and general grammar.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Spoken in", "[[Poland]], [[Lithuania]], and bordering regions" },
			{ "Date", "2021" },
			{ "Writing system", "[[Latin script]] (Polish alphabet)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Polish people",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👥",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "West Slavic ethnic group",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Poles" },
		summary = "**Polish people**, or **Poles**, are a West Slavic [[Ethnicity|ethnic group]] and a [[Nation|nation]] who share a common [[History of Poland|history]], culture, the [[Polish language]] and are identified with the country of [[Poland]] in [[Central Europe]]. The preamble to the Constitution of the Republic of Poland defines the Polish nation as comprising all the [[Citizenship|citizens]] of Poland, regardless of heritage or ethnicity. The majority of Poles adhere to Roman Catholicism.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The population of self-declared Poles in Poland is estimated at 37,394,000 out of an overall population of 38,512,000 (based on the 2011 census), of whom 36,522,000 declared Polish alone. A wide-ranging Polish diaspora (the Polonia) exists throughout [[Eurasia]], the [[Americas]], and [[Australasia]]. Today, the largest urban concentrations of Poles are within the Warsaw metropolitan area and the Katowice urban area. Ethnic Poles are considered to be the descendants of the ancient West Slavic Lechites and other tribes that inhabited the Polish territories during the [[Late antiquity|late antiquity]] period. Poland's [[Recorded history|recorded history]] dates back over a thousand years to c. 930–960 AD, when the Western Polans – an influential tribe in the Greater Poland region – united various Lechitic clans under what became the Piast dynasty, thus creating the first Polish state.",
			},
		},
		related = { "AllMusic" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Languages", "Predominantly [[Polish language|Polish]]" },
			{ "Population", "c. 60 million" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Political campaign",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Attempt to influence the decision-making process within a specific group",
		tags = { "Politics and government", "Politics" },
		summary = "A **political campaign** is an organized effort which seeks to influence the decision making progress within a specific group. In [[Democracy|democracies]], political campaigns often refer to [[Election|electoral]] campaigns, by which representatives are chosen or [[Referendum|referendums]] are decided. In modern politics, the most high-profile political campaigns are focused on general elections and candidates for [[Head of state|head of state]] or [[Head of government|head of government]], often a president or prime minister.",
	},
	{
		title = "Political economy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "📈",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of the development of social production",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Comparative economy" },
		summary = "**Political economy** – sometimes referred to as **comparative economy** – is an [[Interdisciplinarity|interdisciplinary]] field in [[Political science|political science]] and [[Economics|economics]] that studies the relationship between political and economic systems, including how they influence each other.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The field originated within the 16th-century Western moral philosophy, with theoretical works exploring the administration of states' wealth. The earliest works of political economy are usually attributed to the British scholars [[Adam Smith]], Thomas Malthus, and [[David Ricardo]], although the work of the French physiocrats preceded them. Various thinkers, from [[John Stuart Mill]] to [[Karl Marx]], saw economics and politics as inseparable. By the mid-18th century, political economy emerged as a distinct field, encompassing the study of phenomena that is now categorised under economics. In the late-19th century, economics had become an independent discipline separate from political economy with the rise of mathematical modeling, coinciding with the publication of the influential textbook Principles of Economics by Alfred Marshall in 1890.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Padma Bhushan" },
	},
	{
		title = "Political freedom",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Concept in history and political thought",
		tags = { "Society", "Social status" },
		aliases = { "Political autonomy", "Political agency" },
		summary = "**Political freedom**, also called **political autonomy** or **political agency**, is a central [[Concept|concept]] in [[History|history]] and [[Political philosophy|political thought]] and one of the most important features of [[Democracy|democratic]] societies. Political freedom has been described as freedom from oppression or coercion, the absence of disabling conditions for an individual and the fulfillment of enabling conditions, or the absence of life conditions of compulsion in society, such as economic compulsion.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although political freedom is often interpreted negatively as the freedom from unreasonable external constraints on action, it can also refer to the positive exercise of rights, capacities and possibilities for action and the exercise of social or group rights. The concept can also include freedom from internal constraints on political action or speech such as social [[Conformity|conformity]], consistency, or inauthentic behaviour. The concept of political freedom is closely connected with the concepts of [[Civil liberties|civil liberties]] and [[Human rights|human rights]], which in democratic societies are usually afforded legal protection from the [[State (polity)|state]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Political party",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎉",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Organization coordinating policy priorities and candidates for government positions",
		tags = { "Politics and government", "Politics" },
		summary = "A **political party** is an [[Organization|organization]] that coordinates candidates to compete in [[Election|elections]] and participate in governance. It is common for the members of a party to hold similar ideas about [[Politics|politics]], and parties may promote specific ideological or policy goals.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Political parties have become a major part of the politics of almost every country, as modern party organizations developed and spread around the world over the last few centuries. Although some countries have no political parties, this is extremely rare. Most countries have [[Multi-party system|several parties]], while others [[One-party state|only have one]]. Parties are important in the politics of autocracies as well as democracies, although [[Democracy|democracies]] usually have more political parties than autocracies. Autocracies often have a single party that [[Government|governs]] the country, and some political scientists consider competition between two or more parties to be an essential part of democracy. Parties can develop from existing divisions in society, like the divisions between lower and upper [[Social class|classes]], and they streamline the process of making political decisions by encouraging their members to cooperate.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Political philosophy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🤔",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of the foundations of politics",
		tags = { "Philosophy", "Other branches of philosophy" },
		aliases = { "Political theory" },
		summary = "**Political philosophy**, also called **political theory**, is the study of the theoretical and conceptual foundations of [[Politics|politics]]. It examines the nature, scope, and legitimacy of political institutions, such as [[State (polity)|states]]. The field investigates different forms of government, ranging from [[Democracy|democracy]] to [[Authoritarianism|authoritarianism]], as well as the values guiding political action, such as [[Justice|justice]], equality, and [[Liberty|liberty]]. As a normative field, political [[Philosophy|philosophy]] focuses on desirable norms and values, in contrast to [[Political science|political science]], which primarily emphasizes empirical description.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Political ideologies are systems of ideas and principles that outline how society should work. [[Anarchism]] rejects the coercive power of centralized [[Government|governments]]. It proposes a stateless society to promote liberty and equality. Conservatism seeks to preserve traditional institutions and practices. It is skeptical of the human ability to radically [[Social change|reform society]], arguing that drastic changes can destroy the wisdom of past generations. [[Liberalism]] advocates for individual rights and [[Civil liberties|liberties]], the [[Rule of law|rule of law]], [[Private property|private property]], and tolerance. It holds that governments should protect these values to enable individuals to pursue personal goals without external interference. [[Socialism]] emphasizes collective ownership and equal distribution of basic goods.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Political science",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Scientific study of politics and social science",
		tags = { "Politics and government", "Politics" },
		aliases = { "Poli sci" },
		summary = "**Political science** (sometimes abbreviated as **poli sci**) is a field which aims to study the scientific aspect of [[Politics|politics]]. It deals with systems of governance and [[Power (political science)|power]], and the analysis of political activities, [[Political philosophy|political thought]], political behavior, and associated [[Constitution|constitutions]] and [[Law|laws]]. Specialists in the field are **political scientists**. Unlike [[Political philosophy|political philosophy]], which is primarily normative and evaluates politics through value judgments, political science emphasizes descriptive and explanatory means of articulating [[De facto and de jure|what is]] (the de facto, status quo, lex non scripta, etc.) and favors [[Empirical evidence|empirical evidence]] over ethical judgements.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Political science emerged as a distinct academic discipline in the late 19th century, influenced by the rise of [[Empiricism|empiricism]] and [[Positivism|positivism]]. It emerged from history, philosophy, and law amid university professionalization and the founding of bodies such as the American Political Science Association in 1903. The behavioral revolution of the 1950s played a significant role in shifting emphasis to scientifically explaining human behavior from an unbiased and neutral point of view using [[Quantitative research|quantitative research methods]] such as surveys, [[Historiography|historical analysis]], and empirical testing.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Politics",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Activities associated with group decisions",
		tags = { "Politics and government", "Politics" },
		summary = "**Politics** is the activity of settling affairs in an organized [[Society|society]]. Politics is usually concerned with resolving issues within a society via a [[Government|government]]. Politicians are individuals engaged in the activity of politics. The branch of [[Social science|social science]] that studies politics and government is referred to as [[Political science|political science]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A variety of methods are deployed in politics, which include [[Political campaign|promoting one's own political views]] among people, negotiation with other political subjects, making [[Law|laws]], and exercising internal and external force, including warfare against adversaries. Politics is exercised on a wide range of social levels, from clans and tribes of traditional [[Society|societies]], through modern [[Local government|local governments]], [[Company|companies]] and [[Institution|institutions]] up to [[Sovereign state|sovereign states]], to the international level. In modern [[State (polity)|states]], people often form political parties to represent their ideas. Members of a party often agree to take the same position on many issues and agree to support the same changes to law and the same leaders. An [[Election|election]] is usually a competition between different parties. A political system is a framework which defines acceptable political methods within a society.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Polity",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👥",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Group of people with a collective identity",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **polity** is a group of [[Human|people]] with a collective identity, who are organized by some form of [[Politics|political]], institutionalized, [[Social relation|social relations]] and have a capacity to mobilize resources. It is the unit or entity of a political community or body politic.",
	},
	{
		title = "Pollution",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Adverse environmental contamination",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Pollution** is the introduction of contaminants into the [[Natural environment|natural environment]] that cause harm. Pollution can take the form of any substance (solid, liquid, or gas) or energy (such as radioactivity, heat, sound, or light). Pollutants, the components of pollution, can be either foreign substances/energies or naturally occurring contaminants.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although environmental pollution can be caused by natural events, the word pollution generally implies that the contaminants [[Human impact on the environment|have a human source]], such as [[Manufacturing|manufacturing]], extractive industries, poor [[Waste management|waste management]], transportation or agriculture. Pollution is often classed as point source (coming from a highly concentrated specific site, such as a [[Factory|factory]], mine, construction site), or nonpoint source pollution (coming from widespread distributed sources, such as microplastics or agricultural runoff). Many sources of pollution were unregulated parts of [[Industrialisation|industrialization]] during the 19th and 20th centuries until the emergence of environmental [[Regulation|regulation]] and pollution policy in the later half of the 20th century. Sites where historically polluting industries released persistent pollutants may have legacy pollution long after the source of the pollution is stopped.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Polyatomic ion",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ion containing two or more atoms",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Molecular ion" },
		summary = 'A **polyatomic ion** (also known as a **molecular ion**) is a [[Covalent bond|covalent bonded]] set of two or more [[Atom|atoms]], or of a metal complex, that can be considered to behave as a single unit and that usually has a net charge that is not zero, or in the special case of a zwitterion, where spatially separated charges may lead the net charge to be variable depending on acidity conditions. The term [[Molecule|molecule]] may or may not be used to refer to a polyatomic ion, depending on the definition used. The prefix poly- carries the meaning "many" in Greek, but even ions of two atoms are commonly described as polyatomic.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There may be more than one atom in the structure that has non-zero charge, therefore the net charge of the structure may have a [[Ion|cationic]] (positive) or [[Ion|anionic]] (negative) nature depending on those atomic details. In older literature, a polyatomic ion may instead be referred to as a [[Radical (chemistry)|radical]] (or less commonly, as a radical group). In contemporary usage, the term radical refers to various [[Radical (chemistry)|free radicals]], which are species that have an unpaired electron and need not be charged. A simple example of a polyatomic ion is the [[Hydroxide|hydroxide]] ion, which consists of one oxygen atom and one hydrogen atom, jointly carrying a net charge of [[Elementary charge|−1]]; its chemical formula is OH-. In contrast, an ammonium ion consists of one nitrogen atom and four hydrogen atoms, with a charge of +1; its chemical formula is NH4+. Polyatomic ions often are useful in the context of [[Acid–base reaction|acid–base chemistry]] and in the formation of [[Salt (chemistry)|salts]].",
			},
		},
	},
