	{
		title = "Polynesians",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Austronesian ethnolinguistic group",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Polynesians** are an ethnolinguistic group comprising closely related [[Ethnicity|ethnic groups]] native to [[Polynesia]], which encompasses the islands within the Polynesian Triangle in the [[Pacific Ocean]]. They trace their early prehistoric origins to Island Southeast Asia and are part of the larger [[Austronesian peoples|Austronesian]] ethnolinguistic group, with an Urheimat in Taiwan. They speak the Polynesian languages, a branch of the Oceanic subfamily within the Austronesian language family. The Indigenous [[Māori people|Māori]] people form the largest Polynesian population, followed by Samoans, Native Hawaiians, Tahitians, Tongans, and Cook Islands Māori.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As of 2012, there were an estimated 2 million ethnic Polynesians (both full and part) worldwide. The vast majority either inhabit independent Polynesian nation-states ([[Samoa]], [[Niue]], [[Cook Islands]], [[Tonga]], and [[Tuvalu]]) or form minorities in countries such as [[Australia]], [[Chile]] ([[Easter Island]]), [[New Zealand]], [[France]] ([[French Polynesia]] and [[Wallis and Futuna]]), and the [[United States]] ([[Hawaii]] and [[American Samoa]]), as well as in the [[British Overseas Territories|British Overseas Territory]] of the Pitcairn Islands. New Zealand had the highest population of Polynesians, estimated at 110,000 in the 18th century. Polynesians have acquired a reputation as great navigators, with their canoes reaching the most remote corners of the Pacific and allowing the settlement of islands as far apart as Hawaii, Rapanui (Easter Island), and Aotearoa (New Zealand).",
			},
		},
		related = { "Debye" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Population", "c. 3,300,000" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Polynomial ring",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Algebraic structure",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Polynomial algebra", "Indeterminates" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], especially in the field of [[Algebra|algebra]], a **polynomial ring** or **polynomial algebra** is a [[Ring (mathematics)|ring]] formed from the [[Set (mathematics)|set]] of [[Polynomial|polynomials]] in one or more **indeterminates** (traditionally also called [[Variable (mathematics)|variables]]) with [[Coefficient|coefficients]] in another [[Ring (mathematics)|ring]], often a [[Field (mathematics)|field]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Often, the term "polynomial ring" refers implicitly to the special case of a polynomial ring in one indeterminate over a field. The importance of such polynomial rings relies on the high number of properties that they have in common with the ring of the [[Integer|integers]]. Polynomial rings occur and are often fundamental in many parts of mathematics such as [[Number theory|number theory]], [[Commutative algebra|commutative algebra]], and [[Algebraic geometry|algebraic geometry]]. In [[Ring theory|ring theory]], many classes of rings, such as unique factorization domains, regular rings, group rings, rings of formal power series, Ore polynomials, graded rings, have been introduced for generalizing some properties of polynomial rings. A closely related notion is that of the ring of polynomial functions on a [[Vector space|vector space]], and, more generally, ring of regular functions on an [[Algebraic variety|algebraic variety]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Polyphony",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Simultaneous lines of independent melody",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Polyphony** is a type of musical texture consisting of two or more simultaneous lines of independent [[Melody|melody]], as opposed to a musical texture with just one voice (monophony) or a texture with one dominant melodic voice accompanied by [[Chord (music)|chords]] (homophony).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In the Western musical tradition, the term polyphony is usually used to refer to music of the late [[Medieval music|Middle Ages]] and [[Renaissance music|Renaissance]]. [[Baroque music|Baroque]] forms such as [[Fugue|fugue]], which might be called polyphonic, are usually described instead as [[Counterpoint|contrapuntal]]. Also, as opposed to the species terminology of counterpoint, polyphony was generally either "pitch-against-pitch" (sometimes called "point-against-point") or a sustained-pitch in one part with melismas in another. In all cases the concept was probably what Margaret Bent (1999) calls "dyadic counterpoint", with each part being written generally against one other part, with parts modified at the end if necessary. This point-against-point concept is opposed to "successive composition", where voices were written in an order with each new voice fitting into the previous construction.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Polysaccharide",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Long carbohydrate polymers such as starch, glycogen, cellulose, and chitin",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Polysaccharides" },
		summary = '**Polysaccharides** are "compounds consisting of a large number of monosaccharides linked glycosidically". They are the most abundant [[Carbohydrate|carbohydrates]] in [[Food|food]]. Their structures range from linear to highly branched polymers. Examples include storage polysaccharides such as [[Starch|starch]], [[Glycogen|glycogen]], and galactogen and structural polysaccharides such as hemicellulose and [[Chitin|chitin]]. The term "glycan" is synonymous with polysaccharide, but often glycans are discussed in the context of glycoconjugates, i.e. hybrids of polysaccharides and [[Protein|proteins]] or [[Lipid|lipids]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Polysaccharides are often heterogeneous, containing slight modifications of the repeating unit. They may be amorphous (e.g. [[Starch|starch]]) or insoluble in water (e.g. [[Cellulose|cellulose]]). Saccharides are generally composed of simple carbohydrates called monosaccharides with the general formula (CH2O)n where n is three or more. Examples of monosaccharides are [[Glucose|glucose]], [[Fructose|fructose]], and glyceraldehyde. Polysaccharides, meanwhile, have a general formula of Cx(H2O)y where x and y are usually large numbers between 200 and 2500. When the repeating units in the polymer backbone are six-carbon monosaccharides, as is often the case, the general formula simplifies to (C6H10O5)n, where typically 40 ≤ n ≤ 3000. Polysaccharides are highly branched. In fact, starch holds I2[[Iodine|iodine]] molecules in the helical portion. The starch-I2 is dark blue in colour.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Live Science" },
	},
	{
		title = "Pompey",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Roman general and statesman (106–48 BC)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus", "Pompey the Great" },
		summary = "**Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus**, known in English as **Pompey** or **Pompey the Great**, was a [[Roman people|Roman]] general and statesman who was prominent in the final decades of the [[Roman Republic]]. As a young man, he was a partisan and protégé of the dictator Sulla, after whose death he achieved significant military and political success.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A member of the senatorial nobility, Pompey entered into a military career at an early age. He rose to prominence serving Sulla as a commander during the civil war of 83–81 BC. His early success as a general allowed him to bypass the traditional cursus honorum (the sequence of public offices required for political advancement), and he was elected as [[Roman consul|consul]] on three occasions (70, 55, 52 BC). He celebrated three triumphs and served as a commander in the Sertorian War, the Third Servile War, the Third Mithridatic War, and in various other military campaigns. Pompey\'s early success led dictator Sulla to grant him the cognomen Magnus – "the Great" – reportedly in reference to Pompey\'s admiration for [[Alexander the Great]]. His adversaries, however, referred to him as adulescentulus carnifex ("teenage butcher") due to his perceived ruthlessness.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Welsh language" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "29 September 106 BC" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Picenum, Italy" },
			{ "Died", "28 September 48 BC (aged 57)" },
			{ "Occupation", "Soldier, politician" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pontic–Caspian steppe",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "One of the Eurasian steppes",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Pontic–Caspian steppe** is a [[Steppe|steppe]] extending across [[Eastern Europe]] to [[Central Asia]], formed by the Caspian and Pontic steppes. It stretches from the northern shores of the [[Black Sea]] (the Pontus Euxinus of antiquity) to the northern area around the [[Caspian Sea]], where it ends at the Ural-Caspian narrowing, which joins it with the Kazakh Steppe in Central Asia, making it a part of the larger [[Eurasian Steppe]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Geopolitically, the Pontic–Caspian Steppe extends from northeastern [[Bulgaria]] and southeastern [[Romania]] through [[Moldova]], southern and eastern [[Ukraine]], through the [[North Caucasus]] of [[Southern Russia|southern Russia]], and into the Lower Volga region where it straddles the border of southern Russia and western [[Kazakhstan]]. Biogeographically, it is a part of the Palearctic realm, and of the temperate grasslands, savannas, and shrublands [[Biome|biome]]. The area corresponds to Cimmeria, Scythia, and Sarmatia of [[Classical antiquity|classical antiquity]]. Across several millennia, numerous tribes of nomadic horsemen used the steppe; many of them went on to conquer lands in the settled regions of [[Central and Eastern Europe]], [[West Asia]], and [[South Asia]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Area", "994,000" },
		},
