	{
		title = "Postmodern literature",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "20th-century literary form and movement",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Postmodern literature** is a [[Genre|genre]] of [[Literature|literature]] characterized by the use of metafiction, unreliable narration, self-reference, and intertextuality (literature building upon or reimagining other literature), and which often presents both historical and political themes. As a style of experimental literature, it emerged strongly in the [[United States]] in the 1960s through the writings of authors such as [[Kurt Vonnegut]], Thomas Pynchon, William Gaddis, Philip K. Dick, Kathy Acker, and John Barth. Beginning within or alongside the counterculture of the 1960s, postmodernists often challenge [[Authority|authorities]] and the status quo.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"This inspiration is, among other things, seen through how postmodern literature is highly self-reflexive about the political issues it speaks to. Precursors to postmodern literature include [[Miguel de Cervantes]]' [[Don Quixote]] (1605–1615), Laurence Sterne's Tristram Shandy (1760–1767), James Hogg's Private Memoires and Confessions of a Justified Sinner (1824), Thomas Carlyle's Sartor Resartus (1833–1834), and [[Jack Kerouac]]'s [[On the Road]] (1957), but postmodern literature was particularly prominent in the 1960s and 1970s. In the 21st century, [[American literature]] still features a strong current of postmodern writing, like the postironic Dave Eggers' A Heartbreaking Work of Staggering Genius (2000) and Jennifer Egan's A Visit from the Goon Squad (2011). These works also further develop the postmodern form.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
