		tags = { "Basics", "Grammar" },
		aliases = { "Pro" },
		summary = "In [[Linguistics|linguistics]] and [[Grammar|grammar]], a **pronoun** (glossed **pro**) is a word or a group of words that one may substitute for a [[Noun|noun]] or noun phrase.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Pronouns have traditionally been regarded as one of the parts of speech, but some modern theorists would not consider them to form a single class, in view of the variety of functions they perform cross-linguistically. An example of a pronoun is "you", which can be either singular or plural. The use of pronouns often involves anaphora, where the meaning of the pronoun is dependent on an antecedent. For example, in the sentence That poor man looks as if he needs a new coat, the meaning of the pronoun he is dependent on its antecedent, that poor man. The [[Adjective|adjective]] form of the word "pronoun" is "**pronominal**". A pronominal is also a word or phrase that acts as a pronoun. For example, in That\'s not the one I wanted, the phrase the one (containing the prop-word one) is a pronominal.',
