	{
		title = "Public domain",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Works outside the scope of copyright law",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **public domain** (**PD**) consists of all creative work to which no exclusive [[Intellectual property|intellectual property]] rights apply. Those rights may have expired, be forfeit, waived or may be inapplicable. Because no one holds the exclusive rights, anyone can legally use or reference those works without permission.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As examples, the works of [[William Shakespeare]], [[Ludwig van Beethoven]], [[Miguel de Cervantes]], Zoroaster, Lao Zi, [[Confucius]], [[Aristotle]], L. Frank Baum, [[Leonardo da Vinci]] and [[Georges Méliès]] are in the public domain either by virtue of their having been created before [[Copyright|copyright]] existed, or by their copyright term having expired. Some works are not covered by a country's copyright laws, and are therefore in the public domain; for example, in the United States, items excluded from copyright include the formulae of [[Classical mechanics|Newtonian physics]] and cooking recipes. Other works are actively dedicated by their authors to the public domain (see waiver); examples include reference implementations of cryptographic algorithms.",
