		title = "Renaissance humanism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Revival in the study of Classical antiquity",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Renaissance humanism** is a [[Worldview|worldview]] centered on the nature and importance of humanity that emerged from the study of [[Classical antiquity|classical antiquity]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Renaissance humanists sought to create a [[Citizenship|citizenry]] able to speak and write with eloquence and clarity, and thus capable of engaging in the civic life of their communities and persuading others to [[Virtue|virtuous]] and prudent actions. Humanism, while set up by a small elite who had access to books and education, was intended as a cultural movement to influence all of society. It was a program to revive the cultural heritage, literary legacy, and moral philosophy of the Greco-Roman civilization. It first began [[Italian Renaissance|in Italy]] and then spread across [[Western Europe]] in the 14th, 15th, and 16th centuries. During the period, the term humanist referred to teachers and students of the [[Humanities|humanities]], known as the, which included the study of [[Latin]] and [[Ancient Greek|Greek]] literatures, [[Grammar|grammar]], [[Rhetoric|rhetoric]], [[History|history]], [[Poetry|poetry]], and moral philosophy.",
			},
		},
		related = { "National Bureau of Statistics of China" },
	},
	{
		title = "Renaissance philosophy",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🤔",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Period of European thought (1355–1650)",
		tags = { "Philosophy", "Philosophy by region and period" },
		summary = "**Renaissance philosophy** encompasses the philosophical thought of the period corresponding to the [[Renaissance]] in Europe, running roughly from 1400 to 1600. It therefore overlaps both with late [[Medieval philosophy|medieval philosophy]], which in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries was influenced by notable figures such as Albert the Great, [[Thomas Aquinas]], [[William of Ockham]], and Marsilius of Padua, and early modern philosophy, which conventionally starts with [[René Descartes]] and his publication of the Discourse on Method in 1637.",
