	{
		title = "Retreat of the government of the Republic of China to Taiwan",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "1949 event in the Chinese Civil War",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "Following their defeat in the Chinese Civil War, the remnants of the government of the Republic of China (ROC), alongside many refugees, retreated to the island of Taiwan (Formosa) beginning on December 7, 1949. The exodus is sometimes called the **Great Retreat** in [[Taiwan]]. The members of the [[Kuomintang]] (KMT), its officers, and approximately 2 million ROC troops took part in the retreat, in addition to many civilians and refugees, fleeing the advance of the People's Liberation Army (PLA) of the [[Chinese Communist Party]] (CCP).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The CCP, who now effectively controlled most of [[Mainland China|mainland China]], spent the subsequent years purging remnant Nationalist forces in [[Western China|western]] and southern China, solidifying the rule of the newly established [[China|People's Republic of China]] (PRC). ROC troops mostly fled to Taiwan from provinces in southern China, in particular [[Sichuan|Sichuan Province]], where the last stand of the ROC's main army took place. The flight to Taiwan took place over four weeks after [[Mao Zedong]] had [[Proclamation of the People's Republic of China|declared the founding]] of the PRC in Beijing on October 1, 1949. In addition, some of the ROC troops in Yunnan also fled to Burma, where the insurgency lasted until 1961. ROC Army troops remained in control of Kinmen and other islands on the mainland side of the [[Taiwan Strait]], and prevented the PRC from advancing towards the island of Taiwan through the Battle of Kinmen.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Imperator" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Date", "7 December 1949" },
			{ "Location", "[[Taipei]], Taiwan Province, Republic of China" },
			{ "Participants", "Government of the Republic of China and the Republic of China Armed Forces" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Revenue",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Total amount of income generated by the sale of goods or services",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'In [[Accounting|accounting]], **revenue** is the total amount of income generated by the sale of [[Product (business)|goods and services]] related to the primary operations of a [[Business|business]]. Commercial revenue may also be referred to as **sales** or as **turnover**. Some [[Company|companies]] receive revenue from [[Interest|interest]], royalties, or other fees. "Revenue" may refer to income in general, or it may refer to the amount, in a monetary unit, earned during a period of time, as in "Last year, company X had revenue of $42 million". Revenue is reported on the income statement as the "top line" due to its position at the very top of the statement.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'This is to be contrasted with the "bottom line" which shows profit or net income, which is calculated by deducting total expenses from revenue. In general usage, revenue is the total amount of income by the sale of goods or services related to the company\'s operations. Sales revenue is income received from selling goods or services over a period of time. Tax revenue is income that a government receives from taxpayers. Fundraising revenue is income received by a charity from donors etc. to further its social purposes. In more formal usage, revenue is a calculation or estimation of periodic income based on a particular standard accounting practice or the rules established by a government or government agency. Two common accounting methods, cash basis accounting and accrual basis accounting, do not use the same process for measuring revenue.',
			},
		},
	},
