		title = "Riddle",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Statement with a double meaning used as a puzzle",
		tags = { "Entertainment", "Entertainment and leisure" },
		summary = "A **riddle** is a statement, [[Question|question]], or [[Phrase|phrase]] having a double or veiled meaning, put forth as a [[Puzzle|puzzle]] to be solved. Riddles are of two types: **enigmas**, which are problems generally expressed in [[Metaphor|metaphorical]] or [[Allegory|allegorical]] language that require ingenuity and careful thinking for their solution, and **conundrums**, which are questions relying for their effects on punning in either the question or the answer.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Archer Taylor says that "we can probably say that riddling is a universal art" and cites riddles from hundreds of different cultures including Finnish, Hungarian, American Indian, Chinese, Russian, Dutch, and Filipino sources amongst many others. Many riddles and riddle-themes are internationally widespread. In the assessment of Elli Köngäs-Maranda (originally writing about Malaitian riddles, but with an insight that has been taken up more widely), whereas [[Mythology|myths]] serve to encode and establish social norms, "riddles make a point of playing with conceptual boundaries and crossing them for the intellectual pleasure of showing that things are not quite as stable as they seem" — though the point of doing so may still ultimately be to "play with boundaries, but ultimately to affirm them".',
			},
