	{
		title = "SI base unit",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "📏",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "One of the seven units of measurement that define the metric system",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "SI base units" },
		summary = "The **SI base units** are the standard [[Unit of measurement|units of measurement]] defined by the [[International System of Units]] (SI) for the seven [[Physical quantity|base quantities]] of what is now known as the International System of Quantities: they are notably a basic set from which all other [[International System of Units|SI units]] can be [[SI derived unit|derived]]. The units and their physical quantities are the [[Second|second]] for [[Time|time]], the [[Metre|metre]] (also spelled meter) for [[Length|length]] or [[Distance|distance]], the [[Kilogram|kilogram]] for [[Mass|mass]], the [[Ampere|ampere]] for [[Electric current|electric current]], the [[Kelvin|kelvin]] for [[Thermodynamic temperature|thermodynamic temperature]], the [[Mole (unit)|mole]] for [[Amount of substance|amount of substance]], and the [[Candela|candela]] for luminous intensity. The SI base units are a fundamental part of modern [[Metrology|metrology]], and thus part of the foundation of modern science and technology.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The SI base units form a set of mutually independent dimensions as required by [[Dimensional analysis|dimensional analysis]] commonly employed in science and technology. The names and symbols of SI base units are written in lowercase, except the symbols of those named after a person, which are written with an initial capital letter. For example, the metre has the symbol m, but the [[Kelvin|kelvin]] has symbol K, because it is named after Lord Kelvin and the [[Ampere|ampere]] with symbol A is named after [[André-Marie Ampère]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "SI derived unit",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📏",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Measurement unit derived from basic metric value",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "SI derived units" },
		summary = "**SI derived units** are [[Unit of measurement|units of measurement]] derived from the seven [[SI base unit|SI base units]] specified by the [[International System of Units]] (SI). They can be expressed as a product (or ratio) of one or more of the base units, possibly scaled by an appropriate power of exponentiation. Some are dimensionless, as when the units cancel out in ratios of like quantities.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'**SI coherent derived units** involve only a trivial proportionality factor, not requiring conversion factors. The SI has special names for 22 of these coherent derived units (for example, [[Hertz|hertz]], the SI unit of measurement of frequency), but the rest merely reflect their derivation: for example, the square metre (m2), the SI derived unit of area; and the kilogram per cubic metre (kg/m3 or kg⋅m−3), the SI derived unit of [[Density|density]]. The names of SI coherent derived units, when written in full, are always in lowercase. However, the symbols for units named after persons are written with an uppercase initial letter. For example, the symbol for hertz is "Hz", while the symbol for [[Metre|metre]] is "m".',
			},
		},
		related = { "Princeton University Press" },
	},
	{
		title = "Sadness",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Negative emotional state",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Emotions and traits" },
		summary = "**Sadness** is an emotional pain associated with, or characterized by, feelings of disadvantage, loss, despair, [[Grief|grief]], helplessness, disappointment and sorrow. An individual experiencing sadness may become quiet or lethargic, and withdraw themselves from others. An example of severe sadness is depression, a [[Mood (psychology)|mood]] which can be brought on by major depressive disorder or persistent depressive disorder. Crying can be an indication of sadness.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Sadness is one of the six basic emotions described by Paul Ekman, along with [[Happiness|happiness]], [[Anger|anger]], surprise, [[Fear|fear]], and [[Disgust|disgust]].",
			},
