	{
		title = "Saint Peter",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Apostle of Jesus",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Peter the Apostle", "Simon Peter", "Cephas" },
		summary = "**Saint Peter**, also known as **Peter the Apostle** and **Simon Peter**, or **Cephas** was one of the Twelve Apostles of Jesus and one of the first leaders of the early Christian Church. He appears repeatedly and prominently in all four New Testament gospels, as well as the Acts of the Apostles. Catholic and Orthodox tradition treats Peter as the first bishop of Rome – or pope – and also as the first bishop of Antioch.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The ancient Christian churches venerate Peter as a major saint and the founder of the Church of Antioch and the Church of Rome, but they differ in their attitudes regarding the authority of his successors. According to Catholic teaching, Jesus promised Peter a special position in the church. In the New Testament, the name \"Simon Peter\" is found 19 times. He is the brother of Andrew, and they both were fishermen. The Gospel of Mark, in particular, is traditionally thought to show the influence of Peter's preaching and eyewitness accounts. He is also mentioned as Peter or Cephas in Paul's First Letter to the Corinthians and the Epistle to the Galatians. The New Testament also includes two general epistles, First Peter and Second Peter, which are traditionally attributed to him, but modern scholarship generally rejects the Petrine authorship of both.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Pope" },
			{ "Born", "c. 1 BC" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Bethsaida, Herodian tetrarchy, [[Roman Empire]]" },
			{ "Died", "Between 64 and 68 AD (aged 63–67)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sakoku",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Isolationist policy of Japan (1633–1853)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Sakoku** was the isolationist foreign policy of the [[Japan|Japanese]] [[Tokugawa shogunate]] under which, during the [[Edo period]] (from 1603 to 1868), relations and trade between Japan and other countries were severely limited, and almost all foreign nationals were banned from entering Japan, while common Japanese people were kept from leaving the country. The policy was enacted by the shogunate government (bakufu) under Tokugawa Iemitsu through a number of edicts and policies from 1633 to 1639.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Japan was not completely isolated under the sakoku policy. Sakoku was a system in which strict regulations were placed on commerce and foreign relations by the shogunate and certain feudal domains (han). There was extensive trade with [[Qing dynasty|China]] through the port of Nagasaki, in the far west of Japan, with a residential area for the Chinese. The policy stated that the only [[Europe|European]] influence permitted was the [[Dutch East India Company|Dutch]] factory at Dejima in Nagasaki. Western scientific, technical and medical innovations flowed into Japan through Rangaku ("Dutch learning"). Trade with [[Joseon|Korea]] was limited to the Tsushima Domain, today part of Nagasaki Prefecture, and the wakan in Choryang, part of present-day [[Busan]]. There were also diplomatic exchanges done through the Joseon Tongsinsa from Korea.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sales",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Activities related to the exchange of goods",
		tags = { "Business and economics" },
		summary = '**Sales** are activities related to selling or the number of goods sold in a given targeted time period. The delivery of a service for a cost is also considered a sale. A period during which goods are sold for a reduced price may also be referred to as a "sale".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The seller, or the provider of the [[Goods and services|goods or services]], completes a sale in an interaction with a buyer, which may occur at the point of sale or in response to a purchase order from a customer. There is a passing of title (property or ownership) of the item, and the settlement of a [[Price|price]], in which agreement is reached on a price for which transfer of ownership of the item will occur. The seller, not the purchaser, typically executes the sale and it may be completed prior to the obligation of [[Payment|payment]]. In the case of indirect interaction, a person who sells goods or service on behalf of the owner is known as a **salesman** or **saleswoman** or **salesperson**, but this often refers to someone selling goods in a store/shop, in which case other terms are also common, including salesclerk, shop assistant, and retail clerk.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sales tax",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tax on the sales of certain goods and services",
		tags = { "Business and economics" },
		summary = "A **sales tax** is a [[Tax|tax]] paid to a governing body for the sales of certain goods and services. Usually, laws allow the seller to collect funds for the tax from the consumer at the point of purchase.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When a tax on goods or services is paid to a governing body directly by a consumer, it is usually called a use tax. Often, laws provide for the exemption of certain goods or services from sales and use tax, such as food, education, and medicines. A [[Value-added tax|value-added tax]] (VAT) collected on goods and services is related to a sales tax. See [[Value-added tax|Comparison with sales tax]] for key differences.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Fishing", "Mining" },
