		emoji = "🦭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Device for making an impression in wax or other medium",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Seal" },
		summary = 'A **seal** is a device for making an impression in wax, [[Clay|clay]], [[Paper|paper]], or some other medium, including an embossment on paper, and is also the impression thus made. The original purpose was to authenticate a document, or to prevent interference with a package or envelope by applying a seal which had to be broken to open the container (hence the modern English verb "to seal", which implies secure closing without an actual wax seal).',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The seal-making device is also referred to as the seal matrix or die; the imprint it creates as the **seal impression** (or, more rarely, the sealing). If the impression is made purely as a relief resulting from the greater pressure on the paper where the high parts of the matrix touch, the seal is known as a dry seal; in other cases ink or another liquid or liquefied medium is used, in another color than the paper. In most traditional forms of dry seal the design on the seal matrix is in intaglio (cut below the flat surface) and therefore the design on the impressions made is in [[Relief|relief]] (raised above the surface). The design on the impression will reverse (be a mirror-image of) that of the matrix, which is especially important when script is included in the design, as it very often is.",
			},
		},
		related = { "The Times of India" },
	},
	{
		title = "Seal script",
		category = "Concept",
