	{
		title = "Shanghainese",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Wu Chinese variety spoken in Shanghai",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Shanghainese language", "Shanghai dialect", "Hu language" },
		summary = "The **Shanghainese language**, also known as the **Shanghai dialect**, or **Hu language**, is a variety of [[Wu Chinese]] spoken in the central districts of the city of [[Shanghai]] and its surrounding areas. It is classified as part of the [[Sino-Tibetan languages|Sino-Tibetan language family]]. Shanghainese, like the rest of the Wu language group, is mutually unintelligible with other [[Varieties of Chinese|varieties of Chinese]], such as [[Mandarin Chinese|Mandarin]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Shanghainese belongs to a separate group of the Taihu Wu subgroup. With nearly 14 million speakers, Shanghainese is also the largest single form of Wu Chinese. Since the late 19th century, it has served as the [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] of the entire Yangtze River Delta region, but in recent decades its status has declined relative to Mandarin, which most Shanghainese speakers can also speak. Like other Wu varieties, Shanghainese is rich in [[Vowel|vowels]] and [[Consonant|consonants]], with around twenty unique vowel qualities, twelve of which are [[Phoneme|phonemic]]. Similarly, Shanghainese also has voiced obstruent initials, which is rare outside of Wu and Xiang varieties. Shanghainese also has a low number of tones compared to other languages in Southern China and has a system of tone sandhi similar to Japanese pitch accent.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Spoken in", "[[China]]" },
			{ "Region", "[[Shanghai]], Nantong (urban variety traditionally in the urban center of Shanghai)" },
			{ "Writing system", "[[Chinese characters]]" },
			{ "Speakers", "15.86 million (including bendihua)" },
			{ "Date", "2012" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Shellfish",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Culinary and fisheries term for exoskeleton-bearing aquatic invertebrates",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Shellfish**, in colloquial and fisheries usage, are [[Exoskeleton|exoskeleton]]-bearing [[Aquatic animal|aquatic]] [[Invertebrate|invertebrates]] used as [[Human food|food]], including various species of [[Mollusca|molluscs]], [[Crustacean|crustaceans]], and [[Echinoderm|echinoderms]]. Although most kinds of shellfish are harvested from [[Seawater|saltwater]] environments, some are found in [[Fresh water|freshwater]]. In addition, a few species of land crabs are eaten, for example Cardisoma guanhumi in the [[Caribbean]]. Shellfish are among the most common food allergens.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Due to narrowing in the meaning of the English word fish over the centuries, shellfish no longer fall under what is usually considered [[Fish|fish]]. Most shellfish are low on the food chain and eat a diet composed mainly of [[Phytoplankton|phytoplankton]] and [[Zooplankton|zooplankton]]. Many varieties of shellfish, and [[Crustacean|crustaceans]] in particular, are actually closely related to [[Insect|insects]] and arachnids. Crustaceans make up one of the main [[Subphylum|subphyla]] of the phylum Arthropoda. Molluscs include cephalopods (squids, octopuses, cuttlefish) and bivalves (clams, oysters), as well as gastropods (aquatic species like whelks and winkles; land species like snails and slugs). Molluscs used as a food source by humans include many [[Species|species]] of clams, mussels, [[Oyster|oysters]], winkles, and [[Scallop|scallops]]. Some crustaceans that are typically eaten are shrimp, lobsters, crayfish, crabs and barnacles.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Shiji",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Historical account of ancient China",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Records of the Grand Historian", "The Grand Scribe's Records" },
		summary = "The **Shiji**, also known as **Records of the Grand Historian** or **The Grand Scribe's Records**, is a Chinese historical text that is the first of the Twenty-Four Histories of imperial China. It was written during the late 2nd and early 1st centuries BC by the early [[Han dynasty]] historian [[Sima Qian]], building upon work begun by his father Sima Tan. The work covers a 2,500-year period from the age of the legendary [[Yellow Emperor]] to the reign of [[Emperor Wu of Han]] in the author's own time, and describes the world as it was known to the Chinese of the Western Han dynasty.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The Shiji has been called a "foundational text in Chinese civilization". Following [[Confucius]] and [[Qin Shi Huang]], "Sima Qian was one of the creators of Imperial China; by providing definitive biographies, he effectively shaped the enduring images of these two earlier figures." The Shiji set the model for all subsequent dynastic histories of China. In contrast to Western historiographical conventions, the Shiji does not treat history as "a continuous, sweeping narrative", but rather breaks it up into smaller, overlapping units dealing with famous leaders, individuals, and major topics of significance.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Author", "[[Sima Qian]]" },
			{ "Country", "China" },
			{ "Language", "[[Classical Chinese]]" },
			{ "Published", "c. 91 BC" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Shopping",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Buying goods",
		tags = { "Business and economics" },
		summary = "**Shopping** is an activity in which a customer browses the available goods or services presented by one or more retailers with the potential intent to purchase a suitable selection of them. A [[Retail|typology of shopper types]] has been developed by scholars which identifies one group of shoppers as recreational shoppers, that is, those who enjoy shopping and view it as a [[Leisure|leisure]] activity.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Online shopping has become a major disruptor in the retail industry as consumers can now search for product information and place product orders across different regions. Online retailers deliver their products directly to the consumers' home, offices, or wherever they want. The B2C (business to consumer) process has made it easy for consumers to select any product online from a retailer's website and to have it delivered relatively quickly. Using online shopping methods, consumers do not need to consume energy by physically visiting physical stores. This way they save time and the cost of traveling. A retailer or a shop is a [[Business|business]] that presents a selection of goods and offers to trade or sell them to customers for money or other goods. Shoppers' shopping experiences may vary.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Subsidy" },
	},
	{
		title = "Shopping center",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Commercial trading complex",
		tags = { "Business and economics" },
		aliases = { "Shopping centre", "Shopping complex", "Shopping arcade" },
		summary = "A **shopping center** (**shopping centre** in [[English in the Commonwealth of Nations|Commonwealth English]]), **shopping complex**, **shopping arcade**, **shopping plaza**, or **galleria**, is a group of shops built together, sometimes under one roof.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The first known collections of retailers under one roof are [[Marketplace|public markets]], dating back to ancient times, and Middle Eastern covered markets, bazaars and souqs. In Paris, about 150 covered passages were built between the late 18th century and 1850, and a wealth of shopping arcades were built across Europe in the 19th century. In the United States, the widespread use of the automobile in the 1920s led to the first shopping centers consisting of a few dozen shops that included parking for cars. Starting in 1946, larger, open air centers anchored by department stores were built (sometimes as a collection of adjacent retail properties with different owners), and then enclosed shopping malls starting with Victor Gruen's Southdale Center near [[Minneapolis]] in 1956.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Shyness",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Feeling of apprehension, discomfort or awkwardness in the presence of other people",
		tags = { "Psychology", "Interpersonal relations" },
		aliases = { "Diffidence" },
		summary = "**Shyness** (also called **diffidence**) is a psychological trait characterized by social inhibition and apprehension in response to new social environments or perceived social judgement. It is frequently associated with social avoidance. Although shyness can be an attribute of people who have low [[Self-esteem|self-esteem]], the trait is often defined by a concern regarding the negative evaluation of others, such as mocking or rejection. While shyness is considered a normal personality variant, it is distinct from clinical conditions such as social anxiety disorder, which involves impairment in daily functioning.",
		related = { "Cooperation", "Honour" },
	},
	{
		title = "Sibling",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "One of two or more individuals having at least one parent in common",
		tags = { "Family members" },
		summary = "A **sibling** is a relative that shares at least one [[Parent|parent]] with the other person. A male sibling is a [[Brother|brother]], and a female sibling is a [[Sister|sister]]. A person with no siblings is an only child.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While some circumstances can cause siblings to be raised separately (such as foster care or [[Adoption|adoption]]), most societies have siblings grow up together. This causes the development of strong emotional bonds, with siblinghood considered a unique type of relationship. The emotional bond between siblings is often complicated and is influenced by factors such as parental treatment, birth order, [[Personality psychology|personality]], and personal experiences outside the family. Medically, a full-sibling is a first-degree relative and a half-sibling is a second-degree relative as they are related by 50% and 25%, respectively.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Pet", "Mother" },
	},
	{
		title = "Sign language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Language that uses manual communication and body language to convey meaning",
		tags = { "Language", "Language families" },
		aliases = { "Sign languages", "Signed languages" },
		summary = "**Sign languages** (also known as **signed languages**) are [[Language|languages]] that use the visual-manual modality to convey meaning rather than spoken words. They are expressed through manual articulation in combination with non-manual markers, and are full-fledged [[Natural language|natural languages]] with their own grammar and lexicon. Sign languages are not universal and are usually not [[Mutual intelligibility|mutually intelligible]], although there are similarities among different sign languages.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Wherever communities of deaf and hard of hearing people exist, sign languages have developed as a means of communication and form the core of local Deaf cultures. Although signing is used primarily by deaf and hard of hearing people, it is also used by hearing individuals, such as those with deaf family members including children of deaf adults (CODAs). Sign languages are distinct from [[Body language|body language]], a type of nonverbal communication. Linguists also distinguish natural sign languages from other systems that are precursors to them or derived from them, such as constructed manual codes for spoken languages, home sign, baby sign language, and signs used with non-human primates. The number of sign languages worldwide is not precisely known. Each country generally has its own native sign language, and some have more than one.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Signal",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Varying physical quantity that conveys information",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **signal** is both the process and the result of transmission of [[Data|data]] over some media accomplished by embedding some variation. Signals are important in multiple subject fields, including [[Signal processing|signal processing]], [[Information theory|information theory]] and [[Biology|biology]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In signal processing, a signal is a function that conveys [[Information|information]] about a phenomenon. Any quantity that can vary over space or time can be used as a signal to share messages between observers. The IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing includes [[Audio signal|audio]], [[Video|video]], speech, [[Image|image]], [[Sonar|sonar]], and [[Radar|radar]] as examples of signals. A signal may also be defined as any observable change in a quantity over space or time (a time series), even if it does not carry information. In nature, signals can be actions done by an organism to alert other organisms, ranging from the release of plant chemicals to warn nearby plants of a predator, to sounds or motions made by animals to alert other animals of food. Signaling occurs in all organisms, even at cellular levels, with [[Cell signaling|cell signaling]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Silicate mineral",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Rock-forming minerals with predominantly silicate anions",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Silicate minerals" },
		summary = "**Silicate minerals** are rock-forming [[Mineral|minerals]] made up of [[Silicate|silicate]] groups. They are the largest and most important class of minerals and make up approximately 90 percent of [[Earth's crust]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In [[Mineralogy|mineralogy]], the crystalline forms of [[Silicon dioxide|silica]] (SiO2) are usually considered to be tectosilicates, and they are classified as such in the Dana classification system (75.1). However, the Nickel–Strunz classification categorizes them as oxide minerals (4.DA). Silica is found in nature as the mineral [[Quartz|quartz]] and its polymorphs. On Earth, a wide variety of silicate minerals occur in an even wider range of combinations as a result of the processes that have been forming and re-working the crust for billions of years. These processes include partial [[Melting|melting]], [[Crystallization|crystallization]], fractionation, metamorphism, [[Weathering|weathering]], and diagenesis. Living organisms also contribute to this geologic cycle. For example, a type of [[Plankton|plankton]] known as [[Diatom|diatoms]] construct their [[Exoskeleton|exoskeletons]] ("frustules") from silica extracted from [[Seawater|seawater]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Silicon dioxide",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Oxide of silicon",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Silica" },
		summary = "**Silicon dioxide**, also known as **silica**, is an [[Oxide|oxide]] of [[Silicon|silicon]] with the [[Chemical formula|chemical formula]] SiO2, and is commonly found in nature as [[Quartz|quartz]]. In many parts of the world, silica is the major constituent of [[Sand|sand]]. Silica is one of the most complex and abundant families of [[Material|materials]], existing as a compound of several [[Mineral|minerals]] and as a synthetic product. Examples include fused quartz, fumed silica, opal, and aerogels. It is used in structural materials, microelectronics, and as components in the food and pharmaceutical industries. All forms are white or colorless, although impure samples can be colored or opaque.",
		sections = { { "Overview", "Silicon dioxide is a common fundamental constituent of [[Glass|glass]]." } },
		related = { "Indian Administrative Service" },
	},
	{
		title = "Silt",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🟫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Classification of soil or sediment",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Silt** is granular material of a size between [[Sand|sand]] and [[Clay|clay]] and composed mostly of broken grains of [[Quartz|quartz]]. Silt may occur as a [[Soil|soil]] (often mixed with sand or clay) or as [[Sediment|sediment]] mixed in suspension with water. Silt usually has a floury feel when dry, and lacks [[Plasticity (physics)|plasticity]] when wet. Silt can also be felt by the tongue as granular when placed on the front teeth (even when mixed with clay particles).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Silt is a common material, making up 45% of average modern mud. It is found in many river deltas and as wind-deposited accumulations, particularly in central Asia, north China, and North America. It is produced in both very hot climates (through such processes as collisions of quartz grains in dust storms) and very cold climates (through such processes as glacial grinding of quartz grains.) Loess is soil rich in silt, which makes up some of the most fertile agricultural land on Earth. However, silt is very vulnerable to erosion, and it has poor mechanical properties, making construction on silty soil problematic. The failure of the Teton Dam in 1976 has been attributed to the use of unsuitable loess in the dam core, and liquefication of silty soil is a significant earthquake hazard.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Simplified Chinese characters",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔣",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Standardized set of Chinese characters",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Simplified Chinese characters** are one of two standardized [[Chinese characters|character sets]] used to write the [[Chinese language]], with the other being traditional characters. Their widespread adoption during the 20th century was part of efforts by the [[China|People’s Republic of China]] (PRC) to promote literacy. Today, they are the standard forms used in [[Mainland China|mainland China]], [[Malaysia]], and [[Singapore]], while traditional characters are officially used in [[Hong Kong]], [[Macau]], and [[Taiwan]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Simplification of Chinese script typically involves reducing a character's total stroke count or streamlining which strokes appear in a given component. By systematically simplifying radicals, large swaths of the character set are altered at once. Many simplifications were drawn from popular cursive forms, while variant characters with identical pronunciation and meaning were consolidated into a single standardized form, usually the simplest available. Many characters were left untouched and are thus identical in both orthographies. The PRC government has never officially declared the simplification process complete. A second round of simplified characters, promulgated in 1977 in the wake of the Cultural Revolution, was largely composed of artificial new variants designed to reduce stroke counts rather than drawing on existing cursive forms.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Logographic" },
			{ "Languages", "[[Chinese language|Chinese]]" },
			{ "Family", "Oracle bone script" },
			{ "Published", "1956, 1964, 1977, 1988, 2013" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sindhi language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗣️",
