		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Social marginalisation", "Social marginalization" },
		summary = '**Social exclusion** or **social marginalisation** or **social marginalization** is a social disadvantage and relegation to the fringes of [[Society|society]]. It is a term that has been used widely in [[Europe]] and was first used in [[France]] in the late 20th century. In the EU context, the [[European Commission]] defines it as "a situation whereby a person is prevented (or excluded) from contributing to and benefiting from economic and social progress". It is used across disciplines including [[Education|education]], [[Sociology|sociology]], [[Psychology|psychology]], [[Health care|healthcare]], [[Politics|politics]] and [[Economics|economics]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Social exclusion is the process in which individuals are blocked from (or denied full access to) various [[Rights|rights]], opportunities and resources that are normally available to members of a different group, and which are fundamental to social integration and observance of human rights within that particular group (e.g. due process). Alienation or disenfranchisement resulting from social exclusion can be connected to a person's [[Social class|social class]], race, skin color, religious affiliation, ethnic origin, [[Caste|caste]], [[Education|educational status]], childhood relationships, [[Standard of living|living standards]], political opinions, and/or appearance. Anyone who appears to deviate in any way from the perceived norms of a population may thereby become subject to coarse or subtle forms of social exclusion.",
			},
		},
	},
