		description = "Half of Earth that is south of the Equator",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Southern Hemisphere** is the half ([[Hemispheres of Earth|hemisphere]]) of [[Earth]] that is south of the [[Equator|equator]]. It contains all or part of five [[Continent|continents]] (the whole of [[Antarctica]], the whole of [[Oceania]], about 90% of [[South America]], about one-third of [[Africa]], and some islands off the continental [[Mainland|mainland]] of [[Asia]]) and four [[Ocean|oceans]] (the whole [[Southern Ocean]], the majority of the [[Indian Ocean]], the [[Atlantic Ocean|South Atlantic Ocean]], and the [[Pacific Ocean|South Pacific Ocean]]), as well as [[New Zealand]] and most of the Pacific Islands in [[Oceania]]. Its surface is 80.9% water, compared with 60.7% water in the [[Northern Hemisphere]], and it contains 32.7% of Earth's land.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"More than 850 million people live in the Southern Hemisphere, representing around 10–12% of the total global human population. Owing to the tilt of [[Earth's rotation]] relative to the [[Sun]] and the [[Ecliptic|ecliptic plane]], [[Summer|summer]] is from December to February (inclusive) and [[Winter|winter]] is from June to August (inclusive). September 22 or 23 is the vernal [[Equinox|equinox]] and March 20 or 21 is the autumnal equinox. The [[South Pole]] is in the centre of the southern hemispherical region.",
