	{
		title = "Sovereign state",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "State having the highest authority over a territory",
		tags = { "Politics and government", "State structure and administration" },
		summary = 'A **sovereign state** is a [[State (polity)|state]] that has [[Sovereignty|sovereignty]], that is, the highest [[Authority|authority]] over a [[Territory|territory]]. It is commonly understood that [[Sovereignty|a sovereign state is independent]]. When referring to a specific [[Polity|polity]], the term "[[Country|country]]" may also refer to a constituent country, or a [[Dependent territory|dependent territory]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A sovereign [[State (polity)|state]] is required to have a permanent population, defined territory, a [[Government|government]] not under the control of another, and the capacity to [[International relations|interact with other sovereign states]]. In actual practice, recognition or non-recognition by other states plays an important role in determining the status of a country. Although the political existence of the sovereign state is independent of recognition by the other states, unrecognized states often have difficulty engaging in [[Diplomacy|diplomatic relations]] with other sovereign states.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sovereignty",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Supreme authority within a territory",
		tags = { "Politics and government", "State structure and administration" },
		summary = "**Sovereignty** is generally defined as supreme, [[Independence|independent]] control and lawmaking [[Authority|authority]] over a [[Territory|territory]]. It is expressed through the power to rule and make law. Sovereignty entails [[Hierarchy|hierarchy]] within a [[State (polity)|state]] as well as external autonomy, which refers to the ability of a state to act independently in international affairs. In any state, sovereignty is assigned to the person, body or institution that has the ultimate authority over its citizens and the power to modify existing laws. In [[Political philosophy|political theory]], sovereignty is a substantive term designating supreme legitimate [[Authority|authority]] over some [[Polity|polity]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Under the UN Charter, the Organization is based on the sovereign equality of its members, while Article 2(7) restricts UN intervention in matters essentially within a state's domestic jurisdiction but does not prejudice enforcement measures under Chapter VII. Under Article 42, the [[United Nations Security Council|Security Council]] may take action by air, sea or land forces as may be necessary when measures under Article 41 would be inadequate or have proved inadequate to maintain or restore international peace and security. A state is generally considered to have sovereignty over a territory when it has consistently exercised [[State (polity)|state]] authority there without objection from other states. [[De facto and de jure|De jure]] sovereignty refers to the legal right to do so; [[De facto and de jure|de facto]] sovereignty refers to the factual ability to do so.",
			},
		},
	},
