		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Spanish people" },
		summary = "**Spaniards**, or **Spanish people**, are an [[Ethnicity|ethnic group]] and nation indigenous to [[Spain]]. [[Genetics|Genetically]] and ethnolinguistically, Spaniards belong to the broader [[Southern Europe|Southern]] and [[Western Europe|Western European]] populations, exhibiting a high degree of continuity with other [[Indo-European languages|Indo-European]]-derived ethnic groups in the region. Spain is also home to a diverse array of national and regional identities, shaped by its complex [[History of Spain|history]]. These include various languages and dialects, many of which are direct descendants of [[Latin]], the language imposed during [[Hispania|Roman rule]]. Among them, [[Spanish language|Castilian]] is the most widely spoken and the only official language across the entire country.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Commonly spoken regional languages include, most notably, the sole surviving indigenous language of Iberia, [[Basque language|Basque]], as well as other Latin-descended [[Romance languages]] like Spanish itself, [[Catalan language|Catalan]] and Galician. Many populations outside Spain have ancestors who emigrated from Spain and share elements of Hispanic cultures. The most notable of these comprise [[Hispanic America]]. The [[Roman Republic]] conquered Iberia during the 2nd and 1st centuries BC. [[Hispania]], the name given to Iberia by the Romans as a province of the Empire, underwent a process of linguistic and cultural Romanization, and as such, the majority of local languages in Spain today, with the exception of [[Basque language|Basque]], evolved out of [[Vulgar Latin]] which was introduced by the [[Roman people|ancient Romans]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spanish America",
