	{
		title = "Speciation",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Evolutionary process",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Speciation** is the [[Evolution|evolutionary]] process by which [[Population (biology)|populations]] evolve to become distinct [[Species|species]]. The biologist Orator F. Cook coined the term in 1906 for cladogenesis, the splitting of lineages, as opposed to anagenesis, phyletic evolution within lineages. [[Charles Darwin]] was the first to describe the role of [[Evolution|natural selection]] in speciation in his 1859 book [[On the Origin of Species]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are four geographic modes of speciation in nature, based on the extent to which speciating [[Population (biology)|populations]] are isolated from one another: allopatric, peripatric, parapatric, and sympatric. Whether [[Genetic drift|genetic drift]] is a minor or major contributor to speciation is the subject of much ongoing discussion. Rapid sympatric speciation can take place through polyploidy, such as by doubling of chromosome number; the result is progeny which are immediately reproductively isolated from the parent population. New species can also be created through hybridization, followed by reproductive isolation, if the hybrid is favoured by natural selection.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Speculative fiction",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genre of fiction including science fiction, horror and fantasy",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Speculative fiction** is an umbrella [[Genre|genre]] of [[Fiction|fiction]] that encompasses all the subgenres that depart from [[Realism (arts)|realism]], or strictly imitating everyday [[Reality|reality]], instead presenting [[Fantasy|fantastical]], [[Supernatural|supernatural]], futuristic, or other highly imaginative realms or beings.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"This catch-all [[Genre|genre]] includes, but is not limited to: [[Fantasy|fantasy]], [[Science fiction|science fiction]], social science fiction, science fantasy, superhero, paranormal and supernatural [[Horror fiction|horror]], alternate history, [[Magical realism|magical realism]], slipstream, weird fiction, utopia, dystopia, and apocalyptic and post-apocalyptic fiction. In other words, the genre presents individuals, events, or places beyond the ordinary real world. The term speculative fiction has been used for works of [[Literature|literature]], [[Film|film]], [[Television show|television]], [[Drama|drama]], [[Video game|video games]], radio, and hybrid media.",
