	{
		title = "Speech",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Human vocal communication using language",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Speech** is the use of the [[Human voice|human voice]] as a medium for [[Language|language]]. [[Spoken language]] combines [[Vowel|vowel]] and [[Consonant|consonant]] sounds to form units of meaning like [[Word|words]], which belong to a language's [[Lexicon|lexicon]]. There are many different intentional [[Speech act|speech acts]], such as informing, declaring, asking, persuading, directing; acts may vary in various aspects like enunciation, intonation, [[Loudness|loudness]], and tempo to convey meaning.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While normally used to facilitate [[Communication|communication]] with others, people may also use speech without the intent to communicate. Speech may nevertheless express emotions or desires; people talk to themselves sometimes in acts that are a development of what some [[Psychologist|psychologists]] (e.g., [[Lev Vygotsky]]) have maintained is the use of silent speech in an interior monologue to vivify and organize [[Cognition|cognition]], sometimes in the momentary adoption of a dual persona as self addressing self as though addressing another person. Solo speech can be used to memorize or to test one's memorization of things, and in prayer or in meditation.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Linguistic typology" },
	},
	{
		title = "Speech act",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📈",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Utterance that serves a performative function",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		summary = 'In the [[Philosophy of language|philosophy of language]] and [[Linguistics|linguistics]], a **speech act** is something expressed by an individual that not only presents information but performs an action as well. The phrase "I resign" is a classic example of a speech act as the very act of saying the words enacts the resignation.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"According to Kent Bach, \"almost any speech act is really the performance of several acts at once, distinguished by different aspects of the speaker's intention: there is the act of saying something, what one does in saying it, such as requesting or promising, and how one is trying to affect one's audience\". The contemporary use of the term speech act goes back to J. L. Austin's development of performative utterances and his theory of locutionary, illocutionary, and perlocutionary acts. Speech acts serve their function once they are said or communicated. These are commonly taken to include acts such as apologizing, promising, ordering, answering, requesting, complaining, warning, inviting, refusing, and congratulating.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sperm",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🦠",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Male reproductive cell",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "Sperm cells contribute approximately half of the nuclear [[Gene|genetic information]] to the diploid offspring (excluding, in most cases, [[Mitochondrial DNA|mitochondrial DNA]]). Animals produce motile sperm with a tail known as a [[Flagellum|flagellum]], which are known as **spermatozoa**, while some [[Red algae|red algae]] and [[Mushroom|fungi]] produce non-motile sperm cells, known as **spermatia**. [[Flowering plant|Flowering plants]] contain non-motile sperm inside [[Pollen|pollen]], while some more basal plants like ferns and some [[Gymnosperm|gymnosperms]] have motile sperm.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'This process involves the production of several successive sperm cell precursors, starting with spermatogonia, which [[Cellular differentiation|differentiate]] into spermatocytes. The spermatocytes then undergo [[Meiosis|meiosis]], reducing their chromosome number by half, which produces spermatids. The spermatids then mature and, in animals, construct a tail, or flagellum, which gives rise to the mature, motile sperm cell. This whole process occurs constantly and takes around 3 months from start to finish. Sperm cells cannot divide and have a limited lifespan, but after fusion with egg cells during [[Fertilisation|fertilization]], a new organism begins developing, starting as a totipotent [[Zygote|zygote]]. The [[Human|human]] sperm cell is haploid, so that its 23 [[Chromosome|chromosomes]] can join the 23 chromosomes of the female egg to form a diploid cell with 46 paired chromosomes. The word sperm is derived from the Greek word, sperma, meaning "seed".',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spice trade",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🤝",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Historic international commerce",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **spice trade** involved historical civilizations in [[Asia]], [[Northeast Africa]] and [[Europe]]. Spices, such as [[Cinnamon|cinnamon]], cassia, [[Cardamom|cardamom]], [[Ginger|ginger]], [[Black pepper|pepper]], [[Nutmeg|nutmeg]], star anise, [[Clove|clove]], and turmeric, were known and used in antiquity and traded in the [[Eastern world|Eastern World]]. These spices found their way into the [[Near East]] before the beginning of the Christian era, with fantastic tales hiding their true sources.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The maritime aspect of the trade was dominated by the [[Austronesian peoples]] in [[Southeast Asia]], namely the ancient Indonesian sailors who established routes from Southeast Asia to Sri Lanka and India (and later China) by 1500 BC. These goods were then transported by land toward the Mediterranean and the [[Greco-Roman world]] via the incense route and the Roman–India routes by [[India|Indian]] and [[Iran|Persian]] traders. The Austronesian maritime trade lanes later expanded into the Middle East and East Africa by the 1st millennium AD, resulting in the Austronesian colonization of [[Madagascar]]. Within specific regions, [[Somalia]] (5th century BC – 11th century AD) had pioneered the [[Red Sea]] route before the 1st century AD. During the first millennium AD, Somalis became the maritime trading power of the [[Red Sea]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Henri Marie Ducrotay de Blainville" },
