		title = "Stained glass",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🥛",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Colored glass and works that are made from it",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '**Stained glass** refers to coloured [[Glass|glass]] as a material or art and architectural works created from it. Although it is traditionally made in flat panels and used as [[Window|windows]], the creations of modern stained glass artists also include three-dimensional structures and [[Sculpture|sculpture]]. Modern vernacular usage has often extended the term "stained glass" to include domestic lead light and objets d\'art created from glasswork, for example in the famous lamps of Louis Comfort Tiffany.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As a material, stained glass is glass that has been coloured by adding [[Salt (chemistry)|metallic salts]] during its manufacture. It may then be further decorated in various ways. The coloured glass may be crafted into a stained-glass window, in which small pieces of glass are arranged to form patterns or pictures, held together (traditionally) by strips of lead, called cames or calms, and supported by a rigid frame. [[Paint|Painted]] details and yellow-coloured silver stain are often used to enhance the design. The term stained glass is also applied to enamelled glass in which the colors have been painted onto the glass and then fused to the glass in a kiln. Stained glass, as an [[Art|art]] and a [[Craft|craft]], requires the artistic skill to conceive an appropriate and workable design, and the engineering skills to assemble the piece.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Standard Chinese",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Standard form of Mandarin Chinese",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Standard Chinese**, often colloquially called **Mandarin Chinese**, is the modern [[Standard language|standardized]] form of [[Mandarin Chinese]], largely based on the Beijing dialect. It is the [[Official language|official]] [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] of the [[China|People's Republic of China]], one of the six [[Official languages of the United Nations|official languages of the United Nations]], one of the national languages of Taiwan, and one of the four official languages of Singapore. It is a pluricentric language with local variations in [[Mainland China]], [[Taiwan]] and [[Singapore]], which mainly differ in [[Accent (sociolinguistics)|accent]] and [[Lexicon|lexicon]]. In Mainland China, Standard Chinese is most commonly called Putonghua, while in Taiwan it is most commonly called Guoyu.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Like other [[Sinitic languages]], Standard Chinese is an analytic language with mostly compound words, and has five [[Tone (linguistics)|phonemic tones]] (four classic tones and a neutral tone) with topic-prominent organization and subject–verb–object (SVO) word order. Compared with other [[Varieties of Chinese|varieties of Chinese]], Standard Chinese has fewer tones, [[Vowel|vowels]] and final [[Consonant|consonants]], but more initial consonants.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Spoken in", "[[China]]" },
			{ "Speakers", "[[First language|L1]] and [[Second language|L2]]: 80% of China" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Standard German",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Standardized variety of German language",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Standard High German", "High German" },
		summary = "**Standard German**, also **Standard High German** or **High German**, is the umbrella term for the [[Standard language|standardized varieties]] of the [[German language]], which are used in formal contexts and for communication between different dialect areas. German is a pluricentric Dachsprache with currently three codified (or standardised) specific national varieties: German Standard German, Austrian Standard German and Swiss Standard German.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Regarding the spelling and punctuation, a recommended standard is published by the Council for German Orthography which represents the governments of all majority and minority German-speaking countries and dependencies. Adherence is obligatory for government institutions, including schools. Although there is no official standards body regulating pronunciation, there is a long-standing [[De facto and de jure|de facto]] standard pronunciation (Bühnendeutsch), most commonly used in formal speech and teaching materials; it is similar to the formal German spoken in and around Hanover. Adherence to those standards by private individuals and companies, including the print and audio-visual media, is voluntary. Austrian German has had standard pronunciation exceptions since 1904 (Luick's österreichische Bühnenaussprache).",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Region", "German-speaking Europe" },
			{ "Date", "2019" },
			{ "Writing system", "[[Latin script]] (German alphabet) German Braille" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Standard language",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🗣️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Language variety with substantially codified usage",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Standard variety", "Standard dialect", "Standardized dialect" },
		summary = "A **standard language** (or **standard variety**, **standard dialect**, **standardized dialect** or simply **standard**) is any [[Variety (linguistics)|language variety]] that has undergone substantial codification in its [[Grammar|grammar]], [[Lexicon|lexicon]], [[Writing system|writing system]], or other features and that stands out among related varieties in a community as the one with the highest status or prestige, particularly in formal public and academic contexts. Often, it is the prestige language variety of a whole country.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In [[Linguistics|linguistics]], the process of a variety becoming organized into a standard, for instance by being widely expounded in grammar books or other reference works, and also the process of making people's language usage conform to that standard, is called **standardization**. Typically, the varieties that undergo standardization are those associated with centres of commerce and government, used frequently by educated people and in news broadcasting, and taught widely in schools and to non-native learners of the language.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Standard of living",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Measure of income, comfort and services",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Standard of living** is the level of income, comforts and services available to an individual, community or [[Society|society]]. A contributing factor to an individual's [[Quality of life|quality of life]], standard of living is generally concerned with objective metrics outside an individual's personal control, such as economic, societal, political, and environmental matters. Individuals or groups use the standard of living to evaluate where to live in the world, or when assessing the success of society.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In international law, an "adequate standard of living" was first described in the [[Universal Declaration of Human Rights]] and further described in the [[International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights]]. To evaluate the impact of policy for [[Sustainable development|sustainable development]], different disciplines have defined Decent Living Standards in order to evaluate or compare relative living experience. During much of its use in economics, improvements to standard of living were thought to be directly connected to economic growth, as well as increase amount of energy consumption and other materials. This allows for [[Climate change mitigation|climate change mitigation]] by demand reduction as well as other sustainable development practices.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Standard temperature and pressure",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Reference values for temperature and pressure",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "STP", "Standard conditions for temperature and pressure" },
		summary = "**Standard temperature and pressure** (**STP**), or **standard conditions for temperature and pressure**, are various standard sets of conditions for experimental measurements used to allow comparisons to be made between different sets of data. The most used standards are those of the [[International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry]] (IUPAC) and the [[National Institute of Standards and Technology]] (NIST), although these are not universally accepted. Other organizations have established a variety of other definitions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In industry and [[Commerce|commerce]], the standard conditions for temperature and pressure are often necessary for expressing the volumes of gases and liquids and related quantities such as the rate of volumetric flow (the volumes of gases vary significantly with temperature and pressure): standard cubic meters per second (Sm3/s), and normal cubic meters per second (Nm3/s). Many technical publications (books, journals, advertisements for equipment and machinery) simply state "standard conditions" without specifying them, often substituting the term with older "normal conditions", or "NC". In special cases this can lead to confusion and errors. Good practice always incorporates the reference conditions of temperature and pressure. If not stated, some [[Room temperature|room environment conditions]] are supposed, close to 1 [[Standard atmosphere (unit)|atm]] [[Pressure|pressure]], 25 °C, and 0% [[Humidity|humidity]].',
			},
		},
		related = { "Google Books" },
	},
	{
		title = "Standing wave",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "〰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Wave that remains in a constant position",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Stationary wave" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], a **standing wave**, also known as a **stationary wave**, is a [[Wave|wave]] that oscillates in time but whose peak amplitude profile does not move in space. The peak [[Amplitude|amplitude]] of the wave oscillations at any point in space is constant with respect to time, and the oscillations at different points throughout the wave are in phase. The locations at which the absolute value of the amplitude is minimum are called nodes, and the locations where the absolute value of the amplitude is maximum are called antinodes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Standing waves were first described scientifically by [[Michael Faraday]] in 1831. Faraday observed standing waves on the surface of a liquid in a vibrating container. This phenomenon can occur because the medium is moving in the direction opposite to the movement of the wave, or it can arise in a stationary medium as a result of interference between two waves traveling in opposite directions. The most common cause of standing waves is the phenomenon of [[Resonance|resonance]], in which standing waves occur inside a resonator due to interference between waves reflected back and forth at the resonator's resonant frequency. For waves of equal [[Amplitude|amplitude]] traveling in opposing directions, there is on [[Average|average]] no net [[Flux|propagation of energy]].",
			},
		},
	},
