		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '**Steam** is [[Water vapor|water vapor]], often mixed with [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]] and/or an [[Aerosol|aerosol]] of liquid [[Water|water]] droplets. This may occur by [[Evaporation|evaporation]] or by [[Boiling|boiling]], where heat is applied until water reaches the enthalpy of vaporization. Superheated or **saturated steam** is invisible; however, **wet steam**, a visible mist or aerosol of water droplets, is often referred to as "steam".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When liquid water becomes steam, it increases in [[Volume|volume]] by 1,700 times at [[Standard temperature and pressure|standard temperature and pressure]]; this change in volume can be converted into [[Work (physics)|mechanical work]] by [[Steam engine|steam engines]] such as reciprocating piston-type engines and [[Steam turbine|steam turbines]]. Piston-type steam engines played a central role in the [[Industrial Revolution]], and steam-based generation today produces 80 percent of the world's electricity. If liquid water comes into contact with a very hot surface or depressurizes quickly below its vapor pressure, it can create a steam explosion.",
