	{
		title = "Stratosphere",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Layer of the atmosphere above the troposphere",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Stratosphere** is the second-lowest layer of [[Atmosphere of Earth|Earth's atmosphere]], located above the [[Troposphere|troposphere]] and below the mesosphere. The stratosphere is composed of stratified [[Temperature|temperature]] zones, with the warmer layers of [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]] located higher (closer to [[Outer space|outer space]]) and the cooler layers lower (closer to the [[Planetary surface|planetary surface]] of the Earth). The increase of temperature with [[Altitude|altitude]] is a result of the absorption of the [[Sun]]'s [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] (UV) radiation by the [[Ozone layer|ozone layer]], where [[Ozone|ozone]] is exothermically photolyzed into [[Oxygen|oxygen]] in a cyclical fashion.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"This temperature inversion is in contrast to the troposphere, where temperature decreases with altitude, and between the troposphere and stratosphere is the tropopause border that demarcates the beginning of the temperature inversion. Near the [[Equator|equator]], the lower edge of the stratosphere is as high as 20 km, at mid-latitudes around 10 km, and at the [[Geographical pole|poles]] about 7 km. Temperatures range from an average of -51 °C near the tropopause to an average of -15 °C near the mesosphere. Stratospheric temperatures also vary within the stratosphere as the [[Season|seasons]] change, reaching particularly low temperatures in the polar night (winter). [[Wind|Winds]] in the stratosphere can far exceed those in the troposphere, reaching near 60 m/s in the Southern [[Polar vortex|polar vortex]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Stratovolcano",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌋",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of conical volcano composed of layers of lava and tephra",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Composite volcano" },
		summary = "A **stratovolcano**, also known as a **composite volcano**, is a typically conical [[Volcano|volcano]] built up by many alternating layers ([[Stratum|strata]]) of hardened [[Lava|lava]] and tephra. Unlike shield volcanoes, stratovolcanoes are characterized by a steep profile with a summit crater and explosive eruptions. Some have collapsed summit craters called [[Caldera|calderas]]. The lava flowing from stratovolcanoes typically cools and solidifies before spreading far, due to high [[Viscosity|viscosity]]. The [[Magma|magma]] forming this lava is often felsic, having high to intermediate levels of [[Silicon dioxide|silica]] (as in rhyolite, dacite, or andesite), with lesser amounts of less viscous mafic [[Magma|magma]]. Extensive felsic lava flows are uncommon, but can travel as far as 8 km.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term composite volcano is used because strata are usually mixed and uneven instead of neat layers. They are among the most common types of volcanoes; more than 700 stratovolcanoes have erupted lava during the Holocene Epoch (the last 11,700 years), and many older, now extinct, stratovolcanoes erupted lava as far back as [[Archean]] times. Stratovolcanoes are typically found in [[Subduction|subduction zones]] but they also occur in other geological settings. Zephyria Tholus is one of two mountains in the Aeolis region of Mars that have been proposed as possible stratovolcanoes.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Research and development" },
	},
