		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'In [[Literary criticism|literary criticism]], **stream of consciousness** is a narrative mode or method that attempts "to depict the multitudinous thoughts and feelings which pass through the mind" of a narrator. It is usually in the form of an interior monologue which is disjointed or has irregular punctuation. While critics have pointed to various literary precursors, it was not until the 20th century that this technique was fully developed by [[Modernism|modernist]] writers such as [[Marcel Proust]], [[James Joyce]], Dorothy Richardson and [[Virginia Woolf]].',
		sections = {
			{
