	{
		title = "Streaming media",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Multimedia delivery method",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Streaming media** is the method by which [[Multimedia|multimedia]] data is delivered through a [[Computer network|network]] for playback using a digital media player or media player software. Media is transferred in a stream of packets from a [[Server (computing)|server]] to a client and is rendered in real-time or near real-time; this contrasts with file downloading, a process in which the end-user obtains an entire media file before consuming the content. Streaming is more commonly used for video on demand, [[Streaming television|streaming television]], and music streaming services over the Internet.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While streaming is most commonly associated with multimedia from a remote server over the Internet, it also includes offline multimedia between devices on a local area network. For example, using DLNA and a home server, or in a personal area network between two devices using Bluetooth (which uses [[Radio wave|radio waves]] rather than IP). Online streaming was initially popularized by RealNetworks and [[Microsoft]] in the 1990s and has since grown to become the globally most popular method for consuming music and videos, with numerous competing subscription services being offered since the 2010s. Audio streaming to wireless speakers, often using Bluetooth, is another use that has become prevalent during that decade. Live streaming is the real-time delivery of content during production, much as live television broadcasts content via linear and cable channels.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Streaming television",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📺",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Distribution of television content via the public internet",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Streaming television** is the digital distribution of [[Television|television]] media content, such as [[Television show|series]] and films, over [[Internet]]-based [[Streaming media|streaming media]] platforms. In contrast to over-the-air, [[Cable television|cable]], and [[Satellite television|satellite]] transmissions, or IPTV service, streaming television is provided as over-the-top media (OTT). Television content includes productions made by or for OTT services, and acquired by them with licensing agreements. The length of a streaming television series episode can be anywhere from 30 to 60 minutes (some episodes may be longer). Similar to the running time of theatrical films, the average length of a film licensed or produced by a streaming platform is 90 to 120 minutes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Streaming television is available via paid subscription or as free ad-supported service. By 2023, streaming television represented 38% of global TV viewing with 1.8 billion subscriptions to streaming platforms. In 2024, streaming television became "the dominant form of TV viewing" in the United States. It surpassed cable and network television viewing in 2025. Of the top streaming platforms, [[Netflix]] had over 325 million subscribers as of December 2025, making it the most popular global streaming television service.',
			},
