	{
		title = "Sunlight",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Light emitted by the Sun",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '**Sunlight** is the portion of the [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]] which is emitted by the [[Sun]] (i.e. solar radiation) and received by the [[Earth]], in particular the [[Visible spectrum|visible]] light perceptible to the [[Human eye|human eye]] as well as invisible [[Infrared|infrared]] (typically perceived by humans as warmth) and [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] (which can have physiological effects such as sunburn) lights. However, according to the American Meteorological Society, there are "conflicting conventions as to whether all three [...] are referred to as light, or whether that term should only be applied to the visible portion of the spectrum".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Upon reaching the Earth, sunlight is scattered and filtered through the [[Atmosphere of Earth|Earth's atmosphere]] as daylight when the Sun is above the [[Horizon|horizon]]. When direct solar radiation is not blocked by [[Cloud|clouds]], it is experienced as **sunshine**, a combination of bright [[Light|light]] and radiant heat (atmospheric). When blocked by clouds or reflected off other objects, sunlight is diffused. Sources estimate a global average of between 164 watts to 340 watts per square meter over a 24-hour day; this figure is estimated by NASA to be about a quarter of Earth's average total solar irradiance. Sunlight takes about 8.3 minutes to reach Earth from the surface of the Sun. A photon starting at the center of the Sun and changing direction every time it encounters a [[Charged particle|charged particle]] would take between 10,000 and 170,000 years to get to the surface.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Supercontinent",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Landmass comprising more than one continental core, or craton",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'In [[Geology|geology]], a **supercontinent** is the assembly of most or all of [[Earth]]\'s [[Continent|continental blocks]] or cratons to form a single large [[Landmass|landmass]]. However, some geologists use a different definition, "a grouping of formerly dispersed continents", which leaves room for interpretation and is easier to apply to [[Precambrian]] times. To separate supercontinents from other groupings, a limit has been proposed in which a continent must include at least about 75% of the continental crust then in existence in order to qualify as a supercontinent.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Moving under the forces of [[Plate tectonics|plate tectonics]], supercontinents have assembled and dispersed multiple times in the geologic past. According to modern definitions, a supercontinent does not exist today; the closest is the current [[Afro-Eurasia|Afro-Eurasian]] landmass, which covers approximately 57% of Earth's total land area. The last period in which the continental landmasses were near to one another was 336 to 175 million years ago, forming the supercontinent [[Pangaea]]. The positions of continents have been accurately determined back to the early [[Jurassic]], shortly before the breakup of Pangaea. Pangaea's predecessor [[Gondwana]] is not considered a supercontinent under the first definition since the landmasses of Baltica, Laurentia and Siberia were separate at the time. A future supercontinent, termed Pangaea Proxima, is hypothesized to form within the next 250 million years.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Supernatural",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚖️",
