	{
		title = "Syllable",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Unit of speech sounds",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		summary = 'A **syllable** is a basic unit of organization within a sequence of speech sounds, such as within a word. In [[Phonology|phonology]] and studies of languages, syllables are often considered the "building blocks" of words. A syllable usually consists of a nucleus (most often a [[Vowel|vowel]]), which may be preceded by an onset and followed by a coda (collectively margins, which are most often [[Consonant|consonants]]). Syllables may bear properties such as [[Stress (linguistics)|stress]] and [[Tone (linguistics)|tone]] and be subject to operations such as reduplication. Speech can usually be divided up into a whole number of syllables: for example, the word ignite is made of two syllables: ig and nite.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Most languages of the world use relatively simple syllable structures that often alternate between vowels and consonants. Despite being present in virtually all human languages, syllables still have no precise definition that is valid for all known languages. A common criterion for finding syllable boundaries is native-speaker intuition, but individuals sometimes disagree on them. The earliest attested instances of syllabic writing are on tablets written around 2800 BC in the [[Sumer|Sumerian]] city of [[Ur]], which predate the earliest instances of alphabetic writing by several centuries. The shift from pictographic writing to syllabic writing has been called "the most important advance in the [[History of writing|history of writing]]". A word that consists of a single syllable (like [[English language|English]] dog) is called a monosyllable (and is said to be monosyllabic).',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Symbol",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Something that represents an idea, process, or physical entity",
		tags = { "Language", "Basics" },
		summary = 'A **symbol** is a mark, sign, or [[Word|word]] that indicates, signifies, or is understood as representing an [[Idea|idea]], [[Physical object|object]], relationship, or mathematical formula. Symbols allow people to go beyond what is known or seen by creating linkages between otherwise different [[Concept|concepts]] and experiences. All [[Communication|communication]] is achieved through the use of symbols: for example, a red octagon is a common symbol for "STOP"; on [[Map|maps]], blue lines often represent rivers; and a red rose often symbolizes love and compassion. [[Numerical digit|Numerals]] are symbols for [[Number|numbers]]; [[Letter (alphabet)|letters]] of an [[Alphabet|alphabet]] may be symbols for certain [[Phoneme|phonemes]]; and personal [[Name|names]] are symbols representing individuals. The academic study of symbols is called [[Semiotics|semiotics]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In the arts, [[Artistic symbol|symbolism]] is the use of a concrete element to represent a more abstract idea. In [[Cartography|cartography]], an organized collection of symbols forms a legend for a map.",
			},
		},
