		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Group of transformations under which the object is invariant",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Group theory|group theory]], the **symmetry group** of a geometric object is the [[Group (mathematics)|group]] of all transformations under which the object is invariant, endowed with the group operation of composition. Such a transformation is an invertible mapping of the ambient space which takes the object to itself, and which preserves all the relevant structure of the object. A frequent notation for the symmetry group of an object X is G = Sym(X).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"For an object in a metric space, its symmetries form a subgroup of the isometry group of the ambient space. This article mainly considers [[Symmetry|symmetry]] groups in [[Euclidean geometry]], but the concept may also be studied for more general types of geometric structure.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Syncretism",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Combination of distinct beliefs or schools of thought",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Syncretism** is the combining or merging of various distinct beliefs or schools of thought, particularly religious ones. For instance, features and components of one religion are incorporated and absorbed into another. It can include assimilation of several originally discrete [[Tradition|traditions]], especially in theology and [[Mythology|mythology]], thus asserting an underlying unity and allowing for an inclusive approach to multiple faiths.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While syncretism in art and culture is sometimes likened to [[Eclecticism|eclecticism]], in the realm of religion, it specifically denotes a more integrated merging of beliefs into a unified system, distinct from eclecticism, which implies a selective adoption of elements from different traditions without necessarily blending them into a new, cohesive belief system.",
			},
