	{
		title = "Terrain",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Dimension and shape of land surfaces",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Topographical relief" },
		summary = "**Terrain**, alternatively **relief** or **topographical relief**, is the [[Dimension|dimension]] and shape of a given surface of a [[Land|land]]. In [[Physical geography|physical geography]], terrain is the lay of the land. This is usually expressed in terms of the [[Elevation|elevation]], slope, and orientation of terrain features. Terrain affects surface water flow and distribution. Over a large area, it can affect [[Weather|weather]] and [[Climate|climate]] patterns. [[Bathymetry]] is the study of underwater relief, while hypsometry studies terrain relative to [[Sea level|sea level]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Terrestrial ecosystem",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ecological system found on land",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Terrestrial ecosystems" },
		summary = "**Terrestrial ecosystems** are [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]] that are found on land. Examples include [[Tundra|tundra]], [[Taiga|taiga]], temperate deciduous forest, tropical rain forest, [[Grassland|grassland]], [[Desert|deserts]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Terrestrial ecosystems differ from [[Aquatic ecosystem|aquatic ecosystems]] by the predominant presence of [[Soil|soil]] rather than water at the surface and by the extension of plants above this soil/water surface in terrestrial ecosystems. There is a wide range of water availability among terrestrial ecosystems (including water scarcity in some cases), whereas water is seldom a limiting factor to organisms in aquatic ecosystems. Because water buffers temperature fluctuations, terrestrial ecosystems usually experience greater diurnal and seasonal temperature fluctuations than do aquatic ecosystems in similar climates. Terrestrial ecosystems are of particular importance especially in meeting Sustainable Development Goal 15 that targets the conservation-restoration and sustainable use of terrestrial ecosystems.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Territories of the United States",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Territories of the United States** are subnational geographical and political areas governed as [[Administrative division|administrative divisions]] and dependent territories under the [[Sovereign state|sovereignty]] of the [[United States]]. Although all are subject to the constitutional and territorial [[Jurisdiction|jurisdiction]] of the U.S. federal government, territories differ from [[U.S. state|states]] and Indian reservations in that they are not inherently sovereign. While states have dual sovereignty and Native American tribes have tribal sovereignty in relation to the federal government, the self-governing powers of territories ultimately derive from the U.S. Congress, as per the Territorial Clause in Article IV of the U.S. Constitution.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Territories are classified as "organized" or "unorganized" depending on whether they operate under an organic act, and "incorporated" or "unincorporated" depending on whether the U.S. Constitution applies fully or partially to them. As areas belonging to, but not integral parts of, the U.S., territories are their own distinct [[Nation|nations]] centered around a collective [[Identity (social science)|identity]] based on their [[Border|land]], [[History|history]], [[Ethnicity|ethnicity]], [[Culture|culture]], and [[Language|language]]. All territories of the U.S. are insular areas. The U.S. has sovereignty over three [[Archipelago|archipelagos]] or islands in the [[Caribbean Sea]] and eleven in the [[Pacific Ocean]].',
			},
		},
		related = { "Albert Medal (Royal Society of Arts)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Languages", "[[American English|English]], Spanish, Samoan, Chamorro, Carolinian" },
			{ "People are called", "American" },
			{ "Area", "22294.19 km²" },
			{ "Population", "3,623,895" },
			{ "Currency", "[[United States dollar]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Territory",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Area of land under a jurisdiction",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **territory** is an area of land, sea, or space, belonging or connected to a particular country, person, or animal.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In [[International relations|international politics]], a territory is usually a geographic area which has not been granted the powers of self-government, i.e. an area that is under the [[Jurisdiction|jurisdiction]] of a [[Sovereign state|sovereign state]]. As a subdivision, a territory in most [[Country|countries]] is an organized division of an area that is controlled by a country but is not formally developed into, or incorporated into, a [[Politics|political unit]] of that country, which political units are of equal status to one another and are often referred to by words such as "provinces", "regions", or "states". In its narrower sense, it is "a geographic region, such as a colonial possession, that is dependent on an external government."',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tertiary education",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎓",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Third stage of education for adults",
		tags = { "Education" },
		aliases = { "Higher education", "Post-secondary education" },
		summary = "**Tertiary education** (also called **higher education** or **post-secondary education**) is the educational level following the completion of [[Secondary education|secondary education]]. The [[World Bank Group|World Bank]] defines tertiary education as including universities, colleges, and vocational schools. Higher education is taken to include undergraduate and [[Postgraduate education|postgraduate education]], while vocational education beyond secondary education is known as further education in the [[United Kingdom]], or included under the category of continuing education in the [[United States]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Tertiary education generally culminates in the receipt of certificates, diplomas, or [[Academic degree|academic degrees]]. Higher education represents levels 5, 6, 7, and 8 of the 2011 version of the International Standard Classification of Education structure. Tertiary education at a nondegree level is sometimes referred to as further education or continuing education as distinct from higher education. [[UNESCO]] stated that tertiary education focuses on learning endeavors in specialized fields. It includes academic and higher vocational education. The [[World Bank Group|World Bank]]'s 2019 World Development Report on the future of work argues that, given the future of work and the increasing role of technology in value chains, tertiary education is becoming even more relevant for workers to compete in the labor market.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tertiary sector",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Service sector",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Service sector", "Tertiary sector of the economy" },
		summary = 'In [[Economics|economics]], the **tertiary sector** (also known as the **service sector**) is the economic sector which comprises the provision of [[Service (economics)|services]] as opposed to the manufacture of finished goods. Services (also known as "intangible goods") include attention, advice, access, experience and affective labour.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The tertiary sector involves the provision of services to other businesses as well as to final consumers. Services may involve the [[Transport|transport]], distribution and sale of goods from a producer to a consumer, as may happen in wholesaling and retailing, pest control or [[Financial services|financial services]]. The goods may be transformed in the process of providing the service, as happens in the [[Restaurant|restaurant]] industry. However, the focus is on people by interacting with them and serving the customers rather than transforming the physical goods. The production of information has been long regarded as a service, but some economists now attribute it to a fourth sector, called the quaternary sector.",
			},
		},
	},
