		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Kra–Dai language",
		tags = { "Specific languages", "Other languages" },
		aliases = { "Thai", "Central Thai" },
		summary = "**Thai**, or **Central Thai**, is a Tai language of the [[Kra–Dai languages|Kra–Dai]] [[Language family|language family]] spoken by the Central Thai, [[Mon people|Mon]], Lao Wiang, and Phuan people in Central Thailand and the vast majority of Thai Chinese enclaves throughout the country. It is the sole [[Official language|official language]] of [[Thailand]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Thai is the most spoken of over 60 languages of Thailand by both number of native and overall speakers. Over half of its vocabulary is derived from or borrowed from [[Pali]], [[Sanskrit]], [[Mon language|Mon]] and Old Khmer. It is a tonal and analytic language. Thai has a complex [[Orthography|orthography]] and system of relational markers. Spoken Thai, depending on standard [[Sociolinguistics|sociolinguistic]] factors such as age, gender, class, spatial proximity, and the urban/rural divide, is partly [[Mutual intelligibility|mutually intelligible]] with [[Lao language|Lao]], Isan, and some fellow Thai topolects. These languages are written with slightly different scripts, but are linguistically similar and effectively form a [[Dialect continuum|dialect continuum]]. The Thai language is spoken by over 70 million people in Thailand as of 2024.",
