		summary = "**Traditional Chinese characters** are one of two standardized [[Chinese characters|character sets]] used to write the [[Chinese language]], with the other being [[Simplified Chinese characters|simplified characters]]. Traditional characters predominated in written Chinese, the historical [[Lingua franca|lingua franca]] of [[East Asia]], until the mid-20th century. Today, they are used in [[Taiwan]], [[Hong Kong]], and [[Macau]], as well as in most [[Overseas Chinese|overseas Chinese]] communities outside of Southeast Asia.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In the mid-20th century, [[China|People\'s Republic of China]] (PRC) began standardizing simplified Chinese, mostly with characters that existed before as variants, sometimes merging previously distinct character forms. "Traditional" as such is a retronym applied to non-simplified character. Korean [[Hanja|hanja]] remain virtually identical to traditional Chinese characters. Despite the debates on traditional and simplified Chinese, the two scripts are mutually intelligible to most native speakers, and many Chinese-language platforms allow users to switch between them.',
