		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Transcendental idealism** is a philosophical system founded by German [[Philosophy|philosopher]] [[Immanuel Kant]] in the 18th century. Kant's [[Epistemology|epistemological]] program is found throughout his [[Critique of Pure Reason]] (1781). By transcendental (a term that deserves special clarification) Kant means that his philosophical approach to knowledge transcends mere consideration of [[Sense|sensory]] evidence (the hallmark of the empiricist philosophers who immediately preceded him) and requires an understanding of the [[Mind|mind]]'s innate modes of processing that sensory evidence.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In the "Transcendental Aesthetic" section of the Critique of Pure Reason, Kant outlines how [[Space|space]] and [[Time|time]] are pure forms of human [[Intuition|intuition]] contributed by our own faculty of sensibility. Space and time do not have an existence "outside" of us, but are the "subjective" forms of our sensibility and hence the necessary a priori conditions under which the objects we encounter in our experience can appear to us at all. Kant describes time and space not only as "[[Empiricism|empirically]] real" but transcendentally ideal. Kant argues that the conscious subject recognizes the objects of experience not as they are in themselves, but only the way they [[Phenomenon|appear to us]] under the conditions of our [[Perception|sensibility]].',
			},
