	{
		title = "Varangians",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Slavic and Greek designation of Vikings",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Varangians** were [[Vikings|Viking]] warriors, traders and settlers, mostly from present-day [[Sweden]], who settled in the territories of present-day [[Belarus]], [[Russia]] and [[Ukraine]] from the 8th and 9th centuries and established the state of [[Kievan Rus']] as well as the principalities of Polotsk and Turov. They also formed the [[Byzantine Empire|Byzantine]] Varangian Guard.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"According to the 12th-century Primary Chronicle, a group of Varangians known as the Rus' settled in Novgorod in 862 under the leadership of Rurik. Before Rurik, the Rus' might have ruled an earlier hypothetical polity known as the Rus' Khaganate. Rurik's relative Oleg conquered [[Kyiv|Kiev]] in 882 and established the state of Kievan Rus', which was later ruled by Rurik's descendants. Engaging in trade, piracy, and mercenary service, Varangians roamed the river systems and portages of Gardariki, as the areas north of the [[Black Sea]] were known in the Norse sagas. They controlled the Volga trade route (between the Varangians and the Muslims), connecting the [[Baltic Sea|Baltic]] to the [[Caspian Sea]] and the Dnieper and Dniester trade route (between Varangians and the Greeks) leading to the Black Sea and [[Constantinople]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Varieties of Chinese",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "There are hundreds of local [[Chinese language]] varieties forming a branch of the [[Sino-Tibetan languages|Sino-Tibetan language family]], many of which are not [[Mutual intelligibility|mutually intelligible]]. Variation is particularly strong in the more mountainous southeast part of mainland China. The varieties are typically classified into ten primary groups: [[Mandarin Chinese|Mandarin]], Jin, [[Wu Chinese|Wu]], Hui, [[Min Chinese|Min]], Xiang, Gan, [[Hakka Chinese|Hakka]], [[Yue Chinese|Yue]], and Pinghua though some varieties remain unclassified. These groups are neither [[Clade|clades]] nor individual languages defined by mutual intelligibility, but are identified by common correspondences with selected features of [[Middle Chinese]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Chinese varieties differ in their [[Phonology|phonology]], [[Vocabulary|vocabulary]] and [[Syntax|syntax]]. Southern varieties tend to have fewer initial consonants than northern and central varieties, but more often preserve the Middle Chinese final consonants. All have phonemic [[Tone (linguistics)|tones]], with northern varieties tending to have fewer distinctions than southern ones. Many have tone sandhi, with the most complex patterns in the coastal area from [[Zhejiang]] to eastern [[Guangdong]]. There are major lexical and grammatical differences between northern and southern varieties, but often some northern areas have features found in the south, and vice versa. [[Standard Chinese]] takes its phonology from the Beijing dialect, with vocabulary from the Mandarin group and grammar based on literature in the [[Written vernacular Chinese|modern written vernacular]]. It is the official language of China, Taiwan and one of the four official languages of [[Singapore]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Region", "[[China]], [[Taiwan]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Variety (botany)",
