	{
		title = "Vassal",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Person aligned with a lord or monarch",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Liege subject" },
		summary = "A **vassal** or **liege subject** is a person regarded as having a mutual obligation to a lord or [[Monarch|monarch]], in the context of the [[Feudalism|feudal]] system in [[Middle Ages|medieval Europe]] and elsewhere. While the subordinate party is called a vassal, the dominant party is called a [[Suzerainty|suzerain]]. The rights and obligations of a vassal are called **vassalage**, while the rights and obligations of a suzerain are called [[Suzerainty|suzerainty]]. The obligations of a vassal often included military support by knights in exchange for certain privileges, usually including land held as a tenant or fief.",
		sections = { { "Overview", "In contrast, fealty (fidelitas) is sworn, unconditional loyalty to a monarch." } },
		related = { "Korean Wave" },
	},
	{
		title = "Vaudeville",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Entertainment genre",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Vaudeville** is a [[Theatre|theatrical]] genre of variety entertainment which became popular in the United States and Canada from the early 1870s until the early 1930s.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In some ways analogous to music hall from [[Victorian era|Victorian United Kingdom]], a typical North American vaudeville performance was made up of a series of separate, unrelated acts grouped together on a common bill. Types of acts have included [[Popular music|popular]] and [[Classical music|classical]] musicians, singers, dancers, comedians, trained animals, [[Magic (illusion)|magicians]], ventriloquists, strongmen, female and male impersonators, acrobats, [[Clown|clowns]], [[Mime artist|mimes]], illustrated songs, jugglers, athletes, lecturing celebrities, or scenes from [[Play (theatre)|plays]], one-act plays, minstrels, and films. A vaudeville performer is often referred to as a vaudevillian. Vaudeville developed from many sources, including the concert saloon, minstrelsy, freak shows, dime museums, and literary American burlesque.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Vector field",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "➡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Assignment of a vector to each point in a subset of Euclidean space",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A vector field on a [[Euclidean plane|plane]] can be visualized as a collection of arrows with given magnitudes and directions, each attached to a point on the plane. Vector fields often have [[Unit of measurement|unit of measurement]] (for example, metres or kilometres per hour), forming a vector physical quantity. They may be used to model, for example, the speed and direction of a moving fluid throughout [[Three-dimensional space|three dimensional space]], such as the [[Wind|wind]], or the strength and direction of some [[Force|force]], such as the [[Magnetic field|magnetic]] or [[Gravity|gravitational]] force, as it changes from one point to another point.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The elements of differential and integral calculus extend naturally to vector fields. When a vector field represents [[Force|force]], the line integral of a vector field represents the [[Work (physics)|work]] done by a force moving along a path, and under this interpretation [[Conservation of energy|conservation of energy]] is exhibited as a special case of the [[Fundamental theorem of calculus|fundamental theorem of calculus]]. Vector fields can usefully be thought of as representing the velocity of a moving flow in space, and this physical intuition leads to notions such as the divergence (which represents the rate of change of [[Volume|volume]] of a flow) and curl (which represents the rotation of a flow). A vector field is a special case of a vector-valued function, whose domain's dimension has no relation to the dimension of its range; for example, the position vector of a space curve is defined only for smaller subset of the ambient space.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Vector quantity",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "➡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physical quantity that is a vector",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Vector physical quantity", "Physical vector" },
		summary = "In the [[Natural science|natural sciences]], a **vector quantity** (also known as a **vector physical quantity**, **physical vector**, or simply **vector**) is a [[Vector (mathematics and physics)|vector]]-valued [[Physical quantity|physical quantity]]. It is typically formulated as the product of a [[Unit of measurement|unit of measurement]] and a vector numerical value (unitless), often a [[Euclidean vector]] with magnitude and [[Direction (geometry)|direction]]. For example, a position vector in physical space may be expressed as three [[Cartesian coordinate system|Cartesian coordinates]] with SI unit of meters.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In [[Physics|physics]] and [[Engineering|engineering]], particularly in [[Mechanics|mechanics]], a physical vector may be endowed with additional structure compared to a geometrical vector. A **bound vector** is defined as the combination of an ordinary vector quantity and a point of application or point of action. Bound vector quantities are formulated as a directed line segment, with a definite initial point besides the magnitude and direction of the main vector. For example, a [[Force|force]] on the [[Euclidean plane]] has two Cartesian components in SI unit of newtons (describing the magnitude and direction of the force) and an accompanying two-dimensional position vector in meters (describing the point of application of the force), for a total of four numbers on the plane (and six in space).",
			},
		},
		related = { "Forest Lawn Memorial Park (Glendale, California)" },
	},
