	{
		title = "Verb",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Part of speech that conveys an action",
		tags = { "Basics", "Grammar" },
		summary = "A **verb** is a [[Word|word]] that generally conveys an action (bring, read, walk, run, learn), an occurrence (happen, become), or a state of being (be, exist, stand). In the usual description of [[English language|English]], the basic form, with or without the particle to, is the infinitive. In many [[Language|languages]], verbs are [[Inflection|inflected]] (modified in form) to encode [[Grammatical tense|tense]], aspect, mood, and voice. A verb may also agree with the person, [[Grammatical gender|gender]] or number of some of its arguments, such as its subject, or object.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In English, three tenses exist: present, to indicate that an action is being carried out; past, to indicate that an action has been done; and future, to indicate that an action will be done, expressed with the auxiliary verb will or shall. Lucy will go to school. (action, future) Mike Trout became the American League (AL) Most Valuable Player (MVP) in 2014. (occurrence, past) the [[Universe]] is expanding. (state of being, present) Every language discovered so far makes some form of [[Noun|noun]]-verb distinction, possibly because of the graph-like nature of communicated meaning by humans, i.e. nouns being the "entities" and verbs being the "links" between them.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Vernacular",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Common speech variety of a specific population",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Vernacular language" },
		summary = "**Vernacular** is the ordinary, informal, [[Spoken language|spoken]] form of a language or dialect. The term particularly carries the perception of a language variety with lower [[Social status|social status]] or less prestige than [[Standard language|standard language]], which is more codified, institutionally promoted, [[Literary language|literary]], or formal. More narrowly, any particular variety of a language that does not hold a widespread high-status perception, and sometimes even carries social stigma, is also called a **vernacular**, **vernacular dialect**, **nonstandard dialect**, etc. and is typically its speakers' native variety.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Regardless of any such stigma, all nonstandard dialects are full-fledged varieties of language with their own consistent grammatical structure, [[Phonology|sound system]], body of vocabulary, etc. Less commonly, vernacular can also refer to written language that intentionally reflects or imitates the aforementioned form of spoken language.",
			},
		},
		related = { "The World Factbook" },
	},
	{
