	{
		title = "Volatile (astrogeology)",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Elements and compounds that are readily vaporized",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Volatiles" },
		summary = "**Volatiles** are the group of [[Chemical element|chemical elements]] and [[Chemical compound|chemical compounds]] that can be readily [[Volatility (chemistry)|vaporized]]. In contrast with volatiles, elements and compounds that are not readily vaporized are known as refractory substances.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"On planet Earth, the term often refers to the volatile components of [[Magma|magma]]. In astrogeology, volatiles are investigated in the [[Crust (geology)|crust]] or [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]] of a planet or moon. Volatiles include [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]], [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]], [[Oxygen|oxygen]], [[Water|water]], [[Ammonia|ammonia]], [[Methane|methane]], carbon dioxide, [[Nitrogen dioxide|nitrogen dioxide]], sulfur dioxide, [[Hydrogen sulfide|hydrogen sulfide]], phosphine, halogens, noble gases and others.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Volatility (chemistry)",
		category = "Concept",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tendency of a substance to vaporize",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Volatility" },
		summary = "In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], **volatility** is a material quality which describes how readily a substance vaporizes. At a given [[Temperature|temperature]] and [[Pressure|pressure]], a substance with high volatility is more likely to exist as a vapour, while a substance with low volatility is more likely to be a [[Liquid|liquid]] or [[Solid|solid]]. Volatility can also describe the tendency of a vapor to condense into a liquid or solid; less volatile substances will more readily condense from a vapor than highly volatile ones. Differences in volatility can be observed by comparing how fast substances within a group evaporate (or [[Sublimation|sublimate]] in the case of solids) when exposed to the atmosphere.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In general, solids are much less volatile than liquids, but there are some exceptions. Solids that sublimate (change directly from solid to vapor) such as dry ice (solid carbon dioxide) or [[Iodine|iodine]] can vaporize at a similar rate as some liquids under standard conditions.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Songwriters Hall of Fame" },
