		summary = "**Western European Time** (**WET**, UTC+00:00) is a [[Time zone|time zone]] covering parts of western [[Europe]] and consists of countries using UTC+00:00 (also known as [[Greenwich Mean Time]], abbreviated GMT). It is one of the three standard time zones in the [[European Union]] along with [[Central European Time]] and [[Eastern European Time]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The following Western European countries and regions use UTC+00:00 in winter months: [[Portugal]], since 1912 with pauses (except [[Azores]], UTC−01:00) [[United Kingdom]] and Crown Dependencies, since 1847 in [[England]], [[Scotland]], [[Wales]], the [[Channel Islands]], and the [[Isle of Man]], and since 1916 in [[Northern Ireland]], with pauses [[Republic of Ireland|Ireland]], since 1916, except between 1968 and 1971 [[Canary Islands]], since 1946 (rest of Spain is [[Central European Time|CET]], UTC+01:00) [[Faroe Islands]], since 1908 North Eastern [[Greenland]] (Danmarkshavn and surrounding area) [[Iceland]], since 1968, without summer time changes All the above countries except Iceland implement [[Daylight saving time|daylight saving time]] in summer (from the last Sunday in March to the last Sunday in October each year), switching to [[Western European Summer Time]] (WEST, UTC+01:00), which is one hour ahead of WET.",
