		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Western civilization", "European civilization", "Occidental culture" },
		summary = '**Western culture**, also known as **Western civilization**, **European civilization**, **Occidental culture**, **Western society** or simply **the West**, is the externally cohesive yet internally diverse [[Culture|culture]] and [[Cultural heritage|cultural heritage]] of the [[Western world]]. The term "Western" encompasses the social norms, ethical values, [[Tradition|traditional customs]], belief systems, political systems, artifacts and [[Technology|technologies]] primarily rooted in [[History of Europe|European]] and [[History of the Mediterranean region|Mediterranean]] histories. A broad concept, "Western culture" does not relate to a region with fixed members or geographical confines.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'It generally refers to the classical era cultures of [[Ancient Greece|ancient Greece]], [[Roman Empire|ancient Rome]], and their Christian successors that expanded across the [[Mediterranean basin]] and [[Europe]], and later circulated around the world predominantly through [[Colonization|colonization]] and [[Globalization|globalization]]. The exact English phrase “Western civilization” appears to have become common in the late 1800s. One study notes that it was being used without special explanation by at least 1890, while earlier 19th century English-language writers generally talked about “European civilization”. Previous to that, "Christendom" was the only widely used umbrella concept for centuries. Historically, scholars have closely associated the idea of Western culture with the [[Classical antiquity|classical era]] of [[Greco-Roman world|Greco-Roman antiquity]].',
			},
		},
