		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Westminster model", "Westminster parliamentary system" },
		summary = "The **Westminster system**, or **Westminster model**, is a type of [[Parliamentary system|parliamentary]] government found in, and elsewhere derived from, the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]]. Key aspects of the system include an executive branch made up of members of the legislature which is responsible to the legislature, the presence of parliamentary opposition parties, and a ceremonial [[Head of state|head of state]] who is separate from the [[Head of government|head of government]]. The term derives from the [[Palace of Westminster]], the seat of the British parliament. The Westminster system can be contrasted with the [[Presidential system|presidential system]], which originated in the [[United States]], and with the semi-presidential system based on the government of [[France]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The Westminster system is used, or was once used, in the national and [[Administrative division|subnational]] legislatures of most former colonies of the [[British Empire]] upon gaining self-government, beginning with the Province of Canada in 1848. However, many former colonies have since adopted other forms of government. The Parliament of the United Kingdom and the associated Westminster system played a "vanguard role" with worldwide influence on the spread of [[Democracy|democracy]], thus it is often known as "the mother of parliaments".',
