		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Individuals with full or nearly full European ancestry in Latin America originate from [[European colonization of the Americas|European settlers]] and immigrants who arrived in the [[Americas]] during the colonial and post-colonial periods. These populations are now found throughout Latin America. Most immigrants who settled Latin America for the past five centuries were from [[Spain]] and [[Portugal]]; after independence, the most numerous non-[[Iberian Peninsula|Iberian]] immigrants were from [[France]], [[Italy]], and [[Germany]], followed by other Europeans as well, [[Armenians]] and [[West Asia|West Asians]] (such as Levantine). Composing 33-36% of the population as of 2010 (according to some sources), White Latin Americans constitute the second largest racial-[[Ethnicity|ethnic group]] in the region after [[Mestizo|mestizos]] ([[Multiracial people|mixed]] Amerindian and European people). Latin American populations have often participated in interracial marriage since the beginning of the colonial period.",
