		emoji = "🏅",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sports or recreational activities which are played on snow or ice",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Winter activities", "Winter sport" },
		summary = "**Winter sports** or **winter activities** are competitive [[Sport|sports]] or non-competitive recreational activities which are played on [[Snow|snow]] or [[Ice|ice]]. Most are variations of [[Skiing|skiing]], [[Ice skating|ice skating]] and sledding. Traditionally, such games were only played in cold areas during [[Winter|winter]], but artificial snow and artificial ice allow more flexibility. Playing areas and fields consist of either snow or ice.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Artificial ice can also be used to provide ice rinks for [[Ice skating|ice skating]], [[Ice hockey|ice hockey]], para ice hockey, ringette, broomball, bandy, rink bandy, rinkball, and spongee in a milder climate. The sport of [[Speed skating|speed skating]] uses a frozen circular track of ice, but in some facilities the track is combined in an enclosed area used for sports requiring an ice rink or the rink itself is used. Alternatively, ice cross downhill uses a track with various levels of elevation and a combination of bends. Tour skating at times includes speed skaters, though tour skates are more common. Common individual sports include: cross-country skiing, [[Alpine skiing|alpine skiing]], [[Snowboarding|snowboarding]], [[Ski jumping|ski jumping]], [[Speed skating|speed skating]], [[Figure skating|figure skating]], [[Luge|luge]], skeleton, [[Bobsleigh|bobsleigh]], ski orienteering and snowmobiling.",
