	{
		title = "Croatia",
		category = "Country",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🗺️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Country in Central and Southeast Europe",
		tags = { "Europe", "Southern Europe" },
		aliases = { "Republic of Croatia" },
		summary = "**Croatia**, officially the **Republic of Croatia**, is a country in [[Central Europe|Central]] and [[Southeastern Europe]], on the coast of the [[Adriatic Sea]]. It borders [[Slovenia]] to the northwest, [[Hungary]] to the northeast, [[Serbia]] to the east, [[Bosnia and Herzegovina]] and [[Montenegro]] to the southeast, and shares a maritime border with [[Italy]] to the west. The Croatian archipelago contains over 1,000 islands and islets, the [[Adriatic Sea|largest overseas territory]] on the Adriatic Sea. Its capital, largest city and main cultural and economic centre is [[Zagreb]]. Major urban centers include Split, Rijeka, and Osijek. The country is composed of twenty counties spanning 56594 km² within four administrative regions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Croatia has a population of nearly 3.9 million as of 2026. Croatian history began with the 6th century arrival of the Croats to Roman Illyria. Settled as two duchies a century later, Croatia was granted independence in 878 under Duke Branimir and elevated into a kingdom under King Tomislav in 925. The Trpimirović dynastic succession crisis placed Croatia into a [[Personal union|personal union]] with Hungary in 1102. In 1527, amid Ottoman conflict, Croatia aligned with the [[Habsburg monarchy]]. It reorganized into the State of Slovenes, Croats, and Serbs before merging into the [[Kingdom of Yugoslavia]] in 1918. During [[World War II]], most of Croatia was invaded by the [[Axis powers]] who installed a puppet state known as the Independent State of Croatia. A resistance movement restored sovereignty, emerging as the Socialist Republic of Croatia within the [[Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia]] in 1943.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Capital", "[[Zagreb]]" },
