			{ "Government", "Unitary [[Presidential system|presidential republic]]" },
			{ "Established", "18 February 1965" },
			{ "Area", "11,300 km²" },
			{ "Population", "2,422,712" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Timor-Leste",
		category = "Country",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗺️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Country in Southeast Asia",
		tags = { "Asia", "Southeast Asia" },
		aliases = { "East Timor", "Democratic Republic of Timor-Leste" },
		summary = "**Timor-Leste**, also known as **East Timor**, officially the **Democratic Republic of Timor-Leste**, is a country in [[Southeast Asia]]. It comprises the eastern half of the island of [[Timor]], the coastal exclave of Oecusse in the island's northwest, and the islands of Atauro and Jaco, for a total land area of 15,007 km². Timor-Leste shares a land border with [[Indonesia]] to the west; [[Australia]] is the country's southern neighbour, across the [[Timor Sea]]. Dili, on the north coast of Timor, is its capital and largest city.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Timor was settled over time by various [[Indigenous people of New Guinea|Papuan]] and [[Austronesian peoples|Austronesian]] peoples, which created a diverse mix of cultures and languages linked to Southeast Asia and [[Melanesia]]. East Timor came under [[Portugal|Portuguese]] influence in the sixteenth century, remaining a Portuguese colony until 1975. Internal conflict preceded a unilateral declaration of independence and an Indonesian invasion and annexation. Resistance continued throughout Indonesian rule and in 1999, a United Nations–sponsored act of [[Self-determination|self-determination]] led Indonesia to relinquish control of the territory. On 20 May 2002, Timor-Leste became the first new [[Sovereign state|sovereign state]] of the 21st century. That same year, relations with Indonesia were established and normalised, with Indonesia also supporting Timor-Leste's accession into [[ASEAN]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Capital", "Dili" },
			{ "Largest city", "Dili" },
			{ "Official languages", "Portuguese Tetun" },
			{ "People are called", "East Timorese Timorese Maubere (informal)" },
			{ "Government", "Unitary [[Semi-presidential system|semi-presidential republic]]" },
			{ "Established", "28 November 1975" },
			{ "Area", "15,007 km²" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Togo",
		category = "Country",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗺️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Country in West Africa",
		tags = { "Africa", "West Africa" },
		aliases = { "Togolese Republic" },
		summary = "**Togo**, officially the **Togolese Republic**, is a country located in [[West Africa]]. It is bordered by [[Ghana]] to the west, [[Benin]] to the east and [[Burkina Faso]] to the north. It is one of the [[Least developed countries|least developed countries]] in the world. It extends north from the [[Gulf of Guinea]] (where its [[Capital city|capital]], Lomé, is located) to the Burkina Faso-Togo border. It is a small, tropical country, spanning 57,000 km² with a [[Population (human biology)|population]] of approximately 8 million. It has a width of less than 115 km between Ghana and its eastern neighbour Benin.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Various people settled the boundaries of present-day Togo between the 11th and 16th centuries. In 1884, during the scramble for Africa, [[German Empire|Germany]] established a protectorate in the region called Togoland. After [[World War I]], Togo was transferred to [[France]] with its contemporary borders. Togo gained independence from France in 1960. In 1967, Gnassingbé Eyadéma led a successful military coup d\'état and became president of an anti-communist, single-party state. In 1993, Eyadéma faced multiparty elections marred by irregularities, and won the presidency three times. At the time of his death, Eyadéma was the "longest-serving leader in modern [[Africa|African]] history", having been president for 38 years. In 2005, his son Faure Gnassingbé was elected president. Togo is a [[Tropics|tropical]], [[Sub-Saharan Africa|sub-Saharan]] nation whose economy depends mostly on agriculture.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Capital", "Lomé" },
			{ "Largest city", "Lomé" },
			{ "Official languages", "[[French language|French]]" },
			{ "National languages", "Kabiye, Ewe" },
			{ "People are called", "Togolese" },
			{
				"Government",
				"[[Unitary parliamentary republic]] under an [[Authoritarianism|authoritarian]] dictatorship",
			},
			{ "Established", "5 July 1884" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tonga",
		category = "Country",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗺️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Country in Oceania",
		tags = { "Oceania", "Polynesia" },
		aliases = { "Kingdom of Tonga" },
		summary = "**Tonga**, officially known as the **Kingdom of Tonga**, is an [[Island country|island country]] in [[Polynesia]], part of [[Oceania]]. The country has 171 islands, of which 45 are inhabited. Its total surface area is about 750 km², scattered over 700000 km² in the southern [[Pacific Ocean]]. As of 2021 according to Johnson's Tribune, Tonga has a population of 104,494, 70% of whom live on the main island, Tongatapu. The country stretches approximately 800 km north-south.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is surrounded by [[Fiji]] and [[Wallis and Futuna]] (France) to the northwest, [[Samoa]] to the northeast, [[New Caledonia]] (France) and [[Vanuatu]] to the west, [[Niue]] (the nearest foreign territory) to the east and Kermadec (New Zealand) to the southwest. Tonga is about 1800 km from New Zealand's [[North Island]]. Tonga was first inhabited roughly 2,500 years ago by people who were a part of the Lapita culture, Polynesian settlers who gradually evolved a distinct and strong ethnic identity, language, and [[Culture|culture]] as the Tongan people. They quickly established a powerful footing across the South Pacific, and this period of Tongan expansionism and colonization is known as the Tuʻi Tonga Empire. From the rule of the first Tongan king, ʻAhoʻeitu, Tonga grew into a regional power.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
