	{
		title = "Trinidad and Tobago",
		category = "Country",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗺️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Country in the Caribbean",
		tags = { "North America", "Caribbean" },
		aliases = { "Republic of Trinidad and Tobago" },
		summary = "**Trinidad and Tobago**, officially the **Republic of Trinidad and Tobago**, is the southernmost [[Archipelagic state|archipelagic country]] in the [[Caribbean]], comprising the main islands of Trinidad and Tobago, along with several smaller islets. The capital city is Port of Spain, while its largest and most populous municipality is Chaguanas. Trinidad and Tobago comprises the southernmost islands of the Caribbean eastern islands chain, and it is close to the continent of [[South America]], being north to northeast of Venezuela and northwest of Guyana.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Trinidad and Tobago is located 6 nmi north to northeast off the coast of [[Venezuela]], 70 nmi south of [[Grenada]], and 155 NM southwest of [[Barbados]]. [[Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Indigenous peoples]] inhabited Trinidad for centuries prior to [[Spanish Empire|Spanish colonisation]], following the arrival of [[Christopher Columbus]] in 1498. Spanish governor José María Chacón surrendered the island to a British fleet under Sir Ralph Abercromby's command in 1797. Trinidad and Tobago were ceded to Britain in 1802 under the Treaty of Amiens as separate states and unified in 1889. Trinidad and Tobago obtained independence in 1962, and became a republic in 1976. Unlike most Caribbean countries and territories, which rely heavily on tourism, the economy is primarily industrial, based on large reserves of [[Petrochemical|oil and gas]]. The country sees fewer hurricanes than most of the Caribbean because it is farther south.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Capital", "Port of Spain" },
			{ "Largest city", "Chaguanas" },
			{ "Official languages", "English" },
			{ "Languages", "Trinidadian and Tobagonian creoles" },
			{ "People are called", "Trinidadian and Tobagonian, Trini or Trinbagonian (colloquial)" },
			{ "Government", "[[Unitary parliamentary republic]]" },
			{ "Established", "3 January 1958 – 14 January 1962" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tunisia",
		category = "Country",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🗺️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Country in North Africa",
		tags = { "Africa", "North Africa" },
		aliases = { "Republic of Tunisia" },
		summary = "**Tunisia**, officially the **Republic of Tunisia**, is a country in the [[Maghreb]] region of [[North Africa]]. It is bordered by [[Algeria]] to the west and southwest, [[Libya]] to the southeast, and the [[Mediterranean Sea]] to the north and east. Tunisia also shares maritime borders with [[Italy]] through the islands of [[Sicily]] and [[Sardinia]] to the north and [[Malta]] to the east. It features the archaeological sites of [[Carthage]] dating back to the 9th century BC, as well as the Great Mosque of Kairouan. Known for its ancient architecture, souks, and blue coasts, it covers 163610 km², and has a population of 12.1 million.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It contains the eastern end of the [[Atlas Mountains]] and the northern reaches of the [[Sahara]] desert; much of its remaining territory is [[Arable land|arable land]]. Its 1300 km of coastline includes the African conjunction of the western and eastern parts of the [[Mediterranean basin|Mediterranean Basin]]. Tunisia is home to Africa's northernmost point, Cape Angela. Located on the northeastern coast, [[Tunis]] is the capital of the country, which is itself named after Tunis. The official language of Tunisia is [[Modern Standard Arabic|Arabic]]. The vast majority of Tunisia's population is [[Arabs|Arab]] and Muslim. Vernacular Tunisian Arabic is the most spoken language, and [[French language|French]] serves as an administrative and educational language in some contexts, but has no official status. Beginning in early antiquity, Tunisia was inhabited by the indigenous [[Berbers]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Capital", "[[Tunis]]" },
			{ "Largest city", "capital" },
			{ "Official languages", "[[Modern Standard Arabic|Arabic]]" },
			{ "People are called", "Tunisian" },
			{ "Government", "Unitary [[Presidential system|presidential republic]]" },
			{ "Established", "800" },
			{ "Area", "163610 km²" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Turkey",
		category = "Country",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🦃",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Country in Southeastern Europe and West Asia",
		tags = { "Asia", "West Asia" },
		aliases = { "Republic of Türkiye" },
		summary = "**Turkey**, officially the **Republic of Türkiye**, is a country mainly located in [[Anatolia]] in [[West Asia]], with a smaller part called East Thrace in [[Southeastern Europe|Southeast Europe]]. It borders the [[Black Sea]] to the north; [[Georgia (country)|Georgia]], [[Armenia]], [[Azerbaijan]], and [[Iran]] to the east; [[Iraq]], [[Syria]], and the [[Mediterranean Sea]] to the south; and the [[Aegean Sea]], [[Greece]], and [[Bulgaria]] to the west. Turkey is home to over 86 million people; most are ethnic [[Turkish people|Turks]], while Kurds are the largest ethnic minority. Officially a secular state, Turkey has a Muslim-majority population. [[Ankara]] is Turkey's capital and second-largest city. [[Istanbul]] is its largest city and economic center.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Other major cities include [[İzmir]], [[Bursa]], and [[Antalya]]. First inhabited by modern humans during the Late Paleolithic, present-day Turkey was home to various ancient peoples. The Hattians were assimilated by the [[Hittites]] and other Anatolian peoples. Classical Anatolia transitioned into cultural Hellenization after [[Alexander the Great]]'s conquests, and later Romanization during the [[Roman Empire|Roman]] and [[Byzantine Empire|Byzantine]] eras. The [[Seljuk Empire|Seljuk Turks]] began migrating into Anatolia in the 11th century, starting the Turkification process. Beginning in 1299, the [[Ottoman Empire|Ottomans]] united the principalities and expanded. [[Mehmed II]] conquered Constantinople (modern-day Istanbul) in 1453. During the reigns of [[Selim I]] and [[Suleiman the Magnificent]], the Ottoman Empire became a global power. From 1789 onwards, the empire saw major changes, reforms, centralization, and rising nationalism while its territory declined.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Capital", "[[Ankara]]" },
			{ "Largest city", "[[Istanbul]]" },
			{ "Official languages", "[[Turkish language|Turkish]]" },
			{ "People are called", "Turkish, Turk" },
			{ "Government", "Unitary [[Presidential system|presidential republic]]" },
			{ "Established", "c. 1299" },
			{ "Area", "783,562 km²" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Turkmenistan",
		category = "Country",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🗺️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Country in Central Asia",
		tags = { "Asia", "Central Asia" },
		summary = "**Turkmenistan** is a [[Landlocked country|landlocked country]] in [[Central Asia]], bordered by [[Kazakhstan]] and [[Uzbekistan]] to the north, [[Afghanistan]] to the southeast, [[Iran]] to the south and southwest, and the [[Caspian Sea]] to the west. It is one of six independent [[Organization of Turkic States|Turkic states]]. Ashgabat is the capital and largest city. With over 7 million people, Turkmenistan is the 35th most-populous country in Asia and has the lowest population of the Central Asian republics while being one of the most sparsely populated nations located on the [[Asia|Asian continent]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Turkmenistan has long served as a thoroughfare for several empires and cultures. Merv is one of the oldest oasis-cities in Central Asia, and was once among the biggest cities in the world. It was also one of the great cities of the Islamic world and an important stop on the [[Silk Road]]. Annexed by the [[Russian Empire]] in 1881, Turkmenistan figured prominently in the anti-Bolshevik movement in Central Asia. In 1925, Turkmenistan became a constituent republic of the [[Soviet Union]], the Turkmen Soviet Socialist Republic (Turkmen SSR); it became independent after the [[Dissolution of the Soviet Union|dissolution of the Soviet Union]] in 1991. Turkmenistan is widely criticized for its poor human rights record, including for its treatment of minorities, and its lack of press and religious freedoms.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Capital", "Ashgabat" },
			{ "Largest city", "Ashgabat" },
			{ "Official languages", "Turkmen" },
			{ "People are called", "Turkmenistani Turkmen" },
			{
				"Government",
				"Unitary [[Presidential system|presidential republic]] under a totalitarian hereditary dictatorship",
			},
			{ "Established", "22 August 1990" },
			{ "Area", "491,210 km²" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tuvalu",
		category = "Country",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🗺️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Island country in Oceania",
		tags = { "Oceania", "Polynesia" },
		summary = "**Tuvalu** is an [[Archipelagic state|archipelagic country]] in the [[Polynesia|Polynesian]] sub-region of [[Oceania]] in the [[Pacific Ocean]], about midway between [[Hawaii]] and [[Australia]]. It lies east-northeast of the Santa Cruz Islands (which belong to [[Solomon Islands]]), northeast of [[Vanuatu]], southeast of [[Nauru]], south of [[Kiribati]], west of Tokelau, northwest of [[Samoa]] and [[Wallis and Futuna]], and north of [[Fiji]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Tuvalu is composed of three [[Reef|reef]] islands and six [[Atoll|atolls]] spread out between the [[Latitude|latitude]] of 5° and 10° south and between the [[Longitude|longitude]] of 176° and 180°. They lie west of the [[International Date Line]]. The 2022 census determined that Tuvalu had a population of 10,643, making it the 194th most populous country and also the 2nd least populated country in the United Nations, exceeding only [[Niue]] and the [[Vatican City]] in population. Tuvalu's total land area is 25.14 km². It is the 4th smallest country in the world, the only smaller nations being Monaco, Nauru and Vatican City. The first inhabitants of Tuvalu were [[Polynesians]] arriving as part of the migration of Polynesians into the Pacific that began about three thousand years ago.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
