		description = "Branch of engineering",
		tags = { "Engineering", "Disciplines" },
		aliases = { "Electrical engineer" },
		summary = "**Electrical engineering** is an [[Engineering|engineering]] discipline concerned with the study, design, and application of equipment, devices, and systems that use [[Electricity|electricity]], [[Electronics|electronics]], and [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]]. It emerged as an identifiable occupation in the latter half of the 19th century after the commercialization of the electric telegraph, the telephone, and electrical power generation, distribution, and use.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Electrical engineering emcompasses many different fields, including [[Computer engineering|computer engineering]], systems engineering, power engineering, [[Telecommunications|telecommunications]], radio-frequency engineering, [[Signal processing|signal processing]], [[Instrumentation|instrumentation]], control engineering, photovoltaic cells, [[Electronics|electronics]], and [[Optics|optics]] and photonics. Many of these disciplines overlap with other engineering branches, spanning a huge number of specializations including hardware engineering, power electronics, electromagnetics and waves, microwave engineering, [[Nanotechnology|nanotechnology]], [[Electrochemistry|electrochemistry]], renewable energies, mechatronics/control, and electrical materials science. Machine learning and computer science techniques are also further studied due to being historically developed as fields of electrical engineering. Electrical engineers typically hold a [[Academic degree|degree]] in electrical engineering, electronic or electrical and electronic engineering.",
			},
