	{
		title = "The Sound of Music (film)",
		category = "Film",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🎬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "1965 film by Robert Wise",
		tags = { "Specific films", "Musical" },
		aliases = { "The Sound of Music" },
		summary = "**The Sound of Music** is a 1965 American [[Musical film|musical]] drama film produced and directed by Robert Wise from a screenplay written by Ernest Lehman. It is based on the 1959 stage musical composed by Richard Rodgers, with lyrics by Oscar Hammerstein II and a book by Lindsay and Crouse, itself based on the 1949 memoir The Story of the Trapp Family Singers by Maria von Trapp. The film stars [[Julie Andrews]] and Christopher Plummer, with Richard Haydn, Peggy Wood, Charmian Carr, and Eleanor Parker.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Set in Salzburg, Austria, it is a fictional retelling of Maria von Trapp's experiences as governess to seven children, her eventual marriage with their father Captain Georg von Trapp, and their escape during the Anschluss in 1938. Filming took place from March to September 1964 in Los Angeles and Salzburg. The Sound of Music was released by 20th Century-Fox in the United States on March 2, 1965, at first as a limited roadshow theatrical release. It initially received mixed reviews from film critics. However, the film was a major commercial success, becoming the number-one box office film after four weeks, and the highest-grossing film of 1965. By November 1966, The Sound of Music had become the highest-grossing film in history, surpassing [[Gone with the Wind (film)|Gone with the Wind]], and it held that distinction for five years.",
			},
		},
		related = { "2001: A Space Odyssey", "The Jazz Singer" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Director", "Robert Wise" },
			{ "Producer", "Robert Wise" },
			{ "Music", "Richard Rodgers Oscar Hammerstein II (songs) Irwin Kostal (score)" },
			{ "Studio", "Argyle Enterprises" },
			{ "Released", "2 March 1965" },
			{ "Country", "United States" },
			{ "Language", "English" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "The Wizard of Oz",
		category = "Film",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧙",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "1939 film based on the book by L. Frank Baum",
		tags = { "Specific films", "Science fiction/Fantasy" },
		summary = '**The Wizard of Oz** is a 1939 American [[Musical film|musical]] fantasy film produced by [[Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer]]. Based on the 1900 novel The Wonderful Wizard of Oz by L. Frank Baum, it was primarily directed by Victor Fleming, who left production to take over the troubled [[Gone with the Wind (film)|Gone with the Wind]]. The screenplay is credited to Noel Langley, Florence Ryerson, and Edgar Allan Woolf, but includes contributions from other writers. The film stars [[Judy Garland]], Frank Morgan, Ray Bolger, Jack Haley, Bert Lahr, Billie Burke, and Margaret Hamilton. The music was composed by Harold Arlen and adapted by Herbert Stothart, with lyrics by Edgar "Yip" Harburg.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The film is celebrated for its use of three-strip Technicolor, fantasy storytelling, musical score, and memorable characters. It was a critical success and was nominated for five [[Academy Awards]], including Best Picture, winning Best Original Song for "Over the Rainbow" and Best Original Score for Stothart; an Academy Juvenile Award was presented to Judy Garland. It was on a preliminary list of submissions from the studios for an Academy Award for Cinematography (Color) but was not nominated. While it was sufficiently popular at the box office, it failed to make a profit until its 1949 re-release, earning only $3 million on a $2.7 million budget, making it MGM\'s most expensive production at the time. The 1956 television broadcast premiere of the film on [[CBS]] reintroduced it to the public. According to the U.S. [[Library of Congress]], it is the most seen film in cinematic history.',
			},
		},
		related = { "2001: A Space Odyssey", "The Jazz Singer" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Director", "Victor Fleming" },
			{ "Producer", "Mervyn LeRoy" },
			{ "Music", "Herbert Stothart" },
			{ "Studio", "[[Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer]]" },
			{ "Released", "10 August 1939" },
			{ "Country", "United States" },
			{ "Language", "English" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tokyo Story",
		category = "Film",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🎬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "1953 Japanese film by Yasujiro Ozu",
		tags = { "Specific films", "Drama film" },
		summary = "**Tokyo Story** is a 1953 [[Cinema of Japan|Japanese]] [[Drama (film and television)|drama film]] directed by [[Yasujirō Ozu]] and starring Chishū Ryū and Chieko Higashiyama, about an aging couple who travel to [[Tokyo]] to visit their grown children.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Upon release, it did not immediately gain international recognition and was considered "too Japanese" to be marketable by Japanese film exporters. It was screened in 1957 in London, where it won the inaugural Sutherland Trophy the following year, and received praise from U.S. film critics after a 1972 screening in New York City. Tokyo Story is widely regarded as Ozu\'s [[Masterpiece|magnum opus]] and one of the greatest films in the history of cinema. It was voted the greatest film of all time in the 2012 edition of a poll of film directors by [[Sight and Sound]] magazine.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Mirror (1975 film)", "The Wizard of Oz" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Director", "[[Yasujirō Ozu]]" },
			{ "Producer", "Takeshi Yamamoto" },
			{ "Music", "Takanobu Saitō" },
			{ "Studio", "Shochiku" },
			{ "Released", "3 November 1953" },
			{ "Country", "Japan" },
			{ "Language", "Japanese" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Traditional animation",
		category = "Film",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Animation technique in which frames are hand-drawn",
		tags = { "Film and television" },
		aliases = { "Classical animation", "Cel animation", "Hand-drawn animation" },
		summary = "**Traditional animation** (or **classical animation**, **cel animation**, or **hand-drawn animation**) is a term retronymically applied to the dominant form of [[Animation|animation]] of the 20th century in the [[United States]] until the shift to [[Computer animation|computer animation]] in the industry, in which each frame is [[Drawing|drawn]] by hand. Today, the process is still commonly used, primarily in the form of **digital ink and paint**, which combines hand-drawn elements with computer compositing techniques.",
	},
	{
		title = "Venice Film Festival",
		category = "Film",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🎉",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Annual film festival in Italy",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Venice International Film Festival" },
		summary = 'The **Venice Film Festival** or **Venice International Film Festival** is an annual [[Film festival|film festival]] held in [[Venice]]. It is the world\'s oldest film festival and one of the "Big Five" International film festivals worldwide, which include the [[Film festival|Big Three European Film Festivals]] (Venice, [[Cannes Film Festival|Cannes]], Berlin), alongside the Toronto International Film Festival in Canada and the Sundance Film Festival in the United States. In 1951, FIAPF formally accredited the festival.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Founded by Giuseppe Volpi, member of the National Fascist Party and grandfather of producer Marina Cicogna, in Venice in August 1932, the festival is part of the Venice Biennale, one of the world's oldest exhibitions of art, created by the Venice City Council on 19 April 1893. The range of work at the Venice Biennale now covers Italian and international art, architecture, dance, music, theatre, and cinema. These works are experienced at separate exhibitions: the International Art Exhibition, the International Festival of Contemporary Music, the International Theatre Festival, the International Architecture Exhibition, the International Festival of Contemporary Dance, the International Kids' Carnival, and the annual Venice Film Festival, which is arguably the best-known of all the events.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Location", "[[Venice]], Italy" },
			{ "Founded", "6 August 1932" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Apex predator",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Predator at the top of a food chain",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Top predator", "Superpredator" },
		summary = "An **apex predator**, also known as a **top predator** or **superpredator**, is a [[Predation|predator]] at the top of a food chain with few natural predators of their own, if any.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Apex predators are usually defined in terms of trophic dynamics, meaning that they occupy the highest trophic levels. Food chains are often far shorter on land, usually limited to being secondary consumers – for example, [[Wolf|wolves]] prey mostly upon large [[Herbivore|herbivores]] (primary consumers), which eat plants (primary producers). The apex predator concept is applied in wildlife management, [[Conservation biology|conservation]], and ecotourism. Apex predators have a long evolutionary history, dating at least to the [[Cambrian]] period when animals such as Anomalocaris and Timorebestia dominated the seas. [[Human|Humans]] have for many centuries interacted with other apex predators including the [[Wolf|wolf]], [[Bird of prey|birds of prey]], and [[Cormorant|cormorants]] to hunt game animals, birds, and fish respectively. More recently, humans have started interacting with apex predators in new ways.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Bacon",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🥓",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of salt-cured pork",
		tags = { "Food types", "Meat and other animal products" },
		summary = "**Bacon** is a type of [[Curing (food preservation)|salt-cured]] [[Pork|pork]] made from various cuts, typically the belly or less fatty parts of the back. It is eaten as a side dish (particularly in [[Breakfast|breakfasts]]), used as a central ingredient (e.g., the BLT sandwich), or as a flavouring or accent.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Bacon is also used for barding and larding roasts, especially game, including venison and pheasant, and may also be used to insulate or flavour roast joints by being layered onto the meat. The word is derived from the [[Proto-Germanic language|Proto-Germanic]], meaning back meat. Meat from other animals, such as [[Beef|beef]], [[Lamb and mutton|lamb]], chicken, goat, or turkey, may also be cut, cured, or otherwise prepared to resemble bacon, and may even be referred to as, for example, "turkey bacon". Such use is common in areas with significant Jewish and Muslim populations as both religions prohibit the consumption of pork. Vegetarian bacons such as "soy bacon" also exist.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "[[Curing (food preservation)|Cured]] [[Pork|pork]]" },
			{ "Main ingredients", "[[Curing (food preservation)|Salt-cured]] pork belly" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Baking",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Food-producing method",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		summary = "**Baking** is a method of preparing food or [[Clay|clay]] that uses dry heat, typically in an [[Oven|oven]], hot ashes, or on hot stones. The most commonly baked food is [[Bread|bread]]. Heat is gradually transferred from the surface of unbaked goods to their center, typically at elevated temperatures surpassing 300 °F (148 °C). Dry heat cooking imparts a distinctive richness to foods through the processes of caramelization and surface browning. As heat travels through, it transforms batters and doughs into baked goods and more with a firm dry crust and a softer center. Baking can be combined with [[Grilling|grilling]] to produce a hybrid [[Barbecue|barbecue]] variant by using both methods simultaneously, or one after the other.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Baking is related to barbecuing because the concept of the masonry oven is similar to that of a smoke pit. Baking has traditionally been performed at home for day-to-day meals and in bakeries and restaurants for local consumption. When production was industrialized, baking was automated by machines in large factories. The art of baking remains a fundamental skill and is important for nutrition, as baked goods, especially bread, are a common and important food, both from an economic and cultural point of view. A person who prepares baked goods as a profession is called a baker.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Bar (establishment)",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		summary = 'Bars often also sell [[Snack|snacks]], such as chips (crisps) or [[Peanut|peanuts]], for consumption on their premises. Some types of bars, such as pubs, may also serve food from a [[Restaurant|restaurant]] menu. The term "bar" refers both to the countertop where drinks are prepared and served and also by extension to the entirety of the establishment in which the bar is located.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The term derives from the [[Metal|metal]] or wooden bar (barrier) that is often located along the length of the "bar".',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Beef",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍖",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Meat from cattle",
		tags = { "Food types", "Meat and other animal products" },
		summary = "**Beef** is the culinary name for [[Meat|meat]] from [[Cattle|cattle]] (Bos taurus). Beef can be prepared in various ways; cuts are often used for steak, which can be cooked to varying degrees of doneness, while trimmings are often ground or minced, as found in most [[Hamburger|hamburgers]]. Beef contains [[Protein|protein]], [[Iron|iron]], and vitamin B12. Beef has a high environmental impact, being a primary driver of [[Deforestation|deforestation]] with the highest [[Greenhouse gas emissions|greenhouse gas emissions]] of any agricultural product.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In prehistoric times, humans hunted [[Aurochs|aurochs]] and later [[Domestication|domesticated]] them. Since that time, numerous breeds of cattle have been [[Selective breeding|bred]] specifically for the quality or quantity of their meat. Today, beef is the third most widely consumed meat in the world, after [[Pork|pork]] and [[Poultry|poultry]]. As of 2018, the United States, Brazil, and China were the largest producers of beef. Some religions and cultures prohibit beef consumption, especially Indian religions like Hinduism. Buddhists are also against animal slaughtering, but they do not have a wrongful eating doctrine.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Breakfast",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Meal eaten in the morning",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Basics" },
		summary = '**Breakfast** is the first [[Meal|meal]] of the day, usually eaten in the morning. The word in English refers to breaking the [[Fasting|fasting]] period of the previous night. Various "typical" or "traditional" breakfast menus exist, with food choices varying by regions and traditions worldwide.',
		related = { "Dieting" },
	},
	{
		title = "Breakfast cereal",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Processed food made from grain",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Food types" },
		summary = "**Breakfast cereal** is a category of food, including food products, made from [[Food processing|processed]] [[Cereal|cereal grains]], that are eaten as part of [[Breakfast|breakfast]] or as a snack food, primarily in Western societies.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although warm, cooked cereals like [[Oat|oat]] meal, [[Maize|maize]] grits, and [[Wheat|wheat]] farina have the longest history as traditional breakfast foods, branded and ready-to-eat cold cereals (many produced via the process of extrusion) appeared around the late 19th century. These processed, precooked, packaged cereals are most often served in a quick and simple preparation with [[Dairy product|dairy products]], traditionally cow's milk. These modern cereals can also be paired with [[Yogurt|yoghurt]] or plant-based milks, or eaten plain. [[Fruit]] or [[Nut (fruit)|nuts]] are sometimes added, and may enhance the nutritional benefits. Some companies promote their products for the health benefits that come from eating oat-based and high-[[Dietary fiber|fiber]] cereals.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Butter",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧈",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Dairy product",
		tags = { "Food types", "Dairy and analogues" },
		summary = "**Butter** is a [[Dairy product|dairy product]] made from the fat and protein components of churned [[Cream|cream]]. It is a semi-solid [[Emulsion|emulsion]] at [[Room temperature|room temperature]], consisting of approximately 81% butterfat. It is used at room temperature as a spread, melted as a [[Condiment|condiment]], and used as a fat in baking, sauce-making, pan frying, and other cooking procedures.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most frequently made from cow's milk, butter can also be manufactured from the milk of other mammals, including sheep, goats, buffalo, and yaks. It is made by churning milk or cream to separate the fat globules from the buttermilk. Salt has been added to butter since antiquity to help [[Food preservation|preserve]] it, particularly when being transported; salt may still play a preservation role but is less important today as the entire supply chain is usually refrigerated. In modern times, salt may be added for taste and food coloring for color. Rendering butter, removing the water and milk solids, produces clarified butter (including ghee), which is almost entirely butterfat. Butter is a [[Gel|gel]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Candy",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sweet confection",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Food types" },
		aliases = { "Sweets", "Lollies" },
		summary = "**Candy**, also known as **sweets** and **lollies**, is a confection that features [[Sugar|sugar]] as a principal ingredient. The category, called sugar confectionery, encompasses any sweet confection, including [[Chocolate|chocolate]] or chewing gum. [[Vegetable|Vegetables]], [[Fruit|fruit]], or [[Nut (fruit)|nuts]] which have been glazed with sugar are considered candied.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Candy has large amounts of sugar or sugar substitutes, usually made in smaller pieces. However, candy also depends upon how people treat the food. They are eaten casually, often with fingers, as a snack between meals, but each culture has its own ideas of what constitutes candy rather than dessert. The same food may be a candy in one culture and a [[Dessert|dessert]] in another.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Sugar confectionery" },
			{ "Main ingredients", "[[Sugar]] or [[Honey|honey]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Canning",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Method of preserving food",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		summary = "**Canning** is a method of [[Food preservation|food preservation]] in which food is processed and sealed in an airtight container (jars like Mason jars, and steel and tin cans). Canning provides a shelf life that typically ranges from one to five years, although under specific circumstances, it can be much longer. A freeze-dried canned product, such as canned dried [[Lentil|lentils]], could last as long as 30 years in an edible state.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In 1974, samples of canned food from the wreck of the Bertrand, a steamboat that sank in the Missouri River in 1865, were tested by the National Food Processors Association. Although appearance, smell, and vitamin content had deteriorated, there was no trace of microbial growth and the 109-year-old food was determined to be still safe to eat.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cereal",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Grass that has edible grain",
		tags = { "Food types", "Grains" },
		summary = "A **cereal** is a grass (family [[Poaceae]]) cultivated for its edible [[Grain|grain]]. Cereals are the world's largest [[Crop|crops]], and are therefore [[Staple food|staple foods]]. They include [[Rice|rice]], [[Wheat|wheat]], [[Rye|rye]], [[Oat|oats]], [[Barley|barley]], [[Millet|millet]], and [[Maize|maize]] (US: corn). Edible grains from other plant families, such as [[Amaranth|amaranth]], [[Buckwheat|buckwheat]] and [[Quinoa|quinoa]], are pseudocereals. Most cereals are [[Annual plant|annuals]], producing one crop from each planting, though rice is sometimes grown as a [[Perennial|perennial]]. Winter varieties are hardy enough to be planted in the autumn, becoming dormant in the winter, and harvested in spring or early summer; spring varieties are planted in spring and harvested in late summer.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term cereal is derived from the name of the [[Roman Empire|Roman]] goddess of grain crops and fertility, Ceres. Cereals were domesticated in the [[Neolithic]] around 8,000 years ago. Wheat and barley were domesticated in the [[Fertile Crescent]]. Rice and some millets were domesticated in [[East Asia]], while [[Sorghum|sorghum]] and other millets were domesticated in [[Sudan]]. Maize was domesticated by [[Indigenous peoples of the Americas]] in Mexico about 9,000 years ago. In the 20th century, cereal productivity was greatly increased by the [[Green Revolution]]. This increase in production has accompanied a growing international trade, with some countries producing large portions of the cereal supply for other countries. Cereals provide food eaten directly as whole grains, usually cooked, or they are ground to [[Flour|flour]] and made into [[Bread|bread]], [[Porridge|porridge]], and other products.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Chicken as food",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Most popular and affordable source of high-quality protein",
		tags = { "Food types", "Meat and other animal products" },
		summary = '[[Chicken]] is the most common type of [[Poultry|poultry]] and the second most consumed meat in the world, after [[Pork|pork]]. Owing to the relative ease and low cost of raising chickens—in comparison to mammals such as [[Cattle|cattle]] or pigs—chicken [[Meat|meat]] (commonly called just "chicken") and chicken eggs have become prevalent in numerous cuisines.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Chicken can be prepared in a vast range of ways, including [[Baking|baking]], [[Grilling|grilling]], barbecuing, [[Frying|frying]], [[Boiling|boiling]], and roasting. Since the latter half of the 20th century, prepared chicken has become a staple of [[Fast food|fast food]]. Chicken is sometimes cited as being more healthy than red meat, with lower concentrations of [[Cholesterol|cholesterol]] and saturated fat. The poultry farming industry that accounts for chicken production takes on a range of forms across different parts of the world. In [[Developed country|developed countries]], chickens are typically subject to intensive farming methods while less-developed areas raise chickens using more traditional farming techniques. The [[United Nations]] estimated there to be 19 billion chickens on Earth in 2011, making them outnumber humans more than two to one.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Course", "Starter, main meal, side dish" },
			{ "Served", "Hot or cold" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Chinese cuisine",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Culinary traditions of China",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Cuisine" },
		summary = "**Chinese cuisine** comprises [[Food|cuisines]] originating from China, that varies with the country's large population and geographic diversity, as well as from [[Overseas Chinese|Chinese people from other parts of the world]]. Due to the Chinese diaspora and the historical power of the country, Chinese cuisine has profoundly influenced other cuisines in [[Asia]] and beyond, with modifications made to cater to local palates. Chinese food staples like [[Rice|rice]], [[Soy sauce|soy sauce]], [[Noodle|noodles]], [[Tea|tea]], chili oil, and [[Tofu|tofu]], and utensils such as [[Chopsticks|chopsticks]] and the wok, can now be found worldwide.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The world's earliest eating establishments recognizable as [[Restaurant|restaurants]] in the modern sense first emerged in the [[Song dynasty]] China during the 11th and 12th centuries. Street food became an integral aspect of Chinese food culture in the 7th century during the [[Tang dynasty]], and the street food culture of much of [[Southeast Asia]] was established by workers imported from China during the late 19th century. The preferences for [[Seasoning|seasoning]] and cooking techniques in Chinese provinces depend on differences in [[Social class|social class]], religion, historical background, and ethnic groups. Geographic features including mountains, rivers, forests, and deserts also have a strong effect on the locally available ingredients, considering that the climate of China varies from [[Tropical savanna climate|tropical]] in the south to [[Subarctic climate|subarctic]] in the northeast.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Condiment",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Substance added to food for flavour",
		tags = { "Food types", "Seasonings and condiments" },
		summary = "A **condiment** is a preparation that is added to food, typically after cooking, to enhance the [[Flavoring|flavor]], to complement the dish, or to impart a specific flavor. Such specific flavors generally add sweetness or pungency, or sharp or piquant flavors. The seasonings and spices common in many different cuisine arise from global introductions of foreign trade. Condiments include those added to cooking to impart flavor, such as barbecue sauce and [[Soy sauce|soy sauce]]; those added before serving, such as [[Mayonnaise|mayonnaise]] in a sandwich; and those added tableside to taste, such as [[Ketchup|ketchup]] with fast food. Condiments can also provide other health benefits to diets that lack micronutrients.",
	},
	{
		title = "Cooking oil",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🛢️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Oil used in food and cooking, of vegetable or animal origin",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		aliases = { "Edible oil" },
		summary = "**Cooking oil** (also known as **edible oil**) is a plant or animal liquid [[Fat|fat]] used in [[Frying|frying]], [[Baking|baking]], and [[Cooking|other types of cooking]]. Oil allows higher cooking temperatures than water, making [[Cooking|cooking]] faster and more flavorful, while likewise distributing heat, reducing burning and uneven cooking. It sometimes imparts its own flavor. Cooking oil is also used in food preparation and flavoring not involving heat, such as salad dressings and bread dips.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Cooking oil is typically a liquid at room temperature, although some oils that contain saturated fat, such as coconut oil, palm oil and palm kernel oil, are solid. There are a wide variety of cooking oils from plant sources, such as olive oil, palm oil, soybean oil, canola oil (rapeseed oil), corn oil, peanut oil, sesame oil, sunflower oil and other vegetable oils, as well as animal-based oils like [[Butter|butter]], ghee, schmaltz, and [[Lard|lard]]. Oil can be flavored with aromatic foodstuffs such as [[Herb|herbs]], [[Chili pepper|chilies]] or [[Garlic|garlic]]. Cooking spray is an [[Aerosol|aerosol]] of cooking oil.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cream",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Dairy product",
		tags = { "Food types", "Dairy and analogues" },
		summary = "**Cream** is a [[Dairy product|dairy product]] composed of the higher-fat layer skimmed from the top of [[Milk|milk]] before homogenization. The butterfat, which is less dense, rises to the top and is skimmed off, resulting in gravity cream. The industrial production of cream instead uses centrifugal separators to make separator cream. Cream is often sold in grades defined by their butterfat content. It contains high levels of saturated fat, in ascending proportion from light cream to heavy cream.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Cream skimmed from milk may be called "sweet cream" to distinguish it from cream skimmed from whey, a [[By-product|by-product]] of [[Cheese|cheese]]-making. Whey cream has a lower fat content and tastes more salty, tangy, and "cheesy". In many countries, partially fermented cream is also sold as sour cream, crème fraîche, and so on. Both forms have many culinary uses in both sweet and savoury dishes. Cream produced by [[Cattle|cattle]] (particularly Jersey cattle) grazing on natural pasture often contains some fat-soluble [[Carotenoid|carotenoid]] pigments derived from the [[Plant|plants]] they eat; traces of these intensely coloured pigments concentrated during separation give cream a slightly yellow hue, hence the name of the yellow-tinged off-white colour cream. Carotenoids are also the origin of [[Butter|butter]]\'s yellow colour. Cream from [[Goat|goat]]\'s milk, [[Water buffalo|water buffalo]] milk, or from cows fed indoors on grain or grain-based pellets, is white.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Culinary arts",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Art of the preparation, cooking, and presentation of food",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		summary = "**Culinary arts** are the [[Food|cuisine]] arts of the preparation, [[Cooking|cooking]], and presentation of food, usually in the form of [[Meal|meals]]. People working in this field – especially in establishments such as [[Restaurant|restaurants]] – are commonly called chefs or cooks, although, at its most general, the terms culinary artist and culinarian are also used.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Expert chefs are in charge of making meals that are both aesthetically beautiful and delicious. This often requires understanding of food science, nutrition, and diet. Delicatessens and relatively large institutions like hotels and hospitals rank as their principal workplaces after restaurants.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Curing (food preservation)",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Food preservation and flavouring processes",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Curing" },
		summary = "**Curing** is any of various [[Food preservation|food preservation]] and [[Seasoning|flavoring]] processes of foods such as [[Meat|meat]], fish and [[Vegetable|vegetables]], by the addition of salt, with the aim of drawing moisture out of the food by the process of [[Osmosis|osmosis]]. Because curing increases the solute concentration in the food and hence decreases its water potential, the food becomes inhospitable for the [[Microorganism|microbe]] growth that causes food spoilage. Curing can be traced back to [[Ancient history|antiquity]], and was the primary method of preserving meat and fish until the late 19th century. Dehydration was the earliest form of food curing.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"**Meat preservation** in general (of meat from [[Livestock|livestock]], game, and [[Poultry|poultry]]) comprises the set of all treatment processes for preserving the properties, taste, texture, and color of raw, partially cooked, or cooked meats while keeping them edible and safe to consume. Curing has been the dominant method of meat preservation for thousands of years, although modern developments like [[Refrigeration|refrigeration]] and synthetic preservatives have begun to complement and supplant it. For [[Developing country|less-developed countries]], curing remains a key process in the production, transport and availability of meat. Some traditional cured meat (such as authentic Parma ham and some authentic Spanish chorizo and Italian salami) is cured with salt alone.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Polydor Records" },
	},
	{
		title = "Dairy product",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Food product made from milk",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Dairy products", "Milk products" },
		summary = "**Dairy products** or **milk products** are food products made from (or containing) [[Milk|milk]]. The most common dairy animals are [[Cattle|cattle]], [[Water buffalo|water buffalo]], [[Goat|goat]], and [[Sheep|sheep]]. Dairy products consumed around the world include [[Yogurt|yogurt]], [[Cheese|cheese]], [[Milk|milk]], and [[Butter|butter]]. A facility that produces dairy products is a [[Dairy|dairy]]. Dairy products are consumed worldwide to varying degrees. Some people avoid some or all dairy products because of lactose intolerance, [[Veganism|veganism]], environmental concerns, or other reasons or beliefs.",
	},
	{
		title = "Dessert",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Dish of sweet foods",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Food types" },
		summary = "Some cultures sweeten foods that are more commonly savory to create desserts. Desserts commonly conclude a [[Meal|meal]] as a course in many [[Restaurant|restaurants]], while in some parts of the world, there is no tradition of a dessert course to conclude a meal.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Historically, the dessert course consisted entirely of foods 'from the storeroom' (de l'office), including fresh, stewed, preserved, and dried fruits; nuts; cheese and other dairy dishes; [[Cookie|dry biscuits (cookies)]] and wafers; and ices and [[Ice cream|ice creams]]. Sweet dishes from the kitchen, such as freshly prepared pastries, meringues, custards, puddings, and baked fruits, were served in the entremets course, not in the dessert course. By the 20th century, though, sweet entremets had come to be included among the desserts. The modern term dessert can apply to many sweets, including [[Fruit|fruit]], custards, gelatins, puddings, [[Cookie|biscuits]], [[Cookie|cookies]], macaroons, [[Pastry|pastries]], [[Pie|pies]], tarts, [[Cake|cakes]], [[Ice cream|ice creams]], and sweet soups.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Sweet" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Diet (nutrition)",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sum of food consumed by an organism",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Diet" },
		summary = "In nutrition, **diet** is the sum of food consumed by a person or other [[Organism|organism]]. The word diet often implies the use of specific intake of [[Nutrition|nutrition]] for health or weight-management reasons (with the two often being related). Although humans are omnivores, each culture and each person holds some food preferences or some food taboos. This may be due to personal tastes or ethical reasons. Individual dietary choices may be more or less healthy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Complete nutrition requires ingestion and absorption of vitamins, minerals, essential amino acids from protein and essential fatty acids from fat-containing food, also food energy in the form of [[Carbohydrate|carbohydrate]], protein, and fat. Dietary habits and choices play a significant role in the [[Quality of life|quality of life]], [[Health|health]] and [[Longevity|longevity]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dieting",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Regulation of the consumption of food",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Basics" },
		summary = "As weight loss depends on [[Calorie|calorie]] intake, different kinds of calorie-reduced diets, such as those emphasising particular macronutrients (low-fat, low-carbohydrate, etc.), have been shown to be no more effective than one another. As weight regain is common, diet success is best predicted by long-term adherence. Regardless, the outcome of a diet can vary widely depending on the individual.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The first popular diet was "Banting", named after English undertaker William Banting. In his 1863 pamphlet, Letter on Corpulence, Addressed to the Public, he outlined the details of a particular low-carbohydrate, low-calorie diet that led to his own dramatic weight loss. Some guidelines recommend dieting to lose weight for people with weight-related health problems, but not for otherwise healthy people. One survey found that almost half of all American adults attempt to lose weight through dieting, including 66.7% of obese adults and 26.5% of normal weight or underweight adults. Dieters who are overweight (but not obese), who are normal weight, or who are underweight may have an increased mortality rate as a result of dieting.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Eating", "Vegetable" },
	},
	{
		title = "Dinner",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌞",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Largest and most formal meal of the day",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Dinner** usually refers to what is in many Western cultures the biggest and most formal [[Meal|meal]] of the day. Historically, the largest meal used to be eaten around midday, and called dinner. Especially among the elite, it gradually migrated to later in the day over the 16th to 19th centuries. The word has different meanings depending on culture, and may mean a meal of any size eaten at any time of day. In particular, it is still sometimes used for a meal at noon or in the early afternoon on special occasions, such as a Christmas dinner. In hot climates, the main meal is more likely to be eaten in the evening, after the temperature has fallen.",
		related = { "Dieting" },
	},
	{
		title = "Drink",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Liquid intended for human consumption",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Beverage" },
		summary = "A **drink** or **beverage** is a [[Liquid|liquid]] intended for [[Human|human]] consumption. [[Water]] is the common basis for drinks. In addition to their vital function of satisfying thirst, drinks play important roles in human [[Culture|culture]]. Common types of drinks include plain [[Drinking water|drinking water]], [[Milk|milk]], [[Juice|juice]], smoothies and soft drinks. Traditionally warm beverages encompass [[Coffee|coffee]], [[Tea|tea]], and hot chocolate. Caffeinated drinks that contain the stimulant [[Caffeine|caffeine]] have been consumed for centuries.",
	},
	{
		title = "Drinking",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ingestion of water or other liquids",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Drinking** is the act of ingesting [[Water|water]] or other [[Liquid|liquids]] into the body through the mouth, [[Proboscis|proboscis]], or elsewhere. Humans drink by swallowing, completed by peristalsis in the esophagus. The physiological processes of drinking vary widely among other [[Animal|animals]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most animals [[Drinking water|drink water]] to maintain bodily hydration, although many can survive on the water gained from their food. Water is required for many [[Physiology|physiological]] processes. Both inadequate and (less commonly) excessive water intake are associated with health problems.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Drinking water",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Water safe for consumption",
		tags = { "Drinks", "Non-alcoholic drinks" },
		summary = "It is often supplied through taps, in which case it is also called tap water.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The amount of drinking water required to maintain good health varies, and depends on physical activity, age, health-related issues, and environmental conditions. For those who work in a hot climate, up to 16 L a day may be required. As many as two billion people lack safe drinking water. Developing countries are most affected by unsafe drinking water.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Juice", "Soft drink" },
	},
	{
		title = "Eating",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ingestion of food",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Feeding", "Consuming" },
		summary = '**Eating** (also known as **feeding** or **consuming**) is the ingestion of [[Food|food]] for [[Digestion|digestion]]. In [[Biology|biology]], this is typically done to provide [[Heterotroph|heterotrophic]] [[Organism|organisms]] with the essential [[Nutrient|nutrients]] and energy needed for [[Metabolism|metabolism]] and physical growth, since they are incapable of acquiring nutrition and energy intrinsically like autotrophs and therefore must ingest external [[Organic matter|organic matters]] in order to survive. Animals and phagotrophs eat and digest food internally, as opposed to [[Decomposer|decomposers]] such as [[Mushroom|fungi]] and microbes, who secrete [[Enzyme|enzymes]] to digest organic matters externally before absorbing the nutrients and thus do not "eat" food.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"For [[Human|humans]], eating is more complex, but is typically an activity of daily living. Human eating is usually organized into routine sessions known as [[Meal|meals]], where proper courses of [[Cooking|cooked]] food are consumed, typically with a decent quantity of [[Staple food|staples]]; and more time-flexible casual eatings known as [[Snack|snacks]], where small quantities of typically non-staple food (often convenience food, sometimes raw foods such as [[Fruit|fruits]] and nuts) are consumed more for the purpose of degustation than to satiate hunger. Formal sessions of eating, e.g. in a [[Party|gathering]] or a date, are known as **dining**, and large dining events where many people are invited to eat food together are known as banquets or feasts. The [[Craft|craft]] of preparing and presenting meals for formal eating is called [[Culinary arts|culinary arts]], and the person performing such tasks is known as a cook, chef or cater depending on the respective nature of food service.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Eggs as food",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Edible animal product",
		tags = { "Food types", "Meat and other animal products" },
		summary = "Humans and other [[Hominidae|hominids]] have consumed [[Egg|eggs]] for millions of years. The most widely consumed eggs are those of fowl, especially [[Chicken|chickens]]. People in Southeast Asia began harvesting chicken eggs for food around 1500 BCE. Eggs of other [[Bird|birds]], such as [[Duck|ducks]] and ostriches, are eaten regularly but much less commonly than those of chickens. People may also eat the eggs of [[Reptile|reptiles]], [[Amphibian|amphibians]], and [[Fish|fish]]. Fish eggs consumed as food are known as roe or caviar.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Chicken|Hens]] and other egg-laying creatures are raised throughout the world, and [[Mass production|mass production]] of chicken eggs is a global industry. In 2009, an estimated 62.1 million metric tons of eggs were produced worldwide from a total laying flock of approximately 6.4 billion hens. There are issues of regional variation in demand and expectation, as well as current debates concerning methods of mass production. In 2012, the [[European Union]] banned battery husbandry of chickens.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Fasting",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Willing abstinence from, or reduced consumption of, food and/or drink",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '**Fasting** is the act of refraining from [[Eating|eating]], and sometimes [[Drinking|drinking]]. However, from a [[Physiology|physiological]] context, "fasting" may refer to the [[Metabolism|metabolic]] status of a person who has not eaten overnight (before "[[Breakfast|breakfast]]"), or to the metabolic state achieved after complete [[Digestion|digestion]] and [[Nutrient|absorption]] of a meal. Metabolic changes in the fasting state begin after absorption of a meal (typically 3–5 hours after eating).',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A diagnostic fast is a prolonged fast, from 1–100 hours long (depending on age), conducted under observation, to facilitate the investigation of a health complication (usually hypoglycemia). Many people may also fast as part of a medical procedure or a check-up, such as preceding a colonoscopy or surgery, or before certain medical tests. Intermittent fasting is a technique sometimes used for weight loss or other health benefits that incorporates regular fasting into a person's dietary schedule.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Filter feeder",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Animals that feed by straining food from water",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Filter feeders" },
		summary = "**Filter feeders** are [[Aquatic animal|aquatic animals]] that feed on [[Organic matter|organic matter]], [[Food|food]] particles or smaller [[Organism|organisms]] ([[Bacteria|bacteria]], microalgae and [[Zooplankton|zooplanktons]]) suspended in water, typically by having the water pass over or through a specialized [[Filtration|filtering]] [[Organ (biology)|organ]] that sieves out and/or traps solids. Filter feeders can play an important role in condensing [[Biomass (ecology)|biomass]] and removing excess nutrients (such as [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]] and phosphate) from the local waterbody, and are therefore considered water-cleaning ecosystem engineers. They are also important in bioaccumulation and, as a result, are indicator organisms.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Filter feeders can be sessile, [[Plankton|planktonic]], nektonic or even neustonic (in the case of the buoy barnacle) depending on the [[Species|species]] and the [[Ecological niche|niches]] they have [[Evolution|evolved]] to occupy. Extant species that rely on such method of feeding encompass numerous [[Phylum|phyla]], including poriferans ([[Sponge|sponges]]), cnidarians ([[Jellyfish|jellyfish]], sea pens and [[Coral reef|corals]]), [[Arthropod|arthropods]] ([[Krill|krill]], mysids and barnacles), [[Mollusca|molluscs]] (bivalves, such as clams, [[Scallop|scallops]] and [[Oyster|oysters]]), [[Echinoderm|echinoderms]] (sea lilies) and [[Chordate|chordates]] (lancelets, sea squirts and salps, as well as many [[Marine life|marine]] [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]] such as most species of [[Forage fish|forage fish]], American paddlefish, silver and bighead carps, baleen whales, [[Manta ray|manta ray]] and three species of [[Shark|sharks]]—the [[Whale shark|whale shark]], basking shark and megamouth shark). Some water birds, such as [[Flamingo|flamingos]] and certain [[Duck|duck]] species, though predominantly terrestrial, are also filter feeders when [[Foraging|foraging]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Fish as food",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fish eaten by humans",
		tags = { "Food types", "Meat and other animal products" },
		summary = "Many species of [[Fish|fish]] are [[Fishing|caught]] by humans and consumed as food in virtually all regions around the world. Their [[Meat|meat]] has been an important dietary source of [[Protein (nutrient)|protein]] and other [[Nutrient|nutrients]] in the human diet.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The English language does not have a special culinary name for food prepared from fish like with other animals (as with [[Pig|pig]] vs. [[Pork|pork]]), or as in other languages (such as Spanish pez vs. pescado). In culinary and [[Fishery|fishery]] contexts, fish may include so-called [[Shellfish|shellfish]] such as [[Mollusca|molluscs]], [[Crustacean|crustaceans]], and [[Echinoderm|echinoderms]]; but, more expansively, [[Seafood|seafood]] covers both fish and other [[Marine life|marine life]] used as food. Since 1961, the average annual increase in global apparent food fish consumption (3.2 percent) has outpaced [[Population growth|population growth]] (1.6 percent) and exceeded the increase in consumption of meat from all [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial animals]] except [[Poultry|poultry]] (4.9 percent), both combined (2.8 percent) and individually (bovine, ovine, porcine, et cetera). In [[Per capita|per capita]] terms, food fish consumption has grown from 9.0 kg in 1961, to 20.2 kg in 2015, at an average rate of about 1.5 percent per year.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Flavoring",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Food additive used to change its aroma or taste",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Flavouring", "Flavor", "Flavour" },
		summary = "A **flavoring** (or **flavouring**), also known as **flavor** (or **flavour**) or **flavorant**, is a food additive that is used to improve the [[Taste|taste]] or smell of food. It changes the perceptual impression of [[Food|food]] as determined primarily by the chemoreceptors of the [[Taste|gustatory]] and olfactory systems. Along with additives, other components, like sugars, determine the taste of food.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A flavoring is defined as a substance that gives another substance taste, altering the characteristics of the solute, causing it to become sweet, sour, tangy, etc. Although the term, in common language, denotes the combined chemical sensations of taste and smell, the same term is used in the fragrance and flavors industry to refer to edible chemicals and extracts that alter the flavor of food and food products through the sense of smell. Owing to the high cost, or unavailability, of natural flavor extracts, most commercial flavorings are "nature-identical", which means that they are the [[Chemical compound|chemical]] equivalent of natural flavors, but chemically synthesized rather than having been extracted from source materials.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Aridity" },
	},
	{
		title = "Flour",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Cereal, seed, vegetable or root ground into powder",
		tags = { "Food types", "Grains" },
		summary = "**Flour** is a powder used to make many different [[Food|foods]], including [[Baking|baked goods]], as well as thickening dishes. It is made by grinding [[Grain|grains]], [[Bean|beans]], [[Nut (fruit)|nuts]], [[Seed|seeds]], roots, or [[Vegetable|vegetables]] using a mill.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Cereal]] flour, particularly wheat flour, is the main ingredient of [[Bread|bread]], which is a [[Staple food|staple food]] for many cultures. Archaeologists have found evidence of humans making cereal flour over 32,000 years ago. Other cereal flours include corn flour, which has been important in [[Mesoamerica|Mesoamerican]] cuisine since ancient times and remains a staple in the Americas, while [[Rye|rye]] flour is a constituent of bread in both [[Central Europe]] and [[Northern Europe]]. Cereal flour consists either of the endosperm, germ, and bran together, known as whole-grain flour, or of the endosperm alone, which is known as refined flour. 'Meal' is technically differentiable from flour as having slightly coarser particle size, known as degree of comminution. However, the word 'meal' is synonymous with 'flour' in some parts of the world.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Variations", "Cereal Bean Nuts Seeds Roots Vegetables" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Food and Agriculture Organization",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🚜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Specialized agency of the United Nations",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations", "FAO" },
		summary = 'The **Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations** (**FAO**) is a specialized agency of the [[United Nations]] that leads international efforts to defeat hunger and improve nutrition and [[Food security|food security]]. Its [[Latin]] motto, translates to "let there be bread". It was founded on 16 October 1945 in Quebec City, Canada.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The FAO comprises 194 members, including 193 countries and one member organization, the [[European Union]] (EU). Its headquarters is in [[Rome]], Italy, and it maintains regional and field offices worldwide, operating in over 130 countries. It helps governments and development agencies coordinate their activities to improve and develop agriculture, [[Forestry|forestry]], [[Fishery|fisheries]], and land and [[Water resources|water resources]]. It also conducts research, provides technical assistance to projects, operates educational and training programs, and collects agricultural output, production, and development data. The FAO is governed by a biennial conference representing each member country and the EU, which elects a 49-member executive council. The director-general, as of 2019 Qu Dongyu of China, serves as the chief administrative officer. Various committees govern matters such as finance, programs, agriculture, and fisheries.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "United Nations specialized agency" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Active" },
			{ "Headquarters", "[[Rome]], Italy" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Food preservation",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Inhibition of microbial growth in food",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		summary = "**Food preservation** includes processes that make [[Food|food]] more resistant to [[Microorganism|microorganism]] growth and slow the [[Redox|oxidation]] of [[Fat|fats]]. This slows down the [[Decomposition|decomposition]] and rancidification process. Food preservation may also include processes that inhibit visual deterioration, such as the enzymatic browning reaction in apples after they are cut during food preparation. By preserving [[Food|food]], food waste can be reduced, which is an important way to decrease production costs and increase the efficiency of food systems, improve [[Food security|food security]] and [[Nutrition|nutrition]] and contribute towards environmental sustainability. For instance, it can reduce the environmental impact of food production.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Many processes designed to preserve food involve more than one food preservation method. Different food preservation methods have different impacts on the quality of the food and food systems. Some traditional methods of preserving food have been shown to have a lower energy input and carbon footprint compared to modern methods. Some methods of food preservation are also known to create [[Carcinogen|carcinogens]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Food processing",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Transformation of raw ingredients into a food like product, or of food into other forms",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Food processing** is the transformation of [[Agriculture|agricultural products]] into [[Food|food]], or of one form of food into other forms. Food processing takes many forms, from grinding [[Grain|grain]] into raw [[Flour|flour]] to home cooking and complex industrial methods used in the making of convenience foods. Some food processing methods play important roles in reducing food waste and improving [[Food preservation|food preservation]], thus reducing the total environmental impact of agriculture and improving [[Food security|food security]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Food Processing Levels (FPL) are defined according to physical and chemical changes occurring during food treatments. FPL are required in processed food classifications, such as the Nova classification, to categorise processed foods according to their FPL for different purposes. Primary food processing is necessary to make most foods edible while secondary food processing turns ingredients into familiar foods, such as [[Bread|bread]]. Tertiary food processing results in ultra-processed foods and has been widely criticized for promoting overnutrition and [[Obesity|obesity]], containing too much [[Sugar|sugar]] and [[Salt|salt]], too little [[Dietary fiber|fiber]], and otherwise being unhealthful in respect to dietary needs of humans and [[Animal husbandry|farmed animals]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Foraging" },
	},
	{
		title = "Food security",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Measure of food availability",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Food security** is the state of having reliable access to a sufficient quantity of affordable, healthy [[Human food|food]]. The availability of food for people of any class, gender, status, ethnicity, or religion is another element of food protection. Similarly, [[Household|household]] food security is considered to exist when all the members of a family have consistent access to enough food for an active, [[Health|healthy]] life. Food-secure individuals do not live in hunger or fear of starvation. Food security includes resilience to future disruptions of food supply. Such a disruption could occur due to various risk factors such as [[Drought|droughts]] and [[Flood|floods]], shipping disruptions, fuel shortages, economic instability, and wars.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'**Food insecurity** is the opposite of food security: a state where there is only limited or uncertain availability of suitable food. The concept of food security has evolved over time. The four pillars of food security include availability, access, utilization, and stability. In addition, there are two more dimensions that are important: agency and [[Sustainability|sustainability]]. These six dimensions of food security are reinforced in conceptual and legal understandings of the right to food. The World Food Summit in 1996 declared that "food should not be used as an instrument for political and economic pressure." Food insecurity goes beyond hunger; it includes people who do not have regular access to nutritious and sufficient food, even if they may not necessarily be suffering from hunger.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Foraging",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Searching for wild food resources",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Foraging** is searching for wild food resources. It affects an animal's fitness because it plays an important role in an animal's ability to survive and reproduce. Foraging theory is a branch of behavioral ecology that studies the foraging behavior of animals in response to the environment where the animal lives.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Behavioral ecologists use economic models and categories to understand foraging; many of these models are a type of optimal model. Thus foraging theory is discussed in terms of optimizing a payoff from a foraging decision. The payoff for many of these models is the amount of energy an animal receives per unit time, more specifically, the highest ratio of energetic gain to cost while foraging. Foraging theory predicts that the decisions that maximize energy per unit time and thus deliver the highest payoff will be selected for and persist. Key words used to describe foraging behavior include resources, the elements necessary for survival and reproduction which have a limited supply, predator, any organism that consumes others, prey, an organism that is eaten in part or whole by another, and patches, concentrations of resources.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
	},
	{
		title = "French cuisine",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Culinary tradition",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Cuisine" },
		summary = '**French cuisine** is the [[Cooking|cooking]] traditions and practices of [[France]]. In the 14th century, Guillaume Tirel, a court chef known as "Taillevent", wrote Le Viandier, one of the earliest recipe collections of medieval France. In the 17th and 18th centuries, chefs François Pierre La Varenne and Marie-Antoine Carême spearheaded movements that shifted French cooking away from its foreign influences such as from [[Italian cuisine]] and developed France\'s own indigenous style.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"They play different roles regionally and nationally, with many variations and appellation d'origine contrôlée (AOC) (regulated appellation) laws. Culinary tourism and the Guide Michelin helped to acquaint commoners with the cuisine bourgeoise of the urban elites and the peasant cuisine of the French countryside starting in the 20th century. Many dishes that were once regional have proliferated in variations across the country. Knowledge of French cooking has contributed significantly to Western cuisines. Its criteria are used widely in Western cookery school boards and culinary education. In November 2010, French gastronomy was added by the [[UNESCO]] to its lists of the world's [[Lists of Intangible Cultural Heritage elements|\"intangible cultural heritage\"]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
