		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		summary = "**Fruit preserves** are preparations of [[Fruit|fruits]] whose main preserving agent is [[Sugar|sugar]] and sometimes [[Acid|acid]], often stored in glass jars and used as a [[Condiment|condiment]] or spread.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'There are many varieties of fruit preserves globally, distinguished by the method of preparation, type of fruit used, and its place in a meal. Sweet fruit preserves such as jams, jellies, and marmalades are often eaten at breakfast with bread or as an ingredient of a pastry or dessert, whereas more savory and acidic preserves made from "[[Vegetable|vegetable]] fruits" such as tomato, squash or [[Zucchini|zucchini]], are eaten alongside savory foods such as cheese, cold meats, and curries.',
