	},
	{
		title = "Meal",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Eating that takes place at a specific time",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Basics" },
		summary = "A **meal** is an occasion that occurs at a specific time and involves the [[Eating|consumption]] of [[Human food|food]]. The English names used for specific meals vary, depending on the speaker's culture, the time of day, or the size of the meal. A meal is different from a [[Snack|snack]] in that meals are generally larger, more varied, and more filling.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Though they can be eaten anywhere, meals usually take place in homes, restaurants, and cafeterias. Regular meals occur on a daily basis, typically several times a day. Special meals are normally held in conjunction with celebratory or momentous occasions such as [[Birthday|birthdays]], [[Wedding|weddings]], anniversaries, funerals, and holidays. The type of food that is served or consumed at any given time depends on regional customs. Three main meals are typically eaten in the morning, early afternoon, and evening in most civilizations. Furthermore, the names of meals are often interchangeable by custom as well. Some serve [[Dinner|dinner]] as the main meal at midday, with supper as the late afternoon/early evening meal, while others may call their midday meal [[Lunch|lunch]] and their early evening meal supper or dinner. Except for [[Breakfast|breakfast]], these names can vary from region to region or even from family to family.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Meat",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🍖",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Animal flesh eaten as food",
		tags = { "Food types", "Meat and other animal products" },
		summary = "**Meat** is animal [[Tissue (biology)|tissue]], mostly [[Muscle|muscle]], that is eaten as food. Humans have hunted and farmed other animals for meat since prehistory. The [[Neolithic Revolution]] allowed the domestication of vertebrates, including [[Chicken|chickens]], [[Sheep|sheep]], [[Goat|goats]], [[Pig|pigs]], [[Horse|horses]], and [[Cattle|cattle]], starting around 11,000 years ago. Since then, [[Selective breeding|selective breeding]] has enabled farmers to produce meat with the qualities desired by producers and consumers. Meat is important to economies, cultures and faiths around the world.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Meat is mainly composed of water, protein, and fat. Its quality is affected by many factors, including the genetics, health, and nutritional status of the animal involved. Without preservation, bacteria and fungi decompose and spoil unprocessed meat within hours or days. Meat production significantly harms the environment by contributing to global warming, pollution, and [[Biodiversity loss|biodiversity loss]]. Some people ([[Vegetarianism|vegetarians]] and vegans) choose not to eat meat for ethical, environmental, health or religious reasons.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mexican cuisine",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Culinary traditions of Mexico",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Cuisine" },
		summary = "**Mexican cuisine** consists of the food traditions of [[Mexico]]. Its earliest roots lie in Mesoamerican cuisine. Mexican cuisine's ingredients and methods arise from the area's first agricultural communities, such as those of the [[Olmecs|Olmec]] and [[Maya civilization|Maya]], who domesticated [[Maize|maize]], created the standard process of nixtamalization, and established foodways. Successive waves of other [[Mesoamerica|Mesoamerican]] groups brought with them their cooking methods. These included the [[Teotihuacan|Teotihuacanos]], Toltec, Huastec, [[Zapotec civilization|Zapotec]], Mixtec, Otomi, Purépecha, Totonac, Mazatec, Mazahua, and Nahua. With the Mexica formation of the multi-ethnic Triple Alliance ([[Aztecs|Aztec Empire]]), culinary foodways became infused (Aztec cuisine).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Today's food staples native to the land include corn ([[Maize|maize]]), turkey, beans, squash, [[Amaranth|amaranth]], chia, avocados, tomatoes, tomatillos, [[Cocoa bean|cacao]], vanilla, [[Agave|agave]], spirulina, sweet potato, [[Cactus|cactus]], and chili pepper. Its history over the centuries has resulted in regional cuisines based on local conditions, including Baja Med, Chiapas, Veracruz, Oaxacan, Lebanese Mexican and the American cuisines of New Mexican and Tex-Mex. After the Spanish Conquest of the Aztec empire and the rest of Mesoamerica, Spaniards introduced a number of other foods, the most important of which were meats from domesticated animals (beef, pork, chicken, goat, and sheep), dairy products (especially cheese and milk), rice, sugar, olive oil and various fruits and vegetables.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Japanese cuisine" },
	},
	{
		title = "Milk",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🥛",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Nutrient-rich liquid produced by mammals",
		tags = { "Food types", "Dairy and analogues" },
		summary = "**Milk** is usually a white liquid [[Food|food]] (but can be shades of yellow, cream, pink, or brown) produced by the mammary glands of lactating mammals. It is the primary source of [[Nutrition|nutrition]] for young [[Mammal|mammals]] before they are able to [[Digestion|digest]] solid food. Milk contains many nutrients, including [[Calcium|calcium]] and [[Protein|protein]], as well as [[Lactose|lactose]] and saturated fat; the [[Enzyme|enzyme]] lactase is needed to break down lactose. Immune factors and immune-modulating components in milk contribute to milk immunity.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As an agricultural product, milk is collected from farm animals, mostly [[Cattle|cattle]], on a [[Dairy|dairy]]. It is used by humans as a [[Drink|drink]] and as the base ingredient for dairy products. The ability for adult humans to digest milk relies on lactase persistence, so lactose intolerant individuals have trouble digesting lactose. In 2011, dairy farms produced around 730 e6tonne of milk from 260 million dairy cows. [[India]] is the world's largest producer of milk and the leading exporter of skimmed milk powder. New Zealand, Germany, and the Netherlands are the largest exporters of milk products. Between 750 and 900 million people live in dairy-farming households.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mustard (condiment)",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Condiment made from mustard seeds",
		tags = { "Food types", "Seasonings and condiments" },
		aliases = { "Mustard" },
		summary = "**Mustard** is a [[Condiment|condiment]] made from the seeds of a mustard plant, which may be the white/yellow mustard (Sinapis alba), brown mustard (Brassica juncea), or black mustard (Rhamphospermum nigrum).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The seed itself has a strong, [[Pungency|pungent]], and somewhat bitter taste. The taste of mustard condiments ranges from sweet to spicy. Mustard is commonly paired with meats, vegetables, and cheeses, especially as a condiment for [[Sandwich|sandwiches]], [[Hamburger|hamburgers]], and [[Hot dog|hot dogs]]. It is also used as an ingredient in many dressings, glazes, [[Sauce|sauces]], [[Soup|soups]], relishes, and marinades. As a paste or as individual seeds, mustard is used as a condiment in the cuisine of [[Indian cuisine|India]] and Bangladesh, the Mediterranean, northern and [[Southeastern Europe|southeastern Europe]], Asia, the Americas, and Africa, making it one of the most popular and widely used spices and condiments in the world.",
