	{
		title = "Pickling",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Procedure of preserving food in brine or vinegar",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		summary = '**Pickling** is the process of [[Food preservation|preserving]] or extending the shelf life of [[Food|food]] by either anaerobic fermentation in [[Brine|brine]] or immersion in [[Vinegar|vinegar]]. The pickling procedure typically affects the food\'s texture and flavor. The resulting food is called a pickle, or, if named, the name is prefaced with the word "pickled". Foods that are pickled include [[Vegetable|vegetables]], fruits, [[Mushroom|mushrooms]], [[Meat|meats]], [[Fish|fish]], [[Dairy|dairy]] and [[Eggs as food|eggs]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Pickling solutions are typically highly acidic, with a [[PH|pH]] of 4.6 or lower, and high in salt, preventing [[Enzyme|enzymes]] from working and micro-organisms from multiplying. Pickling can preserve [[Decomposition|perishable]] foods for months, or in some cases years. Antimicrobial herbs and spices, such as mustard seed, [[Garlic|garlic]], [[Cinnamon|cinnamon]] or [[Clove|cloves]], are often added. If the food contains sufficient moisture, a pickling brine may be produced simply by adding dry salt. For example, sour cabbage, sauerkraut, and [[Kimchi|kimchi]] are produced by salting the [[Vegetable|vegetables]] to draw out excess water. Natural fermentation at room temperature, by lactic acid bacteria, produces the required acidity. Other pickles are made by placing vegetables in vinegar. Unlike the [[Canning|canning]] process, pickling (which includes fermentation) does not require that the food be completely sterile.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pie",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🥧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Savoury or sweet baked dish made with pastry",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Food types" },
		summary = "A **pie** is a [[Baking|baked]] dish which is usually made of a [[Pastry|pastry]] dough casing that contains a filling of various sweet or savoury ingredients. Sweet pies may be filled with fruit (as in an apple pie), nuts (pecan pie), [[Fruit preserves|fruit preserves]] (jam tart), brown sugar (tarte au sucre), sweetened vegetables (rhubarb pie), or with thicker fillings based on eggs and dairy (as in custard pie and cream pie). Savoury pies may be filled with meat (as in a steak pie or a bacon and egg pie), eggs and cheese (such as quiches or British flans), or a mixture of meat and vegetables (pot pie or Corned beef pie).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Pies can be made from two pieces of pastry dough, known as a base and a top (also known as a two-crust or double crust) or just a top (also known as a top-crust), or just a base (also known as a single-crust or bottom-crust). Common pastry used include Shortcrust pastry, Puff pastry and Filo. However, some pies may use baking powder biscuits, mashed potatoes, Digestive biscuits and crumbs. Pies can be a variety of sizes, ranging from bite-size to those designed for multiple servings.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Main ingredients", "[[Pastry]]" },
			{ "Variations", "Fruit, meat, transparent, custard, cream, no-bake, vegan" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pork",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍖",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Meat from a pig",
		tags = { "Food types", "Meat and other animal products" },
		summary = "**Pork** is the culinary name for the [[Meat|meat]] of the [[Pig|pig]] (Sus domesticus). It is the second most commonly consumed type of meat worldwide, following [[Poultry|poultry]], with evidence of pig [[Animal husbandry|husbandry]] dating back to 8000–9000 [[Common Era|bce]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Pork is eaten both freshly cooked and preserved; [[Curing (food preservation)|curing]] extends the shelf life of pork products. [[Ham]], gammon, [[Bacon|bacon]], and [[Sausage|pork sausage]] are examples of preserved pork. Charcuterie is the branch of [[Cooking|cooking]] devoted to prepared [[Meat|meat]] products, many from pork. Pork is one of the most popular meats in the [[Western world]], particularly in [[Central Europe]]. It is also very popular in [[East Asia|East]] and [[Southeast Asia]] ([[Mainland Southeast Asia]], [[Philippines]], [[Singapore]], and East Timor). The meat is highly prized in Asian cuisines, especially in [[China]] (including [[Hong Kong]]) and [[Northeast India]], for its fat content and texture. Some religions and cultures prohibit pork consumption, notably Islam and Judaism.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Porridge",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Food",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Food types" },
		summary = "**Porridge** is a type of semi-solid [[Food|food]] made by soaking, poaching or [[Boiling|boiling]], in [[Milk|milk]] or [[Water|water]], ground, crushed or chopped [[Starch|starchy]] [[Plant|plants]], typically [[Grain|grain]]. It is a traditional staple food of many countries.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Other spellings of porridge include porage (obsolete, United Kingdom), parritch (Scottish/Northern English), and parrige (Scottish and Caribbean). Porridge is often cooked or served with added flavourings such as salt, [[Sugar|sugar]], [[Honey|honey]], [[Fruit|fruit]], jam or syrup, or it can be mixed with [[Spice|spices]], fish, meat or [[Vegetable|vegetables]] to make a savoury dish. It is usually served hot in a bowl or a pot, depending on its consistency. Many variants of porridge have their own names. [[Oat]] porridge is known as oatmeal in [[North America]], and is one of the most common types. Others include polenta, made from maize; poi, from [[Taro|taro]]; gruel, a thinner version of oat porridge; congee, an Asian savoury rice porridge; and brose and crowdie, Scottish uncooked porridges made from oats in water.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Course", "Breakfast" },
			{ "Served", "Hot" },
			{ "Main ingredients", "Starchy plants (e.g. grain), water or milk, flavourings" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Poultry",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Domesticated birds kept by humans for their eggs, meat or feathers",
		tags = { "Food types", "Meat and other animal products" },
		summary = "**Poultry** are [[Domestication|domesticated]] birds kept by [[Human|humans]] for the purpose of harvesting [[Animal product|animal products]] such as [[Meat|meat]], [[Eggs as food|eggs]] or [[Feather|feathers]]. The practice of [[Animal husbandry|raising]] poultry is known as poultry farming. These birds are most typically members of the superorder Galloanserae (fowl), especially the order [[Galliformes]] (which includes [[Chicken|chickens]] and [[Domestic turkey|turkeys]]). The term also includes waterfowls of the family Anatidae ([[Duck|ducks]] and geese) but does not include wild birds hunted for food known as game or quarry.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Recent genomic studies involving the four extant junglefowl species reveals that the domestication of chicken, the most populous poultry species, occurred around 8,000 years ago in [[Southeast Asia]]. This was previously believed to have occurred around 5,400 years ago, also in Southeast Asia. The process may have originally occurred as a result of people hatching and rearing young birds from eggs collected from the wild, but later involved keeping the birds permanently in captivity. Domesticated chickens may have been used for cockfighting at first and quail kept for their songs, but people soon realised the advantages of having a captive-bred source of food. Selective breeding for fast growth, egg-laying ability, conformation, plumage and docility took place over the centuries, and modern [[Breed|breeds]] often look very different from their wild ancestors.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Restaurant",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Single establishment that prepares and serves food",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		summary = "A **restaurant** is a [[Business|business]] that prepares and serves [[Food|food]] and beverages to customers. Meals are generally served and eaten on the premises, but many restaurants also offer take-out and food delivery services. Restaurants vary greatly in appearance and offerings, featuring a wide variety of [[Food|cuisines]] and service models — ranging from inexpensive fast-food restaurants and cafeterias to mid-priced family restaurants to high-priced luxury establishments.",
	},
	{
		title = "Salad",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🥗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Food mixture, served chilled or at room temperature",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Food types" },
		summary = "A **salad** is a dish consisting of mixed ingredients, frequently [[Vegetable|vegetables]]. They are typically served chilled or at room temperature, though some can be served warm. Condiments called salad dressings, which exist in a variety of flavors (generally savory), are usually used to make a salad.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'**Garden salads** have a base of raw leafy greens (sometimes young "baby" greens) such as [[Lettuce|lettuce]], arugula (rocket), [[Kale|kale]] or [[Spinach|spinach]]; they are common enough that the word salad alone often refers specifically to garden salads. Other types of salad include caprese salad, bean salad, tuna salad, bread salads (such as fattoush, panzanella), vegetable salads without leafy greens (such as Greek salad, potato salad, coleslaw), rice-, pasta- and [[Noodle|noodle]]-based salads, fruit salads and dessert salads.',
