		tags = { "Food types", "Seasonings and condiments" },
		aliases = { "Soya sauce" },
		summary = "**Soy sauce** (sometimes called **soya sauce** in [[British English]]) is a liquid [[Condiment|condiment]] of [[China|Chinese]] origin, traditionally made from a fermented paste of [[Soybean|soybeans]], roasted [[Cereal|grain]], [[Brine|brine]], and Aspergillus oryzae or Aspergillus sojae molds (kōji). It is recognized for its saltiness and pronounced umami taste.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Soy sauce was created in its current form about 2,200 years ago during the Western Han dynasty of ancient China. Since then, it has become an important ingredient in East and Southeast Asian cooking as well as a condiment worldwide.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Region", "[[East Asia]] and [[Southeast Asia]]" },
			{ "Type", "[[Condiment]]" },
			{ "Main ingredients", "Soybeans" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spice",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌶️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Food flavouring",
		tags = { "Food types", "Seasonings and condiments" },
		summary = "In the culinary arts, a **spice** is a [[Seed|seed]], [[Fruit|fruit]], [[Root|root]], [[Bark (botany)|bark]], or other [[Plant|plant]] substance primarily used for flavouring or [[Color|colouring]] food. Spices are distinguished from [[Herb|herbs]], which are the leaves, flowers, or stems of plants used for flavouring or as a garnish. Spices and herbs are both [[Seasoning|seasonings]]. Spices are sometimes used in [[Medicine|medicine]], religious rituals, [[Cosmetics|cosmetics]], or [[Perfume|perfume]] production. They are usually classified into spices, spice seeds, and herbal categories. For example, [[Vanilla|vanilla]] is commonly used as an ingredient in fragrance manufacturing. Plant-based sweeteners such as [[Sugar|sugar]] are not considered spices.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Spices can be used in various forms, including fresh, whole, dried, grated, chopped, crushed, ground, or extracted into a tincture. These processes may occur before the spice is sold, during meal preparation in the kitchen, or even at the table when serving a dish, such as grinding peppercorns as a condiment. Certain spices are rarely available fresh or whole, and are typically purchased in ground form. Small seeds, such as fennel and mustard, can be used either in their whole form or as a powder. A whole dried spice has the longest shelf life, so it can be purchased and stored in larger amounts, making it cheaper on a per-serving basis. A fresh spice, such as [[Ginger|ginger]], is usually more flavourful than its dried form, but fresh spices are more expensive and have a much shorter shelf life.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Staple food",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Major portion of a standard diet",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Food staple", "Staple" },
		summary = "A **staple food**, **food staple**, or simply **staple**, is a [[Human food|food]] that is eaten often and in such quantities that it constitutes a dominant portion of a standard [[Diet (nutrition)|diet]] for an individual or a population group, supplying a large fraction of energy needs and generally forming a significant proportion of the intake of other [[Nutrient|nutrients]] as well. For [[Human|humans]], a staple food of a specific [[Society|society]] may be eaten as often as every day or every meal, and most people live on a diet based on just a small variety of food staples.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Specific staples vary from place to place, but typically are inexpensive or readily available foods that supply one or more of the macronutrients and micronutrients needed for survival and health: [[Carbohydrate|carbohydrates]], [[Protein|proteins]], [[Fat|fats]], [[Mineral (nutrient)|minerals]], and vitamins. Typical examples include [[Grain|grains]] ([[Cereal|cereals]] and [[Legume|legumes]]), [[Seed|seeds]], [[Nut (fruit)|nuts]], and [[Root vegetable|root vegetables]] ([[Tuber|tubers]] and [[Root|roots]]). Among them, [[Cereal|cereals]] ([[Rice|rice]], [[Wheat|wheat]], [[Oat|oat]], [[Maize|maize]], etc.), [[Legume|legumes]] ([[Lentil|lentils]] and [[Bean|beans]]), and tubers (e.g. [[Potato|potato]], [[Taro|taro]], and [[Yam (vegetable)|yam]]) account for about 90% of the world's food calorie intake. Early [[Agriculture|agricultural]] [[Civilization|civilizations]] valued the [[Crop|crop]] foods that they established as staples because, in addition to providing necessary [[Nutrition|nutrition]], they are suitable for storage over long periods of time without decay.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Steaming",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Cooking technique",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		summary = "**Steaming** is a method of [[Cooking|cooking]] using [[Steam|steam]]. In steaming, the food is fully immersed in steam, with no air present, so that the steam condenses on the food, and then the condensate drips down away from the food. This is often achieved in a food steamer, a kitchen appliance made specifically to cook food with steam, but food can also be steamed in a wok. In the American Southwest, steam pits used for cooking have been found dating back about 5,000 years. Steaming is considered a healthy cooking technique that can be used for many kinds of foods.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Compared to full immersion in [[Boiling|boiling]] water, steaming can be far faster and more energy-efficient because it requires heating much less water and takes advantage of the excellent thermodynamic heat transfer properties of steam.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Stew",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍽️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Dish of ingredients cooked in liquid",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Food types" },
		summary = "A **stew** is a combination of solid [[Food|food]] ingredients that have been [[Cooking|cooked]] in [[Soup|liquid]] and served in the resultant gravy. Ingredients can include any combination of [[Vegetable|vegetables]] and may include [[Meat|meat]], especially tougher meats suitable for slow-cooking, such as [[Beef|beef]], [[Pork|pork]], venison, rabbit, [[Lamb and mutton|lamb]], [[Poultry|poultry]], [[Sausage|sausages]], and [[Seafood|seafood]]. While water can be used as the stew-cooking liquid, stock is also common. [[Seasoning|Seasonings]] and flavourings may also be added. Stews are typically cooked at a relatively low temperature (simmered, not [[Boiling|boiled]]), allowing flavours to mingle.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Stewing is suitable for the least tender cuts of meat that become tender and juicy with the slow, moist heat method. This makes it popular for low-cost cooking. Cuts with a certain amount of marbling and gelatinous connective tissue give moist, juicy stews, while lean meat may easily become dry. Stews are thickened by reduction or with [[Flour|flour]], either by coating pieces of meat with flour before searing or by using a roux or beurre manié, a dough consisting of equal parts fat and flour. Thickeners like cornstarch, potato starch, or [[Arrowroot|arrowroot]] may also be used. Stew is very prominent in England, with beef stew and dumplings having been one of England's national dishes in the 14th century. Other European countries also have their own recipes.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Country", "Worldwide" },
			{ "Region", "Unknown" },
			{ "Type", "Stew" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tofu",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧈",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Soy-based food used as a protein source",
		tags = { "Food types", "Dairy and analogues" },
		aliases = { "Bean curd" },
		summary = "**Tofu** or **bean curd** is a food prepared by pressing the curds of coagulated [[Soy milk|soy milk]] into solid white blocks of varying softness: 'silken', 'soft', 'firm', and 'extra (or super) firm'.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It originated in China and has been consumed for over 2,000 years. Tofu is a traditional component of many East Asian and Southeast Asian cuisines; in modern Western cooking, it is often used as a meat substitute. Nutritionally, tofu is low in [[Calorie|calories]], while providing a rich source of [[Protein (nutrient)|protein]]. Cultivation of tofu has one of the lowest needs for land use (1.3 m2/ 1000 kcal) and emits some of the lowest amounts of greenhouse gas emissions per gram of edible protein of any food (1.6 kg CO2/ 100 g protein).",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Main ingredients", "[[Soy milk]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Veganism",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Non-usage of animal products",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Veganism** is the practice of abstaining from the use of [[Animal product|animal products]] and the consumption of animal source foods, and an associated philosophy that rejects the commodity status of animals. A person who practices veganism is known as a **vegan**; the word is also used to describe foods and materials that are compatible with veganism.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Ethical veganism excludes all forms of animal use, whether in agriculture for labour or food (e.g., meat, fish and other animal seafood, eggs, honey, and [[Dairy product|dairy products]] such as milk or cheese), in clothing and fashion (e.g., [[Leather|leather]], [[Wool|wool]], [[Fur|fur]], and some [[Cosmetics|cosmetics]]), in entertainment (e.g., [[Rodeo|rodeos]], [[Bullfighting|bullfighting]], [[Zoo|zoos]], [[Pet|pet]] ownership, [[Circus|circuses]], dolphinariums and [[Horse racing|horse racing]]), in services (e.g., mounted police, [[Working animal|working animals]], K9s and animal testing), and in medicine (e.g., bile bear farms, wildlife trade). People who follow a vegan diet for the benefits to the environment, their health, or for religion are regularly also described as vegans. Although individuals have been renouncing the consumption of products of animal origin since ancient times, the term "veganism" itself was coined in 1944 by Donald and Dorothy Watson.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Vegetable",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🥕",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Edible plant part consumed as food",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Vegetables" },
		summary = "**Vegetables** are edible parts of [[Plant|plants]] that are consumed by humans or other animals as [[Food|food]]. This original meaning is still commonly used, and is applied to plants collectively to refer to all edible plant matter, including flowers, [[Fruit|fruits]], stems, [[Leaf vegetable|leaves]], roots, and seeds. An alternative definition is applied somewhat arbitrarily, often by culinary and cultural tradition; it may include savoury fruits such as [[Tomato|tomatoes]] and [[Cucurbita|squash]], [[Flower|flowers]] such as [[Broccoli|broccoli]], and seeds such as pulses, but exclude foods derived from some plants that are fruits, flowers, [[Nut (fruit)|nuts]], and [[Cereal|cereal]] grains.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Originally, vegetables were collected from the wild by [[Hunter-gatherer|hunter-gatherers]] and entered cultivation in several parts of the world, probably during the period 10,000 BC to 7,000 BC, when a new [[History of agriculture|agricultural way of life]] developed. At first, plants that grew locally were cultivated, but as time went on, trade brought common and exotic crops from elsewhere to add to domestic types. Nowadays, most vegetables are grown all over the world as climate permits, and crops may be cultivated in protected environments in less suitable locations. China is the largest producer of vegetables, and global trade in agricultural products allows consumers to purchase vegetables grown in faraway countries. The scale of production varies from [[Subsistence agriculture|subsistence farmers]] supplying the needs of their family for food, to agribusinesses with vast acreages of single-product crops.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Vegetable oil",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🛢️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Oil extracted from seeds or from other parts of plants",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Preparation and serving" },
		aliases = { "Vegetable oils", "Vegetable fats" },
		summary = "**Vegetable oils**, or **vegetable fats**, are [[Oil|oils]] extracted from seeds or from other parts of edible plants. Like animal fats, vegetable fats are mixtures of triglycerides. Soybean oil, canola oil, grapeseed oil, and cocoa butter are examples of seed oils, or fats from seeds. Olive oil, palm oil, and rice bran oil are examples of fats from other parts of plants. In common usage, vegetable oil may refer exclusively to vegetable fats which are liquid at room temperature. There is no evidence that seed oils are unhealthy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Vegetable oils have been used for thousands of years (dating back to at least 6000 BC for olive oil) and were historically important for cooking, lighting, medicine, and industry. Palm oil later becoming a major global commodity during the [[Industrial Revolution]]. In the 19th and 20th centuries, scientific advances (like understanding fats and hydrogenation) led to modern products such as [[Margarine|margarine]] and shortening. Oils like soybean and canola became dominant in global markets. Today, vegetable oils are widely used in food, manufacturing (e.g., cosmetics, paints, lubricants), and [[Biofuel|biofuel]], and are produced mainly by pressing or chemical extraction. Global output is rising significantly due to demand, especially for palm oil.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Vegetarianism",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍖",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Abstaining from the consumption of meat",
		tags = { "Cooking, food and drink", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Vegetarianism** is the practice of abstaining from the [[Eating|consumption]] of [[Meat|meat]] (red meat, [[Poultry|poultry]], [[Seafood|seafood]], [[Freshwater fish|freshwater fish]], insects, and the flesh of any other [[Animal|animal]]). A person who practices vegetarianism is known as a **vegetarian**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Vegetarianism may be adopted for various reasons. Many people object to eating meat out of respect for sentient animal life. Such ethical motivations have been codified under various religious beliefs as well as [[Animal rights|animal rights]] advocacy. Other motivations for vegetarianism are health-related, political, environmental, cultural, [[Aesthetics|aesthetic]], economic, taste-related, or relate to other [[Personality psychology|personal]] preferences. A healthy vegetarian dietary pattern is one of three healthy eating patterns recommended in the Dietary Guidelines for Americans, 2020–2025, alongside the Healthy U.S.-Style and Healthy Mediterranean-Style patterns. A small number of towns and cities around the world are exclusively vegetarian or have outlawed meat, including Rishikesh in India, which banned meat, fish, and eggs in 1956. A larger number of towns and cities are vegetarian-friendly.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Vinegar",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Liquid consisting mainly of acetic acid and water",
		tags = { "Food types", "Seasonings and condiments" },
		summary = "**Vinegar** is an odorous [[Aqueous solution|aqueous solution]] of diluted [[Acetic acid|acetic acid]] and trace compounds that may include flavorings or naturally occurring [[Organic compound|organic compounds]]. Vinegar typically contains from 4% to 18% acetic acid by volume.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Usually, the acetic acid is produced by a double fermentation – converting simple sugars to ethanol using [[Yeast|yeast]], and then converting ethanol to acetic acid using acetic acid bacteria. Many types of vinegar are made, depending on source materials. The product is now mainly used in the [[Culinary arts|culinary arts]] as a flavorful, acidic cooking ingredient, [[Salad|salad]] dressing, or [[Pickling|pickling]] agent. Various types are used as [[Condiment|condiments]] or garnishes, including balsamic vinegar and malt vinegar. As an easily manufactured mild [[Acid|acid]], it has a wide variety of industrial and domestic uses, including functioning as a household cleaner.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Yogurt",
		category = "Food",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🥛",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Food produced by bacterial fermentation of milk",
		tags = { "Food types", "Dairy and analogues" },
		summary = "**Yogurt** is a food produced by bacterial fermentation of [[Milk|milk]]. The bacteria ferment the sugars in the milk and produce [[Lactic acid|lactic acid]], which acts on milk protein to give yogurt its texture and characteristic tart flavor. Yogurt is most commonly made from cow's milk, but milk from [[Water buffalo|water buffalo]], [[Goat|goats]], [[Sheep|ewes]], mares, [[Camel|camels]], and [[Yak|yaks]] can also be used.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The main bacteria used to produce yogurt are Lactobacillus delbrueckii subsp. bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus. Other lactobacilli and bifidobacteria are sometimes added during or after culturing yogurt. Some countries require yogurt to contain a specific amount of colony-forming units (CFU) of bacteria; for example, in China, the requirement for the number of lactobacillus bacteria is at least 1 million CFU per milliliter. Some countries also regulate which bacteria can be used: for example, in France, a product can only be labeled as or if it has been fermented exclusively by Lactobacillus delbrueckii subsp. bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus, a requirement that aligns with the international definition of yogurt in the on fermented milk (CXS 243-2003).",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Fermented dairy product" },
			{ "Served", "Chilled" },
			{ "Main ingredients", "Milk, bacteria" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "ABC islands",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏝️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Three Dutch-ruled islands in the Leeward Antilles",
		tags = { "North America", "Caribbean" },
		summary = "The **ABC islands** are the physical group of Aruba, Bonaire, and Curaçao, the three westernmost islands of the Leeward Antilles in the [[Caribbean Sea]]. These islands have a shared political history and a status of Dutch underlying ownership, since the Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1814 ceded them back to the [[Kingdom of the Netherlands]], as Curaçao and Dependencies from 1815. They are a short distance north of the Falcón State, Venezuela. Aruba and Curaçao are autonomous, self governing [[Kingdom of the Netherlands|constituent countries]] of the [[Kingdom of the Netherlands]], while Bonaire is a special municipality of the [[Netherlands]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Territories of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, the countries, and its special municipalities, are outside the [[European Union]]; citizens have Dutch nationality and the former colonial power benefits from preferential trade, mineral and natural resource rights, particularly offshore.",
