		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Earth's southern polar ice cap",
		tags = { "Other hydrologic features", "Glaciers" },
		summary = "The **Antarctic ice sheet** is a [[Ice sheet|continental glacier]] covering 98% of the [[Antarctica|Antarctic]] [[Continent|continent]], with an area of 14 million km² and an average thickness of over 2 km. It is the largest of [[Ice sheet|Earth's two current ice sheets]], containing 26.5 e6km3 of ice, which is equivalent to 61% of all [[Fresh water|fresh water]] on Earth. Its surface is nearly continuous, and the only ice-free areas on the continent are the dry valleys, nunataks of the Antarctic mountain ranges, and sparse coastal [[Bedrock|bedrock]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is often subdivided into the [[Antarctic Peninsula]] (AP), the East Antarctic Ice Sheet (EAIS), and the West Antarctic Ice Sheet (WAIS), due to the large differences in glacier mass balance, [[Glacier|ice flow]], and [[Topography|topography]] between the three regions. Because the East Antarctic Ice Sheet is over 10 times larger than the West Antarctic Ice Sheet and located at a higher [[Elevation|elevation]], it is less vulnerable to climate change than the WAIS. As of early 2020s, there is still net mass gain over the EAIS (due to increased [[Precipitation|precipitation]] freezing on top of the ice sheet), yet the ice loss from the WAIS glaciers such as Thwaites and Pine Island Glacier is far greater. By 2100, net ice loss from Antarctica alone would add around 11 cm to the global [[Sea level rise|sea level rise]].",
