		tags = { "Bodies of water", "Oceans and seas" },
		summary = "The **Barents Sea** is a marginal sea of the [[Arctic Ocean]], located off the northern coasts of [[Norway]] and [[Russia]] and divided between Norwegian and Russian territorial waters. It was known earlier among Russians as the Murman Sea; the current name of the sea is after the historical [[Netherlands|Dutch]] navigator [[Willem Barentsz]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Barents Sea is a rather shallow [[Continental shelf|shelf]] sea with an average depth of 230 m, and it is an important site for both [[Fishing|fishing]] and hydrocarbon exploration. It is bordered by the Kola Peninsula to the south, the shelf edge towards the [[Norwegian Sea]] to the west, the [[Archipelago|archipelagos]] of [[Svalbard]] to the northwest, Franz Josef Land to the northeast and [[Novaya Zemlya]] to the east. The islands of Novaya Zemlya, an extension of the northern end of the [[Ural Mountains]], separate the Barents Sea from the [[Kara Sea]].",
