	{
		title = "Equator",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Imaginary line halfway between Earth's North and South poles",
		tags = { "Basics", "Earth" },
		summary = "The **equator** is the [[Circle of latitude|circle of latitude]] that divides [[Earth]] into the [[Northern Hemisphere|Northern]] and [[Southern Hemisphere|Southern]] [[Hemispheres of Earth|Hemispheres]]. It is an imaginary line located at 0 degrees [[Latitude|latitude]], about 21,639 nmi in circumference, located halfway between the [[North Pole|North]] and [[South Pole|South]] Poles. The term can also be used for any other celestial body that is roughly spherical.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In [[Three-dimensional space|spatial]] (3D) [[Geometry|geometry]], as applied in [[Astronomy|astronomy]], the equator of a rotating spheroid (such as a [[Planet|planet]]) is the parallel (circle of latitude) at which latitude is defined to be 0°. It is an imaginary line on the spheroid, equidistant from its [[Geographical pole|poles]], dividing it into northern and southern hemispheres. In other words, it is the intersection of the spheroid with the [[Euclidean plane|plane]] [[Perpendicular|perpendicular]] to its axis of [[Rotation|rotation]] and midway between its [[Geographical pole|geographical poles]]. On and near the equator (on Earth), noontime [[Sunlight|sunlight]] appears almost directly overhead (no more than about 23° from the zenith) every day, year-round. Consequently, the equator has a rather stable daytime temperature throughout the year.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Erie Canal",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🛶",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Waterway in upstate New York, U.S.",
		tags = { "Bodies of water", "Canals" },
		summary = "The **Erie Canal** is a historic [[Canal|canal]] in upstate New York that runs east–west between the Hudson River and [[Lake Erie]]. Completed in 1825, the canal was the first navigable waterway connecting the [[Atlantic Ocean]] to the upper [[Great Lakes]] above [[Niagara Falls]], vastly reducing the costs of transporting people and goods across the Appalachians. The Erie Canal accelerated the settlement of the [[Great Lakes region]], the westward expansion of the United States, and the economic ascendancy of [[New York (state)|New York state]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A canal from the Hudson River to the Great Lakes was first proposed in the 1780s, but a formal survey was not conducted until 1808. The New York State Legislature authorized construction in 1817. Political opponents of the canal, referring to its lead supporter New York Governor DeWitt Clinton, denigrated the project as "Clinton\'s Folly" and "Clinton\'s Big Ditch". The canal saw quick success upon opening on October 26, 1825, with toll revenue covering the state\'s construction debt within the first year of operation. The westward connection gave [[New York City]] a strong advantage over all other US ports and brought major growth to canal cities such as Albany, Utica, Syracuse, Rochester, and Buffalo. The construction of the Erie Canal was a landmark [[Civil engineering|civil engineering]] achievement in the early history of the United States.',
			},
		},
