	{
		title = "Gulf of Mexico",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Marginal sea of the Atlantic Ocean",
		tags = { "Bodies of water", "Oceans and seas" },
		summary = "The **Gulf of Mexico** is an oceanic basin and a marginal sea of the Atlantic Ocean, mostly surrounded by the North American continent. It is bounded on the northeast, north, and northwest by the Gulf Coast of the United States; on the southwest and south by the Mexican states of Tamaulipas, Veracruz, Tabasco, Campeche, Yucatán, and Quintana Roo; and on the southeast by [[Cuba]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Gulf of Mexico basin is roughly oval and is about 810 nmi wide. Its floor consists of [[Sedimentary rock|sedimentary rocks]] and recent sediments. It is connected to part of the Atlantic Ocean through the Straits of Florida between the U.S. and Cuba, and with the [[Caribbean Sea]] via the Yucatán Channel between Mexico and Cuba. The Gulf of Mexico has relatively narrow connections to the Atlantic Ocean, which causes only very small tidal ranges to occur. Moisture from the Gulf of Mexico also contributes to weather across the United States, including severe weather in Tornado Alley. The size of the gulf basin is about 1.6 million km². Almost half of the basin consists of shallow [[Continental shelf|continental shelf]] waters. The volume of water in the basin is roughly 642e15 US gal (642e15 US gal).",
