			{ "Largest city", "Rome" },
			{ "Currency", "Lira" },
			{ "People are called", "[[Italians|Italian]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Kingdom of Naples",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "State in the Italian peninsula (1282–1816)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { '"on this side of the lighthouse"' },
		summary = 'The **Kingdom of Naples**, officially the **Kingdom of Sicily** citra Pharum or **"on this side of the lighthouse"** was a state that ruled the part of the [[Italian Peninsula]] south of the [[Papal States]] between 1282 and 1816. It was established by the War of the Sicilian Vespers (1282–1302). Until then, the island of Sicily and [[Southern Italy|southern Italy]] had constituted the "[[Kingdom of Sicily]]". When the island of Sicily revolted and was conquered by the [[Crown of Aragon]], it became a separate kingdom also called the Kingdom of Sicily. This left the Neapolitan mainland in the possession of Charles of Anjou who continued to use the name "Kingdom of Sicily".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Charles' daughter Joanna II adopted King Alfonso V of Aragon as heir, who would then unite Naples into his [[Crown of Aragon|Aragonese]] dominions in 1442. As part of the Italian Wars, France briefly ruled the territory in 1494 and at the beginning of the 16th century; it then went to war with Spain over the kingdom in 1502, a conflict that ended in a victory for [[Ferdinand II of Aragon|Ferdinand II]], who was in full control of the kingdom by 1504. The Spanish held control of Naples throughout the 17th century where it remained an important source of economic and military power for the Spanish Crown. After the War of the Spanish Succession in the early 18th century, the possession of the kingdom again changed hands; the 1714 Treaty of Rastatt saw Naples given to Charles VI of the Austrian Habsburgs.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Nintendo Switch 2" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Government", "[[Absolute monarchy]]" },
			{ "Capital", "[[Naples]]" },
			{ "People are called", "Neapolitan" },
			{ "Official languages", "[[Italian language|Italian]], [[Latin]], Neapolitan" },
			{ "Currency", "Tarì, Tornesel, Ducat, Neapolitan lira, Cavallo" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Kingdom of Prussia",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "German state (1701–1918)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Kingdom of Prussia** was a German state that existed from 1701 to 1918. It played a significant role in the [[Unification of Germany|unification of Germany]] in 1871 and was a major constituent of the [[German Empire]] until its dissolution in 1918. The Kingdom of Prussia took its name from the historical region of Prussia, and controlled most of said region, although it was based in the Margraviate of Brandenburg, and its capital was [[Berlin]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The kings of Prussia were from the House of Hohenzollern. The [[Polity|polity]] of Brandenburg-Prussia, predecessor of the kingdom, became a military power under Frederick William, Elector of Brandenburg, known as "The Great Elector". As a kingdom, Prussia continued its rise to power, especially during the reign of [[Frederick the Great|Frederick II "the Great"]]. Frederick the Great was instrumental in starting the Seven Years\' War (1756–1763), holding his own against [[Habsburg monarchy|Austria]], [[Russian Empire|Russia]], [[Kingdom of France|France]] and [[Sweden]] and establishing Prussia\'s dominant role among the German states, as well as establishing the country as a European [[Great power|great power]] through the victories of the powerful Prussian Army. Prussia made attempts to unify all the German states (excluding the German cantons in [[Switzerland]]) under its rule, and whether Austria would be included in such a unified German domain became an ongoing question.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Conservation status", "Kingdom" },
			{ "Capital", "[[Berlin]] Königsberg (1806–1809)" },
			{
				"Government",
				"[[Absolute monarchy]] (until 1848) [[Constitutional monarchy|Semi-constitutional monarchy]] (from 1848)",
			},
			{ "Era", "[[New Imperialism]], [[World War I]]" },
			{ "Currency", "1701–1750, 1750–1857, 1857–1873, 1873–1914, 1914–1918" },
			{ "People are called", "Prussian" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Kingdom of Scotland",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sovereign state in Europe (843–1707)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Kingdom of Scotland** was a [[Sovereign state|sovereign state]] in northwest [[Europe]], traditionally said to have been founded in 843. Its territories expanded and shrank, but it came to occupy the northern third of the island of [[United Kingdom|Great Britain]], sharing a land border to the south with the [[Kingdom of England]]. During the [[Middle Ages]], Scotland engaged in intermittent conflict with England, most prominently the Wars of Scottish Independence, which saw the Scots maintain and assert their independence from the English.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Following Scotland's annexation of the Hebrides and the Northern Isles from [[Norway]] in 1266 and 1472 respectively, and England's capture of Berwick in 1482, the territory of the Kingdom of Scotland corresponded to that of modern-day [[Scotland]], bounded by the [[North Sea]] to the east, the [[Atlantic Ocean]] to the north and west, and the North Channel and [[Irish Sea]] to the southwest. In 1603, James VI of Scotland became [[Monarchy of the United Kingdom|King of England]], forming a personal union between Scotland and England. In 1707, during the reign of Queen Anne, the two kingdoms were united to form the [[Kingdom of Great Britain]] under the terms of the Acts of Union. [[The Crown]] was the most important element of Scotland's government. The Scottish monarchy in the Middle Ages was a largely itinerant institution, before [[Edinburgh]] developed as a [[Capital city|capital city]] in the second half of the 15th century.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Era", "[[Middle Ages]], Early modern" },
			{ "Government", "Unitary parliamentary [[Constitutional monarchy|semi-constitutional monarchy]]" },
			{ "Currency", "Pound Scots" },
			{ "People are called", "Scottish" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Kingdom of Serbia",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Country in Southeast Europe (1882–1918)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Kingdom of Serbia** was a country located in [[Southeastern Europe]] which was created when the ruler of the Principality of Serbia, Milan I, was proclaimed king in 1882.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Since 1817, the Principality was ruled by the Obrenović dynasty (replaced by the Karađorđević dynasty for a short time). The Principality of Serbia, under the [[Suzerainty|suzerainty]] of the [[Ottoman Empire]], [[De facto and de jure|de facto]] achieved full independence when the very last Ottoman troops left [[Belgrade]] in 1867. The Congress of Berlin in 1878 recognized the formal independence of the Principality of Serbia, and in its composition Nišava, Pirot, Toplica and Vranje districts entered the South part of Serbia. In 1882, Serbia was elevated to the status of a kingdom, initially maintaining a foreign policy friendly to [[Austria-Hungary]] before turning to the [[Russian Empire]] and [[French Third Republic|France]] following a coup d'état in 1903. Between 1912 and 1913, Serbia greatly enlarged its territory through engagement in the First and Second Balkan Wars – Sandžak-Raška, Kosovo Vilayet and Vardar Macedonia were annexed.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
