	{
		title = "Lake Balkhash",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Lake in southeastern Kazakhstan",
		tags = { "Lakes", "Asia" },
		aliases = { "Lake Balqash" },
		summary = "**Lake Balkhash**, also known as **Lake Balqash**, is a lake in southeastern [[Kazakhstan]]. It is one of the largest lakes in Asia and the 15th largest in the world. It is located in the eastern part of [[Central Asia]] and sits in the Balkhash-Alakol Basin, an [[Endorheic basin|endorheic]] (closed) basin. The basin drains seven rivers, the primary of which is the Ili, bringing most of the riparian inflow; others, such as the Karatal, bring surface and subsurface flow. The Ili is fed by [[Precipitation|precipitation]], largely vernal snowmelt, from the mountains of China's [[Xinjiang]] region.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The lake currently covers about 16400 km². However, like the [[Aral Sea]], it is shrinking due to diversion and extraction of water from its feeders. The lake has a narrow, quite central, [[Strait|strait]]. The lake's western part is [[Fresh water|fresh water]] and its eastern half is [[Saline water|saline]]. The eastern part is on average 1.7 times deeper than the west. The largest shore city is named Balkhash and has about 66,000 inhabitants. Main local economic activities include mining, ore processing and fishing. There is concern about the lake's shallowing due to [[Desertification|desertification]] of microclimates and water extraction for multiplied industrial output. Moreover, the impacts of climate change may also negatively affect the lake and its ecosystems.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Caspian Sea" },
		infobox = {
