			{ "Area", "16400 km²" },
			{ "Depth", "5.8 m" },
			{ "Deepest point", "26 m" },
			{ "Volume", "100 km³" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lake Chad",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Lake in West-Central Africa",
		tags = { "Lakes", "Africa" },
		summary = "**Lake Chad** is an [[Endorheic basin|endorheic]] freshwater lake located at the junction of four countries: [[Nigeria]], [[Niger]], [[Chad]], and [[Cameroon]], in western and [[Central Africa|central Africa]] respectively, with a catchment area in excess of 1000000 km². It is an important wetland [[Ecosystem|ecosystem]] in West-Central Africa. The lake is rich in aquatic resources and is one of the important freshwater fish- producing areas in Africa.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Lake Chad is divided into deeper southern parts and shallower northern parts. The lake's main source of water is the Chari River which has many tributaries. The water level varies greatly seasonally, and the area of the lake also changes dramatically. In the 19th century, it still had an area of 28000 km². However, due to [[Climate change|climate change]] and human water diversion, the lake shrunk significantly between 1960 and 1990, the open water area decreasing from 25,000 km2 in 1960 to just 2000 km2 in 1980. The lake is now no longer estimated to be shrinking, as rainfall recovery has partially occurred. Despite the stabilisation of the lake's water volume, Lake Chad is still under threat from climate change.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Kati Thanda–Lake Eyre" },
		infobox = {
			{
				"Location",
				"[[Sahel|Sahelian zone]] at the conjunction of [[Chad]], [[Cameroon]], [[Nigeria]], and [[Niger]]",
			},
			{ "Area", "2000 km²" },
			{ "Deepest point", "2 m" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lake Erie",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "One of the Great Lakes in North America",
		tags = { "Lakes", "North America" },
		summary = "**Lake Erie** is the fourth-largest [[Lake|lake]] by surface area of the five [[Great Lakes]] in [[North America]] and the eleventh-largest globally. It is the southernmost, shallowest, and smallest by [[Volume|volume]] of the Great Lakes and also has the shortest average water residence time. At its deepest point, Lake Erie is 210 ft deep, making it the only Great Lake whose deepest point is above sea level.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Located on the [[Canada–United States border|International Boundary]] between Canada and the United States, Lake Erie's northern shore is the [[Provinces and territories of Canada|Canadian province]] of [[Ontario]], specifically the Ontario Peninsula, with the [[U.S. state|U.S. states]] of [[Michigan]], [[Ohio]], [[Pennsylvania]], and [[New York (state)|New York]] on its western, southern, and eastern shores. These jurisdictions divide the surface area of the lake with water boundaries. The largest city on the lake is [[Cleveland]], anchoring the third largest U.S. metro area in the [[Great Lakes region]], after Greater Chicago and Metro Detroit. Other major cities along the lake shore include Buffalo, New York; Erie, Pennsylvania; and Toledo, Ohio. Situated below [[Lake Huron]], Erie's primary inlet is the Detroit River.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Great Bear Lake" },
		infobox = {
