		title = "Mackenzie River",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Largest river system in Canada",
		tags = { "Rivers", "North America" },
		summary = "The **Mackenzie River** is a river in the Canadian boreal forest and [[Tundra|tundra]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Mackenzie River flows through a vast, thinly populated region of forest and [[Tundra|tundra]] entirely within the [[Northwest Territories]] in Canada, although its many tributaries reach into five other [[Provinces and territories of Canada|Canadian provinces and territories]]. Its extensive watershed drains about 20 percent of Canada. It is the largest river flowing into the Arctic from [[North America]], and including its tributaries has a total length of 4241 km, registering the 13th longest river system and 12th largest drainage basin on Earth. The ultimate source of the Mackenzie River is Thutade Lake, in the Northern Interior of [[British Columbia]]. The Mackenzie valley is believed to have been the path taken by prehistoric peoples during the initial human migration from Asia to North America over 10,000 years ago, despite sparse evidence.",
			},
