		tags = { "Islands", "Asia" },
		aliases = { "Moluccas" },
		summary = "The **Maluku Islands** or the **Moluccas** are an [[Archipelago|archipelago]] in the eastern part of [[Indonesia]]. [[Tectonics|Tectonically]] they are located on the Halmahera plate within the Molucca Sea Collision Zone. Geographically located in West Melanesia, the Moluccas have been considered a geographical and cultural intersection of [[Asia]] and [[Oceania]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The islands were known as the **Spice Islands** because of the [[Nutmeg|nutmeg]], [[Nutmeg|mace]], and [[Clove|cloves]] that were exclusively found there, the presence of which sparked European colonial interests in the 16th century. The Maluku Islands formed a single [[Provinces of Indonesia|province]] from Indonesian independence until 1999, when they were split into two provinces. A new province, North Maluku, incorporates the area between Morotai and Sula, with the arc of islands from Buru and Seram to Wetar remaining within the existing Maluku Province. North Maluku is predominantly Muslim, and its capital is Sofifi on Halmahera island. Maluku province has a larger Christian population, and its capital is Ambon.",
