		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Religious mountain in Tibet Autonomous Region in China",
		tags = { "Mountain peaks", "Asia" },
		summary = "**Mount Kailash** is a mountain in Ngari Prefecture, [[Tibet Autonomous Region]] of [[China]]. It lies in the Gangdise Shan mountain range of the Transhimalaya, in the western part of the [[Tibetan Plateau]]. The peak of Mount Kailash is located at an elevation of 6638 m, near the western trijunction of China, [[India]], and [[Nepal]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Mount Kailash is located close to Manasarovar and Rakshastal lakes. The sources of four rivers–[[Indus River|Indus]], Sutlej, [[Brahmaputra River|Brahmaputra]], and Ghaghara–lie in the vicinity of the region. The mountain is considered sacred in Bon, Buddhism, Hinduism, and Jainism. Various groups undertake [[Pilgrimage|pilgrimages]] to the mountain, which generally involves trekking towards Lake Manasarovar and a circumambulation of the mountain. While the mountain has been surveyed by climbers in the past, there has been no recorded successful ascent of the mountain. The climbing of the mountain is prohibited by the Chinese government due to its religious significance.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Krakatoa", "Mount Pinatubo" },
