		title = "Normandy",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Geographical and cultural region of northwest Europe",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Normandy** is a geographical and cultural region in northwestern Europe, roughly coextensive with the historical [[Duchy of Normandy]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Normandy comprises mainland Normandy (a part of France) and insular Normandy (mostly the British [[Channel Islands]]). It covers 30,627 km². Its population in 2017 was 3,499,280. The inhabitants of Normandy are known as Normans; the region is the historic homeland of the Norman language. Large settlements include Rouen, Caen, Le Havre and Cherbourg. The cultural region of Normandy is roughly similar to the historical Duchy of Normandy, which includes small areas now part of the departments of Mayenne and Sarthe. The Channel Islands are also historically part of Normandy; they cover 194 km² and comprise two bailiwicks: Guernsey and Jersey, which are British Crown Dependencies. Normandy\'s name comes from the settlement of the territory by [[Vikings]] ("[[Vikings|Northmen]]") starting in the 9th century, and confirmed by treaty in the 10th century between King Charles III of France and the Viking jarl Rollo.',
