	{
		title = "North India",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Region of India",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**North India** is a geographical region, loosely defined as a cultural region comprising the northern part of [[India]] wherein Indo-Aryans (speaking Indo-Aryan languages) form the prominent majority population, while [[Tibeto-Burman languages|Tibeto-Burmans]] (speaking Tibeto-Burman languages) form the significant minority population. It extends from the [[Himalayas|Himalayan mountain range]] in the north to the [[Indo-Gangetic Plain]], the Thar Desert, till Central Highlands. It occupies nearly two-quarters of the area and population of India and includes one of the three mega cities of India: [[Delhi]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In a more specific and administrative sense, North India can also be used to represent the northern Indo-Gangetic Plain within this broader expanse, to the Thar Desert. Several major rivers flow through the region including the Indus, the [[Ganges]], the [[Yamuna]] and the Narmada rivers. North India includes the states of [[Himachal Pradesh]], [[Uttarakhand]], [[Punjab, India|Punjab]], [[Haryana]], [[Rajasthan]], [[Uttar Pradesh]], and the [[Union territory|union territories]] of [[Chandigarh]], [[Delhi]], [[Jammu and Kashmir (union territory)|Jammu and Kashmir]] and [[Ladakh]]. Occasionally, states of [[Western India|Western]], Central and Eastern India are referred as "North Indian" in a broader term. The majority of people in North India speak [[Indo-Aryan languages]].',
