	{
		title = "Northeast China",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Geographical region",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Northeast China** is a geographical region of China, consisting officially of the [[Provinces of China|provinces]] Liaoning, Jilin, and Heilongjiang. The heartland of the region is the Northeast China Plain, China's largest [[Plain|plain]], with an area of over 350000 km². The region is separated from the [[Russian Far East]] to the north and east by the Amur, Argun and Ussuri Rivers; from [[North Korea]] to the south by the Yalu and Tumen Rivers; and from North China to the west by the Greater Khingan Range and Yan Mountains. It is also bounded by the Bohai Bay and [[Yellow Sea]] to the southwest, about 100 km from East China's Jiaodong Peninsula across the Bohai Strait, due to be connected via a proposed undersea tunnel.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The four prefectures of [[Inner Mongolia]] (which is part of North China) east of the Greater Khingan, i.e. Chifeng, Tongliao, Hinggan and Hulunbuir, are sometimes also considered part of Northeast China, and together with the aforementioned three provinces form what was historically known as Inner Manchuria, in contrast to the Outer Manchuria (or \"Outer Northeast\" in Chinese literatures) annexed by the [[Russian Empire]] during the mid-19th century. Northeast China is one of the country's most important breadbaskets due to its fertile black soil, producing over 20% of China's total [[Staple food|staple]] food production in 2020. It was also one of the first regions of China to undergo industrialization, and the pioneering region during the [[Planned economy|planned economy]] era that followed the founding of the People's Republic of China, earning it the honorific nickname \"the Republic's eldest son\".",
			},
		},
