	{
		title = "Outback",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sparsely populated interior of Australia",
		tags = { "Land relief", "Oceania" },
		summary = 'The **Outback** is a remote, vast, sparsely populated area of [[Australia]]. It is more remote than what Australians call “the bush”. While often envisaged as being [[Aridity|arid]], the Outback regions extend from the northern to southern Australian coastlines and encompass a number of climatic zones, including [[Tropical climate|tropical]] and monsoonal climates in northern areas, arid areas in the "red centre" and semi-arid and [[Temperate climate|temperate]] climates in southerly regions. The total population is estimated at 607,000 people.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Geographically, the Outback is unified by a combination of factors, most notably a low human population density, a largely intact [[Natural environment|natural environment]] and, in many places, low-intensity land uses, such as [[Pastoralism|pastoralism]] (livestock grazing) in which production is reliant on the natural environment. The Outback is deeply ingrained in Australian heritage, history and [[Folklore|folklore]]. In Australian art the subject of the Outback has been vogue, particularly in the 1940s. In 2009, as part of the Q150 celebrations, the [[Queensland]] Outback was announced as one of the Q150 Icons of Queensland for its role as a "natural attraction".',
			},
		},
		related = { "Great Dividing Range", "Uluru" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Population", "607,000 (Rangelands)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Oxidation state",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Hypothetical charge of an atom if all its bonds to different atoms were fully ionic",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Oxidation number" },
		summary = "In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], the **oxidation state**, or **oxidation number**, is the hypothetical charge of an atom assuming all of its [[Chemical bond|bonds]] to other atoms are fully ionic. It describes the degree of [[Redox|oxidation]] (loss of [[Electron|electrons]]) of an [[Atom|atom]] in a [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]]. Conceptually, the oxidation state may be positive, negative or zero. Oxidation state is useful for estimating the distribution of [[Electric charge|electric charge]] within molecules, especially for those containing primarily [[Ionic bonding|ionic bonds]], and less so for those with primarily [[Covalent bond|covalent bonds]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The oxidation state of an atom is capable of qualitative explanation of patterns in chemical reactions. It does not represent an atom's actual charge. The oxidation states of atoms in a given compound may vary depending on the [[Electronegativity|electronegativity]] scale used. It is also relevant to the nomenclature conventions of [[Inorganic compound|inorganic compounds]]. Oxidation states are typically represented by [[Integer|integers]] which may be positive, zero, or negative. In some cases, the average oxidation state of an element is a fraction, such as 8/3 for [[Iron|iron]] in magnetite Fe3O4 (see below). The highest known oxidation state is +9, displayed by [[Iridium|iridium]] in the tetroxoiridium(IX) cation (IrO4+). An even higher +10 is predicted to be possible for [[Platinum|platinum]] in tetroxoplatinum(X), PtO4(2+). The lowest oxidation state is −5, as for [[Boron|boron]] in Al3BC and [[Gallium|gallium]] in pentamagnesium digallide (Mg5Ga2).",
			},
		},
		related = { "Random House Webster's Unabridged Dictionary" },
	},
	{
		title = "Ozone layer",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Region of the stratosphere",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Ozone shield" },
		summary = "The **ozone layer** or **ozone shield** is a region of [[Earth]]'s [[Stratosphere|stratosphere]] that [[Absorption (electromagnetic radiation)|absorbs]] most of the [[Sun]]'s [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] radiation. It contains a high concentration of [[Ozone|ozone]] (O3) in relation to other parts of the atmosphere, although still small in relation to other gases in the stratosphere. The ozone layer peaks at 8 to 15 [[Parts-per notation|parts per million]] of ozone, while the average ozone concentration in Earth's atmosphere as a whole is about 0.3 parts per million. The ozone layer is mainly found in the lower portion of the stratosphere, from approximately 15 to 35 km above Earth, although its thickness varies seasonally and geographically.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The ozone layer was discovered in 1913 by French physicists Charles Fabry and Henri Buisson. Measurements of the sun showed that the radiation sent out from its surface and reaching the ground on Earth is usually consistent with the [[Spectrum|spectrum]] of a black body with a temperature in the range of 5500–6000 K, except that there was no radiation below a [[Wavelength|wavelength]] of about 310 nm at the [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] end of the spectrum. It was deduced that the missing radiation was being absorbed by something in the atmosphere. Eventually the spectrum of the missing radiation was matched to only one known chemical, ozone. Its properties were explored in detail by the British meteorologist G. M. B. Dobson, who developed a simple spectrophotometer (the Dobsonmeter) that could be used to measure stratospheric ozone from the ground.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pacific Coast Ranges",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⛰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Series of mountain ranges along the Pacific coast of North America",
		tags = { "Land relief", "North America" },
		summary = "The **Pacific Coast Ranges** are the series of [[Mountain range|mountain ranges]] that stretch along the West Coast of [[North America]] from [[Alaska]] south to northern and central Mexico. Although they are commonly thought to be the westernmost mountain range of the continental United States and Canada, the geologically distinct Insular Mountains of Vancouver Island lie farther west.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Pacific Coast Ranges are part of the North American Cordillera (sometimes known as the Western Cordillera, or in Canada, as the Pacific Cordillera and/or the Canadian Cordillera), which includes the [[Rocky Mountains]], the Columbia Mountains, the Interior Mountains, the Interior Plateau, the [[Sierra Nevada]], the Great Basin mountain ranges, and other ranges and various plateaus and basins. The **Pacific Coast Ranges** designation, however, only applies to the Western System of the Western Cordillera, which comprises the Saint Elias Mountains, Coast Mountains, Insular Mountains, Olympic Mountains, Cascade Range, Oregon Coast Range, California Coast Ranges, Transverse Ranges, Peninsular Ranges, and the Sierra Madre Occidental.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Great Plains" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Country", "United States, Canada, Mexico" },
			{ "Part of", "North American Cordillera" },
			{ "Highest point", "Mount Logan" },
			{ "Elevation", "5959 m" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pacific Islands Forum",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏝️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Intergovernmental organisation of island nations in the Pacific Ocean",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "PIF" },
		summary = "The **Pacific Islands Forum** (**PIF**; [[French language|French]]: Forum des îles du Pacifique; Fijian: Bose ni Yatu Pasifika; [[Māori language|Māori]]: Te Huinga Moutere o Te Moananui-a-Kiwa; Samoan: Fono a Atumotu o le Pasefika; Tahitian: Te mau motu no Patitifa; Tok Pisin: Pasifik Ailan Forum; Tongan: Fakataha 'a e Ngaahi 'Otu Motu Pasifiki) is an inter-governmental organisation which aims to enhance cooperation among countries and territories of [[Oceania]], including formation of a trade bloc and regional peacekeeping operations.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'It was founded in 1971 as the **South Pacific Forum** (**SPF**), and changed its name in 1999 to "Pacific Islands Forum", so as to be more inclusive of the Forum\'s Oceania-spanning membership of both north and south Pacific [[Island country|island countries]], including [[Australia]] and [[New Zealand]]. The mission of the Pacific Islands Forum is "to work in support of Forum member governments, to enhance the economic and social well-being of the people of the South Pacific by fostering cooperation between governments and between international agencies, and by representing the interests of Forum members in ways agreed by the Forum". Its decisions are implemented by the Pacific Islands Forum Secretariat (PIFS), which grew out of the South Pacific Bureau for Economic Co-operation (SPEC).',
			},
		},
		related = { "Winter Palace" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Established", "1971" },
			{ "Area", "8,509,959 km²" },
			{ "Population", "42.837 million" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Palestine (region)",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Geographic region in West Asia",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Historic Palestine", "Land of Palestine" },
		summary = "The region of **Palestine**, also known as **historic Palestine** or **land of Palestine**, is a geographical area in [[West Asia]]. It generally encompasses the area inhabited by the modern states of [[Israel]] and [[Palestine]], though over history it has also been used to refer to an area both smaller and larger. Other names for part or all of this region include [[Canaan]], the Promised Land, the Land of Israel, the [[Holy Land]], and Judea.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The earliest written record referring to Palestine as a geographical region is in the [[Histories (Herodotus)|Histories]] of [[Herodotus]] in the 5th century BCE, which calls the area Palaistine, referring to the territory previously held by Philistia, a state that existed in that area from the 12th to the 7th century BCE. The [[Roman Empire]] conquered the region in 63 BCE and appointed client kings to rule over it until Rome began directly ruling over the region and established a predominately-[[Jews|Jewish]] province named "Judaea" in 6 CE. Roman authorities renamed the province of Judaea to "Syria Palaestina" in c. 135 CE to punish Jews for the Bar Kokhba Revolt and permanently sever ties between Jews and the province. In 390, during the Byzantine period, the region was split into the provinces of Palaestina Prima, Palaestina Secunda, and Palaestina Salutaris.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Largest city", "[[Jerusalem]]" },
			{ "Languages", "Arabic, [[Modern Hebrew|Hebrew]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Palk Strait",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Strait between India and Sri Lanka",
		tags = { "Bodies of water", "Straits" },
		summary = "**Palk Strait** is a [[Strait|strait]] between the Pamban Island in the [[States and union territories of India|Indian state]] of [[Tamil Nadu]] and the Mannar Island in the Northern Province of Sri Lanka. It links the [[Bay of Bengal]] to the Laccadive Sea. It is bound by a [[Archipelago|chain]] of low lying islands and shoals that are collectively called Ram Setu (Adam's Bridge) on one side and the Gulf of Mannar on the other side. The Palk Bay forms the south-western part of the strait. It stretches for about 137 km and is 64 to 137 km wide. It is named after Robert Palk, who was a governor of [[Madras Presidency|Madras]] (1755–1763) during the [[Company rule in India|British Raj]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Several rivers including the Vaigai flow into the strait. The shallow waters in the strait and the presence of many small islands and [[Reef|reefs]] make it difficult for large ships to pass through, although fishing boats and small craft navigate the waters. [[Dredging]] the sea to make it deeper for navigation and plans for a bridge over the waters have been proposed.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Location", "[[Bay of Bengal]]–Laccadive Sea" },
			{ "Type", "[[Strait]]" },
			{ "Name origin", "Robert Palk" },
			{ "Length", "137 km" },
			{ "Deepest point", "35 m" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pamir Mountains",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⛰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mountain range in Central Asia",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Pamir Mountains** are a mountain range in [[Central Asia]] and [[South Asia]]. They are located at a junction with other notable mountain ranges, namely the [[Tian Shan]], the [[Karakoram]], the [[Kunlun Mountains|Kunlun]], the [[Hindu Kush]], and the [[Himalayas]]. They are among the world's highest mountains.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Much of the range lies in the Gorno-Badakhshan region of [[Tajikistan]]. Spanning the border parts of four countries, to the south, they border the Hindu Kush mountains along [[Afghanistan]]\'s Wakhan Corridor in Badakhshan Province and Chitral and [[Gilgit-Baltistan]] regions of [[Pakistan]]. To the north, they join the Tian Shan mountains along the Alay Valley of [[Kyrgyzstan]]. To the east, they extend to the range that includes [[China]]\'s Kongur Tagh, in the "Eastern Pamirs", separated by the Yarkand valley from the Kunlun Mountains. Since the [[Victorian era]], they have been known as the "Roof of the World", presumably a translation from [[Persian language|Persian]].',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Country", "[[Tajikistan]], [[Kyrgyzstan]], [[Afghanistan]], [[China]], [[Pakistan]]" },
			{ "Highest point", "Kongur Tagh" },
			{ "Elevation", "7649 m" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pampas",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "South American low grasslands",
		tags = { "Land relief", "South America" },
		aliases = { "Pampas Plains" },
		summary = "The **Pampas**, also known as the **Pampas Plains**, are fertile low [[Grassland|grasslands]] in southern [[South America]] that cover more than 1200000 km² and include the [[Argentina|Argentine]] [[Provinces of Argentina|provinces]] of Buenos Aires, La Pampa, Santa Fe, Entre Ríos, and Córdoba; all of [[Uruguay]]; and [[Brazil]]'s southernmost [[Federative units of Brazil|state]], Rio Grande do Sul. The vast plains are a natural region, interrupted only by the low Ventana and Tandil hills, near Bahía Blanca and Tandil (Argentina), with a height of 1300 m and 500 m, respectively. This ecoregion has been changed by humans, especially since the release of animals like cattle, pigs, and especially sheep onto these plains.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The climate is temperate, with [[Precipitation|precipitation]] of 600 to 1200 mm that is more or less evenly distributed throughout the year, making the soils appropriate for [[Agriculture|agriculture]]. The area is also one of the distinct physiography provinces of the larger [[Paraná River|Paraná]]–Paraguay plain division. It is considered that the limit of the Pampas plain is to the north with the Atlantic Forest and the [[Gran Chaco|Great Chaco Plain]], to the west with the Pampas Mountains and the Cuyo Region, and to the south with [[Patagonia]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
