	{
		title = "Red Sea",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Arm of the Indian Ocean between Asia and Africa",
		tags = { "Bodies of water", "Oceans and seas" },
		summary = "The **Red Sea** is a mediterranean sea lying between the Arabian Peninsula and Africa. It is an inlet of the [[Indian Ocean]]. Its connection to the ocean is in the south, through the Bab-el-Mandeb Strait and the [[Gulf of Aden]]. To the north of the Red Sea lies the [[Sinai Peninsula]], the [[Gulf of Aqaba]], and the Gulf of Suez, which leads to the [[Suez Canal]]. It is underlain by the Red Sea Rift, which is part of the [[Great Rift Valley]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Red Sea has a surface area of roughly 438,000 km², is about 2250 km long, and 355 km across at its widest point. It has an average depth of 490 m, and in the central Suakin Trough, it reaches its maximum depth of 2730 m. The Red Sea is quite shallow, with approximately 40% of its area being less than 100 m deep, and approximately 25% being less than 50 m deep. The extensive shallow shelves are noted for their marine life and [[Coral reef|corals]]. More than 1,000 [[Invertebrate|invertebrate]] species and 200 types of soft and hard coral live in the sea. The Red Sea is the world's northernmost [[Tropics|tropical]] sea and has been designated a Global 200 ecoregion.",
			},
