		title = "Regional power",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "State wielding power within a geographical region",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'A **regional power** is a [[Sovereign state|sovereign state]] that exercises significant power in [[International relations|international relations]] within its [[Region|geographical region]]. States that wield unrivaled power and influence within a region of the world possess regional hegemony. These states operate across the international power spectrum – small, [[Middle power|middle]], and [[Great power|great]] – with the latter two flexing regional influence as "global swing states".',
	},
	{
		title = "Remote sensing",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Obtaining information through non-contact sensors",
		tags = { "Basics", "Cartography" },
		summary = "**Remote sensing** is the acquisition of [[Information|information]] about an [[Physical object|object]] or [[Phenomenon|phenomenon]] without making physical contact with the object, in contrast to in situ or on-site [[Observation|observation]]. The term is applied especially to acquiring information about [[Earth]] and other [[Planet|planets]]. Remote sensing is used in numerous fields, including [[Geophysics|geophysics]], [[Geography|geography]], land [[Surveying|surveying]] and most [[Earth science]] disciplines (e.g. exploration geophysics, [[Hydrology|hydrology]], [[Ecology|ecology]], [[Meteorology|meteorology]], [[Oceanography|oceanography]], glaciology, [[Geology|geology]]). It also has military, intelligence, commercial, economic, planning, and humanitarian applications, among others.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In current usage, the term remote sensing generally refers to the use of [[Satellite|satellite]]- or airborne-based [[Sensor|sensor]] technologies to detect and classify objects on Earth. It includes the surface and the [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]] and [[Ocean|oceans]], based on propagated signals (e.g. [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]]). It may be split into "active" remote sensing (when a signal is emitted by a sensor mounted on a satellite or aircraft to the object and its reflection is detected by the sensor) and "passive" remote sensing (when the reflection of sunlight is detected by the sensor).',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Republicanism",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Political ideology centered on citizenship in a state organized as a republic",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Republicanism** is a [[Ideology|political ideology]] that promotes the [[Republic|republican]] system of government, in which sovereignty resides in the people and their elected representatives, as opposed to [[Hereditary monarchy|hereditary monarchy]] or other forms of absolute personal power. It is founded on several key principles, including civic virtue, active political participation, [[Civics|civic education]], the fight against corruption (anti-corruption), a preference for a balanced and mixed constitution, government limited by constitutional laws, freedom as non-domination, and a commitment to the [[Rule of law|rule of law]] and the common good.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Historically, it emphasizes the idea of self-governance and ranges from the rule of a representative minority or [[Aristocracy|aristocracy]] to popular sovereignty. It has had different definitions and interpretations which vary significantly based on historical context and methodological approach. In countries ruled by a monarch or similar ruler, or with a monarch whose role is primarily ceremonial such as the United Kingdom, "republicanism" is simply the wish to replace the hereditary monarchy by some form of elected republic. Republicanism may also refer to the non-ideological scientific approach to politics and governance.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Rhine",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Major river in Western Europe",
		tags = { "Rivers", "Europe" },
		summary = "The **Rhine** is one of the major rivers of [[Europe]]. The river begins in the Swiss canton of Graubünden in the southeastern Swiss Alps. It forms part of the Swiss–Liechtenstein border, then part of the Swiss–Austrian border. From Lake Constance downstream, it forms part of the Swiss–German border. After that, the Rhine defines much of the Franco-German border. It then flows in a mostly northerly direction through the German Rhineland. Finally, the Rhine turns to flow predominantly west to enter the [[Netherlands]], eventually emptying into the [[North Sea]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It drains an area of 185,000 km2, it is the second-longest river in [[Central Europe|Central]] and [[Western Europe]] (after the [[Danube]]), at about 1230 km with an average discharge of about 2900 m³/s. It also contains the most powerful waterfall in Europe, the Rhine Falls. The International Commission for the Hydrology of the Rhine Basin (CHR) and EUWID contend that the river could experience a massive decrease in volume, or even dry up completely in case of drought, within the next 30 to 80 years, as a result of the climate crisis. The Rhine and the Danube comprised much of the [[Roman Empire]]'s northern inland boundary, and the Rhine has been a vital navigable waterway bringing trade and goods deep inland since those days. The various castles and defenses built along it attest to its prominence as a waterway in the [[Holy Roman Empire]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
