	{
		title = "Ring of Fire",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔥",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tectonic belt of earthquakes and volcanoes",
		tags = { "Physical geography", "Land relief" },
		aliases = { "Pacific Ring of Fire", "Rim of Fire", "Girdle of Fire" },
		summary = "The **Ring of Fire** (also known as the **Pacific Ring of Fire**, the **Rim of Fire**, the **Girdle of Fire** or the **Circum-Pacific belt**) is a [[Tectonics|tectonic]] belt of [[Earthquake|earthquakes]] and [[Volcano|volcanoes]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is about 25,000 mi long and up to about 500 km wide, and surrounds most of the [[Pacific Ocean]]. The Ring of Fire contains between 750 and 915 active or dormant volcanoes, around two-thirds of the world total. The exact number of volcanoes within the Ring of Fire depends on which regions are included. About 90% of the world's earthquakes, including most of its largest, occur within the belt. The Ring of Fire is not a single geological structure. It was created by the [[Subduction|subduction]] of different tectonic plates at convergent boundaries around the Pacific Ocean. These include the Antarctic, Nazca and Cocos plates subducting beneath the South American plate; the Pacific and Juan de Fuca plates beneath the North American plate; the Philippine plate beneath the Eurasian plate; and a complex boundary between the Pacific and Australian plate.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Transantarctic Mountains" },
	},
	{
		title = "Rio Grande",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Major river forming part of the United States and Mexico border",
		tags = { "Rivers", "North America" },
		aliases = { "Río Bravo", "Del Norte", "Tó Ba'áadi" },
		summary = "The **Rio Grande** ( or ), in the United States, or the **Río Bravo** (**del Norte**), in Mexico, also known as **Tó Ba'áadi** in Navajo, is one of the principal rivers (along with the [[Colorado River]]) in the [[Southwestern United States]] and in northern Mexico. The length of the Rio Grande is 1896 mi, making it the fourth longest river in the United States and in [[North America]] by main stem. It originates in south-central [[Colorado]], in the United States, and flows to the [[Gulf of Mexico]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Rio Grande [[Drainage basin|drainage basin]] (watershed) has an area of 182200 sq mi; however, the [[Endorheic basin|endorheic basins]] that are adjacent to and within the greater drainage basin of the Rio Grande increase the total drainage-basin area to 336000 sq mi. The Rio Grande with its fertile valley, along with its tributaries, is a vital water source for seven U.S. and Mexican states, and flows primarily through arid and semi-arid lands. After traversing the length of [[New Mexico]], the Rio Grande becomes the [[Mexico–United States border]], between the U.S. state of [[Texas]] and the northern Mexican states of Chihuahua and Coahuila, Nuevo León and Tamaulipas; a short segment of the Rio Grande is a partial state-boundary between the U.S. states of New Mexico and Texas.",
