	{
		title = "Seawater",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Water from a sea or an ocean",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Sea water" },
		summary = "**Seawater**, or **sea water**, is [[Water|water]] from a [[Ocean|sea]] or [[Ocean|ocean]]. On average, seawater in the world's oceans has a [[Salinity|salinity]] of about 3.5% (35 g/L, 35 ppt, 600 mM). This means that every kilogram (roughly one liter by volume) of seawater has approximately 35 g of dissolved salts (predominantly [[Sodium|sodium]] (Na+) and [[Chloride|chloride]] (Cl-) [[Ion|ions]]). The average density at the surface is 1.025 kg/L. Seawater is [[Density|denser]] than both [[Fresh water|fresh water]] and pure water (density 1.0 kg/L at 4 °C) because the dissolved salts increase the mass by a larger proportion than the volume. The freezing point of seawater decreases as salt concentration increases.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"At typical salinity, it [[Freezing|freezes]] at about -2 °C. The coldest seawater still in the liquid state ever recorded was found in 2010, in a stream under an [[Antarctic]] [[Glacier|glacier]]: the measured temperature was -2.6 °C. Seawater [[PH|pH]] is typically limited to a range between 7.5 and 8.4. However, there is no universally accepted reference pH-scale for seawater and the difference between measurements based on different reference scales may be up to 0.14 units.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Eastern Time Zone" },
