	{
		title = "Seine",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Major river in northern France",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Seine** is a 777 km [[River|river]] in northern [[France]]. Its [[Drainage basin|drainage basin]] is in the Paris Basin (a geological relative lowland) covering most of northern France. It rises at Source-Seine, 30 km northwest of Dijon in northeastern France in the Langres plateau, flowing through [[Paris]] and into the [[English Channel]] at Le Havre (and Honfleur on the left bank). It is navigable by ocean-going vessels as far as Rouen, 120 km from the sea. Over 60 percent of its length, as far as Burgundy, is negotiable by large barges and most tour boats, and nearly its whole length is available for recreational boating; excursion boats offer sightseeing tours of the river banks in the capital city, Paris.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are 37 bridges in Paris across the Seine (the most famous of which are the Pont Alexandre III and the Pont Neuf) and dozens more outside the city.",
