	{
		title = "South Asia",
		category = "Geography",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sub-region of the Asian continent",
		tags = { "Regions and country subdivisions", "Asia" },
		summary = "**South Asia** is the southern sub-region of [[Asia]] that is defined in both geographical and [[Ethnicity|ethnic]]-[[Culture|cultural]] terms. South Asia, with a population of 2.04 billion, contains a quarter (25%) of the world's population. As commonly conceptualised, the modern [[State (polity)|states]] of South Asia include [[Bangladesh]], [[Bhutan]], [[India]], the [[Maldives]], [[Nepal]], [[Pakistan]], and [[Sri Lanka]], with [[Afghanistan]] also often included, which may also be classified as part of [[Central Asia]]. South Asia borders [[East Asia]] to the northeast, Central Asia to the northwest, [[West Asia]] to the west and [[Southeast Asia]] to the east.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Southeast Asia and Maritime South Asia are the only subregions of Asia that lie partly within the [[Southern Hemisphere]]. The [[British Indian Ocean Territory]] and two out of 26 atolls of the Maldives in South Asia lie entirely within the Southern Hemisphere. Topographically, it is dominated by the [[Indian subcontinent]] and is bounded by the [[Indian Ocean]] in the south, and the [[Himalayas]], [[Karakoram]], and [[Pamir Mountains]] in the north. [[Neolithic|Settled life]] emerged on the Indian subcontinent in the western margins of the [[Indus River]] Basin 9,000 years ago, evolving gradually into the [[Indus Valley Civilisation]] of the third millennium BCE. By 1200 BCE, an [[Proto-language|archaic form]] of [[Sanskrit]], an Indo-European language, had diffused into India from the northwest, with the [[Dravidian languages]] being supplanted in the northern and western regions.",
			},
		},
