		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Uluru is sacred to the Pitjantjatjara, the [[Aboriginal Australians|Aboriginal people]] of the area, known as the Aṉangu. The area around the formation is home to an abundance of springs, [[Depression (geology)|waterholes]], rock caves and [[Cave painting|ancient paintings]]. Uluru is listed as a [[UNESCO]] [[World Heritage Site]]. Uluru and Kata Tjuta (also known as the Olgas) are the two major features of the Uluṟu-Kata Tjuṯa National Park. Uluru is one of Australia's most recognisable natural landmarks and has been a popular destination for tourists since the late 1930s. It is also one of the most important indigenous sites in Australia.",
