	{
		title = "Feudalism",
		category = "History",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Legal and military structure in medieval Europe",
		tags = { "Post-classical" },
		aliases = { "Feudal system", "Feudal", "Feudal monarchy" },
		summary = "**Feudalism**, also known as the **feudal system**, was a combination of various generally cohesive and convergent legal, economic, military, cultural, religious, and political institutions and customs that flourished in [[Middle Ages|medieval Europe]] from the 9th to 15th centuries. Broadly defined, it was a way of structuring society around relationships derived from the holding of land in exchange for service or labour.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The classic definition, by François Louis Ganshof (1944), describes a set of reciprocal legal and military obligations of the warrior [[Nobility|nobility]] and revolved around the key concepts of lords, [[Vassal|vassals]], and fiefs. A broader definition, as described by Marc Bloch (1939), includes not only the obligations of the warrior nobility but the obligations of all three estates of the realm: the [[Nobility|nobility]], the [[Clergy|clergy]], and the [[Peasant|peasantry]], all of whom were bound by a system of manorialism; this is sometimes referred to as a "feudal society". Although it is derived from the Latin word or (fief), which was used during the medieval period, the term feudalism and the system it describes were not conceived of as a formal political system by the people who lived during the Middle Ages.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "First Bulgarian Empire",
		category = "History",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "681–1018 state in Southeast Europe",
		tags = { "Europe", "Eastern Europe and Central" },
		summary = "During the 9th and 10th century, Bulgaria reached the height of its power and stretched from the Danube Bend to the [[Black Sea]] and from the [[Dnieper]] River to the [[Adriatic Sea]], becoming a major power capable of competing with the Byzantine Empire.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As the state solidified its position in the Balkans, it entered into a centuries-long interaction, sometimes friendly and sometimes hostile, with the Byzantine Empire. Bulgaria became Byzantium's primary threat to its north, resulting in several wars. The two powers also enjoyed periods of peace and cooperation, most notably the alliance during the Second Arab Siege of Constantinople, where the Bulgarian army broke the siege and destroyed the Arab army, thus preventing an Arab invasion of Southeastern Europe. [[Byzantine Empire|Byzantium]] had a strong cultural influence on Bulgaria, which also led to the eventual adoption of Christianity in 864. After the disintegration of the Avar Khaganate, Bulgaria expanded its territory northwest to the Pannonian Plain.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Era", "Middle Ages" },
