	{
		title = "History of Ethiopia",
		category = "History",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "History by country", "Africa" },
		summary = 'Ethiopia is one of the oldest countries in Africa; the emergence of Ethiopian civilization dates back thousands of years. Abyssinia or rather "Ze Etiyopia" was inhabited by Abyssinians (Habesha), specifically the Amhara and Tigrayans (speakers of Semitic languages) and the Cushitic, Oromo and Agaw. The Eastern [[Escarpment|escarpment]] of the Ethiopian highlands and the lowlands were the home of the various Muslim groups that formed the Ifat and [[Adal Sultanate|Adal]] sultanates such as the Argobba, Afars, Harari/Harla and Somalis. In the central and south were found the ancient Sidama, Semitic Gurage and Omotic Wolaita, among others.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"One of the first kingdoms to rise to power in the territory was the kingdom of Damot in the 10th century BC, which established its capital at Yeha. In the first century AD, the Aksumite Kingdom rose to power in the modern Tigray Region with its capital at Aksum and grew into a major power on the Red Sea, subjugating South Arabia and Meroe and its surrounding areas. In the early fourth century, during the reign of Ezana, Christianity was declared the state religion and not long after, the Aksumite empire fell into decline with the rise of Islam in the Arabian peninsula, which slowly shifted trade away from the Christian Aksum. It eventually became isolated, its economy slumped and Aksum's commercial domination of the region ended. The Aksumites gave way to the Zagwe dynasty, who established a new capital at Lalibela before giving way to the Solomonic dynasty in the 13th century.",
			},
