	{
		title = "History of Vietnam",
		category = "History",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "History by country", "Asia" },
		summary = "[[Vietnam]], with its coastal strip, rugged mountainous interior, and two major deltas, became home to numerous cultures throughout history. Its strategic geographical position in Southeast Asia also made it a crossroads of trade and a focal point of conflict, contributing to its complex and eventful past. During the Paleolithic period, the first Ancient East Eurasian hunter-gatherers arrived at least 40,000 years ago. Since the [[Neolithic]] period, [[Austroasiatic languages|Austroasiatic]], as well as [[Austronesian languages|Austronesian]], Hmong–Mien, [[Kra–Dai languages|Kra–Dai]], and [[Sino-Tibetan languages|Sino-Tibetan]] peoples that lived across Southeast Asia and Southern China, contributed to the agricultural development and ethnolinguistic landscape of Vietnam.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In the first millennium BCE the Đông Sơn culture emerged, based on rice cultivation and focused on the indigenous chiefdoms of Văn Lang and Âu Lạc. Following the 111 BCE Han conquest of Nanyue, much of Vietnam came under Chinese dominance for a thousand years. The period nonetheless saw numerous uprisings, and Vietnamese kingdoms occasionally enjoyed de facto independence. Buddhism and Hinduism arrived by the 2nd century CE, making Vietnam the first place which shared influences of both [[Chinese culture|Chinese]] and Indian cultures. Independence was regained when the Ngô dynasty was established in 939, and the next millennium saw a succession of local dynasties: Ngô, Đinh, Anterior Lê, Lý, [[Trần dynasty|Trần]], Hồ, [[Lê dynasty|Later Lê]], Mạc, Revival Lê, Tây Sơn, and finally [[Nguyễn dynasty|Nguyễn]].",
