	{
		title = "History of mathematics",
		category = "History",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "➗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "History by subject matter", "History of science and technology" },
		summary = "The **history of mathematics** deals with the origin of discoveries in [[Mathematics|mathematics]] and the mathematical methods and notation of the past. Before the modern age and worldwide spread of knowledge, written examples of new mathematical developments have come to light only in a few locales. From 3000 BC the [[Mesopotamia|Mesopotamian]] states of [[Sumer]], Akkad and [[Assyria]], followed closely by [[Ancient Egypt]] and the Levantine state of [[Ebla]] began using [[Arithmetic|arithmetic]], [[Algebra|algebra]] and [[Geometry|geometry]] for taxation, [[Commerce|commerce]], trade, and in [[Astronomy|astronomy]], to record time and formulate [[Calendar|calendars]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The earliest mathematical texts available are from Mesopotamia and [[Ancient Egypt|Egypt]] – Plimpton 322 (Babylonian c. 2000 – 1900 BC), the Rhind Mathematical Papyrus (Egyptian c. 1800 BC) and the Moscow Mathematical Papyrus (Egyptian c. 1890 BC). All these texts mention the so-called Pythagorean triples, so, by inference, the [[Pythagorean theorem]] seems to be the most ancient and widespread mathematical development, after basic arithmetic and geometry. [[Ancient Greek mathematics|Greek mathematics]] greatly refined the methods (especially through the introduction of deductive reasoning and mathematical rigor in [[Mathematical proof|proofs]]) and expanded the subject matter of mathematics. The [[Roman people|ancient Romans]] used [[Applied mathematics|applied mathematics]] in [[Surveying|surveying]], structural engineering, [[Mechanical engineering|mechanical engineering]], bookkeeping, creation of [[Lunar calendar|lunar]] and solar calendars, and even [[Roman art|arts and crafts]].",
			},
