	{
		title = "History of opera",
		category = "History",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🎭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Aspect of musical history",
		tags = { "History by subject matter", "History of art" },
		summary = "The **history of opera** has a relatively short duration within the context of the [[History of music|history of music]] in general. It appeared in 1597, when the first [[Opera|opera]], Dafne, by Jacopo Peri, was created. Since then it has developed parallel to the various musical currents that have followed one another over time up to the present day, generally linked to the current concept of classical music.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Opera is a musical genre that combines symphonic music, usually performed by an orchestra, and a written dramatic text—expressed in the form of a [[Libretto|libretto]]—interpreted vocally by singers of different tessitura: [[Tenor|tenor]], baritone, and bass for the male register, and [[Soprano|soprano]], mezzo-soprano, and contralto for the female, in addition to the so-called white voices (those of children) or in falsetto (castrato, countertenor). Generally, the musical work contains overtures, interludes and musical accompaniments, while the sung part can be in choir or solo, duet, trio, or various combinations, in different structures such as [[Recitative|recitative]] or [[Aria|aria]]. There are various genres, such as classical opera, chamber opera, operetta, musical, singspiel, and zarzuela.",
			},
