	{
		title = "History of science",
		category = "History",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "History by subject matter", "History of science and technology" },
		summary = "The **history of science** covers the development of [[Science|science]] from [[Ancient history|ancient times]] to the [[Present|present]]. It encompasses all three major [[Branches of science|branches of science]]: [[Natural science|natural]], [[Social science|social]], and formal. Protoscience, early sciences, and natural philosophies such as [[Alchemy|alchemy]] and [[Astrology|astrology]] that existed during the [[Bronze Age]], [[Iron Age]], [[Classical antiquity|classical antiquity]] and the [[Middle Ages]], declined after the emergence of modern sciences during the [[Scientific Revolution]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The earliest roots of scientific thinking and practice can be traced to [[Ancient Egypt]] and [[Mesopotamia]] during the 3rd and 2nd millennia BCE. These civilizations' contributions to [[Mathematics|mathematics]], [[Astronomy|astronomy]], and [[Medicine|medicine]] influenced later Greek [[Natural philosophy|natural philosophy]] of classical antiquity, wherein formal attempts were made to provide explanations of events in the [[Universe|physical world]] based on natural causes. After the [[Fall of the Western Roman Empire|fall of the Western Roman Empire]], knowledge of Greek conceptions of the world deteriorated in Latin-speaking [[Western Europe]] during the early centuries (400 to 1000 CE) of the Middle Ages, but continued to thrive in the [[Greek language|Greek]]-speaking [[Byzantine Empire]]. Aided by translations of Greek texts, the [[Hellenistic period|Hellenistic]] worldview was preserved and absorbed into the [[Arabic]]-speaking Muslim world during the [[Islamic Golden Age]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "History of geology" },
	},
	{
		title = "History of sociology",
		category = "History",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "History by subject matter" },
		summary = "[[Sociology]] as a scholarly discipline emerged, primarily out of [[Age of Enlightenment|Enlightenment]] thought, as a positivist [[Science|science]] of [[Society|society]] shortly after the [[French Revolution]]. Its genesis owed to various key movements in the [[Philosophy of science|philosophy of science]] and the [[Epistemology|philosophy of knowledge]], arising in reaction to such issues as [[Modernity|modernity]], [[Capitalism|capitalism]], [[Urbanization|urbanization]], rationalization, secularization, [[Colonization|colonization]] and [[Imperialism|imperialism]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"During its nascent stages, within the late 19th century, sociological deliberations took particular interest in the emergence of the modern [[Nation state|nation state]], including its constituent [[Institution|institutions]], units of [[Socialization|socialization]], and its means of [[Surveillance|surveillance]]. As such, an emphasis on the concept of modernity, rather than the Enlightenment, often distinguishes sociological discourse from that of classical [[Political philosophy|political philosophy]]. Likewise, social analysis in a broader sense has origins in the common stock of philosophy, therefore pre-dating the sociological field. Various quantitative [[Social research|social research]] techniques have become common tools for governments, businesses, and organizations, and have also found use in the other social sciences. Divorced from theoretical explanations of social dynamics, this has given [[Social research|social research]] a degree of autonomy from the discipline of sociology.",
			},
		},
		related = { "History of science", "History of art" },
	},
	{
		title = "History of spaceflight",
		category = "History",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "History by subject matter", "History of science and technology" },
		summary = "[[Spaceflight]] began in the 20th century following theoretical and practical breakthroughs by Konstantin Tsiolkovsky, [[Robert H. Goddard]], and Hermann Oberth, each of whom published works proposing [[Rocket|rockets]] as the means for spaceflight. The [[Soviet Union]] took the lead in the post-war [[Space Race]], launching the [[Sputnik 1|first satellite]], the first animal, the [[Yuri Gagarin|first human]] and the [[Valentina Tereshkova|first woman]] into [[Orbit|orbit]]. The United States landed the [[Apollo 11|first men on the Moon]] in 1969. Through the late 20th century, France, the United Kingdom, Japan, and China were also working on projects to reach space.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Following the end of the Space Race, spaceflight has been characterized by greater international cooperation, cheaper access to [[Low Earth orbit|low Earth orbit]] and an expansion of commercial ventures. Interplanetary probes have visited all of the planets in the [[Solar System]], and humans have remained in orbit for long periods aboard space stations such as [[Mir]] and the [[International Space Station|ISS]]. Most recently, China has emerged as the third nation with the capability to launch independent crewed missions, while operators in the commercial sector have developed reusable booster systems and craft launched from airborne platforms. In 2020, [[SpaceX]] became the first commercial operator to successfully launch a crewed mission to the [[International Space Station]] with Crew Dragon Demo-2.",
			},
		},
		related = { "History of science", "History of mathematics" },
	},
	{
		title = "History of sport",
		category = "History",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏅",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of the development of sport over time",
		tags = { "History by subject matter", "History of games and sport" },
		aliases = { "History of sports" },
		summary = "The **history of sports** extends back to the Ancient world in 7000 BC. The physical activity that developed into sports had early links with warfare and [[Entertainment|entertainment]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The study of the [[History|history]] of sport provides insights into [[Sociology|social change]] and the evolution of [[Sport|sport]] itself, as sporting activities have often been linked to the development of basic human skills (see also [[Play (activity)|play]]). However, the further one traces back into history, the scarcer the evidence becomes, making it increasingly difficult to support theories about the origins and purposes of sport. As far back as the beginnings of sport, it was related to military training. For example, competition was used as a mean to determine whether individuals were fit and useful for service. [[Team sport|Team sports]] were used to train and to prove the capability to fight in the military and also to work together as a team (military unit).",
			},
		},
		related = { "History of science", "History of art" },
