	{
		title = "Macedonia (ancient kingdom)",
		category = "History",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ancient Greek kingdom in the southern Balkans",
		tags = { "Ancient", "Europe" },
		aliases = { "Macedonia", "Macedon" },
		summary = "**Macedonia**, also called **Macedon**, was an [[Classical antiquity|ancient]] [[Monarchy|kingdom]] on the periphery of [[Archaic Greece|Archaic]] and [[Classical Greece|Classical]] Greece, which later became the dominant state of Hellenistic Greece. The kingdom was founded and initially ruled by the royal Argead dynasty, which was followed by the Antipatrid and Antigonid dynasties. Home to the [[Ancient Macedonians|ancient Macedonians]], the earliest kingdom was centred on the northeastern part of the Greek peninsula, and bordered by Epirus to the southwest, Illyria to the northwest, Paeonia to the north, Thrace to the east and Thessaly to the south.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Before the 4th century BC, Macedonia was a small kingdom centered in Aegae, outside of the area dominated by the [[Polis|city-states]] of [[Classical Athens|Athens]], [[Sparta]], and Thebes, and briefly subordinate to the [[Achaemenid Empire]]. During the reign of king [[Philip II of Macedon|PhilipII]] (359–336 BC), Macedonia with its capital in Pella, subdued mainland Greece, Paeonia, and the Thracian [[Odrysian kingdom]] through conquest and diplomacy. With a reformed army containing phalanxes wielding the sarissa pike, PhilipII defeated the old powers of Athens and Thebes in the Battle of Chaeronea in 338BC. PhilipII's son [[Alexander the Great]], leading a federation of Greek states founded by his father, accomplished the objective of commanding the whole of Greece when he destroyed Thebes after the city revolted.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Era", "[[Classical antiquity|Classical Antiquity]]" },
			{ "Government", "Hereditary monarchy" },
			{ "Capital", "Aigai (before 399 BC), Pella (399–168 BC)" },
			{ "Currency", "Tetradrachm" },
			{ "People are called", "[[Ancient Macedonians|Macedonian]], Makedon" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Magadha",
		category = "History",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Region and Mahajanapada in ancient eastern India",
		tags = { "Asia", "South Asia" },
		aliases = { "Mahajanapada Period" },
		summary = "**Mahajanapada Period** Magadha among the 16 great ancient kingdoms.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"**Haryanka Dynasty** Early territorial expansion under Bimbisara and Ajatashatru. **Nanda Empire** Magadha's dominance over Northern India. **Maurya Empire** Pan-Indian expansion originating from Pataliputra. **Shunga Empire** Post-Mauryan dynasty controlling central and eastern India. **Gupta Empire** The Classical Age centered in Magadha. **Magadha** was a region and kingdom in ancient India, based in the [[Indo-Gangetic Plain|eastern Ganges Plain]]. It was one of the sixteen Mahajanapadas during the Second Urbanization period. The region was ruled by several dynasties, which overshadowed, conquered, and incorporated the other Mahajanapadas. Magadha played an important role in the development of Jainism and Buddhism and formed the core of the Haryankan Empire, Nanda Empire, Mauryan Empire, Shunga Empire and [[Gupta Empire]].",
			},
