	{
		title = "Mamluk",
		category = "History",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Asia", "West Asia" },
		summary = "Upon completion of their training they were freed, but remained part of the ruling military caste, forming elite regiments and, in some periods and regions, rising to sovereign power.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'They also recruited from the [[Egyptians]]. The "Mamluk/Ghulam Phenomenon", as David Ayalon dubbed the creation of the specific warrior class, was of great political importance; for one thing, it endured for nearly 1,000 years, from the 9th century to the early 19th. (See: Ghilman.) Over time, Mamluks became a powerful military knightly class in various Muslim societies that were controlled by dynastic Arab rulers. Particularly in [[Egypt]] and [[Syria]], but also in the [[Ottoman Empire]], [[Levant]], [[Iraq|Mesopotamia]], and India, Mamluks held political and military power. In some cases, they attained the rank of [[Sultan|sultan]], while in others they held regional power as [[Emir|emirs]] or beys. Most notably, Mamluk factions seized the sultanate centered on [[Egypt]] and [[Syria]], and controlled it as the Mamluk Sultanate (1250–1517).',
