	{
		title = "Sengoku period",
		category = "History",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Era in Japanese history (1467–1615)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Sengoku period** was an era of civil war, political fragmentation and social change in Japanese history. It conventionally began with the Ōnin War in 1467, although the Kyōtoku incident of 1454 and the Meiō incident of 1493 have also been proposed as starting points. Its end is variously dated to [[Oda Nobunaga]]'s entry into [[Kyoto]] in 1568, the fall of the Ashikaga shogunate in 1573, [[Toyotomi Hideyoshi]]'s unification of Japan in 1590, the Battle of Sekigahara in 1600 or the Siege of Osaka in 1615. The period overlaps with the later Muromachi period and, under broader definitions, the Azuchi–Momoyama period.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The Ōnin War devastated much of Kyoto and accelerated the decline of the Ashikaga shogunate. Although the Ashikaga shōguns remained in office until 1573, their ability to control the provinces became increasingly limited. Provincial governors, their deputies and local warrior families established autonomous regional regimes. The most successful rulers, known as [[Daimyo|sengoku daimyō]], built castles and castle towns, conducted land surveys, issued territorial laws and organized warriors more directly under their authority. The period is associated with gekokujō, or "the low overthrowing the high", as retainers displaced their lords and junior branches contested control of warrior families. It also saw the growth of armed leagues known as ikki, including village and provincial associations as well as the Buddhist Ikkō-ikki.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Serfdom",
		category = "History",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Status of peasants under feudalism",
		tags = { "Post-classical" },
		summary = "It developed during [[Late antiquity|late antiquity]] and the [[Early Middle Ages]] in Europe and lasted in some countries until the mid-19th century and became the status of many [[Peasant|peasants]] under [[Feudalism|feudalism]], specifically relating to manorialism and similar systems.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Serfs who occupied a plot of land were required to work for the lord of the manor who owned that land. In return, they were entitled to protection, justice, and the right to cultivate certain fields within the manor to maintain their own subsistence. Serfs were often required not only to work on the lord\'s fields, but in his mines and forests and to labour to maintain roads. The manor formed the basic unit of feudal society, and the lord of the manor and the villeins, and to a certain extent the serfs, were bound legally: by taxation in the case of the former, and economically and socially in the latter. Conversely, serfdom grew stronger in [[Central Europe|Central]] and [[Eastern Europe]], where it had been less common (this phenomenon was known as "second serfdom"). In Eastern Europe, the institution persisted until the mid-19th century.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Post-classical history", "Nobility" },
	},
	{
		title = "Shang dynasty",
		category = "History",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chinese dynasty (c. 1600 – c. 1046 BC)",
		tags = { "Asia", "East Asia" },
		aliases = { "Yin dynasty" },
		summary = "The **Shang dynasty**, also known as the **Yin dynasty**, was a [[Dynasties of China|Chinese royal dynasty]] that ruled in the [[Yellow River]] valley during the 2nd millennium BC, traditionally succeeding the [[Xia dynasty]] and followed by the Western Zhou dynasty. The classic account of the Shang comes from texts such as the Book of Documents, Bamboo Annals and [[Shiji]]. Modern scholarship dates the dynasty between the 16th and 11th centuries BC, with more agreement surrounding the end date than beginning date.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Shang dynasty is the earliest dynasty within traditional [[History of China|Chinese history]] that is firmly supported by archaeological evidence. The archaeological site of Yinxu, near modern-day Anyang, corresponds to the final Shang capital of Yin. Tens of thousands of bronze, [[Jade|jade]], stone, bone, and ceramic artefacts have been uncovered at Yinxu. Most prominently, the site has yielded the earliest known examples of Chinese writing—a corpus primarily consisting of [[Divination|divination]] texts inscribed on oracle bones, which were usually either turtle shells or ox scapulae. More than 20,000 oracle bones were discovered during the initial scientific excavations during the 1920s and 1930s, with over four times as many having been found since. The inscriptions provide critical insight into many topics from the politics, economy, and religious practices to the art and medicine of the early stages of Chinese history.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Era", "[[Bronze Age]]" },
			{ "Government", "[[Monarchy]]" },
			{ "Currency", "Shell money, Shell mussels, Tong Bei" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sheba",
		category = "History",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ancient South Arabian kingdom",
		tags = { "Asia", "West Asia" },
		aliases = { "Saba" },
		summary = "**Sheba**, or **Saba**, was an ancient South Arabian kingdom that existed in Yemen before 275 CE. It likely began its existence between c. 1000 BCE and c. 800 BCE. Its inhabitants were the Sabaeans, who, as a people, were indissociable from the kingdom itself for much of the 1st millennium BCE. Modern historians agree that the heartland of the Sabaean civilization was located in the region around Marib and Sirwah. In some periods, they expanded to much of [[Yemen|modern Yemen]] and even parts of the [[Horn of Africa]], particularly [[Eritrea]] and [[Ethiopia]]. The kingdom's native language was Sabaic, which was a variety of Old South Arabian.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Among [[South Arabia|South Arabians]] and Abyssinians, Sheba\'s name carried prestige, as it was widely considered to be the birthplace of South Arabian civilization as a whole. The first Sabaean kingdom lasted from the 8th century BCE to the 1st century BCE: this kingdom can be divided into the "mukarrib" period, where it reigned supreme over all of South Arabia; and the "kingly" period, a long period of decline to the neighbouring kingdoms of Ma\'in, Hadhramaut, and Qataban, ultimately ending when a newer neighbour, Himyar, annexed them. Sheba was originally confined to the region of Marib (its capital city) and its surroundings. At its height, it encompassed much of the southwestern parts of the [[Arabian Peninsula]] before eventually declining to the regions of Marib. However, it re-emerged from the 1st to 3rd centuries CE.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Seleucid Empire", "Fertile Crescent" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Capital", "Marib [[Sanaa]]" },
			{ "Official languages", "Sabaic" },
			{ "People are called", "Sabaeans" },
			{ "Government", "[[Monarchy]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sikh Empire",
		category = "History",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Empire on the Indian subcontinent, 1799–1849",
		tags = { "Asia", "South Asia" },
		summary = "The **Sikh Empire** was a [[Regional power|regional power]] based in the [[Punjab]] region of [[South Asia]]. It existed from 1799, when maharaja Ranjit Singh captured [[Lahore]], to 1849, when it was defeated and annexed by the [[East India Company|British East India Company]] following the Second Anglo-Sikh War. At its peak in the mid-19th century, the empire extended from Gilgit and Tibet in the north to the deserts of Sindh in the south and from the Khyber Pass in the west to the Sutlej in the east, and was divided into 8 provinces.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Religiously diverse, with an estimated population of 12 million in 1800 (making it the 12th most populous state at the time), it was the last major region of the Indian subcontinent to be conquered by the [[British Raj|British Empire]]. In 1799, Ranjit Singh of the Sukerchakia Misl captured Lahore from the Sikh triumvirate which had been ruling it since 1765, and was confirmed on the possession of Lahore by the Durrani ruler, Zaman Shah. He was formally crowned on 12 April 1801 by Sahib Singh Bedi, a descendant of Guru Nanak. Ranjit Singh rose to power in a very short period, from a leader of a single misl to finally becoming the Maharaja of Punjab. By 1813 all the remaining Sikh misls had been annexed by Ranjit Singh, and the following years saw progressive expulsion of the [[Durrani Empire|Afghans]] from Punjab; the Afghan influence east of [[Indus River|Indus]] ended after the fall of Multan in 1818.",
			},
		},
