		title = "Wends",
		category = "History",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Historical term for Slavs",
		tags = { "Europe", "Western Europe" },
		summary = "**Wends** is a historical name for [[Slavs]] who inhabited present-day northeast Germany. It refers not to a homogeneous people, but to various people, tribes, or groups depending on where and when it was used. In the modern day, communities identifying as Wendish exist in [[Slovenia]], [[Austria]], Lusatia, the [[United States]] (such as the Texas Wends), and in [[Australia]]. One present-day group, the Lusatian Sorbs of Eastern Germany, remain in the Wendish homeland.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In German-speaking Europe during the [[Middle Ages]], the term "Wends" was interpreted as synonymous with "Slavs" and sporadically used in literature to refer to West Slavs and [[South Slavs]] living within the [[Holy Roman Empire]]. The name has possibly survived in Finnic languages, denoting modern [[Russia]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Western Roman Empire",
		category = "History",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Western half of the Roman Empire (395–476)",
		tags = { "Ancient", "Europe" },
		summary = "In modern [[Historiography|historiography]], the **Western Roman Empire** was the [[Roman Empire]]'s western provinces collectively during any period in which they were administered separately from the [[Byzantine Empire|eastern provinces]] by a separate, independent imperial court. Particularly from AD 395 to 476, there were separate, coequal courts dividing the governance of the empire into western and eastern provinces with a distinct imperial succession in the separate courts.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The terms Western Roman Empire and [[Byzantine Empire|Eastern Roman Empire]] were coined in modern times to describe political entities that were [[De facto and de jure|de facto]] independent; contemporary [[Roman Empire|Romans]] did not consider the Empire to have been split into two empires but viewed it as a single [[Polity|polity]] governed by two imperial courts for administrative purposes. The Western Empire collapsed in 476, and the Western imperial court in [[Ravenna]] disappeared by 554, at the end of [[Justinian I|Justinian]]'s Gothic War, marking one of the most transformative political changes during the era of [[Late antiquity|late antiquity]]. Although there were periods with more than one emperor ruling jointly before, the view that it was impossible for a single emperor to govern the entire Empire was institutionalized by emperor [[Diocletian]] following the disastrous civil wars and disintegrations of the Crisis of the Third Century.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Era", "Late antiquity" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Empire" },
			{ "Government", "[[Autocracy]]" },
			{ "Official languages", "[[Latin]]" },
			{ "Currency", "Roman currency" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Western imperialism in Asia",
		category = "History",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "Asia", "Basics" },
		summary = "The influence and [[Imperialism|imperialism]] of the [[Western world|West]] peaked in Asian territories from the [[Colonialism|colonial period]] beginning in the 16th century, and substantially reduced with 20th century decolonization. It originated in the 15th-century search for [[Trade route|trade routes]] to the [[Indian subcontinent]] and [[Southeast Asia]], in response to [[Ottoman Empire|Ottoman]] control of the [[Silk Road]]. This led to the [[Age of Discovery]], and introduction of early modern warfare into what Europeans first called the [[East Indies]], and later the [[Far East]]. By the 16th century, the [[Age of Sail]] expanded European influence and development of the [[Spice trade|spice trade under colonialism]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"European-style [[Colonial empire|colonial empires]] and imperialism operated in Asia throughout six centuries of colonialism, formally ending with the independence of Portuguese Macau in 1999. The empires introduced Western concepts of [[Nation|nation]] and the [[Multinational state|multinational state]]. European political power, commerce, and culture in Asia gave rise to growing trade in commodities—a key development in the rise of the [[Capitalism|free market]] economy. In the 16th century, the Portuguese broke the monopoly of the Arabs and Italians in trade between Asia and Europe by its discovery of the sea route to India around the Cape of Good Hope. The ensuing rise of the rival [[Dutch East India Company]] gradually eclipsed Portuguese influence in Asia. Dutch forces first established independent bases in the East and between 1640-60 wrested Malacca, Ceylon, some southern Indian ports, and the lucrative [[Japan]] trade from the Portuguese.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "White people in the United Kingdom",
		category = "History",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Racial and multi-ethnic group",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = '**White people in the United Kingdom** are a multi-ethnic group consisting of British residents who identify as and are perceived to be "[[White people|white people]]." White people constitute the historical and current majority of the people living in the [[United Kingdom]], with 83.0% of the population identifying as white in the 2021 United Kingdom census.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The [[Office for National Statistics]] designates white people into several subgroups, with small terminology variations between the administrative jurisdictions of England and Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland. These are local: White British, White Irish, White Gypsy or Irish Traveller, and immigrant descended Other White, and in Scotland; White Polish. In [[Northern Ireland]] ethnic group data is collected differently, where only the term 'White' is used, and with National Identity ('British', 'Irish', 'Northern Irish', or combinations) collected separately.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
