            <string name="Name">Vital_28</string>
            <string name="Source"><![CDATA[-- Imported from English Wikipedia lead sections (CC BY-SA 4.0), filtered for Wiki Race.
-- Generated by tools/wiki/build_pages.py — edit the generator, not this file.
return {
	{
		title = "Barometer",
		category = "Invention",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Scientific instrument used to measure atmospheric pressure",
		tags = { "Tools", "Measuring instruments" },
		summary = "A **barometer** is a scientific instrument that is used to measure air pressure. Pressure tendency, which is derived from barometric readings, can forecast short term changes in the weather. Many measurements of air pressure are used within surface weather analysis to help find surface troughs, pressure systems and [[Weather front|frontal boundaries]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Barometers and pressure altimeters (the most basic and common type of altimeter) are essentially the same instrument, but used for different purposes. A pressure altimeter is used to estimate [[Altitude|altitude]] by measuring the pressure of the atmosphere surrounding the altimeter and comparing the result to the expected atmospheric pressure at various altitudes, while a barometer is kept at a constant altitude and measures subtle pressure changes caused by weather and elements of weather. The average atmospheric pressure on the Earth's surface varies between 940 and 1040 hPa (mbar). The average atmospheric pressure at sea level is 1013 hPa (mbar).",
			},
		},
		related = { "Calipers", "Ruler" },
	},
	{
		title = "Bearing (mechanical)",
		category = "Invention",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mechanism to constrain relative movement to the desired motion and reduce friction",
		tags = { "Machinery and tools", "Components" },
		aliases = { "Bearing" },
		summary = "A **bearing** is a machine element that constrains relative motion to only the desired motion and reduces [[Friction|friction]] between moving parts. The design of the bearing may, for example, provide for free [[Line (geometry)|linear]] movement of the moving part or for free rotation around a fixed axis; or, it may prevent a motion by controlling the [[Vector (mathematics and physics)|vectors]] of normal forces that bear on the moving parts. Most bearings facilitate the desired motion by minimizing friction. Bearings are classified broadly according to the type of operation, the motions allowed, or the directions of the loads (forces) applied to the parts.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The term "bearing" is derived from the verb "to bear"; a bearing being a machine element that allows one part to bear (i.e., to support) another. The simplest bearings are bearing surfaces, cut or formed into a part, with varying degrees of control over the form, size, roughness, and location of the surface. Other bearings are separate devices installed into a machine or machine part. The most sophisticated bearings for the most demanding applications are very precise components; their manufacture requires some of the highest standards of current technology.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Pipe (fluid conveyance)" },
	},
	{
		title = "Binoculars",
		category = "Invention",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Pair of telescopes mounted side-by-side",
		tags = { "Optical technology", "Optical instruments" },
		aliases = { "Field glasses" },
		summary = "**Binoculars** or **field glasses** are two refracting telescopes mounted side-by-side and aligned to point in the same direction, allowing the viewer to use both eyes (binocular vision) when viewing distant objects. Most binoculars are sized to be held using both hands, although sizes vary widely from opera glasses to large pedestal-mounted military models.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Unlike a (monocular) telescope, binoculars give users a three-dimensional image: each eyepiece presents a slightly different image to each of the viewer's eyes and the [[Parallax|parallax]] allows the visual cortex to generate an impression of depth.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Brush",
		category = "Invention",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🛠️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tool with bristles used for cleaning, grooming, or applying liquid coatings",
		tags = { "Tools", "Artist's tools" },
		summary = "A **brush** is a common tool with bristles, wire, or other filaments. It generally comprises a handle or block to which filaments are affixed in either a parallel or perpendicular orientation, depending on the way the brush is to be gripped during use. The material of both the block and bristles or filaments is chosen to withstand hazards of its intended use, such as corrosive chemicals, heat, or abrasion. It is used for cleaning, grooming [[Hair|hair]], [[Cosmetics|makeup]], [[Painting|painting]], surface finishing, and many other purposes. It is one of the most basic and versatile tools in use today, and the average household may contain several dozen varieties.",
	},
	{
		title = "Calipers",
		category = "Invention",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🛠️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tool used to measure dimensions of an object",
		tags = { "Tools", "Measuring instruments" },
		aliases = { "Callipers" },
		summary = "**Calipers** or **callipers** are an instrument used to [[Measurement|measure]] the linear dimensions of an object or hole; namely, the [[Length|length]], width, thickness, diameter or depth of an object or hole. The word caliper comes from a corrupt form of caliber.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Many types of calipers permit reading out a measurement on a ruled scale, a dial, or an electronic digital display. A common association is to calipers using a sliding vernier scale. Some calipers can be as simple as a compass with inward or outward-facing points, but with no scale (measurement indication). The tips of the caliper are adjusted to fit across the points to be measured, and then kept at that span while moved to separate measuring device, such as a [[Ruler|ruler]], or simply transferred directly to a workpiece. Calipers are used in many fields such as [[Mechanical engineering|mechanical engineering]], [[Metalworking|metalworking]], [[Forestry|forestry]], [[Woodworking|woodworking]], [[Science|science]] and [[Medicine|medicine]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Spectrometer", "Instrumentation" },
	},
	{
		title = "Celestial navigation",
		category = "Invention",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Navigation using astronomical objects to determine position",
		tags = { "Navigation and timekeeping", "Navigation" },
		aliases = { "Astronavigation" },
		summary = "**Celestial navigation**, also known as **astronavigation**, is the practice of position fixing using stars and other celestial bodies that enables a [[Navigator|navigator]] to accurately determine their actual current physical position in space or on the surface of the Earth without relying solely on estimated positional calculations, commonly known as dead reckoning. Celestial navigation is performed without using [[Satellite navigation|satellite navigation]] or other similar modern electronic or digital positioning means.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Celestial [[Navigation|navigation]] uses "sights," or timed angular measurements, taken typically between a celestial body (e.g., the [[Sun]], the [[Moon]], a [[Planet|planet]], or a [[Star|star]]) and the visible [[Horizon|horizon]]. Celestial navigation can also take advantage of measurements between celestial bodies without reference to the Earth\'s horizon, such as when the Moon and other selected bodies are used in the practice called "lunars" or the lunar distance method, used for determining precise time when time is unknown.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Centrifuge",
		category = "Invention",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏋️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Device using centrifugal force to separate fluids",
		tags = { "Machinery", "Industrial machinery" },
		summary = "A **centrifuge** is a device that uses [[Centrifugal force|centrifugal force]] to subject a specimen to a specified constant force – for example, to separate various components of a [[Fluid|fluid]]. This is achieved by spinning the fluid at high speed within a container, thereby separating fluids of different [[Density|densities]] (e.g., cream from milk) or liquids from solids. It works by causing denser substances and particles to move outward in the radial direction. At the same time, objects that are less dense are displaced and moved to the centre. In a laboratory centrifuge that uses sample tubes, the radial acceleration causes denser particles to settle to the bottom of the tube, while low-density substances rise to the top.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A centrifuge can be a very effective filter that separates contaminants from the main body of fluid. Industrial scale centrifuges are commonly used in manufacturing and waste processing to sediment suspended solids, or to separate immiscible liquids. An example is the cream separator found in [[Dairy|dairies]]. Very high speed centrifuges and ultracentrifuges able to provide very high accelerations can separate fine particles down to the nano-scale, and molecules of different masses. Large centrifuges are used to simulate high gravity or acceleration environments (for example, high-G training for test pilots). Medium-sized centrifuges are used in [[Washing machine|washing machines]] and at some [[Swimming pool|swimming pools]] to draw water out of fabrics. Gas centrifuges are used for isotope separation, such as to enrich nuclear fuel for fissile isotopes.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Chain",
		category = "Invention",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Series of connected links which are typically made of metal",
		tags = { "Machinery and tools", "Components" },
		summary = "A **chain** is a serial assembly of connected links typically made of metal, with an overall character similar to that of a [[Rope|rope]] in that it is flexible and [[Curve|curved]] in compression, but [[Line (geometry)|linear]], rigid, and load-bearing in tension. A chain may consist of two or more links. Chains can be classified by their design, which can be dictated by their use: Those designed for lifting, such as when used with a hoist; for pulling; or for securing, such as with a bicycle lock, have links that are [[Torus|torus]]-shaped, which make the chain flexible in two [[Dimension|dimensions]] (the fixed third dimension being a chain's length). Small chains serving as jewellery are a mostly decorative analogue of such types.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Those designed for transferring power in machines have links designed to mesh with the teeth of the sprockets of the machine, and are flexible in only one dimension. They are known as roller chains, though there are also non-roller chains such as block chains. Two distinct chains can be connected using a quick link, carabiner, shackle, or clevis. The load can be transferred from one chain to another object by a chain stopper.",
			},
		},
	},
