	{
		title = "Charge-coupled device",
		category = "Invention",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Digital imaging circuit",
		tags = { "Optical technology", "Optical instruments" },
		aliases = { "CCD" },
		summary = "A **charge-coupled device** (**CCD**) is an [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuit]] containing an array of linked, or coupled, [[Capacitor|capacitors]]. Under the control of an external circuit, each capacitor can transfer its [[Electric charge|electric charge]] to a neighboring capacitor. CCD sensors are a major technology used in digital imaging.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In a CCD [[Image sensor|image sensor]], [[Pixel|pixels]] are represented by p-doped metal–oxide–semiconductor (MOS) capacitors. These MOS capacitors, the basic building blocks of a CCD, are biased above the threshold for inversion when image acquisition begins, allowing the conversion of incoming [[Photon|photons]] into electron charges at the semiconductor-oxide interface; the CCD is then used to read out these charges. Although CCDs are not the only technology to allow for light detection, CCD image sensors are widely used in professional, medical, and scientific applications where high-quality image data are required. In applications with less exacting quality demands, such as consumer and professional [[Digital camera|digital cameras]], active pixel sensors, also known as CMOS sensors (complementary MOS sensors), are generally used.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
