	{
		title = "Ziggurat of Ur",
		category = "Landmark",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌞",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Early Bronze Age ziggurat in present-day Iraq",
		tags = { "Architecture", "Specific structures" },
		aliases = { "Ziggurat", "Great Ziggurat", "Of Ur" },
		summary = "The **Ziggurat** (or **Great Ziggurat**) **of Ur** is a Neo-Sumerian ziggurat in what was the city of [[Ur]] near Nasiriyah, in present-day Dhi Qar Province, [[Iraq]]. The structure was built during the [[Bronze Age|Early Bronze Age]] (21st century BC) by King Ur-Nammu of the [[Third Dynasty of Ur]]. It had crumbled to ruins by the 6th century BC of the Neo-Babylonian period, when it was restored by King Nabonidus.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Its remains were excavated in the 1920s and 1930s by international teams led by Sir Leonard Woolley. Under Saddam Hussein in the 1980s, they were encased by a partial reconstruction of the façade and the monumental staircase. Today, Ur Tourist City is being developed adjacent to the site to serve Christian pilgrims and tourists, based on later religious traditions that identify a nearby complex as the home of the patriarch Abraham. The Ziggurat of Ur is the best-preserved of those known from [[Mesopotamia]], besides the ziggurat of Dur Untash (Chogha Zanbil). It is one of three well-preserved structures of the Neo-Sumerian city of Ur, along with the Royal Mausolea and the Palace of Ur-Nammu (the E-hursag).",
			},
		},
		related = { "Alhambra" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Location", "Tell el-Muqayyar, Dhi Qar, [[Iraq]]" },
			{ "Type", "Temple" },
			{ "Length", "64 m" },
			{ "Area", "2880 m²" },
			{ "Height", "c. 30 m (original)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "1",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Natural number",
		tags = { "Basics" },
		summary = '**1** (**one**, **unit**, **unity**) is a [[Number|number]], numeral, and [[Grapheme|grapheme]]. It is the first and smallest positive integer of the infinite sequence of [[Natural number|natural numbers]]. This fundamental property has led to its unique uses in other fields, ranging from science to sports, where it commonly denotes the first, leading, or top thing in a group. 1 is the unit of [[Counting|counting]] or [[Measurement|measurement]], and represents a single thing. The representation of 1 evolved from ancient Sumerian and Babylonian symbols to the modern Arabic numeral. Linguistically, in English, "one" is a determiner for singular nouns and a gender-neutral pronoun.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In mathematics, 1 is the multiplicative identity, meaning that any number multiplied by 1 equals the same number. 1 is by convention not considered a [[Prime number|prime number]]. In [[Digital electronics|digital technology]], 1 represents the "on" state in [[Binary number|binary code]], the foundation of [[Computing|computing]]. Philosophically, 1 symbolizes the ultimate reality or source of existence in various traditions.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Atomic number", "1" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Abelian group",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "➗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Commutative group (mathematics)",
		tags = { "Algebra" },
		aliases = { "Commutative group" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], an **abelian group**, also called a **commutative group**, is a [[Group (mathematics)|group]] in which the result of applying the group operation to two group elements does not depend on the order in which they are written. That is, the group operation is commutative. With addition as an operation, the [[Integer|integers]] and the [[Real number|real numbers]] form abelian groups, and the concept of an abelian group may be viewed as a generalization of these examples. Abelian groups are named after the Norwegian mathematician [[Niels Henrik Abel]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The concept of an abelian group underlies many fundamental [[Algebraic structure|algebraic structures]], such as [[Field (mathematics)|fields]], [[Ring (mathematics)|rings]], [[Vector space|vector spaces]], and algebras. The theory of abelian groups is generally simpler than that of their non-abelian counterparts, and finite abelian groups are very well understood and fully classified.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Abstract algebra",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "✖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of mathematics",
		tags = { "Algebra" },
		aliases = { "Modern algebra" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], more specifically [[Algebra|algebra]], **abstract algebra** or **modern algebra** is the study of [[Algebraic structure|algebraic structures]], which are [[Set (mathematics)|sets]] with specific [[Operation (mathematics)|operations]] acting on their elements. Algebraic structures include [[Group (mathematics)|groups]], [[Ring (mathematics)|rings]], [[Field (mathematics)|fields]], [[Module (mathematics)|modules]], [[Vector space|vector spaces]], lattices, and algebras over a field. The term abstract algebra was coined in the early 20th century to distinguish it from older parts of algebra, and more specifically from [[Elementary algebra|elementary algebra]], the use of [[Variable (mathematics)|variables]] to represent numbers in computation and reasoning.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The abstract perspective on algebra has become so fundamental to advanced mathematics that it is simply called "algebra", while the term "abstract algebra" is seldom used except in pedagogy. Algebraic structures, with their associated homomorphisms, form mathematical categories. [[Category theory]] gives a unified framework to study properties and constructions that are similar for various structures. Universal algebra is a related subject that studies types of algebraic structures as single objects. For example, the structure of groups is a single object in universal algebra, which is called the variety of groups.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Abstract machine",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Theoretical computer used for defining a model of computation",
		tags = { "Discrete mathematics" },
		summary = "In [[Computer science|computer science]], an **abstract machine** is a theoretical model that allows for a detailed and precise analysis of how a computer system functions. It is similar to a [[Function (mathematics)|mathematical function]] in that it receives inputs and produces outputs based on predefined rules. Abstract machines vary from literal machines in that they are expected to perform correctly and independently of hardware. A typical abstract machine consists of a definition in terms of input, output, and the set of allowable operations used to turn the former into the latter. They can be used for purely theoretical reasons as well as models for real-world computer systems.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In the [[Theory of computation|theory of computation]], abstract machines are often used in thought experiments regarding computability or to analyse the complexity of [[Algorithm|algorithms]]. This use of abstract machines is fundamental to the field of [[Computational complexity theory|computational complexity theory]], such as with finite state machines, Mealy machines, push-down automata, and Turing machines.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Combinatorics", "Mathematical logic" },
	},
	{
		title = "Addition",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "➗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Arithmetic operation",
		tags = { "Basics" },
		summary = 'The addition of two [[Natural number|whole numbers]] results in the total or [[Summation|sum]] of those values combined. For example, the adjacent image shows two columns of apples, one with three apples and the other with two apples, totaling to five apples. This observation is expressed as, which is read as "three plus two [[Equality (mathematics)|equals]] five".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Addition belongs to arithmetic, a branch of [[Mathematics|mathematics]]. In [[Algebra|algebra]], another area of mathematics, addition can also be performed on abstract objects such as [[Euclidean vector|vectors]], [[Matrix (mathematics)|matrices]], and elements of additive groups. Addition has several important properties. It is [[Commutative property|commutative]], meaning that the order of the numbers being added does not matter, so 3 + 2 = 2 + 3. It is also [[Associative property|associative]], meaning that when one adds more than two numbers, the order in which addition is performed does not matter. Addition also obeys rules concerning related operations such as subtraction and multiplication. Performing addition is one of the simplest numerical tasks to perform. Addition of very small numbers is accessible to toddlers; the most basic task, 1 + 1, can be performed by infants as young as five months, and even by some members of other animal species.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Algebraic geometry",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "📐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of mathematics",
		tags = { "Algebra" },
		summary = "**Algebraic geometry** is a branch of [[Mathematics|mathematics]] which uses [[Abstract algebra|abstract algebraic]] techniques, mainly from [[Commutative algebra|commutative algebra]], to solve [[Geometry|geometrical problems]]. Classically, it studies zeros of multivariate polynomials; the modern approach generalizes this in a few different aspects.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The fundamental objects of study in algebraic geometry are [[Algebraic variety|algebraic varieties]], which are geometric manifestations of solutions of systems of polynomial equations. Examples of the most studied classes of algebraic varieties are [[Line (geometry)|lines]], [[Circle|circles]], [[Parabola|parabolas]], [[Ellipse|ellipses]], [[Hyperbola|hyperbolas]], cubic curves like elliptic curves, and quartic curves like lemniscates and Cassini ovals. These are plane algebraic curves. A point of the plane lies on an algebraic curve if its coordinates satisfy a given [[Algebraic equation|polynomial equation]]. Basic questions involve the study of points of special interest like singular points, inflection points and points at infinity. More advanced questions involve the [[Topology|topology]] of the curve and the relationship between curves defined by different equations.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Gaussian elimination" },
	},
	{
		title = "Algebraic number theory",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of number theory",
		tags = { "Discrete mathematics" },
		summary = "**Algebraic number theory** is a branch of [[Number theory|number theory]] that uses the techniques of [[Abstract algebra|abstract algebra]] to study the [[Integer|integers]], rational numbers, and their generalizations. Number-theoretic questions are expressed in terms of properties of algebraic objects such as algebraic number fields and their rings of integers, finite fields, and function fields. These properties, such as whether a [[Ring (mathematics)|ring]] admits unique factorization, the behavior of ideals, and the Galois groups of [[Field (mathematics)|fields]], can resolve questions of primary importance in number theory, like the existence of solutions to [[Diophantine equation|Diophantine equations]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Algebraic structure",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔣",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Set with operations obeying given axioms",
		tags = { "Algebra" },
		aliases = { "Algebraic system", "Underlying set", "Carrier set" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], an **algebraic structure** or **algebraic system** consists of a nonempty [[Set (mathematics)|set]] A (called the **underlying set**, **carrier set** or **domain**), a collection of [[Operation (mathematics)|operations]] on A (typically [[Binary operation|binary operations]] such as addition and multiplication), and a finite set of identities (known as [[Axiom|axioms]]) that these operations must satisfy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"An algebraic structure may be based on other algebraic structures with operations and axioms involving several structures. For instance, a [[Vector space|vector space]] involves a second structure called a [[Field (mathematics)|field]], and an operation called scalar multiplication between elements of the field (called [[Scalar (mathematics)|scalars]]), and elements of the vector space (called [[Vector (mathematics and physics)|vectors]]). [[Abstract algebra]] is the name that is commonly given to the study of algebraic structures. The general theory of algebraic structures has been formalized in universal algebra. [[Category theory]] is another formalization that includes other [[Mathematical structure|mathematical structures]] and [[Function (mathematics)|functions]] between structures of the same type (homomorphisms).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Algebraic topology",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "➗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of mathematics",
		tags = { "Algebra" },
		summary = "**Algebraic topology** is a branch of [[Mathematics|mathematics]] that uses tools from [[Abstract algebra|abstract algebra]] to study [[Topological space|topological spaces]]. The basic goal is to find algebraic invariants that classify topological spaces up to homeomorphism, though usually most classify up to [[Homotopy|homotopy equivalence]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although algebraic topology primarily uses algebra to study topological problems, using topology to solve algebraic problems is sometimes also possible. Algebraic topology, for example, allows for a convenient proof that any subgroup of a free group is again a free group.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Gaussian elimination" },
	},
	{
		title = "Algebraic variety",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "➗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mathematical object studied in the field of algebraic geometry",
		tags = { "Algebra" },
		aliases = { "Algebraic varieties" },
		summary = "**Algebraic varieties** are the central objects of study in [[Algebraic geometry|algebraic geometry]], a sub-field of [[Mathematics|mathematics]]. Classically, an algebraic variety is defined as the set of solutions of a system of polynomial equations over the [[Real number|real]] or [[Complex number|complex numbers]]. Modern definitions generalize this concept in several different ways, while attempting to preserve the geometric intuition behind the original definition.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Conventions regarding the definition of an algebraic variety differ slightly. For example, some definitions require an algebraic variety to be irreducible, which means that it is not the [[Union (set theory)|union]] of two smaller [[Set (mathematics)|sets]] that are closed in the Zariski topology. Under this definition, non-irreducible algebraic varieties are called **algebraic sets**. Other conventions do not require irreducibility. The [[Fundamental theorem of algebra|fundamental theorem of algebra]] establishes a link between [[Algebra|algebra]] and [[Geometry|geometry]] by showing that a monic polynomial (an algebraic object) in one variable with complex number coefficients is determined by the set of its roots (a geometric object) in the [[Complex plane|complex plane]]. Generalizing this result, Hilbert's Nullstellensatz provides a fundamental correspondence between ideals of [[Polynomial ring|polynomial rings]] and algebraic sets.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Analytic geometry",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of geometry using a coordinate system",
		tags = { "Geometry" },
		aliases = { "Coordinate geometry", "Cartesian geometry" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], **analytic geometry**, also known as **coordinate geometry** or **Cartesian geometry**, is the study of [[Geometry|geometry]] using a [[Coordinate system|coordinate system]]. This contrasts with synthetic geometry.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Analytic geometry is used in [[Physics|physics]] and [[Engineering|engineering]], and also in [[Aviation|aviation]], [[Aerospace engineering|rocketry]], space science, [[Spaceflight|spaceflight]], [[Statistics|statistics]], [[Economics|economics]], and the [[Social science|social sciences]]. It is the foundation of most modern fields of geometry, including [[Algebraic geometry|algebraic]], [[Differential geometry|differential]], discrete and computational geometry. Usually the Cartesian coordinate system is applied to manipulate equations for planes, straight lines, and circles, often in two and sometimes three dimensions. Geometrically, one studies the Euclidean plane (two dimensions) and Euclidean space. As taught in school books, analytic geometry can be explained more simply: it is concerned with defining and representing geometric shapes in a numerical way and extracting numerical information from shapes' numerical definitions and representations.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Analytic number theory",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Exploring properties of the integers with complex analysis",
		tags = { "Discrete mathematics" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], **analytic number theory** is a branch of [[Number theory|number theory]] that uses methods from [[Mathematical analysis|mathematical analysis]] to solve problems about the [[Integer|integers]]. It is often said to have begun with [[Peter Gustav Lejeune Dirichlet]]'s 1837 introduction of Dirichlet L-functions to give the first proof of Dirichlet's theorem on arithmetic progressions. It is well known for its results on prime numbers (involving the [[Prime number theorem|Prime Number Theorem]] and Riemann zeta function) and additive number theory (such as the Goldbach conjecture and Waring's problem).",
	},
	{
		title = "Ancient Greek mathematics",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "➗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mathematics of Ancient Greece and the Mediterranean, 5th BC to 6th AD",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Greek mathematics" },
		summary = "**Ancient Greek mathematics** refers to the history of mathematical ideas and texts in [[Ancient Greece]] during [[Classical antiquity|classical]] and [[Late antiquity|late antiquity]], mostly from the 5th century BC to the 6th century AD. Greek mathematicians lived in cities spread around the shores of the ancient [[Mediterranean Sea|Mediterranean]], from [[Anatolia]] to [[Italy]] and [[North Africa]], but were united by Greek culture and the [[Ancient Greek|Greek language]]. The development of mathematics as a theoretical discipline and the use of [[Deductive reasoning|deductive reasoning]] in [[Mathematical proof|proofs]] is an important difference between Greek mathematics and those of preceding civilizations.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The early history of Greek mathematics is obscure, and traditional narratives of [[Theorem|mathematical theorems]] found before the fifth century BC are regarded as later inventions. It is now generally accepted that treatises of deductive mathematics written in Greek began circulating around the mid-fifth century BC, but the earliest complete work on the subject is [[Euclid's Elements]], written during the [[Hellenistic period]]. The works of renowned mathematicians [[Archimedes]] and [[Apollonius of Perga|Apollonius]], as well as of the astronomer [[Hipparchus]], also belong to this period. In the Imperial Roman era, [[Ptolemy]] used trigonometry to determine the positions of stars in the sky, while Nicomachus and other ancient philosophers revived ancient [[Number theory|number theory]] and harmonics.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Indonesian rupiah" },
	},
	{
		title = "Angle",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 1,
