	{
		title = "Area",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "➗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Size of a two-dimensional surface",
		tags = { "Geometry" },
		summary = "**Area** is the [[Measure (mathematics)|measure]] of a region's size on a [[Surface (mathematics)|surface]]. The area of a plane region or plane area refers to the area of a [[Shape|shape]] or planar lamina, while [[Surface area|surface area]] refers to the area of an open surface or the boundary of a three-dimensional object. Area can be understood as the amount of material with a given thickness that would be necessary to fashion a model of the shape, or the amount of [[Paint|paint]] necessary to cover the surface with a single coat. It is the two-dimensional analogue of the [[Length|length]] of a curve (a one-dimensional concept) or the [[Volume|volume]] of a solid (a three-dimensional concept).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Two different regions may have the same area (as in squaring the circle); by synecdoche, "area" sometimes is used to refer to the region, as in a "polygonal area". The area of a shape can be measured by comparing the shape to [[Square|squares]] of a fixed size. In the [[International System of Units]] (SI), the standard unit of area is the square metre (written as m2), which is the area of a square whose sides are one [[Metre|metre]] long. A shape with an area of three square metres would have the same area as three such squares. In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], the unit square is defined to have area one, and the area of any other shape or surface is a dimensionless [[Real number|real number]]. There are several well-known [[Formula|formulas]] for the areas of simple shapes such as [[Triangle|triangles]], rectangles, and [[Circle|circles]]. Using these formulas, the area of any [[Polygon|polygon]] can be found by dividing the polygon into triangles.',
