	{
		title = "Cardinality",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔣",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Size of a set in mathematics",
		tags = { "Discrete mathematics" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], **cardinality** is an inherent property of [[Set (mathematics)|sets]], roughly meaning the number of individual [[Mathematical object|objects]] they contain, which may be [[Infinity|infinite]]. The concept is understood through one-to-one correspondences between sets. That is, if their objects can be paired such that each object has a pair, and no object is paired more than once.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Two sets are said to be **equinumerous** or have the same cardinality if there exists a one-to-one correspondence between them. Otherwise, under the [[Axiom of choice|axiom of choice]], one of the two sets must be equinumerous with a strict subset of the other and is said to be strictly smaller than it; the other set is strictly larger. Using this concept, it is possible to show there are different sizes of infinity. Uncountable sets are those strictly larger than the set of natural numbers. The set of all real numbers and the powerset of the set of natural numbers are proven to be uncountable by so-called diagonal arguments. Cantor\'s theorem generalizes these arguments to show there is an infinite hierarchy of infinities. For finite sets, cardinality recovers the usual concept of size as "number of elements." However, it is more often difficult to ascribe "sizes" to infinite sets.',
			},
		},
