		summary = "**Combinatorics** is an area of [[Mathematics|mathematics]] primarily concerned with [[Counting|counting]], both as a means and as an end to obtaining results, and certain properties of finite [[Mathematical structure|structures]]. It is closely related to many other areas of mathematics and has many applications ranging from [[Logic|logic]] to statistical physics and from [[Evolutionary biology|evolutionary biology]] to [[Computer science|computer science]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Combinatorics is well known for the breadth of the problems it tackles. Combinatorial problems arise in many areas of [[Pure mathematics|pure mathematics]], notably in [[Algebra|algebra]], [[Probability theory|probability theory]], [[Topology|topology]], and [[Geometry|geometry]], as well as in its many application areas. Many combinatorial questions have historically been considered in isolation, giving an ad hoc solution to a problem arising in some mathematical context. In the later twentieth century, however, powerful and general theoretical methods were developed, making combinatorics into an independent branch of mathematics in its own right. One of the oldest and most accessible parts of combinatorics is [[Graph theory|graph theory]], which by itself has numerous natural connections to other areas. Combinatorics is used frequently in computer science to obtain formulas and estimates in the analysis of algorithms.",
