		tags = { "Geometry" },
		aliases = { "Convex" },
		summary = "In [[Geometry|geometry]], a set of points is **convex** if it contains every [[Line segment|line segment]] between two points in the set. For example, a solid cube is a convex set, but anything that is hollow or has an indent, such as a crescent shape, is not convex.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The boundary of a convex set in the plane is always a convex curve. The intersection of all the convex sets that contain a given subset A of Euclidean space is called the convex hull of A. It is the smallest convex set containing A. A convex function is a real-valued function defined on an [[Interval (mathematics)|interval]] with the property that its epigraph (the set of points on or above the [[Graph of a function|graph]] of the function) is a convex set. Convex minimization is a subfield of [[Mathematical optimization|optimization]] that studies the problem of minimizing convex functions over convex sets. The branch of mathematics devoted to the study of properties of convex sets and convex functions is called convex analysis. Spaces in which convex sets are defined include the [[Euclidean space|Euclidean spaces]], the affine spaces over the [[Real number|real numbers]], and certain [[Non-Euclidean geometry|non-Euclidean geometries]].",
			},
