	{
		title = "Correlation",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "➗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Statistical relationship",
		tags = { "Statistics and probability" },
		summary = "In [[Statistics|statistics]], **correlation** is a type of statistical relationship between two [[Random variable|random variables]] or bivariate data. It usually refers to the extent to which a pair of quantities are linearly related. More generally, an arbitrary relationship between variables is called an **association**, meaning the degree to which the variability in one can be accounted for by the other.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The presence of a correlation is not sufficient to infer the presence of a [[Causality|causal]] relationship, and this is often stated as "correlation does not imply causation". Furthermore, the concept of correlation is not the same as dependence: if two variables are independent, then they are uncorrelated, but the opposite is not necessarily true – even if two variables are uncorrelated, they might be dependent on each other. Correlations are useful because they can indicate a predictive relationship that can be exploited in practice. For example, an electrical utility may produce less power on a mild day based on the correlation between electricity demand and weather. In this example, there is a causal relationship, because [[Extreme weather|extreme weather]] causes people to use more electricity for heating or cooling.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Stochastic process", "Markov chain" },
