		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], [[Physics|physics]], and [[Engineering|engineering]], a **Euclidean vector** or simply a **vector** (sometimes called a **geometric vector** or **spatial vector**) is a geometric object that has [[Magnitude (mathematics)|magnitude]] (or length) and [[Direction (geometry)|direction]]. Euclidean vectors can be added and scaled to form a [[Vector space|vector space]]. A [[Vector quantity|vector quantity]] is a vector-valued [[Physical quantity|physical quantity]], including [[Unit of measurement|units of measurement]] and possibly a support, formulated as a directed line segment.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A vector is what is needed to \"carry\" the point A to the point B; the Latin word means 'carrier'. It was first used by 18th century astronomers investigating planetary revolution around the Sun. The magnitude of the vector is the distance between the two points, and the direction refers to the direction of displacement from A to B. Many algebraic operations on [[Real number|real numbers]] such as [[Addition|addition]], [[Subtraction|subtraction]], [[Multiplication|multiplication]], and negation have close analogues for vectors, operations which obey the familiar algebraic laws of commutativity, associativity, and distributivity. These operations and associated laws qualify [[Euclidean space|Euclidean]] vectors as an example of the more generalized concept of vectors defined simply as elements of a [[Vector space|vector space]]. Vectors play an important role in [[Physics|physics]]: the [[Velocity|velocity]] and [[Acceleration|acceleration]] of a moving object and the [[Force|forces]] acting on it can all be described with vectors.",
			},
		},
	},
