		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Arithmetic operation",
		tags = { "Basics" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], **exponentiation**, denoted bn, is an [[Binary operation|binary operation]] of the base, b, and the exponent, n. When n is a positive [[Integer|integer]], exponentiation corresponds to repeated [[Multiplication|multiplication]] of the base, where the exponent indicates how many copies of the base are multiplied together; that is, bn is the [[Product (mathematics)|product]] of multiplying b by itself n times:",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"More involved definitions allow complex base and exponent, as well as certain types of [[Matrix (mathematics)|matrices]] as base or exponent. Exponentiation is used extensively in many fields, including [[Economics|economics]], [[Biology|biology]], [[Chemistry|chemistry]], [[Physics|physics]], and [[Computer science|computer science]], with applications such as compound interest, [[Population growth|population growth]], chemical reaction kinetics, [[Wave|wave]] behavior, and public-key cryptography, respectively. Constant exponents are often used to describe [[Unit of measurement|units of measurement]] and quantity dimensions. For instance, since [[Force|force]] is mass times acceleration, it is measured in kg m/sec2. Using M for mass, L for length, and T for time, the expression M L T–2 is used in [[Dimensional analysis|dimensional analysis]] to describe force.",
