	{
		title = "Expression (mathematics)",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "➗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Symbolic description of a mathematical object",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], an **expression** is an arrangement of symbols following the context-dependent, syntactic conventions of mathematical notation. Symbols can denote [[Number|numbers]], [[Variable (mathematics)|variables]], [[Operation (mathematics)|operations]], and [[Function (mathematics)|functions]]. Other symbols include punctuation marks and brackets, used for grouping where there is not a well-defined [[Order of operations|order of operations]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Expressions are commonly distinguished from formulas: expressions usually denote [[Mathematical object|mathematical objects]], whereas formulas are statements about mathematical objects, such as an [[Equality (mathematics)|equality]]. This is analogous to [[Natural language|natural language]], where a noun phrase refers to an object, and a whole [[Sentence (linguistics)|sentence]] refers to a [[Fact|fact]]. However, formulas are often considered as expressions that can be evaluated to the Boolean values true or false. To evaluate an expression means to find a numerical value equivalent to the expression. Expressions can be evaluated or simplified by replacing operations that appear in them with their result. An expression is often used to define a [[Function (mathematics)|function]], by taking the variables to be arguments, or inputs, of the function, and assigning the output to be the evaluation of the resulting expression. An expression with no variables would define a constant function.",
			},
		},
