		tags = { "Discrete mathematics" },
		summary = 'In [[Logic|logic]], [[Mathematics|mathematics]], [[Computer science|computer science]], and [[Linguistics|linguistics]], a **formal language** is a set of strings whose symbols are taken from a set called "alphabet".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The alphabet of a formal language consists of symbols that concatenate into strings (also called "words"). Words that belong to a particular formal language are sometimes called well-formed words. A formal language is often defined by means of a formal grammar such as a regular grammar or context-free grammar. In computer science, formal languages are used, among others, as the basis for defining the grammars of [[Programming language|programming languages]] and controlled natural languages (i.e., formalized versions of subsets of natural languages). In [[Computational complexity theory|computational complexity theory]], decision problems are typically defined as formal languages, and complexity classes are defined as the sets of the formal languages that can be parsed by machines with limited computational power.',
