		tags = { "Calculus and analysis" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], the **limit of a function** is a fundamental concept in [[Calculus|calculus]] and [[Mathematical analysis|analysis]] concerning the behavior of that [[Function (mathematics)|function]] near a particular input which may or may not be in the domain of the function.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Formal definitions, first devised in the early 19th century, are given below. Informally, a function f assigns an output f(x) to every input x. We say that the function has a limit L at an input p, if f(x) gets closer and closer to L as x moves closer and closer to p. More specifically, the output value can be made arbitrarily close to L if the input to f is taken sufficiently close to p. On the other hand, if some inputs very close to p are taken to outputs that stay a fixed distance apart, then we say the limit does not exist. The notion of a limit has many applications in [[Calculus|modern calculus]]. In particular, the many definitions of [[Continuous function|continuity]] employ the concept of limit: roughly, a function is continuous if all of its limits agree with the values of the function.",
