		tags = { "Other" },
		aliases = { "LP", "Linear optimization" },
		summary = "**Linear programming** (**LP**), also called **linear optimization**, is a method to achieve the best outcome (such as maximum profit or lowest cost) in a [[Mathematical model|mathematical model]] whose requirements and objective are represented by linear relationships. Linear programming is a special case of mathematical programming (also known as [[Mathematical optimization|mathematical optimization]]).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"More formally, linear programming is a technique for the [[Mathematical optimization|optimization]] of a linear objective function, subject to linear equality and linear inequality constraints. Its feasible region is a convex polytope, which is a set defined as the intersection of finitely many half spaces, each of which is defined by a linear inequality. Its objective function is a [[Real number|real]]-valued affine (linear) function defined on this polytope. A linear programming [[Algorithm|algorithm]] finds a point in the polytope where this function has the largest (or smallest) value if such a point exists. Linear programming can be applied to various fields of study. It is widely used in mathematics and, to a lesser extent, in business, [[Economics|economics]], and some engineering problems.",
