				"Overview",
				"One-dimensional manifolds include [[Line (geometry)|lines]] and [[Circle|circles]], but not self-crossing curves such as a figure-eight. Two-dimensional manifolds are also called surfaces. Examples include the [[Euclidean plane|plane]], the [[Sphere|sphere]], and the [[Torus|torus]], and also the Klein bottle and real projective plane. The concept of a manifold is central to many parts of [[Geometry|geometry]] and modern [[Mathematical physics|mathematical physics]] because it allows complicated structures to be described in terms of well-understood topological properties of simpler spaces. Manifolds naturally arise as solution sets of systems of equations and as [[Graph of a function|graphs]] of functions. The concept has applications in computer-graphics given the need to associate pictures with coordinates (e.g. CT scans). Manifolds can be equipped with additional structure. One important class of manifolds are differentiable manifolds; their differentiable structure allows [[Calculus|calculus]] to be done.",
