			{
				"Overview",
				'In [[Philosophy of mathematics|philosophy of mathematics]], the concept of "mathematical objects" touches on topics of [[Existence|existence]], identity, and the nature of [[Reality|reality]]. In [[Metaphysics|metaphysics]], objects are often considered entities that possess [[Property (philosophy)|properties]] and can stand in various relations to one another. Philosophers debate whether mathematical objects have an independent existence outside of [[Thought|human thought]] (realism), or if their existence is dependent on mental constructs or language ([[Idealism|idealism]] and nominalism). Objects can range from the concrete: such as physical objects usually studied in [[Applied mathematics|applied mathematics]], to the abstract, studied in [[Pure mathematics|pure mathematics]]. What constitutes an "object" is foundational to many areas of philosophy, from [[Ontology|ontology]] (the study of being) to [[Epistemology|epistemology]] (the study of knowledge).',
