		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], a **ring** is an [[Algebraic structure|algebraic structure]] consisting of a set with two [[Binary operation|binary operations]] typically called addition and multiplication and denoted like [[Addition|addition]] and [[Multiplication|multiplication]] of integers. They work similarly to integer addition and multiplication, except that multiplication in a ring does not need to be commutative. Ring elements may be numbers such as integers or [[Complex number|complex numbers]], but they may also be non-numerical objects such as [[Polynomial|polynomials]], square matrices, [[Function (mathematics)|functions]], and power series.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"More formally, a ring is a set that is endowed with two binary operations (addition and multiplication) such that the ring is an [[Abelian group|abelian group]] with respect to addition. The multiplication is [[Associative property|associative]], is [[Distributive property|distributive]] over the addition operation, and has a multiplicative identity element. Some authors apply the term ring to a further generalization, often called a rng, that omits the requirement for a multiplicative identity, and instead call the structure defined above a ring with identity. A [[Commutative ring|commutative ring]] is a ring with a commutative multiplication. This property has profound implications on ring properties. [[Commutative algebra]], the theory of commutative rings, is a major branch of [[Ring theory|ring theory]]. Its development has been greatly influenced by problems and ideas of [[Algebraic number theory|algebraic number theory]] and [[Algebraic geometry|algebraic geometry]].",
			},
