		tags = { "Calculus and analysis" },
		aliases = { "Series" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]], a **series** is, roughly speaking, an [[Addition|addition]] of [[Infinity|infinitely]] many terms, one after the other. The study of series is a major part of [[Calculus|calculus]] and its generalization, [[Mathematical analysis|mathematical analysis]]. Series are used in most areas of mathematics, even for studying finite structures in [[Combinatorics|combinatorics]] through generating functions. The mathematical properties of infinite series make them widely applicable in other quantitative disciplines such as [[Physics|physics]], [[Computer science|computer science]], [[Statistics|statistics]] and finance.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Among the [[Ancient Greece|Ancient Greeks]], the idea that a potentially infinite [[Summation|summation]] could produce a finite result was considered [[Paradox|paradoxical]], most famously in Zeno's paradoxes. Nonetheless, infinite series were applied practically by Ancient Greek mathematicians including [[Archimedes]], for instance in the quadrature of the parabola. The mathematical side of Zeno's paradoxes was resolved using the concept of a [[Limit (mathematics)|limit]] during the 17th century, especially through the early calculus of [[Isaac Newton]]. The resolution was made more rigorous and further improved in the 19th century through the work of [[Carl Friedrich Gauss]] and [[Augustin-Louis Cauchy]], among others, answering questions about which of these sums exist via the completeness of the real numbers and whether series terms can be rearranged or not without changing their sums using absolute convergence and conditional convergence of series.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Approximation theory" },
