	{
		title = "Set theory",
		category = "Mathematics",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔣",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of mathematics that studies sets",
		tags = { "Discrete mathematics" },
		summary = "**Set theory** is the branch of [[Mathematical logic|mathematical logic]] that studies [[Set (mathematics)|sets]], which can be informally described as collections of objects. Although objects of any kind can be collected into a set, set theory – as a branch of [[Mathematics|mathematics]] – is mostly concerned with those that are relevant to mathematics as a whole.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The modern study of set theory was initiated by the German mathematicians [[Richard Dedekind]] and [[Georg Cantor]] in the 1870s. In particular, Georg Cantor is commonly considered the founder of set theory. The non-formalized systems investigated during this early stage go under the name of [[Naive set theory|naive set theory]]. After the discovery of paradoxes within naive set theory (e.g. [[Russell's paradox]], Cantor's paradox, and the Burali-Forti paradox, et al.), various axiomatic systems were proposed in the early twentieth century, of which [[Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory]] (with or without the [[Axiom of choice|axiom of choice]]) is still the best-known and most studied. Set theory is commonly employed as a foundational system for the whole of mathematics, particularly in the form of Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory with the axiom of choice.",
			},
		},
