		tags = { "Discrete mathematics" },
		summary = 'In [[Theoretical computer science|theoretical computer science]] and [[Mathematics|mathematics]], the **theory of computation** is the branch that deals with what problems can be solved on a model of computation using an [[Algorithm|algorithm]], how efficiently they can be solved and to what degree (e.g., approximate solutions versus precise ones). The field is divided into three major branches: automata theory and [[Formal language|formal languages]], [[Computability theory|computability theory]], and [[Computational complexity theory|computational complexity theory]], which are linked by the question: "What are the fundamental capabilities and limitations of computers?".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In order to perform a rigorous study of computation, computer scientists work with a mathematical [[Abstraction|abstraction]] of computers called a model of computation. Several models exist for this purpose, such as the Turing machine. Computer scientists study the Turing machine because it is simple to formulate, can be analyzed and used to prove results, and because it represents what many consider the most powerful possible "reasonable" model of computation (see Church–Turing thesis). It might seem that the potentially infinite memory capacity is an unrealizable attribute, but any decidable problem solved by a Turing machine will always require only a finite amount of memory. So in principle, any problem that can be solved (decided) by a Turing machine can be solved by a computer that has a finite amount of memory.',
