		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Technically, a tuple of n [[Real number|numbers]] can be understood as the [[Cartesian coordinate system|Cartesian coordinates]] of a location in a n-dimensional Euclidean space. When, this space is called the three-dimensional Euclidean space (or simply "Euclidean space" when the context is clear). In [[Classical physics|classical physics]], it serves as a model of the physical [[Universe|universe]], in which all known [[Matter|matter]] exists. When relativity theory is considered, it can be considered a local subspace of space-time. While this space remains the most widely used way to model the world as it is experienced, it is only one example of a 3-manifold. In this classical example, when the three values refer to measurements in different directions ([[Coordinate system|coordinates]]), any three directions can be chosen, provided that these directions do not lie in the same [[Euclidean plane|plane]].',
