	{
		title = "Alternative rock",
		category = "Music",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🪨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Music genre",
		tags = { "Music genres and forms", "Popular music" },
		aliases = { "Alternative music", "Alt-rock", "Alternative" },
		summary = '**Alternative rock** (also known as **alternative music**, **alt-rock** or simply **alternative**) is a category of [[Rock music|rock music]] that evolved from the independent music underground of the 1970s. Alternative rock acts achieved mainstream success in the 1990s with the likes of the [[Grunge|grunge]] subgenre in the United States, and the Britpop and shoegaze subgenres in the United Kingdom and Ireland. During this period, many record labels were looking for "alternatives", as many corporate rock, [[Hard rock|hard rock]], and glam metal acts from the 1980s were beginning to grow stale throughout the [[Music industry|music industry]]. The emergence of Generation X as a [[Culture|cultural]] force in the 1990s also contributed greatly to the rise of alternative music.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"\"Alternative\" refers to the genre's distinction from mainstream or commercial rock or pop. The term's original meaning was broader, referring to musicians influenced by the musical style or independent, DIY ethos of late-1970s [[Punk rock|punk rock]]. Traditionally, alternative rock varied in terms of its sound, social context, and regional roots. Throughout the 1980s, magazines and zines, college radio airplay, and word of mouth had increased the prominence and highlighted the diversity of alternative rock's distinct styles (and music scenes), such as noise pop, indie rock, [[Grunge|grunge]], and shoegaze.",
			},
