	{
		title = "Classical period (music)",
		category = "Music",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎵",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Era of classical music (c. 1730–1820)",
		tags = { "Music genres and forms", "Western classical music" },
		summary = "The **Classical period** was an era of [[Classical music|classical music]] between roughly 1750 and 1820.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The classical period falls between the [[Baroque music|Baroque]] and [[Romantic music|Romantic]] periods. It is mainly homophonic, using a clear [[Melody|melody]] line over a subordinate chordal [[Accompaniment|accompaniment]], but [[Counterpoint|counterpoint]] was by no means forgotten, especially in liturgical vocal music and, later in the period, secular instrumental music. It also makes use of style galant which emphasizes light elegance in place of the Baroque's dignified seriousness and impressive grandeur. Variety and contrast within a piece became more pronounced than before, and the [[Orchestra|orchestra]] increased in size, range, and power. The [[Harpsichord|harpsichord]] declined as the main keyboard instrument and was superseded by the [[Piano|piano]] (or fortepiano).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Common practice period",
		category = "Music",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎵",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Western music history period (c. 1650 to 1900)",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In Western classical music, the **common practice period** was the period of about 250 years during which the [[Tonality|tonal system]] was regarded as the only basis for composition. It began when composers' use of the tonal system had clearly superseded earlier systems and ended when some composers began using significantly modified versions of the tonal system and developing other systems as well. Most features of the common practice (the accepted concepts of composition during this time) persisted from the mid-[[Baroque music|Baroque]] period through the Classical and [[Romantic music|Romantic]] periods, roughly from 1650 to 1900.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'There was much stylistic evolution during these centuries, with patterns and conventions flourishing and then declining, such as the sonata form. The most prominent unifying feature throughout the period is a [[Harmony|harmonic]] language to which music theorists can today apply Roman numeral chord analysis; however, the "common" in common practice period does not directly refer to any type of harmony, rather it refers to the fact that for over two hundred years only one system was used.',
			},
		},
	},
