		description = "19th-century solo piano composition series",
		tags = { "Specific musical works", "Classical" },
		aliases = { "Nocturnes" },
		summary = "[[Frédéric Chopin]] wrote 21 **nocturnes** for solo [[Piano|piano]] between 1827 and 1846. They are generally considered among the finest short solo works for the instrument and hold an important place in contemporary concert repertoire. Although Chopin did not invent the nocturne, he popularized and expanded on it, building on the form developed by Irish composer John Field.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Chopin's nocturnes numbered 1 to 18 were published during his lifetime, in twos or threes, in the order of composition. However, numbers 19 and 20 were actually written first, prior to Chopin's departure from [[Poland]], but published posthumously. Number 21 was not originally entitled \"nocturne\" at all, but since its publication in 1938 as such, it is generally included with publications and recordings of the set.",
			},
