	{
		title = "Scott Joplin",
		category = "Musician",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "American composer and pianist (1868–1917)",
		tags = { "Musicians and composers", "Jazz" },
		summary = '**Scott Joplin** (November 24, 1868 – April 1, 1917) was an American [[Composer|composer]] and pianist. [[Honorific nicknames in popular music|Dubbed]] the "King of [[Ragtime]]", he composed more than 40 ragtime pieces, one ragtime ballet (original version of "The Ragtime Dance", 1899/1902), and two operas. One of his first and most popular pieces, the "Maple Leaf Rag", became the genre\'s first and most influential hit, later being recognized as the quintessential rag. Joplin considered ragtime to be a form of [[Classical music|classical music]] meant to be played in concert halls and largely disdained the performance of ragtime as honky tonk music most common in saloons.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Joplin grew up in a musical family of railway laborers in Texarkana, Texas. During the late 1880s, he traveled the [[Southern United States|American South]] as a musician. He went to Chicago for the World\'s Fair of 1893, which helped make ragtime a national craze by 1897. Joplin moved to Sedalia, Missouri, in 1894 and worked as a piano teacher. He began publishing music in 1895, and his "Maple Leaf Rag" in 1899 brought him fame and a steady income. In 1901, Joplin moved to [[St. Louis]] and two years later scored his first opera, A Guest of Honor. It was confiscated—along with his belongings—for non-payment of bills and is now considered lost. In 1907, Joplin moved to New York City to (unsuccessfully) find a producer for a new opera. In 1916, Joplin descended into dementia from neurosyphilis. His 1917 death marks the end of the "Ragtime era".',
			},
		},
		related = {
			"Antônio Carlos Jobim",
			"Billie Holiday",
			"Celia Cruz",
			"Charlie Parker",
			"Django Reinhardt",
			"Ella Fitzgerald",
			"John Coltrane",
			"Miles Davis",
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "24 November 1868" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Texarkana, Texas or Linden, Texas, US (disputed)" },
			{ "Died", "1 April 1917" },
			{ "Education", "George R. Smith College" },
			{ "Occupation", "Composer, pianist, music teacher" },
			{ "Years active", "1895–1917" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sergei Prokofiev",
		category = "Musician",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Russian composer and pianist (1891–1953)",
		tags = { "Composers", "20th century modern" },
		summary = 'As the creator of acknowledged masterpieces across numerous [[Music genre|music genres]], he is regarded as one of the major composers of the 20th century. His works include such widely heard pieces as the March from The Love for Three Oranges, the suite Lieutenant Kijé, the ballet Romeo and Juliet—from which "Dance of the Knights" is taken—and Peter and the Wolf. Of the established forms and genres in which he worked, he created—excluding juvenilia—seven completed operas, seven [[Symphony|symphonies]], eight ballets, five piano concertos, two violin concertos, a cello concerto, a symphony-concerto for cello and orchestra, and nine completed piano sonatas.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A graduate of the Saint Petersburg Conservatory, Prokofiev initially made his name as an iconoclastic composer-pianist, achieving notoriety with a series of ferociously dissonant and virtuosic works for his instrument, including his first two piano concertos. In 1915, Prokofiev made a decisive break from the standard composer-pianist category with his orchestral Scythian Suite, compiled from music originally composed for a ballet commissioned by Sergei Diaghilev of the Ballets Russes. Diaghilev commissioned three further ballets from Prokofiev—Chout, Le pas d'acier and The Prodigal Son—which, at the time of their original production, all caused a sensation among both critics and colleagues. Prokofiev's one operatic success during his lifetime was The Love for Three Oranges, composed for the Chicago Opera and performed over the following decade in Europe and Russia.",
			},
		},
		related = { "John Williams", "Arnold Schoenberg" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Birthplace", "Sontsovka, Russian Empire (now in [[Ukraine]])" },
			{ "Born", "27 April 1891" },
			{ "Died", "5 March 1953" },
			{ "Education", "Saint Petersburg Conservatory" },
			{ "Occupation", "Composer pianist conductor" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sergei Rachmaninoff",
		category = "Musician",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🎤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Russian composer and pianist (1873–1943)",
		tags = { "Composers", "Post-romantic/early modern" },
		summary = "Rachmaninoff is widely considered one of the finest pianists of his day and, as a composer, one of the last great representatives of [[Romantic music|Romanticism]] in Russian classical music. Early influences of Tchaikovsky, Rimsky-Korsakov, and other Russian composers gave way to a thoroughly personal idiom notable for its song-like melodicism, [[Music|expressiveness]], dense [[Counterpoint|contrapuntal]] textures, and rich [[Orchestration|orchestral]] colours. The piano is featured prominently in Rachmaninoff's compositional output and he used his skills as a performer to fully explore the expressive and technical possibilities of the instrument.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Born into a musical family, Rachmaninoff began learning the piano at the age of four. He studied piano and composition at the Moscow Conservatory, from which he graduated in 1892, having already written several compositions. In 1897, following the disastrous premiere of his Symphony No. 1, Rachmaninoff entered a four-year depression and composed little, until supportive therapy allowed him to complete his very well-received Piano Concerto No. 2 in 1901. Rachmaninoff went on to become conductor of the Bolshoi Theatre from 1904 to 1906, and relocated to [[Dresden]], Germany, in 1906. He later embarked upon his first tour of the United States as a concert pianist in 1909. After the [[Russian Revolution]], Rachmaninoff and his family left Russia permanently, settling in New York in 1918.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Gustav Mahler", "Claude Debussy" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "1 April 1873" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Semyonovo, Russia" },
			{ "Died", "28 March 1943" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Shakira",
		category = "Musician",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🎤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Colombian singer-songwriter (born 1977)",
		tags = { "Popular music", "Non-English language popular music" },
		summary = 'Referred to as the "[[Honorific nicknames in popular music|Queen of Latin Music]]", she has had a significant impact on the musical landscape of [[Latin America]] and has been credited with popularizing Hispanophone music globally, contributing to increased learning and use of the [[Spanish language]] worldwide. She is also credited with opening the doors of the international market in [[Popular music|popular music]] for other Latin artists. Her accolades include four Grammy Awards and fifteen Latin Grammy Awards.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Shakira made her recording debut with Sony Music Colombia at the age of 14. She rose to prominence with the albums Pies Descalzos (1995) and Dónde Están los Ladrones? (1998). Her first English release, Laundry Service (2001), sold over 13 million copies worldwide, becoming the best-selling album by a female Latin artist. Her success was further solidified with the Spanish-language albums Fijación Oral, Vol. 1 (2005), Sale el Sol (2010), El Dorado (2017), and Las Mujeres Ya No Lloran (2024), all of which topped the Billboard Top Latin Albums chart, making her the first woman with number-one albums across four different decades. Her English albums Oral Fixation, Vol. 2 (2005), She Wolf (2009), and Shakira (2014) achieved platinum status worldwide.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Paul McCartney", "Ray Charles" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "2 February 1977" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Barranquilla, Atlántico, Colombia" },
			{ "Occupation", "Singer, songwriter, dancer, record producer" },
			{ "Years active", "1990–present" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Stevie Wonder",
		category = "Musician",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🎤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "American musician (born 1950)",
		tags = { "Popular music", "Blues, R&B, soul and funk" },
		aliases = { "Stevland Hardaway Morris" },
		summary = "**Stevland Hardaway Morris**, known professionally as **Stevie Wonder**, is an American singer-songwriter, musician, and record producer. He is widely regarded as one of the most influential musicians of the 20th century, and is credited as a pioneer and influence by musicians across a range of genres that include R&B, pop, [[Soul music|soul]], gospel, [[Funk|funk]], and [[Jazz|jazz]]. Wonder's use of synthesizers and other [[Electronic musical instrument|electronic musical instruments]] in the 1970s reshaped the conventions of contemporary R&B. He also helped drive such genres into the [[Album era|album era]], crafting his LPs as cohesive and consistent, in addition to socially conscious statements with complex compositions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Blind since shortly after his birth, Wonder was a [[Child prodigy|child prodigy]] who signed with Motown\'s Tamla label at the age of 11, where he was given the professional name **Little Stevie Wonder**. As a teenager, he established himself as one of Motown\'s most successful acts, known for his excited [[Harmonica|harmonica]] playing and high-pitched singing. His single "Fingertips" topped the [[Billboard Hot 100]] when he was 13, making him the youngest solo artist ever to top the chart. Wonder\'s critical and commercial peak, termed his "classic period" (1972–1976), began with the albums Music of My Mind and Talking Book (1972), which abandoned the Motown sound in favor of a synthesizer- and keyboard-driven one.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Ray Charles", "James Brown" },
		infobox = {
