	{
		title = "Palace",
		category = "Object",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏯",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Grand residence, especially a royal or episcopal one",
		tags = { "Housing", "Residential and housing units" },
		summary = "A **palace** is a large residence, often serving as a royal residence or the home for a [[Head of state|head of state]] or another high-ranking dignitary, such as a bishop or archbishop. The word is derived from the [[Latin]] word palātium, for Palatine Hill in Rome, which housed the [[Roman Empire|Imperial]] residences.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most European languages have a version of the term (palats, palais, palazzo, palacio, etc.) and many use it to describe a broader range of buildings than English. In many parts of Europe, the equivalent term is also applied to large private houses in cities, especially of the [[Aristocracy|aristocracy]]. It is also used for some large official buildings that have never had a residential function; for example in French-speaking countries Palais de Justice is the usual name of important courthouses. Many historic palaces such as parliaments, museums, hotels, or office buildings are now put to other uses. The word is also sometimes used to describe an elaborate building used for public entertainment or exhibitions such as a movie palace. A palace is typically distinguished from a [[Castle|castle]] in that the latter is fortified or has the style of a fortification, whereas a palace does not.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Igloo" },
	},
	{
		title = "Perfume",
		category = "Object",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mixture of fragrant substance",
		tags = { "Clothing and fashion" },
		summary = '**Perfume** is a mixture of fragrant [[Essential oil|essential oils]] or aroma compounds (fragrances), fixatives and [[Solvent|solvents]], usually in liquid form, used to give the human body, animals, food, objects, and living-spaces an agreeable scent. Perfumes can be defined as substances that emit and diffuse a pleasant and fragrant odor. They consist of artificial mixtures of aromatic chemicals and essential oils. The 1939 Nobel Laureate for Chemistry, Leopold Ružička stated in 1945 that "right from the earliest days of scientific chemistry up to the present time, perfumes have substantially contributed to the development of [[Organic chemistry|organic chemistry]] as regards methods, systematic classification, and theory."',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ancient texts and archaeological excavations show the use of perfumes in some of the earliest human civilizations. Modern perfumery began in the late nineteenth century with the commercial synthesis of aroma compounds such as vanillin and coumarin, which allowed for the composition of perfumes with smells previously unattainable solely from natural aromatics.",
