	{
		title = "Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "16th century Imam and General of the Adal Sultanate",
		tags = { "Early modern", "Africa" },
		summary = '**Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi** was the Imam of the [[Adal Sultanate]] from 1527 to 1543. Commonly named Ahmed **Gragn** in [[Amharic]] and **Gurey** in [[Somali language|Somali]], both meaning the left-handed, he led the invasion and conquest of [[Ethiopian Empire|Abyssinia]] from the Sultanate of Adal during the Ethiopian–Adal War. He is often referred to as the "King of Zeila" in medieval texts.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Dubbed \"The African Attila\" by Orientalist Frederick A. Edwards, Imam Ahmed's conquests reached all the way to the borders of the [[Funj Sultanate|Sultanate of Funj.]] Imam Ahmed won nearly all his battles against the Ethiopians before 1541 and after his victory at Battle of Amba Sel, the Ethiopian Emperor, Dawit II was never again in a position to offer a pitched battle to his army and was subsequently forced to live as an outlaw constantly hounded by Imam Ahmed's soldiers, the Malassay. Ahmed **Gragn** was subsequently defeated following a [[Portuguese Empire|Portuguese]] intervention, which drew his war into a broader geo-political struggle between the Portuguese and [[Ottoman Empire|Ottoman]] empires. The memory and legacy of his invasion nonetheless persists in both modern [[Ethiopia]] and [[Somalia]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Baybars", "Askia Muhammad I" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "c. 1506" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Hubat, [[Adal Sultanate]]" },
			{ "Died", "21 February 1543" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ahmose I",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Pharaoh of Ancient Egypt",
		tags = { "Africa", "Egypt" },
		summary = '**Ahmose I** (Amosis, Aahmes; meaning "Iah (the Moon) is born"; c. 1560 BC - c. 1525 BC) was a [[Pharaoh|pharaoh]] and founder of the [[Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt|18th Dynasty of Egypt]] in the [[New Kingdom of Egypt]], the era in which [[Ancient Egypt|ancient Egypt]] achieved the peak of its power. His reign is usually dated to the mid-16th century BC at the beginning of the [[Bronze Age|Late Bronze Age]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"During his rule, Ahmose I completed the conquest and expulsion of the Hyksos, restored Theban rule over Lower and Upper Egypt, and successfully reasserted Egyptian power in its formerly subject territories of [[Nubia]] and [[Canaan]]. He then reorganized the administration of the country, reopened [[Quarry|quarries]], [[Mining|mines]], and [[Trade route|trade routes]] and began massive construction projects of a type that had not been undertaken since the time of the [[Middle Kingdom of Egypt|Middle Kingdom]]. This building program culminated in the construction of the last pyramid built by native Egyptian rulers. Ahmose I's reign laid the foundations for the [[New Kingdom of Egypt|New Kingdom]], under which Egyptian power reached its peak.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Ptolemy I Soter", "Thutmose III" },
	},
	{
		title = "Ajatashatru",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "King of Magadha from 492 to 460 BCE",
		tags = { "Asia", "Southern Asia" },
		aliases = { "Ajatasattu", "Kunika", "Kuniya" },
		summary = "**Ajatasattu** ([[Pali|Pāli]]: Ajātasattu) or **Ajatashatru** in the Buddhist tradition, or **Kunika** (Kūṇika) and **Kuniya** (Kūṇiya) in the Jain tradition (reigned c. 492 to 460 BCE, or c. 405 to 373 BCE), was one of the most important kings of the Haryanka dynasty of Magadha in East India. He was the son of King Bimbisara and was a contemporary of both Mahavira and Gautama Buddha. He forcefully took over the kingdom of Magadha from his father and imprisoned him. He fought a war against the Vajjika League, led by the Licchavis, and conquered the republic of Vaishali. The city of [[Pataliputra]] was formed by fortification of a village by Ajatashatru.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ajatashatru followed policies of conquest and expansion. He defeated his neighbouring rivals including the king of Kosala; his brothers, at odds with him, went to Kashi, which had been given to Bimbisara as dowry and led to a war between Magadha and Kosala. Ajatashatru occupied Kashi and captured the smaller kingdoms. Magadha under Ajatashatru became the most powerful kingdom in North India.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Kanishka" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Died", "460 BCE or c. 373 BCE" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Akbar",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mughal emperor from 1556 to 1605",
		tags = { "Asia and Oceania", "Southern Asia" },
		aliases = { "Akbar the Great" },
		summary = "**Akbar** (Jalal-ud-Din Muhammad Akbar, 15 October 1542 – 27 October 1605), also known as **Akbar the Great**, was the third Mughal emperor, who reigned from 1556 to 1605. Akbar succeeded his father, [[Humayun]], under a regent, Bairam Khan, who helped the young emperor expand and consolidate Mughal domains in the [[Indian subcontinent]]. He is generally considered one of the greatest Mughal emperors.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Akbar gradually enlarged the [[Mughal Empire]] to include much of the Indian subcontinent through Mughal military, political, cultural, and economic dominance. To unify the vast Mughal state, Akbar established a centralised system of administration and adopted a policy of conciliating conquered rulers through marriage and diplomacy. To preserve peace and order in a religiously and culturally diverse empire, he adopted policies that won him the support of his non-Muslim subjects, including abolishing the sectarian tax and appointing them to high civil and military posts. Under Akbar, Mughal India developed a strong and stable economy, which tripled in size and wealth, leading to commercial expansion and greater patronage of an Indo-Persian culture.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Iskandar Muda", "Robert Clive" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "15 October 1542" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Amarkot, Amarkot Kingdom, Rajputana (modern-day Umerkot, [[Sindh]], Pakistan)" },
			{ "Died", "27 October 1605" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Akhenaten",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Eighteenth Dynasty Egyptian pharaoh",
		tags = { "Africa", "Egypt" },
		aliases = { "Akhenaton", "Echnaton", "Khuenaten" },
		summary = '**Akhenaten**, also spelled **Akhenaton**, **Echnaton**, and **Khuenaten**, was an [[Ancient Egypt|ancient Egyptian]] [[Pharaoh|pharaoh]] reigning c. 1353–1336 or 1351–1334 BC, the tenth ruler of the [[Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt|Eighteenth Dynasty]]. Originally named **Amenhotep IV**, in the fifth year of his reign he adopted the name "Akhenaten".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As a pharaoh, Akhenaten is noted for abandoning the traditional, polytheistic ancient Egyptian religion, and introducing Atenism, or worship centered around Aten. The views of Egyptologists differ as to whether the religious policy was absolutely monotheistic, or whether it was monolatristic, syncretistic, or henotheistic. This culture shift away from traditional religion was reversed after his death. Akhenaten's monuments were dismantled and hidden, his statues were destroyed, and his name excluded from lists of rulers compiled by later pharaohs. Traditional religious practice was gradually restored, notably under his close successor [[Tutankhamun]], who changed his name from Tutankhaten early in his reign.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Ramesses III" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Died", "1336 BC or 1334 BC (aged c. 38)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Akira Kurosawa",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Japanese filmmaker (1910–1998)",
		tags = { "Directors", "Asia" },
		summary = "**Akira Kurosawa** was a Japanese filmmaker who directed 30 feature films in a career spanning six decades. With a bold and dynamic style strongly influenced by Western cinema yet distinct from it, he is widely regarded as one of the greatest and most influential filmmakers in the [[History of film|history of cinema]]. Known as a hands-on filmmaker, he was heavily involved with all aspects of production as a director, writer, producer, and editor.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Following a brief stint as a painter, Kurosawa entered the [[Cinema of Japan|Japanese film industry]] in 1936. After years of working on numerous films as an assistant director and screenwriter, he made his directorial debut during [[World War II]] with the popular action film Sanshiro Sugata (1943), released when he was 33. Rashomon (1950) premiered in [[Tokyo]] and became the surprise winner of the [[Golden Lion]] at the 1951 Venice Film Festival. The film's commercial and critical success opened Western film markets to Japanese films for the first time, which in turn led to international recognition for other Japanese filmmakers. He became much less prolific after the 1960s, though his later work—including two of his final films, Kagemusha (1980) and Ran (1985)—continued to receive critical acclaim. In 1990, Kurosawa accepted the Academy Honorary Award.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Abbas Kiarostami", "Ang Lee", "Luis Buñuel", "Wong Kar-wai" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "23 March 1910" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Shinagawa, Tokyo, Japan" },
			{ "Died", "6 September 1998" },
			{ "Occupation", "Film director screenwriter producer editor painter" },
			{ "Years active", "1936–1993" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Al-Biruni",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Persian scholar and polymath (973–1050)",
		tags = { "Pre-modern figures", "Post-classical history" },
		aliases = { "Abu Rayhan Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Biruni" },
		summary = '**Abu Rayhan Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Biruni**, known as **al-Biruni** (c. 973) was a Khwarazmian [[Iranian peoples|Iranic]] scholar and [[Polymath|polymath]] during the [[Islamic Golden Age]]. He has been called variously "Father of Comparative Religion", "Father of modern [[Geodesy|geodesy]]", Founder of Indology and the first [[Anthropologist|anthropologist]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Al-Biruni was well versed in [[Physics|physics]], [[Mathematics|mathematics]], [[Astronomy|astronomy]], and [[Natural science|natural sciences]]; he also distinguished himself as a historian, chronologist, and [[Linguistics|linguist]]. He studied almost all the sciences of his day and was rewarded abundantly for his research in many fields of knowledge. Royalty and other powerful elements in society funded al-Biruni's research and sought him out with specific projects in mind. Influential in his own right, al-Biruni was himself influenced by the scholars of other nations, such as the Greeks, from whom he took inspiration when he turned to the study of philosophy. A gifted linguist, he was conversant in Khwarezmian, [[Persian language|Persian]], Arabic, and [[Sanskrit]], and also knew [[Ancient Greek|Greek]], [[Biblical Hebrew|Hebrew]], and [[Syriac language|Syriac]]. He spent much of his life in Ghazni, then capital of the [[Ghaznavid Empire|Ghaznavids]], in modern-day central-eastern Afghanistan.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Abu Bakr al-Razi", "Ibn al-Haytham", "Shen Kuo" },
		infobox = {
