	{
		title = "Al-Zahrawi",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Berber Andalusian physician, surgeon and chemist (936–1013)",
		tags = { "Pre-modern figures", "Post-classical history" },
		aliases = { "Abū al-Qāsim Khalaf ibn al-'Abbās al-Zahrāwī al-Ansari" },
		summary = "**Abū al-Qāsim Khalaf ibn al-'Abbās al-Zahrāwī al-Ansari** (c. 936–1013), popularly known as **al-Zahrawi**, [[Latinisation of names|Latinised]] as **Albucasis** or **Abulcasis** (from Arabic Abū al-Qāsim), was an Arab physician, surgeon and chemist from [[Al-Andalus|al-Andalus]]. He is considered one of the greatest surgeons of the [[Middle Ages]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Al-Zahrawi's principal work is the Kitab al-Tasrif, a thirty-volume [[Encyclopedia|encyclopedia]] of medical practices. The surgery chapter of this work was later translated into [[Latin]], attaining popularity and becoming the standard textbook in Europe for the next five hundred years. Al-Zahrawi's pioneering contributions to the field of surgical procedures and instruments had an enormous impact in the East and West well into the [[Modern era|modern period]], where some of his discoveries are still applied in medicine to this day. He pioneered the use of catgut for internal stitches, and his surgical instruments are still used today to treat people. He also developed surgical devices for Caesarean sections and cataract surgeries.",
			},
		},
