		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Anatomist, physician and author (1514–1564)",
		tags = { "Medicine", "Early modern" },
		aliases = { "Andries van Wezel" },
		summary = "**Andries van Wezel** (31 December 1514 – 15 October 1564), Latinized as **Andreas Vesalius**, was an anatomist and physician who wrote De Humani Corporis Fabrica Libri Septem (On the fabric of the human body in seven books), which is considered one of the most influential books on human anatomy and a major advance over the long-dominant work of [[Galen]]. Vesalius is often referred to as the founder of modern human anatomy. He was born in [[Brussels]], which was then part of the [[Habsburg Netherlands]]. He was a professor at the University of Padua (1537–1542) and later became Imperial physician at the court of [[Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor|Emperor Charles V]].",
