		tags = { "Asia", "Central Asia, Iran, Caucasus" },
		aliases = { "Cyrus II of Persia" },
		summary = "**Cyrus II of Persia** (c. 600 – 530 BC), commonly known as **Cyrus the Great**, was the founder of the [[Achaemenid Empire]]. Hailing from Persis, he brought the [[Achaemenid dynasty]] to power by defeating the Median Empire and embracing all of the previous civilized states of the [[Ancient Near East|ancient Near East]], expanding vastly across most of [[West Asia]] and much of [[Central Asia]] to create what would soon become the largest empire in history at the time. The Achaemenid Empire's greatest territorial extent was achieved under [[Darius the Great]], whose rule stretched from [[Southeastern Europe|Southeast Europe]] and [[Northeast Africa]] in the west to the [[Indus River|Indus Valley]] in the east.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"After absorbing the Median Empire, Cyrus conquered [[Lydia]] and eventually the [[Neo-Babylonian Empire]], granting him control of [[Anatolia]] and the [[Fertile Crescent]], respectively. He was succeeded by his son Cambyses II, whose campaigns led to the conquests of [[Ancient Egypt|Egypt]], [[Nubia]], and [[Cyrenaica]] during his short reign. To the [[Ancient Greece|Greeks]], he was known as Cyrus the Elder and was particularly renowned among contemporary scholars because of his habitual policy of tolerance for peoples' customs and religions in the lands that he conquered. Similarly, he is exalted in Judaism for his role in freeing the [[Jews|Jewish people]] from the [[Babylonian captivity]] by issuing the Edict of Restoration following the Persian conquest of Babylon.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Xerxes I" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "c. 600 BC" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Anshan, Persis, Kingdom of Anshan (now Fars, Iran)" },
			{ "Died", "530 BC" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Daniel Bernoulli",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Swiss mathematician and physicist (1700–1782)",
		tags = { "Mathematicians", "Early modern" },
		summary = "He is particularly remembered for his applications of mathematics to mechanics, especially [[Fluid mechanics|fluid mechanics]], and for his pioneering work in [[Probability|probability]] and [[Statistics|statistics]]. His name is commemorated in [[Bernoulli's principle]], a particular example of the [[Conservation of energy|conservation of energy]], which describes the mathematics of the mechanism underlying the operation of two important technologies of the 20th century: the carburetor and the aeroplane [[Wing|wing]].",
