	{
		title = "Daniel of Galicia",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "King of Ruthenia from 1253 to 1266",
		tags = { "Eastern Europe", "Russia and Ukraine" },
		aliases = { "Daniel Romanovich" },
		summary = "**Daniel Romanovich** (1201–1266) was Prince of Galicia (1205–1207; 1211–1212; 1230–1232; 1233–1234; 1238–1266), Volhynia (1205–1208; 1215–1238), Grand Prince of Kiev (1240), and [[King of Ruthenia]] (1253–1266). He was the son of Roman Mstislavich of Volhynia and Anne-Euphrosyne, the daughter of [[Byzantine Empire|Byzantine emperor]] Isaac II Angelos.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Under Daniel's long rule the Romanovichi branch of Rurikids restored control over Galicia, and the reunited principality rose to become a prominent power, eventually being elevated to the status of a [[Kingdom of Galicia–Volhynia|kingdom]]. Daniel's rule managed to withstand the Mongol invasions, making him a defining personality in the latter period of the existence of [[Kievan Rus']] following the decline of [[Kyiv|Kiev]]. The Galician-Volhynian Chronicle praised Daniel as an exemplary ruler, brave warrior and wise statesman, whose qualities as a monarch made him second only to Solomon.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Yaroslav the Wise" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "1201" },
			{ "Died", "1266" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dante Alighieri",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Italian writer and philosopher (1265–1321)",
		tags = { "Post-classical", "Europe" },
		aliases = { "Dante" },
		summary = "**Dante Alighieri**, widely known [[Mononym|mononymously]] as **Dante**, was an Italian poet, writer, and philosopher. His [[Divine Comedy]], originally called and later christened by [[Giovanni Boccaccio]], is widely considered one of the most important poems of the [[Middle Ages]] and the greatest literary work in the [[Italian language]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"At a time when Latin was still the dominant language for scholarly and literary writing—and when many Italian poets drew inspiration from French or Provençal traditions—Dante broke with both by writing in the [[Vernacular|vernacular]], specifically his native Tuscan dialect. His (On Eloquence in the Vernacular) was one of the first scholarly defenses of the vernacular. His use of the Florentine dialect for works such as The New Life (1295) and Divine Comedy helped establish the modern-day standardized Italian language. His work set a precedent that important Italian writers such as [[Petrarch]] and Boccaccio would later follow. Dante was instrumental in establishing the literature of Italy, and is considered to be among the country's national poets and the Western world's greatest literary icons.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Chinua Achebe", "Chrétien de Troyes", "Naguib Mahfouz", "Wole Soyinka" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "c. May 1265" },
			{ "Birthplace", "[[Florence]]" },
			{ "Died", "14 September 1321 (aged c. 56)" },
			{ "Occupation", "Statesman, poet, language theorist, [[Political philosophy|political theorist]]" },
			{ "Notable works", "[[Divine Comedy]]" },
			{ "Language", "Italian (Tuscan), [[Latin]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Darius the Great",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Persian ruler from 522 to 486 BCE",
		tags = { "Asia", "Central Asia, Iran, Caucasus" },
		summary = "He ruled the empire at its territorial peak, when it included much of [[West Asia]], parts of the [[Balkans]] (Thrace–Macedonia and Paeonia) and the [[Caucasus]], most of the [[Black Sea]]'s coastal regions, [[Central Asia]], the Indus Valley in the far east, and portions of [[North Africa]] and [[Northeast Africa]] including Egypt (Mudrâya), eastern Libya, and coastal Sudan.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Darius ascended the throne after overthrowing the Achaemenid monarch Bardiya (or Smerdis), whom he claimed to be an imposter named Gaumata. The new king met with rebellions throughout the empire but quelled each of them. Darius organized the empire by dividing it into administrative provinces, each governed by a [[Satrap|satrap]]. He organized a new uniform monetary system, and he made Aramaic a co-official language of the empire alongside Old Persian. He also put the empire in better standing by improving roads and introducing standard weights and measures. Through these changes, the Achaemenid Empire became centralized and unified. Darius undertook other construction projects throughout his realm, primarily focusing on [[Susa]], Pasargadae, [[Persepolis]], [[Babylon]], and Egypt.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Gwanggaeto the Great", "Xerxes I" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "c. 550 BCE" },
			{ "Died", "October 486 BCE" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "David Attenborough",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "English broadcaster and natural historian (born 1926)",
		tags = { "Journalists" },
		aliases = { "Sir David Frederick Attenborough" },
		summary = "**Sir David Frederick Attenborough** is an English broadcaster, natural historian, and writer. His presenting career began as host of Zoo Quest in 1954, and has spanned seven decades; it includes the nine documentary series forming The Life Collection, Natural World, Wildlife on One, the Planet Earth franchise, The Blue Planet and Frozen Planet. He is the only person to have won BAFTA Awards in black-and-white, colour, high-definition, 3D and 4K resolution. Over his life, he has collected dozens of honorary degrees and awards, including three Primetime Emmy Awards for Outstanding Narrator and one Daytime Emmy Award for Outstanding Daytime Personality – Non-Daily.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Attenborough was a senior manager at the [[BBC]], working as controller of BBC Two and director of programming for BBC Television in the 1960s and 1970s. While his earlier work focused primarily on the [[Natural environment|natural world]], his later work has been more vocal in support for [[Environmentalism|environmental causes]]. He has advocated for restoring planetary [[Biodiversity|biodiversity]], limiting population growth, switching to [[Renewable energy|renewable energy]], [[Climate change mitigation|mitigating climate change]], reducing meat consumption and setting aside more areas for natural preservation. On his broadcasting and passion for nature, [[NPR]] stated that Attenborough "roamed the globe and shared his discoveries and enthusiasms with his patented semi-whisper way of narrating". He is widely considered a national treasure in the UK, though he does not embrace the term.',
			},
		},
		related = { "William Randolph Hearst", "Joseph Pulitzer" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "8 May 1926" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Isleworth, Middlesex, England" },
			{ "Years active", "1951–present" },
			{ "Education", "Clare College, Cambridge (MA)" },
			{ "Occupation", "Broadcaster natural historian conservationist writer" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "David Hilbert",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "German mathematician (1862–1943)",
		tags = { "Mathematicians", "Modern" },
		summary = "**David Hilbert** was a German [[Mathematician|mathematician]] and [[Philosophy of mathematics|philosopher of mathematics]] and one of the most influential mathematicians of all time.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Hilbert discovered and developed a broad range of fundamental ideas including invariant theory, the [[Calculus of variations|calculus of variations]], [[Commutative algebra|commutative algebra]], [[Algebraic number theory|algebraic number theory]], the foundations of geometry, spectral theory of operators and its application to integral equations, [[Mathematical physics|mathematical physics]], and the [[Foundations of mathematics|foundations of mathematics]] (particularly [[Proof theory|proof theory]]). He adopted and defended [[Georg Cantor]]'s set theory and transfinite numbers. In 1900, he presented a [[Hilbert's problems|collection of problems]] that set a course for mathematical research of the 20th century. Hilbert and his students contributed to establishing rigor and developed important tools used in modern mathematical physics. He was a co-founder of proof theory and [[Mathematical logic|mathematical logic]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "William Rowan Hamilton" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "23 January 1862" },
