	{
		title = "Erwin Schrödinger",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Austrian physicist (1887–1961)",
		tags = { "Physics", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "Erwin Rudolf Josef Alexander Schrödinger", "Schroedinger", "Schrodinger" },
		summary = '**Erwin Rudolf Josef Alexander Schrödinger** (12 August 1887 – 4 January 1961), sometimes anglicised as **Schroedinger** or **Schrodinger**, was an Austrian theoretical [[Physicist|physicist]] who developed fundamental results in [[Quantum field theory|quantum theory]]. In particular, he is recognized for devising the [[Schrödinger equation]], an equation that provides a way to calculate the [[Wave function|wave function]] of a system and how it changes dynamically in time. He coined the term "[[Quantum entanglement|quantum entanglement]]" in 1935. Schrödinger shared the 1933 [[Nobel Prize in Physics]] with [[Paul Dirac]] "for the discovery of new productive forms of atomic theory".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In addition, Schrödinger wrote many works on various aspects of [[Physics|physics]]: [[Statistical mechanics|statistical mechanics]] and [[Thermodynamics|thermodynamics]], physics of [[Dielectric|dielectrics]], [[Color theory|color theory]], [[Electromagnetism|electrodynamics]], [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]], and [[Cosmology|cosmology]], and he made several attempts to construct a unified field theory. In his book, What Is Life?, Schrödinger addressed the problems of [[Genetics|genetics]], looking at the phenomenon of life from the point of view of physics. He also paid great attention to the philosophical aspects of science, ancient and oriental philosophical concepts, ethics, and religion. He also wrote on philosophy and theoretical biology. In popular culture, he is best known for "[[Schrödinger\'s cat]]", a [[Thought experiment|thought experiment]]. He and Dirac tied for eighth in a Physics World poll of the greatest physicists of all time. He returned to Vienna in 1956, with an emeritus professor position, and died of tuberculosis in 1961.',
			},
		},
		related = { "C. V. Raman" },
		infobox = {
