		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Italian electrical engineer and inventor (1874–1937)",
		tags = { "Inventors and engineers", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "Guglielmo Giovanni Maria Marconi, 1st Marquess of Marconi" },
		summary = '**Guglielmo Giovanni Maria Marconi, 1st Marquess of Marconi** (25 April 1874 – 20 July 1937), was an Italian [[Electrical engineering|electrical engineer]], inventor, and politician known for his creation of a practical [[Radio wave|radio wave]]-based wireless telegraph system. This led to his being largely credited as the inventor of [[Radio|radio]] and sharing the 1909 [[Nobel Prize in Physics]] with Ferdinand Braun "in recognition of their contributions to the development of wireless telegraphy."',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As an entrepreneur and a businessman, Marconi founded The Wireless Telegraph & Signal Company (later the Marconi Company) in the United Kingdom in 1897. In 1929, he was ennobled as a marquess by King Victor Emmanuel III, thus becoming the 1st Marchese di Marconi. In 1931, he set up Vatican Radio for Pope Pius XI.",
