		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "German writer and literary critic (1797–1856)",
		tags = { "Western Europe", "Germany and Austria" },
		aliases = { "Christian Johann Heinrich Heine" },
		summary = "**Christian Johann Heinrich Heine** was a German poet, writer and [[Literary criticism|literary critic]]. He is best known outside Germany for his early [[Lyric poetry|lyric poetry]], which was set to music in the form of Lieder (art songs) by composers such as [[Felix Mendelssohn]], [[Robert Schumann]] and [[Franz Schubert]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Heine's later verse and prose are distinguished by their satirical wit and irony. He is considered a member of the Young Germany movement. His radical political views led to many of his works being banned by German authorities—which, however, only added to his fame. He spent the last 25 years of his life as an [[Expatriate|expatriate]] in Paris. As an exile in Paris, Heine became a celebrity avatar reflective of the liberal and cosmopolitan values of the mainstream press. In the Third Reich, Heine's name was invoked as an archetype of an influential Jewish opinion columnist.",
