		description = "Ancient Greek philosopher (fl. c. 500 BC)",
		tags = { "Philosophers", "Ancient" },
		summary = "**Heraclitus** was a [[Pre-Socratic philosophy|pre-Socratic]] Greek philosopher from the city of Ephesus, which was then part of the [[Achaemenid Empire|Persian Empire]]. He exerts a wide influence on [[Western philosophy]], both [[Ancient philosophy|ancient]] and [[Modern philosophy|modern]], through the works of such authors as [[Plato]], [[Aristotle]], the [[Stoicism|Stoics]], [[Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel]], [[Friedrich Nietzsche]], and [[Martin Heidegger]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Little is known of Heraclitus\'s life. He wrote a single work, of which only fragments survive. Even in ancient times, his [[Paradox|paradoxical]] philosophy, appreciation for wordplay, and cryptic, oracular epigrams earned him the epithets "the dark" and "the obscure". He was considered arrogant and depressed, a misanthrope who was subject to melancholia. Consequently, he became known as "the weeping philosopher" in contrast to the ancient atomist philosopher [[Democritus]], who was known as "the laughing philosopher". The central ideas of Heraclitus\'s philosophy are the unity of opposites and the concept of change. Heraclitus saw harmony and justice in strife. He viewed the world as constantly in flux, always "becoming" but never "being". He expressed this in sayings like "Everything [[Fluid dynamics|flows]]" and "No man ever steps in the same river twice".',
			},
		},
		related = { "Li Si" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Region", "[[Western philosophy]]" },
			{ "Era", "[[Pre-Socratic philosophy]]" },
			{ "Born", "c. 6th century BC" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Ephesus, [[Ionia]], Persian Empire" },
			{ "Died", "c. 5th century BC" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Heraclius",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Byzantine emperor from 610 to 641",
		tags = { "Eastern Europe", "Byzantine Empire" },
		summary = "**Heraclius** was Byzantine emperor from 610 to 641. His rise to power began in 608, when he and his father, Heraclius the Elder, the Exarch of Africa, led a revolt against the unpopular emperor Phocas.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Heraclius's reign was marked by wars against the [[Sasanian Empire|Persians]] and the [[Arabs]]. Heraclius immediately took charge of the Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628 upon his accession, but his leadership initially did little to stem Persian ascendency in the war; after personally losing the Battle of Antioch (613), the Persians were able to take Syria and Egypt, reducing the Empire to [[Anatolia|Asia Minor]]. Heraclius, however, avoided total defeat and initiated reforms to rebuild and strengthen the military. Heraclius drove the Persians out of Asia Minor and pushed deep into their territory, defeating them in 627 at the Battle of Nineveh and devastating much of [[Mesopotamia]]. Heraclius won the war, but his Empire was deeply strained. Heraclius soon lost many of his newly regained lands to the Rashidun Caliphate. Emerging from the [[Arabian Peninsula]], the Arabs quickly conquered the [[Sasanian Empire]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Sviatoslav I", "Justinian I" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "c. 575" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Cappadocia, Byzantine Empire" },
			{ "Died", "11 February 641 (aged 66)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Herbert Spencer",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "English philosopher and political theorist (1820–1903)",
		tags = { "Political writers", "Modern" },
		summary = '**Herbert Spencer** (27 April 1820 – 8 December 1903) was an English [[Polymath|polymath]] active as a [[Philosophy|philosopher]], [[Psychologist|psychologist]], [[Biologist|biologist]], [[Sociology|sociologist]], and [[Anthropologist|anthropologist]]. Spencer originated the expression "survival of the fittest", which he coined in Principles of Biology (1864) after reading [[Charles Darwin]]\'s 1859 book [[On the Origin of Species]]. The term strongly suggests [[Evolution|natural selection]], yet Spencer saw evolution as extending into realms of sociology and ethics, so he also supported Lamarckism.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Spencer developed an all-embracing conception of evolution as the progressive development of the physical world, biological organisms, the human mind, and human culture and societies. As a polymath, he contributed to a wide range of subjects, including ethics, religion, anthropology, economics, political theory, philosophy, literature, astronomy, biology, sociology, and psychology. During his lifetime he achieved tremendous authority, mainly in English-speaking academia. Spencer was "the single most famous European intellectual in the closing decades of the nineteenth century" but his influence declined sharply after 1900: "Who now reads Spencer?" asked [[Talcott Parsons]] in 1937.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Eduard Bernstein", "Peter Kropotkin" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "27 April 1820" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Derby, Derbyshire, England" },
			{ "Died", "8 December 1903" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hergé",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Belgian cartoonist (1907–1983)",
		tags = { "Visual artists", "Western painters and illustrators" },
		aliases = { "Georges Prosper Remi" },
		summary = "**Georges Prosper Remi**, known by the pen name **Hergé**, from the French pronunciation of his reversed initials RG, was a Belgian comic strip artist. He is best known for creating [[The Adventures of Tintin]], the series of comic albums that are considered one of the most popular European comics of the 20th century. He was also responsible for two other well-known series, Quick & Flupke (1930–1940) and The Adventures of Jo, Zette and Jocko (1936–1957).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Born to a lower-middle-class family in Etterbeek, [[Brussels]], Hergé began his career by contributing illustrations to [[Scouting]] magazines, developing his first comic series, The Adventures of Totor, for Le Boy-Scout Belge in 1926. Working for the conservative Catholic newspaper Le Vingtième Siècle, he created The Adventures of Tintin in 1929 on the advice of its editor Norbert Wallez. Revolving around the actions of boy reporter Tintin and his dog Snowy, the series' early instalments – Tintin in the Land of the Soviets, Tintin in the Congo, and Tintin in America – were designed as conservative propaganda for children. Domestically successful, after serialisation the stories were published in book form, with Hergé continuing the series and also developing both the Quick & Flupke and Jo, Zette and Jocko series for Le Vingtième Siècle.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Aubrey Beardsley", "Jack Kirby", "M. C. Escher", "Norman Rockwell", "Winsor McCay" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "22 May 1907" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Etterbeek, [[Brussels]], [[Belgium]]" },
			{ "Died", "3 March 1983" },
			{ "Notable works", "[[The Adventures of Tintin]], The Adventures of Jo, Zette and Jocko, Quick & Flupke" },
		},
