			{ "Birthplace", "Delft, County of Holland, [[Dutch Republic]]" },
			{ "Died", "28 August 1645 (aged 62)" },
			{ "Education", "[[Leiden University]]" },
			{
				"Main interests",
				"Philosophy of war, [[International law|international law]], [[Political philosophy|political philosophy]]",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Humayun",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mughal emperor from 1530 to 1540 and from 1555 to 1556",
		tags = { "Asia and Oceania", "Southern Asia" },
		aliases = { "Nasir al-Din Muhammad" },
		summary = "**Nasir al-Din Muhammad**, commonly known by his [[Regnal name|regnal name]] **Humayun**, was the second Mughal emperor, who ruled much of what is now eastern [[Afghanistan]], [[Bangladesh]], [[North India|Northern India]], and [[Pakistan]] from 1530 to 1540 and again from 1555 to his death in 1556. At the time of his death, the Mughal Empire spanned almost one million square kilometers.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"On 26 December 1530, Humayun succeeded his father [[Babur]] to the throne of Delhi as ruler of the Mughal territories in the Indian subcontinent. Humayun was an inexperienced ruler when he came to power at the age of 22. His half-brother Kamran Mirza inherited [[Kabul]] and [[Kandahar]], the northernmost parts of their father's empire; the two half-brothers became bitter rivals. Early in his reign, Humayun lost his entire empire to [[Sher Shah Suri]] but regained it 15 years later with Safavid aid. His return from [[Iran|Persia]] was accompanied by a large retinue of Persian noblemen, signaling an important change in Mughal court culture. The Central Asian origins of the dynasty were largely overshadowed by the influences of [[Persian art]], architecture, [[Persian language|language]], and [[Persian literature|literature]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Qianlong Emperor" },
		infobox = {
