	{
		title = "Jean Racine",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "17th-century French dramatist (1639–1699)",
		tags = { "Europe", "France" },
		aliases = { "Jean-Baptiste Racine" },
		summary = '**Jean-Baptiste Racine** was a French dramatist, one of the three great playwrights of 17th-century France, along with [[Molière]] and Corneille, as well as an important literary figure in the Western tradition and world literature. Racine was primarily a [[Tragedy|tragedian]], producing such "examples of neoclassical perfection" as Phèdre, Andromaque, and Athalie. He did write one comedy, Les Plaideurs, and a muted tragedy, Esther, for the young.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Racine\'s plays displayed his mastery of the dodecasyllabic (12 syllable) French alexandrine. His writing is renowned for its elegance, purity, speed, and fury, and for what American poet Robert Lowell described as a "diamond-edge", and the "glory of its hard, electric rage". Racine\'s dramaturgy is marked by his psychological insight, the prevailing passion of his characters, and the nakedness of both plot and [[Stage (theatre)|stage]].',
