	{
		title = "John Maynard Keynes",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "British economist (1883–1946)",
		tags = { "Economists" },
		aliases = { "John Maynard Keynes, 1st Baron Keynes" },
		summary = '**John Maynard Keynes, 1st Baron Keynes**, was an English economist whose writings are considered the basis for the school of thought known as Keynesian economics, as well as its various offshoots. Originally trained in mathematics, he built on and refined earlier work on the causes of business cycles. His ideas, further developed after his death as New Keynesianism, are seen as foundational to mainstream macroeconomics. He has been referred to as the "father of [[Macroeconomics|macroeconomics]]" and is considered one of the most influential economists of the 20th century.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Keynes was educated at King's College at the [[University of Cambridge]], where he graduated in 1904 with a [[Bachelor of Arts|B.A.]] in mathematics. During the [[Great Depression]] of the 1930s, Keynes spearheaded the Keynesian Revolution, challenging the ideas of neoclassical economics that held that [[Free market|free markets]] would, in the short to medium term, automatically provide full employment, as long as workers were flexible in their wage demands. He argued that aggregate demand (total spending in the economy) determined the overall level of economic activity, and that inadequate aggregate demand could lead to prolonged periods of high unemployment, and since wages and labour costs are rigid downwards, the economy will not automatically rebound to full employment. Keynes advocated the use of fiscal and monetary policies to mitigate the adverse effects of economic recessions and depressions.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Lucille Ball" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "5 June 1883" },
			{ "Birthplace", "[[Cambridge]], Cambridgeshire, [[England]]" },
			{ "Died", "21 April 1946" },
			{ "Education", "King's College, Cambridge ([[Bachelor of Arts|BA]])" },
			{ "Field", "[[Political economy]], [[Probability]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "John Milton",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "English poet and civil servant (1608–1674)",
		tags = { "Europe", "Great Britain and Ireland" },
		summary = "**John Milton** (9 December 1608 – 8 November 1674) was an English poet, [[Polemic|polemicist]], and civil servant. His 1667 [[Epic poetry|epic poem]] [[Paradise Lost]] was written in blank verse and included 12 books, written in a time of immense religious flux and political upheaval. Paradise Lost elevated Milton's reputation as one of history's greatest poets. He also served as a civil servant for the Commonwealth of England under its Council of State and later under [[Oliver Cromwell]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Milton achieved fame and recognition during his lifetime. His celebrated Areopagitica (1644), condemning pre-publication censorship, is among history\'s most influential and impassioned defences of [[Freedom of speech|freedom of speech]] and [[Freedom of the press|freedom of the press]]. His desire for freedom extended beyond his philosophy and was reflected in his style, which included his introduction of new words to the English language, coined from [[Latin]] and [[Ancient Greek]]. He was the first modern writer to employ unrhymed verse outside the theatre or translations. Milton is described as the "greatest English author" by his biographer William Hayley, and he remains generally regarded "as one of the preeminent writers in the [[English literature|English language]]", though critical reception has oscillated in the centuries since his death, often on account of his republicanism. [[Samuel Johnson]] praised Paradise Lost as "a poem which...',
			},
		},
		related = { "Jonathan Swift", "Ben Jonson" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "9 December 1608" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Bread Street, [[City of London]], England" },
			{ "Died", "8 November 1674" },
			{ "Occupation", "Poet, Intellectual, Civil Servant" },
			{ "Education", "Christ's College, Cambridge ([[Bachelor of Arts|BA]], [[Master of Arts|MA]])" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "John Rawls",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "American philosopher (1921–2002)",
		tags = { "Philosophers", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "John Bordley Rawls" },
		summary = "**John Bordley Rawls** was an American [[Philosophy|philosopher]], most active in [[Ethics|moral]], [[Jurisprudence|legal]], and [[Political philosophy|political philosophy]]. Rawls has been described as one of the most influential modern liberal political philosophers of the 20th century.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In 1990, Will Kymlicka wrote in his introduction to the field that "it is generally accepted that the recent rebirth of normative [[Political philosophy|political philosophy]] began with the publication of John Rawls\'s A Theory of Justice in 1971". Rawls\'s theory of "justice as fairness" recommends equal basic liberties, equality of opportunity, and facilitating the maximum benefit to the least advantaged members of society in any case where inequalities may occur. His argument for these principles of social justice uses a [[Thought experiment|thought experiment]] called the "original position", in which people deliberatively select what kind of society they would choose to live in if they did not know which social position they would personally occupy.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Michel Foucault", "Alfred North Whitehead" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "21 February 1921" },
			{ "Birthplace", "[[Baltimore]], [[Maryland]], U.S." },
			{ "Died", "24 November 2002" },
			{ "Region", "[[Western philosophy]]" },
			{ "Era", "[[Contemporary philosophy]]" },
			{ "Teacher", "Walter Terence Stace" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "John Singer Sargent",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🎤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "American painter (1856–1925)",
		tags = { "Western painters and illustrators", "Modern era" },
		summary = '**John Singer Sargent** was an American [[Expatriate|expatriate]] artist, considered the "leading portrait painter of his generation" for his evocations of Belle Époque and Edwardian-era luxury. He created roughly 900 oil paintings and more than 2,000 watercolors, as well as countless sketches and charcoal drawings. His oeuvre documents worldwide travel, from [[Venice]] to the Tyrol, Corfu, Capri, Spain, the Middle East, [[Montana]], [[Maine]], and [[Florida]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Born in [[Florence]] to American parents, he was trained there and in Paris before moving to London, living most of his life in Europe. He enjoyed international acclaim as a portrait painter. An early submission to the Paris Salon in the 1880s, his Portrait of Madame X, was intended to consolidate his position as a society painter in Paris but instead resulted in scandal. During the year following the scandal, Sargent departed for England, where he continued a successful career as a portrait artist. From the beginning, Sargent's work is characterized by remarkable technical facility, particularly in his ability to draw with a brush, which in later years inspired admiration as well as criticism for its supposed superficiality.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Mark Rothko", "Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "12 January 1856" },
			{ "Birthplace", "[[Florence]], Grand Duchy of Tuscany" },
			{ "Died", "15 April 1925" },
			{ "Citizenship", "United States" },
			{ "Field", "Painting" },
			{ "Movement", "[[Impressionism]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "John Snow",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "❄️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "English epidemiologist and physician (1813–1858)",
		tags = { "Medicine", "Modern" },
		summary = "**John Snow** (15 March 1813 – 16 June 1858) was an English physician and a leader in the development of anaesthesia and [[Hygiene|medical hygiene]]. He is considered one of the founders of modern epidemiology and early germ theory, in part because of his work in tracing the source of a cholera outbreak in London's Soho, which he identified as a particular public water pump. Snow's findings inspired fundamental changes in the water and waste systems of London, which led to similar changes in other cities, and a significant improvement in general [[Public health|public health]] around the world.",
		related = { "Louis Pasteur", "Robert Koch" },
		infobox = {
