	{
		title = "Joseph Conrad",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Polish-British writer (1857–1924)",
		tags = { "Europe", "Eastern Europe" },
		summary = "**Joseph Conrad** was a Polish-British novelist and story writer. He is regarded as one of the greatest writers in the English language and – though he did not speak English fluently until his twenties (always with a strong foreign accent) – he became a master prose stylist who brought a non-English sensibility into [[English literature]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"He wrote novels and stories, many in nautical settings, that depicted crises of human individuality in the midst of what he saw as an indifferent, inscrutable, and amoral world. Conrad is considered a literary impressionist by some and an early [[Literary modernism|modernist]] by others, though his works also contain elements of 19th-century [[Literary realism|realism]]. His narrative style and anti-heroic characters, as in Lord Jim, have influenced numerous authors. Many dramatic films have been adapted from and inspired by his works. Numerous writers and critics have commented that his fictional works, written mostly in the first two decades of the 20th century, seem to have anticipated later world events.",
			},
		},
		related = {
			"Constantine P. Cavafy",
			"Fernando Pessoa",
			"Henryk Sienkiewicz",
			"José Saramago",
