	{
		title = "Joseph II, Holy Roman Emperor",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Holy Roman Emperor from 1765 to 1790",
		tags = { "Western Europe", "Holy Roman Empire, Austria" },
		aliases = { "Joseph II" },
		summary = "**Joseph II** (13 March 1741 – 20 February 1790) was [[Holy Roman Emperor]] from 18 August 1765 and sole ruler of the [[Habsburg monarchy]] from 29 November 1780 until his death. He was the eldest son of Empress [[Maria Theresa]] and her husband, Emperor Francis I, and the brother of [[Marie Antoinette]], Leopold II, Maria Carolina of Austria, and Maria Amalia, Duchess of Parma. He was thus the first ruler in the Austrian dominions of the union of the Houses of [[House of Habsburg|Habsburg]] and Lorraine, styled Habsburg-Lorraine.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Joseph was a proponent of enlightened absolutism like his brother Leopold II; however, his commitment to secularising, liberalising and modernising reforms resulted in significant opposition, which resulted in failure to fully implement his programs. Meanwhile, despite making some territorial gains, his reckless foreign policy badly isolated Austria. He has been ranked with [[Catherine the Great]] of [[Russian Empire|Russia]] and [[Frederick the Great]] of [[Kingdom of Prussia|Prussia]] as one of the three great [[Age of Enlightenment|Enlightenment]] monarchs. False but influential letters depict him as a somewhat more radical philosophe than he probably was. His policies are now known as Josephinism. He was a supporter of the arts, particularly of composers such as [[Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart]] and Antonio Salieri. He died with no known surviving legitimate offspring and was succeeded by his younger brother Leopold II.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor", "Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "13 March 1741" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Vienna, Austria" },
			{ "Died", "20 February 1790" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Joseph Lister",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "English scientist, surgeon and antiseptic pioneer (1827–1912)",
		tags = { "Medicine", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "Joseph Lister, 1st Baron Lister" },
		summary = "**Joseph Lister, 1st Baron Lister** (5 April 1827 – 10 February 1912) was an English surgeon, medical scientist, experimental pathologist and pioneer of antiseptic surgery and preventive healthcare. Lister revolutionised the craft of surgery by the use of close anatomical observation, in the same manner that John Hunter revolutionised the science of surgery.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Lister's contributions were four-fold. Firstly, as a surgeon at the Glasgow Royal Infirmary, he introduced carbolic acid (modern-day [[Phenol|phenol]]) as a steriliser for surgical instruments, patients' skins, sutures, surgeons' hands, and wards, promoting the principle of antiseptics. Thirdly, he advanced diagnostic science by analysing specimens using microscopes. Fourthly, he devised strategies to increase the chances of survival after surgery.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Louis Pasteur", "Robert Koch" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "5 April 1827" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Upton House, West Ham, England" },
			{ "Died", "10 February 1912" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Joseph Priestley",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "English chemist and polymath (1733–1804)",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Early modern" },
		summary = "**Joseph Priestley** was an English [[Chemist|chemist]], Unitarian, [[Natural philosophy|natural philosopher]], separatist theologian, [[Linguistics|grammarian]], multi-subject educator and [[Classical liberalism|classical liberal]] political theorist. He published over 150 works, and conducted experiments in several areas of science.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Priestley is credited with his independent discovery of [[Oxygen|oxygen]] by the thermal decomposition of mercuric oxide, having isolated it in 1774. During his lifetime, Priestley\'s considerable scientific reputation rested on his invention of carbonated water, his writings on [[Electricity|electricity]], and his discovery of several "airs" (gases), the most famous being what Priestley dubbed "dephlogisticated air" (oxygen). Priestley\'s determination to defend phlogiston theory and to reject what would become the chemical revolution eventually left him isolated within the scientific community. Priestley\'s science was integral to his theology, and he consistently tried to fuse [[Age of Enlightenment|Enlightenment]] rationalism with Christian theism. In his metaphysical texts, Priestley attempted to combine theism, [[Materialism|materialism]], and [[Determinism|determinism]], a project that has been called "audacious and original".',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Birthplace", "Birstall, Yorkshire, England" },
			{ "Died", "6 February 1804" },
			{
				"Occupation",
				"[[Chemist]], natural philosopher, theologian, [[Linguistics|grammarian]], [[Teacher|teacher]]",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Joseph Pulitzer",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Hungarian-American newspaper publisher (1847–1911)",
		tags = { "Journalists" },
		summary = "**Joseph Pulitzer** was a Hungarian-American politician and a newspaper publisher of the St. Louis Post-Dispatch and the New York World. He became a leading national figure in the U.S. [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]] and served one term representing New York's 9th congressional district.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Circulation reached a million copies a day, and the journalism opened the way to mass-circulation newspapers that depended on advertising revenue, rather than on cover price or on political-party subsidies. Such newspapers attracted readers by using multiple forms of news, gossip, entertainment, and advertising. Pulitzer's name is best known for the [[Pulitzer Prize|Pulitzer Prizes]] established in 1917 as a result of the specified endowment in his will to [[Columbia University]]. The university awards prizes annually to recognize and reward excellence in American journalism, photography, literature, history, poetry, music, and drama. Pulitzer also funded the Columbia School of Journalism with his philanthropic bequest; it opened in 1912.",
			},
		},
		related = {
			"David Attenborough",
			"Edward R. Murrow",
			"Horace Greeley",
			"Nellie Bly",
			"Svetlana Alexievich",
