	{
		title = "Justinian I",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Roman emperor from 527 to 565",
		tags = { "Eastern Europe", "Byzantine Empire" },
		aliases = { "Justinian the Great" },
		summary = "**Justinian I**, also known as **Justinian the Great**, was [[Roman emperor]] from 527 to 565. His reign was marked by the ambitious but only partly realized (restoration of the Empire). This ambition was expressed by the partial recovery of the territories of the defunct [[Western Roman Empire]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"His general, Belisarius, swiftly conquered the Vandal Kingdom in North Africa. Subsequently, Belisarius, Narses, and other generals conquered the Ostrogothic Kingdom, restoring Dalmatia, [[Sicily]], [[Italian Peninsula|Italy]], and [[Rome]] to the empire after more than half a century of rule by the [[Ostrogoths]]. The praetorian prefect Liberius reclaimed the south of the [[Iberian Peninsula]], establishing the province of Spania. These campaigns re-established Roman control over the western Mediterranean, increasing the Empire's annual revenue by over a million. During his reign, Justinian also subdued the Tzani, a people on the east coast of the [[Black Sea]] that had never been under Roman rule before. He engaged the [[Sasanian Empire]] in the east during Kavad I's reign, and later again during [[Khosrow I]]'s reign; this second conflict was partially initiated due to his ambitions in the west.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Leo III the Isaurian" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "482" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Tauresium (probably Gradište near Skopje), [[Byzantine Empire|Eastern Roman Empire]]" },
			{ "Died", "14 November 565 (aged 82/83)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Justus von Liebig",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "German chemist (1803–1873)",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "Justus Freiherr von Liebig" },
		summary = "**Justus Freiherr von Liebig** (12 May 1803 – 18 April 1873) was a German [[Scientist|scientist]] who made major contributions to the theory, practice, and pedagogy of chemistry, as well as to agricultural and [[Biology|biological]] [[Chemistry|chemistry]]; he is considered one of the principal founders of [[Organic chemistry|organic chemistry]]. As a professor at the University of Giessen, he devised the modern laboratory-oriented teaching method, and for such innovations, he is regarded as one of the most outstanding chemistry teachers of all time.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'He has been described as the "father of the [[Fertilizer|fertilizer]] industry" for his emphasis on [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]] and [[Mineral|minerals]] as essential plant [[Nutrient|nutrients]], and his popularization of the law of the minimum, which states that plant growth is limited by the scarcest nutrient resource, rather than the total amount of resources available. He also developed a manufacturing process for beef extracts, and with his consent a company, called Liebig Extract of Meat Company, was founded to exploit the concept; it later introduced the Oxo brand beef bouillon cube. He popularized an earlier invention for condensing vapors, which came to be known as the Liebig condenser.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "12 May 1803" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Darmstadt, Hesse-Darmstadt" },
			{ "Died", "18 April 1873" },
			{ "Field", "[[Chemistry]]" },
			{ "Worked at", "University of Giessen, [[LMU Munich|Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München]]" },
			{ "Education", "[[University of Bonn]], University of Erlangen–Nuremberg" },
			{ "Teacher", "Karl Wilhelm Gottlob Kastner" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Jöns Jacob Berzelius",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Swedish chemist (1779–1848)",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Modern" },
		summary = '**Jöns Jacob Berzelius** was a Swedish chemist. Berzelius is considered, along with [[Robert Boyle]], [[John Dalton]], and [[Antoine Lavoisier]], to be one of the founders of modern [[Chemistry|chemistry]]. Berzelius became a member of the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences in 1808 and served from 1818 as its principal functionary. He is known in Sweden as the "Father of Swedish Chemistry". During his lifetime he did not customarily use his first given name, and was universally known simply as Jacob Berzelius.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Although Berzelius began his career as a physician, his enduring contributions were in the fields of [[Electrochemistry|electrochemistry]], chemical bonding and stoichiometry. In particular, he is noted for his determination of atomic weights and his experiments that led to a more complete understanding of the principles of stoichiometry, which is the branch of chemistry pertaining to the quantitative relationships between elements in chemical compounds and chemical reactions and that these occur in definite proportions. This understanding came to be known as the "Law of Constant Proportions". Berzelius was a strict [[Empiricism|empiricist]], expecting that any new theory must be consistent with the sum of contemporary chemical knowledge. He developed improved methods of chemical analysis, which were required to develop the basic data in support of his work on stoichiometry.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Harold Urey" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "20 August 1779" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Väversunda, Östergötland, Sweden" },
			{ "Died", "7 August 1848" },
			{ "Field", "[[Chemistry]]" },
			{ "Worked at", "Karolinska Institute" },
			{ "Education", "Uppsala University" },
			{ "Teacher", "Johann Afzelius" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Jürgen Habermas",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "German social philosopher (1929–2026)",
		tags = { "Philosophers", "Modern" },
		summary = "**Jürgen Habermas** (18 June 1929 – 14 March 2026) was a German philosopher and social theorist in the tradition of [[Critical theory|critical theory]] and [[Pragmatism|pragmatism]]. His work addressed communicative rationality and the public sphere. He held professorships at [[Heidelberg University]] and Goethe University Frankfurt and directed the Max Planck Institute in Starnberg.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Associated with the Frankfurt School, Habermas\'s work focused on the foundations of [[Epistemology|epistemology]] and [[Social theory|social theory]], the analysis of advanced capitalism and democracy, the [[Rule of law|rule of law]] in a critical social-evolutionary context, and contemporary politics, particularly German politics. His major works include The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere (1962), a social history of the emergence and decline of bourgeois public discourse, and The Theory of Communicative Action (1981), which advanced a theory of rationality grounded in interpersonal linguistic communication rather than in instrumental or strategic reason. He developed the concept of discourse ethics and argued that the Enlightenment remained an "unfinished project" requiring correction rather than abandonment.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Michel Foucault", "Alfred North Whitehead" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "18 June 1929" },
			{ "Birthplace", "[[Düsseldorf]], Weimar Republic" },
			{ "Died", "14 March 2026" },
			{ "Era", "[[Contemporary philosophy]]" },
			{ "Region", "[[Western philosophy]]" },
			{ "Teacher", "Erich Rothacker" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Kabir",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "15th-16th century Indian poet and sant",
		tags = { "Asia", "Southern Asia" },
		summary = "His writings influenced Hinduism's Bhakti movement, and his verses are found in Sikhism's scripture Guru Granth Sahib, the of Saint Garib Das, and Kabir Sagar of Dharamdas. Today, Kabir is an important personality in Hinduism, Sikhism and in Sufism. He was a disciple of Ramananda, the founder of the Ramanandi Sampradaya.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'He was born in the city of [[Varanasi]] in what is now [[Uttar Pradesh]], he is known for being critical of organised religions. He questioned what he regarded to be the meaningless and unethical practices of all religions, primarily what he considered to be the wrong practices in Hinduism and Islam. During his lifetime, he was threatened by both Hindus and Muslims for his views. When he died, several Hindus and the Muslims he had inspired claimed him as theirs. Kabir\'s suggestion was that "truth" is with the person who is on the path of righteousness, who considers everything, living and non living, as divine, and who is passively detached from the affairs of the world. To know the truth, suggested Kabir, drop the "I", or the ego. Kabir\'s legacy survives and continues through the Kabir panth ("Path of Kabir"), Sant Mat sect that recognises Kabir as its founder.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Bai Juyi", "Han Yu", "Murasaki Shikibu", "Ouyang Xiu" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "1398" },
