				"[[University of Bonn]] [[Humboldt University of Berlin|University of Berlin]] [[University of Jena]]",
			},
			{ "Era", "[[19th-century philosophy]]" },
			{ "Region", "[[Western philosophy]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Karl Pearson",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "English eugenicist and polymath (1857–1936)",
		tags = { "Mathematicians", "Modern" },
		summary = "**Karl Pearson** was an English biostatistician and [[Mathematician|mathematician]]. He has been credited with establishing the discipline of mathematical statistics. He founded the world's first university statistics department at [[University College London]] in 1911, and contributed significantly to the field of biometrics. Pearson was also a proponent of Social Darwinism and [[Eugenics|eugenics]], and his thought is an example of what is today described as [[Scientific racism|scientific racism]]. Pearson was a protégé and biographer of Sir Francis Galton. He edited and completed both William Kingdon Clifford's Common Sense of the Exact Sciences (1885) and Isaac Todhunter's History of the Theory of Elasticity, Vol. 1 (1886–1893) and Vol.",
