		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Austrian mathematician and theoretical physicist (1844–1906)",
		tags = { "Physics", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "Ludwig Eduard Boltzmann" },
		summary = "**Ludwig Eduard Boltzmann** ( or; 20 February 1844 – 5 September 1906) was an Austrian [[Mathematician|mathematician]] and [[Theoretical physics|theoretical physicist]]. His greatest achievements were the development of [[Statistical mechanics|statistical mechanics]] and the statistical explanation of the [[Second law of thermodynamics|second law of thermodynamics]]. In 1877, he provided the current definition of [[Entropy|entropy]], where is the number of microstates whose [[Energy|energy]] equals the system's energy, interpreted as a measure of the statistical disorder of a system. [[Max Planck]] named the constant kB the Boltzmann constant.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Statistical mechanics is one of the pillars of modern [[Physics|physics]]. It describes how macroscopic observations (such as [[Temperature|temperature]] and [[Pressure|pressure]]) are related to microscopic parameters that fluctuate around an average. It connects thermodynamic quantities (such as [[Heat capacity|heat capacity]]) to microscopic behavior, whereas, in classical thermodynamics, the only available option would be to measure and tabulate such quantities for various materials.",
