		description = "Austrian philosopher and logician (1889–1951)",
		tags = { "Philosophers", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "Ludwig Josef Johann Wittgenstein" },
		summary = "**Ludwig Josef Johann Wittgenstein** was an Austro-British [[Philosophy|philosopher]] who worked in [[Logic|logic]], [[Philosophy of mathematics|philosophy of mathematics]], [[Philosophy of mind|philosophy of mind]], and [[Philosophy of language|philosophy of language]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Wittgenstein was born in Vienna into one of Europe's richest families. From 1911 to 1913, he studied at the [[University of Cambridge]] under [[Bertrand Russell]] and interacted closely with [[John Maynard Keynes]] and G. E. Moore, among others. During this time, he inherited a fortune, part of which he donated to various poets and artists. He also began what would become his first important philosophical work. However, at the start of World War I, he left academia to enlist in the Austro-Hungarian Army. He worked as a schoolteacher around Vienna. Despite these setbacks, his philosophical work was published in the Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus in 1921, followed by an English translation in 1922 with help from Frank P. Ramsey, to considerable discussion and acclaim.",
