		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Spanish-Mexican filmmaker (1900–1983)",
		tags = { "Directors", "Europe" },
		aliases = { "Luis Buñuel Portolés" },
		summary = "**Luis Buñuel Portolés** was a Spanish and Mexican filmmaker who worked in [[France]], [[Mexico]], and [[Spain]]. He has been widely considered by many film critics, historians, and directors to be one of the greatest and most influential filmmakers of all time. Buñuel's works are known for their [[Avant-garde|avant-garde]] surrealism which was also infused with political commentary.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Often associated with the surrealist movement, Buñuel's career spanned the 1920s through the 1970s. He collaborated with surrealist painter [[Salvador Dalí]] on Un Chien Andalou (1929) and L'Age d'Or (1930). Both are considered masterpieces of surrealist cinema. Buñuel then transitioned to making artful, surrealistic and satirical films. He earned the at the 1961 Cannes Film Festival forViridiana (1961), which criticized the [[Francoist Spain|Francoist dictatorship]]. He satirized social conditions in The Exterminating Angel (1962) and The Discreet Charm of the Bourgeoisie (1972), the latter of which won the Academy Award for Best Foreign Language Film. He also directed Diary of a Chambermaid (1964) and Belle de Jour (1967). He earned the National Society of Film Critics Award for Best Director for his final film, That Obscure Object of Desire (1977).",
