	{
		title = "Michael Faraday",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "English chemist and physicist (1791–1867)",
		tags = { "Physics", "Modern" },
		summary = "**Michael Faraday** (22 September 1791 – 25 August 1867) was an English [[Chemist|chemist]] and [[Physicist|physicist]] who contributed vastly to the study of [[Electrochemistry|electrochemistry]] and [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]]. His main discoveries include the principles underlying [[Electromagnetic induction|electromagnetic induction]], diamagnetism, and [[Electrolysis|electrolysis]]. Although Faraday received little formal education, as a self-made man, he was one of the most influential scientists in history. It was by his research on the [[Magnetic field|magnetic field]] around a [[Electrical conductor|conductor]] carrying a [[Direct current|direct current]] that Faraday established the concept of the [[Electromagnetic field|electromagnetic field]] in physics.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Faraday also established that [[Magnetism|magnetism]] could affect rays of light and that there was an underlying relationship between the two phenomena. He similarly discovered the principles of electromagnetic induction, diamagnetism, and the laws of electrolysis. His inventions of [[Electric motor|electromagnetic rotary devices]] formed the foundation of electric motor technology, and it was largely due to his efforts that electricity became practical for use in technology. The [[International System of Units|SI]] unit of [[Capacitance|capacitance]], the [[Farad|farad]], is named after him. As a chemist, Faraday discovered [[Benzene|benzene]] and tetrachloroethylene, investigated the clathrate hydrate of chlorine, invented an early form of the Bunsen burner and the system of oxidation numbers, and popularised terminology such as "[[Anode|anode]]", "[[Cathode|cathode]]", "[[Electrode|electrode]]" and "[[Ion|ion]]".',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "22 September 1791" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Newington Butts, Surrey, [[England]]" },
			{ "Died", "25 August 1867" },
			{ "Fields", "[[Chemistry]] [[Physics|physics]]" },
			{ "Worked at", "Royal Institution (1821–1867)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Michel Foucault",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "French philosopher (1926–1984)",
		tags = { "Philosophers", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "Paul-Michel Foucault" },
		summary = "**Paul-Michel Foucault**, known as **Michel Foucault**, was a French philosopher, literary critic, and historian of ideas who analyzed the relationships between [[Power (political science)|power]], [[Knowledge|knowledge]], and [[Liberty|liberty]], and how they are used as a form of [[Social control|social control]] through institutions. Sometimes called a [[Structuralism|structuralist]] or [[Postmodernism|postmodernist]], he rejected these labels and sought to critique [[Authority|authority]] without limiting himself. His thought has influenced academics in many areas of study, including [[Anthropology|anthropology]], [[Communication studies|communication studies]], criminology, [[Cultural studies|cultural studies]], [[Feminism|feminism]], literary theory, [[Psychology|psychology]], and [[Sociology|sociology]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"His arguments against homophobia, racial prejudice, and other [[Ideology|ideological doctrines]] have also shaped scholarship on [[Critical theory|critical theory]] and [[Marxism]]. Born in Poitiers, France, into an upper-middle-class family, Foucault was educated at the Lycée Henri-IV, at the, developing an interest in philosophy and coming under the influence of his tutors Jean Hyppolite and Louis Althusser. At the [[University of Paris]] ([[University of Paris|Sorbonne]]), he earned degrees in philosophy and psychology. After several years as a cultural diplomat abroad, he returned to France and published his first major book, The History of Madness (1961). While working at the University of Clermont-Ferrand (1960–66), he produced The Birth of the Clinic (1963) and The Order of Things (1966), which have a structuralist affinity from which he later distanced himself.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
