		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Persian scholar and vizier of the Seljuk Empire (1018–1092)",
		tags = { "Asia", "Central Asia, Iran, Caucasus" },
		aliases = { "Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī Ṭūsī", "Niẓām al-Mulk" },
		summary = "**Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī Ṭūsī** (1018 – 1092), better known by his honorific title of **Niẓām al-Mulk**, was a [[Persians|Persian]] Sunni scholar, [[Jurist|jurist]], and vizier of the [[Seljuk Empire]]. Widely regarded as one of the most influential statesmen in medieval Islamic history, the administrative policies and bureaucratic frameworks formalized by Nizam al-Mulk deeply influenced Perso-Islamic statecraft for centuries.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"One of his most important legacies was the founding of a system of madrasas in cities across the Seljuk Empire which were called the Nizamiyyas after him. He is also traditionally credited with authoring the Siyasatnama (Book of Government), a political and administrative treatise that uses historical anecdotes to pragmatically discuss justice, effective rule, and the role of the bureaucracy in Islamic society.",
			},
