			{ "Worked at", "University of Basel" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Paul Cézanne",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "French painter (1839–1906)",
		tags = { "Western painters and illustrators", "Modern era" },
		summary = "**Paul Cézanne** was a French [[Post-Impressionism|Post-Impressionist]] painter whose work introduced new modes of representation, influenced [[Avant-garde|avant-garde]] artistic movements of the early 20th century and formed the bridge between late 19th-century [[Impressionism]] and early 20th-century [[Cubism]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While his early works were influenced by [[Romanticism]]—such as the murals in the Jas de Bouffan country house—and [[Realism (arts)|Realism]], Cézanne arrived at a new pictorial language through intense examination of Impressionist forms of expression. He altered conventional approaches to [[Perspective (graphical)|perspective]] and broke established rules of [[Academic art|academic art]] by emphasizing the underlying structure of objects in a composition and the formal qualities of art. Cézanne strived for a renewal of traditional design methods on the basis of the impressionistic colour space and colour modulation principles. Cézanne's often repetitive, exploratory brushstrokes are highly characteristic and recognizable. He used planes of colour and small brushstrokes that build up to form complex fields. The paintings convey Cézanne's intense study of his subjects.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Caspar David Friedrich", "Gerhard Richter", "John Singer Sargent" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "19 January 1839" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Aix-en-Provence, France" },
			{ "Died", "22 October 1906" },
			{ "Known for", "Painting" },
			{ "Movement", "[[Impressionism]], [[Post-Impressionism]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Paul Dirac",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "British physicist (1902–1984)",
		tags = { "Physics", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "Paul Adrien Maurice Dirac" },
		summary = '**Paul Adrien Maurice Dirac** was an English theoretical [[Physicist|physicist]] who is considered to be one of the founders of [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]]. Dirac laid the foundations for both [[Quantum electrodynamics|quantum electrodynamics]] and [[Quantum field theory|quantum field theory]], coining the former term. He was Lucasian Professor of Mathematics at the [[University of Cambridge]] from 1932 to 1969, and a professor of physics at Florida State University from 1970 to 1984. Dirac shared the 1933 [[Nobel Prize in Physics]] with [[Erwin Schrödinger]] "for the discovery of new productive forms of atomic theory."',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Dirac graduated from the University of Bristol with a [[Bachelor of Science]] in Electrical Engineering in 1921, and a [[Bachelor of Arts]] in Mathematics in 1923. Dirac then graduated from [[St John\'s College, Cambridge]], with a [[Doctor of Philosophy]] in Physics in 1926, writing the first ever thesis on quantum mechanics. Dirac formulated the Dirac equation, one of the most important results in physics, in 1928. The equation connected [[Theory of relativity|special relativity]] and quantum mechanics and predicted the existence of [[Antimatter|antimatter]]. The equation has been regarded by some physicists as "the real seed of modern physics". Dirac wrote a famous paper in 1931, which further predicted the existence of antimatter. Dirac also contributed greatly to the reconciliation of [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]] with quantum mechanics.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Albert A. Michelson" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "8 August 1902" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Bristol, England" },
			{ "Died", "20 October 1984" },
			{ "Citizenship", "United Kingdom Switzerland (until 1919)" },
			{ "Fields", "[[Physics]]" },
			{ "Worked at", "[[University of Cambridge]] Florida State University" },
			{ "Teacher", "Ralph Fowler" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Paul Gauguin",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "French artist (1848–1903)",
		tags = { "Western painters and illustrators", "Modern era" },
		aliases = { "Eugène Henri Paul Gauguin" },
		summary = "**Eugène Henri Paul Gauguin** was a French painter, sculptor, printmaker, ceramist, and writer, whose work has been primarily associated with the Post-Impressionist and [[Symbolism (movement)|Symbolist]] movements. He was also an influential practitioner of wood engraving and [[Woodcut|woodcuts]] as art forms. While only moderately successful during his lifetime, Gauguin has since been recognized for his experimental use of color and Synthetist style that were distinct from [[Impressionism]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Gauguin was born in Paris in 1848, amidst the tumult of [[Revolutions of 1848|Europe's revolutionary year]]. In 1850, Gauguin's family settled in [[Peru]], where he experienced a privileged childhood (until the age of six) that left a lasting impression on him. Later, financial struggles led them back to France, where Gauguin received formal education. Initially working as a stockbroker, Gauguin started painting in his spare time, his interest in art kindled by visits to galleries and exhibitions. The financial crisis of 1882 significantly impacted his brokerage career, prompting a shift to full-time painting. Gauguin's art education was largely self-taught and informal, shaped significantly by his associations with other artists rather than [[Academic art|academic training]]. His entry into the art world was facilitated by his acquaintance with [[Camille Pissarro]], a leading Impressionist.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Gerhard Richter", "John Constable" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "7 June 1848" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Paris, France" },
			{ "Died", "8 May 1903" },
			{ "Field", "Painting, sculpture, [[Ceramic art|ceramics]], [[Engraving|engraving]]" },
			{ "Movement", "[[Post-Impressionism]], Primitivism, Synthetism" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Paul Klee",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Swiss-German painter (1879–1940)",
		tags = { "Western painters and illustrators", "Modern era" },
		summary = "**Paul Klee** was a Swiss-born German artist. His highly individual style was influenced by movements in art that included [[Expressionism|expressionism]], [[Cubism|cubism]], and [[Surrealism|surrealism]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Klee was a natural draftsman who experimented with and eventually deeply explored [[Color theory|color theory]], writing about it extensively. His lectures Writings on Form and Design Theory (Schriften zur Form und Gestaltungslehre), published in English as the Paul Klee Notebooks, are held to be as important for modern art as [[Leonardo da Vinci]]'s A Treatise on Painting was for the [[Renaissance]]. He and his colleague, Russian painter [[Wassily Kandinsky]], both taught at the [[Bauhaus]] school of art, design and architecture in [[Germany]]. His works reflect his dry humor and his sometimes childlike perspective, his personal moods and beliefs, and his musicality.",
			},
		},
