	{
		title = "Rajaraja I",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chola emperor from 985 to 1014",
		tags = { "Asia", "Southern Asia" },
		aliases = { "Rajaraja the Great" },
		summary = "**Rājarāja I** (Middle Tamil: Rājarāja Colan; Rājarāja Śōḷa; 3 November 947 – January/February 1014), also known as **Rajaraja the Great**, was a Chola emperor who reigned from 985 to 1014. He was known for his conquests of [[South India|southern India]] and the Anuradhapura kingdom of [[Sri Lanka]], as well as increasing Chola influence across the Indian Ocean. Rajaraja's birth name is Arulmozhi Varman.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Rajaraja's empire encompassed vast territories, including regions of the Pandya country, the Chera country, and northern Sri Lanka. He also extended his influence over strategic islands such as Lakshadweep, [[Thailand|Thiladhunmadulu]] atoll, and parts of the [[Maldives]] in the Indian Ocean. His conquests were not limited to the south; he also launched successful campaigns against the Western Gangas and the Western Chalukyas, extending Chola authority as far as the Tungabhadra River. In the east, Rajaraja faced fierce opposition from the Telugu Chola king Jata Choda Bhima over control of Vengi. This region held significant strategic importance due to its access to resources and trade routes. The conflict between the two rulers intensified as they vied for dominance in the region, resulting in significant battles and shifting allegiances.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Alauddin Khalji", "Harsha" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "3 November 947" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Thanjavur, Chola Empire (modern-day [[Tamil Nadu]], India)" },
			{ "Died", "January 1014" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ralph Waldo Emerson",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "American philosopher (1803–1882)",
		tags = { "Philosophers", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "Waldo" },
		summary = '**Ralph Waldo Emerson** (May 25, 1803 – April 27, 1882), who went by his middle name **Waldo**, was an American essayist, lecturer, philosopher, minister, abolitionist, and poet who led the transcendentalist movement of the mid-19th century. He was seen as a champion of [[Individualism|individualism]] and [[Critical thinking|critical thinking]], as well as a prescient critic of the countervailing pressures of society and [[Conformity|conformity]]. The German philosopher [[Friedrich Nietzsche]] thought he was "the most gifted of the Americans", and [[Walt Whitman]] called Emerson his "master".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Emerson gradually moved away from the religious and social beliefs of his contemporaries, formulating and expressing the philosophy of Transcendentalism in his 1836 essay, Nature. His speech "The American Scholar", given in 1837, was called America\'s "intellectual Declaration of Independence" by Oliver Wendell Holmes Sr. Emerson wrote most of his important essays as lectures, and then revised them for print. His first two collections of essays, Essays: First Series (1841) and Essays: Second Series (1844), represent the core of his thinking. They include the well-known essays "Self-Reliance", "The Over-Soul", "Circles", "The Poet", and "Experience". Together with "Nature", these essays made the mid-1830s to the mid-1840s Emerson\'s most fertile period. Emerson wrote on a number of subjects, never espousing fixed philosophical tenets.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "25 May 1803" },
			{ "Birthplace", "[[Boston]], Massachusetts, U.S." },
			{ "Died", "27 April 1882" },
			{ "Education", "Harvard University" },
			{ "Era", "[[19th-century philosophy]]" },
			{ "Region", "American philosophy" },
			{ "Worked at", "Harvard College" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Rama I",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "King of Siam from 1782 to 1809",
		tags = { "Asia and Oceania", "Southeastern Asia" },
		aliases = { "Phutthayotfa Chulalok", "King Phutthayotfa Chulalok the Great" },
		summary = "**Phutthayotfa Chulalok**, posthumously honoured as **King Phutthayotfa Chulalok the Great**, also known by his [[Regnal name|regnal name]] **Rama I**, was the founder of the Rattanakosin Kingdom (now [[Thailand]]) and the first King of Siam from the reigning Chakri dynasty. He ascended the throne in 1782, following the deposition of King Taksin of Thonburi. He was also celebrated as the founder of Rattanakosin (now [[Bangkok]]) as the new capital of the reunited kingdom.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Rama I, whose given name was Thongduang, was born from a [[Mon people|Mon]] male line descent family, great-grandson of Kosa Pan. His father served in the royal court of the [[Ayutthaya Kingdom]]. Thongduang and his younger brother Bunma served King Taksin in wars against the Burmese [[Konbaung dynasty]] and helped him in the reunification of Siam. During this time he emerged as Siam's most powerful military leader. Thongduang was the first Somdet Chao Phraya, the highest rank the nobility could attain, equaled to that of royalty. In 1782, he took control of Siam and crowned himself as the monarch. The most famous event in his reign was the Burmese–Siamese War (1785–1786), which was the last major Burmese assault on Siam. Rama I's reign marked a revival of Siamese culture and state organization following the collapse of the Siamese kingdom in 1767, whose capital was then situated at Ayutthaya.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Qianlong Emperor" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "20 March 1737" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Ayutthaya, [[Ayutthaya Kingdom|Ayutthaya]]" },
			{ "Died", "7 September 1809" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ramesses II",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Pharaoh of Egypt from 1279 to 1213 BC",
		tags = { "Africa", "Egypt" },
		aliases = { "Ramesses the Great" },
		summary = "**Ramesses II**, commonly known as **Ramesses the Great**, was the third [[Pharaoh|pharaoh]] of the Nineteenth Dynasty of Egypt. Ramesses II is often regarded as the greatest, most celebrated, and most powerful pharaoh of the [[New Kingdom of Egypt|New Kingdom]], which itself was the most powerful period of [[Ancient Egypt|ancient Egypt]]. He is also widely considered one of ancient Egypt's most successful warrior pharaohs, conducting no fewer than 15 military campaigns, all resulting in victories, excluding the Battle of Kadesh, which is generally considered a stalemate.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'His 66-year rule was also the longest recorded reign of any pharaoh (and one of the longest in history), possibly alongside Pepi II, who lived 1,000 years earlier and is said to have reigned for over 90 years. In [[Ancient Greek literature|ancient Greek sources]], he is called **Ozymandias**, derived from the first part of his Egyptian-language regnal name:. Contemporary documents, particularly [[Cuneiform|cuneiform]] letters discovered at Hattusa indicate that the name Ramesses was pronounced Riamessesa and the full titulary pronounced Wasmuriya Satepnaria Riamessesa Maiamana. Ramesses was also referred to as the "Great God" by successor pharaohs, who prayed to enjoy his longevity and glory. Ramesses ascended to the throne at age 25, and for the early part of his reign, he focused on building cities, temples, and monuments.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "c. 1303 BC" },
			{ "Died", "13 August 1213 BC (aged 90–91)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ramesses III",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Pharaoh of Egypt from 1185 to 1154 BC",
		tags = { "Africa", "Egypt" },
		aliases = { "Usermaatre Meryamun Ramesses III" },
		summary = "**Usermaatre Meryamun Ramesses III** was the second [[Pharaoh]] of the Twentieth Dynasty in [[Ancient Egypt]]. Some scholars date his reign from 26 March 1185 to 15 April 1154 BC, and he is considered to be the last pharaoh of the [[New Kingdom of Egypt|New Kingdom]] to have wielded substantial power.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'His long reign saw the decline of Egyptian political and economic power, linked to a series of invasions and internal economic problems that also plagued pharaohs before him. This coincided with a decline in the cultural sphere of Ancient Egypt. The defensive strategy of Ramesses III was able to slow down Egypt\'s decline, though it meant that his successors had a weaker military. He has also been described as a "warrior Pharaoh" due to his strong military strategies. At the Battle of the Delta and Battle of Djahy his armies defeated the invaders known as "the Sea Peoples", who had caused destruction in other civilizations and empires. He was able to save Egypt from collapsing at the time when [[Late Bronze Age collapse|many other empires fell]] during the [[Bronze Age|Late Bronze Age]]; however, the damage of the invasions took a toll on Egypt.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Ptolemy I Soter", "Thutmose III" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "1217 BC?" },
			{ "Died", "1154 BC (aged 61-62)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ramkhamhaeng",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "King of Sukhothai from 1279 to 1298",
		tags = { "Asia", "Southeastern Asia" },
		aliases = { "Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng the Great" },
		summary = "**Ramkhamhaeng**, commonly known as **Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng the Great**, was the third king of the Phra Ruang dynasty, ruling the Sukhothai Kingdom—a historical polity in what is now [[Thailand]]—from 1279 to 1298, during its most prosperous era.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"He is credited with the creation of the Thai alphabet and with firmly establishing Theravada Buddhism as the state religion of the kingdom.",
