	{
		title = "Robert Boyle",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Anglo-Irish scientist (1627–1691)",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Early modern" },
		summary = "**Robert Boyle** was an [[Anglo-Irish people|Anglo-Irish]] natural philosopher, [[Chemist|chemist]], [[Physicist|physicist]], [[Alchemy|alchemist]] and inventor. Boyle is largely regarded today as the first modern chemist, and therefore one of the founders of modern [[Chemistry|chemistry]], and one of the pioneers of modern experimental [[Scientific method|scientific method]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"He is best known for Boyle's law, which describes the inversely proportional relationship between the absolute [[Pressure|pressure]] and [[Volume|volume]] of a gas, if the temperature is kept constant within a closed system. Among his works, The Sceptical Chymist is seen as a cornerstone book in the field of chemistry. He was a devout and pious Anglican and is noted for his works in theology.",
