	{
		title = "Robert Bresson",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "French filmmaker (1901–1999)",
		tags = { "Europe", "France" },
		summary = "**Robert Bresson** was a French filmmaker. Known for his ascetic approach, Bresson made a notable contribution to the art of cinema; his non-professional actors, ellipses, and sparse use of scoring have led his works to be regarded as preeminent examples of minimalist film. Much of his work is known for being tragic in story and nature.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Bresson is among the most highly regarded filmmakers of all time. He has the highest number of films (seven) that made the 2012 Sight and Sound critics\' poll of the Greatest Films of All Time. His works A Man Escaped (1956), Pickpocket (1959) and Au hasard Balthazar (1966) were ranked among the top 100, and other films like Mouchette (1967) and (1983) also received many votes. [[Jean-Luc Godard]] once wrote, "He is the [[Cinema of France|French cinema]], as [[Fyodor Dostoevsky|Dostoevsky]] is the [[Russian literature|Russian novel]] and [[Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart|Mozart]] is German music."',
