	{
		title = "Robert Clive",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "British military officer (1725–1774)",
		tags = { "Asia and Oceania", "Southern Asia" },
		aliases = { "Robert Clive, 1st Baron Clive", "Clive of India" },
		summary = "**Robert Clive, 1st Baron Clive** (29 September 1725 – 22 November 1774), also known as **Clive of India**, was the first British Governor of the [[Bengal Presidency]]. Clive has been widely credited for laying the foundation of the British [[East India Company]] (EIC) rule in [[Bengal]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'He began as a "writer" (the term used then in India for an office clerk) for the EIC in 1744; however, after being caught up in military action during the fall of Madras, Clive joined the EIC\'s private army. Clive rapidly rose through the military ranks of the EIC and was eventually credited with establishing [[Company rule in India|Company rule]] in [[Bengal]] by winning the Battle of Plassey in 1757. In return for supporting the Nawab Mir Jafar as ruler of Bengal, Clive was guaranteed a jagir of £90,000 per year, which was the rent the EIC would otherwise pay to the Nawab for their tax-farming concession. When Clive left India in January 1767 he had a fortune of £401,102 which he remitted through the [[Dutch East India Company]]. Blocking impending French mastery of India, Clive improvised a 1751 military expedition that ultimately enabled the EIC to adopt the French strategy of indirect rule via puppet government.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Humayun", "Babur" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "29 September 1725" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Styche, Shropshire, England" },
			{ "Died", "22 November 1774" },
			{ "Education", "Merchant Taylors' School" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Robert Falcon Scott",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "British Antarctic explorer (1868–1912)",
		tags = { "Explorers", "Modern" },
		summary = "Captain **Robert Falcon Scott** (6 June 1868 – c. 29 March 1912) was a British naval officer and explorer who led two expeditions to the [[Antarctic]] regions: the Discovery Expedition of 1901–1904 and the Terra Nova Expedition of 1910–1913.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"On the first expedition, Scott set a new southern record by marching to latitude 82°S and discovered the Antarctic Plateau, on which the South Pole is located. On the second venture, Scott led a party of five which reached the [[South Pole]] on 17 January 1912, less than five weeks after Amundsen's South Pole expedition. On the return journey from the Pole, a planned meeting with supporting dog teams from the base camp failed, despite Scott's written instructions, and at a distance of 162 mi from their base camp at Hut Point and approximately 12.5 mi from the next depot, Scott and his companions died. When Scott and his party's bodies were discovered, they had in their possession the first Antarctic [[Fossil|fossils]] discovered. The fossils were determined to be from the Glossopteris tree and proved that Antarctica was once forested and joined to other continents.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Edmund Hillary", "Valentina Tereshkova" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "6 June 1868" },
			{ "Birthplace", "Plymouth, Devon, England" },
			{ "Died", "c. 29 March 1912" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Robert Frost",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "American poet (1874–1963)",
		tags = { "Americas", "US and Canada" },
		aliases = { "Robert Lee Frost" },
		summary = "**Robert Lee Frost** (March26, 1874 – January29, 1963) was an American poet. Known for his realistic depictions of rural life and his command of American [[Colloquialism|colloquial speech]], Frost frequently wrote about settings from rural life in [[New England]] in the early 20th century, using them to examine complex social and philosophical themes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Frequently honored during his lifetime, Frost is the only poet to receive four Pulitzer Prizes for Poetry. He became one of America\'s rare "public literary figures, almost an artistic institution". Appointed United States Poet Laureate in 1958, he also received the [[Congressional Gold Medal]] in 1960, and in 1961 was named poet laureate of [[Vermont]]. Randall Jarrell wrote: "Robert Frost, along with Stevens and [[T. S. Eliot|Eliot]], seems to me the greatest of the American poets of this century. Frost\'s virtues are extraordinary. No other living poet has written so well about the actions of ordinary men; his wonderful dramatic monologues or dramatic scenes come out of a knowledge of people that few poets have had, and they are written in a verse that uses, sometimes with absolute mastery, the rhythms of actual speech". In his 1939 essay "The Figure a Poem Makes", Frost explains his poetics:',
			},
		},
		related = { "Henry Wadsworth Longfellow", "Maya Angelou" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Born", "26 March 1874" },
			{ "Birthplace", "[[San Francisco]], California, U.S." },
			{ "Died", "29 January 1963" },
			{ "Occupation", "Poet, playwright" },
			{ "Notable works", "A Boy's Will, North of Boston, New Hampshire" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Robert Fulton",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "American engineer and inventor (1765–1815)",
		tags = { "Inventors and engineers", "Early modern" },
		summary = "In 1807, that steamboat traveled on the Hudson River with passengers from [[New York City]] to Albany and back again, a round trip of 300 nmi, in 62 hours. The success of his steamboat changed river traffic and trade on major American rivers.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Fulton became interested in [[Steam engine|steam engines]] and the idea of steamboats in 1777 when he was around age 12 and visited state delegate William Henry of Lancaster, Pennsylvania, who was interested in this topic. Henry had learned about inventor [[James Watt]] and his Watt steam engine on an earlier visit to [[England]]. Fulton is also credited with inventing some of the world's earliest naval torpedoes for use by the [[Royal Navy]].",
			},
		},
