	{
		title = "Sappho",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ancient Greek lyric poet (c. 630–c. 570 BC)",
		tags = { "Europe", "Greece" },
		summary = '**Sappho** ( Sapphṓ; Aeolic Greek Psápphō; c. 630) was an [[Ancient Greek|ancient Greek]] poet from Eresos or Mytilene on the island of Lesbos. She is known for her lyric poetry, written to be sung while accompanied by music. In ancient times, she was widely regarded as one of the greatest lyric poets and was given names such as the "Tenth Muse" and "The Poetess". Most of Sappho\'s poetry is now lost, and what is not has mostly survived in fragmentary form; only the Ode to Aphrodite is certainly complete. As well as lyric poetry, ancient commentators claimed that Sappho wrote elegiac and iambic poetry.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Three epigrams formerly attributed to Sappho have survived, but these are actually [[Hellenistic period|Hellenistic]] imitations of Sappho's style. Little is known of Sappho's life. She was from a wealthy family from Lesbos, though her parents' names are uncertain. Ancient sources say that she had three brothers: Charaxos, Larichos and Eurygios. Two of them, Charaxos and Larichos, are mentioned in the Brothers Poem discovered in 2014. She also appears to have had a daughter, traditionally identified with Cleïs, who is mentioned in two of Sappho's fragments, 98 and 132. Sappho was exiled to Sicily around 600BC, and may have continued to work until around 570BC. Sappho was a prolific poet, probably composing around 10,000 lines. She was best-known in antiquity for her love poetry; other themes in the surviving fragments of her work include family and religion.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sargon of Akkad",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Founder of the Akkadian Empire",
		tags = { "Asia", "Western Asia" },
		aliases = { "Sargon the Great" },
		summary = "**Sargon of Akkad**, also known as **Sargon the Great**, was the first ruler of the [[Akkadian Empire]], known for his conquests of the [[Sumer|Sumerian]] city-states in the 24th to 23rd centuries BC. He is sometimes identified as the first person in [[Recorded history|recorded history]] to rule over an [[Empire|empire]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'He was the founder of the "Sargonic" or "Old Akkadian" dynasty, which ruled for about a century after his death until the Gutian conquest of Sumer. The Sumerian King List makes him the cup-bearer to King Ur-Zababa of Kish before becoming king himself. His empire, which he ruled from his archaeologically as-yet-unidentified capital, Akkad, is thought to have included most of [[Mesopotamia]] and parts of the [[Levant]], Hurrian and [[Elam|Elamite]] territory. Sargon appears as a legendary figure in [[Neo-Assyrian Empire|Neo-Assyrian]] literature of the 8th to 7th centuries BC. Tablets with fragments of a Sargon Birth Legend were found in the Library of Ashurbanipal.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Ashurbanipal" },
