	{
		title = "Werner Heisenberg",
		category = "Person",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👤",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "German physicist (1901–1976)",
		tags = { "Physics", "Modern" },
		aliases = { "Werner Karl Heisenberg" },
		summary = "**Werner Karl Heisenberg** was a German theoretical [[Physicist|physicist]], one of the main pioneers of the theory of [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]] and a principal scientist in the German nuclear program during World War II.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Heisenberg published his Umdeutung paper in 1925, a major reinterpretation of [[Old quantum theory|old quantum theory]]. In the subsequent series of papers with [[Max Born]] and Pascual Jordan, during the same year, his matrix formulation of quantum mechanics was substantially elaborated. He is known for the [[Uncertainty principle|uncertainty principle]], which he published in 1927. He received the [[Nobel Prize in Physics]] in 1932 "for the creation of quantum mechanics". Heisenberg also made contributions to the theories of the [[Fluid dynamics|hydrodynamics]] of turbulent flows, the [[Atomic nucleus|atomic nucleus]], [[Ferromagnetism|ferromagnetism]], [[Cosmic ray|cosmic rays]], and subatomic particles. He introduced the concept of a wave function collapse. He was also instrumental in planning the first West German [[Nuclear reactor|nuclear reactor]] at the Kernforschungszentrum Karlsruhe, together with one in Garching at the Technische Hochschule München, in 1957 (both were research reactors).',
			},
		},
		related = { "Robert Millikan" },
		infobox = {
