		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "German founder of psychology (1832–1920)",
		tags = { "Psychologists" },
		aliases = { "Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt" },
		summary = "**Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt** was a German physiologist, philosopher, professor, and one of the fathers of modern [[Psychology|psychology]]. Wundt, who distinguished psychology as a science from philosophy and [[Biology|biology]], was the first person to call himself a [[Psychologist|psychologist]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'He is widely regarded as the "father of [[Experimental psychology|experimental psychology]]". In 1879, at the University of Leipzig, Wundt founded the first formal laboratory for psychological research. This marked psychology as an independent field of study. He also established the first [[Academic journal|academic journal]] for psychological research, Philosophische Studien (from 1883 to 1903), followed by Psychologische Studien (from 1905 to 1917), to publish the institute\'s research. A survey published in American Psychologist in 1991 ranked Wundt\'s reputation as first for "all-time eminence", based on ratings provided by 29 American historians of psychology. [[William James]] and [[Sigmund Freud]] were ranked a distant second and third.',
			},
		},
