	{
		title = "Berry (botany)",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🍓",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Botanical fruit with fleshy pericarp, containing one or many seeds",
		tags = { "Anatomy and morphology", "Plant anatomy" },
		aliases = { "Berry" },
		summary = 'In [[Botany|botany]], a **berry** is a fleshy [[Fruit|fruit]] produced from a single [[Flower|flower]] containing one ovary. Berries so defined include [[Grape|grapes]], currants, and [[Tomato|tomatoes]], as well as [[Cucumber|cucumbers]], [[Eggplant|eggplants]] (aubergines), persimmons and [[Banana|bananas]], but exclude certain fruits that meet the culinary definition of berries, such as strawberries and raspberries. The berry is the most common type of fleshy fruit in which the entire outer layer of the ovary wall ripens into a potentially edible "pericarp". Berries may be formed from one or more carpels from the same flower (i.e. from a simple or a compound ovary).',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The [[Seed|seeds]] are usually embedded in the fleshy interior of the ovary, but there are some non-fleshy exceptions, such as Capsicum species, with air rather than pulp around their seeds. A plant that bears berries is said to be **bacciferous** or **baccate**. In everyday English, a "berry" is any small edible fruit. Berries are usually juicy, round, brightly coloured, [[Taste|sweet]] or [[Taste|sour]], and do not have a stone or pit, although many small seeds may be present.',
			},
		},
	},
