	{
		title = "Boletus edulis",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍄",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of mushroom, widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere",
		tags = { "Fungi", "Edible fungi" },
		aliases = { "Cep", "Penny bun", "Porcino" },
		summary = "**Boletus edulis** (English: **cep**, **penny bun**, **porcino**) is a basidiomycete fungus, and the [[Type species|type species]] of the genus Boletus. It is prized as an [[Edible mushroom|edible mushroom]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The fungus produces spore-bearing fruit bodies above ground in summer and autumn. The fruit body has a large brown cap which can reach 30 cm in diameter and 3 kg in weight. Like other boletes, it has tubes extending downward from the underside of the cap, rather than gills; spores escape at maturity through the tube openings (pores). The pore surface of the fruit body is whitish when young, aging to a greenish-yellow. The stout stipe (or stem) is white or yellowish, up to 25 cm tall and 7 cm thick, and partially covered with a raised network pattern (reticulations). The fungus grows in deciduous and [[Conifer|coniferous]] forests and tree plantations, forming symbiotic ectomycorrhizal associations with living trees by enveloping the tree's underground roots with sheaths of fungal tissue.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Flowering plant", "Sea urchin" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Boletus edulis" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Botany",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of plant life",
		tags = { "Botany" },
		aliases = { "Plant science" },
		summary = "**Botany**, also called **plant science**, is the branch of [[Natural science|natural science]] and [[Biology|biology]] that studies the growth, reproduction and [[Evolution|evolution]] of [[Plant|plants]] over the last 3.5 billion years of life on earth. As with all areas of [[Biology|biology]] and [[Medicine|medicine]], plants can be studied at range of levels from their [[Biochemistry|biochemistry]], [[Molecular biology|molecular biology]], [[Cell (biology)|cell biology]] and [[Genetics|genetics]] to their [[Ecology|ecology]], pathology, [[Plant anatomy|anatomy]], [[Plant morphology|morphology]] and taxonomy. A **botanist** or **plant scientist** is a [[Scientist|scientist]] who specialises in this field.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Plant|Plants]] can be defined in multiple ways including approximately 410,000 [[Species|species]] of [[Embryophyte|land plants]], and some 391,000 species of [[Vascular plant|vascular plants]], of which approximately 369,000 are [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] and approximately 20,000 [[Bryophyte|bryophytes]]. Medieval physic gardens, often attached to monasteries, contained plants that possibly had medicinal benefits. They were forerunners of the first botanical gardens attached to [[University|universities]], founded from the 1540s onwards. One of the earliest was the Padua botanical garden. These gardens facilitated the academic study of plants. Efforts to catalogue and describe their collections were the beginnings of plant taxonomy and led in 1753 to the [[Binomial nomenclature|binomial system of nomenclature]] of [[Carl Linnaeus]] that remains in use to this day for the naming of all biological species.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Brassicaceae",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of flowering plants",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Brassicales" },
		aliases = { "Cruciferae", "Mustards", "Crucifers" },
		summary = "**Brassicaceae** or (the older but equally valid) **Cruciferae** is a medium-sized and economically important [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] commonly known as the **mustards**, the **crucifers**, or the **cabbage family**. Most are [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous plants]], while some are [[Shrub|shrubs]]. The [[Leaf|leaves]] are simple (although are sometimes deeply incised), lack stipules, and appear alternately on stems or in rosettes. The [[Inflorescence|inflorescences]] are terminal and lack bracts. The flowers have four free sepals, four free alternating [[Petal|petals]], two shorter free stamens and four longer free stamens. The [[Fruit|fruit]] has seeds in rows, divided by a thin wall (or septum).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As of 2023, the family was divided into two subfamilies, Brassicoideae and Aethionemoideae, and as of 2025, it contained over 350 accepted [[Genus|genera]]. Pieris rapae and other butterflies of the family Pieridae are some of the best-known pests of Brassicaceae species planted as commercial crops. Trichoplusia ni (cabbage looper) moth is also becoming increasingly problematic for crucifers due to its resistance to commonly used pest control methods. Some rarer Pieris butterflies, such as P. virginiensis, depend upon native mustards for their survival in their native habitats.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Brassicaceae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Brazil nut",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🥜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of tree and its edible seeds",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Ericales" },
		aliases = { "Bertholletia excelsa" },
		summary = "**Brazil nut** (**Bertholletia excelsa**) refers to a South American tree of the [[Monotypic taxon|monotypic]] genus Bertholletia in the family Lecythidaceae as well as the tree's commercially-harvested edible seeds. It is one of the largest and longest-lived trees in the [[Amazon rainforest]]. The fruit and its nutshell – containing the edible [[Nut (fruit)|nut]] – are relatively large and weigh as much as 2 kg in total. As food, Brazil nuts are notable for diverse content of micronutrients, especially a high amount of [[Selenium|selenium]]. The wood of the Brazil nut tree is prized for its quality in carpentry, flooring, and heavy construction.",
		sections = {
