	{
		title = "Breadfruit",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Edible fruit-bearing tree",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Rosales" },
		aliases = { "Artocarpus altilis" },
		summary = "**Breadfruit** (**Artocarpus altilis**) is a species of [[Flowering plant|flowering tree]] in the [[Morus (plant)|mulberry]] and [[Jackfruit|jackfruit]] family [[Moraceae]]. Its name is derived from the texture of the moderately ripe fruit when cooked, similar to freshly baked bread and having a potato-like flavor.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Breadfruit was spread into [[Oceania]] via the Austronesian expansion and is believed to have been selectively bred in [[Polynesia]] from the breadnut (Artocarpus camansi). British and French navigators introduced a few Polynesian seedless varieties to Caribbean islands during the late 18th century. Today it is widely grown in tropical regions throughout South and Southeast Asia, the Pacific and Caribbean islands, lowland Central America, northern South America and Africa. In addition to the fruit serving as a [[Staple food|staple food]] in many cultures, the light, sturdy timber of breadfruit has been used for making furniture, houses, and surfboards.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Fig" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Artocarpus altilis" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Data Deficient" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Broccoli",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🥦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Edible green plant in the cabbage family",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Brassicales" },
		summary = "**Broccoli** ([[Brassica oleracea]] var. italica) is an edible green plant in the [[Brassicaceae|cabbage family]] (family Brassicaceae, genus Brassica) whose large flowering head, [[Plant stem|stalk]] and small associated leaves are eaten as a [[Vegetable|vegetable]]. Broccoli is classified in the [[Brassica oleracea|Italica]] cultivar group of the species [[Brassica oleracea]]. Broccoli has large flower heads, or florets, usually dark green, arranged in a tree-like structure branching out from a thick [[Plant stem|stalk]], which is usually light green. Leaves surround the mass of flower heads. Broccoli resembles [[Cauliflower|cauliflower]], a different but closely related cultivar group of the same Brassica species.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'It can be eaten either raw or cooked. Broccoli is a particularly rich source of vitamin C and vitamin K. Contents of its characteristic [[Sulfur|sulfur]]-containing glucosinolate compounds, isothiocyanates and sulforaphane, are diminished by boiling but are better preserved by [[Steaming|steaming]], [[Microwave|microwaving]] or stir-frying. Rapini, sometimes called "broccoli rabe", is a distinct species from broccoli, forming similar but smaller heads, and is actually a type of [[Turnip|turnip]] (Brassica rapa).',
			},
		},
		related = { "Arabidopsis thaliana" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Species", "[[Brassica oleracea]]" },
			{ "Origin", "[[Roman Italy|Italy]], more than 2,000 years ago" },
			{ "Genus", "Brassica" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Bromeliaceae",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of monocot flowering plants",
		tags = { "Monocots", "Poales" },
		aliases = { "Bromeliads" },
		summary = "**Bromeliaceae**, members of which are called **bromeliads**, is a [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of monocot [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] of about 80 genera and 3700 known species, native mainly to the [[Tropics|tropical]] [[Americas]], with several species found in the American [[Subtropics|subtropics]] and one in tropical [[West Africa|west Africa]], Pitcairnia feliciana.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'It is the sister group to the Typhaceae, and together this clade is sister to the remaining lineages in the Poales. Bromeliaceae is the only family within the order that has septal nectaries and inferior ovaries. These inferior ovaries characterize the Bromelioideae, a subfamily of the Bromeliaceae. The family includes both [[Epiphyte|epiphytes]], such as [[Spanish moss]] (Tillandsia usneoides), and terrestrial species, such as the [[Pineapple|pineapple]] (Ananas comosus). Many bromeliads, colloquially called "tank bromeliads", are able to store water in a structure (a "tank") formed by their tightly overlapping [[Leaf|leaf]] bases. However, the family is diverse enough to include the tank bromeliads, grey-leaved epiphyte Tillandsia species that gather water only from leaf structures called trichomes, and many desert-dwelling succulents.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Oryza sativa" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Bromeliaceae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Bryophyte",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Terrestrial plants that lack vascular tissue",
		tags = { "Non-flowering plants", "Bryophytes" },
		aliases = { "Bryophytes", "Bryophyta sensu lato" },
		summary = "**Bryophytes** are a group of [[Embryophyte|land plants]] (embryophytes), sometimes treated as a taxonomic division referred to as **Bryophyta sensu lato**, that contains three groups of non-vascular land plants: the liverworts, [[Hornwort|hornworts]], and [[Moss|mosses]]. In the strict sense, the division **Bryophyta** consists of the mosses only. Bryophytes are characteristically limited in size and prefer moist habitats although some species can survive in drier environments. The bryophytes consist of about 20,000 plant species. Bryophytes produce enclosed reproductive structures (gametangia and sporangia), but they do not produce [[Flower|flowers]] or [[Seed|seeds]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Though bryophytes were considered a [[Paraphyly|paraphyletic]] group in recent years, almost all of the most recent [[Phylogenetics|phylogenetic]] evidence supports the [[Monophyly|monophyly]] of this group, as originally classified by Wilhelm Schimper in 1879.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Embryophytes" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Buckwheat",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of flowering plant",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Caryophyllales" },
		aliases = { "Fagopyrum esculentum", "Common buckwheat" },
		summary = '**Buckwheat** (**Fagopyrum esculentum**) or **common buckwheat** is a [[Flowering plant|flowering plant]] in the knotweed family Polygonaceae cultivated for its grain-like seeds and as a cover crop. Buckwheat cultivation originated around the 6th millennium BC in the region of what is now [[Yunnan|Yunnan Province]] in southwestern [[China]]. The name "buckwheat" is used for several other species, such as Fagopyrum tataricum, a domesticated food plant raised in Asia.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Despite its name, buckwheat is not closely related to [[Wheat|wheat]], nor is it a [[Cereal|cereal]] or a member of the [[Poaceae|grass family]]. It is related to sorrel, knotweed, and rhubarb. Buckwheat is considered a pseudocereal because the high [[Starch|starch]] content of the seeds enables buckwheat to be cooked and consumed like a cereal.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Fagopyrum esculentum" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Bulb",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Short plant stem with fleshy leaves or leaf bases for food storage and water",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Botany|botany]], a **bulb** is a short underground stem with fleshy [[Leaf|leaves]] or leaf bases that function as food storage organs during dormancy. In [[Gardening|gardening]], plants with other kinds of storage organ are also called ornamental bulbous plants or just bulbs.",
	},
	{
		title = "Cabbage",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🥬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Leafy vegetable in the flowering plant family Brassicaceae",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Brassicales" },
		summary = '**Cabbage**, comprising several [[Cultivar|cultivars]] of [[Brassica oleracea]], is a leafy green, red (purple), or white (pale green) [[Biennial plant|biennial plant]] grown as an [[Annual plant|annual]] vegetable crop for its dense-leaved heads. It is descended from the wild cabbage ([[Brassica oleracea|B. oleracea var. oleracea]]), and belongs to the "cole crops" or brassicas, meaning it is closely related to [[Broccoli|broccoli]] and [[Cauliflower|cauliflower]] (var. botrytis); Brussels sprouts (var. gemmifera); and Savoy cabbage (var. sabauda).',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A cabbage generally weighs between 500 and 1000 g. Smooth-leafed, firm-headed green cabbages are the most common, with smooth-leafed purple cabbages and crinkle-leafed savoy cabbages of both colours being rarer. Under conditions of long sunny days, such as those found at high northern latitudes in summer, cabbages can grow quite large. As of 2012, the heaviest cabbage was 62.71 kg. Cabbage heads are generally picked during the first year of the plant's [[Biological life cycle|life cycle]], but plants intended for seed are allowed to grow a second year and must be kept separate from other cole crops to prevent cross-pollination. Cabbage was most likely [[Domestication|domesticated]] somewhere in [[Europe]] in [[Ancient history|ancient history]] before 1000 BC. Cabbage use in cuisine has been documented since [[Classical antiquity|antiquity]]. It was described as a table luxury in the [[Roman Empire]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Eruca sativa" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Species", "[[Brassica oleracea]]" },
			{ "Origin", "Europe, prior to 1000 BC" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Camellia",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of flowering plants in the tea family",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Ericales" },
		summary = "**Camellia** ( or ) is a [[Genus|genus]] of [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] in the family [[Theaceae]]. They are found in tropical and subtropical areas in [[East Asia|eastern]] and [[South Asia|southern Asia]], from the [[Himalayas]] east to [[Japan]] and [[Indonesia]]. There are more than 220 described [[Species|species]]; almost all are found in southern China and Indochina. Camellias are popular ornamental, tea, and woody-oil plants cultivated worldwide for centuries. Over 26,000 cultivars, with more than 51,000 cultivar names, including synonyms, have been registered or published.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The leaves of C. sinensis are processed to create [[Tea|tea]], and so are of particular economic importance in East Asia, Southeast Asia, and the Indian subcontinent, with the processed leaves widely sold and consumed globally. The ornamental C. japonica, C. sasanqua and their [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrids]] are the source of hundreds of garden [[Cultivar|cultivars]]. C. oleifera produces tea seed oil, used in cooking and cosmetics.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Brazil nut", "Primulaceae" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Camellia" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Campanulaceae",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of flowering plants comprising bellflowers",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Asterales" },
		aliases = { "Bellflower family" },
		summary = "The [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Campanulaceae** (also **bellflower family**), of the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] Asterales, contains nearly 2400 [[Species|species]] in 84 [[Genus|genera]] of [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous]] [[Flowering plant|plants]], [[Shrub|shrubs]], and rarely small [[Tree|trees]], often with milky sap. Among them are several familiar garden plants belonging to the genera Campanula (bellflower), Lobelia, and Platycodon (balloonflower). Campanula rapunculus (rampion or r. bellflower) and Codonopsis lanceolata are eaten as vegetables. Campanula rapunculoides (creeping bellflower) may be a troublesome weed, particularly in gardens, while Legousia spp. may occur in arable fields.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most current classifications include the segregate family Lobeliaceae in Campanulaceae as subfamily Lobelioideae. A third subfamily, Cyphioideae, includes the genus Cyphia, and sometimes also the genera Cyphocarpus, Nemacladus, Parishella and Pseudonemacladus. Alternatively, the last three genera are placed in Nemacladoideae, while Cyphocarpus is placed in its own subfamily, Cyphocarpoideae. This family is almost [[Cosmopolitan distribution|cosmopolitan]], occurring on all continents except [[Antarctica]]. In addition, species of the family are native to many remote oceanic islands and archipelagos. [[Hawaii]] is particularly rich, with well over 100 endemic species of Hawaiian lobelioids. Continental areas with high diversity are South Africa, California and the northern [[Andes]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Chicory", "Dahlia" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Campanulaceae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Canna (plant)",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of flowering plants",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		aliases = { "Canna", "Cannas", "Canna lilies" },
		summary = "**Canna** is a [[Genus|genus]] of [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] consisting of 10 [[Species|species]] known as **cannas** or **canna lilies**. It is the only genus in the family Cannaceae. All of the genus's species are native to the [[Neotropical realm|American tropics]] and were naturalized in Europe, India and Africa in the 1860s. Although they grow native to the tropics, most cultivars have been developed in temperate climates and are easy to grow in most countries of the world, as long as they receive at least 6–8 hours average sunlight during the summer, and are moved to a warm location for the winter. See the Canna cultivar gallery for photographs of Canna cultivars.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Cannas are not true lilies, but have been assigned by the APG II system of 2003 to the order Zingiberales in the monocot clade Commelinids, together with their closest relatives, the gingers, spiral gingers, bananas, arrowroots, heliconias, and birds of paradise. The plants have large foliage, so [[Horticulture|horticulturists]] have developed selected forms as large-flowered garden plants. Cannas are also used in agriculture as a source of [[Starch|starch]] for human and animal consumption. C. indica and C. glauca have been grown into many cultivars in India and Africa.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Monocotyledon", "Garlic" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Canna" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cardamom",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌶️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Spice",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		aliases = { "Cardamon", "Cardamum" },
		summary = "**Cardamom**, sometimes **cardamon** or **cardamum**, is a [[Spice|spice]] made from the seeds of several plants in the [[Genus|genera]] Elettaria and Amomum in the family Zingiberaceae. Both genera are native to the [[Indian subcontinent]] and [[Indonesia]]. They are recognized by their small seed pods: triangular in cross-section and spindle-shaped, with a thin, papery outer shell and small, black seeds; Elettaria pods are light green and smaller, while Amomum pods are dark brown and larger.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Species used for cardamom are native throughout tropical and subtropical Asia. The first references to cardamom are found in [[Sumer]], and in Ayurveda. In the 21st century, it is cultivated mainly in [[India]], [[Indonesia]], and [[Guatemala]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Alstroemeria", "Philodendron" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Uses", "Flavoring, spice" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Carnivorous plant",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Plants that consume animals",
		tags = { "Botany" },
		aliases = { "Carnivorous plants" },
		summary = "**Carnivorous plants** are [[Plant|plants]] that derive some or most of their [[Plant nutrition|nutrients]] from trapping and consuming [[Animal|animals]] or protozoans, typically [[Insect|insects]] and other [[Arthropod|arthropods]], and occasionally small [[Mammal|mammals]] and [[Bird|birds]]. They have adapted to grow in waterlogged sunny places where the [[Soil|soil]] is thin or poor in nutrients, especially [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]], such as acidic [[Bog|bogs]]. They can be found on all continents except [[Antarctica]], as well as many Pacific islands. In 1875, [[Charles Darwin]] published Insectivorous Plants, the first [[Treatise|treatise]] to recognize the significance of carnivory in plants, describing years of his research.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"True carnivory is believed to have [[Convergent evolution|evolved independently]] at least 12 times in five different [[Order (taxonomy)|orders]] of flowering plants, and is represented by more than a dozen [[Genus|genera]]. [[Venus flytrap|Venus flytraps]] (Dionaea muscipula), pitcher plants, and bladderworts (Utricularia spp.) can be seen as exemplars of key traits genetically associated with carnivory: trap leaf development, prey digestion, and nutrient absorption. There are at least 800 species of carnivorous plants. The number of known species has increased by approximately 3 species per year since the year 2000. Additionally, over 300 protocarnivorous plant species in several genera show some but not all of these characteristics. A 2020 assessment has found that roughly one quarter are threatened with [[Extinction|extinction]] from human actions.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Carrot",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🥕",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Root vegetable",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Apiales" },
		summary = "The **carrot** (Daucus carota subsp. sativus) is a [[Root vegetable|root vegetable]] in the umbellifer family [[Apiaceae]]. Carrots are typically orange in colour due to their beta-carotene content, which makes them a rich source of vitamin A. Heirloom variants may be white, yellow, red, purple, or black. They are [[Biennial plant|biennial plants]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The vegetable is a domesticated form of the wild carrot, native to Europe and Southwestern Asia. The domesticated plant probably originated in [[Greater Iran|Iran]] where it was cultivated for its leaves and seeds. In 2022, world production of carrots (combined with [[Turnip|turnips]]) topped 42 million tonnes, with China producing 44% of the total. During the [[World War II|Second World War]], a myth that carrots help people see in the dark was spread as propaganda to explain British pilots' ability to fight in the dark; the real explanation was the introduction of [[Radar|radar]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cashew",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Flowering plant in the family Anacardiaceae",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Sapindales" },
		aliases = { "Anacardium occidentale" },
		summary = "**Cashew** is the common name of a tropical [[Evergreen|evergreen tree]] **Anacardium occidentale**, in the family Anacardiaceae. It is the source of the cashew [[Nut (fruit)|nut]] and the cashew apple. The tree can grow as tall as 14 m.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Cashews are native to [[Tropics|tropical]] [[South America]]. Portuguese explorers encountered them in the 16th century and spread them to [[India]] and other tropical regions of the world. The dwarf cultivars, growing up to 6 m, are the most profitable, maturing sooner and producing greater yields. In 2023, 3.9 million tons of cashew nuts were harvested globally, led by the [[Ivory Coast]] and [[India]]. Treated cashews are eaten on their own as a snack, used in recipes, or processed into cashew cheese or cashew butter. Additionally, derivatives from the shell are used in products such as varnishes, [[Lubricant|lubricant]], and paints.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Mahogany" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Anacardium occidentale" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cassava",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌿",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Staple crop",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Malpighiales" },
		aliases = { "Manihot esculenta", "Manioc", "Yuca" },
		summary = "**Manihot esculenta**, [[Common name|commonly called]] **cassava**, **manioc**, or **yuca** (among numerous regional names), is a woody [[Shrub|shrub]] of the spurge family, [[Euphorbiaceae]], native to South America, from Brazil, Paraguay and parts of the [[Andes]]. Although a [[Perennial|perennial]] plant, cassava is extensively cultivated in [[Tropics|tropical]] and [[Subtropics|subtropical]] regions as an annual [[Crop|crop]] for its edible [[Starch|starchy]] [[Tuber|tuberous]] root. Cassava is predominantly consumed in boiled form, but substantial quantities are processed to extract cassava starch, called tapioca, which is used for food, animal feed, and industrial purposes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Brazilian, and the related garri of West Africa, is an edible coarse flour obtained by grating cassava roots, pressing moisture off the obtained grated pulp, and finally drying and roasting it. Cassava is the third-largest source of carbohydrates in food in the tropics, after [[Rice|rice]] and [[Maize|maize]], making it an important [[Staple food|staple]]; more than 500 million people depend on it. It offers the advantage of being exceptionally drought-tolerant, and able to grow productively on poor soil. The largest producer is [[Nigeria]], while [[Thailand]] is the largest exporter of cassava starch. Cassava is grown in sweet and bitter varieties; both contain toxins, but the bitter varieties have them in much larger amounts. Farmers may also choose bitter cultivars to minimise crop losses.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Hypericum", "Willow" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Manihot esculenta" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cauliflower",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🥕",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Vegetable in the species Brassica oleracea",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Brassicales" },
		summary = '**Cauliflower** (Brassica oleracea var. botrytis) is a [[Vegetable|vegetable]] belonging to the species [[Brassica oleracea]] in the family [[Brassicaceae]] (the mustard or [[Cabbage|cabbage]] family). It is one of several cultivated forms of the species along with cabbage, [[Broccoli|broccoli]], Brussels sprouts, [[Kale|kale]], [[Kohlrabi|kohlrabi]], and others. The edible portion of the plant is its dense head of undeveloped flower buds known as the "curd". The head is usually white but may also be green, orange, or purple. Several cultivars exist, including the Romanesco variety, whose spiral curds grow in [[Fractal|fractal]] patterns.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Cauliflower was domesticated in the [[Mediterranean basin|Mediterranean region]] during antiquity, most likely by [[Selective breeding|selective breeding]] of wild cabbage. It is grown worldwide as a cool-season crop and is widely used in cooking, where it may be eaten raw, steamed, roasted, or incorporated into other dishes. World cauliflower production (combined with [[Broccoli|broccoli]]) in 2024 was 27 million [[Tonne|tonnes]], led by [[China]] and [[India]] with 73% of the total.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Papaya" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Species", "[[Brassica oleracea]]" },
			{ "Origin", "Northeast [[Mediterranean Sea|Mediterranean]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cedrus",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of plants (coniferous trees)",
		tags = { "Non-flowering plants", "Gymnosperms" },
		aliases = { "Cedar" },
		summary = "**Cedrus**, with the common English name **cedar**, is a genus of [[Conifer|coniferous]] trees in the plant family Pinaceae (subfamily Abietoideae). They are [[Native species|native]] to the mountains of the western [[Himalayas]] and the Mediterranean region at high altitudes. The trees grow tall with a cylindrical trunk and a wide leafy crown. The cones are erect; the leaves grow in tufts of 15–45 needle leaves, which can be bright green or blue-green with a waxy coat. When the cones are mature, they disintegrate to release the seeds, which are winged. Both pollen and seeds are wind-dispersed.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Cedars are often grown as bonsai. Cedar wood and cedarwood oil are naturally repellent to moths, and have an attractive, long-persistent scent.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cupressus", "Sequoioideae" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Cedrus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Celery",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of edible plant",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Apiales" },
		summary = "Celery (Apium graveolens Dulce Group or Apium graveolens var. dulce) is a cultivated plant belonging to the species Apium graveolens in the family [[Apiaceae]] that has been used as a [[Vegetable|vegetable]] since ancient times.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The original wild species has been selectively bred over centuries into three primary cultivar groups: stalk celery (Dulce Group), consumed for its fibrous edible stalks; leaf celery (Secalinum Group), grown for its aromatic leaves; and celeriac (Rapaceum Group), cultivated for its large, edible hypocotyl. Celery is characterized by its long, ribbed stalks, pinnate leaves, and small white flowers arranged in umbels. Celery is commonly consumed raw in [[Salad|salads]], cooked in [[Soup|soups]] and [[Stew|stews]], or [[Juice|juiced]]. Celery seeds, which have a strong, aromatic flavor, are used as a spice or processed into celery salt. Celery is among a large group of foods that may provoke allergic reactions. Historically, celery has held culinary and symbolic significance. Literary evidence indicates celery cultivation in [[Ancient Greece]], while [[Ancient Egypt|ancient Egyptians]] incorporated it into funeral garlands.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Hedera" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Genus", "Apium" },
			{ "Species", "Apium graveolens" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cell wall",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Outermost layer of some cells",
		tags = { "Cell biology", "Plant cells" },
		summary = "A **cell wall** is a structural layer that surrounds some [[Cell (biology)|cell types]], found immediately outside the [[Cell membrane|cell membrane]]. It can be tough, flexible, and sometimes rigid. Primarily, it provides the cell with structural support, shape, protection, and functions as a selective barrier. Another vital role of the cell wall is to help the cell withstand osmotic pressure and mechanical stress. Cell walls are found in most [[Prokaryote|prokaryotes]], with the exception of mollicute bacteria. Among the [[Eukaryote|eukaryotes]], cells walls are prevalent in [[Mushroom|fungi]], [[Algae|algae]] and [[Plant|plants]] but absent from animals and many other taxa.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The composition of cell walls varies across taxonomic groups, [[Species|species]], cell type, and the [[Cell cycle|cell cycle]]. In [[Embryophyte|land plants]], the primary cell wall comprises [[Polysaccharide|polysaccharides]] like [[Cellulose|cellulose]], hemicelluloses, and pectin. Often, other [[Polymer|polymers]] such as lignin, suberin or cutin are anchored to or embedded in plant cell walls. [[Algae]] exhibit cell walls composed of glycoproteins and [[Polysaccharide|polysaccharides]], such as carrageenan and agar, distinct from those in land plants. Bacterial cell walls contain peptidoglycan, while archaeal cell walls vary in composition, potentially consisting of glycoprotein S-layers, pseudopeptidoglycan, or polysaccharides. Fungi possess cell walls constructed from the polymer [[Chitin|chitin]], specifically N-acetylglucosamine. [[Diatom|Diatoms]] have a unique cell wall composed of biogenic silica.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Chanterelle",
