	{
		title = "Chili pepper",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌶️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Varieties of pepper of the genus Capsicum",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Solanales" },
		aliases = { "Chili peppers", "Chilli" },
		summary = "**Chili peppers**, also spelled **chile** or **chilli**, are varieties of [[Fruit|berry-fruit]] plants from the [[Genus|genus]] Capsicum, which are members of the nightshade family [[Solanaceae]], cultivated for their [[Pungency|pungency]] (spicy heat). They are used as a [[Spice|spice]] to add [[Pungency|pungency]] in many cuisines. Capsaicin and the related capsaicinoids give chili peppers their intensity when ingested or applied topically. Chili peppers exhibit a range of heat and flavors. This diversity is the reason behind the availability of different types of chili powder, each offering its own taste and heat level.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Chili peppers originated in Central or South America and were first cultivated in Mexico. European explorers brought chili peppers back to the [[Old World]] in the late 15th century as part of the Columbian exchange, which led to the cultivation of multiple varieties across the world for food and traditional medicine. Five Capsicum species have been widely cultivated: annuum, baccatum, chinense, frutescens, and pubescens.",
			},
		},
	},
