	{
		title = "Date palm",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌴",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Palm tree cultivated for its sweet fruit",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		aliases = { "Phoenix dactylifera" },
		summary = "**Phoenix dactylifera**, commonly known as the **date palm**, is a flowering-plant species in the palm family [[Arecaceae]], native to the region from the [[Arab states of the Persian Gulf|Gulf States]] to the Lower [[Indus River|Indus Valley]]. It is cultivated for its edible sweet fruit called **dates**. The species is widely cultivated across [[North Africa|northern Africa]], the [[Middle East]], [[Australia]], [[South Asia|South]] and [[Southeast Asia]], [[Portugal]], [[Spain]], coastal [[Mediterranean basin]], and the desert regions of [[Southern California]]. It is naturalised in many [[Tropics|tropical]] and [[Subtropics|subtropical]] regions worldwide. P. dactylifera is the [[Type species|type species]] of genus Phoenix, which contains 12–19 species of wild date palms.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Date palms reach up to 30 m in height, growing singly or in clumps with several stems from a single root system. Slow-growing, they can reach over 100years of age when maintained properly. Their born fruit (dates) are oval-cylindrical, 3 to 7 cm long, and about 2.5 cm in diameter, with colour ranging from dark brown to bright red or yellow, depending on variety. Containing 63–64% sugar by mass when dried, dates are consumed as sweet snacks on their own or with confections. There is archaeological evidence of date cultivation in Arabia since the 6thmillennium BCE. Dates are "emblematic of [[Oasis|oasis]] agriculture and highly symbolic in Christian and Jewish religions".',
			},
		},
		related = { "Iris (plant)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Phoenix dactylifera" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dietary fiber",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Portion of plant-derived food that cannot be completely digested",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Roughage" },
		summary = "**Dietary fiber** (also spelled **fibre**), or **roughage**, is the portion of plant-derived [[Food|food]] that cannot be completely broken down by human digestive enzymes. Dietary fibers are diverse in chemical composition and can be grouped generally by their [[Solubility|solubility]], [[Viscosity|viscosity]] and [[Fermentation|fermentability]], which affect how fibers are processed in the body. Dietary fiber has two main subtypes: soluble and insoluble. They are components of many foods from plants, such as [[Legume|legumes]], whole grains, [[Cereal|cereals]], [[Vegetable|vegetables]], [[Fruit|fruits]], [[Nut (fruit)|nuts]], and [[Seed|seeds]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Dietary fiber consists of non-[[Starch|starch]] [[Polysaccharide|polysaccharides]] and other plant components, such as [[Cellulose|cellulose]], resistant starch, resistant dextrins, inulins, lignins, [[Chitin|chitins]], pectins, beta-glucans, and oligosaccharides. Food sources of dietary fiber have traditionally been divided according to proportion of soluble vs. insoluble fiber. The two types are defined according to their characteristics of viscosity and fermentability, with different advantages between the types. Plant foods contain both types in varying amounts. Bulking fibers, such as cellulose and hemicellulose (including psyllium), absorb and hold water, promoting bowel movement regularity. Viscous fibers, such as beta-glucan and psyllium, thicken the fecal mass.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Diospyros",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of trees and shrubs",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Ericales" },
		summary = "**Diospyros** is a [[Genus|genus]] of over 700 [[Species|species]] of [[Deciduous|deciduous]] and [[Evergreen|evergreen]] [[Tree|trees]] and [[Shrub|shrubs]]. The majority are native to the [[Tropics|tropics]], with only a few species extending into [[Temperate climate|temperate]] regions. Individual species valued for their hard, heavy, dark timber, are commonly known as ebony trees, while others are valued for their fruit and known as persimmon trees. Some are useful as ornamentals and many are of local ecological importance. Species of this genus are generally dioecious, with separate male and female plants.",
		related = { "Rhododendron", "Camellia" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Diospyros" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Douglas fir",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of tree",
		tags = { "Non-flowering plants", "Gymnosperms" },
		aliases = { "Pseudotsuga menziesii" },
		summary = "The **Douglas fir** (**Pseudotsuga menziesii**) is an [[Evergreen|evergreen]] [[Conifer|conifer]] [[Species|species]] in the pine family, Pinaceae. It is the tallest tree in the Pinaceae family. It is [[Native species|native]] to western [[North America]] and is also known as **Douglas-fir**, **Douglas spruce**, **Oregon pine**, and **Columbian pine**. There are three varieties: coast Douglas-fir (P. menziesii var. menziesii), Rocky Mountain Douglas-fir (P. menziesii var. glauca) and Mexican Douglas-fir (P. menziesii var. lindleyana).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Despite its [[Common name|common names]], it is not a true [[Fir|fir]] ([[Genus|genus]] Abies), [[Spruce|spruce]] (genus Picea), or [[Pine|pine]] (genus Pinus). It is also not a hemlock (genus Tsuga); the genus name Pseudotsuga means "false hemlock".',
			},
		},
		related = { "Ginkgo biloba" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Pseudotsuga menziesii" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Drupe",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fleshy fruit with hard inner layer (endocarp or stone) surrounding the seed",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Stone fruit" },
		summary = "In [[Botany|botany]], a **drupe**, or **stone fruit**, is a type of [[Fruit|fruit]] in which an outer fleshy part (exocarp, or skin, and mesocarp, or flesh) surrounds a single shell (the pit, stone, or pyrena) of hardened endocarp with a [[Seed|seed]] (kernel) inside. Drupes do not split open to release the seed, i.e., they are indehiscent. These fruits usually develop from a single carpel, and mostly from flowers with superior ovaries (polypyrenous drupes are exceptions).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The definitive characteristic of a drupe is that the hard, woody (lignified) stone is derived from the ovary wall of the [[Flower|flower]]. In an aggregate fruit, which is composed of small, individual drupes (such as a [[Raspberry|raspberry]]), each individual is termed a **drupelet**, and may together form an aggregate fruit. Such fruits are often termed berries, although botanists use a [[Berry (botany)|different definition of berry]]. Other fleshy fruits may have a stony enclosure that comes from the seed coat surrounding the seed, but such fruits are not drupes. [[Flowering plant|Flowering plants]] that produce drupes include coffee, jujube, [[Mango|mango]], [[Olive|olive]], most palms (including açaí, [[Date palm|date]], sabal and oil palms), pistachio, white sapote, [[Cashew|cashew]], and all members of the genus Prunus, including the [[Almond|almond]], [[Apricot|apricot]], [[Cherry|cherry]], damson, [[Peach|peach]], nectarine, and [[Plum|plum]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Durian",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tropical fruit",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Others" },
		summary = "The **durian** is the edible fruit of several tree [[Species|species]] belonging to the [[Genus|genus]] Durio. There are 30 recognised species, at least nine of which produce edible fruit. Durio zibethinus, native to [[Borneo]], [[Sumatra]], and the [[Malay Peninsula]], is the only species available on the international market. It has over 300 named varieties in Thailand and over 200 in Malaysia as of 2021. Other species are sold in their local regions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Known in some regions as the "king of fruits", the durian is distinctive for its large size, strong odour, and thorn-covered rind. The fruit can grow as large as 30 cm long and 15 cm in diameter, and it typically weighs 1 to 3 kg. Its shape ranges from oblong to round, the colour of its husk from green to brown, and its flesh from pale yellow to red, depending on the species. The flavour of the flesh is complex with savoury notes. The texture is creamy and pudding-like. Some people regard the durian as having a pleasantly sweet fragrance, whereas many others find the aroma overpowering and unpleasant. The persistence of its strong odour, which may linger for several days, has led some hotels and public transportation services in [[Southeast Asia]], such as in [[Singapore]] and [[Bangkok]], to ban the fruit.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Cornus", "Platanus" },
	},
	{
		title = "Edible mushroom",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍄",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Edible fungi fruit bodies",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Edible mushrooms" },
		summary = "**Edible mushrooms** are the fleshy fruit bodies of numerous species of properly [[Mushroom|identified]] and prepared [[Mushroom|fungi]]. Edibility may be defined by criteria including their palatability and absence of dangerous mycotoxins. Edible mushrooms are consumed for their nutritional and culinary value, often either being cultivated or harvested wild. Easily cultivated and common wild mushrooms are often available in markets; those that are more difficult to obtain may be collected on a smaller scale.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"To ensure safety, wild mushrooms must be correctly identified before their edibility can be assumed. Additionally, mushrooms were consumed medicinally in [[Traditional medicine|traditional medicine]], but are not upheld by evidence. Edible species typically must be cooked, sometimes requiring parboiling or slow cooking to destroy toxins, with only select species able to be eaten raw. Many can also be [[Canning|canned]], dried, pickled, or salted.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Terrestrial animal", "Filter feeder" },
	},
	{
		title = "Eggplant",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍆",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Plant species",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Solanales" },
		aliases = { "Solanum melongena" },
		summary = "**Solanum melongena** is a plant [[Species|species]] in the nightshade family [[Solanaceae]], grown worldwide for its edible [[Fruit|fruit]], typically used as a [[Vegetable|vegetable]] in cooking. It is known by a number of names, including **eggplant**, **aubergine**, and **brinjal**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most commonly purple, the spongy, absorbent fruit is used in several cuisines. It is a [[Berry (botany)|berry]] by [[Botany|botanical]] definition. As a member of the genus Solanum, it is related to the [[Tomato|tomato]], [[Chili pepper|chili pepper]], and [[Potato|potato]], although those are of the [[Americas]] region while the eggplant is of the [[Eurasia]] region. Like the tomato, its skin and seeds can be eaten, but it is usually eaten cooked. Eggplant is nutritionally low in macronutrient and micronutrient content, but the capability of the fruit to absorb oils and flavors into its flesh through cooking expands its use in the [[Culinary arts|culinary arts]]. It was originally [[Domestication|domesticated]] from the wild nightshade species thorn or bitter apple, S. incanum, probably with two independent domestications: one in [[South Asia]], and one in [[East Asia]]. In 2023, world production of eggplants was 61 e6t, with China and India combining for 85% of the total.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Bell pepper" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Solanum melongena" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Elm",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌳",
