	{
		title = "Eucalyptus",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of flowering plants",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Myrtales" },
		summary = "**Eucalyptus** is a genus of more than 700 species of [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] in the family [[Myrtaceae]]. Most species of Eucalyptus are trees, often mallees, and a few are shrubs. Along with several other genera in the tribe Eucalypteae, including Corymbia and Angophora, they are commonly known as eucalypts or \"gum trees\". Species of Eucalyptus have bark that is either smooth, fibrous, hard, or stringy and leaves that have oil glands. The sepals and [[Petal|petals]] are fused to form a \"cap\" or operculum over the stamens, hence the name from Greek eû 'well' and kaluptós 'covered'.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most species of Eucalyptus are native to Australia, and every state and territory has representative species. A few species are [[Native species|native]] to islands north of Australia, and a smaller number are only found outside the continent. Eucalypts have been grown in plantations in many other countries because they are fast-growing, have valuable timber, or can be used for pulpwood, [[Honey|honey]] production, or [[Essential oil|essential oils]]. In some countries, however, they have been removed because of the danger of forest fires due to their high flammability.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Guava" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Eucalyptus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Eudicots",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Clade of flowering plants",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Eudicots" },
		aliases = { "Eudicotyledons" },
		summary = "The **eudicots** or **eudicotyledons** are [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] that have two seed leaves (cotyledons) upon germination. The term derives from [[Dicotyledon|dicotyledon]] (etymologically, eu = true; di = two; cotyledon = seed leaf). Historically, authors have used the terms **tricolpates** or **non-magnoliid dicots**. The current botanical terms were introduced in 1991, by evolutionary botanist James A. Doyle and paleobotanist Carol L. Hotton, to emphasize the later evolutionary divergence of tricolpate [[Dicotyledon|dicots]] from earlier, less specialized, dicots.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Scores of familiar plants are eudicots, including many commonly cultivated and edible plants, numerous [[Tree|trees]], tropicals and ornamentals. Among the most well-known eudicot genera are those of the [[Sunflower|sunflower]] ([[Helianthus]]), dandelion ([[Taraxacum]]), forget-me-not (Myosotis), [[Cabbage|cabbage]] (Brassica), [[Apple|apple]] (Malus), buttercup (Ranunculus), [[Maple|maple]] ([[Maple|Acer]]) and [[Macadamia|macadamia]] (Macadamia). Most leafy, mid-latitude trees are also classified as eudicots, with notable exceptions being the magnolias and American tulip tree (Liriodendron)—which belong to the [[Magnoliids|magnoliids]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Eudicots" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Euphorbiaceae",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of Eudicot flowering plants",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Malpighiales" },
		aliases = { "Spurge family" },
		summary = "**Euphorbiaceae**, the **spurge family**, is a large family of [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]]. In English, they are also commonly called **euphorbias**, which is also the name of the type genus of the family. Most spurges, such as Euphorbia paralias, are [[Herbaceous plant|herbs]], but some, especially in the [[Tropics|tropics]], are [[Shrub|shrubs]] or [[Tree|trees]], such as Hevea brasiliensis. Some, such as Euphorbia canariensis, are succulent and resemble [[Cactus|cacti]] because of [[Convergent evolution|convergent evolution]]. This family has a [[Cosmopolitan distribution|cosmopolitan global distribution]]. The greatest diversity of species is in the tropics; however, the Euphorbiaceae also have many species in nontropical areas of all continents except [[Antarctica]].",
		related = { "Viola (plant)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Euphorbiaceae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Evergreen",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Plant that has leaves in all seasons",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Botany|botany]], an **evergreen** is a plant which has [[Leaf|foliage]] that remains green and functional throughout the year. This contrasts with [[Deciduous|deciduous]] plants, which lose their foliage completely during the winter or dry season. Consisting of many different species, the unique feature of evergreen plants lends itself to various environments and purposes.",
	},
	{
		title = "Fabaceae",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of legume flowering plants",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Fabales" },
		aliases = { "Leguminosae", "Bean family" },
		summary = "**Fabaceae** or **Leguminosae**, commonly known as the **legume**, **pea**, or **bean family**, is a large and agriculturally important [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]]. It includes [[Tree|trees]], [[Shrub|shrubs]], and [[Perennial|perennial]] or [[Annual plant|annual]] [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous plants]], which are easily recognized by their fruit ([[Legume|legume]]) and their compound, stipulate leaves. The family is widely distributed, and is the third-largest land plant family in number of species, behind only the Orchidaceae and [[Asteraceae]], with about 765 genera and nearly 20,000 known species.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The five largest genera of the family are Astragalus (over 3,000 species), Acacia (over 1,000 species), Indigofera (around 700 species), Crotalaria (around 700 species), and [[Mimosa]] (around 400 species), which constitute about a quarter of all legume species. The c. 19,000 known legume species amount to about 7% of flowering plant species. Fabaceae is the most common family found in tropical rainforests and dry forests of the Americas and Africa. Recent molecular and morphological evidence supports the conclusion that the Fabaceae is a single [[Monophyly|monophyletic]] family. This conclusion has been supported not only by the degree of interrelation shown by different groups within the family compared with that found among the Leguminosae and their closest relations, but also by all the recent [[Phylogenetics|phylogenetic]] studies based on [[DNA]] sequences.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Tamarind" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Fabaceae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Fennel",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Flowering plant species in the carrot family",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Apiales" },
		aliases = { "Foeniculum vulgare" },
		summary = "**Fennel** (**Foeniculum vulgare**) is a [[Flowering plant|flowering plant]] species in the [[Apiaceae|carrot family]]. It is a hardy, perennial [[Herb|herb]] with yellow [[Flower|flowers]] and feathery [[Leaf|leaves]]. It is indigenous to the shores of the [[Mediterranean Sea|Mediterranean]] but has become widely naturalized in many parts of the world, especially on dry soils near the sea coast and on riverbanks.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is a highly flavorful herb used in [[Cooking|cooking]] and, along with the similar-tasting [[Anise|anise]], is one of the primary ingredients of absinthe. **Florence fennel** or **finocchio** is a selection with a swollen, bulb-like stem base (sometimes called bulb fennel) that is used as a [[Vegetable|vegetable]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Hedera" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Foeniculum vulgare" },
		},
