	{
		title = "Flax",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of flowering plant",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Malpighiales" },
		aliases = { "Common flax", "Linum usitatissimum" },
		summary = "**Flax**, also known as **common flax** or **line**, is a [[Flowering plant|flowering plant]], **Linum usitatissimum**, in the family Linaceae. It is cultivated as a food and fiber crop in regions of the world with [[Temperate climate|temperate climates]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Textiles made from flax are known in English as [[Linen|linen]] and are traditionally used for bed sheets, underclothes, and table linen. Its seeds are known as flaxseed or linseed and its oil is known as linseed oil. In addition to referring to the plant, the word "flax" may refer to the unspun fibers of the flax plant. The plant species is known only as a cultivated plant and appears to have been domesticated just once from the wild species Linum bienne, called pale flax. The plants called "flax" in New Zealand are, by contrast, members of the genus Phormium.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Viola (plant)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Linum usitatissimum" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Flowering plant",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Clade of seed plants that produce flowers",
		tags = { "Plants", "Flowering plants" },
		aliases = { "Flowering plants", "Angiospermae", "Angiosperm" },
		summary = "**Flowering plants** are [[Plant|plants]] that bear [[Flower|flowers]] and [[Fruit|fruits]], and form the [[Clade|clade]] **Angiospermae**. The term **angiosperm** is derived from the [[Ancient Greek|Greek]] words and, meaning that the [[Seed|seeds]] are enclosed within a fruit. The group was formerly called **Magnoliophyta**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Angiosperms are by far the most diverse group of [[Embryophyte|land plants]] with 64 [[Order (taxonomy)|orders]], 416 [[Family (taxonomy)|families]], approximately 13,000 known [[Genus|genera]] and 300,000 known [[Species|species]]. They include all forbs (flowering plants without a [[Wood|woody]] [[Plant stem|stem]]), [[Grass|grasses]] and grass-like plants, a vast majority of broad-leaved trees, [[Shrub|shrubs]] and [[Vine|vines]], and most [[Aquatic plant|aquatic plants]]. Angiosperms are distinguished from the other major seed plant clade, the [[Gymnosperm|gymnosperms]], by having [[Flower|flowers]], [[Xylem|xylem]] consisting of vessel elements instead of tracheids, endosperm within their seeds, and fruits that completely envelop the seeds. The ancestors of flowering plants diverged from the common ancestor of all living gymnosperms before the end of the [[Carboniferous]], over 300 million years ago. In the [[Cretaceous]], angiosperms diversified explosively, becoming the dominant group of plants across the planet.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Boletus edulis" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Angiosperms" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Forage",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Plant material eaten by grazing livestock",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Forage** is a plant material (mainly plant leaves and stems) eaten by [[Grazing|grazing]] [[Livestock|livestock]]. Historically, the term forage has meant only plants eaten by the animals directly as pasture, crop residue, or immature [[Cereal|cereal]] crops, but it is also used more loosely to include similar plants cut for [[Fodder|fodder]] and carried to the animals, especially as [[Hay|hay]] or silage.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While the term forage has a broad definition, the term **forage crop** is used to define crops, annual or biennial, which are grown to be utilized by grazing or harvesting as a whole crop.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Nasdaq-100" },
	},
	{
		title = "Fraxinus",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of plants",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Lamiales" },
		aliases = { "Ash" },
		summary = "**Fraxinus**, commonly called **ash**, is a [[Genus|genus]] of plants in the olive and lilac family, [[Oleaceae]], and comprises 45–65 species of usually medium-to-large [[Tree|trees]], most of which are [[Deciduous|deciduous]] (dropping their leaves in autumn), although some [[Subtropics|subtropical]] species are [[Evergreen|evergreen]]. The genus is widespread throughout much of Europe, Asia, and North America.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The leaves are usually opposite, and mostly pinnately compound (divided into leaflets in a feather-like arrangement). The seeds, known as "keys", are botanically fruits of the type called samara. Some species are dioecious, having male and female flowers on separate plants. Ash wood is strong and elastic, and used for the handles of tools. Musical instrument makers use it for [[Electric guitar|electric guitars]] and for drum shells. The Morgan Motor Company makes the frames of sports cars from ash wood. In [[Greek mythology]], the Meliae were the nymphs of ash trees.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Verbena" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Fraxinus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Fruit",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🍎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Seed-bearing part of a flowering plant",
		tags = { "Anatomy and morphology", "Plant anatomy" },
		summary = "In [[Botany|botany]], a **fruit** is the [[Seed|seed]]-bearing structure in [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] (angiosperms) that is formed from the ovary after [[Flower|flowering]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Fruits are the means by which angiosperms disseminate their seeds. Edible fruits in particular have long propagated using the movements of humans and other animals in a [[Symbiosis|symbiotic relationship]] that is the means for seed dispersal for the one group and [[Nutrition|nutrition]] for the other; humans, and many other animals, have become dependent on fruits as a source of food. Consequently, fruits account for a substantial fraction of the world's [[Agriculture|agricultural]] output, and some (such as the [[Apple|apple]] and the [[Pomegranate|pomegranate]]) have acquired extensive cultural and symbolic meanings. In common language and culinary usage, fruit normally means the seed-associated fleshy structures (or produce) of plants that are typically sweet (or sour) and edible in the raw state, such as apples, bananas, grapes, lemons, oranges, and strawberries.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gametophyte",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌿",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Haploid stage in the life cycle of plants and algae",
		tags = { "Biological processes and physiology", "Plant reproduction" },
		summary = "A **gametophyte** is one of the two [[Alternation of generations|alternating]] [[Multicellular organism|multicellular phases]] in the life cycles of [[Plant|plants]]. It is a haploid multicellular organism that develops from a haploid [[Spore|spore]], that has one set of chromosomes. Cell division of the zygote results in a new diploid multicellular organism, the second stage in the life cycle known as the [[Sporophyte|sporophyte]]. The sporophyte can produce haploid spores by [[Meiosis|meiosis]] that on germination produce a new generation of gametophytes.",
	},
