	{
		title = "Garlic",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧄",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of edible plant",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		aliases = { "Allium sativum" },
		summary = "**Garlic** (**Allium sativum**) is a [[Species|species]] of [[Bulb|bulbous]] [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] in the [[Genus|genus]] Allium. Its close relatives include the [[Onion|onion]], shallot, [[Leek|leek]], chives, Welsh onion, and Chinese onion. Garlic is native to [[Central Asia]], northeastern [[Iran]] and the foothills of [[Himalayas]]. It has naturalized in many other parts of the world, including [[Southern Europe|Mediterranean Europe]] and China. There are two subspecies and hundreds of [[Cultivar|varieties]] of garlic.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Garlic has been used for thousands of years as a seasoning, culinary ingredient, and [[Traditional medicine|traditional medical]] remedy. It was known in many ancient civilizations, including the [[Neo-Babylonian Empire|Babylonians]], [[Ancient Egypt|Egyptians]], [[Jews]], [[Roman Empire|Romans]], and [[History of China|Chinese]], and remains significant in many cuisines and folk treatments, especially across the Mediterranean and Asia. Sulfur compounds give garlic its pungent odor and flavor. There is insufficient clinical evidence to indicate that consuming garlic has any health benefits. Garlic is cultivated in a variety of climates and countries; [[China]] was the largest producer in 2024, accounting for 73% of the world's supply, according to the United Nations FAOSTAT.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Canna (plant)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Allium sativum" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ginger",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌿",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of plant used as a spice",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		aliases = { "Ginger root" },
		summary = "**Ginger** (Zingiber officinale) is a [[Flowering plant|flowering plant]] whose [[Rhizome|rhizome]], **ginger root** or ginger, is widely used as a [[Spice|spice]] and a folk medicine. It is an herbaceous perennial that grows annual pseudostems (false stems made of the rolled bases of leaves) about one meter tall, bearing narrow leaf blades. The [[Inflorescence|inflorescences]] bear flowers having pale yellow petals with purple edges, and arise directly from the rhizome on separate shoots.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ginger is in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Zingiberaceae, which also includes turmeric (Curcuma longa), [[Cardamom|cardamom]] (Elettaria cardamomum), and galangal. Ginger originated in [[Maritime Southeast Asia]] and was likely domesticated first by the [[Austronesian peoples]]. It was transported with them throughout the [[Indo-Pacific]] during the Austronesian expansion (c. 5,000 [[Before Present|BP]]), reaching as far as [[Hawaii]]. Ginger is one of the first spices to have been exported from Asia, arriving in [[Europe]] with the [[Spice trade|spice trade]], and was used by [[Ancient Greece|ancient Greeks]] and [[Roman Empire|Romans]]. The distantly related dicots in the genus Asarum are commonly called wild ginger because of their similar taste. Ginger has been used in [[Traditional medicine|traditional medicine]] in China, India and Japan for centuries, and as a modern [[Dietary supplement|dietary supplement]]. In 2023, world production of ginger was 4.9 million [[Tonne|tonnes]], led by India with 45% of the total.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Anthurium" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Zingiber officinale" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ginkgo biloba",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of tree",
		tags = { "Non-flowering plants", "Gymnosperms" },
		aliases = { "Ginkgo", "Maidenhair tree" },
		summary = '**Ginkgo biloba**, commonly known as **ginkgo**, also known as the **maidenhair tree**, and often misspelled "gingko" (see Taxonomy below) is a species of [[Gymnosperm|gymnosperm]] tree native to [[East Asia]]. It is the last living species in the order Ginkgoales, which first appeared over 290 million years ago. [[Fossil|Fossils]] similar to the living species, belonging to the genus Ginkgo, extend back to the [[Middle Jurassic]] epoch about 170 million years ago. The tree was cultivated early in [[Human history|human history]], remains commonly planted, and is widely regarded as a living fossil.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'It was known historically as "silver fruit" or "white fruit" in [[Chinese language|Chinese]] and called "ginkgo" due to a centuries-old transcription error. It is closely related to [[Cycad|cycads]] and characterized by unique [[Seed|seeds]] that resemble [[Apricot|apricots]] but are not true [[Fruit|fruits]]. G. biloba, once widespread but thought [[Extinction|extinct]] in the wild for centuries, is now commonly cultivated in East Asia, with some genetically diverse populations possibly representing rare wild survivors in [[Southwestern China|southwestern China]]\'s mountainous regions. Others show extreme longevity; G. biloba specimens have been measured in excess of 1,600 years, and the largest living trees are estimated to exceed 3,500 years. Today, it is widely planted in [[City|cities]] worldwide for its [[Pollution|pollution]] tolerance and ornamental value.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Conifer", "Douglas fir" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Ginkgo biloba" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Endangered" },
		},
	},
	{
