	{
		title = "Lemon",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍋",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Yellow citrus fruit",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Sapindales" },
		aliases = { "Citrus × limon" },
		summary = "The **lemon** (**Citrus × limon**) is a [[Species|species]] of small [[Evergreen|evergreen]] tree in the [[Citrus]] genus of the [[Flowering plant|flowering plant]] family Rutaceae. A true lemon is a [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] of the citron and the bitter orange. Its origins are uncertain, but some evidence suggests lemons originated during the 1st millennium BC in what is now [[Northeast India|northeastern India]]. Some other citrus fruits are called lemon.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The yellow fruit of the lemon tree is used throughout the world, primarily for its [[Juice|juice]]. The pulp and rind are used in cooking and baking. The juice of the lemon is about 5–6% [[Citric acid|citric acid]], giving it a sour taste. This makes it a key ingredient in [[Drink|drinks]] and foods such as lemonade and lemon meringue pie. In 2024, world production was 23 million [[Tonne|tonnes]], led by India and Mexico with 31% of the total.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Citrus × limon" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lentil",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of plant with edible seeds",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Fabales" },
		aliases = { "Vicia lens", "Lens culinaris" },
		summary = "The **lentil** (**Vicia lens** or **Lens culinaris**) is an [[Annual plant|annual]] [[Legume|legume]] grown for its lens-shaped edible [[Seed|seeds]] or pulses, also called lentils. It is about 40 cm tall, and the seeds grow in [[Legume|pods]], usually with two seeds in each.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Lentil seeds are used around the world for culinary purposes, most commonly in [[Stew|stews]] or [[Soup|soups]]. In cuisines of the Indian subcontinent, where lentils are a [[Staple food|staple]], split lentils (often with their hulls removed) known as dal are often cooked into a thick [[Curry|curry]] that is usually eaten with [[Rice|rice]] or roti.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Vicia lens" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lettuce",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🥬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of annual plant of the daisy family, most often grown as a leaf vegetable",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Asterales" },
		aliases = { "Lactuca sativa" },
		summary = "**Lettuce** (**Lactuca sativa**) is an [[Annual plant|annual plant]] of the family [[Asteraceae]] mostly grown as a [[Leaf vegetable|leaf vegetable]]. The leaves are most often used raw in green salads, although lettuce is also seen in other kinds of food, such as sandwiches, wraps and soups; it can also be grilled. Its stem and seeds are sometimes used; celtuce (asparagus lettuce) is one variety grown for its stems, which are eaten either raw or cooked. In addition to its main use as a leafy green, it has also gathered religious and medicinal significance over centuries of human consumption.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Europe and North America originally dominated the market for lettuce, but by the late 20th century the consumption of lettuce had spread throughout the world. In 2023, world production of lettuce (and [[Chicory|chicory]]) was 28 million tonnes, led by China with 53% of the total. Lettuce was originally farmed by the ancient Egyptians, who transformed it from a plant whose seeds were used to obtain oil into an important food crop raised for its succulent leaves and oil-rich seeds. Lettuce spread to the Greeks and Romans; the latter gave it the name, from which the English lettuce is derived. By 50 AD, many types were described, and lettuce appeared often in medieval writings, including several herbals. The 16th through 18th centuries saw the development of many [[Variety (botany)|varieties]] in Europe, and by the mid-18th century, cultivars were described that can still be found in modern gardens.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Sonchus" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Lactuca sativa" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Liliaceae",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of flowering plants in order Liliales, including lilies",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		summary = "The lily [[Family (taxonomy)|family]], **Liliaceae**, consists of about 15 [[Genus|genera]] and 610 [[Species|species]] of [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] within the order Liliales. They are monocotyledonous, [[Perennial|perennial]], herbaceous geophytes, often growing from [[Bulb|bulbs]] although some have [[Rhizome|rhizomes]]. The [[Leaf|leaves]] are linear in shape, with their veins usually arranged parallel to the edges, single and arranged alternating on the stem, or in a rosette at the base. The flowers are large with six colored or patterned petaloid tepals (undifferentiated [[Petal|petals]] and sepals) arranged in two whorls of three, six stamens and a superior ovary. The fruit can be a berry or capsule, with seeds dispersed by animals or wind, respectively.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'First described in 1789, the lily family became a [[Paraphyly|paraphyletic]] "catch-all" (wastebasket) group of lilioid monocots that did not fit into other families and included many genera now included in other families (and in some cases in other orders). Consequently, treatments of "Liliaceae" often include these other taxa. The family likely evolved between 82 and 52 [[Million years ago|million years ago]] during the [[Late Cretaceous]] to Early Paleogene periods. Plants in this family have evolved with a fair amount of morphological diversity despite their genetic similarity. Liliaceae are widely distributed, mainly in temperate regions of the Northern Hemisphere and the flowers are insect pollinated. Most of their genera, [[Lilium]] in particular, face considerable herbivory pressure from deer in some areas, both wild and domestic.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Heliconia" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Liliaceae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lilium",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of plants",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		summary = '**Lilium** is a [[Genus|genus]] of [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous]] [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] growing from bulbs, all with large and often prominent flowers. Lilies are a group of flowering plants which are important in [[Culture|culture]] and literature in much of the world. Most species are native to the [[Northern Hemisphere]] and their range is [[Temperate climate|temperate climates]] and extends into the [[Subtropics|subtropics]]. Many other plants have "lily" in their [[Common name|common names]], but do not belong to the same genus and are therefore not true lilies.',
		related = { "Canna (plant)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Lilium" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lily of the valley",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of flowering plant in the asparagus family",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		aliases = { "Convallaria majalis" },
		summary = "**Lily-of-the-valley** (**Convallaria majalis**), also written as **lily of the valley**, is a woodland [[Flowering plant|flowering plant]] with sweetly scented, pendent, bell-shaped white flowers borne in sprays in spring. It is native to [[Europe]], [[West Asia|Western Asia]] and [[North Asia|Northern Asia]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The former [[Variety (botany)|varieties]] Convallaria majalis var. montana (native to eastern North America) and Convallaria majalis var. keiskei (native to eastern Asia), are now split as the separate species Convallaria pseudomajalis, and Convallaria keiskei, respectively. Other names include **May bells**, **Our Lady's tears**, and **Mary's tears**. Its French name, sometimes appearing in the names of perfumes imitating the flower's scent. In pre-modern England, the plant was known as **glovewort** (as it was a wort used to create a salve for sore hands), or **Apollinaris** (according to a legend that it was discovered by [[Apollo]]).",
			},
		},
		related = { "Monocotyledon", "Garlic" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Convallaria majalis" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lima bean",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of plant",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Fabales" },
		aliases = { "Phaseolus lunatus", "Butter bean", "Sieva bean" },
		summary = "A **lima bean** (**Phaseolus lunatus**), also commonly known as **butter bean**, **sieva bean**, **double bean** or **Madagascar bean**, is a [[Legume|legume]] grown for its edible [[Seed|seeds]] or [[Bean|beans]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Although lima beans and butter beans are the same species, they are sometimes considered distinct in culinary use, the former being small and green, the latter large and yellow. In areas where both are considered to be lima beans, the green variety may be labeled as "baby" (and less commonly "junior") limas.',
			},
