	{
		title = "Monocotyledon",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Clade of flowering plants",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		aliases = { "Monocotyledons", "Monocots", "Lilianae" },
		summary = '**Monocotyledons**, commonly referred to as **monocots** (**Lilianae** sensu Chase & Reveal), are [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] whose [[Seed|seeds]] contain only one [[Embryo|embryonic]] leaf, or cotyledon. A monocot [[Taxon|taxon]] has been in use for several decades, but with various ranks and under several different names. The APG IV system recognises its [[Monophyly|monophyly]] but does not assign it to a taxonomic rank, and instead uses the term "monocots" to refer to the group.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Monocotyledons are contrasted with the [[Dicotyledon|dicotyledons]], which have two cotyledons. Unlike the monocots however, the dicots are not [[Monophyly|monophyletic]] and the two cotyledons are instead the ancestral characteristic of all flowering plants. Botanists now classify dicots into the [[Eudicots|eudicots]] ("true dicots") and several [[Basal (phylogenetics)|basal]] lineages from which both the eudicots and monocots emerged. The monocots are extremely important economically, culturally, and ecologically, and make up a majority of plant biomass used in agriculture. Common crops such as [[Date palm|dates]], [[Onion|onions]], [[Garlic|garlic]], [[Rice|rice]], [[Wheat|wheat]], [[Maize|maize]], and [[Sugarcane|sugarcane]] are all monocots. The [[Poaceae|grasses]] alone cover over 40% of Earth\'s land area and contribute a significant portion of the human diet. [[Orchid|Orchids]], [[Tulip|tulips]], [[Narcissus (plant)|daffodils]], and [[Lilium|lilies]] are among the common houseplants belonging to the monocot group.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Amaryllis" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Monocots" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Moraceae",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of flowering plants",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Rosales" },
		aliases = { "Mulberry", "Fig family" },
		summary = "**Moraceae** is a family of [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] comprising about 48 genera and over 1100 species, and is commonly known as the **mulberry** or **fig family**. Most are widespread in tropical and subtropical regions, less so in temperate climates; however, their distribution is cosmopolitan overall. The only common characteristics within the family are the presence of latex-producing glands in the leaves and stems, and milky sap in the soft tissues; but generally useful field characters include two carpels sometimes with one reduced, compound inconspicuous flowers, and compound fruits. The family includes well-known plants such as the common fig, [[Breadfruit|breadfruit]], [[Jackfruit|jackfruit]] and mulberry.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The 'flowers' of Moraceae are often pseudanthia (reduced [[Inflorescence|inflorescences]]).",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Moraceae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Morus (plant)",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of plants",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Rosales" },
		aliases = { "Morus", "Mulberries" },
		summary = "**Morus**, a [[Genus|genus]] of flowering plants in the family [[Moraceae]], consists of 19 species of [[Deciduous|deciduous]] trees commonly known as **mulberries**, growing wild and under cultivation in many [[Temperate climate|temperate]] world regions. Generally, the genus has 64 subordinate taxa, though the three most common are referred to as white, red, and black, originating from the color of their dormant buds and not necessarily the fruit color (Morus alba, M. rubra, and M. nigra, respectively), with numerous [[Cultivar|cultivars]] and some taxa currently unchecked and awaiting taxonomic scrutiny. M. alba is native to [[South Asia]], but is widely distributed across [[Europe]], [[Southern Africa]], [[South America]], and [[North America]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"M. alba is also the species most preferred by the [[Bombyx mori|silkworm]]. It is regarded as an [[Invasive species|invasive species]] in Brazil, the United States and some states of Australia. The closely related genus Broussonetia is also commonly known as mulberry, notably the paper mulberry (Broussonetia papyrifera). Despite their similar appearance, mulberries are not closely related to raspberries or blackberries. All three species belong to the Rosales order. But while the mulberry is a tree belonging to the [[Moraceae]] family (also including the fig, [[Jackfruit|jackfruit]], and other fruits), raspberries and blackberries are brambles and belong to the [[Rosaceae]] family.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Elm" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Morus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Moss",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌿",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Division of typically non-vascular land plants",
		tags = { "Non-flowering plants", "Bryophytes" },
		aliases = { "Mosses" },
		summary = "**Mosses** are small, non-vascular [[Flower|flowerless]] [[Plant|plants]] in the taxonomic [[Phylum|division]] Bryophyta sensu stricto. Bryophyta sensu lato may also refer to the parent group, [[Bryophyte|bryophytes]], which comprises liverworts, mosses, and [[Hornwort|hornworts]]. Mosses typically form dense green clumps or mats, often in damp or shady locations. The individual plants are usually composed of simple [[Leaf|leaves]] that are generally only one cell thick, attached to a [[Plant stem|stem]] that may be branched or unbranched and has only a limited role in conducting water and nutrients. Although some species have conducting tissues, these are generally poorly developed and structurally different from similar tissue found in vascular plants.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Mosses do not have [[Seed|seeds]] and after fertilisation develop [[Sporophyte|sporophytes]] with unbranched stalks topped with single capsules containing spores. They are typically 0.2-10 cm tall, though some species are much larger. Dawsonia superba, the tallest moss in the world, can grow to 60 cm in height. There are approximately 12,000 species. Mosses are commonly confused with liverworts, hornworts and [[Lichen|lichens]]. Although often described as non-vascular plants, many mosses have advanced vascular systems. Like liverworts and hornworts, the haploid [[Gametophyte|gametophyte]] generation of mosses is the dominant phase of the [[Biological life cycle|life cycle]]. This contrasts with the pattern in all vascular plants (seed plants and pteridophytes), where the diploid sporophyte generation is dominant.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Liverwort" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Bryophyta" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Myrtaceae",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Myrtle family of plants",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Myrtales" },
		aliases = { "Myrtle family" },
		summary = "**Myrtaceae**, the **myrtle family**, is a family of [[Dicotyledon|dicotyledonous]] [[Plant|plants]] placed within the order Myrtales. All species are woody, contain [[Essential oil|essential oils]], and have flower parts in multiples of four or five. The [[Leaf|leaves]] are [[Evergreen|evergreen]], alternate to mostly opposite, simple, and usually entire (i.e., without a toothed margin). The [[Flower|flowers]] have a base number of five petals, though in several genera, the petals are minute or absent. The stamens are usually very conspicuous, brightly coloured, and numerous.",
		related = { "Guava" },
		infobox = {
