	},
	{
		title = "Oak",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tree or shrub in the genus Quercus",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Fagales" },
		aliases = { "Quercus" },
		summary = "An **oak** is a [[Hardwood|hardwood]] tree or shrub in the [[Genus|genus]] **Quercus** of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the [[Northern Hemisphere]], with some 500 species, both [[Deciduous|deciduous]] and [[Evergreen|evergreen]]. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle [[Eocene]]. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into [[Old World]] and New World [[Clade|clades]], but many oak species [[Hybrid (biology)|hybridise]] freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ecologically, oaks are [[Keystone species|keystone species]] in habitats from Mediterranean semi-desert to subtropical [[Rainforest|rainforest]]. They live in association with many kinds of [[Mushroom|fungi]], including [[Truffle|truffles]]. Oak timber is strong and hard, and has found many uses in construction and furniture-making. The bark was traditionally used for tanning leather. Almost a third of oak species are threatened with extinction due to climate change, invasive pests, and [[Habitat destruction|habitat loss]]. In culture, the oak tree is a symbol of strength and serves as the national tree of many countries. In Indo-European and related religions, the oak is associated with thunder gods. Individual oak trees of cultural significance include the Royal Oak in Britain, the Charter Oak in the United States, and the Guernica Oak in the Basque Country.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Alder" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Quercus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Oat",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Cereal grass and grain",
		tags = { "Monocots", "Poales" },
		aliases = { "Avena sativa", "Common oat" },
		summary = "The **oat** (**Avena sativa**), sometimes called the **common oat**, is a [[Species|species]] of [[Cereal|cereal]] grass (Avena) grown for [[Fodder|fodder]] and for its seed, which is known by the same name (usually in the plural). Oats appear to have been [[Domestication|domesticated]] as a secondary crop, as their seeds resembled those of other cereals closely enough for them to be included by early cultivators. Oats tolerate cold winters less well than cereals such as [[Wheat|wheat]], [[Barley|barley]], and [[Rye|rye]], but need less summer heat and more rain, making them important in areas such as Northwest Europe that have cool, wet summers. They can tolerate low-nutrient and acid soils.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Oats are used for human consumption as oatmeal, including as steel cut oats or rolled oats. Global production is dominated by Canada and Russia; global trade is a small part of production, most of the grain being consumed within the producing countries. One of the most common uses of oats is as [[Livestock|livestock]] feed; the crop can also be grown as groundcover and ploughed in as a green manure.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Juncus" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Avena sativa" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Okra",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of edible plant",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Others" },
		aliases = { "Abelmoschus esculentus", "Lady's fingers" },
		summary = "**Okra**, **Abelmoschus esculentus**, known in some English-speaking countries as **lady's fingers**, is a [[Flowering plant|flowering plant]] in the [[Malvaceae|mallow family]] native to [[West Africa]]. Cultivated in tropical, subtropical, and warm temperate regions around the world for its edible green (red in some cultivars) seed pods, okra is used in the [[Food|cuisines]] of many countries.",
		related = { "Gardenia", "Nelumbo" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Abelmoschus esculentus" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Oleaceae",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of flowering plants",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Lamiales" },
		aliases = { "Olive family", "Lilac family" },
		summary = "**Oleaceae**, also known as the **olive family** or sometimes the **lilac family**, is a [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomic]] [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of [[Flowering plant|flowering]] [[Shrub|shrubs]], [[Tree|trees]], and a few lianas in the [[Order (taxonomy)|order]] Lamiales. It presently comprises 28 [[Genus|genera]], one of which is recently [[Extinction|extinct]]. The extant [[Genus|genera]] include Cartrema, which was resurrected in 2012. The number of species in the Oleaceae is variously estimated in a wide range around 700. The flowers are often numerous and highly odoriferous. The family has a sub[[Cosmopolitan distribution|cosmopolitan distribution]], ranging from the [[Subarctic|subarctic]] to the southernmost parts of Africa, Australia, and South America.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Notable members include [[Olive|olive]], [[Fraxinus|ash]], jasmine, and several popular [[Ornamental plant|ornamental plants]] including privet, forsythia, fringetrees, and lilac.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Oleaceae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Olive",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🫒",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fruit of a cultivated plant",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Lamiales" },
		summary = "The cultivated olive (botanical name Olea europaea subsp. europaea) is a [[Subspecies|subspecies]] of [[Subtropics|subtropical]] evergreen tree in the family [[Oleaceae]]. Modern [[Cultivar|cultivars]] are traced primarily to the [[Near East]], [[Aegean Sea]], and [[Strait of Gibraltar]]. Olea europaea is the [[Type species|type species]] for its [[Genus|genus]], Olea, which lends its name to the [[Oleaceae]] plant family. The olive [[Fruit|fruit]] is classed botanically as a [[Drupe|drupe]], similar in structure and function to the [[Cherry|cherry]] or [[Peach|peach]]. The term [[Oil|oil]] was originally synonymous with olive oil.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The olive has deep historical, economic, and cultural significance in the Mediterranean. It was one of the first fruit trees to be domesticated, being first cultivated in the [[Eastern Mediterranean]] between 6,000 and 4,000 BC. It gradually spread around the [[Mediterranean basin|Mediterranean Basin]] via trade and human migration. Olive cultivation was vital to the growth and prosperity of several Mediterranean civilisations. The fruit is eaten as table olives; the oil extracted from the fruit is used in food and formerly for lamp fuel; and the wood is used for decorative objects. The olive branch is a symbol of peace. The olive is a core ingredient in Middle Eastern and Mediterranean cuisines, particularly in the form of olive oil, and a defining feature of local landscapes, commerce, and folk traditions. The olive is cultivated throughout the Mediterranean Basin, and in other regions with a suitable climate.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Onion",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧅",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Bulbous vegetable",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		aliases = { "Bulb onion", "Common onion" },
		summary = "The **onion**, also known as the **bulb onion** or **common onion**, is a [[Vegetable|vegetable]] that is the most widely cultivated species of the genus Allium. The shallot is a [[Variety (botany)|botanical variety]] of the onion which was classified as a separate species until 2011. The onion's close relatives include [[Garlic|garlic]], scallion, [[Leek|leek]], and chives.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The genus contains several other species cultivated for food, such as the Japanese bunching onion Allium fistulosum, the tree onion Allium × proliferum, and the Canada onion Allium canadense. The name wild onion is applied to a number of Allium species, but A. cepa is exclusively known from cultivation. Its ancestral wild original form is not known, although escapes from cultivation have become established in some regions. The onion is most frequently a [[Biennial plant|biennial]] or a [[Perennial|perennial plant]], but is usually treated as an [[Annual plant|annual]] and harvested in its first growing season. The onion plant has a fan of hollow, bluish-green leaves, and its bulb at the base of the plant begins to swell when a certain day-length is reached. The bulbs are composed of shortened, compressed, underground stems surrounded by fleshy modified scale (leaves) that envelop a central bud at the tip of the stem.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Colchicum" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Allium cepa" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Orange (fruit)",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Citrus fruit",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Sapindales" },
		aliases = { "Orange", "Sweet orange" },
		summary = "The **orange**, also called **sweet orange** to distinguish it from the bitter orange ([[Citrus]] × aurantium), is the [[Fruit|fruit]] of a tree in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Rutaceae. Botanically, this is the hybrid Citrus × sinensis, between the pomelo (Citrus maxima) and the [[Mandarin orange|mandarin orange]] (Citrus reticulata). The [[Chloroplast|chloroplast]] [[Genome|genome]], and therefore the maternal line, is that of pomelo. Hybrids of the sweet orange form later types of mandarin and the [[Grapefruit|grapefruit]]. The sweet orange has had its full genome sequenced.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The orange originated in a region encompassing Southern China, [[Northeast India]], and [[Myanmar]]; the earliest mention of the sweet orange was in [[Chinese literature]] in 314 BC. Orange trees are widely grown in tropical and subtropical areas for their sweet fruit. The fruit of the orange tree can be eaten fresh or processed for its juice or fragrant peel. In 2024, 67 million [[Tonne|tonnes]] of oranges were grown worldwide, with [[Brazil]] producing 23% of the total, followed by [[China]] and [[Mexico]]. Oranges, variously understood, have featured in human culture since ancient times. They first appear in Western art in the [[Arnolfini Portrait]] by [[Jan van Eyck]], but they had been depicted in Chinese art centuries earlier, as in Zhao Lingrang's [[Song dynasty]] fan painting Yellow Oranges and Green Tangerines.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Lychee" },
	},
	{
		title = "Orchid",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of flowering plants in the order Asparagales",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		aliases = { "Orchids", "Orchidaceae" },
		summary = "**Orchids** are plants that belong to the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] **Orchidaceae**, a diverse and widespread group of [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] with blooms that are often colourful and fragrant. Orchids are [[Cosmopolitan distribution|cosmopolitan]] plants, living in diverse habitats on every continent except Antarctica. The world's richest diversity of orchid genera and species is in the [[Tropics|tropics]]. Many species are [[Epiphyte|epiphytes]], living on trees. The flowers and their pollination mechanisms are highly specialized, attracting insect pollinators by colour, pattern, scent, pheromones, and sometimes by mimicking female insects. Orchids have very small seeds, relying on fungal partners for germination.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some orchids have no leaves, either photosynthesizing with their roots or relying entirely on fungal partners for food. Orchidaceae is one of the two largest families of flowering plants. It contains about 28,000 currently accepted species in around 700 [[Genus|genera]]. That represents some 6–11% of all species of seed plants. [[Horticulture|Horticulturists]] run many orchid societies around the world; they have produced many [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrids]] and [[Cultivar|cultivars]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Asparagus" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Orchidaceae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ornamental plant",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Plant that is grown for decorative purposes",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Ornamental plants", "Garden plants" },
		summary = '**Ornamental plants** or **garden plants** are [[Plant|plants]] that are primarily grown because of their appearance, but also for qualities such as scent or how they shape physical space. Many [[Flowering plant|flowering plants]] and garden varieties tend to be specially bred [[Cultivar|cultivars]] that improve on the original [[Species|species]] in qualities such as color, shape, scent, and long-lasting blooms. But "ornamental plant" is not a category recognised in scientific [[Botany|botany]] as many garden plants are of the original species, not altered by [[Plant breeding|plant breeding]]. There are many examples of fine ornamental plants that can provide height, privacy, and beauty for any garden. These ornamental perennial plants have seeds that allow them to reproduce.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"One of the beauties of ornamental grasses is that they are very versatile and low maintenance. Almost all types of plants have ornamental varieties: trees, [[Shrub|shrubs]], climbers, grasses, succulents, [[Aquatic plant|aquatic plants]], herbaceous [[Perennial|perennials]] and [[Annual plant|annual plants]]. Non-botanical classifications include houseplants, bedding plants, hedges, plants for cut flowers and foliage plants. The cultivation of ornamental plants comes under floriculture and tree nurseries, which is a major branch of [[Horticulture|horticulture]]. Commonly, ornamental garden plants are grown for the display of aesthetic features including [[Flower|flowers]], [[Leaf|leaves]], scent, overall foliage texture, fruit, [[Plant stem|stem]] and bark, and [[Aesthetics|aesthetic]] form. In some cases, unusual features may be considered to be of interest, such as the prominent thorns of Rosa sericea and [[Cactus|cacti]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Oryza sativa",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of plant",
		tags = { "Monocots", "Poales" },
		aliases = { "Asian cultivated rice" },
		summary = "**Oryza sativa**, having the common name **Asian cultivated rice**, is the much more common of the two [[Rice|rice]] species cultivated as a [[Cereal|cereal]], the other species being O. glaberrima, African rice. It was first domesticated in the [[Yangtze|Yangtze River]] basin in China 13,500 to 8,200 years ago.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Oryza sativa belongs to the genus Oryza and the BOP clade in the grass family [[Poaceae]]. With a [[Genome|genome]] consisting of 430Mbp across 12 [[Chromosome|chromosomes]], it is renowned for being easy to genetically modify and is a model organism for the study of the biology of cereals and [[Monocotyledon|monocots]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Spanish moss" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Oryza sativa" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pandanus",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌴",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of palm-like monocot trees and shrubs",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		summary = "**Pandanus** is a [[Genus|genus]] of monocots with about 578 accepted [[Species|species]]. They are palm-like, dioecious trees and shrubs native to the [[Old World]] tropics and subtropics. Common names include **pandan**, **screw palm** and **screw pine**. The genus is classified in the order Pandanales, family Pandanaceae, and is the largest in the family.",
		related = { "Monocotyledon", "Garlic" },
		infobox = {
