	{
		title = "Plum",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Edible fruit",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Rosales" },
		summary = "A **plum** is a [[Fruit|fruit]] of some species in Prunus subg. Prunus. Dried plums are usually called prunes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Plums are likely to have been one of the first fruits domesticated by humans, with origins in [[Eastern Europe]], the [[Caucasus Mountains]] and [[China]]. They were brought to [[United Kingdom|Britain]] from Asia, and their cultivation has been documented in [[Andalusia]], southern [[Spain]]. Plums are a diverse group of species, with trees reaching a height of 5–6 m when pruned. The fruit is a [[Drupe|drupe]], with a firm and juicy flesh. In 2023, China produced 55% of the world total of plums. Japanese or Chinese plums dominate the fresh fruit market, while European plums are also common in some regions.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Poaceae",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of flowering plants commonly known as grasses",
		tags = { "Monocots", "Poales" },
		aliases = { "Gramineae", "True grasses" },
		summary = "**Poaceae**, also called **Gramineae**, is a large and nearly ubiquitous [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of [[Monocotyledon|monocotyledonous]] flowering plants commonly known as **true grasses**. It includes the [[Cereal|cereal]] grasses, [[Bamboo|bamboos]], the grasses of natural [[Grassland|grassland]] and species cultivated in lawns and pasture. Poaceae is the most well-known family within the informal group known as [[Grass|grass]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"With around 780 [[Genus|genera]] and around 12,000 species, the Poaceae is the fifth-largest plant family, following the [[Asteraceae]], Orchidaceae, [[Fabaceae]] and [[Rubiaceae]]. The Poaceae are the most economically important plant family, including [[Staple food|staple foods]] from domesticated [[Cereal|cereal]] crops such as [[Maize|maize]], [[Wheat|wheat]], [[Rice|rice]], [[Oat|oats]], [[Barley|barley]], and [[Millet|millet]] for people and as [[Forage|feed]] for [[Livestock|meat-producing animals]]. They provide, through direct human consumption, a substantial portion of all dietary energy: rice provides 20%, wheat supplies 19%, and maize (corn) 5%. Some members of the Poaceae are used as building materials ([[Bamboo|bamboo]], thatch, and straw); others can provide a source of [[Biofuel|biofuel]], primarily via the conversion of maize to ethanol. Grasses have [[Plant stem|stems]] that are hollow except at the nodes and narrow alternate leaves borne in two ranks. The lower part of each leaf encloses the stem, forming a leaf-sheath.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Poaceae" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Podocarpus",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of coniferous trees",
		tags = { "Non-flowering plants", "Gymnosperms" },
		summary = "**Podocarpus** is a [[Genus|genus]] of [[Conifer|conifers]], the most numerous and widely distributed of the podocarp family, the Podocarpaceae. Podocarpus species are [[Evergreen|evergreen]] [[Shrub|shrubs]] or [[Tree|trees]], usually from 1 to 25 m tall, known to reach 40 m at times. The cones have two to five fused cone scales, which form a fleshy, berry-like, brightly coloured receptacle at maturity. The fleshy cones attract [[Bird|birds]], which then eat the cones and disperse the seeds in their droppings. [[Plants of the World Online]] accepts 116 species. Other authorities place 97 to 107 species in the genus depending on the circumscription of the species.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Species are cultivated as ornamental plants for parks and large gardens. The [[Cultivar|cultivar]] 'County Park Fire' has won the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit.",
