	{
		title = "Soybean",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Legume grown for its edible bean",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Fabales" },
		aliases = { "Soy bean", "Soya bean", "Glycine max" },
		summary = "The **soybean**, **soy bean**, or **soya bean** (**Glycine max**) is a species of [[Legume|legume]] native to [[East Asia]], widely grown for its edible [[Bean|bean]]. Soy is a staple crop, the world's most grown legume, and an important animal feed.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Soy is a key food source, owing to its high protein and oil content. Soybean oil is widely used in cooking, as well as in industry. Traditional unfermented food uses of soybeans include edamame, as well as [[Soy milk|soy milk]], from which [[Tofu|tofu]] and tofu skin are made. Fermented soy foods include [[Soy sauce|soy sauce]], fermented bean paste, nattō, and tempeh. Fat-free (defatted) soybean meal is a significant and cheap source of protein for animal feeds and many packaged meals. For example, soybean products, such as textured vegetable protein (TVP), are ingredients in many meat and [[Dairy|dairy]] substitutes. Soy-based foods are traditionally associated with East Asian cuisines, and still constitute a major part of East Asian diets, but processed soy products are increasingly used in Western cuisines. Soy was [[Domestication|domesticated]] from the wild soybean (Glycine soja) in north-central China between 6,000 and 9,000 years ago.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Glycine max" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spanish moss",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌿",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of plant, Tillandsia usneoides",
		tags = { "Monocots", "Poales" },
		aliases = { "Tillandsia usneoides" },
		summary = '**Spanish moss** (**Tillandsia usneoides**) is an [[Epiphyte|epiphytic]] [[Flowering plant|flowering plant]] that often grows upon large trees in [[Tropical climate|tropical]] and subtropical climates. It is native to much of [[Mexico]], [[Bermuda]], the Bahamas, [[Central America]], [[South America]] (as far south as northern [[Patagonia]]), the [[Southern United States]], and [[West Indies]]. It has been naturalized in [[Queensland]] ([[Australia]]). It is colloquially known as "old man\'s beard" in several places, and known as "grandpa\'s beard" in [[French Polynesia]]. It has the widest distribution of any bromeliad.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most known in the [[United States]], it commonly is found on the southern live oak (Quercus virginiana) and bald cypress (Taxodium distichum) in the lowlands, swamps, and marshes of the mid-Atlantic and Southeastern states, from the coast of southeastern [[Virginia]] to [[Florida]] and west to southern [[Arkansas]] and [[Texas]]. While it superficially resembles its namesake, the [[Lichen|lichen]] Usnea, it is neither a [[Lichen|lichen]] nor a [[Moss|moss]] (instead being a member of the bromeliad family, Bromeliaceae), and it is not native to [[Spain]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Typha" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Tillandsia usneoides" },
			{ "Conservation status", "Least Concern" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sphagnum",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌿",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of mosses, peat moss",
		tags = { "Non-flowering plants", "Bryophytes" },
		aliases = { "Sphagnum moss", "Bog moss", "Quacker moss" },
		summary = "**Sphagnum** is a [[Genus|genus]] of approximately 380 accepted [[Species|species]] of [[Moss|mosses]], commonly known as **sphagnum moss**, also **bog moss** and **quacker moss** (although that term is also sometimes used for [[Peat|peat]]). Accumulations of Sphagnum can store water, since both living and dead plants can hold large quantities of water inside their cells; plants may hold 16 to 26 times as much water as their dry weight, depending on the species. The empty cells help retain water in drier conditions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As Sphagnum moss grows, it can slowly spread into drier conditions, forming larger mires, both raised [[Bog|bogs]] and blanket bogs. Thus, Sphagnum can influence the composition of such habitats, with some describing Sphagnum as 'habitat manipulators' or 'autogenic ecosystem engineers'. These [[Peat|peat]] accumulations then provide habitat for a wide array of peatland plants, including sedges and ericaceous shrubs, as well as orchids and carnivorous plants. Sphagnum and the peat formed from it do not decay readily because of the phenolic compounds embedded in the moss's [[Cell wall|cell walls]]. In addition, bogs, like all [[Wetland|wetlands]], develop anaerobic soil conditions, which produces slower anaerobic decay rather than aerobic microbial action. Peat moss can also acidify its surroundings by taking up [[Ion|cations]], such as [[Calcium|calcium]] and [[Magnesium|magnesium]], and releasing [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] ions.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Liverwort" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Sphagnum" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spinach",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species of flowering plant",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Caryophyllales" },
		aliases = { "Spinacia oleracea" },
		summary = "**Spinach** (**Spinacia oleracea**) is a leafy green [[Flowering plant|flowering plant]] native to [[Central Asia|Central]] and [[West Asia|Western Asia]]. It is of the order Caryophyllales, family Amaranthaceae, subfamily Chenopodioideae. Its leaves are a common vegetable consumed either fresh, cooked or after storage (using [[Food preservation|preservation techniques]] like [[Canning|canning]], freezing, or dehydration). The taste differs considerably between cooked and raw: the high oxalate content may be reduced by blanching.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is an [[Annual plant|annual plant]] (rarely [[Biennial plant|biennial]]), growing as tall as 1 ft. Spinach may overwinter in temperate regions. The [[Leaf|leaves]] are alternate, simple, ovate to triangular, and very variable in size: 2–30 cm long and 1–15 cm broad, with larger leaves at the base of the plant and small leaves higher on the flowering stem. The [[Flower|flowers]] are inconspicuous, yellow-green, 3–4 mm in diameter, and mature into a small, hard, dry, lumpy [[Fruit|fruit]] cluster 5–10 mm across containing several [[Seed|seeds]]. In 2024, world production of spinach was 31 million [[Tonne|tonnes]], with China alone accounting for 92% of the total.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Spinacia oleracea" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sporophyte",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌿",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Diploid multicellular stage in the life cycle of a plant or alga",
		tags = { "Biological processes and physiology", "Plant reproduction" },
		summary = "A **sporophyte** is one of the two [[Alternation of generations|alternating]] [[Multicellular organism|multicellular phases]] in the [[Biological life cycle|life cycles]] of [[Plant|plants]] and [[Algae|algae]]. It is a diploid multicellular organism which produces asexual [[Spore|spores]]. This stage [[Alternation of generations|alternates with]] a multicellular haploid [[Gametophyte|gametophyte]] phase.",
		related = { "Plant reproductive morphology" },
	},
	{
		title = "Spruce",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌿",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of coniferous evergreen trees",
		tags = { "Non-flowering plants", "Gymnosperms" },
		aliases = { "Picea" },
		summary = "A **spruce** is a [[Tree|tree]] of the genus **Picea**, a genus of some 37 species of [[Conifer|coniferous]] [[Evergreen|evergreen]] trees in the family Pinaceae, found in the northern [[Temperate climate|temperate]] and boreal ([[Taiga|taiga]]) regions of the [[Northern Hemisphere]]. Picea is treated either in the [[Subfamily|subfamily]] Pinoideae, or the sole genus in its own subfamily **Piceoideae**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Spruces can be distinguished from other [[Genus|genera]] of the family Pinaceae by their needles (leaves), which are four-sided and attached singly to small persistent peg-like structures (pulvini) on the twigs. The needles are shed when 4–10 years old, leaving the twigs rough with the retained pegs. Pests of spruce forestry include green spruce aphid, eastern spruce budworm, European spruce bark beetle, and great spruce bark beetle. Spruce is a major producer of timber for construction, and of pulp for paper. It is the standard material for the soundboards of stringed instruments like acoustic guitars. Native Americans use the roots of some species for weaving baskets. The Norway spruce is widely used for Christmas trees.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Juniper" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Picea" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Stoma",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "In plants, a variable pore between paired guard cells",
		tags = { "Anatomy and morphology", "Plant anatomy" },
		summary = "The pore is bordered by a pair of specialized [[Ground tissue|parenchyma]] cells known as guard cells that regulate the size of the stomatal opening.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term is usually used collectively to refer to the entire stomatal complex, consisting of the paired guard cells and the pore itself, which is referred to as the stomatal aperture. Air, containing [[Oxygen|oxygen]], which is used in [[Cellular respiration|respiration]], and carbon dioxide, which is used in [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]], passes through stomata by gaseous [[Diffusion|diffusion]]. Water vapour diffuses through the stomata into the atmosphere as part of a process called [[Transpiration|transpiration]]. Stomata are present in the [[Sporophyte|sporophyte]] generation of the vast majority of land plants, with the exception of liverworts, as well as some mosses and hornworts. In vascular plants the number, size and distribution of stomata varies widely. [[Dicotyledon|Dicotyledons]] usually have more stomata on the lower surface of the leaves than the upper surface. [[Monocotyledon|Monocotyledons]] such as [[Onion|onion]], [[Oat|oat]] and [[Maize|maize]] may have about the same number of stomata on both leaf surfaces.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Strawberry",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍓",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Edible fruit",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Rosales" },
		aliases = { "Garden strawberry", "Fragaria × ananassa" },
		summary = "The **garden strawberry** (or simply **strawberry**; **Fragaria × ananassa**) is a widely grown [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] plant cultivated worldwide for its [[Fruit|fruit]]. The genus Fragaria, the strawberries, is in the rose family, [[Rosaceae]]. The fruit is appreciated for its aroma, bright red colour, juicy texture, and sweetness. It is eaten either fresh or in prepared foods such as [[Fruit preserves|jam]], [[Ice cream|ice cream]], and [[Chocolate|chocolates]]. Artificial strawberry flavourings and aromas are widely used in commercial products. Botanically, the strawberry is not a [[Berry (botany)|berry]], but an aggregate accessory fruit. Each apparent 'seed' on the outside of the strawberry is actually an achene, a botanical fruit with a seed inside it.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The garden strawberry was first bred in [[Brittany]], France, in the 1750s via a cross of F. virginiana from eastern North America and F. chiloensis, which was brought from Chile by Amédée-François Frézier in 1714. [[Cultivar|Cultivars]] of F. × ananassa have replaced the musk strawberry F. moschata in commercial production. In 2024, world production of strawberries exceeded ten million tons, led by [[China]] with 38% of the total. Strawberries have appeared in literature and art from Roman times; [[Virgil]] wrote about the snake lurking beneath the strawberry, an image reinterpreted by later writers including [[William Shakespeare|Shakespeare]]. Strawberries appear in Italian, Flemish, and German paintings, including [[Hieronymus Bosch]]'s [[The Garden of Earthly Delights]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Ficus sycomorus" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Fragaria × ananassa" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sugar beet",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Plant grown commercially for sugar production",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Caryophyllales" },
		summary = "A **sugar beet** is a plant whose root contains a high concentration of [[Sucrose|sucrose]] and is grown commercially for [[Sugar|sugar]] production. In plant breeding, it is known as the Altissima cultivar group of the common beet (Beta vulgaris). Together with other beet cultivars, such as [[Beetroot|beetroot]] and [[Chard|chard]], it belongs to the subspecies Beta vulgaris subsp. vulgaris but is classified as var. saccharifera. Its closest wild relative is the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Sugar beets are grown in climates that are too cold for [[Sugarcane|sugarcane]]. In 2024, Russia and Germany were the two largest sugar beet producers contributing to a world total of 294 million [[Tonne|tonnes]]. [[Sugarcane]] accounts for most of the rest of sugar produced globally.",
			},
		},
