	{
		title = "Yeast",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍄",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Informal group of fungi",
		tags = { "Organisms", "Fungi" },
		summary = "A **yeast** is any species of [[Mushroom|fungus]] that grows primarily in a unicellular form and reproduces via budding or [[Fission (biology)|fission]]. Yeasts are [[Eukaryote|eukaryotic]] microorganisms that originated hundreds of millions of years ago, with at least 1,500 [[Species|species]] currently recognized. They constitute about 1% of all described fungal species.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some yeast species have the ability to develop multicellular characteristics by forming strings of connected budding cells known as pseudohyphae or false hyphae or in some cases [[Hypha|true hyphae]], or quickly evolve into a [[Multicellular organism|multicellular]] cluster with specialised [[Organelle|cell organelle]] functions. Yeast sizes vary greatly, depending on species and environment, typically measuring 3–4 [[Micrometre|m]] in [[Diameter|diameter]], although some yeasts can grow to 40 m in size. Most yeasts reproduce [[Asexual reproduction|asexually]] by [[Mitosis|mitosis]], and many do so by the asymmetric division process known as budding. With their single-celled growth habit, yeasts can be contrasted with molds, which grow [[Hypha|hyphae]]. Fungal species that can take both forms (depending on temperature or other conditions) are called dimorphic fungi. [[Saccharomyces cerevisiae|S. cerevisiae]] is also an important model organism in modern [[Cell (biology)|cell biology]] research, and is one of the most thoroughly studied eukaryotic microorganisms.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Ascomycota", "Basidiomycota", "Zygomycota" },
	},
	{
		title = "Yucca",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Plant genus in the agave subfamily",
		tags = { "Flowering plants", "Monocots" },
		summary = "**Yucca** is both the scientific name and [[Common name|common name]] for a genus native to North America from [[Panama]] to southern Canada. It contains 50 accepted species. In addition to yucca, they are also known as **Adam's needle** or **Spanish-bayonet**. The genus is generally classified in the asparagus family in a subfamily with the [[Agave]], though historically it was part of the lily family. The species range from small shrubby plants to tree-like giants, such as the Joshua tree. All yuccas have rosettes of leaves that taper to points and inflorescences with many flowers that are mainly cream white with thick petals.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Though adapted to a wide range of climates the plants are xerophytes, ones that specialize in dry living conditions. The tight relationship between the yucca plants and their pollinators, the yucca moths from the genera Tegeticula and Parategeticula, is a well known example of evolutionary [[Mutualism (biology)|mutualism]]. They are an important part of the ecology of North American [[Desert|deserts]], providing shelter to small animals and creating habitats. The human uses of yuccas include garden plants, as food, and for extracts. The flower petals of various species are eaten as a part of local cuisine, particularly in [[Central America]] and Mexico. Historically, the yucca was extensively used for its fibers to make cords, baskets, mats, and sandals. It continues to be used by native peoples for traditional soaps.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Colchicum" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Yucca" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Zucchini",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌿",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Edible summer squash",
		tags = { "Eudicots", "Cucurbitales" },
		summary = "Zucchini, courgette, or Cucurbita pepo var. cylindrica is a summer squash, a [[Vine|vining]] [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous plant]] whose fruit are harvested when their immature [[Seed|seeds]] and epicarp (rind) are still soft and edible. It is closely related, but not identical, to the marrow; its fruit may be called marrow when mature.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ordinary zucchini fruit are any shade of green, though the golden zucchini is a deep yellow or orange. At maturity, they can grow to nearly 1 meter in length, but they are normally harvested at about 15–25 cm. In [[Botany|botany]], the zucchini's fruit is a [[Berry (botany)|pepo]], a [[Berry (botany)|berry]] (the swollen ovary of the zucchini flower) with a hardened epicarp. In cookery, it is treated as a vegetable, usually cooked and eaten as an accompaniment or savory dish, though occasionally used in sweeter cooking. Zucchini descend from squashes first [[Domestication|domesticated]] in Mexico and Central America over 7,000 years ago, but the zucchini itself was bred in [[Milan]] in the late 19th century. Causes include stressed growing conditions, and cross pollination with ornamental squashes.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Genus", "[[Cucurbita]]" },
			{ "Species", "Cucurbita pepo" },
			{ "Origin", "19th-century northern Italy" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Zygomycota",
		category = "Plant",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👑",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Division or phylum of the kingdom Fungi",
		tags = { "Organisms", "Fungi" },
		aliases = { "Zygote fungi" },
		summary = "**Zygomycota**, or **zygote fungi**, is a former [[Phylum|division]] or [[Phylum|phylum]] of the kingdom [[Mushroom|Fungi]]. The members are now part of two [[Phylum|phyla]]: the Mucoromycota and Zoopagomycota. Approximately 1060 species are known. They are mostly terrestrial in habitat, living in soil or on decaying plant or animal material. Zygomycete [[Hypha|hyphae]] may be coenocytic, forming septa only where [[Gamete|gametes]] are formed or to wall off dead hyphae. Zygomycota is no longer recognised as it was not believed to be truly [[Monophyly|monophyletic]].",
