		description = "Branch of dynamics concerned with studying the motion of air",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Fluid mechanics" },
		summary = '**Aerodynamics** is the study of the motion of [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]], particularly when affected by a [[Solid|solid]] object, such as an [[Airplane|airplane]] wing. It involves topics covered in the field of [[Fluid dynamics|fluid dynamics]] and its subfield of gas dynamics, and is an important domain of study in [[Aeronautics|aeronautics]]. The term aerodynamics is often used synonymously with gas dynamics, the difference being that "gas dynamics" applies to the study of the motion of all gases, and is not limited to air. The formal study of aerodynamics began in the modern sense in the eighteenth century, although observations of fundamental concepts such as aerodynamic drag were recorded much earlier.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Since then, the use of aerodynamics through [[Mathematics|mathematical]] analysis, empirical approximations, wind tunnel experimentation, and [[Computer simulation|computer simulations]] has formed a rational basis for the development of heavier-than-air flight and a number of other technologies. Recent work in aerodynamics has focused on issues related to compressible flow, [[Turbulence|turbulence]], and boundary layers and has become increasingly computational in nature.",
