	{
		title = "Alkali metal",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Group of highly reactive chemical elements",
		tags = { "Chemical elements", "Chemical element groups" },
		aliases = { "Alkali metals" },
		summary = "The **alkali metals** consist of the [[Chemical element|chemical elements]] [[Lithium|lithium]] (Li), [[Sodium|sodium]] (Na), [[Potassium|potassium]] (K), [[Rubidium|rubidium]] (Rb), [[Caesium|caesium]] (Cs), and [[Francium|francium]] (Fr). Together with [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] they constitute [[Group (periodic table)|group 1]], which lies in the s-block of the [[Periodic table|periodic table]]. All alkali metals have their outermost electron in an s-orbital: this shared electron configuration results in them having very similar characteristic properties. Indeed, the alkali metals provide the best example of group trends in properties in the periodic table, with elements exhibiting well-characterised homologous behaviour. This family of elements is also known as the **lithium family** after its leading element.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The alkali metals are all [[Lustre (mineralogy)|shiny]], [[Hardness|soft]], highly [[Reactivity (chemistry)|reactive]] metals at [[Standard temperature and pressure|standard temperature and pressure]] and readily lose their [[Valence electron|outermost electron]] to form cations with [[Electric charge|charge]] +1. They can all be cut easily with a knife due to their softness, exposing a shiny surface that tarnishes rapidly in air due to [[Redox|oxidation]] by atmospheric moisture and [[Oxygen|oxygen]] (and in the case of lithium, [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]]). Because of their high reactivity, they must be stored under oil to prevent reaction with air, and are found naturally only in salts and never as the free elements. Caesium, the fifth alkali metal, is the most reactive of all the metals. All the alkali metals react with water, with the heavier alkali metals reacting more vigorously than the lighter ones.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Alkaline earth metal",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Group of chemical elements",
		tags = { "Chemical elements", "Chemical element groups" },
		aliases = { "Alkaline earth metals" },
		summary = "The **alkaline earth metals** are six [[Chemical element|chemical elements]] in [[Group (periodic table)|group]] 2 of the [[Periodic table|periodic table]]. They are [[Beryllium|beryllium]] (Be), [[Magnesium|magnesium]] (Mg), [[Calcium|calcium]] (Ca), [[Strontium|strontium]] (Sr), [[Barium|barium]] (Ba), and [[Radium|radium]] (Ra). The elements have very similar properties: they are all shiny, silvery-white, somewhat [[Reactivity (chemistry)|reactive]] [[Metal|metals]], and they are less reactive than the [[Alkali metal|alkali metals]] at [[Standard temperature and pressure|standard temperature and pressure]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Together with [[Helium|helium]], these elements have in common an outer [[Atomic orbital|s orbital]] which is full—that is, this orbital contains its full complement of two electrons, which the alkaline earth metals readily lose to form [[Ion|cations]] with [[Electric charge|charge]] +2, and an [[Oxidation state|oxidation state]] of +2. Helium is grouped with the [[Noble gas|noble gases]] and not with the alkaline earth metals, but it is theorized to have some similarities to beryllium when forced into bonding and has sometimes been suggested to belong to group 2. All the discovered alkaline earth metals occur in nature, although radium occurs only through the [[Decay chain|decay chain]] of [[Uranium|uranium]] and [[Thorium|thorium]] and not as a primordial element. There have been experiments, all unsuccessful, to try to synthesize element 120, the next potential member of the group.",
			},
