		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		aliases = { "Alkaloids" },
		summary = "**Alkaloids** are a broad class of naturally occurring [[Organic compound|organic compounds]] that contain at least one [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]] atom. Some synthetic compounds of similar structure may also be termed alkaloids.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Alkaloids are produced by a large variety of organisms including [[Bacteria|bacteria]], [[Mushroom|fungi]], plants, and [[Animal|animals]]. They can be purified from crude extracts of these organisms by acid-base extraction, or solvent extractions followed by silica-gel column chromatography. Alkaloids have a wide range of pharmacological activities including antimalarial (e.g. quinine), antiasthma (e.g. ephedrine), anticancer (e.g. homoharringtonine), cholinomimetic (e.g. galantamine), vasodilatory (e.g. vincamine), antiarrhythmic (e.g. quinidine), analgesic (e.g. morphine), antibacterial (e.g. chelerythrine), and antihyperglycemic activities (e.g. berberine). Although alkaloids act on a diversity of metabolic systems in humans and other animals, they almost uniformly evoke a bitter taste. The boundary between alkaloids and other nitrogen-containing natural compounds is not clear-cut.",
