	{
		title = "Americium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Am" },
		summary = "**Americium** is a [[Synthetic element|synthetic chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Am** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 95. It is radioactive and a transuranic member of the [[Actinide|actinide]] series in the [[Periodic table|periodic table]], located under the [[Lanthanide|lanthanide]] element [[Europium|europium]] and was thus named after the [[Americas]] by analogy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Americium was first produced in 1944 by the group of Glenn T. Seaborg from [[Berkeley, California]], at the Metallurgical Laboratory of the [[University of Chicago]], as part of the Manhattan Project. Although it is the third element in the transuranic series, it was discovered fourth, after the heavier [[Curium|curium]]. The discovery was kept secret and released to the public only in November 1945. Most americium is produced by [[Uranium|uranium]] or [[Plutonium|plutonium]] being bombarded with [[Neutron|neutrons]] in [[Nuclear reactor|nuclear reactors]] – one [[Tonne|tonne]] of spent nuclear fuel contains about 100 grams of americium. It is widely used in commercial ionization chamber smoke detectors, as well as in neutron sources and industrial gauges.",
			},
