	{
		title = "Amide",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Carbon–nitrogen bond" },
		aliases = { "Organic amide", "Carboxamide" },
		summary = "In [[Organic chemistry|organic chemistry]], an **amide**, also known as an **organic amide** or a **carboxamide**, is a [[Chemical compound|compound]] with the general formula R\\sC(\\dO)\\sNR′R″, where R, R', and R″ represent any group, typically organyl groups or [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] atoms. The amide functional group plays an important role in the chemistry of life where, as peptide bonds, they link [[Amino acid|amino acids]] together to form [[Protein|proteins]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Amides can be viewed as a derivative of a [[Carboxylic acid|carboxylic acid]] (R\\sC(\\dO)\\sOH) with the hydroxyl group (\\sOH) replaced by an amino group (\\sNR′R″); or, equivalently, an acyl (alkanoyl) group (R\\sC(\\dO)\\s) joined to an amino group. Common amides are formamide (H\\sC(\\dO)\\sNH2), acetamide (H3C\\sC(\\dO)\\sNH2), benzamide (C6H5\\sC(\\dO)\\sNH2), and dimethylformamide (H\\sC(\\dO)\\sN(\\sCH3)2). Amides are qualified as primary, secondary, and tertiary according to the number of acyl groups bounded to the nitrogen atom.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Amine",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical compounds and groups containing nitrogen with a lone pair (:N)",
		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Carbon–nitrogen bond" },
		aliases = { "Amines" },
		summary = "In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], **amines** are [[Organic compound|organic compounds]] that contain carbon–nitrogen bonds. Amines are formed when one or more hydrogen atoms in [[Ammonia|ammonia]] are replaced by alkyl or aryl groups. The nitrogen atom in an amine possesses a lone pair of electrons. Amines can also exist as heterocyclic compounds. Aniline is the simplest aromatic amine, consisting of a benzene ring bonded to an amino group.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Amines are classified into three types: primary (1°), secondary (2°), and tertiary (3°) amines. The [[Functional group|functional group]] \\sNH2 present in primary amines is called the amino group.",
