	{
		title = "Amoeba",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Cellular body type",
		tags = { "Organisms", "Other eukaryotes" },
		aliases = { "Ameba", "Amoebas", "Amebas" },
		summary = "An **amoeba** or **ameba** (;: **amoebas** or **amebas** (less commonly, **amoebae** or **amebae** )), often called an **amoeboid**, is a type of [[Cell (biology)|cell]] or [[Unicellular organism|unicellular organism]] with the ability to alter its shape, primarily by extending and retracting pseudopods. Amoebae do not form a single [[Taxon|taxonomic group]]; instead, they are found in every major lineage of [[Eukaryote|eukaryotic]] organisms. Amoeboid cells occur not only among the [[Protozoa|protozoa]], but also in [[Mushroom|fungi]], [[Algae|algae]], and [[Animal|animals]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Microbiologists often use the terms "amoeboid" and "amoeba" interchangeably for any organism that exhibits amoeboid movement. In older classification systems, most amoebae were placed in the [[Class (taxonomy)|class]] or [[Subphylum|subphylum]] Sarcodina, a grouping of [[Unicellular organism|single-celled organisms]] that possess pseudopods or move by protoplasmic flow. However, molecular phylogenetic studies have shown that Sarcodina is not a [[Monophyly|monophyletic]] group whose members share [[Common descent|common descent]]. Consequently, amoeboid organisms are no longer classified together in one group. The best known amoeboid protists are Chaos carolinense and Amoeba proteus, both of which have been widely cultivated and studied in classrooms and laboratories.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Amoebozoa",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Phylum of protozoans",
		tags = { "Organisms", "Other eukaryotes" },
		summary = '**Amoebozoa** is a major [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomic]] group containing about 2,400 described species of [[Amoeba|amoeboid]] [[Protist|protists]], often possessing blunt, fingerlike, lobose pseudopods and tubular [[Mitochondrion|mitochondrial]] cristae. In traditional classification schemes, Amoebozoa is usually ranked as a [[Phylum|phylum]] within either the [[Kingdom (taxonomy)|kingdom]] Protista or the kingdom [[Protozoa]]. In the classification favored by the International Society of Protistologists, it is retained as an unranked "supergroup" within Eukaryota. [[Molecular genetics|Molecular genetic]] analysis supports Amoebozoa as a [[Monophyly|monophyletic]] [[Clade|clade]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Modern studies of eukaryotic phylogenetic trees identify it as the sister group to Opisthokonta, another major clade which contains both [[Mushroom|fungi]] and [[Animal|animals]] as well as several other clades comprising some 300 species of unicellular eukaryotes. Amoebozoa and Opisthokonta are sometimes grouped together in a high-level [[Taxon|taxon]], named Amorphea. Amoebozoa includes many of the best-known amoeboid organisms, such as Chaos, Entamoeba, Pelomyxa and the genus Amoeba itself. Free-living species are common in both salt and freshwater as well as soil, moss and leaf litter. While the majority of amoebozoan species are unicellular, the group also includes several clades of slime molds, which have a macroscopic, multicellular stage of life during which individual amoeboid cells remain together after multiple cell division to form a macroscopic plasmodium or, in cellular slime molds, aggregate to form one.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Amoebozoa" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Amorphous solid",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Non-crystalline solid",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Non-crystalline solid" },
		summary = 'In [[Condensed matter physics|condensed matter physics]] and [[Materials science|materials science]], an **amorphous solid** (or **non-crystalline solid**) is a [[Solid|solid]] that lacks the long-range order that is a characteristic of a [[Crystal|crystal]]. The terms "[[Glass|glass]]" and "glassy solid" are sometimes used synonymously with amorphous solid; however, these terms refer specifically to amorphous materials that undergo a glass transition. Examples of amorphous solids include glasses, metallic glasses, and certain types of [[Plastic|plastics]] and [[Polymer|polymers]].',
	},
	{
		title = "Amount of substance",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ratio of the number of particles in a sample to Avogadro's constant",
		tags = { "Chemistry" },
		summary = "In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], the **amount of substance** (symbol n) in a given sample of [[Matter|matter]] is defined as a ratio between the number of elementary entities (N) and the Avogadro constant (NA). It is one of the seven base quantities of the [[International System of Units]], SI. The unit of amount of substance in the SI is the [[Mole (unit)|mole]] (symbol: **mol**), a [[SI base unit|base unit]]. The elementary entities are usually [[Molecule|molecules]], [[Atom|atoms]], [[Ion|ions]], or ion pairs of a specified kind. The particular [[Substance (chemistry)|substance]] sampled may be specified using a subscript or in parentheses, e.g., the amount of [[Sodium chloride|sodium chloride]] (NaCl) could be denoted as nNaCl or n(NaCl).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Sometimes, the amount of substance is referred to as the **chemical amount** or, informally, as the "**number of moles**" in a given sample of matter. The latter term is deprecated by the IUPAC because, for a substance X, the correct meaning of "number of moles" is n(X)/mol. The amount of substance in a sample can be calculated from measured quantities, such as [[Mass|mass]] or [[Volume|volume]], given the molar mass of the substance or the molar volume of an ideal gas at a given [[Temperature|temperature]] and [[Pressure|pressure]].',
			},
		},
		related = { "Phase diagram" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Dimension", "wikidata" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ampere",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "SI base unit of electric current",
		tags = { "Units of measurement", "Electromagnetism" },
		aliases = { "), often shortened to" },
		summary = "The **ampere** (symbol: **A**), often shortened to **amp**, is the unit of [[Electric current|electric current]] in the [[International System of Units]] (SI). One ampere is equal to 1 [[Coulomb|coulomb]] (C) moving past a point per second. It is named after [[France|French]] mathematician and physicist [[André-Marie Ampère]] (1775–1836), considered the father of [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]] along with [[Denmark|Danish]] physicist [[Hans Christian Ørsted]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Prior to the redefinition, the ampere was defined as the current passing through two parallel wires 1 [[Metre|metre]] apart that produces a magnetic force of 2×10^-7 [[Newton (unit)|newtons]] per metre. The earlier [[Centimetre–gram–second system of units|CGS system]] has two units of current, one structured similarly to the SI's and the other using [[Coulomb's law]] as a fundamental relationship, with the CGS unit of charge defined by measuring the force between two charged metal plates. The CGS unit of current is then defined as one unit of charge per second.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Symbol", "A" },
			{ "Dimension", "I" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Amplitude",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Measure of change in a periodic variable",
		tags = { "Physics", "Waves" },
		summary = "The **amplitude** of a [[Periodic function|periodic]] [[Variable (mathematics)|variable]] is a measure of its change in a single period (such as [[Frequency|time]] or [[Wavelength|spatial period]]). The amplitude of a non-periodic signal is its [[Magnitude (mathematics)|magnitude]] compared with a reference value. There are various definitions of amplitude (see below), which are all [[Function (mathematics)|functions]] of the magnitude of the differences between the variable's extreme values. In older texts, the phase of a periodic function is sometimes called the amplitude.",
	},
	{
		title = "Analytical chemistry",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of the separation, identification, and quantification of matter",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Analytical chemistry" },
		aliases = { "Chemical analysis" },
		summary = "**Analytical chemistry** (or **chemical analysis**) is the branch of [[Chemistry|chemistry]] concerned with the development and application of methods to identify the chemical composition of materials and quantify the amounts of components in mixtures. It focuses on methods to identify unknown compounds, possibly in a mixture or solution, and quantify a compound's presence in terms of [[Amount of substance|amount of substance]] (in any phase), [[Concentration|concentration]] (in aqueous or [[Solution (chemistry)|solution]] phase), percentage by mass or number of [[Mole (unit)|moles]] in a mixture of compounds (or partial pressure in the case of gas phase).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It encompasses both classical techniques (e.g. titration, gravimetric analysis) and modern instrumental approaches (e.g. [[Spectroscopy|spectroscopy]], [[Chromatography|chromatography]], [[Mass spectrometry|mass spectrometry]], [[Electrochemistry|electrochemical methods]]). Modern analytical chemistry is deeply intertwined with data analysis and chemometrics, and is increasingly shaped by trends such as automation, miniaturization, and real-time sensing, with applications across fields as diverse as [[Biochemistry|biochemistry]], [[Medicinal chemistry|medicinal chemistry]], forensic science, [[Archaeology|archaeology]], nutritional science, agricultural chemistry, [[Chemical synthesis|chemical synthesis]], [[Metallurgy|metallurgy]], [[Chemical engineering|chemical engineering]] and [[Materials science|materials science]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Anatomy",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of the structure of organisms",
		tags = { "Anatomy and morphology", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Anatomy** is the branch of [[Morphology (biology)|morphology]] concerned with the study of the internal and external structure of [[Organism|organisms]] and their parts. Anatomy is a branch of [[Natural science|natural science]] that deals with the structural organization of living things. It is an old science, having its beginnings in prehistoric times.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Anatomy is inherently tied to [[Developmental biology|developmental biology]], [[Embryology|embryology]], comparative anatomy, [[Evolutionary biology|evolutionary biology]], and [[Phylogenetic tree|phylogeny]], as these are the processes by which anatomy is generated, both over immediate and long-term timescales. Anatomy and [[Physiology|physiology]], which study the structure and function of organisms and their parts respectively, make a natural pair of related disciplines, and are often studied together. Human anatomy is one of the essential basic sciences that are applied in medicine, and is often studied alongside physiology. Anatomy is a complex and dynamic field that is constantly evolving as discoveries are made. In recent years, there has been a significant increase in the use of advanced imaging techniques, such as [[Magnetic resonance imaging|MRI]] and CT scans, which allow for more detailed and accurate visualizations of the body's structures.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Angular momentum",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Conserved physical quantity; rotational analogue of linear momentum",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Classical mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Moment of momentum", "Rotational momentum" },
		summary = "**Angular momentum** (sometimes called **moment of momentum** or **rotational momentum**) is the rotational analog of [[Momentum|linear momentum]]. It is an important [[Physical quantity|physical quantity]] because it is a [[Conservation law|conserved quantity]] – the total angular momentum of an isolated system remains constant. Angular momentum has both a [[Direction (geometry)|direction]] and a magnitude, and both are conserved. Bicycles and motorcycles, flying discs, rifled bullets, and [[Gyroscope|gyroscopes]] owe their useful properties to conservation of angular momentum. Conservation of angular momentum is also why [[Hurricane|hurricanes]] form spirals and [[Neutron star|neutron stars]] have high rotational rates. In general, conservation limits the possible motion of a system, but it does not uniquely determine it.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The three-dimensional angular momentum for a point particle is classically represented as a pseudovector **r** × **p**, the [[Cross product|cross product]] of the particle's position vector **r** (relative to some origin) and its momentum vector; the latter is in [[Newton's laws of motion|Newtonian mechanics]]. Unlike linear momentum, angular momentum depends on where this origin is chosen, since the particle's position is measured from it. Angular momentum is an extensive quantity; that is, the total angular momentum of any composite system is the sum of the angular momenta of its constituent parts. For a [[Continuum mechanics|continuous]] rigid body or a [[Fluid|fluid]], the total angular momentum is the volume integral of angular momentum density (angular momentum per unit volume in the limit as volume shrinks to zero) over the entire body. Similar to conservation of linear momentum in the absence of external forces, angular momentum is conserved if there is no external [[Torque|torque]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Dimension", "wikidata" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Animal coloration",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "General appearance of an animal",
		tags = { "Zoology" },
		summary = "**Animal coloration** is the general appearance of an animal resulting from the reflection or emission of [[Light|light]] from its surfaces. Some animals are brightly coloured, while others are hard to see. In some species, such as the [[Peafowl|peafowl]], the male has strong patterns, conspicuous colours and is iridescent, while the female is far less visible.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are several separate reasons why animals have evolved colours. [[Camouflage]] enables an animal to remain hidden from view. Some animals use flashes of colour to divert attacks by startling predators. Zebras may possibly use motion dazzle, confusing a predator's attack by moving a bold pattern rapidly. Some animals are coloured for physical protection, with pigments in the skin to protect against sunburn, while some frogs can lighten or darken their skin for temperature regulation. Finally, animals can be coloured incidentally. Animals coloured in these ways can have striking natural patterns. Animals produce colour in both direct and indirect ways. Direct production occurs through the presence of visible coloured cells known as [[Pigment|pigment]] which are particles of coloured material such as freckles.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Animal locomotion",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Self-propulsion by an animal",
		tags = { "Zoology", "Ethology" },
		summary = "In [[Ethology|ethology]], **animal locomotion** is any of a variety of methods that [[Animal|animals]] use to move from one place to another. Some modes of locomotion are (initially) self-propelled, e.g., [[Running|running]], swimming, [[Jumping|jumping]], [[Flight|flying]], hopping, soaring and [[Gliding flight|gliding]]. Many animal species depend on their environment for transportation, a type of mobility called passive locomotion, e.g., sailing (some [[Jellyfish|jellyfish]]), kiting ([[Spider|spiders]]), rolling (some [[Beetle|beetles]] and spiders) or riding other animals (phoresis).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Animals move for a variety of reasons, such as to [[Foraging|find food]], a mate, a suitable microhabitat, or to escape predators. For many animals, the ability to move is essential for survival and, as a result, [[Evolution|natural selection]] has shaped the locomotion methods and mechanisms used by moving organisms. For example, [[Animal migration|migratory animals]] that travel vast distances (such as the Arctic tern) typically have a locomotion mechanism that costs very little energy per unit distance, whereas non-migratory animals that must frequently move quickly to escape predators are likely to have energetically costly, but very fast, locomotion. The anatomical structures that animals use for movement, including cilia, legs, [[Wing|wings]], arms, fins, or [[Tail|tails]] are sometimes referred to as locomotory organs or locomotory structures.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Animal migration",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Periodic large-scale movement of animals, usually seasonal",
		tags = { "Zoology", "Ethology" },
		summary = "**Animal migration** is the relatively long-distance movement of individual [[Animal|animals]], usually on a seasonal basis. It is the most common form of migration in ecology. It is found in all major animal groups, including [[Bird migration|birds]], mammals, [[Fish migration|fish]], reptiles, amphibians, insects, and crustaceans. The cause of migration may be local climate, local availability of food, the season of the year or for mating.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"To be counted as a true migration, and not just a local dispersal or irruption, the movement of the animals should be an annual or seasonal occurrence, or a major habitat change as part of their life. An annual event could include [[Northern Hemisphere]] birds migrating south for the winter, or [[Wildebeest|wildebeest]] migrating annually for seasonal grazing. A major habitat change could include young Atlantic salmon or sea lamprey leaving the river of their birth when they have reached a few inches in size. Some traditional forms of [[Human migration|human migration]] fit this pattern. Migrations can be studied using traditional identification tags such as bird rings, or tracked directly with electronic tracking devices. Before animal migration was understood, folklore explanations were formulated for the appearance and disappearance of some species, such as that barnacle geese grew from goose barnacles.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Antimatter",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Material composed of antiparticles",
		tags = { "Particle physics", "Antimatter" },
		summary = 'In [[Modern physics|modern physics]], **antimatter** is defined as [[Matter|matter]] composed of the [[Antiparticle|antiparticles]] (or "partners") of the corresponding [[Subatomic particle|particles]] in "ordinary" matter, and can be thought of as matter with reversed charges and parity, or going backward in time (see CPT symmetry). Antimatter occurs in natural processes like [[Cosmic ray|cosmic ray]] collisions and some types of [[Radioactivity|radioactive decay]]. Antiparticles can also be generated at [[Particle accelerator|particle accelerators]], but only a tiny fraction of these have successfully been bound together in experiments to form antiatoms. Total artificial production has been only a few nanograms.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"No macroscopic amount of antimatter has ever been assembled due to the extreme cost and difficulty of production and handling. Nonetheless, antimatter is an essential component of widely available applications related to [[Beta decay|beta decay]], such as positron emission tomography, radiation therapy, and industrial imaging. In theory, a particle and its antiparticle (for example, a [[Proton|proton]] and an [[Antiproton|antiproton]]) have the same [[Mass|mass]], but opposite [[Electric charge|electric charge]], and other differences in quantum numbers. A collision between any particle and its anti-particle partner leads to their mutual annihilation, giving rise to different proportions of intense [[Photon|photons]] ([[Gamma ray|gamma rays]]), [[Neutrino|neutrinos]], and sometimes less-massive particle–antiparticle pairs. The majority of the total energy of annihilation emerges in the form of [[Ionizing radiation|ionizing radiation]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Antimony",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Sb" },
		summary = "**Antimony** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]] with the [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Sb** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 51. A lustrous grey metal or [[Metalloid|metalloid]], it occurs in nature mainly in the form of the sulfide mineral stibnite (Sb2S3). Antimony compounds have been known since [[Ancient history|ancient history]] and were powdered for use as cosmetics (often known by the Arabic name kohl) and as medicine.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"China is the largest producer of antimony and its compounds, with most production coming from the Xikuangshan Mine in Hunan. The industrial methods for refining antimony from stibnite are roasting followed by reduction with carbon, or direct reduction of stibnite with iron. The most common applications for metallic antimony are in [[Alloy|alloys]] with [[Lead|lead]] and [[Tin|tin]], which have improved properties for solders, bullets, and plain bearings. It improves the rigidity of lead-alloy plates in lead–acid batteries. Antimony trioxide is a prominent additive for [[Halogen|halogen]]-containing flame retardants. Antimony is used as a [[Dopant|dopant]] in [[Semiconductor device|semiconductor devices]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Antiparticle",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Particle with opposite charges",
		tags = { "Particle physics", "Antimatter" },
		summary = 'In [[Particle physics|particle physics]], every type of [[Particle|particle]] of "ordinary" matter (as opposed to [[Antimatter|antimatter]]) is associated with an **antiparticle** with the same [[Mass|mass]] but with opposite physical charges (such as [[Electric charge|electric charge]]). For example, the antiparticle of the [[Electron|electron]] is the [[Positron|positron]] (also known as an antielectron). While the electron has a negative electric charge, the positron has a positive electric charge, and is produced naturally in certain types of [[Radioactivity|radioactive decay]]. The opposite is also true: the antiparticle of the positron is the electron.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Some particles, such as the [[Photon|photon]], are their own antiparticle. Otherwise, for each pair of antiparticle partners, one is designated as the normal particle (the one that occurs in matter usually interacted with in daily life). The other (usually given the prefix "anti-") is designated the antiparticle. Particle–antiparticle pairs can annihilate each other, producing photons; since the charges of the particle and antiparticle are opposite, total charge is conserved. For example, the positrons produced in natural radioactive decay quickly annihilate themselves with electrons, producing pairs of [[Gamma ray|gamma rays]], a process exploited in positron emission tomography. The laws of nature are very nearly symmetrical with respect to particles and antiparticles. For example, an [[Antiproton|antiproton]] and a [[Positron|positron]] can form an antihydrogen [[Atom|atom]], which is believed to have the same properties as a [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] atom.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Apicomplexa",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Organisms", "Other eukaryotes" },
		summary = "They are a kind of protozoan. Most possess a unique form of [[Organelle|organelle]] structure that comprises a type of non-photosynthetic [[Plastid|plastid]] called an apicoplast—with an [[Cell membrane|apical complex membrane]]. The organelle's apical shape is an adaptation that the apicomplexan applies in penetrating a host cell.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Apicomplexa are unicellular and spore-forming. Most are obligate endoparasites of animals, except Nephromyces, a [[Symbiosis|symbiont]] in marine animals, originally classified as a chytrid fungus. Motile structures such as [[Flagellum|flagella]] or pseudopods are present only in certain [[Gamete|gamete]] stages. The Apicomplexa are a diverse group that includes organisms such as the coccidia, gregarines, piroplasms, haemogregarines, and plasmodia. The name Apicomplexa derives from two [[Latin]] words—apex (top) and complexus (infolds)—for the set of [[Organelle|organelles]] in the sporozoite. Most of the Apicomplexa can use gliding motility, that uses adhesions and small static myosin motors. The other main lines of this obsolete grouping were the Ascetosporea (a group of [[Rhizaria]]), the Myxozoa (highly derived cnidarian [[Animal|animals]]), and the Microsporidia (derived from [[Mushroom|fungi]]).",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Apicomplexa" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Applied science",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Practical application of scientific knowledge",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Applied science** is the application of the [[Scientific method|scientific method]] and scientific knowledge to attain practical goals. It includes a broad range of disciplines, such as [[Engineering|engineering]] and [[Medicine|medicine]]. Applied science is often contrasted with basic science, which is focused on advancing scientific theories and laws that explain and predict natural or other phenomena.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are applied natural sciences, as well as applied formal and social sciences. Applied science examples include genetic epidemiology which applies [[Statistics|statistics]] and [[Probability theory|probability theory]], and applied psychology, including criminology.",
