	{
		title = "Atomic nucleus",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Core of an atom composed of nucleons",
		tags = { "Physics", "Nuclear physics" },
		aliases = { "Atomic nuclei" },
		summary = "The **atomic nucleus** is the small, dense region consisting of [[Proton|protons]] and [[Neutron|neutrons]] at the center of an [[Atom|atom]], discovered in 1911 by [[Ernest Rutherford]] at the University of Manchester based on the 1909 [[Rutherford scattering experiments|Geiger–Marsden gold foil experiment]]. After the discovery of the neutron in 1932, models for a nucleus composed of protons and neutrons were quickly developed by Dmitri Ivanenko and [[Werner Heisenberg]]. An atom is composed of a positively charged nucleus, with a cloud of negatively charged [[Electron|electrons]] surrounding it, bound together by electrostatic force. Almost all of the [[Mass|mass]] of an atom is located in the nucleus, with a very small contribution from the electron cloud.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Protons and neutrons are bound together to form a nucleus by the nuclear force. The diameter of the nucleus is in the range of 1.70 fm (1.70×10^-15 m) for [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] (the diameter of a single proton) to about 11.7 fm for [[Uranium|uranium]]. These dimensions are much smaller than the diameter of the atom itself (nucleus + electron cloud), by a factor of about 27,000 (uranium's atomic radius is about 156 pm (156×10^-12 m)) to about 60,000 (hydrogen's atomic radius is about 52.92 pm). The branch of physics involved with the study and understanding of the atomic nucleus, including its composition and the forces that bind it together, is called [[Nuclear physics|nuclear physics]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Atomic number",
		category = "Science",
