		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Field of physics that studies the atom",
		tags = { "Atomic, molecular and optical physics", "Atomic physics" },
		summary = "**Atomic physics** is the field of [[Physics|physics]] that studies [[Atom|atoms]] as an isolated system of [[Electron|electrons]] and an [[Atomic nucleus|atomic nucleus]]. Atomic physics typically refers to the study of atomic structure and the interaction between atoms. It is primarily concerned with [[Electron configuration|the way in which electrons are arranged around the nucleus]] and the processes by which these arrangements change. This comprises [[Ion|ions]], neutral atoms and, unless otherwise stated, it can be assumed that the term atom includes ions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Physicists distinguish between atomic physics—which deals with the atom as a system consisting of a nucleus and electrons—and [[Nuclear physics|nuclear physics]], which studies [[Nuclear reaction|nuclear reactions]] and special properties of atomic nuclei. As with many scientific fields, strict delineation can be highly contrived and atomic physics is often considered in the wider context of atomic, molecular, and optical physics. As a result, atomic physics research groups are usually classified as such.",
