		tags = { "Atmosphere", "Atmospheric optics" },
		summary = "These collisions excite [[Oxygen|oxygen]] and [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]], which then emit light of different colours such as green, red, and purple. When observed in [[Polar regions|high-latitude regions]] they are called **polar lights** and **aurora polaris**. In the [[Arctic]] they are called the **northern lights** or **aurora borealis**; in the [[Antarctic]], the term **southern lights** or **aurora australis** is used. Auroras display dynamic patterns of radiant light that appear as curtains, rays, spirals or dynamic flickers covering the entire sky.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Auroras are the result of disturbances in the Earth's [[Magnetosphere|magnetosphere]] caused by enhanced speeds of [[Solar wind|solar wind]] from coronal holes and coronal mass ejections. These disturbances alter the trajectories of [[Charged particle|charged particles]] in the magnetospheric [[Plasma (physics)|plasma]]. These particles, mainly [[Electron|electrons]] and [[Proton|protons]], precipitate into the upper [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]] (thermosphere/exosphere). The resulting [[Ionization|ionization]] and excitation of atmospheric constituents emit light of varying colour and complexity. The form of the aurora, occurring within bands around both polar regions, is also dependent on the amount of [[Acceleration|acceleration]] imparted to the precipitating particles. Other [[Planet|planets]] in the [[Solar System]], [[Brown dwarf|brown dwarfs]], [[Comet|comets]], and some [[Natural satellite|natural satellites]] also host auroras.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Mirage" },
