	{
		title = "Barium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Ba" },
		summary = "**Barium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Ba** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 56. It is the fifth element in group 2 and is a soft, silvery [[Alkaline earth metal|alkaline earth metal]]. Because of its high chemical [[Reactivity (chemistry)|reactivity]], barium is never found in nature as a [[Free element|free element]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The most common minerals of barium are barite (barium sulfate, BaSO4) and witherite (barium carbonate, BaCO3). The name barium originates from the alchemical derivative \"baryta\" from [[Greek language|Greek]] (barys), meaning 'heavy'. Baric is the adjectival form of barium. Barium was identified as a new element in 1772, but not reduced to a metal until 1808 with the advent of [[Electrolysis|electrolysis]]. Barium has few industrial applications. Historically, it was used as a getter for [[Vacuum tube|vacuum tubes]] and in oxide form as the emissive coating on indirectly heated cathodes. It is a component of YBCO (high-temperature superconductors) and electroceramics, and is added to steel and cast iron to reduce the size of carbon grains within the microstructure. Barium compounds are added to fireworks to impart a green color. Barium sulfate is used as an insoluble additive to [[Oil well|oil well]] drilling fluid.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Baryon",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Hadron (subatomic particle) that is composed of three quarks",
		tags = { "Subatomic particles", "Fermions" },
		summary = "In [[Particle physics|particle physics]], a **baryon** is a type of composite [[Subatomic particle|subatomic particle]] that contains an odd number of valence quarks, conventionally three. [[Proton|Protons]] and [[Neutron|neutrons]] are examples of baryons; because baryons are composed of [[Quark|quarks]], they belong to the [[Hadron|hadron]] family of particles. Baryons are also classified as [[Fermion|fermions]] because they have half-integer [[Spin (physics)|spin]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The name "baryon", introduced by Abraham Pais, comes from the [[Ancient Greek|Greek]] word for "heavy", because, at the time of their naming, most known elementary particles had lower masses than the baryons. Each baryon has a corresponding [[Antiparticle|antiparticle]] (antibaryon) where their corresponding [[Quark|antiquarks]] replace quarks. For example, a [[Proton|proton]] is made of two [[Up quark|up quarks]] and one [[Down quark|down quark]]; and its corresponding antiparticle, the [[Antiproton|antiproton]], is made of two up antiquarks and one down antiquark. Baryons participate in the residual strong force, which is [[Force carrier|mediated]] by particles known as [[Meson|mesons]]. The most familiar baryons are [[Proton|protons]] and [[Neutron|neutrons]]. These particles make up most of the mass of the visible [[Matter|matter]] in the [[Universe|universe]] and compose the [[Atomic nucleus|nucleus]] of every [[Atom|atom]] ([[Electron|electrons]], the other major component of the atom, are members of a different family of particles called [[Lepton|leptons]]; leptons do not interact via the strong force).',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Basalt",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Magnesium- and iron-rich extrusive igneous rock",
		tags = { "Geology", "Petrology" },
		summary = "**Basalt**; ) is an aphanitic (fine-grained) extrusive [[Igneous rock|igneous rock]] formed from the rapid cooling of low-[[Viscosity|viscosity]] [[Lava|lava]] rich in [[Magnesium|magnesium]] and [[Iron|iron]] (mafic lava) exposed at or very near the [[Planetary surface|surface]] of a [[Terrestrial planet|rocky planet]] or [[Natural satellite|moon]]. More than 90% of all volcanic rock on Earth is basalt. Rapid-cooling, fine-grained basalt has the same chemical composition and mineralogy as slow-cooling, coarse-grained gabbro. The eruption of basalt lava is observed by geologists at about 20 volcanoes per year. Basalt is also an important rock type on other planetary bodies in the [[Solar System]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"For example, the bulk of the plains of Venus, which cover ~80% of the surface, are basaltic; the lunar maria are plains of flood-basaltic lava flows; and basalt is a common rock on the surface of [[Mars]]. Molten basalt lava has a low viscosity due to its relatively low [[Silicon dioxide|silica]] content (between 45% and 52%), resulting in rapidly moving lava flows that can spread over great areas before cooling and solidifying. Flood basalts are thick sequences of many such flows that can cover hundreds of thousands of square kilometres and constitute the most voluminous of all volcanic formations. Basaltic [[Magma|magmas]] within Earth are thought to originate from the upper mantle. The chemistry of basalts thus provides clues to processes deep in Earth's interior.",
			},
