		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Inorganic compounds" },
		aliases = { "Base" },
		summary = 'In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], there are three definitions in common use of the word "**base**": Arrhenius bases, Brønsted bases, and Lewis bases. All definitions agree that bases are substances that react with [[Acid|acids]], as originally proposed by G.-F. Rouelle in the mid-18th century.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In 1884, [[Svante Arrhenius]] proposed that a base is a substance which dissociates in [[Aqueous solution|aqueous solution]] to form [[Hydroxide|hydroxide]] ions OH−. These ions can react with hydrogen ions (H+ according to Arrhenius) from the dissociation of acids to form water in an [[Acid–base reaction|acid–base reaction]]. A base was therefore a metal hydroxide such as NaOH or Ca(OH)2. Such aqueous hydroxide solutions were also described by certain characteristic properties. They are slippery to the touch, can taste [[Taste|bitter]], and change the color of pH indicators (e.g., turn red litmus paper blue). In water, by altering the autoionization [[Chemical equilibrium|equilibrium]], bases yield solutions in which the hydrogen ion activity is lower than it is in pure water, i.e., the water has a [[PH|pH]] higher than 7.0 at standard conditions. A soluble base is called an alkali if it contains and releases OH− ions quantitatively.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Organophosphorus chemistry" },
	},
