	{
		title = "Bay",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Recessed, coastal body of water connected to an ocean or lake",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Oceanic and coastal landforms" },
		aliases = { "Gulf" },
		summary = "A **bay** is a recessed, [[Coast|coastal]] body of water that directly connects to a larger main body of water, such as an [[Ocean|ocean]], a [[Lake|lake]], or another bay. A large bay is usually called a gulf, [[Ocean|sea]], sound, or bight. A cove is a small, circular bay with a narrow entrance. A [[Fjord|fjord]] is an elongated bay formed by glacial action.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A bay can be the [[Estuary|estuary]] of a river, such as the Chesapeake Bay, an estuary of the Susquehanna River. Bays may also be nested within each other; for example, Fig Tree Bay in [[Cyprus]] or James Bay is an arm of [[Hudson Bay]] in northeastern Canada. Some bays are large enough to have varied marine geology, such as the [[Bay of Bengal]] (2,600,000 km²) and Hudson Bay (1,230,000 km²). The land surrounding a bay often reduces the strength of [[Wind|winds]] and blocks [[Wave|waves]]. Bays may have as wide a variety of shoreline characteristics as other shorelines. In some cases, bays have beaches, which "are usually characterized by a steep upper foreshore with a broad, flat fronting terrace". Bays were significant in the history of [[Human settlement|human settlement]] because they provided easy access to marine resources like [[Fishery|fisheries]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Beach",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Area of loose particles at the edge of a body of water",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Fluvial landforms" },
		summary = "A **beach** is a [[Landform|landform]] alongside a body of water which consists of loose particles. The particles composing a beach are typically made from [[Rock (geology)|rock]], such as [[Sand|sand]], [[Gravel|gravel]], shingle, pebbles, etc., or biological sources, such as mollusc shells or coralline algae. Sediments settle in different densities and structures, depending on the local [[Wave|wave]] action and [[Weather|weather]], creating different textures, colors and gradients or layers of material.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Though some beaches form on inland freshwater locations such as [[Lake|lakes]] and [[River|rivers]], most beaches are in coastal areas where [[Wind wave|wave]] or [[Ocean current|current]] action [[Deposition (geology)|deposits]] and reworks sediments. Erosion and changing of beach geologies happens through natural processes, like wave action and [[Extreme weather|extreme weather events]]. Where wind conditions are correct, beaches can be backed by coastal dunes which offer protection and regeneration for the beach. However, these natural forces have become more extreme [[Effects of climate change|due to climate change]], permanently altering beaches at very rapid rates. Some estimates describe as much as 50 percent of the earth's sandy beaches disappearing by 2100 due to climate-change driven sea level rise. Sandy beaches occupy about one third of global coastlines. These beaches are popular for [[Recreation|recreation]], playing important economic and cultural roles—often driving local tourism industries.",
			},
		},
	},
