		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Hydrocarbons" },
		summary = "**Benzene** is an [[Organic compound|organic]] [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]] with the [[Chemical formula|molecular formula]] C6H6. The benzene [[Molecule|molecule]] is composed of six [[Carbon|carbon]] atoms joined in a planar hexagonal ring with one [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] atom attached to each. As it contains only [[Carbon|carbon]] and hydrogen atoms, benzene is a [[Hydrocarbon|hydrocarbon]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Benzene is a natural constituent of [[Petroleum|petroleum]] and is one of the elementary [[Petrochemical|petrochemicals]]. Because of the cyclic continuous pi bonds between the carbon atoms and satisfying the rules of aromaticity and Hückel's rule, benzene is classed as an aromatic hydrocarbon. Benzene is a colorless and highly [[Combustibility and flammability|flammable]] liquid with a sweet smell, and is partially responsible for the aroma of [[Gasoline|gasoline]]. It is used primarily as a [[Precursor (chemistry)|precursor]] to the manufacture of chemicals with more complex structures, such as ethylbenzene and cumene, of which billions of kilograms are produced annually. Although benzene is a major [[Chemical industry|industrial chemical]], it finds limited use in consumer items because of its toxicity. Benzene is a volatile organic compound. Benzene is classified as a [[Carcinogen|carcinogen]].",
