	{
		title = "Beta particle",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ionizing radiation",
		tags = { "Physics", "Nuclear physics" },
		aliases = { "Beta ray", "Beta radiation" },
		summary = "A **beta particle**, also called **beta ray** or **beta radiation**, is a high-energy, high-speed [[Electron|electron]] or [[Positron|positron]] emitted by the [[Radioactivity|radioactive decay]] of an [[Atomic nucleus|atomic nucleus]], known as [[Beta decay|beta decay]]. There are two forms of beta decay, − decay and + decay, which produce electrons and positrons, respectively.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Beta particles with an energy of 0.5 MeV have a range of about one metre in the air, and can't pass through clothing or thin metal sheets; the distance is dependent on the particle's energy and the air's [[Density|density]] and composition. Beta particles are a type of [[Ionizing radiation|ionizing radiation]], and for radiation protection purposes, they are regarded as being more ionizing than [[Gamma ray|gamma rays]], but less ionizing than [[Alpha particle|alpha particles]]. The higher the ionising effect, the greater the damage to living tissue, but also the lower the penetrating power of the radiation through matter. The ionising effect of a beta particle is caused by the particle knocking electrons from shells by either the electrostatic force or energy transfer from the beta particle to the electron by collision. They produce roughly 100 ion pairs per cm in air, this means an ion plus a released electron.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Binding energy",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Energy required to separate particles",
		tags = { "Atomic, molecular and optical physics", "Atomic physics" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]] and [[Chemistry|chemistry]], **binding energy** is the smallest amount of [[Energy|energy]] required to remove a [[Particle|particle]] from a system of particles or to disassemble a system of particles into individual parts. In the former meaning the term is predominantly used in [[Condensed matter physics|condensed matter physics]], [[Atomic physics|atomic physics]], and chemistry, whereas in [[Nuclear physics|nuclear physics]] the term separation energy is used. A bound system is typically at a lower [[Energy level|energy level]] than its unbound constituents. According to relativity theory, a E decrease in the total energy of a system is accompanied by a decrease m in the total [[Mass|mass]], where.",
	},
	{
		title = "Binomial nomenclature",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Species naming system",
		tags = { "Genetics and taxonomy" },
		aliases = { "Binary nomenclature" },
		summary = 'In [[Taxonomy (biology)|taxonomy]], **binomial nomenclature** ("two-term naming system"), also called **binary nomenclature**, is a formal system of naming [[Species|species]] of living things by giving each a name composed of two parts, both of which use Latin grammatical forms, although they can be based on words from other languages. Such a name is called a **binomial name** (often shortened to just "binomial"), a **binomen**, **binominal name**, or a **scientific name**; more informally, it is also called a **Latin name**. In the International Code of Zoological Nomenclature (ICZN), the system is also called **binominal nomenclature**, with an "n" before the "al" in "binominal", which is not a typographic error, meaning "two-name naming system".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The first part of the name – the generic name – identifies the [[Genus|genus]] to which the species belongs, whereas the second part – the specific name or specific epithet – distinguishes the species within the genus. For example, modern humans belong to the genus [[Homo]] and within this genus to the species [[Human|Homo sapiens]]. The formal introduction of this system of naming species is credited to [[Carl Linnaeus]], effectively beginning with his work Species Plantarum in 1753. But as early as 1622, Gaspard Bauhin introduced in his book Pinax theatri botanici (English, Illustrated Exposition of Plants) containing many names of genera that were later adopted by Linnaeus. Binomial nomenclature was introduced in order to provide succinct, relatively stable and verifiable names that could be used and understood internationally, unlike common names which are usually different in every language.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Biochemistry",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of chemical processes of living organisms",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		aliases = { "Biological chemistry" },
		summary = "**Biochemistry**, or **biological chemistry** (distinct from [[Chemical biology|chemical biology]]), is the study of chemical processes within and relating to living [[Organism|organisms]]. A sub-discipline of both [[Chemistry|chemistry]] and [[Biology|biology]], biochemistry may be divided into three fields: structural biology, enzymology, and [[Metabolism|metabolism]]. Over the last decades of the 20th century, biochemistry has become successful at explaining living processes through these three disciplines. Almost all areas of the life sciences are being uncovered and developed through biochemical methodology and research.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Biochemistry focuses on understanding the chemical basis that allows [[Biomolecule|biological molecules]] to give rise to the processes that occur within living [[Cell (biology)|cells]] and between cells, in turn relating greatly to the understanding of [[Tissue (biology)|tissues]] and [[Organ (biology)|organs]] as well as organism structure and function. Biochemistry is closely related to [[Molecular biology|molecular biology]], the study of the [[Molecule|molecular]] mechanisms of biological phenomena. Much of biochemistry deals with the structures, functions, and interactions of biological [[Macromolecule|macromolecules]] such as [[Protein|proteins]], [[Nucleic acid|nucleic acids]], [[Carbohydrate|carbohydrates]], and [[Lipid|lipids]]. They provide the structure of cells and perform many of the functions associated with life. The [[Chemistry|chemistry]] of the cell also depends upon the reactions of small [[Molecule|molecules]] and [[Ion|ions]]. These can be inorganic (for example, [[Water|water]] and [[Metal|metal]] ions) or [[Organic compound|organic]] (for example, the [[Amino acid|amino acids]], which are used to synthesize proteins).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Biodiversity",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Variety and variability of life forms",
		tags = { "Ecology" },
		summary = "**Biodiversity** is the variability of [[Life|life on Earth]]. It can be measured on various levels, for example, genetic variability, [[Species diversity|species diversity]], ecosystem diversity, and [[Phylogenetics|phylogenetic]] diversity. Diversity is not distributed evenly on [[Earth]] — it is greater in the [[Tropics|tropics]] as a result of the warm [[Climate|climate]] and high primary productivity in the region near the [[Equator|equator]]. [[Tropical forest]] [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]] cover less than one-fifth of Earth's terrestrial area and contain about 50% of the world's species. There are latitudinal gradients in species diversity for both marine and terrestrial [[Taxon|taxa]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Since [[Abiogenesis|life began on Earth]], six major mass extinctions and several minor events have led to large and sudden drops in biodiversity. The [[Phanerozoic]] aeon (the last 540 million years) marked a rapid growth in biodiversity via the [[Cambrian explosion]]. In this period, the majority of [[Multicellular organism|multicellular]] [[Phylum|phyla]] first appeared. The next 400 million years included repeated, massive biodiversity losses. Those events have been classified as mass extinction events. In the [[Carboniferous]], rainforest collapse may have led to a great loss of [[Plant|plant]] and [[Animal|animal]] life. The [[Permian–Triassic extinction event]], 251 million years ago, was the worst; vertebrate recovery took 30 million years. [[Human impact on the environment|Human activities]] have led to an ongoing [[Biodiversity loss|biodiversity loss]] and an accompanying loss of genetic diversity. This process is often referred to as Holocene extinction, or the sixth mass extinction.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Biogeochemical cycle",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical transfer pathway between Earth's biological and non-biological parts",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Cycle of matter" },
		summary = "A **biogeochemical cycle**, or more generally a **cycle of matter**, is the movement and transformation of [[Chemical element|chemical elements]] and compounds between living organisms, the atmosphere, and the Earth's crust. Major biogeochemical cycles include the [[Carbon cycle|carbon cycle]], the nitrogen cycle and the [[Water cycle|water cycle]]. In each cycle, the chemical element or molecule is transformed and cycled by living organisms and through various geological forms and reservoirs, including the atmosphere, the soil and the oceans. It can be thought of as the pathway by which a [[Substance (chemistry)|chemical substance]] cycles (is turned over or moves through) the biotic compartment and the abiotic compartments of [[Earth]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The biotic compartment is the [[Biosphere|biosphere]] and the abiotic compartments are the [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]], [[Lithosphere|lithosphere]] and [[Hydrosphere|hydrosphere]]. For example, in the carbon cycle, atmospheric carbon dioxide is absorbed by plants through [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]], which converts it into [[Organic compound|organic compounds]] that are used by organisms for energy and growth. [[Carbon]] is then released back into the atmosphere through respiration and [[Decomposition|decomposition]]. Additionally, carbon is stored in [[Fossil fuel|fossil fuels]] and is released into the atmosphere through human activities such as burning fossil fuels. In the nitrogen cycle, atmospheric nitrogen gas is converted by plants into usable forms such as [[Ammonia|ammonia]] and [[Nitrate|nitrates]] through the process of [[Nitrogen fixation|nitrogen fixation]]. These compounds can be used by other organisms, and nitrogen is returned to the atmosphere through denitrification and other processes.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Biological pigment",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Substances produced by living organisms",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		aliases = { "Biochrome" },
		summary = "A **biological pigment**, also known simply as a **pigment** or **biochrome**, is a substance produced by living organisms that have a [[Color|color]] resulting from selective [[Absorption (electromagnetic radiation)|color absorption]]. Biological pigments include **plant pigments** and **flower pigments**. Many biological structures, such as [[Skin|skin]], [[Eye|eyes]], feathers, [[Fur|fur]] and [[Hair|hair]] contain pigments such as [[Melanin|melanin]] in specialized cells called chromatophores. In some species, pigments accrue over very long periods during an individual's lifespan.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Pigment color differs from structural color in that it is the same for all viewing angles, whereas structural color is the result of selective [[Reflection (physics)|reflection]] or [[Iridescence|iridescence]], usually because of multilayer structures. For example, [[Butterfly|butterfly]] wings typically contain structural color, although many butterflies have cells that contain pigment as well.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Biome",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Biogeographical unit with a particular biological community",
		tags = { "Ecology" },
		summary = "A **biome** is a distinct geographical region with specific [[Climate|climate]], [[Vegetation|vegetation]], animal life, and an [[Ecosystem|ecosystem]]. It consists of a biological community that has formed in response to its physical environment and regional [[Climate|climate]]. In 1935, Arthur Tansley added the climatic and soil aspects to the idea, calling it [[Ecosystem|ecosystem]]. The International Biological Program (1964–74) projects popularized the concept of biome.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'However, in some contexts, the term biome is used in a different manner. In German literature, particularly in the Walter terminology, the term is used similarly as biotope (a concrete geographical unit), while the biome definition used in this article is used as an international, non-regional, terminology—irrespectively of the continent in which an area is present, it takes the same biome name—and corresponds to his "zonobiome", "orobiome" and "pedobiome" (biomes determined by climate zone, [[Altitude|altitude]], or [[Soil|soil]]).',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Biomechanics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of the mechanics of biological systems",
		tags = { "Basics" },
		summary = "**Biomechanics** is the study of the structure, function and motion of the mechanical aspects of biological systems using the methods of [[Mechanics|mechanics]]. It operates at any level, from whole [[Organism|organisms]] to [[Organ (biology)|organs]], [[Cell (biology)|cells]] and cell organelles, and even proteins. Biomechanics is a branch of [[Biophysics|biophysics]].",
