		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		aliases = { "Biological molecule" },
		summary = "A **biomolecule** or **biological molecule** is loosely defined as a [[Molecule|molecule]] produced by a living [[Organism|organism]] and essential to one or more typically [[Biological process|biological processes]]. Biomolecules include large [[Macromolecule|macromolecules]] such as [[Protein|proteins]], [[Carbohydrate|carbohydrates]], [[Lipid|lipids]], and [[Nucleic acid|nucleic acids]], as well as small molecules such as vitamins and [[Hormone|hormones]]. A general name for this class of material is biological materials. Biomolecules are an important element of living organisms. They are often endogenous, i.e. produced within the organism, but organisms usually also need exogenous biomolecules, for example certain [[Nutrient|nutrients]], to survive.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Biomolecules and their reactions are studied in [[Biology|biology]] and its subfields of [[Biochemistry|biochemistry]] and [[Molecular biology|molecular biology]]. Most biomolecules are [[Organic compound|organic compounds]], and just four [[Chemical element|elements]]—[[Oxygen|oxygen]], [[Carbon|carbon]], [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]], and [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]]—make up 96% of the [[Human body|human body]]\'s mass. But many other elements, such as the various biometals, are also present in small amounts. The uniformity of both specific types of molecules (the biomolecules) and of certain [[Metabolic pathway|metabolic pathways]] are invariant features among the wide diversity of life forms; thus these biomolecules and metabolic pathways are referred to as "biochemical universals" or "theory of material unity of the living beings", a unifying concept in biology, along with cell theory and evolution theory.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Biosphere",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Global sum of all ecosystems on Earth",
		tags = { "Ecology" },
		aliases = { "Ecosphere" },
		summary = "The **biosphere**, also called the **ecosphere**, is the worldwide sum of all [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]]. It can also be termed the zone of [[Life|life]] on the [[Earth]]. The biosphere (which is technically a spherical shell) is virtually a closed system with regard to matter, with minimal inputs and outputs. Regarding [[Energy|energy]], it is an open system, with [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]] capturing [[Solar energy|solar energy]] at a rate of around 100 [[Watt|terawatts]]. By the most general biophysiological definition, the biosphere is the global [[Ecology|ecological]] system integrating all living beings and their relationships, including their interaction with the elements of the [[Lithosphere|lithosphere]], [[Cryosphere|cryosphere]], [[Hydrosphere|hydrosphere]], and [[Atmosphere of Earth|atmosphere]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The biosphere is postulated to have evolved, beginning with a process of biopoiesis (life created naturally from non-living matter, such as simple organic compounds) or biogenesis (life created from living matter), at least some 3.5 billion years ago. In a general sense, biospheres are any closed, self-regulating systems containing ecosystems. This includes artificial biospheres such as Biosphere 2 and BIOS-3, and potentially [[Astrobiology|ones on other planets or moons]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Bipedalism",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Terrestrial locomotion using two limbs",
		tags = { "Zoology", "Ethology" },
		summary = "**Bipedalism** is a form of terrestrial locomotion where an animal moves by means of its two rear (or lower) [[Limb (anatomy)|limbs]] or legs. An [[Animal|animal]] or [[Machine|machine]] that usually moves in a **bipedal** manner is known as a **biped**, meaning 'two feet'. Types of bipedal movement include [[Walking|walking]] or [[Running|running]] (a **bipedal gait**) and [[Jumping|hopping]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Several groups of modern species are habitual bipeds whose normal method of locomotion is two-legged. In the [[Triassic]] period some groups of [[Archosaur|archosaurs]], a group that includes [[Crocodile|crocodiles]] and [[Dinosaur|dinosaurs]], developed bipedalism; among the [[Dinosaur|dinosaurs]], all the early forms and many later groups were habitual or exclusive bipeds; the [[Bird|birds]] are members of a clade of exclusively bipedal dinosaurs, the theropods. Within [[Mammal|mammals]], habitual bipedalism has evolved multiple times, with the macropods, kangaroo rats and mice, springhare, hopping mice, [[Pangolin|pangolins]] and [[Hominini|hominin]] apes such as australopithecines, including [[Homo|humans]], as well as many other extinct groups evolving the trait independently. A larger number of modern species intermittently or briefly use a bipedal gait. Several [[Lizard|lizard]] species move bipedally when running, usually to escape from threats.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Bismuth",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Bi" },
		summary = "**Bismuth** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Bi** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 83. It is a post-transition metal and one of the pnictogens, with chemical properties resembling its lighter group 15 siblings [[Arsenic|arsenic]] and [[Antimony|antimony]]. Elemental bismuth occurs naturally, and its sulfide and [[Oxide|oxide]] forms are important commercial [[Ore|ores]]. The [[Free element|free element]] is 86% as dense as lead. It is a brittle metal with a silvery-white color when freshly produced. [[Passivation (chemistry)|Surface oxidation]] generally gives samples of the metal a somewhat rosy cast. Further oxidation under heat can give bismuth a vividly [[Iridescence|iridescent]] appearance due to thin-film interference.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Bismuth is the most diamagnetic element and of all the metals, it is among the most electrically resistive and least [[Thermal conductivity and resistivity|thermally conductive]] known. Bismuth was formerly understood to be the element with the highest atomic mass whose nuclei do not spontaneously decay, but in 2003, it was found to be very slightly radioactive. The metal\'s only primordial isotope, bismuth-209, undergoes [[Alpha decay|alpha decay]] with a [[Half-life|half-life]] roughly a billion times longer than the estimated [[Age of the universe|age of the universe]]. Bismuth metal has been known since ancient times. Before modern analytical methods, bismuth\'s metallurgical similarities to lead and [[Tin|tin]] often led it to be confused with those metals. The etymology of "bismuth" is uncertain. The name may come from mid-16th-century [[Neo-Latin|neo-Latin]] translations of the German words or, meaning "white mass", which were rendered as or.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Niobium" },
	},
	{
		title = "Bit",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Unit of information",
		tags = { "Quantity", "Data" },
		summary = "The **bit** is the most basic unit of information in [[Computing|computing]] and digital [[Communication|communication]]. The name is a portmanteau of **binary digit**. The bit represents a logical state with one of two possible values.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The relation between these values and the physical states of the underlying storage or device is a matter of convention, and different assignments may be used even within the same device or [[Computer program|program]]. It may be physically implemented with a two-state device. A contiguous group of binary digits is commonly called a bit string, a bit vector, or a single-dimensional (or multi-dimensional) bit array. A group of eight bits is called one [[Byte|byte]], but historically, the size of the byte is not strictly defined. Frequently, half, full, double and quadruple words consist of a number of bytes which is a low power of two. A string of four bits is usually a nibble. As a unit of information, the bit is also known as a shannon, named after Claude E. Shannon.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Black",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Darkest color",
		tags = { "Color", "Color terms" },
		summary = "**Black** is a [[Color|color]] that results from the absence or complete [[Absorption (electromagnetic radiation)|absorption]] of [[Visible spectrum|visible]] [[Light|light]]. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like [[White|white]] and [[Grey|grey]]. It is often used [[Symbol|symbolically]] or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the [[Age of Enlightenment]], and [[Night|night]] versus [[Daytime|day]]. Since the [[Middle Ages]], black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Black was one of the first colors used by artists in [[Neolithic]] cave paintings. It was used in ancient Egypt and Greece as the color of the underworld. In the [[Roman Empire]], it became the color of mourning, and over the centuries it was frequently associated with death, evil, witches, and [[Magic (supernatural)|magic]]. In the 14th century, it was worn by royalty, clergy, judges, and government officials in much of Europe. It became the color worn by English romantic poets, businessmen and statesmen in the 19th century, and a high fashion color in the 20th century. Black is the most common [[Ink|ink]] color used for printing books, newspapers and documents, as it provides the highest contrast with white paper and thus is the easiest color to read. Similarly, black text on a white screen is the most common format used on computer screens.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Source", "[[Web colors|HTML/CSS]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Black-body radiation",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Thermal electromagnetic radiation",
		tags = { "Physics", "Thermodynamics" },
		summary = "**Black-body radiation** is the thermal [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]] emitted from a body in [[Thermodynamic equilibrium|thermodynamic equilibrium]] with its environment. A black body is an idealized opaque and non-reflective body. The radiation emitted is a continuous spectrum over all possible radiation wavelengths that depends only on the body's [[Temperature|temperature]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A perfectly-insulated enclosure which is in thermal equilibrium internally contains black-body radiation and will emit it through a hole made in its wall, provided the hole is small enough to have a negligible effect upon the equilibrium. The thermal radiation spontaneously emitted by many ordinary objects can be approximated as black-body radiation. Of particular importance, although planets and stars (including the [[Earth]] and [[Sun]]) are neither in thermal equilibrium with their surroundings nor perfect black bodies, black-body radiation is still a good first approximation for the energy they emit. A black body at room temperature (23 °C) radiates mostly in the [[Infrared|infrared]] spectrum, which cannot be perceived by the human eye, but can be sensed by some reptiles.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Blizzard",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of snowstorm",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Storms" },
		summary = "A **blizzard** is a severe snowstorm characterized by strong sustained winds and low visibility, lasting for a prolonged period of time—typically at least three or four hours. A ground blizzard is a weather condition where snow that has already fallen is being blown by wind. Blizzards can have an immense size and usually stretch to hundreds or thousands of kilometres.",
		related = { "Storm", "Dust storm" },
	},
	{
		title = "Blue",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Colour between violet and cyan on the visible spectrum of light",
		tags = { "Color", "Color terms" },
		summary = "**Blue** is one of the three [[Primary color|primary colours]] in the [[RGB color model|RGB (additive) colour model]], as well as in the RYB colour model (traditional colour theory). It lies between violet and [[Cyan|cyan]] on the spectrum of [[Light|visible light]]. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 [[Nanometre|nanometres]]. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Blue has been an important colour in art and decoration since ancient times. The semi-precious stone lapis lazuli was used in ancient [[Egypt]] for jewellery and ornament and later, in the [[Renaissance]], to make the pigment ultramarine, the most expensive of all pigments. In the eighth century Chinese artists used cobalt blue to colour fine blue and white porcelain. In the [[Middle Ages]], European artists used it in the windows of cathedrals. Europeans wore clothing coloured with the vegetable dye woad until it was replaced by the finer indigo from America. In the 19th century, synthetic blue dyes and pigments gradually replaced organic dyes and mineral pigments. Dark blue became a common colour for military uniforms and later, in the late 20th century, for business suits.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Frequency", "~670–610" },
			{ "Source", "[[Web colors|HTML/CSS]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Body of water",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Any significant accumulation of water, generally on a planet's surface",
		tags = { "Water", "Hydrology" },
		summary = "A **body of water** is a significant accumulation of [[Water|water]] on the surface of the earth. The term most often refers to [[Ocean|oceans]], [[Ocean|seas]], and [[Lake|lakes]], but it includes smaller pools of water such as [[Pond|ponds]], [[Wetland|wetlands]], or more rarely, puddles. A body of water does not have to be still or contained; [[River|rivers]], [[Stream|streams]], [[Canal|canals]], and other [[Landform|geographical features]] where accumulated water moves from one place to another are also considered bodies of water.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most are naturally occurring and geographical features, but some are artificial. There are types that can be either. For example, most [[Reservoir|reservoirs]] are created by engineering [[Dam|dams]], but some natural lakes are used as reservoirs. Similarly, most [[Harbor|harbors]] are naturally occurring [[Bay|bays]], but some harbors have been created through construction. Bodies of water that are navigable are known as [[Waterway|waterways]]. Bodies of water are affected by gravity, which is what may create the [[Tide|tidal]] effects. The impact of [[Climate change|climate change]] on water is likely to intensify as observed through the [[Sea level rise|rising sea levels]], water [[Ocean acidification|acidification]] and [[Flood|flooding]]. This means that climate change has pressure on water bodies. [[Climate change]] significantly affects bodies of water through rising temperatures, altered precipitation patterns, and sea-level rise.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Bog",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🟫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of wetland with peat-rich soil",
		tags = { "Biomes", "Wetlands" },
		aliases = { "Bogland" },
		summary = "A **bog** or **bogland** is a [[Wetland|wetland]] that accumulates [[Peat|peat]] as a deposit of dead plant materials – often [[Moss|mosses]], typically [[Sphagnum|sphagnum]] moss. It is one of the four main [[Wetland|types of wetlands]]. Other names for bogs include mire, mosses, quagmire, and muskeg; alkaline mires are called fens. A bayhead is another type of bog found in the forest of the Gulf Coast states in the United States. They are often covered in [[Ericaceae|heath or heather]] shrubs rooted in the sphagnum moss and peat. The gradual accumulation of decayed plant material in a bog functions as a [[Carbon sink|carbon sink]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Bogs occur where the water at the ground surface is [[Acid|acidic]] and low in nutrients. A bog usually is found at a freshwater soft spongy ground that is made up of decayed plant matter which is known as peat. They are generally found in cooler northern climates and are formed in poorly draining lake basins. In contrast to fens, they derive most of their water from precipitation rather than mineral-rich ground or surface water. Water flowing out of bogs has a characteristic brown colour, which comes from dissolved peat [[Tannin|tannins]]. In general, the low fertility and cool climate result in relatively slow plant growth, but decay is even slower due to low oxygen levels in saturated bog soils. Hence, peat accumulates. Large areas of the landscape can be covered many meters deep in peat.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Bohr model",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Atomic model introduced by Niels Bohr in 1913",
		tags = { "Atomic, molecular and optical physics", "Atomic physics" },
		aliases = { "Rutherford–Bohr model" },
		summary = "In [[Atomic physics|atomic physics]], the **Bohr model** or **Rutherford–Bohr model** is an obsolete model of the [[Atom|atom]] that incorporated some early quantum concepts. Developed from 1911 to 1918 by [[Niels Bohr]] and building on [[Ernest Rutherford]]'s discovery of the atom's nucleus, it supplanted the plum pudding model of [[J. J. Thomson]] only to be replaced by the quantum atomic model in the 1920s. It consists of a small, dense [[Atomic nucleus|atomic nucleus]] surrounded by [[Orbit|orbiting]] [[Electron|electrons]]. It is analogous to the structure of the [[Solar System]], but with attraction provided by [[Coulomb's law|electrostatic force]] rather than [[Gravity|gravity]], and with the electron energies quantized (assuming only discrete values).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In the history of atomic physics, it followed and ultimately replaced several earlier models, including Joseph Larmor's Solar System model (1897), Jean Perrin's model (1901), the cubical model (1902), Hantaro Nagaoka's Saturnian model (1904), the plum pudding model (1904), Arthur Haas's quantum model (1910), the Rutherford model (1911), and John William Nicholson's nuclear quantum model (1912). The improvement over the 1911 Rutherford model mainly concerned the new [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanical]] interpretation introduced by Haas and Nicholson, but forsaking any attempt to explain radiation according to [[Classical physics|classical physics]]. The model's key success lies in explaining the Rydberg formula for hydrogen's spectral emission lines. While the Rydberg formula had been known experimentally, it did not gain a theoretical basis until the Bohr model was introduced.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Boiling",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physical process",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Phase transitions" },
		aliases = { "Ebullition" },
		summary = "**Boiling** or **ebullition** is the rapid [[Phase transition|phase transition]] from [[Liquid|liquid]] to [[Gas|gas]] or [[Vapor|vapour]]; the reverse of boiling is [[Condensation|condensation]]. Boiling occurs when a liquid is heated to its [[Boiling point|boiling point]], so that the vapour pressure of the liquid is equal to the pressure exerted on the liquid by the [[Standard atmosphere (unit)|surrounding atmosphere]]. Boiling and [[Evaporation|evaporation]] are the two main forms of liquid vapourization.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are two main types of boiling: nucleate boiling, where small bubbles of vapour form at discrete points; and critical heat flux boiling, where the boiling surface is heated above a certain critical temperature and a film of vapour forms on the surface. Transition boiling is an intermediate, unstable form of boiling with elements of both types. The boiling point of water is 100 °C or 212 °F, but is lower with the decreased atmospheric pressure found at higher altitudes. The sensitivity of different micro-organisms to heat varies, but if water is held at 100 °C for one minute, most micro-organisms and viruses are inactivated. Ten minutes at a temperature of 70 °C (158 °F) is also sufficient to inactivate most [[Bacteria|bacteria]]. Boiling water is also used in several [[Cooking|cooking]] methods including boiling, parboiling, blanching, [[Steaming|steaming]], and poaching.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Boric acid",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Weak acid with formula H3BO3",
		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Inorganic compounds" },
		aliases = { "Orthoboric acid" },
		summary = "**Boric acid**, more specifically **orthoboric acid**, is a compound of [[Boron|boron]], [[Oxygen|oxygen]], and [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] with formula B(OH)3. It may also be called **hydrogen orthoborate**, **trihydroxidoboron** or **boracic acid**. It is usually encountered as colorless crystals or a white powder, that dissolves in [[Water|water]], and occurs in nature as the mineral sassolite.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Boric acid is often used as an antiseptic, insecticide, flame retardant, neutron absorber, or precursor to other boron compounds. The term "boric acid" is also used generically for any oxyacid of boron, such as metaboric acid HBO2 and tetraboric acid H2B4O7.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Boron",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Boron** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **B** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 5. In its crystalline form it is a brittle, dark, lustrous [[Metalloid|metalloid]]; in its [[Amorphous solid|amorphous]] form it is a brown powder. As the lightest element of the boron group it has three [[Valence electron|valence electrons]] for forming [[Covalent bond|covalent bonds]], resulting in many compounds such as [[Boric acid|boric acid]], the mineral sodium borate, and the ultra-hard crystals of boron carbide and boron nitride.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Boron is synthesized entirely by cosmic ray spallation and [[Supernova|supernovas]] and not by [[Stellar nucleosynthesis|stellar nucleosynthesis]], so it is a low-abundance element in the [[Solar System]] and in the [[Crust (geology)|Earth's crust]]. It constitutes about 0.001 percent by weight of Earth's crust. It is concentrated on Earth by the water-solubility of its more common naturally occurring compounds, the borate minerals. These are mined industrially as evaporites, such as borax and kernite. The largest known deposits are in [[Turkey]], the largest producer of boron minerals. Elemental boron is found in small amounts in [[Meteoroid|meteoroids]], but chemically uncombined boron is not otherwise found naturally on Earth. Several allotropes exist: [[Amorphous solid|amorphous]] boron is a brown powder; crystalline boron is silvery to black, extremely hard (9.3 on the Mohs scale), and a poor [[Electrical conductor|electrical conductor]] at room temperature.",
			},
		},
	},
}
]]></string>
