	{
		title = "Caesium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Cesium", "Cs" },
		summary = "**Caesium** ([[International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry|IUPAC]] spelling; also spelled **cesium** in [[American English]]) is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Cs** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 55. It is a soft, silvery-golden [[Alkali metal|alkali metal]] with a melting point of 28.5 °C, which makes it one of only five elemental [[Metal|metals]] that are [[Liquid|liquid]] at or near [[Room temperature|room temperature]]. Caesium has physical and chemical properties similar to those of [[Rubidium|rubidium]] and [[Potassium|potassium]]. It is pyrophoric and reacts with [[Water|water]] even at −116 °C. It is the least [[Electronegativity|electronegative]] stable element, with a value of 0.79 on the Pauling scale. It has only one stable [[Isotope|isotope]], caesium-133. Caesium is mined mostly from pollucite.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Caesium-137, a fission product, is extracted from waste produced by nuclear reactors. It has the largest atomic radius of all elements whose radii have been measured or calculated, at about 260 picometres. The German chemist Robert Bunsen and physicist [[Gustav Kirchhoff]] discovered caesium in 1860 by the then newly developed method of flame spectroscopy. The first small-scale applications for caesium were as a "getter" in [[Vacuum tube|vacuum tubes]] and in the light-sensitive [[Anode|anodes]] of photoelectric cells. Caesium is widely used in highly accurate [[Atomic clock|atomic clocks]]. In 1967, the [[International System of Units]] began using a specific hyperfine transition of neutral caesium-133 atoms to define the [[SI base unit|basic unit]] of time, the [[Second|second]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Caffeine",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Central nervous system stimulant",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		summary = "It is mainly used for its eugeroic (wakefulness promoting), ergogenic (physical performance-enhancing), or nootropic (cognitive-enhancing) properties (particularly in workplace settings); it is also used recreationally, often in social settings. Caffeine acts by blocking the binding of adenosine at a number of adenosine receptor types, inhibiting the centrally depressant effects of adenosine and enhancing the release of acetylcholine. Caffeine has a three-dimensional structure similar to that of adenosine, which allows it to bind and block its receptors.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Caffeine also increases cyclic AMP levels through nonselective inhibition of phosphodiesterase, increases calcium release from intracellular stores, and antagonizes GABA receptors, although these mechanisms typically occur at concentrations beyond usual human consumption. Caffeine is a bitter, white crystalline purine, a methylxanthine [[Alkaloid|alkaloid]], and is chemically related to the adenine and guanine bases of [[DNA|deoxyribonucleic acid]] (DNA) and [[RNA|ribonucleic acid]] (RNA). It is found in the seeds, fruits, nuts, or leaves of a number of plants native to [[Africa]], [[East Asia]], and [[South America]] and helps to protect them against herbivores and from competition by preventing the germination of nearby seeds, as well as encouraging consumption by select animals such as honey bees.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Melts at", "235" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Calcium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Ca" },
		summary = '**Calcium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Ca** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 20. As an [[Alkaline earth metal|alkaline earth metal]], calcium is a reactive metal that forms a dark oxide-nitride layer when exposed to air. Its physical and chemical properties are most similar to its heavier homologues [[Strontium|strontium]] and [[Barium|barium]]. It is the fifth most abundant element in Earth\'s crust, and the third most abundant metal, after [[Iron|iron]] and [[Aluminium|aluminium]]. The most common calcium compound on Earth is [[Calcium carbonate|calcium carbonate]], found in [[Limestone|limestone]] and the fossils of early sea life; [[Gypsum|gypsum]], anhydrite, fluorite, and apatite are also sources of calcium. The name comes from the [[Latin]] word calx (meaning "lime"), which was obtained from heating limestone.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some calcium compounds were known to the ancients, though their chemistry was unknown until the seventeenth century. Pure calcium was isolated in 1808 via [[Electrolysis|electrolysis]] of its oxide by [[Humphry Davy]], who named the element. Calcium compounds are widely used in many industries: in foods and pharmaceuticals for calcium supplementation, in the paper industry as bleaches, as components in cement and electrical insulators, and in the manufacture of soaps. On the other hand, the metal in pure form has few applications due to its high reactivity; still, in small quantities it is often used as an alloying component in steelmaking, and sometimes, as a calcium–lead alloy, in making automotive batteries. Calcium is the most abundant metal and the fifth-most abundant element in the [[Human body|human body]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Calcium carbonate",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical compound",
		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Salts" },
		aliases = { "CaCO3" },
		summary = "**Calcium carbonate** is a [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]] with the [[Chemical formula|chemical formula]] **CaCO3**. It is a common substance found in [[Rock (geology)|rocks]] as the [[Mineral|minerals]] [[Calcite|calcite]] and [[Aragonite|aragonite]], most notably in chalk and [[Limestone|limestone]], eggshells, gastropod shells, [[Shellfish|shellfish]] skeletons and [[Pearl|pearls]]. Materials containing much calcium carbonate or resembling it are described as calcareous. Calcium carbonate is the active ingredient in agricultural lime. Limescale is calcium carbonate produced when calcium [[Ion|ions]] in hard water react with carbonate ions. Calcium carbonate has medical use as a [[Calcium|calcium]] supplement or as an antacid, but excessive consumption can be hazardous and cause hypercalcemia and digestive issues.",
		related = { "Ammonium nitrate", "Sodium nitrate" },
