	{
		title = "Canyon",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏜️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Deep chasm between cliffs",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Fluvial landforms" },
		summary = "Rivers have a natural tendency to cut through underlying surfaces, eventually wearing away rock layers as [[Sediment|sediments]] are removed downstream. A river bed will gradually reach a baseline elevation, which is the same elevation as the body of water into which the river drains. The processes of weathering and erosion will form canyons when the river's headwaters and [[Estuary|estuary]] are at significantly different elevations, particularly through regions where softer rock layers are intermingled with harder layers more resistant to weathering.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A canyon may also refer to a rift between two mountain peaks, such as those in ranges including the [[Rocky Mountains]], the [[Alps]], the [[Himalayas]] or the [[Andes]]. Usually, a river or stream carves out such splits between mountains. Examples of mountain-type canyons are Provo Canyon in Utah or Yosemite Valley in California's [[Sierra Nevada]]. Canyons within mountains, or gorges that have an opening on only one side, are called box canyons. Slot canyons are very narrow canyons that often have smooth walls. Steep-sided valleys in the [[Seabed|seabed]] of the continental slope are referred to as submarine canyons. Unlike canyons on land, submarine canyons are thought to be formed by turbidity currents and landslides.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Alluvial fan" },
	},
	{
		title = "Capacitance",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ability of a body to store an electrical charge",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrical circuits" },
		summary = "**Capacitance** is the ability of an object to store [[Electric charge|electric charge]]. It is measured by the change in charge in response to a difference in [[Electric potential|electric potential]], expressed as the ratio of those quantities. Commonly recognized are two closely related notions of capacitance: self-capacitance and mutual capacitance. An object that can be electrically charged exhibits self-capacitance, for which the electric potential is measured between the object and ground. Mutual capacitance is measured between two components, and is particularly important in the operation of the [[Capacitor|capacitor]], an elementary linear [[Electronic component|electronic component]] designed to add capacitance to an electric circuit.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The capacitance between two [[Electrical resistance and conductance|conductors]] depends on the opposing surface area of the conductors, the distance between those conductors, and the permittivity of any [[Dielectric|dielectric]] material between them. For many dielectric materials, the permittivity, and thus the capacitance, is independent of the potential difference between the conductors and the total charge on them. The [[International System of Units|SI]] unit of capacitance is the [[Farad|farad]] (symbol: F), named after the English physicist [[Michael Faraday]]. A 1 farad capacitor, when charged with 1 [[Coulomb|coulomb]] of electrical charge, has a potential difference of 1 [[Volt|volt]] between its plates. The reciprocal of capacitance is called elastance.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Dimension", "wikidata" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Carbohydrate",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Organic compound that consists only of carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		summary = "A **carbohydrate** is a [[Sugar|sugar]] (saccharide) or a sugar derivative. Together with [[Amino acid|amino acids]], [[Fatty acid|fats]], and [[Nucleic acid|nucleic acids]], the carbohydrates are one of the major families of biomolecules.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Carbohydrates perform numerous roles in living organisms. Polysaccharides serve as an [[Energy|energy]] store (e.g., [[Starch|starch]] and [[Glycogen|glycogen]]) and as structural components (e.g., cellulose in plants and [[Chitin|chitin]] in arthropods and fungi). The 5-carbon monosaccharide ribose is an important component of coenzymes (e.g., [[Adenosine triphosphate|ATP]], FAD and NAD) and the backbone of the genetic molecule known as [[RNA]]. The related deoxyribose is a component of [[DNA]]. Carbohydrates are central to [[Nutrition|nutrition]] and are found in a wide variety of natural and processed foods. Starch is a polysaccharide and is abundant in cereals (wheat, maize, rice), potatoes, and processed food based on cereal [[Flour|flour]], such as [[Bread|bread]], pizza or pasta.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Carbon monoxide",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Inorganic compounds" },
		summary = "Carbon monoxide consists of one [[Carbon|carbon]] atom and one [[Oxygen|oxygen]] atom connected by a triple bond. It is the simplest carbon oxide. In coordination complexes, the carbon monoxide ligand is called carbonyl. It is a key ingredient in many processes in industrial chemistry.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The most common source of carbon monoxide is the partial [[Combustion|combustion]] of carbon-containing compounds. Numerous environmental and biological sources generate carbon monoxide. Carbon monoxide has important biological roles across [[Kingdom (taxonomy)|biological kingdoms]]. It is produced by many organisms, including humans. It is isoelectronic with both [[Cyanide|cyanide]] anion CN- and molecular [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]] N2.",
