	{
		title = "Carboxylic acid",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Carbon–oxygen bond" },
		aliases = { "Carboxyl group" },
		summary = "In [[Organic chemistry|organic chemistry]], a **carboxylic acid** is a polar, organic acid that contains a **carboxyl group** (\\sC(\\dO)\\sOH) attached to an [[Substituent|R-group]]. The general formula of a carboxylic acid is often written as **R\\sCOOH** or **R\\sCO2H**, sometimes as R\\sC(O)OH, with R referring to an organyl group (e.g., alkyl, alkenyl, aryl), or [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]], or other groups. Carboxylic acids occur widely. Important examples include the [[Amino acid|amino acids]] and [[Fatty acid|fatty acids]]. Deprotonation of a carboxylic acid gives a carboxylate [[Ion|anion]]. Carboxylic acids are often considered weak acids as they only partially ionise (dissociate) in water.",
	},
	{
		title = "Carnivore",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Organism that eats mostly or exclusively animal tissue",
		tags = { "Zoology", "Ethology" },
		aliases = { "Meat-eater", "Carnivorous" },
		summary = "A **carnivore**, or **meat-eater**, is an [[Animal|animal]] or [[Plant|plant]] whose [[Nutrition|nutrition]] and [[Energy|energy]] requirements are met by [[Eating|consumption]] of animal [[Tissue (biology)|tissues]] (mainly [[Muscle|muscle]], fat and other soft tissues) as [[Food|food]], whether through [[Predation|predation]] or [[Scavenger|scavenging]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Carotenoid",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Class of chemical compounds; yellow, orange or red plant pigments",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		aliases = { "Carotenoids" },
		summary = "**Carotenoids** are yellow, orange, and red [[Organic compound|organic]] [[Pigment|pigments]] that are produced by [[Plant|plants]] and [[Algae|algae]], as well as several bacteria, archaea, and [[Mushroom|fungi]]. Carotenoids give the characteristic color to [[Pumpkin|pumpkins]], [[Carrot|carrots]], [[Parsnip|parsnips]], [[Maize|corn]], [[Tomato|tomatoes]], canaries, [[Flamingo|flamingos]], [[Salmon|salmon]], [[Lobster|lobster]], [[Shrimp|shrimp]], and daffodils. Over 1,100 identified carotenoids can be further categorized into two classes – xanthophylls (which contain oxygen) and carotenes (which are purely [[Hydrocarbon|hydrocarbons]] and contain no oxygen). Some 40 to 50 carotenoids found in fruits and vegetables are consumed in the human diet.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"All are derivatives of tetraterpenes, meaning that they are produced from 8 isoprene units and contain 40 carbon atoms. In general, carotenoids absorb wavelengths ranging from 400 to 550 nanometers (violet to green light). This causes the compounds to be deeply colored yellow, orange, or red. Carotenoids are the dominant pigment in autumn leaf coloration of about 15-30% of tree species, but many plant colors, especially reds and purples, are due to polyphenols. Carotenoids serve two key roles in plants and algae: they absorb light energy for use in [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]], and they provide photoprotection via non-photochemical quenching. Carotenoids that contain unsubstituted beta-ionone rings have vitamin A activity. In the eye, lutein, meso-zeaxanthin, and zeaxanthin are present as macular pigments whose importance in visual function, as of 2026, remains under clinical research.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cast iron",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Iron-carbon alloy",
		tags = { "Chemical mixtures", "Specific alloys" },
		summary = '**Cast iron** is a class of [[Iron|iron]]–[[Carbon|carbon]] [[Alloy|alloys]] with a carbon content of more than 2% and silicon content around 1–3%. Its usefulness derives from its relatively low melting temperature. The alloying elements determine the form in which its carbon appears: white cast iron has its carbon combined into the iron carbide [[Chemical compound|compound]] cementite, which is very hard, but brittle, as it allows cracks to pass straight through; grey cast iron has graphite flakes which deflect a passing crack and initiate countless new cracks as the material breaks, and ductile cast iron has spherical graphite "nodules" which stop the crack from further progressing.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Carbon (C), ranging from 1.8 to 4% by weight, and [[Silicon|silicon]] (Si), 1–3% by weight, are the main alloying elements of cast iron. Iron alloys with lower carbon content are known as [[Steel|steel]]. Cast iron tends to be brittle, except for malleable cast irons. With its relatively low melting point, good fluidity, castability, excellent machinability, resistance to deformation and wear resistance, cast irons have become an engineering material with a wide range of applications and are used in pipes, machines and [[Automotive industry|automotive industry]] parts, such as cylinder heads, cylinder blocks and gearbox cases. Some alloys are resistant to damage by [[Redox|oxidation]]. In general, cast iron is notoriously difficult to [[Welding|weld]]. The earliest cast-iron artifacts date to the 8th century BC, and were discovered by archaeologists in what is now [[Jiangsu]], China.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Catalysis",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Process of increasing the rate of a chemical reaction",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Chemical reactions" },
		aliases = { "Catalyst" },
		summary = "**Catalysis** is the increase in rate of a [[Chemical reaction|chemical reaction]] due to an added substance known as a **catalyst**. Catalysts are not consumed by the reaction and remain unchanged after the reaction. If the reaction is rapid and the catalyst is recycled quickly, a very small amount of catalyst often suffices; mixing, surface area, and temperature are important factors in reaction rate. Catalysts generally react with one or more reactants to form intermediates that subsequently give the final reaction product, in the process of regenerating the catalyst.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The rate increase occurs because the catalyst allows the reaction to occur by an alternative mechanism which may be much faster than the noncatalyzed mechanism. However the noncatalyzed mechanism does remain possible, so that the total rate (catalyzed plus noncatalyzed) can only increase in the presence of the catalyst and never decrease. Catalysis may be classified as either homogeneous, whose components are dispersed in the same phase (usually gaseous or liquid) as the reactant, or heterogeneous, whose components are not in the same phase. [[Enzyme|Enzymes]] and other biocatalysts are often considered as a third category. Furthermore, a nanocatalyst is a nanosize catalyst used in the field of applied nanoscience. Catalysis is nearly ubiquitous in the [[Chemical industry|chemical industry]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cave",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🕳️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Natural void under a planetary surface",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Caves" },
		aliases = { "Caves", "Caverns" },
		summary = "**Caves** and **caverns** are natural voids under the [[Planetary surface|surface]] of the [[Earth]] and have also been observed in other [[Terrestrial planet|rocky]] worlds (e.g., on Mars). Caves often form by the [[Weathering|weathering]] of rock and can extend deep underground. **Exogene caves** are smaller openings that extend a relatively short distance underground (such as rock shelters). Caves which extend farther underground than the opening is wide are called **endogene caves**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Speleology is the science of exploration and study of all aspects of caves and the cave environment. Visiting or exploring caves for recreation may be called caving, potholing, or spelunking.",
			},
