		emoji = "🦠",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Organelle in eukaryotic cells",
		tags = { "Cell biology", "Organelles and other cell parts" },
		summary = "In [[Cell (biology)|cell biology]] a **centriole** is a cylindrical [[Organelle|organelle]] composed mainly of a protein called tubulin. Centrioles are found in most [[Eukaryote|eukaryotic]] [[Cell (biology)|cells]], but are not present in conifers ([[Conifer|Pinophyta]]), flowering plants ([[Flowering plant|angiosperms]]) and most [[Mushroom|fungi]], and are only present in the male gametes of charophytes, [[Bryophyte|bryophytes]], seedless [[Vascular plant|vascular plants]], [[Cycad|cycads]], and Ginkgo. A bound pair of centrioles, surrounded by a highly ordered mass of dense material, called the pericentriolar material (PCM), makes up a structure called a centrosome.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Centrioles are typically made up of nine sets of short microtubule triplets, arranged in a cylinder. Deviations from this structure include crabs and [[Drosophila melanogaster]] embryos, with nine doublets, and [[Caenorhabditis elegans]] sperm cells and early embryos, with nine singlets. Additional proteins include centrin, cenexin and tektin. Centrioles are involved in the formation of cilia during interphase, as well as in organizing the aster and mitotic spindle during cell division.",
