		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Cholesterol is biosynthesized by all animal [[Cell (biology)|cells]] and is an essential structural and signaling component of animal [[Cell membrane|cell membranes]]. In [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]], hepatic cells typically produce the greatest amounts. In the brain, astrocytes produce cholesterol and transport it to [[Neuron|neurons]]. It is absent among [[Prokaryote|prokaryotes]] ([[Bacteria|bacteria]] and [[Archaea|archaea]]), although there are some exceptions, such as Mycoplasma, which require cholesterol for growth. Cholesterol also serves as a [[Precursor (chemistry)|precursor]] for the biosynthesis of steroid hormones, bile acid, and vitamin D. François Poulletier de la Salle first identified cholesterol in solid form in gallstones in 1769. In 1815, chemist Michel Eugène Chevreul named the compound "cholesterine".',
