		aliases = { "Ciliates" },
		summary = "The **ciliates** are a group of [[Alveolate|alveolates]] characterized by the presence of hair-like organelles called cilia, which are identical in structure to [[Flagellum|eukaryotic flagella]], but are in general shorter and present in much larger numbers, with a different undulating pattern than flagella. Cilia occur in all members of the group (although the peculiar Suctoria only have them for part of their [[Biological life cycle|life cycle]]) and are variously used in swimming, crawling, attachment, feeding, and sensation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Ciliates are an important group of [[Protist|protists]], common almost anywhere there is water—in lakes, ponds, oceans, rivers, and wet soils, including anoxic (oxygen-depleted) habitats. About 4,500 unique free-living species have been described, and the potential number of extant species is estimated at 27,000–40,000. Ciliate species range in size from as little as 10 m in some colpodeans to as much as 4 mm in length in some geleiids, and include some of the most [[Morphology (biology)|morphologically]] complex protozoans. In most systems of taxonomy, "**Ciliophora**" is ranked as a [[Phylum|phylum]] under any of several [[Kingdom (taxonomy)|kingdoms]], including Chromista, Protista or [[Protozoa]]. In some older systems of classification, such as the influential taxonomic works of Alfred Kahl, ciliated protozoa are placed within the [[Class (taxonomy)|class]] "**Ciliata**" (a term which can also refer to a genus of fish).',
			},
