		summary = "**Citric acid** is an [[Organic compound|organic compound]] with the formula C6H8O7. It is a [[Transparency and translucency|colorless]] weak organic acid. It occurs naturally in [[Citrus|citrus fruits]]. In [[Biochemistry|biochemistry]], it is an intermediate in the citric acid cycle, which occurs in the [[Metabolism|metabolism]] of all aerobic organisms.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"More than two million tons of citric acid are manufactured every year. It is used widely as acidifier, [[Flavoring|flavoring]], preservative, and chelating agent. A **citrate** is a derivative of citric acid; that is, the [[Salt (chemistry)|salts]], [[Ester|esters]], and the [[Polyatomic ion|polyatomic anion]] found in solutions and salts of citric acid. An example of the former, a salt is trisodium citrate; an ester is triethyl citrate. When citrate [[Ion|trianion]] is part of a salt, the formula of the citrate trianion is written as C6H5O73– or C3H5O(COO)33–.",
