		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Description of large objects' physics",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Classical mechanics" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], **classical mechanics** is a [[Theoretical physics|theory]] that describes the effect of forces on the [[Kinematics|motion]] of macroscopic objects and [[Continuum mechanics|bulk matter]], without considering [[Quantum mechanics|quantum]] effects, and often without incorporating [[Theory of relativity|relativistic]] effects either.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is used in describing the motion of objects such as projectiles and [[Particle|particles]], parts of machinery, [[Spacecraft|spacecraft]], planets, [[Star|stars]], [[Galaxy|galaxies]], deformable solids, [[Fluid mechanics|fluids]], macromolecules and other objects. The development of classical mechanics involved [[Scientific Revolution|substantial change in the methods and philosophy]] of physics. The qualifier classical distinguishes this type of mechanics from new methods developed after the [[History of physics|revolutions in physics of the early 20th century]] which revealed limitations in classical mechanics. Some modern sources include [[Relativistic mechanics|relativistic mechanics]] in classical mechanics, as representing the subject matter in its most developed and accurate form. The earliest formulation of classical mechanics is often referred to as [[Newton's laws of motion|Newtonian mechanics]].",
