		summary = '**Classical physics** consists of [[Scientific theory|scientific theories]] in the field of [[Physics|physics]] that are non-quantum or both non-quantum and non-relativistic, depending on the context. In historical discussions, classical physics refers to pre-1900 physics, while [[Modern physics|modern physics]] refers to post-1900 physics, which incorporates elements of [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]] and the [[Theory of relativity|theory of relativity]]. However, relativity is based on classical field theory rather than [[Quantum field theory|quantum field theory]], and is often categorized as a part of "classical physics".',
