		tags = { "Meteorology", "Clouds" },
		summary = "In [[Meteorology|meteorology]], a **cloud** is an [[Aerosol|aerosol]] consisting of a visible mass of miniature liquid droplets, ice crystals, or other particles, suspended in the [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]] of a [[Planet|planetary]] body or similar space. [[Water]], primarily, comprises the droplets and crystals. On [[Earth]], clouds are formed as a result of saturation of the [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]] when it is cooled to its dew point, or when it gains sufficient [[Moisture|moisture]], usually in the form of [[Water vapor|water vapor]], from an adjacent source to raise the dew point to the ambient [[Temperature|temperature]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Clouds are seen in the Earth's homosphere, which includes the [[Troposphere|troposphere]], [[Stratosphere|stratosphere]], and mesosphere. **Nephology** is the science of clouds, which is undertaken in the cloud physics branch of [[Meteorology|meteorology]]. The [[World Meteorological Organization]] uses two methods of naming clouds in their respective layers of the homosphere, [[Latin]] and [[Common name|common name]]. [[Genus]] types in the troposphere, the atmospheric layer closest to Earth's surface, have [[Latin]] names because of the universal adoption of Luke Howard's nomenclature that was formally proposed in 1802. It became the basis of a modern international system that divides clouds into five physical forms which can be further divided or classified into [[Altitude|altitude]] levels to derive ten basic genera.",
