	{
		title = "Coast",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Area where land meets the sea or ocean",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Oceanic and coastal landforms" },
		aliases = { "Coastline", "Shoreline", "Seashore" },
		summary = "A **coast** (also called the **coastline**, **shoreline**, or **seashore**) is the [[Land|land]] next to the [[Ocean|sea]] or the line that forms the boundary between the land and the [[Ocean|ocean]] or a [[Lake|lake]]. Coasts are influenced by the [[Topography|topography]] of the surrounding landscape and by aquatic [[Erosion|erosion]], such as that caused by [[Wind wave|waves]]. The [[Geology|geological]] composition of [[Rock (geology)|rock]] and [[Soil|soil]] dictates the type of shore that is created. [[Earth]] has about 620,000.0 km of coastline.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Coasts are important zones in natural [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]], often home to a wide range of [[Biodiversity|biodiversity]]. On land, they harbor ecosystems, such as freshwater or [[Estuary|estuarine]] [[Wetland|wetlands]], that are important for birds and other [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial animals]]. In wave-protected areas, coasts harbor salt marshes, [[Mangrove|mangroves]], and seagrasses, all of which can provide nursery habitat for fin[[Fish|fish]], [[Shellfish|shellfish]], and other [[Aquatic animal|aquatic animals]]. Rocky shores are usually found along exposed coasts and provide habitat for a wide range of sessile animals (e.g. mussels, [[Starfish|starfish]], barnacles) and various kinds of [[Seaweed|seaweeds]]. In physical oceanography, a shore is the wider fringe that is geologically modified by the action of the body of water past and present, and the [[Beach|beach]] is at the edge of the shore, including the [[Intertidal zone|intertidal zone]] where there is one.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
