		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mixture of an insoluble substance microscopically dispersed throughout another substance",
		tags = { "Chemical substances", "Chemical mixtures" },
		summary = "A **colloid** is a [[Mixture|mixture]] in which one substance, consisting of microscopically [[Dispersion (chemistry)|dispersed]] insoluble [[Particle|particles]], is suspended throughout another substance. Some definitions specify that the particles must be dispersed in a [[Liquid|liquid]], while others extend the definition to include substances like [[Aerosol|aerosols]] and [[Gel|gels]]. A colloid has a dispersed phase (the suspended particles) and a continuous phase (the medium of suspension). The term **colloidal suspension** refers unambiguously to the overall mixture. (In a narrower sense, a colloid is distinguished from other types of suspension by the smaller particle size.)",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some colloids are translucent because of the Tyndall effect, which is the [[Scattering|scattering]] of light by particles in the colloid. Other colloids may be [[Opacity|opaque]] or have a slight color. Colloidal suspensions are the subject of interface and colloid science. This field of study began in 1845 with Francesco Selmi, who called them pseudosolutions, and was later expanded by [[Michael Faraday]] and Thomas Graham, who coined the term colloid in 1861.",
