	{
		title = "Combustion",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical reaction between a fuel and oxygen",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Chemical reactions" },
		aliases = { "Burning" },
		summary = "**Combustion**, or **burning**, is a high-temperature exothermic [[Redox|redox]] [[Chemical reaction|chemical reaction]] between a [[Fuel|fuel]] (the reductant) and an oxidant, usually atmospheric [[Oxygen|oxygen]], that produces oxidized, often gaseous products, in a mixture termed as smoke. Combustion does not always result in [[Fire|fire]], because a flame is only visible when substances undergoing combustion vaporize, but when it does, a flame is a characteristic indicator of the reaction. While activation energy must be supplied to initiate combustion (e.g., using a lit match to light a fire), the heat from a flame may provide enough energy to make the reaction self-sustaining. The study of combustion is known as **combustion science**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Combustion is often a complicated sequence of elementary [[Radical (chemistry)|radical reactions]]. Solid fuels, such as [[Wood|wood]] and [[Coal|coal]], first undergo endothermic pyrolysis to produce gaseous fuels whose combustion then supplies the heat required to produce more of them. Combustion is often hot enough that incandescent [[Light|light]] in the form of either glowing or a flame is produced. A simple example can be seen in the combustion of [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] and [[Oxygen|oxygen]] into [[Water|water]] [[Vapor|vapor]], a reaction which is commonly used to fuel [[Rocket engine|rocket engines]]. This reaction releases 242kJ/mol of heat and reduces the [[Enthalpy|enthalpy]] accordingly (at constant temperature and pressure): Uncatalyzed combustion in air requires relatively high temperatures. Complete combustion is stoichiometric concerning the fuel, where there is no remaining fuel, and ideally, no residual oxidant.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Competition (biology)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of species interaction",
		tags = { "Ecology" },
		summary = "**Competition** is an interaction between [[Organism|organisms]] or [[Species|species]] in which both require one or more resources that are in limited supply (such as [[Food|food]], [[Water|water]], or [[Territory (animal)|territory]]). Competition lowers the fitness of both organisms involved since the presence of one of the organisms always reduces the amount of the resource available to the other.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In the study of community ecology, competition within and between members of a [[Species|species]] is an important biological interaction. Competition is one of many interacting biotic and [[Abiotic component|abiotic]] factors that affect community structure, species diversity, and population dynamics (shifts in a population over time). There are three major mechanisms of competition: interference, exploitation, and apparent competition (in order from most direct to least direct). Interference and exploitation competition can be classed as "real" forms of competition, while apparent competition is not, as organisms do not share a resource, but instead share a predator. Competition among members of the same species is known as intraspecific competition, while competition between individuals of different species is known as interspecific competition.',
