		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Phase transitions" },
		summary = "**Condensation** is the change of the [[State of matter|state of matter]] from the [[Gas|gas phase]] into the [[Liquid|liquid phase]], and is the reverse of vaporization. The word most often refers to the [[Water cycle|water cycle]]. It can also be defined as the change in the state of water vapor to liquid water when in contact with a liquid or solid surface or cloud condensation nuclei within the [[Atmosphere of Earth|atmosphere]]. When the transition happens from the gaseous phase into the solid phase directly, the change is called deposition. Condensation is usually associated with water.",
	},
	{
		title = "Condensed matter physics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of physics",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Condensed matter physics** is the field of [[Physics|physics]] that deals with the macroscopic and microscopic physical properties of [[Matter|matter]], especially the [[Solid|solid]] and [[Liquid|liquid]] [[State of matter|phases]], that arise from electromagnetic forces between [[Atom|atoms]] and [[Electron|electrons]]. More generally, the subject deals with condensed phases of matter: systems of many constituents with strong interactions among them. More exotic condensed phases include the [[Superconductivity|superconducting]] phase exhibited by certain materials at extremely low cryogenic [[Temperature|temperatures]], the ferromagnetic and antiferromagnetic phases of [[Spin (physics)|spins]] on crystal lattices of atoms, the Bose–Einstein condensates found in ultracold atomic systems, and liquid crystals.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Condensed matter physicists seek to understand the behavior of these phases by experiments to measure various material properties, and by applying the [[Scientific law|physical laws]] of [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]], [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]], [[Statistical mechanics|statistical mechanics]], and other [[Theoretical physics|physics theories]] to develop mathematical models and predict the properties of very large groups of atoms. The diversity of systems and phenomena available for study makes condensed matter physics the most active field of contemporary physics: as of 2009 one third of all American physicists self-identify as condensed matter physicists, and the Division of Condensed Matter Physics is the largest division of the American Physical Society. These include solid state and soft matter physicists, who study [[Quantum mechanics|quantum]] and non-quantum physical properties of matter respectively.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Conglomerate (geology)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🪨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sedimentary rock composed of smaller rock fragments",
		tags = { "Geology", "Petrology" },
		aliases = { "Conglomerate" },
		summary = "**Conglomerate** is a [[Sedimentary rock|sedimentary rock]] made up of rounded [[Gravel|gravel]]-sized pieces of rock surrounded by finer-grained [[Sediment|sediments]] (such as [[Sand|sand]], [[Silt|silt]], or [[Clay|clay]]). The larger fragments within conglomerate are called clasts, while the finer sediment surrounding the clasts is called the matrix. The clasts and matrix are typically cemented by [[Calcium carbonate|calcium carbonate]], [[Iron oxide|iron oxide]], [[Silicon dioxide|silica]], or hardened clay.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Conglomerates form when rounded gravels deposited by water or glaciers become solidified and cemented by pressure over time. They can be found in sedimentary rock sequences of all ages but probably make up less than 1 percent by weight of all sedimentary rocks. They are closely related to sandstones in origin, and exhibit many of the same types of sedimentary structures, such as tabular and trough cross-bedding and graded bedding. **Fanglomerates** are poorly sorted, matrix-rich conglomerates that originated as debris flows on [[Alluvial fan|alluvial fans]] and likely contain the largest accumulations of gravel in the geologic record. [[Breccia|Breccias]] are similar to conglomerates, but have clasts that have angular (rather than rounded) shapes.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Rock (geology)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Sedimentary" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Conservation biology",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of threats to biological diversity",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Conservation biology** is the study of the conservation of nature and of [[Earth]]'s [[Biodiversity|biodiversity]] with the aim of protecting [[Species|species]], their [[Habitat|habitats]], and [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]] from excessive rates of [[Extinction|extinction]] and the erosion of biotic interactions. It is an [[Interdisciplinarity|interdisciplinary]] subject drawing on [[Natural science|natural]] and [[Social science|social sciences]], and the practice of natural resource management.",
		sections = { { "Overview", "The conservation ethic is based on the findings of conservation biology." } },
	},
	{
		title = "Conservation law",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Scientific law regarding conservation of a physical property",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], a **conservation law** states that a particular measurable property of an isolated [[Physical system|physical system]] does not change as the system evolves over time. Exact conservation laws include [[Mass–energy equivalence|conservation of mass-energy]], conservation of linear momentum, conservation of angular momentum, and conservation of electric charge. There are also many approximate conservation laws, which apply to such quantities as [[Conservation of mass|mass]], parity, lepton number, baryon number, strangeness, hypercharge, etc. These quantities are conserved in certain classes of physics processes, but not in all.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A local conservation law is usually expressed mathematically as a continuity equation, a [[Partial differential equation|partial differential equation]] which gives a relation between the amount of the quantity and the "transport" of that quantity. It states that the amount of the conserved quantity at a point or within a volume can only change by the amount of the quantity which flows in or out of the volume. From Noether\'s theorem, every differentiable [[Symmetry (physics)|symmetry]] leads to a local conservation law. Other conserved quantities can exist as well.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Conservation of energy",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Law of physics and chemistry",
		tags = { "Physics", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Law of conservation of energy" },
		summary = "The **law of conservation of energy** states that the total [[Energy|energy]] of an isolated system remains constant; it is said to be [[Conservation law|conserved]] over time. In the case of a closed system, the principle says that the total amount of energy within the system can only be changed through energy entering or leaving the system. Energy can neither be created nor destroyed; rather, it can only be transformed or transferred from one form to another. For instance, [[Chemical energy|chemical energy]] is converted to [[Kinetic energy|kinetic energy]] when a stick of dynamite explodes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"If one adds up all forms of energy that were released in the explosion, such as the kinetic energy and [[Potential energy|potential energy]] of the pieces, as well as heat and sound, one will get the exact decrease of chemical energy in the combustion of the dynamite. Classically, the conservation of energy was distinct from the [[Conservation of mass|conservation of mass]]. This implies that mass can be converted to energy, and vice versa. This is observed in the [[Nuclear binding energy|nuclear binding energy]] of atomic nuclei, where a mass defect is measured. It is believed that mass-energy equivalence becomes important in extreme physical conditions, such as those that likely existed in the universe very shortly after the Big Bang or when [[Black hole|black holes]] emit Hawking radiation.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Conservation of mass",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Scientific law that a closed system's mass remains constant",
		tags = { "Chemistry" },
		aliases = { "Law of conservation of mass", "Principle of mass conservation" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]] and [[Chemistry|chemistry]], the **law of conservation of mass** or **principle of mass conservation** states that for any system which is closed to all incoming and outgoing transfers of [[Matter|matter]], the [[Mass|mass]] of the system must remain constant over time.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The law implies that mass can neither be created nor destroyed, although it may be rearranged in space, or the entities associated with it may be changed in form. For example, in [[Chemical reaction|chemical reactions]], the mass of the chemical components before the reaction is equal to the mass of the components after the reaction. Thus, during any chemical reaction and low-energy thermodynamic processes in an isolated system, the total mass of the [[Reagent|reactants]], or starting materials, must be equal to the mass of the products. The concept of mass conservation is widely used in many fields such as [[Chemistry|chemistry]], [[Mechanics|mechanics]], and [[Fluid dynamics|fluid dynamics]]. The formulation of this law was of crucial importance in the progress from [[Alchemy|alchemy]] to the modern [[Natural science|natural science]] of chemistry. In general, mass is not conserved. The conservation of mass is a law that holds only in the classical limit.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Continental shelf",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Coastal and oceanic landform",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Oceanic and coastal landforms" },
		aliases = { "Shelf sea" },
		summary = 'A **continental shelf** is a portion of a [[Continent|continent]] that is submerged under an area of relatively shallow water, known as a **shelf sea**. Much of these shelves were exposed by drops in [[Sea level|sea level]] during glacial periods. The shelf surrounding an [[Island|island]] is known as an "insular shelf."',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The continental margin, between the continental shelf and the [[Abyssal plain|abyssal plain]], comprises a steep continental slope, surrounded by the flatter continental rise, in which [[Sediment|sediment]] from the continent above cascades down the slope and accumulates as a pile of sediment at the base of the slope. Extending as far as 500 km (310 mi) from the slope, it consists of thick sediments deposited by turbidity currents from the shelf and slope. The continental rise's gradient is intermediate between the gradients of the slope and the shelf. Under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, the name continental shelf was given a legal definition as the stretch of the [[Seabed|seabed]] adjacent to the shores of a particular country to which it belongs.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Continuum mechanics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of physics which studies the behavior of materials modeled as continuous media",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Continuous medium", "Continuum" },
		summary = "**Continuum mechanics** is a branch of [[Mechanics|mechanics]] that deals with the deformation of and transmission of [[Force|forces]] through [[Material|materials]] modeled as a **continuous medium** (also called a **continuum**) rather than as discrete particles.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Continuum mechanics deals with deformable bodies, as opposed to rigid bodies. A continuum model assumes that the substance of the object completely fills the space it occupies. While ignoring the fact that matter is made of [[Atom|atoms]], this provides a sufficiently accurate description of matter on length scales much greater than that of inter-atomic distances. The concept of a continuous medium allows for intuitive analysis of bulk matter by using differential equations that describe the behavior of such matter according to physical laws, such as [[Mass|mass]] conservation, momentum conservation, and energy conservation. Information about the specific material is expressed in constitutive relationships. Continuum mechanics treats the physical properties of solids and fluids independently of any particular [[Coordinate system|coordinate system]] in which they are observed.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Convection",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fluid flow that occurs due to heterogeneous fluid properties and body forces",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Fluid mechanics" },
		summary = "**Convection** is the transfer of heat through the physical movement of fluids (liquids or gases), where warmer, less-dense material rises and cooler, denser material sinks.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is a single-phase or multiphase fluid flow that occurs spontaneously through the combined effects of material property heterogeneity and body forces on a [[Fluid|fluid]]. When the cause of the convection is unspecified, convection due to the effects of thermal expansion (change in [[Density|density]]) and [[Gravity|gravity]]/[[Buoyancy|buoyancy]] can be assumed (see convection in heat transfer). Convective flow may be transient (such as when a multiphase [[Mixture|mixture]] of [[Oil|oil]] and [[Water|water]] separates) or steady state (see convection cell). The convection may be due to [[Gravity|gravitational]], [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetic]] or fictitious body forces. Heat transfer by natural convection plays a role in the structure of [[Atmosphere of Earth|Earth's atmosphere]], its [[Ocean|oceans]], and its [[Earth's mantle|mantle]]. Discrete convective cells in the atmosphere can be identified by clouds, with stronger convection resulting in [[Thunderstorm|thunderstorms]]. Natural convection also plays a role in stellar physics.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Copper",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🟠",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Cu" },
		summary = "**Copper** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Cu** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 29. It is a soft, malleable, and [[Ductility|ductile]] metal with very high [[Thermal conductivity and resistivity|thermal]] and [[Electrical resistivity and conductivity|electrical conductivity]]. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a [[Building material|building material]], and as a constituent of various metal [[Alloy|alloys]], such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and [[Coin|coins]], and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Copper is one of the few native metals, meaning metals that occur naturally in a directly usable, unalloyed metallic form. This led to very early human use in several regions, from c. 8000 BC. Thousands of years later, it was the first metal to be [[Smelting|smelted]] from sulfide ores, c. 5000 BC; the first metal to be cast into a shape in a mold, c. 4000 BC; and the first metal to be purposely alloyed with another metal, [[Tin|tin]], to create [[Bronze|bronze]], c. 3500 BC. Commonly encountered compounds are copper(II) salts, which often impart blue or green colors to such minerals as azurite, malachite, and turquoise, and have been used widely and historically as [[Pigment|pigments]]. Copper used in buildings, usually for roofing, oxidizes to form a green patina of compounds called verdigris. Copper is sometimes used in decorative art, both in its elemental metal form and in compounds as pigments.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Coriolis force",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏋️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Apparent force in a rotating reference frame",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Classical mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Coriolis effect" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], the **Coriolis force** is a pseudo-force that acts on objects in motion within a frame of reference that rotates with respect to an [[Inertial frame of reference|inertial frame]]. In a reference frame with [[Clockwise|clockwise]] rotation, the force acts to the left of the motion of the object. In one with anticlockwise (or counterclockwise) rotation, the force acts to the right. Deflection of an object due to the Coriolis force is called the **Coriolis effect**. Though recognized previously by others, the mathematical expression for the Coriolis force appeared in an 1835 paper by French scientist Gaspard-Gustave de Coriolis, in connection with the theory of water wheels.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Early in the 20th century, the term Coriolis force began to be used in connection with [[Meteorology|meteorology]]. On Earth, the Coriolis force will affect the movement of air and water over long distance. Because of Earth's rotation, the moving object does not travel in a straight line relative to the Earth's surface but follows a curved path. This deflection (clockwise in the [[Northern Hemisphere]] and counterclockwise in the [[Southern Hemisphere]]) significantly influences large-scale circulation of winds and ocean currents. In popular (non-technical) usage of the term \"Coriolis effect\", the rotating reference frame implied is almost always the [[Earth]]. Because the Earth rotates, Earth-bound observers need to account for the Coriolis force to correctly analyze the motion of objects.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Corrosion",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Gradual destruction of materials by chemical reaction with its environment",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Chemical reactions" },
		summary = "**Corrosion** is a [[Erosion|natural process]] that converts a refined [[Metal|metal]] into a more chemically stable [[Oxide|oxide]]. It is the gradual deterioration of materials (usually a metal) by chemical or electrochemical reaction with their environment. Corrosion engineering is the field dedicated to controlling and preventing corrosion.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Rusting, the formation of red-orange [[Iron|iron]] oxides, is perhaps the most familiar example of electrochemical corrosion. This type of corrosion typically produces [[Oxide|oxides]] or [[Salt (chemistry)|salts]] of the original metal and results in a distinctive coloration. Corrosion can also occur in materials other than metals, such as ceramics or polymers, although, in this context, the term degradation is more common. Corrosion degrades the useful properties of materials and structures including mechanical strength, appearance, and permeability to liquids and gases. Many structural [[Alloy|alloys]] corrode merely from exposure to moisture in air, but the process can be strongly affected by exposure to certain substances. Corrosion can be concentrated locally to form a pit or crack, or it can extend across a wide area, more or less uniformly corroding the surface.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Coulomb",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "SI derived unit of electric charge",
		tags = { "Units of measurement", "Electromagnetism" },
		summary = "The **coulomb** (symbol: **C**) is the unit of [[Electric charge|electric charge]] in the [[International System of Units]] (SI). It is defined to be equal to the electric charge delivered by a 1 [[Ampere|ampere]] current in 1 [[Second|second]], with the [[Elementary charge|elementary charge]] e as a defining constant in the SI.",
		infobox = {
			{ "Symbol", "C" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Coulomb's law",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fundamental physical law of electromagnetism",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrostatics" },
		aliases = { "Coulomb's inverse-square law" },
		summary = "**Coulomb's inverse-square law**, or simply **Coulomb's law**, is a [[Scientific law|scientific law]] of [[Physics|physics]] that describes the amount of force between two [[Electric charge|electrically charged]] particles at rest. This electric force is conventionally called the electrostatic force or **Coulomb force**. Although the law was known earlier, it was first published in 1785 by French physicist Charles-Augustin de Coulomb. Coulomb's law was essential to the development of the [[Classical electromagnetism|theory of electromagnetism]] and may even be its starting point, as it allowed meaningful discussions of the amount of electric charge in a particle.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The law states that the magnitude, or absolute value, of the attractive or repulsive electrostatic [[Force|force]] between two point charges is directly proportional to the product of the magnitudes of their charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them. Coulomb discovered that bodies with like electrical charges repel: Coulomb also showed that oppositely charged bodies attract according to an inverse-square law: Here q1 and q2 are the quantities of each charge, and the scalar r is the distance between the charges. The proportionality constant depends upon the unit of charge used and may be called the **Coulomb constant**. The force is along the straight line joining the two bodies. If the charges have the same sign, the electrostatic force between them makes them repel; if they have different signs, the force between them makes them attract.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Covalent bond",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical bond by sharing of electron pairs",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Chemical bonds" },
		summary = "A **covalent bond** is a [[Chemical bond|chemical bond]] that involves the sharing of [[Electron|electrons]] to form electron pairs between [[Atom|atoms]]. These electron pairs are known as **shared pairs** or **bonding pairs**. The stable balance of attractive and repulsive forces between atoms, when they share [[Electron|electrons]], is known as covalent bonding. For many [[Molecule|molecules]], the sharing of electrons allows each atom to attain the equivalent of a full valence shell, corresponding to a stable electronic configuration. In organic chemistry, covalent bonding is much more common than [[Ionic bonding|ionic bonding]], where atoms are separate and merely associated by electrostatic attraction.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Covalent bonding also includes many kinds of interactions, including -bonding, -bonding, [[Metallic bonding|metal-to-metal bonding]], agostic interactions, bent bonds, three-center two-electron bonds and three-center four-electron bonds. The term "covalence" was introduced by Irving Langmuir in 1919, with Nevil Sidgwick using "co-valent link" in the 1920s. Merriam-Webster dates the specific phrase covalent bond to 1939, recognizing its first known use. The prefix co- (jointly, partnered) indicates that "co-valent" bonds involve shared "[[Valence (chemistry)|valence]]", as detailed in [[Valence bond theory|valence bond theory]]. In the molecule H2, the [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] atoms share the two electrons via covalent bonding. The covalent character of a bond, as opposed to a more ionic nature, is greatest between atoms that have similar [[Electronegativity|electronegativities]], regardless of whether the elements are the same as each other.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cretaceous",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Third and last period of the Mesozoic Era",
		tags = { "Geological history", "Geological periods" },
		summary = "The **Cretaceous** is a geological period that lasted from about 143.1 to 66 Ma (million years ago). It is the third and final period of the [[Mesozoic]] Era, as well as the longest. At around 77.1 million years, it is the ninth and longest geological period of the entire [[Phanerozoic]]. The name is derived from the Latin, 'chalk', which is abundant in deposits from the latter half of the period. It is usually abbreviated **K**, for its German translation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Cretaceous was a period with a relatively warm [[Climate|climate]], resulting in high [[Sea level|eustatic sea levels]] that created numerous shallow inland seas. These oceans and seas were populated with now-[[Extinction|extinct]] marine reptiles, ammonites and rudists; while on land, [[Dinosaur|dinosaurs]] continued to dominate. The world was largely ice-free – although there is some evidence of brief periods of glaciation during the cooler first half – and forests extended to the poles. Many of the dominant taxonomic groups present in modern times can be ultimately traced back to origins in the Cretaceous. During this time, new groups of [[Mammal|mammals]] and [[Bird|birds]] appeared, including the earliest relatives of placentals and [[Marsupial|marsupials]] (Eutheria and Metatheria respectively), with the earliest [[Crown group|crown group]] birds appearing towards the end of the Cretaceous.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Colour", "Cretaceous" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mass extinction event about 66 million years ago",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geological history" },
		aliases = { "Cretaceous–Paleogene", "K–Pg", "Cretaceous–Tertiary" },
		summary = "The **Cretaceous–Paleogene** (**K–Pg**) **extinction event**, formerly known as the **Cretaceous–Tertiary** (**K–T**) **extinction event**, was a major [[Extinction event|mass extinction]] of three-quarters (75%) of the plant and animal [[Species|species]] on [[Earth]] which occurred around 66 million years ago. The event caused the extinction of all the non-avian [[Dinosaur|dinosaurs]] and most other tetrapods weighing more than 25 kg, excepting some ectothermic species such as sea turtles and crocodilians. It marked the ends of both the [[Cretaceous]] period and the [[Mesozoic]] era, and logged the beginning of the current geological era, the [[Cenozoic]] era.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In the geologic record, the K–Pg event is marked by a thin layer of [[Sediment|sediment]] called the K–Pg boundary or K–T boundary, which can be found throughout the world in marine and terrestrial rocks. The boundary clay shows unusually high levels of the metal [[Iridium|iridium]], which is more common in [[Asteroid|asteroids]] than in the [[Earth's crust]]. It is generally thought that the K–Pg extinction resulted from the impact of a massive asteroid 10 to 15 km wide, creating the Chicxulub impact crater and devastating the global environment primarily through a lingering impact winter which halted [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]] in [[Plant|plants]] and [[Plankton|plankton]]. The impact hypothesis, also known as the Alvarez hypothesis, was bolstered by the discovery of the 180 km Chicxulub crater in the [[Gulf of Mexico]]'s [[Yucatán Peninsula]] in the early 1990s.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Late Devonian mass extinction" },
	},
	{
		title = "Critical point (thermodynamics)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Temperature and pressure point where phase boundaries disappear",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Phase transitions" },
		aliases = { "Critical point", "Critical state" },
		summary = "In [[Thermodynamics|thermodynamics]], a **critical point** (or **critical state**) is the end point of a phase equilibrium curve. One example is the liquid–vapor critical point, the end point of the pressure–temperature curve that designates conditions under which a [[Liquid|liquid]] and its [[Vapor|vapor]] can coexist. At higher temperatures, the gas comes into a supercritical phase, and so cannot be liquefied by pressure alone. At the critical point, defined by a critical temperature Tc and a critical pressure pc, [[Phase (matter)|phase]] boundaries vanish. Other examples include the liquid–liquid critical points in mixtures, and the ferromagnet–paramagnet transition (Curie temperature) in the absence of an external magnetic field.",
	},
	{
		title = "Crust (geology)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Outermost solid shell of astronomical bodies",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geology" },
		aliases = { "Crust" },
		summary = "In [[Geology|geology]], the **crust** is the outermost solid shell of a [[Planet|planet]], [[Dwarf planet|dwarf planet]], or [[Natural satellite|natural satellite]]. It is usually distinguished from the underlying [[Mantle (geology)|mantle]] by its chemical makeup; however, in the case of icy satellites, it may be defined based on its phase (solid crust vs. liquid mantle).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The crusts of [[Earth]], [[Mercury (planet)|Mercury]], [[Venus]], [[Mars]], [[Io (moon)|Io]], the [[Moon]] and other planetary bodies formed via [[Igneous rock|igneous]] processes and were later modified by [[Erosion|erosion]], [[Impact crater|impact cratering]], volcanism, and sedimentation. Most [[Terrestrial planet|terrestrial planets]] have fairly uniform crusts. Earth, however, has two distinct types: [[Continental crust|continental crust]] and [[Oceanic crust|oceanic crust]]. These two types have different chemical compositions and physical properties and were formed by different geological processes.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cryosphere",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Earth's surface where water is frozen",
		tags = { "Earth science", "Water" },
		summary = "The **cryosphere** is an umbrella term for those portions of [[Earth]]'s surface where [[Water|water]] is in solid form. This includes [[Sea ice|sea ice]], [[Ice|ice]] on lakes or rivers, [[Snow|snow]], [[Glacier|glaciers]], [[Ice cap|ice caps]], [[Ice sheet|ice sheets]], and frozen ground (which includes [[Permafrost|permafrost]]). Thus, there is an overlap with the [[Hydrosphere|hydrosphere]]. The cryosphere is an integral part of the global [[Climate system|climate system]]. It also has important feedbacks on the climate system. These feedbacks come from the cryosphere's influence on surface energy and moisture fluxes, [[Cloud|clouds]], the [[Water cycle|water cycle]], atmospheric and oceanic circulation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Through these feedback processes, the cryosphere plays a significant role in the global climate and in climate model response to global changes. Approximately 10% of the Earth's surface is covered by ice, but this is rapidly decreasing. Current reductions in the cryosphere ([[Effects of climate change|caused by climate change]]) are measurable in [[Ice sheet|ice sheet melt]], glaciers decline, [[Effects of climate change|sea ice decline]], permafrost thaw and snow cover decrease.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Crystal",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Solid material with highly ordered microscopic structure",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Crystallography" },
		aliases = { "Crystalline solid" },
		summary = "A **crystal** or **crystalline solid** is a [[Solid|solid]] material whose constituents (such as [[Atom|atoms]], [[Molecule|molecules]], or [[Ion|ions]]) are arranged in a highly ordered microscopic structure, forming a crystal lattice that extends in all directions. In addition, macroscopic single crystals are usually identifiable by their [[Geometry|geometrical shape]], consisting of flat faces with specific, characteristic orientations. The scientific study of crystals and crystal formation is known as [[Crystallography|crystallography]]. The process of crystal formation via mechanisms of crystal growth is called [[Crystallization|crystallization]] or solidification.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The word crystal derives from the [[Ancient Greek]] word (krustallos), meaning both "[[Ice|ice]]" and "[[Quartz|rock crystal]]", from (kruos), "icy cold, frost". Examples of large crystals include snowflakes, [[Diamond|diamonds]], and [[Salt|table salt]]. Most inorganic solids are not crystals but polycrystals, i.e. many microscopic crystals fused together into a single solid. Polycrystals include most [[Metal|metals]], rocks, [[Ceramic|ceramics]], and [[Ice|ice]]. A third category of solids is [[Amorphous solid|amorphous solids]], where the atoms have no periodic structure whatsoever. Examples of amorphous solids include [[Glass|glass]], [[Wax|wax]], and many [[Plastic|plastics]]. Despite the name, lead crystal, crystal glass, and related products are not crystals, but rather types of glass, i.e. amorphous solids.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Crystallization",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Process by which a solid with a highly organized structure forms",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Crystallography" },
		summary = "**Crystallization** is a process that leads to solids with a uniform pattern of [[Atom|atoms]] or [[Molecule|molecules]], i.e. a [[Crystal|crystal]]. The uniform nature of a crystalline solid can be contrasted with [[Amorphous solid|amorphous solids]] in which atoms or molecules lack regular organization. Crystallization can occur by various routes including precipitation from solution, [[Freezing|freezing]] of a [[Liquid|liquid]], or deposition from a gas. Attributes of the resulting crystal can depend largely on factors such as [[Temperature|temperature]], air [[Pressure|pressure]], cooling rate, or [[Solution (chemistry)|solute concentration]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Crystallization occurs in two main phases. The first is nucleation, the appearance of a crystalline phase from either a supercooled liquid or a supersaturated solvent. The second step is known as crystal growth, which is the increase in the size of particles and leads to a crystal state. An important feature of this step is that loose particles form layers at the crystal's surface and lodge themselves into open inconsistencies such as pores, cracks, etc. Crystallization is also a chemical solid–liquid separation technique, in which mass transfer of a solute from the liquid solution to a pure solid crystalline phase occurs. In [[Chemical engineering|chemical engineering]], crystallization occurs in a crystallizer. Crystallization is therefore related to precipitation, although the result is not [[Amorphous solid|amorphous]] or disordered, but a crystal.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Crystallography",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Scientific study of crystal structures",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Crystallography" },
		summary = "**Crystallography** is the branch of science devoted to the study of molecular and [[Crystal structure|crystalline structure]] and properties. In July 2012, the [[United Nations]] recognised the importance of the science of crystallography by proclaiming 2014 the International Year of Crystallography.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Crystallography is a broad topic, and many of its subareas, such as [[X-ray crystallography]], are themselves important scientific topics. Crystallography ranges from the fundamentals of [[Crystal structure|crystal structure]] to the mathematics of crystal geometry, including those that are not periodic or quasicrystals. At the atomic scale it can involve the use of X-ray diffraction to produce experimental data that the tools of [[X-ray crystallography]] can convert into detailed positions of atoms, and sometimes electron density. At larger scales it includes experimental tools such as orientational imaging to examine the relative orientations at the grain boundary in materials. Crystallography plays a key role in many areas of biology, chemistry, and physics, as well as in emerging developments in these fields.",
			},
		},
	},
