		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Genus of dense, towering vertical clouds",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Clouds" },
		aliases = { "Cumulonimbus" },
		summary = "**Cumulonimbus** is a dense, towering, vertical [[Cloud|cloud]], typically forming from [[Water vapor|water vapor]] condensing in the lower [[Troposphere|troposphere]] that builds upward carried by powerful [[Buoyancy|buoyant]] air currents. Above the lower portions of the cumulonimbus the water vapor becomes ice crystals, such as [[Snow|snow]] and graupel, the interaction of which can lead to [[Hail|hail]] and to [[Lightning|lightning]] formation, respectively.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When causing [[Thunderstorm|thunderstorms]], these clouds may be called **thunderheads**. Cumulonimbus can form alone, in clusters, or along squall lines. These clouds are capable of producing lightning and other dangerous severe weather, such as [[Tornado|tornadoes]], hazardous [[Wind|winds]], and large hailstones. Cumulonimbus progress from overdeveloped cumulus congestus clouds and may further develop as part of a supercell. Cumulonimbus is abbreviated as **Cb**.",
