		summary = "In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], a **cyanide** is an inorganic [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]] that contains a C\\tN [[Functional group|functional group]]. This group, known as the **cyano group**, consists of a [[Carbon|carbon]] atom triple-bonded to a [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]] atom.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Salt (chemistry)|Ionic]] cyanides contain the cyanide [[Ion|anion]] −C\\tN. Covalent cyanides contain the \\sC\\tN group, and are usually called nitriles if the group is linked by a single [[Covalent bond|covalent bond]] to carbon atom. For example, in acetonitrile CH3\\sC\\tN, the cyanide group is bonded to methyl \\sCH3. In tetracyanomethane C(\\sC\\tN)4, four cyano groups are bonded to carbon. The cyano group may be covalently bonded to atoms other than carbon, e.g., in cyanogen azide N3\\sC\\tN, phosphorus tricyanide P(\\sC\\tN)3 and trimethylsilyl cyanide (CH3)3Si\\sC\\tN. It is obtained by [[Acid|acidification]] of cyanide salts.",
