		tags = { "Organisms", "Prokaryotes" },
		aliases = { "Cyanobacteriota" },
		summary = '**Cyanobacteria** are a group of autotrophic gram-negative bacteria of the [[Phylum|phylum]] **Cyanobacteriota** that can obtain biological energy via oxygenic photosynthesis. Cyanobacteria originated in a [[Fresh water|freshwater]] or terrestrial environment, and first appeared in the middle [[Archean|Archean eon]]. They are probably the most numerous [[Taxon|taxon]] to have ever existed on [[Earth]]. The name "cyanobacteria" refers to their bluish green ([[Cyan|cyan]]) color, which forms the basis of cyanobacteria\'s informal [[Common name|common name]], **blue-green algae**.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Cyanobacteria are the first organisms known to have produced [[Oxygen|oxygen]]. Their photopigments can absorb the red- and blue-spectrum frequencies of [[Sunlight|sunlight]] (thus reflecting a greenish color) to split water molecules into hydrogen ions and [[Oxygen|oxygen]]. The hydrogen ions are used to react with carbon dioxide to produce complex [[Organic compound|organic compounds]] such as [[Carbohydrate|carbohydrates]] (a process known as [[Biological carbon fixation|carbon fixation]]), and the [[Oxygen|oxygen]] is released as a byproduct.",
