		title = "Degenerate matter",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of dense exotic matter in physics",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "States of matter" },
		summary = "**Degenerate matter** occurs when the [[Pauli exclusion principle]] significantly alters a [[State of matter|state of matter]] at low temperature. The term is used in [[Astrophysics|astrophysics]] to refer to dense stellar objects such as [[White dwarf|white dwarfs]] and [[Neutron star|neutron stars]], where thermal pressure alone is not enough to prevent [[Gravitational collapse|gravitational collapse]]. The term also applies to metals in the Fermi gas approximation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Degenerate matter is usually modelled as an ideal Fermi gas, an ensemble of non-interacting fermions. In a quantum mechanical description, particles limited to a finite volume may take only a discrete set of energies, called quantum states. The [[Pauli exclusion principle]] prevents identical fermions from occupying the same quantum state. At lowest total energy (when the thermal energy of the particles is negligible), all the lowest energy quantum states are filled. This state is referred to as full degeneracy. This **degeneracy pressure** remains non-zero even at absolute zero temperature. Adding particles or reducing the volume forces the particles into higher-energy quantum states. In this situation, a compression force is required, and is made manifest as a resisting pressure.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Bose–Einstein condensate", "Gas" },
