		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Dielectric medium" },
		summary = "In [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]], a **dielectric** (or **dielectric medium**) is an [[Insulator (electricity)|electrical insulator]] that can be polarised by an applied [[Electric field|electric field]]. When a dielectric material is placed in an electric field, [[Electric charge|electric charges]] do not flow through the material as they do in an [[Electrical conductor|electrical conductor]], because they have no loosely bound, or free, [[Electron|electrons]] that may drift through the material, but instead they shift, only slightly, from their average equilibrium positions, causing **dielectric polarisation**. Because of dielectric polarisation, positive charges are displaced in the direction of the field and negative charges shift in the direction opposite to the field.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"This creates an internal electric field that reduces the overall field within the dielectric itself. If a dielectric is composed of weakly [[Chemical bond|bonded]] molecules, those molecules not only become polarised, but also reorient so that their symmetry axes align to the field. The study of dielectric properties concerns storage and dissipation of electric and magnetic energy in materials. Dielectrics are important for explaining various phenomena in [[Electronics|electronics]], [[Optics|optics]], [[Solid-state physics|solid-state physics]] and cell biophysics.",
			},
