		aliases = { "Dinoflagellates", "Dinophytes", "Dinoflagellata" },
		summary = "The **dinoflagellates**, also called **dinophytes**, are a [[Monophyly|monophyletic]] group of single-celled [[Eukaryote|eukaryotes]] constituting the phylum **Dinoflagellata** and are usually considered [[Protist|protists]]. Dinoflagellates are mostly [[Marine life|marine]] [[Plankton|plankton]], but they are also common in [[Freshwater ecosystem|freshwater habitats]]. Their populations vary with sea surface temperature, [[Salinity|salinity]], and depth. Many dinoflagellates are [[Photosynthesis|photosynthetic]], but a large fraction of these are in fact mixotrophic, combining photosynthesis with ingestion of prey (phagotrophy and myzocytosis).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In terms of number of species, dinoflagellates are one of the largest groups of marine eukaryotes, although substantially smaller than [[Diatom|diatoms]]. Some species are [[Endosymbiont|endosymbionts]] of marine animals and play an important part in the biology of [[Coral reef|coral reefs]]. Some dinoflagellates produce resting stages, called dinoflagellate cysts or dinocysts, as part of their lifecycles; this occurs in 84 of the 350 described freshwater species and a little more than 10% of the known marine species. Dinoflagellates are [[Alveolate|alveolates]] possessing two [[Flagellum|flagella]], the ancestral condition of bikonts. About 1,555 species of free-living marine dinoflagellates are currently described. Another estimate suggests about 2,000 living species, of which more than 1,700 are marine (free-living, as well as benthic) and about 220 are from fresh water.",
