		description = "Land area where water converges to a common outlet",
		tags = { "Water", "Hydrology" },
		summary = "A **drainage basin** is an area of land in which all flowing surface water converges to a single point, such as a river mouth, or flows into another [[Body of water|body of water]], such as a [[Lake|lake]] or [[Ocean|ocean]]. A basin is separated from adjacent basins by a perimeter, the [[Drainage divide|drainage divide]], made up of a succession of elevated features, such as ridges and [[Hill|hills]]. A basin may consist of smaller basins that merge at river [[Confluence|confluences]], forming a hierarchical pattern.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In North America, this is commonly called a **watershed**, though in other English-speaking places, "watershed" is used only in its original sense, that of the [[Drainage divide|drainage divide]] line. Other terms for a drainage basin are **catchment area**, **catchment basin**, **drainage area**, **river basin**, **water catchment**, **water basin**, and impluvium. A drainage basin\'s boundaries are determined by watershed delineation, a common task in environmental engineering and science. In a closed drainage basin, or [[Endorheic basin|endorheic basin]], rather than flowing to the ocean, water converges toward the interior of the basin, known as a sink, which may be a permanent lake, a [[Dry lake|dry lake]], or a point where surface water is lost underground. Drainage basins are similar but not identical to hydrologic units, which are drainage areas delineated so as to nest into a multi-level hierarchical drainage system.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Drought",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Period with less precipitation than normal",
		tags = { "Earth science", "Natural disasters" },
		summary = "A **drought** is a period of drier-than-normal conditions. A drought can last for days, months or years. Drought often has large impacts on the [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]] and [[Agriculture|agriculture]] of affected regions, and causes harm to the local [[Economy|economy]]. Annual dry seasons in the [[Tropics|tropics]] significantly increase the chances of a drought developing, with subsequent increased [[Wildfire|wildfire]] risks. [[Heat wave|Heat waves]] can significantly worsen drought conditions by increasing [[Evapotranspiration|evapotranspiration]]. This dries out forests and other vegetation, and increases the amount of fuel for wildfires.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Drought is a recurring feature of the climate in most parts of the world, becoming more extreme and less predictable due to [[Climate change|climate change]], which dendrochronological studies date back to 1900. There are three kinds of drought effects, environmental, economic and social. Environmental effects include the drying of [[Wetland|wetlands]], more and larger wildfires, [[Biodiversity loss|loss of biodiversity]]. Economic impacts of drought result due to negative disruptions to [[Agriculture|agriculture]] and livestock farming (causing [[Food security|food insecurity]]), forestry, public [[Water supply|water supplies]], river navigation (due to e.g.: lower water levels), electric power supply (by affecting [[Hydropower|hydropower]] systems) and impacts on human health. Social and health costs include the negative effects on the health of people directly exposed to this phenomenon (excessive heat waves), high food costs, stress caused by failed harvests, water scarcity, etc.",
			},
		},
	},
