		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physical property when materials or objects return to original shape after deformation",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Solid mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Elasticity" },
		summary = "In [[Continuum mechanics|continuum mechanics]] and [[Materials science|materials science]], **elasticity** is the ability of a [[Physical object|body]] to resist a distorting influence and to return to its original size and shape when that influence or [[Force|force]] is removed. Solid objects will [[Deformation (engineering)|deform]] when adequate loads are applied to them; if the material is elastic, the object will return to its initial shape and size after removal. This is in contrast to [[Plasticity (physics)|plasticity]], in which the object fails to do so and instead remains in its deformed state.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The physical reasons for elastic behavior can be quite different for different materials. In [[Metal|metals]], the [[Crystal structure|atomic lattice]] changes size and shape when forces are applied (energy is added to the system). When forces are removed, the lattice goes back to the original lower energy state. For rubbers and other [[Polymer|polymers]], elasticity is caused by the stretching of polymer chains when forces are applied. [[Hooke's law]] states that the force required to deform elastic objects should be [[Proportionality (mathematics)|directly proportional]] to the distance of deformation, regardless of how large that distance becomes. This is known as perfect elasticity, in which a given object will return to its original shape no matter how strongly it is deformed.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
