		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mode of arrangement of electrons in different shells of an atom",
		tags = { "Atomic, molecular and optical physics", "Atomic physics" },
		summary = "In [[Atomic physics|atomic physics]] and [[Quantum chemistry|quantum chemistry]], the **electron configuration** is the distribution of [[Electron|electrons]] of an [[Atom|atom]] or [[Molecule|molecule]] (or other physical structure) in [[Atomic orbital|atomic]] or [[Molecular orbital|molecular orbitals]]. For example, the electron configuration of the [[Neon|neon]] atom is 1s2 2s2 2p6, meaning that the 1s, 2s, and 2p [[Electron shell|subshells]] are occupied by two, two, and six electrons, respectively.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Electronic configurations describe each electron as moving independently in an [[Atomic orbital|orbital]], in an average [[Field (physics)|field]] created by the [[Atomic nucleus|nuclei]] and all the other electrons. Mathematically, configurations are described by Slater determinants or configuration state functions. According to the laws of quantum mechanics, a [[Energy level|level of energy]] is associated with each electron configuration. In certain conditions, electrons are able to move from one configuration to another by the emission or absorption of a [[Quantum|quantum]] of energy, in the form of a [[Photon|photon]]. Knowledge of the electron configuration of different atoms is useful in understanding the structure of the [[Periodic table|periodic table of elements]], for describing the [[Chemical bond|chemical bonds]] that hold atoms together, and in understanding the [[Chemical formula|chemical formulas]] of compounds and the geometries of molecules.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
