		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Multicellular diploid eukaryote in its earliest stage of development",
		tags = { "Biological processes and physiology", "Animal ontogeny" },
		summary = "An **embryo** is the initial stage of development for a [[Multicellular organism|multicellular organism]]. The resulting fusion of these two cells produces a single-celled [[Zygote|zygote]] that undergoes many cell divisions that produce [[Cell (biology)|cells]] known as blastomeres. The blastomeres are arranged as a solid ball that when reaching a certain size, called a morula, takes in fluid to create a cavity called a blastocoel. The structure is then termed a blastula, or a blastocyst in [[Mammal|mammals]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The mammalian blastocyst hatches before implantating into the endometrial lining of the womb. Once implanted the embryo will continue its development through the next stages of gastrulation, neurulation, and organogenesis. Gastrulation, possibly induced in part by paracrine signalling from the amnion, is the formation of the three germ layers that will form all of the different parts of the body. Neurulation forms the [[Nervous system|nervous system]], and organogenesis is the development of all the various tissues and organs of the body. A newly developing human is typically referred to as an embryo until the ninth week after conception, when it is then referred to as a fetus. In other multicellular organisms, the word embryo can be used more broadly to any early developmental or life cycle stage prior to birth or hatching.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Embryology",
