	{
		title = "Energy level",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Different states of quantum systems",
		tags = { "Atomic, molecular and optical physics", "Atomic physics" },
		aliases = { "Energy levels" },
		summary = "A [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanical]] system or [[Particle|particle]] that is bound—that is, confined spatially—can only take on certain discrete values of energy, called **energy levels**. This contrasts with [[Classical mechanics|classical]] particles, which can have any amount of energy. The term is commonly used for the energy levels of the electrons in atoms, [[Ion|ions]], or [[Molecule|molecules]], which are bound by the electric field of the [[Atomic nucleus|nucleus]], but can also refer to energy levels of nuclei or vibrational or rotational energy levels in molecules. The energy spectrum of a system with such discrete energy levels is said to be quantized.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In [[Chemistry|chemistry]] and [[Atomic physics|atomic physics]], an electron shell, or principal energy level, may be thought of as the [[Orbit|orbit]] of one or more [[Electron|electrons]] around an [[Atom|atom]]\'s [[Atomic nucleus|nucleus]]. The closest shell to the nucleus is called the "1 shell" (also called "K shell"), followed by the "2 shell" (or "L shell"), then the "3 shell" (or "M shell"), and so on further and further from the nucleus. The shells correspond with the principal quantum numbers or are labeled alphabetically with letters used in the X-ray notation (K, L, M, N, …). Each shell can contain only a fixed number of electrons: The first shell can hold up to two electrons, the second shell can hold up to eight (2 + 6) electrons, the third shell can hold up to 18 (2 + 6 + 10) and so on. The general formula is that the nth shell can in principle hold up to 2n2 electrons.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Enthalpy",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Measure of energy in a thermodynamic system",
		tags = { "Physics", "Thermodynamics" },
		summary = "**Enthalpy** is the sum of a [[Thermodynamic system|thermodynamic system]]'s [[Internal energy|internal energy]] and the product of its [[Pressure|pressure]] and [[Volume|volume]]. It is a state function in [[Thermodynamics|thermodynamics]] used in many measurements in chemical, biological, and physical systems at a constant external pressure, which is conveniently provided by Earth's ambient atmosphere. The pressure-volume term is very small for solids and liquids at common conditions, and fairly small for gases. Therefore, enthalpy is a stand-in for [[Energy|energy]] in chemical systems; bond, lattice, solvation, and other chemical \"energies\" are actually enthalpy differences.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As a state function, enthalpy depends only on the final configuration of internal energy, pressure, and volume, not on the path taken to achieve it. In the [[International System of Units]] (SI), the unit of measurement for enthalpy is the [[Joule|joule]]. Other historical conventional units still in use include the [[Calorie|calorie]] and the British thermal unit (BTU). The total enthalpy of a system cannot be measured directly because the internal energy contains components that are unknown, not easily accessible, or are not of interest for the thermodynamic problem at hand. In practice, a change in enthalpy is the preferred expression for measurements at constant pressure, because it simplifies the description of energy transfer.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Entropy",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Property of a thermodynamic system",
		tags = { "Physics", "Thermodynamics" },
		summary = "**Entropy** is a thermodynamic state variable that quantifies the [[Probability distribution|probabilistic distribution]] of accessible microstates in a system. The term and the concept are used in diverse fields, from classical thermodynamics (where it was first recognized), to the microscopic description of nature in statistical physics, and the principles of [[Information theory|information theory]]. It has far-ranging applications in [[Chemistry|chemistry]] and [[Physics|physics]], in biological systems and their relation to life, in [[Cosmology|cosmology]], [[Economics|economics]], and information systems including the transmission of information in [[Telecommunications|telecommunication]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Entropy is central to the [[Second law of thermodynamics|second law of thermodynamics]], which states that the entropy of an isolated system left to spontaneous evolution cannot decrease with time. As a result, isolated systems evolve toward [[Thermodynamic equilibrium|thermodynamic equilibrium]], where the entropy is highest. "High" entropy means that energy is more disordered or dispersed, while "low" entropy means that energy is more ordered or concentrated. A consequence of the second law of thermodynamics is that certain processes are irreversible. The thermodynamic concept was referred to by Scottish scientist and engineer William Rankine in 1850 with the names thermodynamic function and heat-potential. In 1865, German physicist Rudolf Clausius, one of the leading founders of the field of thermodynamics, defined it as the quotient of an infinitesimal amount of [[Heat|heat]] to the instantaneous [[Temperature|temperature]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Environmental chemistry",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Scientific study",
		tags = { "Chemistry" },
		summary = "**Environmental chemistry** is the [[Science|scientific study]] of the chemical and [[Biochemistry|biochemical]] phenomena that occur in natural places. It should not be confused with green chemistry, which seeks to reduce potential pollution at its source. It can be defined as the study of the sources, reactions, transport, effects, and fates of chemical species in the [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]], [[Soil|soil]], and water environments; and the effect of human activity and biological activity on these. Environmental chemistry is an [[Interdisciplinarity|interdisciplinary]] science that includes [[Atmospheric chemistry|atmospheric]], aquatic and soil chemistry, as well as heavily relying on [[Analytical chemistry|analytical chemistry]] and being related to environmental and other areas of science.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Environmental chemistry involves first understanding how the uncontaminated environment works, which chemicals in what concentrations are present naturally, and with what effects. Without this it would be impossible to accurately study the effects humans have on the [[Natural environment|environment]] through the release of chemicals. Environmental [[Chemist|chemists]] draw traditional chemical concepts as well as [[Sampling (statistics)|sampling]] and [[Analytical chemistry|analytical techniques]].",
