		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Large biological molecule that acts as a catalyst",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		summary = "An **enzyme** is a biological macromolecule, usually a [[Protein|protein]], that acts as a biological [[Catalysis|catalyst]], accelerating chemical reactions without being consumed in the process. The [[Molecule|molecules]] on which enzymes act are called substrates, which are converted into [[Product (chemistry)|products]]. Nearly all [[Metabolism|metabolic processes]] within a [[Cell (biology)|cell]] depend on enzyme catalysis to occur at biologically relevant rates. A [[Metabolic pathway|metabolic pathway]] is typically composed of a series of enzyme-catalyzed steps.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The study of enzymes is known as enzymology, and a related field focuses on pseudoenzymes—proteins that have lost catalytic activity but may retain regulatory or scaffolding functions, often indicated by alterations in their [[Amino acid|amino acid]] sequences or unusual 'pseudocatalytic' behavior. Enzymes are known to catalyze over 5,000 types of biochemical reactions. Other biological catalysts include catalytic RNA molecules, or ribozymes, which are sometimes classified as enzymes despite being composed of RNA rather than protein. More recently, biomolecular condensates have been recognized as a third category of biocatalysts, capable of catalyzing reactions by creating interfaces and gradients—such as ionic gradients—that drive biochemical processes, even when their component proteins are not intrinsically catalytic.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
