	{
		title = "Equivalence principle",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Hypothesis that inertial and gravitational masses are equivalent",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Theory of relativity" },
		summary = "The **equivalence principle** is the hypothesis that the observed equivalence of gravitational and [[Mass|inertial mass]] is a consequence of nature. The weak form, known for centuries, relates to masses of any composition in free fall taking the same trajectories and landing at identical times. The extended form by [[Albert Einstein]] requires [[Theory of relativity|special relativity]] to also hold in free fall and requires the weak equivalence to be valid everywhere. This form was a critical input for the development of the theory of [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]]. The strong form requires Einstein's form to work for stellar objects.",
