	{
		title = "Erosion",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🟫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Natural processes removing soil and rock",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geomorphology" },
		summary = "**Erosion** is the action of surface processes (such as [[Surface runoff|water flow]] or [[Wind|wind]]) that removes [[Soil|soil]], [[Rock (geology)|rock]], or dissolved material from one location on the [[Earth's crust]] and then transports it to another location where it is deposited. Erosion is distinct from [[Weathering|weathering]] which involves no movement. Removal of rock or soil as clastic [[Sediment|sediment]] is referred to as physical or mechanical erosion; this contrasts with chemical erosion, where soil or rock material is removed from an area by dissolution. Eroded sediment or solutes may be transported just a few millimetres, or for thousands of kilometres.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Agents of erosion include [[Rain|rainfall]]; [[Bedrock|bedrock]] wear in [[River|rivers]]; coastal erosion by the sea and [[Wind wave|waves]]; [[Glacier|glacial]] plucking, abrasion, and scour; areal flooding; [[Aeolian processes|wind]] abrasion; [[Groundwater|groundwater]] processes; and mass movement processes in steep [[Landscape|landscapes]] like [[Landslide|landslides]] and debris flows. The rates at which such processes act control how fast a surface is eroded. Typically, physical erosion proceeds the fastest on steeply sloping surfaces, and rates may also be sensitive to some climatically controlled properties including amounts of water supplied (e.g., by rain), storminess, wind speed, wave fetch, or atmospheric temperature (especially for some ice-related processes). Feedbacks are also possible between rates of erosion and the amount of eroded material that is already carried by, for example, a river or glacier.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Escherichia coli",
		category = "Science",
