		summary = "These compounds contain a [[Functional group|distinctive functional group]]. Analogues derived from [[Oxygen|oxygen]] replaced by other [[Chalcogen|chalcogens]] belong to the ester category as well. According to some authors, organyl derivatives of acidic hydrogen of other acids are esters as well (e.g. amides), but not according to the [[International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry|IUPAC]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Glycerides are fatty acid esters of [[Glycerol|glycerol]]; they are important in biology, being one of the main classes of [[Lipid|lipids]] and comprising the bulk of animal fats and [[Vegetable oil|vegetable oils]]. Lactones are cyclic carboxylic esters; naturally occurring lactones are mainly 5- and 6-membered ring lactones. Lactones contribute to the aroma of fruits, butter, cheese, [[Vegetable|vegetables]] like [[Celery|celery]] and other foods. Esters can be formed from oxoacids (e.g. esters of [[Acetic acid|acetic acid]], carbonic acid, [[Sulfuric acid|sulfuric acid]], [[Phosphoric acid|phosphoric acid]], [[Nitric acid|nitric acid]], xanthic acid), but also from acids that do not contain oxygen (e.g. esters of thiocyanic acid and trithiocarbonic acid). Organyl esters of carboxylic acids typically have a pleasant smell; those of low molecular weight are commonly used as fragrances and are found in [[Essential oil|essential oils]] and pheromones.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Estuary",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Partially enclosed coastal body of brackish water",
		tags = { "Water", "Hydrology" },
		summary = "An **estuary** is a partially enclosed [[Coast|coastal]] body of [[Brackish water|brackish water]] where freshwater from rivers or streams meets and mixes with saltwater from the open [[Ocean|sea]]. Estuaries form transition zones between riverine and marine environments and are classified as ecotones, areas where different ecosystems overlap. They are influenced by both marine processes (such as tides, [[Wave|waves]], and [[Saline water|saline water]] intrusion) and fluvial processes (including freshwater flow and sediment input). The mixing of [[Seawater|seawater]] and [[Fresh water|freshwater]] provides high levels of nutrients both in the water column and in [[Sediment|sediment]], making estuaries among the most productive natural habitats in the world.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most existing estuaries originated during the [[Holocene]] epoch with the flooding of river-eroded or glacially scoured valleys when the sea level began to rise about 10,000–12,000 years ago. Estuaries can be classified using four fundamental criteria. These include salinity distribution, [[Geomorphology|geomorphology]], water circulation and vertical stratification, and system energetics. They can have many different names, such as [[Bay|bays]], [[Harbor|harbors]], [[Lagoon|lagoons]], inlets, or sounds, although some of these water bodies do not strictly meet the above definition of an estuary and could be fully saline. Many estuaries suffer [[Habitat destruction|degeneration]] from a variety of factors including [[Soil erosion|soil erosion]], [[Deforestation|deforestation]], overgrazing, [[Overfishing|overfishing]] and the filling of wetlands.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ethane",
		category = "Science",
