		tags = { "Cell biology", "Organelles and other cell parts" },
		summary = "A **flagellum** ([[Latin]] for 'whip' or 'scourge') is a hair-like appendage that protrudes from certain [[Plant|plant]] and animal sperm cells, from [[Mushroom|fungal]] [[Spore|spores]] (zoospores), and from a wide range of [[Microorganism|microorganisms]] to provide motility. Many [[Protist|protists]] with flagella are known as [[Flagellate|flagellates]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Across the three domains of [[Bacteria]], [[Archaea]], and Eukaryota, the flagellum has a different structure, protein composition, and mechanism of propulsion but shares the same function of providing motility. The [[Latin]] word flagellum means "whip" to describe its lash-like swimming motion. The flagella of bacteria and archaea, prokaryotes, are rotor-like structures that propel the organism via roatation. While very similar to bacterial flagella, the flagellum in archaea is called the archaellum to note its difference from the bacterial flagellum. Eukaryotic flagella differ from prokaryotic flagella as they are large membrane protrusions supported by the cytoskeleton, which wave back and forth rather than rotate. Eukaryotic cilia are very similar to flagella but are much shorter. Surface-attached cilia and flagella are used to swim or move fluid from one region to another.',
