		tags = { "Mechanics", "Fluid mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Flying" },
		summary = "**Flight** or **flying** is the [[Motion|motion]] of an [[Physical object|object]] through an [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]] or through the [[Vacuum|vacuum]] of [[Outer space|space]], in this case also called [[Spaceflight|spaceflight]], without contacting any [[Planetary surface|planetary surface]]. This can be achieved by generating aerodynamic lift associated with [[Gliding flight|gliding]] or propulsive thrust, aerostatically using [[Buoyancy|buoyancy]], or by ballistic movement.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Many things can fly, from animal aviators such as [[Bird|birds]], [[Bat|bats]] and [[Insect|insects]], to natural gliders/parachuters such as patagial animals, anemochorous [[Seed|seeds]] and ballistospores, to human inventions like [[Airplane|aircraft]] ([[Airplane|airplanes]], [[Helicopter|helicopters]], [[Airship|airships]], [[Balloon|balloons]], etc.) and [[Rocket|rockets]] which may propel [[Spacecraft|spacecraft]] and spaceplanes. The engineering aspects of flight are the purview of [[Aerospace engineering|aerospace engineering]]. It has three subfields: [[Aeronautics|aeronautics]] — the study of vehicles that travel through the atmosphere. astronautics — the study of vehicles that travel through space. ballistics — the study of the flight of projectiles.",
