	{
		title = "Flood",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Water overflow submerging usually-dry land",
		tags = { "Earth science", "Natural disasters" },
		summary = 'A **flood** is an overflow of water (or rarely other fluids) that submerges land that is usually dry. In the sense of "flowing water", the word may also be applied to the inflow of the [[Tide|tide]]. Floods are of significant concern in [[Agriculture|agriculture]], [[Civil engineering|civil engineering]] and [[Public health|public health]]. Human changes to the environment often increase the intensity and frequency of flooding. Examples for human changes are land use changes such as [[Deforestation|deforestation]] and removal of wetlands, changes in waterway course or [[Flood management|flood controls]] such as with [[Levee|levees]]. Global environmental issues also influence causes of floods, namely [[Climate change|climate change]] which causes an intensification of the water cycle and [[Sea level rise|sea level rise]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"For example, climate change makes [[Extreme weather|extreme weather events]] more frequent and stronger. This leads to more intense floods and increased flood risk. Natural types of floods include river flooding, groundwater flooding, coastal flooding, and urban flooding, sometimes known as flash flooding. Tidal flooding may include elements of both river and coastal flooding processes in estuary areas. There is also the intentional flooding of land that would otherwise remain dry. This may take place for agricultural, military, or river-management purposes. For example, agricultural flooding may occur in preparing paddy fields for the growing of semi-aquatic rice in many countries. Flooding may occur as an overflow of water from water bodies, such as a [[River|river]], [[Lake|lake]], sea or ocean. In these cases, the water overtops or breaks [[Levee|levees]], resulting in some of that water escaping its usual boundaries.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Floodplain",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Land adjacent to a water body which is flooded during periods of high water",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Fluvial landforms" },
		aliases = { "Flood plain", "Bottomlands" },
		summary = "A **floodplain** or **flood plain** or **bottomlands** is an area of land adjacent to a [[River|river]]. Floodplains stretch from the banks of a river channel to the base of the enclosing valley, and experience [[Flood|flooding]] during periods of high [[Discharge (hydrology)|discharge]]. The soils usually consist of clays, [[Silt|silts]], sands, and gravels deposited during floods.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Because of regular flooding, floodplains frequently have high [[Soil fertility|soil fertility]] since nutrients are deposited with the flood waters. This can encourage farming; some important agricultural regions, such as the [[Nile]] and Mississippi [[Drainage basin|river basins]], heavily exploit floodplains. Agricultural and urban regions have developed near or on floodplains to take advantage of the rich soil and freshwater. However, the risk of inundation has led to increasing efforts to [[Flood management|control flooding]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
