		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Aspects of fluid mechanics involving fluid flow",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Fluid mechanics" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], [[Physical chemistry|physical chemistry]], and [[Engineering|engineering]], **fluid dynamics** is a subdiscipline of [[Fluid mechanics|fluid mechanics]] that describes the flow of [[Fluid|fluids]] – [[Liquid|liquids]] and [[Gas|gases]]. It has several subdisciplines, including [[Aerodynamics|aerodynamics]] (the study of air and other gases in motion) and hydrodynamics (the study of water and other liquids in motion).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Fluid dynamics offers a systematic structure—which underlies these practical disciplines—that embraces empirical and semi-empirical laws derived from flow measurement and used to solve practical problems. The solution to a fluid dynamics problem typically involves the calculation of various properties of the fluid, such as flow velocity, [[Pressure|pressure]], [[Density|density]], and [[Temperature|temperature]], as functions of space and time. Before the twentieth century, "**hydrodynamics**" was synonymous with fluid dynamics. This is still reflected in names of some fluid dynamics topics, like magnetohydrodynamics and hydrodynamic stability, both of which can also be applied to gases.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Fluid mechanics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of physics",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Fluid mechanics" },
		summary = "**Fluid mechanics** is the branch of [[Physics|physics]] concerned with the [[Mechanics|mechanics]] of [[Fluid|fluids]] ([[Liquid|liquids]], [[Gas|gases]], and [[Plasma (physics)|plasmas]]) and the [[Force|forces]] on them. Originally applied to water (**hydromechanics**), it found applications in a wide range of disciplines, including [[Mechanical engineering|mechanical]], [[Aerospace engineering|aerospace]], [[Civil engineering|civil]], [[Chemical engineering|chemical]], and [[Biomedical engineering|biomedical engineering]], as well as [[Geophysics|geophysics]], [[Oceanography|oceanography]], [[Meteorology|meteorology]], [[Astrophysics|astrophysics]], and [[Biology|biology]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It can be divided into fluid statics, the study of various fluids at rest; and [[Fluid dynamics|fluid dynamics]], the study of the effect of forces on fluid motion. It is a branch of [[Continuum mechanics|continuum mechanics]], a subject which models matter without using the information that it is made out of atoms; that is, it models matter from a macroscopic viewpoint rather than from microscopic. Fluid mechanics, especially fluid dynamics, is an active field of research, typically mathematically complex. Many problems are partly or wholly unsolved and are best addressed by numerical methods, typically using computers. A modern discipline, called computational fluid dynamics (CFD), is devoted to this approach. Particle image velocimetry, an experimental method for visualizing and analyzing fluid flow, also takes advantage of the highly visual nature of fluid flow.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Fluorescence",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Emission of light by a substance that has absorbed light",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrodynamics" },
		summary = "**Fluorescence** is one of two kinds of photoluminescence, the emission of [[Light|light]] by a substance that has absorbed light or other [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]]. When exposed to [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] radiation, many substances will glow (fluoresce) with colored visible light. The color of the light emitted depends on the chemical composition of the substance. Fluorescent materials generally cease to glow nearly immediately when the radiation source stops. This distinguishes them from the other type of light emission, phosphorescence. Phosphorescent materials continue to emit light for some time after the radiation stops. This difference in duration is a result of [[Spin (physics)|quantum spin]] effects.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Fluorescence occurs when a [[Photon|photon]] from incoming radiation is absorbed by a [[Molecule|molecule]], exciting it to a higher [[Energy level|energy level]], followed by the emission of light as the molecule returns to a lower energy state. The emitted light may have a longer [[Wavelength|wavelength]] and, therefore, a lower [[Photon|photon]] [[Energy|energy]] than the absorbed radiation. For example, the absorbed radiation could be in the [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] region of the [[Electromagnetic spectrum|electromagnetic spectrum]] (invisible to the human eye), while the emitted light is in the [[Visible spectrum|visible region]]. This gives the fluorescent substance a distinct [[Color|color]], best seen when exposed to UV light, making it appear to glow in the dark. However, any light with a shorter wavelength may cause a material to fluoresce at a longer wavelength. Fluorescent materials may also be excited by certain wavelengths of visible light, which can mask the glow, yet their colors may appear bright and intensified.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Eddy current" },
	},
	{
