		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Fluorine** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has the [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **F** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 9. It is the lightest [[Halogen|halogen]] and exists at [[Standard temperature and pressure|standard conditions]] as pale yellow diatomic gas. Fluorine is extremely [[Reactivity (chemistry)|reactive]] as it reacts with all other [[Periodic table|elements]] except for the light [[Noble gas|noble gases]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Among the elements, fluorine ranks [[Abundance of the chemical elements|24th in cosmic abundance and 13th in crustal abundance]]. Fluorite, the primary mineral source of fluorine, which gave the element its name, was first described in 1529; as it was added to metal [[Ore|ores]] to lower their melting points for [[Smelting|smelting]], the Latin verb meaning to flow gave the mineral its name. Only in 1886 did French chemist Henri Moissan isolate elemental fluorine using low-temperature [[Electrolysis|electrolysis]], a process still employed for modern production. Industrial production of fluorine gas for uranium enrichment, its largest application, began during the Manhattan Project in [[World War II]]. Owing to the expense of refining pure fluorine, most commercial applications use fluorine compounds, with about half of mined fluorite used in [[Steelmaking|steelmaking]].",
			},
