	{
		title = "Gamma ray",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Penetrating form of electromagnetic radiation",
		tags = { "Physics", "Nuclear physics" },
		summary = "Lower energy gamma radiation overlaps the upper end of [[X-ray]] radiation; they are distinguished by their different origins. Gamma ray [[Photon|photons]] have [[Photon energy|photon energy]] at the lower end from 104 eV to 107 eV; ultra-high-energy gamma rays have energies over 1014 eV. Paul Villard, a French [[Chemist|chemist]] and [[Physicist|physicist]], discovered gamma radiation in 1900 while studying [[Radioactivity|radiation]] emitted by [[Radium|radium]]. In 1903, [[Ernest Rutherford]] named this radiation gamma rays based on their relatively strong penetration of [[Matter|matter]]; in 1900, he had already named two less penetrating types of decay radiation (discovered by [[Henri Becquerel]]) alpha rays and beta rays in ascending order of penetrating power.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Gamma rays from radioactive decay are in the energy range from ten kiloelectronvolts (keV) to 10 megaelectronvolts (MeV), corresponding to the typical energy levels in nuclei with reasonably long lifetimes. The energy spectrum of gamma rays can be used to identify the decaying radionuclides using gamma spectroscopy. Very-high-energy gamma rays in the 100–1000 teraelectronvolt (TeV) range have been observed from astronomical sources such as the Cygnus X-3 microquasar. Natural sources of gamma rays originating on Earth are mostly a result of radioactive decay and secondary radiation from atmospheric interactions with [[Cosmic ray|cosmic ray]] particles. However, there are other rare natural sources, such as terrestrial gamma-ray flashes, which produce gamma rays from electron action upon the nucleus.",
			},
