		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "State of matter",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "States of matter" },
		summary = "**Gas** is a [[State of matter|state of matter]] with neither fixed [[Volume|volume]] nor fixed [[Shape|shape]]. It is a compressible form of [[Fluid|fluid]], in contrast to a [[Liquid|liquid]]. A pure gas consists of individual [[Atom|atoms]] (e.g. a [[Noble gas|noble gas]] like [[Neon|neon]]), or [[Molecule|molecules]] (e.g. [[Oxygen|oxygen]] (O2) or carbon dioxide). Pure gases can also be [[Mixture|mixed]] together such as in the [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]]. What distinguishes gases from liquids and [[Solid|solids]] is the vast separation of the individual gas [[Particle|particles]]. This separation can make some gases invisible to the human observer.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The gaseous state of matter occurs between the liquid and [[Plasma (physics)|plasma]] states, the latter of which provides the upper-temperature boundary for gases. Bounding the lower end of the temperature scale lie degenerative quantum gases which are gaining increasing attention. High-density atomic gases super-cooled to very low temperatures are classified by their statistical behavior as either Bose gases or Fermi gases. For a comprehensive listing of these exotic states of matter, see list of states of matter.",
			},
