		tags = { "Subatomic particles", "Bosons" },
		summary = "In [[Particle physics|particle physics]], a **gauge boson** is a [[Boson|bosonic]] [[Elementary particle|elementary particle]] that acts as the [[Force carrier|force carrier]] for elementary fermions. Elementary particles whose interactions are described by a [[Gauge theory|gauge theory]] interact with each other by the exchange of gauge bosons, usually as virtual particles.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Photon|Photons]], W and Z bosons, and [[Gluon|gluons]] are gauge bosons. All known gauge bosons have a spin of 1 and therefore are vector bosons. For comparison, the [[Higgs boson]] has spin zero and the hypothetical graviton has a spin of 2. Gauge bosons are different from the other kinds of bosons: first, fundamental scalar bosons (the Higgs boson); second, [[Meson|mesons]], which are composite bosons, made of [[Quark|quarks]]; third, larger composite, non-force-carrying bosons, such as certain [[Atom|atoms]].",
