	{
		title = "Gene",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Sequence of DNA that determines traits in an organism",
		tags = { "Genetics and taxonomy" },
		summary = "In [[Biology|biology]], the word **gene** has two meanings. The Mendelian gene is a basic unit of [[Heredity|heredity]]. The molecular gene is a sequence of [[Nucleotide|nucleotides]] in [[DNA]] that is transcribed to produce [[RNA]]. There are two types of molecular genes: protein-coding genes and non-coding genes. During [[Gene expression|gene expression]] (the synthesis of RNA or protein from a gene), DNA is first [[Transcription (biology)|copied into RNA]]. RNA can be directly functional or be the intermediate template for the synthesis of a protein.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Genes and their regulatory sequences are mainly responsible for determining the physical traits, or the [[Phenotype|phenotype]] of an organism. The genotype is the total amount of DNA in an organism including genes, other functional elements, and non-functional DNA. A gene can acquire [[Mutation|mutations]] in its sequence, leading to different variants, known as alleles, in the [[Population (biology)|population]]. These alleles encode slightly different versions of a gene, which may be expressed as different phenotypic traits. Populations [[Evolution|evolve]] when the frequencies of alleles shift due mostly to [[Evolution|natural selection]] and [[Genetic drift|genetic drift]]. Some of these alleles are located in genes.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Gene expression",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Conversion of a gene's sequence into a mature gene product or products",
		tags = { "Genetics and taxonomy" },
		summary = "**Gene expression** is the process by which the information contained within a [[Gene|gene]] is used to produce a functional gene product, such as a [[Protein|protein]] or a functional [[RNA]] molecule. This process involves multiple steps, including the [[Transcription (biology)|transcription]] of the gene's sequence into RNA. For protein-coding genes, this RNA is further [[Translation (biology)|translated]] into a chain of amino acids that folds into a protein, while for non-coding genes, the resulting RNA itself serves a functional role in the cell. Gene expression enables cells to utilize the genetic information in genes to carry out a wide range of biological functions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While expression levels can be regulated in response to cellular needs and environmental changes, some genes are expressed continuously with little variation.",
			},
