		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geological history" },
		summary = "The **geological history of Earth** follows the major geological events in Earth's past based on the [[Geologic time scale|geologic time scale]], a system of chronological measurement based on the study of the planet's rock layers ([[Stratigraphy|stratigraphy]]). Earth formed [[Age of Earth|approximately 4.54 billion years ago]] through accretion from the solar nebula, a disk-shaped mass of dust and gas remaining from the formation of the Sun, which also formed the rest of the [[Solar System]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Initially, Earth was molten due to extreme [[Volcanism|volcanism]] and frequent collisions with other bodies. Eventually, the outer layer of the planet cooled to form a solid [[Crust (geology)|crust]] when water began accumulating in the atmosphere. The [[Moon]] formed soon afterwards, possibly as a result of the impact of a protoplanet with Earth. Outgassing and volcanic activity produced the primordial atmosphere. [[Condensation|Condensing]] [[Water vapor|water vapor]], augmented by ice delivered from [[Asteroid|asteroids]], produced the oceans. However, in 2020, researchers reported that sufficient water to fill the oceans may have always been on [[Earth]] since the beginning of the planet's formation. As the surface continually reshaped itself over hundreds of millions of years, continents formed and broke apart. They [[Plate tectonics|migrated across the surface]], occasionally combining to form a [[Supercontinent|supercontinent]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event", "Permian–Triassic extinction event" },
