		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Scientific study of landforms",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geomorphology" },
		summary = "**Geomorphology** is the scientific study of the origin and evolution of [[Topography|topographic]] and [[Bathymetry|bathymetric]] features generated by physical, chemical or biological processes operating at or near [[Earth|Earth's surface]]. Geomorphologists seek to understand why [[Landscape|landscapes]] look the way they do, to understand [[Landform|landform]] and [[Terrain|terrain]] history and dynamics and to predict changes through a combination of field observations, physical experiments and numerical modeling. Geomorphologists work within disciplines such as [[Physical geography|physical geography]], [[Geology|geology]], [[Geodesy|geodesy]], engineering geology, [[Archaeology|archaeology]], [[Climatology|climatology]], and geotechnical engineering. This broad base of interests contributes to many research styles and interests within the field.",
	},
	{
		title = "Geophysics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physics of the Earth and its vicinity",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geophysics" },
		summary = "**Geophysics** is a physical science concerned with the processes and [[Physical property|properties]] of [[Earth]] and its surrounding space environment, studied using [[Quantitative research|quantitative]] and [[Observation|observational]] methods. It focuses primarily on Earth’s shape and its [[Gravity of Earth|gravitational]], [[Earth's magnetic field|magnetic]], and [[Electromagnetic field|electromagnetic fields]]. It also studies internal structure, [[Earth|composition]], and dynamics, and their surface expression in [[Tectonics|tectonics]], [[Volcanism|volcanism]], and rock formation. Geophysics also encompasses a broader Earth-system and [[Planetary science|planetary perspective]], including the [[Ocean|oceans]], [[Atmosphere of Earth|atmosphere]], [[Cryosphere|cryosphere]], [[Ionosphere|ionosphere]], magnetosphere, as well as solar–terrestrial interactions and analogous processes on the [[Moon]], other planets, and their satellites.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is one of the oldest [[Science|sciences]], dating back to [[Ancient history|antiquity]] with the development of early seismometers and magnetic compasses, and later extending to [[Isaac Newton|Newtonian]] analyses of tides, precession, and Earth’s physical properties. Today, geophysics is pursued for fundamental scientific understanding and practical applications, including the exploration of mineral and energy resources, assessment and mitigation of natural hazards, [[Groundwater|groundwater]] and [[Natural environment|environmental]] studies, [[Archaeological excavation|archaeological investigations]], and environmental monitoring. Geophysics integrates [[Theory|theory]], observation, and [[Experiment|experiment]] to [[Research|investigate]] Earth and planetary systems across diverse spatial and temporal scales. Its [[Scientific method|methods]] include [[Field research|field measurements]], [[Laboratory|laboratory]] experiments, [[Remote sensing|remote sensing]], numerical modelling, and data analysis of both natural and instrumented records, including [[Signal processing|signal processing]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Germanium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Germanium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has the [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Ge** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 32. It is lustrous, hard-brittle, grayish-white and similar in appearance to [[Silicon|silicon]]. It is a [[Metalloid|metalloid]] (sometimes considered a nonmetal) in the carbon group that is chemically similar to [[Silicon|silicon]]. Like silicon, germanium naturally [[Chemical reaction|reacts]] and forms complexes with [[Oxygen|oxygen]] in nature.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Because it seldom appears in high concentration, germanium was found comparatively late in the discovery of the elements. Germanium ranks 50th [[Abundance of elements in Earth's crust|in abundance of the elements in the Earth's crust]]. In 1869, [[Dmitri Mendeleev]] predicted its existence and some of its [[Chemical property|properties]] from its position on his [[Periodic table|periodic table]], and called the element **ekasilicon**. On February 6, 1886, Clemens Winkler at Freiberg University found the new element, along with [[Silver|silver]] and [[Sulfur|sulfur]], in the mineral argyrodite. Winkler named the element after Germany, his country of birth. Germanium is mined primarily from sphalerite (the primary ore of [[Zinc|zinc]]), though germanium is also recovered commercially from silver, [[Lead|lead]], and [[Copper|copper]] ores. Elemental germanium is used as a semiconductor in [[Transistor|transistors]] and various other electronic devices. Historically, the first decade of semiconductor electronics was based entirely on germanium.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Geyser",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Natural explosive eruption of hot water",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Volcanic landforms" },
		summary = "A **geyser** is a spring with an intermittent water discharge ejected turbulently and accompanied by steam. The formation of geysers is fairly rare and is caused by particular hydrogeological conditions that exist only in a few places on Earth.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Generally, geyser field sites are located near active volcanic areas, and the geyser effect is due to the proximity of [[Magma|magma]]. Surface water works its way down to an average depth of around 2000 m where it contacts hot rocks. The pressurized water boils, and this causes the geyser effect of hot water and steam spraying out of the geyser's surface vent. A geyser's eruptive activity may change or cease due to ongoing [[Deposition (geology)|deposition]] of [[Mineral|minerals]] within their plumbing, exchange of functions with nearby [[Hot spring|hot springs]], [[Earthquake|earthquake]] influences, and human intervention. Like many other natural phenomena, geysers are not unique to Earth. Jet-like eruptions, often called cryogeysers, have been observed on several of the [[Natural satellite|moons]] of the outer Solar System.",
			},
		},
	},
