		aliases = { "Glacial landforms" },
		summary = "**Glacial landforms** are [[Landform|landforms]] created by the action of [[Glacier|glaciers]]. Most of today's glacial landforms were created by the movement of large [[Ice sheet|ice sheets]] during the [[Quaternary glaciation|Quaternary glaciations]]. Some areas, like Fennoscandia and the southern [[Andes]], have extensive occurrences of glacial landforms; other areas, such as the [[Sahara]], display rare and very old fossil glacial landforms.",
	},
	{
		title = "Glacier",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Persistent body of ice that moves downhill under its own weight",
		tags = { "Water", "Glaciology" },
		summary = "A **glacier** is a persistent body of natural [[Ice|ice]], a form of [[Rock (geology)|rock]], that is constantly moving under its own weight. A glacier forms where the accumulation of [[Snow|snow]] exceeds its ablation over many years, often centuries. It slowly flows and deforms under [[Stress (mechanics)|stresses]] induced by gravity, undergoing both [[Ductility|ductile]] and brittle deformation, and acquiring distinguishing surface features, such as crevasses and seracs. As a glacier moves, it abrades underlying rock and debris to create [[Glacial landform|glacial landforms]], such as cirques, [[Moraine|moraines]], and [[Fjord|fjords]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although a glacier may flow into a body of water, and can include [[Ice shelf|ice shelves]] and ice tongues, it originates only on land and is distinct from the much thinner [[Sea ice|sea ice]] and lake ice that form on the surface of freezing bodies of water. On Earth, 99% of glacial ice is contained within vast [[Ice sheet|ice sheets]] (also known as \"continental glaciers\") in the [[Polar regions|polar regions]], but glaciers may be found in [[Mountain range|mountain ranges]] on every continent other than the Australian mainland, including Oceania's high-latitude oceanic island countries such as New Zealand. Between latitudes 35°N and 35°S, glaciers occur only in the [[Himalayas]], [[Andes]], and a few high mountains in East Africa, Mexico, [[New Guinea]] and on Zard-Kuh in Iran. With more than 7,000 known glaciers, [[Pakistan]] has more glacial ice than any other country outside the polar regions. Glaciers cover about 10% of Earth's land surface.",
			},
