		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Naturally produced monosaccharide",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		summary = "**Glucose** is a [[Sugar|sugar]] with the [[Chemical formula|molecular formula]] C6H12O6. It is the most abundant monosaccharide, a subcategory of [[Carbohydrate|carbohydrates]]. It is made from water and carbon dioxide during [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]] by [[Plant|plants]] and most [[Algae|algae]]. It is used by plants to make [[Cellulose|cellulose]], the most abundant carbohydrate in the world, for use in [[Cell wall|cell walls]], and by all living [[Organism|organisms]] to make [[Adenosine triphosphate|adenosine triphosphate]] (ATP), which is used by the cell as energy. Glucose is often abbreviated as **Glc**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In energy metabolism, glucose is the most important source of energy in all [[Organism|organisms]]. Glucose for metabolism is stored as a [[Polymer|polymer]], in plants mainly as amylose and amylopectin, and in animals as [[Glycogen|glycogen]]. Glucose is a monosaccharide containing six carbon atoms and an [[Aldehyde|aldehyde]] group, and is therefore an aldohexose. The glucose molecule can exist in an open-chain (acyclic) as well as ring (cyclic) form. Glucose is naturally occurring and is found in its free state in fruits and other parts of plants. In animals, it is released from the breakdown of glycogen in a process known as glycogenolysis. Glucose, as intravenous sugar solution, is on the World Health Organization's List of Essential Medicines. It is also on the list in combination with [[Sodium chloride|sodium chloride]] (table salt). The suffix -ose is a chemical classifier denoting a sugar.",
			},
		},
