	{
		title = "Grazing",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Feeding livestock on forage",
		tags = { "Zoology", "Ethology" },
		summary = "In [[Agriculture|agriculture]], **grazing** is a method of [[Animal husbandry|animal husbandry]] whereby domestic [[Livestock|livestock]] are allowed outdoors to free range (roam around) and consume wild [[Vegetation|vegetation]] in order to convert the otherwise indigestible (by the human gut) [[Cellulose|cellulose]] within [[Grass|grass]] and other [[Forage|forages]] into [[Meat|meat]], [[Milk|milk]], [[Wool|wool]] and other [[Animal product|animal products]]. Grazing is often done on lands that are unsuitable for arable farming, although there are occasions where [[Arable land|arable lands]] and even prior farmlands are intentionally kept or converted to pastures to raise commercially valuable grazing animals.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Farmers may employ many different strategies of grazing for [[Crop yield|optimum production]]: grazing may be continuous, seasonal, or rotational within a grazing period. Longer rotations are found in ley farming, alternating arable and fodder crops; in rest rotation, deferred rotation, and mob grazing, giving grasses a longer time to recover or leaving land fallow. Patch-burn sets up a rotation of fresh grass after burning with two years of rest. Conservation grazing proposes to use grazing animals to improve the biodiversity of a site. Grazing livestock on open grasslands, i.e. [[Pastoralism|pastoralism]], has existed as a [[Human|human]] practice since the [[History of agriculture|beginning of agriculture]]; [[Sheep|sheep]] and [[Goat|goats]] were [[Domestication|domesticated]] by [[Nomad|nomads]] before the first permanent settlements were constructed around 7000 BC, enabling [[Cattle|cattle]] and [[Pig|pigs]] to be kept, and people who supervise grazing livestock are called shepherds.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Great Oxidation Event",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Paleoproterozoic surge in atmospheric oxygen",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geological history" },
		summary = "This began approximately 2.46–2.426 billion years ago [[Year|(Ga)]] during the Siderian period and ended around 2.06 billion years ago during the Rhyacian. Geological, [[Isotope|isotopic]], and chemical evidence suggests that biologically produced molecular oxygen ([[Oxygen|dioxygen]] or O2) started to accumulate in the [[Archean]] prebiotic atmosphere by microbial [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]]. It changed the atmosphere from a weakly reducing state practically devoid of oxygen into an oxidizing one containing abundant free oxygen, with oxygen levels being as high as 10% of the modern atmospheric level by the end of the GOE.",
		related = { "Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event", "Permian–Triassic extinction event" },
	},
	{
		title = "Green",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Additive primary color visible between cyan and yellow",
		tags = { "Color", "Color terms" },
		summary = "**Green** is the [[Color|color]] between [[Cyan|cyan]] and [[Yellow|yellow]] on the [[Visible spectrum|visible spectrum]]. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 [[Nanometre|nm]]. In subtractive color systems, used in [[Painting|painting]] and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the [[RGB color model]], used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with [[Red|red]] and [[Blue|blue]], which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is [[Chlorophyll|chlorophyll]], the chemical by which plants [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesize]] and convert sunlight into chemical energy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as [[Camouflage|camouflage]]. Several [[Mineral|minerals]] have a green color, including the [[Emerald|emerald]], which is colored green by its [[Chromium|chromium]] content. During [[Post-classical history|post-classical]] and [[Early modern period|early modern]] Europe, green was the color commonly associated with wealth, merchants, bankers, and the gentry, while red was reserved for the nobility. For this reason, the costume of the [[Mona Lisa]] by [[Leonardo da Vinci]] and the benches in the British House of Commons are green while those in the [[House of Lords]] are red. It also has a long historical tradition as the color of [[Ireland]] and of Gaelic culture. It is the historic color of Islam, representing the lush vegetation of [[Paradise]]. It was the color of the banner of Muhammad, and is found in the flags of nearly all Islamic countries.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Frequency", "≈575–525" },
			{ "Source", "X11" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Greenhouse effect",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Atmospheric heat retention",
		tags = { "Air", "Climatology" },
		summary = "The **greenhouse effect** occurs when [[Greenhouse gas|heat-trapping gases in a planet's atmosphere]] prevent the planet from losing heat to space, raising its surface temperature. Surface heating can happen from an internal heat source (as in the case of [[Jupiter]]) or come from an external source, such as a host star. In the case of [[Earth]], the [[Sun]] emits shortwave radiation ([[Sunlight|sunlight]]) that passes through greenhouse gases to heat the Earth's surface. In response, the Earth's surface emits longwave radiation that is mostly [[Absorption (electromagnetic radiation)|absorbed]] by greenhouse gases, reducing the rate at which the Earth can cool off.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Without the greenhouse effect, the Earth's average surface temperature would be as cold as −18 °C. This is much less than the 20th century average of about 14 °C. In addition to naturally present greenhouse gases, burning of [[Fossil fuel|fossil fuels]] has [[Greenhouse gas emissions|increased amounts]] of carbon dioxide and [[Methane|methane]] in the atmosphere. As a result, at 2023, global warming of about 1.2 C-change has occurred since the [[Industrial Revolution]], with the global average surface temperature increasing at a rate of 0.18 C-change per decade since 1981. All objects with a temperature above [[Absolute zero|absolute zero]] emit thermal radiation. The [[Electromagnetic spectrum|wavelengths of thermal radiation]] emitted by the Sun and Earth differ because their surface temperatures are different. The Sun has a surface temperature of 5,500 °C, so it emits most of its energy as shortwave radiation in near-infrared and visible wavelengths (as sunlight).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Grey",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Intermediate color between black and white",
		tags = { "Color", "Color terms" },
		summary = "**Grey** or **gray** is an intermediate color between [[Black|black]] and [[White|white]]. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of [[Lead|lead]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The first recorded use of grey as a color name in the English language was in 700 [[Common Era|CE]]. Grey is the dominant spelling in European and Commonwealth English, while gray is more common in American English; however, both spellings are valid in both varieties of English. In Europe and North America, surveys show that grey is the color most commonly associated with neutrality, [[Conformity|conformity]], [[Boredom|boredom]], uncertainty, [[Old age|old age]], indifference, and humility. Only one percent of respondents chose it as their favorite color.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Source", "[[Web colors|HTML/CSS]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Groundwater",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Water located beneath the ground surface",
		tags = { "Water", "Hydrology" },
		summary = "**Groundwater** is the [[Water|water]] present beneath [[Earth]]'s surface in rock and soil pore spaces and in the fractures of [[Stratum|rock formations]]. About 30 percent of all readily available [[Fresh water|fresh water]] in the world is groundwater. A unit of rock or an unconsolidated deposit is called an [[Aquifer|aquifer]] when it can yield a usable quantity of water. The depth at which [[Soil|soil]] pore spaces or fractures and voids in rock become completely saturated with water is called the water table. Groundwater is recharged from the surface; it may discharge from the surface naturally at springs and seeps, and can form [[Oasis|oases]] or [[Wetland|wetlands]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Groundwater is also often withdrawn for agricultural, municipal, and industrial use by constructing and operating extraction wells. The study of the distribution and movement of groundwater is hydrogeology, also called groundwater [[Hydrology|hydrology]]. Typically, groundwater is thought of as water flowing through shallow aquifers, but, in the technical sense, it can also contain soil moisture, [[Permafrost|permafrost]] (frozen soil), immobile water in very low permeability [[Bedrock|bedrock]], and deep geothermal or oil formation water. Groundwater is hypothesized to provide lubrication that can possibly influence the movement of [[Fault (geology)|faults]]. It is likely that much of [[Earth]]'s subsurface contains some water, which may be mixed with other fluids in some instances. Groundwater is often cheaper, more convenient and less vulnerable to [[Water pollution|pollution]] than surface water. Therefore, it is commonly used for public [[Drinking water|drinking water]] supplies.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Group (periodic table)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Column of elements in the periodic table of the chemical elements",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], a **group** (also known as a **family**) is a column of elements in the [[Periodic table|periodic table of the chemical elements]]. There are 18 numbered groups in the periodic table; the 14 f-block columns, between groups 2 and 3, are not numbered. The elements in a group have similar physical or chemical characteristics of the outermost [[Electron shell|electron shells]] of their atoms (i.e., the same core charge), because most chemical properties are dominated by the orbital location of the outermost electron.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The modern numbering system of "group 1" to "group 18" has been recommended by the [[International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry]] (IUPAC) since 1988. The 1-18 system is based on each atom\'s s, p and d electrons beyond those in atoms of the preceding [[Noble gas|noble gas]]. Two older incompatible naming schemes can assign the same number to different groups depending on the system being used. The older schemes were used by the Chemical Abstract Service (CAS, more popular in the United States), and by IUPAC before 1988 (more popular in Europe). The system of eighteen groups is generally accepted by the chemistry community, but some dissent exists about membership of elements number 1 and 2 ([[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] and [[Helium|helium]]).',
			},
		},
	},
