	{
		title = "Harmonic oscillator",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physical system that responds to a restoring force proportional to displacement",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Basics" },
		summary = "In [[Classical mechanics|classical mechanics]], a **harmonic oscillator** is a [[System|system]] that, when displaced from its [[Mechanical equilibrium|equilibrium]] position, experiences a restoring force F [[Proportionality (mathematics)|proportional]] to the displacement x: where k is a positive [[Coefficient|constant]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The harmonic oscillator model is important in [[Physics|physics]], because any mass subject to a force in stable equilibrium acts as a harmonic oscillator for small vibrations. Harmonic oscillators occur widely in nature and are exploited in many manmade devices, such as [[Clock|clocks]] and radio circuits. If F is the only force acting on the system, the system is called a **simple harmonic oscillator**, and it undergoes [[Simple harmonic motion|simple harmonic motion]]: sinusoidal [[Oscillation|oscillations]] about the equilibrium point, with a constant [[Amplitude|amplitude]] and a constant [[Frequency|frequency]] (which does not depend on the amplitude). If a frictional force (damping) proportional to the [[Velocity|velocity]] is also present, the harmonic oscillator is described as a **damped oscillator**. Depending on the friction coefficient, the system can: Oscillate with a frequency lower than in the undamped case, and an [[Amplitude|amplitude]] decreasing with time (underdamped oscillator).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Heat",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of energy transfer",
		tags = { "Physics", "Thermodynamics" },
		summary = "In [[Thermodynamics|thermodynamics]], **heat** is defined as [[Energy|energy]] in transfer between a body and its surroundings, other than through [[Work (thermodynamics)|thermodynamic work]] or through transfer of matter.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Rather than by mechanical or other effects in the surroundings, thermodynamic work is defined through changes in the system's macroscopic state variables, in conjugate pairs such as pressure and volume, or magnetisation and magnetic field strength. The thermodynamic definition of heat is by exclusion of other defined modes of transfer in order to ensure a strict logical distinction. In the process of transfer, heat is not necessarily conserved, but can be generated (though not destroyed) by friction. In thermodynamics, [[Temperature|temperature]] is defined in macroscopic terms, through the concepts of heat and work. Heat is not a state variable or state function; it is a process quantity. A thermodynamic system does not contain heat. Heat can enter or leave a body of matter by thermal conduction, by [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]], or through [[Friction|friction]] due to macroscopic mechanical movement.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Dimension", "wikidata" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Heat capacity",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physical property of matter",
		tags = { "Physics", "Thermodynamics" },
		aliases = { "Thermal capacity" },
		summary = "**Heat capacity** or **thermal capacity** is a [[Physical quantity|physical property]] of [[Matter|matter]], defined as the amount of [[Heat|heat]] that must be supplied to an object to produce a unit change in its [[Temperature|temperature]]. The [[International System of Units|SI unit]] of heat capacity is [[Joule|joule]] per [[Kelvin|kelvin]] (J/K). It quantifies the ability of a material or system to store thermal energy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Heat capacity is an extensive property. The corresponding intensive property is the specific heat capacity, found by dividing the heat capacity of an object by its mass. Dividing the heat capacity by the amount of substance in [[Mole (unit)|moles]] yields its molar heat capacity. The volumetric heat capacity measures the heat capacity per [[Volume|volume]]. In [[Architecture|architecture]] and [[Civil engineering|civil engineering]], the heat capacity of a building is often referred to as its thermal mass.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Heat transfer",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Thermal engineering discipline concerning transfer of heat in physical systems",
		tags = { "Physics", "Thermodynamics" },
		summary = "**Heat transfer** is a discipline of thermal engineering that concerns the generation, use, conversion, and exchange of [[Thermal energy|thermal energy]] ([[Heat|heat]]) between physical systems. Heat transfer is classified into various mechanisms, such as thermal conduction, thermal convection, thermal radiation, transfer of energy by phase changes, and evaporative cooling. Engineers also consider the transfer of mass of differing chemical species (mass transfer in the form of advection), either cold or hot, to achieve heat transfer. While these mechanisms have distinct characteristics, they often occur simultaneously in the same system.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Heat conduction, also called diffusion, is the direct microscopic exchanges of kinetic energy of particles (such as molecules) or quasiparticles (such as lattice waves) through the boundary between two systems. When an object is at a different [[Temperature|temperature]] from another body or its surroundings, [[Heat|heat]] flows so that the body and the surroundings reach the same temperature, at which point they are in thermal equilibrium. Such spontaneous heat transfer always occurs from a region of high temperature to another region of lower temperature, as described in the [[Second law of thermodynamics|second law of thermodynamics]]. Heat convection occurs when the bulk flow of a fluid (gas or liquid) carries its heat through the fluid. All convective processes also move heat partly by diffusion, as well.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hectare",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Metric unit of area",
		tags = { "Dimensional space", "Area" },
		summary = "The **hectare** is a non-SI metric unit of [[Area|area]] equal to a square with 100-[[Metre|metre]] sides (1 hm2), that is, 10,000 square metres (10,000 m2), and is primarily used in the measurement of land. There are 100 hectares in one square kilometre. An [[Acre|acre]] is about 1.000 acres and thus one hectare is about 1.000 ha.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In 1795, when the metric system was introduced, the **are** was defined as 100 square metres, or one square decametre, and the hectare ("hecto-" + "are") was thus 100 ares or 1/100 km2 (10000 square metres). When the metric system was further rationalised in 1960, resulting in the [[International System of Units]] (SI), the are was not included as a recognised unit, and from 2026 the hectare is no longer described by the [[International Bureau of Weights and Measures]] (BIPM) as accepted for use with the SI.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Symbol", "ha" },
		},
