	{
		title = "Helium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🎈",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "He" },
		summary = "**Helium** (from ) is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **He** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 2. Its [[Boiling point|boiling point]] is the lowest among all the [[Chemical element|elements]], and it does not have a [[Melting point|melting point]] at standard pressures. It is the second-lightest and second-most [[Abundance of the chemical elements|abundant element]] in the observable [[Universe|universe]], after [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]]. It is present at about 24% of the total elemental mass, which is more than 12 times the mass of all the heavier elements combined. Its abundance is similar to this in both the [[Sun]] and [[Jupiter]], because of the very high [[Nuclear binding energy|nuclear binding energy]] (per [[Nucleon|nucleon]]) of helium-4 with respect to the next three elements after helium.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"This helium-4 binding energy also accounts for why it is a product of both [[Nuclear fusion|nuclear fusion]] and [[Radioactivity|radioactive decay]]. The most common isotope of helium in the universe is helium-4, the vast majority of which was formed during the [[Big Bang]]. Large amounts of new helium are created by nuclear fusion of hydrogen in [[Star|stars]]. Helium was first detected as an unknown, yellow [[Spectral line|spectral line]] signature in sunlight during a solar eclipse in 1868 by Georges Rayet, Captain C. T. Haig, Norman R. Pogson, and Lieutenant John Herschel, and was subsequently confirmed by French astronomer Jules Janssen. Janssen is often jointly credited with detecting the element, along with Norman Lockyer. Janssen recorded the helium spectral line during the solar eclipse of 1868, while Lockyer observed it from Britain. However, only Lockyer proposed that the line was due to a new element, which he named after the Sun.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Hafnium" },
	},
	{
		title = "Herbivore",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Organism that eats mostly or exclusively plant material",
		tags = { "Zoology", "Ethology" },
		aliases = { "Herbivorous" },
		summary = "A **herbivore** is an [[Animal|animal]] anatomically or physiologically [[Evolution|evolved]] to feed on [[Plant|plants]], especially upon [[Vascular tissue|vascular tissues]] such as foliage, [[Fruit|fruits]] or [[Seed|seeds]], as the main component of its [[Diet (nutrition)|diet]]. These more broadly also encompass animals that eat non-vascular autotrophs such as [[Moss|mosses]], [[Algae|algae]] and [[Lichen|lichens]], but do not include those feeding on [[Decomposition|decomposed]] [[Detritus|plant matters]] (i.e. [[Detritivore|detritivores]]) or macrofungi (i.e. fungivores).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As a result of their plant-based diet, herbivorous animals typically have mouth structures (jaws or mouthparts) well [[Adaptation|adapted]] to mechanically break down plant materials, and their digestive systems have special [[Enzyme|enzymes]] (e.g. amylase and cellulase) to digest [[Polysaccharide|polysaccharides]]. Grazing herbivores such as [[Horse|horses]] and [[Cattle|cattles]] have wide flat-crowned teeth that are better adapted for grinding [[Grass|grass]], tree bark and other tougher lignin-containing materials, and many of them evolved [[Ruminant|rumination]] or cecotropic behaviors to better extract nutrients from plants. A large percentage of herbivores also have mutualistic gut flora made up of [[Bacteria|bacteria]] and protozoans that help to degrade the [[Cellulose|cellulose]] in plants, whose heavily cross-linking [[Polymer|polymer]] structure makes it far more difficult to digest than the [[Protein|protein]]- and [[Fat|fat]]-rich animal [[Tissue (biology)|tissues]] that [[Carnivore|carnivores]] eat.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Herd",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of social group in some animals",
		tags = { "Zoology", "Ethology" },
		summary = "A **herd** is a social group of certain animals of the same species, either wild or [[Domestication|domestic]]. The form of collective animal behavior associated with this is called **herding**. These animals are known as gregarious animals.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term herd is generally applied to mammals, and most particularly to the grazing [[Ungulate|ungulates]] that classically display this behaviour. Different terms are used for similar groupings in other species; in the case of birds, for example, the word is flocking, but flock may also be used for mammals, particularly [[Sheep|sheep]] or [[Goat|goats]]. Large groups of [[Carnivora|carnivores]] are usually called packs, and in nature a herd is classically subject to [[Predation|predation]] from pack hunters. Special collective nouns may be used for particular taxa (for example a flock of geese, if not in flight, is sometimes called a gaggle) but for theoretical discussions of behavioural [[Ecology|ecology]], the generic term herd can be used for all such kinds of assemblage. The word herd, as a noun, can also refer to one who controls, possesses and has care for such groups of animals when they are domesticated.",
			},
		},
	},
