	{
		title = "History of Earth",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Records of Earth's development",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geological history" },
		summary = "The natural **history of Earth** concerns the development of [[Planet|planet]] [[Earth]] from its formation to the present day. Nearly all branches of [[Natural science|natural science]] have contributed to the understanding of the main events of Earth's past, characterized by constant [[Geology|geological]] change and biological [[Evolution|evolution]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The [[Geologic time scale|geological time scale]] (GTS), as defined by international convention, depicts the large spans of time from the beginning of Earth to the present, and its divisions chronicle some definitive events of Earth history. Earth formed around 4.54 billion years ago, approximately one-third the [[Age of the universe|age of the universe]], by [[Accretion (astrophysics)|accretion]] from the [[Formation and evolution of the Solar System|solar nebula]]. Volcanic outgassing probably created the primordial [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]] and then the ocean, but the early atmosphere contained almost no [[Oxygen|oxygen]]. Much of Earth was molten because of frequent collisions with other bodies which led to extreme volcanism. While Earth was in its earliest stage (Early Earth), a giant impact collision with a planet-sized body named Theia is thought to have formed the Moon. Over time, Earth cooled, causing the formation of a solid [[Crust (geology)|crust]], and allowing liquid water on the surface.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Late Ordovician mass extinction" },
	},
	{
