	{
		title = "History of life",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "none",
		tags = { "Evolution" },
		summary = "The **history of life** on [[Earth]] traces the processes by which living and extinct [[Organism|organisms]] evolved, from the earliest [[Abiogenesis|emergence of life]] to the present day. Earth formed about 4.54 ± 0.05 billion years ago (abbreviated as Ga, for gigaannum) and evidence suggests that [[Life|life]] emerged prior to 3.7 Ga. The similarities among all known present-day [[Species|species]] indicate that they have diverged through the process of [[Evolution|evolution]] from a [[Common descent|common ancestor]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The earliest clear evidence of life comes from biogenic carbon signatures and stromatolite fossils discovered in 3.7 billion-year-old metasedimentary rocks from western [[Greenland]]. In 2015, possible "remains of biotic life" were found in 4.1 billion-year-old rocks in [[Western Australia]]. There is further evidence of possibly the oldest forms of life in the form of fossilized [[Microorganism|microorganisms]] in [[Hydrothermal vent|hydrothermal vent]] precipitates from the Nuvvuagittuq Belt, that may have lived as early as 4.28 billion years ago, not long after the oceans formed 4.4 billion years ago, and after the [[Age of Earth|Earth formed]] 4.54 ± 0.05 billion years ago. These earliest fossils, however, may have originated from non-biological processes.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Holmium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Ho" },
		summary = "**Holmium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Ho** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 67. It is a [[Rare-earth element|rare-earth element]] and the eleventh member of the lanthanide series of elements. It is a relatively soft, silvery, fairly [[Corrosion|corrosion]]-resistant and [[Ductility|malleable]] metal. Like many other lanthanides, holmium is too reactive to be found in native form, as pure holmium slowly forms a yellowish [[Oxide|oxide]] coating when exposed to air. When isolated, holmium is relatively stable in dry air at room temperature. However, it reacts with water and corrodes readily, and also burns in air when heated.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In nature, holmium occurs together with the other rare-earth metals (like [[Thulium|thulium]]). It is a relatively rare lanthanide, making up 1.4 [[Parts-per notation|parts per million]] of the [[Earth's crust]], an abundance similar to [[Tungsten|tungsten]]. Holmium was discovered through isolation by Swedish chemist Per Theodor Cleve. It was also independently discovered by Jacques-Louis Soret and Marc Delafontaine, who together observed it [[Spectroscopy|spectroscopically]] in 1878. Its oxide was first isolated from rare-earth ores by Cleve in 1878. The element's name comes from Holmia, the Latin name for the city of [[Stockholm]]. Like many other lanthanides, holmium is found in the minerals [[Monazite|monazite]] and gadolinite and is usually commercially extracted from monazite using ion-exchange techniques. Its compounds in nature and in nearly all of its laboratory chemistry are trivalently oxidized, containing Ho(III) [[Ion|ions]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Aluminium", "Uranium" },
	},
	{
		title = "Holocene",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Current geological epoch",
		tags = { "Geological history", "Geological periods" },
		aliases = { "Recent" },
		summary = "The **Holocene** is the current [[Geologic time scale|geological epoch]], beginning approximately 11,700 years ago. It follows the [[Last Glacial Period]], which concluded with the Holocene glacial retreat. The Holocene and the preceding [[Pleistocene]] together form the [[Quaternary]] period. The Holocene is an interglacial period within the ongoing [[Ice age|glacial cycles]] of the Quaternary, and is equivalent to Marine Isotope Stage 1.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Holocene correlates with the last maximum [[Axial tilt|axial tilt]] towards the Sun of the [[Earth|Earth's obliquity]]. The Holocene corresponds with the rapid proliferation, growth, and impacts of the human species worldwide, hence the Holocene is often commonly referred to as the **Age of Humans**, including [[Recorded history|all of its written history]], technological revolutions, development of major [[Civilization|civilizations]], and overall significant transition towards urban living in the present. The human impact on modern-era [[Earth]] and its [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]] may be considered of global significance for the future evolution of living species, including approximately synchronous [[Lithosphere|lithospheric]] evidence, or more recently [[Hydrosphere|hydrospheric]] and [[Atmosphere|atmospheric]] evidence of the human impact.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Colour", "Holocene" },
		},
	},
