		description = "Shared ancestry between a pair of structures or genes in different taxa",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "In [[Biology|biology]], **homology** is similarity in anatomical structures or genes between [[Organism|organisms]] of different taxa due to shared ancestry, regardless of current functional differences. [[Evolutionary biology]] explains homologous structures as retained [[Heredity|heredity]] from a [[Common descent|common ancestor]] after having been subjected to adaptive modifications for different purposes as the result of [[Evolution|natural selection]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term was first applied to biology in a non-evolutionary context by the anatomist [[Richard Owen]] in 1843. Homology was later explained by [[Charles Darwin]]'s theory of evolution in 1859, but had been observed before this from Aristotle's biology onwards, and it was explicitly analysed by Pierre Belon in 1555. A common example of homologous structures is the forelimbs of vertebrates, where the wings of bats and birds, the arms of [[Primate|primates]], the front flippers of [[Blue whale|whales]], and the forelegs of four-legged vertebrates like [[Horse|horses]] and crocodilians are all derived from the same ancestral [[Tetrapod|tetrapod]] structure. In [[Developmental biology|developmental biology]], organs that developed in the [[Embryo|embryo]] in the same manner and from similar origins, such as from matching primordia in successive segments of the same animal, are serially homologous.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hooke's law",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Force needed to pull a spring grows linearly with distance",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Solid mechanics" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], **Hooke's law** is an empirical law which states that the [[Force|force]] (F) needed to extend or compress a [[Spring (device)|spring]] by some distance (x) [[Proportionality (mathematics)|scales linearly]] with respect to that distance—that is, where k is a constant factor characteristic of the spring (i.e., its [[Stiffness|stiffness]]), and x is small compared to the total possible deformation of the spring.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The law is named after 17th-century British physicist [[Robert Hooke]]. He first stated the law in 1676 as a Latin anagram. He published the solution of his anagram in 1678 as: ("as the extension, so the force" or "the extension is proportional to the force"). Hooke states in the 1678 work that he was aware of the law since 1660. It is the fundamental principle behind the spring scale, the manometer, the galvanometer, and the balance wheel of the mechanical clock. The equation holds in many situations where an [[Elasticity (physics)|elastic]] body is deformed. An elastic body or material for which this equation can be assumed is said to be linear-elastic or **Hookean**. Hooke\'s law is a [[Taylor series|first-order linear approximation]] to the real response of springs and other elastic bodies to applied forces.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hormone",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Biological signalling molecule",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		summary = "A **hormone** is a class of [[Cell signaling|signaling molecules]] in [[Multicellular organism|multicellular organisms]] that are sent to distant organs or tissues by complex biological processes to regulate [[Physiology|physiology]] and [[Behavior|behavior]]. Hormones are required for the normal development of [[Animal|animals]], [[Plant|plants]] and [[Mushroom|fungi]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Due to the broad definition of a hormone (as a signaling molecule that exerts its effects far from its site of production), numerous kinds of molecules can be classified as hormones. Substances that can be considered hormones include eicosanoids (e.g. prostaglandins and thromboxanes), [[Steroid|steroids]] (e.g. oestrogen and brassinosteroid), [[Amino acid|amino acid]] derivatives (e.g. epinephrine and auxin), [[Protein|protein]] or peptides (e.g. insulin and CLE peptides), and [[Gas|gases]] (e.g. [[Ethylene|ethylene]] and nitric oxide). Hormones are used to communicate between [[Organ (biology)|organs]] and [[Tissue (biology)|tissues]]. In [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]], hormones are responsible for regulating a wide range of processes including both physiological processes and behavioral activities such as [[Digestion|digestion]], [[Metabolism|metabolism]], [[Respiration (physiology)|respiration]], sensory perception, [[Sleep|sleep]], excretion, lactation, stress induction, growth and development, movement, [[Reproduction|reproduction]], and [[Mood (psychology)|mood]] manipulation.",
			},
		},
	},
