		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Hydrocarbons" },
		summary = "In [[Organic chemistry|organic chemistry]], a **hydrocarbon** is an [[Organic compound|organic compound]] consisting entirely of [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] and [[Carbon|carbon]]. Hydrocarbons are examples of group 14 hydrides. Hydrocarbons are generally colourless and hydrophobic; their odor is usually faint, and may be similar to that of [[Gasoline|gasoline]] or lighter fluid. They occur in a diverse range of molecular structures and phases: they can be [[Gas|gases]] (such as [[Methane|methane]] and [[Propane|propane]]), [[Liquid|liquids]] (such as hexane and [[Benzene|benzene]]), low melting [[Solid|solids]] (such as paraffin wax and naphthalene) or [[Polymer|polymers]] (such as [[Polyethylene|polyethylene]] and polystyrene).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In the [[Fossil fuel|fossil fuel]] industries, hydrocarbon refers to naturally occurring [[Petroleum|petroleum]], [[Natural gas|natural gas]] and [[Coal|coal]], or their hydrocarbon derivatives and purified forms. [[Combustion]] of hydrocarbons is the main source of the world's energy. Petroleum is the dominant raw-material source for organic commodity chemicals such as solvents and polymers. Most anthropogenic (human-generated) emissions of [[Greenhouse gas|greenhouse gases]] are either carbon dioxide released by the burning of fossil fuels, or methane released from the handling of natural gas or from agriculture.",
			},
