	{
		title = "Hydrogen",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🎈",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Hydrogen** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has the [[Chemical symbol|symbol]]**H** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]]1. It is the lightest and [[Abundance of the chemical elements|most abundant]] chemical element in the universe, constituting about 75% of all [[Baryon|normal matter]]. Under standard conditions, hydrogen is a [[Gas|gas]] of diatomic molecules with the [[Chemical formula|formula]]H2, called **dihydrogen**, or sometimes **hydrogen gas**, **molecular hydrogen**, or simply hydrogen. [[Star|Stars]], including the [[Sun]], mainly consist of hydrogen in a plasma state, while on Earth, hydrogen is found as the gasH2 (dihydrogen) and in [[Molecule|molecules]], such as in [[Water|water]] and [[Organic compound|organic compounds]]. The most common isotope of hydrogen, 1H, consists of one [[Proton|proton]], one [[Electron|electron]], and no [[Neutron|neutrons]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Hydrogen gas was first produced artificially in the 17thcentury by the [[Chemical reaction|reaction]] of [[Acid|acids]] with metals. [[Henry Cavendish]], in1766–1781, identified hydrogen gas as a distinct substance and discovered its property of producing water when burned: this is the origin of hydrogen's name, which means water-former (from, and ). Understanding the colors of light absorbed and emitted by hydrogen was a crucial part of the development of [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]]. Hydrogen, typically nonmetallic except under extreme pressure, readily forms [[Covalent bond|covalent bonds]] with most nonmetals, contributing to the formation of compounds like water and various organic substances. Its role is crucial in [[Acid–base reaction|acid–base reactions]], which mainly involve proton exchange among [[Solubility|soluble]] molecules.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hydrogen bond",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎈",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Intermolecular attraction between a hydrogen donor-and-acceptor pair",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Chemical bonds" },
		aliases = { "H-bond", "Dn", "Ac" },
		summary = "In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], a **hydrogen bond** (**H-bond**) is a specific type of molecular interaction that exhibits partial covalent character and cannot be described as a purely electrostatic force**.** It occurs when a [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] (H) atom, [[Covalent bond|covalently]] bonded to a more [[Electronegativity|electronegative]] donor atom or group (**Dn**), interacts with another electronegative atom bearing a lone pair of electrons—the hydrogen bond acceptor (**Ac**). Unlike simple dipole–dipole interactions, hydrogen bonding arises from charge transfer, [[Atomic orbital|orbital]] interactions, and quantum mechanical delocalization, making it a **resonance-assisted interaction** rather than a mere electrostatic attraction.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The general notation for hydrogen bonding is **Dn−H···Ac**, where the solid line represents a polar covalent bond, and the three dots indicate the hydrogen bond. **Hydrogen bond donors** have a protic hydrogen attached to an electronegative atom such as [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]] (N), [[Oxygen|oxygen]] (O), and [[Fluorine|fluorine]] (F). **Hydrogen bond acceptors** have a lone pair of electrons, such as the nitrogen atom of [[Amine|amines]] and [[Amide|amides]] and the oxygen atom of carboxylates and water. The term "hydrogen bond" is generally used for well-defined, localized interactions with significant charge transfer and orbital overlap, such as those in DNA base pairing or ice. In contrast, "hydrogen-bonding interactions" is a broader term used when the interaction is weaker, more dynamic, or delocalized, such as in liquid water, supramolecular assemblies (e.g.: lipid membranes, protein-protein interactions), or weak C-H···O interactions.',
			},
		},
	},
