		tags = { "Earth science", "Water" },
		summary = "The **hydrosphere** is the combined body of [[Water|water]] found on, under, and above the [[Planetary surface|surface]] of a [[Planet|planet]], [[Minor planet|minor planet]], or [[Natural satellite|natural satellite]]. Although [[Earth]]'s hydrosphere has been around for about 4 billion years, it continues to change in shape. This is caused by seafloor spreading and [[Continental drift|continental drift]], which rearranges the land and ocean.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It has been estimated that there are 1.386 billion cubic kilometres (333 million cubic miles) of water on Earth. This includes water in gaseous, liquid and frozen forms as soil moisture, [[Groundwater|groundwater]] and [[Permafrost|permafrost]] in the [[Earth's crust]] (to a depth of 2 km); [[Ocean|oceans]] and seas, [[Lake|lakes]], [[River|rivers]] and [[Stream|streams]], [[Wetland|wetlands]], [[Glacier|glaciers]], ice and snow cover on Earth's surface; vapour, droplets and crystals in the air; and part of living plants, animals and unicellular organisms of the biosphere. [[Saline water|Saltwater]] accounts for 97.5% of this amount, whereas [[Fresh water|fresh water]] accounts for only 2.5%. Of this fresh water, 68.9% is in the form of [[Ice|ice]] and permanent snow cover in the Arctic, the Antarctic and mountain [[Glacier|glaciers]]; 30.8% is in the form of fresh groundwater; and only 0.3% of the fresh water on Earth is in easily accessible lakes, reservoirs and river systems.",
