		tags = { "Biological processes and physiology", "Animal ontogeny" },
		summary = 'In [[Biology|biology]], the **imago** ([[Latin]] for "image") is the last stage an [[Insect|insect]] attains during its [[Metamorphosis|metamorphosis]], its process of growth and development; it is also called the imaginal stage ("imaginal" being "imago" in adjective form), the stage in which the insect attains maturity. It follows the final ecdysis of the immature instars.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In a member of the Ametabola or Hemimetabola, species in which metamorphosis is "incomplete", the final ecdysis follows the last immature or [[Nymph (biology)|nymphal]] stage. In members of the Holometabola, in which there is a pupal stage, the final ecdysis follows emergence from the [[Pupa|pupa]], after which the metamorphosis is complete, although there is a prolonged period of maturation in some species. The imago often is referred to as the adult stage. Members of the order Ephemeroptera (mayflies) do not have a pupal stage, but they briefly pass through an intermediate winged stage called the subimago. The Latin plural of imago is imagines, and this is the term generally used by entomologists when a plural form is required –however, imagoes is also acceptable.',
