	{
		title = "Impact crater",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Circular depression in a solid astronomical body formed by the impact of a smaller object",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geology" },
		summary = "An **impact crater** is a [[Depression (geology)|depression]] in the surface of a [[Solid|solid]] astronomical body formed by the hypervelocity [[Impact event|impact]] of a smaller object. In contrast to volcanic craters, which result from explosion or internal collapse, impact craters typically have raised rims and floors that are lower in elevation than the surrounding terrain. Impact craters are typically circular, though they can be elliptical in shape or even irregular due to events such as landslides. Impact craters range in size from microscopic craters seen on lunar rocks returned by the [[Apollo program|Apollo Program]] to simple bowl-shaped depressions and vast, complex, multi-ringed impact basins.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Meteor Crater is a well-known example of a small impact crater on Earth. Impact craters are the dominant geographic features on many solid Solar System objects including the [[Moon]], [[Mercury (planet)|Mercury]], [[Callisto (moon)|Callisto]], [[Ganymede (moon)|Ganymede]], and most small moons and [[Asteroid|asteroids]]. On other planets and moons that experience more active surface geological processes, such as [[Earth]], [[Venus]], [[Europa (moon)|Europa]], [[Io (moon)|Io]], [[Titan (moon)|Titan]], and [[Triton (moon)|Triton]], visible impact craters are less common because they become [[Erosion|eroded]], buried, or transformed by [[Tectonics|tectonic]] and [[Volcano|volcanic]] processes over time. Where such processes have destroyed most of the original crater [[Topography|topography]], the terms impact structure or astrobleme are more commonly used. In early literature, before the significance of impact cratering was widely recognised, the terms cryptoexplosion or cryptovolcanic structure were often used to describe what are now recognised as impact-related features on Earth.",
