		tags = { "Chemistry", "Chemical bonds" },
		aliases = { "Secondary force" },
		summary = "An **intermolecular force** (**IMF**; also **secondary force**) is the force that mediates interaction between [[Molecule|molecules]], including the [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetic forces of attraction or repulsion]] which act between atoms and other types of neighbouring particles (e.g. [[Atom|atoms]] or [[Ion|ions]]). Intermolecular forces are weak relative to intramolecular forces – the forces which hold a molecule together. For example, the [[Covalent bond|covalent bond]], involving sharing electron pairs between atoms, is much stronger than the forces present between neighboring molecules. Both sets of forces are essential parts of force fields frequently used in molecular mechanics.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The first reference to the nature of microscopic forces is found in Alexis Clairaut's work Théorie de la figure de la Terre, published in Paris in 1743. Other scientists who have contributed to the investigation of microscopic forces include: [[Pierre-Simon Laplace|Laplace]], [[Carl Friedrich Gauss|Gauss]], [[James Clerk Maxwell|Maxwell]], [[Ludwig Boltzmann|Boltzmann]] and [[Linus Pauling|Pauling]]. Attractive intermolecular forces are categorized into the following types: [[Hydrogen bond|Hydrogen bonding]] Ion–dipole forces and ion–induced dipole force Cation–, – and – bonding Van der Waals forces – Keesom force, Debye force, and London dispersion force Cation–cation bonding Salt bridge (protein and supramolecular) Information on intermolecular forces is obtained by macroscopic measurements of properties like [[Viscosity|viscosity]], pressure, volume, temperature (PVT) data.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Internal energy",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Energy contained within a system",
		tags = { "Physics", "Thermodynamics" },
		summary = "The **internal energy** of a [[Thermodynamic system|thermodynamic system]] is the [[Energy|energy]] of the system as a state function, measured as the quantity of energy necessary to bring the system from its standard internal state to its present internal state of interest, accounting for the gains and losses of energy due to changes in its internal state, including such quantities as magnetization. It excludes the [[Kinetic energy|kinetic energy]] of motion of the system as a whole and the [[Potential energy|potential energy]] of position of the system as a whole, with respect to its surroundings and external force fields. The notion of internal energy was introduced by Clausius as part of the formulation of the [[First law of thermodynamics|first law of thermodynamics]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Without a thermodynamic process, the internal energy of an [[Thermodynamic system|isolated]] system does not change, as expressed in the law of [[Conservation of energy|conservation of energy]], a foundation of the first law of thermodynamics. Without transfer of matter, internal energy changes equal the algebraic sum of the heat transferred and the thermodynamic work done. The internal energy cannot be measured absolutely. Thermodynamics concerns changes in the internal energy, not its absolute value. The processes that change the internal energy are transfers, into or out of the system, of matter, or of energy, as [[Heat|heat]], or by [[Work (thermodynamics)|thermodynamic work]]. These processes are measured by changes in the system's properties, such as [[Temperature|temperature]], [[Entropy|entropy]], [[Volume|volume]], electric polarization, and molar constitution.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Explosion" },
	},
	{
		title = "Internal structure of Earth",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Interior of the earth",
		tags = { "Earth science", "Earth" },
		summary = "The **internal structure of Earth** is the spatial variation of chemical and physical properties in the solid Earth. The primary [[Structure|structure]] is a series of layers: Its mechanical structure is of a rigid [[Lithosphere|lithosphere]], a semi-fluid asthenosphere, a semi-fluid mesosphere, a liquid [[Earth's outer core|outer core]], and a solid, rigid [[Earth's inner core|inner core]]. Its chemical structure is of a silicate [[Crust (geology)|crust]], a ferromagnesian [[Mantle (geology)|mantle]], and an iron-nickel core whose flowing upper portion generates the [[Earth's magnetic field]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Scientific understanding of the internal structure of [[Earth]] is based on observations of [[Topography|topography]] and [[Bathymetry|bathymetry]], [[Observation|observations]] of [[Rock (geology)|rock]] in outcrop, samples brought to the surface from greater depths by [[Volcano|volcanoes]] or volcanic activity, analysis of the seismic waves that pass through Earth, measurements of the [[Gravity of Earth|gravitational]] and [[Earth's magnetic field|magnetic fields]] of Earth, and experiments with crystalline solids at pressures and temperatures characteristic of Earth's deep interior.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "International System of Units",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Modern form of the metric system",
		tags = { "Measurement", "Measurement systems" },
		aliases = { "SI" },
		summary = "The **International System of Units**, internationally known by the abbreviation **SI** (from its official [[French language|French]] name, ), is the modern form of the [[Metric system|metric system]] and the world's most widely used system of measurement. It is the only system of measurement with official status in nearly every country in the world, employed in [[Science|science]], [[Technology|technology]], [[Industry (economics)|industry]], and everyday [[Commerce|commerce]]. The [[International Bureau of Weights and Measures]] (abbreviated BIPM from ) coordinates the SI.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The SI comprises a coherent system of [[Unit of measurement|units of measurement]] starting with seven [[SI base unit|base units]], which are the [[Second|second]] (symbol: s, the unit of [[Time|time]]), [[Metre|metre]] (m, [[Length|length]]), [[Kilogram|kilogram]] (kg, [[Mass|mass]]), [[Ampere|ampere]] (A, [[Electric current|electric current]]), [[Kelvin|kelvin]] (K, [[Thermodynamic temperature|thermodynamic temperature]]), [[Mole (unit)|mole]] (mol, [[Amount of substance|amount of substance]]), and [[Candela|candela]] (cd, luminous intensity). The system can accommodate coherent units for an unlimited number of additional quantities. These are called coherent [[SI derived unit|derived units]], which can always be represented as products of powers of the base units. Twenty-two coherent derived units have been provided with special names and symbols. The 7 base units and the 22 coherent derived units with special names and symbols may be used in combination to express other coherent derived units.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Invasive species",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🎆",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Introduced species that harms its new environment",
		tags = { "Ecology" },
		summary = "An **invasive species** is an [[Introduced species|introduced species]] that harms its new environment. Invasive species adversely affect [[Habitat|habitats]] and bioregions, causing [[Ecology|ecological]], [[Natural environment|environmental]], and/or [[Economy|economic]] damage. Since the 20th century, invasive species have become serious economic, social, and environmental threats worldwide.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Invasion of long-established ecosystems by organisms is a natural phenomenon, but human-facilitated introductions have greatly increased the rate, scale, and geographic range of invasion. For millennia, [[Human|humans]] have served as both accidental and deliberate dispersal agents, beginning with their [[Early human migrations|earliest migrations]], accelerating in the [[Age of Discovery]], and accelerating again with the spread of [[International trade|international trade]]. Notable [[Plant|plant]] species that are invasive in many parts of the world include the kudzu vine (Pueraria), [[Pontederia crassipes|water hyacinth]] (Pontederia crassipes), common ragweed (Ambrosia artemisiifolia), and yellow starthistle (Centaurea solstitialis). Notable [[Animal|animal]] species that are invasive include European rabbits (Oryctolagus cuniculus), domestic cats (Felis catus), and red swamp crayfish (Procambarus clarkii).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Iodine",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Iodine** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **I** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 53. The heaviest of the stable [[Halogen|halogens]], it exists at [[Standard temperature and pressure|standard conditions]] as a semi-lustrous, non-metallic solid that melts to form a deep violet liquid at 114 °C, and boils to a violet gas at 184 °C. The element was discovered by the French chemist Bernard Courtois in 1811 and was named two years later by Joseph Louis Gay-Lussac, after the [[Ancient Greek]], meaning 'violet'.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Iodine occurs in many [[Oxidation state|oxidation states]], including iodide (I−), iodate (IO3-), and the various periodate anions. As the heaviest essential [[Mineral (nutrient)|mineral nutrient]], iodine is required for the synthesis of thyroid hormones. Iodine deficiency affects about two billion people and is the leading preventable cause of intellectual disabilities. The dominant producers of iodine today are Chile and Japan. Because of the organ specificity of its uptake by the human body, radioactive isotopes of iodine can also be used to treat thyroid cancer. Iodine is also used as a [[Catalysis|catalyst]] in the industrial production of [[Acetic acid|acetic acid]] and some [[Polymer|polymers]]. It is on the World Health Organization's List of Essential Medicines.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ion",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Particle, atom or molecule with a net electrical charge",
		tags = { "Atomic, molecular and optical physics", "Atomic physics" },
		aliases = { "Anion", "Cation", "Ions" },
		summary = "An **ion** is an [[Atom|atom]] or [[Molecule|molecule]] with a net electrical charge. The charge of an [[Electron|electron]] is considered to be negative by convention and this charge is equal and opposite to the charge of a [[Proton|proton]], which is considered to be positive by convention. The net charge of an ion is not zero because its total number of electrons is unequal to its total number of protons.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A **cation** is a positively charged ion with fewer electrons than protons (e.g. K+ (potassium ion)) while an **anion** is a negatively charged ion with more electrons than protons (e.g. Cl− (chloride ion) and OH− (hydroxide ion)). Opposite electric charges are pulled towards one another by electrostatic force, so cations and anions attract each other and readily form [[Salt (chemistry)|ionic compounds]]. Ions consisting of only a single atom are termed monatomic ions, atomic ions or simple ions, while ions consisting of two or more atoms are termed [[Polyatomic ion|polyatomic ions]] or molecular ions. The net charge of an ion is indicated as a superscript on the right. To indicate a +1 or −1 charge, only a + or − is written respectively, as seen in Na+ (sodium ion) and F− (fluoride ion).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ionic bonding",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical bonding involving attraction between ions",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Chemical bonds" },
		summary = "**Ionic bonding** is a type of [[Chemical bond|chemical bonding]] that involves the [[Coulomb's law|electrostatic attraction]] between oppositely charged [[Ion|ions]], or between two [[Atom|atoms]] with sharply different electronegativities, and is the primary interaction occurring in [[Salt (chemistry)|ionic compounds]]. It is one of the main types of bonding, along with [[Covalent bond|covalent bonding]] and [[Metallic bonding|metallic bonding]]. Ions are atoms (or groups of atoms) with an electrostatic charge. Atoms that gain electrons make negatively charged ions (called [[Ion|anions]]). Atoms that lose electrons make positively charged ions (called cations). This transfer of electrons is known as **electrovalence** in contrast to [[Covalent bond|covalence]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In the simplest case, the cation is a [[Metal|metal]] atom and the anion is a nonmetal atom, but these ions can be more complex, e.g. [[Polyatomic ion|polyatomic ions]] like NH4+ or SO42−. In simpler words, an ionic bond results from the transfer of electrons from a [[Metal|metal]] to a non-metal to obtain a full valence shell for both atoms. Clean ionic bonding – in which one atom or molecule completely transfers an electron to another – cannot exist: all ionic compounds have some degree of [[Covalent bond|covalent bonding]] or electron sharing. Thus, the term "ionic bonding" is given when the ionic character is greater than the covalent character – that is, a bond in which there is a large difference in [[Electronegativity|electronegativity]] between the cation and anion, causing the bonding to be more polar (ionic) than in covalent bonding where electrons are shared more equally.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ionization",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Process by which atoms or molecules acquire charge by gaining or losing electrons",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Phase transitions" },
		aliases = { "Ionisation" },
		summary = "**Ionization** or **ionisation** is the process by which an [[Atom|atom]] or a [[Molecule|molecule]] acquires a negative or positive [[Electric charge|charge]] by gaining or losing [[Electron|electrons]], often in conjunction with other chemical changes. The resulting electrically charged atom or molecule is called an [[Ion|ion]]. Ionization can result from the loss of an electron after collisions with [[Subatomic particle|subatomic particles]], collisions with other atoms, molecules, electrons, [[Positron|positrons]], [[Proton|protons]], [[Antiproton|antiprotons]], and ions, or through the interaction with [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]]. Heterolytic bond cleavage and heterolytic [[Substitution reaction|substitution reactions]] can result in the formation of ion pairs.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ionization can occur through [[Radioactivity|radioactive decay]] by the internal conversion process, in which an excited nucleus transfers its energy to one of the inner-shell electrons causing it to be ejected.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ionizing radiation",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Harmful high-frequency radiation",
		tags = { "Physics", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Ionising radiation" },
		summary = "**Ionizing radiation**, also spelled **ionising radiation**, consists of [[Subatomic particle|subatomic particles]] or [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic waves]] that have enough [[Energy|energy]] per individual photon or particle to [[Ionization|ionize]] atoms or [[Molecule|molecules]] by detaching [[Electron|electrons]] from them. Some particles can travel up to 99% of the [[Speed of light|speed of light]]; their electromagnetic waves are on the high-energy portion of the [[Electromagnetic spectrum|electromagnetic spectrum]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Gamma ray|Gamma rays]], X-rays, and the higher energy ultraviolet part of the electromagnetic spectrum are ionizing radiation. Lower energy [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]], visible [[Light|light]], [[Infrared|infrared]], [[Microwave|microwaves]], and [[Radio wave|radio waves]] are non-ionizing radiation. Nearly all types of [[Laser|laser]] light are non-ionizing radiation. The boundary between ionizing and non-ionizing radiation in the ultraviolet area cannot be sharply defined, as different molecules and atoms ionize at different energies. The energy of ionizing radiation starts around 10 [[Electronvolt|electronvolts]] (eV). Ionizing subatomic particles include [[Alpha particle|alpha particles]], [[Beta particle|beta particles]], and [[Neutron|neutrons]]. These particles are created by [[Radioactivity|radioactive decay]], and almost all are energetic enough to ionize. There are also secondary cosmic particles produced after [[Cosmic ray|cosmic rays]] interact with Earth's atmosphere, including [[Muon|muons]], [[Meson|mesons]], and [[Positron|positrons]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Gauge theory" },
	},
	{
		title = "Iridium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Iridium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has the [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Ir** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 77. This very hard, brittle, silvery-white [[Transition metal|transition metal]] of the platinum group is considered the second-densest naturally occurring metal (after [[Osmium|osmium]]), with a density of 22.56 g/cm³ as defined by experimental [[X-ray crystallography]]. 191Ir and 193Ir are the only two naturally occurring [[Isotope|isotopes]] of iridium, as well as the only stable isotopes; the latter is the more abundant. It is one of the most [[Corrosion|corrosion]]-resistant metals, even at temperatures as high as 2000 °C.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Iridium was discovered in 1803 in the acid-insoluble residues of [[Platinum|platinum]] ores by the British chemist Smithson Tennant. The name iridium, derived from the Greek word iris (rainbow), refers to the various colors of its compounds. Iridium is [[Abundance of elements in Earth's crust|one of the rarest elements]] in [[Crust (geology)|Earth's crust]], with an estimated annual production of only 15,000 lb in 2023. The dominant uses of iridium and its alloys are in high-performance spark plugs, crucibles for recrystallization of [[Semiconductor|semiconductors]] at high temperatures, and electrodes for the production of [[Chlorine|chlorine]] in the chloralkali process. Important compounds of iridium are chlorides and iodides in industrial [[Catalysis|catalysis]]. Iridium is a component of some [[OLED|OLEDs]]. Iridium is found in [[Meteorite|meteorites]] in much higher abundance than in the Earth's crust.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Iron oxide",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Class of chemical compounds composed of iron and oxygen",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "An **iron oxide** is a [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]] composed of [[Iron|iron]] and [[Oxygen|oxygen]]. Several iron [[Oxide|oxides]] are recognized. Often they are non-stoichiometric. Ferric oxyhydroxides are a related class of compounds, perhaps the best known of which is rust.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Iron oxides and oxyhydroxides are widespread in nature and play an important role in many geological and biological processes. They are used as iron ores, [[Pigment|pigments]], [[Catalysis|catalysts]], and in thermite. Iron oxides are inexpensive and durable pigments in paints, coatings and colored concretes. Colors commonly available are in the "earthy" end of the yellow/orange/red/brown/black range. When used as a food coloring, it has E number E172. The earliest applications of [[Paint|paint]] served purely ornamental purposes. Consequently, pigment lacking any adhesive agent—composed mainly of iron oxide—was employed in prehistoric cave art around the 15,000s BC in parts of [[Asia]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Isomer",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical compounds with the same molecular formula but different atomic arrangements",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Isomers" },
		summary = "In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], **isomers** are [[Molecule|molecules]] or [[Polyatomic ion|polyatomic ions]] with an identical molecular formula – that is, the same number of [[Atom|atoms]] of each element – but distinct arrangements of atoms in space. Isomerism refers to the existence or possibility of isomers.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Isomers do not necessarily share similar [[Chemical property|chemical]] or [[Physical property|physical properties]]. Two main forms of isomerism are structural (or constitutional) isomerism, in which [[Chemical bond|bonds]] between the atoms differ; and stereoisomerism (or spatial isomerism), in which the bonds are the same but the relative positions of the atoms differ. Isomeric relationships form a [[Hierarchy|hierarchy]]. Two chemicals might be the same constitutional isomer, but upon deeper analysis be stereoisomers of each other. Two molecules that are the same stereoisomer as each other might be in different conformational forms or be different isotopologues. The depth of analysis depends on the field of study or the chemical and physical properties of interest.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Isotope",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Atoms of the same element, but different mass",
		tags = { "Physics", "Nuclear physics" },
		aliases = { "Isotopes" },
		summary = "**Isotopes** are distinct nuclear species (or [[Nuclide|nuclides]]) of the same [[Chemical element|chemical element]]. They have the same [[Atomic number|atomic number]] (number of protons in their [[Atomic nucleus|nuclei]]) and position in the [[Periodic table|periodic table]] (and hence belong to the same chemical element), but different [[Nucleon|nucleon]] numbers (mass numbers) due to different numbers of [[Neutron|neutrons]] in their nuclei. While all isotopes of a given element have virtually the same chemical properties, they have different [[Atomic mass|atomic masses]] and physical properties.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term isotope comes from the Greek roots and, meaning \"the same place\": different isotopes of an element occupy the same place on the [[Periodic table|periodic table]]. It was coined by Scottish doctor and writer Margaret Todd in a 1913 suggestion to the British chemist Frederick Soddy, who popularized the term. The number of protons within the [[Atomic nucleus|atom's nucleus]] is called its [[Atomic number|atomic number]] and is equal to the number of [[Electron|electrons]] in the neutral (non-ionized) atom. Each atomic number identifies a specific element, but not the isotope; an atom of a given element may have a wide range in its number of [[Neutron|neutrons]]. The number of [[Nucleon|nucleons]] (both protons and neutrons) in the nucleus is the atom's mass number, and each isotope of a given element has a different mass number. For example, carbon-12, carbon-13, and carbon-14 are three isotopes of the element [[Carbon|carbon]] with atomic mass numbers 12, 13, and 14, respectively.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ivory",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🦷",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Material derived from the tusks and teeth of animals",
		tags = { "Geology", "Organic gemstones" },
		summary = '**Ivory** is a hard, white material from the tusks (traditionally from [[Elephant|elephants]]) and teeth of animals, that consists mainly of dentin, one of the physical structures of teeth and tusks. The chemical structure of the teeth and tusks of [[Mammal|mammals]] is the same, regardless of the species of origin, but ivory contains structures of mineralised collagen. The trade in certain teeth and tusks other than elephant is well established and widespread; therefore, "ivory" can correctly be used to describe any mammalian teeth or tusks of commercial interest which are large enough to be carved or scrimshawed.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Besides natural ivory, ivory can also be produced synthetically, hence (unlike natural ivory) not requiring the retrieval of the material from animals. Tagua nuts can also be carved like ivory. The trade of finished goods of ivory products has its origins in the [[Indus River|Indus Valley]]. Ivory is a main product that is seen in abundance and was used for trading in Harappan civilization. Finished ivory products that were seen in Harappan sites include kohl sticks, pins, awls, hooks, toggles, combs, game pieces, [[Dice|dice]], inlay and other personal ornaments. Ivory has been valued since ancient times in art or manufacturing for making a range of items from ivory carvings to false teeth, piano keys, fans, and dominoes. Elephant ivory is the most important source, but ivory from [[Mammoth|mammoth]], [[Walrus|walrus]], [[Hippopotamus|hippopotamus]], [[Sperm whale|sperm whale]], [[Orca|orca]], narwhal and warthog is used as well.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Jade",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🪨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ornamental stone, commonly green",
		tags = { "Geology", "Mineral gemstones" },
		summary = "**Jade** is an umbrella term for two different types of decorative rocks used for jewelry or ornaments. Jade is often referred to by either of two different [[Silicate mineral|silicate mineral]] names: nephrite (a silicate of [[Calcium|calcium]] and [[Magnesium|magnesium]] in the amphibole group of minerals), or jadeite (a silicate of [[Sodium|sodium]] and aluminum in the [[Pyroxene|pyroxene]] group of minerals). Nephrite is typically green, although it may be yellow, white or black. Jadeite varies from white or near-colorless, through various shades of green (including an emerald green, termed 'imperial'), to lavender, yellow, orange, brown and black. Rarely it may be blue.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Both of these names refer to their use as gemstones, and each has a mineralogically more specific name. Both the amphibole jade (nephrite) and pyroxene jade are mineral aggregates (rocks) rather than mineral species. Nephrite was deprecated by the International Mineralogical Association as a mineral species name in 1975 (replaced by tremolite). The name "nephrite" is mineralogically correct for referring to the rock. Jadeite is a legitimate mineral species, differing from the pyroxene jade rock. In China, the name jadeite has been replaced with fei cui, the traditional Chinese name for this gem that was in use long before Damour created the name in 1863. Jade is well known for its ornamental use in East Asian, [[South Asia|South Asian]], and Southeast Asian art. It is commonly used in [[Latin America]], such as [[Mexico]] and [[Guatemala]].',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Colour", "Virtually all colors, mostly green" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Jet stream",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fast-flowing atmospheric air current",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Winds" },
		aliases = { "Jet streams" },
		summary = "**Jet streams** are fast flowing, narrow air currents in the [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]]. It is the physical mechanism of a teleconnection. The main terrestrial jet streams are located near the altitude of the tropopause and are westerly winds, flowing west to east around the globe. The [[Northern Hemisphere]] and the [[Southern Hemisphere]] each have a **polar jet** around their respective [[Polar vortex|polar vortex]] at around 30000 ft above sea level and typically travelling at around 110 mph although often considerably faster. Closer to the equator, somewhat higher and somewhat weaker, is a **subtropical jet**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The northern polar jet flows over the middle to northern latitudes of [[North America]], [[Europe]], and [[Asia]] and their intervening [[Ocean|oceans]], while the southern hemisphere polar jet mostly circles [[Antarctica]]. Jet streams may start, stop, split into two or more parts, combine into one stream, or flow in various directions including opposite to the direction of the remainder of the jet. The [[El Niño–Southern Oscillation]] affects the location of the jet streams, which in turn affects the weather over the tropical [[Pacific Ocean]] and affects the climate of much of the [[Tropics|tropics]] and [[Subtropics|subtropics]], and can affect weather in higher-latitude regions. The term "jet stream" is also applied to some other winds at varying levels in the atmosphere, some global (such as the higher-level polar-night jet), some local (such as the African easterly jet).',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Joule",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "SI unit of energy",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Energy, power, force and pressure" },
		summary = "The **joule** is the unit of [[Energy|energy]] in the [[International System of Units]] (SI). One joule is equal to the amount of [[Work (physics)|work]] done when a force of one [[Newton (unit)|newton]] displaces a body through a distance of one [[Metre|metre]] in the direction of that force. It is also the energy dissipated as heat when an [[Electric current|electric current]] of one [[Ampere|ampere]] passes through a [[Electrical resistance and conductance|resistance]] of one [[Ohm|ohm]] for one second. It is named after the English physicist [[James Prescott Joule]] (1818–1889).",
		infobox = {
			{ "Symbol", "J" },
