	{
		title = "Kaolinite",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Phyllosilicate clay mineral",
		tags = { "Geology", "Mineralogy" },
		summary = '**Kaolinite** is a clay mineral, with the chemical composition [[Aluminium|Al]]2[[Silicon|Si]]2[[Oxygen|O]]5([[Hydroxide|OH]])4. It is a layered [[Silicate mineral|silicate mineral]], with one "tetrahedral" sheet of [[Silicate|silicate]] tetrahedra (SiO4) linked to one "octahedral" sheet of aluminate octahedrons (AlO2(OH)4) through [[Oxygen|oxygen]] [[Atom|atoms]] on one side, and another such sheet through [[Hydrogen bond|hydrogen bonds]] on the other side.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Kaolinite is a soft, earthy, usually white, mineral (dioctahedral phyllosilicate [[Clay|clay]]), produced by the chemical weathering of aluminium silicate minerals like [[Feldspar|feldspar]]. It has a low shrink–swell capacity and a low cation-exchange capacity (1–15 meq/100 g). Rocks that are rich in kaolinite, and halloysite, are known as **kaolin** or **china clay**. In many parts of the world kaolin is colored pink-orange-red by [[Iron oxide|iron oxide]], giving it a distinct rust hue. Lower concentrations of iron oxide yield the white, yellow, or light orange colors of kaolin. Alternating lighter and darker layers are sometimes found, as at Providence Canyon State Park in Georgia, United States. Kaolin is an important raw material in many industries and applications. Commercial grades of kaolin are supplied and transported as powder, lumps, semi-dried noodle or slurry.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
