		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Metric unit of mass",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Mass" },
		aliases = { "Kilogramme" },
		summary = 'The **kilogram** (also spelled **kilogramme**) is the [[SI base unit|base unit]] of [[Mass|mass]] in the [[International System of Units]] (SI), equal to one thousand grams. It has the unit symbol **kg**. The word "kilogram" is formed from the combination of the metric prefix kilo- and gram; it is commonly shortened to "**kilo**" (plural "kilos").',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The kilogram is an SI base unit, defined ultimately in terms of three [[International System of Units|defining constants]] of the SI, namely a specific transition frequency of the caesium-133 atom, the [[Speed of light|speed of light]], and the [[Planck constant]]. A properly equipped [[Metrology|metrology]] laboratory can calibrate a mass measurement instrument such as a Kibble balance as a primary standard for the kilogram mass. The kilogram was originally defined in 1795 during the [[French Revolution]] as the mass of one [[Litre|litre]] of [[Properties of water|water]] (originally at 0 [[Celsius|°C]], later changed to the temperature of its maximum density, approximately 4 °C). The current definition of a kilogram agrees with this original definition to within 30 [[Parts-per notation|parts per million]] (0.003%). In 1799, the [[Platinum|platinum]] Kilogramme des Archives replaced it as the standard of mass.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Pound (mass)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Symbol", "kg" },
		},
