		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrical circuits" },
		summary = "**Kirchhoff's circuit laws** are two [[Equality (mathematics)|equalities]] that deal with the [[Electric current|current]] and potential difference (commonly known as voltage) in the lumped element model of [[Electrical network|electrical circuits]]. They were first described in 1845 by German physicist [[Gustav Kirchhoff]]. This generalized the work of [[Georg Ohm]] and preceded the work of [[James Clerk Maxwell]]. Widely used in [[Electrical engineering|electrical engineering]], they are also called **Kirchhoff's rules** or simply **Kirchhoff's laws**. These laws can be applied in time and frequency domains and form the basis for network analysis.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
