	{
		title = "Lactic acid",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Organic acid",
		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Carbon–oxygen bond" },
		summary = "**Lactic acid** is an organic acid with the molecular formula C3H6O3. In its solid state, it is white and in its liquid state is [[Miscibility|miscible]] with water. When dissolved, it forms a colorless solution. Production includes both artificial synthesis and natural sources. Lactic acid is an alpha-hydroxy acid (AHA) due to the presence of a hydroxyl group adjacent to the carboxyl group. It is a synthetic intermediate in many organic synthesis industries and in various biochemical industries. The conjugate base of lactic acid is called **lactate** (or the lactate anion). The name of the derived acyl group is lactoyl.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Lactic acid is chiral, consisting of two enantiomers. One is known as L-lactic acid, (S)-lactic acid, or-lactic acid, and the other, its mirror image, is D-lactic acid, (R)-lactic acid, or-lactic acid. A mixture of the two in equal amounts is called DL-lactic acid, or racemic lactic acid. In animals, L-lactate is constantly produced from pyruvate via the [[Enzyme|enzyme]] lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) in a process of fermentation during normal [[Metabolism|metabolism]] and exercise. It does not increase in concentration until the rate of lactate production exceeds the rate of lactate removal, which is governed by a number of factors, including monocarboxylate transporters, concentration and isoform of LDH, and oxidative capacity of tissues. This reaction is reversible and redox-linked: LDH reduces pyruvate to lactate using NADH as an electron donor.",
			},
		},
