	{
		title = "Lake",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Large inland body of relatively still water",
		tags = { "Water", "Hydrology" },
		summary = "A **lake** is often a naturally occurring, relatively large and fixed [[Body of water|body of water]] on or near the Earth's surface. It is localized in a [[Depression (geology)|basin]] or interconnected basins surrounded by dry land. Lakes lie completely on land and are separate from the [[Ocean|ocean]], although they may be connected with the ocean by outflowing rivers. Lakes, like other bodies of [[Water|water]], are part of the [[Water cycle|water cycle]], the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Most lakes are [[Fresh water|fresh water]] and account for almost all the world's surface freshwater, but some are [[Salt lake|salt lakes]] with salinities even higher than that of [[Seawater|seawater]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Lakes vary significantly in surface area and volume of water, but in total cover approximately 2.5 X 106 km2 (less than 2%) of the Earth's surface. Lakes are typically larger and deeper than [[Pond|ponds]], which are also water-filled basins on land, although there are no official definitions or scientific criteria distinguishing the two. Lakes are also distinct from [[Lagoon|lagoons]], which are generally shallow tidal pools dammed by sandbars or other material at [[Coast|coastal]] regions of oceans or large lakes. Most lakes are fed by [[Spring (hydrology)|springs]], [[Precipitation|precipitation]], and [[Surface runoff|surface runoff]], and both fed and drained by [[Stream|creeks]], and [[River|rivers]], but some lakes are [[Endorheic basin|endorheic]] without any outflow, while volcanic lakes are filled by precipitation and runoff and do not have any inflow streams. Natural lakes are generally found in [[Mountain|mountainous]] areas (i.e. alpine lakes), dormant volcanic craters, rift zones and areas with ongoing [[Glacier|glaciation]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Limnology" },
	},
	{
		title = "Land",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Earth's dry surface",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Landforms" },
		aliases = { "Dry land", "Ground" },
		summary = "**Land**, also known as **dry land**, **ground**, or **earth**, is the solid terrestrial surface of [[Earth]] not submerged by the [[Ocean|ocean]] or another [[Body of water|body of water]]. It makes up 29.2% of Earth's surface and includes all [[Continent|continents]] and [[Island|islands]]. Earth's land surface is almost entirely covered by regolith, a layer of [[Rock (geology)|rock]], [[Soil|soil]], and [[Mineral|minerals]] that forms the outer part of the [[Earth's crust|crust]]. Land plays an important role in Earth's [[Climate system|climate system]], being involved in the [[Carbon cycle|carbon cycle]], nitrogen cycle, and [[Water cycle|water cycle]]. One-third of land is covered in [[Tree|trees]], another third is used for [[Agriculture|agriculture]], and one-tenth is covered in permanent [[Snow|snow]] and [[Glacier|glaciers]]. The remainder consists of [[Desert|desert]], savannah, and [[Prairie|prairie]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Land [[Terrain|terrain]] varies greatly, consisting of mountains, deserts, plains, plateaus, glaciers, and other [[Landform|landforms]]. In physical geology, the land is divided into two major categories: [[Mountain range|Mountain ranges]] and relatively flat interiors called cratons. Both form over millions of years through [[Plate tectonics|plate tectonics]]. [[Stream|Streams]] – a major part of Earth's water cycle – shape the [[Landscape|landscape]], carve rocks, transport sediments, and replenish groundwater. At high elevations or [[Latitude|latitudes]], snow is compacted and recrystallized over hundreds or thousands of years to form glaciers, which can be so heavy that they warp the Earth's crust. About 30 percent of land has a dry climate, due to losing more water through evaporation than it gains from [[Precipitation|precipitation]]. Since warm air rises, this generates winds, though [[Earth's rotation]] and uneven sun distribution also play a part.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Land reclamation",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎆",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Creating new land from oceans, seas, riverbeds or lakes",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Artificial landforms" },
		aliases = { "Reclamation", "Land fill" },
		summary = "**Land reclamation**, often known as **reclamation**, and also known as **land fill** (not to be confused with a waste [[Landfill|landfill]]), is the process of creating new land from [[Ocean|oceans]], seas, riverbeds or [[Lake|lake]] beds. The land reclaimed is known as **reclamation ground**, **reclaimed land**, or **land fill**.",
	},
	{
		title = "Landform",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Feature of the solid surface of a planetary body",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Landforms" },
		summary = "A **landform** is a [[Land|land]] feature on the solid surface of the Earth or other planetary body. They may be natural or may be anthropogenic (caused or influenced by human activity). Landforms together make up a given [[Terrain|terrain]], and their arrangement in the [[Landscape|landscape]] is known as [[Topography|topography]]. Landforms include [[Hill|hills]], [[Mountain|mountains]], [[Canyon|canyons]], and [[Valley|valleys]], as well as shoreline features such as [[Bay|bays]], [[Peninsula|peninsulas]], and [[Ocean|seas]], including submerged features such as [[Mid-ocean ridge|mid-ocean ridges]], [[Volcano|volcanoes]], and the great oceanic basins.",
	},
}
]]></string>
          </Properties>
        </Item>
