	{
		title = "Lava",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🪨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Molten rock expelled by a volcano during an eruption",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Volcanic landforms" },
		summary = "**Lava** is [[Magma|magma]] that has been expelled from the interior of a [[Terrestrial planet|terrestrial planet]] (such as [[Earth]]) or a [[Natural satellite|moon]] onto its surface. Lava may be erupted at a [[Volcano|volcano]] or through a fracture in the [[Crust (geology)|crust]], on land or underwater, usually at temperatures from 800 to 1200 °C. Lava may be erupted directly onto the land surface or onto the sea floor or it may be ejected into the atmosphere before falling back down. The solid volcanic rock resulting from subsequent cooling of the molten material is often also called lava.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A **lava flow** is an outpouring of lava during an effusive eruption. (An explosive eruption, by contrast, produces a mixture of [[Volcanic ash|volcanic ash]] and other fragments called tephra, not lava flows.) The [[Viscosity|viscosity]] of most molten lava is about that of [[Ketchup|ketchup]], roughly 10,000 to 100,000 times that of water (the latter two substances measured at 25 °C and 1 [[Standard atmosphere (unit)|atm]]). Even so, lava can flow great distances before cooling causes it to solidify, because lava exposed to air quickly develops a solid crust that insulates the remaining liquid lava, helping to keep it hot and inviscid enough to continue flowing.",
			},
		},
