	{
		title = "Laws of thermodynamics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Observational basis of thermodynamics",
		tags = { "Physics", "Thermodynamics" },
		summary = "The **laws of thermodynamics** are a set of [[Scientific law|scientific laws]] which define a group of [[Physical quantity|physical quantities]], such as [[Temperature|temperature]], [[Energy|energy]], and [[Entropy|entropy]], that characterize [[Thermodynamic system|thermodynamic systems]] in [[Thermodynamic equilibrium|thermodynamic equilibrium]]. The laws also use various parameters for thermodynamic processes, such as [[Work (thermodynamics)|thermodynamic work]] and [[Heat|heat]], and establish relationships between them. They state empirical facts that form a basis of precluding the possibility of certain phenomena, such as perpetual motion. In addition to their use in [[Thermodynamics|thermodynamics]], they are important fundamental [[Scientific law|laws]] of [[Physics|physics]] in general and are applicable in other natural sciences.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Traditionally, thermodynamics has recognized three fundamental laws, simply named by an ordinal identification, the first law, the second law, and the third law. A more fundamental statement was later labelled as the zeroth law after the first three laws had been established. The zeroth law of thermodynamics defines thermal equilibrium and forms a basis for the definition of temperature: if two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, then they are in thermal equilibrium with each other. The [[First law of thermodynamics|first law of thermodynamics]] states that, when energy passes into or out of a system (as [[Work (thermodynamics)|work]], [[Heat|heat]], or [[Matter|matter]]), the system's [[Internal energy|internal energy]] changes in accordance with the law of [[Conservation of energy|conservation of energy]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lead",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Pb" },
		summary = "**Lead** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]] with the [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Pb** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 82. It is a heavy metal, [[Density|denser]] than most common materials. Lead is soft, [[Ductility|malleable]], and has a relatively low [[Melting point|melting point]]. When freshly cut or melted, it appears shiny silvery with a bluish tint, but [[Tarnish|tarnishes]] to dull gray on exposure to air. Lead has the highest atomic number of any stable element, and three of its [[Isotope|isotopes]] are endpoints of major nuclear [[Decay chain|decay chains]] of heavier elements.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Lead is a relatively un-reactive post-transition metal. Its weak metallic character is shown by its amphoteric behavior: lead and lead oxides react with both [[Acid|acids]] and [[Base (chemistry)|bases]], and it tends to form [[Covalent bond|covalent bonds]]. Lead compounds usually occur in the +2 [[Oxidation state|oxidation state]] rather than the +4 state common in lighter members of the carbon group, with exceptions mostly limited to organolead compounds. Like the lighter members of the group, lead can bond with itself, forming chains and polyhedral structures. Since lead is easily extracted from its [[Ore|ores]], prehistoric people in the Near East were aware of it. Galena is a principal ore of lead which often bears [[Silver|silver]]. Interest in silver helped initiate widespread extraction and use of lead in [[Roman Empire|ancient Rome]]. Lead production declined after the [[Fall of the Western Roman Empire|fall of Rome]] and did not reach comparable levels until the [[Industrial Revolution]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lepton",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Class of elementary particles",
		tags = { "Subatomic particles", "Fermions" },
		summary = "In [[Particle physics|particle physics]], a **lepton** is an [[Elementary particle|elementary particle]] of half-integer spin ([[Spin (physics)|spin]] 1/2) that does not undergo [[Strong interaction|strong interactions]]. Two main classes of leptons exist: [[Electric charge|charged]] leptons (also known as the [[Electron|electron]]-like leptons or muons), including the [[Electron|electron]], [[Muon|muon]], and tauon, and neutral leptons, better known as [[Neutrino|neutrinos]]. Charged leptons can combine with other particles to form various composite particles such as [[Atom|atoms]] and positronium, while neutrinos rarely interact with anything, and are consequently rarely observed. The best known of all leptons is the [[Electron|electron]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are six types of leptons, known as flavours, grouped in three generations. The [[Standard Model|first-generation]] leptons, also called electronic leptons, comprise the [[Electron|electron]] and the electron neutrino; the second are the muonic leptons, comprising the [[Muon|muon]] and the muon neutrino; and the third are the tauonic leptons, comprising the [[Tau (particle)|tau]] and the tau neutrino. Electrons have the least mass of all the charged leptons. The heavier muons and taus will rapidly change into electrons and neutrinos through a process of particle decay: the transformation from a higher mass state to a lower mass state. Thus electrons are stable and the most common charged lepton in the [[Universe|universe]], whereas muons and taus can only be produced in high-energy collisions (such as those involving [[Cosmic ray|cosmic rays]] and those carried out in [[Particle accelerator|particle accelerators]]).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lichen",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍄",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Symbiosis of fungi with algae",
		tags = { "Organisms" },
		summary = 'A **lichen** is a hybrid [[Colony (biology)|colony]] of [[Algae|algae]] or [[Cyanobacteria|cyanobacteria]] living symbiotically among [[Hypha|filaments]] of multiple [[Mushroom|fungus]] species, along with bacteria embedded in the cortex, or "skin", in a [[Mutualism (biology)|mutualistic]] relationship. Lichens are the lifeform that first brought the term symbiosis (as Symbiotismus) into biological context.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Lichens have since been recognized as important actors in nutrient cycling and producers which many higher trophic feeders feed on, such as [[Reindeer|reindeer]], [[Gastropod|gastropods]], [[Nematode|nematodes]], mites, and [[Springtail|springtails]]. Lichens have properties different from those of their component organisms. They come in many colors, sizes, and forms and are sometimes plant-like, but are not [[Plant|plants]]. They may have tiny, leafless branches (fruticose) or flat, leaf-like structures (foliose); they may grow crust-like, adhering tightly to a surface (substrate) like a thick coat of paint (crustose), have a powder-like appearance (leprose), or feature other growth forms. A macrolichen is a lichen that is either bush-like or leafy; all other lichens are termed microlichens. Here, "macro" and "micro" do not refer to size, but to the growth form.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Life",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Matter with biological processes",
		tags = { "Basics" },
		summary = "**Life** is the capacity in [[Matter|matter]], formed of one or more units called [[Cell (biology)|cells]], for [[Biological process|processes]] such as [[Cell signaling|cell signaling]], [[Homeostasis|homeostasis]], [[Metabolism|metabolism]], cell growth, [[Adaptation|adaptation]], response to stimuli, and [[Reproduction|reproduction]]. All life eventually reaches a state of death. Many philosophical definitions of living systems have been proposed, such as self-organizing systems. Defining life is further complicated by [[Virus|viruses]], which replicate only in [[Host (biology)|host]] cells, and the possibility of [[Extraterrestrial life|extraterrestrial life]], which could be very different from life on [[Earth]]. Life exists all over the Earth in air, water, and [[Soil|soil]], with many [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]] forming the [[Biosphere|biosphere]]. Some of these are harsh environments occupied only by [[Extremophile|extremophiles]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The life in a particular ecosystem is called its **biota**. Life has been studied since ancient times, with theories such as Empedocles's [[Materialism|materialism]] asserting that it was composed of four eternal elements, and [[Aristotle]]'s hylomorphism asserting that living things have souls and embody both form and matter. Life originated at least 3.5 billion years ago, resulting in a universal common ancestor. This evolved into all the [[Species|species]] that exist now, by way of many [[Extinction|extinct]] species, some of which have left traces as [[Fossil|fossils]]. Attempts to classify living things, too, began with Aristotle. Modern classification began with [[Carl Linnaeus]]'s system of [[Binomial nomenclature|binomial nomenclature]] in the 1740s. Living things are composed of [[Biochemistry|biochemical molecules]], formed mainly from a few core [[Chemical element|chemical elements]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Life" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lift (force)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏋️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Force perpendicular to flow of surrounding fluid",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Fluid mechanics" },
		summary = "When a [[Fluid|fluid]] flows around an object, the fluid exerts a [[Force|force]] on the object. **Lift** is the [[Euclidean vector|component]] of this force that is [[Perpendicular|perpendicular]] to the oncoming flow direction. It contrasts with the [[Drag (physics)|drag]] force, which is the component of the force parallel to the flow direction. Lift conventionally acts in an upward direction in order to counter the force of [[Gravity|gravity]], but it may act in any direction perpendicular to the flow.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"If the surrounding fluid is air, the force is called an aerodynamic force. In water or any other liquid, it is called a [[Fluid dynamics|hydrodynamic force]]. Dynamic lift is distinguished from other kinds of lift in fluids. Aerostatic lift or [[Buoyancy|buoyancy]], in which an internal fluid is lighter than the surrounding fluid, does not require movement and is used by balloons, blimps, dirigibles, boats, and submarines. Planing lift, in which only the lower portion of the body is immersed in a liquid flow, is used by motorboats, surfboards, windsurfers, sailboats, and water-skis.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Light-year",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "📅",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Distance that light travels in one year",
		tags = { "Dimensional space", "Length and distance" },
		aliases = { "Ly", "Lyr" },
		summary = 'A **light-year**, alternatively spelled **light year** (**ly** or **lyr**), is a [[Unit of length|unit of length]] used to express astronomical distances and is equal to exactly 9,460,730,472,580.8 km, which is approximately 9.46 trillion kilometres or 5.88 trillion miles. As defined by the [[International Astronomical Union]] (IAU), a light-year is the distance that [[Speed of light|light travels in vacuum]] in one [[Julian year (astronomy)|Julian year]] (365.25 days). Despite its inclusion of the word "year", the term is a unit of [[Distance|distance]], not a unit of time.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The light-year is most often used when expressing distances to stars and other distances on a [[Galaxy|galactic]] scale, especially in non-specialist contexts and [[Popular science|popular science]] publications. The unit most commonly used in professional [[Astronomy|astronomy]] is the [[Parsec|parsec]] (pc), approximately 3.26 light-years.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Symbol", "ly" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Limestone",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🪨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of sedimentary rock",
		tags = { "Geology", "Petrology" },
		summary = "**Limestone** is a type of carbonate [[Sedimentary rock|sedimentary rock]] which is the main source of the material lime. It is composed mostly of the [[Mineral|minerals]] [[Calcite|calcite]] and [[Aragonite|aragonite]], which are different crystal forms of [[Calcium carbonate|calcium carbonate]] CaCO3. Limestone forms when these minerals precipitate out of water containing dissolved [[Calcium|calcium]]. This can take place through both [[Biological process|biological]] and nonbiological processes, though biological processes, such as the accumulation of corals and shells in the sea, have likely been more important for the last 540 million years. Limestone often contains [[Fossil|fossils]] which provide scientists with information on ancient environments and on the [[Evolution|evolution]] of life.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"About 20% to 25% of sedimentary rock is carbonate rock, and most of this is limestone. The remaining carbonate rock is mostly dolomite, a closely related rock, which contains a high percentage of the mineral dolomite, CaMg(CO3)2. Magnesian limestone is an obsolete and poorly defined term used variously for dolomite, for limestone containing significant dolomite (dolomitic limestone), or for any other limestone containing a significant percentage of [[Magnesium|magnesium]]. Most limestone was formed in shallow marine environments, such as continental shelves or platforms, though smaller amounts were formed in many other environments. Much dolomite is secondary dolomite, formed by chemical alteration of limestone. Limestone is exposed over large regions of the Earth's surface, and because limestone is slightly [[Solubility|soluble]] in rainwater, these exposures often are eroded to become [[Karst|karst]] landscapes.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Gneiss" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Sedimentary" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Limnology",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Science of inland aquatic ecosystems",
		tags = { "Water", "Hydrology" },
		summary = "**Limnology** is the study of inland aquatic ecosystems. It includes aspects of the [[Biology|biological]], [[Chemistry|chemical]], [[Physics|physical]], and [[Geology|geological]] characteristics of natural and man-made [[Body of water|bodies of]] [[Fresh water|fresh]] and [[Saline water|saline]] water. This includes the study of [[Lake|lakes]], [[Reservoir|reservoirs]], [[Pond|ponds]], [[River|rivers]], springs, [[Stream|streams]], [[Wetland|wetlands]], and [[Groundwater|groundwater]]. Water systems are often categorized as either running (lotic) or standing (lentic).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Limnology includes the study of the drainage basin, the movement of water through the basin, and the biogeochemical changes that occur en route. A more recent sub-discipline of limnology, termed landscape limnology, studies, manages, and seeks to conserve these [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]] using a landscape perspective, by explicitly examining connections between an aquatic ecosystem and its [[Drainage basin|drainage basin]]. Recently, the need to understand global inland waters as part of the Earth system created a sub-discipline called global limnology. This approach considers processes in inland waters on a global scale, like the role of inland aquatic ecosystems in global [[Biogeochemical cycle|biogeochemical cycles]]. Limnology is closely related to aquatic ecology and hydrobiology, which study aquatic organisms and their interactions with the abiotic (non-living) environment.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lipid",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Lipid that is soluble in nonpolar solvents with their respective range",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		aliases = { "Lipids" },
		summary = "**Lipids** are a broad group of organic compounds that include [[Fat|fats]], [[Wax|waxes]], sterols, fat-soluble vitamins (such as vitamins A, D, E and K), monoglycerides, diglycerides, phospholipids, and others. The functions of lipids include storing energy, signaling, and acting as structural components of [[Cell membrane|cell membranes]]. Lipids have applications in the cosmetic and [[Food industry|food industries]], and in [[Nanotechnology|nanotechnology]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Lipids are broadly defined as hydrophobic or amphiphilic small molecules; the amphiphilic nature of some lipids allows them to form structures such as vesicles, multilamellar/unilamellar liposomes, or membranes in an aqueous environment. Biological lipids originate entirely or in part from two distinct types of biochemical subunits or "building-blocks": ketoacyl and isoprene groups. Using this approach, lipids may be divided into eight categories: [[Fatty acid|fatty acyls]], glycerolipids, glycerophospholipids, sphingolipids, saccharolipids, and polyketides (derived from condensation of ketoacyl subunits); and sterol lipids and prenol lipids (derived from condensation of isoprene subunits). Although the term lipid is sometimes used as a synonym for fats, fats are a subgroup of lipids called triglycerides.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Liquid",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "State of matter",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "States of matter" },
		summary = "**Liquid** is a [[State of matter|state of matter]] with a definite volume but no fixed shape. When confined in a container and subject to a force such as [[Gravity|gravity]], liquids will adapt to the internal shape of the container in the direction of the force. Liquids are nearly incompressible, maintaining their volume even under pressure. The [[Density|density]] of a liquid is usually close to that of a [[Solid|solid]], and much higher than that of a [[Gas|gas]]. Liquids are a form of [[Condensed matter physics|condensed matter]] alongside solids, and a form of [[Fluid|fluid]] alongside gases.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A liquid is composed of atoms or molecules held together by intermolecular bonds of intermediate strength. These forces allow the particles to move around one another while remaining closely packed. In contrast, solids have particles that are tightly bound by strong intermolecular forces, limiting their movement to small vibrations in fixed positions. Gases, on the other hand, consist of widely spaced, freely moving particles with only weak intermolecular forces. As temperature increases, the molecules in a liquid vibrate more intensely, causing the distances between them to increase. At the [[Boiling point|boiling point]], the cohesive forces between the molecules are no longer sufficient to keep them together, and the liquid transitions into a gaseous state. Conversely, as temperature decreases, the distance between molecules shrinks.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lithium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Li" },
		summary = "**Lithium** (from, stone) is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Li** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 3. It is a soft, silvery-white [[Alkali metal|alkali metal]]. Under [[Standard temperature and pressure|standard conditions]], it is the least dense metal and the least dense solid element. Like all alkali metals, lithium is highly [[Reactivity (chemistry)|reactive]] and flammable, and must be stored in vacuum, inert atmosphere, or inert liquid such as purified [[Kerosene|kerosene]] or mineral oil. It exhibits a metallic luster when pure, but quickly [[Corrosion|corrodes]] in air to a dull silvery gray, then black tarnish. It does not occur freely in nature, but occurs mainly as pegmatitic minerals, which were once the main source of lithium.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Due to its solubility as an ion, it is present in ocean water and is commonly obtained from [[Brine|brines]]. Lithium metal is isolated [[Electrolysis|electrolytically]] from a mixture of lithium chloride and potassium chloride. The [[Atomic nucleus|nucleus]] of the lithium atom verges on instability, since the two stable lithium [[Isotope|isotopes]] found in nature have among the lowest [[Nuclear binding energy|binding energies]] per [[Nucleon|nucleon]] of all stable [[Nuclide|nuclides]]. Because of its relative nuclear instability, lithium is less common in the [[Solar System]] than 28 of the first 34 chemical elements even though its nuclei are very light: it is an exception to the trend that heavier nuclei are less common. For related reasons, lithium has important uses in [[Nuclear physics|nuclear physics]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lithosphere",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Outermost shell of a terrestrial-type planet or natural satellite",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geology" },
		summary = "A **lithosphere** is the rigid outermost rocky shell of a [[Terrestrial planet|terrestrial planet]] or [[Natural satellite|natural satellite]]. On [[Earth]], it is composed of the [[Crust (geology)|crust]] and the lithospheric mantle, the topmost portion of the upper mantle that behaves elastically on time scales of up to thousands of years or more. The crust and upper mantle are distinguished on the basis of chemistry and [[Mineralogy|mineralogy]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Litre",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Unit of volume",
		tags = { "Dimensional space", "Volume" },
		aliases = { "Liter", "And", "Other symbol used" },
		summary = "The **litre** ([[English in the Commonwealth of Nations|Commonwealth spelling]]) or **liter** ([[American English|American spelling]]) (SI symbols **L** and **l**, other symbol used: **ℓ**) is a metric unit of [[Volume|volume]]. It is equal to 1 cubic decimetre (dm3), 1000 cubic centimetres (cm3) or 0.001 cubic metres (m3). A cubic decimetre (or litre) occupies a volume of 10 cm × 10 cm × 10 cm (see figure) and is thus equal to one-thousandth of a cubic metre.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The original French [[Metric system|metric system]] used the litre as a [[SI base unit|base unit]]. The word litre is derived from an older French unit, the litron, whose name came from Byzantine [[Greek language|Greek]]—where it was a unit of weight, not volume—via Late Medieval Latin, and which equalled approximately 0.82 litres. The litre was also used in several subsequent versions of the metric system but is [[International System of Units|not an SI unit]]; the SI unit of volume is the cubic metre (m3). The spelling used by the [[International Bureau of Weights and Measures]] is "litre", a spelling which is shared by most English-speaking countries. The spelling "liter" is predominantly used in [[American and British English spelling differences|American English]]. One litre of liquid water has a [[Mass|mass]] of almost exactly one [[Kilogram|kilogram]], because the kilogram was originally defined in 1795 as the mass of one cubic decimetre of water at the temperature of melting ice (0 degC).',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Symbol", "L" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Longevity",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Longer than typical lifespan, especially of humans",
		tags = { "Biological processes and physiology", "Animal ontogeny" },
		summary = "**Longevity** may refer to especially long-lived members of a population, whereas [[Life expectancy|life expectancy]] is defined [[Statistics|statistically]] as the average number of years remaining at a given age. For example, a population's life expectancy at birth is the same as the average age at death for all people born in the same year (in the case of cohorts).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Longevity studies may involve putative methods to extend life. Longevity has been a topic not only for the scientific community but also for writers of travel, [[Science fiction|science fiction]], and [[Utopia|utopian]] novels. The legendary fountain of youth appeared in the work of the Ancient Greek historian [[Herodotus]]. There are difficulties in authenticating the longest human life span, owing to inaccurate or incomplete birth statistics. Fiction, legend, and folklore have proposed or claimed life spans in the past or future vastly longer than those verified by modern standards, and longevity narratives and unverified longevity claims frequently speak of their existence in the present.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lorentz force",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏋️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Force acting on charged particles in electric and magnetic fields",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrodynamics" },
		summary = "In [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]], the **Lorentz force** is the [[Force|force]] exerted on a [[Charged particle|charged particle]] by [[Electric field|electric]] and [[Magnetic field|magnetic fields]]. It determines how charged particles move in electromagnetic environments and underlies many physical phenomena, from the operation of [[Electric motor|electric motors]] and [[Particle accelerator|particle accelerators]] to the behavior of [[Plasma (physics)|plasmas]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Lorentz force has two components. The **electric force** acts in the direction of the electric field for positive charges and opposite to it for negative charges, tending to accelerate the particle in a straight line. The **magnetic force** is perpendicular to both the particle's velocity and the magnetic field, and it causes the particle to move along a curved trajectory, often circular or helical in form, depending on the directions of the fields. Variations on the force law describe the magnetic force on a current-carrying wire (sometimes called **Laplace force**), and the [[Electromotive force|electromotive force]] in a wire loop moving through a magnetic field, as described by [[Faraday's law of induction]]. Together with [[Maxwell's equations]], which describe how electric and magnetic fields are generated by charges and currents, the Lorentz force law forms the foundation of classical electrodynamics.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lorentz transformation",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of linear transformations",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Theory of relativity" },
		aliases = { "Lorentz transformations" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], the **Lorentz transformations** are a six-parameter family of linear transformations from a [[Frame of reference|coordinate frame]] in [[Spacetime|spacetime]] to another frame that moves at a constant [[Velocity|velocity]] relative to the former. The respective inverse transformation is then parameterized by the negative of this velocity. The transformations are named after the Dutch [[Physicist|physicist]] [[Hendrik Lorentz]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'When speed v is much smaller than c, the Lorentz factor is negligibly different from 1, but as v approaches c, grows without bound. The value of v must be smaller than c for the transformation to make sense. Expressing the speed as a fraction of the speed of light, an equivalent form of the transformation is Frames of reference can be divided into two groups: [[Inertial frame of reference|inertial]] (relative motion with constant velocity) and non-inertial (accelerating, moving in curved paths, rotational motion with constant angular velocity, etc.). The term "Lorentz transformations" only refers to transformations between inertial frames, usually in the context of special relativity. In each [[Frame of reference|reference frame]], an observer can use a local coordinate system (usually [[Cartesian coordinate system|Cartesian coordinates]] in this context) to measure lengths, and a clock to measure time intervals.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Low-pressure area",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Area with air pressures lower than adjacent areas",
		tags = { "Air", "Meteorology" },
		aliases = { "LPA", "Low area", "Low" },
		summary = "In [[Meteorology|meteorology]], a **low-pressure area** (**LPA**), **low area**, or **low** is a region where the [[Atmospheric pressure|atmospheric pressure]] is lower than that of surrounding locations. It is the opposite of a [[High-pressure area|high-pressure area]]. Low-pressure areas are commonly associated with inclement weather (such as cloudy, windy, with possible rain or storms), while [[High-pressure area|high-pressure areas]] are associated with lighter winds and clear skies. Winds circle anti-clockwise around lows in the northern hemisphere, and clockwise in the southern hemisphere, by dint of opposing [[Coriolis force|Coriolis forces]]. Large low-pressure, rotating air masses whose winds spiral inward are termed [[Cyclone|cyclones]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Low-pressure systems form under areas of wind divergence that occur in the upper levels of the [[Atmosphere of Earth|atmosphere]] (aloft). The formation process of a low-pressure area is known as cyclogenesis. In [[Meteorology|meteorology]], atmospheric divergence aloft occurs in two kinds of places: The first is in the area on the east side of upper troughs, which form half of a Rossby wave within the Westerlies (a trough with large [[Wavelength|wavelength]] that extends through the troposphere). A second is an area where wind divergence aloft occurs ahead of embedded shortwave troughs, which are of smaller wavelength. Diverging winds aloft, ahead of these troughs, cause atmospheric lift within the [[Troposphere|troposphere]] below as air flows upwards away from the surface, which lowers surface pressures as this upward motion partially counteracts the force of gravity packing the air close to the ground.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lutetium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Lu" },
		summary = "**Lutetium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Lu** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 71. It is a silvery white [[Metal|metal]], which resists corrosion in dry air, but not in moist air. Lutetium is the last element in the [[Lanthanide|lanthanide]] series, and it is traditionally counted among the [[Rare-earth element|rare earth elements]]; it can also be classified as the first element of the sixth-period [[Transition metal|transition metals]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Lutetium was independently discovered in 1907 by French scientist Georges Urbain, Austrian mineralogist Baron Carl Auer von Welsbach, and American chemist Charles James. All of these researchers found lutetium as an impurity in [[Ytterbium|ytterbium]]. The dispute on the priority of the discovery occurred shortly after, with Urbain and Welsbach accusing each other of publishing results influenced by the published research of the other; the naming honor went to Urbain, as he had published his results earlier. He chose the name lutecium for the new element honoring the Latin name for Paris, Lutetia. In 1949 the spelling was changed to lutetium. In 1909, the priority was finally granted to Urbain and his names were adopted as official ones; however, the name cassiopeium (or later cassiopium) for element 71 proposed by Welsbach was used by many German scientists until the 1950s.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Berkelium" },
	},
	{
		title = "Lysosome",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦠",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Cell membrane organelle",
		tags = { "Cell biology", "Organelles and other cell parts" },
		summary = "There are normally hundreds of lysosomes in the cytosol, where they function as the cell's degradation center. Their primary responsibility is for catabolic degradation of proteins, polysaccharides and lipids into their respective building-block molecules: amino acids, monosaccharides, and free fatty acids. The breakdown is done by various enzymes, for example proteases, glycosidases and lipases.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"With an acidic lumen limited by a single-bilayer lipid membrane, the lysosome holds an environment isolated from the rest of the cell. The lower pH creates optimal conditions for the over 60 different hydrolases inside. Lysosomes receive extracellular particles through endocytosis, and intracellular components through autophagy. They can also fuse with the plasma membrane and secrete their contents, a process called lysosomal exocytosis. After degradation lysosomal products are transported out of the lysosome through specific membrane proteins or via vesicular membrane trafficking to be recycled or to be utilized for energy. Aside from cellular clearance and secretion, lysosomes mediate biological processes like plasma membrane repair, cell [[Homeostasis|homeostasis]], energy metabolism, cell signaling, and the immune response.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cytoplasm", "Cytoskeleton" },
	},
	{
		title = "Magma",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Hot semifluid material found beneath the surface of Earth",
		tags = { "Geology", "Petrology" },
		summary = "**Magma** is the molten or semi-molten natural material from which all [[Igneous rock|igneous rocks]] are formed. Magma (sometimes colloquially but incorrectly referred to as [[Lava|lava]]) is found beneath the surface of the [[Earth]], and evidence of magmatism has also been discovered on other [[Terrestrial planet|terrestrial planets]] and some [[Natural satellite|natural satellites]]. Besides molten rock, magma may also contain suspended crystals and gas bubbles.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Magma is produced by melting of the [[Mantle (geology)|mantle]] or the [[Crust (geology)|crust]] in various [[Tectonics|tectonic]] settings, which on Earth include subduction zones, continental rift zones, [[Mid-ocean ridge|mid-ocean ridges]] and [[Hotspot (geology)|hotspots]]. Mantle and crustal melts migrate upwards through the crust where they are thought to be stored in magma chambers or trans-crustal crystal-rich mush zones. During magma's storage in the crust, its composition may be modified by fractional crystallization, contamination with crustal melts, magma mixing, and degassing. Following its ascent through the crust, magma may feed a [[Volcano|volcano]] and be extruded as lava, or it may solidify underground to form an intrusion, such as a dike, a sill, a laccolith, a pluton, or a batholith.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Magnesium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Mg" },
		summary = "**Magnesium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Mg** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 12. It is a shiny gray metal having a low density, low melting point, and high chemical reactivity. Like the other [[Alkaline earth metal|alkaline earth metals]] (group 2 of the [[Periodic table|periodic table]]), it occurs naturally only in combination with other elements and almost always has an [[Oxidation state|oxidation state]] of +2. It reacts readily with air to form a thin [[Passivation (chemistry)|passivation]] coating of magnesium oxide that inhibits further corrosion of the metal. The free metal burns with a brilliant-white light. The metal is obtained mainly by [[Electrolysis|electrolysis]] of magnesium [[Salt (chemistry)|salts]] obtained from [[Brine|brine]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is less dense than [[Aluminium|aluminium]] and is used primarily as a component in strong and lightweight alloys that contain aluminium. In the cosmos, magnesium is produced in large, aging [[Star|stars]] by the sequential addition of three helium nuclei to a [[Carbon|carbon]] nucleus. When such stars explode as [[Supernova|supernovas]], much of the magnesium is expelled into the [[Interstellar medium|interstellar medium]], where it may recycle into new star systems. Magnesium is the eighth most [[Abundance of elements in Earth's crust|abundant element in Earth's crust]] and the fourth most common element in the Earth (after [[Iron|iron]], [[Oxygen|oxygen]] and [[Silicon|silicon]]), making up 13% of the planet's mass and a large fraction of the planet's [[Mantle (geology)|mantle]]. It is the third most abundant element dissolved in seawater, after [[Sodium|sodium]] and [[Chlorine|chlorine]]. This element is the eleventh most abundant element by mass in the [[Human body|human body]] and is essential to all cells and some 300 enzymes.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Magnesium sulfate",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical compound with formula MgSO4",
		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Salts" },
		aliases = { "Magnesium sulphate" },
		summary = "**Magnesium sulfate** or **magnesium sulphate** is a [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]], a [[Salt (chemistry)|salt]] with the formula MgSO4, consisting of [[Magnesium|magnesium]] [[Ion|cations]] Mg(2+) (20.19% by mass) and [[Sulfate|sulfate]] [[Ion|anions]] SO4(2-). It is a white [[Crystal|crystalline solid]], [[Solubility|soluble]] in water.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Magnesium sulfate is usually encountered in the form of a hydrate MgSO4*nH2O, for various values of n between 1 and 11. The most common is the heptahydrate MgSO4*7H2O, known as Epsom salt, which is a household chemical with many traditional uses, including bath salts. The main use of magnesium sulfate is in agriculture, to correct soils deficient in magnesium (an essential [[Plant nutrition|plant nutrient]] because of the role of magnesium in [[Chlorophyll|chlorophyll]] and [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]]). The monohydrate is favored for this use; by the mid 1970s, its production was 2.3 million tons per year with production increasing to approximately 2.6 million tons in 2024. The [[Hydrate|anhydrous]] form and several hydrates occur in nature as minerals, and the salt is a significant component of the water from some springs.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Potassium nitrate" },
	},
	{
		title = "Magnetic field",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Property of space that quantifies the magnetic influence at a given location",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Magnetism" },
		summary = "In [[Magnetism|magnetism]] and [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]], **magnetic field** is a physical property of space that quantifies the magnetic influence at a given location. Magnetic fields deflect moving electric charges (including [[Electric current|electric currents]]), apply torques on [[Magnetism|magnets]] to twist them in the direction of the magnetic field, and attract or repel magnets and magnetic material such as [[Iron|iron]]. In addition, a time-varying magnetic field [[Electromagnetic induction|induces]] electrical currents. Magnetic fields are created by magnetic materials and by moving electric charges (including electrical current). The latter is important in creating [[Electromagnet|electromagnets]]: devices that precisely control magnetic fields by changing the current through the electromagnet.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Magnetic fields are used throughout modern science and technology. In [[Electrical engineering|electrical engineering]] and electromechanics it is important in the design and use of [[Electric motor|electric motors]], [[Electric generator|generators]], [[Transformer|transformers]], electromagnets, and [[Inductor|inductors]] among many other devices. In material science, magnetic forces give information about the charge carriers in a material through the Hall effect in addition to other uses. In [[Geology|geology]] and [[Geophysics|geophysics]], [[Earth's magnetic field]] gives information about earth's interior while local magnetic field measurements are used in mineral exploration and other measurements. Too, Earth's magnetic field creates a [[Magnetosphere|magnetosphere]] which shields the Earth's [[Ozone layer|ozone layer]] and the rest of the planet from the [[Solar wind|solar wind]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Magnetostatics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of physics about magnetism in systems with steady electric currents",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Magnetism" },
		summary = "**Magnetostatics** is the study of [[Magnetic field|magnetic fields]] in systems where the currents are steady (not changing with time). It is the magnetic analogue of [[Electrostatics|electrostatics]], where the [[Electric charge|charges]] are stationary. The magnetization need not be static; the equations of magnetostatics can be used to predict fast magnetic switching events that occur on time scales of nanoseconds or less. Magnetostatics is even a good approximation when the currents are not static – as long as the currents do not [[Alternating current|alternate]] rapidly. Magnetostatics is widely used in applications of micromagnetics such as models of magnetic storage devices as in [[Computer memory|computer memory]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Manganese",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Mn" },
		summary = "**Manganese** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has the [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Mn** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 25. It is a hard, brittle, silvery metal, often found in [[Mineral|minerals]] in combination with [[Iron|iron]]. First isolated in the 1770s, manganese is a [[Transition metal|transition metal]] with many industrial [[Alloy|alloy]] uses, particularly in [[Stainless steel|stainless steels]] in which it improves strength, workability, and resistance to wear. Various manganese oxides are used as oxidising agents, as rubber additives, and in glass making, fertilizers, and ceramics. Manganese sulfate can be used as a fungicide.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Manganese is also an essential human dietary element, important in macronutrient metabolism, bone formation, and free radical defense systems. It is a critical component in dozens of [[Protein|proteins]] and [[Enzyme|enzymes]]. It is found mostly in the bones, but also the liver, kidneys, and brain. In the human brain, manganese is bound to manganese metalloproteins, most notably glutamine synthetase in astrocytes. Manganese in the form of the deep violet salt potassium permanganate is commonly used in laboratories as an [[Oxidizing agent|oxidizer]]. Potassium permanganate is also used as a biocide in water treatment. It occurs at the active sites in some enzymes. Of particular interest is the use of a Mn–O cluster, the oxygen-evolving complex, in the production of oxygen by plants.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mangrove",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Shrub growing in brackish water",
		tags = { "Biomes", "Forests" },
		summary = "A **mangrove** is a [[Shrub|shrub]] or tree that grows mainly in coastal [[Saline water|saline]] or [[Brackish water|brackish water]]. Mangroves grow in an equatorial climate, typically along coastlines and tidal rivers. The term is also used for tropical coastal vegetation consisting of such species. Mangroves are taxonomically diverse due to convergent evolution in several plant families. They occur worldwide in [[Tropics|tropical]] and [[Subtropics|subtropical]] coastal areas, mainly between latitudes 30° N and 30° S, with the greatest mangrove area within 5° of the [[Equator|equator]]. Mangrove plant families first appeared during the [[Late Cretaceous]] to [[Paleocene]] epochs and became widely distributed in part due to the [[Plate tectonics|movement of tectonic plates]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The oldest known fossils of mangrove palm date to 75 million years ago. Mangroves are salt-tolerant (halophytic) and are adapted to live in harsh coastal conditions. They contain a complex salt filtration system and a complex root system to cope with saltwater immersion and wave action. They are adapted to the low-oxygen conditions of waterlogged mud, but are most likely to thrive in the upper half of the [[Intertidal zone|intertidal zone]]. The mangrove [[Biome|biome]], often called the mangrove forest or mangal, is a distinct saline [[Woodland|woodland]] or [[Shrubland|shrubland]] habitat characterized by depositional coastal environments, where fine sediments (often with high organic content) collect in areas protected from high-energy wave action.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Jungle" },
	},
	{
		title = "Marble",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🪨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of metamorphic rock",
		tags = { "Geology", "Petrology" },
		summary = "**Marble** is a [[Metamorphic rock|metamorphic rock]] consisting of carbonate minerals (most commonly [[Calcite|calcite]] (CaCO3) or dolomite (CaMg(CO3)2) that have recrystallized under the influence of heat and pressure. It has a crystalline texture, and is typically not foliated (layered), although there are exceptions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In [[Geology|geology]], the term marble refers to metamorphosed [[Limestone|limestone]], but its use in stonemasonry more broadly encompasses unmetamorphosed limestone. Pure white marble arises from very clean limestone or from dolomite rock. However, its signature veins and swirls result from impurities, for example clay, sand, [[Iron oxide|iron oxides]], or chert that were originally present in the protolith—magnesium-rich varieties may show green tones due to serpentine. These impurities are redistributed and recrystallized during metamorphism. Due to its hardness, durability and resistance to temperature, marble is often used in sculpture and [[Construction|construction]]. In sculpture, marble is ideal for fine detail due to its uniform grain, moderate hardness, and ability to scatter light beneath the surface (subsurface scattering), lending the sculpture a lifelike, waxy appearance.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Metamorphic" },
		},
