		summary = "For most species, mating is between two individuals of opposite sexes; however, for hermaphroditic species, copulation is not required because the parent organism is capable of self-fertilization (autogamy). The basis of this is a process called [[Fertilisation|fertilization]], which is the fusion of two [[Gamete|gametes]]. One gamete from the female (egg) and one gamete from the male (sperm) is used for fertilization. Mating may also lead to external fertilization, as seen in amphibians, bony fishes and plants. The term mating also applies to similar processes in fungi and unicellular protists.",
