	{
		title = "Matter",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Something that has mass and volume",
		tags = { "Physics", "Basics" },
		summary = "In physical science, **matter** is any substance that has [[Mass|mass]] and takes up space by having [[Volume|volume]]. All everyday objects that can be touched are ultimately composed of [[Atom|atoms]], which are made up of interacting [[Subatomic particle|subatomic particles]]. In everyday as well as scientific usage, matter generally includes [[Atom|atoms]] and anything made up of them, and any particles (or combination of particles) that act as if they have both rest mass and volume. However it does not include massless particles such as [[Photon|photons]], or other energy phenomena or waves such as [[Light|light]] or [[Heat|heat]]. Matter exists in various [[State of matter|states]] (also known as [[Phase (matter)|phases]]).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'These include classical everyday phases such as [[Solid|solid]], [[Liquid|liquid]], and [[Gas|gas]] – for example [[Water|water]] exists as [[Ice|ice]], liquid water, and gaseous [[Steam|steam]] – but other states are possible, including [[Plasma (physics)|plasma]], [[Bose–Einstein condensate|Bose–Einstein condensates]], fermionic condensates, and quark–gluon plasma. Usually, atoms can be imagined as a [[Atomic nucleus|nucleus]] consisting of [[Subatomic particle|subatomic particles]], [[Proton|protons]] and [[Neutron|neutrons]] and a surrounding "cloud" of orbiting [[Electron|electrons]] that "take up space". However, this is only somewhat correct because subatomic particles and their properties are governed by their [[Quantum mechanics|quantum nature]], which means they do not act as everyday objects appear to act – they can act like [[Wave–particle duality|waves as well as particles]], and they do not have well-defined sizes or positions.',
			},
		},
