	{
		title = "Meadow",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Open habitat vegetated primarily by non-woody plants",
		tags = { "Biomes", "Grasslands" },
		summary = "A **meadow** is an open [[Habitat|habitat]] or field, [[Vegetation|vegetated]] by [[Grass|grasses]], [[Herb|herbs]], and other non-[[Woody plant|woody plants]]. [[Tree|Trees]] or [[Shrub|shrubs]] may sparsely populate meadows, as long as they maintain an open character. Meadows can occur naturally under favourable conditions but are often artificially created from cleared shrub or [[Woodland|woodland]] for the production of [[Hay|hay]], [[Fodder|fodder]] or [[Livestock|livestock]]. Meadow habitats as a group are characterized as semi-natural grasslands, meaning that they are largely composed of [[Native species|species native]] to the region, with only limited human intervention.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Meadows attract a multitude of [[Wildlife|wildlife]] and support [[Plant|flora]] and [[Animal|fauna]] that cannot thrive in other habitats. They are [[Ecology|ecologically]] important since they provide areas for animal courtship displays, nesting, food gathering, [[Pollination|pollinating]] insects, and sometimes sheltering if the vegetation is high enough. Intensified agricultural practices (too frequent mowing, use of mineral fertilizers, manure and insecticides) may lead to declines in the abundance of organisms and species diversity. There are many types of meadow, including agricultural, transitional, and perpetual – each being a unique and important part of the [[Ecosystem|ecosystem]]. Like other biomes, meadows will experience increased pressure (including on their biodiversity) owing to [[Climate change|climate change]], especially as [[Precipitation|precipitation]] and weather conditions change.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Prairie", "Steppe" },
	},
	{
		title = "Meander",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "One of a series of curves in a channel of a matured stream",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Fluvial landforms" },
		summary = "A **meander** is one of a series of regular sinuous curves in the [[Channel (geography)|channel]] of a [[Stream|stream]]. It is produced as a watercourse [[Erosion|erodes]] the [[Sediment|sediments]] of an outer, concave bank (cut bank or river cliff) and deposits sediments on an inner, convex bank which is typically a point bar. The result of this coupled [[Erosion|erosion]] and sedimentation is the formation of a sinuous course as the channel migrates back and forth across the axis of a [[Floodplain|floodplain]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The zone within which a meandering stream periodically shifts its channel is known as a **meander belt**. It typically ranges from 15 to 18 times the width of the channel. Over time, meanders migrate downstream, sometimes in such a short time as to create [[Civil engineering|civil engineering]] challenges for local municipalities attempting to maintain stable roads and bridges. The degree of meandering of the channel of a river, stream, or other watercourse is measured by its sinuosity. The sinuosity of a watercourse is the ratio of the length of the channel to the straight line down-valley distance. Streams or rivers with a single channel and sinuosities of 1.5 or more are defined as **meandering** streams or rivers.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Measurement",
