		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Science concerned with physical bodies subjected to forces or displacements",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Mechanics** is the area of [[Physics|physics]] concerned with the relationships between [[Force|force]], [[Matter|matter]], and [[Motion|motion]] among [[Physical object|physical objects]]. Forces applied to objects may result in displacements, which are changes of an object's position relative to its environment.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Theoretical expositions of this branch of [[Physics|physics]] have their origins in [[Ancient Greece]], for instance, in the writings of [[Aristotle]] and [[Archimedes]] (see History of classical mechanics and Timeline of classical mechanics). During the [[Early modern period|early modern period]], scientists such as [[Galileo Galilei]], [[Johannes Kepler]], [[Christiaan Huygens]], and [[Isaac Newton]] laid the foundation for what is now known as [[Classical mechanics|classical mechanics]]. In the 20th century the concepts of classical mechanics were challenged by new discoveries, leading to fundamentally new approaches including [[Relativistic mechanics|relativistic mechanics]] and [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]].",
			},
		},
