	{
		title = "Mile",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Unit of length",
		tags = { "Dimensional space", "Length and distance" },
		aliases = { "International mile", "Statute mile" },
		summary = "The **mile**, sometimes the **international mile** or **statute mile** to distinguish it from other miles, is a British imperial unit and United States customary unit of [[Length|length]]; both are based on the older English unit of length equal to 5,280 [[Foot (unit)|English feet]], or 1,760 yards. For everyday use, five miles equates roughly to eight kilometres. Mileage is the term used informally for a distance expressed in miles.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"With qualifiers, mile is also used to describe or translate a wide range of units derived from or roughly equivalent to the Roman mile (roughly 1.48 km), such as the nautical mile (now 1.852 km exactly), the Italian mile (roughly 1.852 km), and the Chinese mile (now 500 m exactly). The Romans divided their mile into 5,000, but the greater importance of furlongs in the [[Kingdom of England|Elizabethan-era England]] meant that the statute mile was made equivalent to 8 furlongs or 5,280 [[Foot (unit)|feet]] in 1593. This form of the mile then spread across the [[British Empire]], some successor states of which continue to employ the mile. The [[United States Geological Survey|US Geological Survey]] now employs the metre for official purposes, but legacy data from its 1927 geodetic datum has meant that a separate US survey mile (6336/3937 km) continues to see some use, although it was officially phased out in 2022.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Symbol", "mi. or mi" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mineral",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Crystalline chemical element or compound formed by geologic processes",
		tags = { "Geology", "Mineralogy" },
		aliases = { "Mineral species" },
		summary = "In [[Geology|geology]] and [[Mineralogy|mineralogy]], a **mineral** or **mineral species** is, broadly speaking, a [[Solid|solid]] substance with a fairly well-defined chemical composition and a specific [[Crystal structure|crystal structure]] that occurs naturally in pure form.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The [[Geology|geological]] definition of mineral normally excludes compounds that occur only in living organisms. However, some minerals are often biogenic (such as [[Calcite|calcite]]) or chemically [[Organic compound|organic compounds]] (such as mellite). Moreover, living organisms often synthesize inorganic minerals (such as hydroxylapatite) that also occur in rocks. The concept of mineral is distinct from [[Rock (geology)|rock]], which is any bulk solid geologic material that is relatively homogeneous at a large enough scale. A rock may consist of one type of mineral or may be an aggregate of two or more different types of minerals, spacially segregated into distinct [[Phase (matter)|phases]]. Some natural solid substances without a definite crystalline structure, such as opal or obsidian, are more properly called mineraloids. If a chemical compound occurs naturally with different crystal structures, each structure is considered a different mineral species.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mineral (nutrient)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical elements essential for life",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'In the context of [[Nutrition|nutrition]], a **mineral** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]. Some "minerals" are essential for life, but most are not. Minerals are one of the four groups of essential nutrients; the others are vitamins, essential fatty acids, and essential amino acids. The five major minerals in the human body are [[Calcium|calcium]], [[Phosphorus|phosphorus]], [[Potassium|potassium]], [[Sodium|sodium]], and [[Magnesium|magnesium]]. The remaining minerals are called "trace elements". The generally accepted trace elements are [[Iron|iron]], [[Chlorine|chlorine]], [[Cobalt|cobalt]], [[Copper|copper]], [[Zinc|zinc]], [[Manganese|manganese]], [[Molybdenum|molybdenum]], [[Iodine|iodine]], [[Selenium|selenium]], and [[Bromine|bromine]]; there is some evidence that there may be more.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The four organogenic elements, namely [[Carbon|carbon]], [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]], [[Oxygen|oxygen]], and [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]] (CHON), that comprise roughly 96% of the [[Human body|human body]] by weight, are usually not considered as minerals (nutrient). In fact, in nutrition, the term "mineral" refers more generally to all the other functional and structural elements found in living organisms. Plants obtain minerals from [[Soil|soil]]. Animals ingest plants, thus moving minerals up the food chain. Larger organisms may also consume soil (geophagia) or use mineral resources such as salt licks to obtain minerals. Finally, although mineral and elements are in many ways synonymous, minerals are only bioavailable to the extent that they can be absorbed. To be absorbed, minerals either must be soluble or readily extractable by the consuming organism. For example, molybdenum is an essential mineral, but metallic molybdenum has no nutritional benefit.',
			},
		},
	},
