		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Property of a mass in motion",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Classical mechanics" },
		summary = "It is a [[Vector quantity|vector quantity]], possessing a magnitude and a direction. If m is an object's mass and **v** is its velocity (also a vector quantity), then the object's momentum **p** is: In the [[International System of Units]] (SI), the [[Unit of measurement|unit of measurement]] of momentum is the [[Kilogram|kilogram]] [[Metre per second|metre per second]] (kg⋅m/s), which is [[Dimensional analysis|dimensionally equivalent]] to the newton-second.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Newton's laws of motion|Newton's second law of motion]] states that the rate of change of a body's momentum is equal to the net force acting on it. Momentum depends on the [[Frame of reference|frame of reference]], but in any [[Inertial frame of reference|inertial frame of reference]], it is a conserved quantity, meaning that if a closed system is not affected by external forces, its total momentum does not change. Momentum is also conserved in [[Theory of relativity|special relativity]] (with a modified formula) and, in a modified form, in [[Electromagnetism|electrodynamics]], [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]], [[Quantum field theory|quantum field theory]], and [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]]. It is an expression of one of the fundamental symmetries of space and time: translational symmetry. Advanced formulations of classical mechanics, [[Lagrangian mechanics|Lagrangian]] and [[Hamiltonian mechanics]], allow one to choose coordinate systems that incorporate symmetries and constraints.",
			},
