		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Glacially formed accumulation of debris",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Glacial landforms" },
		summary = "A **moraine** is any accumulation of unconsolidated debris (regolith and [[Rock (geology)|rock]]), sometimes referred to as glacial till, that occurs in both currently and formerly [[Glacier|glaciated]] regions and that has been previously carried along by a glacier or [[Ice sheet|ice sheet]]. It may consist of partly rounded particles ranging in size from boulders (in which case it is often referred to as boulder [[Clay|clay]]) down to [[Gravel|gravel]] and [[Sand|sand]], in a groundmass of finely divided clayey material sometimes called glacial flour. Lateral moraines are those formed at the side of the ice flow, and terminal moraines are those formed at the foot, marking the maximum advance of the glacier.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Other types include ground moraines (till-covered areas forming sheets on flat or irregular topography) and medial moraines (formed where two glaciers meet).",
