	{
		title = "Motion",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏃",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Change in the position of an object",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Classical mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Motion (physics)" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], **motion** is the change in [[Position (geometry)|position]] of an object or fluid with respect to a reference frame over a given [[Time|time]]. Motion is mathematically described in terms of [[Vector quantity|vector quantities]] such as displacement (with [[Direction (geometry)|direction]] and [[Distance|distance]]), [[Velocity|velocity]] (direction and [[Speed|speed]]), [[Acceleration|acceleration]], etc. The **relative motion** of an object with respect to an observer is the object's motion described in the observer's comoving frame, quantified in terms of relative position, relative velocity, etc. The branch of physics describing the motion of objects without reference to their cause is called [[Kinematics|kinematics]], while the branch studying [[Force|forces]] and their effect on motion is called [[Dynamics (mechanics)|dynamics]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"If an object is not in motion relative to a given frame of reference, it is said to be at rest, motionless, immobile, stationary, or to have a constant or time-invariant position with reference to its surroundings. Modern physics holds that, as there is no absolute frame of reference, [[Isaac Newton]]'s concept of absolute motion cannot be determined. Everything in the universe can be considered to be in motion. Motion applies to various physical systems: objects, bodies, [[Matter|matter]] [[Particle|particles]], matter fields, [[Radioactivity|radiation]], radiation fields, radiation particles, curvature, and [[Spacetime|space-time]]. The concept of motion also applies to images, shapes, and boundaries. In general, the term motion signifies a continuous change in the position or configuration of a physical system in space.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Moulting",
		category = "Science",
