	{
		title = "Mountain",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⛰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Large natural elevation of the Earth's surface",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Landforms" },
		summary = "A **mountain** is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed [[Bedrock|bedrock]]. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a [[Hill|hill]]. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2000 ft. It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller [[Plateau|plateau]] with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in [[Mountain range|mountain ranges]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Mountains are formed through tectonic forces, [[Erosion|erosion]], or [[Volcanism|volcanism]], which act on time scales of up to tens of millions of years. Once mountain building ceases, mountains are slowly leveled through the action of [[Weathering|weathering]], through slumping and other forms of mass wasting, as well as through erosion by [[River|rivers]] and [[Glacier|glaciers]]. High elevations on mountains produce [[Alpine climate|colder climates]] than at [[Sea level|sea level]] at similar latitude. These colder climates strongly affect the ecosystems of mountains: different elevations have different plants and animals. Because of the less hospitable terrain and climate, mountains tend to be used less for agriculture and more for resource extraction, such as [[Mining|mining]] and logging, along with recreation, such as mountain climbing and [[Skiing|skiing]]. The highest mountain on Earth is [[Mount Everest]] in the [[Himalayas]] of [[Asia]], whose summit is 8850 m above mean sea level.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mountain pass",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⛰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Route through a mountain range or over a ridge",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Landforms" },
		summary = "A **mountain pass** is a navigable route through a [[Mountain range|mountain range]] or over a ridge. Since mountain ranges can present formidable barriers to travel, passes have played a key role in trade, war, and both [[Human migration|human]] and [[Animal migration|animal migration]] throughout history. At lower elevations it may be called a **hill pass**. A mountain pass is typically formed between two volcanic peaks or created by erosion from water or wind.",
		sections = { { "Overview", "A narrow pass or gorge between mountains or hills is called **defile**." } },
	},
	{
		title = "Mountain range",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⛰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Geographic area containing several geologically related mountains",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Landforms" },
		aliases = { "Hill range" },
		summary = "A **mountain range** or **hill range** is a series of [[Mountain|mountains]] or [[Hill|hills]] arranged in a line and connected by high ground. A **mountain system** or **mountain belt** is a group of mountain ranges with similarity in form, structure, and alignment that have arisen from the same cause, usually an orogeny. Mountain ranges are formed by a variety of geological processes, but most of the significant ones on Earth are the result of [[Plate tectonics|plate tectonics]]. Mountain ranges are also found on many planetary mass objects in the [[Solar System]] and are likely a feature of most [[Terrestrial planet|terrestrial planets]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Mountain ranges are usually segmented by [[Highland|highlands]] or [[Mountain pass|mountain passes]] and [[Valley|valleys]]. Individual mountains within the same mountain range do not necessarily have the same geologic structure or [[Petrology|petrology]]. They may be a mix of different orogenic expressions and terranes, for example [[Thrust fault|thrust sheets]], uplifted blocks, [[Fold (geology)|fold]] mountains, and volcanic landforms resulting in a variety of rock types.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Muon",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Subatomic particle",
		tags = { "Subatomic particles", "Fermions" },
		summary = "A **muon** (; from the [[Greek alphabet|Greek]] letter mu used to represent it) is an [[Elementary particle|elementary particle]] similar to the [[Electron|electron]], with an [[Electric charge|electric charge]] of −1 [[Elementary charge|e]] and a spin of 1/2 ħ, but with a much greater mass. It is classified as a [[Lepton|lepton]]. As with other leptons, the muon is not thought to be composed of any constituent particles.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The muon is an unstable [[Subatomic particle|subatomic particle]] with a mean lifetime of 2.2 s. Muon decay is slower than many other unstable particles because the decay is mediated by the [[Weak interaction|weak interaction]] and because the mass difference between the muon and the set of its decay products is small, providing few kinetic degrees of freedom for decay. Muon decay always produces an electron (or positron) and two types of [[Neutrino|neutrinos]]. Like all elementary particles, the muon has a corresponding [[Antiparticle|antiparticle]] of opposite charge (+1 e) but equal [[Mass|mass]] and spin: the **antimuon** (also called a positive muon). There is also a third lepton, the [[Tau (particle)|tau]], approximately 17 times heavier than the muon. Due to their greater mass, muons accelerate more slowly than electrons in electromagnetic fields, and emit less [[Bremsstrahlung|bremsstrahlung]] (deceleration radiation).",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Discovered", "Carl D. Anderson, Seth Neddermeyer (1936)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Muscle contraction",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Activation of tension-generating sites in muscle",
		tags = { "Biological processes and physiology", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Muscle contraction** is the activation of tension-generating sites within muscle cells. In [[Physiology|physiology]], muscle contraction does not necessarily mean muscle shortening because muscle tension can be produced without changes in muscle length (isometric contraction), such as when holding something heavy in the same position. The termination of muscle contraction is followed by **muscle relaxation**, which is a return of the muscle fibers to their low tension-generating state.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"For the contractions to happen, the muscle cells must rely on the interaction of two types of filament: thin and thick filaments. The major constituent of thin filaments is a chain formed by helical coiling of two strands of actin, and thick filaments predominantly consist of chains of the motor-protein myosin. Together, these two filaments form myofibrils - the basic functional organelles in the skeletal muscle system. In [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]], skeletal muscle contractions are [[Nervous system|neurogenic]] as they require synaptic input from motor neurons. A single motor neuron is able to innervate multiple muscle fibers, thereby causing the fibers to contract at the same time. Once innervated, the protein filaments within each skeletal muscle fiber slide past each other to produce a contraction, which is explained by the sliding filament theory.",
