	{
		title = "Navier–Stokes equations",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🟰",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Equations of motion for viscous fluids",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Fluid mechanics" },
		summary = "The **Navier–Stokes equations** describe the motion of [[Viscosity|viscous]] fluids. This system of [[Partial differential equation|partial differential equations]] was named after Claude-Louis Navier and George Gabriel Stokes, who developed them over a few decades of progressive work, from 1822 (Navier) to 1842–1850 (Stokes). [[Siméon Denis Poisson]] independently achieved the same results.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Navier–Stokes equations mathematically express [[Momentum|momentum]] balance for Newtonian fluids and make use of the [[Conservation of mass|conservation of mass]]. They are sometimes accompanied by an equation of state relating [[Pressure|pressure]], [[Temperature|temperature]] and [[Density|density]]. They arise from applying Newton's second law to [[Fluid dynamics|fluid motion]], together with the assumption that the [[Stress (mechanics)|stress]] in the fluid is the sum of a diffusing viscous term (proportional to the gradient of velocity) and a pressure term—hence describing viscous flow. The Navier–Stokes equations generalize the Euler equations in that the latter model only considers inviscid flow. The Navier–Stokes equations are of great scientific and engineering interest because they may be used to model a wide variety of scenarios. Coupled with [[Maxwell's equations]], they comprise the fundamentals of magnetohydrodynamics.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Neodymium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Nd" },
		summary = "**Neodymium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Nd** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 60. It is the fourth member of the [[Lanthanide|lanthanide]] series and is considered to be one of the rare-earth metals. It is a hard, slightly [[Ductility|malleable]], silvery metal that quickly [[Tarnish|tarnishes]] in air and moisture. When oxidized, neodymium reacts quickly, producing pink, purple/blue, and yellow compounds in overwhelmingly the +3 [[Oxidation state|oxidation state]], although highly rare and reactive +2 and +4 compounds are known. It is generally regarded as having one of the most complex spectra of the elements. Neodymium was discovered in 1885 by the Austrian chemist Carl Auer von Welsbach, who also discovered [[Praseodymium|praseodymium]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Neodymium is present in significant quantities in the minerals [[Monazite|monazite]] and bastnäsite. Neodymium is not found naturally in metallic form or unmixed with other lanthanides, and it is usually refined for general use. Neodymium is fairly common—about as common as [[Cobalt|cobalt]], [[Nickel|nickel]], or [[Copper|copper]]—and is [[Abundance of elements in Earth's crust|widely distributed]] in the Earth's [[Crust (geology)|crust]]. Most of the world's commercial neodymium is [[Mining|mined]] in China, as is the case with many other rare-earth metals. Neodymium [[Chemical compound|compounds]] were first commercially used as [[Glass|glass]] dyes in 1927 and remain a popular additive. The color of neodymium compounds comes from the Nd(3+) [[Ion|ion]] and is often a reddish-purple. This color changes with the type of lighting because of the interaction of the sharp light absorption bands of neodymium with ambient light enriched with the sharp visible emission bands of [[Mercury (element)|mercury]], trivalent [[Europium|europium]] or [[Terbium|terbium]].",
			},
