		tags = { "Water", "Oceanography" },
		aliases = { "Oceanology", "Sea science", "Ocean science" },
		summary = "**Oceanography**, also known as **oceanology**, **sea science**, **ocean science**, and **marine science**, is the scientific study of the [[Ocean|ocean]], including its physics, chemistry, biology, and geology.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is an [[Earth science]], which covers a wide range of topics, including [[Ocean current|ocean currents]], [[Wind wave|waves]], and geophysical fluid dynamics; [[Flux|fluxes]] of various chemical substances and physical properties within the ocean and across its boundaries; [[Ecosystem|ecosystem]] dynamics; and [[Plate tectonics|plate tectonics]] and [[Seabed|seabed]] geology. Oceanographers draw upon a wide range of disciplines to deepen their understanding of the world’s oceans, incorporating insights from [[Astronomy|astronomy]], [[Biology|biology]], [[Chemistry|chemistry]], [[Geography|geography]], [[Geology|geology]], [[Hydrology|hydrology]], [[Meteorology|meteorology]] and [[Physics|physics]].",
