		tags = { "Units of measurement", "Electromagnetism" },
		summary = "The **ohm** is the unit of [[Electrical resistance and conductance|electrical resistance]] in the [[International System of Units|International System of Units (SI)]]. It is named after German physicist [[Georg Ohm]] (1789–1854). Various empirically derived standard units for electrical resistance were developed in connection with early [[Telegraphy|telegraphy]] practice, and the British Association for the Advancement of Science proposed a unit derived from existing units of mass, length and time, and of a convenient scale for practical work as early as 1861.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Following the 2019 revision of the SI, in which the [[Ampere|ampere]] and the [[Kilogram|kilogram]] were redefined in terms of fundamental constants, the ohm is now also defined as an exact value in terms of these constants.",
