	{
		title = "Ohm's law",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Law of electrical current and voltage",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrical circuits" },
		summary = "**Ohm's law** states that in a well-behaved [[Electrical conductor|conductor]] (a so-called ohmic conductor), the [[Electric current|electric current]] between two points is directly [[Proportionality (mathematics)|proportional]] to the [[Voltage|voltage]] (the difference of [[Electric potential|electric potential]]) across the two points. Introducing the constant of proportionality, the [[Electrical resistance and conductance|resistance]], one arrives at the following mathematical equation used to describe this relationship:",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"or, equivalently, at the same equation expressed in terms of the reciprocal constant of proportionality, the electrical conductance, where I is the current through the conductor, V is the voltage measured across the conductor, R is the [[Electrical resistance and conductance|resistance]] of the conductor, and G=1/R is the [[Electrical resistance and conductance|conductance]] of the conductor. More specifically, Ohm's law states that the R (or, equivalently, G) in this relation is constant, independent of the current. If the resistance is not constant, the previous equation cannot be called Ohm's law, but it can still be used as a definition of [[Electrical resistance and conductance|static/DC resistance]]. Ohm's law is an empirical relation which accurately describes the conductivity of the vast majority of [[Electrical conductor|electrically conductive materials]] over many orders of magnitude of current. However some materials do not obey Ohm's law; these are called non-ohmic.",
			},
		},
	},
