	{
		title = "Ontogeny",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Origination and development of an organism",
		tags = { "Biological processes and physiology", "Animal ontogeny" },
		aliases = { "Ontogenesis" },
		summary = "**Ontogeny** (also **ontogenesis**) is the origination and development of an [[Organism|organism]] (both physical and psychological, e.g., moral development), usually from the time of [[Fertilisation|fertilization]] of the egg to adult. The term can also be used to refer to the study of the entirety of an organism's lifespan.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ontogeny is the developmental history of an organism within its own lifetime, as distinct from [[Phylogenetic tree|phylogeny]], which refers to the [[Evolution|evolutionary]] history of a species. Another way to think of ontogeny is that it is the process of an organism going through all of the developmental stages over its lifetime. While developmental (i.e., ontogenetic) processes can influence subsequent evolutionary (e.g., phylogenetic) processes (see evolutionary developmental biology and recapitulation theory), individual organisms develop (ontogeny), while species evolve (phylogeny). Ontogeny, [[Embryology|embryology]] and [[Developmental biology|developmental biology]] are closely related studies and those terms are sometimes used interchangeably. Aspects of ontogeny are morphogenesis, the development of form and shape of an organism; tissue growth; and [[Cellular differentiation|cellular differentiation]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
