		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fungus-like eukaryotic microorganism",
		tags = { "Organisms", "Other eukaryotes" },
		aliases = { "Oomycetes", "Oomycota" },
		summary = "The **oomycetes**, or **Oomycota**, form a distinct [[Phylogenetic tree|phylogenetic]] lineage of [[Mushroom|fungus]]-like [[Eukaryote|eukaryotic]] [[Microorganism|microorganisms]] within the Stramenopiles. Asexual reproduction involves the formation of chlamydospores and sporangia, producing motile zoospores. One oomycete, the mycoparasite Pythium oligandrum, is used for biocontrol, attacking plant pathogenic fungi. The oomycetes are also often referred to as **water molds** (or **water moulds**), although the water-preferring nature which led to that name is not true of most species, which are terrestrial pathogens.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Oomycetes were originally grouped with [[Mushroom|fungi]] due to similarities in morphology and lifestyle. However, molecular and phylogenetic studies revealed significant differences between fungi and oomycetes which means the latter are now grouped with the stramenopiles (which include some types of [[Algae|algae]]). The Oomycota have a very sparse fossil record; a possible oomycete has been described in [[Amber|amber]] from the [[Cretaceous]].",
