	{
		title = "Ore",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🪨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Rock or sediment with valuable metals, minerals and elements",
		tags = { "Geology", "Petrology" },
		summary = "**Ore** is natural [[Rock (geology)|rock]] or [[Sediment|sediment]] that contains one or more valuable [[Mineral|minerals]], typically including [[Metal|metals]], concentrated above background levels, and that is economically viable to mine and process. Ore grade refers to the concentration of the desired material it contains. The value of the metals or minerals a rock contains must be weighed against the cost of extraction to determine whether it is of sufficiently high grade to be worth mining and is therefore considered an ore. A complex ore is one containing more than one valuable mineral.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Minerals of interest are generally oxides, sulfides, silicates, or native metals such as copper or [[Gold|gold]]. Ore bodies are formed by a variety of [[Geology|geological]] processes generally referred to as ore genesis and can be classified based on their deposit type. Ore is extracted from the earth through [[Mining|mining]] and treated or refined, often via [[Smelting|smelting]], to extract the valuable metals or minerals. Some ores, depending on their composition, may pose threats to health or surrounding ecosystems. The word ore is of [[Anglo-Saxons|Anglo-Saxon]] origin, meaning "lump of metal".',
