	{
		title = "Organ (biology)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Collection of tissues with similar functions",
		tags = { "Anatomy and morphology", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Organ", "Organ (anatomy)" },
		summary = "In a multicellular [[Organism|organism]], an **organ** is a collection of [[Tissue (biology)|tissues]] joined in a structural unit to serve a common function. In the hierarchy of life, an organ lies between [[Tissue (biology)|tissue]] and an organ system. Tissues are formed from same type [[Cell (biology)|cells]] to act together in a function. Tissues of different types combine to form an organ which has a specific function. The intestinal wall, for example, is formed by epithelial tissue and smooth muscle tissue.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"An organ's tissues are broadly classified into parenchyma, the functional tissue, and stroma, the structural tissue with supportive, connective, or ancillary functions. The primary tissues that form an organ generally have common [[Embryology|embryologic]] origins, often arising from the same germ layer. Organs are present in most multicellular [[Organism|organisms]]. In [[Unicellular organism|single-celled organisms]] such as eukaryotes, the functional analogue of an organ is an [[Organelle|organelle]]. In plants, there are three main organs. The number of organs in any organism depends on the definition used. There are approximately 79 organs in the human body; the exact number remains debated.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Organelle",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🦠",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Specialized subunit within a cell",
		tags = { "Cell biology", "Organelles and other cell parts" },
		aliases = { "Membrane-bound organelle" },
		summary = "An **organelle** is a specialized subunit, within a [[Cell (biology)|biological cell]], that has a specific function. The name organelle comes from the idea that these structures are parts of cells, as [[Organ (biology)|organs]] are to the [[Human body|body]], hence organelle, the suffix -elle being a diminutive. Organelles are either separately enclosed within their own lipid bilayers (also called membrane-bound organelles) or are spatially distinct functional units without a surrounding lipid bilayer (non-membrane bounded organelles).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although most organelles are functional units within cells, some functional units that extend outside of cells are often termed organelles, such as cilia, the [[Flagellum|flagellum]] and archaellum, and the trichocyst (these could be referred to as membrane bound in the sense that they are attached to (or bound to) the membrane). Organelles are identified by [[Microscopy|microscopy]], and can also be purified by cell fractionation. There are many types of organelles, particularly in eukaryotic cells. They include structures that make up the endomembrane system (such as the nuclear envelope, [[Endoplasmic reticulum|endoplasmic reticulum]], and [[Golgi apparatus]]), and other structures such as [[Mitochondrion|mitochondria]] and [[Plastid|plastids]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Organic chemistry",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Subdiscipline of chemistry, focusing on carbon compounds",
		tags = { "Chemistry" },
		summary = "**Organic chemistry** is a subdiscipline within [[Chemistry|chemistry]] involving the [[Science|scientific]] study of the structure, properties, and reactions of [[Organic compound|organic compounds]] and [[Organic matter|organic materials]] (i.e. [[Matter|matter]] in its various forms that contain [[Carbon|carbon]] [[Atom|atoms]]). It involves studying the structure of organic material to determine the structural formula, analyzing [[Physical property|physical]] and [[Chemical property|chemical properties]], and evaluating [[Reactivity (chemistry)|chemical reactivity]] to understand the behavior of organic compounds.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The range of chemicals studied in organic chemistry includes [[Hydrocarbon|hydrocarbons]] (compounds containing only [[Carbon|carbon]] and [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]]) as well as compounds based on carbon, but also containing other elements, especially [[Oxygen|oxygen]], [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]], [[Sulfur|sulfur]], [[Phosphorus|phosphorus]] (included in many biochemicals) and the [[Halogen|halogens]]. [[Organometallic chemistry]] is the study of compounds containing carbon–[[Metal|metal]] bonds. Organic compounds form the basis of all known life and constitute the majority of known chemicals. The bonding patterns of carbon, with its [[Valence (chemistry)|valence]] of four—formal single, double, and triple bonds, plus structures with delocalized electrons—make the array of organic compounds structurally diverse, and their range of applications enormous.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Organic compound",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Carbon-containing chemical compound",
		tags = { "Chemical substances", "Chemical compounds" },
		aliases = { "Organic compounds" },
		summary = '**Organic compounds** are a subclass of [[Chemical compound|chemical compounds]] of [[Carbon|carbon]]. Little consensus exists among [[Chemist|chemists]] on the exact definition of organic compound; the only universally accepted definition is the quasi-tautological "organic compounds are the [[Academic discipline|subject matter]] of [[Organic chemistry|organic chemistry]]".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Generally, any large [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]] containing a carbon–hydrogen or carbon–carbon bond is accepted as an organic compound. Thus [[Alkane|alkanes]] (e.g. [[Ethane|ethane]], CH3\\sCH3) and their derivatives are typically considered organic. For historical and disciplinary reasons, small molecules containing carbon are generally not accepted: [[Cyanide|cyanide]] ion (CN−), hydrogen cyanide (HCN), chloroformic acid (ClCO2H), carbon dioxide (CO2), and [[Carbonate|carbonate]] ion (CO3(2−)) may all be excluded. Because of carbon's ability to catenate (form chains with other carbon [[Atom|atoms]]), millions of organic compounds are known. Although organic compounds make up only a small percentage of [[Earth's crust]], they are of central importance because all known life is based on organic compounds.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Organism",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Individual living life form",
		tags = { "Basics" },
		summary = "An **organism** is any [[Life|living]] thing that functions as an [[Individual|individual]]. Such a definition raises more problems than it solves, not least because the concept of an individual is also difficult. Several criteria, few of which are widely accepted, have been proposed to define what constitutes an organism. Among the most common is that an organism has autonomous [[Reproduction|reproduction]], growth, and [[Metabolism|metabolism]]. This would exclude [[Virus|viruses]], even though they [[Evolution|evolve]] like organisms.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Other problematic cases include colonial organisms; a colony of eusocial insects is organised adaptively, and has germ-soma specialisation, with some insects reproducing, others not, like cells in an animal\'s body. The body of a siphonophore, a jelly-like marine animal, is composed of organism-like zooids, but the whole structure looks and functions much like an animal such as a [[Jellyfish|jellyfish]], the parts collaborating to provide the functions of the colonial organism. The evolutionary biologists David Queller and Joan Strassmann suggest that "organismality", the qualities or attributes that define an entity as an organism, has evolved socially as groups of simpler units (from cells upwards) came to cooperate without conflicts. They propose that cooperation should be used as the "defining trait" of an organism.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
