	{
		title = "Ozone",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Triatomic oxygen molecule",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Trioxygen", "O3" },
		summary = "**Ozone**, also called **trioxygen**, is an [[Inorganic compound|inorganic]] [[Molecule|molecule]] with the [[Chemical formula|chemical formula]] **O3**. It is a pale-blue gas with a distinctively [[Pungency|pungent]] odour. It is an allotrope of [[Oxygen|oxygen]] that is much less stable than the diatomic allotrope O2, breaking down in the lower atmosphere to O2 (dioxygen). Ozone is formed from dioxygen by the action of [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] (UV) light and electrical discharges within the [[Atmosphere of Earth|Earth's atmosphere]]. It is present in very low concentrations throughout the atmosphere, with its highest concentration high in the [[Ozone layer|ozone layer]] of the [[Stratosphere|stratosphere]], which absorbs most of the [[Sun]]'s [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet (UV)]] radiation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ozone's odour is reminiscent of [[Chlorine|chlorine]], and detectable by many people at concentrations of as little as 0.1 ppm in air. Ozone's O3 structure was determined in 1865. The molecule was later proven to have a bent structure and to be weakly diamagnetic. At [[Standard temperature and pressure|standard temperature and pressure]], ozone is a pale blue gas that condenses at cryogenic temperatures to a dark blue [[Liquid|liquid]] and finally a violet-black [[Solid|solid]]. Ozone's instability with regard to more common dioxygen is such that both concentrated gas and liquid ozone may decompose explosively at elevated temperatures, physical shock, or fast warming to the boiling point. It is therefore used commercially only in low concentrations. Ozone is a powerful oxidising agent (far more so than dioxygen) and has many industrial and consumer applications related to oxidation.",
			},
