	{
		title = "Paleogene",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "First period of the Cenozoic Era",
		tags = { "Geological history", "Geological periods" },
		summary = 'It is the first period of the [[Cenozoic]] Era, the tenth period of the [[Phanerozoic]] and is divided into the [[Paleocene]], [[Eocene]], and [[Oligocene]] epochs. The earlier term Tertiary Period was used to define the time now covered by the Paleogene Period and subsequent Neogene Period; despite no longer being recognized as a formal [[Stratigraphy|stratigraphic term]], "Tertiary" still sometimes remains in informal use.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Much of the world's modern vertebrate diversity originated in a rapid surge of diversification in the early Paleogene, as survivors of the [[Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event]] took advantage of empty ecological niches left behind by the extinction of the non-avian dinosaurs, pterosaurs, marine reptiles, and primitive fish groups. Mammals continued to diversify from relatively small, simple forms into a highly diverse group ranging from small-bodied forms to very large ones, radiating into multiple [[Order (taxonomy)|orders]] and colonizing the [[Bat|air]] and [[Marine mammal|marine ecosystems]] by the [[Eocene]]. [[Bird|Birds]], the only surviving group of dinosaurs, quickly diversified from the very few neognath and [[Palaeognathae|paleognath]] clades that survived the extinction event, also radiating into multiple orders, colonizing different ecosystems and achieving an extreme level of morphological diversity.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Colour", "Paleogene" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Paleontology",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🦴",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of past life through fossils",
		tags = { "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Palaeontology" },
		summary = "**Paleontology** or **palaeontology** is the scientific study of the life of the past, mainly but not exclusively through the study of [[Fossil|fossils]]. Paleontologists use fossils as a means to classify organisms, measure geologic time, and assess the interactions between [[Prehistory|prehistoric]] organisms and their [[Natural environment|natural environment]]. While paleontological observations are known from at least the 6th century BC, the foundation of paleontology as a science dates back to the work of [[Georges Cuvier]] in 1796.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The field developed rapidly over the course of the following decades, and the French word paléontologie was introduced for the study in 1822, which was derived from the [[Ancient Greek]] word for 'ancient' and words describing relatedness and a field of study. Further advances in the field accompanied the work of [[Charles Darwin]] who popularized the concept of [[Evolution|evolution]]. Together, evolution and extinction can be understood as complementary processes that shaped the [[History of life|history of life]]. Paleontology overlaps the most with the fields of [[Geology|geology]] and [[Biology|biology]]. It draws on technology and analysis of a wide range of sciences to apply them to the study of life and environments of the past, particularly for the subdisciplines of paleobiology and paleoecology that are analogous to biology and [[Ecology|ecology]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Paleozoic",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "First era of the Phanerozoic Eon",
		tags = { "Geological history", "Geological periods" },
		aliases = { "Era" },
		summary = "The **Paleozoic** **Era** is the first of three geological eras of the [[Phanerozoic]] Eon. Beginning 538.8 million years ago (Ma), it succeeds the Neoproterozoic (the last era of the Proterozoic Eon) and ends 251.9 Ma at the start of the [[Mesozoic]] Era. The Paleozoic is subdivided into six [[Geologic time scale|geologic periods]], [[Cambrian]], [[Ordovician]], [[Silurian]], [[Devonian]], [[Carboniferous]] and [[Permian]]. Some geological timescales divide the Paleozoic informally into early and late sub-eras: the Early Paleozoic consisting of the Cambrian, Ordovician and Silurian; the Late Paleozoic consisting of the Devonian, Carboniferous and Permian.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The name Paleozoic was first used by Adam Sedgwick (1785–1873) in 1838 to describe the Cambrian and Ordovician periods. It was redefined by John Phillips (1800–1874) in 1840 to cover the Cambrian to Permian periods. It is derived from the [[Ancient Greek|Greek]] palaiós and zōḗ meaning "ancient life". The Paleozoic was a time of dramatic geological, climatic, and evolutionary change. The Cambrian witnessed the most rapid and widespread diversification of life in Earth\'s history, known as the [[Cambrian explosion]], in which most modern [[Phylum|phyla]] first appeared. [[Arthropod|Arthropods]], [[Mollusca|molluscs]], [[Fish|fish]], [[Amphibian|amphibians]], [[Reptile|reptiles]], and [[Synapsida|synapsids]] all evolved during the Paleozoic. Life began in the ocean but eventually transitioned onto land, and by the late Paleozoic, great [[Forest|forests]] of primitive plants covered the continents, many of which formed the [[Coal|coal]] beds of [[Europe]] and eastern [[North America]].',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Colour", "Paleozoic" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Palladium",
