		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Pd" },
		summary = "**Palladium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has the [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Pd** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 46. It is a rare and lustrous silvery-white metal discovered in 1802 by the English chemist William Hyde Wollaston. He named it after the asteroid Pallas (formally 2 Pallas), which was itself named after the [[Epithet|epithet]] of the Greek goddess [[Athena]], acquired by her when she slew Pallas. Palladium, [[Platinum|platinum]], [[Rhodium|rhodium]], [[Ruthenium|ruthenium]], [[Iridium|iridium]] and [[Osmium|osmium]] form together a group of elements referred to as the platinum group metals. They have similar chemical properties, but palladium has the lowest melting point and is the least dense of them.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"More than half the supply of palladium and its congener platinum is used in catalytic converters, which convert as much as 90% of the harmful gases in automobile exhaust ([[Hydrocarbon|hydrocarbons]], [[Carbon monoxide|carbon monoxide]], and [[Nitrogen dioxide|nitrogen dioxide]]) into nontoxic substances ([[Nitrogen|nitrogen]], carbon dioxide and [[Water vapor|water vapor]]). Palladium is also used in electronics, dentistry, [[Medicine|medicine]], hydrogen purification, chemical applications, electrochemical sensors, electrosynthesis, groundwater treatment, and jewelry. [[Ore]] deposits of palladium and other platinum group metals are rare. The most extensive deposits have been found in the norite belt of the Bushveld Igneous Complex covering the Transvaal Basin in South Africa; the Stillwater Complex in [[Montana]], United States; the Sudbury Basin and Thunder Bay District of [[Ontario]], Canada; and the Norilsk Complex in Russia.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Americium" },
	},
	{
		title = "Pangaea",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
