	{
		title = "Physics beyond the Standard Model",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Theories trying to extend known physics",
		tags = { "Physics", "Particle physics" },
		aliases = { "BSM" },
		summary = "**Physics beyond the Standard Model** (**BSM**) is the theoretical developments needed to explain the deficiencies of the [[Standard Model]], such as the inability to explain the fundamental parameters of the Standard Model, the strong CP problem, neutrino oscillations, matter–antimatter asymmetry, and the nature of [[Dark matter|dark matter]] and [[Dark energy|dark energy]]. Another problem lies within the [[Quantum field theory|mathematical framework]] of the Standard Model itself: the Standard Model is inconsistent with that of [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]], and one or both theories break down under certain conditions, such as [[Gravitational singularity|spacetime singularities]] like the [[Big Bang]] and [[Black hole|black hole]] [[Event horizon|event horizons]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Theories that lie beyond the Standard Model include various extensions of the standard model through [[Supersymmetry|supersymmetry]], such as the Minimal Supersymmetric Standard Model (MSSM) and Next-to-Minimal Supersymmetric Standard Model (NMSSM), and entirely novel explanations, such as [[String theory|string theory]], M-theory, and extra dimensions. As these theories tend to reproduce the entirety of current phenomena, the question of which theory is the right one, or at least the "best step" towards a [[Theory of everything|Theory of Everything]], can only be settled via experiments, and is one of the most active areas of research in both [[Theoretical physics|theoretical]] and experimental physics.',
			},
