		tags = { "Biological processes and physiology", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Physiology** is the [[Science|scientific]] study of functions and mechanisms in a [[Life|living system]]. As a [[Branches of science|subdiscipline]] of [[Biology|biology]], physiology focuses on how [[Organism|organisms]], organ systems, individual [[Organ (biology)|organs]], [[Cell (biology)|cells]], and [[Biomolecule|biomolecules]] carry out [[Chemistry|chemical]] and [[Physics|physical]] functions in a living system. According to the classes of [[Organism|organisms]], the field can be divided into medical physiology, [[Zoology|animal physiology]], plant physiology, cell physiology, and comparative physiology.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Central to physiological functioning are [[Biophysics|biophysical]] and biochemical processes, [[Homeostasis|homeostatic]] control mechanisms, and [[Cell signaling|communication]] between cells. Physiological state is the condition of normal function. The [[Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine]] is awarded by the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences for exceptional scientific achievements in physiology related to the field of [[Medicine|medicine]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
