		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Pt" },
		summary = '**Platinum** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Pt** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 78. It is a [[Density|dense]], [[Ductility|malleable]], [[Ductility|ductile]], highly unreactive, [[Precious metal|precious]], silverish-white [[Transition metal|transition metal]]. Its name originates from [[Spanish language|Spanish]], a diminutive of plata "silver".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Platinum is a member of the platinum group of elements and group 10 of the [[Periodic table|periodic table of elements]]. It has six naturally occurring [[Isotope|isotopes]]. It is one of the [[Abundance of elements in Earth's crust|rarer elements in Earth's crust]], with an average abundance of approximately 5 g/kg. It occurs in some [[Nickel|nickel]] and [[Copper|copper]] ores along with some native deposits. Because of its scarcity in Earth's crust, barely a few hundred metric [[Tonne|tonnes]] are produced annually, and given its critical and important uses, it is highly valuable as well as a major precious metal commodity. Platinum does not [[Corrosion|corrode]], even at high temperatures, and is therefore considered a noble metal. Consequently, platinum is often found chemically uncombined as native platinum. Because it occurs naturally in the alluvial sands of various rivers, it was first used by [[Pre-Columbian era|pre-Columbian]] South American natives to produce artifacts.",
			},
		},
