		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Hydrocarbons" },
		aliases = { "Polythene", "PE", "Polyethene" },
		summary = "**Polyethylene** or **polythene** (abbreviated **PE**; [[International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry|IUPAC]] name **polyethene** or **poly(methylene)**) is the most commonly produced [[Plastic|plastic]]. It is a [[Polymer|polymer]], primarily used for packaging (plastic bags, plastic films, geomembranes and containers including bottles, [[Cup|cups]], jars, folders, etc.). As of 2017, over 100 million [[Tonne|tonnes]] of polyethylene [[Resin|resins]] are being produced annually, accounting for 34% of the total plastics market.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Many kinds of polyethylene are known, with most having the [[Chemical formula|chemical formula]] (C2H4)n. PE is usually a mixture of similar [[Polymer|polymers]] of [[Ethylene|ethylene]], with various values of n. It can be low-density or high-density and many variations thereof. Its properties can be modified further by crosslinking or copolymerization. All forms are nontoxic as well as chemically resilient, contributing to polyethylene's popularity as a multi-use plastic. However, polyethylene's chemical resilience also makes it a long-lived and decomposition-resistant pollutant when disposed of improperly. Being a [[Hydrocarbon|hydrocarbon]], polyethylene is colorless to opaque (without impurities or colorants) and combustible.",
			},
