		tags = { "Chemistry" },
		summary = "**Polymer chemistry** is a sub-discipline of [[Chemistry|chemistry]] that focuses on the structures, [[Chemical synthesis|chemical synthesis]], and [[Chemical property|chemical]] and physical properties of [[Polymer|polymers]] and [[Macromolecule|macromolecules]]. The principles and methods used within polymer chemistry are also applicable through a wide range of other chemistry sub-disciplines like [[Organic chemistry|organic chemistry]], [[Analytical chemistry|analytical chemistry]], and [[Physical chemistry|physical chemistry]]. Many materials have polymeric structures, from fully inorganic [[Metal|metals]] and [[Ceramic|ceramics]] to [[DNA]] and other [[Biomolecule|biological molecules]]. However, polymer chemistry is typically related to synthetic and [[Organic compound|organic compositions]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Chemical synthesis|Synthetic]] polymers are ubiquitous in commercial materials and products in everyday use, such as [[Plastic|plastics]], and rubbers, and are major components of [[Composite material|composite]] materials. Polymer chemistry can also be included in the broader fields of polymer science or even [[Nanotechnology|nanotechnology]], both of which can be described as encompassing polymer physics and polymer engineering.",
			},
