	{
		title = "Potassium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Potassium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **K** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]]19. It is a silvery white metal that is soft enough to easily cut with a knife. Potassium metal reacts rapidly with atmospheric [[Oxygen|oxygen]] to form flaky white potassium peroxide in only seconds of exposure. It was first isolated from potash, the ashes of plants, from which its name derives. In the [[Periodic table|periodic table]], potassium is one of the [[Alkali metal|alkali metals]], all of which have a single [[Valence electron|valence electron]] in the outer electron shell, which is easily removed to create [[Ion|an ion with a positive charge]] (which combines with [[Ion|anions]] to form [[Salt (chemistry)|salts]]). In nature, potassium occurs only in ionic salts.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Elemental potassium reacts vigorously with water, generating sufficient heat to ignite [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] emitted in the reaction, and burning with a lilac-colored flame. It is found dissolved in seawater (which is 0.04% potassium by weight), and occurs in many [[Mineral|minerals]] such as orthoclase, a common constituent of [[Granite|granites]] and other [[Igneous rock|igneous rocks]]. Potassium is chemically very similar to [[Sodium|sodium]], the previous element in [[Group (periodic table)|group]] 1 of the periodic table. They have a similar first ionization energy, which allows for each atom to give up its sole outer electron. Naturally occurring potassium is composed of three [[Isotope|isotopes]], of which 40K is radioactive. Traces of 40K are found in natural sources of potassium, and it is the most common radioisotope in the human body. Potassium ions are vital for the functioning of all living cells.",
			},
