		summary = "**Praseodymium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Pr** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 59. It is the third member of the [[Lanthanide|lanthanide]] series and is considered one of the rare-earth metals. It is a soft, silvery, malleable and ductile [[Metal|metal]], valued for its magnetic, electrical, chemical, and optical properties. It is too reactive to be found in native form, and pure praseodymium metal slowly develops a green oxide coating when exposed to air.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Praseodymium always occurs naturally together with the other rare-earth metals. It is the sixth-most abundant rare-earth element and fourth-most abundant lanthanide, making up 9.1 [[Parts-per notation|parts per million]] of the Earth's crust, an abundance similar to that of [[Boron|boron]]. In 1841, Swedish chemist Carl Gustav Mosander extracted a rare-earth oxide residue he called didymium from a residue he called \"lanthana\", in turn separated from [[Cerium|cerium]] salts. In 1885, the Austrian chemist Carl Auer von Welsbach separated didymium into two elements that gave salts of different colours, which he named praseodymium and [[Neodymium|neodymium]]. The name praseodymium comes from the Ancient Greek (prasinos), meaning '[[Leek|leek]]-green', and (didymos) 'twin'.",
