	{
		title = "Precious metal",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Rare, naturally occurring metallic chemical element of high economic and cultural value",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Precious metals" },
		summary = "**Precious metals** are rare, naturally occurring [[Metal|metallic]] [[Mineral|minerals]] of high economic value. Precious metals, particularly the noble metals, are more [[Corrosion|corrosion]] resistant and less [[Reactivity (chemistry)|chemically reactive]] than most elements. They are usually [[Ductility|ductile]] and have a high [[Lustre (mineralogy)|lustre]]. Historically, precious metals were important as [[Money|currency]] but they are now regarded mainly as investment and industrial [[Raw material|raw materials]]. [[Gold]], [[Silver|silver]], [[Platinum|platinum]], and [[Palladium|palladium]] each have an ISO 4217 currency code.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The best known precious metals are the precious coinage metals, which are gold and silver. Although both have industrial uses, they are better known for their uses in [[Art|art]], [[Jewellery|jewelry]], and coinage. Other precious metals include the platinum group metals: [[Ruthenium|ruthenium]], [[Rhodium|rhodium]], [[Palladium|palladium]], [[Osmium|osmium]], [[Iridium|iridium]], and [[Platinum|platinum]], of which platinum is the most widely traded. The demand for precious metals is driven not only by their practical use but also by their role as [[Investment|investments]] and a store of value. Historically, precious metals have commanded much higher prices than common industrial metals.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Precipitation",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Condensed water vapor that falls from clouds",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Precipitation" },
		aliases = { "Precipitation (meteorology)" },
		summary = 'In [[Meteorology|meteorology]], **precipitation** is any product of the [[Condensation|condensation]] of atmospheric [[Water vapor|water vapor]] that falls from clouds due to gravitational pull. The main forms of precipitation include drizzle, [[Rain|rain]], rain and snow mixed ("sleet" in Commonwealth usage), [[Snow|snow]], ice pellets, graupel and [[Hail|hail]]. Precipitation occurs when cloud particles become heavy enough that atmospheric motions are insufficient to keep them aloft, such that they "precipitate" or fall. Thus, [[Fog|fog]] and mist are not precipitation; their water vapor does not condense sufficiently to precipitate, so fog and mist do not fall.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"(Such a non-precipitating combination is a [[Colloid|colloid]].) Two processes, possibly acting together, can lead to air becoming saturated with water vapor: cooling the air or adding water vapor to the air. Precipitation forms as smaller droplets coalesce via collision with other rain drops or ice crystals within a cloud. Short, intense periods of rain in scattered locations are called showers. [[Moisture]] that is lifted or otherwise forced to rise over a layer of sub-freezing air at the surface may be condensed by the low temperature into clouds and rain. This process is typically active when freezing rain occurs. A stationary front is often present near the area of freezing rain and serves as the focus for forcing moist air to rise.",
			},
		},
	},
