	{
		title = "Protein",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Biomolecule consisting of chains of amino acid residues",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		aliases = { "Proteins" },
		summary = "**Proteins** are large [[Biomolecule|biomolecules]] and [[Macromolecule|macromolecules]] that comprise one or more long chains of [[Amino acid|amino acid]] residues. Proteins perform a vast array of functions within organisms, including catalysing metabolic reactions, [[DNA replication]], [[Cell signaling|responding to stimuli]], providing [[Cytoskeleton|structure to cells]] and organisms, and transporting molecules from one location to another. Proteins differ from one another primarily in their sequence of amino acids, which is dictated by the nucleotide sequence of their [[Gene|genes]], and which usually results in protein folding into a specific 3D structure that determines its activity.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A linear chain of amino acid residues is called a polypeptide. A protein contains at least one long polypeptide. Short polypeptides, containing less than 20–30 residues, are rarely considered to be proteins and are commonly called peptides. The individual amino acid residues are bonded together by peptide bonds and adjacent amino acid residues. The sequence of amino acid residues in a protein is defined by the [[DNA sequencing|sequence]] of a gene, which is encoded in the [[Genetic code|genetic code]]. In general, the genetic code specifies 20 standard amino acids; but in certain organisms the genetic code can include selenocysteine and—in certain [[Archaea|archaea]]—pyrrolysine. Shortly after or even during synthesis, the residues in a protein are often chemically modified by post-translational modification, which alters the physical and chemical properties, folding, stability, activity, and ultimately, the function of the proteins.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Protein (nutrient)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Nutrient for the human body",
		tags = { "Biochemistry and molecular biology" },
		summary = "**Proteins** are essential [[Nutrient|nutrients]] for the [[Human body|human body]]. They are one of the constituents of [[Tissue (biology)|body tissue]] and also serve as a [[Fuel|fuel source]]. As fuel, proteins have the same energy density as [[Carbohydrate|carbohydrates]]: 17 [[Joule|kJ]] (4 kcal) per gram. From a nutritional perspective, the defining characteristic of protein is its [[Amino acid|amino acid]] composition. Protein content in foods is commonly measured based on nitrogen content, as nitrogen is a fundamental component of amino acids.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Protein|Proteins]] are [[Polymer|polymer]] chains made of amino acids linked by peptide bonds. During human [[Digestion|digestion]], proteins are broken down in the stomach into smaller polypeptide chains via [[Hydrochloric acid|hydrochloric acid]] and protease actions. This is crucial for the absorption of the essential amino acids that cannot be biosynthesized by the body. There are nine essential amino acids that humans cannot biosynthesize and thus must obtain from their diet to prevent protein-energy malnutrition and resulting death. They are phenylalanine, valine, threonine, tryptophan, methionine, leucine, isoleucine, lysine, and histidine. There has been debate as to whether there are eight or nine essential amino acids. The consensus seems to lean toward nine since histidine is not synthesized in adults. There are five amino acids that the human body can synthesize: alanine, aspartic acid, asparagine, glutamic acid and serine.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Protist",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Eukaryotes other than animals, plants or fungi",
		tags = { "Organisms", "Other eukaryotes" },
		aliases = { "Protoctist" },
		summary = "A **protist** or **protoctist** is any [[Eukaryote|eukaryotic]] [[Organism|organism]] that is not an [[Animal|animal]], [[Embryophyte|land plant]], or [[Mushroom|fungus]]. Protists do not form a [[Clade|natural group]], or clade, but are a [[Paraphyly|paraphyletic]] group encompassing the entire eukaryote tree of life, from which land plants, animals, and fungi evolved. They are primarily single-celled, exhibiting a wide range of forms such as amoebae, ciliates, thick-walled microalgae and, more commonly, flagellates. Several transitions to multicellularity have occurred among protists, from [[Colony (biology)|colonies]] with alternating cell types to giant slime molds, fungus-like organisms, and seaweeds with differentiated [[Tissue (biology)|tissues]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Protists were historically regarded as a separate [[Taxonomic rank|taxonomic]] [[Kingdom (taxonomy)|kingdom]] known as **Protista** or **Protoctista**, or were lumped together as part of the traditional plant and animal kingdoms as [[Algae|algae]] and [[Protozoa|protozoa]], respectively. With the advent of molecular phylogenetics and electron microscopy studies, some protists were shown to be more closely related to animals or plants than to other protists, and algae were found to be intermixed with protozoa. The classification suffered major revisions, as seemingly unrelated forms were found to be evolutionarily related, and vice versa. In modern classifications, protists are spread across several large clades known as supergroups, many of them containing disparate forms. For example, the [[Archaeplastida]] includes mostly phototrophs like [[Red algae|red]] and [[Green algae|green algae]], from which land plants evolved. Opisthokonta groups fungi, animals, and their single-celled relatives.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Proton",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Subatomic particle with positive charge",
		tags = { "Subatomic particles", "Fermions" },
		summary = "Its mass is slightly less than the mass of a [[Neutron|neutron]] and approximately 1,836 times the mass of an [[Electron|electron]] (the proton-to-electron mass ratio). Protons and neutrons, each with a mass of approximately one [[Dalton (unit)|dalton]], are jointly referred to as [[Nucleon|nucleons]] (particles present in atomic nuclei).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'One or more protons are present in the [[Atomic nucleus|nucleus]] of every [[Atom|atom]]. They provide the attractive electrostatic central force which binds the atomic electrons. The number of protons in the nucleus is the defining property of an element, and is referred to as the [[Atomic number|atomic number]] (represented by the symbol Z). Since each [[Chemical element|element]] is identified by the number of protons in its nucleus, each element has its own atomic number, which determines the number of atomic electrons and consequently the identity and chemical characteristics of the element. The word proton is [[Greek language|Greek]] for "first", and the name was given to the hydrogen nucleus by [[Ernest Rutherford]] in 1920. In previous years, Rutherford had discovered that the [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] nucleus (known to be the lightest nucleus) could be extracted from the nuclei of [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]] by atomic collisions.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Classification", "[[Baryon]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Protozoa",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Single-celled organisms",
		tags = { "Organisms", "Other eukaryotes" },
		summary = 'Historically, protozoans were regarded as "one-celled animals".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When first introduced by Georg Goldfuss, in 1818, the taxon Protozoa was erected as a [[Class (taxonomy)|class]] within the Animalia, with the word 'protozoa' meaning \"first animals\", because they often possess [[Animal|animal]]-like behaviours, such as motility and [[Predation|predation]], and lack a [[Cell wall|cell wall]], as found in plants and many [[Algae|algae]]. This classification remained widespread in the 19th and early 20th century, and even became elevated to a variety of higher ranks, including phylum, subkingdom, [[Kingdom (taxonomy)|kingdom]], and then sometimes included within the paraphyletic **[[Protist|Protoctista]]** or **Protista**. By the 1970s, it became usual to require that all taxa be [[Monophyly|monophyletic]] (all members being derived from one common ancestor that is itself regarded as belonging in the taxon), and [[Monophyly|holophyletic]] (containing all of the known descendants of that common ancestor).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pupa",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐞",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Insect life stage",
		tags = { "Biological processes and physiology", "Animal ontogeny" },
		summary = "Insects that go through a pupal stage are holometabolous: they go through four distinct stages in their life cycle, the stages thereof being [[Egg|egg]], [[Larva|larva]], pupa and [[Imago|imago]] (adult). The processes of entering and completing the pupal stage are controlled by the insect's hormones, especially juvenile hormone, prothoracicotropic hormone, and ecdysone. The act of becoming a pupa is called **pupation**, and the act of emerging from the pupal case is called **eclosion** or emergence.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The pupae of different groups of insects have different names such as chrysalis for the pupae of [[Butterfly|butterflies]] and tumbler for those of the mosquito family. Unlike the fully motile larval and imago stages, the pupal stage of an insect is typically sessile, where the pupa remains anchored to a location until the metamorphosis is completed, and may be enclosed in protective structures such as cocoons, nests or [[Exoskeleton|shells]].",
			},
		},
