	{
		title = "Purple",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Range of colors with the hues between blue and magenta",
		tags = { "Color", "Color terms" },
		summary = "**Purple** is a [[Color|color]] similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining [[Red|red]] and [[Blue|blue]] pigments. In the [[CMYK color model]] used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with [[Cyan|cyan]] pigment, [[Black|black]] pigment, or both. In the [[RGB color model]] used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to [[Color theory|color theory]], purple is considered a cool color.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Purple has long been associated with royalty, originally because Tyrian purple dye—made from the secretions of sea snails—was extremely expensive in antiquity. Purple was the color worn by Roman magistrates; it became the imperial color worn by the rulers of the [[Byzantine Empire]] and the [[Holy Roman Empire]], and later by Roman Catholic bishops. Similarly in [[Japan]], the color is traditionally associated with the [[Emperor of Japan|emperor]] and aristocracy. According to contemporary surveys in Europe and the United States, purple is the color most often associated with rarity, royalty, luxury, ambition, magic, mystery, piety and spirituality.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Source", "[[Web colors|HTML color names]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Pyroxene",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Group of inosilicate minerals with single chains of silica tetrahedra",
		tags = { "Geology", "Mineralogy" },
		aliases = { "Pyroxenes", "Px" },
		summary = "The **pyroxenes** (commonly abbreviated **Px**) are a group of important rock-forming inosilicate minerals found in many [[Igneous rock|igneous]] and [[Metamorphic rock|metamorphic]] rocks. Pyroxenes have the general formula XY(Si,Al)2O6, where X represents ions of [[Calcium|calcium]] (Ca), [[Sodium|sodium]] (Na), [[Iron|iron]] (Fe(II)) or [[Magnesium|magnesium]] (Mg) and more rarely [[Zinc|zinc]], [[Manganese|manganese]] or [[Lithium|lithium]], and Y represents ions of smaller size, such as [[Chromium|chromium]] (Cr), [[Aluminium|aluminium]] (Al), [[Magnesium|magnesium]] (Mg), [[Cobalt|cobalt]] (Co), [[Manganese|manganese]] (Mn), [[Scandium|scandium]] (Sc), [[Titanium|titanium]] (Ti), [[Vanadium|vanadium]] (V) or even iron (Fe(II) or Fe(III)). Although aluminium substitutes extensively for silicon in silicates such as [[Feldspar|feldspars]] and amphiboles, the substitution occurs only to a limited extent in most pyroxenes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"They share a common structure consisting of single chains of silica tetrahedra. Pyroxenes that crystallize in the [[Monoclinic crystal system|monoclinic]] system are known as **clinopyroxenes** and those that crystallize in the orthorhombic system are known as **orthopyroxenes**. The name pyroxene is derived from the [[Ancient Greek]] words for 'fire' and 'stranger'. Pyroxenes were so named due to their presence in volcanic [[Lava|lavas]], where they are sometimes found as crystals embedded in volcanic glass; it was assumed they were impurities in the glass, hence the name meaning \"fire stranger\". However, they are simply early-forming minerals that crystallized before the lava erupted. The upper mantle of Earth is composed mainly of olivine and pyroxene minerals. Pyroxene and [[Feldspar|feldspar]] are the major minerals in [[Basalt|basalt]], andesite, and gabbro rocks.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Asbestos" },
	},
	{
		title = "Qualitative research",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Form of research",
		tags = { "Science basics", "Research methods" },
		summary = "**Qualitative research** is a type of research that aims to gather and analyse non-numerical (descriptive) data in order to gain an understanding of individuals' [[Social reality|social reality]], including understanding their attitudes, beliefs, and motivation. This type of research typically involves in-depth interviews, focus groups, or [[Field research|field observations]] in order to collect data that is rich in detail and context. Qualitative research is often used to explore complex phenomena or to gain insight into people's experiences and perspectives on a particular topic.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is particularly useful when researchers want to understand the meaning that people attach to their experiences or when they want to uncover the underlying reasons for people's behavior. Qualitative methods include [[Ethnography|ethnography]], grounded theory, discourse analysis, and interpretative phenomenological analysis. Qualitative research methods have been used in [[Sociology|sociology]], [[Anthropology|anthropology]], [[Political science|political science]], [[Psychology|psychology]], [[Communication studies|communication studies]], social work, folklore, educational research, [[Information science|information science]] and [[Software engineering|software engineering]] research.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Quantitative research",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "All procedures for the numerical representation of empirical facts",
		tags = { "Science basics", "Research methods" },
		summary = "**Quantitative research** is a research strategy that focuses on [[Quantity|quantifying]] the collection and analysis of data. It is formed from a [[Deductive reasoning|deductive]] approach where emphasis is placed on the testing of theory, shaped by [[Empiricism|empiricist]] and [[Positivism|positivist]] philosophies.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Associated with the [[Natural science|natural]], [[Applied science|applied]], formal, and [[Social science|social sciences]] this research strategy promotes the objective empirical investigation of observable [[Phenomenon|phenomena]] to test and understand relationships. This is done through a range of quantifying methods and techniques, reflecting on its broad utilization as a research strategy across differing [[Academic discipline|academic disciplines]]. The objective of quantitative research is to develop and employ [[Mathematical model|mathematical models]], [[Scientific theory|theories]], and [[Hypothesis|hypotheses]] pertaining to phenomena. The process of [[Measurement|measurement]] is central to quantitative research because it provides the fundamental connection between empirical [[Observation|observation]] and mathematical expression of quantitative relationships. Quantitative data is any data that is in numerical form such as statistics, percentages, etc.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Quantum chemistry",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemistry based on quantum physics",
		tags = { "Chemistry" },
		aliases = { "Molecular quantum mechanics" },
		summary = "**Quantum chemistry**, or **molecular quantum mechanics**, is a branch of [[Physical chemistry|physical chemistry]] which applies [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]] to chemical systems to predict [[Physical property|physical]] and [[Chemical property|chemical properties]] of [[Molecule|molecules]] and [[Material|materials]]. It often involves calculating electronic [[Wave function|wave functions]] at the atomic level. These calculations make approximations to make simulations computationally feasible while capturing the relevant contributions to the computed wave functions and observable properties including the structures, spectra, and [[Thermodynamics|thermodynamics]] of a system. Quantum chemistry also covers the computation of quantum effects on molecular dynamics and [[Chemical kinetics|chemical kinetics]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Quantum chemistry studies focus on the electronic ground state and excited states of atoms, molecules, and ions. Such calculations allow chemical reactions to be described with respect to pathways, intermediates, and transition states. [[Spectroscopy|Spectroscopic]] properties may also be predicted. Typically, such studies assume the electronic wave function is adiabatically parameterized by the nuclear positions. A wide variety of approaches are used, including semi-empirical methods, density functional theory, Hartree–Fock calculations, quantum Monte Carlo methods, and coupled cluster methods. Understanding electronic structure and molecular dynamics through the development of computational solutions to the [[Schrödinger equation]] is a central goal of quantum chemistry.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Quantum chromodynamics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Theory of the strong nuclear interactions",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Quantum mechanics" },
		aliases = { "QCD" },
		summary = "In [[Theoretical physics|theoretical physics]], **quantum chromodynamics** (**QCD**) is the study of the [[Strong interaction|strong interaction]] between [[Quark|quarks]] mediated by [[Gluon|gluons]]. Quarks are fundamental particles that make up composite [[Hadron|hadrons]] such as the [[Proton|proton]], [[Neutron|neutron]] and [[Pion|pion]]. QCD is a type of [[Quantum field theory|quantum field theory]] called a non-abelian gauge theory, with symmetry group SU(3). The QCD analog of electric charge is a property called color. Gluons are the [[Force carrier|force carriers]] of the theory, just as photons are for the electromagnetic force in [[Quantum electrodynamics|quantum electrodynamics]]. The theory is an important part of the [[Standard Model]] of [[Particle physics|particle physics]]. A large body of experimental evidence for QCD has been gathered over the years.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"QCD exhibits three salient properties: Color confinement. Due to the force between two color charges remaining constant as they are separated, the energy grows until a quark–antiquark pair is [[Mass–energy equivalence|spontaneously produced]], turning the initial hadron into a pair of hadrons instead of isolating a color charge. Although analytically unproven, color confinement is well established from lattice QCD calculations and decades of experiments. Asymptotic freedom, a steady reduction in the strength of interactions between quarks and gluons as the energy scale of those interactions increases (and the corresponding length scale decreases). The asymptotic freedom of QCD was discovered in 1973 by David Gross and Frank Wilczek, and independently by David Politzer in the same year. For this work, all three shared the 2004 [[Nobel Prize in Physics]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Quantum tunnelling" },
	},
	{
		title = "Quantum electrodynamics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Quantum field theory of electromagnetism",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Quantum mechanics" },
		aliases = { "QED" },
		summary = "In [[Particle physics|particle physics]], **quantum electrodynamics** (**QED**) is the [[Theory of relativity|relativistic]] [[Quantum field theory|quantum field theory]] of [[Electromagnetism|electrodynamics]]. In essence, it describes how [[Light|light]] and [[Matter|matter]] interact and is the first theory where full agreement between [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]] and [[Theory of relativity|special relativity]] is achieved. QED mathematically describes all [[Phenomenon|phenomena]] involving [[Electric charge|electrically charged]] particles interacting by means of exchange of [[Photon|photons]] and represents the [[Quantum mechanics|quantum]] counterpart of [[Classical electromagnetism|classical electromagnetism]] giving a complete account of matter and light interaction.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In technical terms, QED can be described as a perturbation theory of the electromagnetic quantum vacuum. [[Richard Feynman]] called it "the jewel of physics" for its extremely accurate predictions of quantities like the anomalous magnetic moment of the electron and the Lamb shift of the [[Energy level|energy levels]] of [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]]. It is the most precise and stringently tested theory in physics. However, the prediction of vacuum zero energy made by QED has 10^120 magnitude of difference compared to experimental observation value. This is called the cosmological constant problem or vacuum catastrophe.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Quantum tunnelling" },
	},
	{
		title = "Quantum entanglement",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physics phenomenon",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Quantum mechanics" },
		summary = "**Quantum entanglement** is the phenomenon in which the quantum state of each [[Subatomic particle|particle]] in a group cannot be described independently of the state of the others, even when the particles are separated by a large distance. The topic of quantum entanglement is at the heart of the disparity between [[Classical physics|classical physics]] and [[Quantum mechanics|quantum physics]]: entanglement is a primary feature of quantum mechanics not present in [[Classical mechanics|classical mechanics]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Measurement|Measurements]] of physical properties such as position, [[Momentum|momentum]], [[Spin (physics)|spin]], and [[Polarization (waves)|polarization]] performed on entangled particles can, in some cases, be found to be perfectly correlated. For example, if a pair of entangled particles is generated such that their total spin is known to be zero, and one particle is found to have clockwise spin on a first axis, then the spin of the other particle, measured on the same axis, is found to be anticlockwise. This behavior gives rise to seemingly [[Paradox|paradoxical]] effects: any measurement of a particle's properties results in an apparent and irreversible wave function collapse of that particle and changes the original quantum state. With entangled particles, such measurements affect the entangled system as a whole.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Quantum field theory",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Theoretical framework in physics",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Quantum mechanics" },
		aliases = { "QFT" },
		summary = "In [[Theoretical physics|theoretical physics]], **quantum field theory** (**QFT**) is a theoretical framework that combines field theory, [[Theory of relativity|special relativity]] and [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]]. QFT is used in [[Particle physics|particle physics]] to construct physical models of [[Subatomic particle|subatomic particles]] and in [[Condensed matter physics|condensed matter physics]] to construct models of quasiparticles. The current Standard Model of particle physics is based on QFT.",
	},
	{
		title = "Quantum gravity",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Description of gravity using discrete values",
		tags = { "Physics", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "QG" },
		summary = "**Quantum gravity** (**QG**) is a field of [[Theoretical physics|theoretical physics]] that seeks unification of the theory of [[Gravity|gravity]] with the principles of [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]]. It deals with environments in which neither gravitational nor quantum effects can be ignored, such as in the vicinity of [[Black hole|black holes]] or similar compact astrophysical objects, as well as in the early stages of the universe moments after the [[Big Bang]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Three of the four [[Fundamental interaction|fundamental interactions]] of nature are described within the framework of quantum mechanics and [[Quantum field theory|quantum field theory]]: the [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetic interaction]], the [[Strong interaction|strong interaction]], and the [[Weak interaction|weak interaction]]; this leaves gravity as the only interaction that has not been fully accommodated. The current understanding of gravity is based on [[Albert Einstein]]'s general theory of relativity, which incorporates his theory of special relativity and deeply modifies the understanding of concepts like time and space.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Quantum tunnelling",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Quantum mechanical phenomenon",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Quantum mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Barrier penetration", "Tunnelling" },
		summary = "In physics, **quantum tunnelling**, **barrier penetration**, or simply **tunnelling** is a [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanical]] phenomenon in which an object such as an electron or atom passes through a potential energy barrier that, according to [[Classical mechanics|classical mechanics]], should not be passable due to the object not having sufficient energy to pass or surmount the barrier.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Tunnelling is a consequence of the [[Matter wave|wave nature of matter]] and quantum indeterminacy. The quantum [[Wave function|wave function]] describes the states of a particle or other [[Physical system|physical system]] and wave equations such as the [[Schrödinger equation]] describe their evolution. In a system with a short, narrow potential barrier, a small part of wavefunction can appear outside of the barrier representing a probability for tunnelling through the barrier. Since the probability of transmission of a wave packet through a barrier decreases exponentially with the barrier height, the barrier width, and the tunnelling particle's mass, tunnelling is seen most prominently in low-mass particles such as [[Electron|electrons]] tunnelling through atomically narrow barriers. However tunnelling has been observed with protons and even atoms and tunnelling has been used to explain physical effects with particles this large.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Quantum chromodynamics", "Quantum electrodynamics" },
	},
	{
		title = "Quark",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Elementary particle, fundamental constituent of matter",
		tags = { "Subatomic particles", "Fermions" },
		summary = "A **quark** is a type of [[Elementary particle|elementary particle]] and a fundamental constituent of [[Matter|matter]]. Quarks combine to form composite particles called [[Hadron|hadrons]], the most stable of which are [[Proton|protons]] and [[Neutron|neutrons]], the components of [[Atomic nucleus|atomic nuclei]]. All commonly observable matter is composed of up quarks, down quarks, and [[Electron|electrons]]. Owing to a phenomenon known as color confinement, quarks are never found in isolation; they can be found only within hadrons, which include [[Baryon|baryons]] (such as protons and neutrons) and [[Meson|mesons]], or in quark–gluon plasmas. For this reason, much of what is known about quarks has been drawn from observations of hadrons.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Quarks have various intrinsic [[Physical property|properties]], including [[Electric charge|electric charge]], [[Mass|mass]], color charge, and [[Spin (physics)|spin]]. They are the only elementary particles in the [[Standard Model]] of [[Particle physics|particle physics]] to experience all four [[Fundamental interaction|fundamental interactions]], also known as fundamental forces ([[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]], [[Gravity|gravitation]], [[Strong interaction|strong interaction]], and [[Weak interaction|weak interaction]]), as well as the only known particles whose electric charges are not [[Integer|integer]] multiples of the [[Elementary charge|elementary charge]]. There are six types, known as flavors, of quarks: [[Up quark|up]], [[Down quark|down]], charm, strange, top, and bottom. Up and down quarks have the lowest [[Mass|masses]] of all quarks. The heavier quarks rapidly change into up and down quarks through a process of particle decay: the transformation from a higher mass state to a lower mass state.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Discovered", "SLAC (c. 1968)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Quartz",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mineral made of silicon and oxygen",
		tags = { "Geology", "Mineralogy" },
		summary = "**Quartz** is a hard [[Mineral|mineral]] composed of silica ([[Silicon dioxide|silicon dioxide]]). Its [[Atom|atoms]] are linked in a continuous framework of SiO4 silicon–oxygen tetrahedra, with each oxygen atom being shared between two tetrahedra, giving an overall [[Chemical formula|chemical formula]] of SiO2. Therefore, quartz is classified structurally as a [[Silicate mineral|framework silicate mineral]] and compositionally as an oxide mineral. Quartz is the second most common mineral or mineral group in Earth's [[Lithosphere|lithosphere]], comprising about 12% by mass.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Quartz exists in two forms, the normal -quartz and the high-temperature -quartz, both of which are chiral. The transformation from -quartz to -quartz takes place abruptly at 573 °C. Since the transformation is accompanied by a significant change in volume, it can easily induce microfracturing of ceramics or rocks passing through this temperature threshold. There are many different varieties of quartz, several of which are classified as [[Gemstone|gemstones]]. Since antiquity, varieties of quartz have been the most commonly used minerals in the making of [[Jewellery|jewelry]] and hardstone carvings, especially in [[Europe]] and [[Asia]]. Quartz is the [[Mineral|mineral]] defining the value of 7 on the Mohs scale of hardness, a qualitative scratch method for determining the hardness of a material.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Category", "Tectosilicate minerals" },
			{ "Formula", "SiO2" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Quaternary",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Third and current period of the Cenozoic Era, from 2.58 million years ago to the present",
		tags = { "Geological history", "Geological periods" },
		summary = "The **Quaternary** is the current and most recent of the three [[Geologic time scale|periods]] of the Cenozoic Era in the [[Geologic time scale|geologic time scale]] of the [[International Commission on Stratigraphy]] (ICS), as well as the current and most recent of the twelve periods of the [[Phanerozoic]] eon. It follows the [[Neogene]] Period and spans from 2.6 million years ago to the present.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Quaternary Period is divided into two epochs: the [[Pleistocene]] (2.6 million years ago to 12 thousand years ago) and the [[Holocene]] (12 thousand years ago to today); a proposed third epoch, the Anthropocene, was rejected in 2024 by International Union of Geological Sciences (IUGS), the governing body of the [[International Commission on Stratigraphy]] (ICS). The Quaternary is typically defined by the [[Quaternary glaciation]], the cyclic growth and decay of continental [[Ice sheet|ice sheets]] related to the Milankovitch cycles and the associated climate and environmental changes that they caused.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Colour", "Quaternary" },
