	{
		title = "Radio wave",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "〰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of electromagnetic radiation",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrodynamics" },
		aliases = { "Hertzian waves", "Radio waves" },
		summary = "**Radio waves** (formerly called **Hertzian waves**) are a type of [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]] with the lowest frequencies and the longest [[Wavelength|wavelengths]] in the [[Electromagnetic spectrum|electromagnetic spectrum]], typically with frequencies below 300 gigahertz (GHz) and wavelengths greater than 1 mm, about the diameter of a grain of rice. Radio waves with frequencies above about 1 GHz and wavelengths shorter than 30 centimeters are called [[Microwave|microwaves]]. Like all electromagnetic waves, radio waves in a vacuum travel at the [[Speed of light|speed of light]], and in the Earth's atmosphere at a slightly lower speed. Radio waves are generated by [[Charged particle|charged particles]] undergoing [[Acceleration|acceleration]], such as time-varying [[Electric current|electric currents]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Naturally occurring radio waves are emitted by [[Lightning|lightning]] and astronomical objects, and are part of the blackbody radiation emitted by all warm objects. Radio waves are generated artificially by an electronic device called a transmitter, which is connected to an [[Antenna (radio)|antenna]], which radiates the waves. They are received by another antenna connected to a [[Radio receiver|radio receiver]], which processes the received signal. Radio waves are very commonly used in modern technology for fixed and mobile radio communication, [[Broadcasting|broadcasting]], [[Radar|radar]] and radio navigation systems, [[Communications satellite|communications satellites]], wireless computer networks, and many other applications.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Radiolaria",
		category = "Science",
