	{
		title = "Red",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Primary color",
		tags = { "Color", "Color terms" },
		summary = "**Red** is the [[Color|color]] at the long wavelength end of the [[Visible spectrum|visible spectrum]] of light, next to [[Orange (colour)|orange]] and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 [[Nanometre|nanometers]]. It is a primary color in the [[RGB color model]] and a secondary color (made from magenta and [[Yellow|yellow]]) in the [[CMYK color model]], and is the complementary color of [[Cyan|cyan]]. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red [[Pink|pink]] to the dark red burgundy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Red pigment made from ochre was one of the first colors used in [[Prehistoric art|prehistoric art]]. The [[Ancient Egypt|Ancient Egyptians]] and Mayans colored their faces red in ceremonies; [[Roman Empire|Roman]] generals had their bodies colored red to celebrate victories. It was also an important color in [[China]], where it was used to color early pottery and later the gates and walls of palaces. In the [[Renaissance]], the brilliant red costumes for the nobility and wealthy were dyed with kermes and cochineal. The 19th century brought the introduction of the first synthetic red dyes, which replaced the traditional dyes. Red became a symbolic color of [[Communism|communism]] and [[Socialism|socialism]]; Soviet Russia adopted a red flag following the Bolshevik Revolution in 1917. The Soviet red banner would subsequently be used throughout the entire history of the [[Soviet Union]]. China adopted its own red flag following the Chinese Communist Revolution.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Frequency", "~480–400" },
			{ "Source", "X11" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Red algae",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌿",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Division of plant life",
		tags = { "Organisms", "Other eukaryotes" },
		aliases = { "Rhodophyta" },
		summary = "**Red algae**, or **Rhodophyta**, make up one of the oldest and largest [[Phylum|phyla]] of [[Eukaryote|eukaryotic]] [[Algae|algae]], containing over 7,000 recognized species within over 900 [[Genus|genera]] amidst ongoing taxonomic revisions. The majority of species (6,793) are in the class Florideophyceae, and mostly consist of [[Multicellular organism|multicellular]] marine algae, including many notable [[Seaweed|seaweeds]]. Red algae are abundant in marine habitats. Approximately 5% of red algae species occur in freshwater environments, with greater concentrations in warmer areas. This may be due to an evolutionary bottleneck in which the last common ancestor lost about 25% of its core genes and much of its evolutionary plasticity.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Red algae form a distinct group characterized by eukaryotic cells without flagella and [[Centriole|centrioles]], [[Chloroplast|chloroplasts]] without external [[Endoplasmic reticulum|endoplasmic reticulum]] or unstacked (stroma) thylakoids, and use phycobiliproteins as accessory pigments, which give them their red color. Despite their name, red algae can vary in color from bright green, soft pink, resembling brown algae, to shades of red and purple, and may be almost black at greater depths and are only called red algae due to the phycobiliproteins and the reddish members. Unlike green algae, red algae store sugars as food reserves outside the chloroplasts as floridean starch, a type of starch that consists of highly branched amylopectin without amylose. The life history of red algae is typically an [[Alternation of generations|alternation of generations]] that may have three generations rather than two.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Scientific name", "Rhodophyta" },
		},
