	{
		title = "Rhodium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Rh" },
		summary = "**Rhodium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Rh** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 45. It is a very rare, dark silvery-white, hard, corrosion-resistant [[Transition metal|transition metal]]. It is a noble metal and a member of the platinum group. It has only one naturally occurring [[Isotope|isotope]], which is 103Rh. Naturally occurring rhodium is usually found as a free metal or as an alloy with similar metals and rarely as a chemical compound in minerals such as bowieite and rhodplumsite. It is one of the rarest and most valuable [[Precious metal|precious metals]]. Rhodium is a group 9 element (cobalt group). It is used for the enhancement of jewelry.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Rhodium is found in [[Platinum|platinum]] or [[Nickel|nickel]] ores with the other members of the platinum group metals. It was discovered in 1803 by William Hyde Wollaston in one such ore, and named for the rose color of one of its [[Chlorine|chlorine]] compounds. The element's major use (consuming about 80% of world rhodium production) is as one of the [[Catalysis|catalysts]] in the three-way catalytic converters in automobiles. Because rhodium metal is inert against [[Corrosion|corrosion]] and most aggressive chemicals, and because of its rarity, rhodium is usually [[Alloy|alloyed]] with platinum or [[Palladium|palladium]] and applied in high-temperature and corrosion-resistant coatings. White gold is often plated with a thin rhodium layer to improve its appearance, while sterling silver is often rhodium-plated to resist tarnishing. Rhodium detectors are used in [[Nuclear reactor|nuclear reactors]] to measure the neutron flux level.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ribosome",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Macromolecular machine that synthesizes proteins in cells",
		tags = { "Cell biology", "Organelles and other cell parts" },
		summary = "A **ribosome** is a ribonucleoprotein particle found in all [[Cell (biology)|cells]] that synthesizes [[Protein|proteins]] by [[Translation (biology)|translating]] genetic information encoded in messenger RNA (mRNA). During [[Translation (biology)|translation]], the ribosome decodes successive codons in an mRNA molecule and, with the aid of transfer RNA (tRNA), links [[Amino acid|amino acids]] into a polypeptide chain. Each ribosome comprises a small and a large subunit, each composed of one or more ribosomal RNA (rRNA) molecules and many ribosomal proteins.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ribosomes differ in size, composition and organization among [[Bacteria|bacteria]], [[Archaea|archaea]] and [[Eukaryote|eukaryotes]], but they share a core structure that reflects a common evolutionary origin. Distinct ribosomes are also found in eukaryotic [[Mitochondrion|mitochondria]] and, in plants and algae, [[Chloroplast|chloroplasts]]. Ribosomes are assembled through ribosome biogenesis, which involves rRNA synthesis and processing together with the assembly of ribosomal proteins into functional subunits. First observed by George Emil Palade in 1955, ribosomes have been the subject of extensive structural and functional research. Their discovery was recognized by the [[Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine]] in 1974, while determination of their atomic structure and mechanism through [[X-ray crystallography]] and cryo-electron microscopy was recognized by the [[Nobel Prize in Chemistry]] in 2009.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "River",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Natural flowing freshwater stream",
		tags = { "Water", "Hydrology" },
		summary = "A **river** is a natural [[Stream|stream]] of [[Fresh water|fresh water]] that flows on [[Land|land]] or inside caves towards another [[Body of water|body of water]] at a lower [[Elevation|elevation]], such as an [[Ocean|ocean]], [[Lake|lake]], or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the [[Water cycle|water cycle]], the processes by which [[Water|water]] moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through [[Precipitation|precipitation]], whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or [[Snow|snow]], or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Rivers flow in channeled watercourses and merge in [[Confluence|confluences]] to form drainage basins, areas where surface water eventually flows to a common outlet. [[Drainage divide|Drainage divides]] keep rivers separated from other courses of water and causes upstream water within the confines of the divide to fall into the downhill stream. Rivers have a great effect on the landscape around them. They may regularly overflow their banks and [[Flood|flood]] the surrounding area, spreading nutrients to the surrounding area. Sediment or alluvium carried by rivers shapes the landscape around it, forming deltas and [[Island|islands]] where the flow slows down. Rivers rarely run in a straight line, instead, they bend or [[Meander|meander]]; the locations of a river's banks can change frequently. Rivers get their alluvium from [[Erosion|erosion]], which carves rock and soil into canyons and valleys.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "River delta",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Silt deposition landform at the mouth of a river",
		tags = { "Water", "Hydrology" },
		summary = "A **river delta** is a [[Landform|landform]], typically triangular, created by the [[Deposition (geology)|deposition]] of the [[Sediment|sediments]] that are carried by the waters of a [[River|river]], where the river merges with a body of slow-moving water or with a body of stagnant water. The creation of a river delta occurs at the river mouth, where the river merges into an [[Ocean|ocean]], a [[Ocean|sea]], or an [[Estuary|estuary]], into a [[Lake|lake]], a [[Reservoir|reservoir]], or (more rarely) into another river that cannot carry away the sediment supplied by the feeding river. Etymologically, the term river delta derives from the triangular shape of the uppercase Greek letter delta.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In [[Hydrology|hydrology]], the dimensions of a river delta are determined by the balance between the watershed processes that supply [[Sediment|sediment]] and the watershed processes that redistribute, sequester, and export the supplied sediment into the receiving basin. River deltas are important in human [[Civilization|civilization]], as they are major agricultural production centers and population centers. They can provide [[Coast|coastline]] defence and can impact drinking water supply. They are also [[Ecology|ecologically]] important, with different species' assemblages depending on their landscape position. On geologic timescales, they are also important [[Carbon sink|carbon sinks]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Limnology" },
	},
	{
		title = "Rock (geology)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🪨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Naturally occurring mineral aggregate",
		tags = { "Geology", "Petrology" },
		aliases = { "Stone" },
		summary = "In [[Geology|geology]], a **rock** (also called a **stone**) is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of [[Mineral|minerals]] or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its [[Chemical compound|chemical composition]], and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the [[Earth's crust|crust]], and most of its interior, except for the liquid [[Earth's outer core|outer core]] and pockets of [[Magma|magma]] in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including [[Petrology|petrology]] and [[Mineralogy|mineralogy]]. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Rocks are usually grouped into three main groups: [[Igneous rock|igneous rocks]], [[Sedimentary rock|sedimentary rocks]] and [[Metamorphic rock|metamorphic rocks]]. Igneous rocks are formed when [[Magma|magma]] cools in the Earth's crust, or [[Lava|lava]] cools on the ground surface or the seabed. Sedimentary rocks are formed by diagenesis and lithification of [[Sediment|sediments]], which in turn are formed by the [[Weathering|weathering]], transport, and [[Deposition (geology)|deposition]] of existing rocks. Metamorphic rocks are formed when existing rocks are subjected to such high pressures and temperatures that they are transformed without significant melting. Humanity has made use of rocks since the time the earliest humans lived. This early period, called the [[Stone Age]], saw the development of many stone tools. Stone was then used as a major component in the construction of buildings and early [[Infrastructure|infrastructure]].",
			},
		},
	},
