		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Movement of an object which leaves at least one  point unchanged",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Classical mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Rotational motion", "Rotary motion" },
		summary = "**Rotation**, also known as **rotational motion** or **rotary motion**, is the [[Motion|movement]] of an object that leaves at least one point unchanged. In 2 dimensions, a plane figure can rotate in either a [[Clockwise|clockwise]] or counterclockwise sense around a point called the center of rotation. In 3 dimensions, a solid figure rotates around an imaginary line called an **axis of rotation**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The special case of a rotation with an internal axis passing through the body's own [[Center of mass|center of mass]] is known as a **spin** (or autorotation). In that case, the surface intersection of the internal spin axis can be called a pole; for example, [[Earth's rotation]] defines the [[Geographical pole|geographical poles]]. A rotation around an axis completely external to the moving body is called a **revolution** (or **orbit**), as in a planetary orbit, e.g., Earth's orbit around the [[Sun]]. The ends of the external axis of revolution can be called the orbital poles. Either type of rotation is involved in a corresponding type of angular velocity (spin angular velocity and orbital angular velocity) and [[Angular momentum|angular momentum]] (spin angular momentum and orbital angular momentum).",
			},
		},
	},
