		summary = "**Rubidium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Rb** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 37. It is a very soft, whitish-grey solid in the [[Alkali metal|alkali metal]] group, similar to [[Potassium|potassium]] and [[Caesium|caesium]]. Rubidium is the first alkali metal in the group to have a density higher than [[Properties of water|water]]. On Earth, natural rubidium comprises two [[Isotope|isotopes]]: 72% is a stable isotope 85Rb, and 28% is slightly radioactive 87Rb, with a [[Half-life|half-life]] of 48.8 billion years – more than three times as long as the estimated [[Age of the universe|age of the universe]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"German chemists Robert Bunsen and [[Gustav Kirchhoff]] discovered rubidium in 1861 by the newly developed technique, flame spectroscopy. The name comes from the [[Latin]] word, meaning deep red, the color of its emission spectrum. Rubidium's compounds have various chemical and electronic applications. Rubidium metal is easily vaporized and has a convenient spectral absorption range, making it a frequent target for [[Laser|laser]] manipulation of [[Atom|atoms]]. Rubidium is not a known nutrient for any [[Organism|living organisms]]. However, rubidium [[Ion|ions]] have similar properties and the same charge as potassium ions, and are actively taken up and treated by animal cells in similar ways.",
			},
