		aliases = { "Ru" },
		summary = "**Ruthenium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Ru** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 44. It is a rare [[Transition metal|transition metal]] belonging to the platinum group of the [[Periodic table|periodic table]]. Like the other metals of the platinum group, ruthenium is unreactive to most chemicals. Karl Ernst Claus, a Russian scientist of Baltic-German ancestry, discovered the element in 1844 at Kazan State University and named it in honor of [[Russian Empire|Russia]], using the Latin name Ruthenia. Ruthenium is usually found as a minor component of [[Platinum|platinum]] ores; the annual production has risen from about 19 [[Tonne|tonnes]] in 2009 to 35.5 tonnes in 2017. Most ruthenium produced is used in wear-resistant electrical contacts and thick-film resistors.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A minor application for ruthenium is in platinum [[Alloy|alloys]] and as a chemical [[Catalysis|catalyst]]. A new application of ruthenium is as the capping layer for extreme-ultraviolet photomasks in semiconductor lithography. Ruthenium is generally found in ores with the other platinum-group metals in the [[Ural Mountains]] and in [[North America|North]] and [[South America]]. Small but commercially important quantities are also found in pentlandite extracted from Sudbury, Ontario, and in pyroxenite deposits in [[South Africa]].",
