	{
		title = "Sand",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Granular material composed of finely divided rock and mineral particles",
		tags = { "Geology", "Petrology" },
		summary = "**Sand** is a granular material composed of finely divided [[Mineral|mineral]] particles. Sand has various compositions but is usually defined by its grain size. Sand grains are smaller than [[Gravel|gravel]] and coarser than [[Silt|silt]]. Sand can also refer to a textural class of soil or soil type; i.e., a soil containing more than 85 percent sand-sized particles by mass.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The composition of sand varies, depending on the local rock sources and conditions, but the most common constituent of sand in inland [[Continent|continental]] settings and non-[[Tropics|tropical]] coastal settings is [[Silicon dioxide|silica]] (silicon dioxide, or SiO2), usually in the form of [[Quartz|quartz]]. [[Calcium carbonate]] is the second most common type of sand. One such example of this is [[Aragonite|aragonite]], which has been created over the past 500million years by various forms of life, such as [[Coral reef|coral]] and [[Shellfish|shellfish]]. It is the primary form of sand apparent in areas where reefs have dominated the ecosystem for millions of years, as in the [[Caribbean]]. Somewhat more rarely, sand may be composed of calcium sulfate, such as [[Gypsum|gypsum]] and selenite, as is found in places such as White Sands National Park and Salt Plains National Wildlife Refuge in the U.S.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Conglomerate (geology)" },
	},
	{
		title = "Sandstone",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🪨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of sedimentary rock",
		tags = { "Geology", "Petrology" },
		summary = "**Sandstone** is a clastic sedimentary rock composed mainly of sand-sized (0.0625 to 2 mm) [[Silicate mineral|silicate]] grains, cemented together by another mineral. Sandstones comprise about 20–25% of all [[Sedimentary rock|sedimentary rocks]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most sandstone is composed of [[Quartz|quartz]] or [[Feldspar|feldspar]] because they are the most resistant [[Mineral|minerals]] to the [[Weathering|weathering]] processes at the Earth's surface. Like uncemented [[Sand|sand]], sandstone may be imparted any color by impurities within the minerals, but the most common colors are tan, brown, yellow, red, grey, pink, white, and black. Because sandstone beds can form highly visible cliffs and other [[Topography|topographic]] features, certain colors of sandstone have become strongly identified with certain regions, such as the red rock deserts of Arches National Park and other areas of the [[Southwestern United States|American Southwest]]. Rock formations composed of sandstone usually allow the percolation of water and other fluids and are porous enough to store large quantities, making them valuable [[Aquifer|aquifers]] and petroleum reservoirs.",
