	{
		title = "Sapphire",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Gem variety of the mineral corundum",
		tags = { "Geology", "Mineral gemstones" },
		summary = '**Sapphire** is a precious [[Gemstone|gemstone]], a variety of the mineral corundum, consisting of aluminium oxide with trace amounts of elements such as [[Iron|iron]], [[Titanium|titanium]], [[Cobalt|cobalt]], [[Lead|lead]], [[Chromium|chromium]], [[Vanadium|vanadium]], [[Magnesium|magnesium]], [[Boron|boron]], and [[Silicon|silicon]]. The name sapphire is derived from the Latin word sapphirus, itself from the [[Greek language|Greek]] word sappheiros, which referred to lapis lazuli. Sapphire with colors other than its typical blue is referred to as "fancy sapphire", and can be in yellow, purple, orange, and green colors; "parti sapphires" show two or more colors. Red corundum stones also occur, but are called [[Ruby|rubies]] rather than sapphires. Pink-colored corundum may be classified either as ruby or sapphire depending on the locale.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Commonly, natural sapphires are cut and polished into gemstones and worn in [[Jewellery|jewelry]]. They also may be created synthetically in laboratories for industrial or decorative purposes in large crystal boules. It occurs in association with [[Ruby|ruby]], zircon, biotite, muscovite, [[Calcite|calcite]], dravite and [[Quartz|quartz]]. Sapphire has a remarkable [[Hardness|hardness]], with a score of 9 on the Mohs scale, the third-hardest mineral after [[Diamond|diamond]] at 10 and moissanite at 9.5. Non-ornamental applications include [[Infrared|infrared]] [[Optics|optical]] components, high-durability [[Window|windows]], [[Clock|wristwatch]] crystals and movement bearings, and very thin electronic wafers, which are used as the [[Insulator (electricity)|insulating]] substrates of special-purpose solid-state electronics such as integrated circuits and GaN-based blue [[Light-emitting diode|LEDs]].",
