	{
		title = "Schrödinger equation",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🟰",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Description of a quantum-mechanical system",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Quantum mechanics" },
		summary = "The **Schrödinger equation** is a [[Partial differential equation|partial differential equation]] that governs the [[Wave function|wave function]] of a non-relativistic quantum-mechanical system. Its discovery was a significant landmark in the development of [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]]. It is named after [[Erwin Schrödinger]], an Austrian physicist, who postulated the equation in 1925 and published it in 1926, forming the basis for the work that resulted in his [[Nobel Prize in Physics]] in 1933.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Conceptually, the Schrödinger equation is the quantum counterpart of Newton's second law in [[Classical mechanics|classical mechanics]]. Given a set of known initial conditions, Newton's second law makes a mathematical prediction as to what path a given [[Physical system|physical system]] will take over time. The Schrödinger equation gives the evolution over time of the [[Wave function|wave function]], the quantum-mechanical characterization of an isolated physical system. The equation was postulated by Schrödinger based on a postulate of [[Louis de Broglie]] that all matter has an associated [[Matter wave|matter wave]]. The equation predicted bound states of the atom in agreement with experimental observations. The Schrödinger equation is not the only way to study quantum mechanical systems and make predictions.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Science",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Systematic endeavour to gain knowledge",
		tags = { "Science basics" },
		summary = "**Science** is a systematic discipline that builds and organises [[Knowledge|knowledge]] in the form of testable explanations about [[Natural science|nature]] and [[Social science|society]]. It is driven by the [[Scientific method|scientific method]]: an empirical cycle that typically involves making observations, producing hypotheses, testing them with [[Empirical evidence|evidence]], and drawing conclusions. Science encompasses this process and the body of knowledge it produces, which the scientific community continuously challenges, validates, and organises.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Modern science is typically divided into two – or three – major branches: the [[Natural science|natural sciences]], which study the physical world, and the [[Social science|social sciences]], which study individuals and societies. While referred to as the formal sciences, the study of [[Logic|logic]], mathematics, and [[Theoretical computer science|theoretical computer science]] are typically regarded as separate because they rely on [[Deductive reasoning|deductive reasoning]] instead of the [[Scientific method|scientific method]] as their main methodology. Meanwhile, [[Applied science|applied sciences]] are disciplines that use scientific knowledge for practical purposes, such as engineering and medicine. The [[History of science|history of science]] spans the majority of the historical record, with the earliest identifiable predecessors to modern science dating to the [[Bronze Age]] in [[Ancient Egypt|Egypt]] and [[Mesopotamia]] (c. 3000–1200 BCE).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Scientific law",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Statement based on repeated empirical observations that describes some natural phenomenon",
		tags = { "Science basics" },
		aliases = { "Scientific laws", "Laws of science", "Physical law" },
		summary = "**Scientific laws** or **laws of science** are statements, based on repeated [[Experiment|experiments]] or [[Observation|observations]], that describe or predict a range of natural phenomena. The term law has diverse usage in many cases (approximate, accurate, broad, or narrow) across all fields of [[Natural science|natural science]] ([[Physics|physics]], [[Chemistry|chemistry]], [[Astronomy|astronomy]], geoscience, [[Biology|biology]]). Laws are developed from data and can be further developed through [[Mathematics|mathematics]]; in all cases they are directly or indirectly based on [[Empirical evidence|empirical evidence]]. It is generally understood that they implicitly reflect, though they do not explicitly assert, causal relationships fundamental to reality, and are discovered rather than invented.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Scientific laws summarize the results of experiments or observations, usually within a certain range of application. In general, the accuracy of a law does not change when a new theory of the relevant phenomenon is worked out, but rather the scope of the law's application, since the mathematics or statement representing the law does not change. As with other kinds of scientific knowledge, scientific laws do not express absolute certainty, as mathematical laws do. A scientific law may be contradicted, restricted, or extended by future observations. A law can often be formulated as one or several statements or [[Equation|equations]], so that it can predict the outcome of an experiment. Laws differ from hypotheses and postulates, which are proposed during the [[Scientific method|scientific process]] before and during validation by experiment and observation.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Scientific method",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Interplay between observation, experiment, and theory in science",
		tags = { "Science basics" },
		summary = "The **scientific method** is an [[Empirical evidence|empirical]] method for acquiring [[Knowledge|knowledge]] through careful [[Observation|observation]], rigorous [[Skepticism|skepticism]], hypothesis testing, and experimental validation. Developed from ancient and medieval practices, it acknowledges that [[Philosophy of science|cognitive assumptions]] can distort the interpretation of the [[Perception|observation]]. The scientific method has characterized [[Science|science]] since at least the 17th century. Scientific inquiry includes creating a testable [[Hypothesis|hypothesis]] through [[Inductive reasoning|inductive reasoning]], testing it through experiments and statistical analysis, and adjusting or discarding the hypothesis based on the results.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although procedures vary across [[Branches of science|fields]], the underlying process is often similar. In more detail: the scientific method involves making [[Conjecture|conjectures]] (hypothetical explanations), predicting the logical consequences of hypothesis, then carrying out experiments or empirical observations based on those predictions. A hypothesis is a conjecture based on knowledge obtained while seeking answers to the question. Hypotheses can be very specific or broad but must be falsifiable, implying that it is possible to identify a possible outcome of an experiment or observation that conflicts with predictions deduced from the hypothesis; otherwise, the hypothesis cannot be meaningfully tested. While the scientific method is often presented as a fixed sequence of steps, it actually represents a set of general principles.",
			},
		},
	},
