	{
		title = "Sea ice",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Outcome of seawater as it freezes",
		tags = { "Water", "Glaciology" },
		summary = "**Sea ice** forms as [[Seawater|seawater]] freezes. Because [[Ice|ice]] is less [[Density|dense]] than liquid water, it floats on the ocean's surface (just like [[Fresh water|fresh water]] ice). Sea ice covers about 7% of the Earth's surface and about 12% of the world's oceans. Much of the world's sea ice is enclosed within the polar ice packs in the Earth's [[Polar regions|polar regions]]: the Arctic ice pack of the [[Arctic Ocean]] and the Antarctic ice pack of the [[Southern Ocean]]. Polar packs naturally undergo significant yearly cycling, reaching their greatest surface extent in winter and retreating in summer.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Within the ice, salty brine channels provide habitat for microorganisms that form the base of unique food webs. The presence or absence of sea ice also shapes navigation routes, regional weather, and global [[Ocean current|ocean circulation]]. Sea ice plays a key role in Earth's climate. Its white surface reflects the Sun's energy back into space, helping to keep the planet cool in a process known as the [[Albedo|albedo]] effect. Sea ice also insulates the ocean below, limiting the transfer of heat, water vapor, and gases such as carbon dioxide between the sea and the atmosphere. Satellite records have shown a marked decline in Arctic sea ice extent and thickness in recent decades, a trend linked to global [[Climate change|climate change]]. Antarctic sea ice shows more regional variability but is recently also experiencing declines.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sea level",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Geographical reference point from which various heights are measured",
		tags = { "Water", "Oceanography" },
		aliases = { "Mean sea level", "MSL" },
		summary = "**Mean sea level** (**MSL**, often shortened to **sea level**) is an [[Average|average]] surface level of one or more among [[Earth]]'s coastal [[Body of water|bodies of water]] from which heights such as [[Elevation|elevation]] may be measured. The global MSL is a type of vertical datum – a standardised [[Geodetic datum|geodetic datum]] – that is used, for example, as a chart datum in [[Cartography|cartography]] and [[Navigation|marine navigation]], or, in aviation, as the standard sea level at which [[Atmospheric pressure|atmospheric pressure]] is measured to calibrate altitude and, consequently, aircraft flight levels. A common and relatively straightforward mean sea-level standard is instead a long-term average of tide gauge readings at a particular reference location.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term above sea level generally refers to the [[Height above mean sea level|height above mean sea level]] (AMSL). The term **APSL** means above present sea level, comparing sea levels in the past with the level today. Earth's radius at sea level is 6,378.137 km (3,963.191 mi) at the equator. It is 6,356.752 km (3,949.903 mi) at the poles and 6,371.001 km (3,958.756 mi) on average. This flattened spheroid, combined with local gravity anomalies, defines the [[Geoid|geoid]] of the Earth, which approximates the local mean sea level for locations in the open ocean. The geoid includes a significant depression in the Indian Ocean, whose surface dips as much as 106 m below the global mean sea level (excluding minor effects such as tides and currents).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Seabed",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Bottom of the ocean",
		tags = { "Water", "Oceanography" },
		summary = "The **seabed** is the bottom of the [[Ocean|ocean]]. Alternatively, it is known as the **seafloor**, **sea floor**, **ocean floor**, or **ocean bottom**. The floor of all the world's oceans is known as the seabed.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The structure of the seabed of the global ocean is governed by [[Plate tectonics|plate tectonics]]. Most of the ocean is very deep, where the seabed is known as the [[Abyssal plain|abyssal plain]]. Seafloor spreading creates mid-ocean ridges along the center line of major ocean basins, where the seabed is slightly shallower than the surrounding abyssal plain. From the abyssal plain, the seabed slopes upward toward the continents and becomes, in order from deep to shallow, the continental rise, slope, and [[Continental shelf|shelf]]. The depth within the seabed itself, such as the depth down through a sediment core, is known as the "depth below seafloor". The ecological environment of the seabed and the deepest waters are collectively known, as a [[Habitat|habitat]] for creatures, as the "benthos". Most of the seabed throughout the world\'s oceans is covered in layers of marine sediments.',
			},
		},
