		aliases = { "Macroalgae" },
		summary = "**Seaweed**, or **macroalgae**, refers to thousands of species of macroscopic, [[Multicellular organism|multicellular]], [[Ocean|marine]] [[Algae|algae]]. The term includes some types of Rhodophyta (red), [[Brown algae|Phaeophyta]] (brown) and [[Chlorophyta]] (green) macroalgae. Seaweed species such as [[Kelp|kelps]] provide essential nursery habitat for fisheries and other marine species and thus protect food sources; other species, such as [[Plankton|planktonic]] algae, play a vital role in capturing carbon and producing at least 50% of Earth's oxygen.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Natural seaweed ecosystems are sometimes under threat from human activity. For example, mechanical [[Dredging|dredging]] of kelp destroys the resource and dependent fisheries. Humans have a long history of cultivating seaweeds for their uses. In recent years, seaweed farming has become a global agricultural practice, providing food, source material for various chemical uses (such as carrageenan), cattle feeds and fertilizers. Due to their importance in marine ecologies and for absorbing carbon dioxide, recent attention has been on cultivating seaweeds as a potential [[Climate change mitigation|climate change mitigation]] strategy for biosequestration of carbon dioxide, alongside other benefits like nutrient pollution reduction, increased habitat for coastal aquatic species, and reducing local [[Ocean acidification|ocean acidification]].",
