	{
		title = "Semiconductor",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Material of moderate electrical conductivity",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrical circuits" },
		summary = 'A **semiconductor** is a material with [[Electrical resistivity and conductivity|electrical conductivity]] between that of a [[Electrical conductor|conductor]] and an [[Insulator (electricity)|insulator]]. Its conductivity can be modified by adding impurities ("doping") to its [[Crystal structure|crystal structure]]. When two regions with different doping levels are present in the same crystal, they form a semiconductor junction. The term "semiconductors" is sometimes used to refer to [[Semiconductor device|semiconductor devices]] such as microchips and computer processors, which work using the physical properties of semiconductors.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The behavior of [[Charge carrier|charge carriers]], which include [[Electron|electrons]], [[Ion|ions]], and electron holes, at these junctions is the basis of [[Diode|diodes]], [[Transistor|transistors]], and most modern [[Electronics|electronics]]. Some examples of semiconductors are [[Silicon|silicon]], [[Germanium|germanium]], gallium arsenide, and elements near the so-called "metalloid staircase" on the [[Periodic table|periodic table]]. After silicon, gallium arsenide is the second-most common semiconductor and is used in laser diodes, [[Solar cell|solar cells]], microwave-frequency [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuits]], and others. Silicon is a critical element for fabricating most [[Electronic circuit|electronic circuits]]. [[Semiconductor device|Semiconductor devices]] can display a range of different useful properties, such as passing current more easily in one direction than the other, showing variable resistance, and having sensitivity to light or heat.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sense",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physiological capacity",
		tags = { "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Sensation" },
		summary = "A **sense** is a biological system used by an [[Organism|organism]] for **sensation**, the process of gathering [[Information|information]] about the surroundings through the detection of stimuli. During sensation, sense organs collect various stimuli (such as a sound or smell) for transduction, meaning transformation into a form that can be understood by the brain. Sensation and [[Perception|perception]] are fundamental to nearly every aspect of an organism's [[Cognition|cognition]], [[Behavior|behavior]] and [[Thought|thought]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although, in some cultures, five human senses were traditionally identified as such (namely [[Visual perception|sight]], smell, touch, [[Taste|taste]], and [[Hearing|hearing]]), many more are now recognized. Senses used by non-human organisms are even greater in variety and number. In organisms, a sensory organ consists of a group of interrelated sensory cells that respond to a specific type of physical stimulus. Via cranial and spinal nerves (nerves of the central and peripheral nervous systems that relay sensory information to and from the brain and body), the different types of sensory receptor cells (such as mechanoreceptors, photoreceptors, chemoreceptors, thermoreceptors) in sensory organs transduct sensory information from these organs towards the central nervous system, finally arriving at the sensory cortices in the brain, where sensory signals are processed and interpreted (perceived).",
			},
		},
	},
