	{
		title = "Silicon",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Silicon** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Si** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 14. It is a hard, brittle crystalline solid with a blue-grey metallic lustre, and is a tetravalent non-metal (sometimes considered as a [[Metalloid|metalloid]]) and [[Semiconductor|semiconductor]]. It is a member of group 14 in the periodic table: [[Carbon|carbon]] is above it; and [[Germanium|germanium]], [[Tin|tin]], [[Lead|lead]], and flerovium are below it. It is relatively unreactive. Silicon is a significant element that is essential for several physiological and metabolic processes in plants. Silicon is widely used as a semiconductor material in various electrical devices such as [[Transistor|transistors]], [[Solar cell|solar cells]], and [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuits]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It dominates semiconductor applications due to its low cost, excellent electronic properties, and adaptable physical characteristics. Because of its high chemical affinity for oxygen, it was not until 1823 that Jöns Jakob Berzelius was first able to prepare it and characterize it in pure form. Its [[Oxide|oxides]] form a family of [[Ion|anions]] known as [[Silicate|silicates]]. Its melting and boiling points of 1414 °C and 3265 °C, respectively, are the second highest among all the metalloids and nonmetals, being surpassed only by [[Boron|boron]]. Silicon is the eighth [[Abundance of the chemical elements|most common element]] in the universe by mass, but very rarely occurs in its pure form in the Earth's crust. It is widely distributed throughout space in cosmic dusts, [[Minor planet|planetoids]], and [[Planet|planets]] as various forms of [[Silicon dioxide|silicon dioxide]] (silica) or [[Silicate|silicates]].",
			},
