		tags = { "Atmosphere", "Atmospheric optics" },
		summary = "The **sky** is an unobstructed view upward from the [[Planetary surface|surface]] of the [[Earth]]. It includes the [[Atmosphere of Earth|atmosphere]] and [[Outer space|outer space]]. It may also be considered a place between the ground and outer space, thus distinct from outer space.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In the field of [[Astronomy|astronomy]], the sky is also called the [[Celestial sphere|celestial sphere]]. This is an abstract sphere, concentric to the Earth, on which the [[Sun]], [[Moon]], [[Planet|planets]], and stars appear to be drifting. The celestial sphere is conventionally divided into designated areas called [[Constellation|constellations]]. Usually, the term sky informally refers to a perspective from the Earth's surface; however, the meaning and usage can vary. An observer on the surface of the Earth can see a small part of the sky, which resembles a dome (sometimes called the sky bowl) appearing flatter during the day than at [[Night|night]]. In some cases, such as in discussing the weather, the sky refers to only the lower, denser layers of the atmosphere. The [[Daytime|daytime]] sky appears blue because air molecules scatter shorter wavelengths of [[Sunlight|sunlight]] more than longer ones (redder light).",
