		summary = "**Sodium bicarbonate** (IUPAC name: **sodium hydrogen carbonate**), commonly known as **baking soda** or **bicarbonate of soda**, or **saleratus**, is a [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]] with the chemical formula **NaHCO3**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Sodium bicarbonate is a white solid that is [[Crystal|crystalline]] but often appears as a fine powder. It has a slightly salty, alkaline taste resembling that of [[Sodium carbonate|sodium carbonate]] ("washing soda"). The natural mineral form is nahcolite, although it is more commonly found as a component of the mineral trona. It can often be found near baking powder in grocery aisles. The term baking soda is most common in the United States, while bicarbonate of soda is more common in Australia, the United Kingdom, and New Zealand. The prefix bi- in "bicarbonate" comes from an outdated naming system predating molecular knowledge. The modern chemical formulas of these compounds now express their precise chemical compositions which were unknown when the name bi-carbonate of potash was coined (see also: bicarbonate).',
