		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Salts" },
		aliases = { "Washing soda", "Soda ash", "Sal soda" },
		summary = '**Sodium carbonate** (also known as **washing soda**, **soda ash**, **sal soda**, and **soda crystals**) is the [[Inorganic compound|inorganic compound]] with the formula Na2CO3 and its various [[Hydrate|hydrates]]. All forms are white, odorless, water-soluble salts that yield alkaline solutions in [[Water|water]]. Historically, it was extracted from the ashes of plants grown in sodium-rich soils, and because the ashes of these sodium-rich plants were noticeably different from ashes of wood (once used to produce potash), sodium [[Carbonate|carbonate]] became known as "soda ash". It is produced in large quantities from [[Sodium chloride|sodium chloride]] and [[Limestone|limestone]] by the Solvay process, as well as by carbonating [[Sodium hydroxide|sodium hydroxide]] which is made using the chloralkali process.',
