	{
		title = "Soil",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🟫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Earth, a natural material",
		tags = { "Earth", "Pedology" },
		summary = "**Soil**, also commonly referred to as **earth**, is a [[Mixture|mixture]] of [[Organic matter|organic matter]], minerals, [[Gas|gases]], [[Water|water]], and [[Organism|organisms]] that together support the [[Life|life]] of [[Plant|plants]] and soil organisms. Some scientific definitions distinguish dirt from soil by restricting the former term specifically to displaced soil.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Soil consists of a solid collection of [[Mineral|minerals]] and [[Organic matter|organic matter]] (the **soil matrix**), as well as a porous phase (Pore space) that holds gases (the **soil atmosphere**) and a liquid phase that holds water and dissolved substances both organic and inorganic, in [[Ion|ionic]] or in [[Molecule|molecular]] form (the **soil solution**). Accordingly, soil is a complex three-[[State of matter|state]] system of solids, liquids, and gases. Soil is a product of several factors: the influence of [[Climate|climate]], [[Terrain|relief]] (elevation, orientation, and slope of terrain), organisms, and the soil's parent materials (original minerals) interacting over time. It continually undergoes development through numerous physical, chemical, and biological processes, which include [[Weathering|weathering]] with associated [[Erosion|erosion]]. Given its complexity and strong internal connectedness, soil ecologists regard soil as an [[Ecosystem|ecosystem]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Soil fertility",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🟫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ability of a soil to sustain agricultural plant growth",
		tags = { "Earth", "Pedology" },
		summary = "**Soil fertility** refers to the ability of [[Soil|soil]] to sustain agricultural plant growth, i.e. to provide plant [[Habitat|habitat]] and result in sustained and consistent [[Crop yield|yields]] of high quality (see also soil health).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The following properties contribute to soil fertility in most situations: Sufficient soil depth for adequate root growth and water retention; Good internal [[Drainage|drainage]], allowing sufficient soil aeration for optimal root growth (although some plants, such as [[Rice|rice]], tolerate waterlogging); Topsoil or horizon O is with sufficient soil organic matter for healthy soil structure and soil moisture retention; Soil pH in the range 5.5 to 7.0 (suitable for most plants but some prefer or tolerate more acid or alkaline conditions); Adequate concentrations of [[Plant nutrition|essential plant nutrients]] in plant-available forms; Presence of a range of microorganisms that support plant growth. In lands used for [[Agriculture|agriculture]] and other human activities, maintenance of soil fertility typically requires the use of soil conservation practices.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Soil formation",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🟫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Process of soil formation",
		tags = { "Earth", "Pedology" },
		aliases = { "Pedogenesis" },
		summary = "**Soil formation**, also known as **pedogenesis**, is the process of [[Soil|soil]] genesis as regulated by the effects of place, environment, and history. Biogeochemical processes act to both create and destroy order (anisotropy) within soils. These alterations lead to the development of layers, termed soil horizons, distinguished by differences in color, structure, texture, and [[Soil|chemistry]]. These features occur in patterns of soil type distribution, forming in response to differences in soil forming factors.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Pedogenesis is studied as a branch of pedology, the study of soil in its natural environment. Other branches of pedology are the study of soil morphology and soil classification. The study of pedogenesis is important to understanding soil distribution patterns in current (soil geography) and past (paleopedology) geologic periods.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Soil science",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study of soil as a natural resource on the surface of Earth",
		tags = { "Earth", "Pedology" },
		summary = "**Soil science** is the study of [[Soil|soil]] as a [[Natural resource|natural resource]] on the surface of the [[Earth]] including [[Soil formation|soil formation]], classification and mapping; physical, chemical, biological, and fertility properties of soils; and these properties in relation to the use and management of soils.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The diversity of names of disciplines associated with soil science is related to the various associations concerned. Indeed, [[Engineering|engineers]], [[Agronomy|agronomists]], [[Chemist|chemists]], [[Geologist|geologists]], [[Physical geography|physical geographers]], ecologists, [[Biologist|biologists]], microbiologists, [[Forestry|silviculturists]], [[Public health|sanitarians]], [[Archaeology|archaeologists]], and specialists in regional planning, all contribute to further knowledge of soils and the advancement of the soil sciences. Soil scientists have raised concerns about how to preserve soil and arable land in a world with a growing population, possible future water crisis, increasing per-capita food consumption, and land degradation.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Solid",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "State of matter",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "States of matter" },
		summary = "**Solid** is a [[State of matter|state of matter]] in which [[Atom|atoms]] are closely packed and are difficult to move past each other. Solids resist compression, expansion, or external forces that would alter its shape, with the degree of resistance being dependent upon the specific material under consideration. Solids also always possess the least kinetic energy per atom/molecule relative to other phases or, equivalently stated, solids are formed when matter in the liquid / gas phase is cooled below a certain temperature. This temperature is called the [[Melting point|melting point]] of the substance and is an intrinsic property; i.e. independent of how much of the matter there is.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The vast majority of substances, when in the solid state, can be arranged in one of a few ubiquitous structures. Solids are characterized by structural rigidity and resistance to applied external forces and pressure. Unlike liquids, solids do not flow to take on the shape of their container, nor do they expand to fill the entire available volume like a gas. Much like the other three fundamental phases, solids also expand when heated, the thermal energy put into increasing the distance and reducing the [[Potential energy|potential energy]] between atoms. However, solids do this to a much lesser extent. When heated to their melting point or [[Sublimation|sublimation point]], solids melt into a liquid or sublimate directly into a gas, respectively. For solids that directly sublimate into a gas, the melting point is replaced by the sublimation point.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Degenerate matter" },
	},
	{
		title = "Solid mechanics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of mechanics concerned with solid materials and their behaviors",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Solid mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Mechanics of solids" },
		summary = "**Solid mechanics** (also known as **mechanics of solids**) is the branch of [[Continuum mechanics|continuum mechanics]] that studies the behavior of [[Solid|solid]] materials, especially their motion and deformation under the action of [[Force|forces]], [[Temperature|temperature]] changes, phase changes, and other external or internal agents.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Solid mechanics is fundamental for [[Civil engineering|civil]], [[Aerospace engineering|aerospace]], nuclear, [[Biomedical engineering|biomedical]] and [[Mechanical engineering|mechanical engineering]], for [[Geology|geology]], and for many branches of [[Physics|physics]] and [[Chemistry|chemistry]] such as [[Materials science|materials science]]. It has specific applications in many other areas, such as understanding the [[Anatomy|anatomy]] of living beings, and the design of dental prostheses and surgical implants. One of the most common practical applications of solid mechanics is the Euler–Bernoulli beam equation. Solid mechanics extensively uses [[Tensor|tensors]] to describe stresses, strains, and the relationship between them. Solid mechanics is a vast subject because of the wide range of solid materials available, such as steel, wood, concrete, biological materials, textiles, geological materials, and plastics.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Solid-state physics",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of physics focused on matter in the solid state",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Solid-state physics** is the study of rigid [[Matter|matter]], or solids, through methods such as solid-state chemistry, [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]], [[Crystallography|crystallography]], [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]], and [[Metallurgy|metallurgy]]. It is the largest branch of [[Condensed matter physics|condensed matter physics]]. Solid-state physics studies how the large-scale properties of solid materials result from their [[Atom|atomic]]-scale properties. Thus, solid-state physics forms a theoretical basis of [[Materials science|materials science]]. Along with solid-state chemistry, it also has direct applications in the technology of [[Transistor|transistors]] and [[Semiconductor|semiconductors]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Solution (chemistry)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Homogeneous mixture of a solute and a solvent",
		tags = { "Chemical substances", "Chemical mixtures" },
		aliases = { "Solution" },
		summary = 'In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], a **solution** is defined by [[International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry|IUPAC]] as "A liquid or solid phase containing more than one substance, when for convenience one (or more) substance, which is called the solvent, is treated differently from the other substances, which are called solutes. When, as is often but not necessarily the case, the sum of the mole fractions of solutes is small compared with unity, the solution is called a dilute solution." One parameter of a solution is the [[Concentration|concentration]], which is a measure of the amount of solute in a given amount of solution or solvent. The term "[[Aqueous solution|aqueous solution]]" is used when one of the solvents is [[Water|water]].',
