	{
		title = "Sound",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Audible vibration that travels via pressure waves in matter",
		tags = { "Physics", "Waves" },
		aliases = { "Sound wave" },
		summary = "**Sound** is a [[Phenomenon|phenomenon]] in which [[Pressure|pressure]] disturbances propagate through an [[Elasticity (physics)|elastic]] [[Matter|material]] medium. In the context of [[Physics|physics]], it is characterised as a mechanical wave of pressure or related quantities (e.g. displacement), whereas in [[Physiology|physiological]]-[[Psychology|psychological]] contexts it implies a [[Hearing|hearing]] process. Though sensitivity to sound varies among all organisms, the human ear is sensitive to audio frequencies ranging from 20 [[Hertz|Hz]] to 20 kHz. More broadly, acoustic waves encompass mechanical longitudinal waves in general. Examples of the significance and application of sound include [[Music|music]], medical imaging techniques, oral language and parts of [[Science|science]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Space",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Framework of distances and directions",
		tags = { "Physics", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Space** is a [[Three-dimensional space|three-dimensional]] continuum containing [[Position (geometry)|positions]] and [[Direction (geometry)|directions]]. In [[Classical physics|classical physics]], physical space is often conceived in three linear [[Dimension|dimensions]]. Modern physicists usually consider it, with [[Time|time]], to be part of a boundless four-dimensional continuum known as [[Spacetime|spacetime]]. The concept of space is considered to be of fundamental importance to an understanding of the physical [[Universe|universe]]. However, disagreement continues between philosophers over whether it is itself an entity, a relationship between entities, or part of a conceptual framework.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In the 19th and 20th centuries mathematicians began to examine geometries that are [[Non-Euclidean geometry|non-Euclidean]], in which space is conceived as curved, rather than flat, as in the [[Euclidean space]]. According to [[Albert Einstein]]'s theory of [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]], space around [[Gravitational field|gravitational fields]] deviates from Euclidean space. Experimental tests of general relativity have confirmed that non-Euclidean geometries provide a better model for the shape of space.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spacetime",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⏳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mathematical model combining space and time",
		tags = { "Physics", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Space-time continuum" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], **spacetime**, or the **space-time continuum**, is a [[Mathematical model|mathematical model]] that fuses the [[Three-dimensional space|three dimensions of space]] and the one dimension of [[Time|time]] into a single four-dimensional continuum. Spacetime diagrams are useful in visualizing and understanding [[Theory of relativity|relativistic]] effects, such as how different observers perceive where and when events occur.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Until the turn of the 20th century, the assumption had been that the three-dimensional [[Geometry|geometry]] of the universe (its description in terms of locations, shapes, distances, and directions) was distinct from time (the measurement of when events occur within the universe). However, [[Space|space]] and time took on new meanings with the [[Lorentz transformation]] and [[Theory of relativity|special theory of relativity]]. In 1908, Hermann Minkowski presented a [[Geometry|geometric]] interpretation of special relativity that fused time and the three spatial dimensions into a single four-dimensional continuum now known as Minkowski space. This interpretation proved vital to the [[Theory of relativity|general theory of relativity]], wherein spacetime is curved by mass and energy.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Species",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Basic unit of taxonomic classification, below genus",
		tags = { "Genetics and taxonomy" },
		summary = "A **species** is the basic unit of [[Taxonomy (biology)|classification]] and a [[Taxonomic rank|taxonomic rank]] of an organism, as well as a unit of [[Biodiversity|biodiversity]]. Other ways of defining species include their karyotype, [[DNA]] sequence, [[Morphology (biology)|morphology]], behaviour, or [[Ecological niche|ecological niche]]. In addition, palaeontologists use the concept of the chronospecies since [[Fossil|fossil]] reproduction cannot be examined. The most recent rigorous estimate for the total number of species of [[Eukaryote|eukaryotes]] is between 8 and 8.7 million. About 14% of these had been described by 2011. All species are given a two-part [[Binomial nomenclature|binomial name]]. The first part is the name of the [[Genus|genus]] to which the species belongs.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The second part is called the specific name or the specific epithet (in botanical nomenclature, also sometimes in zoological nomenclature). For example, Boa constrictor is one of the species of the genus Boa, with constrictor being the specific name. While the definitions given above may seem adequate at first glance, when looked at more closely they represent problematic species concepts. For example, the boundaries between closely related species become unclear with [[Hybrid (biology)|hybridisation]], in a species complex of hundreds of similar microspecies, and in a ring species. Also, among organisms that reproduce only [[Asexual reproduction|asexually]], the concept of a reproductive species breaks down, and each clonal lineage is potentially a microspecies.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spectroscopy",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Study involving matter and electromagnetic radiation",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Analytical chemistry" },
		summary = "**Spectroscopy** is the field of study that measures and interprets [[Electromagnetic spectrum|electromagnetic spectra]] as it interacts with matter. In narrower contexts, spectroscopy is the precise study of [[Color|color]] as generalized from radiated [[Light|visible light]] to all bands of the electromagnetic spectrum.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Spectroscopy, primarily in the electromagnetic spectrum, is a fundamental exploratory tool in the fields of [[Astronomy|astronomy]], [[Chemistry|chemistry]], [[Materials science|materials science]], and [[Physics|physics]], allowing the composition, physical and electronic structure of matter to be investigated at the atomic, [[Molecule|molecular]] and macro scale, and over astronomical distances. Historically, spectroscopy originated as the study of the wavelength dependence of the absorption by gas phase matter of visible light dispersed by a [[Prism (optics)|prism]]. Current applications of spectroscopy include biomedical spectroscopy in the areas of [[Tissue (biology)|tissue]] analysis and medical imaging. [[Matter wave|Matter waves]] and acoustic waves can be considered forms of radiative energy, and recently [[Gravitational wave|gravitational waves]] have been associated with a spectral signature in the context of the Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory (LIGO).",
			},
		},
		related = { "Absorption spectroscopy" },
	},
	{
		title = "Spectrum",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Continuous range of values, such as wavelengths in physics",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The word spectrum was first used scientifically in [[Optics|optics]] to describe the [[Rainbow|rainbow]] of colors in [[Light|visible light]] after passing through a prism. In the optical spectrum, light [[Wavelength|wavelength]] is viewed as continuous, and spectral colors are seen to blend into one another smoothly when organized in order of their corresponding wavelengths. As scientific understanding of light advanced, the term came to apply to the entire [[Electromagnetic spectrum|electromagnetic spectrum]], including radiation not visible to the human eye.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Spectrum has since been applied by analogy to topics outside optics. In these uses, values within a spectrum may not be associated with precisely quantifiable numbers or definitions. Such uses imply a broad range of conditions or behaviors grouped together and studied under a single title for ease of discussion. Nonscientific uses of the term spectrum are sometimes misleading. For instance, a single left–right spectrum of political opinion does not capture the full range of people's political beliefs. Political scientists use a variety of biaxial and multiaxial systems to more accurately characterize political opinion. In most modern usages of spectrum there is a unifying theme between the extremes at either end. This was not always true in older usage.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Speed",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Magnitude of velocity",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Classical mechanics" },
		summary = "In [[Kinematics|kinematics]], the **speed** of a moving object is the rate at which the [[Distance|distance]] travelled by the object changes with time. The **average speed** of an object in an interval of time is the distance travelled by the object divided by the duration of the interval; the **instantaneous speed** is the [[Limit (mathematics)|limit]] of the average speed as the duration of the time interval approaches zero. Speed is the magnitude of velocity (a [[Vector quantity|vector]]), which indicates additionally the [[Direction (geometry)|direction]] of motion.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Speed has the [[Dimensional analysis|dimensions]] of distance divided by time. The [[International System of Units|SI unit]] of speed is the [[Metre per second|metre per second]] (m/s). The kilometre per hour (km/h) or, in the US and the UK, miles per hour (mph), are also used as units of speed. For air and marine travel, the knot is commonly used. The fastest possible speed at which energy or information can travel, according to [[Theory of relativity|special relativity]], is the [[Speed of light|speed of light]] in vacuum c = 299792458 metres per second (approximately 1079000000 km/h or 671000000 mph). [[Matter]] cannot reach the speed of light, as this would require an infinite amount of energy. In relativity physics, the concept of rapidity replaces the classical idea of speed.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Dimension", "L T−1" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Speed of sound",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Speed of sound wave through elastic medium",
		tags = { "Physics", "Waves" },
		summary = "The **speed of sound** is the distance travelled per unit of time by a [[Sound|sound wave]] as it propagates through an elastic medium. More simply, the speed of sound is how fast vibrations travel. At 20 °C, the speed of sound in air is about 343 m/s, or 1 km in 2.92 s or one [[Mile|mile]] in 4.69 s. It depends strongly on temperature as well as the medium through which a [[Sound|sound wave]] is propagating. At 0 °C, the speed of sound in dry [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]] (sea level 14.7 psi) is about 331 m/s. The speed of sound in an ideal gas depends only on its temperature and composition. The speed has a weak dependence on [[Frequency|frequency]] and pressure in dry air, deviating slightly from ideal behavior.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In colloquial speech, speed of sound refers to the speed of sound waves in [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]]. The speed of sound varies from substance to substance, however: typically, sound travels most slowly in [[Gas|gases]], faster in [[Liquid|liquids]], and fastest in solids. For example, while sound travels at 343 m/s in air, it travels in [[Fresh water|fresh water]] at 1481 m/s at a temperature of 20 °C (slightly more than 4.3 times as fast) and at 5,120 m/s in [[Iron|iron]] (almost 15 times as fast). In an exceptionally [[Stiffness|stiff]] material such as [[Diamond|diamond]], sound travels at 12,000 m/s (39,000 ft/s), – about 35 times its speed in air and about the fastest it can travel under normal conditions. In theory, the speed of sound is actually the speed of vibrations. Sound waves in solids are composed of compression waves (just as in gases and liquids) and a different type of sound wave called a shear wave, which occurs only in solids.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spin (physics)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Intrinsic quantum property of particles",
		tags = { "Physics", "Particle physics" },
		aliases = { "Spin" },
		summary = "**Spin** is an intrinsic form of [[Angular momentum|angular momentum]] carried by [[Elementary particle|elementary particles]], and thus by composite particles such as [[Hadron|hadrons]], [[Atomic nucleus|atomic nuclei]], and atoms. Spin is quantized, and accurate models for the interaction with spin require relativistic quantum mechanics or [[Quantum field theory|quantum field theory]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The existence of [[Electron|electron]] spin angular momentum is inferred from experiments, such as the Stern–Gerlach experiment, in which silver atoms were observed to possess two possible discrete angular momenta despite having no orbital angular momentum. The relativistic spin–statistics theorem connects electron spin quantization to the [[Pauli exclusion principle]]: observations of exclusion imply half-integer spin, and observations of half-integer spin imply exclusion. Spin is described mathematically as a vector for some particles such as photons, and as a spinor for other particles such as electrons. Spinors behave similarly to [[Euclidean vector|vectors]]: they have definite magnitudes and change under rotations; however, they use an unconventional "direction". All elementary particles of a given kind have the same magnitude of spin angular momentum, though its direction may change.',
			},
		},
		related = { "String theory" },
	},
	{
		title = "Spring (hydrology)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Point at which water emerges from an aquifer to the surface",
		tags = { "Water", "Hydrology" },
		summary = "A **spring** is a natural exit point at which [[Groundwater|groundwater]] emerges from an [[Aquifer|aquifer]] and [[Fluid dynamics|flows]] across the ground surface as surface water. It is a component of the [[Hydrosphere|hydrosphere]], as well as a part of the [[Water cycle|water cycle]]. Springs have long been important for [[Human|humans]] as a source of [[Fresh water|fresh water]], especially in [[Aridity|arid]] regions which have relatively little annual rainfall.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Springs are driven out onto the surface by various natural forces, such as [[Gravity|gravity]] and hydrostatic pressure. A spring produced by the emergence of geothermally heated groundwater is known as a [[Hot spring|hot spring]]. The yield of spring water varies widely from a volumetric flow rate of nearly zero to more than 14000 L/s for the biggest springs.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Spring (season)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "One of the Earth's four temperate seasons",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Seasons" },
		aliases = { "Spring", "Springtime" },
		summary = "**Spring**, also known as **springtime**, is one of the four [[Temperate climate|temperate]] [[Season|seasons]], succeeding [[Winter|winter]] and preceding [[Summer|summer]]. There are various technical definitions of spring, but local usage of the term varies according to local climate, cultures and customs. When it is spring in the [[Northern Hemisphere]], it is [[Autumn|autumn]] in the [[Southern Hemisphere]] and vice versa. At the spring [[Equinox|equinox]], also called the vernal equinox, days and nights are approximately twelve hours long, with daytime length increasing and nighttime length decreasing as the season progresses until the summer [[Solstice|solstice]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The spring equinox is in March in the Northern Hemisphere and in September in the Southern Hemisphere, while the summer solstice is in June in the Northern Hemisphere and in December in the Southern Hemisphere. Spring and "springtime" refer to the season, and also to ideas of rebirth, rejuvenation, renewal, resurrection and regrowth. [[Subtropics|Subtropical]] and [[Tropics|tropical]] areas have climates better described in terms of other seasons, e.g. dry or wet, monsoonal or cyclonic. Cultures may have local names for seasons which have little equivalence to the terms originating in Europe.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Stainless steel",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Iron-based alloy resistant to rust and corrosion",
		tags = { "Chemical mixtures", "Specific alloys" },
		summary = "**Stainless steel** is an [[Iron|iron]]-based [[Alloy|alloy]] that contains [[Chromium|chromium]], making it resistant to rust and [[Corrosion|corrosion]]. Alternatively, it is known as **inox**, **corrosion-resistant steel** (**CRES**), **Nirosta** (an abbreviation of the German term nichtrostender Stahl) or **rustless steel**. Stainless steel's resistance to corrosion comes from its chromium content of 10.5% or more, which forms a [[Passivation (chemistry)|passive film]] that protects the material and can self-heal when exposed to oxygen. It can be further alloyed with elements like [[Molybdenum|molybdenum]], [[Carbon|carbon]], [[Nickel|nickel]] and [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]] to enhance specific properties for various applications.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The alloy's properties, such as luster and resistance to corrosion, are useful in many applications. Stainless steel can be rolled into sheets, plates, bars, wire, and tubing. These can be used in cookware, [[Cutlery|cutlery]], surgical instruments, major appliances, vehicles, construction material in large buildings, industrial equipment (e.g., in paper mills, [[Chemical plant|chemical plants]], water treatment), and storage tanks and tankers for chemicals and food products. Some grades are also suitable for [[Forging|forging]] and casting. The biological cleanability of stainless steel is superior to both copper and [[Aluminium|aluminium]], and comparable to glass. Its cleanability, strength, and corrosion resistance have prompted the use of stainless steel in pharmaceutical and food processing plants. Different types of stainless steel are labeled with an AISI three-digit number.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Standard Model",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏋️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Theory of forces and subatomic particles",
		tags = { "Physics", "Particle physics" },
		summary = "The **Standard Model** of [[Particle physics|particle physics]] is the [[Scientific theory|theory]] describing three of the four known fundamental forces ([[Electromagnetism|electromagnetic]], [[Weak interaction|weak]] and [[Strong interaction|strong interactions]] – excluding [[Gravity|gravity]]) in the [[Universe|universe]] and classifying all known [[Elementary particle|elementary particles]]. It was developed in stages throughout the latter half of the 20th century, through the work of many scientists worldwide, with the current formulation being finalized in the mid-1970s upon experimental confirmation of the existence of [[Quark|quarks]]. Since then, proof of the top quark (1995), the tau neutrino (2000), and the [[Higgs boson]] (2012) have added further credence to the Standard Model.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In addition, the Standard Model has predicted with great accuracy the various properties of weak neutral currents and the W and Z bosons. For example, it does not fully explain why there is more matter than anti-matter, incorporate the full theory of gravitation as described by [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]], or account for the universe's accelerating expansion as possibly described by [[Dark energy|dark energy]]. The model does not contain any viable [[Dark matter|dark matter]] particle that possesses all of the required properties deduced from observational [[Physical cosmology|cosmology]]. The Standard Model without modifications also does not incorporate neutrino oscillations and their non-zero masses, but extensions have been proposed that can account for these features. The development of the Standard Model was driven by [[Theoretical physics|theoretical]] and experimental particle physicists alike.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Supersymmetry" },
	},
