	{
		title = "Stress (mechanics)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physical quantity that expresses internal forces in a continuous material",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Solid mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Stress" },
		summary = "In [[Continuum mechanics|continuum mechanics]], **stress** is a [[Physical quantity|physical quantity]] that describes [[Force|forces]] present during deformation. For example, an object being pulled apart, such as a stretched elastic band, is subject to tensile stress and may undergo elongation. An object being pushed together, such as a crumpled sponge, is subject to compressive stress and may undergo shortening. The greater the force and the smaller the cross-sectional area of the body on which it acts, the greater the stress. Stress has dimension of force per area, with SI units of [[Newton (unit)|newtons]] per square meter (N/m2) or [[Pascal (unit)|pascal]] (Pa).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Stress expresses the internal forces that neighbouring [[Particle|particles]] of a continuous material exert on each other, while strain is the measure of the relative deformation of the material. For example, when a [[Solid|solid]] vertical bar is supporting an overhead [[Weight|weight]], each particle in the bar pushes on the particles immediately below it. When a [[Liquid|liquid]] is in a closed container under [[Pressure|pressure]], each particle gets pushed against by all the surrounding particles. The container walls and the [[Pressure|pressure]]-inducing surface (such as a piston) push against them in (Newtonian) reaction. These macroscopic forces are actually the net result of a very large number of [[Intermolecular force|intermolecular forces]] and [[Statistical mechanics|collisions]] between the particles in those [[Molecule|molecules]]. Stress is frequently represented by a lowercase Greek letter sigma.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Dimension", "wikidata" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "String theory",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Theory of subatomic structure",
		tags = { "Physics", "Particle physics" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], **string theory** is a theoretical framework in which the point-like particles of [[Particle physics|particle physics]] are replaced by one-dimensional objects called strings. String theory describes how these strings move through space and interact with each other by [[Vibration|vibrations]]. On distance scales larger than the string scale, a string acts like a particle, with its [[Mass|mass]], charge, and other properties determined by the vibrational state of the string. In string theory, one of the many vibrational states of the string corresponds to the graviton, a [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanical]] particle that carries the [[Gravity|gravitational force]]. Thus, string theory is a theory of [[Quantum gravity|quantum gravity]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"String theory is a broad and varied subject that attempts to address a number of deep questions of fundamental physics. String theory has contributed a number of advances to [[Mathematical physics|mathematical physics]], which have been applied to a variety of problems in [[Black hole|black hole]] physics, early universe [[Physical cosmology|cosmology]], [[Nuclear physics|nuclear physics]], and [[Condensed matter physics|condensed matter physics]], and it has stimulated a number of major developments in [[Pure mathematics|pure mathematics]]. Because string theory potentially provides a unified description of gravity and [[Particle physics|particle physics]], it is a candidate for a [[Theory of everything|theory of everything]], a self-contained [[Mathematical model|mathematical model]] that describes all [[Fundamental interaction|fundamental forces]] and forms of [[Matter|matter]]. Despite much work on these problems, it is not known to what extent string theory describes the real world or how much freedom the theory allows in the choice of its details.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Particle" },
	},
	{
		title = "Strong interaction",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Binding of quarks in subatomic particles",
		tags = { "Physics", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Strong force", "Strong nuclear force" },
		summary = "In [[Nuclear physics|nuclear physics]] and [[Particle physics|particle physics]], the **strong interaction**, also called the **strong force** or **strong nuclear force**, is one of the four known [[Fundamental interaction|fundamental interactions]]. It confines [[Quark|quarks]] into [[Proton|protons]], [[Neutron|neutrons]], and other [[Hadron|hadron]] particles, and also binds neutrons and protons to create atomic nuclei, where it is called the nuclear force.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most of the [[Mass–energy equivalence|mass]] of a [[Proton|proton]] or [[Neutron|neutron]] is the result of the strong interaction energy; the individual quarks provide only about 1% of the mass of a proton. At the range of 10−15 m (1 femtometer, slightly more than the radius of a [[Nucleon|nucleon]]), the strong force is approximately 100 times as strong as [[Electromagnetism|electromagnetism]], 106 times as strong as the [[Weak interaction|weak interaction]], and 1038 times as strong as [[Gravity|gravitation]]. In the context of atomic nuclei, the force binds protons and neutrons together to form a nucleus and is called the nuclear force (or residual strong force). Because the force is mediated by massive, short lived mesons on this scale, the residual strong interaction obeys a distance-dependent behavior between nucleons that is quite different from when it is acting to bind quarks within hadrons.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Strontium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Strontium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Sr** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 38. An [[Alkaline earth metal|alkaline earth metal]], it is a soft silver-white yellowish [[Metal|metallic]] element that is highly [[Reactivity (chemistry)|chemically reactive]]. The metal forms a dark oxide layer when it is exposed to air. Strontium has physical and chemical properties similar to those of its two vertical neighbors in the periodic table, [[Calcium|calcium]] and [[Barium|barium]]. It occurs naturally mainly in the minerals celestine and strontianite, and is mostly mined from these.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Both strontium and strontianite are named after Strontian, a village in Scotland near which the mineral was discovered in 1790 by Adair Crawford and William Cruickshank. It was identified as a new element the next year from its crimson-red flame test color. Strontium was first isolated as a metal in 1808 by [[Humphry Davy]] using the then newly discovered process of [[Electrolysis|electrolysis]]. During the 19th century, strontium was mostly used in the production of sugar from [[Sugar beet|sugar beets]] (see strontian process). At the peak of production of television [[Cathode ray tube|cathode-ray tubes]], as much as 75% of strontium consumption in the United States was used for the faceplate glass. With the replacement of cathode-ray tubes with other display methods, consumption of strontium has dramatically declined.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Subatomic particle",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Particle smaller than an atom",
		tags = { "Particle physics", "Subatomic particles" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], a **subatomic particle** is a [[Particle|particle]] smaller than an [[Atom|atom]]. According to the [[Standard Model|Standard Model of particle physics]], a subatomic particle can be a composite particle or an [[Elementary particle|elementary particle]]. A composite particle, such as a [[Proton|proton]] or a [[Neutron|neutron]], is composed of other particles while an elementary particle, such as an [[Electron|electron]], is not composed of other particles. [[Particle physics]] and [[Nuclear physics|nuclear physics]] study these particles and how they interact.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most force-carrying particles such as photons or gluons are called bosons and, although they have quanta of energy, do not have rest mass or discrete diameters (other than pure energy wavelength) and are unlike the former particles that have rest mass and cannot overlap or combine, which are called fermions. The W and Z bosons, however, are an exception to this rule and have relatively large rest masses at approximately 80 GeV/c2 and 90 GeV/c2 respectively. Experiments show that light can behave like a stream of particles (called [[Photon|photons]]) as well as exhibiting wave-like properties. This led to the concept of [[Wave–particle duality|wave–particle duality]] to reflect that quantum-scale particles behave both like particles and like [[Wave|waves]]; they are occasionally called wavicles to reflect this.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Substance (chemistry)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Form of matter",
		tags = { "Chemistry" },
		aliases = { "Substance", "Chemical substance" },
		summary = "In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], a **substance** is a unique form of [[Matter|matter]] with constant chemical composition and characteristic [[Chemical property|properties]]. Chemical substances may take the form of a single [[Chemical element|element]] or [[Chemical compound|chemical compounds]]. If two or more chemical substances can be combined without [[Chemical reaction|reacting]], they may form a chemical [[Mixture|mixture]]. If a mixture is separated to isolate one chemical substance to a desired degree, the resulting substance is said to be chemically pure.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Chemical substances can exist in several different physical [[State of matter|states]] or [[Phase (matter)|phases]] (e.g. [[Solid|solids]], [[Liquid|liquids]], [[Gas|gases]], or [[Plasma (physics)|plasma]]) without changing their chemical composition. Substances [[Phase transition|transition]] between these phases of matter in response to changes in [[Temperature|temperature]] or [[Pressure|pressure]]. Some chemical substances can be combined or converted into new substances by means of [[Chemical reaction|chemical reactions]]. Chemicals that do not possess this ability are said to be inert. Pure water is an example of a chemical substance, with a constant composition of two hydrogen [[Atom|atoms]] [[Chemical bond|bonded]] to a single oxygen atom (i.e. H2O). The atomic ratio of hydrogen to oxygen is always 2:1 in every [[Molecule|molecule]] of water. Pure water will tend to [[Boiling point|boil]] near 100 °C, an example of one of the characteristic properties that define it.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Substitution reaction",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical reaction in which one functional group in a compound is replaced by another",
		tags = { "Chemistry", "Chemical reactions" },
		aliases = { "Single displacement reaction", "Single substitution reaction)" },
		summary = "A **substitution reaction** (also known as **single displacement reaction** or **single substitution reaction)** is a [[Chemical reaction|chemical reaction]] during which one [[Functional group|functional group]] in a [[Chemical compound|compound]] is replaced by another functional group. Substitution reactions are of prime importance in [[Organic chemistry|organic chemistry]]. Substitution reactions in organic chemistry are classified either as electrophilic or nucleophilic depending upon the reagent involved, whether a reactive intermediate involved in the reaction is a carbocation, a carbanion or a [[Radical (chemistry)|free radical]], and whether the substrate is aliphatic or aromatic. Detailed understanding of a reaction type helps to predict the product outcome in a reaction.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It also is helpful for optimizing a reaction with regard to variables such as temperature and choice of [[Solvent|solvent]]. A good example of a substitution reaction is halogenation. When [[Chlorine|chlorine]] gas (Cl2) is irradiated, some of the molecules are split into two chlorine [[Radical (chemistry)|radicals]] (Cl•), whose free electrons are strongly nucleophilic. One of them breaks a C–H covalent bond in CH4 and grabs the hydrogen atom to form the electrically neutral HCl. The other radical reforms a covalent bond with the CH3• to form CH3Cl (methyl chloride).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sulfate",
