	{
		title = "Summer",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Hottest of the four temperate seasons",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Seasons" },
		aliases = { "Summertime" },
		summary = "**Summer** or **summertime** is the [[Heat|hottest]] and brightest of the four [[Temperate climate|temperate]] [[Season|seasons]], occurring after [[Spring (season)|spring]] and before [[Autumn|autumn]]. At or centered on the summer solstice, daylight hours are the longest and darkness hours are the shortest, with day length decreasing as the season progresses after the solstice. The earliest sunrises and latest sunsets also occur near the date of the solstice. The date of the beginning of summer varies according to definition, [[Climate|climate]], tradition, and culture. When it is summer in the [[Northern Hemisphere]], it is [[Winter|winter]] in the [[Southern Hemisphere]], and vice versa.",
	},
	{
		title = "Superconductivity",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "0️⃣",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Electrical conductivity with exactly zero resistance",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrical circuits" },
		aliases = { "Superconductors" },
		summary = "**Superconductivity** is a set of physical properties observed in **superconductors**: materials where [[Electrical resistance and conductance|electrical resistance]] is exactly zero and [[Magnetic field|magnetic fields]] are expelled from the material. Unlike an ordinary metallic [[Electrical conductor|conductor]], whose resistance decreases gradually as its temperature is lowered, even down to near [[Absolute zero|absolute zero]], a superconductor has a characteristic [[Phase transition|critical temperature]] below which the resistance drops abruptly to zero. An [[Electric current|electric current]] through a loop of superconducting wire can persist indefinitely with no power source.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The superconductivity phenomenon was discovered in 1911 by Dutch physicist Heike Kamerlingh Onnes. Like [[Ferromagnetism|ferromagnetism]] and atomic spectral lines, superconductivity is a phenomenon which can only be explained by [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]]. It is characterized by the Meissner effect, the complete cancellation of the magnetic field in the interior of the superconductor during its transitions into the superconducting state. The occurrence of the Meissner effect indicates that superconductivity cannot be understood simply as the idealization of perfect conductivity in [[Classical physics|classical physics]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Superposition principle",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fundamental principle of physics",
		tags = { "Physics", "Waves" },
		aliases = { "Superposition property" },
		summary = "The **superposition principle**, also known as **superposition property**, states that, for all linear systems, the net response caused by two or more stimuli is the sum of the responses that would have been caused by each stimulus individually. So that if input A produces response X, and input B produces response Y, then input (A + B) produces response (X + Y).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Superposition can be defined by two simpler properties: additivity and homogeneity for [[Scalar (mathematics)|scalar]] a. This principle has many applications in [[Physics|physics]] and [[Engineering|engineering]] because many physical systems can be modeled as linear systems. For example, a beam can be modeled as a linear system where the input stimulus is the load on the beam and the output response is the deflection of the beam. The importance of linear systems is that they are easier to analyze mathematically; there is a large body of mathematical techniques, frequency-domain linear transform methods such as [[Fourier transform|Fourier]] and [[Laplace transform|Laplace]] transforms, and linear operator theory, that are applicable. Because physical systems are generally only approximately linear, the superposition principle is only an approximation of the true physical behavior.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Supersymmetry",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Symmetry between bosons and fermions",
		tags = { "Physics", "Particle physics" },
		summary = "**Supersymmetry** is a [[Theory|theoretical framework]] in [[Physics|physics]] that suggests the existence of a symmetry between [[Particle physics|particles]] with integer [[Spin (physics)|spin]] ([[Boson|bosons]]) and particles with half-integer spin ([[Fermion|fermions]]). It proposes that for every known particle, there exists a partner particle with different spin properties. There have been multiple experiments on supersymmetry that have failed to provide evidence that it exists in [[Nature|nature]]. If evidence is found, supersymmetry could help explain certain phenomena, such as the nature of [[Dark matter|dark matter]] and the hierarchy problem in particle physics.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A **supersymmetric theory** is a theory in which the equations for [[Force|force]] and the equations for [[Matter|matter]] are identical. In [[Theoretical physics|theoretical]] and [[Mathematical physics|mathematical physics]], any theory with this property has the principle of supersymmetry (SUSY). Dozens of supersymmetric theories exist. In theory, supersymmetry is a type of spacetime symmetry between two basic classes of particles: [[Boson|bosons]], which have an integer-valued [[Spin (physics)|spin]] and follow [[Bose–Einstein statistics]], and [[Fermion|fermions]], which have a half-integer-valued spin and follow Fermi–Dirac statistics. The names of bosonic partners of fermions are prefixed with s-, because they are scalar particles. For example, if the [[Electron|electron]] existed in a supersymmetric theory, then there would be a particle called a selectron (superpartner electron), a bosonic partner of the electron.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Particle" },
	},
	{
		title = "Surface tension",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tendency of a liquid surface to shrink to reduce surface area",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Fluid mechanics" },
		summary = "**Surface tension** is the energy per unit area due to having a surface in a liquid. It has the [[Dimensional analysis|dimension]] of [[Force|force]] per unit [[Length|length]], or [[Energy|energy]] per unit [[Area|area]]. The two are equivalent, but when referring to energy per unit of area, it is common to use the term surface energy, which is a more general term in the sense that it applies also to [[Solid|solids]]. Surface tension is used for liquids, while surface stress and surface energy are more commonly used for solids.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"An example of its relevance is the tendency of [[Liquid|liquid]] surfaces at rest to shrink to the minimum [[Surface area|surface area]] possible. Because of the relatively high attraction of water molecules to each other through a web of [[Hydrogen bond|hydrogen bonds]], water has a higher surface tension (72.8 millinewtons (mN) per meter at 20 °C) than most other liquids. This allows objects with a higher density than water such as razor blades and insects (e.g. water striders) to float on a water surface without becoming even partly submerged. The magnitude of the surface tension is connected to the forces between the molecules at the surface. Therefore surfactants are often used to reduce it so there is more contact between the liquid and another material, for instance detergents. It can also lead to pressure inside water bubbles, as well as many other phenomena; it is a classic, well-studied property common to all liquids.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Sustainability",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Societal goal and normative concept",
		tags = { "Ecology" },
		aliases = { "Sustainable" },
		summary = "**Sustainability** or **being** **sustainable** is the ability to continue over a long period of time. In modern usage it generally refers to a state in which the environment, economy, and society will continue to exist over a long period of time. Many definitions emphasize the environmental dimension. This can include addressing key environmental problems, such as [[Climate change|climate change]] and [[Biodiversity loss|biodiversity loss]]. The idea of sustainability can guide decisions at the global, national, organizational, and individual levels. A related concept is that of [[Sustainable development|sustainable development]], and the terms are often used to mean the same thing.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'"Sustainability is often thought of as a long-term goal (i.e. a more sustainable world), while sustainable development refers to the many processes and pathways to achieve it." Details around the economic dimension of sustainability are controversial. Scholars have discussed this under the concept of weak and strong sustainability. For example, there will always be tension between the ideas of "welfare and prosperity for all" and environmental conservation, so trade-offs are necessary. It would be desirable to find ways that separate economic growth from harming the environment. This means using fewer resources per unit of output even while growing the economy. This decoupling reduces the environmental impact of economic growth, such as [[Pollution|pollution]]. Doing this is difficult. It is challenging to measure sustainability as the concept is complex, contextual, and dynamic.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Swamp",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Forested wetland",
		tags = { "Biomes", "Wetlands" },
		summary = "A **swamp** is a forested [[Wetland|wetland]]. Swamps are considered to be transition zones because both land and water play a role in creating this environment. Swamps vary in size and are located all around the world. The water of a swamp may be [[Fresh water|fresh water]], [[Brackish water|brackish water]], or [[Seawater|seawater]]. **Freshwater swamps** form along large rivers or lakes where they are critically dependent upon rainwater and seasonal flooding to maintain natural water level fluctuations. **Saltwater swamps** are found along tropical and subtropical coastlines. Some swamps have hammocks, or dry-land protrusions, covered by aquatic vegetation, or vegetation that tolerates periodic inundation or soil saturation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The two main types of swamp are "true" or swamp forests and "transitional" or shrub swamps. In the boreal regions of Canada, the word swamp is colloquially used for what is more formally termed a [[Bog|bog]], fen, or muskeg. Some of the world\'s largest swamps are found along major rivers such as the [[Amazon River|Amazon]], the [[Mississippi River|Mississippi]], and the [[Congo River|Congo]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Symbiogenesis",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Evolutionary theory",
		tags = { "Cell biology", "Organelles and other cell parts" },
		aliases = { "Endosymbiotic theory", "Serial endosymbiotic theory" },
		summary = "**Symbiogenesis** (**endosymbiotic theory**, or **serial endosymbiotic theory**) is the leading [[Evolution|evolutionary]] theory of the origin of [[Eukaryote|eukaryotic]] cells from prokaryotic organisms. The theory holds that [[Mitochondrion|mitochondria]], [[Plastid|plastids]] such as [[Chloroplast|chloroplasts]], and possibly other organelles of eukaryotic cells are descended from formerly free-living prokaryotes (more closely related to the [[Bacteria]] than to the [[Archaea]]) taken one inside the other in [[Endosymbiont|endosymbiosis]]. Mitochondria appear to be [[Phylogenetic tree|phylogenetically]] related to Rickettsiales bacteria, while chloroplasts are thought to be related to [[Cyanobacteria|cyanobacteria]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The idea that chloroplasts were originally independent organisms that merged into a symbiotic relationship with other one-celled organisms dates back to the 19th century, when it was espoused by researchers such as Andreas Schimper. The endosymbiotic theory was articulated in 1905 and 1910 by the Russian botanist Konstantin Mereschkowski, and advanced and substantiated with microbiological evidence by Lynn Margulis in 1967.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Symbiosis",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ecological interaction between taxa",
		tags = { "Ecology" },
		summary = 'In 1879, Heinrich Anton de Bary defined symbiosis as "the living together of unlike organisms". The term is sometimes more exclusively used in a restricted, mutualistic sense, where both symbionts contribute to each other\'s subsistence. This means that they benefit each other in some way.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Symbiosis is diverse and can be classified in multiple ways. It can be obligate, meaning that one or both of the organisms depend on each other for survival, or facultative, meaning that they can subsist independently. When one organism lives on the surface of another, such as head lice on humans, it is called ectosymbiosis; when one partner lives inside the tissues of another, such as Symbiodinium within [[Coral reef|coral]], it is termed [[Endosymbiont|endosymbiosis]]. Where the interaction reduces both parties' fitness, it is called [[Competition (biology)|competition]]; where just one party's fitness is reduced, it is called amensalism. Where one benefits but the other is largely unaffected, this is termed [[Commensalism|commensalism]]. Finally, where both parties benefit, the relationship is described as [[Mutualism (biology)|mutualistic]]. Symbiosis has often driven the [[Evolution|evolution]] of species; mutualism has enabled species for example to colonise new environments.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Symmetry (physics)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Feature of a system that is preserved under some transformation",
		tags = { "Physics", "Basics" },
		summary = "A **symmetry** of a [[Physical system|physical system]] is a physical or mathematical feature of the system (observed or intrinsic) that is preserved or remains unchanged under some transformation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A family of particular transformations may be continuous (such as [[Rotation|rotation]] of a circle) or discrete (e.g., [[Reflection (physics)|reflection]] of a bilaterally symmetric figure, or rotation of a regular polygon). Continuous and discrete transformations give rise to corresponding types of symmetries. Mathematically, symmetries are described by [[Symmetry group|symmetry groups]]; continuous symmetries can be described by Lie groups while discrete symmetries are described by finite groups, lattice groups or other discrete groups. Symmetries are frequently amenable to mathematical formulations such as group representations. Symmetries can be exploited to simplify many problems in physics and explain physical phenomena such as conservation laws, but symmetry principles also play a foundational role in all of the fundamental theories of modern physics.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Syngas",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🦴",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fossil fuel derived from other hydrocarbon sources",
		tags = { "Chemical substances", "Chemical mixtures" },
		aliases = { "Synthesis gas" },
		summary = "**Syngas**, or **synthesis gas**, is a mixture of [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] and [[Carbon monoxide|carbon monoxide]] in various ratios. The gas often contains some carbon dioxide and [[Methane|methane]]. It is principally used for producing [[Ammonia|ammonia]] or methanol. Syngas is combustible and can be used as a fuel. Historically, it has been used as a replacement for [[Gasoline|gasoline]] when gasoline supply has been limited; for example, wood gas was used to power cars in Europe during [[World War II|WWII]] (in Germany alone, half a million cars were built or rebuilt to run on wood gas).",
		related = { "Colloid", "Emulsion" },
	},
}
]]></string>
