	{
		title = "Temperate climate",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Main climate class",
		tags = { "Air", "Climatology" },
		aliases = { "Temperate climates", "Temperate" },
		summary = "In [[Geography|geography]], the **temperate climates** of [[Earth]] occur in the [[Middle latitudes|middle latitudes]] (approximately 23.5° to 66.5° N/S of the Equator), which span between the [[Tropics|tropics]] and the [[Polar regions|polar regions of Earth]]. These zones generally have wider [[Temperature|temperature]] ranges throughout the year and more distinct [[Season|seasonal]] changes compared to [[Tropical climate|tropical climates]], where such variations are often small; they usually differ only in the amount of [[Precipitation|precipitation]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In temperate climates, not only do [[Latitude|latitudinal positions]] influence temperature changes, but various sea currents, prevailing wind direction, continentality (how large a landmass is) and altitude also shape temperate climates. The [[Köppen climate classification]] defines a climate as "temperate" **C**, when the mean temperature is above -3 °C but below 18 °C in the coldest month to account for the persistence of frost. However, some adaptations of Köppen set the minimum at 0 °C. The broader definition of temperate climates may include [[Continental climate|continental climates]], classified as **D** and having more extreme temperatures, with mean temperatures in the coldest month usually being below -3 °C.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Temperature",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physical quantity of hot and cold",
		tags = { "Physics", "Thermodynamics" },
		summary = "In classical thermodynamics and kinetic theory, **temperature** reflects the average [[Kinetic energy|kinetic energy]] of the particles in a system, providing a quantitative measure of how energy is distributed among microscopic degrees of freedom.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Temperature is [[Measurement|measured]] with a [[Thermometer|thermometer]]. Thermometers are calibrated in various temperature scales that historically have relied on various reference points and thermometric substances for definition. The most common scales are the [[Celsius]] scale with the unit symbol °C (formerly called centigrade), the [[Fahrenheit]] scale (°F), and the [[Kelvin]] scale (K), with the third being used predominantly for scientific purposes. The kelvin is one of the seven base units in the [[International System of Units]] (SI). [[Absolute zero]], i.e., zero kelvin, 0 K = −273.15 °C, or -459.67 °F, is the lowest point in the [[Thermodynamic temperature|thermodynamic temperature]] scale. Experimentally, it can be approached very closely but not actually reached, as recognized in the third law of thermodynamics. It would be impossible to extract energy as heat from a body at that temperature.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Dimension", "wikidata" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Terbium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Tb" },
		summary = "**Terbium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Tb** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 65. It is a silvery-white, [[Rare-earth element|rare earth]] [[Metal|metal]] that is [[Ductility|malleable]] and [[Ductility|ductile]]. The ninth member of the [[Lanthanide|lanthanide]] series, terbium is a fairly electropositive metal that reacts with water, evolving [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] gas. Terbium is never found in nature as a free element, but it is contained in many [[Mineral|minerals]], including cerite, gadolinite, [[Monazite|monazite]], xenotime and euxenite.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Swedish chemist Carl Gustaf Mosander discovered terbium as a chemical element in 1843. He detected it as an impurity in yttrium oxide (Y2O3). [[Yttrium]] and terbium, as well as [[Erbium|erbium]] and [[Ytterbium|ytterbium]], are named after the village of Ytterby in Sweden. Terbium was not isolated in pure form until the advent of ion exchange techniques. Terbium is used to [[Dopant|dope]] calcium fluoride, calcium tungstate and [[Strontium|strontium]] molybdate in solid-state devices, and as a crystal stabilizer of fuel cells that operate at elevated temperatures. As a component of Terfenol-D (an alloy that expands and contracts when exposed to magnetic fields more than any other alloy), terbium is of use in actuators, in naval [[Sonar|sonar]] systems and in [[Sensor|sensors]]. Terbium is considered non-hazardous, though its biological role and toxicity have not been researched in depth. Most of the world's terbium supply is used in green [[Phosphor|phosphors]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
