	{
		title = "Thorium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Th" },
		summary = "**Thorium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Th** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 90. Thorium is a weakly radioactive light silver metal which [[Tarnish|tarnishes]] olive grey when it is exposed to air, forming thorium dioxide; it is moderately soft, [[Ductility|malleable]], and has a high [[Melting point|melting point]]. Thorium is an electropositive [[Actinide|actinide]] whose chemistry is dominated by the +4 [[Oxidation state|oxidation state]]; it is quite reactive and can ignite in air when finely divided.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"All known thorium [[Isotope|isotopes]] are unstable. The most stable isotope, 232Th, has a [[Half-life|half-life]] of 14.0 billion years, or about the [[Age of the universe|age of the universe]]; it decays very slowly via [[Alpha decay|alpha decay]], starting a [[Decay chain|decay chain]] named the thorium series that ends at stable 208[[Lead|Pb]]. On Earth, thorium and [[Uranium|uranium]] are the only elements with no stable or nearly-stable isotopes that still occur naturally in large quantities as [[Primordial nuclide|primordial elements]]. Thorium is estimated to be over three times as abundant as uranium in the Earth's crust, and is chiefly refined from [[Monazite|monazite]] sands as a by-product of extracting [[Rare-earth element|rare-earth elements]]. Thorium was discovered in 1828 by the Swedish chemist [[Jöns Jacob Berzelius]] during his analysis of a new mineral found by Morten Thrane Esmark in [[Norway]] on Løvøya island near Brevik in the Langesund fjord. He named it after [[Thor]], the Norse god of thunder and war.",
			},
		},
	},
