	{
		title = "Thulium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Tm" },
		summary = "**Thulium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Tm** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 69. It is the thirteenth element in the [[Lanthanide|lanthanide]] series of [[Metal|metals]]. It is the second-least abundant lanthanide in the Earth's crust, after radioactively unstable [[Promethium|promethium]]. It is an easily workable metal with a bright silvery-gray luster. It is fairly soft and slowly [[Tarnish|tarnishes]] in air. Despite its high price and rarity, thulium is used as a [[Dopant|dopant]] in solid-state lasers. Artificial radioactive isotopes of thulium are used as radiation sources in some portable [[X-ray]] devices.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In 1879, the Swedish chemist Per Teodor Cleve separated two previously unknown components, which he called holmia and thulia, from the rare-earth mineral erbia; these were the oxides of [[Holmium|holmium]] and thulium, respectively. His example of thulium oxide contained impurities of ytterbium oxide. A relatively pure sample of thulium oxide was first obtained in 1911. The metal itself was first obtained in 1936 by Wilhelm Klemm and Heinrich Bommer. Like the other [[Lanthanide|lanthanides]], its most common [[Oxidation state|oxidation state]] is +3, seen in its oxide, halides and other compounds. In [[Aqueous solution|aqueous solution]], like compounds of other late lanthanides, soluble thulium compounds form coordination complexes with nine water molecules.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Aluminium", "Uranium" },
	},
	{
		title = "Thunderstorm",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⛈️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Storm characterized by lightning",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Storms" },
		aliases = { "Electrical storm", "Lightning storm" },
		summary = "A **thunderstorm**, also known as an **electrical storm** or a **lightning storm**, is a storm characterized by the presence of [[Lightning|lightning]] and thunder. Relatively weak thunderstorms are sometimes called **thundershowers**. Thunderstorms occur in [[Cumulonimbus cloud|cumulonimbus clouds]]. They are usually accompanied by strong [[Wind|winds]] and often produce heavy rain and sometimes snow, sleet, or [[Hail|hail]], but some thunderstorms can produce little or no precipitation at all. Thunderstorms may line up in a series or become a rainband, known as a squall line. Strong or severe thunderstorms include some of the most dangerous weather phenomena, including large hail, strong winds, and [[Tornado|tornadoes]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some of the most persistent severe thunderstorms, known as supercells, rotate as do cyclones. While most thunderstorms move with the mean wind flow through the layer of the [[Troposphere|troposphere]] that they occupy, vertical wind shear sometimes causes a deviation in their course at a right angle to the wind shear direction. Thunderstorms result from the rapid upward movement of warm, moist air, sometimes along a [[Weather front|front]]. However, some kind of cloud forcing, whether it is a front, shortwave trough, or another system is needed for the air to rapidly accelerate upward. As the warm, moist air moves upward, it cools, condenses, and forms a cumulonimbus cloud that can reach heights of over 20 km. As the rising air reaches its dew point temperature, water vapor condenses into water droplets or ice, reducing pressure locally within the thunderstorm cell.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Blizzard" },
	},
	{
		title = "Tide",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Change in sea level due to gravity",
		tags = { "Water", "Oceanography" },
		aliases = { "Tides" },
		summary = "**Tides** are the periodic rise and fall of [[Sea level|sea level]] resulting from the differential [[Gravity|gravitational]] forces exerted primarily by the [[Moon]] and the [[Sun]], combined with inertial effects associated with the Earth–Moon system’s orbital motion and the [[Earth's rotation]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"While these astronomical forcings generate the fundamental tidal potential, actual observed tides are strongly modified by terrestrial factors, including the geometry of ocean basins, continental boundaries, [[Bathymetry|bathymetry]], the [[Coriolis force|coriolis effect]], frictional dissipation within shallow seas and the tidal resonance of coastlines. Tides vary on timescales ranging from hours to years due to a number of factors, which determine the lunitidal interval. To make accurate records, tide gauges at fixed stations measure water level over time. Gauges ignore variations caused by waves with periods shorter than minutes. These data are compared to the reference (or datum) level usually called mean sea level.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tin",
		category = "Science",
