	{
		title = "Titanium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Ti" },
		summary = "**Titanium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Ti** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 22. Found in nature only as an [[Oxide|oxide]], it can be reduced to produce a lustrous [[Transition metal|transition metal]] with a silver [[Color|color]], low [[Density|density]], and high strength that is resistant to [[Corrosion|corrosion]] in sea water, aqua regia, and [[Chlorine|chlorine]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Titanium was discovered in Cornwall, [[Kingdom of Great Britain|Great Britain]], by William Gregor in 1791 and was named by Martin Heinrich Klaproth after the Titans of [[Greek mythology]]. The element occurs within a number of [[Mineral|minerals]], principally rutile and ilmenite, which are widely distributed in the [[Earth's crust]] and [[Lithosphere|lithosphere]]; it is found in almost all living things, as well as bodies of water, rocks, and soils. The metal is extracted from its principal mineral ores by the Kroll and Hunter processes. The most common compound, [[Titanium dioxide|titanium dioxide]] (TiO2), is a popular photocatalyst and is used in the manufacture of white pigments. Other compounds include titanium tetrachloride (TiCl4), a component of smoke screens and [[Catalysis|catalysts]]; and titanium trichloride (TiCl3), which is used as a catalyst in the production of [[Polypropylene|polypropylene]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Titanium dioxide",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical compound",
		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Inorganic compounds" },
		aliases = { "Titanium(IV) oxide", "Titania" },
		summary = "**Titanium dioxide**, also known as **titanium(IV) oxide** or **titania**, is the [[Inorganic compound|inorganic compound]] derived from [[Titanium|titanium]] with the chemical formula TiO2. When used as a [[Pigment|pigment]], it is called **titanium white**, **Pigment White 6** (**PW6**), or **CI 77891**. It is a white solid that is insoluble in water, although mineral forms can appear black. As a pigment, it has a wide range of applications, including [[Paint|paint]], sunscreen, and food coloring. When used as a food coloring, it has E number E171. World production in 2014 exceeded 9 million tonnes. It has been estimated that titanium dioxide is used in two-thirds of all pigments, and pigments based on the oxide have been valued at a price of $13.2 billion.",
	},
	{
		title = "Tornado",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌪️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Violently rotating column of air",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Storms" },
		aliases = { "Twister" },
		summary = "A **tornado**, also known as a **twister**, is a rapidly rotating column of [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]] that extends vertically from the surface of the [[Earth]] to the base of a [[Cumulonimbus cloud|cumulonimbus]] or [[Cumulus cloud|cumulus cloud]]. Tornadoes are often (but not always) visible in the form of a condensation funnel originating from the cloud base, with a cloud of rotating debris and dust close to the ground. Most tornadoes have wind speeds less than 110 mph, are about 250 ft across, and travel several kilometers (a few miles) before dissipating. The most extreme tornadoes can attain wind speeds of more than 300 mph, can be more than 2 mi in diameter, and can stay on the ground for more than 100 km.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Types of tornadoes include the multiple-vortex tornado, landspout, and waterspout. Waterspouts are characterized by a spiraling funnel-shaped wind current, connecting to a large cumulus or cumulonimbus cloud. They are generally classified as non-supercellular tornadoes that develop over bodies of water, but there is disagreement about whether to classify them as true tornadoes. These spiraling columns of air often develop in tropical areas close to the [[Equator|equator]], and are less common at high latitudes. Similar phenomena in nature include the gustnado, dust devil, fire whirl, and steam devil. Tornadoes most commonly occur in North America, particularly in central and southeastern regions of the United States colloquially known as Tornado Alley; the United States has by far the most tornadoes of any country in the world.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Torque",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏋️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Turning force around an axis",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Classical mechanics" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]] and [[Mechanics|mechanics]], **torque** is the [[Rotation|rotational]] correspondent of linear [[Force|force]]. It is also referred to as the **moment of force**, or simply the **moment**. Just as a linear force is a push or a pull applied to a body, a torque can be thought of as a twist applied to an object with respect to a chosen axis. For example, when driving a [[Screw|screw]], a screwdriver applies torque to the screw, causing it to tend to rotate around its axis.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Torque is generally referred to using different vocabulary depending on geographical location and field of study, with torque generally being associated with physics and moment being associated with engineering. This article follows the definition used in US physics in its usage of the word torque. Torque is typically represented mathematically using the lowercase [[Greek alphabet|Greek letter]] tau. When being referred to as moment of force, it is commonly denoted by M.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Dimension", "wikidata" },
