	{
		title = "Trade winds",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Equatorial east-to-west prevailing winds",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Winds" },
		aliases = { "Easterlies" },
		summary = "The **trade winds**, or **easterlies**, are east-to-west prevailing winds that flow in Earth's equatorial region. The trade winds blow mainly from the northeast in the [[Northern Hemisphere]] and from the southeast in the [[Southern Hemisphere]], strengthening during the winter and when the Arctic oscillation is in its warm phase. Trade winds have been used by captains of [[Sailing ship|sailing ships]] to cross the world's oceans for centuries. They enabled [[European colonization of the Americas]], and trade routes to become established across the [[Atlantic Ocean]] and the [[Pacific Ocean]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In [[Meteorology|meteorology]], they act as the steering flow for tropical storms that form over the Atlantic, Pacific, and southern [[Indian Ocean|Indian]] oceans and cause rainfall in [[East Africa]], [[Madagascar]], [[North America]], and [[Southeast Asia]]. Shallow cumulus clouds are seen within trade wind regimes and are capped from becoming taller by a trade wind inversion, which is caused by descending air aloft from within the subtropical ridge. The weaker the trade winds become, the more rainfall can be expected in the neighboring landmasses. The trade winds also transport nitrate- and phosphate-rich Saharan dust to all [[Latin America]], the [[Caribbean Sea]], and to parts of southeastern and southwestern North America. Sahara dust is on occasion present in sunsets across [[Florida]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Transcription (biology)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Process of copying a segment of DNA into RNA",
		tags = { "Cell biology", "Cell processes" },
		aliases = { "Transcription" },
		summary = "**Transcription** is the process of duplicating a segment of [[DNA]] into [[RNA]] for the purpose of [[Gene expression|gene expression]]. Some segments of DNA are transcribed into RNA molecules that can encode [[Protein|proteins]], called messenger RNA (mRNA). Other segments of DNA are transcribed into RNA molecules called non-coding RNAs (ncRNAs).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Both DNA and RNA are [[Nucleic acid|nucleic acids]], composed of nucleotide sequences. During transcription, a DNA sequence is read by an RNA polymerase, which produces a complementary RNA strand called a primary transcript. In virology, the term transcription is used when referring to mRNA synthesis from a viral RNA molecule. The [[Genome|genome]] of many RNA viruses is composed of negative-sense RNA which acts as a template for positive sense viral messenger RNA - a necessary step in the synthesis of viral proteins needed for viral replication. This process is catalyzed by a viral RNA dependent RNA polymerase.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Transition metal",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Series of chemical elements",
		tags = { "Chemical elements", "Chemical element groups" },
		aliases = { "Transition element" },
		summary = "In chemistry, a **transition metal** (or **transition element**) is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]] in the d-block of the [[Periodic table|periodic table]] (groups 3 to 12), though the elements of group 12 (and less often group 3) are sometimes excluded. The [[Lanthanide|lanthanide]] and [[Actinide|actinide]] elements (the f-block) are called **inner transition metals** and are sometimes considered to be transition metals as well.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"They are lustrous metals with good [[Electrical resistivity and conductivity|electrical]] and [[Thermal conductivity and resistivity|thermal]] conductivity. Most (with the exception of group 11 and group 12) are hard and strong, and have high melting and boiling temperatures. They form compounds in any of two or more different [[Oxidation state|oxidation states]] and bind to a variety of ligands to form coordination complexes that are often coloured. They form many useful [[Alloy|alloys]] and are often employed as [[Catalysis|catalysts]] in elemental form or in compounds such as coordination complexes and oxides. Most are strongly paramagnetic because of their unpaired d electrons, as are many of their compounds. All of the elements that are [[Ferromagnetism|ferromagnetic]] near room temperature are transition metals ([[Iron|iron]], [[Cobalt|cobalt]] and [[Nickel|nickel]]) or inner transition metals ([[Gadolinium|gadolinium]]).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Translation (biology)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🧬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Cellular process of protein synthesis",
		tags = { "Cell biology", "Cell processes" },
		summary = "**Translation** is the process in [[Cell (biology)|biological cells]] in which [[Protein|proteins]] are produced using [[RNA]] molecules as templates. The generated protein is a sequence of [[Amino acid|amino acids]] determined by the sequence of nucleotides in the RNA. The nucleotides are considered three at a time. Each such triple results in the addition of one specific amino acid to the protein being generated. The matching from nucleotide triple to amino acid is called the [[Genetic code|genetic code]]. The translation is performed by a large complex of functional RNA and proteins called [[Ribosome|ribosomes]]. The entire process is called [[Gene expression|gene expression]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In translation, messenger RNA (mRNA) is decoded in a ribosome, outside the nucleus, to produce a specific [[Amino acid|amino acid]] chain, or polypeptide. The polypeptide later folds into an active protein and performs its functions in the cell. The polypeptide can also start folding during protein synthesis. The ribosome facilitates decoding by inducing the binding of complementary transfer RNA (tRNA) anticodon sequences to mRNA codons. The tRNAs carry specific amino acids that are chained together into a polypeptide as the mRNA passes through and is "read" by the ribosome. The three stages of translation are initiation, elongation, and termination.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Transparency and translucency",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Physical property of allowing light",
		tags = { "Atomic, molecular and optical physics", "Optics" },
		aliases = { "Transparency", "Pellucidity", "Diaphaneity" },
		summary = "In the field of [[Optics|optics]], **transparency** (also called **pellucidity** or **diaphaneity**) is the [[Physical property|physical property]] of allowing [[Light|light]] to pass through the material without appreciable scattering of light. On a macroscopic scale (one in which the dimensions are much larger than the wavelengths of the [[Photon|photons]] in question), the photons can be said to follow Snell's law. **Translucency** (also called **translucence** or **translucidity**) is the physical property of allowing light to pass through the material (with or without scattering of light).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It allows light to pass through but the light does not necessarily follow Snell's law on the macroscopic scale; the photons may be scattered at either of the two interfaces, or internally, where there is a change in the index of [[Refraction|refraction]]. In other words, a translucent material is made up of components with different indices of refraction, while a transparent material is made up of components with a uniform index of refraction. Transparent materials appear clear, with the overall appearance of one color, or any combination leading up to a brilliant [[Spectrum|spectrum]] of every color. The opposite property of translucency is [[Opacity|opacity]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Transpiration",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Process of water movement through a plant",
		tags = { "Biological processes and physiology", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Transpiration** is the process of [[Water|water]] movement through a [[Plant|plant]] and its [[Evaporation|evaporation]] from aerial parts, such as [[Leaf|leaves]], [[Plant stem|stems]] and [[Flower|flowers]]. It is a passive process that requires no energy expense by the plant. Transpiration also cools plants, changes osmotic pressure of cells, and enables mass flow of [[Plant nutrition|mineral nutrients]]. When water uptake by the roots is less than the water lost to the [[Atmosphere of Earth|atmosphere]] by evaporation, plants close small pores called stomata to decrease water loss, which slows down nutrient uptake and decreases CO2 absorption from the atmosphere limiting metabolic processes, [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]], and growth.",
	},
	{
		title = "Triassic",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "First period of the Mesozoic Era",
		tags = { "Geological history", "Geological periods" },
		summary = "The **Triassic** ( try-A-sik) is a geologic period and a stratigraphic system that spans 50.5 million years from the end of the [[Permian]] Period 251.902 Ma (million years ago) to the beginning of the [[Jurassic]] Period 201.4 Ma. The Triassic Period is the first and shortest geologic period of the [[Mesozoic|Mesozoic Era]], and the seventh period of the [[Phanerozoic|Phanerozoic Eon]]. The start and the end of the Triassic Period featured major [[Extinction event|extinction events]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Chronologically, the Triassic Period is divided into three epochs: (i) the Early Triassic, (ii) the Middle Triassic, and (iii) the [[Late Triassic]]. The Triassic Period began after the [[Permian–Triassic extinction event]] that much reduced the [[Biosphere|biosphere]] of planet Earth. The fossil record of the Triassic Period presents three categories of organisms: (i) animals that survived the Permian–Triassic extinction event, (ii) new animals that briefly flourished in the Triassic biosphere, and (iii) new animals that evolved and dominated the [[Mesozoic]] Era. [[Reptile|Reptiles]], especially [[Archosaur|archosaurs]], were the chief terrestrial vertebrates during this time. A specialized group of archosaurs, called [[Dinosaur|dinosaurs]], first appeared in the Late Triassic but did not become dominant until the succeeding Jurassic Period.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Colour", "Triassic" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Triassic–Jurassic extinction",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mass extinction ending the Triassic period",
		tags = { "Earth", "Geological history" },
		aliases = { "Triassic–Jurassic", "Tr-J", "Triassic–Jurassic mass extinction" },
		summary = "The **Triassic–Jurassic** (**Tr-J**) **extinction event**, often called the **Triassic–Jurassic mass extinction** (**TJME**) or **end-Triassic extinction**, marks the boundary between the [[Triassic]] and [[Jurassic]] periods,. It represents one of five major [[Extinction event|extinction events]] during the [[Phanerozoic]], profoundly affecting life on land and in the oceans.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In the seas, about 23–34% of marine [[Genus|genera]] disappeared; [[Coral reef|corals]], [[Bivalvia|bivalves]], brachiopods, [[Bryozoa|bryozoans]], and [[Radiolaria|radiolarians]] suffered severe losses of diversity and [[Conodont|conodonts]] were completely wiped out, while marine vertebrates, gastropods, and benthic [[Foraminifera|foraminifera]] were relatively unaffected. On land, all archosauromorph reptiles other than crocodylomorphs, [[Dinosaur|dinosaurs]], and [[Pterosaur|pterosaurs]] became extinct. Crocodylomorphs, dinosaurs, pterosaurs, and mammals were left largely untouched, allowing them to become the dominant land animals for the next 135 million years. Plants were likewise significantly affected by the crisis, with floral communities undergoing radical ecological restructuring across the extinction event. Older hypotheses have proposed that gradual changes in climate and sea levels may have been the cause, or perhaps one or more asteroid strikes.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event", "Permian–Triassic extinction event" },
	},
	{
		title = "Triple point",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Thermodynamic point where three matter phases exist",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "Phase transitions" },
		summary = "In [[Thermodynamics|thermodynamics]], the **triple point** of a substance is the [[Temperature|temperature]] and [[Pressure|pressure]] at which the three [[Phase (matter)|phases]] ([[Gas|gas]], [[Liquid|liquid]], and [[Solid|solid]]) of that substance coexist in [[Thermodynamic equilibrium|thermodynamic equilibrium]]. It is that temperature and pressure at which the [[Sublimation|sublimation]], [[Melting|fusion]], and vapourisation curves meet. For example, the triple point of [[Mercury (element)|mercury]] occurs at a temperature of −38.8 °C and a pressure of 0.165 m[[Pascal (unit)|Pa]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In addition to the triple point for the solid, liquid, and gas phases, a triple point may involve more than one solid phase in substances with multiple polymorphs. Helium-4 is unusual in that it has no sublimation/deposition curve and therefore no triple points where its solid phase meets its gas phase. Instead, it has a vapor-liquid-superfluid point, a solid-liquid-superfluid point, a solid-solid-liquid point, and a solid-solid-superfluid point. None of these should be confused with the lambda point, which is not any kind of triple point. The first mention of the term "triple point" was on August 3, 1871, by James Thomson, brother of [[Lord Kelvin]]. The triple points of several substances are used to define points in the ITS-90 international temperature scale, ranging from the triple point of hydrogen (13.8033 K) to the triple point of water (273.16 K, 0.01 °C, or 32.018 °F).',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tropical climate",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Major climate group in Köppen classification",
		tags = { "Air", "Climatology" },
		summary = "**Tropical climate** is the first of the five major climate groups in the [[Köppen climate classification]] identified with the letter **A**. Tropical climates are defined by a monthly average temperature of 27 °C or higher in the coolest month, featuring hot temperatures and high humidity all year-round. Annual precipitation is often abundant in tropical climates, and shows a seasonal rhythm but may have seasonal dryness to varying degrees. There are normally only two seasons in tropical climates, a wet (rainy/[[Monsoon|monsoon]]) season and a dry season. The annual temperature range in tropical climates is normally very small. Sunlight is intense in these climates.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are three basic types of tropical climates within the tropical climate group: [[Tropical rainforest climate|tropical rainforest climate]] (**Af**), [[Tropical monsoon climate|tropical monsoon climate]] (**Am**) and [[Tropical savanna climate|tropical savanna or tropical wet and dry climate]] (**Aw** for dry winters, and **As** for dry summers), which are classified and distinguished by the precipitation levels of the driest month in those regions.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tropical rainforest",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌴",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Tropical broadleaf forest with high rainfall",
		tags = { "Biomes", "Forests" },
		aliases = { "Tropical rainforests" },
		summary = "**Tropical rainforests** are dense and warm [[Rainforest|rainforests]] with high rainfall typically found between 10° north and south of the [[Equator]]. They are a subset of the [[Tropical forest|tropical forest]] [[Biome|biome]] that occurs roughly within the 28° latitudes (in the torrid zone between the Tropic of Cancer and [[Tropic of Capricorn]]). Tropical rainforests are a type of tropical moist broadleaf forest, that includes the more extensive seasonal tropical forests. True rainforests usually occur in [[Tropical rainforest climate|tropical rainforest climates]] where no [[Dry season|dry season]] occurs; all months have an average [[Precipitation|precipitation]] of at least 60 mm. Seasonal tropical forests with [[Tropical monsoon climate|tropical monsoon]] or [[Tropical savanna climate|savanna]] climates are sometimes included in the broader definition.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Tropical rainforests ecosystems are distinguished by their consistent, high temperatures, exceeding 18 °C monthly, and substantial annual rainfall. The abundant rainfall results in nutrient-poor, leached soils, which profoundly affect the flora and fauna adapted to these conditions. These rainforests are renowned for their significant [[Biodiversity|biodiversity]]. They are home to 40–75% of all species globally, including half of the world's animal and plant species, and two-thirds of all flowering plant species. Their dense [[Insect|insect]] population and variety of trees and higher plants are notable. Described as the \"world's largest pharmacy\", over a quarter of natural medicines have been discovered in them. However, tropical rainforests are threatened by human activities, such as logging and agricultural expansion, leading to [[Habitat fragmentation|habitat fragmentation]] and [[Habitat destruction|loss]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Temperate broadleaf and mixed forests" },
