	{
		title = "Tundra",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌡️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Biome with frigid temperatures",
		tags = { "Earth", "Biomes" },
		summary = "In [[Physical geography|physical geography]], a **tundra** is a type of [[Biome|biome]] where tree growth is hindered by frigid temperatures and short growing seasons. There are three regions and associated types of tundra: Arctic, Alpine, and Antarctic.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Tundra vegetation is composed of dwarf [[Shrub|shrubs]], [[Cyperaceae|sedges]], [[Poaceae|grasses]], [[Moss|mosses]], and [[Lichen|lichens]]. Scattered trees grow in some tundra regions. The ecotone (or ecological boundary region) between the tundra and the forest is known as the tree line or timberline. The tundra soil is rich in [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]] and [[Phosphorus|phosphorus]]. The soil also contains large amounts of biomass and decomposed biomass that has been stored as [[Methane|methane]] and carbon dioxide in the [[Permafrost|permafrost]], making the tundra soil a [[Carbon sink|carbon sink]]. As global warming heats the ecosystem and causes soil thawing, the permafrost carbon cycle accelerates and releases much of these soil-contained greenhouse gases into the atmosphere, creating a feedback cycle that contributes to [[Climate change|climate change]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Area", "11,563,300" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Tungsten",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Tungsten** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **W** (from ) and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 74. It is a metal found naturally on [[Earth]] almost exclusively in [[Chemical compound|compounds]] with other elements. It was identified as a distinct element in 1781 and first isolated as a metal in 1783. Its important [[Ore|ores]] include scheelite and wolframite, the latter lending the element its alternative name.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The [[Free element|free element]] is remarkable for its robustness, especially the fact that it has the highest [[Melting point|melting point]] of all known elements, at 3422 °C. It also has the highest [[Boiling point|boiling point]], at 5930 °C. Its density is 19.254 g/cm3, comparable with that of [[Uranium|uranium]] and [[Gold|gold]], and much higher (about 1.7 times) than that of [[Lead|lead]]. Polycrystalline tungsten is an intrinsically brittle and [[Hardness|hard]] material (under standard conditions, when uncombined), making it difficult to [[Metalworking|work into metal]]. However, pure single-crystalline tungsten is more [[Ductility|ductile]] and can be cut with a hard-steel hacksaw. Tungsten occurs in many alloys, which have numerous applications, including incandescent [[Light bulb|light bulb]] filaments, X-ray tubes, electrodes in gas tungsten arc welding, superalloys, and radiation shielding. Tungsten's hardness and high [[Density|density]] make it suitable for military applications in penetrating projectiles.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Berkelium" },
