		emoji = "🏞️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Low area between hills, often with a river running through it",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Landforms" },
		summary = "A **valley** is an elongated low area often running between [[Hill|hills]] or [[Mountain|mountains]] and typically containing a [[River|river]] or [[Stream|stream]] running from one end to the other. Most valleys are formed by [[Erosion|erosion]] of the land surface by rivers or streams over a very long period. Some valleys are formed through erosion by [[Glacier|glacial ice]]. These glaciers may remain present in valleys in high mountains or polar areas.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"At lower latitudes and altitudes, glacially formed valleys may have been created or enlarged during [[Ice age|ice ages]] but now are ice-free and occupied by streams or rivers. In [[Desert|desert]] areas, valleys may be entirely dry or carry a watercourse only rarely. In [[Karst|areas of limestone bedrock]], dry valleys may result from drainage now taking place [[Cave|underground]] rather than at the surface. Rift valleys arise principally from [[Tectonics|earth movements]], rather than erosion. Many different types of valleys are described by geographers, using terms that may be global in use or else applied only locally.",
