	{
		title = "Vanadium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Vanadium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **V** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 23. It is a hard, silvery-grey, [[Ductility|malleable]] [[Transition metal|transition metal]]. The elemental metal is rarely found in nature, but once isolated artificially, the formation of an [[Oxide|oxide]] layer ([[Passivation (chemistry)|passivation]]) somewhat stabilizes the free metal against further [[Redox|oxidation]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'[[Spain|Spanish]] scientist Andrés Manuel del Río discovered compounds of vanadium in 1801 by analyzing a new [[Lead|lead]]-bearing mineral he called "brown lead". Though he initially presumed its qualities were due to the presence of a new element, he was later erroneously convinced by French chemist Hippolyte Victor Collet-Descotils that the element was just [[Chromium|chromium]]. Then in 1830, Nils Gabriel Sefström generated chlorides of vanadium, thus proving there was a new element, and named it "vanadium" after the Scandinavian goddess of beauty and fertility, Vanadís (Freyja). The name was based on the wide range of colors found in vanadium compounds. Del Río\'s lead mineral was ultimately named vanadinite for its vanadium content. In 1867, Henry Enfield Roscoe obtained the pure element. Vanadium occurs naturally in about 65 [[Mineral|minerals]] and [[Fossil fuel|fossil fuel]] deposits.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Vapor",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Substances in the gas phase at a temperature lower than its critical point",
		tags = { "Condensed matter physics", "States of matter" },
		aliases = { "Vapour" },
		summary = "In physics, a **vapor** ([[American English]]) or **vapour** ([[English in the Commonwealth of Nations|Commonwealth English]]; [[American and British English spelling differences|see spelling differences]]) is a substance in the [[Gas|gas]] phase at a [[Temperature|temperature]] lower than its critical temperature, which means that the vapor can be [[Condensation|condensed]] to a [[Liquid|liquid]] by increasing the [[Pressure|pressure]] on it without reducing the temperature of the vapor. A vapor is different from an [[Aerosol|aerosol]]. An aerosol is a suspension of tiny particles of liquid, solid, or both within a gas.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"For example, water has a critical temperature of 647 K, which is the highest temperature at which liquid water can exist at any pressure. In the [[Atmosphere of Earth|atmosphere]] at ordinary temperatures gaseous water (known as [[Water vapor|water vapor]]) will condense into a liquid if its partial pressure is increased sufficiently. A vapor may coexist with a liquid (or a solid). When this is true, the two phases will be in equilibrium, and the gas-partial pressure will be equal to the equilibrium vapor pressure of the liquid (or solid).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Vector (mathematics and physics)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Broad concept generalizing scalars in mathematics and physics",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = 'In [[Mathematics|mathematics]] and [[Physics|physics]], a **vector** is a generalization of a single [[Number|number]]. It may denote a [[Vector quantity|vector quantity]], i.e., [[Physical quantity|physical quantity]] that cannot be expressed by a single [[Scalar (physics)|scalar quantity]]. The term may also be used to refer to elements of [[Vector space|vector spaces]], that can be added together and multiplied ("scaled") by [[Scalar (mathematics)|scalars]]. In some contexts, vectors are tuples, which are finite sequences (of numbers or other objects) of a fixed length.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Historically, vectors were introduced in [[Geometry|geometry]] and physics (typically in [[Mechanics|mechanics]]) for quantities that have both a magnitude and a direction, such as displacements, [[Force|forces]] and [[Velocity|velocity]]. Such quantities are represented by geometric vectors in the same way as [[Distance|distances]], [[Mass|masses]] and [[Time|time]] are represented by [[Real number|real numbers]]. Both geometric vectors and tuples can be added and scaled, and these vector operations led to the concept of a vector space, which is a [[Set (mathematics)|set]] equipped with a vector addition and a scalar multiplication that satisfy some [[Axiom|axioms]] generalizing the main properties of operations on the above sorts of vectors. A vector space formed by geometric vectors is called a Euclidean vector space, and a vector space formed by tuples is called a coordinate vector space. Many vector spaces are considered in mathematics, such as extension fields, [[Polynomial ring|polynomial rings]], algebras and [[Function space|function spaces]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Velocity",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🏃",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Speed and direction of a motion",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Classical mechanics" },
		summary = '**Velocity** is a measurement of [[Speed|speed]] in a certain direction of [[Motion|motion]]. It is a fundamental concept in [[Kinematics|kinematics]], the branch of [[Classical mechanics|classical mechanics]] that describes the motion of [[Physical object|physical objects]]. Velocity is a [[Vector quantity|vector quantity]], meaning that both magnitude and [[Direction (geometry)|direction]] are needed to define it (**velocity vector**). The [[Scalar (physics)|scalar]] absolute value ([[Magnitude (mathematics)|magnitude]]) of velocity is called speed, a quantity that is measured in metres per second (m/s or m⋅s−1) in the [[International System of Units|SI]] (International System of Units) system. For example, "5 metres per second" is a scalar, whereas "5 metres per second east" is a vector. If there is a change in speed, direction or both, then the object is said to be undergoing an [[Acceleration|acceleration]].',
		infobox = {
			{ "Dimension", "L T−1" },
		},
