	{
		title = "Voltage",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Difference in electric potential between two points in space",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrostatics" },
		aliases = { "Electrical", "Potential difference", "Electric pressure" },
		summary = "**Voltage**, also known as (**electrical**) **potential difference**, **electric pressure**, or **electric tension**, is the difference in [[Electric potential|electric potential]] between two points. In a [[Electrostatics|static]] [[Electric field|electric field]], it corresponds to the work needed per unit of [[Electric charge|charge]] to move a positive test charge from the first point to the second point. In the [[International System of Units]] (SI), the [[SI derived unit|derived unit]] for voltage is the [[Volt|volt]] (V).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The voltage between points can be caused by the build-up of [[Electric charge|electric charge]] (e.g., a [[Capacitor|capacitor]]), and from an [[Electromotive force|electromotive force]] (e.g., [[Electromagnetic induction|electromagnetic induction]] in a [[Electric generator|generator]]). On a macroscopic scale, a potential difference can be caused by electrochemical processes (e.g., cells and batteries), the pressure-induced piezoelectric effect, photovoltaic effect, and the thermoelectric effect. Since voltage is the difference in electric potential, it is a physical [[Scalar (physics)|scalar]] [[Quantity|quantity]]. A voltmeter can be used to measure the voltage between two points in a system. Often a common reference potential such as the ground of the system is used as one of the points. In this case, voltage is often mentioned at a point without completely mentioning the other measurement point. A voltage can be associated with either a source of energy or the loss, dissipation, or storage of energy.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Walker circulation",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Theorerical model of air flow in the atmosphere",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Atmospheric circulation" },
		aliases = { "Walker cell" },
		summary = "The **Walker circulation**, also known as the **Walker cell**, is a conceptual model of the air flow in the [[Tropics|tropics]] in the lower atmosphere ([[Troposphere|troposphere]]). According to this model, parcels of air follow a closed circulation in the zonal and vertical directions. This circulation, which is roughly consistent with observations, is caused by differences in heat distribution between ocean and land. In addition to motions in the zonal and vertical direction the tropical atmosphere also has considerable motion in the meridional direction as part of, for example, the Hadley Circulation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Walker circulation is associated with the pressure gradient force that results from a [[High-pressure area|high pressure system]] over the eastern Pacific Ocean, and a low pressure system over [[Indonesia]]. The Walker circulations of the tropical Indian, Pacific, and Atlantic basins result in westerly surface winds in northern summer in the first basin and easterly winds in the second and third basins. As a result, the temperature structure of the three oceans display dramatic asymmetries. The equatorial Pacific and Atlantic both have cool surface temperatures in northern summer in the east, while cooler surface temperatures prevail only in the western Indian Ocean. These changes in surface temperature reflect changes in the depth of the thermocline. Changes in the Walker circulation with time occur in conjunction with changes in surface temperature.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Water cycle",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Biogeochemical cycle for movement of water on Earth",
		tags = { "Water", "Hydrology" },
		aliases = { "Hydrologic cycle", "Hydrological cycle" },
		summary = "The **water cycle** (or **hydrologic cycle** or **hydrological cycle**) is a [[Biogeochemical cycle|biogeochemical cycle]] that involves the continuous change in form of [[Water|water]] on, above and below the surface of the [[Earth]] across different reservoirs. The mass of water on Earth remains fairly constant over time. However, the partitioning of the water into the major reservoirs of [[Ice|ice]], [[Fresh water|fresh water]], [[Saline water|salt water]] and [[Atmosphere|atmospheric water]] is variable and depends on [[Climate|climatic variables]]. The water moves from one reservoir to another, such as from river to [[Ocean|ocean]], or from the ocean to the atmosphere due to a variety of physical and chemical processes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The processes that drive these movements, or fluxes, are [[Evaporation|evaporation]], [[Transpiration|transpiration]], [[Condensation|condensation]], [[Precipitation|precipitation]], [[Sublimation|sublimation]], infiltration, [[Surface runoff|surface runoff]], and subsurface flow. In doing so, the water goes through different phases: liquid, solid ([[Ice|ice]]) and [[Water vapor|vapor]]. The ocean plays a key role in the water cycle as it is the source of 86% of global evaporation. The water cycle is driven by energy exchanges in the form of heat transfers between different phases. The energy released or absorbed during a phase change can result in [[Temperature|temperature]] changes. Heat is absorbed as water transitions from the liquid to the vapor phase through evaporation. This heat is also known as the latent heat of vaporization. Conversely, when water condenses or melts from solid ice it releases energy and heat.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Water vapor",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Gaseous phase of water",
		tags = { "Chemical compounds", "Inorganic compounds" },
		aliases = { "Water vapour", "Aqueous vapor" },
		summary = "**Water vapor**, **water vapour**, or **aqueous vapor** is the [[Gas|gaseous]] phase of [[Properties of water|water]]. It is one [[Phase (matter)|state]] of water within the [[Hydrosphere|hydrosphere]]. Water [[Vapor|vapor]] can be produced from the [[Evaporation|evaporation]] or [[Boiling|boiling]] of liquid water or from the [[Sublimation|sublimation]] of [[Ice|ice]]. Water vapor is transparent, like most constituents of the atmosphere. Under typical atmospheric conditions, water vapor is continuously generated by evaporation and removed by [[Condensation|condensation]]. It is less dense than most of the other constituents of [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]] and triggers [[Convection|convection]] currents that can lead to clouds and fog.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Being a component of Earth's hydrosphere and hydrologic cycle, it is particularly abundant in [[Atmosphere of Earth|Earth's atmosphere]], where it acts as a [[Greenhouse gas|greenhouse gas]] and warming feedback, contributing more to total greenhouse effect than non-condensable gases such as carbon dioxide and [[Methane|methane]]. Use of water vapor, as [[Steam|steam]], has been important for cooking, and as a major component in energy production and transport systems since the [[Industrial Revolution]]. Water vapor is a relatively common atmospheric constituent, present even in the solar atmosphere as well as every planet in the [[Solar System]] and many [[Astronomical object|astronomical objects]] including [[Natural satellite|natural satellites]], [[Comet|comets]] and even large [[Asteroid|asteroids]]. Likewise the detection of extrasolar water vapor would indicate a similar distribution in other planetary systems.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Organosilicon chemistry" },
	},
	{
		title = "Waterfall",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💦",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Point in a river or brook where water flows over a vertical drop",
		tags = { "Geomorphology", "Fluvial landforms" },
		summary = "A **waterfall** is any point in a [[River|river]] or [[Stream|stream]] where [[Water|water]] flows over a vertical drop or a series of steep drops. Waterfalls also occur where meltwater drops over the edge of a tabular [[Iceberg|iceberg]] or [[Ice shelf|ice shelf]]. Waterfalls can be formed in several ways, but the most common method of formation is that a river courses over a top layer of resistant [[Bedrock|bedrock]] before falling onto softer rock, which [[Erosion|erodes]] faster, leading to an increasingly high fall. Waterfalls have been studied for their impact on [[Species|species]] living in and around them.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Humans have had a distinct relationship with waterfalls since prehistory, travelling to see them, exploring and naming them. They can present formidable barriers to navigation along rivers. Waterfalls are religious sites in many cultures. Since the 18th century, they have received increased attention as tourist destinations, sources of [[Hydropower|hydropower]], and—particularly since the mid-20th century—as subjects of research.",
			},
		},
