	{
		title = "Wave",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "〰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Dynamic disturbance in a medium or field",
		tags = { "Physics", "Waves" },
		summary = "In [[Mathematics|mathematics]] and physical science, a **wave** is a propagating dynamic disturbance (change from equilibrium) of one or more quantities. Periodic waves oscillate repeatedly about an equilibrium (resting) value at some [[Frequency|frequency]]. When the entire [[Waveform|waveform]] moves in one direction, it is said to be a **traveling wave**; by contrast, a pair of identical [[Superposition principle|superimposed]] periodic waves traveling in opposite directions makes a [[Standing wave|standing wave]]. In a standing wave, the amplitude of vibration has nulls at some positions where the wave amplitude appears smaller or even zero.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"There are two types of waves that are most commonly studied in [[Classical physics|classical physics]]: mechanical waves and [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic waves]]. In a mechanical wave, [[Stress (mechanics)|stress]] and strain fields oscillate about a [[Mechanical equilibrium|mechanical equilibrium]]. A mechanical wave is a local deformation (strain) in some physical medium that propagates from particle to particle by creating local stresses that cause strain in neighboring particles too. For example, [[Sound|sound]] waves are variations of the local pressure and particle motion that propagate through the medium. Other examples of mechanical waves are seismic waves, gravity waves, surface waves and string vibrations. In an electromagnetic wave (such as light), coupling between the electric and magnetic fields sustains propagation of waves involving these fields according to [[Maxwell's equations]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
