	{
		title = "Wave function",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📈",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Mathematical description of quantum state",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Quantum mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Wavefunction" },
		summary = "In [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]], a **wave function** (or **wavefunction**) is a mathematical description of the quantum state of an isolated quantum system. The most common symbols for a wave function are the Greek letters and (lower-case and capital psi, respectively).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'According to the [[Superposition principle|superposition principle]] of quantum mechanics, wave functions can be added together and multiplied by complex numbers to form new wave functions and form a [[Hilbert space]]. The inner product of two wave functions is a measure of the overlap between the corresponding physical states and is used in the foundational probabilistic interpretation of quantum mechanics, the Born rule, relating transition probabilities to inner products. The [[Schrödinger equation]] determines how wave functions evolve over time, and a wave function behaves qualitatively like other [[Wave|waves]], such as water waves or waves on a string, because the Schrödinger equation is mathematically a type of [[Wave equation|wave equation]]. This explains the name "wave function", and gives rise to [[Wave–particle duality|wave–particle duality]].',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Wave interference",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "〰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Phenomenon resulting from the superposition of two waves",
		tags = { "Physics", "Waves" },
		aliases = { "Interference" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]], **interference** is a phenomenon in which two coherent [[Wave|waves]] are combined by adding their intensities or displacements with due consideration for their phase difference. The resultant wave may have greater amplitude (**constructive interference**) or lower amplitude (**destructive interference**) if the two waves are in phase or out of phase, respectively. Interference effects can be observed with all types of waves, for example, light, [[Radio wave|radio]], [[Sound|acoustic]], surface water waves, gravity waves, or [[Matter wave|matter waves]] as well as in loudspeakers as electrical waves.",
	},
	{
		title = "Wavelength",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "〰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Distance over which a wave's shape repeats",
		tags = { "Physics", "Waves" },
		aliases = { "Spatial period" },
		summary = "In [[Physics|physics]] and [[Mathematics|mathematics]], **wavelength** or **spatial period** of a [[Wave|wave]] or [[Periodic function|periodic function]] is the distance over which the wave's shape repeats. In other words, it is the distance between consecutive corresponding points of the same phase on the wave, such as two adjacent crests, troughs, or zero crossings. Wavelength is a characteristic of both traveling waves and [[Standing wave|standing waves]], as well as other spatial wave patterns. The [[Multiplicative inverse|inverse]] of the wavelength is called the spatial frequency. Wavelength is commonly designated by the Greek letter lambda. For a modulated wave, wavelength may refer to the carrier wavelength of the signal.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term wavelength may also apply to the repeating envelope of modulated waves or waves formed by interference of several sinusoids. Assuming a sinusoidal wave moving at a fixed wave speed, wavelength is inversely proportional to the [[Frequency|frequency]] of the wave: waves with higher frequencies have shorter wavelengths, and lower frequencies have longer wavelengths. Wavelength depends on the medium (for example, vacuum, air, or water) that a wave travels through. Examples of waves are [[Sound|sound waves]], [[Light|light]], water waves, and periodic electrical signals in a [[Electrical conductor|conductor]]. A [[Sound|sound]] wave is a variation in air pressure, while in [[Light|light]] and other [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]] the strength of the [[Electric field|electric]] and the [[Magnetic field|magnetic field]] vary. Water waves are variations in the height of a body of water. In a crystal lattice vibration, atomic positions vary.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Wave–particle duality",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "〰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Concept in quantum mechanics",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Quantum mechanics" },
		summary = "**Wave–particle duality** is the concept in [[Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]] that fundamental entities of the universe, like [[Photon|photons]] and [[Electron|electrons]], exhibit [[Particle|particle]] or wave properties according to the experimental circumstances. It expresses the inability of the [[Classical physics|classical]] concepts such as particle or wave to fully describe the behavior of quantum objects. During the 19th and early 20th centuries, [[Light|light]] was found to behave as a wave, then later was discovered to have a particle-like behavior, whereas electrons behaved like particles in early experiments, then later were discovered to have wave-like behavior. The concept of duality arose to name these seeming contradictions.",
