		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Yearly period of high rainfall",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Seasons" },
		aliases = { "Rainy season", "Monsoon season" },
		summary = "The** wet season** (sometimes called the **rainy season** or **monsoon season**) is the time of year when most of a region's average annual rainfall occurs. Generally, the season lasts at least one month. The term **green season** is also sometimes used as a [[Euphemism|euphemism]] by tourist authorities. Areas with wet seasons are dispersed across portions of the [[Tropics|tropics]] and [[Subtropics|subtropics]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Under the [[Köppen climate classification]], for [[Tropical climate|tropical climates]], a wet season month is defined as a month where average precipitation is 60 mm or more. In contrast to areas with [[Savanna|savanna]] climates and [[Monsoon|monsoon]] regimes, [[Mediterranean climate|Mediterranean climates]] have wet winters and dry summers. Dry and rainy months are characteristic of tropical seasonal forests: in contrast to [[Tropical rainforest|tropical rainforests]], which do not have dry or wet seasons, since their rainfall is equally distributed throughout the year. Some areas with pronounced rainy seasons will see a break in rainfall mid-season, when the Intertropical Convergence Zone or monsoon trough moves to higher latitudes in the middle of the warm season. When the wet season occurs during a warm season, or [[Summer|summer]], [[Precipitation|precipitation]] falls mainly during the late afternoon and early evening.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Wetland",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ecosystem that is flooded or saturated with water",
		tags = { "Biomes", "Wetlands" },
		summary = "A **wetland** is a distinct semi-aquatic [[Ecosystem|ecosystem]] whose groundcovers are flooded or saturated in [[Water|water]], either permanently, for years or decades, or only seasonally. Flooding results in [[Oxygen|oxygen]]-poor (anoxic) processes taking place, especially in the [[Soil|soils]]. Wetlands form a transitional zone between waterbodies and dry lands, and are different from other [[Terrestrial ecosystem|terrestrial]] or [[Aquatic ecosystem|aquatic ecosystems]] due to their [[Vegetation|vegetation]]'s [[Root|roots]] having adapted to oxygen-poor waterlogged soils.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"They are considered among the most biologically diverse of all ecosystems, serving as [[Habitat|habitats]] to a wide range of aquatic and semi-aquatic [[Plant|plants]] and [[Animal|animals]], with often improved water quality due to plant removal of excess nutrients such as [[Nitrate|nitrates]] and [[Phosphorus|phosphorus]]. Wetlands exist on every [[Continent|continent]], except [[Antarctica]]. The water in wetlands is either [[Fresh water|freshwater]], [[Brackish water|brackish]] or [[Seawater|saltwater]]. Common types of wetlands are described based on the dominant [[Plant|plants]] and the source of the water. For example, [[Marsh|marshes]] are wetlands dominated by [[Aquatic plant|emergent]] herbaceous vegetation such as reeds, cattails, and sedges. [[Swamp|Swamps]] are dominated by woody vegetation, such as [[Tree|trees]] and [[Shrub|shrubs]] (although reed swamps in Europe are dominated by reeds, not trees). Mangrove forests are wetlands with [[Mangrove|mangroves]] and halophytic woody plants that have evolved to tolerate salty water.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "White",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Lightest color",
		tags = { "Color", "Color terms" },
		summary = "**White** is the lightest [[Color|color]] and is achromatic (having no chroma). It is the color of objects such as [[Snow|snow]], chalk, and [[Milk|milk]], and is the opposite of [[Black|black]]. White objects fully (or almost fully) reflect and [[Scattering|scatter]] all the [[Visible spectrum|visible wavelengths]] of [[Light|light]]. White on [[Television|television]] and computer screens is created by a mixture of [[Red|red]], [[Blue|blue]], and [[Green|green]] light. The color white can be given with white [[Pigment|pigments]], especially [[Titanium dioxide|titanium dioxide]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In ancient Egypt and ancient Rome, priestesses wore white as a symbol of purity, and Romans wore white togas as symbols of citizenship. It was the royal color of the kings of France as well as the flag of [[Bourbon Restoration in France|monarchist France]] from 1815 to [[July Revolution|1830]], and of the monarchist movement that opposed the [[Bolsheviks]] during the Russian Civil War (1917–1922). Greek temples and Roman temples were faced with white marble, and beginning in the 18th century, with the advent of [[Neoclassical architecture|neoclassical architecture]], white became the most common color of new churches, capitols, and other government buildings, especially in the United States. It was also widely used in 20th century [[Modern architecture|modern architecture]] as a symbol of modernity and simplicity.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Source", "[[Web colors|HTML/CSS]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Wildfire",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔥",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Uncontrolled fire in forests or open spaces",
		tags = { "Earth science", "Natural disasters" },
		aliases = { "Forest fire", "Brushfire", "Bushfire" },
		summary = "A **wildfire**, **forest fire**, **brushfire** or a **bushfire** is an unplanned and uncontrolled [[Fire|fire]] in an area of [[Combustibility and flammability|combustible]] [[Vegetation|vegetation]]. Some natural forest ecosystems depend on wildfire. Modern forest management often engages in prescribed burns to mitigate fire risk and promote natural forest cycles. Controlled burns can turn into wildfires if they escape containment lines.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Wildfires can be classified by cause of ignition, physical properties, combustible material present, and the effect of weather on the fire. Wildfire severity results from a combination of factors such as available fuels, physical setting, and weather. Climatic cycles with wet periods that create substantial fuels, followed by [[Drought|drought]] and heat, often precede severe wildfires. These cycles have been intensified by [[Climate change|climate change]], and can be exacerbated by curtailment of mitigation measures (such as budget or equipment funding), or sheer enormity of the event. Wildfires are a common [[Natural disaster|disaster]] in many parts of the world, for example in Russia ([[Siberia]]), the United States (for example in [[California]], [[Washington (state)|Washington]], Oregon, [[Texas]], [[Florida]]), [[Canada]] and [[Australia]]. Wildfires can severely impact humans and their settlements.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Wildlife conservation",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Human Practice of protecting wild plant and animal species and their habitats",
		tags = { "Ecology" },
		summary = "**Wildlife conservation** refers to the practice of protecting wild [[Species|species]] and their [[Habitat|habitats]] in order to maintain healthy [[Wildlife|wildlife]] species or populations and to restore, protect or enhance natural [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]]. Major threats to wildlife include [[Habitat destruction|habitat destruction]], degradation, fragmentation, [[Overexploitation|overexploitation]], [[Poaching|poaching]], pollution, climate change, and the illegal wildlife trade. The [[International Union for Conservation of Nature|IUCN]] estimates that 42,100 species of the ones assessed are at risk for [[Extinction|extinction]]. Expanding to all existing species, a 2019 UN report on [[Biodiversity|biodiversity]] put this estimate even higher at a million species.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is also being acknowledged that an increasing number of ecosystems on Earth containing endangered species are disappearing. To address these issues, there have been both national and international governmental efforts to preserve Earth's wildlife. Prominent conservation agreements include the 1973 Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and the 1992 Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD). There are also numerous [[Non-governmental organization|nongovernmental organizations]] (NGO's) dedicated to conservation such as the Nature Conservancy, World Wildlife Fund, and Conservation International.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Wind",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Winds" },
		summary = "**Wind** is the natural movement of [[Atmosphere of Earth|air]] or other [[Gas|gases]] relative to a [[Planetary surface|planet's surface]]. Winds occur on a range of scales, from [[Thunderstorm|thunderstorm]] flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of [[Land|land]] surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in [[Absorption (electromagnetic radiation)|absorption]] of [[Solar energy|solar energy]] between the climate zones on [[Earth]]. The study of wind is known as anemology.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The two main causes of large-scale [[Atmospheric circulation|atmospheric circulation]] are the differential heating between the equator and the poles, and the rotation of the planet, which is called the [[Coriolis force|Coriolis effect]]. Within the tropics and subtropics, thermal low circulations over terrain and high plateaus can drive [[Monsoon|monsoon]] circulations. In coastal areas the sea breeze/land breeze cycle can define local winds; in areas that have variable terrain, mountain and valley breezes can prevail. Winds are commonly classified by their spatial scale, their [[Speed|speed]] and direction, the forces that cause them, the regions in which they occur, and their effect. Winds have various defining aspects such as [[Velocity|velocity]] (wind speed), the density of the gases involved, and energy content or wind energy. In [[Meteorology|meteorology]], winds are often referred to according to their strength, and the direction from which the wind is blowing.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Winter",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Coldest of the four temperate seasons",
		tags = { "Meteorology", "Seasons" },
		summary = "**Winter** is the coldest and darkest [[Season|season]] of the year in [[Temperate climate|temperate]] and [[Polar regions|polar climates]]. It occurs after [[Autumn|autumn]] and before [[Spring (season)|spring]], when the [[Hemispheres of Earth|hemisphere]] is oriented away from the [[Sun]] as a result of the [[Axial tilt|tilt of Earth's axis]]. Different cultures define different dates as the start of winter, and some use a definition based on weather.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When it is winter in the [[Northern Hemisphere]], it is [[Summer|summer]] in the [[Southern Hemisphere]], and vice versa. Winter typically brings [[Precipitation|precipitation]] that, depending on a region's [[Climate|climate]], is mainly [[Rain|rain]] or [[Snow|snow]]. The moment of winter solstice is when the Sun's elevation with respect to the North or South Pole is at its most negative value; that is, the Sun is at its farthest below the horizon as measured from the pole. The day on which this occurs has the shortest day and the longest night, with [[Daytime|day length]] increasing and [[Night|night length]] decreasing as the season progresses after the solstice. The earliest sunset and latest sunrise dates outside the polar regions differ from the date of the winter solstice and depend on latitude; this is due to the variation in the solar day throughout the year caused by the Earth's elliptical orbit (see: earliest and latest sunrise and sunset).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Work (physics)",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Process of energy transfer to an object via force application through displacement",
		tags = { "Mechanics", "Classical mechanics" },
		summary = "In science, **work** is the [[Energy|energy]] transferred to or from an [[Physical object|object]] via the application of [[Force|force]] along a displacement. In its simplest form, for a constant force aligned with the direction of motion, the work equals the [[Product (mathematics)|product]] of the force strength and the distance traveled. A force is said to do positive work if it has a component in the direction of the displacement of the point of application. A force does negative work if it has a component opposite to the direction of the displacement at the point of application of the force.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"For example, when a ball is held above the ground and then dropped, the work done by the gravitational force on the ball as it falls is positive, and is equal to the weight of the ball (a force) multiplied by the distance to the ground (a displacement). If the ball is thrown upwards, the work done by the gravitational force is negative, and is equal to the weight multiplied by the displacement in the upwards direction. Both force and displacement are [[Vector (mathematics and physics)|vectors]]. The work done is given by the [[Dot product|dot product]] of the two vectors, where the result is a [[Scalar (physics)|scalar]]. When the force F is constant and the angle between the force and the displacement s is also constant, then the work done is given by: If the force and/or displacement is variable, then work is given by the line integral:",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Wrought iron",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Iron alloy with a very low carbon content",
		tags = { "Chemical mixtures", "Specific alloys" },
		summary = '**Wrought iron** is an [[Iron|iron]] [[Alloy|alloy]] with a very low [[Carbon|carbon]] content (less than 0.05%) in contrast to that of [[Cast iron|cast iron]] (2.1% to 4.5%), or 0.25% for low-carbon "mild" steel. Wrought iron is manufactured by heating and melting high-carbon [[Cast iron|cast iron]] in an open charcoal or coke hearth or furnace in a process known as puddling. The high temperatures cause the excess carbon to oxidise, the iron being stirred or puddled during the process in order to achieve this. As the carbon content reduces, the melting point of the iron increases, ultimately to a level which is higher than can be achieved by the hearth, hence the wrought iron is never fully molten and many impurities remain.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The primary advantage of wrought iron over [[Cast iron|cast iron]] is its malleability – where cast iron is too brittle to bend or shape without breaking, wrought iron is highly malleable, and much easier to bend. Wrought iron is a semi-fused mass of iron with fibrous slag inclusions (up to 2% by weight), which give it a wood-like "grain" that is visible when it is etched, rusted, or bent to failure. Wrought iron is tough, malleable, [[Ductility|ductile]], [[Corrosion|corrosion]] resistant, and easily forge welded, but is more difficult to [[Welding|weld]] electrically. Before the development of effective methods of [[Steelmaking|steelmaking]] and the availability of large quantities of steel, wrought iron was the most common form of malleable iron. It was given the name wrought because it was hammered, rolled, or otherwise worked while hot enough to expel molten slag. The modern functional equivalent of wrought iron is mild steel, also called low-carbon steel.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "X-ray",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Form of electromagnetic radiation",
		tags = { "Electromagnetism", "Electrodynamics" },
		summary = "An **X-ray** is a form of high-energy [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]] with a [[Wavelength|wavelength]] shorter than those of [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] rays and longer than those of [[Gamma ray|gamma rays]]. Roughly, X-rays have a [[Wavelength|wavelength]] ranging from 10 [[Nanometre|nanometers]] to 10 picometers, corresponding to [[Frequency|frequencies]] in the range of 30 [[Hertz|petahertz]] to 30 [[Hertz|exahertz]] (3×10^16 Hz to 3×10^19 Hz) and photon energies in the range of 100 [[Electronvolt|eV]] to 100 keV, respectively.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"X-rays were discovered in 1895 by the German scientist [[Wilhelm Röntgen|Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen]], who named it X-radiation to signify an unknown type of radiation. X-rays can penetrate many solid substances such as construction materials and living tissue, so X-ray radiography is widely used in medical diagnostics (e.g., checking for broken bones) and [[Materials science|materials science]] (e.g., identification of some chemical elements and detecting weak points in construction materials). However X-rays are [[Ionizing radiation|ionizing radiation]] and exposure can be hazardous to health, causing [[DNA]] damage, cancer and, at higher intensities, burns and radiation sickness. Their generation and use is strictly controlled by public health authorities.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Xenon",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Xe" },
		summary = "**Xenon** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Xe** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 54. It is a dense, colorless, odorless [[Noble gas|noble gas]] found in [[Atmosphere of Earth|Earth's atmosphere]] in trace amounts. Although generally unreactive, it can undergo a few [[Chemical reaction|chemical reactions]] such as the formation of xenon hexafluoroplatinate, the first noble gas compound to be synthesized.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Xenon is used in flash lamps and arc lamps, and as a general anesthetic. The first excimer laser design used a xenon dimer molecule (Xe2) as the lasing medium, and the earliest [[Laser|laser]] designs used xenon flash lamps as pumps. Xenon is also used to search for hypothetical weakly interacting massive particles and as a propellant for ion thrusters in spacecraft. Naturally occurring xenon consists of seven stable isotopes and two long-lived radioactive isotopes. More than 40 unstable xenon isotopes undergo [[Radioactivity|radioactive decay]], and the isotope ratios of xenon are an important tool for studying the early history of the [[Solar System]]. Radioactive xenon-135 is produced by [[Beta decay|beta decay]] from iodine-135 (a product of [[Nuclear fission|nuclear fission]]), and is the most significant (and unwanted) neutron absorber in [[Nuclear reactor|nuclear reactors]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Year",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "📅",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Unit of time based on Earth's orbit",
		tags = { "Units of measurement", "Time" },
		summary = "A **year** is a unit of time based on how long it takes the [[Earth]] to orbit the [[Sun]]. In scientific use, the tropical year (approximately 365 solar days, 5 hours, 48 minutes, 45 seconds) and the sidereal year (about 20 minutes longer) are more exact. The modern calendar year, as reckoned according to the [[Gregorian calendar]], approximates the tropical year by using a system of leap years.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term 'year' is also used to indicate other periods of roughly similar duration, such as the lunar year (a roughly 354-day cycle of twelve of the Moon's phases – see [[Lunar calendar|lunar calendar]]), as well as periods loosely associated with the calendar or astronomical year, such as the seasonal year, the fiscal year, the academic year, etc. Due to the Earth's [[Axial tilt|axial tilt]], the course of a year sees the passing of the [[Season|seasons]], marked by changes in [[Weather|weather]], the hours of daylight, and, consequently, [[Vegetation|vegetation]] and [[Soil fertility|soil fertility]]. In [[Temperate climate|temperate]] and subpolar regions around the planet, four seasons are generally recognized: [[Spring (season)|spring]], [[Summer|summer]], [[Autumn|autumn]], and [[Winter|winter]]. In [[Tropics|tropical]] and [[Subtropics|subtropical]] regions, several geographical sectors do not present defined seasons; but in the [[Tropics|seasonal tropics]], the annual [[Wet season|wet]] and [[Dry season|dry seasons]] are recognized and tracked.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Yellow",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🍊",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Color between orange and green on the visible spectrum of light",
		tags = { "Color", "Color terms" },
		summary = "**Yellow** is a [[Color|color]] between [[Green|green]] and [[Orange (colour)|orange]] on the [[Visible spectrum|spectrum of light]]. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 [[Nanometre|nm]]. It is a [[Primary color|primary color]] in subtractive color systems, used in [[Painting|painting]] or color printing. In the [[RGB color model]], used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. [[Carotenoid|Carotenoids]] give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, [[Maize|corn]], canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Sunlight]] has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths (green, blue, and violet). Because it was widely available, yellow ochre pigment was one of the first colors used in art; the Lascaux cave in France has a painting of a yellow horse, dated to about 17,000 years ago. Ochre and orpiment pigments were used to represent gold and skin color in Egyptian tombs, then in the murals in Roman villas. In the early Christian church, yellow was the color associated with the Pope and the golden keys of the Kingdom, but it was also associated with Judas Iscariot and used to mark heretics. In China, bright yellow was the color of the Middle Kingdom, and could be worn only by the emperor and his household; special guests were welcomed on a yellow carpet.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Brown" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Frequency", "521–512" },
			{ "Source", "[[Web colors|HTML/CSS]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Ytterbium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Yb" },
		summary = "**Ytterbium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Yb** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 70. It is a metal, the fourteenth element in the [[Lanthanide|lanthanide]] series, which is the basis of the relative stability of its +2 [[Oxidation state|oxidation state]]. Like the other lanthanides, its most common oxidation state is +3, as in its [[Oxide|oxide]], halides, and other compounds. In [[Aqueous solution|aqueous solution]], like compounds of other late lanthanides, soluble ytterbium compounds form complexes with nine water molecules. Because of its closed-shell electron configuration, its density, melting point and boiling point are much lower than those of most other lanthanides.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In 1878, Swiss chemist Jean Charles Galissard de Marignac separated from the rare earth "erbia", another independent component, which he called "ytterbia", for Ytterby, the village in Sweden near where he found the new component of [[Erbium|erbium]]. He suspected that ytterbia was a compound of a new element that he called "ytterbium". Four elements were named after the village, the others being [[Yttrium|yttrium]], [[Terbium|terbium]], and [[Erbium|erbium]]. In 1907, the new earth "lutecia" was separated from ytterbia, from which the element "lutecium", now [[Lutetium|lutetium]], was extracted by Georges Urbain, Carl Auer von Welsbach, and Charles James. After some discussion, Marignac\'s name "ytterbium" was retained. A relatively pure sample of the metal was first obtained in 1953. At present, ytterbium is mainly used as a [[Dopant|dopant]] of stainless steel or active laser media, and less often as a [[Gamma ray|gamma ray]] source.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Yttrium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		summary = "**Yttrium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Y** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 39. It is a silvery-metallic [[Transition metal|transition metal]] chemically similar to the [[Lanthanide|lanthanides]] and has often been classified as a [[Rare-earth element|rare-earth element]]. Yttrium is almost always found in combination with lanthanide elements in rare-earth minerals and is never found in nature as a free element. 89Y is the only stable [[Isotope|isotope]] and the only isotope found in the [[Crust (geology)|Earth's crust]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The most important present-day use of yttrium is as a component of [[Phosphor|phosphors]], especially those used in LEDs. Historically, it was once widely used in the red phosphors in television set [[Cathode ray tube|cathode ray tube]] displays. Yttrium is also used in the production of [[Electrode|electrodes]], [[Electrolyte|electrolytes]], electronic filters, [[Laser|lasers]], superconductors, various medical applications, and tracing various materials to enhance their properties. Yttrium has no known [[Biology|biological]] role.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Zinc",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Zn" },
		summary = "**Zinc** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Zn** and atomic number 30. It is a slightly brittle metal at [[Room temperature|room temperature]] and has a shiny blue whitish appearance when surface oxidation is removed. It is the first element in group 12 (IIB) of the [[Periodic table|periodic table]]. Zinc is the 24th most abundant [[Abundance of elements in Earth's crust|element in Earth's crust]], with an average concentration of 70 grams per ton. Zinc also has five stable [[Isotope|isotopes]]; the most abundant of which, Zn-64, comprises nearly half of zinc's total abundance. In some respects, zinc is chemically similar to [[Magnesium|magnesium]]: both elements exhibit only one normal oxidation state (+2), and the Zn2+ and Mg2+ [[Ion|ions]] are of similar size.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The most common zinc [[Ore|ore]] is sphalerite (zinc blende), a zinc sulfide mineral. The largest concentration of economically feasible lodes in descending order are located in China, Peru, and Australia, among others. Zinc is refined industrially by froth flotation of the ore, roasting, and final extraction using [[Electricity|electricity]] (electrowinning). Zinc is an essential trace element for humans, animals, plants and for [[Microorganism|microorganisms]] and is necessary for both prenatal and postnatal development. It is the second most abundant trace metal in humans after iron, an important cofactor for many [[Enzyme|enzymes]], and the only metal which appears in all [[Enzyme|enzyme classes]]. Zinc is also an essential nutrient element for coral growth. However, consumption of excess zinc may cause ataxia, lethargy, and copper deficiency.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Zirconium",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemical element with atomic number 40",
		tags = { "Chemical elements" },
		aliases = { "Zr" },
		summary = "**Zirconium** is a [[Chemical element|chemical element]]; it has [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] **Zr** and [[Atomic number|atomic number]] 40. First identified in 1789, isolated in impure form in 1824, and manufactured at scale by 1925, pure zirconium is a lustrous [[Transition metal|transition metal]] with a greyish-white color that closely resembles [[Hafnium|hafnium]] and, to a lesser extent, [[Titanium|titanium]]. It is solid at room temperature, [[Ductility|ductile]], [[Ductility|malleable]] and [[Corrosion|corrosion]]-resistant. The name zirconium is derived from the name of the mineral zircon, the most important source of zirconium. Besides zircon, zirconium occurs in over 140 other minerals, including baddeleyite and eudialyte; most zirconium is produced as a byproduct of minerals mined for titanium and [[Tin|tin]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Zirconium forms a variety of [[Inorganic chemistry|inorganic]] compounds, such as zirconium dioxide, and organometallic compounds, such as zirconocene dichloride. Five isotopes occur naturally, four of which are stable. The metal and its alloys are mainly used as a refractory and opacifier; zirconium alloys are used to clad nuclear fuel rods due to their low neutron absorption and strong resistance to corrosion, and in space vehicles and turbine blades where high heat resistance is necessary. Zirconium also finds uses in flashbulbs, biomedical applications such as dental implants and prosthetics, deodorant, and [[Water purification|water purification]] systems. Zirconium compounds have no known biological role, though the element is widely distributed in nature and appears in small quantities in biological systems without adverse effects. There is no indication of zirconium as a carcinogen.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Aluminium", "Uranium" },
	},
	{
		title = "Zoology",
		category = "Science",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Scientific study of animals",
		tags = { "Zoology" },
		summary = "**Zoology** is the scientific study of [[Animal|animals]]. Its studies include the [[Anatomy|structure]], [[Embryology|embryology]], classification, [[Ethology|habits]], and distribution of all animals, both living and [[Extinction|extinct]], and how they interact with their [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]]. Zoology is one of the primary branches of [[Biology|biology]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although humans have always been interested in the natural history of the animals they saw around them, and used this knowledge to domesticate certain species, the formal study of zoology can be said to have originated with [[Aristotle]]. He viewed animals as living organisms, studied their structure and development, and considered their adaptations to their surroundings and the function of their parts. Modern zoology has its origins during the [[Renaissance]] and early modern period, with [[Carl Linnaeus]], [[Antonie van Leeuwenhoek]], [[Robert Hooke]], [[Charles Darwin]], [[Gregor Mendel]] and many others. The study of animals has largely moved on to deal with form and function, adaptations, relationships between groups, behaviour and ecology. Zoology has increasingly been subdivided into disciplines such as classification, [[Physiology|physiology]], [[Biochemistry|biochemistry]] and [[Evolution|evolution]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
