		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Notable stars" },
		summary = "**Alpha Centauri** is a [[Star system|star system]] in the southern [[Constellation|constellation]] of Centaurus. It consists of three [[Star|stars]]: Rigil Kentaurus, Toliman, and [[Proxima Centauri]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Rigil Kentaurus and Toliman are Sun-like stars (class G and K, respectively) that together form the [[Binary star|binary star]] system Centauri AB. It is the brightest star in the constellation and the third-brightest in the [[Night sky|night sky]], outshone by only [[Sirius]] and [[Canopus]]. Centauri AB are the nearest binary stars to the Sun at a distance of 4.344 light-years. The pair orbit around a common centre with an orbital period of 79 years. Their elliptical orbit is [[Orbital eccentricity|eccentric]], so that the distance between A and B varies from 35.6 [[Astronomical unit|astronomical units]] (AU), or about the distance between [[Pluto]] and the Sun, to 11.2 AU, or about the distance between [[Saturn]] and the Sun. [[Proxima Centauri]] is a small faint red dwarf ([[Stellar classification|class M]]). The distance between Proxima Centauri and Centauri AB is about 13000 AU, equivalent to about 430 times the radius of [[Neptune]]'s orbit.",
			},
