	{
		title = "Asteroid",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "☄️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Minor planet found within the inner Solar System",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Planetary science" },
		summary = "An **asteroid** is a [[Minor planet|minor planet]]—an object larger than a [[Meteoroid|meteoroid]] (thus 1 meter or larger) that is neither a [[Planet|planet]] nor an identified [[Comet|comet]]—that orbits within the [[Solar System|inner Solar System]] or is co-orbital with [[Jupiter]] (Trojan asteroids). Asteroids are rocky, metallic, or icy bodies with no atmosphere, and are broadly classified into C-type ([[Carbon|carbonaceous]]), M-type ([[Metal|metallic]]), or S-type ([[Silicon dioxide|silicaceous]]). The size and shape of asteroids vary significantly, ranging from small rubble piles under a kilometer across to [[Ceres (dwarf planet)|Ceres]], a [[Dwarf planet|dwarf planet]] almost 1000 km in diameter.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A body is classified as a [[Comet|comet]], not an asteroid, if it shows a coma (tail) when warmed by solar radiation, although recent observations suggest a continuum between these types of bodies. Of the roughly one million known asteroids, the greatest number are located between the orbits of [[Mars]] and [[Jupiter]], approximately 2 to 4 [[Astronomical unit|astronomical units]] (AU) from the [[Sun]], in a region known as the main [[Asteroid belt|asteroid belt]]. The total mass of all the asteroids combined is only 3% that of [[Moon|Earth's Moon]]. The majority of main belt asteroids follow slightly elliptical, stable orbits, revolving in the same direction as the [[Earth]] and taking from three to six years to complete a full [[Orbit|orbit]] around the Sun. Asteroids have been observed from Earth since their discovery in 1801. The first close-up observation of an asteroid was made by the Galileo spacecraft.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Asteroid belt",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "☄️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Region between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Solar System" },
		summary = "The **asteroid belt** is a [[Torus|torus]]-shaped region in the [[Solar System]], centered on the [[Sun]] and roughly spanning the space between the orbits of the planets [[Jupiter]] and [[Mars]]. It contains a great many solid, irregularly shaped bodies called [[Asteroid|asteroids]] or [[Minor planet|minor planets]]. The identified objects are of many sizes, but much smaller than [[Planet|planets]], and, on average, are about one million kilometers (or six hundred thousand miles) apart. This asteroid belt is also called the **main asteroid belt** or **main belt** to distinguish it from other asteroid populations in the Solar System.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The asteroid belt is the innermost and smallest circumstellar disc in the Solar System. Its total mass is estimated to be 3% that of the [[Moon]], with about 60% contained in the four largest asteroids: [[Ceres (dwarf planet)|Ceres]], Vesta, Pallas, and Hygiea. Classes of small Solar System bodies in other regions are the near-Earth objects, the centaurs, trojans, and the trans-Neptunian objects ([[Kuiper belt]] objects, [[Scattered disc|scattered disc]] objects, sednoids, and [[Oort cloud]] objects.) Ceres, the only object in the asteroid belt large enough to be classified as a [[Dwarf planet|dwarf planet]], is about 950 km in diameter, whereas Vesta, Pallas, and Hygeia have mean diameters less than 600 km. The remaining mineralogically classified bodies range in size down to a few metres. The asteroid material is so thinly distributed that numerous uncrewed spacecraft have traversed it without incident.",
			},
		},
	},
