	{
		title = "Canis Major",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Constellation in the southern celestial hemisphere",
		tags = { "Celestial mechanics and astrometry", "Constellations" },
		summary = '**Canis Major** is a [[Constellation|constellation]] in the southern celestial hemisphere. In the second century, it was included in [[Ptolemy]]\'s 48 constellations, and is counted among the 88 modern constellations. Its name is [[Latin]] for "greater dog" in contrast to Canis Minor, the "lesser dog"; both figures are commonly represented as following the constellation of [[Orion (constellation)|Orion]], the hunter, through the sky. The [[Milky Way]] passes through Canis Major and several [[Open cluster|open clusters]] lie within its borders, most notably M41.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Canis Major contains [[Sirius]], the brightest star in the night sky, known as the "dog star". It is bright because of its proximity to the [[Solar System]] and its intrinsic brightness. In contrast, the other bright stars of the constellation are stars of great distance and high [[Luminosity|luminosity]]. At magnitude 1.5, Epsilon Canis Majoris (Adhara) is the second-brightest star of the [[Constellation|constellation]] and the brightest source of extreme ultraviolet radiation in the night sky. Next in brightness are the yellow-white supergiant Delta (Wezen) at 1.8, the blue-white giant Beta (Mirzam) at 2.0, blue-white supergiants Eta (Aludra) at 2.4 and Omicron2 at 3.0, and white spectroscopic binary Zeta (Furud), also at 3.0. The red hypergiant VY CMa is one of the largest stars known, while the [[Neutron star|neutron star]] RX J0720.4-3125 has a radius of a mere 5 km.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Ursa Major" },
	},
	{
		title = "Canopus",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⭐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Brightest star in the constellation of Carina",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Notable stars" },
		summary = "**Canopus** is the brightest star in the southern [[Constellation|constellation]] of Carina and the second-brightest star in the [[Night sky|night sky]]. It is [[Bayer designation|designated]] ** Carinae**, which is romanized ([[Transliteration|transliterated]]) to **Alpha Carinae**. With a visual apparent magnitude of −0.74, it is outshone only by [[Sirius]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It has a luminosity over 10,000 times the luminosity of the Sun, is nine to ten times as massive, and has expanded to 71 times the Sun's radius. Its enlarged [[Photosphere|photosphere]] has an [[Effective temperature|effective temperature]] of around 7400 K. Canopus is undergoing [[Stellar core|core]] helium burning and is currently in the so-called blue loop phase of its [[Stellar evolution|evolution]], having already passed through the red-giant branch after exhausting the hydrogen in its core. Canopus is a source of X-rays, which are likely being emitted from its corona. The prominent appearance of Canopus means it has been the subject of mythological lore among many ancient peoples. Its proper name is believed to originate from the [[Greek mythology|mythological]] Canopus, a navigator for Menelaus, the king of [[Sparta]]. The acronycal rising marked the date of the Ptolemaia festival in Egypt. In ancient India, it was named Agastya after the revered Vedic sage.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Capella" },
	},
	{
		title = "Capella",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⭐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Brightest star in the constellation Auriga",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Notable stars" },
		summary = '**Capella** is the brightest [[Star|star]] in the northern [[Constellation|constellation]] of Auriga. It has the [[Bayer designation]] ** Aurigae**, which is [[Latinisation of names|Latinised]] to **Alpha Aurigae** and abbreviated **Alpha Aur** or ** Aur**. Capella is the sixth-brightest star in the [[Night sky|night sky]], and the third-brightest in the [[Northern celestial hemisphere|northern celestial hemisphere]] after [[Arcturus]] and [[Vega]]. A prominent object in the northern sky, it is circumpolar to observers north of 44°N. Its name meaning "little goat" in [[Latin]], Capella depicted the goat Amalthea that suckled [[Zeus]] in classical mythology. Capella is relatively close, at 42.9 light-years.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is one of the brightest [[X-ray]] sources in the sky, thought to come primarily from the corona of Capella Aa. The secondary pair, are around 10,000 [[Astronomical unit|astronomical units]] (AU) from the first. They are faint, small and relatively cool red dwarfs. Capella Aa and Capella Ab have exhausted their core [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]], and cooled and expanded, moving off the [[Main sequence|main sequence]]. They are in a very tight circular orbit about 0.74 AU apart, and orbit each other every 104 days. An aging red clump star, it is fusing [[Helium|helium]] to [[Carbon|carbon]] and [[Oxygen|oxygen]] in its core. It is in the Hertzsprung gap, corresponding to a brief [[Subgiant|subgiant]] evolutionary phase as it expands and cools to become a [[Red giant|red giant]]. Several other stars in the same visual field have been catalogued as companions but are physically unrelated.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Betelgeuse" },
	},
	{
		title = "Carina Nebula",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Interstellar clouds in the constellation Carina",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Notable nebulae" },
		aliases = { "Eta Carinae Nebula", "NGC 3372", "Great Carina Nebula" },
		summary = "The **Carina Nebula** or **Eta Carinae Nebula** (catalogued as **NGC 3372**; also known as the **Great Carina Nebula**) is a large, complex area of bright and [[Dark nebula|dark nebulosity]] in the constellation Carina, located in the Carina–Sagittarius Arm of the Milky Way galaxy. The nebula is approximately 8500 light-years from [[Earth]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The nebula has within its boundaries the large Carina OB1 [[Stellar kinematics|association]] and several related [[Open cluster|open clusters]], including numerous [[Stellar classification|O-type]] stars and several [[Wolf–Rayet star|Wolf–Rayet stars]]. Carina OB1 encompasses the [[Star cluster|star clusters]] Trumpler 14 and Trumpler 16. Trumpler 14 is one of the youngest known star clusters at half a million years old and contains stars like the O2 supergiant HD 93129A. Trumpler 16 is the home of many extremely luminous stars, such as WR 25 and the Eta Carinae star system. Trumpler 15, Collinder 228, Collinder 232, NGC 3324, and NGC 3293 are also considered members of the association. NGC 3293 is the oldest and furthest from Trumpler 14, indicating sequential and ongoing star formation. The nebula is one of the largest diffuse nebulae in Earth's sky.",
			},
