	{
		title = "Celestial mechanics",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of astronomy",
		tags = { "Celestial mechanics and astrometry", "Celestial mechanics" },
		summary = "**Celestial mechanics** is the [[History of astronomy|branch of]] [[Astronomy|astronomy]] that deals with the [[Motion|motions]] and gravitational interactions of objects in outer space. Historically, celestial mechanics applies principles of [[Physics|physics]] ([[Classical mechanics|classical mechanics]]) to astronomical objects, such as [[Star|stars]] and [[Planet|planets]], to produce ephemeris data. The computation of the motion of the bodies through [[Orbital mechanics|orbital mechanics]] can be simplified by using an appropriate [[Inertial frame of reference|inertial frame of reference]]. This leads to the use of various different coordinate systems, such as the Heliocentric (Sun-centered) coordinate system.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In a binary system of objects interacting through gravity, [[Newton's laws of motion|Newtonian mechanics]] can used to produce a set of orbital elements that will predict with reasonable accuracy the future position of the two bodies. Where one of the bodies is sufficiently massive, [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]] must be included to predict apsidal precession. The problem becomes more complicated when another body is added, creating a three-body problem that can not be solved exactly. Perturbation theory is used to find an approximate solution to this problem.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Celestial sphere",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔵",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Conceptual tool in astronomy",
		tags = { "Celestial mechanics and astrometry", "Celestial sphere" },
		summary = "In [[Astronomy|astronomy]] and [[Navigation|navigation]], the **celestial sphere** is an [[Abstraction|abstract]] [[Sphere|sphere]] that has an arbitrarily large [[Radius|radius]] and is concentric to [[Earth]]. All objects in the [[Sky|sky]] can be conceived as being [[Projective geometry|projected]] upon the inner surface of the celestial sphere, which may be centered on Earth or the observer. If centered on the observer, half of the sphere would resemble a [[Sphere|hemispherical]] screen over the observing location.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The celestial sphere is a conceptual tool used in spherical astronomy to specify the position of an [[Astronomical object|object]] in the sky without consideration of its linear distance from the observer. The celestial equator divides the celestial sphere into [[Northern celestial hemisphere|northern]] and southern hemispheres.",
			},
