	{
		title = "Chronology of the universe",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "History and future of the universe",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Physical cosmology" },
		summary = "The **chronology of the universe** describes the history and future of the [[Universe|universe]] according to the current understanding of [[Physical cosmology|physical cosmology]]. In this model, the earliest stage that is supported by observational evidence is known as [[Cosmic inflation|inflation]], which occurred 13.8 billion years ago. During this epoch, space underwent a period of extremely rapid expansion in a tiny fraction of a second. Once inflation ended, the [[Energy|energy]] that was responsible for it was converted into [[Particle|particles]] and [[Radioactivity|radiation]], heating up the universe into a very hot and dense state, initiating the [[Big Bang]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"At some early stage, baryogenesis produced a small excess of [[Matter|matter]] over [[Antimatter|antimatter]]. Most matter and antimatter particles annihilated each other in pairs, leaving behind a small excess of matter and large amount of radiation. As the universe cooled further, many other heavy particles annihilated each other or decayed, eventually leaving behind a [[Plasma (physics)|plasma]] that was dominated by [[Proton|protons]], [[Neutron|neutrons]], [[Electron|electrons]], [[Photon|photons]], and [[Neutrino|neutrinos]]. After the first second, the plasma became dilute enough so that neutrinos ceased interacting efficiently with the other particles and instead started free streaming through the universe, producing the primordial neutrino background. Five seconds later, electrons and [[Positron|positrons]] annihilated each other, transferring their energy into the rest of the plasma.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Constellation",
