	{
		title = "Cygnus X-1",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "X-ray binary in Cygnus containing a stellar black hole",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Other notable objects" },
		aliases = { "Cyg X-1" },
		summary = "**Cygnus X-1** (abbreviated **Cyg X-1**) is a galactic X-ray source in the [[Constellation|constellation]] Cygnus and was the first such source widely accepted to be a [[Black hole|black hole]]. It was discovered in 1964 during a rocket flight and is one of the strongest X-ray sources detectable from Earth, producing a peak X-ray flux density of 2.3×10^-23 W/(m2⋅Hz) (2.3×10^3 jansky). It remains among the most studied [[Astronomical object|astronomical objects]] in its class. The compact object is now estimated to have a mass about 21.2 times the [[Solar mass|mass of the Sun]] and has been shown to be too small to be any known kind of normal star or other likely object besides a black hole.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'If so, the radius of its [[Event horizon|event horizon]] has 300 km "as upper bound to the linear dimension of the source region" of occasional X-ray bursts lasting only for about 1 ms. Cygnus X-1 is a high-mass X-ray binary system located about 7,000 [[Light-year|light-years]] away, that includes a blue supergiant [[Variable star|variable star]]. The supergiant and black hole are separated by about 0.2 AU, or 20% of the distance from Earth to the Sun. A [[Stellar wind|stellar wind]] from the star provides material for an [[Accretion disk|accretion disk]] around the X-ray source. Matter in the inner disk is heated to millions of degrees, generating the observed X-rays. A pair of relativistic jets, arranged [[Perpendicular|perpendicularly]] to the disk, are carrying part of the energy of the infalling material away into interstellar space.',
			},
