	{
		title = "Dark energy",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Energy driving the accelerated expansion of the universe",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Physical cosmology" },
		summary = "In [[Physical cosmology|physical cosmology]] and [[Astronomy|astronomy]], **dark energy** is a proposed form of [[Energy|energy]] that affects the [[Universe|universe]] on its largest scales. Its primary effect is to drive the accelerating expansion of the universe. It also slows the rate of structure formation. Assuming that the lambda-CDM model of cosmology is correct, dark energy dominates the universe, contributing 68% of the total mass-energy in the present-day [[Observable universe|observable universe]] while [[Dark matter|dark matter]] and [[Baryon|ordinary (baryonic) matter]] contribute 27% and 5%, respectively, and other components such as [[Neutrino|neutrinos]] and [[Photon|photons]] are nearly negligible.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Dark energy's [[Density|density]] is very low: 7e-30 g/cm3 (6e-10 J/m3 in mass-energy), much lower than the density of ordinary matter or dark matter within galaxies. However, it dominates the universe's mass–energy content because it is uniform across space. The first observational evidence for dark energy's existence came from measurements of [[Supernova|supernovae]]. Type Ia supernovae have constant luminosity, which means they can be used to accurately measure distances. Comparing this distance to the [[Redshift|redshift]] (which measures the speed at which the supernova is receding) shows that the [[Hubble's law|universe's expansion]] is accelerating. Prior to this observation, scientists thought that the gravitational attraction of [[Matter|matter]] and energy in the universe would cause the universe's expansion to slow over time.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dark matter",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Hypothetical invisible cosmic material",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Physical cosmology" },
		summary = "Dark matter is implied by [[Gravity|gravitational]] effects that cannot be explained by [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]] unless more matter is present than can be observed. Such effects occur in the context of [[Galaxy formation and evolution|formation and evolution of galaxies]], gravitational lensing, the [[Observable universe|observable universe]]'s current structure, mass position in galactic collisions, the motion of galaxies within [[Galaxy cluster|galaxy clusters]], and [[Cosmic microwave background|cosmic microwave background]] anisotropies. Dark matter is thought to serve as gravitational scaffolding for cosmic structures. After the [[Big Bang]], dark matter clumped into blobs along narrow filaments with superclusters of galaxies forming a cosmic web at scales on which entire galaxies appear like tiny particles.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In the standard Lambda-CDM model of cosmology, the [[Mass–energy equivalence|mass–energy]] content of the universe is 5% ordinary matter, 26.8% dark matter, and 68.2% a form of energy known as [[Dark energy|dark energy]]. Thus, dark matter constitutes 85% of the total mass, while dark energy and dark matter constitute 95% of the total mass–energy content. While the density of dark matter is significant in the halo around a galaxy, its local density in the [[Solar System]] is much less than normal matter. The total of all the dark matter out to the [[Neptune|orbit]] of [[Neptune]] would add up to about 1017 kg, the same as a large [[Asteroid|asteroid]]. Dark matter is classified as "cold", "warm", or "hot" according to [[Velocity|velocity]] (more precisely, its free streaming length). Recent models have favored a cold dark matter scenario, in which structures emerge by the gradual accumulation of particles.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dark nebula",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of interstellar cloud that obscures visible light",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Absorption nebula" },
		summary = "A **dark nebula** or **absorption nebula** is a type of interstellar cloud, particularly [[Molecular cloud|molecular clouds]], that is so dense that it obscures the [[Visible spectrum|visible wavelengths]] of [[Light|light]] from objects behind it, such as background stars and emission or reflection nebulae. The [[Extinction (astronomy)|extinction]] of the light is caused by [[Cosmic dust|interstellar dust]] grains in the coldest, densest parts of molecular clouds. Clusters and large complexes of dark nebulae are associated with Giant Molecular Clouds. Isolated small dark nebulae are called Bok globules. Like other interstellar dust or material, the objects obscured are visible only using [[Radio wave|radio waves]] or [[Infrared|infrared]] light, using techniques from radio and infrared astronomy",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Dark clouds appear so because of sub-micrometre-sized dust particles, coated with frozen [[Carbon monoxide|carbon monoxide]] and nitrogen, which effectively block the passage of light at visible wavelengths. Also present are molecular hydrogen, atomic helium, C18O (CO with [[Oxygen|oxygen]] as the 18O isotope), CS, NH3 ([[Ammonia|ammonia]]), H2CO ([[Formaldehyde|formaldehyde]]), c-C3H2 (cyclopropenylidene) and a molecular ion N2H+ (diazenylium), all of which are relatively transparent. The form of such dark clouds is very irregular: they have no clearly defined outer boundaries and sometimes take on convoluted serpentine shapes. In the central regions of dark nebulae and molecular clouds, events such as the [[Star formation|formation of stars]] and maser emission take place.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dwarf galaxy",
