		description = "Small planetary-mass object",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Planetary science" },
		summary = 'A **dwarf planet** is a small planetary-mass object that is in direct orbit around the [[Sun]], [[Mass|massive]] enough to be gravitationally rounded, but insufficient to achieve orbital dominance like the eight classical [[Planet|planets]] of the [[Solar System]]. The prototypical dwarf planet is [[Pluto]], which for decades was regarded as a planet before the "dwarf" concept was adopted in 2006. Many planetary geologists consider dwarf planets and planetary-mass moons to be planets, but since 2006 the [[International Astronomical Union|IAU]] and many astronomers have excluded them from the roster of planets.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Planetary geologists are therefore particularly interested in them. Of these ten, two have been visited by spacecraft (Pluto and Ceres) and seven others have at least one known moon (Eris, Haumea, Makemake, Gonggong, Quaoar, Orcus, and Salacia), which allows their masses and thus an estimate of their densities to be determined. Mass and density in turn can be fit into geophysical models in an attempt to determine the nature of these worlds. Only one, Sedna, has neither been visited nor has any known moons, making an accurate estimate of mass difficult. Some astronomers include many smaller bodies as well, but there is no consensus that these are likely to be dwarf planets.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Eagle Nebula",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Open cluster in the constellation Serpens",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Notable nebulae" },
		summary = 'The **Eagle Nebula** is a young [[Open cluster|open cluster]] of [[Star|stars]] in the [[Constellation|constellation]] of Serpens. Alternatively, it is catalogued as **Messier 16**, **M16**, or **NGC 6611**, and sometimes referred to as the **Star Queen Nebula**. This cluster discovered by Jean-Philippe de Cheseaux in 1745–46. Both the "Eagle" and the "Star Queen" refer to visual impressions of the dark silhouette near the center of the nebula, an area made famous as the "Pillars of Creation" imaged by the [[Hubble Space Telescope]]. The [[Nebula|nebula]] contains several active [[Star formation|star-forming]] [[Interstellar medium|gas and dust]] regions, including the aforementioned Pillars of Creation. The Eagle Nebula lies in the Sagittarius Arm of the [[Milky Way]].',
		related = { "Carina Nebula" },
