		summary = "In [[Cosmology|cosmology]], **galaxy filaments** are the largest known structures in the [[Universe|universe]], consisting of walls of galactic [[Supercluster|superclusters]]. These massive, thread-like formations can commonly reach 50 to 80 [[Parsec|megaparsecs]] (50 to 80 Mpc)—with the largest found to date being Quipu (400 megaparsecs), and possibly the still unconfirmed Hercules–Corona Borealis Great Wall at around 3 Gpc in length—and form the boundaries between [[Void (astronomy)|voids]]. Due to the accelerating expansion of the universe, the individual [[Galaxy cluster|clusters]] of gravitationally bound [[Galaxy|galaxies]] that make up galaxy filaments are moving away from each other at an accelerated rate; in the far future they will dissolve.",
