		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The intense radiation of most observed GRBs is thought to be released during a [[Supernova|supernova]] or superluminous supernova as a high-mass [[Star|star]] implodes to form a [[Neutron star|neutron star]] or a [[Black hole|black hole]]. Short-duration (sGRB) events are a subclass of GRB signals that are now known to originate from the cataclysmic merger of binary neutron stars. The sources of most GRB are billions of [[Light-year|light years]] away from [[Earth]], implying that the explosions are both extremely energetic (a typical burst releases as much energy in a few seconds as the [[Sun]] will in its entire 10-billion-year lifetime) and extremely rare (a few per galaxy per million years). All GRBs in recorded history have originated from outside the Milky Way galaxy, although a related class of phenomena, soft gamma repeaters, are associated with magnetars within our galaxy.",
