		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Notable nebulae" },
		aliases = { "Barnard 33 or B33" },
		summary = "The **Horsehead Nebula** (also known as **Barnard 33 or B33**) is a small [[Dark nebula|dark nebula]] located about 1,375 [[Light-year|light-years]] (422 pc) from [[Earth]] in the constellation of [[Orion (constellation)|Orion]]. The nebula is located just to the south of Alnitak, the easternmost star of Orion's Belt, and is part of the much larger Orion molecular cloud complex. It appears within the southern region of the dense dust cloud known as Lynds 1630, along the edge of the much larger, active star-forming [[H II region]] called IC 434. It is one of the most identifiable nebulae because of its resemblance to a [[Horse|horse's head]].",
		related = { "Crab Nebula" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "dark" },
			{ "Constellation", "[[Orion (constellation)|Orion]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hubble sequence",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Galaxy morphological classification scheme advocated by Edwin Hubble",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **Hubble sequence** is a [[Galaxy morphological classification|morphological classification scheme]] for [[Galaxy|galaxies]] published by [[Edwin Hubble]] in 1926. It is often colloquially known as the **Hubble tuning-fork diagram** because the shape in which it is traditionally represented resembles a tuning fork. It was invented by John Henry Reynolds and Sir James Jeans.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The tuning fork scheme divided regular galaxies into three broad classes – [[Elliptical galaxy|ellipticals]], [[Lenticular galaxy|lenticulars]] and [[Spiral galaxy|spirals]] – based on their visual appearance (originally on photographic plates). A fourth class contains galaxies with an [[Irregular galaxy|irregular]] appearance. The Hubble sequence is the most commonly used system for classifying galaxies, both in professional astronomical research and in amateur astronomy.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hubble's law",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚖️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Observation in physical cosmology",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Physical cosmology" },
		aliases = { "Hubble–Lemaître law" },
		summary = "**Hubble's law**, officially the **Hubble–Lemaître law**, is the observation in [[Physical cosmology|physical cosmology]] that [[Galaxy|galaxies]] are moving away from Earth at speeds proportional to their distance. Thus, the farther a galaxy is from the Earth, the faster it moves away. A galaxy's recessional velocity is typically determined by measuring its [[Redshift|redshift]], a shift in the frequency of [[Light|light]] emitted by the galaxy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The discovery of Hubble's law is attributed to work published by [[Edwin Hubble]] in 1929, but the notion of the universe expanding at a calculable rate was first derived from [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]] equations in 1922 by Alexander Friedmann. The Friedmann equations showed the universe might be expanding, and presented the expansion speed if that were the case. Before Hubble, astronomer Carl Wilhelm Wirtz had, in 1922 and 1924, deduced with his own data that galaxies that appeared smaller and dimmer had larger redshifts and thus that more distant galaxies recede faster from the observer. In 1927, [[Georges Lemaître]] concluded that the universe might be expanding by noting the proportionality of the recessional velocity of distant bodies to their respective distances.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hyades (star cluster)",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⭐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Open cluster in the constellation Taurus",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Other notable objects" },
		aliases = { "Hyades" },
		summary = "The **Hyades** is the nearest [[Open cluster|open cluster]] and one of the best-studied [[Star cluster|star clusters]]. Located about 153 light-years away from the [[Sun]], it consists of a roughly spherical group of hundreds of stars sharing the same age, place of origin, chemical characteristics, and motion through space.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'From the perspective of observers on [[Earth]], the Hyades Cluster appears in the [[Constellation|constellation]] Taurus, where its brightest stars form a "V" shape along with the still-brighter Aldebaran. However, Aldebaran is unrelated to the Hyades, as it is located much closer to Earth (65 light-years) and merely happens to lie along the same line of sight. The [[Stellar kinematics|moving group]] CWNU 1242 also shares the line of sight to Hyades but is likewise not associated with Hyades being 291 parsecs away. The five brightest member stars of the Hyades have consumed the hydrogen fuel at their cores and have [[Stellar evolution|evolved]] into [[Giant star|giant stars]]. Together with Aldebaran, four of these stars, Gamma, Delta1, Epsilon, and Theta2 Tauri, form an [[Asterism (astronomy)|asterism]] that is traditionally identified as the head of Taurus the Bull.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Cygnus X-1", "Virgo Supercluster" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Constellation", "Taurus" },
			{ "Age", "625 million years" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Hydrothermal vent",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fissure in a planet's surface from which heated water emits",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Hydrothermal vents" },
		summary = "**Hydrothermal vents** are fissures on the [[Seabed|seabed]] from which geothermally heated water discharges. They are commonly found near [[Volcano|volcanically]] active places, areas where tectonic plates are moving apart at [[Mid-ocean ridge|mid-ocean ridges]], ocean basins, and [[Hotspot (geology)|hotspots]]. The dispersal of hydrothermal fluids throughout the global ocean at active vent sites creates hydrothermal plumes. Hydrothermal deposits are rocks and mineral ore deposits formed by the action of hydrothermal vents.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Hydrothermal vents exist because the Earth is both geologically active and has large amounts of water on its surface and within its crust. Relative to the majority of the deep sea, the areas around hydrothermal vents are biologically more productive, often hosting complex communities fueled by the chemicals dissolved in the vent fluids. Chemosynthetic bacteria and archaea found around hydrothermal vents form the base of the food chain, supporting diverse organisms including giant tube worms, clams, limpets, and shrimp. Active hydrothermal vents are thought to exist on [[Jupiter]]'s moon [[Europa (moon)|Europa]] and [[Saturn]]'s moon [[Enceladus]], and it is speculated that ancient hydrothermal vents once existed on [[Mars]]. Hydrothermal vents have been [[Hypothesis|hypothesized]] to have been a significant factor to starting [[Abiogenesis|abiogenesis]] and the survival of primitive [[Life|life]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Impact event",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Collision of two astronomical objects",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Planetary science" },
		summary = "An **impact event** is a collision between [[Astronomical object|astronomical objects]] causing measurable effects. Impact events have been found to regularly occur in [[Planetary system|planetary systems]], though the most frequent involve [[Asteroid|asteroids]], [[Comet|comets]] or [[Meteoroid|meteoroids]] and have minimal effect. When large objects impact [[Terrestrial planet|terrestrial planets]] such as the [[Earth]], there can be significant physical and [[Biosphere|biospheric]] consequences, as the impacting body is usually traveling at several kilometres per second (km/s). The minimum impact speed for bodies striking Earth is 11.2 km/s, the [[Escape velocity]] of the Earth.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Atmosphere|While planetary atmospheres]] can mitigate some of these impacts through the effects of atmospheric entry, many large bodies retain sufficient energy to reach the surface and cause substantial damage. This results in the formation of [[Impact crater|impact craters]] and structures, shaping the dominant landforms found across various types of solid objects found in the [[Solar System]]. Their prevalence and ubiquity present the strongest empirical evidence of the frequency and scale of these events. Impact events appear to have played a significant role in the [[Formation and evolution of the Solar System|evolution of the Solar System]] since its formation. Major impact events have significantly shaped Earth's history, and the formation of the Earth–Moon system has been hypothesized to be the result of a giant impact. Interplanetary impacts have also been proposed to explain the retrograde rotation of [[Uranus]] and [[Venus]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Interplanetary medium",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Material which fills the Solar System",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Solar System" },
		aliases = { "IPM", "Interplanetary space" },
		summary = 'The **interplanetary medium** (**IPM**) or **interplanetary space** consists of the mass and energy which fills the [[Solar System]], and through which all the larger Solar System bodies, such as [[Planet|planets]], [[Dwarf planet|dwarf planets]], [[Asteroid|asteroids]], and [[Comet|comets]], move. The IPM stops at the heliopause, outside of which the [[Interstellar medium|interstellar medium]] begins. Before 1950, interplanetary space was widely considered to either be an empty vacuum, or consisting of "aether".',
		related = { "Uranus", "Kuiper belt" },
	},
	{
		title = "Interstellar medium",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Matter and radiation in the space between the star systems in a galaxy",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "ISM" },
		summary = "The **interstellar medium** (**ISM**) is the [[Matter|matter]] and radiation that exists in the [[Outer space|space]] between the [[Star system|star systems]] in a [[Galaxy|galaxy]]. This matter includes [[Gas|gas]] in [[Ion|ionic]], [[Atom|atomic]], and molecular form, as well as [[Cosmic dust|dust]] and [[Cosmic ray|cosmic rays]]. It fills interstellar space and blends smoothly into the surrounding intergalactic medium. The [[Energy|energy]] that occupies the same volume, in the form of [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]], is the **interstellar radiation field**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although the [[Density|density]] of atoms in the ISM is usually far below that in the best laboratory vacuums, the mean free path between collisions is short compared to typical interstellar lengths, so on these scales the ISM behaves as a gas (more precisely as a [[Plasma (physics)|plasma]], as it's at least slightly ionized) and not as a collection of non-interacting particles. The interstellar medium is composed of multiple phases distinguished by whether matter is ionic, atomic, or molecular, and the temperature and density of the matter. The interstellar medium is composed primarily of [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]], followed by [[Helium|helium]] with trace amounts of [[Carbon|carbon]], [[Oxygen|oxygen]], and [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]]. The thermal pressures of these phases are in rough equilibrium with one another. [[Magnetic field|Magnetic fields]] and turbulent motions also provide pressure in the ISM, and are typically more important, [[Dynamics (mechanics)|dynamically]], than the thermal pressure.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Io (moon)",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌙",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Innermost Galilean moon of Jupiter",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Solar System" },
		aliases = { "Io" },
		summary = "**Io** is the innermost and second smallest of the four Galilean moons of [[Jupiter]]. Slightly larger than Earth's [[Moon]], Io is the fourth-largest natural satellite in the [[Solar System]], has the highest density and strongest surface gravity of any natural satellite, and the lowest amount of water by atomic ratio of any known [[Astronomical object|astronomical object]] in the Solar System.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"With over 400 active [[Volcano|volcanoes]], Io is the most geologically active object in the Solar System. This extreme geologic activity results from tidal heating from [[Friction|friction]] generated within Io's interior as it is pulled between Jupiter and two other Galilean moons—[[Europa (moon)|Europa]] and [[Ganymede (moon)|Ganymede]]. Several volcanoes produce plumes of [[Sulfur|sulfur]] and sulfur dioxide as high as 500 km above the surface. Io's surface is also dotted with more than 100 mountains uplifted by extensive compression at the base of Io's [[Silicate|silicate]] crust. Some of these peaks are taller than [[Mount Everest]], the highest point on Earth's surface. Unlike most moons in the outer Solar System, which are mostly composed of water [[Ice|ice]], Io is primarily composed of silicate rock surrounding a molten [[Iron|iron]] or iron sulfide core. Most of Io's surface is composed of extensive plains with a frosty coating of sulfur and sulfur dioxide.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "rocky round moon" },
			{ "Discovered by", "[[Galileo Galilei]]" },
			{ "Discovered", "8 January 1610" },
			{ "Named after", "Ἰ Īō" },
			{ "Radius", "1821.6 km (0.28592 Earths)" },
			{ "Volume", "2.531906×10^10 km3 (0.023 Earths)" },
			{ "Mass", "8.931938×10^22 kg (0.015 Earths)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Irregular galaxy",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Class of galaxy",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Galaxy morphological classification" },
		summary = "An **Irregular galaxy** is a [[Galaxy|galaxy]] that does not have a distinct regular shape, unlike a [[Spiral galaxy|spiral]] or an [[Elliptical galaxy|elliptical galaxy]]. Irregular galaxies do not fall into any of the regular classes of the [[Hubble sequence]], and they are often chaotic in appearance, with neither a nuclear bulge nor any trace of spiral arm structure. Other galaxies have an axis of symmetry for their blue-light distribution, which cannot be found in the sporadic organization of irregular galaxies. This absence of structure in an irregular galaxy leads to little density waves in these galaxies. This makes irregular galaxies prime areas to study [[Star formation|star formation]] without the effects of density waves.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Collectively they are thought to make up about a quarter of all galaxies. A large chunk of the [[Local Group]] is made up of irregular galaxies, most being distant and classified as dwarf irregulars. Irregulars are broken down into two more subcategories along with dwarf. Some irregular galaxies were once spiral or elliptical galaxies but were deformed by an uneven external gravitational force, or may be young galaxies between spiral and elliptical status. Irregular galaxies may contain abundant amounts of gas and dust. This is not necessarily true for dwarf irregulars. Irregular galaxies may also be formed in galaxy collisions. Irregular galaxies are commonly small, about one tenth the mass of the [[Milky Way]] galaxy, though there are also unusual cases of large irregulars like UGC 6697.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Kepler's laws of planetary motion",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏃",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Laws describing planetary orbits",
		tags = { "Celestial mechanics and astrometry", "Celestial mechanics" },
		summary = "In [[Astronomy|astronomy]], **Kepler's laws of planetary motion** give good approximations for the orbits of planets around the Sun. They were published by [[Johannes Kepler]] from 1609 to 1621 in three works Astronomia nova, Harmonice Mundi and Epitome Astronomiae Copernicanae. The laws were based on Kepler's concept of solar fibrils adapted to the accurate astronomical data of [[Tycho Brahe]]. These laws replaced the circular orbits and epicycles of Copernicus's heliostatic model of the planets with a [[Heliocentrism|heliocentric]] model that described elliptical orbits with planetary velocities that vary accordingly. The three laws state that: The orbit of a planet is an [[Ellipse|ellipse]] with the Sun at one of the two [[Focus (geometry)|foci]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A [[Line segment|line segment]] joining a planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas during equal intervals of time. The square of a planet's [[Orbital period|orbital period]] is proportional to the cube of the length of the semi-major axis of its orbit. The elliptical orbits of planets were indicated by calculations of the orbit of [[Mars]]. From this, Kepler inferred that other bodies in the [[Solar System]], including those farther away from the Sun, also have elliptical orbits. The second law establishes that when a planet is closer to the Sun, it travels faster. The third law expresses that the farther a planet is from the Sun, the longer its orbital period.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Kuiper belt",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Area of the Solar System beyond the planets, comprising small bodies",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Solar System" },
		summary = 'The **Kuiper belt** is a circumstellar disc in the outer [[Solar System]], extending from the [[Orbit|orbit]] of [[Neptune]] at 30 [[Astronomical unit|astronomical units]] (AU) to approximately 50 AU from the [[Sun]]. It is similar to the [[Asteroid belt|asteroid belt]], but is far larger—20 times as wide and 20–200 times as massive. Like the asteroid belt, it consists mainly of small bodies or remnants from when the [[Formation and evolution of the Solar System|Solar System formed]]. While many [[Asteroid|asteroids]] are composed primarily of [[Rock (geology)|rock]] and [[Metal|metal]], most Kuiper belt objects are composed largely of frozen [[Volatile (astrogeology)|volatiles]] (termed "ices"), such as [[Methane|methane]], [[Ammonia|ammonia]], and [[Water|water]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Kuiper belt is home to most of the objects that astronomers generally accept as [[Dwarf planet|dwarf planets]]: Orcus, [[Pluto]], Haumea, Quaoar, and Makemake. Some of the Solar System's [[Natural satellite|moons]], such as Neptune's [[Triton (moon)|Triton]] and [[Saturn]]'s Phoebe, may have originated in the region. The Kuiper belt is named in honor of the Dutch astronomer Gerard Kuiper, who conjectured the existence of a version of the belt in 1951. There were researchers before and after him who proposed similar hypotheses, such as Kenneth Edgeworth in the 1930s. The most direct prediction of the belt was by astronomer Julio Ángel Fernández, who published a paper in 1980 suggesting the existence of a comet belt beyond Neptune which could serve as a source for short-period comets. In 1992, minor planet 15760 Albion was discovered, the first Kuiper belt object (KBO) since Pluto (in 1930) and Charon (in 1978).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Lagrange point",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Equilibrium points near two orbiting bodies",
		tags = { "Celestial mechanics and astrometry", "Celestial mechanics" },
		aliases = { "Lagrange points", "Lagrangian points", "Libration points" },
		summary = "In [[Celestial mechanics|celestial mechanics]], the **Lagrange points**, also called the **Lagrangian points** or **libration points**, are points of [[Mechanical equilibrium|equilibrium]] for small-mass objects under the [[Gravity|gravitational]] influence of two massive [[Orbit|orbiting]] bodies. Mathematically, this involves the solution of the restricted three-body problem.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Normally, the two massive bodies exert an unbalanced gravitational force at a point, altering the orbit of any other celestial body at that point. At the Lagrange points, the [[Gravity|gravitational]] forces of the two large bodies and the centrifugal pseudo-force balance each other. This can make Lagrange points an excellent location for satellites, as orbit corrections, and hence fuel requirements, needed to maintain the desired orbit are kept at a minimum. For any combination of two orbital bodies, there are five Lagrange points, L1 to L5, all in the orbital plane of the two large bodies. There are five Lagrange points for the Sun–Earth system, and five different Lagrange points for the Earth–Moon system. L1, L2, and L3 are on the line through the centers of the two large bodies, while L4 and L5 each act as the third [[Vertex (geometry)|vertex]] of an equilateral triangle formed with the centers of the two large bodies.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Large Magellanic Cloud",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "☁️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Satellite galaxy of the Milky Way",
		tags = { "Astronomical objects", "Notable galaxies" },
		aliases = { "LMC" },
		summary = "The **Large Magellanic Cloud** (**LMC**) is a [[Dwarf galaxy|dwarf galaxy]] and satellite galaxy of the [[Milky Way]]. At a distance of around 50 kpc, the LMC is the second- or third-closest [[Galaxy|galaxy]] to the Milky Way, after the Sagittarius Dwarf Spheroidal (c. 16 kpc away) and the possible dwarf irregular galaxy called the Canis Major Overdensity. It is about 9.86 kpc across and has roughly one-hundredth the mass of the Milky Way, making it the fourth-largest galaxy in the [[Local Group]] after the [[Andromeda Galaxy]] (M31), the Milky Way, and the [[Triangulum Galaxy]] (M33).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The LMC is classified as a Magellanic spiral. It contains a stellar bar that is geometrically off-center, suggesting that it was once a barred dwarf spiral galaxy before its spiral arms were disrupted, likely by tidal interactions from the nearby [[Small Magellanic Cloud]] (SMC) and the Milky Way's gravity. The LMC is predicted to merge with the Milky Way in approximately 2.4 billion years.",
