	{
		title = "Luminosity",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "📏",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Measurement of radiant electromagnetic power emitted by an object",
		tags = { "Basics", "Observational astronomy" },
		summary = "**Luminosity** is an absolute measure of radiated [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic energy]] per unit time, and is synonymous with the radiant power emitted by a light-emitting object. In [[Astronomy|astronomy]], luminosity is the total amount of electromagnetic [[Energy|energy]] emitted per unit of [[Time|time]] by a [[Star|star]], [[Galaxy|galaxy]], or other luminous [[Astronomical object|astronomical object]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In [[International System of Units|SI]] units, luminosity is measured in joules per second, or [[Watt|watts]]. In astronomy, the luminosity of the Sun, L☉ is often used as a unit of luminosity. Luminosity can also be stated in the astronomical [[Magnitude (astronomy)|magnitude]] system: the [[Absolute magnitude|absolute bolometric magnitude]] (Mbol) of an object is a logarithmic measure of its total energy emission rate, while [[Absolute magnitude|absolute magnitude]] is a logarithmic measure of the luminosity within some specific [[Wavelength|wavelength]] range or filter band. In contrast, the term brightness in astronomy is generally used to refer to an object's apparent brightness to an observer. Apparent brightness depends on the luminosity of the object, the distance between the object and observer, and on any [[Extinction (astronomy)|absorption]] of light along the path from object to observer. [[Apparent magnitude]] is a logarithmic measure of apparent brightness.",
			},
