	},
	{
		title = "Main sequence",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🎸",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Continuous band of stars that appears on plots of stellar color versus brightness",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Stellar astronomy" },
		summary = "In [[Astronomy|astronomy]], the **main sequence** is a classification of [[Star|stars]] which appear on plots of stellar color versus [[Absolute magnitude|brightness]] as a continuous and distinctive band. Stars spend the majority of their lives on the main sequence, during which core [[Stellar nucleosynthesis|hydrogen burning]] is dominant. These **main-sequence stars**, or sometimes interchangeably dwarf stars, are the most numerous true stars in the universe and include the [[Sun]]. Color-magnitude plots are known as [[Hertzsprung–Russell diagram|Hertzsprung–Russell diagrams]] after Ejnar Hertzsprung and Henry Norris Russell.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"When a [[Nebula|gaseous nebula]] undergoes sufficient [[Gravitational collapse|gravitational collapse]], the high [[Pressure|pressure]] and [[Temperature|temperature]] concentrated at the core will trigger the [[Nuclear fusion|nuclear fusion]] of [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] into [[Helium|helium]] (see [[Star|stars]]). The [[Thermal energy|thermal energy]] from this process [[Radioactivity|radiates]] out from the hot, dense core, generating a strong pressure gradient. It is this pressure gradient that counters the star's collapse under gravity, maintaining the star in a state of hydrostatic equilibrium. The star's position on the main sequence is determined primarily by the mass, but also by age and chemical composition. As a result, radiation is not the only method of energy transfer in stars. [[Convection]] plays a role in the movement of energy, particularly in the cores of stars greater than 1.3 to 1.5 times the [[Solar mass|Sun's mass]], again depending on age and chemical composition.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Stellar kinematics" },
	},
	{
		title = "Maunakea Observatories",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
