	{
		title = "Outer space",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Void between celestial bodies",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Outer space**, or simply **space**, is the expanse that exists beyond [[Atmosphere of Earth|Earth's atmosphere]] and between [[Astronomical object|celestial bodies]]. It contains ultra-low levels of particle densities, constituting a near-perfect vacuum of predominantly [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]] and [[Helium|helium]] [[Plasma (physics)|plasma]], permeated by [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic radiation]], [[Cosmic ray|cosmic rays]], neutrinos, [[Magnetic field|magnetic fields]] and [[Cosmic dust|dust]]. The baseline [[Temperature|temperature]] of outer space, as set by the background radiation from the [[Big Bang]], is 2.7255 K.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The plasma between galaxies is thought to account for about half of the baryonic (ordinary) matter in the [[Observable universe|observable universe]], having a number density of less than one hydrogen atom per cubic metre and a kinetic temperature of millions of kelvins. Local concentrations of matter have condensed into [[Star|stars]] and [[Galaxy|galaxies]]. Intergalactic space takes up most of the volume, but even galaxies and [[Star system|star systems]] consist almost entirely of empty space. Most of the remaining [[Mass–energy equivalence|mass-energy]] is made up of an unknown form, dubbed [[Dark matter|dark matter]] and [[Dark energy|dark energy]]. Outer space does not begin at a definite altitude above Earth\'s surface. The Kármán line, an altitude of 100 km above [[Sea level|sea level]], is conventionally used as the start of outer space in space treaties and for aerospace records keeping. Certain portions of the upper [[Stratosphere|stratosphere]] and the mesosphere are sometimes referred to as "near space".',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Parallax",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Difference in apparent position with viewing angle",
		tags = { "Celestial mechanics and astrometry", "Astrometry" },
		summary = "**Parallax** is a displacement or difference in the apparent position of an object viewed along two different lines of sight and is measured by the angle or half-angle of inclination between those two lines. Due to [[Perspective (graphical)|foreshortening]], nearby objects show a larger parallax than farther objects, so parallax can be used to determine distances.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'To measure large distances, such as the distance of a planet or a star from [[Earth]], astronomers use the principle of parallax. Here, the term parallax is the semi-angle of inclination between two sight-lines to the star, as observed when Earth is on opposite sides of the Sun in its orbit. These distances form the lowest rung of what is called "the [[Cosmic distance ladder|cosmic distance ladder]]"; they are the first in a succession of methods by which astronomers determine the distances to celestial objects and serve as a basis for other distance measurements in astronomy, which form the higher rungs of the ladder.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Photometry (astronomy)",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Determination of light intensities of astronomical bodies",
		tags = { "Celestial mechanics and astrometry", "Astrometry" },
		aliases = { "Photometry" },
		summary = 'In [[Astronomy|astronomy]], **photometry**, from [[Ancient Greek|Greek]] photo- ("light") and -metry ("measure"), is a technique used in [[Astronomy|astronomy]] that is concerned with [[Measurement|measuring]] the [[Flux|flux]] or intensity of light radiated by [[Astronomical object|astronomical objects]]. This light is measured through a [[Telescope|telescope]] using a [[Photometer|photometer]], often made using electronic devices such as a CCD photometer or a photoelectric photometer that converts light into an electric current by the [[Photoelectric effect|photoelectric effect]]. When calibrated against standard stars (or other light sources) of known intensity and colour, photometers can measure the brightness or [[Apparent magnitude|apparent magnitude]] of celestial objects.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The methods used to perform photometry depend on the wavelength region under study. At its most basic, photometry is conducted by gathering light and passing it through specialized photometric optical bandpass filters, and then capturing and recording the light energy with a photosensitive instrument. Standard sets of passbands (called a photometric system) are defined to allow accurate comparison of observations. A more advanced technique is spectrophotometry that is measured with a spectrophotometer and observes both the amount of radiation and its detailed [[Electromagnetic spectrum|spectral distribution]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Photosphere",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⭐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Star's outer shell from which light is radiated",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "The **photosphere** is a star's outer shell from which [[Light|light]] is radiated. It extends into a star's surface until the [[Plasma (physics)|plasma]] becomes opaque, equivalent to an optical depth of approximately 2/3, or equivalently, a depth from which 50% of light will escape without being scattered.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Stars, except perhaps [[Neutron star|neutron stars]], have no solid or liquid surface. Therefore, the photosphere is typically used to describe the [[Sun]]'s or another [[Star|star]]'s visual surface.",
