	{
		title = "Planet",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Large, round non-stellar astronomical object",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Planetary science" },
		summary = "A **planet** is a large, rounded [[Astronomical object|astronomical body]] that is generally required to be in [[Orbit|orbit]] around a [[Star|star]], stellar remnant, or [[Brown dwarf|brown dwarf]], and is not one itself. The [[Solar System]] has eight planets by the most restrictive definition of the term: the [[Terrestrial planet|terrestrial planets]] [[Mercury (planet)|Mercury]], [[Venus]], [[Earth]], and [[Mars]], and the [[Giant planet|giant planets]] [[Jupiter]], [[Saturn]], [[Uranus]], and [[Neptune]]. The best available theory of planet formation is the [[Nebular hypothesis|nebular hypothesis]], which posits that an interstellar cloud collapses out of a [[Nebula|nebula]] to create a young [[Protostar|protostar]] orbited by a protoplanetary disk.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Planets grow in this disk by the gradual accumulation of material driven by [[Gravity|gravity]], a process called [[Accretion (astrophysics)|accretion]]. The word planet comes from the Greek (planḗtai) wanderers. Planets have historically had religious associations: [[History of astronomy|multiple cultures]] identified celestial bodies with gods, and these connections with mythology and [[Folklore|folklore]] persist in the schemes for naming newly discovered Solar System bodies. Earth itself was recognized as a planet when [[Heliocentrism|heliocentrism]] supplanted geocentrism during the 16th and 17th centuries. With the development of the [[Telescope|telescope]], the meaning of planet broadened to include objects only visible with assistance: the [[Natural satellite|moons]] of the planets beyond Earth; the ice giants Uranus and Neptune; [[Ceres (dwarf planet)|Ceres]] and other bodies later recognized to be part of the [[Asteroid belt|asteroid belt]]; and Pluto, later found to be the largest member of the collection of icy bodies known as the [[Kuiper belt]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Planetary nebula",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of emission nebula created by dying red giants",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Stellar astronomy" },
		summary = "A **planetary nebula** is a type of emission nebula consisting of an expanding, glowing shell of [[Ionization|ionized]] gas ejected from [[Red giant|red giant]] stars late in their lives.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The term "planetary nebula" is a misnomer because they are unrelated to [[Planet|planets]]. The term originates from the planet-like round shape of these [[Nebula|nebulae]] observed by astronomers through early telescopes. The first usage may have occurred during the 1780s with the English astronomer [[William Herschel]] who described these nebulae as resembling planets; however, as early as January 1779, the French astronomer Antoine Darquier de Pellepoix described in his observations of the [[Ring Nebula]], "very dim but perfectly outlined; it is as large as [[Jupiter]] and resembles a fading planet". Though the modern interpretation is different, the old term is still used. All planetary nebulae form at the end of the life of a star of intermediate mass, about 1-8 solar masses. It is expected that the [[Sun]] will form a planetary nebula at the end of its life cycle.',
			},
