	{
		title = "Planetary system",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔣",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Set of non-stellar objects in orbit around a star",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Stellar astronomy" },
		summary = "A **planetary system** consists of a set of non-stellar [[Astronomical object|bodies]] which are [[Gravity|gravitationally]] bound to and in [[Orbit|orbit]] of a [[Star|star]] or [[Star system|star system]]. Generally speaking, such systems will include [[Planet|planets]], and may include other objects such as [[Dwarf planet|dwarf planets]], [[Asteroid|asteroids]], [[Natural satellite|natural satellites]], [[Meteoroid|meteoroids]], [[Comet|comets]], planetesimals, and circumstellar disks. The [[Solar System]] is an example of a planetary system, in which [[Earth]], seven other planets, and other celestial objects are bound to and revolve around the [[Sun]]. The term **exoplanetary system** is sometimes used in reference to planetary systems other than the Solar System.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'By convention planetary systems are named after their host, or parent, star, as is the case with the Solar System being named after "Sol" (Latin for sun). Of particular interest to [[Astrobiology|astrobiology]] is the habitable zone of planetary systems where planets could have surface liquid water, and thus, the capacity to support Earth-like life.',
			},
