	{
		title = "Radio galaxy",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌌",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of active galaxy that is very luminous at radio wavelengths",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Active galactic nucleus" },
		summary = "A **radio galaxy** is a [[Galaxy|galaxy]] with giant regions of [[Radio wave|radio]] emission extending well beyond its visible structure. These energetic radio lobes are powered by jets from its [[Active galactic nucleus|active galactic nucleus]]. They have luminosities up to 1039 [[Watt|W]] at [[Radio wave|radio wavelengths]] between 10 MHz and 100 GHz. The radio emission is due to the synchrotron process. The observed structure in radio emission is determined by the interaction between twin jets and the external medium, modified by the effects of relativistic beaming. The host galaxies are almost exclusively large [[Elliptical galaxy|elliptical galaxies]]. Radio-loud active galaxies can be detected at large distances, making them valuable tools for observational cosmology.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Much work has been done on the effects of these objects on the intergalactic medium, particularly in [[Galaxy groups and clusters|galaxy groups and clusters]]. The term "radio galaxy" is often used to refer to the entire jet system, rather than solely to its host galaxy. Some scientists consider the term "black hole jet system" more accurate and less confusing. Radio galaxies that reach the size of around 0.7 megaparsecs or more, are commonly called "giant radio galaxies".',
