	{
		title = "Ring system",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💍",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Ring of cosmic dust orbiting an astronomical object",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Planetary science" },
		summary = "A **ring system** is a disc or [[Torus|torus]] orbiting an [[Astronomical object|astronomical object]] that is composed of numerous solid bodies such as [[Cosmic dust|dust]] particles, [[Meteoroid|meteoroids]], [[Minor planet|minor planets]], moonlets, or stellar objects.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Ring systems are best known as planetary rings, common components of satellite systems around [[Giant planet|giant planets]] such as the [[Rings of Saturn|rings of Saturn]], or circumplanetary disks. But they can also be galactic rings and circumstellar discs, belts of minor planets, such as the [[Asteroid belt|asteroid belt]] or [[Kuiper belt]], or rings of interplanetary dust, such as around the [[Sun]] at distances of [[Mercury (planet)|Mercury]], [[Venus]], and [[Earth]], in mean motion resonance with these planets. Evidence suggests that ring systems may also be found around other types of astronomical objects, including moons and [[Brown dwarf|brown dwarfs]]. In the [[Solar System]], all four giant planets (Jupiter, [[Rings of Saturn|Saturn]], Uranus, and Neptune) have ring systems, but [[Saturn]]'s ring system is the biggest and the most visible one out of the four. Ring systems around minor planets have also been discovered via occultations.",
			},
		},
