	{
		title = "Saturn V",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🪐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "American super heavy-lift expendable rocket",
		tags = { "Space", "Crewed spacecraft" },
		summary = "The **Saturn V** is a retired American super heavy-lift launch vehicle developed by [[NASA]] under the [[Apollo program]] for human exploration of the Moon. The rocket was human-rated, had three stages, and was liquid-fueled. Its final launch was Skylab, the first American [[International Space Station|space station]], converted from its own third stage.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Saturn V was the first launch vehicle to carry humans beyond [[Low Earth orbit|low Earth orbit]] (LEO), with the only other to do so being the Space Launch System (SLS) for the Artemis II lunar flyby in 2026. The Saturn V holds the record for the largest payload capacity to low Earth orbit, 140000 kg, which included the S-IVB third stage with remaining propellant to send the Apollo command and service module and Lunar Module to the Moon. At 34.5 meganewtons, it is the fourth most powerful rocket by [[Thrust|thrust]] at launch and the second most powerful to carry humans, after SLS at 39 meganewtons. The largest production model of the Saturn family of rockets, the Saturn V was designed at the Marshall Space Flight Center (MSFC) in Huntsville, Alabama. This group had developed the first US launch vehicles, the Redstone rocket family, under the Army Ballistic Missile Agency. All engines were built by Rocketdyne.",
			},
		},
