	{
		title = "Solar eclipse",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🌘",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Event wherein the Sun is obscured by the Moon",
		tags = { "Celestial mechanics and astrometry", "Celestial mechanics" },
		summary = "A **solar eclipse** occurs when the [[Moon]] passes between [[Earth]] and the [[Sun]], thereby obscuring the view of the Sun from a small part of Earth, totally or partially. Such an alignment occurs approximately every six months, during the eclipse season in its new moon phase, when the Moon's orbital plane is closest to [[Ecliptic|the plane of Earth's orbit]]. In a total [[Eclipse|eclipse]], the disk of the Sun is fully obscured by the Moon. In partial and annular eclipses, only part of the Sun is obscured. Unlike a [[Lunar eclipse|lunar eclipse]], which may be viewed from anywhere on the [[Night|night]] side of Earth, a solar eclipse can only be viewed from a relatively small area of the world.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"As such, although total solar eclipses occur somewhere on Earth every 18 months on average, they recur at any given place only once every 360 to 410 years. If the Moon were in a perfectly circular orbit and in the same orbital plane as Earth, there would be total solar eclipses at every new moon. Instead, because the Moon's orbit is tilted at about 5 degrees to Earth's orbit, its shadow usually misses Earth. Solar (and lunar) eclipses therefore happen only during eclipse seasons, resulting in at least two, and up to five, solar eclipses each year, no more than two of which can be total. Total eclipses are rarer because they require a more precise alignment between the centers of the Sun and Moon, and because the Moon's apparent size in the sky is sometimes too small for it to fully cover the Sun. An eclipse is a natural phenomenon.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Solar wind",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Stream of charged particles from the Sun",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Stellar astronomy" },
		summary = "The **solar wind** is a stream of [[Charged particle|charged particles]] released from the [[Sun]]'s outermost atmospheric layer, the corona. This [[Plasma (physics)|plasma]] mostly consists of [[Electron|electrons]], [[Proton|protons]] and [[Alpha particle|alpha particles]] with [[Kinetic energy|kinetic energy]] between 0.5 keV. The composition of the solar wind plasma also includes a mixture of particle species found in the solar plasma: trace amounts of heavy ions and [[Atomic nucleus|atomic nuclei]] of [[Chemical element|elements]] such as [[Carbon|carbon]], [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]], [[Oxygen|oxygen]], [[Neon|neon]], [[Magnesium|magnesium]], [[Silicon|silicon]], [[Sulfur|sulfur]], and [[Iron|iron]]. There are also rarer traces of some other nuclei and isotopes such as [[Phosphorus|phosphorus]], [[Titanium|titanium]], [[Chromium|chromium]], and [[Nickel|nickel]]'s [[Isotope|isotopes]] 58Ni, 60Ni, and 62Ni. Superimposed with the solar-wind plasma is the interplanetary magnetic field.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The solar wind varies in [[Density|density]], [[Temperature|temperature]] and [[Speed|speed]] over time and over solar latitude and longitude. Its particles can escape the Sun's [[Gravity|gravity]] because of their high energy resulting from the high temperature of the corona, which in turn is a result of the coronal magnetic field. The boundary separating the corona from the solar wind is called the Alfvén surface. At a distance of more than a few solar radii from the Sun, the solar wind reaches speeds of 250 km/s and is supersonic, meaning it moves faster than the speed of fast magnetosonic waves. The flow of the solar wind is no longer supersonic at the termination shock. Other related phenomena include the [[Aurora|aurora]] (northern and southern lights), comet tails that always point away from the Sun, and geomagnetic storms that can change the direction of magnetic field lines.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Stellar magnetic field" },
	},
	{
		title = "Solstice",
		category = "Space",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "☀️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Twice-annual astronomical event when the Sun is farthest from above the Earth's equator",
		tags = { "Celestial mechanics and astrometry", "Celestial sphere" },
		summary = "A **solstice** is the time when the [[Sun]] reaches its most northerly or southerly sun path relative to the celestial equator on the [[Celestial sphere|celestial sphere]]. Two solstices occur annually, around 20–22 June and 20–22 December. In many countries, the [[Season|seasons]] of the year are defined by reference to the solstices and the [[Equinox|equinoxes]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The term solstice can also be used in a broader sense, as the day when this occurs. For locations not too close to the equator or the poles, the dates with the longest and shortest periods of daylight are the summer and winter solstices, respectively. Terms with no ambiguity as to which hemisphere is the context are "June solstice" and "December solstice", referring to the months in which they take place every year.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Astronomical coordinate systems" },
