		tags = { "Astronomy", "Stellar astronomy" },
		summary = "A **starburst galaxy** is one undergoing an exceptionally high rate of [[Star formation|star formation]], as compared to the long-term average rate of star formation in the [[Galaxy|galaxy]], or the star formation rate observed in most other galaxies.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"For example, the star formation rate of the [[Milky Way]] galaxy is approximately 3 [[Solar mass|M☉]]/yr, while starburst galaxies can experience star formation rates of 100 M☉/yr or more. While many main-sequence galaxies use up their molecular gas reservoir in 1-2 Gyr, the high rate of star formation in starbursts can deplete all of their star-forming material in scales of 0.1 Gyr - much shorter than the age of the galaxy. As such, the starburst nature of a galaxy is a phase, and one that typically occupies a brief period of a [[Galaxy formation and evolution|galaxy's evolution]]. The majority of starburst galaxies are in the midst of a merger or close encounter with another galaxy. Starburst galaxies include M82, NGC 4038/NGC 4039 (the Antennae Galaxies), and IC 10.",
