		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Flow of gas ejected from the upper atmosphere of a star",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **stellar wind** is a flow of gas ejected from the upper atmosphere of a [[Star|star]]. It is distinguished from the bipolar outflows characteristic of young stars by being less collimated, although stellar winds are not generally spherically symmetric.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Different [[Stellar classification|types]] of stars have different types of stellar winds. These include [[Red giant|red giants]] and supergiants, and asymptotic giant branch stars. These winds are understood to be driven by radiation pressure on [[Cosmic dust|dust]] condensing in the upper atmosphere of the stars. Young T Tauri stars often have very powerful stellar winds. Such winds are driven by radiation pressure on the resonance absorption lines of heavy elements such as carbon and nitrogen. These high-energy stellar winds blow stellar wind bubbles. G-type stars like the [[Sun]] have a wind driven by their hot, magnetized corona. The Sun's wind is called the [[Solar wind|solar wind]]. These winds consist mostly of high-energy [[Electron|electrons]] and [[Proton|protons]] (about 1 keV) that are able to escape the star's [[Gravity|gravity]] because of the high [[Temperature|temperature]] of the corona.",
