		aliases = { "Triton" },
		summary = "**Triton** is the largest [[Natural satellite|natural satellite]] of the [[Planet|planet]] [[Neptune]]. It is the only moon of Neptune massive enough to be rounded under its own gravity and hosts a thin, hazy atmosphere. Triton orbits Neptune in a retrograde orbit—revolving in the opposite direction to the parent planet's rotation—the only large moon in the [[Solar System]] to do so. Triton is thought to have once been a [[Dwarf planet|dwarf planet]] from the [[Kuiper belt]], captured into Neptune's orbit by the latter's [[Gravity|gravity]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"At 2710 km in diameter, Triton is the seventh-largest moon in the Solar System, the second-largest planetary moon in relation to its primary (after Earth's [[Moon]]), and larger than all of the known [[Dwarf planet|dwarf planets]]. The mean [[Density|density]] is 2.061 g, reflecting a composition of approximately 30–45% [[Ice|water ice]] by [[Mass|mass]], with the rest being mostly rock and metal. Triton is differentiated, with a [[Crust (geology)|crust]] of primarily ice atop a probable subsurface ocean of liquid water and a solid rocky-metallic core at its center. Although Triton's orbit is nearly circular with a very low [[Orbital eccentricity|orbital eccentricity]] of 0.000016, its interior may still experience tidal heating through obliquity tides. Triton is one of the most geologically active worlds in the Solar System, with an estimated average surface age of less than 100 million years old.",
