		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Theories about the end of the universe",
		tags = { "Astronomy", "Physical cosmology" },
		summary = "The **ultimate fate of the universe** is a topic in [[Physical cosmology|physical cosmology]], whose theoretical restrictions allow possible scenarios for the [[Chronology of the universe|evolution of the universe]] and the ultimate fate of the [[Universe|universe]] to be described and evaluated. Based on available observational evidence, deciding the fate and evolution of the universe has become a valid cosmological question, being beyond the mostly [[Falsifiability|untestable]] constraints of mythological or theological beliefs. Several possible futures have been predicted by different scientific hypotheses, including that the universe might have existed for a finite or infinite duration, or towards explaining the manner and circumstances of its beginning.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Observations made by astronomer [[Edwin Hubble]] during the 1930s–1950s found that galaxies appeared to be moving away from each other, leading to the currently accepted [[Big Bang]] theory. This suggests that the universe began very dense about [[Age of the universe|13.787 billion years ago]], and it has [[Expansion of the universe|expanded]] and (on average) become less dense ever since. Confirmation of the Big Bang mostly depends on knowing the rate of expansion, average density of matter, and the physical properties of the [[Mass–energy equivalence|mass–energy]] in the universe. There is a strong consensus among [[Cosmology|cosmologists]] that the shape of the universe is considered "flat" (parallel lines stay parallel), and the universe will continue to expand forever.',
			},
