	{
		title = "Kabaddi",
		category = "Sport",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏅",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Contact team sport popular in South Asia",
		tags = { "Sports", "Team sports" },
		summary = "**Kabaddi** is a [[Contact sport|contact]] [[Team sport|team sport]] played between two teams of seven players. It is one of the traditional games of South Asia. In the game, a raider enters the opposing half of the court to tag the defenders and attempt to return within 30 seconds without being tackled. Points are awarded for successful tags, while defenders earn a point for tackling the raider. Tagged or tackled players are temporarily out but can re-enter when their team scores. Raids alternate between teams throughout the game.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'It is popular in South Asia and nearby Asian countries. Although accounts of kabaddi are found in [[History of India|ancient India]], the game was popularised as a [[Competition|competitive sport]] in the 20th century. It is the national sport of [[Bangladesh]]. It is the third most popular and viewed sport in India after [[Cricket|cricket]] and [[Association football|football]]. It is the state game of the [[States and union territories of India|Indian states]] of [[Punjab]], [[Bihar]], [[Chhattisgarh]], [[Haryana]], [[Karnataka]], [[Kerala]], [[Maharashtra]], [[Odisha]], [[Andhra Pradesh]], [[Tamil Nadu]], [[Telangana]], and [[Uttar Pradesh]]. There are two major forms: "Punjabi kabaddi", also called "circle style", consists of the traditional forms of the sport that are played outdoors on a circular field, and the "standard/rectangular style", which is played indoors on a rectangular court, and is played in major professional leagues and international competitions such as the [[Asian Games]].',
			},
		},
		related = { "National Hockey League", "La Liga" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Region", "[[Indian subcontinent]]" },
			{ "Category", "[[Team sport]], [[Contact sport]]" },
			{ "Venue", "Kabaddi court" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Karate",
		category = "Sport",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🥋",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Japanese and Okinawan martial art",
		tags = { "Sports", "Combat sport and martial arts" },
		aliases = { "Karate-do" },
		summary = '**Karate**, also **karate-do**, is a [[Martial arts|martial art]] developed in the Ryukyu Kingdom. It developed from the indigenous Ryukyuan martial arts (called te, "hand"; tī in Okinawan) under the influence of [[Chinese martial arts]]. While modern karate is primarily a striking art that uses punches and kicks, traditional karate training also employs throwing and joint locking techniques. A karate practitioner is called a **karate-ka**.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Beginning in the 1300s, early [[Chinese martial arts|Chinese martial artists]] brought their techniques to Okinawa. Despite the Ryukyu Kingdom being turned into a puppet state by Japanese samurai in 1609, after the Invasion of Ryukyu, its cultural ties to China remained strong. Since Ryukyuans were banned from carrying swords under samurai rule, groups of young aristocrats created unarmed combat methods as a form of resistance, combining Chinese and local styles of martial arts. Training emphasized self-discipline. This blend of martial arts became known as kara-te, which translates to "Tang hand." Initially, there were no uniforms, colored belts, ranking systems, or standardized styles. Many elements essential to modern karate were actually incorporated a century ago.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Mixed martial arts", "Kickboxing" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Hardness", "Full-contact, semi-contact, light-contact" },
			{ "Country", "Ryukyu Kingdom (Present day Okinawa prefecture, Japan)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Kickboxing",
		category = "Sport",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Full-contact hybrid martial art and combat sport",
		tags = { "Sports", "Combat sport and martial arts" },
		summary = "**Kickboxing** is an umbrella term for different styles of full-contact hybrid boxing type, combat sports, and [[Martial arts|martial arts]] based on punching and kicking. Kickboxing originated in various times in history. The fight takes place in a boxing ring, normally with boxing gloves, mouth guards, shorts, and bare feet to favor the use of kicks. Kickboxing is practiced for self-defense, general [[Physical fitness|fitness]], or for [[Competition|competition]]. Some styles of kickboxing include: full contact karate, [[Muay Thai]], Japanese kickboxing, Lethwei, Sanda, and Savate.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Although since the dawn of humanity people have faced each other in hand-to-hand combat, the first documentation on the use of kicking and punching in sports combat is from [[Ancient Greece|ancient Greece]] and ancient India. But nevertheless, the term kickboxing originated in [[Japan]], in the 1960s, and developed in the late 1950s from [[Karate|karate]] mixed with [[Boxing|boxing]], having some influence, with competitions held since then. American kickboxing originated in the 1970s and was brought to prominence in September 1974, when the Professional Karate Association (PKA) held the first World Championships. Historically, kickboxing can be considered a hybrid martial art formed from the combination of elements of various traditional styles.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Hardness", "[[Contact sport|Full-contact]]" },
			{ "Country", "Various" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "La Liga",
		category = "Sport",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚽",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Spanish association football league",
		tags = { "Sports", "Team sports" },
		aliases = { "Campeonato Nacional de Liga de Primera División", "Primera División", "LaLiga EA Sports" },
		summary = "The **Campeonato Nacional de Liga de Primera División**, commonly known as the **Primera División** or **La Liga**, and officially known as **LaLiga EA Sports** for sponsorship reasons, is a professional [[Association football|association football]] league in [[Spain]] and the highest level of the Spanish football league system. Governed by the Liga Nacional de Fútbol Profesional, it is contested by 20 teams over 38 matchdays.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A total of 63 teams have competed in La Liga since its inception. Nine clubs have been crowned Spanish champions. [[Real Madrid]] and [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] have dominated the competition, with 36 and 29 titles respectively. Other successful clubs include Valencia, Atlético Madrid, Athletic Bilbao, and Real Sociedad, while Deportivo La Coruña have also won the title, reflecting a history of success across several Spanish clubs. As of the 2024–25 season, La Liga is ranked third in the UEFA coefficient rankings based on performances in European competitions over the past five seasons, behind England's [[Premier League]] and Italy's Serie A. La Liga has led the coefficient rankings for more years than any other league and has produced the continent's top-rated club more often than any other league.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Kabaddi" },
		infobox = {
