	{
		title = "Mascot",
		category = "Sport",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚽",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Figure representing a public identity",
		tags = { "Sports", "Basics" },
		summary = "A **mascot** is any human, animal, or object thought to bring [[Luck|luck]], or anything used to represent a group with a common public identity, such as a [[School|school]], sports team, society, military unit, or [[Brand|brand name]]. Mascots are also used as fictional, representative spokespeople for consumer products.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In sports, mascots are also used for merchandising. Team mascots are often related to their respective team nicknames. This is especially true when the team's nickname is something that is a living animal and/or can be [[Anthropomorphism|made to have human-like characteristics]]. For more abstract nicknames, the team may opt to have an unrelated character serve as the mascot. For example, the [[Sport|athletic]] teams of the University of Alabama are nicknamed the Crimson Tide, while their mascot is an elephant named Big Al. Team mascots may take the form of a [[Logo|logo]], person, live animal, inanimate object, or a costumed character, and often appear at team matches and other related events.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mind sport",
		category = "Sport",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏅",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Game of skill based on intellectual ability",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "A **mind sport** is a game of skill in which the outcome is determined mainly by [[Intellectual|intellectual]] ability rather than physical effort. Mind sports usually require strategic thinking, concentration, memory, logic, pattern recognition, planning, and decision-making. They are often played in competitive settings with formal rules, rankings, governing bodies, and international championships.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term became widely used in the late 20th century, especially after the creation of the Mind Sports Olympiad in 1997, Since then, mind sports have gained broader recognition through organizations such as the International Mind Sports Association (IMSA), which governs major international competitions in games such as [[Chess|chess]], bridge, draughts, [[Go (game)|go]], [[Xiangqi|xiangqi]]. Mind sports can include board games, card games, abstract strategy games, memory competitions, puzzle solving, and other activities in which mental performance is the primary factor. [[Chess]] and [[Contract bridge|contract bridge]] are among the best known examples and are recognized by the [[International Olympic Committee]] as sports. Other activities commonly described as mind sports include [[Go (game)|go]], draughts, poker, [[Mahjong|mahjong]], shogi, [[Xiangqi|xiangqi]], renju, [[Scrabble]], speedcubing, and memory sports.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference" },
	},
	{
		title = "Mixed martial arts",
		category = "Sport",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🎨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Full-contact combat sport",
		tags = { "Sports", "Combat sport and martial arts" },
		aliases = { "MMA" },
		summary = "**Mixed martial arts** (**MMA**) is a full-contact [[Combat sport|combat sport]] based on striking and grappling, incorporating techniques from various combat sports from around the world.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The precursors of modern MMA existed in various parts of the world since the ancient period to the 20th century in different forms. Prior to 1970s, various **inter-stylistic contests** and/or **no-holds-barred bouts** took place throughout different countries in [[East Asia]]. One example is from mid-20th-century [[Hong Kong]], where rooftop street fighting contests between different [[Martial arts|martial arts]] styles occurred. As a result, this naturally contributed to the rise of [[Bruce Lee]]'s hybrid martial arts style, Jeet Kune Do in [[United States]]. The modern MMA started to emerge in different forms during the 20th century as well. In [[Brazil]] there was a phenomenon called vale tudo, an early form of modern MMA, which became known for unrestricted fights between various styles such as [[Judo|judo]], [[Brazilian jiu-jitsu]], catch wrestling, Luta Livre, [[Muay Thai]] and [[Capoeira|capoeira]].",
			},
		},
		related = { "Wrestling" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "[[Combat sport]]" },
			{ "Venue", "MMA cage (specially octagonal), ring" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mountaineering",
		category = "Sport",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏔️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Activity involving ascending mountains",
		tags = { "Sports", "Other individual sports" },
		summary = '**Mountaineering** is a set of outdoor activities that involves ascending [[Mountain|mountains]]. Mountaineering-related activities include traditional outdoor [[Climbing|climbing]], [[Skiing|skiing]], and traversing via ferratas that have become sports in their own right. Indoor climbing, sport climbing, and bouldering are also considered variants of mountaineering by some, but are part of a wide group of mountain sports. In everyday language, the term "mountaineering" is synonymous with **mountain climbing** or **alpinism.**',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Unlike most sports, mountaineering lacks widely applied formal rules, regulations, and governance; mountaineers adhere to a large variety of techniques and philosophies (including grading and guidebooks) when climbing mountains. Numerous local alpine clubs support mountaineers by hosting resources and social activities. A federation of alpine clubs, the International Climbing and Mountaineering Federation (UIAA), is the [[International Olympic Committee]]-recognized world organization for mountaineering and climbing. The consequences of mountaineering on the natural environment can be seen in terms of individual components of the environment (land relief, soil, vegetation, fauna, and landscape) and the location/zone of mountaineering activity (hiking, trekking, or climbing zone).",
			},
		},
		related = { "Table tennis" },
	},
	{
		title = "Muay Thai",
		category = "Sport",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏅",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Combat sport of Thailand",
		tags = { "Sports", "Combat sport and martial arts" },
		aliases = { "Muaythai", "Thai boxing", "Art of Eight Limbs" },
		summary = '**Muay Thai** or **Muaythai**, sometimes referred to as **Thai boxing**, the **Art of Eight Limbs** or the **Science of Eight Limbs**, is a Thai martial art and [[Contact sport|full-contact]] [[Combat sport|combat sport]] that uses stand-up striking, sweeps, and various clinching techniques. The name "Art of Eight Limbs" refers to the combined use of fists, elbows, knees and shins. Muay Thai became widespread internationally in the late 20th to 21st century, when Westernised practitioners from Thailand began competing in [[Kickboxing|kickboxing]] and mixed-rules matches as well as matches under Muay Thai rules around the world. The professional league is governed by the Professional Boxing Association of Thailand, sanctioned by the Sports Authority of Thailand.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Muay Thai is related to other martial art styles of the Indian cultural sphere such as Musti-yuddha, Muay Chaiya, Muay Boran, Muay Lao, Lethwei, Kun Khmer, Benjang and Tomoi. A practitioner of Muay Thai is known as a Nak Muay. Western practitioners in Thailand are sometimes called Nak Muay Farang, meaning "foreign boxer".',
			},
		},
		related = { "Karate" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Country", "[[Thailand]]" },
