		description = "Academic field within biology",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Agriscience" },
		summary = "**Agricultural science** (or **agriscience** for short) is a broad multidisciplinary field of [[Biology|biology]] that encompasses the parts of exact, natural, economic and [[Social science|social sciences]] that are used in the practice and understanding of [[Agriculture|agriculture]]. Professionals of the agricultural science are called agricultural scientists or agriculturists.",
	},
	{
		title = "Agronomy",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Science of producing and using plants",
		tags = { "Agronomy and horticulture", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Agronomy** is the sub-discipline of plant [[Agriculture|agriculture]] focused on the science and technology of crop production and soil management as it pertains to field crops in the context of industrial agriculture and cultivation with [[Intensive farming|intensive farming]] methods. Agronomy has come to include the research of plant genetics, plant physiology, [[Meteorology|meteorology]], and [[Soil science|soil science]]. It is the application of a combination of sciences such as [[Biology|biology]], [[Chemistry|chemistry]], [[Economics|economics]], [[Ecology|ecology]], [[Earth science|earth science]], and [[Genetics|genetics]]. Professionals in the field are known as agriculturists or better known as agronomists, as well as certified crop advisors (CCA) in the US.",
	},
	{
		title = "Amateur radio",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Non-commercial use of the radio spectrum",
		tags = { "Media and communication" },
		aliases = { "Ham radio" },
		summary = '**Amateur radio**, also known as **Ham radio**, is the use of specific bands and frequencies within the radio spectrum for non-commercial communication, technical experimentation, self-training, recreation, radiosport, contesting, and emergency communications. A surge of popular interest in radio experimentation in the early 20th century necessitated that parts of the radio spectrum be allocated to non-professionals. In 1927 a radio amateur was defined as "a duly authorized person interested in radioelectric practice with a purely personal aim and without pecuniary interest", meaning without monetary or similar reward.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The definition distinguished amateur activity from commercial broadcasting, public safety services, and professional two-way radio uses such as maritime, aviation, and taxi communication. The amateur radio service (amateur service and amateur-satellite service) is established by the [[International Telecommunication Union]] (ITU) through its Radio Regulations. National governments set technical and operational rules for transmissions and issue individual station licences with unique call signs. Call signs must be used in transmissions, at least every ten minutes and at the end of the conversation. Amateur radio operators must hold an amateur radio licence, granted after an examination that tests knowledge of radio theory, [[Electronics|electronics]], and national regulations. Operators are authorised to transmit on frequency ranges allocated internationally as the amateur radio bands.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Image", "Telecommunications" },
	},
	{
		title = "Amplifier",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Electronic device/component that increases the strength of a signal",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Components" },
		aliases = { "Electronic amplifier", "Amp" },
		summary = "An **amplifier**, **electronic amplifier** or (informally) **amp** is an electronic device that can increase the magnitude of a signal (a time-varying [[Voltage|voltage]] or [[Electric current|current]]). It is a two-port electronic circuit that uses electric power from a [[Power supply|power supply]] to increase the [[Amplitude|amplitude]] (magnitude of the voltage or current) of a signal applied to its input terminals, producing a proportionally greater amplitude signal at its output. The amount of amplification provided by an amplifier is measured by its [[Gain (electronics)|gain]]: the ratio of output voltage, current, or power to input. An amplifier is defined as a circuit that has a power gain greater than one.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"An amplifier can be either a separate piece of equipment or an [[Electrical network|electrical circuit]] contained within another device. Amplification is fundamental to modern electronics, and amplifiers are widely used in almost all electronic equipment. Amplifiers can be categorized in different ways. One is by the [[Frequency|frequency]] of the electronic signal being amplified. For example, audio amplifiers amplify signals of less than 20 kHz, radio frequency (RF) amplifiers amplify frequencies in the range between 20 kHz and 300 GHz, and servo amplifiers and instrumentation amplifiers may work with very low frequencies down to direct current.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Waveguide" },
