	{
		title = "Animal husbandry",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Management of farm animals",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Animal husbandry" },
		summary = "**Animal husbandry** is the branch of [[Agriculture|agriculture]] concerned with the [[Human|human]] practices of raising captive or [[Domestication|domesticated]] [[Animal|animals]] for harvesting valuable [[Animal product|animal products]] or to train animals to provide [[Working animal|labour]] or services.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It involves the day-to-day management, feeding, nutrition and health, [[Selective breeding|selective breeding]], assisted birth, grooming, [[Marketing|marketing]] and harvest of products such as [[Meat|meat]], [[Milk|milk]], [[Egg|eggs]], fibre and [[Hide (skin)|hide]] of primarily domesticated [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial]] [[Mammal|mammals]] ([[Cattle|cattle]], [[Sheep|sheep]]/[[Goat|goats]], [[Horse|horses]], [[Pig|pigs]], etc.) and [[Bird|birds]] (i.e. [[Poultry|poultry]], mainly [[Chicken|chicken]], ducks and geese) collectively known as [[Livestock|livestock]], although the practice can be extended to various [[Reptile|reptiles]] (e.g. crocodile, snake and turtle farming) and [[Amphibian|amphibians]] (e.g. [[Frog|frog]] farming) — collectively known as herpetoculture — and [[Insect|insects]] (e.g. [[Beekeeping|beekeeping]], sericulture, maggot and cockroach farming) and worms (e.g. earthworm and leech farming), as well as the cultivation of [[Aquatic animal|aquatic animals]] ([[Aquaculture|aquaculture]]) such as [[Fish|fish]], [[Crustacean|crustaceans]] (e.g. [[Shrimp|shrimps]], [[Crab|crabs]], [[Crayfish|crayfish]] and [[Lobster|lobsters]]), [[Mollusca|molluscs]] (e.g. clams, [[Oyster|oysters]], abalones, geoducks and potentially coleoids) and [[Echinoderm|echinoderms]] (primarily [[Sea cucumber|sea cucumbers]]).",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Antenna (radio)",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Device that transmits and receives radio waves",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Components" },
		aliases = { "Antenna", "Aerial" },
		summary = "In radio-frequency engineering, an **antenna** ([[American English]]) or **aerial** ([[British English]]) is a structure used to convert [[Alternating current|alternating electric currents]] into [[Radio wave|radio waves]] for transmission, and to convert radio waves back into electric currents for reception. It is the interface between radio waves propagating through space and electric currents moving in metal [[Electrical conductor|conductors]], used with a transmitter or receiver. In transmission, a radio transmitter supplies an electric current to the antenna's [[Electric terminal|terminals]], and the antenna radiates the energy from the current as [[Electromagnetic radiation|electromagnetic waves]] (radio waves).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'In reception, an antenna intercepts some of the power of a radio wave in order to produce an electric current at its terminals, that is applied to a receiver to be [[Amplifier|amplified]]. Antennas are essential components of all [[Radio|radio]] equipment. An antenna is an array of conductor segments (elements), electrically connected to the receiver or transmitter. Antennas can be designed to transmit and receive radio waves in all horizontal directions equally (omnidirectional antennas), or preferentially in a particular direction (directional, or high-gain, or "beam" antennas). Strong directivity and good transmitting efficiency when transmitting are hard to achieve with antennas whose dimensions are much smaller than a half [[Wavelength|wavelength]].',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "[[Passivity (engineering)|Passive]]" },
			{ "Inventor", "[[Heinrich Hertz]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Aquaculture",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🚜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Farming of aquatic organisms",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Forms" },
		aliases = { "Aquiculture", "Aquafarming" },
		summary = '**Aquaculture** (less commonly spelled **aquiculture**), also known as **aquafarming**, is the controlled cultivation ("farming") of aquatic organisms such as [[Fish|fish]], [[Crustacean|crustaceans]], mollusks, [[Algae|algae]] and other organisms of value such as [[Aquatic plant|aquatic plants]] (e.g. lotus). Aquaculture involves cultivating [[Fresh water|freshwater]], [[Brackish water|brackish water]], and saltwater populations under controlled or semi-natural conditions and can be contrasted with [[Commercial fishing|commercial fishing]], which is the harvesting of wild fish. Aquaculture is also a practice used for restoring and rehabilitating marine and freshwater ecosystems. Mariculture, commonly known as marine farming, is aquaculture in [[Seawater|seawater]] habitats and lagoons, as opposed to freshwater aquaculture.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Pisciculture is a type of aquaculture that consists of fish farming to obtain [[Fish as food|fish products as food]]. Aquaculture can also be defined as the breeding, growing, and harvesting of fish and other aquatic plants, also known as farming in water. It is an environmental source of food and commercial products that help to improve healthier habitats and are used to reconstruct the population of endangered aquatic species. Technology has increased the growth of fish in coastal marine waters and open oceans due to the increased demand for seafood. Aquaculture can be conducted in completely artificial facilities built on land (onshore aquaculture), as in the case of fish tank, ponds, aquaponics or raceways, where the living conditions rely on human control such as water quality (oxygen), feed or temperature.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Aqueduct (water supply)",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💧",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Structure constructed to convey water",
		tags = { "Industry", "Food, water and health" },
		aliases = { "Aqueduct" },
		summary = "An **aqueduct** is a watercourse constructed to carry water from a source to a distribution point far away. In modern engineering, the term aqueduct is used for any system of pipes, ditches, canals, tunnels, and other structures used for this purpose. The term aqueduct also often refers specifically to a bridge carrying an artificial watercourse.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Aqueducts were used in [[Ancient Greece|ancient Greece]], the [[Ancient Near East|ancient Near East]], ancient Rome, ancient Aztec, and ancient Inca. The simplest aqueducts are small ditches cut into the earth. Much larger channels may be used in modern aqueducts. Aqueducts sometimes run for some or all of their path through tunnels constructed underground. Modern aqueducts may also use pipelines. Historically, agricultural societies have constructed aqueducts to irrigate crops and supply large cities with drinking water.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Refrigeration", "Laundry" },
	},
	{
		title = "Assembly line",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Manufacturing process",
		tags = { "Industry", "Basics" },
		summary = "An **assembly line**, often called progressive assembly, is a [[Manufacturing|manufacturing]] process where the unfinished product moves in a direct line from workstation to workstation, with parts added in sequence until the final product is completed. By mechanically moving parts to workstations and transferring the unfinished product from one workstation to another, a finished product can be assembled faster and with less labor than having workers carry parts to a stationary product.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Assembly lines are common methods of assembling complex items such as [[Car|automobiles]] and other [[Transport|transportation]] equipment, [[Household|household]] appliances and [[Consumer electronics|electronic goods]]. Workers in charge of the works of assembly line are called **assemblers**.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Authentication",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Act of proving an assertion",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Cryptography" },
		summary = "**Authentication** is the act of proving an assertion, such as the identity of a computer system user. In contrast with identification, the act of indicating a person or thing's identity, authentication is the process of verifying that identity.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Authentication is relevant to multiple fields. In [[Art|art]], antiques, and [[Anthropology|anthropology]], a common problem is verifying that a given artifact was produced by a certain person, or in a certain place (i.e. to assert that it is not counterfeit), or in a given period of history (e.g. by determining the age via carbon dating). In [[Computer science|computer science]], verifying a user's identity is often required to allow access to confidential data or systems. It might involve validating personal identity documents.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Automation",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Use of various control systems for operating equipment",
		tags = { "Industry", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Automation** describes a wide range of technologies that reduce human intervention in processes, mainly by predetermining decision criteria, subprocess relationships, and related actions, as well as embodying those predeterminations in machines. Automation has been achieved by various means including mechanical, hydraulic, pneumatic, electrical, electronic devices, and [[Computer|computers]], usually in combination. Complicated systems, such as modern [[Factory|factories]], [[Airplane|airplanes]], and ships typically use combinations of all of these techniques.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The benefits of automation includes labor savings, reducing waste, savings in [[Electricity|electricity]] costs, savings in material costs, and improvements to quality, accuracy, and precision. Automation includes the use of various equipment and [[Control system|control systems]] such as machinery, processes in factories, boilers, and heat-treating [[Oven|ovens]], switching on telephone networks, steering, stabilization of ships, [[Airplane|aircraft]] and other applications and [[Transport|vehicles]] with reduced human intervention. Examples range from a household thermostat controlling a boiler to a large industrial control system with tens of thousands of input measurements and output control signals.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Barcode",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⚙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Optical machine-readable representation of data",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Data storage" },
		aliases = { "Bar code" },
		summary = "A **barcode** or **bar code** is a method of representing data in a visual, machine-readable symbolic form. Initially, barcodes represented data by varying the widths, spacings and sizes of parallel lines. These barcodes, commonly referred to as linear or one-dimensional (1D), can be scanned by optical scanners known as barcode readers. Later, two-dimensional (2D) variants were developed, using rectangles, dots, hexagons and other patterns, called matrix codes or 2D barcodes. Despite being often included as barcodes, these do not use bars as linear barcodes do. These can be read using purpose-built 2D optical scanners.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Both matrix codes and linear barcodes can also be read by a digital camera connected to a computer. After a photograph of the barcode is taken, software is used to analyze the image to deconstruct and decode the code. A [[Mobile device|mobile device]] with a built-in camera, such as a [[Smartphone|smartphone]], can function as this type of barcode reader using specialized application software. The barcode was invented by Norman Joseph Woodland and Bernard Silver and patented in the US in 1952. The invention was based on [[Morse code]] that was extended to thin and thick bars. However, it took over twenty years before this invention became commercially successful. UK magazine Modern Railways December 1962 pages 387–389 record how British Railways had already perfected a barcode-reading system capable of correctly reading rolling stock travelling at 100 mph with no mistakes.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Read-only memory" },
	},
	{
		title = "Barn",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🏢",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Agricultural building used for storage and as a covered workplace",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Buildings and infrastructure" },
		summary = "A **barn** is an agricultural building usually on [[Farm|farms]] and used for various purposes. In [[North America]], a barn refers to structures that house [[Livestock|livestock]], including [[Cattle|cattle]] and [[Horse|horses]], as well as equipment and [[Fodder|fodder]], and often grain. In the [[British Isles]] and in Europe, the term barn is restricted mainly to storage structures for unthreshed cereals and [[Fodder|fodder]], the terms **byre**, **shippon** or **shippen** being applied to cow shelters, whereas horses are kept in buildings known as stables. In mainland Europe, however, barns were often part of integrated structures known as byre-dwellings (or housebarns in US literature).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In addition, barns may be used for equipment storage, as a covered workplace, and for activities such as threshing.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Granary" },
	},
	{
		title = "Beekeeping",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🍯",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Management of honey bee colonies",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Forms" },
		aliases = { "Apiculture" },
		summary = '**Beekeeping** (or **apiculture**, from ) is the maintenance of [[Bee|bee]] colonies, commonly in artificial beehives. [[Bee|Honey bees]] in the genus Apis are the most commonly kept species but other honey producing bees such as Melipona stingless bees are also kept. Beekeepers (or apiarists) keep bees to collect [[Honey|honey]] and other products of the hive: beeswax, propolis, bee pollen, and royal jelly. Other sources of beekeeping income include [[Pollination|pollination]] of crops, raising queens, and production of package bees for sale. Bee hives are kept in an apiary or "bee yard".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The earliest evidence of humans collecting honey are from Spanish caves paintings dated 6,000 BCE, however it is not until 3,100 BCE that there is evidence from Egypt of beekeeping being practiced. In the modern era, beekeeping is often used for crop pollination and the collection of its by products, such as wax and propolis. The largest beekeeping operations are agricultural businesses but many small beekeeping operations are run as a [[Hobby|hobby]]. As beekeeping technology has advanced, beekeeping has become more accessible, and urban beekeeping was described as a growing trend as of 2016. Some studies have found city-kept bees are healthier than those in rural settings because there are fewer pesticides and greater biodiversity in cities.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Biofuel",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fuel derived from biological sources",
		tags = { "Industry", "Energy and fuel" },
		summary = '**Biofuel** is a [[Fuel|fuel]] that is produced over a short time span from biomass, rather than by the very slow natural processes involved in the formation of [[Fossil fuel|fossil fuels]] such as oil. Biofuel can be produced from plants or from agricultural, domestic or industrial bio waste. Biofuels are mostly used for transportation, but can also be used for heating and electricity. Biofuels (and bioenergy in general) are regarded as a [[Renewable energy|renewable energy]] source. The use of biofuel has been subject to criticism regarding the "food vs fuel" debate, varied assessments of their sustainability, and ongoing [[Deforestation|deforestation]] and [[Biodiversity loss|biodiversity loss]] as a result of biofuel production.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In general, biofuels emit fewer [[Greenhouse gas emissions|greenhouse gas emissions]] when burned in an engine and are generally considered carbon-neutral fuels as the carbon emitted has been captured from the atmosphere by the crops used in production. However, life-cycle assessments of biofuels have shown large emissions associated with the potential land-use change required to produce additional biofuel feedstocks. The outcomes of lifecycle assessments (LCAs) for biofuels are highly situational and dependent on many factors including the type of feedstock, production routes, data variations, and methodological choices. Estimates about the climate impact from biofuels vary widely based on the methodology and exact situation examined.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Biotechnology",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🔬",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Field of science that uses cells for the creation of products",
		tags = { "Biotechnology" },
		summary = "**Biotechnology** is a multidisciplinary field that involves the integration of [[Natural science|natural sciences]] and engineering sciences to achieve the application of organisms and parts thereof for products and services.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The term biotechnology was first used by Károly Ereky in 1919 to refer to the production of products from raw materials with the aid of living organisms. The core principle of biotechnology involves harnessing biological systems and organisms, such as [[Bacteria|bacteria]], [[Yeast|yeast]], and plants, to perform specific tasks or produce valuable substances. Biotechnology has a significant impact on many areas of society, from medicine to agriculture to environmental science. One of the key techniques used in biotechnology is [[Genetic engineering|genetic engineering]], which allows scientists to modify the genetic makeup of organisms to achieve desired outcomes. This can involve inserting genes from one organism into another, and consequently, creating new traits or modifying existing ones through gene editing.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Polymerase chain reaction" },
	},
	{
		title = "Bleach",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🧪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Chemicals used to whiten or disinfect",
		tags = { "Industry", "Food, water and health" },
		summary = '**Bleach** is the generic name for any chemical product that is used industrially or domestically to remove color from (i.e. to whiten) fabric, fiber, or food (in a process called bleaching) or to disinfect after cleaning. It often refers specifically to a dilute solution of sodium hypochlorite, also called "liquid bleach".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Many bleaches have broad-spectrum bactericidal properties, making them useful for disinfecting and sterilizing. Liquid bleach is one of the few compounds capable of fully annihilating [[DNA]], making it commonplace for sanitizing laboratory equipment. They are used in swimming pool sanitation to control bacteria, viruses, and algae and in many places where sterile conditions are required. They are also used in many industrial processes, notably in the bleaching of wood pulp. Bleaches work by reacting with many colored organic compounds, such as natural pigments, and turning them into colorless ones. While most bleaches are [[Oxidizing agent|oxidizing agents]] (chemicals that can remove electrons from other molecules), some are reducing agents (that donate electrons). [[Chlorine]], a powerful oxidizer, is the active agent in many household bleaches.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Water supply network" },
	},
	{
		title = "Breed",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Specific group of domestic animals",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Animal husbandry" },
		summary = 'A **breed** is a specific group of breedable domestic animals having homogeneous appearance ([[Phenotype|phenotype]]), homogeneous [[Behavior|behavior]], and/or other characteristics that distinguish it from other organisms of the same [[Species|species]]. In the literature, there exist several slightly deviating definitions. Breeds are formed through genetic isolation and either natural [[Adaptation|adaptation]] to the environment or [[Selective breeding|selective breeding]], or a combination of the two. Despite the centrality of the idea of "breeds" to [[Animal husbandry|animal husbandry]] and agriculture, no single, scientifically accepted definition of the term exists.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'A breed is therefore not an objective or biologically verifiable classification but is instead a term of art amongst groups of breeders who share a consensus around what qualities make some members of a given species members of a nameable subset. Another point of view is that a breed is consistent enough in type to be logically grouped together and when mated within the group produce the same type. When bred together, individuals of the same breed pass on these predictable traits to their offspring, and this ability – known as "breeding true" – is a requirement for a breed. [[Plant]] breeds are more commonly known as [[Cultivar|cultivars]]. The offspring produced as a result of breeding animals of one breed with other animals of another breed are known as [[Crossbreed|crossbreeds]] or mixed breeds.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Broadcasting",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Distribution of audio or audiovisual content to dispersed audiences",
		tags = { "Media and communication" },
		summary = "**Broadcasting** is the distribution of [[Sound|audio]] and audiovisual content to dispersed audiences via an electronic mass communications medium, typically using [[Radio wave|radio waves]] in a one-to-many model. Broadcasting began with AM radio, which became popular around 1920 with the spread of [[Vacuum tube|vacuum tube]] radio transmitters and [[Radio receiver|receivers]]. Before this, most implementations of electronic communication (early [[Radio|radio]], [[Telephone|telephone]], and telegraph) were one-to-one, with the message intended for a single recipient.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The term broadcasting evolved from its use as the agricultural method of sowing seeds in a field by casting them broadly about. It was later adopted for describing the widespread distribution of information by printed materials or by telegraph. Examples applying it to "one-to-many" radio transmissions of an individual station to multiple listeners appeared as early as 1898. Over-the-air broadcasting is usually associated with [[Radio broadcasting|radio]] (audio) and television, though more recently, both radio and television transmissions have begun to be distributed by cable ([[Cable television|cable television]]). The receiving parties may include the general public or a relatively small subset; the point is that anyone with the appropriate receiving technology and equipment (e.g., a radio or television set) can receive the signal.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cable television",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "📺",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Television content transmitted via signals on coaxial or fibre-optic cable",
		tags = { "Media and communication" },
		summary = "**Cable television** is a system for delivering broadcast programming to viewers by [[Radio frequency|radio frequency]] (RF) signals transmitted through coaxial cable and through fibre-optic cable. Cable television is contrasted with terrestrial television, wherein the television signal is transmitted over-the-air by [[Radio wave|radio waves]] and received by a television antenna; and with [[Satellite television|satellite television]], wherein a [[Communications satellite|communications satellite]] transmits the television signal over-the-air by way of [[Radio wave|radio waves]] which then are received by a satellite dish antenna. A **cable channel** (also **cable network**) is television programming transmitted by cable (coaxial or optical fibre) and by [[Satellite television|satellite television]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Alternative terms for a cable channel include non-broadcast channel and programming service, the latter term is used in legal contexts. In the U.S., in 1948, in order to provide television services to the rural areas of the country where the geography blocked television signals, the **Community Antenna Television** (CATV) program erected large television antennas atop hills and mountains, from which the coaxial cable was directly connected to the individual houses of the community. In 1968, 6.4 percent of households in the U.S. had cable television service, 7.5 percent in 1978, 52.8 percent in 1988, and 62.4 percent of households in 1994. Moreover, FM radio programming, high-speed Internet, and telephony communication services also are transmitted by cables.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Calculator",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Device used for calculations",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Basics" },
		summary = "A **calculator** is typically a portable [[Electronics|electronic]] device used to perform calculations, ranging from basic [[Arithmetic|arithmetic]] to complex [[Mathematics|mathematics]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The first solid-state electronic calculator was created in the early 1960s. Pocket-sized devices became available in the 1970s, especially after the Intel 4004, the first [[Microprocessor|microprocessor]], was developed by Intel for the Japanese calculator company Busicom. Modern electronic calculators vary from cheap, give-away, credit-card-sized models to sturdy desktop models with built-in printers. They became popular in the mid-1970s as the incorporation of [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuits]] reduced their size and cost. By the end of that decade, prices had dropped to the point where a basic calculator was affordable to most and they became common in schools. In addition to general-purpose calculators, there are those designed for specific markets. For example, there are scientific calculators, which include trigonometric and statistical calculations. Some calculators even have the ability to do computer algebra.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Abacus" },
	},
	{
		title = "Capacitor",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Electronic component",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Components" },
		summary = "A **capacitor** is a device that stores [[Electrical energy|electrical energy]] by accumulating [[Electric charge|electric charges]] on two closely spaced surfaces that are insulated from each other. It is a [[Passivity (engineering)|passive]] [[Electronic component|electronic component]] with two [[Electric terminal|terminals]]. A capacitor was originally known as a **condenser**, a term still encountered in a few compound names, such as the condenser microphone. Colloquially, a capacitor may be called a **cap**.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The utility of a capacitor depends on its [[Capacitance|capacitance]]. While some capacitance exists between any two electrical conductors in proximity in a circuit, a capacitor is a component designed specifically to add capacitance to some part of the circuit. The physical form and construction of practical capacitors vary widely and many types of capacitor are in common use. Most capacitors contain at least two [[Electrical conductor|electrical conductors]], often in the form of metallic plates or surfaces separated by a [[Dielectric|dielectric]] medium. A conductor may be a foil, thin film, sintered bead of metal, or an [[Electrolyte|electrolyte]]. The nonconducting dielectric acts to increase the capacitor's charge capacity. Materials commonly used as dielectrics include [[Glass|glass]], [[Ceramic|ceramic]], plastic film, [[Paper|paper]], [[Mica|mica]], air, and [[Oxide|oxide layers]].",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "[[Passivity (engineering)|Passive]]" },
			{ "Inventor", "Ewald Georg von Kleist (1745) Pieter van Musschenbroek (1746)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Carpentry",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🤝",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Skilled trade",
		tags = { "Industry", "Material and chemical" },
		summary = "**Carpentry** is a skilled trade and a [[Craft|craft]] in which the primary work performed is the cutting, shaping and installation of building materials during the construction of [[Building|buildings]], [[Shipbuilding|ships]], timber bridges, [[Concrete|concrete]] formwork, etc. Carpenters traditionally worked with natural wood and did rougher work such as framing, but today many other materials are also used and sometimes the finer trades of cabinetmaking and furniture building are considered carpentry. In the United States, 98.5% of carpenters are male, and it was the fourth most male-dominated occupation in the country in 1999. In 2006 in the United States, there were about 1.5 million carpentry positions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Carpenters are usually the first tradesmen on a job and the last to leave. Carpenters normally framed post-and-beam buildings until the end of the 19th century; now this old-fashioned carpentry is called timber framing. Carpenters learn this trade by being employed through an apprenticeship training—normally four years—and qualify by successfully completing that country's competence test in places such as the [[United Kingdom]], the [[United States]], [[Canada]], [[Switzerland]], [[Australia]] and [[South Africa]]. It is also common that the skill can be learned by gaining work experience other than a formal training program, which may be the case in many places.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Professional" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cathode ray tube",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Vacuum tube used to display images",
		tags = { "Media and communication" },
		summary = "The images may represent electrical [[Waveform|waveforms]] on an [[Oscilloscope|oscilloscope]], a frame of [[Video|video]] on an analog [[Television set|television set]] (TV), digital raster graphics on a [[Computer monitor|computer monitor]], or other phenomena like [[Radar|radar]] targets. A CRT in a TV is commonly called a **picture tube**. CRTs have also been used as memory devices, in which case the screen is not intended to be visible to an observer. The term cathode ray was used to describe electron beams when they were first discovered, before it was understood that what was emitted from the cathode was a beam of electrons.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A CRT works by electrically heating a tungsten coil which in turn heats a cathode in the rear of the CRT, causing it to emit electrons which are modulated and focused by electrodes. The electrons are steered by deflection coils or plates, and an anode accelerates them towards the [[Phosphor|phosphor]]-coated screen, which generates light when hit by the electrons. The entire front area of the tube is scanned repeatedly and systematically in a fixed pattern called a raster. In color devices, an image is produced by controlling the intensity of each of three electron beams, one for each additive [[Primary color|primary color]] (red, green, and blue). In modern CRT monitors and TVs, the beams are bent by magnetic deflection, using a deflection yoke. Electrostatic deflection is commonly used in [[Oscilloscope|oscilloscopes]]. The tube is a glass envelope that is heavy, fragile, and long from the front screen face to its rear end.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cavity magnetron",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Device for generating microwaves",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Components" },
		summary = "The **cavity magnetron** is a high-power [[Vacuum tube|vacuum tube]] used in early [[Radar|radar]] systems and subsequently in [[Microwave oven|microwave ovens]] and in linear particle accelerators. A cavity magnetron generates [[Microwave|microwaves]] using the interaction of a stream of [[Electron|electrons]] with a [[Magnetic field|magnetic field]], while moving past a series of cavity resonators, which are small, open cavities in a metal block. Electrons pass by the cavities and cause microwaves to oscillate within, similar to the functioning of a whistle producing a tone when excited by an air stream blown past its opening. The resonant frequency of the arrangement is determined by the cavities' physical dimensions.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Unlike other vacuum tubes, such as a klystron or a traveling-wave tube (TWT), the magnetron cannot function as an [[Amplifier|amplifier]] for increasing the intensity of an applied microwave signal; the magnetron serves solely as an electronic oscillator generating a microwave signal from direct-current electricity supplied to the vacuum tube. The use of magnetic fields as a means to control the flow of an electric current was spurred by the invention of the Audion by [[Lee de Forest]] in 1906. Albert Hull of General Electric Research Laboratory, USA, began development of magnetrons to avoid de Forest's patents, but these were never completely successful. Other experimenters picked up on Hull's work and a key advance, the use of two cathodes, was introduced by Habann in Germany in 1924.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Central processing unit",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Central computer component that executes instructions",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Computer hardware" },
		aliases = { "CPU", "Central processor", "Main processor" },
		summary = "A **central processing unit** (**CPU**), also known as a **central processor**, **main processor**, or simply **processor**, is the primary processor in a given [[Computer|computer]]. Its [[Electronic circuit|electronic circuitry]] executes instructions of a [[Computer program|computer program]], such as [[Arithmetic|arithmetic]], logic, controlling, and input/output (I/O) operations. This role contrasts with that of external components, such as main memory and I/O circuitry, and specialized coprocessors such as graphics processing units (GPUs).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The form, design, and implementation of CPUs have changed over time, but their fundamental operation remains almost unchanged. Modern CPUs devote a lot of semiconductor area to caches and instruction-level parallelism to increase performance and to CPU modes to support [[Operating system|operating systems]] and virtualization. Most modern CPUs are implemented on [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuit]] (IC) [[Microprocessor|microprocessors]], with one or more CPUs on a single IC chip. Microprocessor chips with multiple CPUs are called multi-core processors (MCPs). The individual physical CPUs, called processor cores, can also be multithreaded to support CPU-level multithreading. An IC that contains a CPU may also contain [[Computer memory|memory]], peripheral interfaces, and other components of a computer; such integrated devices are variously called microcontrollers or systems on chip (SoCs).",
			},
		},
		related = { "ENIAC" },
	},
	{
		title = "Ceramic",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Inorganic, nonmetallic solid prepared by the action of heat",
		tags = { "Industry", "Material and chemical" },
		summary = "A **ceramic** is any of the various hard, brittle, heat-resistant, and corrosion-resistant [[Material|materials]] made by shaping and then firing an inorganic, nonmetallic material, such as [[Clay|clay]], at a high temperature. Common examples are earthenware, [[Porcelain|porcelain]], and [[Brick|brick]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'The earliest ceramics made by humans were fired clay bricks used for building house walls and other structures. Other [[Pottery|pottery]] objects such as pots, vessels, vases and [[Figurine|figurines]] were made from [[Clay|clay]], either by itself or mixed with other materials like [[Silicon dioxide|silica]], hardened by sintering in fire. Later, ceramics were glazed and fired to create smooth, colored surfaces, decreasing porosity through the use of glassy, amorphous ceramic coatings on top of the crystalline ceramic substrates. Ceramics now include domestic, industrial, and building products, as well as a wide range of materials developed for use in advanced ceramic engineering, such as [[Semiconductor|semiconductors]]. The word ceramic comes from the [[Ancient Greek]] word, meaning "of or for [[Pottery|pottery]]". The earliest known mention of the root ceram- is the Mycenaean Greek ke-ra-me-we, workers of ceramic, written in [[Linear B]] syllabic script.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Charcoal",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚫",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Lightweight black carbon residue",
		tags = { "Industry", "Energy and fuel" },
		summary = "**Charcoal** is a lightweight black residue made of [[Carbon|carbon]] that is produced by strongly heating [[Wood|wood]] (or other [[Animal|animal]] and [[Plant|plant]] materials) in minimal [[Oxygen|oxygen]] to remove all [[Water|water]] and [[Volatility (chemistry)|volatile constituents]]. In the traditional version of this pyrolysis process, called charcoal burning, often by forming a charcoal kiln, the heat is supplied by burning part of the starting material itself, with a limited supply of [[Oxygen|oxygen]]. The material can also be heated in a closed retort. Modern charcoal briquettes used for outdoor cooking may contain many other additives, e.g. [[Coal|coal]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Charcoal has diverse applications, including as various fuels such as industrial fuel, [[Cooking|cooking]] and heating fuels, and [[Metallurgy|metallurgical]] fuel in [[Iron|iron]] and [[Steel|steel]] production, as well as a reducing agent in chemical processes, and as a raw material in pyrotechnics. It is also utilized in [[Cosmetics|cosmetics]], [[Horticulture|horticulture]], [[Animal husbandry|animal husbandry]], [[Medicine|medicine]] using activated charcoal, and environmental sustainability efforts, such as [[Carbon sequestration|carbon sequestration]]. The early history of wood charcoal production spans [[Ancient history|ancient times]], rooted in the abundance of wood in various regions. The process typically involves stacking wood billets to form a conical pile, allowing air to enter through openings at the bottom, and igniting the pile gradually. Charcoal burners, skilled professionals tasked with managing the delicate operation, often lived in isolation to tend their wood piles.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Chemical plant",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Industrial process plant that manufactures chemicals",
		tags = { "Industry", "Material and chemical" },
		summary = "A **chemical plant** is an industrial process [[Factory|plant]] that manufactures (or otherwise processes) chemicals usually on a large scale. The general objective of a chemical plant is to create new material wealth via the chemical or biological transformation and or separation of materials. Chemical plants use specialized equipment, units, and technology in the manufacturing process.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Other kinds of plants, such as polymer, pharmaceutical, food, and some beverage production facilities, power plants, [[Oil refinery|oil refineries]] or other [[Oil refinery|refineries]], natural gas processing and biochemical plants, [[Water|water]] and wastewater treatment, and [[Pollution|pollution]] control equipment use many technologies that have similarities to chemical plant technology such as fluid systems and chemical reactor systems. Some would consider an [[Oil refinery|oil refinery]] or a pharmaceutical or [[Polymer|polymer]] manufacturer to be effectively a chemical plant. [[Petrochemical]] plants (plants using chemicals from petroleum as a raw material or feedstock) are usually located adjacent to an [[Oil refinery|oil refinery]] to minimize [[Transport|transportation]] costs for the feedstocks produced by the refinery. Speciality chemical and fine chemical plants are usually much smaller and not as sensitive to location.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Circuit breaker",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Automatic circuit protection device",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Components" },
		summary = "A **circuit breaker** is an electrical safety device designed to protect an [[Electrical network|electrical circuit]] from damage caused by current in excess of that which the equipment can safely carry (overcurrent). Its basic function is to interrupt current flow to protect equipment and to prevent [[Fire|fire]]. Unlike a [[Fuse (electrical)|fuse]], which interrupts once and then must be replaced, a circuit breaker can be reset (either manually or automatically) to resume normal operation.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Circuit breakers are commonly installed in distribution boards. Apart from its safety purpose, a circuit breaker is also often used as a main [[Nintendo Switch|switch]] to manually disconnect ("rack out") and connect ("rack in") electrical power to a whole electrical sub-network. Circuit breakers are made in varying current ratings, from devices that protect low-current circuits or individual household appliances, to switchgear designed to protect high-voltage circuits feeding an entire city. Any device which protects against excessive current by automatically removing power from a faulty system, such as a circuit breaker or [[Fuse (electrical)|fuse]], can be referred to as an over-current protection device (OCPD).',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Circuit design",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "📐",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Engineering process",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Basics" },
		summary = 'In [[Electrical engineering|electrical engineering]], the process of **circuit design** can cover systems ranging from complex [[Electronics|electronic]] systems down to the individual [[Transistor|transistors]] within an [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuit]]. One person can often do the design process without needing a planned or structured design process for simple circuits. Still, teams of designers following a systematic approach with intelligently guided [[Computer simulation|computer simulation]] are becoming increasingly common for more complex designs. In integrated circuit design automation, the term "circuit design" often refers to the step of the design cycle which outputs the schematics of the integrated circuit. Typically this is the step between logic design and physical design.',
	},
	{
		title = "Cloning",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Process of producing individual organisms with identical genomes",
		tags = { "Biotechnology" },
		summary = "**Cloning** is the process of producing individual [[Organism|organisms]] with identical [[Genome|genomes]], by either natural or artificial means. In nature, some organisms produce clones through [[Asexual reproduction|asexual reproduction]]; this reproduction of an organism by itself without a mate is known as parthenogenesis. In the field of [[Biotechnology|biotechnology]], cloning is the process of creating cloned organisms of [[Cell (biology)|cells]] and of DNA fragments.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The artificial cloning of organisms, sometimes known as reproductive cloning, is often accomplished via somatic-cell nuclear transfer (SCNT), a cloning method in which a viable [[Embryo|embryo]] is created from a somatic cell and an egg cell. In 1996, Dolly the sheep achieved notoriety for being the first mammal cloned from an adult somatic cell. Another example of artificial cloning is molecular cloning, a technique in [[Molecular biology|molecular biology]] in which a single living cell is used to clone a large population of cells that contain identical DNA molecules. In bioethics, there are a variety of ethical positions regarding the practice and possibilities of cloning. The use of embryonic stem cells, which can be produced through SCNT, in some stem cell research has attracted controversy. Cloning has been proposed as a means of reviving extinct species.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Polymerase chain reaction" },
	},
	{
		title = "Coal",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🪨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Combustible sedimentary rock composed primarily of carbon",
		tags = { "Industry", "Energy and fuel" },
		summary = "**Coal** is a combustible black or brownish-black [[Sedimentary rock|sedimentary rock]], formed as [[Stratum|layers]] called coal seams. Coal is mostly [[Carbon|carbon]] with variable amounts of other [[Chemical element|elements]], chiefly [[Hydrogen|hydrogen]], [[Sulfur|sulfur]], [[Oxygen|oxygen]], and [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]]. It is a [[Fossil fuel|fossil fuel]], formed when [[Organic matter|plants decay]] into [[Peat|peat]] which is converted into coal by the heat and pressure of deep burial over millions of years. Vast deposits formed from [[Wetland|wetlands]] called coal forests that covered much of the [[Tropics|tropics]] during the late Carboniferous and early [[Permian]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Coal is used primarily as a fuel. While coal has been known and used for thousands of years, its usage was limited until the [[Industrial Revolution]]. With the invention of the [[Steam engine|steam engine]], coal consumption increased. In 2020, coal supplied about a quarter of the world's primary energy and over a third of its [[Electricity generation|electricity]]. Some [[Iron|iron]] and [[Steel|steel]]-making and other industrial processes burn coal. The extraction and burning of coal damages the environment and human health, causing premature death and illness, and is the largest source of carbon dioxide contributing to [[Climate change|climate change]]. Over fifteen billion tonnes of carbon dioxide were emitted by burning coal in 2024, which was more than a quarter of total global [[Greenhouse gas emissions|greenhouse gas emissions]]. As part of worldwide energy transition, many countries have reduced or eliminated their use of coal power.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Sedimentary" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Communications satellite",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🛰️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Artificial satellite that relays radio signals",
		tags = { "Media and communication" },
		summary = "A **communications satellite** is an [[Satellite|artificial satellite]] that relays and amplifies [[Radio|radio]] telecommunication signals via a transponder; it creates a communication channel between a source transmitter and a [[Radio receiver|receiver]] at different locations on [[Earth]]. Communications satellites are used for [[Satellite television|television]], [[Telephone|telephone]], [[Radio|radio]], [[Internet|internet]], and military applications. Some communications satellites are in [[Geostationary orbit|geostationary orbit]] 22236 mi above the [[Equator|equator]], so that the satellite appears stationary at the same point in the sky; therefore the satellite dish antennas of ground stations can be aimed permanently at that spot and do not have to move to track the satellite.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'However, most form satellite constellations in [[Low Earth orbit|low Earth orbit]], where ground antennas must track the satellites and switch between them frequently. The [[Radio wave|radio waves]] used for telecommunications links travel by line of sight and so are obstructed by the curve of the Earth. The purpose of communications satellites is to relay the signal around the curve of the Earth allowing communication between widely separated geographical points. Communications satellites operate across a wide range of radio and [[Microwave|microwave]] frequencies. To avoid signal interference, international organizations have regulations for which frequency ranges or "bands" certain organizations are allowed to use. This allocation of bands minimizes the risk of signal interference.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Typewriter" },
	},
	{
		title = "Composite material",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Material made from a combination of two or more unlike substances",
		tags = { "Industry", "Material and chemical" },
		aliases = { "Composite", "Composition material" },
		summary = "A **composite** or **composite material** (also **composition material**) is a [[Material|material]] which is produced from two or more constituent materials. These constituent materials have notably dissimilar chemical or physical properties and are merged to create a material with properties unlike the individual elements. Within the finished structure, the individual elements remain separate and distinct, distinguishing composites from [[Mixture|mixtures]] and solid solutions. Composite materials with more than one distinct layer are called composite laminates.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Typical engineered composite materials are made up of a binding agent forming the matrix and a filler material (particulates or fibres) giving substance, e.g.: [[Concrete]], [[Reinforced concrete|reinforced concrete]] and [[Masonry|masonry]] with cement, lime or [[Mortar (masonry)|mortar]] (which is itself a composite material) as a binder Composite wood such as glulam and plywood with wood glue as a binder Reinforced plastics, such as [[Fiberglass|fiberglass]] and fibre-reinforced polymer with [[Resin|resin]] or thermoplastics as a binder Ceramic matrix composites (composite ceramic and metal matrices) Metal matrix composites Advanced composite materials, often first developed for [[Spacecraft|spacecraft]] and [[Airplane|aircraft]] applications Composite materials can be less expensive, lighter, stronger or more durable than common materials. Some are inspired by biological structures found in plants and animals.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Computer architecture",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🏛️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Set of rules describing computer system",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Computer science" },
		summary = "In [[Computer science|computer science]] and [[Computer engineering|computer engineering]], a **computer architecture** is the conceptual design and operational structure of a [[Computer|computer]] system that define how component parts are organized and interact to execute [[Computer program|programs]] efficiently. It is often a general description that ignores precise implementation details. It covers the instruction set architecture, CPU microarchitecture, [[Computer memory|memory]], and input/output systems.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Computer architecture also considers tradeoffs between performance, cost, power, reliability, and security.",
			},
