	{
		title = "Consumer electronics",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Electronic products for everyday use",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Home electronics" },
		summary = '**Consumer electronics**, also known as **home electronics**, are electronic devices intended for everyday household use. Consumer electronics include those used for [[Entertainment|entertainment]], [[Communication|communications]], and [[Recreation|recreation]]. Historically, these products were referred to as **"black goods"** in American English due to many products being housed in black or dark casings. This term is used to distinguish them from "white goods", which are meant for housekeeping tasks, such as [[Washing machine|washing machines]] and [[Refrigerator|refrigerators]]. In British English, they are often called **"brown goods"** by producers and sellers.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Since the 2010s, this distinction has been absent in big box consumer electronics stores, whose inventories include entertainment, communication, and home office devices, as well as home appliances. [[Radio broadcasting]] in the early 20th century brought the first major consumer product, the [[Radio receiver|broadcast receiver]]. Later products included [[Telephone|telephones]], [[Television|televisions]], [[Calculator|calculators]], [[Camera|cameras]], [[Video game console|video game consoles]], [[Smartphone|mobile phones]], [[Personal computer|personal computers]], and MP3 players. In the 2010s, consumer electronics stores often sold [[GPS]], automotive electronics (vehicle audio), video game consoles, [[Electronic musical instrument|electronic musical instruments]] such as [[Synthesizer|synthesizer]] keyboards, [[Karaoke|karaoke]] machines, [[Digital camera|digital cameras]], and video players. Such video playing equipment included VCRs in the 1980s and 1990s, and eventually DVD players and Blu-ray players. Stores also sold smart light fixtures, network devices, camcorders, and [[Smartphone|smartphones]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cooling tower",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🗼",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Device which rejects waste heat to the atmosphere through the cooling of a water stream",
		tags = { "Energy and fuel", "Electricity generation and distribution" },
		summary = "A **cooling tower** is a device that rejects waste heat to the [[Atmosphere|atmosphere]] through the cooling of a coolant stream, usually a water stream, to a lower temperature. Cooling towers may either use the [[Evaporation|evaporation]] of water to remove heat and cool the working fluid to near the wet-bulb air temperature or, in the case of dry cooling towers, rely solely on air to cool the working fluid to near the dry-bulb air temperature using radiators.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Common applications include cooling the circulating water used in oil refineries, [[Petrochemical|petrochemical]] and other [[Chemical plant|chemical plants]], thermal power stations, nuclear power stations and HVAC systems for cooling buildings. The classification is based on the type of air induction into the tower: the main types of cooling towers are natural draft and induced draft cooling towers. Cooling towers vary in size from small roof-top units to very large hyperboloid structures that can be up to 200 m tall and 100 m in diameter, or rectangular structures that can be over 40 m tall and 80 m long. Hyperboloid cooling towers are often associated with [[Nuclear power plant|nuclear power plants]], although they are also used in many coal-fired plants and to some extent in some large chemical and other industrial plants. The [[Steam turbine|steam turbine]] is what necessitates the cooling tower to condense and recirculate the water.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Power station", "Electric light" },
	},
	{
		title = "Crop",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Plant product which can be grown and harvested",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Basics" },
		summary = "A **crop** is a plant or plant product harvested for human use. Crops are cultivated at scale to produce food, fiber, fuel, and other products. Crops have been central to human civilization since the first agricultural revolution, a key stage in the broader [[History of agriculture|history of agriculture]], when early societies domesticated plants for food and trade. Today, a small number of [[Staple food|staple crops]] such as rice, wheat, maize, and sugarcane account for the majority of global production. Because of their economic importance, crops are studied within several scientific disciplines, including [[Agronomy|agronomy]], [[Agricultural science|agricultural science]], [[Horticulture|horticulture]], and [[Forestry|forestry]].",
	},
	{
		title = "Cryptography",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Practice and study of secure communication techniques",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Cryptography" },
		aliases = { "Cryptology" },
		summary = "**Cryptography**, or **cryptology**, is the practice and study of techniques for secure communication in the presence of adversarial behavior. More generally, cryptography is about constructing and analyzing protocols that prevent third parties or the public from reading private messages. Modern cryptography exists at the intersection of the disciplines of [[Mathematics|mathematics]], [[Computer science|computer science]], information security, [[Electrical engineering|electrical engineering]], digital signal processing, physics, and others. Core concepts related to information security (data confidentiality, data integrity, [[Authentication|authentication]] and non-repudiation) are also central to cryptography.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Practical applications of cryptography include electronic commerce, chip-based payment cards, digital currencies, computer passwords and military communications. Cryptography prior to the modern age was effectively synonymous with [[Encryption|encryption]], converting readable information (plaintext) to unintelligible nonsense text (ciphertext), which can only be read by reversing the process (decryption). The sender of an encrypted (coded) message shares the decryption (decoding) technique only with the intended recipients to preclude access from adversaries. The cryptography literature often uses the names "Alice" (or "A") for the sender, "Bob" (or "B") for the intended recipient, and "Eve" (or "E") for the eavesdropping adversary.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Cultivar",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Plant or grouping of plants selected for desirable characteristics",
		tags = { "Agronomy and horticulture", "Basics" },
		summary = 'A **cultivar** is a kind of [[Horticulture|cultivated]] plant that people have selected for desired [[Phenotypic trait|traits]] and which retains those traits when propagated. Methods used to propagate cultivars include division, root and stem cuttings, offsets, grafting, tissue culture, or carefully controlled seed production. Most cultivars arise from deliberate human [[Genetic engineering|manipulation]], but some originate from [[Native species|wild plants]] that have distinctive characteristics. Cultivar names are chosen according to rules of the International Code of Nomenclature for Cultivated Plants (ICNCP), and not all cultivated plants qualify as cultivars. Horticulturists generally believe the word cultivar was coined as a term meaning "**cultivated variety**".',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Popular [[Ornamental plant|ornamental plants]] like [[Rose|roses]], [[Camellia|camellias]], [[Narcissus (plant)|daffodils]], [[Rhododendron|rhododendrons]], and [[Azalea|azaleas]] are commonly cultivars produced by [[Selective breeding|breeding and selection]] or as sports, for floral colour or size, plant form, or other desirable characteristics. Since the advent of [[Genetic engineering|genetic engineering]] in the 1970s and the rise of its application in crop breeding in the 1980s, very few wild plants are used as commercial food sources. Trees used in [[Forestry|forestry]] are also special selections grown for their enhanced quality and yield of [[Lumber|timber]], for example American timber company Weyerhaeuser is the leading grower of genetically modified [[Douglas fir|Douglas-fir]] trees, one of the most commonly harvested trees. Cultivars form a major part of Liberty Hyde Bailey's broader group, the cultigen, which is defined as a plant whose origin or selection is primarily due to intentional human activity.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Dairy",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🥛",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Place where milk is stored and where butter, cheese and yoghurt are made or sold",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Forms" },
		aliases = { "Dairy farm" },
		summary = "A **dairy farm** is a place where [[Milk|milk]] is stored and where [[Butter|butter]], [[Cheese|cheese]], and other dairy products are made, or a place where those products are sold. It may be a room, a building, or a larger establishment. In the United States, the word may also describe a dairy farm or the part of a mixed farm dedicated to milk for human consumption, whether from [[Cattle|cows]], [[Water buffalo|buffaloes]], [[Goat|goats]], [[Yak|yaks]], [[Sheep|sheep]], [[Horse|horses]] or [[Camel|camels]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The attributive dairy describes milk-based products, derivatives, and processes, and the animals and workers involved in their production, for example dairyman, dairymaid, dairy cattle or dairy goat. A dairy farm produces milk and a dairy factory processes it into a variety of dairy products. These establishments constitute the global dairy industry, part of the [[Food industry|food industry]]. The word dairy comes from an Old English word for female servant, as milking was historically done by dairymaids.",
			},
