		related = { "Qanat" },
	},
	{
		title = "Die casting",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Metal casting process",
		tags = { "Industry", "Metallurgy" },
		summary = "**Die casting** is a metal casting process that is characterized by forcing molten [[Metal|metal]] under high pressure into a mold cavity. The mold cavity is created using two hardened tool steel dies which have been machined into shape and work similarly to an injection mold during the process. Most die castings are made from non-ferrous metals, specifically [[Zinc|zinc]], [[Copper|copper]], [[Aluminium|aluminium]], [[Magnesium|magnesium]], [[Lead|lead]], pewter, and [[Tin|tin]]-based alloys. Depending on the type of metal being cast, a hot- or cold-chamber machine is used.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The casting equipment and the metal dies represent large capital costs and this tends to limit the process to high-volume production. Manufacture of parts using die casting is relatively simple, involving only four main steps, which keeps the incremental cost per item low. It is especially suited for a large quantity of small- to medium-sized castings, which is why die casting produces more castings than any other casting process. Die castings are characterized by a very good surface finish (by casting standards) and dimensional consistency.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Rolling (metalworking)" },
	},
	{
		title = "Digital data",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Discrete, discontinuous representation of information",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Digital information" },
		summary = "**Digital data** or **digital information**, in [[Information theory|information theory]] and information systems, is [[Data|data]] or [[Information|information]] represented as a string of [[Discrete mathematics|discrete]] symbols, each of which can take on one of only a finite number of values from some alphabet, such as letters or digits. An example is a text document, which consists of a string of alphanumeric characters. The most common form of digital data in modern information systems is binary data, which is represented by a string of binary digits (bits) each of which can have one of two values, either 0 or 1.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Digital data can be contrasted with analog data, which is represented by a value from a continuous range of [[Real number|real numbers]]. Analog data is transmitted by an [[Analog signal|analog signal]], which not only takes on continuous values but can vary continuously with time, a continuous real-valued function of time. An example is the air pressure variation in a [[Sound|sound wave]]. Data requires interpretation to become [[Information|information]]. In modern (post-1960) computer systems, all data is digital. Digital data also serves as the main input for data science, where data is collected, [[Data processing|processed]], analyzed, and modeled using methods from [[Statistics|statistics]], [[Computer science|computer science]] and [[Artificial intelligence|machine learning]] for pattern recognition and support [[Decision-making|decision-making]]. The word digital comes from the same source as the words digit and digitus (the [[Latin]] word for finger), as fingers are often used for counting.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Diode",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Two-terminal electronic component",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Components" },
		summary = "A **diode** is a two-[[Electric terminal|terminal]] [[Electronic component|electronic component]] that conducts [[Electric current|electric current]] primarily in one direction (asymmetric [[Electrical resistance and conductance|conductance]]). It has low (ideally zero) [[Electrical resistance and conductance|resistance]] in one direction and high (ideally infinite) resistance in the other.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"A [[Semiconductor|semiconductor]] diode, the most commonly used type today, is a crystalline piece of semiconductor material with a p–n junction connected to two electrical terminals. It has an exponential current–voltage characteristic. Semiconductor diodes were the first [[Semiconductor device|semiconductor electronic devices]]. The discovery of asymmetric electrical conduction across the contact between a [[Crystal|crystalline]] mineral and a metal was made by German physicist Ferdinand Braun in 1874. Today, most diodes are made of [[Silicon|silicon]], but other semiconducting materials such as gallium arsenide and [[Germanium|germanium]] are also used. The obsolete **thermionic diode** is a [[Vacuum tube|vacuum tube]] with two [[Electrode|electrodes]], a heated [[Cathode|cathode]] and a plate, in which [[Electron|electrons]] can flow in only one direction, from the cathode to the plate.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Active" },
			{ "Inventor", "Jagadish Chandra Bose" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Domestication",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Selective breeding of plants and animals to serve humans",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Animal husbandry" },
		aliases = { "Domesticated" },
		summary = "**Domestication** is a multi-generational [[Mutualism (biology)|mutualistic]] relationship in which an animal species, such as [[Human|humans]] or leafcutter ants, takes over control and care of another species, such as sheep or [[Mushroom|fungi]], to obtain from them a steady supply of resources, such as [[Meat|meat]], [[Milk|milk]], or labor. The process is gradual and geographically diffuse, based on trial and error. Domestication affects [[Gene|genes]] for behavior in animals, making them less aggressive. In plants, domestication affects genes for [[Morphology (biology)|morphology]], such as increasing seed size and stopping the shattering of cereal seedheads. Such changes both make domesticated [[Organism|organisms]] easier to handle and reduce their ability to survive in the wild.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"[[Terrestrial animal|Terrestrial]] [[Vertebrate|vertebrates]] are the first animals to be domesticated by humans, with the earliest domestication of vertebrates being the domestication of dogs from [[Wolf|wolves]], who diverged from their wild relatives and developed a [[Commensalism|commensal]] relationship with [[Upper Paleolithic]]/[[Mesolithic]] humans at least 15,000 years ago during the [[Late Pleistocene]]. Other animals, including [[Goat|goats]], [[Sheep|sheep]], and cows, were domesticated around 11,000 years ago. Among birds, the [[Chicken|chicken]] was first domesticated in [[East Asia]] at least 3,250 years ago. The [[Horse|horse]] came under domestication around 5,500 years ago in [[Central Asia|central Asia]] as a riding animal.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "ENIAC",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "First electronic general-purpose digital computer",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Computer hardware" },
		summary = '**ENIAC** was the first [[Computer programming|programmable]], [[Electronics|electronic]], general-purpose digital computer, completed in 1945. Other computers had some of these features, but ENIAC was the first to have them all. ENIAC was Turing-complete and able to solve "a large class of numerical problems" through reprogramming.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'ENIAC was designed by John Mauchly and J. Presper Eckert to calculate artillery firing tables for the United States Army\'s Ballistic Research Laboratory (which later became a part of the Army Research Laboratory). ENIAC was completed in 1945 and first put to work for practical purposes on December 10, 1945. ENIAC was formally dedicated at the [[University of Pennsylvania]] on February 15, 1946, having cost $487,000, and called a "Giant Brain" by the press. It had a speed on the order of one thousand times faster than that of electro-mechanical machines. ENIAC was formally accepted by the U.S. Army Ordnance Corps in July 1946. It was transferred to Aberdeen Proving Ground in Aberdeen, Maryland in 1947, where it was in continuous operation until 1955.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Computer hardware", "Central processing unit" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Built", "1945" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electric battery",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔋",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Power supply with electrochemical cells",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Components" },
		aliases = { "Battery (electricity)" },
		summary = "An **electric battery** is a source of [[Electric power|electric power]] consisting of one or more electrochemical cells with external connections for powering electrical devices. When a battery is supplying power, its positive [[Electric terminal|terminal]] is the [[Cathode|cathode]] and its negative terminal is the [[Anode|anode]]. The terminal marked negative is the source of [[Electron|electrons]]. When a battery is connected to an external electric load, those negatively charged electrons flow through the circuit and reach the positive terminal, thus causing a [[Redox|redox]] reaction by attracting positively charged [[Ion|ions]], or cations.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Thus, higher energy reactants are converted to lower energy products, and the [[Gibbs free energy|free-energy]] difference is delivered to the external circuit as electrical energy. Historically the term "battery" specifically referred to a device composed of multiple cells; however, the usage has evolved to include devices composed of a single cell. Primary (single-use or "disposable") batteries are used once and discarded, as the [[Electrode|electrode]] materials are irreversibly changed during discharge; a common example is the alkaline battery used for flashlights and a multitude of portable electronic devices. Secondary (rechargeable) batteries can be discharged and recharged multiple times using an applied electric current; the original composition of the electrodes can be restored by reverse current.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Active" },
			{ "Inventor", "[[Alessandro Volta]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electric generator",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Device that converts other energy to electrical energy",
		tags = { "Energy and fuel", "Electricity generation and distribution" },
		aliases = { "Generator", "Electrical generator", "Electromagnetic generator" },
		summary = "In [[Electricity generation|electricity generation]], a **generator**, also called an **electric generator**, **electrical generator**, and **electromagnetic generator** is an electromechanical device that converts [[Mechanical energy|mechanical energy]] to [[Electrical energy|electrical energy]] for use in an external [[Electrical network|circuit]]. In most generators which are rotating machines, a source of kinetic power rotates the generator's shaft, and the generator produces an [[Electric current|electric current]] at its output terminals which flows through an external circuit, powering electrical loads. Sources of mechanical energy used to drive generators include [[Steam turbine|steam turbines]], gas turbines, water turbines, [[Internal combustion engine|internal combustion engines]], [[Wind turbine|wind turbines]] and even hand cranks.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Generators produce nearly all of the electric power for worldwide electric power grids. The first electromagnetic generator, the Faraday disk, was invented in 1831 by British scientist [[Michael Faraday]]. The reverse conversion of electrical energy into mechanical energy is done by an [[Electric motor|electric motor]], and motors and generators are very similar. Some motors can be used in a "backward" sense as generators, if their shaft is rotated they will generate electric power. In addition to its most common usage for electromechanical generators described above, the term generator is also used for photovoltaic, fuel cell, and magnetohydrodynamic powered devices that use [[Solar power|solar power]] and chemical fuels, respectively, to generate electrical power.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Gas-turbine engine" },
	},
	{
		title = "Electric light",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Device for producing light from electricity",
		tags = { "Energy and fuel", "Electricity generation and distribution" },
		aliases = { "Lamp" },
		summary = "An **electric light**, **lamp**, or **light bulb** is an electrical device that produces [[Light|light]] from [[Electricity|electricity]]. It is the most common form of artificial [[Lighting|lighting]]. Lamps usually have a base made of [[Ceramic|ceramic]], [[Metal|metal]], [[Glass|glass]], or [[Plastic|plastic]] that secures them in the socket of a light fixture. The electrical connection to the socket may be made with a screw-thread base, two metal pins, two metal caps or a bayonet mount.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The three main categories of electric lights are incandescent lamps, which produce light by a [[Light bulb|filament]] heated white-hot by [[Electric current|electric current]], gas-discharge lamps, which produce light by means of an electric arc through a gas, such as fluorescent lamps, and LED lamps, which produce light by a flow of electrons across a band gap in a [[Semiconductor|semiconductor]]. Modern electric [[Light|light sources]] come in a profusion of types and sizes adapted to many applications. Most modern electric lighting is powered by centrally generated electric power, but lighting may also be powered by mobile or standby electric generators or battery systems. [[Electric battery|Battery]]-powered light is often reserved for when and where stationary lights fail, often in the form of flashlights or electric lanterns, as well as in vehicles.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Inventor", "[[Humphry Davy]]" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electric power distribution",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Final stage of electricity delivery to individual consumers in a power grid",
		tags = { "Energy and fuel", "Electricity generation and distribution" },
		summary = "**Electric power distribution** is the final stage in the delivery of electricity. Electricity is carried from the [[Electric power transmission|transmission system]] to individual consumers. Distribution substations connect to the transmission system and lower the transmission voltage to **medium voltage** ranging between 2 kV and 33 kV with the use of [[Transformer|transformers]]. Primary distribution lines carry this medium voltage power to distribution transformers located near the customer's premises. Distribution transformers again lower the voltage to the utilization voltage used by lighting, industrial equipment and household appliances. Often several customers are supplied from one transformer through secondary distribution lines.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Commercial and residential customers are connected to the secondary distribution lines through service drops. Customers demanding a much larger amount of power may be connected directly to the primary distribution level or the subtransmission level. The transition from transmission to distribution happens in a power substation, which has the following functions: [[Circuit breaker|Circuit breakers]] and switches enable the substation to be disconnected from the [[Electrical grid|transmission grid]] or for distribution lines to be disconnected. Transformers step down transmission voltages, 35 kV or more, down to primary distribution voltages. These are medium voltage circuits, usually 600 V. From the transformer, power goes to the busbar that can split the distribution power off in multiple directions. The bus distributes power to distribution lines, which fan out to customers.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Gas-turbine engine" },
	},
	{
		title = "Electric power transmission",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Bulk movement of electrical energy",
		tags = { "Energy and fuel", "Electricity generation and distribution" },
		summary = "**Electric power transmission** is the bulk movement of [[Electrical energy|electrical energy]] from a generating site, such as a power plant, to an electrical substation. A long conductor used to facilitate such movement is called a transmission line. The interconnected transmission lines form a transmission network. In the power industry, electric power transmission is distinct from the local wiring between high-voltage substations and customers, which is typically referred to as [[Electric power distribution|electric power distribution]], even though power distribution is semantically a type of power transmission in common parlance. The combined transmission and distribution network is part of electricity delivery, known as the [[Electrical grid|electrical grid]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Transmission lines transmit either [[Alternating current|alternating current]] (AC) or [[Direct current|direct current]] (DC). To increase the efficiency of long-distance electric power transmission, the [[Voltage|voltage]] is often increased for transmission, then reduced for local distribution. This is because higher voltages corresponds to lower [[Electric current|currents]] and lower losses caused by such currents. The AC voltage level is often changed with [[Transformer|transformers]]. A wide area synchronous grid, known as an interconnection in North America, directly connects generators delivering AC power with the same relative frequency to many consumers. North America has four major interconnections: Western, Eastern, Quebec and Texas. One grid connects most of continental Europe.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electrical connector",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Device used to join electrical conductors",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Components" },
		aliases = { "Electrically connected" },
		summary = "Components of an [[Electrical network|electrical circuit]] are **electrically connected** if an [[Electric current|electric current]] can run between them through an [[Electrical conductor|electrical conductor]]. An **electrical connector** is an electromechanical device used to create an electrical connection between parts of an electrical circuit, or between different electrical circuits, thereby joining them into a larger circuit.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The connection may be removable (as for portable equipment), require a tool for assembly and removal, or serve as a permanent electrical joint between two points. An adapter can be used to join dissimilar connectors. Most electrical connectors have a gender – i.e. the male component, called a plug, connects to the female component, or socket. Thousands of configurations of connectors are manufactured for power, data, and audiovisual applications.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Electrical grid",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Interconnected network for delivering electricity to consumers",
		tags = { "Energy and fuel", "Electricity generation and distribution" },
		aliases = { "Electricity network" },
		summary = "An **electrical grid** (or **electricity network**) is an interconnected network for electricity delivery from producers to consumers. Electrical grids consist of [[Power station|power stations]], electrical substations to step [[Voltage|voltage]] up or down, [[Electric power transmission|electric power transmission]] to carry power over long distances, and finally [[Electric power distribution|electric power distribution]] to customers. In that last step, voltage is stepped down again to the required service voltage. Power stations are typically built close to energy sources and far from densely populated areas. Electrical grids vary in size and can cover whole countries or continents. From small to large there are microgrids, wide area synchronous grids, and super grids.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The combined transmission and distribution network is part of electricity delivery, known as the power grid. Grids are nearly always synchronous, meaning all distribution areas operate with three phase [[Alternating current|alternating current]] (AC) frequencies synchronized (so that voltage swings occur at almost the same time). This allows transmission of AC power throughout the area, connecting the electricity generators with consumers. Grids can enable more efficient electricity markets. Although electrical grids are widespread, As of 2016, 1.4 billion people worldwide were not connected to an electricity grid. As [[Electrification|electrification]] increases, the number of people with access to grid electricity is growing. About 840 million people (mostly in Africa), which is ca. 11% of the World's population, had no access to grid electricity in 2017, down from 1.2 billion in 2010.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cooling tower" },
	},
	{
		title = "Electricity generation",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Process of generating electrical power",
		tags = { "Energy and fuel", "Electricity generation and distribution" },
		summary = "**Electricity generation** is the process of generating [[Electric power|electric power]] from sources of primary energy. For utilities in the electric power industry, it is the stage prior to its delivery ([[Electric power transmission|transmission]], [[Electric power distribution|distribution]], etc.) to end users or its storage, using for example, the pumped-storage method.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Consumable electricity is not freely available in nature, so it must be "produced", transforming other forms of energy to electricity. Production is carried out in [[Power station|power stations]], also called "power plants". Electricity is most often generated at a power plant by electromechanical [[Electric generator|generators]], primarily driven by [[Heat engine|heat engines]] fueled by [[Combustion|combustion]] or [[Nuclear fission|nuclear fission]], but also by other means such as the [[Kinetic energy|kinetic energy]] of flowing water and wind. Other energy sources include solar photovoltaics and [[Geothermal power|geothermal power]]. There are exotic and speculative methods to recover energy, such as proposed fusion reactor designs which aim to directly extract energy from intense magnetic fields generated by fast-moving charged particles generated by the fusion reaction (see magnetohydrodynamics).',
			},
		},
		related = { "Cooling tower" },
	},
	{
		title = "Electronics",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🔭",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Branch of physics and electrical engineering",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Basics" },
		summary = "**Electronics** is a scientific and engineering discipline that studies and applies the principles of [[Physics|physics]] to design, create, and operate devices that manipulate [[Electron|electrons]] and other [[Electric charge|electrically charged particles]]. It is a subfield of physics and [[Electrical engineering|electrical engineering]] which uses active devices such as [[Transistor|transistors]], [[Diode|diodes]], and [[Integrated circuit|integrated circuits]] to control and [[Amplifier|amplify]] the flow of [[Electric current|electric current]] and to convert it from one form to another, such as from [[Alternating current|alternating current]] (AC) to [[Direct current|direct current]] (DC) or from [[Analog signal|analog]] signals to digital signals.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Electronic devices have significantly influenced the development of many aspects of modern society, such as [[Telecommunications|telecommunications]], entertainment, education, health care, industry, and security. The main driving force behind the advancement of electronics is the semiconductor industry, which continually produces ever-more sophisticated electronic devices and circuits in response to global demand. The semiconductor industry is one of the global economy's largest and most profitable industries, with annual revenues exceeding $481 billion in 2018.",
			},
