	{
		title = "Laundry",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "👕",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Washing of clothing and other textiles",
		tags = { "Industry", "Food, water and health" },
		summary = "**Laundry** is the washing of [[Clothing|clothing]] and other [[Textile|textiles]], and, more broadly, their drying and [[Ironing|ironing]] as well. Laundry has been part of history since [[Human|humans]] began to wear clothes, so the methods by which different cultures have dealt with this universal human need are of interest to several branches of scholarship.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Laundry work has traditionally been highly gendered, with the responsibility in most cultures falling to women (formerly known as laundresses or washerwomen). The [[Industrial Revolution]] gradually led to mechanized solutions to laundry work, notably the [[Washing machine|washing machine]] and later the tumble dryer. Laundry, like cooking and child care, is still done both at home and by commercial establishments outside the home. The word "laundry" may refer to the clothing itself, or to the place where the cleaning happens. An individual home may have a laundry room; a utility room includes, but is not restricted to, the function of washing clothes. An apartment building or student hall of residence may have a shared laundry facility. A stand-alone business is referred to as a self-service laundry (launderette in [[British English]] or laundromat in [[North American English]]).',
			},
		},
		related = { "Water purification" },
	},
	{
		title = "Light-emitting diode",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💡",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Semiconductor light source",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Components" },
		aliases = { "LED" },
		summary = "A **light-emitting diode** (**LED**) is an [[Electronic component|electronic component]] that uses a [[Semiconductor|semiconductor]] to [[Light|emit light]] when [[Electric current|current]] flows through it. [[Electron|Electrons]] in the semiconductor recombine with electron holes, thereby releasing energy in the form of [[Photon|photons]]. The color of the light (corresponding to the energy of the photons) is determined by the energy required for electrons to cross the band gap of the [[Semiconductor|semiconductor]]. White light is obtained by using multiple semiconductors or a layer of light-emitting [[Phosphor|phosphor]] on the semiconductor device.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Appearing as practical electronic components in 1962, the earliest LEDs emitted low-intensity [[Infrared|infrared]] (IR) light. Infrared LEDs are used in [[Remote control|remote-control]] circuits, such as those used with a wide variety of consumer electronics. The first visible-light LEDs were of low intensity and limited to red. Early LEDs were often used as indicator lamps, replacing small [[Light bulb|incandescent bulbs]], and in seven-segment displays. Later developments produced LEDs available in [[Visible spectrum|visible]], [[Ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] (UV), and infrared wavelengths with high, low, or intermediate light output; for instance, white LEDs suitable for room and outdoor lighting. LEDs have also given rise to new types of displays and sensors, while their high switching rates have uses in advanced communications technology.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Filter (signal processing)" },
		infobox = {
			{ "Type", "Active electronic component" },
			{ "Inventor", "H. J. Round (1907) Oleg Losev (1927) James R. Biard (1961) Nick Holonyak (1962)" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Liquid-crystal display",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💎",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Display that uses the light-modulating properties of liquid crystals",
		tags = { "Media and communication" },
		aliases = { "LCD" },
		summary = "A **liquid-crystal display** (**LCD**) is a [[Flat-panel display|flat-panel display]] or other electronically modulated optical device that uses the light-modulating properties of liquid crystals combined with [[Polarizer|polarizers]] to display information. Liquid crystals do not emit light directly but instead use a backlight or reflector to produce images in color or monochrome.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"LCDs are available to display arbitrary images (as in a general-purpose computer display) or fixed images with low information content, which can be displayed or hidden: preset words, digits, and seven-segment displays (as in a digital clock) are all examples of devices with these displays. They use the same basic technology, except that arbitrary images are made from a matrix of small [[Pixel|pixels]], while other displays have larger elements. LCDs are used in a wide range of applications, including LCD televisions, [[Computer monitor|computer monitors]], instrument panels, aircraft cockpit displays, and indoor and outdoor signage. Small LCD screens are common in LCD projectors and portable consumer devices such as [[Digital camera|digital cameras]], [[Clock|watches]], [[Calculator|calculators]], and [[Smartphone|mobile telephones]], including [[Smartphone|smartphones]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Livestock",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "🐾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Animals kept to produce material goods",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Animal husbandry" },
		summary = "**Livestock** are the [[Domestication|domesticated]] [[Animal|animals]] that are raised in an [[Agriculture|agricultural]] setting mainly to provide labor and produce diversified [[Animal product|animal products]] for human consumption such as [[Meat|meat]], eggs, [[Milk|milk]], [[Fur|fur]], [[Leather|leather]], and [[Wool|wool]]. The term is sometimes used to refer solely to animals which are raised for consumption, and sometimes used to refer solely to farmed [[Ruminant|ruminants]], such as [[Cattle|cattle]], [[Sheep|sheep]], and [[Goat|goats]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Animal husbandry practices have varied widely across cultures and periods. They continue to play a major economic and cultural role in numerous communities. Livestock farming practices have largely shifted to intensive animal farming. Such farming increases the yield of the various commercial outputs, but also impairs animal welfare, the [[Environmental degradation|environment]], and [[Public health|public health]]. In particular, beef, dairy and sheep are an outsized source of greenhouse gas emissions from agriculture.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Loudspeaker",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Converts an electrical audio signal into a corresponding sound",
		tags = { "Media and communication" },
		aliases = { "Speaker", "Speaker system" },
		summary = "A **loudspeaker** (commonly referred to as a **speaker** or, more fully, a **speaker system**) is a combination of one or more speaker drivers, an enclosure, and electrical connections (possibly including a crossover network). The speaker driver is an electroacoustic transducer that converts an electrical [[Audio signal|audio signal]] into a corresponding [[Sound|sound]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The driver is a linear motor connected to a diaphragm, which transmits the motor's movement to produce sound by moving air. An audio signal, typically originating from a microphone, recording, or radio broadcast, is electronically amplified to a power level sufficient to drive the motor, reproducing the sound corresponding to the original unamplified signal. This process functions as the inverse of a [[Microphone|microphone]]. In fact, the dynamic speaker driver—the most common type—shares the same basic configuration as a dynamic microphone, which operates in reverse as a [[Electric generator|generator]]. The dynamic speaker was invented in 1925 by Edward W. Kellogg and Chester W. Rice.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Amateur radio" },
	},
	{
		title = "MS-DOS",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Microsoft computer operating system",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Operating systems" },
		summary = '**MS-DOS** is an [[Operating system|operating system]] for x86-based [[Personal computer|personal computers]] mostly developed by [[Microsoft]]. Collectively, MS-DOS, its alternate branding as IBM PC DOS, and a few operating systems attempting to be compatible with MS-DOS, are sometimes referred to as "DOS" (which is also the generic acronym for disk operating system). MS-DOS was the main operating system for IBM PC compatibles during the 1980s, from which point it was gradually superseded by operating systems offering a [[Graphical user interface|graphical user interface]] (GUI), in various generations of the graphical [[Microsoft Windows]] operating system.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"IBM licensed and released it in 1981 as PC DOS 1.0 for use in its IBM Personal Computer. Although MS-DOS and PC DOS were initially developed in parallel by Microsoft and IBM, the two products diverged after twelve years, in 1993, with recognizable differences in compatibility, syntax and capabilities. Beginning in 1988 with DR-DOS, several competing products were released for the x86 platform. Initially, MS-DOS was targeted at Intel 8086 processors running on computer hardware using floppy disks to store and access not only the operating system, but application software and user data as well. Progressive version releases delivered support for other mass storage media in ever greater sizes and formats, along with added feature support for newer processors and rapidly evolving computer architectures.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Developer", "[[Microsoft]]" },
			{ "Family", "DOS" },
			{ "Released", "12 August 1981" },
			{ "Language", "[[English language|English]]" },
		},
