		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🪟",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Computer operating system",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Operating systems" },
		aliases = { "Windows" },
		summary = "**Windows** is a proprietary [[Operating system|operating system]] developed by [[Microsoft]]. Windows is grouped into families that cater to particular sectors of the computing industry – Windows for [[Personal computer|personal computers]], Windows Server for [[Server (computing)|servers]], and Windows IoT for [[Embedded system|embedded systems]]. Windows itself is further grouped into editions that cater to different users – Home for home users, Professional for advanced users, Education for schools, and Enterprise for corporations. Windows is sold both as a [[Retail|consumer retail]] product and to computer manufacturers, who bundle and distribute it with their systems.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The first version of Windows, Windows 1.0, was released on November 20, 1985 as a graphical operating system shell for [[MS-DOS]] in response to growing interest in [[Graphical user interface|graphical user interfaces]] (GUIs). The name Windows is a reference to the windowing system in GUIs. The 1990 release of Windows 3.0 catapulted its market success and led to the launch of various other product families, including the (now-defunct) Windows Mobile, Windows Phone, and Windows CE/Embedded Compact. Windows is the most popular desktop operating system in the world, with a 71% market share as of July 2026, and the second-most popular operating system overall, behind [[Android (operating system)|Android]]. As of July 2026, Windows 11 is the most used desktop version of Windows, with a market share of 69%.",
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Developer", "[[Microsoft]]" },
			{ "Released", "20 November 1985" },
			{ "Language", "110 languages" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Mobile device",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Small, handheld computing device",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Computer hardware" },
		aliases = { "Handheld device" },
		summary = "A **mobile device** or **handheld device** is a computer small enough to hold and operate in hand. Mobile devices are typically battery-powered and possess a [[Flat-panel display|flat-panel display]] and one or more built-in [[Input device|input devices]], such as a [[Touchscreen|touchscreen]] or keypad. Modern mobile devices often emphasize wireless networking, to both the [[Internet]] and to other devices in their vicinity, such as headsets or in-car entertainment systems, via [[Wi-Fi]], Bluetooth, cellular networks, or near-field communication.",
	},
	{
		title = "Natural gas",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Gaseous fossil fuel",
		tags = { "Industry", "Energy and fuel" },
		aliases = { "Methane gas", "Fossil gas" },
		summary = "**Natural gas** (also **gas**, **methane gas** or **fossil gas**) is a [[Fossil fuel|fossil fuel]], naturally occurring in geological formations. Typically, the gas is a mix of gaseous [[Hydrocarbon|hydrocarbons]], primarily [[Methane|methane]] (95%), small amounts of higher [[Alkane|alkanes]], and traces of carbon dioxide and [[Nitrogen|nitrogen]], [[Hydrogen sulfide|hydrogen sulfide]] and [[Helium|helium]]. Methane is a colorless and odorless gas, and, after carbon dioxide, is the second-greatest [[Greenhouse gas|greenhouse gas]] that contributes to global [[Climate change|climate change]]. Because natural gas is odorless, a commercial odorizer, such as methanethiol, that smells of hydrogen sulfide (rotten eggs) is added to the gas for the ready detection of gas leaks.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Natural gas is a [[Fossil fuel|fossil fuel]] that is formed when layers of [[Organic matter|organic matter]] (primarily marine microorganisms) are thermally decomposed under oxygen-free conditions, subjected to intense heat and pressure underground over millions of years. The energy that the decayed organisms originally obtained from the sun via [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]] is stored as chemical energy within the molecules of methane and other hydrocarbons. Most natural gas is collected from underground geological formations, often alongside other fossil fuels like [[Coal|coal]] and [[Petroleum|oil]] (petroleum). Natural gas is often a byproduct of petroleum production, where it can either be vented without burning, flared (burned off) or collected. The extraction and consumption of natural gas is a major industry. According to a 2025 IEA report, demand has increased in recent years.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Natural rubber",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Polymer harvested from certain trees",
		tags = { "Industry", "Material and chemical" },
		aliases = { "India rubber", "Latex", "Amazonian rubber", "Rubber" },
		summary = "**Rubber**, also called **India rubber**, **latex**, **Amazonian rubber**, **caucho**, or **caoutchouc**, as initially produced, consists of [[Polymer|polymers]] of the organic compound isoprene, with minor impurities of other organic compounds.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Types of polyisoprene that are used as natural rubbers are classified as elastomers. Currently, rubber is harvested mainly in the form of the latex from the Pará rubber tree (Hevea brasiliensis) or others. The latex is a sticky, milky and white colloid drawn off by making incisions in the bark and collecting the fluid in vessels in a process called "tapping". Manufacturers refine this latex into the rubber that is ready for commercial processing. Natural rubber is used extensively in many applications and products, either alone or in combination with other materials. In most of its useful forms, it has a large stretch ratio and high resilience and also is buoyant and water-proof. Industrial demand for rubber-like materials began to outstrip natural rubber supplies by the end of the 19th century, leading to the synthesis of synthetic rubber in 1909 by chemical means.',
			},
		},
		related = { "Abrasive" },
	},
	{
		title = "Nuclear power",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Power generated from nuclear reactions",
		tags = { "Industry", "Energy and fuel" },
		summary = "**Nuclear power** is the use of [[Nuclear reaction|nuclear reactions]] to produce [[Electricity|electricity]]. Nuclear power can be obtained from [[Nuclear fission|nuclear fission]], nuclear decay and [[Nuclear fusion|nuclear fusion]] reactions. Presently, the vast majority of electricity from nuclear power is produced by nuclear fission of [[Uranium|uranium]] and [[Plutonium|plutonium]] in [[Nuclear power plant|nuclear power plants]]. Nuclear decay processes are used in niche applications such as radioisotope thermoelectric generators in some space probes such as [[Voyager 2]]. Reactors producing controlled fusion power have been operated since 1958 but have yet to generate net power and are not expected to be commercially available in the near future.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The first [[Nuclear power plant|nuclear power plant]] was built in the 1950s. The global installed nuclear capacity grew to 100GW in the late 1970s, and then expanded during the 1980s, reaching 300GW by 1990. The 1979 Three Mile Island accident in the [[United States]] and the 1986 Chernobyl disaster in the [[Soviet Union]] resulted in increased regulation and public opposition to nuclear power plants. Nuclear power plants supplied 2,602 terawatt hours (TWh) of electricity in 2023, equivalent to about 9% of global electricity generation, and were the second largest low-carbon power source after [[Hydroelectricity|hydroelectricity]]. As of November 2025 there are 416 civilian fission reactors in the world, with overall capacity of 376GW, 63 under construction and 87 planned, with a combined capacity of 66GW and 84GW, respectively.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Oil refinery",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🛢️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Facility that processes crude oil",
		tags = { "Industry", "Energy and fuel" },
		aliases = { "Petroleum refinery" },
		summary = "An **oil refinery** or **petroleum refinery** is an industrial process [[Factory|plant]] where [[Petroleum|petroleum]] (crude oil) is transformed and refined into products such as [[Gasoline|gasoline]] (petrol), [[Diesel fuel|diesel fuel]], asphalt base, fuel oils, heating oil, [[Kerosene|kerosene]], liquefied petroleum gas and petroleum naphtha. [[Petrochemical]] feedstock like [[Ethylene|ethylene]] and propylene can also be produced directly by cracking crude oil without the need of using refined products of crude oil such as naphtha. The crude oil feedstock has typically been processed by an oil production plant. There is usually an oil depot at or near an oil refinery for the storage of incoming crude oil feedstock as well as bulk liquid products.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In 2020, the total capacity of global refineries for crude oil was about 101.2 million barrels per day. Oil refineries are typically large, sprawling industrial complexes with extensive piping running throughout, carrying streams of [[Fluid|fluids]] between large chemical processing units, such as [[Distillation|distillation]] columns. In many ways, oil refineries use many different technologies and can be thought of as types of [[Chemical plant|chemical plants]]. Since December 2008, the world's largest oil refinery has been the Jamnagar Refinery owned by Reliance Industries, located in [[Gujarat]], India, with a processing capacity of 1.24 Moilbbl per day. Oil refineries are an essential part of the petroleum industry's downstream sector.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Oil well",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🛢️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Well drilled to extract crude oil and/or gas",
		tags = { "Industry", "Energy and fuel" },
		summary = "An **oil well** is a drillhole boring in the [[Earth]] that is designed to bring [[Petroleum|petroleum]] oil hydrocarbons to the surface. Usually some [[Natural gas|natural gas]] is released as associated petroleum gas along with the oil. A well that is designed to produce only gas may be termed a **gas well**. Wells are created by drilling down into an oil or gas reserve and if necessary equipped with extraction devices such as pumpjacks. Creating the wells can be an expensive process, costing at least hundreds of thousands of dollars, and costing much more when in difficult-to-access locations, e.g., offshore.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The process of modern drilling for wells first started in the 19th century but was made more efficient with advances to oil drilling rigs and technology during the 20th century. Wells are frequently sold or exchanged between different oil and gas companies as an asset – in large part because during a drop in the price of oil and gas, a well may be unproductive, but if prices rise, even low-production wells may be economically valuable. Moreover, new methods, such as hydraulic fracturing (a process of injecting gas or liquid to force more oil or natural gas production) have made some wells viable. However, peak oil and climate policy surrounding fossil fuels have made fewer of these wells and costly techniques viable.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Open-source software",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Software with publicly available source code",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Computer software" },
		aliases = { "OSS" },
		summary = "**Open-source software** (**OSS**) is computer software whose [[Source code|source code]] is publicly available, allowing users to use, study, modify, and distribute it — in contrast with proprietary (closed-source) software. These abilities are typically granted through open licenses or, more rarely, by dedication to the [[Public domain|public domain]]. The development of open-source software can be based on open collaboration, a decentralized model of production. It is commonly defined according to the Open Source Definition of the Open Source Initiative, but the precise meaning of open source remains a source of conflicts.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Open-source software offers a range of benefits, but also presents specific risks and challenges. It can provide improved accessibility through lower and shared costs, greater adaptability through customization, transparency with auditable code, better interoperability via open standards, and independence from vendor lock-in, among others. Due to its ubiquity in modern software, open-source software raises major cybersecurity concerns, as well as significant maintenance challenges. Open-source software has become the backbone of the global digital infrastructure, powering domains such as web servers, cloud computing, supercomputers, mobile devices, artificial intelligence, and Internet of Things. A 2022 estimate suggests that between 80 and 96 percent of the code making up software on the market today, including proprietary software, is of open-source origin.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Word processor" },
	},
	{
		title = "Operating system",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 1,
		emoji = "💾",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Software that manages computer hardware resources",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Operating systems" },
		summary = "An **operating system** (**OS**) is system software that manages [[Computer hardware|computer hardware]] and software resources, and provides common services for [[Computer program|computer programs]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Time-sharing operating systems schedule tasks for efficient use of the system and may also include accounting software for cost allocation of processor time, mass storage, peripherals, and other resources. Operating systems are found on many devices that contain a computer – from cellular phones and video game consoles to [[Web server|web servers]] and [[Supercomputer|supercomputers]]. As of November 2025, [[Android (operating system)|Android]] is the most popular operating system, with a 38% market share, followed by [[Microsoft Windows]] at 33%, [[IOS|iOS]] and iPadOS at 15%, [[MacOS|macOS]] at 4%, and [[Linux]] at 1%. Android, iOS, and iPadOS are operating systems for mobile devices such as smartphones, while Windows, macOS, and Linux are for desktop computers. Linux distributions are dominant in the server and supercomputing sectors and number in the thousands.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Optical disc",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Flat, usually circular disc that encodes binary data",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Data storage" },
		summary = "An **optical disc** is a flat, usually disc-shaped object that stores information in the form of physical variations on its surface that can be read with a beam of light. Optical discs can be reflective, where the light source and detector are on the same side of the disc, or transmissive, where light shines through the disc to be detected on the other side. They may contain [[Analog signal|analog]] or [[Digital electronics|digital]] information, or a mixture of the two. Their main uses are the distribution of electronic media and data, and archival storage.",
		related = { "Read-only memory" },
	},
	{
		title = "Orchard",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Intentionally planted trees or shrubs that are maintained for food production",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Forms" },
		summary = "An **orchard** is an intentional plantation of [[Tree|trees]] or [[Shrub|shrubs]] that is maintained for [[Food|food]] production. Orchards comprise fruit- or [[Nut (fruit)|nut]]-producing trees that are generally grown for commercial production. Orchards are also sometimes a feature of large [[Garden|gardens]], where they serve an aesthetic as well as a productive purpose. A fruit garden is generally synonymous with an orchard, although it is set on a smaller, non-commercial scale and may emphasize berry shrubs in preference to fruit trees. Most [[Temperate climate|temperate]]-zone orchards are laid out in a regular grid, with a grazed or mown grass or bare [[Soil|soil]] base that makes maintenance and fruit gathering easy.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most modern commercial orchards are planted for a single variety of fruit. While the importance of introducing [[Biodiversity|biodiversity]] is recognized in [[Forest|forest]] plantations, introducing genetic diversity in orchard plantations by interspersing other trees might offer benefits. Orchards are sometimes concentrated near bodies of water where [[Climate|climatic]] extremes are moderated and [[Flower|blossom]] time is retarded until [[Frost|frost]] danger is past.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Organic farming",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🚜",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Type of agriculture that emphasizes natural and environmentally friendly techniques",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Forms" },
		aliases = { "Organic agriculture", "Ecological farming", "Biological farming" },
		summary = "**Organic farming**, also known as **organic agriculture** or **ecological farming**, or **biological farming**, is an agricultural system that emphasizes the use of naturally occurring, non-synthetic inputs, such as compost manure, green manure, and bone meal and places emphasis on techniques such as crop rotation, companion planting, and mixed cropping. [[Biological pest control]] methods, such as the fostering of insect predators, are also encouraged.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Organic agriculture can be defined as "an integrated farming system that strives for [[Sustainability|sustainability]], the enhancement of [[Soil fertility|soil fertility]] and biological diversity while, with rare exceptions, prohibiting synthetic pesticides, antibiotics, synthetic fertilizers, genetically modified organisms, and growth hormones". It originated early in the 20th century in reaction to rapidly changing farming practices. In 2019, the global area dedicated to certified organic agriculture amounted to 70 e6ha, with more than half of this area being in Australia. Organic standards are designed to allow the use of naturally occurring substances while prohibiting or severely limiting synthetic substances.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Oscilloscope",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "⏳",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Instrument for displaying time-varying signals",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Basics" },
		aliases = { "Oscillograph", "Scope", "O-scope" },
		summary = "An **oscilloscope** (formerly known as an **oscillograph**, informally **scope** or **O-scope**) is a type of electronic test instrument that graphically displays varying [[Voltage|voltages]] of one or more signals as a function of time. Their main purpose is capturing information on electrical signals for debugging, analysis, or characterization. The displayed [[Waveform|waveform]] can then be analyzed for properties such as [[Amplitude|amplitude]], [[Frequency|frequency]], rise time, time interval, distortion, and others. Originally, calculation of these values required manually measuring the waveform against the scales built into the screen of the instrument. Modern digital instruments may calculate and display these properties directly.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Oscilloscopes are used in the sciences, engineering, biomedical, automotive and the telecommunications industry. General-purpose instruments are used for maintenance of electronic equipment and laboratory work. Special-purpose oscilloscopes may be used to analyze an automotive ignition system or to display the waveform of the heartbeat as an electrocardiogram, for instance.",
			},
