	},
	{
		title = "Weed",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "🌱",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Plant considered undesirable in a particular place or situation",
		tags = { "Agriculture", "Basics" },
		summary = "A **weed** is a [[Plant|plant]] considered undesirable in a particular situation, growing where it conflicts with human preferences, needs, or goals. Plants with characteristics that make them hazardous, aesthetically unappealing, difficult to control in managed environments, or otherwise unwanted in [[Agriculture|farm land]], [[Orchard|orchards]], [[Garden|gardens]], lawns, [[Park|parks]], recreational spaces, residential and industrial areas, may all be considered weeds. The concept of weeds is particularly significant in [[Agriculture|agriculture]], where the presence of weeds in fields used to grow [[Crop|crops]] may cause major losses in yields.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'[[Invasive species]], plants introduced to an environment where their presence negatively impacts the overall functioning and biodiversity of the ecosystem, may also sometimes be considered weeds. [[Taxonomy (biology)|Taxonomically]], the term "weed" has no botanical significance, because a plant that is a weed in one context, is not a weed when growing in a situation where it is wanted. Some plants that are widely regarded as weeds are intentionally grown in gardens and other cultivated settings. For this reason, some plants are sometimes called beneficial weeds. Similarly, volunteer plants from a previous crop are regarded as weeds when growing in a subsequent crop. Thus, alternative nomenclature for the same plants might be hardy pioneers, cosmopolitan species, volunteers, "spontaneous urban vegetation," etc.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Welding",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Fabrication process for joining materials",
		tags = { "Industry", "Metallurgy" },
		summary = "**Welding** is a fabrication process that joins materials, usually [[Metal|metals]] or thermoplastics, primarily by using high temperatures to [[Melting|melt]] the parts together and allow them to cool, causing fusion. Common alternative methods include solvent welding (of thermoplastics) using chemicals to melt materials being bonded without heat, and solid-state welding processes which bond without melting, such as pressure, cold welding, and diffusion bonding.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Metal welding is distinct from lower-temperature bonding techniques such as brazing and [[Soldering|soldering]], which do not melt the base metal (parent metal) and instead require flowing a filler metal to solidify their bonds. In addition to melting the base metal in welding, a filler material is typically added to the joint to form a pool of molten material (the weld pool) that cools to form a joint that can be stronger than the base material. Welding processes use some form of shielding to protect the melting metals from being contaminated or [[Redox|oxidized]]. Many different energy sources can be used for welding, including a gas flame (chemical), an electric arc (electrical), a [[Laser|laser]], an electron beam, friction, and [[Ultrasound|ultrasound]]. While often an industrial process, welding may be performed in many different environments, including in the open air, underwater, and in [[Outer space|outer space]].",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Well",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Excavation or structure to provide access to groundwater",
		tags = { "Industry", "Food, water and health" },
		summary = "A **well** is an excavation or structure created on the earth by digging, driving, or [[Drilling|drilling]] to access [[Liquid|liquid]] resources, usually [[Water|water]]. The oldest and most common kind of well is a water well, to access [[Groundwater|groundwater]] in underground [[Aquifer|aquifers]]. The well water is drawn up by a pump, or using containers, such as buckets that are raised mechanically or by hand. Water can also be injected back into the aquifer through the well. Wells were first constructed at least eight thousand years ago and historically vary in construction from a sediment of a dry watercourse to the [[Qanat|qanats]] of Iran, and the stepwells and sakiehs of India.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Placing a lining in the well shaft helps create stability, and linings of wood or wickerwork date back at least as far as the [[Iron Age]]. Wells have traditionally been sunk by hand digging, as is still the case in rural areas of the developing world. These wells are inexpensive and low-tech as they use mostly manual labour, and the structure can be lined with [[Brick|brick]] or stone as the excavation proceeds. A more modern method called caissoning uses pre-cast reinforced concrete well rings that are lowered into the hole. Driven wells can be created in unconsolidated material with a well hole structure, which consists of a hardened drive point and a screen of perforated pipe, after which a pump is installed to collect the water. Deeper wells can be excavated by hand drilling methods or machine drilling, using a bit in a borehole.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Refrigeration", "Laundry" },
	},
	{
		title = "Wi-Fi",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "👪",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Family of wireless network protocols",
		tags = { "Computing and information technology", "Networks" },
		summary = "**Wi-Fi** is a family of wireless network protocols based on the IEEE 802.11 family of standards, which are commonly used for local area networking of devices and [[Internet]] access, allowing nearby digital devices to exchange data by [[Radio wave|radio waves]]. These are the most widely used computer networks, used globally in home and small office networks to link devices and to provide Internet access with wireless routers and wireless access points in public places such as coffee shops, restaurants, hotels, libraries, and airports.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Wi-Fi is a trademark of the Wi-Fi Alliance, which restricts the use of the term "Wi-Fi Certified" to products that successfully complete interoperability certification testing. Non-compliant hardware is simply referred to as WLAN, and it may or may not work with "Wi-Fi Certified" devices. As of 2017 the Wi-Fi Alliance consisted of more than 800 companies from around the world. As of 2019 over 3.05 billion Wi-Fi-enabled devices are shipped globally each year. Wi-Fi uses multiple parts of the IEEE 802 protocol family and is designed to work well with its wired sibling, [[Ethernet]]. Compatible devices can network through wireless access points with each other as well as with wired devices and the Internet. Different versions of Wi-Fi are specified by various IEEE 802.11 protocol standards, with different radio technologies determining radio bands, maximum ranges, and speeds that may be achieved.',
			},
		},
		infobox = {
			{ "Frequency", "2.4, 5 and 6 GHz" },
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Wind power",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Electrical power generation from wind",
		tags = { "Industry", "Energy and fuel" },
		summary = "**Wind power** is the use of [[Wind|wind]] energy to generate useful work. Historically, wind power was used by sails, [[Windmill|windmills]] and windpumps, but today it is mostly used to generate [[Electricity|electricity]]. This article deals only with wind power for electricity generation. Today, wind power is generated almost completely using [[Wind turbine|wind turbines]], generally grouped into wind farms and connected to the [[Electrical grid|electrical grid]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"In 2025, wind supplied about 2,700 TWh of electricity, which was over 8% of world electricity. With about 100 GW added during 2021, mostly in China and the United States, global installed wind power capacity exceeded 800 GW. 30 countries generated more than a tenth of their electricity from wind power in 2024 and wind generation has nearly tripled since 2015. To help meet the Paris Agreement goals to [[Climate change mitigation|limit climate change]], analysts say it should expand much faster – by over 1% of electricity generation per year. Wind power is a sustainable, [[Renewable energy|renewable energy]] source, and has a much smaller impact on the environment than burning [[Fossil fuel|fossil fuels]]. Wind power is variable, so it needs energy storage or other dispatchable generation energy sources to attain a reliable supply of electricity.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Wind turbine",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "💨",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Device to generate electricity from wind",
		tags = { "Energy and fuel", "Electricity generation and distribution" },
		summary = 'A **wind turbine** is a device that [[Wind power|converts]] the [[Kinetic energy|kinetic energy]] of [[Wind|wind]] into [[Electrical energy|electrical energy]]. As of 2024, hundreds of thousands of large turbines, in installations known as wind farms, were generating over 1,136 gigawatts of power, with 117 GW added each year. Wind turbines are an increasingly important source of intermittent [[Renewable energy|renewable energy]], and are used in many countries to lower energy costs and reduce reliance on [[Fossil fuel|fossil fuels]]. One study claimed that, as of 2009 wind had the "lowest relative greenhouse gas emissions, the least water consumption demands and the most favorable social impacts" compared to photovoltaic, [[Hydroelectricity|hydro]], [[Geothermal power|geothermal]], coal and gas energy sources.',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'Wind turbines are manufactured in a wide range of sizes, with either horizontal or vertical axes, though horizontal is most common. Commercial power production horizontal-axis turbines usually have three blades, upwind of their towers. Vertical-axis turbines can be made to varied designs, including the "eggbeater" Darrieus, the giromill with straight blades, the Savonius with scoops suitable for rooftops and ships, airborne with wings tethered to the ground, floating (mounted on a floating platform), and various unconventional types such as with counter-rotating blades. Blades are most commonly made of glass fiber composites, but carbon fiber which is stiffer, stronger, and less dense is also used. Smaller wind turbines are used for applications such as battery charging and remote devices such as traffic warning signs.',
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Windmill",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "⚙️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Machine that makes use of wind energy",
		tags = { "Industry", "Energy and fuel" },
		summary = "A **windmill** is a machine operated by the force of wind acting on vanes or sails to mill grain (gristmills). Windmills were used throughout the [[High Middle Ages|high medieval]] and [[Early modern period|early modern periods]]; the horizontal or panemone windmill first appeared in [[Iran|Persia]] during the 9th century, and the vertical windmill first appeared in [[Northwestern Europe|northwestern Europe]] in the 12th century.",
	},
	{
		title = "Wire",
		category = "Technology",
		fame = 2,
		emoji = "💻",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Single, usually cylindrical, flexible strand or bar or rod of metal",
		tags = { "Electronics", "Components" },
		summary = "A **wire** is a flexible, round bar of [[Metal|metal]]. Wires are commonly formed by drawing the metal through a hole in a die or draw plate. Wire gauges come in various standard sizes, as expressed in terms of a gauge number or cross-sectional area.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Wires are used to bear mechanical loads, often in the form of [[Wire rope|wire rope]]. In [[Electricity|electricity]] and telecommunications signals, wire can refer to [[Electrical cable|electrical cable]], which can contain a solid core of a single wire or separate strands in stranded or braided forms. Usually [[Cylinder|cylindrical]] in geometry, wire can also be made in square, hexagonal, flattened rectangular, or other cross-sections, either for decorative purposes, or for technical purposes such as high-efficiency voice coils in [[Loudspeaker|loudspeakers]].",
