	{
		title = "Hovercraft",
		category = "Vehicle",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🚗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Air cushion vehicle",
		tags = { "Transportation", "Maritime transport" },
		summary = "Hovercraft use blowers to produce a large volume of air below the hull, or air cushion, that is slightly above [[Atmospheric pressure|atmospheric pressure]]. The pressure difference between the higher-pressure air below the hull and lower pressure ambient air above it produces lift, which causes the hull to float above the running surface. For stability reasons, the air is typically blown through slots or holes around the outside of a disk- or oval-shaped platform, giving most hovercraft a characteristic rounded-rectangle shape.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The first practical design for hovercraft was derived from a British invention in the 1950s. They are now used throughout the world as specialised transports in disaster relief, coastguard, military and survey applications, as well as for sport or passenger service. Very large versions have been used to transport hundreds of people and vehicles across the [[English Channel]], whilst others have military applications used to transport tanks, soldiers and large equipment in hostile environments and terrain. Decline in public demand meant that as of 2025, only two year-round public hovercraft service in the world are still in operation: Hovertravel, which serves between the [[Isle of Wight]] and Southsea in the UK, and Oita Hovercraft resumed services in Oita, Japan in July 2025.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Cargo ship", "Sailing" },
	},
	{
		title = "Jet aircraft",
		category = "Vehicle",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "✈️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Aircraft class powered by jet propulsion engines",
		tags = { "Transportation", "Aviation" },
		aliases = { "Jet" },
		summary = "A **jet aircraft** (or simply **jet**) is an [[Airplane|aircraft]] propelled by one or more [[Jet engine|jet engines]]. Jets are nearly always [[Fixed-wing aircraft|fixed-wing aircraft]], though a wide range of different types of jet aircraft exists – both for civilian and military purposes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Most jet aircraft need to fly at high speeds – either supersonic, or just below the speed of sound (transonic) in order to achieve efficient flight. They typically cruise around Mach 0.8 (981 km/h; 610 mph), and at altitudes around 10,000–15,000 m (33,000–49,000 ft) or higher, where jet engines operate most efficiently. This contrasts with propeller-driven aircraft, which achieve peak efficiency at much lower speeds and altitudes. Jet aircraft are usually designed using the Whitcomb area rule, which states that the total area of the cross-section of an aircraft must be approximately the same as that of a Sears–Haack body. The application of this rule minimizes the production of shockwaves, which waste energy.",
			},
		},
	},
