	{
		title = "Traffic",
		category = "Vehicle",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🚗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Movement of vehicles and pedestrians along land routes",
		tags = { "Transportation", "Road transport" },
		summary = "**Traffic** is the movement of [[Transport|vehicles]] and pedestrians along land routes.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"**Traffic laws** govern and regulate traffic, while **rules of the road** include traffic laws and [[Social norm|informal rules]] that may have developed over time to facilitate the orderly and timely flow of traffic. Organized traffic generally has well-established priorities, lanes, right-of-way, and traffic control at intersections. (International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea govern the oceans and influence some laws for navigating domestic waters.) Traffic is formally organized in many jurisdictions, with marked lanes, junctions, intersections, interchanges, traffic signals, cones, or signs. Traffic is often classified by type: heavy [[Motor vehicle|motor vehicle]] (e.g., [[Car|car]], [[Truck|truck]]), other vehicle (e.g., moped, [[Bicycle|bicycle]]), and pedestrian. Different classes may share speed limits and easement, or may be segregated.",
			},
		},
		related = { "Filling station" },
	},
	{
		title = "Trail",
		category = "Vehicle",
		fame = 3,
		emoji = "🚗",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Path for mostly non-motorized travel through a natural area",
		tags = { "Transportation", "Road transport" },
		aliases = { "Path", "Track" },
		summary = "A **trail**, also known as a **path** or **track**, is a dirt road or a small paved [[Road|road]], usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading [[Motor vehicle|motor vehicles]] may also use it. In the [[United Kingdom]] and [[Republic of Ireland|Ireland]], footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to [[Highway|highways]] in [[North America]]. In the [[United States]], the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails (e.g. the Oregon Trail), which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some trails are restricted to use by only walkers, cyclists or equestrians, or for cattle driving, snowshoeing or cross-country skiing, while others (e.g. bridleways in the UK) are shared-use and can be used by walkers, cyclists and equestrians. Although most trail ban use by motorized vehicles, there are unpaved trails that allow dirt bikes, quad bikes and other off-road vehicles, usually for extreme sports and rally races. In some mountainous regions like the [[Alps]], trails are used by alpine [[Agrarian society|agrarian communities]] for moving livestock between grazing pastures.",
			},
		},
	},
	{
		title = "Train station",
